03183ngmaa2200457 i 9919254416106676DNLM20260226080542.0m     o  c        cr#cna|||a||da240411s2020    ncu015        o   vceng    aDNLMbengerdaeamimcDNM0 aengaspajeng  9United States00aWX 16500aKeepers of the house /ca film by Rhonda Klevansky, Neil Prose, Francesca Talenti ; a project of the Duke University Health Humanities Lab@FHI.  aUnited States 1a[Durham, N.C.] :bDuke, Health Humanites Lab, Franklin Humanities Institute]c[2020?]  a1 online resource (1 video file (approximately 15 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aVideography and editing, Rhonda Klevansky ; animation, Francesca Talenti ; music, JAlbert music; interviewers: Thomas Johnson, Antonia Munoz Salas.0 aInterviewees: Maybelline Barahona, Lorna Fashola, Rosetta McCallum, Isaac Johnson, Barbara Myers, Eddie Oakley, Hilda Self, LaShara Springs, Maria "Gladys" Velasquez ; interviewers: Thomas Johnson, Antonia Munoz Salas.  a"Every day of the week, environmental service workers enter hospital patients' rooms to clean. Their work may bring a measure of dignity and connection to people who are sick, vulnerable, and away from their familiar homes. Because these workers are assigned to the same specific areas of the hospital, they often develop meaningful relationships with patients and their families. In this 15 minute documentary video, eight housekeepers at Duke Hospital and Duke Regional Hospital talk about their special human relationships with patients and the ways in which they believe their work contributes to healing. It is our hope that the film will enable health care providers and students to see the housekeepers in a new light, and give serious consideration to the vital themes of empathy, humility, and teamwork"--Health Humanities Lab, Franklin Humanities Institute website.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aIn English and some Spanish, with English subtitles.1 awill transform digitallyc20260224xborn digital acquisition2pda5DNLM0 aOnline resource; title from title screen (viewed February 24, 2026).22aDuke University.bHospital22aDuke Regional Hospital.12aHousekeeping, Hospital22aHospital-Patient Relations 2aDocumentaries and Factual Films 2aPersonal Narrative1 aKlevansky, Rhonda,efilmmaker,eeditor of moving image work.1 aProse, Neil S.,efilmmaker.1 aTalenti, Francesca,eanimator.2 aJohn Hope Franklin Humanities Institute.bHealth Humanities Lab,eproduction company,eissuing body.4 ahttps://trentcenter.duke.edu/keepers-house-documentary-film  aAUTH02744nam a2200493 i 9919254415606676DNLM20260227085550.0m     o  d        cr#cnu---unuuu250808s2024    vauab   ob   s000 0 eng    a1573151281  a9781962841139q(electronic bk.)  a1962841138q(electronic bk.)  z9781962841122  z196284112X  aN$TbengerdaepncN$TdOCLCOdSFBdOCLCO  apcc  9United States 4aR702b.C43 2024eb00aW 6100aChallenging stories :bexploring the intersections between health and the humanities /c[edited by] E. Thomas Ewing and Priyanka Ganguly. 1aBlacksburg, VA :bVirginia Tech Publishing,c2024.  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (viii, 195 pages)) :billustrations, map  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  a"Published in cooperation with the National Endowment for the Humanities and th National Library of Medicine, National Institutes of Health"--PDF p. 4 of cover.  aIncludes bibliographical references.0 3Some versions:aOpen access versions available from some providers  aChallenging Stories explores experiences of demanding health conditions and attitudes in response to them, across historical and contemporary contexts. This collection of original scholarship brings an interdisciplinary perspective to topics such as maternal health, care for aging adults, births by midwives, family planning, infectious disease outbreaks, biosafety health protocols, blood transfusions, and plant-based health practices. The case studies explored in each chapter contribute to a deeper and richer understanding of how challenging stories are embedded in an appreciation of community and individual health.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.u  https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/ 5DNLM1 awill transform digitallyc20260224xborn digital acquisition2pda5DNLM  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed on February 26, 2026)12aHumanities12aHistory of Medicine22aInterdisciplinary Studies22aNarration1 aEwing, E. Thomas,d1965-eeditor.1 aGanguly, Priyankac(Ph. D. in rhetoric and writing),eeditor.2 aNational Endowment for the Humanities.2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.)40uhttps://doi.org/10.21061/challenging-stories  aAUTH03426nam a2200445 i 9919254415506676DNLM20260227085724.0m     o  d        cr|cn#||||||||260224s2024    vauac   ob    001 0 eng    a9781962841962q(pdf)  a9781962841979q(epub)  z9781962841986 (print)  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aZ 675.M400aCirculating now, full circle :bfrom the historical collections of the National Library of Medicine /cedited by Elizabeth A. Mullen and Jeffrey S. Reznick. 1aBlacksburg, VA :bVirginia Tech Publishing,c2024.  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (xix, 339 pages)) :billustrations, portraits  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  aIn 2013, the National Library of Medicine (NLM) launched its Circulating Now blog to convey the vitality of medical history in our twenty-first-century world. The idea involved welcoming individuals to share stories of their engagement with the NLM collection—which spans eleven centuries, encompasses a variety of digital and physical formats, and originates from nearly every part of the world—as well as stories of the human condition preserved in this vast corpus. A decade on and with hundreds of posts published, Circulating Now has demonstrated the public’s esteem for the NLM collection and has been recognized by The Washington Post as "varied, lively and sometimes surprising." This book is an open access, edited collection of curated posts from Circulating Now. Its introduction presents the strategic conception and impact of the blog as a dynamic library and management resource. Six thematic chapters follow, each copiously illustrated and introduced with a new and original essay describing the content development and reflecting on the programmatic and intellectual significance of the selected posts. This book brings Circulating Now full circle in the twenty-first-century publishing ecosystem, creating a new access point for researchers and augmenting the original blog as a deep knowledge base of searchable information about the Library and its collection. Preserved in the NLM collection and circulating to new readers, Circulating Now, Full Circle will testify in perpetuity to the Library’s timeless stewardship of its globally appreciated collection and its public service to the world.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.u  https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/ 5DNLM1 awill transform digitallyc20260224xborn digital acquisition2pda5DNLM  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed on February 26, 2026)22aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.).tCirculating now.22aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.).12aLibraries, Medical22aHistory of Medicine22aLibrary Services22aBlogging 2aCollected Work1 aMullen, Elizabeth A.,eeditor.1 aReznick, Jeffrey S.q(Jeffrey Stephen),eeditor40uhttps://doi.org/10.21061/circulating-now  aAUTH02830nam a2200385 i 9919252114306676DNLM20260223131114.0m     o  d i      cr|un#||||||||260202s2025    sz b   |ob   i000|0 eng    a9789240118164q(electronic)  z9789240118171q(print)  a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK620050  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Switzerland00aWC 5032 aWorld Health Organization,eauthor,eissuing body.10aWHO guidelines on the management of advanced HIV disease. 1aGeneva :bWorld Health Organization,c[2025]  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (xii, 69 pages)) :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 aAdvanced HIV disease is the major cause of AIDS-related deaths among people living with HIV. In 2017, WHO recommended a package of care that should be provided to everyone with advanced HIV disease to help to reduce mortality and morbidity, along with rapid initiation of antiretroviral therapy (ART). Subsequently, WHO developed recommendations for the management of cryptococcal disease and histoplasmosis, both important fungal  infections in advanced HIV disease, as well as key diagnostic approaches to identify tuberculosis (TB) in advanced HIV disease. These guidelines were integrated into a chapter in the 2021 WHO consolidated guidelines on HIV prevention, testing, treatment, service delivery and monitoring: recommendations for a public health approach. Encouraging HIV programs to link these clinical recommendations with WHO recommendations for service delivery is critical, especially for ensuring sustained engagement as well as reengagement in services to both identify and prevent advanced HIV disease. These 2025 guidelines respond to the need for better approaches to identify advanced HIV disease, improve the poor outcomes of people living with HIV being discharged from hospital and provide updated guidance for treatment for Kaposi’s sarcoma through evidence-informed recommendations. The publication contains recommendations that are from previously published WHO guidelines documents and introduces new recommendations developed in 2025.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed February 23, 2025).12aHIV Infectionsxdrug therapy22aHIV Infectionsxcomplications.22aHIV Infectionsxprevention & control22aAIDS-Related Opportunistic Infectionsxprevention & control 2aPractice Guideline40uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK620050/  aAUTH02603nam a2200397 i 9919252110406676DNLM20260224130408.0m     o  d i      cr|un#||||||||260202s2025    sz a    ob   i000|0 eng    a9789240119468q(electronic)  z9789240119475q(print)  a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK620317  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Switzerland00aWC 503.22 aWorld Health Organization,eauthor,eissuing body.10aWHO updated recommendations on HIV clinical management: recommendations for a public health approach. 1aGeneva :bWorld Health Organization,c[2025]  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (xiv pages)) :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 aThis document provides an overview of the updated World Health Organization recommendations for HIV clinical management, which focus on optimizing antiretroviral therapy, preventing vertical transmission, and enhancing tuberculosis prevention among individuals with HIV. These updates are intended to support global initiatives aimed at ending AIDS as a public health threat by 2030. Key changes include the introduction of new antiretroviral drugs and regimens, revised postnatal prophylaxis and breastfeeding guidelines for managing infants at risk of vertical transmission, and the endorsement of shorter tuberculosis preventive treatments to improve efficacy and adherence. The recommendations are designed to simplify and advance HIV care, promote equity, and contribute to improved health outcomes and quality of life for individuals living with HIV. The updated guidance will be incorporated into the forthcoming revision of the antiretroviral therapy chapter within the Consolidated Guidelines on HIV Prevention, Testing, Treatment, Service Delivery, and Monitoring: Recommendations for a Public Health Approach.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed February 24, 2026).12aHIV Infectionsxdrug therapy12aAntiretroviral Therapy, Highly Activexmethods22aAnti-Retroviral Agentsxtherapeutic use22aHIV Infectionsxprevention & control22aInfectious Disease Transmission, Verticalxprevention & control 2aPractice Guideline40uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK620317/  aAUTH01898nam a2200385 i 9919252114006676DNLM20260223135844.0m     o  d i      cr|un#||||||||260202s2025    sz a   |ob   i000|0 eng    a9789240115774q(electronic)  z9789240115781q(print)  a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK620423  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Switzerland00aWP 5702 aSpecial Programme of Research, Development, and Research Training in Human Reproduction (World Health Organization),eauthor10aGuideline for the prevention, diagnosis and treatment of infertility. 1aGeneva :bWorld Health Organization, Human Reproduction Programme,c[2025].  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (xxii, 237 pages)) :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 aThe objective of this guideline is to provide evidence-based recommendations to prevent, diagnose and treat infertility. It provides a source for countries to adopt, adapt or update their national clinical guidelines for infertility. Implementing the recommendations contained in this guideline will mitigate global inequities in the availability, accessibility, acceptability and quality of fertility care, and support universal access to healthcare.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed February 23, 2026).12aInfertilityxtherapy22aInfertilityxdiagnosis22aInfertilityxprevention & control 2aPractice Guideline2 aWorld Health Organization,eissuing body.40uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK620423/  aAUTH01068nam a2200277 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400025000800440018001050600024001231000039001472450115001862640108003013000027004093360026004363370028004623380027004905040041005176300012005586500012005706500029005827100072006117100098006839990009007819919253714006676DNLM20260220080950.0260217s1976    mdu      b   f000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  9United States00aZ 695.9bV259e 19771 aVan Lenten, Elizabeth J.,eauthor.10aEnzyme key, 1977 :btechnical notes : MEDLARS indexing instructions  supplement /cby Elizabeth J. Van Lenten. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :bNational Library of Medicine, Bibliographic Services Division, Index Section,c1977.  aii, 20 pages ;c28  cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.00aMEDLARS12aEnzymes12aAbstracting and Indexing2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.).bIndex Section,eissuing body.2 iComplemented by (work):aCommission on Biochemical Nomenclature.tEnzyme nomenclature.f1973.  aAUTH00914nam a2200277 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400025000800440018001050600024001231000059001472450069002062640060002753000021003353360026003563370028003823380026004105000019004365000016004555010060004716500049005316500036005806500011006169990009006279919253515906676DNLM20260223081201.0260210s1955    xxu           000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.221 aKark, Robert M.q(Robert Manoah),d1911-2002,eauthor.10aPyridoxine (vitamin B6) deficiency in infants /cRobert M. Kark. 1a[United States] :b[publisher not identified],c[1955?]  a1 sheet ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  asheetbnb2