now313Dec. 2015- :aNew York :bElsevier  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 35, no. 6 (Nov./Dec. 2012).1 aMEDLINEbv35n6, Nov./Dec. 2012-0 aPubMedbv35n6, Nov./Dec. 2012-0 aPMC  aAlso issued in print.  aDescription based on: Vol. 35, no. 6 (Nov./Dec. 2012); title from journal home page (viewed Jan. 24, 2013).  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 36, no. 4 (July/Aug. 2013).12aMedicine 2aPeriodical2 aChanggeng da xue.0 x2319-417000tChang Gung medical journalx2072-0939w(DLC)   2006243672w(OCoLC)7093858040uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=10159982040uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/23194170  aAUTH01905cgm a22004454a 101486642DNLM20260227152703.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081007s2008    mdu052 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14659  a(OCoLC)288982867  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWP 3221 aKohn, Elise C.10aTranslational trials and tribulations /cElise C. Kohn.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCenter for Cancer Research--National Cancer Institute grand rounds  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 2, 2008).  aStreaming video (51 min., 13 sec. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Sept. 23, 2008.3 a(CIT):  Dr. Kohn joined the Laboratory of Pathology to investigate signal transduction molecular targets in invasion, angiogenesis, and ovarian cancer, and maintained her clinical focus in the translational clinical studies of ovarian cancer. Her clinical work has evolved to apply new proteomic technologies to early detection testing and validation of therapeutic targets in clinical trials.  Dr. Kohn has been investigating targeting molecular events of the local tumor microenvironment.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aOvarian Neoplasmsxtherapy22aSignal Transduction 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14659  aAUTH03520cgm a2200421 i 101635878DNLM20260227154946.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140627s2014    mdu066|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18490  a(OCoLC)885014958  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWD 7581 aHopkins, Michael S.eauthor.10aHow to train like an astronaut /cColonel Michael S. Hopkins. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 6 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Jun. 10, 2014.3 a(CIT): The National Institute of Arthritis and Musculoskeletal and Skin Diseases (NIAMS) is proud to present, "How To Train Like an Astronaut," by NASA astronaut Michael S. Hopkins, Colonel, U.S. Air Force.  Astronauts are required to be in optimal health prior to launch. But how do they remain fit while in space for a prolonged period of time, and how do they readjust to Earth"s gravity once they have returned? How does microgravity affect a person"s overall health, and what can we learn about the human body from these experiences? Hear from a NASA astronaut who recently returned to Earth after an extended stay on the International Space Station (ISS). He will share how he trained and prepared for his mission, and how his experiences are helping scientists understand the health effects of living in space. He will also discuss some of the biomedical experiments conducted on the ISS.   About the Presenter: Col. Michael S. Hopkins was selected in July 2009 as one of 14 members of the 20th NASA astronaut class. He graduated from Astronaut Candidate Training in November 2011, which included scientific and technical briefings. He also received intensive instruction in ISS systems, extravehicular activity, robotics, physiological training, flight training, and water and wilderness survival training.    On Sept. 25, 2013, Col. Hopkins accompanied Russian cosmonauts to the ISS, launching from the Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan. He remained on the station for 166 days, returning to Earth on March 10, 2014. For two months after his return, he underwent scientific tests to determine how his extended stay in a microgravity environment affected his body.   Originally from Missouri, Col. Hopkins received a Bachelor of Science in Aerospace Engineering from the University of Illinois in 1991, and a Master of Science in Aerospace Engineering from Stanford University in 1992.   For more information on the ISS: http://www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/station/main/index.html   For more information on the biomedical experiments conducted on ISS: http://www.niams.nih.gov/News_and_Events/NIH_NASA_Activities/.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed July 9, 2014.12aExtraterrestrial Environment22aAerospace Medicine22aPhysical Fitness 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Arthritis and Musculoskeletal and Skin Diseases (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18490  aAUTH02072cas a2200517 i 9918250414706676DNLM20260227090925.0m     o  d        cr#|||||||||||200713c20219999njubr poo     |   a0eng    a  20202044660 a2692-9384l2692-938421  a(OCoLC)1164699382  aDLCbengerdad20260227cDLCdOCLCOdDLCdOCLCOdOCLCFdCASUMdVT2dNLMdDNLM  apccansdp  9United States00aBF72100aW110a155.422310aJCPP Adv2dnlm0 aJCPP adv. 0aJCPP advances00aJCPP advances.1 aJournal of child psychology and psychiatry advances 1a[Hoboken, NJ] :bJohn Wiley & Sons, Inc.,c[2021]-  a1 online resource  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBeg