rk of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aHIV-122aCapsid Proteins22aGene Products, gag22aSpectrometry, Mass, Electrospray Ionization 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11638  aAUTH02192cgm a22004814a 101193208DNLM20260227144308.0vf|cbahoucr|cnu||||||||vf|czahmu031006s2003    mdu054       f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:2580  a(OCoLC)55008264  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQH 450.2bVC no.7 200304aThe George Khoury legacy :bfrom transcription to regenerative medicine /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.30aFrom transcription to regenerative medicine  a[Bethesda, Md.] :bNational Institutes of Health,c[2003]  a1 videocassette of 1 (DVCPro) (54 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/4 in.3master.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (53 min., 30 sec.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aThe NIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture. The George Khoury lecture  aSpeaker, Peter Gruss.  aPresented Sept. 17, 2003.  aAddresses the role of transcription factors in setting up specific cellular programs. Emphasis is on the functional significance of a specific class of transcription factors, Pax4 and Pax6, in the differentiation of the islets of Langerhans and the possibility of utilizing these factors in the regeneration of insulin-producing B-cells.  aAlso available online.  aVHS.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aGene Expression Regulation12aTranscription Factors22aIslets of Langerhansxphysiology22aRegeneration1 aGruss, Peter.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pGeorge Khoury lecture4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=2580  aAUTH02263cgm a22004214a 101535294DNLM20260227153541.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100702s2010    mdu061|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15988  a(OCoLC)667592374  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5681 aPark, Hyunq(Jung-Hyun)10aRegulating cytokine signaling by common G-chain (GC) expression /c[Hyun Park].  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 1 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 23, 2010.3 a(CIT): Upon completion of a remarkably productive postdoctoral fellowship in the NCI during which he discovered coreceptor tuning and the importance of IL-7 receptor modulation in T cell homeostasis, Hyun Park assumed his current position as a tenure track investigator in the Experimental Immunology Branch, NCI.  Hyun received his PhD from the University of Wurzburg, Germany, in the lab of Thomas Huenig where he studied regulation of IL-2 receptor expression during CD8 T cell development and did his postdoctoral training in Al Singer"s lab in the NCI.  In the two years since starting his own laboratory, Hyun has focused on the mechanisms regulating cytokine receptor expression.  In this seminar, Hyun will present unpublished work that identifies novel mechanisms of common gamma-chain (gc) cytokine receptor expression/signaling that control cytokine responsiveness in T cells.   The Immunology Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aCytokinesxphysiology22aReceptors, Antigen, T-Cellxphysiology22aSignal Transductionxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15988  aAUTH03219cgm a2200493 i 101706997DNLM20260227155748.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u170603s2017    mdu057|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:23291  a(OCoLC)32374669  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 13800aBrave new world :brecent evolution of an insect-transmitted pathogen /cRoberto Kolter.1 aRecent evolution of an insect-transmitted pathogen 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2017]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (57 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded May 17, 2017.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series  Any population of organisms, be it made up of animals or plants, is susceptible to invasion by pathogens. Moreover, human-population migration, habitat simplification, and domestication of plants and animals have created novel ecological niches. Some pathogens are able to emerge into these niches and cause diseases that threaten public health and agricultural production. Despite the fact that such pathogens are common, there is often a lack of detailed studies that track their evolutionary origin and the effects of the population's ecological community structure on pathogen virulence, transmission, and epidemic persistence.   For his lecture, Dr. Kolter will discuss the studies his lab is carrying out to understand the evolution and ecology of a fatal bacterial wilt disease transmitted by the striped cucumber beetle (Acalymma vittatum) and caused by the bacterium Erwinia tracheiphila. This bacterial wilt disease is an annual epidemic of cucurbit crop populations (squashes, cucumbers, etc.) that is geographically restricted to plants grown in the temperate zones of the Northeastern and Midwestern United States. Dr. Kolter's laboratory results suggest that the pathogen evolved relatively recently, and the disease gained epidemic proportions as a consequence of Old World hypersusceptible cucurbits (cucumbers, melons) being introduced into North America and the unique ecology of the affected areas.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed June 14, 2017).12aBiological Evolution12aErwiniaxpathogenicity22aColeopteraxpathogenicity22aCucurbitaceaexgenetics22aCucurbitaceaexmicrobiology22aEnterobacteriaceae Infectionsxepidemiology22aGeography 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aKolter, Roberto,d1953-espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23291  aAUTH01690cgm a2200433 a 101587423DNLM20260227154306.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120630s2012    mdu027|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17310  a(OCoLC)801918111  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWI 1491 aHelman, L.q(Lee)14aThe 9th GIST Clinic invitational lecture /cLee Helman.1 aNinth GIST Clinic invitational lecture  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (27 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH GIST Clinic invitational lecture ;v9th  aRecorded June 13, 2012.3 a(CIT): The 9th GIST Clinic Invitational Lecture will feature a talk titled, "The NIH Pediatric and wildtype GIST Clinic: What we have learned and where we are going." It will also feature conclusions reached using data from the past 8 clinics and transitioning the clinic into Clinical trials based on these conclusions.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aGastrointestinal Stromal Tumors22aChild 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aPediatric GIST Clinic invitational lecture ;v8th.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17310  aAUTH01522cas a2200445 i 101749846DNLM20260206170718.0