n. 2015- ; selected citations only before this date  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: Vol. 1, no. 1 (Jan. 2011); title from title page.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 1, no. 2 (Apr. 2011).12aHypertension, Pregnancy-Induced 2aPeriodical2 aInternational Society for the Study of Hypertension in Pregnancy.0 x2210-77974 uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/22107789  aAUTH01414cas a22004694a 101093076DNLM20260227090907.0cr|cn#||||||||010410c20019999ne bn p       0    0eng    a  2001243145  a1566-5240l1566-5240  a(OCoLC)47091629  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9Netherlands00aW1bCU799D10aCurr Mol Med2dnlm00aCurrent molecular medicine.  aHilversum, The Netherlands ;aBoca Raton, FL :bBentham Science Publishers,cc2001-  av.  aNine no. a year,b2009-  aBimonthly,b2001  a8 no. a year,b2002-2008  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Mar. 2001)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, Mar. 2001-0 aPubMedbv1n1, Mar. 2001-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3v. 1, no. 1 (Mar 2001)c20020429fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper12aGenetics, Medical22aGenetic Therapy22aMolecular Biology 2aPeriodical 2aReview0 x1875-56664 uhttp://www.eurekaselect.com/600/journal/current-molecular-medicine  aAUTH03315cgm a2200493 i 9918351571206676DNLM20260227161937.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220226s2022    mdu117|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32189  a(OCoLC)1330399843  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 19400aDemystifying medicine.pEpigenetic contribution to autoimmune diseases /cLindsey Criswell. Anticytokine autoantibodies : important underappreciated cofactors in rare and common conditions / Steven Holland.1 aEpigenetic contribution to autoimmune diseases1 iTitle on PowerPoint:aEpigenetic contribution to systemic lupus erythematosus1 aAnticytokine autoantibodies 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 57 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded February 8, 2022.3 a(CIT): Demystifying Medicine  Dr. Criswell is the director of the National Institute of Arthritis and Musculoskeletal and Skin Diseases (NIAMS), appointed in February 2021.  A board-certified rheumatologist, her research has focused on the genetics and epidemiology of human autoimmune disease, particularly rheumatoid arthritis and systemic lupus erythematosus. Criswell's research team contributed to the identification of more than 30 genes linked to these disorders using genome-wide association and other genetic studies. The title of her talk is "Epigenetic Contribution to Autoimmune Diseases."  Dr. Holland is an NIH Distinguished Investigator and director of the intramural program at the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (NIAID).  As chief of the NIAID Immunopathogenesis Section, he employs an integrated bench-to-bedside model in laboratory work and in the clinical appreciation of disease, which together add new insights into mechanisms of action and avenues of therapy.  Holland's expertise includes immune defects of phagocytes and also cytokines and their receptors in the pathogenesis and therapy of infections.  The title of his talk is "Anticytokine Autoantibodies: Important Underappreciated Cofactors in Rare and Common Conditions."  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed June 7, 2022).12aLupus Erythematosus, Systemicxgenetics22aEpigenomics22aPrimary Immunodeficiency Diseases22aAutoantibodiesximmunology22aCytokinesximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCriswell, Lindsey,espeaker.1 aHolland, Steven M.q(Steven Matthew),d1957-espeaker.12aCriswell, Lindsey.tDemystifying medicine.pEpigenetic contribution to autoimmune diseases12aHolland, Steven M.q(Steven Matthew),d1957-tDemystifying medicine.pAnticytokine autoantibodies40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44349  aAUTH00874cam a22002657a 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003600073042001300109044001000122050001800132060003200150245009800182250001400280260008700294300002000381336002600401337002800427338002700455650004300482650002500525710004900550999000900599101607402DNLM20260205124327.0970213s1972    cc            000 0 chi    aUBYbengcUBYdHUAdOCLCQdDNLM  anlmcopyc  9China14aRS131.64b.K800aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1773 no.100aChang jian bing min jian yin shi liao fa /cGuangxi Zhuangzu Zizhiqu yi yao yan jiu suo bian.  aDi 1 ban.  aNanning :bGuangxi ren min chu ban she :bGuangxi Xin hua shu dian fa xing,c1972.  a73 p. ;c13 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier12aMedicine, Chinese Traditionalxmethods22aFormularies as Topic2 aGuangxi Zhuangzu Zizhiqu yi yao yan jiu suo.  aAUTH01359cgm a22004094a 101478244DNLM20260227152540.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080702s2008    mdu052|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14549  a(OCoLC)269432351  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWP 8701 aGarcia-Closas, Montserrat.10aAdvances in the understanding of genetic susceptibility to breast cancer /cMontserrat Garcia-Closas.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's seminar series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Sept. 30, 2008).  aStreaming video (52 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 16, 2008.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBreast Neoplasmsxgenetics22aGenetic Predisposition to Disease 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14549  aAUTH02721cgm a2200469 a 101578505DNLM20260227154051.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120215s2012    mdu046|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17052  a(OCoLC)794257255  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQV 6001 aRussell, Lemuel T.10aNIH 2012 Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr. observance program /cLemuel T. Russell IV.1 iTitle on PowerPoint screen of keynote speaker:aToxicology, scientific development, civil rights  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (46 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Jan. 12, 2012.3 a(CIT): Join us for the NIH 2012 Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr. Observance Program.  After a long struggle, legislation was signed in 1983 creating a Federal holiday marking the birthday of Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr.. The Federal holiday was first observed in 1986, this year marks the 26th anniversary of the King Federal holiday. In 1994, Congress designated the Martin Luther King Jr. Federal Holiday as a national day of service.  The keynote speaker Lemuel T. Russell IV, Ph.D., is a Regulatory Mammalian Toxicologist for the United States Environmental Protection Agency (USEPA). Dr. Russell works in the New Chemicals Safety and Assessment Branch (NCSAB) Risk Assessment Division (RAD) of the USEPA.  He provides risk and safety evaluation of industrial chemicals under safety and risk assessment in the new and existing chemicals program at the USEPA.  The Duke Ellington School of the Arts will also perform.  Other program participants include Francis Collins, M.D., Ph.D., Director, NIH; Roland Owens, Ph.D., Assistant Director, Office of Intramural Research, NIH; and Sheila Stokes, Esq., Acting Director, Office of Equal Opportunity and Diversity Management, NIH.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aKing, Martin Luther,cJr.,d1929-1968.12aToxicology22aBlack or African American.22aAnniversaries and Special Events22aCivil Rights 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17052  aAUTH01194cas a2200409 a 8611927DNLM20260227091019.0cr|cn#||||||||861023c19709999cauqr p       0    0eng    a0033-2925l0033-2925  a(DNLM)SR0053269(s)  a(OCoLC)01907432  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bPS63H 0aPsychological perspectives00aPsychological perspectives.  aLos Angeles, CA :bC.G. Jung Institute of Los Angeles  av.  aFour no. a year  aSemiannual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Vol. 1, no. 1 (spring 1970).  aDescription based on: Vol. 16, no. 1 (spring 1985).  aAlso issued online. 2aJungian Theory 2aPsychoanalysis 2aPsychoanalytic Theory 2aPsychology 2aPeriodical2 aC.G. Jung Institute of Los Angeles (Calif.)0 x1556-30304 uhttps://www.tandfonline.com/loi/upyp20  aAUTH01443cgm a2200445 a 101578483DNLM20260227153350.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120215s2010    mdu046|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15622  a(OCoLC)778838111  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aE 185.81 aAdams, Russell L.10aNIH Black history month observance, 2010 /cRussell Adams.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (46 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAccess restricted to NIH and HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded Feb. 18, 2010.3 a(CIT): The National Theme is "The History of Black Economic Empowerment."  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aBlack or African Americanxhistory.12aCommercexhistory22aMinority Health22a