tle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 26, 2006).  aStreaming video (54 min., 24 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 17, 2006.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHematopoietic Stem Cellsxcytology22aHematopoiesis 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13011  aAUTH02636cgm a2200445 a 101601865DNLM20260227154533.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130208s2013    mdu068       fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17770  a(OCoLC)828715810  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWW 1051 aRoelfsema, Pieter.10aAlgorithms for perceptual grouping in the primate brain /cPieter Roelfsema.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 8 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aRecorded Jan. 28, 2013.3 a(CIT): Neuroscience Seminar Series   Pieter R. Roelfsema is Director of the Netherlands Institute for Neuroscience in Amsterdam, an Institute of the Royal Academy of Arts and Sciences of the Netherlands. He received his MD degree at the University of Groningen in the Netherlands in 1991. For his PhD, he worked with Wolf Singer at the Max-Planck-Institute for Brain Research in Frankfurt (Germany) on the visual system of the cat and the role of neuronal synchronization. He received his PhD degree at the University of Amsterdam in 1995. His studies center on visual perception, plasticity and memory in the visual system. He uses multi-electrode recording techniques in experimental animals, behavioral paradigms in humans, and computational neuroscience approaches. His work begins with the premise that perceptual or cognitive processes activate many areas of the cerebral cortex. The neurons in these areas must co-ordinate their activity and exchange information, but little is known about the mechanisms that are responsible for this information exchange. One of these perceptual processes is perceptual grouping, that is, the organization of elements in a visual scene that belong to the same object, and this is the topic of his lecture  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aVisual Perceptionxphysiology22aAlgorithms22aPrimatesxphysiology22aVisual Cortexxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17770  aAUTH02687cgm a2200457 i 101771161DNLM20260227160220.0m     o  z f      vz|czau|ucr|cna||||||||200828s2020    mdu061 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35607  a(OCoLC)1197632901  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQS 67700aAngelman syndrome :btreatment opportunities and clinical biomarkers /cBen Philpot. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (61 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience series seminar  aRecorded February 24, 2020.3 a(CIT): Several neurodevelopmental disorders are caused by mutations in different, seemingly unrelated, genes (e.g. – UBE3A and TCF4). However, similar phenotypic manifestations suggest that these disorders share underlying cellular phenotypes, or give rise to perturbances that converge pathophysiologically at the circuit level. Dr. Philpot lab employs an array of modern scientific approaches to understand the pathophysiology underlying monogenic neurodevelopmental disorders, and leverage the knowledge gained from these approaches to develop gene therapies and novel drug treatments. Dr. Philpot lab seeks to understand the pathophysiology underlying monogenic neurodevelopmental disorders, and they use this information to develop therapeutics to treat these disorders. To accomplish these goals, they employ a variety of techniques: electrophysiology, biochemistry, molecular biology, optogenetics, behavioral phenotyping, gene therapy, and small-molecule drug screening. They are particularly focused on developing transformative treatments for Dup15q syndrome, Pitt-Hopkins syndrome, and Angelman syndrome.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed September 22, 2020).12aAngelman Syndromexdrug therapy22aAngelman Syndromexgenetics22aCannabidiolxtherapeutic use22aUbiquitin-Protein Ligases22aBiomarkers 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aPhilpot, Ben,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=35607  aAUTH01390cgm a22004214a 101268246DNLM20260227150247.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu080 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11572  a(OCoLC)70119567  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 58.51 aWolfsberg, Tyra.10aMining genomic information /cTyra Wolfsberg.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed June 8, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 20 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 7, 2002.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aGenomicsxmethods22aDatabases, Genetic22aMolecular Sequence Data 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11572  aAUTH02453cgm a2200469 i 9918975959206676DNLM20260227165018.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u250104s2024    mdu597|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35387  a(DNLM)CIT:35388  a(OCoLC)1542998534  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 1152 aHeterogeneity and Sex/Gender Differences in Obstructive Sleep Apnea (Workshop)d(2024 :cOnline)jauthor.10aHeterogeneity and sex/gender differences in obstructive sleep apnea :bpersonalized approaches to diagnosis, treatment, and implementation. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2024]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (9 hr., 57 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 14 & 15, 2024.3 a(CIT): To delineate the heterogeneity of obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) diagnosis, treatment and implementation; and facilitate personalized and precision based approaches in sleep medicine. To determine how the identification of endotypes and phenotypes in OSA can be utilized for a more tailored-approach to treating OSA across the lifespan as well as in populations that are disproportionately impacted by the negative outcomes of this disorder. The workshop agenda can be accessed through this link: https://acrobat.adobe.com/id/urn:aaid:sc:VA6C2:a36ae483-6a4c-4e8f-ae03-509891f6fa87  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed September 25, 2025).12aSleep Apnea, Obstructivexdiagnosis22aSleep Apnea, Obstructivextherapy22aSleep Apnea, Obstructivexepidemiology22aSex Factors22aPrecision Medicine 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Center on Sleep Disorders Research (National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute),esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=5536040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=55362  aAUTH03212cgm a2200505 i 9918663287206676DNLM20260227164004.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230815s2023    mdu087|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33325  a(OCoLC)1410445015  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWW 27600aDon't dis my ability /cAlbert J. Rizzi. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 27 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aDDM seminar series  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded July 13, 2023.3 a(CIT): Albert J. Rizzi received his B.A. from Manhattanville College and has dual M.S. degrees from St. John"s University in education--one in early childhood and the other in administration and supervision. In 2006, Mr. Rizzi unexpectedly and completely lost his sight. He became an activist and advocate for the blind and visually impaired, providing education, building awareness, and working to achieve the development and dissemination of assistive technologies. LMS Course ID: NIHTC6104-FY23-3 Participants for the DDM Seminar Series will receive 1.5 hours of supervisory refresher credits per event. In addition, participants can submit an entry into FAITAS to receive 1.5 CLPs for FAC-COR, FAC-C and FAC-PPM re-certifications. Credit is offered through the Learning Management System (LMS) for those registering for either the live or archived offering. You must register in LMS prior to attendance to receive credit. For the live webcast, all registrants will be sent a link to launch the videocast on the date of the event. For the archived webcast, you will register and launch the video anytime via the LMS (available approximately one week after the live event). You can watch directly from the videocast website if you don"t need to receive credit in LMS. American sign-language will be provided for in-person seminars while videocasting will include closed captioning. Please call the NIH Training Center at 301-496-6211with any concerns about other reasonable accommodations.For more information go to https://www.ddmseries.od.nih.gov/.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 21, 2023).12aRizzi, Albert J.12aBlindness22aPatient Advocacy22aPolitical Activism22aSelf-Help Devices22aEducation of Persons with Visual Disabilities. 2aUnited States 2aPersonal Narrative 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aRizzi, Albert J.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aDeputy director for management seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=48805  aAUTH02360cgm a2200481 i 9918663287006676DNLM20260227164009.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230815s2023    mdu545|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33327  a(DNLM)CIT:33328  a(OCoLC)1540314812  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.52 aWorkshop on Ethics of Sharing Individual Level Human Brain Data Collected in Biomedical Researchd(2023 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aWorkshop on ethics of sharing individual level human brain data collected in biomedical research /cNIH BRAIN Initiative.1 aNEWG workshop on ethics of sharing individual level human brain data collected in biomedical research 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (9 hr., 5 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded July 17 & 18, 2023.3 a(CIT): The goals of the NIH BRAIN Neuroethics Working Group (NEWG) Workshop on the Ethics of Sharing Individual Level Human Brain Data Collected in Biomedical Research are to explore meaningful ways to categorize human brain data by potential risks of data sharing and any resulting differences in how to treat data and to articulate points-to-consider for researchers in disclosing the potential risks of sharing human brain data.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from 