:cOnline)eauthor.10aBiomarkers of nutrition for development - knowledge indicating dietary sufficiency /cNICHD, NIH.1 aBOND-KIDS :bBiomarkers of Nutrition for Development - Knowledge Indicating Dietary Sufficiency (Working Group 1 Discussion) 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (4 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBOND-KIDS webinar series  aRecorded March 4, 2022.3 a(CIT): Biomarkers of Nutrition for Development - Knowledge Indicating Dietary Sufficiency: BOND-KIDS.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 3, 2022).22aBOND-KIDS (Project)12aNutritional Requirements12aChild22aNutritional Status22aChild Nutritional Physiological Phenomena22aEating22aFood Insecurity22aAdolescent 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aBOND-KIDS (Project).bWorking Group 1,eorganizer. 0aBOND-KIDS webinar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44889  aAUTH02749cgm a2200433 i 101778083DNLM20260227161031.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210408s2021    mdu233|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31638  a(OCoLC)1302731181  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 3482 aNIH Music and Health (Meeting)d(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aLaying the foundation :bdefining the building blocks of music-based interventions /cNational Institutes of Health ; Foundation for the National Institutes of Health ; Renee Fleming Foundation. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (3 hr., 53 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded March 31, 2021.3 a(CIT): NIH Music and Health  The National Institutes of Health (NIH), in collaboration with the Foundation for the NIH and the Renee Fleming Foundation, are sponsoring an expert panel discussion--the first in a series of three meetings intended to develop evidence-based music therapies for brain disorders of aging. The roundtable format will be used to gather input from individuals representing neuroscience, music therapy and music medicine, behavioral intervention development, clinical trial methodology, and patient advocacy and art-based organizations. Mr. Alan Weil, editor of Health Affairs, will serve as facilitator in fostering a dialogue between the NIH organizers and a group of invited experts. The NIH organizers will utilize input gathered from these meetings and the broader scientific community to inform the creation of a toolkit for research on music and health across the lifespan. NIH organizers ultimately hope to develop and disseminate the toolkit, which will include a consolidated set of common data elements for music-based intervention protocols.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 14, 2021).12aBrain Diseasesxtherapy12aAged22aMusic Therapy 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aFoundation for the National Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aRenée Fleming Foundation,esponsoring body2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41532  aAUTH01800cgm a22003974a 101285729DNLM20260227151546.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060822s2006    mdu085|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13303  a(OCoLC)71273724  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4751 aLinn, Stuart M.10aHistory of DNA repair-life in the serendipitous lane :bexcitement and gratification in studying DNA repair /cStuart Linn.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Sept. 5, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 25 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 20, 2006.3 a(CIT): The DNA Repair Interest Group is concerned with all forms of DNA damage and repair. As a major defense against environmental damage to cells DNA repair is present in all organisms examined including bacteria, yeast, drosophila, fish, amphibians, rodents and humans. The members of the DNA Repair Interest Group perform research in areas including DNA repair enzymology and fine structure, mutagenesis, gene and cell cycle regulation, protein structure, and human disease.   For more information, visit the  DNA Repair Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDNA Repair 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13303  aAUTH01484cas a2200457   8003807DNLM20260227090955.0cr||||||||||||800417c19779999enkqr         0    0eng    a   78640984   a0147-8885l0147-8885  aJOHID  a(DNLM)J23050000(s)  a(OCoLC)03456694  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9England00aW1bJO671D10aJ Histotechnol2dnlm 0aJournal of histotechnology00aJournal of histotechnology.  a[Downers Grove, IL :bMayo Publications]2132011- :aLeeds, UK :bManey Pub.2132016-2019 :aAbingdon :bTaylor & Francis3132020- :aBedfordshire :bHistorical Metallurgy Society  av.bill., ports.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-    Sept. 1977-1 aMEDLINEbv42n1, 2019- ; Selected citations only before this date0 aPubMedbv42n1, 2019- ; Selected citations only before this date  aAlso issued online.  aOfficial publication of the National Society for Histotechnology. 2aHistological Techniques