au-Ponty, Maurice,d1908-1961.12aDescartes, René,d1596-1650.12aVisual Perception22aPhilosophy22aMind-Body Relations, Metaphysical 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aAdams, William D.q(William Drea),espeaker.2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.).bHistory of Medicine Division,esponsoring body.2 aNational Endowment for the Humanities,esponsoring body. 0aNLM history talks40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44387  aAUTH02137cgm a22004334a 101546213DNLM20260227153614.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101027s2010    mdu075|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16116  a(OCoLC)702604527  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 3251 aLowry, William,cPh.D.10aProbing the potential of human pluripotent stem cells /cWilliam Lowry.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCRM (NIH Center for Regenerative Medicine) lecture  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 15 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 20, 2010.3 a(CIT): NCRM (NIH Center for Regenerative Medicine) Lecture   Bill Lowry grew up in Seattle, WA and received his B.S. from the University of Washington in 1996. He then moved to New York to do his graduate work in signal transduction and cell biology with Xin-Yun Huang at Cornell Medical College in the fall of 1997. Bill then went across the street to work with Elaine Fuchs at the Rockefeller University in 2002 where he studied the mechanisms of quiescence and activation in stem cells of the Epidermis. In the summer of 2006 Bill joined the Department of Molecular, Cell and Developmental Biology at UCLA to explore adult stem cells and embryonic development. Bill holds the Maria Rowena Ross Term Chair in Cell Biology, and is supported by the NIH, March of Dimes, CRCC, and JCCF.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aInduced Pluripotent Stem Cells22aStem Cell Transplantation 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNCRM lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16116  aAUTH01092nam a2200349 i 9918976859406676DNLM20260220160848.0240615t20232023sz ac    b    000 0 eng    a9783031357893qhardbound  a3031357892qhardbound  z9783031357909qebook  a(OCoLC)1439691233  aOHXbengerdacOHX  apcc  9Switzerland00aWE 862 7aRD2lcco00aModified posterior approach to the hip joint /cK. Mohan Iyer, editor.  aSecond edition. 1aCham, Switzerland :bSpringer,c[2023] 4c©2023  axviii, 145 pages :billustrations (chiefly color), portraits (some color) ;c25 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aArthroplasty, Replacement, Hip22aHip Jointxanatomy & histology22aHip Jointxsurgery1 aIyer, K. Mohan,eeditor.  aAUTH01703cgm a22004574a 101267660DNLM20260227150013.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu107  |||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10525  a(OCoLC)84646340  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 2952 aNIH Research Festivald(2002 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNIH research festival 2002.nPlenary session 1,pBiodefense :ba new NIH mission /cOffice of the Director, NIH.30aBiodefense  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 29, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 46 min., 25 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 16, 2002.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aBioterrorismxprevention & control22aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration22aUnited States Government Agencies22aVaccines 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of the Director.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10525  aAUTH03224cgm a2200457 i 101774274DNLM20260227160420.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31381  a(OCoLC)1229082277  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 58.500aKRAB-Zinc finger proteins and their role in mammal evolution /cTodd Macfarlan. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's seminar series  aRecorded November 6, 2020.3 a(CIT): Kruppel associated box zinc finger proteins (KRAB-ZFPs) constitute the single largest family of transcription factors in mammal genomes. Each KRAB-ZFP utilizes a unique, repetitive array of zinc fingers to achieve highly specific DNA binding and their KRAB domain to recruit the cofactor KAP-1 (and its associated histone modifying enzymes) to establish localized heterochromatin formation.  Unlike most transcription factors, that maintain DNA binding activities and regulatory networks over millions of years of evolution, KRAB-ZFPs massively amplified and diversified in mammals.  Dr. Macfarlan will discuss the work of his lab, NICHD's Section on Mammalian Development and Evolution, that demonstrates that KRAB-ZFPs evolved in response to waves of endogenous retroviruses (ERVs) and other transposable element (TE) invasions of mammal genomes as means to specifically bind and transcriptionally silence them.  This serves to limit the activity of TEs and allow for co-option of ERV-derived regulatory sequences. Dr. Macfarlan will further describe how rare KRAB-ZFPs born at the dawn of mammals were maintained to regulate critical mammalian adaptations allowing live birth.  Finally, he will highlight the fascinating example of the oldest KRAB-ZFP gene, PRDM9; a site specific DNA binding histone methyltransferase, that along with a dual histone methylation specific binding protein, ZCWPW1, co-evolved to control the location of meiotic recombination hotspots. The work of the NICHD"s Section of Mammalian Development and Evolution highlights the unique features of KRAB-ZFP genes that allowed them to play important roles in diverse evolutionary processes shaping mammals.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed January 6, 2021).12aZinc Finger Protein Gli3xgenetics22aEvolution, Molecular22aMammalsxgenetics22aGenome22aRetroviridaexgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aMacfarlan, Todd,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38974  aAUTH01688cas a2200505 a 101535338DNLM20260227090917.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||100708c20069999ne || poo     0    0eng    a  2013243583  a2221-285Xl1818-0876  a(OCoLC)797827352  aCNCGMcCNCGMdCUSdDNLM  apcc  9Netherlands 4aRS110aW1bAS139VL10aAsian J Pharm Sci2dnlm0 aAsian j. pharm. sci.b(Online) 0aAsian journal of pharmceutical sciencesb(Online)00aAsian journal of pharmaceutical sciences.1 aAJPS  aHong Kong :bHong Kong Asiamed Pub. House31a[Amsterdam] :bElsevier B.V.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with 2006 Vol. 1, no. 1.0 aPubMedbv12n1, Jan. 2017-0 aPMC  aAlso issued in print.  aDescription based on: 1 (2006); title from PDF running title (publisher's Web site, viewed Oct. 25, 2013).  aLatest issue consulted: 2013 Vol. 8, no. 1 (viewed Oct. 25, 2013).12aPharmacological Phenomena 2aPeriodical2 aAsian Federation for Pharmaceutical Sciences.2 aShenyang yao ke da 