s00aWO 27500aRethinking general anesthesia /cEmery N. Brown. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded November 18, 2020.3 a(CIT): Dr. Brown is an anesthesiologist and statistician whose research has helped explain the way anesthetic drugs act in the brain.  His discoveries have led to new ways of monitoring patients" brain states during general anesthesia, as well as strategies for drug dosing and precisely controlling the anesthetic state.Dr. Brown also has developed signal-processing algorithms and statistical methods that characterize the dynamic properties of neuroscience data.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed February 3, 2021).12aAnesthesia, General22aAnesthetics, General22aConsciousnessxphysiology22aBrainxdrug effects 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aBrown, E. N.q(Emery N.),espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pDeWitt Stetten, Jr. lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40044  aAUTH02174cgm a2200457 i 101774281DNLM20260227160437.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu745 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31392  a(DNLM)CIT:31397  a(OCoLC)1236084706  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 503.22 aStrategies for an HIV Cure (Meeting)d(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aStrategies for an HIV Cure 2020 /cNational Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (12 hr., 25 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 16 & 17, 2020.3 a(CIT): The National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (NIAID) will host the fifth biennial Strategies for an HIV Cure meeting virtually on November 16-17, 2020. The goal of this meeting is to review research progress by grantees funded through the NIH Martin Delaney Collaboratories for HIV Cure Research program and NIAID's Understanding HIV Rebound P01 grant program. November 16th (Day 1) will feature presentations from the NIH Martin Delaney Collaboratories for HIV Cure Research program grantees.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed February 2, 2021).12aHIV Infectionsxdrug therapy12aHIV Infectionsxvirology22aAnti-HIV Agentsxtherapeutic use22aHIVxdrug effects22aDrug Discovery 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (U.S.),eorganizer,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3873340uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38735  aAUTH01242cgm a22004094a 101267759DNLM20260227145614.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s1998    mdu054 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10639  a(OCoLC)86112966  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWD 21000aMIG 3/2/98.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c1998]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 28, 2007).  aStreaming video (53 min., 48 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 2, 1998.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.12aObesityxgenetics22aAdipose Tissue, Brown22aMitochondria22aMitochondrial Proteins22aThermogenesis 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMitochondria Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10639  aAUTH02326cgm a2200457 i 101658184DNLM20260227155052.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150505s2015    mdu720 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18939  a(DNLM)CIT:18940  a(OCoLC)909852188  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWT 5002 aOptions for Consideration of Chronic Disease Endpoints for Dietary Reference Intakes (DRIs) (Workshop)d(2015 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aOptions for Consideration of Chronic Disease Endpoints for Dietary Reference Intakes (DRIs). 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (12 hr.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded March 10 & 11, 2015.3 a(CIT): The DRI Committees of the U.S. and Canadian governments held this workshop, "Options for Consideration of Chronic Disease Endpoints for Dietary Reference Intakes," on March 10-11, 2015. The objective of the workshop was to critically evaluate key scientific issues involved in using chronic disease endpoints for setting dietary reference intakes and, in this context, to provide information for future decisions as to whether and/or how chronic disease endpoints can be incorporated into the setting of DRI values. Meeting attendees included scientists involved in DRI reviews, individuals from agencies and other groups that sponsor and/or fund DRI reviews, and users of DRIs.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (May 13, 2015).12aChronic Disease22aDiet22aRecommended Dietary Allowances 2aCanada 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1893940uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18940  aAUTH02907cgm a2200469 i 9918625744406676DNLM20260227163727.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230602s2023    mdu773|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33171  a(DNLM)CIT:33176  a(OCoLC)1455526996  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 7002 aAdvances in Aging, Immunity and Chronic Inflammatory Disease (Workshop)d(2023 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aAdvances in aging, immunity and chronic inflammatory diseases /cNIH, NIAID, and NIDCR. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (12 hr., 53 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded May 9 & 10, 2023.3 a(CIT): The National Institute on Aging (NIA), National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (NIAID), and National Institute of Dental and Craniofacial Research (NIDCR) will host a workshop on Advances in Aging, Immunity and Chronic Inflammatory Diseases at Rockledge 2, Room 160 (1st level) on May 9-10, 2023. .May 9:  9:00 AM - 5:40 PM (EST) .May 10:  8:30 AM - 5:15 PM (EST)  This workshop will focus on recent advances (biological and technological) in our knowledge of immunosenescence, aging, and their impact on chronic disease development The past few years have seen major advances in our knowledge of immune function during aging, and improved methods to define immunological states. Notably, advances in single cell technologies have the potential to permit interrogation of metabolic and phenotypic variation of leukocytes, including rare tissue resident populations, and circadian gene regulation in macrophages. The workshop will highlight recent advances, clarify mechanisms involved in accelerated development of immune aging and chronic disease, and promote collaboration between investigators.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 26, 2024).12aInflammationximmunology12aChronic Disease22aImmunosenescence22aAgingxphysiology 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institute on Aging,espon