ps://www.journals.uchicago.edu/toc/ajs/current  aAUTH01213cas a2200433   0356657DNLM20260227090910.0cr|cn#||||||||730507c19729999njumr         0||| 0eng    a0090-4481l0090-4481  aPDANBO  a(DNLM)P06180000(s)  a(OCoLC)01772141  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bPE163N10aPediatr Ann2dnlm 0aPediatric annals00aPediatric annals.  aNew York,bInsight Pub. Co.3 aThorofare NJ :bSlack  av.billus., ports.  aMonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-       Oct. 1972-  aPrint issues ceased with v. 44, no. 12 (2015).1 aMEDLINEbv5n1, Jan. 1976-0 aPubMedbv5n1, Jan. 1976-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  c19950523lNot printed on acid-free paper 2aPediatrics 2aPeriodical0 x1938-23594 uhttps://journals.healio.com/journal/ped  aAUTH03697cgm a22004574a 101475716DNLM20260227152726.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080602s2008    mdu064 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14496  a(OCoLC)262647255  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 165.5.H31 aKieff, Elliott D.,d1943-10aEpstein-Barr virus alteration of lymphocyte growth /cElliott Kieff.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Oct. 5, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 3 min., 40 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 14, 2008.3 a(CIT): Human viruses are responsible for about 20% of human malignancies worldwide and offer unique opportunities for early diagnosis, prevention, and therapeutic intervention.  Our experiments began with the study of the fundamental mechanisms by which Epstein-Barr Virus (EPV), an important causative agent of lymphomas, Hodgkin's Disease and Naso-pharyngeal carcinoma, uniquely and efficiently causes the uncontrolled proliferation and survival of normal human B lymphocytes.  We have discovered that the virus uses only five genes to cause B cell proliferation. These genes encode nuclear proteins that usurp control over Notch regulated cell promoters, including the c-myc promoter, and an integral membrane protein that constitutively activates CD40 signaling pathways. We are currently investigating the genetics and biochemistry of the molecular and sub-molecular processes through which the viral proteins effect changes in cell signal transduction, transcription, growth, and survival so as to validate targets that can be used in assays to identify chemical inhibitors.  EBV is unique among human viruses in inducing continuous proliferation of B lymphocytes, the important host cell for EBV latency. In B lymphocytes, EBV encodes nuclear antigen (EBNA) and integral membrane (LMP) proteins, which induce cell cycle transit and lymphoblast proliferation. In humans with normal T cell function, immune T lymphocytes emerge in response to peptides from the EBNAs and LMPs, which are presented in the context of Class I and II MHC molecules on cell surfaces. Immune T cells kill and enforce tight control of EBV infected lymphocytes.  Ongoing projects in our lab involve:  (1) Elucidation of the molecular and sub-molecular pathways by which Notch and viral proteins regulate the c-myc promoter. Structural studies of a key viral and cellular interaction site. (2) Elucidation of the molecular and sub-molecular pathways by which CD40 and a viral protein co-stimulate cell growth and survival. (3) Proteinomic and cell genetic screens for novel genes in the CD40 and viral protein NF-kB, Junk, and p38 pathways. (4) Sub-molecular mechanisms by which a viral protein permits episome replication and maintenance.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHerpesvirus 4, Human22aB-Lymphocytesxvirology22aCell Proliferation22aNeoplasmsxvirology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14496  aAUTH01805cgm a2200469 i 101772192DNLM20260227160250.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201003s2020    mdu251 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:29271  a(OCoLC)1203133949  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWV 242 aNational Institute on Deafness and Other Communication Disorders (U.S.).bAdvisory Council,eauthor.10aNIDCD Advisory Council meeting, September 10-11, 2020 /cNIDCD, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (4 hr., 11 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 10-11, 2020.3 a(CIT): Presenting the Open Session of the National Deafness and Other Communication Disorders Advisory Council meeting on September 10-11, 2020.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 3, 2020).22aNational Institute on Deafness and Other Communication Disorders (U.S.)12aAdvisory Committees12aDeafness22aCommunication Disorders22aBiomedical Research22aGovernment Agencies 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3791740uno redirect  aAUTH02122cgm a22004694a 101475717DNLM20260227152713.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080602s2008    mdu081|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14499  a(OCoLC)232647292  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 851 aLappin, Debra R.14aThe 21st century patient--a new partnership with the biomedical enterprise /cDebra R. Lappin.3 aTwenty first century patient30aNew partnership with the biomedical enterprise  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aJoseph Leiter lecture  aTitle from screen banner (viewed June 18, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 21 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 14, 2008.3 a(CIT): The NLM/MLA Joseph Leiter Lecture  Debra R. Lappin is a current member of the Advisory Council to the Director of the CDC, former Co-Chair of a National Partners Task Force on Partner and Public Engagement on the CDC's Health Protection Goals.  She was a member of the inaugural Council of Public Representatives, the NIAMS Advisory Council, and the NLM PubMed Central Advisory Council.  Ms. Lappin has participated on a number of committees at the National Academy of Sciences, including the Committee on the Organizational Structure of the NIH, which directly informed the 2006 NIH Reform Act.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aPatient Advocacyxtrends12aPatient Participationxtrends22aConsumer Organizationsxtrends 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.) 0aJoseph Leiter lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14499  aAUTH01459cgm a22004334a 101532577DNLM20260227153503.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100604s2010    mdu132|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15869  a(OCoLC)646388416  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 241 aCollins, Francis S.10aHouse appropriations hearing :bFY2011 budget request for the National Institutes of Health /cFrancis S. Collins.1 iTitle at CIT site:aHouse appropriations hearing with Dr. Francis Collins, April 28, 2010  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (2 hr., 12 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 28, 2010.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aAcademies and Institutesxeconomics12aBiomedical Researchxeconomics22aBudgets22aFinancing, Government 2aUnited States 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15869  aAUTH02400cgm a22004694a 101532587DNLM20260227153619.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100604s2010    mdu064|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15882  a(OCoLC)657026851  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 2901 aAugustson, Erik M.10aWomen's health @ NIH presents National Cancer Institute's Smokefree Women Project :bsmoking cessation strategies for women /cErik M. Augustson.3 aWomen's health at NIH presents National Cancer Institute's Smokefree Women Project30aSmoking cessation strategies for women  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 4 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 10, 2010.3 a(CIT): As part of an ongoing effort to reach and engage women who want to quit smoking, the National Cancer Institute"s Smokefree Women project was developed.  The foundation of the project is http:\\women.smokefree.gov, a free online smoking cessation resource that offers a special focus on topics important to women.  The website provides female smokers with the latest science on tobacco addiction, recommended approaches on how to quit, and a wealth of resources to help support both the immediate and long-term needs as users become, and remain, nonsmokers.  The site features interactive tools as well as social networking through Facebook and Twitter.  This presentation covers smoking cessation treatment with a focus on strategies that may be particularly helpful for women and how Smokefree Women can help you quit smoking today.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aSmokefree Women Project.12aSmoking Cessationxmethods22aHealth Education22aInternet22aWomen's Health 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15882  aAUTH03403cgm a2200433 i 101750708DNLM20260227160008.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190709s2019    mdu058|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27613  a(OCoLC)1110955772  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWF 31000aCytokine interference in infection /cAnne O'Garra. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (58 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded June 12, 2019.3 a(CIT): NIH Director"s Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series   Dr. O'Garra's laboratory studies cells and molecules that control our immune systems to understand how they respond to infections and influence the outcomes and what goes wrong when infections damage healthy cells. For example, some infected individuals are protected while others go on to progress to disease such as tuberculosis (TB), which is caused by infection with Mycobacterium tuberculosis (M. tuberculosis). TB is a major cause of morbidity and mortality worldwide and efforts to control this disease are hampered by difficulties with diagnosis, prevention, and treatment. Most people infected with M. tuberculosis remain asymptomatic or have latent (noninfectious) TB, but they have a 10 percent lifetime risk of developing the active form of the disease.  In 2010, Dr. O'Garra's laboratory identified a gene signature dominated by type I interferon (IFN) inducible genes in the bloodstream of people with TB, which disappears during successful treatment. The lab is also studying the regulation and function of type I IFNs which exacerbate bacterial infections, in part, by inducing a regulatory cytokine called IL-10.  Type I IFNs are cytokines that have been shown for years to be important in the control of viral infections. In experimental models, the lab showed that elevated levels of this cytokine lead to increased TB disease; subsequent studies now provide further knowledge regarding the heterogeneity of this blood signature in some, but not all, asymptomatic TB contacts and how this signature relates to whether they are protected or will develop the disease.   During the lecture, Dr. O"Garra will discuss several potential mechanisms underlying the contribution of type I IFN to the development of TB and diseases caused by other bacterial infections. She will also discuss the impairment of protective responses.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 31, 2019).12aTuberculosis, Pulmonaryxtherapy22aInterferon Type Ixtherapeutic use22aMycobacterium tuberculosisximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aO'Garra, Anne,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27613  aAUTH01815cgm a2200469 i 101686948DNLM20260227155509.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160707s2016    mdu066|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19723  a(OCoLC)953846633  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWE 54400aFibromyalgia integrative training for teens (FIT teens) :bdevelopmenet of a novel non-pharmacologic treatment for juvenile fibromyalgia /cSusmita Kashikar-Zuck.1 iTitle on title screen:aFibromyalgia integrative training for tens (FIT teens) 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 6 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCCIH integrative medicine research lecture  aRecorded June 6, 2016.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from Power Point screen (viewed July 11, 2016).12aAdolescent12aFibromyalgiaxtherapy22aCognitive Behavioral Therapyxmethods22aExercise Therapyxmethods22aFibromyalgiaxpsychology22aIntegrative Medicine 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aKashikar-Zuck, Susmita,espeaker.2 aNational Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNCCIH integrative medicine research lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19723  aAUTH01566cas a2200433 a 9413084DNLM20260227091012.0931230c19879999ja br p       0||| 0jpn    a0914-5508l0914-5508y0387-8031  a(DNLM)SR0064918(s)  a(OCoLC)29703193  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 ajpnbeng  9Japan00aW1bNO695P2 aNihon Nōsotchū no Geka Kenkyūkai. 0aNōsotchū geka10aNōsotchū no geka :bNihon Nōsotchū no Geka Kenkyūkai kikanshi = Surgery for cerebral stroke : official journal of Japanese Conference on Surgery for Cerebral Stroke.31aSurgery for cerebral stroke  aSendai-shi :bDō Kenkyūkai,cShōwa 62 [1987]-3 aSendai-Shi :bDo Kenkyukai  av. :bill.  aBimonthly  aQuarterlyb1987-1991  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aDai 15-kan 1-gō (1987-4)-  aTitle from cover.  aEach issue has a distinctive title.  aAlso issued online.  aArticles in English or Japanese, with summaries in English.12aCerebrovascular Disordersxsurgery 2aConference Proceedings0 x1880-468300aNihon Nōsotchū no Geka Kenkyūkai.tNihon Nōsotchū no Geka Kenkyūkai kōenshūx0387-80314 uhttps://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/browse/scs/  aAUTH02329cgmaa22004694a 101232621DNLM20260227144523.0vf|cbahoucr|cn#||||||||vf|czahmuvf|cjahouhd|bfa012baap041025s2004    mdu050 ||    f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:3567  a(OCoLC)57202282  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aQH 603.I5400aPotassium channels /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  aUnited States.  aBethesda, MD :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004.  a1 videocassette of 1 (DVCPRO) (50 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/4 in.3master.  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (50 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (50 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aWALS--the Margaret Pittman lecture  aTitle from cassette spine label.  aSpeaker, Lily Y. Jan.  aPresented Sept. 22, 2004.3 a(Producer) Potassium channels serve a wide range of functions, as evident from the linkage of potassium channel mutations to diseases of the brain, heart, muscle, kidney, pancreas, and neural crest-derived tissues. For these channels to serve their physiological functions, they have to be at the right place, in the right number, and open at the right time. Recent studies of these different aspects of potassium channel regulation are discussed.  aVideocassette (Betacam SP)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2011.e1 videocassette (50 min.) : sd., col., 1/2 in.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aPotassium Channelsxphysiology1 aJan, Lily Yeh.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pNIH director's Margaret Pittman lecture4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3567  aAUTH02631cgm a2200457 i 101686946DNLM20260227155457.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160707s2016    mdu055|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19721  a(OCoLC)1004826336  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWN 25000aNovel concepts in radiation-induced normal tissue injury /cDeborah E. Citrin. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (55 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's seminar series  aRecorded June 3, 2016.3 a(CIT): Radiation therapy is delivered to over one half of all cancer patients.  Efforts to enhance the efficacy of radiation have centered on technologic improvements in radiation delivery and leveraging a growing understanding of tumor radiobiology.  Despite numerous recent advances in radiation delivery techniques, radiation exposure of normal tissues within the treatment volume can result in chronic injury, such as fibrosis and organ dysfunction.  The study of radiation injury models has provided insight into the molecular events leading to radiation injury.  Chronic oxidative stress after exposure of normal tissues to irradiation can result in progressive inflammation and fibrosis characterized by a predominance of macrophages.  Irradiated organs, such as lung, exhibit a dose dependent senescence of resident stem cells, resulting in parenchymal depletion and organ dysfunction.  Targeting senescence and associated changes in tissue after exposure to irradiation provides a unique opportunity to prevent and mitigate chronic injury from radiation.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed September 22, 2017).12aRadiotherapyxadverse effects22aRadiation Injuriesxprevention & control22aRadiotherapy Dosage22aTissuesxinjuries22aTissuesxradiation effects 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCitrin, D.q(Deborah),espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19721  aAUTH01445cas a2200409 i 101762461DNLM20260227090909.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||200208c20209999ts || poo     |    0eng    a  2020243066  a1573-4056  a(OCoLC)1146272536  aDNLMbengerdad20260227cDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United Arab Emirates00aW110aCurr Med Imaging2d