0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu207 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11430  a(OCoLC)70713354  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 47000aBringing the genome to you /cNational Human Genome Research Institute.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 21, 2006).  aStreaming video (3 hr., 26 min., 30 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 15, 2003.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aGenome, Human 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Human Genome Research Institute (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11430  aAUTH02104cgm a22004094a 101556029DNLM20260227153852.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110209s2010    mdu645 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16420  a(DNLM)CIT:16421  a(OCoLC)728457061  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States10aQU 58.52 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bRecombinant DNA Advisory Committee.10aRecombinant DNA Advisory Committee (RAC), December 2010 /cNIH.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (10 hr., 45 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed May 27, 2010).  aRecorded Dec. 7-8, 2010.3 a(CIT): The RAC is a technical committee whose goal is to consider the current state of knowledge and technology regarding recombinant DNA. This includes review of human gene transfer trials, and an assessment of the ability of DNA recombinants to survive in nature and the potential for transfer of genetic material to other organisms. It also considers hypothetical hazards and methods for monitoring and minimizing risks. Approximately one-third of the 15 members do not have scientific expertise but represent public interests and attitudes. This balance is intended to provide a forum for open public debate of social and scientific issues attendant to recombinant DNA research. The RAC has been overwhelmingly successful in achieving this goal.  aOpen-captioned.12aDNA, Recombinant22aClinical Protocols22aGene Transfer Techniques 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1642040uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16421  aAUTH02018cgm a22004574i 101546227DNLM20260227153654.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101027s2010    mdu064|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16135  a(OCoLC)695525762  aDNLMcDNLMerdadDNLM  9United States10aWW 1501 aNathans, Jeremy H.espeaker.14aThe evolution of primate color vision /cDr. Jeremy Nathans.  a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2010]  a1 online resource (1 hr., 4 min.) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving image2rdacontent  acomputer2rdamedia  aonline resource2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded September 22, 2010.3 a(CIT): This lecture will cover the molecular genetics of human color vision, and the way in which evolutionary changes at the receptor level have shaped the different color vision capacities of different species as well as differences in color vision among individuals within our own species.   Lecture Objectives:  Understand the main themes in the history of color vision research; Understand the way in which gene rearrangements produce variant human red-green color vision; Understand how genetically engineered mice have contributed to research on primate color vision.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aColor Visionxgenetics22aBiological Evolution22aHominidae22aMice, Transgenic22aPrimates 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16135  aAUTH01620cgm a2200445 i 101629956DNLM20260227154615.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140408s2014    mdu066|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18317  a(OCoLC)879416791  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 26.55.I41 aRichardson, Douglas,d1950-eauthor.10aSpatializing health research :bgeospatial frontiers in health and health behaviors research : key pathways forward /cDouglas Richardson and Mei-Po Kwan.1 aGeospatial frontiers in health and health behaviors research :bkey pathways forward 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 6 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBSSR lecture  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Mar. 6, 2014.  aDescription based on version viewed April 11, 2014.12aBiomedical Research12aGeographic Information Systems22aHealth Behavior 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aKwan, Mei-po,eauthor.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aBSSR lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18317  aAUTH01275cgm a22004094a 101294480DNLM20260227151837.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061204s2006    mdu051|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13430  a(OCoLC)213005357  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4771 aSutherland, Betsy M.10aEndogenous and radiation-Induced DNA damage clusters and their repair /cBetsy Sutherland.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 29, 2008).  aStreaming video (51 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 17, 2006.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDNA Damage22aDNA Repair22aDNAxradiation effects 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13430  aAUTH01840cgm a22004334a 101268249DNLM20260227150550.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu092 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11575  a(OCoLC)222125624  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4501 aGreen, Eric D.10aPhysical mapping /cEric Green.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCurrent topics in genome analysis  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 18, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 31 min., 47 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 12, 2000.3 a(CIT): Current Topics in Genome Research was developed to address the rapid advances in genomics and the ever-growing accomplishments of the Human Genome Project. The series consists of 14 sessions on successive Tuesdays, with a mixture of local and outside speakers, and was designed to cover the major areas of genomics. Each lecture provides practical information suitable for a diverse audience.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aPhysical Chromosome Mapping22aGenome Components 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aCurrent topics in genome analysis40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11575  aAUTH01258cgm a22004094a 101294473DNLM20260227151655.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061204s2006    mdu062|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13421  a(OCoLC)191751211  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWD 2101 aFriedman, Jeffrey,d1954