01319936DNLM20260227152221.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071101s2007    mdu088 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14070  a(OCoLC)190867181  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 1021 aWilson, Matthew,cPh. D.10aHippocampal memory reactivation during awake and sleep states /cMatthew Wilson.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Dec. 27, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 27 min., 32 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 15, 2007.3 a(CIT): Research in the  Dr. Wilson's laboratory focuses on the study of information representation across large populations of neurons in the mammalian nervous system, as well as on the mechanisms that underlie formation and maintenance of distributed memories in freely behaving animals. To study the basis of these processes, his lab employs a combination of molecular genetic, electrophysiological, pharmacological, behavioral, and computational approaches.  These studies of learning and memory in awake, behaving animals have led to the exploration of the nature of sleep and its role in memory. Previous theories have suggested that sleep states may be involved in the process of memory consolidation, in which memories are transferred from short to longer-term stores and possibly reorganized into more efficient forms. Recent findings have identified explicit correlates of dreaming during periods of REM sleep allowing examination of dream content as it relates to memory. Experiments involving simultaneous monitoring of areas in the hippocampus and neocortex demonstrated coordinated activity between the hippocampus and the cortex during both awake and sleep states. Taken together, simultaneous monitoring of memory patterns in the hippocampus and neocortex has allowed us to study the differences between these structures in how memories are formed.  They have found evidence that the neocortex may be creating memories that attempt to generalize across experience, while the hippocampus stores the experiences themselves.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMemoryxphysiology22aHippocampusxphysiology22aNeocortexxphysiology22aRats22aSleep Stages22aWakefulness 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14070  aAUTH02067cam a2200493 i 101750918DNLM20260220081102.0190131t20192019caua     b    001 0 eng    a  2018947172  aGBB9194262bnb  a9781526419477q(hbk.)  a9781526419484q(paperback)  a1526419483q(paperback)  z9781526470980q(ePub ebook)  z1526419475q(ePub ebook)  a(OCoLC)1083652273  aUKOBUbengerdad20260220dUKMGBdUKUOYdOCLCOdOCLCFdYDXdFHJdUK5EZdUBCdDNLM  apcc  9United States 4aRA427.8b.G73 201900aWA 59004a6132231 aGreen, Jackie,eauthor.10aHealth promotion :bplanning and strategies /cJackie Green, Ruth Cross, James Woodall, Keith Tones.  a4th edition. 1aLos Angeles :bSAGE,c2019. 4c©2019  axvi, 681 pages :billustrations ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.0 aHealth and health promotion -- Assessing health and its determinants -- Determinants of health actions -- Health promotion planning: a systematic approach -- Information needs -- Health public policy -- Education for health -- Mass communication -- Working with communities -- Settings for health -- Evaluation -- Evidence-based health promotion.8 aThis fourth edition offers a firm foundation in the basics of health promotion before helping students understand the process of planning, implementing, and assessing programmes in the real world.12aHealth Promotionxmethods22aHealth Educationxmethods22aHealth Planning22aPolicy Making22aModels, Organizational1 aCross, Ruth,eauthor.1 aWoodall, James,eauthor.1 aTones, Keith,eauthor.  aAUTH02006cgm a22004334a 101480537DNLM20260227152810.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080801s2008    mdu115|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14602  a(OCoLC)244264632  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 58.52 aNational Human Genome Research Institute (U.S.)10aTribute and farewell to Francis S. Collins, NHGRI director (1993-2008) /cNHGRI.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Aug. 9, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 55 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded July 25, 2008.3 a(CIT): Tribute and farewell to Francis S. Collins, M.D., Ph.D., Director of the National Human Genome Research Institute. Program included remarks from Drs. Elias Zerhouni (NIH), Elizabeth Nabel (NHLBI), Thomas Insel (NIMH), and Harold Varmus (Memorial Sloan-Kettering Cancer Center), Sharon Terry (Genetic Alliance), the Honorable John Porter (FNIH), Acting Surgeon General Steven Galson, Cong. Louise Slaughter (D-NY), and video remarks from Sen. Thomas Harkin (D-IA), with a special performance by The Directors band (Steve Katz, Elias Zerhouni, Steve Libutti, John O'Shea, John Tisdale, Istvan Botos, Robyn Strachan).  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aCollins, Francis S.22aNational Human Genome Research Institute (U.S.)12aGenomics22aAnniversaries and Special Events22aBiomedical Research 2aUnited States 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14602  aAUTH02409nam a2200409 i 101693704DNLM20260210072055.0161004s1541    fr a          000 0 fre    aDNLMbengerdaedcrmbcDNLM  apcc  9France00aWZ 240bD893f 15411 aDu Mont Verd, Raoul,d1450-1550,eauthor.10aSensuyt les fleurs et secretz de medecine :b