0aTX341b.A65500aW1bAN771F10a6642140 aAnnual review of food science and technology.10aAnnu Rev Food Sci Technol2dnlm 0aAnnual review of food science and technologyb(Print)10aAnnual review of food science and technology.  aPalo Alto, CA :bAnnual Reviews  av.  aAnnual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1 (2010).1 aMEDLINEbv1, 2010-0 aPubMedbv1, 2010-  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: Vol. 1 (2010); title from title page.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 1 (2010).12aFood Technology 2aPeriodical0 x1941-14214 uhttp://www.annualreviews.org/journal/food  aAUTH02238cgm a2200409 i 101671893DNLM20260227155143.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u151031s2015    mdu275|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19251  a(OCoLC)935631992  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 26.52 aNIH Digital Summitd(2015 :cBethesda, Md.),jauthor.10aNIH Digital Summit :boptimizing digital to reach patients, scientists, clinicians, and the public /cNational Institutes of Health. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (4 hr., 35 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 19, 2015.3 a(CIT): The National Institutes of Health is hosting a summit to explore how digital is being used by government agencies, clinicians, scientists, patients, and the public to communicate information on health and science. As health information becomes increasingly accessible, it's important to take a look at how these resources are being communicated and shared with the public, how and where clinicians and scientists are discussing important health topics in the digital sphere, and the challenges health communicators face with an ever-evolving digital landscape. The summit is designed to encourage discussion and to strengthen scientific communications communities around the digital and social media strategies used in health and science agencies.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 8, 2015).12aMedical Informatics22aHealth Communication22aInternet 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),ehost institution,eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19251  aAUTH01233cas a2200409 a 101314177DNLM20260210143455.0cr|cn#||||||||070813c20079999ko || p       |    0eng    a  2008243222  a1976-1902l1976-1902  a(OCoLC)212403110  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9Korea (South)00aW1bAS141710aAsian Spine J2dnlm00aAsian spine journal.13aASJ  aSeoul, Korea :bKorean Society of Spine Surgery  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1, no. 1 (June 2007).  aDescription based on first issue; title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 1, no. 2 (Dec. 2007).0 aPubMedbv2n2, 2009-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online; subscription required for full-text online access.12aSpinal Diseases22aSpinal Cord Diseases 2aPeriodical2 aTaehan Chʻŏkchʻu Oegwa Hakhoe.0 x1976-78464 uhttp://www.asianspinejournal.org/  aAUTH01410cas a2200433 a 101273946DNLM20260227090917.0cr|cn ||||||||050722c20079999mduqr p       0    0eng    a  20052149750 a1557-0541l1557-0541y1557-0542  a(OCoLC)61125691  aNSDcNSDdNSDdDNLM  ansdpalcanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bPR668HT10aProg Community Health Partnersh2dnlm00aProgress in community health partnerships :bresearch, education, and action.10aPCHP  aBaltimore, Md. :bJohns Hopkins University Press  av. ;c28 cm.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1.1 (spring 2007)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, Spring 2007-0 aPubMedbv1n1, Spring 2007-  aAlso issued online.12aCommunity Networks22aCommunity Health Services22aMedically Underserved Area 2aPeriodical2 aJohns Hopkins University.bUrban Health Institute.0 x1557-055X4 uhttp://muse.jhu.edu/journals/progress%5Fin%5Fcommunity%5Fhealth%5Fpartnerships%5Fresearch%5Feducation%5Fand%5Faction/  aAUTH02460cgm a2200433 i 9918573266606676DNLM20260227162937.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2022    mdu090|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32771  a(OCoLC)1395883237  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 695.12 aLessons Learned from Global Food and Nutrition Insecurity Webinar Seriesn(2nd :d2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aGlobal Food/Nutrition Insecurity Webinar :bleveraging food environments for food and nutrition security /cFIC, NIH.1 aLeveraging food environments for food and nutrition security 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 30 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 10, 2022.3 a(CIT): The goal is to highlight innovative research projects on nutrition and food insecurity and health outcomes in global settings and identify lessons learned and opportunities for adaption in the US. The objectives are to understand the multi-sector factors and the relationship of food insecurity, diet quality and safety and eating patterns, and diet-related diseases in the international contexts; Explore definition and measurement approaches to food and nutrition insecurity; Learn about innovative research interventions, methodology, and program initiatives across different countries to help address food insecurity and promote health equity in different settings; and Explore gaps, challenges, and opportunities for promoting implementation science research and potential dissemination of impactful food security research interventions.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 22, 2023).12aFood Insecurity22aMalnutrition22aGlobal Health22aSustainable Development 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aFogarty International Center,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=46303  aAUTH01730cas a2200481   0320766DNLM20260224111555.0cr|cn ||||||||720417c19609999cau x         0    0eng    asf 79010148   a0034-3552l0034-3552  aRECBAQ  a(DNLM)R11000000(s)  a(OCoLC)03128510  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bRE174C10aRehabil Couns Bull2dnlm00aRehabilitation counseling bulletin.  aWashington DC :bAmerican Rehabilitation Counseling Association3 32008- :aThousand Oaks, CA :bSage  av.  aFour no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 3, no. 3-       Sept. 1960-  aPlace of publication varies.0 aMEDLINEbSelected citations only0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.  aIssued Sept. 1960-1961? by the Division of Rehabilitation Counseling of the American Personnel and Guidance Association; 1962?-       by the American Rehabilitation Counseling Association.  aContinues the Bulletin of rehabilitation counseling. 2aCounseling 2aRehabilitation 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Personnel and Guidance Association.bDivision of Rehabilitation Counseling.2 aAmerican Rehabilitation Counseling Association.0 x1538-485310tBulletin of rehabilitation counselingw(OCoLC)98806634 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/rcb  aAUTH02737cgm a2200469 i 9918469379806676DNLM20260227162624.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220910s2022    mdu415|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32550  a(DNLM)CIT:32567  a(OCoLC)1388203132  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWB 3562 aNHLBI and OASH State of the Science in Transfusion Medicine Symposiumd(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNHLBI and OASH state of the science in transfusion medicine symposium 2022 /cNHLBI, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (6 hr., 55 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded August 29 & 30, 2022.3 a(CIT):  Hosted by The National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (NHLBI) of NIH and the Office of the Assistant Secretary of Health (OASH) of the Department of Health and Human Services, the purpose is to identify current basic, translational, and clinical research priorities, that if addressed in the next 3-10 years, would increase and diversify the volunteer blood donor pool, identify which blood products from which donors would best match the needs of specific recipient populations, and help ensure safe and effective transfusion strategies. Six working groups have identified draft research priorities: Blood donors and the supply, Optimizing transfusion outcomes for recipients, Emerging infections, Mechanistic aspects of components and transfusion, New methods in computational science for transfusion, Donor and blood recipient health disparities in transfusion medicine. For more information go to https://www.isbtweb.org/events/nhlbi-state-of-the-science-in-transfusion-medicine-2022  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (June 22, 2023).12aBlood Transfusion22aTransfusion Reaction22aBlood Safety22aBlood Donationxsupply & distribution22aBlood Donors 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute,ehost institution.2 aUnited States.bOffice of the Assistant Secretary for Health,ehost institution.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4610740uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=46109  aAUTH02085cgm a2200457 i 9918573278106676DNLM20260227162839.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2022    mdu404|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32734  a(OCoLC)1427438349  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWS 242 aNICHD 60th Anniversary Symposium: Healthy Pregnancies, Heathy Children, Healthy and Optimal Livesd(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNICHD 60th anniversary symposium :bhealthy pregnancies, healthy children, healthy and optimal lives /cNICHD, NIH.1 aHealthy pregnancies, healthy children, healthy and optimal lives 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (6 hr., 44 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 17, 2022.3 a(CIT): Join NICHD as it marks its 60th anniversary to celebrate key advances and milestones in the institute's history, highlight its current contributions to science and public health, and look ahead toward future research endeavors and directions.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 11, 2024).22aEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.)12aChild Health12aAcademies and Institutes22aPregnancy22aQuality of Life22aAnniversaries and Special Events 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=46182  aAUTH02763cgm a2200493 i 101716399DNLM20260227160011.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u171107s2017    mdu108|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:23523  a(OCoLC)1012785939  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWP 48000aTRACO 2017.pLymphoma and small molecules /cJohn T. Schiller and Anton Simeonov.1 aLymphoma and small molecules1 iTitle from Power Point screen:aHPV vaccines to prevent cervical cancer and other HPV-assoicated diseases1 iTitle from Power Point screen:aSmall molecule drug discovery : challenges and opportunities1 aTranslational research in clinical oncology 2017 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2017]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 48 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 17, 2017.3 a(CIT): Translational Research in Clinical Oncology (TRACO)  Recent advances in understanding cancer biology are beginning to be translated into improvements in diagnosis and treatment of cancer. In the post-genome era, we increasingly rely on strong collaboration between basic and clinical scientists to develop novel approaches for treatment of human disease. The NCI Center for Cancer Research (CCR) is one of the largest cancer research organizations in the world, with more than 200 principal investigators, and has played a major role in developing and implementing many new technologies, such as nanotechnology, next generation sequencing, genomics and proteomics.  For more information go to <a href='http://ccr.cancer.gov/training/trainee-resources/courses-workshops/traco'>http://ccr.cancer.gov/training/trainee-resources/courses-workshops/traco.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 20, 2017).12aUterine Cervical Neoplasmsxprevention & control22aDrug Discovery22aMolecular Biology22aPapillomaviridaexpathogenicity22aPapillomavirus Vaccinesxtherapeutic use 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aSchiller, John T.,espeaker.1 aSimeonov, A.q(Anton),espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23523  aAUTH01233cas a2200409 i 101670617DNLM20260227090910.0cr||||||||||||151020c20159