 M. Smith. 1aSt. Louis :bC.V. Mosby Company,c1919.  a59 pages ;c18 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202011232pda5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20260212lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM12aPharmaceutical Solutions22aDrug Dosage Calculations 2aNurses Instruction  aAUTH03573cgm a2200445 i 101649917DNLM20260227154903.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u141224s2014    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18760  a(OCoLC)902835070  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWT 1041 aMurabito, Joanne,eauthor.10aGenetics of aging phenotypes over the life course :ba population perspective /cDr. Joanne Murabito ; Geroscience Interest Group. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Dec. 4, 2014.3 a(CIT): The Trans-NIH GeroScience Interest Group (GSIG) cordially invites you to its fall seminar, featuring Dr. Joanne Murabito. Dr. Murabito is Director of the Clinic at the Framingham Heart Study, and she is an Associate Professor of Medicine at Boston University School of Medicine. Dr. Murabito's research goals have focused on aging and reproductive traits in the Framingham Heart study and beyond. Specifically, her research program focuses on using the tools of epidemiology and high-throughput genetic platforms to better understand the natural history of aging and to detect novel pathways and mechanisms for aging-related traits. Studies in her laboratory encompass analysis of genome-wide association studies of aging phenotypes in human population samples using common and rare variant platforms; and investigation of determinants of healthy aging in the Framingham population. Dr. Murabito"s current projects include the epidemiology of centenarian experience in Framingham, physical activity and function, and biomarkers of frailty. She leads several international consortia dedicated to uncovering genes for aging-related traits, including age at menarche, age at natural menopause, and longevity.  She collaborates with scientists at the Jackson Laboratory Nathan Shock Center of Excellence in the Basic Biology of Aging to further identify the functional relevance of human association findings. Dr. Murabito is keenly interested in how to reach older participants through novel technologies and use those technologies to maintain health. Dr. Murabito's research has demonstrated that multiple genetic loci underlie reproductive aging, which are associated with many downstream aging-related disorders including obesity, DNA damage and repair and immune function.   The GeroScience Interest Group (GSIG) was formed to enhance opportunities for discussion of the intersection between the biology of aging and the biology of disease and conditions that are of interest across ICs.  It is focused on basic biology, but with a longer view towards translation.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed January 28, 2015.12aAgingxgenetics22aCohort Studies22aLongevityxgenetics22aMenopausexgenetics22aPhenotype 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bGeroscience Interest Group,esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18760  aAUTH01684cas a2200469 i 101603896DNLM20260227090919.0cr||||||||||||130312c20139999caubr p       |    0eng    a  2012273902  a2325-9574l2325-9574  a(OCoLC)822329536  aDLCbengerdad20260227dDLCdOCLCQdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW1bJO931VIC10aJ Int Assoc Provid AIDS Care2dnlm00aJournal of the International Association of Providers of AIDS Care.3 aJIAPAC 1aThousand Oaks, CA :bSage Publications,c[2013]-  aBimonthly  atext2rdacontent  aunmediated2rdamedia  avolume2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 12, no. 1, Jan./Feb. 2013.  aPrint ceased in 2018.1 aMEDLINEbv12n1, Jan.-Feb. 2013-0 aPubMedbv12n1, Jan.-Feb. 2013-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: Volume 12, number 2 (March/April 2013); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 12, number 2 (March/April 2013).12aAcquired Immunodeficiency Syndrome22aHIV Infections 2aPeriodical2 aInternational Association of Providers of AIDS Care,eissuing body.0 x2325-958200tJournal of the International Association of Physicians in AIDS Carex1545-1097w(DLC)   2003213132w(OCoLC)523838564 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/jia4 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101603896  aAUTH01651cas a22003737i 101683635DNLM20260220105707.0cr|cn#||||||||160521c20069999ru || p       |    0ruso   a1999-6209l1999-6209  a(OCoLC)1032571894  aDNLMbengerdad20260220dDNLM  9Russia (Federation)00aW1bME8083200aMedit︠s︡inskiĭ vestnik Bashkortostana.1 iEnglish title on t.p. verso:aBashkortostan medical journal 1aUfa :bFGBOU "Bashkirskiĭ gosudarstvennyĭ medit︠s︡inskiĭ universitet" Ministerstva zdravookhranenii︠a︡ RF  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan in 2006.  aAlso issued online.  aText in Russian, summaries and table of contents also in English.  aIssued also by: Ministerstvo zdravookhranenii︠a︡ Respubliki Bashkortostan; and, FGUN "Ufimskiĭ nauchno-issledovatelʹskiĭ institut medit︠s︡iny truda i ėkologii cheloveka" Federalʹnoĭ sluzhby po nadzoru v sfere zashchity prav potrebiteleĭ i blagopoluchii︠a︡ cheloveka.  aDescription based on: Tom 12, no. 3 (Mai/̆Ii︠u︡nʹ 2016); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Tom 12, no. 3 (Mai/̆Ii︠u︡nʹ 2016).12aClinical Medicine 2aBashkiria 2aPeriodical2 aBashkirskiĭ gosudarstvennyĭ medit︠s︡inskiĭ universitet,eissuing body.4 uhttp://www.mvb-bsmu.ru/  aAUTH01262cas a2200445 a 9442139DNLM20260227091025.0cr|cn#||||||||950119c19949999enk x p       0||| 0eng    a1350-6285l1350-6285  aVICOF6  a(DNLM)SR0077934(s)  a(OCoLC)31774493  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9England00aW1bVI905R10aVis cogn2dnlm00aVisual cognition.  aHove, East Sussex, UK :bLawrence Erlbaum Associates,cc1994-3 aHove :bLawrence Erlbaum Associates  av. :bill.  aEight no. a yearb2002-  aFour no. a year,b-1998  a6 no. a year,b1999-2001  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, issue 1-  aTitle from cover.0 aMEDLINEbSelected citations only0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online. 2aCognition 2aVision, Ocular 2aVisual Perception 2aPeriodical0 x1464-07164 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/loi/pvis20  aAUTH04178cgm a22004094a 101510692DNLM20260227153039.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090710s2009    mdu073|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15151  a(OCoLC)711828268  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 503.21 aAltice, Frederick.10aBuilding a bridge :btransitional programs from the criminal justice to the community setting for HIV+ drug users /cFrederick L. Altice.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 13 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 26, 2009.3 a(CIT): American"s thirst for incarceration has resulted in the highest per capita rate of incarceration in the world.  Our failed legal policies on drug abuse contribute greatly to 13 million people interfacing with the criminal justice system each year and 1 in 100 Americans being behind bars.  The failure to adequately treat substance abuse, a chronic and relapsing condition within our criminal justice system, has resulted in a concentration of individuals with HIV/AIDS, viral hepatitis, mental illness and tuberculosis behind bars.  Though in recent years, prisons and jails have become increasingly successful in treating chronic conditions such as HIV/AIDS, but less successful in ensuring continuity of care after release.  As such, 26% of all people living with HIV are released to the community, yet their HIV treatment outcomes less than optimal.  In this presentation, we will review the epidemiology of HIV/AIDS within the criminal justice system and explore evidence-based solutions and alternative mechanisms to improve HIV detection, treatment and continuity of care for those with HIV/AIDS who interface with the criminal justice system.    Dr. Altice"s research interests are focused on the interface between infectious diseases and substance abuse. As a clinical epidemiologist, health services and intervention researcher, he has created novel programs for the treatment of HIV, HCV, and tuberculosis in vulnerable populations, including injection drug users and prison inmates. Specifically, he has been an international leader in research related to adherence to antiretroviral therapy, particularly among HIV+ drug users, has made considerable inroads into novel approaches HIV+ using directly administered antiretroviral therapy and other structural interventions to facilitate adherence both nationally and internationally.  More biographical information:  http://www.med.yale.edu/intmed/faculty/altice.html  HANDOUTS are posted at http://sigs.nih.gov/bssrig/Documents/Forms/AllItems.aspx  This lecture is an installment of the Behavioral and Social Sciences Research Lecture Series sponsored by the NIH Office of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research and organized by the NIH Behavioral and Social Sciences Research Coordinating Committee.  The Behavioral and Social Sciences Research Coordinating Committee (BSSR CC), with support from the Office of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research (OBSSR), convenes a series of guest lectures and symposia on selected topics in the behavioral and social sciences. These presentations by prominent behavioral and social scientists provide the NIH community with overviews of current research on topics of scientific and social interest. The lectures and symposia are approximately 50 minutes in length, with additional time for questions and discussion. All seminars are open to NIH staff and to the general public.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHIV Infections22aJurisprudence 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15151  aAUTH01741cgm a2200409 a 101591631DNLM20260227154347.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120901s2012    mdu055|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17480  a(OCoLC)809886756  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.55.T71 aHausfeld, Jeffrey N.10aTranslational medicine /cJeffrey Hausfeld and Michael Ross.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (55 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded Aug. 7, 2012.3 a(CIT): Society of Physician Entrepreneurs (SoPE)   Drs. Jeffrey Hausfeld and Michael Ross will present their stories:  <ul> <li>How physicians have taken their innovative ideas from opportunities to marketed products</li> <li>How they have overcome translational hurdles along the way, and how they have integrated their experiences with their medical training to accelerate their faculty positions, redefine their careers, change their perspectives and leave a bigger footprint</li> </ul>.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aTranslational Research, Biomedical.22aMarketing of Health Services22aPhysicians 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aSociety of Physician Entrepreneurs40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17480  aAUTH01957cgm a22004574a 101496157DNLM20260227152807.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090203s2009    mdu096 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14852  a(OCoLC)319127426  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 2401 aPalmore, Tara N.10aBacterial sepsis :ba new epidemic and an old receptor /cTara N. Palmore [and John Hanover].  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from PowerPoint (viewed Apr. 3, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 35 min., 37 sec. : sd., col.).  a"2009 Demystifying medicine."  aRecorded Jan. 13, 2009.3 a(CIT): The course includes presentation of patients, pathology, diagnosis and therapy in the context of major disease problems and current research.  Primarily directed toward Ph.D. students, fellows, and staff, it is also of interest to medical students and clinicians.  The course is designed to help bridge the gap between advances in biology and their application to major human diseases.  Each session includes clinical and basic science components which are presented by NIH staff and outside invitees.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBacteremiaxdrug therapy22aAsialoglycoprotein Receptor22aBacteremiaximmunology22aBacteremiaxtherapy22aImmunity, Innate 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14852  aAUTH01558cas a22004697a 9809663DNLM20260227091043.0cr|cnu||||||||980820c19979999gw  x p       0||| 0ger    asn 98039359   a1432-9123l1432-9123  a(DNLM)SR0091885(s)  a(OCoLC)39728394  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 ageraengfeng  amsc  9Germany00aW1bSO887S10aSomnologie (Berl)2dnlm00aSomnologie :bSchlafforschung und Schlafmedizin = Somnology : sleep 