Physiology, Washington University in St. Louis; 11:05 a.m. Break. 11:30 a.m. "NMDA Receptors: Presynaptic, Postsynaptic, Metabotropic?" Craig Jahr, Ph.D., Senior Scientist, Vollum Institute, Oregon Health and Science University; 12:00 p.m. "The Functional Anatomy of the Ionotropic Glutamate Receptor Family" Derek Bowie, Ph.D., Professor, McGill University; 12:30 p.m. "AMPA Receptor Domains and Synaptic Trafficking" Roger Nicoll, M.D., Professor, University of California, San Francisco; 1:00 p.m. Lunch. 2:00 p.m. "Structure of ASIC1-Snake Toxin Complex Reveals Mechanism of Ion Selectivity in ENaC/DEG Channels" Isabelle Baconguis, Ph.D., Vollum Fellow, Vollum Institute, Oregon Health and Science University; 2:30 p.m. "Glutamate Receptors: Lights, Camera, Action" Andrew J R Plested, Ph.D., Group Leader, Molecular Neuroscience and Biophysics, Leibniz-Institut Fur Molekulare Pharmakologie; 3:00 p.m. "G Protein Activation of the GIRK Potassium Channel" Roderick MacKinnon, M.D., Professor, Laboratory of Molecular Neurobiology and Biophysics, Rockefeller University; 3:30 p.m. Break. 4:00 p.m. "Structure, Function, and Biology of NMDA Receptors" Hiro Furukawa, Ph.D., Associate Professor, Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory; 4:30 p.m. "Molecules, Mechanisms and Mark: Adventures in Atomic Exploration at Chemical Synapses" Eric Gouaux, Ph.D., Senior Scientist, Vollum Institute Oregon Health and Science University; 5:00 p.m. "What's in a name? AMPA and Kainate Receptors in Flies and Ctenophores" Mark Mayer, Ph.D., Senior Investigator, Laboratory of Cellular and Molecular Neurophysiology, Eunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development, National Institutes of Health; 5:30 p.m. Closing Remarks Story C. Landis, Ph.D., Scientist Emeritus, NINDS Intramural Program.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 2, 2016).12aReceptors, Glutamate 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19371  aAUTH01769cgm a22004574a 101268315DNLM20260227150421.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu013 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11717  a(OCoLC)84721139  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQV 7371 aGaston, Marilyn H.10a0% disparities, 100% acess [i.e. access] :bpharmaceutical education leads the way : Dr. Marilyn Hughes Gaston, Associate Administrator, HRSA, addresses the Interim Meeting of the American Association of Colleges of Pharmacy in Washington, DC.3 a0% disparities, 100% acess3 a0% disparities, 100% access3 aZero percent disparities, one hundred percent acess  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 12, 2007).  aStreaming video (12 min., 6 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 18, 2001.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aPharmaceutical Services22aEducation, Pharmacy22aHealth Services Accessibility22aProfessional Role 2aUnited States 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11717  aAUTH01659cgm a2200421 a 101591635DNLM20260227154355.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120901s2012    mdu358|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17515  a(OCoLC)811952141  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQV 74500aBridging the gap :bovercoming bottlenecks in the development of therapeutics for infectious diseases /cNIAID.1 aOvercoming bottlenecks in the development of therapeutics for infectious diseases  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (5 hr., 58 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded Aug. 9, 2012.3 a(CIT): Workshop to bring together experts in the field of infectious diseases therapeutics development and NIAID investigators to share information and ideas about how to promote and facilitate product development.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aDrug Discoveryxeconomics22aAnti-Infective Agents22aCommunicable Diseasesxdrug therapy22aDrug Industry 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17515  aAUTH02030cgm a2200409 i 101662994DNLM20260227155110.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150625s2015    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19057  a(OCoLC)916259811  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 171.51 aAkil, H.q(Huda)eauthor.14aThe depressed brain :bsobering and hopeful lessons /cHuda Akil. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded June 10, 2015.3 a(CIT): In her talk, Dr. Akil will discuss the challenges of understanding the pathophysiology of major depressive disorder at the molecular and cellular level and the surprising insights derived from genomics and human postmortem studies. She will describe how animal models and neuroscience studies at multiple levels (from genetic to behavioral) can be used to test and validate new targets for treatment and/or as biomarkers of the illness. Her talk will also focus on the notion of "molecular organizers" that play a broad role in neuroplasticity, development, adaptation to the environment, and responsiveness to treatment.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 30, 2015).12aDepressive Disorder 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19057  aAUTH02018cgm a22004454a 101486626DNLM20260227152653.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081007s2008    mdu849 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14636  a(DNLM)CIT:14638  a(OCoLC)288982859  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 26600aCancer immunology and immunotherapy :brealizing the promise /csponsored by the Center of Excellence in Immunology, NCI.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 2, 2008).  aStreaming video (14 hr., 08 min., 42 sec. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded Sept. 11-12, 2008.3 a(CIT): This meeting will host leaders in the field of cancer immunology and immunotherapy, include recent advances in both translational and clinical research, and should provide an exciting forum for discussion and debate on promising immunologic approaches to prevent and treat cancer.  Sessions will include "Adoptive Cell Transfer", "Monoclonal Antibodies", "Costimulatory and Inhibitory Receptors", "Cancer Vaccines", "Gene Therapy", "New Cytokines", "Regulatory T Cells", and "New Immunotherapy Targets."  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxtherapy22aImmunotherapy22aNeoplasmsximmunology 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.).bCenter of Excellence in Immunology.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1463640uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14638  aAUTH02064cgm a22004934a 101300937DNLM20260227153247.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070302s2007    mdu057 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13621  a(OCoLC)104814671  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 501 aGochuico, Bernadette.10aEthics of informing estranged family members of genetic risks /cBernadette Gochuico and Catherine Ross.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aClinical Center grand rounds  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 15, 2007).  aStreaming video (56 min., 14 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 7, 2007.3 a(CIT): NIH Clinical Center Ethics Grand Rounds    A discussion of a case where a patient, estranged from a family member, found he may have passed along a genetic risk to that person. The case will be presented by Bernadette Gochuico, MD., NHLBI.  Catherine Ross, Esq., a professor of law from George Washington University Law School, specializing in family law, children's rights, law and policy and juvenile justice will discuss the ethical issues.  For more information, visit  http://www.cc.nih.gov/about/news/grcurrent.html.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aGenetic Diseases, Inborn22aAdoption22aCasuistry22aDisclosurexethics22aFamily Relations22aPulmonary Fibrosis 2aCase Reports 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13621  aAUTH01526nam a2200433 i 9918987874506676DNLM20260204105715.0230109t20232023maua     b    001 0 eng    a  2023496365  aGBC3063532bnb  a9780323919302q(paperback)  a0323919308q(paperback)  z9780323919296q(ebook)  a(OCoLC)1363814375  aUKMGBbengerdacUKMGBdOCLCFdBDXdUIUdOCLCOdDLCdIG#  apcc  9United States 4aQR88.3b.L33 202300aQW 14204a660.6/222300aLactic acid bacteria as cell factories :bsynthetic biology and metabolic engineering /cedited by Didier Montet, Ramesh C. Ray, Vasco Ariston De Carvalho Azevedo, Spiros Paramithiotis. 1aCambridge ;aOxford :bWoodhead Publishing,c[2023] 4c©2023  axv, 420 pages :billustrations (black and white, and colour) ;c23 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aApplied biotechnology reviews  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.12aLactobacillales22aSynthetic Biology22aMetabolic Engineering1 aMontet, Didier,eeditor.1 aRay, Ramesh C.,eeditor.1 aAzevedo, Vasco,eeditor.1 aParamithiotis, Spiros,eeditor. 0aApplied biotechnology reviews  aAUTH02564cgm a22004574a 101549261DNLM20260227153740.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101208s2010    mdu709 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16276  a(DNLM)CIT:16284  a(OCoLC)697768559  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWH 1702 aJames B. Herrick Symposium : Sickle Cell Disease Care and Researchd(2010 :cNational Institutes of Health)10aJames B. Herrick Symposium : Sickle Cell Disease Care and Research :bpast, present, and future /csponsors, NHLBI, NHGRI, NIDDK, NICHD, NINDS, NLM, ORDR, NCMHD.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (11 hr., 49 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 16&17, 2010.3 a(CIT): In 1910, Dr. James B. Herrick, an attending physician at Presbyterian Hospital and professor of medicine at Rush Medical College in Chicago, Illinois, published an article on the case of an anemic West Indian patient1. Herrick"s clinical and laboratory findings of the patient"s "peculiar elongated and sickle-shaped" red blood corpuscles represent the first description of sickle cell anemia in Western medical literature.   To mark the centennial of the publication of Herrick"s case report, experts will examine the history and societal impact of the disease and place the disease within the context of existing and future basic, translational and clinical research. Invited speakers will address unresolved challenges and the ongoing burden of the disease. They will also focus on genetic, cellular and clinical perspectives across the lifespan, as well as existing therapeutic options and possible future treatments.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHerrick, James B.q(James Bryan),d1861-1954.12aAnemia, Sickle Cell22aAnemia, Sickle Cellxhistory22aHistory, 20th Century 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1627640uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16284  aAUTH01023cas a2200337 a 9113812DNLM20260217111026.0911024d19882021gw qr p       0||| 0ger    a0933-842Xl0933-842X  a(DNLM)SR0066901(s)  a(OCoLC)24625873  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Germany00aW1bPR319H00aPraxis der klinischen Verhaltensmedizin und Rehabilitation.  aDortmund :bVerlag Modernes Lernen,c[1988-2021]3 aDortmund :bVerlag Modernes Lernen  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 a1. Jahrg., Heft 1 (März 1988)-1 aCeased with 34. Jahrgang, Heft 1 (2021).  aTitle from cover. 2aBehavioral Medicine 2aRehabilitation 2aPeriodical00tPsychosoziale und medizinische Rehabilitationx2750-1353  aAUTH02233cgm a22004454a 101300933DNLM20260227151814.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070302s2007    mdu065|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13616  a(OCoLC)123962814  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 1021 aLeDoux, Joseph E.10aHow does it feel? :bClosing the gap between unconscious and conscious emotion /cJoseph Ledoux.30aClosing the gap between unconscious and conscious emotion  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 21, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 5 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 5, 2007.3 a(CIT): Dr. LeDoux's lab is aimed at understanding the biological mechanisms of emotional memory. They are particularly interested in how the brain learns and stores information about danger. Using classical fear conditioning as a way of inducing emotional memories in rats, they have mapped the neural pathways by which sensory stimuli enter and flow through the brain in the process of fear learning. This work implicated specific circuits within the amygdala as essential for the formation of memories of the fear conditioning experience. It is now clear that the same brain system underlies fear learning in humans. The detailed mechanisms of fear, which can only be uncovered through animal studies, are thus applicable to understanding fear processing in the human brain.  NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aEmotionsxphysiology22aConsciousness22aMemoryxphysiology22aUnconscious, Psychology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13616  aAUTH01628cgm a22004574a 101289617DNLM20260227151359.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2006    mdu051  |||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13119  a(OCoLC)84671932  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2061 aAndonyadis, Christo.10aOverview of caBIG activities for clinical trials management and tissue banking /cChristo Andonyadis and Ian Fore.1 iTitle from PowerPoint screen:aKey tools for clinical trials management and tissue banking  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 13, 2006).  aStreaming video (50 min., 42 sec. : sd., col.).  aThe second of 4 seminars in March 2006 about caBIG, the Cancer Biomedical Informatics Grid.  aRecorded Mar. 15, 2006.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMedical Informatics12aNeoplasms22aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration22aClinical Trials as Topic22aTissue Banks 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13119  aAUTH02385cgm a2200409 i 101746771DNLM20260227155921.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190502s2019    mdu780 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27452  a(DNLM)CIT:31699  a(OCoLC)1107446504  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 70504aThe science of interoception and its roles in nervous system disorders /cNIH Blueprint for Neuroscience Research. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (13 hr., 6 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 16 & April 17, 2019.3 a(CIT): The objectives of this workshop are to identify gaps in research related to the science of interoception, its role(s) in nervous system disorders, and to develop strategies and recommendations to facilitate the advancement of this area of research. The workshop will bring together expertise from diverse fields including basic neuroscience, psychology, physiology, and clinical research to deliberate two important dynamic connections - the connections between brain and body and the connections between basic research and human/clinical research. The primary focus areas for the workshop include- the neural circuitry underlying the dynamic interactions between the central and peripheral nervous systems; interoceptive processes in associated diseases and disorders; effect of modulating interoceptive processes for potential interventions/therapies; and development of technologies and methodologies to enhance interoceptive research.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed June 28, 2019).12aInteroception22aBiomedical Research 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNIH Blueprint for Neuroscience Research,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?2745240uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27453  aAUTH01731cgm a2200457 i 101651656DNLM20260227155206.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150212s2015    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18808  a(OCoLC)904787127  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWD 30800aMyelokathectic immunodeficiency disorders :btales from the clinic /cPhilip M. Murphy.  Ulcerative colitis : an atypical th2 disorder mediated by NKT cells / Warren Strober.1 aUlcerative colitis :ban atypical th2 disorder mediated by NKT cells 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCC grand rounds  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Jan. 21, 2015.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed Feb. 15, 2015.12aImmunologic Deficiency Syndromes22aColitis, Ulcerativeximmunology22aKiller Cells, Naturalximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast12aMurphy, Phil,cDr.tMyelokathectic immunodeficiency disorders.12aStrober, Warren.tUlcerative colitis.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18808  aAUTH05490cgm a22004934a 101468747DNLM20260227152508.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080307s2008    mdu068|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14329  a(OCoLC)226279478  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2671 aSchnitzer, Jan.10aProteomic imaging of endothelium and its caveolae in vivo :bpumping antibody into specific organs and solid tumors /cJan Schnitzer.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 16, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 8 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 27, 2008.3 a(CIT): Cancer and other disease biomarkers and targets may provide key diagnostic, prognostic and therapeutic opportunities including clinical trial surrogates and screens for patient treatment assignment. Blood and tissues are complex with extensive molecular diversity over a wide concentration range that creates discovery challenges for proteomic analysis. Candidate biomarkers require strict validation that is laborious and expensive. Noninvasive imaging provides objective validation for diagnostic and therapeutic targets in vivo. Drugs, gene vectors, and nanoparticles may benefit greatly from improved in vivo delivery through homing to specific disease biomarkers. Yet in vivo barriers limit access to most disease targets including cancer. Our evolving discovery and validation strategies that are intended to overcome these hurdles will be discussed.  We have developed novel systems biology approaches that integrate nanotechnology-based subcellular fractionation, quantitative organellar & subtractive proteomics, bioinformatic interrogation, antibody generation, expression profiling, and various in vivo imaging modalities to quickly identify and validate candidates for pre-clinical and clinical testing. Analysis of rodent and human tumor samples have been compared to focus on clinical meaningful targets and to understand model relevance to human disease. Tissue and tumor microenvironmental influences on endothelial cell expression are extensive. We have developed quantitative proteomic analysis using a new spectral intensity index to identify 300+ proteins concentrated in caveolae; many of which are confirmed by immuno-electron microscopy. Bioinformatic analysis of this subproteome ascribes new functional attributes to the caveolae. Examples of new targets will be presented along with validation studies using antibodies and molecular imaging to demonstrate specific targeting in vivo. Novel targets in caveolae enable antibodies to penetrate deep into solid tumors and single organs and were utilized to improve tissue-specific imaging and treatment. Our recent findings reveal that caveolae not only express tissue-specific proteins but also function to rapidly and actively pump specifically targeted antibodies and nanoparticles across the endothelial cell barrier and into the tissue interstitium. Such pervasive access improves the efficacy of radioimmunotherapy in destroying both stromal and tumor cells and in treating a wide variety of solid tumors. Lastly, functional knockout of newly identified tumor-induced endothelial proteins can greatly affect tumor growth, metastasis, and angiogenesis. These studies illustrate the benefit and power of focused organellar proteomic mapping in vivo. This type of "proteomic imaging of the vascular biomarker space including its in vivo accessible target space and transvascular pumping space" may bode well for application to cancer and many other diseases.  Dr. Jan E. Schnitzer is the Scientific Director of the Sidney Kimmel Cancer Center, located in San Diego. The Sidney Kimmel Cancer Center is an independent, nonprofit research institution dedicated to the development and advancement of biomedical research to eliminate cancer. The SKCC was recently awarded a $14.4 million Program Project Grant, over five years, from the National Cancer Institute, with Dr. Schnitzer as the Principal Investigator of this grant.  Dr. Schnitzer's research has identified "zip code" molecules for cancer that allow drugs to be "mailed" directly to solid tumors via their blood vessels. His lab discovered several years ago techniques to purify specific small parts of cells and tissue that are directly in contact with circulating blood and mediate transport into the tissue.    More information available at  http://nano.cancer.gov/about_alliance/bios_partnerships.asp#Schnitzer.   WALS.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxdrug therapy22aBiomarkers22aCaveolaeximmunology22aDiagnostic Imaging22aDrug Delivery Systems22aEndotheliumximmunology22aProteomicsxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14329  aAUTH01571cgmaa22004094a 101256163DNLM20260227144526.0vf|cbahouvf|cjahoucr|cna||||||||050830s2004    mdu542       f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:3536  a(DNLM)CIT:3537  a(OCoLC)61692668  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aWA 30900aWomen's health interdisciplinary research symposium /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  aUnited States.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  a6 videocassettes of 6 (Betacam SP) (9 hrs., 2 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a3 videocassettes of 3 (VHS) (9 hrs., 2 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aORWH women's health seminar series  aPresented October 4 & 5, 2004.  aAlso available online.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM12aWomen's Health 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aORWH women's health seminar series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=35364 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3537  aAUTH01802cas a2200505 a 8710631DNLM20260227091022.0cr|cn#||||||||871001c19839999ii qr p       0    0eng    asn 87026406   a0970-4388l0970-4388  a(DNLM)SR0052313(s)  a(OCoLC)11848690  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  ansdp  9India00aW1bJO931E10aJ Indian Soc Pedod Prev Dent2dnlm 0aJournal of the Indian Society of Pedodontics and Preventive Dentistry00aJournal of the Indian Society of Pedodontics and Preventive Dentistry.1 iVols. for <2000-> have title:aJournal of Indian Society of Pedodontics and Preventive Dentistry  aChandigarh, India :bPublished on behalf of the Society by H.S. Chawla, Dept. of Dentistry, Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education & Research,c1983-3 32006- :aMumbai :bMedknow Publications  av. :bill., ports.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Mar. 1983)-  aTitle from cover.  aImprint varies.1 aMEDLINEbv1n1,Mar. 1983-1 aPubMedbv1n1,Mar. 1983-1 aIndex to dental literaturex0019-3992bv1n1,Mar. 1983-v17n4,Dec. 1999  aAlso issued online. 2aPediatric Dentistry 2aPreventive Dentistry 2aSocieties, Dental 2aIndia 2aPeriodical2 aIndian Society of Pedodontics and Preventive Dentistry.0 x1998-39054 uhttps://journals.lww.com/jped/pages/default.aspx  aAUTH02000cgm a22004094a 101310925DNLM20260227152053.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070703s2007    mdu314|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13954  a(OCoLC)157002637  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWX 1732 aAmerican Health Information Community.bMeetingn(14th :d2007 :cWashington, D.C.)10aAmerican health information community - June 2007 /cU.S. Department of Health and Human Services.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 16, 2007).  aStreaming video (5 hr., 14 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jun. 12, 2007.3 a(CIT): The American Health Information Community (the Community), is a committee established under the Federal Advisory Committee Act (Pub. L. No. 92-463, 5 U.S.C., App.), by the Secretary of the Department of Health and Human Services.  The Community will advise the Secretary and recommend specific actions to achieve a common interoperability framework for health information technology (IT) and serve as a forum for participation from a broad range of stakeholders to provide input on achieving interoperability of health IT. The Community is made up of 17 voting members, including the Chair, and members have been appointed by the Secretary.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMedical Records Systems, Computerized22aHealth Policy 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13954  aAUTH06614cgm a2200433 i 101649925DNLM20260227154923.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u141224s2014    mdu675 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18774  a(DNLM)CIT:18775  a(OCoLC)906119252  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWB 1462 aNIH Pathways to Prevention: Advancing the Research on Myalgic Encephalomyelitis/Chronic Fatigue Syndrome (Workshop)d(2014 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aNIH Pathways to Prevention :bAdvancing the Research on Myalgic Encephalomyelitis/Chronic Fatigue Syndrome /cNIH Office of Disease Prevention. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (11 hr., 15 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Dec. 9 and 10, 2014.3 a(CIT): Myalgic Encephalomyelitis/Chronic Fatigue Syndrome (ME/CFS) is a complex, multifaceted disorder characterized by extreme fatigue and a host of other symptoms that can worsen after physical or mental activity, but do not improve with rest. In addition to extreme fatigue, people with ME/CFS may also experience:  Widespread muscle and joint pain Sore throat Tender lymph nodes in the neck or armpit Headaches Sleep problems Difficulty with short-term memory or concentration Effects of the illness can range from moderate to debilitating, and can substantially impact everyday functioning. Routine daily activities such as cooking meals, brushing teeth, and caring for children become difficult. Additionally, sensitivity to environmental factors (e.g., noise, light, chemicals) may force many individuals with ME/CFS into seclusion or withdrawal from society.  The onset of ME/CFS symptoms may be sudden--for example, immediately following a viral illness such as the flu--or gradual, with no discernible attachment to a specific event or time. The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) reports over 1 million adults with ME/CFS in the United States, and recent evidence has shown a higher prevalence in females compared to males. Certain racial/ethnic groups have also been found to be at an increased risk for ME/CFS; most notably Native American and African American populations. The economic burden of ME/CFS, including annual health care costs, is estimated to be between $1.9 billion and $7.2 billion.  There are many aspects of ME/CFS that are problematic. First, the underlying mechanisms are unclear. There are differing views as to whether ME/CFS is a central nervous system, metabolic, infectious or post-infectious, cardiovascular, immune system, or other type of disorder. Second, there is little agreement among clinical and research professionals, as well as patient groups, regarding the name of the illness.3 aThe name myalgic encephalomyelitis or ME is more commonly used in Europe and Canada, while the name chronic fatigue syndrome or CFS is used more often in the United States and Australia. Yet the acronym ME/CFS is increasingly being used worldwide. Third, no laboratory tests exist for diagnosing ME/CFS, and its diagnosis is one of exclusion. All other illnesses with overlapping symptoms must be ruled out prior to an ME/CFS diagnosis. Fourth, there are no drugs or therapies approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) to treat ME/CFS. Clinical trials to test drug or therapy effectiveness, and drug development to target the underlying cause, are difficult to conduct because of the unknown causes, varied symptom profile, and lack of concurrence regarding diagnostic criteria. Lastly, medical professionals disagree on many aspects of ME/CFS, including whether the illness is real, and there is no definitive answer about the effectiveness of current therapies (e.g., diet, use of off-label or experimental drugs).  The 2014 Pathways to Prevention Workshop: Advancing the Research on Myalgic Encephalomyelitis/Chronic Fatigue Syndrome will seek to clarify:  How the research on ME/CFS using multiple case definitions has contributed to the state of the current scientific literature on diagnosis, pathophysiology, treatment, cure, and prevention of ME/CFS How the measurement outcomes (tools and measures) currently used by researchers of ME/CFS are able to distinguish among those patients diagnosed with ME/CFS, including the sensitivity of the tools and measures to identify subsets of patients according to duration, severity, nature of the illness, onset characteristics, and other categorizations How the research on treatments or therapies shown to be effective in addressing symptoms of ME/CFS will lead to an understanding of the underlying pathology associated with ME/CFS How innovative research approaches have provided an understanding of the pathophysiology of ME/CFS, and how this knowledge can be applied to the development of effective and safe treatments.  Initial planning for each Pathways to Prevention workshop, regardless of condition, is coordinated by a Working Group that nominates panelists and speakers, and develops and finalizes questions that frame the workshop. After finalizing the questions, an evidence report is prepared by an Evidence-based Practice Center, through a contract with the Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality (AHRQ). During the 2-day workshop, invited experts discuss the body of evidence, and attendees have opportunities to provide comments during open discussion periods. After weighing evidence from the evidence report, expert presentations, and public comments, an unbiased, independent panel prepares a draft report that identifies research gaps and future research priorities. The draft report is posted on the ODP website, and public comments are accepted for 4 weeks. The final report is then released approximately 2 weeks later.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed Feb. 13, 2015.12aFatigue Syndrome, Chronic 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Disease Prevention,esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1877440uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18775  aAUTH01421cgm a22003974a 101267488DNLM20260227145123.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s1998    mdu028 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10111  a(OCoLC)64067511  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 2071 aStoll, Andrew L.q(Andrew Lawrence),d1959-10aBipolar disorder, observational and interventional studies /cAndrew Stoll.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c1998]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 14, 2006).  aStreaming video (28 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 2, 1998.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aBipolar Disorderxdrug therapy22aFatty Acids, Omega-3xtherapeutic use22aFish Oilsxtherapeutic use 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10111  aAUTH01570cgm a22004454a 101267420DNLM20260227145043.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu350 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10032  a(DNLM)CIT:10033  a(OCoLC)65616900  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 58.500aBeyond the beginning :bthe future of genomics II (Airlie II) /cNHGRI.30aFuture of genomics II (Airlie II)  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 4, 2006).  aStreaming video (5 hr., 49 min., 35 sec. : sd., col.).0 aOpening session -- Closing session.  aRecorded Nov. 18 and Nov. 20, 2002.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aGenomics22aGenome, Human 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Human Genome Research Institute (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1003240uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10033  aAUTH01500cgm a22004214a 101267491DNLM20260227145128.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s1998    mdu022 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10114  a(OCoLC)68207219  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 901 aSalem, Norman.10aMetabolism and catabolism of DHA and AA in adults :bclinical implications of alcohol and smoking /cNorman Salem.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c1998]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 25, 2006).  aStreaming video (21 min., 50 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 2, 1998.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aFatty Acids, Unsaturatedxmetabolism22aAlcohol-Related Disordersxmetabolism22aEthanolxpharmacology22aSmokingxmetabolism 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10114  aAUTH02606cgm a22004574a 101267382DNLM20260227144953.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu147|||||||f||||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:9976  a(OCoLC)180195359  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 26.55.I400aGIS in public health :busing mapping and spatial analysis technologies for health protection.3 aGeographical Information Systems in public health  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 29, 2007).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 27 min. : sd., col.).  aModerator, Cynthia Good ; panelists, William D. Henriques, Carol Hanchette, Gerard Rushton, Samuel Soret.  aRecorded May 11, 2000.3 a(CIT): Geographic Information System (GIS) technology has become an important tool for public health professionals to better understand health issues they encounter every day. GIS allows the layering of health, demographic, environmental and other traditional data sources to be analyzed by their location on the earth's surface. GIS is a tool that can serve a wide range of research and surveillance purposes. This program will provide information on essential GIS concepts and terminology, finding and getting data into a GIS, an overview of spatial statistical analysis functions available using GIS software, issues regarding the use of GIS in public health applications, and examples of GIS applications in public health practice and surveillance.  This program will provide a live question and answer session, during which participants nationwide can ask instructors questions via toll free telephone lines, by fax, or via TTY lines.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aGeographic Information Systems12aPublic Health22aBiomedical Technologyxtrends 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aHenriques, William D.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?9976  aAUTH02545cgm a2200469 i 101618062DNLM20260227154921.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130927s2013    mdu063 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18069  a(OCoLC)863034623  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 102.51 aBargmann, Cornelia I.eauthor.10aNeuromodulatory circuits and motivated behavior /cDr. Cori Bargmann. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.) :bcolor, sound).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aWednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded September 9, 2013.3 a(CIT): Genetic variation, internal states, and environmental cues converge on shared neuronal circuits to regulate behaviors.  In the nematode worm Caenorhabditis elegans, an anatomical wiring diagram provides an essential map for innate behaviors, such as preferences for specific stimuli.  Superimposed on this detailed circuit diagram are neuromodulators reflecting internal states, which help select appropriate behavioral responses from a larger number of latent circuits, and lead to both rapid and long-lasting changes in behavior. Dr. Bargmann will discuss a neuromodulatory circuit that generates long-lasting behavioral states through two conserved neuromodulators, serotonin and a neuropeptide. The neurons that produce and respond to each neuromodulator form a distributed circuit orthogonal to the classical wiring diagram. The temporal dynamics of this neuromodulatory circuit supplement fast motor circuits to initiate and maintain long-lasting behavioral states.  aDescription based on version viewed November 8, 2013.12aNeuronsxphysiology22aBehavior, Animalxdrug effects22aMotivationxdrug effects22aNematoda22aNeuropeptides22aNeurotransmitter Agents 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18069  aAUTH01359cas a2200445   0110554DNLM20260227090911.0cr|cn#||||||||670809c19669999fr qr         0    0fre    a0033-8451l0033-8451  aRAPRBA  a(DNLM)R01800000(s)  a(OCoLC)06752813  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 afreaeng  9France00aW1bRA36310aRadioprotection2dnlm00aRadioprotection.  aParis,bDunodc[1966?]-3 3<2004->:aLes Ulis, France :bEDP Sciences  av.billus.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aBulletin of the Société française de radioprotection.  aPrint issues ceased with 2022.0 aMEDLINEbSelected citations only0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online. 2aRadiation Protection 2aPeriodical2 aSociété française de radioprotection.0 tRadionucléides & radioprotectionx1953-01960 tConduite à tenir face aux victimes d'un accident de criticité0 x1769-700X4 uhttp://www.radioprotection.org/  aAUTH02100cgm a2200409 i 101713971DNLM20260227155900.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u170930s2017    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:23487  a(OCoLC)1012810731  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 55.700aBiophysics and biology of K+ channels /cRoderick MacKinnon. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2017]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded September 27, 2017.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series. Ion channels catalyze the diffusion of inorganic ions down their electrochemical gradients across cell membranes. Because the ionic movements are passive, ion channels would seem to be extraordinarily simple physical systems, yet they are responsible for electrical signaling in living cells. Among their many functions, ion channels control the pace of the heart, regulate the secretion of hormones into the bloodstream, and generate the electrical impulses underlying information transfer in the nervous system.  Dr. MacKinnon's research is aimed at understanding the physical and chemical principles underlying ion channel function.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 20, 2017).12aPotassium Channels 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aMacKinnon, Roderick,d1956-espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissung body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23487  aAUTH02815cgm a2200469 i 101739609DNLM20260227155916.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190108s2018    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27243  a(OCoLC)1083261372  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWJ 76200aOf mice and men :bwhat we can learn about human cancer by studying mouse models /cCory Abate-Shen.1 iTitle on PowerPoint:aOf mice and men :blearning about human prostate cancer by studying mouse models 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2018]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded December 19, 2018.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series  Recent genomic-sequencing analyses have unveiled the spectrum of genomic alterations that occur in primary and advanced prostate cancer, raising the question of whether the corresponding genes are functionally relevant for prostate tumorigenesis and whether such functions are associated with particular disease stages. Dr. Abate-Shen's laboratory has generated an extensive series of genetically engineered mouse models (GEMMs) of prostate cancer, based on gain-of-function or loss-of-function of genomic alterations known to occur in human prostate cancer. These GEMMs provide reliable counterparts to study the predicted consequences of the corresponding genomic alterations that occur in human prostate cancer. For her lecture, Dr. Abate-Shen will highlight future directions for the generation of new GEMMs of prostate cancer and consider how we can use GEMMs most effectively to decipher the biological and molecular mechanisms of disease progression, as well as to tackle clinically relevant questions.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed January 18, 2019).12aProstatic Neoplasmsxgenetics22aDisease Models, Animal22aMice, Transgenic22aProstatic Neoplasmsxsecondary 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aAbate-Shen, Cory,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27243  aAUTH01420cas a2200445 a 101498346DNLM20260227090914.0cr||||||||||||090306c20109999it || p       |    0eng    a  2010243473  a2035-5106l2035-5114  a(OCoLC)642309478  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9Italy00aW1bCH818V10aMusculoskelet Surg2dnlm00aMusculoskeletal surgery.  aMilano :bSpringer Verlag Italia  aThree no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 94, no. 1 (May 2010).  aDescription based on: Vol. 94, no. 1 (May 2010).  aPrint issues ceased with v. 98 (2014).1 aMEDLINEbv94n1, May 2010-0 aPubMedbv94n1, May 2010-  aSome issues have supplements.  aAlso issued online.12aMusculoskeletal Diseasesxsurgery22aMusculoskeletal Systemxsurgery22aOrthopedic Procedures 2aPeriodical2 aIstituto ortopedico Rizzoli (Bologna, Italy)0 x2035-511400tChirurgia degli organi di movimentox0009-4749w(DLC)sn 86009311w(OCoLC)015856084 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/12306  aAUTH04301nam a2200421 i 9918976361306676DNLM20260220161245.0230105s2023    sz a     b    000 0 eng    aGBC2K16312bnb  a1340645285a1405207530  a9783031167447q(paperback)  a3031167449q(paperback)  z9783031167454q(electronic bk.)  z3031167457q(electronic bk.)7 a10.1007/978-3-031-16745-42doi  a(OCoLC)1458682089z(OCoLC)1340645285z(OCoLC)1405207530  aEQFbengerdacEQFdOCLCOdYDXdUKMGBdOCLCFdBDXdCDXdYDDdPULdOCLCOdOHX  apcc  9Switzerland 4aRA645.3bI64 202300aWY 20004a610.7322300aInformal caregivers :bfrom hidden heroes to integral part of care /cAndreas Charalambous, editor.  a1st ed. 2023. 1aCham, Switzerland :bSpringer International Publishing :bImprint: Springer,c2023.  avii, 219 pages :billustrations (some color) ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.0 a1. Caregiving and Caregivers -- Concepts, Caregiving Models and Systems -- 2. Caregiving Burden and other psychosocial considerations -- 3. Caregiving Within the Context of Elder Care -- 4. Informal Caregivers in Care Efficiency -- 5. -- Caring for the Informal Carer -- Coping in Caregiving -- 6. Informal Caregivers and health literacy -- 7. The use of information and communication technology among informal caregivers -- Prof Evridiki Papastavrou (Cyprus) -- 8. Family caregivers in palliative care within the hospital context -- Prof Elina Haavisto -- 9. Supporting caregivers of patients with childhood malignancies -- 10. Informal caregivers: the advocacy/policy perspective -- 11. Health behavioral change interventions in caregivers: the Prolepsis project -- 12. Future direction.  aThis book builds on the current trends in informal caregivers' role in the supportive care of cancer patients (as well as other diseases) across the care continuum covering topics from the healthcare professionals and the users' perspectives. Informal caregivers are a critical resource to their care recipients and an essential component of the health care system. The book introduces a comprehensive view of the topic and acknowledges the importance and the complexity of caregiving. Here lays one of the uniqueness of this book, which highlights the areas and the ways that for example interventions in specific settings/groups of patients can actually facilitate the caregiving process. The increasing number of care-dependent people, the adoption of the principle "outpatient before inpatient", the shift of care from inpatient to outpatient and the preference for home care (i.e. majority) are only some of the reasons that contributed to Informal caregiving becoming acentral feature of the health care landscape and will become even more prominent in the decades ahead. The book draws on the experts' high-end, current systematic research evidence and real-life examples on these topics to provide an insightful perspective on undertaking research within this context, and to demonstrate informal caregivers' impact on patients' outcomes. The structure of the book provides multiple perspectives to the topic and makes it appealing to a wide range of recipients including the nursing community, clinicians, social workers, researchers, policy makers, technology experts as well as postgraduate students especially to those practicing specifically in supportive care in cancer. The book fills a gap in this field of expertise not only by familiarizing the reader with a wide range of topics to be considered but it also emphasizes on what the developments in the field in the future would need to take into consideration. Finally, current and future studies can be informed from the practices of preceding studies that are incorporated in the book. .12aCaregivers22aPatient Care22aHome Nursing1 aCharalambous, Andreasc(Of Cyprus University of Technology)eeditor.  aAUTH02266ngm a2200445 i 9918975958806676DNLM20260227165025.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u250104s2024    mdu077|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35399  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 56800aT cell differentiation and function in cancer and autoimmunity /cAndrea Schietinger. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2024]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 17 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH-FDA Immunology Interest Group (IIG) seminar series  aAccess restricted to HHS only.  aRecorded November 20, 2024.3 a(CIT): IIG weekly seminar series features the world-renown experts in the field of immunology. The speakers are nominated by the members of immunology community from the NIH and FDA. The seminars are held on Wednesdays from 4:00 to 5:00 pm and include a presentation of 50 minutes followed by 10 minutes of discussion. The seminars are attended by a large number of immunologists at the NIH, including senior investigators, tenure track investigators, post-doctoral fellows and students and the additional availability of remote viewing via webcast provides an excellent opportunity to present the research to a wide audience. The speaker on November 20, 2024, will be Andrea Schietinger from MSKCC.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 24, 2026).12aCD8-Positive T-Lymphocytes22aCell Differentiation22aImmunotherapy22aLymphocyte Activation 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aSchietinger, Andrea,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aImmunology Interest Group lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=55274  aAUTH02379ngm a2200433 i 9918975959006676DNLM20260227165021.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u250104s2024    mdu008|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35393  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 95000aHistory of clinical research :bblinded studies, randomization, and medical research ethics, part 4 of 4 /cJohn I. Gallin.1 aIntroduction to the principles and practice of clinical research 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2024]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (8 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aThis is a segment of a presentation originally recorded Saturday, January 15, 2022, https://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44867, entitled "Introduction to the Principles and Practice of Clinical Research, History of clinical research : blinded studies, randomization, and medical research ethics, part 4 of 4, by John I. Gallin, M.D., with an additional tribute to Dr. John I. Gallin at the conclusion of the video.  aRecorded November 19, 2024.3 a(CIT): The Introduction to the Principles and Practice of Clinical Research (IPPCR) is a course to train participants on how to effectively conduct clinical research. The course focuses on the spectrum of clinical research and the research process by highlighting epidemiologic methods, study design, protocol preparation, patient monitoring, quality assurance, and Food and Drug Administration (FDA) issues.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast web