txt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  asheetbnb2rdacarrier  aCaption title  a"Read at the Annual Meeting of the Massachusetts Medical Society, June 13, 1911."  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202102232pda5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20260227lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM12aEtherxadministration & dosage12aInsufflationxinstrumentation12aAnesthetics, Inhalationxadministration & dosage2 aMassachusetts Medical Society.  aAUTH02065cgm a22004694a 101503625DNLM20260227153007.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090428s2009    mdu062 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15030  a(OCoLC)423568426  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWP 8701 aHunter, Kent W.10aInherited susceptibility for breast cancer metastasis /cKent W. Hunter.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCenter for Cancer Research-National Cancer Institute grand rounds  aTitle from screen banner (viewed June 24, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 1 min., 22 sec. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Apr. 14, 2009.3 a(CIT): The primary goal of Dr. Hunter's research is to investigate the role of inherited polymorphism in the dissemination and progression of breast cancer.  His laboratory uses an approach termed "systems genetics", a multi-disciplinary approach including genetically engineered mouse models, genome-wide analytical approaches, mouse and human association studies and in vitro and in vivo modeling.  His laboratory was the first to demonstrate that breast cancer metastasis has a significant inherited component.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBreast Neoplasmsxpathology22aGene Expression Profiling.22aGenetic P