ase discussion1 iTitle on PowerPoint:aExploitation in clinical research1 iTitle on PowerPoint:aAncillary care and post-trial benefits  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 41 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded Nov. 9, 2011.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aHuman Experimentationxethics22aClinical Trials as Topicxethics22aDeveloping Countries22aInternationality22aResearch Subjects 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16959  aAUTH03767cgm a2200469 i 101678405DNLM20260227155327.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160303s2016    mdu065|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19470  a(OCoLC)946699240  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWH 17500aWhen Watson and Crick get linked :bDNA interstrand crosslink repair and human disease /cAgata Smogorzewska. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 5 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCI CCR grand rounds  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded February 5, 2016.3 a(CIT): NCI's Center for Cancer Research (CCR) Grand Rounds.     Dr. Smogorzewska received her B.S. in molecular biology and biochemistry from the University of Southern California in 1995, her Ph.D. from The Rockefeller University in 2002 where she was mentored by Dr. Titia de Lange and her M.D. from Weill Cornell Medical College in 2003. Following a residency in clinical pathology at Massachusetts General Hospital, she joined Harvard Medical School as a postdoctoral fellow in Stephen Elledge's lab in 2005. She joined The Rockefeller University as an assistant professor in 2009 and was promoted to associate professor in 2015.   The focus of Dr. Smogorzewska's research is to understand the mechanism of DNA interstrand crosslink (ICL) repair with the overall goal to gain insights into the cellular and organismal consequences of deficiencies in this type of repair.  ICL repair takes place at sites where the two strands of the DNA have become covalently linked by the byproducts of metabolism or chemotherapeutics preventing replication and transcription.  The repair is accomplished in a multi-step process mediated by the Fanconi anemia pathway and factors that promote homologous recombination, including BRCA1 and BRCA2.  Failure of ICL repair results in developmental abnormalities, bone marrow failure, cancer predisposition or kidney failure as seen in Fanconi anemia and karyomegalic interstitial nephritis, two genetic diseases we study. Dr. Smogorzewska's laboratory identified UBE2T, RAD51, and SLX4 as new Fanconi anemia (FANC) genes.   She uncovered the key functions of these proteins in activating the DNA repair of ICLs, regulation of DNA processing by appropriate nucleases, and protection of the DNA from the rogue nucleases.  Besides identification and study of new proteins involved in ICL repair, critical questions that she are addressing include why the ICL repair is particularly important in the stem cells, what endogenous metabolites induce ICLs, why and how deficiency of the ICL repair leads specifically to the head and neck cancers, and what is the mechanism of kidney failure when ICL repair is deficient.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 11, 2016).12aFanconi Anemiaxphysiopathology22aDNA Damage22aDNA Repair22aFanconi Anemia Complementation Group Proteins22aRecombinases 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aSmogorzewska, Agata,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19470  aAUTH01522cas a2200505 a 9000468DNLM20260227091013.0cr|cn#||||||||891221c19889999enk x p       0    0eng    a   89649619 zsn 88008920  a0897-7194l0897-7194  aGRFAEC  a(DNLM)SR0065465(s)  a(OCoLC)17605004  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  ansdp  9England00aW1bGR919CDH0 aGrowth factors (Chur, Switzerland)10aGrowth Factors2dnlm 0aGrowth factorsb(Chur, Switzerland)10aGrowth factors.  aChur ; New York :bHarwood Academic Publishers,c1988-3 aLondon :bInforma Healthcare  av. :bill. (some col.)  aFour no. a year  aEight no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1-  aTitle from cover.  aImprint varies.  aPrint issues ceased with v. 30, no. 6 (Dec. 2012).1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, 1988-1 aPubMedbv1n1, 1988-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3Vol. 1, no. 1 (1988-89)c19921006fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aGrowth Substances 2aPeriodical0 x1029-22924 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/loi/igrf20  aAUTH02604cgm a2200469 i 101678412DNLM20260227155446.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160303s2016    mdu061|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19484  a(OCoLC)946103739  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 8500aTo tweet or not to tweet :bcommunity-based participatory research approaches to advance wellness and violence prevention via social media /cAnn Marie White and Melanie Funchess. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH behavioral and social sciences seminar series  aRecorded February 11, 2016.3 a(CIT): NIH Behavioral and Social Sciences Seminar Series  To ask QUESTIONS during the Q&A session:  Twitter: @NIHOBSSR or #BSSRLectureSeries Email: Isabel.estrada@nih.gov   Many health-related concerns due the interplay of community members" social media and geographical uses exist. However, rapid deployment of massive amounts of social media data and big data analytic tools for sensing can also become a health intervention aid for local communities. We examine this dynamic interplay through a focus on natural helpers, individuals who others routinely turn to for help and support, and whose efforts can reduce violence risks and promote mental health and wellness in local communities.   During this presentation, we will describe how a community can begin to develop and deploy its own sensing methods for health-related content signals in its social media use through academic-community partnered research.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 4, 2016).02aTwitter.12aHealth Behavior22aCommunity-Based Participatory Research22aSocial Media 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aWhite, Ann Marie,espeaker.1 aFunches, Melanie,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aBSSR lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19484  aAUTH01853cas a2200493 i 101632508DNLM20260227090913.0cr||||||||||||140512c20139999dcuqr p       |   a0eng    a  20132005330 a2326-5523l2326-552321  a(OCoLC)826998283  aDLCbengerdad20260227dDLCdOCLCOdOCLCQdDLCdOCLCFdDNLM  apccansdp  9United States00aBF309b.P75900aW1bPS74610a1502230 aPsychology of consciousness (Washington, D.C.)10aPsychol Conscious (Wash D C)2dnlm 0aPsychology of consciousnessb(Washington, D.C. Print)10aPsychology of consciousness :btheory, research, and practice.1 aPSYCON 1aWashington, DC :bAmerican Psychological Association,c2013-313March 2014- :aWashington, DC :bEducational Publishing Foundation of the American Psychological Association  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent 