color, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 27, 2016.3 a(CIT): This NIDDK sponsored symposium highlights the training of physician and non-physician scientists as exemplified by 5 decades of training in the Laboratory of Chemical Biology, NIAMDD, that originated with Christian B. Anfinsen as Lab Chief and one of NIH's Nobel Prize recipients, and continued with Alan Schechter as chief and with a name change to the Molecular Medicine Branch in NIDDK.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed August 18, 2016).12aSchechter, Alan N.12aBiomedical Researchxeducation12aEducation, Medical22aLaboratory Personnelxeducation22aPhysicians22aVocational Guidance 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aSchechter, Alan N.,ehonouree.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body,eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19773  aAUTH01646cgmaa22004334a 101257220DNLM20260227144521.0vf|cbahouvf|cjahoucr|cna||||||||050915s2004    mdu042 ||         vleng    a(OCoLC)61745504  a(DNLM)CIT:3533  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aWD 21000aIdentifying the body mass index of risk for African Americans /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  aUnited States.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (41 min., 31 sec.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (41 min., 31 sec.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aWomen's Health Special Interest Group  aSpeaker, Anne E. Summer.  aPresented Sept. 17, 2004.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aObesityxethnology22aBody Mass Index22aRisk Factors22aBlack or African American.1 aSumner, Anne E.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aWomen's Health Special Interest Group (Series)4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3533  aAUTH01240cam a2200337 i 60611530RDNLM20260206221959.0cr||||||||||||820514s1907    ilu      b    001 0 eng    a(OCoLC)502485  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aHVbL493v 19071 aLeffingwell, Albert,d1845-1916,eauthor.10aWorks.kSelections14aThe vivisection question /cby Albert Leffingwell.  aSecond edition. 1aChicago :bVivisection Reform Society,c1907.  aviii, 267 pages ;c22 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aMainly reprints from various scientific periodicals.  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20250613lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aVivisection4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/60611530R  aAUTH02580cgm a2200397 i 101658209DNLM20260227155103.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150505s2015    mdu064|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18977  a(OCoLC)909852247  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 2661 aRosenberg, Steven A.eauthor.10aCancer immunotherapy by targeting unique cancer mutations /cSteven Rosenberg ; Immunology Interest Group. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 4 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 29, 2015.3 a(CIT): Dr. Steve Rosenberg received his B.A. and M.D. degrees at Johns Hopkins University and a Ph.D. in Biophysics at Harvard University. After completing his residency training in surgery in 1974 at the Peter Bent Brigham Hospital in Boston, Massachusetts, Dr. Rosenberg became the Chief of Surgery at the NCI, NIH, a position he has held to the present time. Dr. Rosenberg has pioneered the development of effective immunotherapies and gene therapies for patients with advanced cancers. His studies of the adoptive transfer of genetically modified lymphocytes have resulted in the regression of metastatic cancer in patients with melanoma, sarcomas and lymphomas. His current research is aimed at defining the host immune response of patients to their cancers. These studies emphasize the ability of human lymphocytes to recognize unique cancer antigens and the identification of anti-tumor T cell receptors that can be exploited to develop new cell transfer immunotherapies for the treatment of cancer patients.  Dr. Rosenberg also oversees the Surgery Branch"s clinical program aimed at translating scientific advances into effective immunotherapies for patients with cancer.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 15, 2015).12aNeoplasmsxtherapy22aImmunotherapyxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group,esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18977  aAUTH02085cas a2200433   7604961DNLM20260205150625.0cr|cn#||||||||760423d19631978mduar    s   f0||| 0eng    a0502-2673l0502-2673  a(OCoLC)02079074  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW2 AbN148vj no.5 etc. 0aCurrent estimates from the Health Interview Survey, United States00aCurrent estimates from the Health Interview Survey, United States.  aRockville, Md.,bU. S. Dept. of Health, Education and Welfare, Public Health Service, Health Resources Administration, National Center for Health Statistics; Washington, for sale by the Supt. of Docs., U. S. Govt. Print. Off.  ano.bill.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 a1962/63-1978.1 aVital and health statistics. Series 10, Data from the National Health Surveyvno. 5, etc  aIssued 1962/63-1969 as Public Health Service publication, no. 1000, Ser. 10, no. 5, etc.; 1970-71 as DHEW publication no. (HSM) 72-1054 and DHEW publication no. (HSM) 73-1505; 1972-    as DHEW publication no. (HRA) 74-1512, etc.  aAlso issued online. 2aHealth Surveys 2aUnited States 2aStatistics2 aHealth Interview Survey.2 aNational Center for Health Statistics (U.S.)00tCurrent estimates from the National Health Interview Survey, United Statesx0891-4591w(DLC)   86658592 0aVital and health statistics.nSeries 10,pData from the National Health Surveyvno. 5, etc. 0aDHEW publication ;vno. (HSM) 72-1054. 0aDHEW publication ;vno. (HSM) 73-1505. 0aDHEW publication ;vno. (HRA) 74-1512, etc. 0aCurrent estimates from the Health Interview Survey4 uhttps://archive.cdc.gov/#/details?url=https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/nhis/quest%5Fdoc.htm  aAUTH02190cgm a2200457 i 101607224DNLM20260227155022.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130427s2013    mdu052|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17882  a(OCoLC)847112266  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 103.51 aPoldrack, Russell A.eauthor.10aRelating mental and neural representations using multivariate fMRI analyses /cRussell Poldrack. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (52 min.) :bcolor, sound)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdm2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Apr. 8, 2013.3 a(CIT): Neuroscience Seminar Series   Dr. Russ Poldrack is Professor of Psychology and Neuroscience and Director of the Imaging Research Center at the University of Texas at Austin. His research uses neuroimaging in humans to examine the intersection of learning and memory, decision making, and executive function. He is also engaged in neuroinformatics efforts that attempt to bridge the gap between data and knowledge, and has written extensively on what kinds of questions neuroimaging can and cannot answer regarding the brain and mental function.    For more information go to <a href='http://neuroseries.info.nih.gov'>http://neuroseries.info.nih.gov.  aDescription based on version viewed May 29, 2013.12aNeuropsychologyxmethods22aBrainxphysiology22aMagnetic Resonance Imaging22aMental Processesxphysiology22aNeuroimagingxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17882  aAUTH02604cgm a2200445 i 101628094DNLM20260227154548.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140312s2014    mdu064|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18256  a(OCoLC)875629811  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQV 7851 aLanger, Robert S.eauthor.10aBiomaterials and biotechnology :bfrom the discovery of angiogenesis inhibitors to the development of drug delivery systems and the foundation of tissue engineering /cDr. Robert S. Langer. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 4 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded January 29, 2014.3 a(CIT): Advanced drug delivery systems are having an enormous impact on human health. Dr. Langer will begin the lecture by discussing his early research on developing the first controlled release systems for macromolecules and the isolation of angiogenesis inhibitors, noting how these advances have led to numerous new therapies. For example, new drug delivery technologies including nanoparticles and nanotechnology are now being studied for use treating cancer and other illnesses. Dr. Langer will next discuss ways of developing novel microchips for drug delivery, elucidating how approaches for creating new biomaterials are evaluated and providing examples where such materials are used in brain cancer and shape-memory applications. By combining mammalian cells, including stem cells, with synthetic polymers, new approaches for engineering tissues are being developed that may someday help in various disease, particularly in the fields of cartilage, skin, blood vessels, and spinal cord repair.  aDescription based on version viewed April 1, 2014.12aBiocompatible Materials12aDrug Delivery Systemsxtrends22aBiotechnology22aNanostructures 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18256  aAUTH01357cgm a22004454a 101268724DNLM20260227151100.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2005    mdu054 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12522  a(OCoLC)165043701  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3141 aKauer, Julie.10aSynaptic plasticity in the dopaminergic reward pathway /cJulie Kauer.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 17, 2007).  aStreaming video (53 min., 15 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 21, 2005.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHippocampus22aInterneurons22aNeuronal Plasticity22aReceptors, Glutamate22aSynapses22aSynaptic Transmission 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12522  aAUTH01733cgm a22004814a 101267993DNLM20260227145953