.).  aRecorded May 9, 2005.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.12aTaurinexanalogs & derivatives22aApoptosis22aInflammation Mediators22aLymphoma, B-Cellxpathology22aMitochondria22aNeutrophils22aOxidants 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMitochondria Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12617  aAUTH01387cgm a22004094a 101267676DNLM20260227145349.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu015 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10546  a(OCoLC)190751308  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 2951 aThompson, Tommy George,d1941-10aAll hands today with HHS Secretary Tommy G. Thompson /cTommy G. Thompson.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 21, 2007).  aStreaming video (14 min., 14 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 12, 2001.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: (c)2001.  aOpen-captioned.12aUnited States.bDepartment of Health and Human Services12aSeptember 11 Terrorist Attacks22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aWebcast1 aUnited States.bDepartment of Health and Human Services.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10546  aAUTH01881cgm a22004094a 101267588DNLM20260227145300.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu076 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10416  a(OCoLC)65360755  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 26.51 aGlaser, John P.q(John Patrick),d1955-10aTechnology value--what's in it for me? /cJohn P. Glaser and Thomas H. Payne.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 14, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 15 min., 27 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 11, 2002.3 a(CIT): Sessions are part of the Clinical Research Information System (CRIS) Educational Series presented by the CRIS Project Management Team.   For more information or to register for CMEs, call 301-594-DCRI or go online.  Speakers Dr. John P. Glaser, vice president and CIO, Partners HealthCare System, Inc., Boston, and Dr. Thomas H. Payne, medical director, Academic Medical Center Information Systems, University of Washington, VA Puget Sound Health Care System.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aMedical Informatics22aQuality of Health Care 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10416  aAUTH03873cgm a2200469 i 101631800DNLM20260227154658.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140501s2014    mdu101|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18402  a(OCoLC)880404126  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWS 350.8.P41 aSuskind, Ron,eauthor.10aAutism's powerful affinities :bprison or pathway? /cRon Suskind. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 41 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIMH special lecture for autism awareness month  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Apr. 24, 2014.3 a(CIT): Guest Speaker: Ron Suskind - Pulitzer Prize-winning Journalist and New York Times bestselling author of "Life, Animated: A Story of Sidekicks, Heroes and Autism."  The National Institute of Mental Health is pleased to invite you to attend a special lecture to recognize National Autism Awareness Month.  In his talk, Ron Suskind will discuss his family's 20 years of experimentation with his autistic son's powerful affinity for animated movies, mostly from Disney, as a tool to open new pathways of communication and social connection.  Based on his experience, he poses provocative questions about how the features of autism might be turned from challenges into strengths to help those on the spectrum achieve their full potential.  A book signing with the author will be held before the lecture at the FAES bookstore, where copies of Mr. Suskind"s new book are available for purchase.  Pulitzer Prize-winning journalist and author Ron Suskind has written some of America"s most important works of nonfiction, framing national debates while exploring the complexities of human experience.  Mr. Suskind"s latest work, "Life, Animated, A Story of Sidekicks, Heroes and Autism", chronicles his family"s twenty year struggle with their youngest son"s autism.  A journey of the heart, he takes readers to the core of the most fundamental human issues: love, faith, friendship, brotherhood, parenthood and how, in moments of darkness, we literally need stories to survive. The book"s powerful stories are prompting a re-examination of how autism is treated, and how "affinities"--in this case Disney movies--may be harnessed to create better outcomes for those with autism.  His previous works include the New York Times bestsellers, "Confidence Men" (2011) "The Way of the World" (2008), "The One Percent Doctrine" (2006), "The Price of Loyalty", and "A Hope in the Unseen".  In addition to his books, he often appears on network television and has been a contributor for The New York Times Magazine and Esquire.  Mr. Suskind was the Wall Street Journal"s senior national affairs reporter from 1993 until his departure in 2000, and won the 1995 Pulitzer Prize for Feature Writing.  He currently lives in Cambridge, Mass., with his wife, Cornelia Kennedy, and is the Senior Fellow at Harvard"s Edmond J. Safra Center for Ethics.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed May 13, 2014.12aAutistic Disorder22aChild22aFamily Relations22aInterpersonal Relations 2aLecture 2aPersonal Narrative 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIMH special lecture for autism awareness month40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18402  aAUTH01637cgm a22004574a 101268680DNLM20260227151121.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2005    mdu058 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12423  a(OCoLC)154875841  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWF 5531 aBottomly, Kim.10aInnate immune receptors and induction of lung inflammatory responses in murine model of asthma /cKim Bottomly.1 iTitle on title screen:aInnate control of CD4 T cell responses to inhaled antigen  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 31, 2007).  aStreaming video (57 min., 11 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 26, 2005.3 a(CIT): The Immunology Interest Group (IIG).  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aAsthmaximmunology22aImmunity, Innate22aSignal Transduction22aTh2 Cells 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12423  aAUTH02330cgm a22004574a 101267635DNLM20260227145833.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu070  |||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10497  a(OCoLC)72772149  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.500aNIH roadmap for medical research press conference /cElias A. Zerhouni.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 4, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 9 min., 58 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 30, 2003.3 a(CIT): On Demand Video Now Available.  NIH Director Elias A. Zerhouni, M.D., and the Institute and Center Directors will be announcing a series of new initiatives known collectively as the NIH Roadmap for Medical Research.  These initiatives are designed to transform the nation's medical research capabilities and speed the movement of research discoveries from the bench to the bedside.  Soon after becoming NIH Director in May 2002, Dr. Zerhouni convened a series of meetings to chart a "roadmap" to accelerate medical research - a process that identified major opportunities and gaps in medical research that no single institute at NIH could tackle alone, but that the agency still needed to address.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration22aAcademies and Institutesxorganization & administration22aOrganizational Objectives22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aUnited States 2aWebcast1 aZerhouni, Elias A.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10497  aAUTH01522cgm a22004094a 101319916DNLM20260227152038.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071101s2007    mdu060|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13905  a(OCoLC)191875411  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.500aNIH Director's Awards ceremony, 2007.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 6, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 0 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded June 13, 2007.3 a(CIT): 2007 NIH Director's Awards Ceremony Awards will be presented in five categories:  NIH Director's Awards, Mentoring Awards, Director's Awards for the NIH Roadmap for Medical Research, Commissioned Corps Awards and Equal Employment Opportunity Awards.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aBiomedical Research22aAwards and Prizes 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13905  aAUTH02069cgm a22004454a 101267378DNLM20260227144950.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu497 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:9972  a(DNLM)CIT:9973  a(OCoLC)85885127  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 530.100aGlobal health :ba challenge to scientists /cFogarty International Center.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 26, 2007).  aStreaming video (8 hr., 17 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 20-21, 2003.3 a(CIT): To mark the 35th anniversary of its establishment, the Fogarty International Center is hosting a two-day scientific symposium.  The symposium highlights the potential of research to reduce global health disparities and the benefits of investing in such research, and will address four major areas: why global health matters, challenges and opportunities, building research partnerships across geographic boundaries, and the impact of building research capacity in developing countries and at the local level.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aGlobal Health22aDeveloping Countries22aInternational Cooperation 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aJohn E. Fogarty I