man subject research for clinical investigators and others who participate in the conduct of research and is open to the entire NIH community as well as to those from outside NIH. Topics include the history of human subject research ethics, principles and guidelines, study design, subject recruitment, informed consent, and international research. The course is open to the entire NIH community as well as to those from outside NIH. The recommended textbook is Ethical and Regulatory Aspects of Clinical Research, edited by Emanuel et al (Johns Hopkins University Press). The course is taught by guest faculty and faculty members from the National Institutes of Health. This is a required academic program for Bioethics fellows.  Ethical Issues in the Use of Stored Tissue and Data Sara Chandros Hull PhD NHGRI and NIH Clinical Center Department of Bioethics    How to think about Incidental Findings Ben Berkman JD NHGRI and NIH Clinical Center Department of Bioethics.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed January 23, 2015.12aGenetic Researchxethics 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aBerkman, Benjaminq(Benjamin E.),eauthor.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18729  aAUTH01520cgm a22004454a 101268594DNLM20260227150930.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu056 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12272  a(OCoLC)123962738  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 1021 aTaghert, Paul.10aMolecular genetics of the neuroendocrine phenotype in Drosophila /cPaul Taghert.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 4, 2007).  aStreaming video (56 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 8, 2004.3 a(CIT): NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This