the technical standards of the practice of cervical cytology and histopathology and to contribute to improving the quality of cytology screening and histological diagnoses of these lesions.  aIssued also on CD-ROM  aMode of access: Internet.12aUterine Cervical Neoplasmsxpathology 2aAtlas1 aFrappart, L.2 aInternational Agency for Research on Cancer.02aHistopathology of the uterine cervix.02aCytopathology of the uterine cervix.40uhttps://screening.iarc.fr/atlashisto.php  aAUTH02275cgm a22004934a 101267346DNLM20260227145214.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu866 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:9938  a(DNLM)CIT:9939  a(DNLM)CIT:9940  a(OCoLC)191751199  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 26600aSymptom management in cancer :bpain, depression, and fatigue /csponsored by NCI and OMAR.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 16, 2008).  aStreaming video (14 hr., 26 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded July 15-17, 2002.3 a(CIT): NIH Consensus Development Conference  Despite advances in early detection and effective treatment, cancer remains one of the most feared diseases, due not only to its association with death but with disability. Among the most common side effects of cancer and treatments for cancer are pain, depression, and fatigue. It is estimated that as many as 90% of patients will experience pain in the course of their illness. Between 20 - 40% will become depressed and a growing number will have fatigue that may persist even after treatment ends. Often these symptoms occur in combination.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxtherapy22aDepressionxtherapy22aFatiguextherapy22aPain Management22aPalliative Care 2aConsensus Development Conference, NIH 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?993840uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?993940uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?9940  aAUTH01759cgm a2200445 a 101537546DNLM20260227153548.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100804s2010    mdu048|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16014  a(OCoLC)728456795  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States10aQZ 2061 aVarmus, Harold.10aNCI town hall meeting, July 2010 /cHarold Varmus.1 aNational Cancer Institute town hall meeting  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video (48 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded July 12, 2010.3 a(CIT): Harold Varmus will convene a town hall meeting in the Main Auditorium of the Natcher Building at 3PM on Monday, July 12, after being sworn-in that morning as the 14th Director of the NCI. Following a brief and informal talk, he will answer questions from the audience and then greet NCI staff members and other attendees at a reception in the atrium of the auditorium.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.)12aNeoplasms22aBiomedical Research22aGovernment Agencies 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16014  aAUTH01468cas a22004814a 101120693DNLM20260227090909.0cr|cn ||||||||001228c20019999nyuqr p       0    0eng    a   00200169 0 a1533-0028l1533-0028  aCCCLCF  a(OCoLC)45632310  aNSDcNSDdNLMdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bCL68910aClin Colorectal Cancer2dnlm00aClinical colorectal cancer.30aColorectal cancer  aDallas, Tex. 