e host of infectious diseases, including chickenpox, measles and the seasonal flu. With a new threat at hand, scientists at the NIH swiftly developed a vaccine candidate against the novel coronavirus that causes COVID-19. The pre-clinical effort was driven in part by Dr. Kizzmekia Corbett.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 18, 2020).12aCoronavirus Infectionsximmunology22aViral Vaccinesxtherapeutic use22aCoronavirus Infectionsxprevention & control22aPneumonia, Viralximmunology22aPneumonia, Viralxprevention & control22aBetacoronavirusximmunology22aRNA, Messengerxtherapeutic use22aDrug Development 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCorbett, Kizzmekia,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH COVID-19 SIG lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38864  aAUTH01964cgm a2200433 i 101775911DNLM20260227160610.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2020    mdu057|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31459  a(OCoLC)1240391879  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 506.100aWhat we are learning about COVID-19 from autopsy findings /cWilliam A. Muller. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (57 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCOVID-19 insights webinar  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded December 14, 2020.3 a(CIT): COVID-19 Insights Webinar Series  We invite you to join a new biweekly NHLBI webinar series entitled "COVID-19 Insights" to learn about groundbreaking research focusing on the host response to SARS-CoV-2 infection. This exciting cross-disciplinary series, featuring both NHLBI staff and researchers throughout the scientific community, will enhance NHLBI listeners' understanding of the latest advances in this fast-moving research, especially as they relate to the NHLBI mission.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 1, 2021).12aCOVID-19xpathology22aAutopsy 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aMuller, William A.,d1953-espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNHLBI COVID-19 insights webinar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38906  aAUTH01493cas a22004814a 101184586DNLM20260227090923.0cr||||||||||||021127c20049999enk x p       0   a0eng    a  20022138840 a1542-6416l1542-641621  a(OCoLC)51088619  aNSDcNSDdOCLCQdNSDdDNLM  ansdpalcdanlmcopyc  9England00aW1bCL768CQ10a57221310aClin Proteomics2dnlm 0aClinical proteomics00aClinical proteomics.1 iAt head of title:aJournal of translational molecular medicine.  aTotowa, NJ :bHumana Press,cc2004-3 32011- :aLondon :bBioMed Central  av. :bill. ;c28 cm.  aFour no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (2004)-  aPrint issues ceased with Vol. 6 (2010).  aTitle from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 2, no. 1-2 (2006).  aPrint issues ceased with v. 6, no.4 (2010).0 aPubMedbv7n1, 2011-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.12aProteomics 2aPeriodical0 x1559-02754 uhttps://clinicalproteomicsjournal.biomedcentral.com/4 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101184586  aAUTH01366cas a2200469 a 8510274DNLM20260224111604.0cr|cn#||||||||851107c19849999enkar         0    0eng    a0264-8725l0264-8725  aBGERES  a(DNLM)SR0054055(s)  a(OCoLC)10604639  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9England00aW1bBI918Q10aBiotechnol Genet Eng Rev2dnlm00aBiotechnology & genetic engineering reviews.30aBiotechnology and genetic engineering reviews  aNewcastle upon Tyne :bIntercept,c1984-2 aNottinham :bNottingham University Press3 3<2014- > :aAbingdon :bTaylor & Francis  av. :bill.  aAnnual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1-1 aMEDLINEbv1, 1984-0 aPubMedbv1, 1984-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online. 2aBiomedical Engineering 2aEnvironmental Microbiology 2aGenetic Engineering 2aMicrobiological Techniques 2aTechnology 2aPeriodical0 x2046-55564 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/loi/tbgr20  aAUTH01416cgm a2200397 i 101775919DNLM20260227160630.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2020    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31472  a(OCoLC)1241125042  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 51000aOpportunities at the intersection of DNA repair pathways /cDr. Roger Greenberg. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded December 15, 2020.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 8, 2021).12aRecombinational DNA Repair12aDNAxgenetics22aDNA Damagexphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aGreenberg, Rogerc(Of University of Pennsylvania)espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bDNA Repair Interest Group,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=34939  aAUTH03390cgm a2200457 i 101773038DNLM20260227160333.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201105s2020    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30338  a(OCoLC)1224228575  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 30200aTranslating thought into blood flow in the brain :bcapillaries as sensors of neural activity /cMark T. Nelson. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 0 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aRecorded October 14, 2020.3 a(CIT): Healthy brain function depends on the finely tuned spatial and temporal delivery of blood-borne nutrients to active neurons via the vast, dense capillary network.  Cerebral small vessel diseases (SVDs) are a central link between stroke and dementia--two co-morbidities that rank among the most pressing human health issues.  Despite the emerging consensus that SVDs are initiated in the endothelium, the early molecular mechanisms remain largely unknown and no specific treatments are yet available.  Deficits in on-demand delivery of blood to active brain regions (functional hyperemia) have been identified as early manifestations of the underlying pathogenesis.  The strong inward-rectifier K+ channel (Kir2.1) in capillary endothelial cells, which senses neuronal activity and initiates a propagating electrical signal that dilates upstream arterioles, is a cornerstone of this functional hyperemia mechanism.  However, whether this mechanism is targeted in SVDs and how resulting signaling deficits might be rescued remains unknown.  Using a genetic mouse model of the most common hereditary SVD (CADASIL, Cerebral Autosomal Dominant Arteriopathy with Subcortical Infarcts and Leukoencephalopathy), we show that impaired functional hyperemia is caused by a complete loss of capillary-to-arteriole electrical signaling due to diminished capillary endothelial Kir2.1 channel activity.  We further linked Kir2.1 deactivation to depletion of phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate (PIP2), a membrane phospholipid essential for Kir2.1 activity.  Strikingly, systemic injection of soluble PIP2 rapidly restored functional hyperemia in SVD, suggesting a possible strategy for rescuing functional hyperemia in brain disorders in which blood flow is disturbed.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 18, 2020).12aBrainxblood supply22aCapillariesxphysiology22aDementiaxphysiopathology22aEndothelium, Vascularxphysiology22aNeuronsxphysiology 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aNelson, Mark T.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38686  aAUTH01021cas a22003491  16040220RDNLM20260205104229.0cr|cn#||||||||820919c19219999xxuar p       0||| 0eng    a0069-0635l0069-0635  a(DNLM)A40980000(s)  a(OCoLC)03382417  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bCA842 aCarnegie Corporation of New York.10aAnnual report.  aNew York.3 aNew York Ny :bCarnegie Corporation Of New York  av.bill., ports.  aAnnual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 a1921/22-  aIssues for 1921/22-1952/53 have title: Report.  aPrint issues ceased with 2005.  aAlso issued online.2 aCarnegie Corporation of New York.tReport.4 uhttps://www.carnegie.org/publications/?t=18  aAUTH01342nam a2200337 i 101776439DNLM20260219092408.0210224r18821882dcu           000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5508 no.141 aGill, Theodore,d1837-1914eauthor.14aThe doctrine of Darwin /cintroductory address of Theodore Gill ; with an appendix on the proper use of the term biology. 1aWashington :bJudd & Detweiler, Printers,c1882.  apages 47-55, 102-104 ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aExtract from the Proceedings of the Biological Society of Washington, vol. I.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM12aDarwin, Charles,d1809-1882.12aDarwin, Charles,d1809-1882.tOn the origin of species.12aSelection, Genetic22aBiology 2aAddress2 aBiological Society of Washington.tProceedings.  aAUTH01598cas a2200469 a 9216789DNLM20260227091025.0cr|cn#||||||||921127c19929999fr  x p       0    0eng    a0145-5680l0145-5680  aCMOBEF  a(DNLM)SR0075820(s)  a(OCoLC)27137595  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9France00aW1bCE12850 aCellular and molecular biology (Noisy-le-Grand, France)10aCell Mol Biol (Noisy-le-grand)2dnlm10aCellular and molecular biology.  aNoisy-le-Grand, France :bR. Wegmann,c1992-3 3<2001->:aNoisy-Le-Grand, France :bC.M.B. Association  av. :bill.  aEight no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 38, nos. 5/6 (Aug./Sept. 1992)-  aPrint issues ceased with v. 50, no. 8 (Dec. 2004).  aSome papers for 2001-   are only available online.1 aMEDLINEbv38n5/6, Aug./Sept. 1992-0 aPubMedbv38n5/6, Aug./Sept. 1992-1 aIndex medicus  aAssumes the numbering and ISSN of another serial with the same title published in Oxford by Pergamon, 1977- .  aSupplements accompany some vols.,    -1996.  aAlso issued online. 2aCell Biology 2aMolecular Biology 2aPeriodical0 x1165-158X4 uhttp://www.cellmolbiol.org/index.php/CMB  aAUTH02828cgm a2200445 i 101684409DNLM20260227155403.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160601s2016    mdu064|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19703  a(OCoLC)953229722  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 21000aOncogene addiction /cDean W. Felsher. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 4 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCI Center for Cancer Research eminent lecture  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded May 23, 2016.3 a(CIT): NCI Center for Cancer Research Eminent Lecture  Dr. Felsher obtained a B.A. in Chemistry at the University of Chicago, a M.D. Ph.D. from the University of California, Los Angeles School of Medicine. He performed his graduate work in Molecular Biology/Immunology under Dr. Jonathan Braun. He trained in Internal Medicine at the Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania and Oncology at the University of California, San Francisco and his post-doctoral research under Dr. J. Michael Bishop. Dr. Felsher has been faculty at Stanford University for 16 years and performing both basic and translational cancer research and is the founding director of the Stanford TRAM Program.  Dr. Felsher has over 20 years of research experience working with experimental mouse models and has pioneered the utilization off the Tetracycline Regulatory System (Tet System) to generate conditional transgenic models of lymphoma, leukemia, osteosarcoma, hepatocellular carcinoma lung adenocarcinoma and renal cell carcinoma. He has demonstrated "reversibility of cancer," "oncogene addiction," and "tumor maintenance" through programs of cellular senescence and angiogenesis and differentiation. He has most recently described a critical role for the immune system in the mechanism of tumor regression upon oncogene inactivation.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 5, 2016).12aOncogenesxgenetics22aMolecular Targeted Therapy22aOncogenesxdrug effects 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aFelsher, Deanq(Dean Walton),espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aCCR eminent lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19703  aAUTH01870cas a2200553   7705432DNLM20260227090912.0cr|cn#||||||||770715c19629999nju x p       0    0eng    a0148-4834l0148-4834y0022-3158  aJNUEAW  a(DNLM)J32180000(s)z(DNLM)J08200000(s)  a(OCoLC)01644709  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bJO79710aJ Nurs Educ2dnlm 4aThe Journal of nursing ed