re /cLaurel Northouse.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 16, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 1 min., 52 sec. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS and NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded May 16, 2006.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxpsychology22aCaregiversxpsychology22aFamily Relations 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13237  aAUTH01400cgm a22004334a 101294804DNLM20260227151338.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061206s2006    mdu059|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12986  a(OCoLC)84712849  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWF 2501 aKaufmann, S. H. E.q(Stefan H. E.)10aCrosspriming and immunity to tuberculosis /cStefan Kaufmann.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 28, 2006).  aStreaming video (59 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 4, 2006.3 a(CIT): The Immunology Interest Group (IIG) is the sponsor.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aTuberculosisximmunology12aTuberculosisxprevention & control22aCross-Priming22aTuberculosis Vaccines 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12986  aAUTH02347cgm a2200445 i 101642816DNLM20260227154802.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140926s2014    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18582  a(OCoLC)894125336  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 4751 aFeinberg, Andy,eauthor.14aThe epigenetic basis of common human disease /cAndrew P. Feinberg. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded September 3, 2014.3 a(CIT): Although epigenetic changes in the cancer genome have been known for three decades, the role of epigenetics in common human disease and its relationship to genetic variation has only recently begun to be explored. The Feinberg lab has been developing whole-genome approaches to the epigenetic analysis of human disease and contributing to a new field of epigenetic epidemiology that integrates genetic, epigenetic, and environmental factors. One of the most exciting developments in this recent work is the idea that epigenetic plasticity under genetic control may confer a survival advantage in evolution, and may also be important in normal tissue differentiation and response to the environment. Dr. Feinberg has suggested a unifying model of cancer in which epigenetic dysregulation allows rapid selection for tumor cell survival at the expense of the host.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed October 24, 2014.12aEpigenesis, Geneticxphysiology12aEpigenomics22aPrecancerous Conditions 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18582  aAUTH01299cas a2200469   7705865DNLM20260227091002.0cr|cn#||||||||770804c19769999enkqr p       0    0eng    a   76649247   a0363-0269l0363-0269  zHEMOD8  a(DNLM)H11140000(s)  a(OCoLC)02720226  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  ansdp  9England00aW1bHE87810aHemoglobin2dnlm00aHemoglobin.  aNew York,bDekker.3 aLondon :bInforma Healthcare  av.bill.  aFour no. a year,b1999-  aSix no. a year,b-1998  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmed