||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:23354  a(OCoLC)992346737  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWB 1021 aGawande, Atul,einterviewee.10aBringing precision delivery to precision medicine /cAtul Gawande. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2017]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (57 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded June 13, 2017.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series  Dr. Gawande is a surgeon, writer, and public health researcher. He practices general and endocrine surgery at Brigham and Women's Hospital in Boston, Massachusetts. He is Professor in the Department of Health Policy and Management at the Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health and the Samuel O. Thier Professor of Surgery at Harvard Medical School. He is also Executive Director of Ariadne Labs, a joint center for health systems innovation, and Chairman of Lifebox, a nonprofit organization making surgery safer globally.   Dr. Gawande has been a staff writer for The New Yorker magazine since 1998 and has written four New York Times bestsellers: Complications, Better, The Checklist Manifesto, and most recently, Being Mortal: Medicine and What Matters in the End. He is the winner of two National Magazine Awards, Academy Health's Impact Award for highest research impact on healthcare, a MacArthur Fellowship, and the Lewis Thomas Award for writing about science.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed June 28, 2017).12aPrecision Medicine 2aInterview 2aWebcast1 aCollins, Francis S.,einterviewer.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23354  aAUTH01773cgm a2200421 a 101571677DNLM20260227154157.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u111018s2011    mdu168|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16888  a(OCoLC)759563367  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.55.H91 aGrady, Christine.10aEthical and regulatory aspects of clinical research.pIntro to framework for human research ethics, history, codes /cChristine Grady, Susan Lederer.1 aIntro to framework for human research ethics, history, codes1 iTitle on screen banner:aEthical and regulatory aspects of clinical research (course 1)  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 48 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded Sept. 28, 2011.3 a(CIT): The course is designed to provide an overview of the important issues in the ethics of human subject research for clinical investigators and others who participate in the conduct of research and is open to the entire NIH community as well as to those from outside NIH.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aHuman Experimentationxethics22aHuman Experimentationxhistory 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16888  aAUTH01853cgm a22004214a 101268596DNLM20260227150905.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu045 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12277  a(OCoLC)123962739  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 3501 aCox, Rachelq(Rachel T.)10aMitochondrial inheritance during Drosophila oogenesis /cRachel Cox.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 13, 2007).  aStreaming video (45 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 8, 2004.3 a(CIT): The concept of heteroplasmy and threshold levels of mitochondrial mutations will be discussed.  The Mitochondria Interest Group (MIG) is an Inter-Institute Interest Group at the National Institutes of Health (NIH), with members from NIH and from around the world! MIG is concerned with all aspects of the mitochondrion and diseases in which the mitochondrion is involved.   For more information, visit the  Mitochondria Interest Group (MIG).  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aMitochondriaxgenetics22aDrosophilaxgenetics22aMutationxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12277  aAUTH02369cas a2200589 a 9880575DNLM20260227091048.0cr|cnu||||||||790628c19779999lauqr p       0   a0eng    asn 81000582 0 a0275-6382l0275-6382y0275-683221  aVCPADJ  a(DNLM)V05677000(s)z(DNLM)J12270000(s)  a(OCoLC)05118950  aMNPcMNPdCOOdDLCdNSDdMULdSERdNSTdAIPdNSTdHULdAGLdNSDdCASdNSTdOCLdNSDdEBZdAGLdNSDdDNLM  ansdpalcdanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bVE929DP0 aSF601.A54 0aL80010a63621110aVet Clin Pathol2dnlm0 aVet. clin. pathol. 0aVeterinary clinical pathology00aVeterinary clinical pathology /cAmerican Society for Veterinary Clinical Pathology.  a1977-<1989> :aColumbia, MO :bVeterinary Practice Pub. Co.2 3<Sept. 15, 1989>-1997 :aSanta Barbara, CA :bVeterinary Practice Pub. Co.3 31998- :aBaton Rouge, LA :bAmerican Society for Veterinary Clinical Pathology  av. :bill. ;c28 cm.  aQuarterly,b<Sept. 15, 1989->  aThree no. per year,b<July/Aug. 1978->  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with v. 6, no. 3 (Nov./Dec. 1977).  aDescription based on: Vol. 12, no. 1, published in 1983; title from cover.  aPrint issues ceased with v. 44, no. 4 (Dec. 2015).1 aMEDLINEbv31n1, 2002-0 aPubMedbv31n1, 2002-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3v. 27, no. 1 ( 1998)c20020716fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper12aPathology, Veterinary22aAnimal Diseases22aClinical Laboratory Techniquesxveterinary 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Society for Veterinary Clinical Pathology.0 x1939-165X00aAmerican Society of Veterinary Clinical Pathologists.tBulletin of the American Society of Veterinary Clinical Pathologistsx0147-0701w(DLC)sc 79005035w(OCoLC)21950304 uhttp://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/journal/10.1111/(ISSN)1939-165X  aAUTH03252cgm a22004334a 101317946DNLM20260227152151.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071003s2007    mdu130 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14040  a(OCoLC)190867162  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4702 aNIH Research Festivald(2007 :cBethesda, Md.)10aChromosomes in modern biology and medicine /cJohn Niederhuber ... [et al.].30aChromosomes in modern biology and medicine  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Oct. 29, 2007).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 9 min., 57 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 25, 2007.3 a(CIT): 20th Anniversary NIH Research Festival    Opening Plenary Session   Chromosomes in Modern Biology and Medicine Chairs: John Niederhuber, Director, NCI, and Tom Misteli, NCI   Chromosomes are the fundamental structural units of our genomes. Since their discovery in the mid 19th century, the elucidation of chromosome biology has been the driving force behind much of modern genetics and molecular biology. The importance of chromosomes has been highlighted recently by the discovery that chromatin structure, epigenetic modifications and spatial organization of the genome are critically important for physiological processes. Defects in chromosome structure are now intimately linked to human disease, particularly cancer, and aging. The wealth of knowledge into chromosome biology gained in the last decades is now being applied for therapeutic purposes by chromosome engineering and chromosomes serve as the basis of routine cytogenetic diagnostic tests. NIH scientists have long been at the forefront of chromosome biology and have made some of the most important contributions by identifying the fundamental mechanisms involved in chromosome organization, epigenetics, and the development of novel genome sequencing, diagnostic and bioengineering tools. This session will highlight some of the cutting-edge efforts in chromosome biology within the NIH and will provide an outlook on the tremendous potential of this field for basic discovery and medicine.   Program:    Opening Remarks John Niederhuber, Director, NCI  Genetics of Common Disease: Empowered At Last Francis Collins, Director, NHGRI   Chromatin Boundaries Gary Felsenfeld, NIDDK   Heterochromatin: A Versatile Platform of the Genome Shiv Grewal, NCI   Cancer: A Disease of the Chromosome Thomas Ried, NCI   For more information, visit  http://researchfestival.nih.gov.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aChromosomes22aHeterochromatin 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aNiederhuber, John E.2 aNIH Research Festivald(2007 :cBethesda, Md.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14040  aAUTH01615cas a2200457   0374617DNLM20260224111546.0cr|cn#||||||||731113c19599999pl  x p       0||| 0pol    a0015-5616l0015-5616  aFMCRAW  a(DNLM)F08520000(s)  a(OCoLC)01778982  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Poland00aW1bFO22510aFolia Med Cracov2dnlm00aFolia medica Cracoviensia.  aKraków :bZakład Narodowy im. Ossolińskich3 aWarsaw :bPolska Akademia Nauk ;aKraków :bUniwersytet Jagielloński, Collegium Medicum  av.billus.  aFour no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 at. 1-        1959-