  a9784898011522  a4898011527  a(OCoLC)51171669  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Japan00aWM 1722 aPanic Grand Round.10aPanic Grand Round '98 :bpanikku shōgai kenkyū saizensen /chensha Fujii Isao, Kamishima Kunitoshi.31aPanic Grand Round  aDai 1-han.  aTōkyō :bRaifu.Saiensu,c1999.  a46 p. :bill.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aPapers presented at the 7th meeting of "Panic Grand Round" in 1998.  aText in Japanese.12aPanic Disorder22aMolecular Biology 2aConference Proceedings1 aFujii, Isao,d1932-  aAUTH02187cgm a22004454a 101267536DNLM20260227152612.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu091 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10338  a(OCoLC)1019835650  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aE 1851 aWilkins, Roger W.,d1932-2017.10a2002 NIH annual African American history month observance /cRoger Wilkins and Bill Grimmett.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 6, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 31 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 25, 2002.3 a(CIT): The theme for the observance will be "Celebrating Pioneers - Standing on the Shoulders From the Past."  Roger Wilkins, LL.B., writer, historian, and civil rights activist, will serve as the keynote speaker. He has a distinguished journalism career and has written for both the New York Times and The Washington Post.  While on the editorial page staff of The Washington Post, he shared a Pulitzer Prize in 1972 for Watergate coverage with Woodward, Bernstein and Herblock. Also, as part of the program, Mr. Bill Grimmett will perform as a character actor portraying Benjamin Banneker, the African American scientist, astrologer and surveyor of the Nation's capital.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aBlack or African Americanxhistory.22aCivil Rightsxhistory22aHistory, 18th Century 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=1508  aAUTH02479cgm a2200421 i 101708383DNLM20260227155726.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u170624s2017    mdu047|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:23328  a(OCoLC)993652375  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 20200aThinking about cancer as an infectious disease /cPatrick Moore. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2017]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (47 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture, annual George Khoury lecture  aRecorded May 31, 2017.3 a(CIT): The Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series presents the annual George Khoury Lecture  Infection causes one in five cancers throughout the world. Why do just seven human viruses cause cancer, but not others? Merkel cell polyomavirus (MCV), the recently discovered cause of Merkel cell carcinoma (a rare type of skin cancer), provides clues on how viruses can cause cancer as well as insights into cancers that are not caused by infection. Tumor-virus studies reveal a fundamental duality between antiviral defenses and tumor suppression. MCV is an example in which mutations to our viral flora, not to the host cells themselves, help to turn this innocuous agent into the cause for the most aggressive skin cancer. MCV also reveals how a small virus, composed of just a few genes, can persist and successfully evade the immune system for decades. This feature has importance not only for viral cancers but also may give clues to other chronic viral infections.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 5, 2017).12aNeoplasmsxvirology22aCommunicable Diseasesxcomplications 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aMoore, Patrickq(Patrick S.),espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pGeorge Khoury lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23328  aAUTH01383cgm a22004214a 101294029DNLM20260227151746.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061129s2006    mdu053|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13510  a(OCoLC)84672322  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3021 aLauritzen, Martin.10aCoupling cerebral blood flow and oxygen metabolism to neuronal firing and synaptic transmission /cMartin Lauritzen.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 11, 2006).  aStreaming video (53 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 20, 2006.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBrainxblood supply12aCerebrovascular Circulationxphysiology22aNeuronsxphysiology22aSynaptic Transmissionxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13510  aAUTH02293cgm a2200457 i 101720650DNLM20260227155832.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u180203s2018    mdu052|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:23661  a(OCoLC)1028039521  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 168.5.F500aNew insights into the human type I interferon response revealed by flavivirus-host interactions /cSonja M. Best. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2018]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (52 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's seminar series  aRecorded January 19, 2018.3 a(CIT):  More than a quarter of the world's population is at annual risk of infection with flaviviruses, which include West Nile virus, Zika virus, dengue virus, yellow fever virus and tick-borne encephalitis virus. Despite being closely related, these viruses have evolved different mechanisms to evade the type I interferon response. This seminar will discuss two distinct interactions between various flaviviruses and human interferon signaling pathways. These two studies provide new insight into regulation of interferon signaling through the IFNAR receptor, as well as the antiviral functions of the interferon response that may contribute to flavivirus evolution and emergence.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed February 26, 2018).12aFlavivirusximmunology22aFlavivirus Infectionsximmunology22aHost-Pathogen Interactionsximmunology22aImmune Evasion22aInterferon Type Ixmetabolism 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aBest, Sonja M.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23661  aAUTH01430cgm a22004214a 101267538DNLM20260227152610.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu083 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10341  a(OCoLC)1130608492  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aE 185.81 aHerman, Alexis M.,d1947-10aNIH 2003 African American history month observance /cAlexis Herman.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 6, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 23 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 25, 2003.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aBlack or African Americanxhistory.22aEmploymentxhistory 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=2280  aAUTH01328cgm a22004094a 101289722DNLM20260227151606.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2006    mdu061 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13335  a(OCoLC)84672011  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2671 aMeyskens, F. L.q(Frank L.)14aThe promises and perils of clinical chemoprevention :b1980-2030 /cFrank L. Meyskens Jr.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 5, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 35 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded July 27, 2006.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxprevention & control22aAntineoplastic Agentsxtherapeutic use22aChemoprevention 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13335  aAUTH02954cgm a22004694a 101501606DNLM20260227153658.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090407s2009    mdu060|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14977  a(OCoLC)319129955  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 2411 aSidransky, David.10aMolecular detection and staging of cancer /cDavid Sidransky.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIDCR 2009 seminar series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Apr. 15, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 18, 2009.3 a(CIT): NIDCR 2009 Seminar Series: From Basic Research to Therapy -- The Latest Frontier  Dr. David Sidransky of Johns Hopkins University will discuss his recent findings of genetic and epigenetic markers of lung and head and neck cancers, and will describe how genetic alterations can be used to predict the development and progression of these cancers. He will also talk about new xenograft models of human cancers and their use in drug development and personalization of cancer therapies, and the use of technologies to track new markers in these models and in clinical samples.   A pioneer in the molecular detection of cancer, Dr. Sidransky was the first to show that oncogenes and tumor suppressor gene mutations could be detected in urine, stool, and sputum from bladder, colon, and lung cancer patients, respectively. He has also shown that such mutations can be detected in surgical margins and lymph nodes, providing information about the spread of disease that cannot be obtained through standard pathologic examination.   Dr. Sidransky is a Professor of Otolaryngology, Oncology, Pathology, Urology, Genetics, and Cellular and Molecular Medicine at Johns Hopkins University (JHU). He is also the director of head and neck cancer research at the JHU Kimmel Comprehensive Cancer Center as well as a principal investigator at JHU's Head and Neck Cancer SPORE, or Specialized Program of Research Excellence.  For more information, visit http://www.nidcr.nih.gov/NewsAndFeatures/Announcements/NewSeminarSeries.htm.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasm Staging22aMolecular Diagnostic Techniques22aNeoplasmsxdiagnosis22aNeoplasmsxgenetics22aNeoplasmsxpathology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIDCR seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14977  aAUTH03371cgm a22004574a 101524408DNLM20260227153629.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100213s2010    mdu075|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15577  a(OCoLC)608617591  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWS 105.5.C81 aBialystok, Ellen.10aLifelong bilingualism :blinguistic costs, cognitive benefits, and long-term consequences /cEllen Bialystok.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBehavioral and Social Sciences Research lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 15 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 14, 2010.3 a(CIT): This lecture is an installment of the Behavioral and Social Sciences Research Lecture Series sponsored by the NIH Office of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research and organized by the NIH Behavioral and Social Sciences Research Coordinating Committee.  The Behavioral and Social Sciences Research Coordinating Committee (BSSR CC), with support from the Office of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research (OBSSR), convenes a series of guest lectures and symposia on selected topics in the behavioral and social sciences. These presentations by prominent behavioral and social scientists provide the NIH community with overviews of current research on topics of scientific and social interest. The lectures and symposia are approximately 50 minutes in length, with additional time for questions and discussion. All seminars are open to NIH staff and to the general public.   A growing body of research points to the importance of specific experiences in modifying cognitive outcomes and supports the interpretation that there is long term brain plasticity. Thus, cognitive systems are fundamentally shaped by our experiences. One such experience is bilingualism, with dramatic outcomes found across the lifespan, beginning in the first year of life. The two primary consequences of bilingualism are slower and more effortful linguistic processing than comparable monolinguals but faster and more efficient executive control, especially applied to nonverbal tasks. Dr. Bialystok will report research illustrating these two opposing outcomes, taking examples from studies with children, adults, and older adults. These effects have been found into older age and are evidenced as well in patients diagnosed with dementia. She will review this evidence and propose a framework for understanding the mechanism that leads to both these positive and negative consequences of bilingualism.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aLanguage Development12aMultilingualism22aCognition22aVocabulary 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research. 0aBSSR lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15577  aAUTH01272cas a2200421 a 100911346DNLM20260227091059.0cr|cnu||||||||991215c20009999cauar         0    0eng    asn 99009629 0 a1527-8204l1527-8204  aARGHC4  a(OCoLC)43033948  aNSDcNSDdCUSdCASdWAUdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bAN771Q10aAnnu Rev Genomics Hum Genet2dnlm00aAnnual review of genomics and human genetics.  aPalo Alto, CA :bAnnual Reviews,cc2000-  av. :bill. ;c24 cm.  aAnnual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1 (2000)-1 aMEDLINEbv1, 2000-1 aPubMedbv1, 2000-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3v. 1 (2000)c20011126fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper that meets the full ANSI standard for Permanent Paper12aGenome22aChromosome Mapping22aGenetics, Medical0 x1545-293X4 uhttp://www.annualreviews.org/loi/genom  aAUTH01855cgm a22004454a 101524396DNLM20260227153438.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100213s2010    mdu070|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15563  a(OCoLC)605750105  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3001 aMarques, David,d1950-10aBrain navigator training /cDavid Marques and Sarah Tyrchniewicz.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 10 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Jan. 20, 2010.3 a(CIT): BrainNavigator is a powerful 3D brain interactive software tool for research in the neurosciences.  The BrainNavigator Rodent Edition combines a number of atlases with 3D "virtual brain" software capabilities to provide imaging for research, education, and structure analysis.  Login is required for every user to have access to BrainNavigator"s custom features like personalized workspace annotation, calibration, notes sharing, 3D slice configuration and saving of 3D model configurations.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBrainxanatomy & histology22aAtlases as Topic22aImaging, Three-Dimensional22aRodentia22aSoftware 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15563  aAUTH01543cas a2200505 a 9433358DNLM20260227091012.0cr|cnu||||||||941028c19949999nyu|| p       0    0eng    asn 92006717   a1066-5277l1066-5277  aJCOBEM  a(DNLM)SR0078401(s)  a(OCoLC)26981925  aNSDPcNSDPdDNLM  9United States00aW1bJO595KK10aJ Comput Biol2dnlm 0aJournal of computational biology00aJournal of computational biology :ba journal of computational molecular cell biology.  aNew York, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.,cc1994-  av. :bill.  aMonthly,b2009-  aTen no. a year,b2005-2008  aQuarterly,b-1999  aSix no. a year,b2000-2004  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (spring 1994)-  aTitle from cover.  aPrint issues ceased with v. 16, no. 12 (Dec. 2009)1 aMEDLINEbv1n1,spring 1994-1 aPubMedbv1n1,spring 1994-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.02aComputer Simulation02aInformation Systems02aMedical Informatics Computing02aMolecular Biology 2aPeriodical0 x1557-86664 