1420081958300630096165000140102471000660103899900090110462931200RDNLM20260227133540.0820626s1901    iluaf         001 0 eng    a   02009817   a(OCoLC)14799555  aDNLMbengerdad20260226dDNLM  9United States00aWAbD554f 19011 aDickson, Charles R.q(Charles Rea),d1858-eauthor.10aFirst aid in accidents /cby Charles R. Dickson ; a manual of instruction ; what to do, and what not to do, in cases of injury and other emergencies ; prepared for the International Association of Railway Surgeons and endorsed by the Association at the 14th annual meeting, Milwaukee, Wis., U.S.A., June 10, 11, 12, 1901. 1aChicago ;aNew York ;aToronto :bFleming H. Revell Company,c1901.  a127 pages, 1 unnumbered leaf of plates :billustrations (some color) ;c17 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes index.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill not digitizec20260227lPhysical condition2pda5DNLM12aFirst Aid2 aInternational Association of Railway Surgeons,eissuing body.  aAUTH04598cgm a22003974a 101322972DNLM20260227152423.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071207s2007    mdu067|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14160  a(OCoLC)212399817  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4751 aLi, Guo-Min.10aMechanism of DNA mismatch repair /cGuo-Min Li.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 25, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 7 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 20, 2007.3 a(CIT): DNA mismatch repair (MMR) is a highly conserved biological pathway that plays a key role in maintaining genomic stability. Defects in MMR predispose to cancers. The genome-maintenance function of the pathway includes correcting DNA biosynthetic errors, suppressing homeologous recombination, and signaling DNA damage response. This presentation reports our recent efforts to understand the molecular mechanisms of MMR in human cells and includes two lines of work: (i) identification and characterization of MMR components, and (ii) reconstitution of the human MMR reaction in a defined system. In the first part of the presentation, two biochemical mutants were generated from HeLa nuclear extracts using a fractionation approach (e.g., ammonium sulfate precipitation and column chromatography), and each mutant was used as a receptor to isolate a nuclear activity capable of restoring MMR to the biochemical mutant in a functional in vitro MMR assay. One complementing activity was identified to be replication protein A (RPA) and the other is high mobility group box 1 protein (HMGB1). Whereas HMGB1 is specifically involved in the excision reaction, RPA plays multiple roles in the MMR reaction, including protecting the nascent ssDNA generated during excision and promoting repair DNA synthesis. RPA is phosphorylated during MMR. Evidence is provided that unphosphorylated RPA, which possesses a much stronger ssDNA binding affinity than phosphrylated RPA, preferentially promotes mismatch excision, while phosphorylated RPA facilitates DNA resynthesis. Similarly, proliferating cellular nuclear antigen (PCNA) was found to be essential for MMR by participating both the MMR initiation and repair resynthesis reactions.    The second part of the presentation focuses on reconstitution of the human MMR reaction using purified human proteins, including MutSa or MutSb, MutLa, RPA, EXO1, HMGB1, PCNA, RFC, DNA polymerase d, and ligase I. In this system, MutSb plays a limited role in repair of base-base mismatches, but it processes insertion/deletion mispairs much more efficiently than MutSa, which efficiently corrects both types of heteroduplexes. MutLa reduces the processivity of EXO1 and terminates EXO1-catalyzed excision upon mismatch removal. In the absence of MutLa, mismatch-provoked excision by EXO1 occurs extensively. RPA and HMGB1 play similar but complementary roles in stimulating MutSa-activated, EXO1-catalyzed excision in the presence of a mismatch, but RPA has a distinct role in facilitating MutLa-mediated excision termination past mismatch. Additionally, these reconstitution experiments show that efficient repair of a single mismatch requires multiple molecules of MutSa-MutLa complex. These data suggest a model for human MMR involving coordinated initiation and termination of mismatch-provoked excision.   The DNA Repair Interest Group is concerned with all forms of DNA damage and repair. As a major defense against environmental damage to cells DNA repair is present in all organisms examined including bacteria, yeast, drosophila, fish, amphibians, rodents and humans. The members of the DNA Repair Interest Group perform research in areas including DNA repair enzymology and fine structure, mutagenesis, gene and cell cycle regulation, protein structure, and human disease.   For more information, visit the  DNA Repair Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDNA Mismatch Repair 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bDNA Repair Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14160  aAUTH02057cgm a2200457 i 9918975957206676DNLM20260227165104.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u250104s2024    mdu601|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35430  a(DNLM)CIT:35440  a(OCoLC)1540315098  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQT 2562 aLife Course Approach to Physical Activity and Cardiovascular Health Research (Workshop) :d(2024 :cOnline)jauthor.12aA life course approach to physical activity and cardiovascular health research :bcritical challenges innovations, and opportunities /cNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2024]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (10 hr., 1 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded December 3 & 4, 2024.3 aThis workshop will explore physical activity research from pregnancy to older adulthood, with an emphasis on reviewing alternatives to moderate-to-vigorous exercise, discussing health equity issues, and setting future physical activity priorities.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed September 22, 2025).12aExercise12aHealthy Lifestyle22aCardiovascular Diseasesxprevention & control22aRisk Reduction Behavior 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=5526640uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=55267  aAUTH02511cgm a2200469 i 9918573260806676DNLM20260227163222.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2023    mdu470|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32906  a(DNLM)CIT:32907  a(OCoLC)1437827762  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWS 350.8.P42 aMinimally Verbal/Non-Speaking Individuals With Autism: Research Directions for Interventions to Promote Language and Communication (Workshop)d(2023 :cOnline)jauthor.10aMinimally verbal/non-speaking individuals with autism :bresearch directions for interventions to promote language and communication (day 1 & 2) /cNIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (7 hr., 50 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded January 24 & 25, 2023.3 a(CIT): The overall goal is a focused workshop to identify research needs and directions for improving language/communication outcomes for minimally verbal, non-speaking  children and adults with autism.  The webinar, open to all, will focus on novel interventions, research designs and methods for intervention studies, and meaningful outcome measures, to foster development of critical research studies addressing the communication needs of this population. The agenda and names of participating speakers can be found at   https://www.nidcd.nih.gov/news/events/minimally-verbalnon-speaking-individuals-autism-research-directions-interventions.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed May 29, 2024).12aAutistic Disorder22aNonverbal Communication22aSign Language22aManual Communication22aCommunication Devices for People with Disabilities22aResearch Design 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute on Deafness and Other Communication Disorders (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4873740uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=48740  aAUTH01824cgm a22004214a 101268361DNLM20260227150516.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu078 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11821  a(OCoLC)70873976  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWH 2001 aGermain, Ron.10aT-lymphocyte activation :bfrom self-recognition to dynamic in vivo visualization and on to in silico modeling /cRon Germain.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Aug. 7, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 18 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 11, 2004.3 a(CIT): The Immunology Interest Group (IIG) organizes activities designed to promote information exchange and interactions among NIH scientists interested in the field of immunology, broadly defined. Interactions are facilitated via 