, no. 1 (1998)c20040308fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aPharmaceutical Preparations 2aPeriodical2 aHrvatsko farmaceutsko društvo.2 aSlovenian Pharmaceutical Society.0 x1846-955800tActa pharmaceutica Jugoslavicax0001-6667w(DLC)sn 86000242w(OCoLC)47662024 uhttps://sciendo.com/journal/ACPH  aAUTH00990nam a2200301 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390200018000800200027000980400025001250420008001500440013001580600013001711000053001842450128002372500023003652640038003883000040004263360026004663370028004923380027005205040041005476500022005887000048006107100021006589990009006799918863382606676DNLM20260227084744.0240716s2023    ck a     b    000 0 spa    a9789585101814  z9789585101821q(ISBNe)  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Colombia00aWY 154.21 aFlórez Bedoya, Alex Rogelio,eauthor,eeditor.10aAtención de enfermería a personas en situación de urgencias /cAlex Rogelio Flórez Bedoya, Juan David Arango Urrea.  aPrimera edición. 1aMedellin :bEditorial CES,c2023.  a293 pages :billustrations ;c21 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aEmergency Nursing1 aArango Urrea, Juan David,eauthor,eeditor.2 aUniversidad CES.  aAUTH02549cgm a2200469 i 9918332581306676DNLM20260227161806.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220104s2021    mdu440|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32076  a(DNLM)CIT:32077  a(OCoLC)1337519375  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 7002 aRole of Inflammation Resolution Roundtable (Conference)d(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aRole of inflammation resolution roundtable /cNIAMS, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (7 hr., 20 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 16 & 17, 2021.3 a(CIT): Uncontrolled and dysfunctional resolution of inflammation may directly link to chronic inflammation, a common pathogenic element among diseases and conditions within the NIAMS core mission. The overall goals of this roundtable are to improve understanding of the intrinsic mechanisms of inflammation resolution and how dysregulated resolution may lead to chronic inflammation; and ultimately, to improve health-related outcomes for patients with musculoskeletal, rheumatic, or skin diseases or conditions where chronic inflammation is a key attribute. Invited participants of the roundtable include key experts on inflammation resolution within NIAMS disease areas as well as outside of the NIAMS disease areas; and colleagues from the FDA and the NIH with an interest in inflammation resolution.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 20, 2022).12aInflammationximmunology12aInflammationxtherapy22aChronic Diseasextherapy22aSignal Transductionximmunology22aInflammation Mediatorsximmunology 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institute of Arthritis and Musculoskeletal and Skin Diseases (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4406640uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44068  aAUTH03151cgm a2200481 i 101771163DNLM20260227160212.0m     o  z f      vz|czau|ucr|cna||||||||200828s2020    mdu064 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35599  a(OCoLC)1197632181  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 35400aNeurobiology of recovery after brain and spinal cord injury in macaque models /cTadashi Isa. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (64 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience series seminar  aRecorded February 3, 20203 a(CIT): Dr. Isa’s Lab aims at elucidating the neural circuit mechanisms for regulation of dexterous motor actions, learning, and the functional recovery after the brain and spinal cord injury. They are conducting the researches in various animal models for their goal, from mice, rats, marmosets, macaque monkeys, and humans, applying multidisciplinary methods, such as electrophysiology, psychophysics, neuroimaging, transgenic animals, viral vectors, and computational modeling. Dynamic properties of the large-scaled neural networks in the brain, which span multiple brain areas experience context-dependent transition in relation to the vigilance, attention or learning stage etc. One of the most typical situations where such transition occurs is during the functional recovery from the brain and/or spinal cord injury. They have been studying the neuronal mechanism of the post-lesion functional recovery and how the recovery is regulated by cognitive functions such as motivation. They believe that with this knowledge, they can develop effective therapeutic strategies to cure the brain/spinal cord injury in the future. A big progress in this field of research has recently been achieved by introduction of a new technique of pathway-selective and reversible manipulation technique using the combination of 2 viral vectors in monkeys, which was developed in Dr. Isa’s lab (Kinoshita et al. Nature 2012), by which they can pin-down the critical circuit(s) for recovery.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed September 22, 2020).12aBrain Injuriesxphysiopathology22aBrain Injuriesxrehabilitation22aSpinal Cord Injuriesxphysiopathology22aSpinal Cord Injuriesxrehabilitation22aNeural Pathwaysxphysiology22aMacaca22aModels, Animal 2aWebcast 2aLecture12aIsa, Tadashi,espeaker.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=35599  aAUTH01876cgm a22004454a 101268080DNLM20260227150132.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu063 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11345  a(OCoLC)70663587  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5681 aAhmed, Rafi.10aMemory CD8 T cell differentiation during acute versus chronic viral infection /cRafi Ahmed.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed June 23, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 2 min., 34 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 10, 2003.3 a(CIT): The Immunology Interest Group (IIG) organizes activities designed to promote information exchange and interactions among NIH scientists interested in the field of immunology, broadly defined. Interactions are facilitated via weekly meetings on current topics as well as an annual Immunology Retreat.  For more information, visit The Immunology Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aCD8-Positive T-Lymphocytes12aImmunologic Memory22aCell Differentiation22aVirus Diseasesximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11345  aAUTH01589cas a2200445 i 9918609886206676DNLM20260209092445.0m     o  d        cr#|||||||||||210429d20222025enkar poo     0   a0eng    a  20232283380 a2752-6143l2752-6143  a(OCoLC)1359610261  aUKMGBbengerdad20260209cUKMGBdDNLM  apcc  9England00aW110aCamb Prism Precis Med2dnlm 0aCambridge prisms. Precision medicine00aCambridge prisms.pPrecision medicine.30aPrecision medicine 1a[Cambridge] :bCambridge Univer