ttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11540  aAUTH02741cgm a2200445 a 101585257DNLM20260227154202.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120525s2012    mdu070|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17254  a(OCoLC)797084276  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 1031 aSabatini, Bernardo.10aSelf stimulation :bhow positive feedback loops wire the brain /cBernardo L. Sabatini.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 10 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded May 9, 2012.3 a(CIT): A great deal of brain development happens after birth. During this period, sensory inputs and patterns of activity within the brain drive the refinement of circuits and the formation of synapses. This is well established for parts of the brain that receive signals from the outside world, such as sensory systems. Much less is known about the function of activity in shaping the development of non-sensory and sub-cortical systems. Dr. Sabatini will show that activity in a recurrent loop between the basal ganglia and cortex acts in a self-reinforcing manner. Thus manipulations that promote the activity of the direct pathway through the basal ganglia, and hence activate cortex, lead to greater innervation of the basal ganglia. His lab proposes that such self-reinforcing systems ensure the correct wiring of sub-cortical systems that lack the stereotyped topographic organization of sensory systems. Lastly, his lab proposes that early perturbations of such mechanisms can contribute to developmental disorders such as cerebral palsy and attention deficit hyperactivity disorder.  The NIH Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series includes weekly scientific talks by some of the top researchers in the biomedical sciences worldwide.  For more information, visit:  The NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aPsychophysiologyxmethods12aSelf Stimulationxphysiology22aBrainxphysiology22aFeedback 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17254  aAUTH01409cas a2200445 a 101562838DNLM20260227090922.0cr||||||||||||110617c20119999ja || p       |    0eng    a  2011243437  a2185-6486l2185-6486y1882-0476  a(OCoLC)732832791  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aengajpn  apcc  9Japan00aW1bNI912T10aMed Mycol J2dnlm00aMedical mycology journal.  aTōkyō :bNihon ishinkin Gakkai  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 52, no. 1 (2011).1 aMEDLINEbv52n1, 2011-0 aPubMedbv52n1, 2011-  aAlso issued online.  aText chiefly in English with some in Japanese.  Search interface available in English and Japanese.  aDescription based on: Vol. 52, no. 1 (2011); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 52, no. 1 (2011).12aMycoses22aMycology 2aPeriodical2 aNihon Ishinkin Gakkai.0 x2186-165X00tNihon Ishinkin Gakkai zasshix0916-4804w(DLC)sn 94038963w(OCoLC)314136204 uhttps://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/browse/mmj  aAUTH01306cgm a2200397 i 101775910DNLM20260227160607.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2020    mdu056|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31445  a(OCoLC)1241125147  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWR 20000aPemphigus /cKim B. Yancey. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (56 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aClinical center grand rounds  aRecorded December 9, 2020.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH 