43nam a2200301 i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003100073042000800104044001800112060003300130100005000163245006400213264006100277300002200338336002600360337002800386338002700414500006000441504004100501540014200542583003900684583004300723650004600766730002000812999000900832101776452DNLM20260220150834.0210224r18841884nyu      b    000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5508 no.181 aGarrigues, Henry Jacques,d1831-1913eauthor.10aPrevention of puerperal infection /cby Henry J. Garrigues. 1aNew York. :bTrow's Printing and Bookbinding Co.,c1884.  a15 pages ;c19 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"Reprinted from The Medical record, December 29, 1883."  aIncludes bibliographical references.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM12aPuerperal Infectionxprevention & control0 aMedical record.  aAUTH06422nam a2200445 i 9918987866006676DNLM20260205095105.0m     o  d f      cr|cn#||||||||250202s2024    enk     ob   f000 0 eng    a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK610845  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9England00aWA 2751 aPandey, Bidhya,eauthor.10aBarriers and facilitators to learning and using first aid skills for road traffic crash victims in Nepal :ba qualitative study /cBidhya Pandey, Amrit Banstola, Gary Smart, Sunil Kumar Joshi, Julie Mytton. 1aSouthampton, UK :bNational Institute for Health and Care Research,cSeptember 2024.  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (pages 109-122))  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aGlobal health research  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 aBACKGROUND: Road traffic injuries are a major global public health problem despite being avoidable and preventable. In many low- and middle-income countries, the victims of road traffic crashes do not receive care at the scene and may die before reaching hospital. In low- and middle-income countries where prehospital emergency medical services are not well established, bystanders have the potential to save lives by providing first aid. Nepal has rising rates of road traffic injuries and emergency medical services are early in development. There is limited evidence on the factors that influence people in Nepal to become trained in first aid and to use their first aid skills. OBJECTIVES: To understand the barriers and facilitators to learning first aid and to applying those skills to help road traffic crash victims in Nepal. DESIGN: Qualitative study using semistructured interviews. SETTING: Kathmandu, Lalitpur, Bhaktapur, Kaski and Makwanpur districts in Nepal. PARTICIPANTS: We interviewed three groups of participants: (1) members of the public who had never been trained in first aid, (2) members of the public who had been trained in first aid and (3) first aid trainers. DATA SOURCES: Interviews explored reasons why people may be interested in being trained and factors that would inhibit being trained or applying their first aid skills. Interviews were transcribed, translated into English and analysed thematically. RESULTS: Respect from the public, a desire to help those in need and confidence to apply skills were important factors in encouraging people to learn and apply first aid. Barriers included lack of time and financial implications of providing first aid. The fear of social and legal consequences if the patient had a poor outcome, a lack of confidence to apply skills and lack of trust shown to first aiders by some members of the community discouraged learning and using first aid skills. LIMITATIONS: Participants were mostly from urban and semiurban areas. People with more difficult access to health care may have held different views. People who were untrained in first aid and agreed to participate may hold different views to those who were not recruited, as they had interest in the topic. CONCLUSIONS: Members of the public can be encouraged to learn and apply first aid skills to help road traffic crash victims, but there are multiple barriers to engagement in first aid. Providing legal protection from prosecution for first aiders, raising public awareness of the value of first aid, addressing financial constraints and provision of refresher training may address these barriers. FUTURE WORK: Research to address the barriers to the creation of a Good Samaritan law appears warranted. The perspectives of other groups often involved in the care and/or transport of road traffic crash victims, such as taxi drivers, would add to our understanding of factors affecting the application of first aid. The gendered stereotypes reported by female first aiders warrant further exploration. FUNDING: This article presents independent research funded by the National Institute for Health and Care Research (NIHR) Global Health Research programme as award number 16/137/49.3 aProviding first aid to road traffic crash victims, together with safe transfer to a health facility, can save lives or reduce the harm sustained. There are increasing numbers of road traffic crashes and injuries in Nepal but limited evidence on the factors that influence people to learn first aid, or to use first aid skills. Therefore, we aimed to explore the barriers and facilitators to learning and using first aid skills to help road crash victims in Nepal. We interviewed three groups of people: (1) members of the public who had never been trained in first aid, (2) members of public who had received first aid training and (3) first aid trainers. The interviews were conducted in Nepali, translated into English, and analysed to identify common themes. Participants said that appreciation from the community and a desire to help those who are in need encouraged them to learn first aid, but they were not always confident to apply their first aid skills. Participants described a fear of social and legal consequences of providing care, and a lack of awareness in the public about why providing first aid was important. Time and financial constraints were further barriers to learning and using first aid skills. Providing legal protection from prosecution for first aiders and raising the awareness of the importance of providing first aid to stabilise injuries prior to transport to hospital appear to be important steps to improve outcomes for road traffic crash victims.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed Feb. 4, 2026).12aAccidents, Traffic22aFirst Aidxpsychology22aWounds and Injuriesxprevention & control 2aNepal1 aBanstola, Amrit,eauthor.1 aSmart, Garyc(Of University of the West of England, Bristol)eauthor.1 aJoshi, S. K.q(Sunil Kumar),eauthor.1 aMytton, Julie,eauthor.2 aNational Institute for Health and Care Research (Great Britain) 0aGlobal health research.x2631-761340uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK610845/  aAUTH01603cas a22004694a 101157207DNLM20260227090919.0m        d        cr|cnu||||||||021017c20019999ne qr pss     0    0eng    a  2002243513  a0972-6292l0972-6292  a(OCoLC)50959849  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9Netherlands10aW110aIndian Pacing Electrophysiol J2dnlm00aIndian pacing and electrophysiology journal.13aIPEJ  a[Bangalore, India?] :bIndian Pacing and Electrophysiology Group,c[2001]-2 32007- :a[Bangalore, India?] :bIndian Heart Rhythm Society31aAmsterdam :bElsevier B.V. on behalf of Indian Heart Rhythm Society  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Oct.-Dec. 2001)-  aTitle from caption (viewed Oct. 17, 2002).0 aPubMedbv1, Oct-Dec 2001-0 aPMC  aVol. 1 complete in one issue.  aMode of access: Internet.  aOfficial journal of the Indian Pacing and Electrophysiology Group.12aCardiac Pacing, Artificial12aElectrophysiology 2aPeriodical2 aIndian Pacing and Electrophysiology Group.4 uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/097262924 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101157207  aAUTH04072ctm a2200577 a 9404805DNLM20260205113121.0hd|bfa012baap940331s17uu    xx            000 0 ara    a(OCoLC)57251390  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM00aWZ 225bI132f 17010 aIbn Buṭlān,d-approximately 1068.10aFī tadbīr al-amrāḍ al-ʻāriḍah ʻalaī al-akthar min al-aghdhīyah al-māʼlūfah wa-al-adwīyah al-mawjūdah liyantafʻ bihā ruhbān al-adīrah wa-man baʻuda ʻan al-madīnah /cIbn Abī al-Ḥasan al-Mukhtār ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʻAbdūn ibn Buṭlān.3 aMaqālah fī ʻilm al-ṭibb3 aTadbīr al-amrāḍ al-ʻāriḍah ʻalaī al-akthar min al-aghdhīyah al-māʼlūfah  c[17--]  a45 leaves, bound, (21 lines) :bpaper ;c21 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aManuscript.  aArabic.  aTitle taken from fol. 1b lines 4-5, near the beginning of the text; the title page (fol. 1a) has short title: al-Maqālah fī ʻilm al-ṭibb (treatise on medicine), probably added in a later hand.  aThe author's name appears at the start of the text (fol. 1b line 3).  aCopy undated; appearance of paper, handwriting, etc., suggest 18th c. date; scribe not named.  aPurchased in 1941 by the Army Medical Library from A. S. Yahuda who acquired it in Erbil in northern Iraq (ELS 1707 med 66).  aThis copy was employed in the edition of the treatise prepared as a doctoral dissertation; S. Y. Jadon, The Arab physician Ibn Buṭlan's (d. 1066) medical manual for the use of monks and country people [Arabic text], Ph.D. diss., University of California, Los Angeles, 1968.  aOnly five other recorded copies of treatise.  aFol. 33 should be placed before fol. 36; incomplete copy of treatise: folios are missing after fol. 31 and 35 (comprising all or parts of bābs 25-33); the end of the 41st bāb and all of the 42nd are missing.  aCatchwords; rubrications; clear large Naskhi; some marginalia.  aPaper is fairly opaque, yellowed; laid lines visible but only traces of chain lines; water damaged; worm-eaten; lower corner very damaged; some folios repaired.  aBound in pink cloth with marbled paper over pasteboards; modern paper pastedowns and endpapers.  aDescribed in: Schullian & Sommer. Cat. of incun. & MSS., 1950, A37.  aMedical guide for monks written in 42 chapters (bāb), by the Christian physician Ibn Buṭlān. Nourishment for sedentary people including chapters on the subjects of humoral balance, secretions and excretions of humors, health preservation, food, mental health, diseases of eyes and eyelids, diseases of respiratory system, diseases of stomach, reproductive system diseases, gout, fevers and wounds, simple and compound remedies, tablets, purgatives and laxatives, suppositories, oils, balms,and ointments.  aNLM microfilm copy shelved as: Film 48-119 no. 45DNLM  aNLM microfilm copy shelved as: Film 9105 no. 15DNLM  aMicrofilm.m17--.bWashington, D.C. :cArmy Medical Library,d1948.e1 microfilm reel ; 35 mm.  aMicrofilm.bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2004.e1 microfilm reel : negative ; 35 mm.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202602052pda5DNLM  aCondition Reviewedc20260205lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM12aHumoralism22aDiet Therapy22aDrug Therapy22aMonks22aSedentary Behavior 2aManuscript1 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.).kManuscript.nMS A 37  aAUTH01551cas a2200493 a 8800535DNLM20260227091014.0cr|cn#||||||||871224c19829999enkfr p       0    0eng    a   83640152 zsn 82000903  a0730-9384l0730-9384  a(DNLM)SR0056448(s)  a(OCoLC)08123994  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  ansdp  9England00aW1bPO242H10aPolitics Life Sci2dnlm 0aPolitics and the life sciences00aPolitics and the life sciences :bthe journal of the Association for Politics and the Life Sciences.1 iAt head of title:aPLSf<Feb. 1992- >  aDeKalb, Ill. :bThe Association,c[c1982]-3 aCambridge :bCambridge University Press  av. :bill.  aSemiannual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (July 1982)-  aTitle from cover.  aImprint varies.1 aMEDLINEbv2n1, Aug. 1983-1 aPubMedbv2n1, Aug. 1983-  aVol. 10 for 1991 has only 1 issue.  aAlso issued online. 2aBehavior 2aBiology 2aPolitics 2aPeriodical2 aAssociation for Politics and the Life Sciences (U.S.)0 x1471-54574 uhttps://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/politics-and-the-life-sciences  aAUTH01998cgm a22004454a 101503618DNLM20260227153639.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090428s2009    mdu112 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15021  a(OCoLC)428770714  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2671 aGottesman, Michael M.10aMultidrug transporters in health and disease /cMichael M. Gottesman. ABC transporters and cancer chemotherapy / Susan E. Bates.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screens (viewed July 1, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 52 min. : sd., col.).  a"Demystifying medicine."  aRecorded Apr. 7, 2009.3 a(CIT): The course includes presentation of patients, pathology, diagnosis and therapy in the context of major disease problems and current research.  Primarily directed toward Ph.D. students, fellows, and staff, it is also of interest to medical students and clinicians.  The course is designed to help bridge the gap between advances in biology and their application to major human diseases.  Each session includes clinical and basic science components which are presented by NIH staff and outside invitees.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDrug Resistance, Neoplasm22aAntineoplastic Agentsxpharmacokinetics22aATP-Binding Cassette Transporters 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)0 aABC transporters and cancer chemotherapy.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15021  aAUTH01446cas a2200433 a 101508602DNLM20260227090916.0cr||||||||||||070301c20089999cauar         0   a0eng    a  20072151040 a1936-1327l1936-1327y0732-1327y0732-058221  a(OCoLC)85479636  aNSDcNSDdNSDdCUSdNSDdEEMdNSDdDLCdDNLM  apccansdp  9United States00aQD71b.A5600aW1bAN769K10a5432140 aAnnual review of analytical chemistry (Palo Alto, Calif.)10aAnnu Rev Anal Chem (Palo Alto Calif)2dnlm0 aAnnu. rev. anal. chem.b(Palo Alto, Calif.) 0aAnnual review of analytical chemistryb(Palo Alto, Calif.)10aAnnual review of analytical chemistry.  aPalo Alto, Calif. :bAnnual Reviews  aAnnual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1 (2008).  aDescription based on first issue; title from title page.2 aMEDLINEbv1, July 2008-0 aPubMedbv1, July 2008-  aAlso issued online.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 2 (2009).12aChemistry Techniques, Analytical0 x1936-13354 uhttp://www.annualreviews.org/loi/anchem  aAUTH02291cgm a22004334a 101300951DNLM20260227151922.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070302s2007    mdu061|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13659  a(OCoLC)104816545  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 3751 aMahadevan, L.q(Lakshminarayanan)10aMathematics, mechanics, and motility /cL. Mahadevan.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 26, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 1 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 28, 2007.3 a(CIT): Life is almost synonymous with autonomous movement; indeed, nature has engineered a number of exquisite mechanisms on molecular, cellular and organismal level to accomplish motility on a range of length and time scales. This leads to the question of the chemical and physical basis of motility and its quantitative description. To uncover the basic features of this rich landscape, I will focus on paradigmatic examples of motility including collective mechanochemistry of macromolecular assemblies and molecular motors at the cellular level, to the water driven movements in fungi and plants, to the cell driven movements in morphogenesis, to the muscle driven movements of snakes and fishes. I hope to show how quantitative experiments, physical analogies and simple mathematical models complement comparative studies and allow us to glean design principles in biological systems, which are constrained by physics and engineered by evolution.   WALS.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aCell Movementxphysiology12aMovementxphysiology22aModels, Theoretical22aMusclesxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13659  aAUTH01392cas a2200445 a 101543508DNLM20260227090925.0cr||||||||||||100719c20119999nyu|| p       |    0eng    a  20102020050 a2156-5333l2156-5333  a(OCoLC)648741436  aDLCcDLCdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW1bJO533RF10aJ Adolesc Young Adult Oncol2dnlm00aJournal of adolescent and young adult oncology.1 aJAYAO  aNew Rochelle, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.  aBimonthly,b2018-  aQuarterly,b2011-2017  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1, no. 1 (2011).1 aMEDLINEbv4n1, Mar. 2015-0 aPubMedbv4n1, Mar. 2015- ; Selected citations only before this date  aAlso issued online.  aDescrip