r|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210408s2021    mdu386|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31576  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 1402 aRare Disease Day (Conference)d(2021cOnline)jauthor.10aRare disease day at NIH 2021 /cNCATS, NIH and Clinical Center, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (6 hr., 26 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded March 1, 2021.3 a(CIT): Rare diseases affect an estimated 30 million people in the United States. On March 1, 2021, NIH will host its annual Rare Disease Day event to raise awareness about these disorders, the people they affect, and current NIH research collaborations underway. Sponsored by the National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (NCATS) and the NIH Clinical Center, the event will feature interactive panel discussions, the sharing of rare stories, and more. Participants can share their thoughts, photos, and experiences via social media using the hashtag #RDDNIH.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed August 11, 2021).12aRare Diseases 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41259  aAUTH02353cgm a2200469 i 9918681339106676DNLM20260227164145.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230929s2023    mdu066|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33480  a(OCoLC)1452436503  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 171.504aThe path of discovery :bfrom 'me too drug' in depression to novel rapid-acting therapeutics /cCarlos Zarate, M.D. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 6 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aPhilip S. Chen Jr. distinguished lecture on innovation and technology transfer ;v16th  aRecorded September 22, 2023.3 a(CIT): 16th Annual Philip S. Chen Jr. Ph.D. Distinguished Lecture on Innovation and Technology Transfer(external link), Lipsett Speaker: Carlos Zarate, M.D., NIMH(external link) Established in 2006, the Philip S. Chen Jr. Ph.D. Distinguished Lecture on Innovation and Technology Transfer honors Dr. Chen"s 41-year NIH career and his remarkable, diverse, and creative contributions to the NIH, especially to its Intramural Research Program and to the field of technology transfer. For more information go to <a href="https://researchfestival.nih.gov/2023">https://researchfestival.nih.gov/2023.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PowerPoint screen (viewed August 9, 2024).12aMajor Depressive Disorderxdrug therapy22aDepressive Disorder, Treatment-Resistantxdrug therapy22aAntidepressive Agents, Second-Generationxtherapeutic use22aSuicidal Ideation22aSuicide Prevention22aInventions 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aZarate, Carlos A.,cJr.espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aPhilip Chen lecture ;v16th.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49943  aAUTH01054nam a2200289 i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003100073044001800104060003200122245005500154264005400209300002100263336002600284337002800310338002600338540014200364583003900506583004300545610004000588650003600628650001700664651001800681710005600699999000900755101778169DNLM20260225114528.0210416s1870    mau           000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5571 no.103aAn appeal to the citizens for St. John's Hospital. 1a[Lowell?] :b[publisher not identified],c[1870?]  a1 sheet ;c21 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediated