eaves of plates (1 folded) :bfolded map ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCover title.  a"Reprint from Annual report of the Executive Board of the City of Rochester, N.Y., for the year ending April 7, 1890."  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202105042pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202105045DNLM12aTyphoid Feverxepidemiology22aEpidemiologic Studies 2aNew York1 aMallory, M. L.q(Maitland Lorenzo),d1848-1894,eauthor.2 aRochester (N.Y.).bExecutive Board.tAnnual report of the Executive Board.  aAUTH01415nam a2200373 i 101778222DNLM20260226080603.0940606s1893    nyua          000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)30560010  aTMBbengcTMBdOCLCGdYUSdOCLCAdOCLCQdOCLCOdOCLCFdOCLCQdOCLCOerdadDNLM  9United States 4aRD663b893K00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5571 no.81 aKeen, William W.q(William Williams),d1837-1932,eauthor.10aVivisection and brain-surgery /cby W.W. Keen. 1aNew York :bHarper & Bros,c1893.  a1 unnumbered page, pages 129-139 pagesbillustrationsc25 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  aDetached from Harper's new monthly magazine, no. 517, June, 1893.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM12aNeurosurgical Procedures12aVivisection22aAnimal Experimentationxethics22aBrain Diseasesxsurgery22aModels, Animal 2aCase Reports0 aHarper's new monthly magazine.  aAUTH01801cgm a22004454a 101520507DNLM20260227153220.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u091202s2009    mdu648 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15417  a(DNLM)CIT:15415  a(OCoLC)506150668  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2062 aNCI Board of Scientific Advisors.bMeetingn(44th :d2009 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNCI Board of Scientific Advisors, November 2009 /cThe National Cancer Institute (NCI) Division of Extramural Activities.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (10 hr., 48 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 2 & 3, 2009.3 a(CIT): Provides scientific advice on a wide variety of matters concerning scientific program policy, progress, and future direction of the NCI's extramural research programs, and concept review of extramural program initiatives.   For more information, visit  http://deainfo.nci.nih.gov/advisory/bsa.htm.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasms22aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1541740uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15415  aAUTH03413cgm a2200457 i 101671899DNLM20260227155213.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u151031s2015    mdu046|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19267  a(OCoLC)930496718  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWP 8701 aSharan, Shyam,eauthor.10aPARP1 loss contributes to BRCA2-induced tumorigenesis :bpart II of the BRCA2-PARP1 story /cShyam K. Sharan. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (46 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCCR grand rounds  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded October 23, 2015.3 a(CIT): Dr. Sharan obtained his B.Sc. in botany and M.Sc. ingenetics from Delhi University, Delhi. In 1994, he obtained his Ph.D. ingenetics under Dr. Terry Magnuson at Case Western Reserve University. He then joined Dr. Allan Bradley's laboratory at Baylor College of Medicine as a HowardHughes Medical Institute Associate. In 1998, Dr. Sharan established the Genetics of Cancer Susceptibility Group in the ABL-Basic Research Program. In1999, Dr. Sharan joined the Center for Cancer Research, NCI. In 2008, Dr.Sharan received the NCI Director's Intramural Innovation Award and in 2009 he received the NIH merit award and the Arthur Flemming Award for his work on functional analysis of BRCA1 and BRCA2.  Dr. Sharan is currently a Senior Investigator at the National Cancer Institute at Frederick and Head of the Genetics of Cancer Susceptibility Section. The long-term objectives of his group are to comprehensively delineate the biological function of BRCA1 and BRCA2 using knockout and transgenic mouse models. Mutations in the BRCA1 and BRCA2 genes play an important role in the development of familial breast and ovarian cancer.  He and his colleagues are engaged in generating an array of mutations identified in human breast cancer patients using humanized mouse models to understand how these mutations result in tumorigenesis. They have developed a mouse embryonic stem cell based functional assay to examine the functional consequences of variants identified in BRCA1 and BRCA2. The human mutations are generated in a bacterial artificialchromosome (BAC) containing full-length human BRCA2 genes.  His laboratory has also developed arecombineering-based technology for manipulating BACs.  They have now undertaken genetic screens toidentify genes that functionally interact with BRCA2 and contribute to tumorigenesis.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed Nov. 20, 2015).12aHereditary Breast and Ovarian Cancer Syndrome22aBRCA2 Proteinxgenetics22aMice22aPoly(ADP-ribose) Polymerase Inhibitors 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19267  aAUTH03153cgm a2200481 i 101773024DNLM20260227160259.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201105s2020    mdu546 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30314  a(DNLM)CIT:30318  a(OCoLC)1429553795  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 84.72 aNavigating Pediatric to Adult Health Care: Lost in Transition Workshopd(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNavigating pediatric to adult health care :blost in transition workshop /cNICHD, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (9 hr., 6 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 30, 2020.3 a(CIT): With improved rates of survival in childhood illnesses in the United States and globally, individuals are living longer with one or more chronic conditions. To meet the needs of diverse populations with chronic physical/medical conditions or intellectual/developmental disabilities as they transition from pediatric to adult centric services, barriers to successful health care transition (HCT), methods and measures for defining HCT, and the identification of promising practices must be better understood. In accordance with leading efforts in the transition field, the health care transition needs to take occur within a context that considers typical developmental processes of the transition to adulthood as well as the multiple domains of functioning that are critical to life success (e.g., schooling, work, housing). The purpose of this workshop is to bring together experts from various backgrounds and disciplines to explore research areas of high priority for youth regarding needed transition services/support as part of routine care. Given increasing coordination efforts on the topic of health care transition across the United States Department of Health and Human Services (HHS), the National Institutes of Health (NIH) is uniquely poised to coordinate health care transition research efforts as they relate to each Institute's, Center's, and Office's (ICO) mission and vision.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 4, 2024).12aTransition to Adult Care22aPatient Transfer22aChronic Disease22aChild22aAdolescent22aYoung Adult 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.),ehost institution.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3857040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38572  aAUTH01731cas a2200493 a 8902881DNLM20260227091014.0cr|cn ||||||||890202c19899999sz  x p       0    0eng    asn 89044012   a1011-6125l1011-6125  aSFUNE4  a(DNLM)SR0063066(s)  a(OCoLC)18665402  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Switzerland00aW1bST445K10aStereotact Funct Neurosurg2dnlm00aStereotactic and functional neurosurgery.  aBasel ;aNew York :bKarger,c[1989-  av. :bill.  aSix no. a year,b2004-  aEight no. a year (irregular),b-2003  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 a52/1/89-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv52n1, 1989-0 aPubMedbv52n1, 1989-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aOfficial journal of the World Society for Stereotactic and Functional Neurosurgery and of the American Society for Stereotactic and Functional Neurosurgery.  3Vol. 52, no. 1 (1989)c19911012fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aNeurosurgical Procedures 2aStereotaxic Techniques 2aPeriodical2 aWorld Society for Stereotactic and Functional Neurosurgery.2 aAmerican Society for Stereotactic and Functional Neurosurgery.0 x1423-037200tApplied neurophysiologyx0302-2773w(DLC)sc 78002231w(OCoLC)19029984 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/224132  aAUTH01420cas a22004694a 101142469DNLM20260227090916.0cr|cnu||||||||020409c20029999sz qr p       0    0eng    a  2005243694  a1109-3099l1109-3099  a(OCoLC)50379387  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9Switzerland00aW1bHO640 aHormones (Athens, Greece)10aHormones (Athens)2dnlm10aHormones :binternational journal of endocrinology and metabolism.  aAthens, Greece :bHellenic Endocrine Society,c[2002]-313<2018-> :a[Cham] :bSpringer International Publishing  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Jan.-Mar. 2002)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv4n1, Jan.-Mar. 2005-1 aPubMedbv4n1, Jan.-Mar. 2005-  aAlso issued online.12aEndocrine System Diseases12aHormones22aEndocrine Glands22aMetabolic Diseases 2aPeriodical2 aHellenikē Endokrinologikē Hetaireia.0 x2520-87214 uhttp://www.hormones.gr/4 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/42000  aAUTH02122cgm a2200433 a 101549221DNLM20260227153649.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101208s2010    mdu158|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16228  a(OCoLC)720559823  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.55.E71 aWertheimer, Alan.10aEthical and regulatory aspects of clinical research.pEthics of international research-2010 /cAlan Wertheimer, Seema Shah, and Joe Millum.1 aEthics of international research  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (2 hr., 38 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 27, 2010.3 a(CIT): Course Objectives: By the end of this course, participants will be able to:  Utilize a systematic framework for evaluating the ethics of a clinical research protocol.  Apply appropriate codes, regulations, and other documents governing the ethical conduct of human subject research to their own research.  Discuss controversial issues relating to human subject research, including Phase 1 research, randomization, children in research, international research, etc.  Identify the critical elements of informed consent and strategies for implementing informed consent for clinical research.  Describe the purpose, function, and challenges of IRBs.   Appreciate the experience of human subjects who have participated in research protocols.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aEthics, Research22aInternationality22aResearch Design 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16228  aAUTH01548cas a2200445 i 101631047DNLM20260227090918.0cr|cn ||||||||130606c20149999pauqr p       0    0eng    a  20132013310 a2329-4515l2329-451521  a(OCoLC)846906049  aDLCbengerdad20260227dDLCdOCLCQdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aISSN RECORD00aW1bAJ10810aAJOB Empir Bioeth2dnlm00aAJOB empirical bioethics.1 aAmerican journal of empirical bioethics 1aPhiladelphia, PA :bTaylor & Francis Group, 2014-  aFour issues per year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Volume 5, number 1 (January/March 2014).1 aMEDLINEbv8n1, 2017-0 aPubMedbv8n1, 2017- ; Selected citations only before this date  aDescription based on: Volume 5, number 1 (January/March 2014).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 5, number 2 (January/March 2014).12aEthics, Research22aBioethical Issues22aEthics, Clinical 2aPeriodical0 x2329-452300tAJOB primary researchx2150-7716w(DLC)   2009203653w(OCoLC)4326635800 tAmerican journal of bioethicsx1526-5161w(DLC)sn 99009204w(OCoLC)422793014 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/loi/uabr20  aAUTH01320cgm a22004094a 101549227DNLM20260227153804.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101208s2010   