H 14000aHematopoietic stem cell derivation from pluripotent stem cells /cGeorge Q. Daley. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2018]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 16 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNHLBI 70th anniversary lecture  aRecorded November 1, 2018.3 a(CIT): The NHLBI 70th Anniversary Lecture Series is designed to highlight not only significant advancements in the field of heart, lung, blood, and sleep research, but to also provide a visionary perspective of the promising opportunities ahead including addressing some of the most compelling questions and critical challenges elicited in the 2016 NHLBI Strategic Vision. The lectures include presentations from thought leaders in the medical field. All clinicians, trainees including fellows, medical students, Ph.D. students, healthcare and research professionals, and interested members of the public. The sixth and final lecture in the series will be presented by Dr.George Q. Daley.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed January 25, 2019).12aHematopoiesis22aBlood Platelets22aHematopoietic Stem Cells22aInduced Pluripotent Stem Cells 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aDaley, George,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNHLBI 70th anniversary lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?26168  aAUTH02860cgm a2200433 i 101706982DNLM20260227155734.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u170603s2017    mdu070|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:23249  a(OCoLC)993655083  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 33700aInteractions between memory and decision systems of the brain /cSheri J.Y. Mizumori. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2017]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 10 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience series seminar  aRecorded May 1, 2017.3 a(CIT): NIH Neuroscience Series Seminar   Understanding the mechanisms of neuroplasticity as related to learning and memory is fundamental to our understanding of the causes of a variety of cognitive disorders as well as the developmental changes in learning that occur across the lifespan. Dr. Mizumori's laboratory attempts to address these issues by using a rodent model of spatial navigation to investigate the dynamic responses of single neurons in the brain, as well as the complex interactions between cells located in different memory-related structures. These studies employ techniques involving recording extracellular signals from many cells simultaneously as animals perform memory tasks. Their studies have examined many processes that contribute to successful and adaptive navigation: 1) the evaluation of sensory input during active locomotion, 2) the integration of current sensory information with past knowledge about an environment, and with internal state information, and 3) the behavioral implementation of highly processed spatial information. More recently, they have been considering these processes in terms of the flexibility of underlying neural representational systems during shifts in cognitive strategy or task demands. They are particularly interested in the role of neuromodulators such as dopamine in flexible memory function.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 5, 2017).12aMemoryxphysiology22aCognition Disordersxetiology22aNeuronal Plasticityxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aMizumori, Sheri Jane,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23249  aAUTH01647cas a2200457 i 101684308DNLM20260227090910.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||160601c20169999ne || poo     |    0eng    a  2018243186  a2467-981Xl2467-981X  a(OCoLC)960107781  aUX1bengerdad20260227cUX1dVT2dOCLCQdOCLCFdUABdU3WdLNDdCASUMdDNLM  apcc  9Netherlands00aW110aClin Neurophysiol Pract2dnlm 0aClinical neurophysiology practice00aClinical neurophysiology practice.1 aCNP 1aAmsterdam :bElsevier,c[2016]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1 (2016).0 aPubMedbv1, 2016-0 aPMC  aArticles continuously published and added to annual volume.  aDescription based on Volume 1 (2016); title from home page (publisher's Web site, viewed June 14, 2018).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 3 (2018) (viewed June 14, 2018).12aNeurophysiology22aNeurology 2aPeriodical2 aInternational Federation of Clinical Neurophysiology,eissuing body.0 tClinical neurophysiologyx1388-2457w(DLC)sn 99038017w(OCoLC)4061848740uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/clinical-neurophysiology-practice40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101684308  aAUTH01409cas a2200445   0007616DNLM20260227090924.0cr|cn ||||||||680608c19259999pau x p       0||| 0eng    a   97669000   a0013-872Xl0013-872X  aETMNA6  a(DNLM)E08360000(s)  a(OCoLC)02044369  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bEN923R10aEntomol News2dnlm00aEntomological news.  aLancaster, Pa.,bAmerican Entomological Society.3 aPhiladelphia Pa :bAmerican Entomological Society  av.billus.  aFive no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 36-   [Jan./Feb.?] 1925-2 aMEDLINEbv78n5, May 1967-v90n2, Apr. 19790 aPubMedbv78n5, May 1967-v90n2, Apr. 19790 aOLDMEDLINE2 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online. 2aEntomology 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Entomological Society.0 x2162-323600tEntomological news and proceedings of the Entomological Section of the Academy of Natural Sciences of Philadelphiaw(DLC)   14022807w(OCoLC)15680074 uhttp://www.bioone.org/loi/entn  aAUTH01477cgm a22004094a 101267545DNLM20260227145234.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu053 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10348  a(OCoLC)70256477  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 30500aCongressional staff briefing on anthrax discovery on Capitol Hill from HHS Humphrey building great hall /cU.S. Department of Health and Human Services.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed June 25, 2006).  aStreaming video (53 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 18, 2001.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aAnthrax22aBacillus anthracis22aBioterrorism22aOccupational Exposure 2aDistrict of Columbia 2aWebcast2 aUnited States.bDepartment of Health and Human Services.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10348  aAUTH03680cgm a22005054a 101518950DNLM20260227153139.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u091105s2009    mdu086|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15329  a(OCoLC)529281650  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 84 AA100aBehavioral economics and health /cSendhil Mullainathan ... [et al.].  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBehavioral and social sciences research lecture series  aTitle from screen banner.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 26 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 23, 2009.3 a(CIT): The National Institutes of Health (NIH) Office of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research (OBSSR) will present a seminar with national experts from the ideas42 research group on behavioral economics and health.    Guest Speakers: .Sendhil Mullainathan, Professor of Economics, Harvard University .Eldar Shafir, Professor of Psychology and Public Affairs, Princeton University .Katherine Baicker, Professor of Health Economics, Harvard University .William Congdon, Research Director, Brookings Institution .Gregory Mills, Senior Research Director, Harvard University .Lisa Gennetian, Senior Research Director, Brookings Institution  Programs that address critical health and social problems have too often been met with limited success. One possible reason for this is that many programs are built on incomplete assumptions about human behavior.  ideas42 is a research organization that draws on behavioral economics to infuse health and social policy efforts - from health insurance expansion to poverty alleviation - with a richer model of human behavior. Emerging science in the field of decision-making and cognitive psychology leads to new insights into the sources and nature of human behavior, as well as to new approaches and tools for addressing them. With a more nuanced understanding of why and how people make decisions, policies can improve people"s lives not simply by mandating, subsidizing, or penalizing, but also by recognizing and responding to the role of human frailties. For example, programs and policies employing these models can make it easier for people to follow their doctor's advice, plan for retirement or search for a better job.  We will describe a behavioral economics approach to human behavior with a focus on health.  Our strategy combines research (infusion of new ideas, testing, and evaluation) with action (program implementation, service delivery, attention to process and on-the ground details) to design effective solutions to our most pressing health and social problems.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aChoice Behavior12aHealth Servicesxstatistics & numerical data22aHealth Servicesxeconomics22aInsurance, Healthxeconomics22aInsurance, Healthxstatistics & numerical data22aMedical Assistancexeconomics22aMedical Assistancexstatistics & numerical data 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aMullainathan, Sendhil.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aBSSR lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15329  aAUTH01426cas a2200457 a 101214977DNLM20260227090925.0m#####o##d########cr cnu||||||||040303c20049999gw uu pss     0   a0eng    a  20042121680 a1548-923Xl1548-923X21  a(OCoLC)54522444  aNSDcNSDdDNLM  ansdpalcdanlmcopyc  9Germany00aW1bIN77S10a610.7321310aInt J Nurs Educ Scholarsh2dnlm0 aInt. j nurs. educ. scholarsh. 0aInternational journal of nursing education scholarship00aInternational journal of nursing education scholarship.13aIJNES  aBerkeley, CA :bBerkeley Electronic Press,c2004-3 3<2012-> :aBerlin :bWalter de Gruyter  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, issue 1 (2004)-  aTitle from PDF caption (viewed Apr. 29, 2004).1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, 2004-0 aPubMedbv1n1, 2004-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued in print.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.12aEducation, Nursing 2aPeriodical0 x2194-577240uhttp://www.degruyter.com/view/j/ijnes?recentIssue=true  aAUTH01719cgm a2200421 a 101156834DNLM20260227144329.0vf|cbahoucr|cna||||||||020913s2002    dcu120       f    vleng    a(OCoLC)49854506  aMRHcMRHdDNLM  anlmcopyc  9United States00aWY 108bVC no.3 20022 aNursing Recognition Day Conferencen(11th :d2002)14aThe role of nursing and public health during times of war and terrorism /c11th Annual Nursing Recognition Day Conference ; [presented by] CMS, Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services [and] United States Department of Health and Human Services, US Public Health Service. 