n.  aPresented Sept. 24, 2003.  aOrganisms use a variety of mechanisms to control duplication and segregation of chromosomes to progeny. In addition, there are regulatory mechanisms that alter gene expression and cell cycle progression in response to perturbations in DNA replication. Focuses on mechanisms used by bacteria to replicate and segregate chromosomal DNA, and to respond to perturbations in regulation.  aAlso available online.  aVHS.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aGene Expression Regulation, Bacterial22aBacillus subtilisxgenetics22aChromosome Segregation22aDNA Replication1 aGrossman, Alan Davis.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=2581  aAUTH01127cam a22003257i 101688004DNLM20260210162737.0761129s1897    gw af      b  000 0 ger    a(OCoLC)2584325  aUUMbengerdad20260210dOCLCQdOCLCGdOCLCQdGBVCPdOCLCQdOCLCOdDNLM0 ageragrc  9Germany 4aAS182b.G812 v.2, no.100aW6bP3 v.7481 box 2141 no.3  a252ssgn1 aWellmann, Max,d1863-1933eauthor.10aKrateuas /cvon M. Wellmann. 1aBerlin :bWeidmannsche Buchhandlung,c1897.  a32 pages, 4 leaves of plates :billustrations ;c28 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"Abhandlungen der Königlichen Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften zu Göttingen. Philologisch-historische Klasse. n.F., Bd. 2, Nro. 1."  aIncludes bibliographical references.02aCrateuas.12aPlants, Medicinal12aPhytotherapy22aManuscripts, Medical as Topic  aAUTH01372cas a2200457   0144731DNLM20260227090959.0cr|cn ||||||||680703c19689999sz br         0    0eng    a0009-3157l0009-3157  aCHTHBK  a(DNLM)C16840000(s)  a(OCoLC)01554049  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Switzerland00aW1bCH39910aChemotherapy2dnlm00aChemotherapy.  