in.  aVHS.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aDNA, Bacterialximmunology12aCpG Islandsximmunology22aOligodeoxyribonucleotidesximmunology22aAdjuvants, Immunologic22aVaccines, DNA1 aKrieg, Arthur M.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=2628  aAUTH02344cgm a22004694a 101267334DNLM20260227145019.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu811 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:9926  a(DNLM)CIT:9927  a(OCoLC)191751195  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQT 3700aBECON 2001, reparative medicine :bgrowing tissues and organs  /csponsored by the NIH Bioengineering Consortium (BECON).30aReparative medicine30aGrowing tissues and organs  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 15, 2008).  aStreaming video (13 hr., 31 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 25-26, 2001.3 a(CIT): The topics to be covered will encompass the use of components of the body, such as genes, proteins, and cells, to either foster tissue regeneration and remodeling in vivo for the purpose of repairing, replacing, maintaining, or enhancing organ function, or to engineer functional tissues in vitro for implantation in vivo as a biological substitute for damaged or diseased tissues and organs.   The NIH BECON symposium seeks to achieve a better understanding of this exciting field, communicate new developments, and explore future research possibilities to ensure that the NIH is poised to facilitate biomedical research incorporating reparative medicine concepts and tools.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Governm