tion  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (winter 1983)-  aPrint issues ceased with v.25, no.4(2008)1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, winter 1983-0 aPubMedbv1n1, winter 1983-1 aHospital literature indexbv1n1, winter 1983-19941 aHospital and health administration indexb1995-1999  aAlso issued online 2aAdministrative Personnelxeducation 2aPublic Health Administration 2aPeriodical2 aAssociation of University Programs in Health Administration.0 x2158-823600tProgram notes (Association of University Programs)x0098-1559w(OCoLC)013881614 uhttps://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/aupha/jhae  aAUTH00898cam a22002531  45000010009000000030005000090050017000140080041000310350020000720400021000920440016001130600023001290600023001521000042001752450129002172600021003463000020003673360026003873370028004133380027004415010129004686000038005979990009006352455031RDNLM20260219150641.0840721s1758    sz            000|0 fred   a(OCoLC)14331680  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Switzerland00aWZ 260bB779e 175800aWZ 260bS927e 17721 aBouvart, Michel-Philippe,d1711-1787.10aExamen d'un livre qui a pour titre, T. Tronchin ... De colica pictonum,cpar un médecin de Paris [i. e. M. P. Bouvart] ...  aGenève,c1758.  a68 p. ;c19 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aNLM copy 2, bound with Struve, O. W.  Essais ou réflexions intéressantes rélatives a la chymie.  Lausanne, 1772.5DNLM12aTronchin, Théodore,d1709-1781.  aAUTH03024cgm a2200469 i 9918351573706676DNLM20260227161834.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220226s2022    mdu110|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32122  a(OCoLC)1342098784  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 50600aDemystifying medicine.pCOVID :bthen, now, and the future /cAnthony Fauci. The global challenge : COVID and future pandemics / Roger Glass.1 aCOVID1 aGlobal challenge :bCOVID and future pandemics 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 50 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded January 4, 2022.3 a(CIT): 2022 Demystifying Medicine Lecture SeriesDr. Fauci, a stranger to no one at the NIH, has been a public face of the COVID pandemic response since January 2020.  He will share his keen insights on COVID, including the newly emerged omicron strain, in a lecture titled "COVID: Then, Now, and the Future."  Dr. Glass has helped to guide the U.S. international response to the COVID pandemic.  This has included spurring international cooperation in vaccine development and distribution, as well as tracing the epidemiology and transmission dynamics of the pandemic on a global scale.  His Demystifying Medicine lecture is titled "The Global Challenge: COVID and Future Pandemics."  Jointly sponsored by FAES and NIH, the Demystifying Medicine course aims to bridge exciting developments in medicine with advances in the basic biological and engineering sciences. When possible, a live patient introduces the course topic by discussing their disease, followed by a physician scientist who describes clinical aspects of the disease including therapy and challenges, followed by bench scientist who describes what we know and don"t know regarding mechanism. The course is designed to excite as well as inform medical and Ph.D. students, fellows, basic and physician scientists, and program planners.For more information go to https://demystifyingmedicine.od.nih.gov.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 1, 2022).12aCOVID-1922aPandemics22aBiomedical Research 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aFauci, Anthony S.,d1940-espeaker.1 aGlass, Roger I.,espeaker.12aFauci, Anthony S.,d1940-tCOVID.12aGlass, Roger I.tGlobal challenge.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44341  aAUTH01233cam a2200349 i 60740300RDNLM20260206222150.0cr||||||||||||820626s1904    nyua          001 0 eng    a(OCoLC)14796153  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aQDbR389i 19041 aRemsen, Ira,d1846-1927,eauthor.10aInorganic chemistry.  aFifth edition, revised. 1aNew York :bHenry Holt and Company,c1904.  axxi, 853 pages :billustrations ;c22 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aAmerican science series, advanced course  aIncludes index.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc202506202pda5DNLM  aConservedc202506232pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aInorganic Chemicals 0aAmerican science series.pAdvanced course4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/60740300R  aAUTH01560cgm a22004694a 101488630DNLM20260227152619.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081104s2008    mdu059 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14697  a(OCoLC)288982971  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWG 1701 aLange, Richard A.10aCoronary revascularization :bcontroversies and concepts /cRichard A. Lange.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aGreat teachers  aTitle from title screen (viewed Nov. 21, 2008).  aStreaming video (58 min., 16 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 8, 2008.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aCoronary Artery Diseasexsurgery12aMyocardial Revascularization22aCoronary Artery Diseasexprevention & control22aCoronary Stenosisxprevention & control22aCoronary Stenosisxsurgery22aTreatment Outcome 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aClinical Center grand rounds great teachers series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14697  aAUTH03519cgm a2200481 i 101629953DNLM20260227154637.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140408s2014    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18313  a(OCoLC)879769229  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.51 aWeng, Chunhua,eauthor.10aBridging the semantic gap between clinical research eligibility criteria and clinical data /cChunhua Weng. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 0 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNLM informatics lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded March 5, 2014.3 a(CIT): With the burgeoning adoption of electronic health records (EHRs), vast amounts of clinical data are increasingly available for computational reuse.  It is imperative that the scientific community leverage Big Data to accelerate clinical and translational science at low cost and large scale.  A critical step toward this goal is matching clinical research eligibility criteria to clinical data for cohort identification.  However, this task is complicated by the semantic gap between free-text eligibility criteria and raw clinical data: each criterion has many ways to describe it and a myriad of clinical data points that represent it.  In fact, the semantic gap is a significant multifactorial problem because of the central role that clinical research eligibility criteria play in clinical and translational research. In a typical study, they undergo a complex evolution: perceived, defined, interpreted, implemented, and adapted by various stakeholders for a series of clinical research tasks.  During the design phase, investigators choose eligibility criteria to define a study"s target population. During screening and recruitment, the criteria are used and interpreted by clinical research coordinators, query analysts, and even research volunteers themselves, each possessing different decision support needs for using the criteria.  Later, they are summarized in meta-analyses for developing clinical practice guidelines and, eventually, interpreted by physicians to screen patients for evidence-based care.  At each step, their intended meanings can be misinterpreted, as in the game of "telephone".  In this lecture, Dr. Weng will describe the ongoing efforts to bridge this semantic gap from multiple angles and the value of using computable clinical research eligibility criteria to understand clinical trial design patterns and their impact on the semantic gap.  aDescription based on version viewed May 6, 2014.12aBiomedical Research22aClinical Trials as Topic22aEligibility Determination22aMedical Informatics Applications22aNatural Language Processing22aSemantics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNLM informatics lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18313  aAUTH01252cam a2200349 i 02340310RDNLM20260206220336.0cr||||||||||||810622s1912    nyu   |       000 0 eng    a   12007677   a(OCoLC)15703455  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aHQ 31bR666s 19121 aRobinson, William J.q(William Josephus),d1867-1936,eauthor.10aSexual problems of to-day /cby William J. Robinson. 1aNew York :bThe Critic and Guide Company,c1912.  a285 pages ;c21 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc202506252pda5DNLM  aConservedc202506272pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNL