f London. Zoology, ser. 2, v. 5, pt. 10.  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aCrustacea 2aIndia2 aLinnean Society of London.  aAUTH01840cgm a22004454a 101507201DNLM20260227144835.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090605s2009    mdu070 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15101  a(OCoLC)1145306242  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4751 aTjian, Robert.10a2009 NIH Asian/Pacific Islander heritage month observance /cRobert Tjian.1 iTitle on title screen:aTranscriptional mechanisms regulating differentiation and stem cell pluripotency  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 22, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 9 min., 55 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 14, 2009.3 a(CIT): The theme of this year's observance is "Leadership in Science- Meeting the Challenges of a Changing World." The keynote speaker is Dr. Robert Tjian, a renown professor of molecular and cell biology, director of Li Ka-Sing Center for Biomedical and Health Science at the University of California, Berkeley, and the new President of the Howard Hughes Medical Institute.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aTranscription Factors, General22aCell Differentiation22aMuscle Development22aPluripotent Stem Cells 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=7621  aAUTH03270cgm a2200421 i 101629972DNLM20260227154610.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140408s2014    mdu062|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18346  a(OCoLC)878098778  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 83.11 aSpring, Bonnie J.eauthor.10aNew technologies and behavior change interventions :bwhat can they do for each other? /cBonnie Spring. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBSSR lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Mar. 24, 2014.3 a(CIT): BSSR Lecture Series  New mHealth technologies are proliferating at an astonishing rate, with many new tools being harnessed for behavior change interventions.  Because they are inexpensive, transportable, and have broad (and growing) penetration, mHealth tools (e.g., smartphone apps, text messaging, accelerometers/pedometers) have the potential to overcome access barriers and extend the reach of behavioral interventions to the large population that may benefit.   The co-emergence of new mHealth tools and new, efficient methodologies for intervention development creates an opportunity to design behavioral interventions more systematically than the traditional "kitchen sink" approach.  The presenter will illustrate via a series of studies of behavioral interventions designed to produce positive diet and activity changes and/or weight loss by fostering self-monitoring, a robust mediator of behavior change.  The results highlight the need for a clear theoretical conceptualization of how to align mHealth self-monitoring tools with established effective intervention components, such as social support and financial incentives.  Developments in sensor technology will soon afford access to continuous, passively acquired, accurate information about behavioral parameters including dietary intake and physical activity.  The field urgently needs a theoretical model to characterize mechanisms whereby self-monitoring performed via these new technological capabilities can be expected to influence behavior.  A key question will be whether new automated self-monitoring capabilities  support maintenance of healthy lifestyle change by making self-regulation less burdensome, or whether they thwart maintenance by eliminating the need to exercise effortful cognitive processes that are integral to self-regulation.  aDescription based on version viewed April 22, 2014.12aTelemedicine22aHealth Behavior 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research,esponsoring body. 0aBSSR lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18346  aAUTH01536cas a2200433 i 101715582DNLM20260227090922.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||171026c20179999sz || poo     |    0eng    a  2018243189  a2509-3304l2509-3304  a(OCoLC)1012495172  aAU@bengerdad20260227cAU@dOCLCOdOCLCFdCGUdDNLM  apcc  9Switzerland00aW110aJ Cogn Enhanc2dnlm00aJournal of cognitive enhancement :btowards the integration of theory and practice. 1aBasel, Switzerland :bSpringer International Publishing,c[2017]-  a1 online resource  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1, issue 1 (March 2017)  a"Towards the integration of theory and practice."0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued in print.  aDescription based on Volume 1, issue 1 (March 2017); title from contents screen and cover thumbnail image (viewed SpringerLink 22 March 2017).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 2, issue 1 (March 2018) (viewed June 18, 2018).12aCognition22aBiomedical Enhancement22aNootropic Agents 2aPeriodical0 x2509-329040uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/41465  aAUTH01637cas a2200457 a 8200911DNLM20260227090956.0cr|cn#||||||||811223c19819999njumr p       0    0eng    a0279-3695l0279-3695y0360-5973  aJPNSDM  a(DNLM)J36530000(s)  a(OCoLC)07816794  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aeng  9United States00aW1bJO858V10aJ Psychosoc Nurs Ment Health Serv2dnlm 0aJournal of psychosocial nursing and mental health services00aJournal of psychosocial nursing and mental health services.  a[Thorofare, N.J. :bC.B. Slack,cc1981-  av. :bill.  aMonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 19, no. 8 (Aug. 1981)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv19n8,Aug. 1981-0 aPubMedbv19n8,Aug. 1981-1 aInternational nursing indexbv19n8,Aug. 1981-v38n5,May 20001 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3Vol. 33, no. 3 (MAR 1995)c19950522fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paperxFurther review is required to determine exactly when acid-free paper was first used for this title 2aMental Health Services 2aPsychiatric Nursing 2aPeriodical00tJournal of psychiatric nursing and mental health servicesx0360-5973w(OCoLC)017760484 uhttps://journals.healio.com/journal/jpn  aAUTH02753cgm a2200457 i 101744889DNLM20260227155905.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190402s2019    mdu050|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27402  a(OCoLC)1099669224  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWV 23200aHow to bust up a bacterial biofilm /cLauren O. Bakaletz. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (50 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded March 27, 2019.3 a(CIT): The Bakaletz laboratory's research focus is attempting to understand the pathogenic mechanisms operational in the highly prevalent pediatric disease, otitis media (OM) (or middle ear infection). Specifically, we are interested in elucidating how upper respiratory tract viruses predispose the middle ear to invasion by any of the three predominant bacterial pathogens of OM (nontypeable Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis and Streptococcus pneumoniae). We are also interested in understanding how bacterial biofilms contribute to the recurrence and chronicity of OM, chronic rhinosinusitis, bronchitis, chronic cough and exacerbations of both COPD and CF. With this enhanced understanding, we then attempt to identify potential molecular targets for the design, development and pre-clinical testing of novel vaccine candidates or therapeutics using an animal model of viral-bacterial superinfection that closely mimics the polymicrobial nature of OM in children. Our long-term goal is to develop novel methods to more effectively treat or preferably, to prevent, otitis media and other respiratory tract infections where there is a biofilm component to the disease course.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 26, 2019).12aOtitis Mediaxdrug therapy12aOtitis Mediaxmicrobiology22aAnti-Bacterial Agentsxpharmacology22aBacterial Physiological Phenomena22aBiofilms 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aBakaletz, Lauren O.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27402  aAUTH01093cas a22003611  2985082RDNLM20260227090923.0cr|cnu||||||||850128c19649999nyuqr p       0    0eng    a0022-2496l0022-2496  aJMTPAJ  a(DNLM)J28360000(s)  a(OCoLC)01783082  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bJO748T10aJ Math Psychol2dnlm00aJournal of mathematical psychology.  aNew York.3 aNew York, NY :bAcademic Press  av.bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-    Feb. 1964-0 aPubMedbv40n3, Sep 1996-v45n5, Oct 2001; selected citations only after this date  aAvailable also online.  aPsychology, Experimental / period.  aPsychometrics / period.4 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/journal-of-mathematical-psychology  aAUTH01639cas a2200505 a 8914819DNLM20260227091028.0cr||||||||||||890831c19809999nyu x p       0    0eng    a   90657708 zsn 88002627  a1040-8401l1040-8401  aCCRIDE