xphysiology22aTime Factors 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13274  aAUTH01852cas a2200529   7810364DNLM20260227091003.0cr||||||||||||781214c19639999mauqr p       0    0eng    a   71001187   a0039-3665l0039-3665  aSFPLA3  a(DNLM)S44160000(s)  a(OCoLC)01651215  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bST919P10aStud Fam Plann2dnlm 0aStudies in family planning00aStudies in family planning.  aNew York,bPopulation Council.31aMalden, MA :bJohn Wiley & Sons Ltd  av.bill.  aQuarterly,b1997-  aMonthly,bJuly 1963-  aMonthly (with combined June/July, Aug./Sept. issues)  aBimonthly,b<Jan./Feb. 1984- >-1996  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. [1]-    July 1963-  aCumulative indexes for 1963/72-1973/75 issued with title: Population Council periodical index.1 aMEDLINEbv60,Dec. 1970-1 aPubMedbv60,Dec. 1970-1 aIndex medicus  aIssues for 1963-70 lack vol. numbering but constitute v. 1.  aSupplements accompany some numbers.  aAlso issued online.  3Vol. 11, no. 1 (1980)c19900914fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paperxFurther review is required to determine exactly when acid-free paper was first used for this title 2aFamily Planning Services 2aPopulation 2aPeriodical2 aPopulation Council.0 x1728-44654 uhttp://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/journal/10.1111/(ISSN)1728-4465  aAUTH02134cgm a2200409 i 101770485DNLM20260227160204.0m     o  z f      vz|czau|ucr|cna||||||||200812s2019    mdu172 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35286  a(OCoLC)1196163946  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.55.T72 aNCATS Day (Conference)d(2019 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aNCATS Day 2019 :bconversations on responsible data sharing /cNational Center for Advancing Translational Sciences, NIH.1 aConversations on responsible data sharing 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hrs. 52 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded December 17, 2019.3 a(CIT): This event provides a forum for sharing and reflecting upon patient and community needs — as well as best practices and perspectives in addressing those needs. The full-day event will feature presentations, interactive discussion and activities, fostering an open dialogue for diverse participants to discuss common roadblocks to translational science and brainstorm ideas for improvement. Patients and patient advocates, caregivers, community partners, community health engagement professionals, clinical and translational researchers, NCATS staff and others are encouraged to attend.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed September 10, 2020).12aTranslational Research, Biomedical22aInformation Dissemination 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=35286  aAUTH01681cgm a2200457 a 101559815DNLM20260227154025.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110503s2011    mdu058 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16554  a(OCoLC)729826536  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWG 1661 aSimons-Morton, Denise.10aMain results of the Action to Control Cardiovascular Disease (ACCORD) trial in type 2 diabetes /cDenise Simons-Morton.  Results of ACCORD medical therapies on diabetic retinopathy / Emily Y. Chew.1 aResults of ACCORD medical therapies on diabetic retinopathy  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (58 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCC grand rounds  aTitle from title screen (viewed May 27, 2011).  aRecorded Mar. 30, 2011.  aOpen-captioned.12aCardiovascular Diseasesxdrug therapy22aCardiovascular Diseasesxcomplications22aClinical Trials as Topic22aDiabetes Mellitus, Type 2xcomplications22aDiabetic Retinopathyxcomplications 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16554  aAUTH02984cgm a2200445 i 101684390DNLM20260227155340.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160601s2016    mdu069|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19659  a(OCoLC)951829985  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 425.5.R300aChange your brain by transforming your mind /cRichard J. Davidson. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 9 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aStephen E. Straus distinguished lecture in the science of complementary health therapies  aRecorded May 3, 2016.3 a(CIT): In this talk, Dr. Davidson will present an overview of studies conducted in his laboratory on neural changes associated with different forms of meditation. Distinctions among three major forms of meditation practice will be made: Focused Attention, Open Monitoring, and Positive Affect Training. These different forms of meditation have different neural and behavioral effects. From the perspective of Western neuroscience, different forms of meditation can be conceptualized as mental training to promote the regulation of emotion and attention. Data from studies on long-term meditation practitioners as well as those with shorter durations of training will be highlighted. Additionally, some longitudinal studies that track changes over time with meditation practice will be reviewed. In addition to the neural changes that have been observed, this talk will also summarize changes that have been found in peripheral biology that may modulate physical health and illness. The central brain circuitry of emotion is especially implicated in peripheral biological changes that have consequences for health. The overall conclusions from these studies is that one can transform the mind through meditation and thereby alter the brain and the periphery in ways that may be beneficial for mental and physical health, and for well-being.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed June 13, 2016).12aMeditationxpsychology22aBrainxphysiology22aNeuronal Plasticity22aStress, Psychologicalxtherapy 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aDavidson, Richard J.,espeaker.2 aNational Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aStephen E. Straus distinguished lecture in the science of complementary health therapies40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19659  aAUTH01651cam a2200385 i 14130200RDNLM20260206222558.0cr||||||||||||810815s1920    wiuaf         000 0 eng    a   21000261   a(OCoLC)6468178  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aUH 390bB711m 19201 aBond, P. S.q(Paul Stanley),d1879-1955eauthor.10aMedical service in modern war :ban exposition of the tactical functions of the Medical Department in campaign /cby P. S. Bond and C. F. Martin ; with introduction by Major General M. W. Ireland. 1aMenasha, Wisconsin :bThe Collegiate Press, George Banta Publishing Company,c[1920]  a76 pages, xiv pages of plates (1 folded) :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc202504112pda5DNLM  aWill con