ch in clinical oncology 2017 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2017]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 50 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 25, 2017.3 a(CIT): Translational Research in Clinical Oncology (TRACO)  Recent advances in understanding cancer biology are beginning to be translated into improvements in diagnosis and treatment of cancer. In the post-genome era, we increasingly rely on strong collaboration between basic and clinical scientists to develop novel approaches for treatment of human disease. The NCI Center for Cancer Research (CCR) is one of the largest cancer research organizations in the world, with more than 200 principal investigators, and has played a major role in developing and implementing many new technologies, such as nanotechnology, next generation sequencing, genomics and proteomics.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (October 16, 2017).12aOvarian Neoplasmsxgenetics12aOvarian Neoplasmsximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aAnnunziata, C.q(Christina),espeaker.1 aGoff, S.q(Stephanie),espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23483  aAUTH01185cas a2200397 a 9301155DNLM20260227091028.0cr|cn#||||||||921231c19929999no  x p       0||| 0eng    a0803-706Xl0803-706X  aIFOPE  a(DNLM)SR0073670(s)  a(OCoLC)27195919  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Norway00aW1bIN7268M00aInternational forum of psychoanalysis.13aIFP  aOslo :bScandinavian University Press,cc1992-3 aOslo :bScandinavian University Press  av.  aFour no. a yearK  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (June 1992)-  aQuarterly.  aAlso issued online.  aPublished under the auspices of the Inter