acontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCI Center for Cancer Research grand rounds  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded January 8, 2016.3 a(CIT): NCI Center for Cancer Research Grand Rounds   Jonathan D. Licht, MD, is the Director of the University of Florida Health Cancer Center, holding the Marshall E. Rinker, Sr. Foundation and David B. and Leighan R. Rinker Chair.  Previously Dr. Licht was Chief of Hematology/Oncology at Northwestern University and an Associate Director of the Robert H. Lurie Comprehensive Cancer Center.   Dr. Licht's laboratory studies aberrant gene regulation, specifically the role of abnormal function of histone methyl transferases and histone demethyalses in diseases such as multiple myeloma and is developing small molecule strategies to normalize gene regulation and treat disease.   NCI funded for nearly 25 years, Dr. Licht is also Principal Investigator of a Leukemia and Lymphoma Society Specialized Center and a Multiple Myeloma Research Foundation program grant both in epigenetics.  He is an investigator in the Dana-Farber SPORE in myeloma and co-leader in a NCI-funded Chicago Physical Sciences Oncology Center.  Dr. Licht is a Senior Editor of Clinical Cancer Research and serves on the editorial boards of Oncogene and Cancer Cell. Dr. Licht is a member of the executive committee of the American Society of Hematology, and will be co-director of the 2017 ASH/EHA Translational Research Training program.  Additionally he sits on the Medical/Scientific Board of the LLS and is a member of the Basic Mechanisms of Cancer Therapeutics Study Section.  Dr. Licht has published nearly 170 original articles, reviews and book chapters and his work has been cited over 11,000 times. Dr. Licht has mentored over 40 graduate students and postdoctoral fellows and 20 faculty members.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 19, 2016).12aLeukemiaxphysiopathology22aHistone Demethylasesxphysiology22aHistone-Lysine N-Methyltransferasexphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aLicht, Jonathan D.,espeaker.2 aCenter for Cancer Research (National Cancer Institute (U.S.)),esponsoring body. 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19420  aAUTH01646cam a2200373 i 63231130RDNLM20260227111416.0820724s1901    nyua     b    001 0 eng    a   01030847   a(OCoLC)1628942  aDNLMbengerdad20260225dDNLM  9United States00aWAAbT944p 19011 aTurneaure, F. E.q(Frederick Eugene),d1866-1951,eauthor.10aPublic water-supplies :brequirements, resources, and the construction of works /cby F. E. Turneaure, and H. L. Russell ; with a chapter on pumping-machinery by D. W. Mead.  aFirst edition, first thousand. 1aNew York :bJ. Wiley & Sons ;aLondon :bChapman & Hall,c1901.  axiv, 746 pages :billustrations ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aPublisher's advertisement on 16 pages bound at end.  aNLM microfilm copy shelved as: Film 9379 no.15DNLM  aIncludes bibliography at end of each chapter and index.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202011192pda5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20260227lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM12aSanitary Engineering22aWater Purification22aWater Supply1 aRussell, H. L.q(Harry Luman),d1866-1954,eauthor.1 aMead, Daniel W.q(Daniel Webster),d1862-1948,econtributor.  aAUTH02204cgm a22004574a 101549267DNLM20260227153738.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101208s2010    mdu063|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16283  a(OCoLC)697768565  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 1791 aJiang, Qing,d1965-10aAnti-inflammatory and anticancer activity of vitamin E forms and their metabolites /cQing Jiang.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aOCCAM monthly lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 3 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 10, 2010.3 a(CIT): The NCI's Office of Cancer Complementary and Alternative Medicine (OCCAM) invites you to view its monthly lecture series.  With the goal of informing the National Cancer Institute (NCI) community about the variety of ongoing research in cancer and complementary and alternative medicine (CAM), NCI's Office of Cancer Complementary and Alternative Medicine (OCCAM) has created a monthly lecture series on cancer CAM. These hour long lectures, occurring from 10 a.m. - 11 a.m. on the second Wednesday of each month, will feature a fifty minute presentation on a cancer CAM topic and allow ten minutes for questions.   For more information, visit  http://www.cancer.gov/cam/news_lectures.html.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aVitamin Extherapeutic use22aInflammationxdrug therapy22aNeoplasmsxdrug therapy22aVitamin Exanalogs & derivatives 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.).bOffice of Cancer Complementary and Alternative Medicine. 0aOCCAM monthly lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16283  aAUTH01537cgm a22004454a 101528397DNLM20260227153335.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100406s2010    mdu070|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15666  a(OCoLC)624405056  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWD 2101 aAllison, David B.q(David Bradley),d1963-10aBrain and obesity lecture, February 2010 /cDavid B. Allison ; National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases.10iTitle on PowerPoint:aConjectures on some curious connections among social status, hunger, fatness, and longevity  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  