edia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Vol. 7, no. 1 (Mar. 1954).  aEach volume contains a numbered "Bibliography supplement."  aAlso issued online.  aContinues Proceedings of the Zoological Society of Bengal. 2aZoology 2aPeriodical2 aZoological Society, Calcutta.0 x0974-691910tProceedings of the Zoological Society of Bengalw(DLC)sn 87022657w(OCoLC)14693114 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/12595  aAUTH00740cam a22002417i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002100073040003500094044001000129060003300139100006700172245004300239264007400282300001300356336002100369337002500390338002300415650003400438655001700472999000900489101606889DNLM20260205123723.0130425s1969    cc            000 0 chi    a(OCoLC)892042981  aDNLMbengerdad20260205dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1769 no.281 aChen, Xiuyuan,dapproximately 1753-approximately 1823eauthor.10aYi xue san zi jing /cChen Nianzu zhu. 1a[Pucheng] :bPucheng wei sheng gong zuo zhe xie hui fan yin,c[1969?]  a27 pages  atext2rdacontent  aunmediated2rdamedia  avolume2rdacarrier12aMedicine, Chinese Traditional 2aPopular Work  aAUTH03606cgm a2200409 a 101565182DNLM20260227154256.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110715s2011    mdu060 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16651  a(OCoLC)745303569  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 5001 aCristea, Ileana M.10aDynamic intricacies of viral infections :ba proteomics perspective /cIleana Cristea ; NIH Proteomics Interest Group.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded May 5, 2011.3 a(CIT): NIH Proteomics Interest Group Seminar Series    Viruses have co-evolved with their hosts, developing remarkable mechanisms for subverting and hijacking cellular processes for their own benefit. The study of virus-host interactions has therefore emerged as a driving force in infectious disease research. Despite these efforts, the protein interactome remains in large part uncharted, and our knowledge of mechanisms controlling the outcome of an infection is limited. The development of new methodologies that can define temporal and spatial host-virus protein interactions is a necessity. Modern proteomics techniques are currently emerging as powerful tools, able of bringing a new perspective to the field of virology. This presentation will describe the integration of targeted proteomic approaches with genetic, molecular biology, and bioinformatics techniques for studying dynamic virus-host protein associations. Strategies for isolating protein complexes, quantifying infection-triggered changes in interactions, and assessing specificity of associations will be presented. Highlights will be shown from our recent studies on human cytomegalovirus (HCMV) infection, including the discovery of parallel processes occurring at distinct cellular sites during the assembly of infectious virions. Additionally, we have observed that certain viral proteins recruit host proteins that elaborate the epigenetic regulation of cell. Our finding that chromatin-remodeling complexes, such as histone deacetylases, are targeted by HCMV indicates a possible mean of controlling virus or host gene expression. Aspects from our studies on several deacetylases during viral infection will be presented. In addition to revealing their functional roles during infection, these studies provided insights into the regulation of these enzymes outside the context of infection. A combinatorial proteomics approach, incorporating CID, HCD and ETD peptide fragmentation using a nLC LTQ Orbitrap Velos-ETD, identified 17 in vivo phosphorylation sites on HDAC5. Functional phosphomutant screening and live cell imaging allowed the characterization of novel sites within functional domains, and identified a previously unrecognized regulatory point of its nuclear import.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aCytomegalovirus Infectionsxgenetics22aCytomegalovirusxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bProteomics Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16651  aAUTH01693cas a2200493 a 9203244DNLM20260227091013.0cr|cnu||||||||920227c19929999sz  x p       0    0eng    a1018-8665l1018-8665  aDERAEG  a(DNLM)SR0073138(s)  a(OCoLC)25275441  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Switzerland00aW1bDE50830 aDermatology (Basel, Switzerland)10aDermatology2dnlm 0aDermatologyb(Basel)10aDermatology :binternational journal for clinical and investigative dermatology.  aBasel ; New York :bKarger,cc1992-  av. :bill.  aEight no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 a184/1/92-1 aMEDLINEbv184n1, 1992-1 aPubMedbv184n1, 1992-1 aIndex medicus  aSupplements accompany some numbers.  aAlso available online.  aOffical organ of: Schweizerische Gesellschaft für Dermatologie und Venereologie, and : Belgian Society for Dermatology and Syphiligraphy.  aContinues: Dermatologica.  3Vol. 184, no. 1 (1992)c19920218fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aSkin Diseases 2aPeriodical2 aSchweizerische Gesellschaft für Dermatologie und Venerologie.2 aSociété belge de dermatologie et de syphiligraphie.0 x1421-983210tDermatologica4 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/224164  aAUTH01400cgm a2200433 a 101585222DNLM20260227154145.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120525s2012    mdu066|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17201  a(OCoLC)795553720  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3591 aWalters, Judithq(Judith R.)10aInside the Parkinsonian brain :bis too much rhythm a bad thing? /cJudith Walters.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 6 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aAnita B. Roberts lecture  aRecorded Apr. 5, 2012.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aParkinson Diseasexphysiopathology22aBasal Gangliaxphysiopathology22aBrain Mapping22aDopaminexdeficiency 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aAnita B. Roberts lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17201  aAUTH01274nam a2200313 i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003100073044001800104060003300122100006300155245004900218264005600267300002200323336002600345337002800371338002700399500001700426500007700443540014200520541007300662583003900735583004300774650003700817700005400854730004300908999000900951101754544DNLM20260220143603.0190919r19061906wau           000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5658 no.111 aWhite, William A.q(William Alanson),d1870-1937,eauthor.10aTypes in mental disease /cWilliam A. White. 1a[Washington] :b[publisher not identified],c[1906]  a11 pages ;c25 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCover title.  a"Reprinted from the Journal of nervous and mental disease, April, 1906."  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  cGift;aAmerican Heritage Center, University of Wyoming;d1998.5DNLM  aWill digitizec202103022pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202103025DNLM12aMental Disordersxclassification1 aGlueck, Bernard,d1884-1972,eformer owner.5DNLM0 aJournal of nervous and mental disease.  aAUTH02003cgm a2200397 a 101585234DNLM20260227154233.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120525s2012    mdu111|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17219  a(OCoLC)803799309  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 33 AA100aCareers in patent processing /cCharles Salahuddin ... [et al.].  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 51 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded Apr. 17, 2012.3 a(CIT): Are you interested in using your technical understanding to communicate and protect the intellectual property of others? Join us to discover what it takes to enter the exciting and rewarding field of patent processing. This panel will include specialists working both privately and within the government and will highlight careers such as a patent agent, patent examiner, and technology transfer specialist. You will learn what steps each speaker took to enter this field, how a scientific background helped their career at various stages, and what additional training is necessary to join the field of patent processing. After brief introductions, the floor will be open for a question and answer session with the panelists. This event is sponsored by FELCOM's Career Development Subcommittee.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aCareer Choice12aPatents as Topic 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aSalahuddin, Charles.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17219  aAUTH02010cgm a22004574a 101468732DNLM20260227152358.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080307s2008    mdu049|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14307  a(OCoLC)421350238  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWR 5001 aThomas, Nancy,d1958-10aMolecular epidemiology of BRAF and NRAS mutations in melanomas /cNancy Thomas.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed June 25, 2009).  aStreaming video (49 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 19, 2008.3 a(CIT): The DNA Repair Interest Group is concerned with all forms of DNA damage and repair. As a major defense against environmental damage to cells DNA repair is present in all organisms examined including bacteria, yeast, drosophila, fish, amphibians, rodents and humans. The members of the DNA Repair Interest Group perform research in areas including DNA repair enzymology and fine structure, mutagenesis, gene and cell cycle regulation, protein structure, and human disease.   For more information, visit the  DNA Repair Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMelanomaxepidemiology12aMelanomaxgenetics12aSkin Neoplasmsxepidemiology12aSkin Neoplasmsxgenetics22aGenes, rasxgenetics22aProto-Oncogene Proteins B-rafxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bDNA Repair Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14307  aAUTH01795cgm a22004334a 101468746DNLM20260227152456.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080307s2008    mdu075|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14327  a(OCoLC)219261865  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 551 aMoon, Randall T.10aWnt signaling in regeneration and regenerative medicine /cRandall T. Moon.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Mar. 29, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 15 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 27, 2008.3 a(CIT): The Stem Cell Interest Group was established to enhance communication and to foster collaboration among scientists from varying disciplines interested in stem cells. Topics of interest include fundamental stem cell biology, ontogeny, gerontology, and the therapeutic potential of stem cells. The SCIG serves as an open forum for discussion and dissemination of knowledge about all aspects of stem cell biology.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aWnt Proteinsxgenetics22aGuided Tissue Regenerationxmethods22aRegenerative Medicine22aSignal Transduction 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bStem Cell Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14327  aAUTH01699nam a2200421 i 9918786988706676DNLM20260214103358.0220328s2021    cc ac         000 0 tibo   a  2020513428  a1313799428  a9787105165025q(paperback)  a7105165022q(paperback)  aCh-Tib-2020513428 ; 21  a(OCoLC)1313444030z(OCoLC)1313799428  aDLCbengerdacDLCdOCLCFdVA@dOCLCOdIULdCLU  alcodeapcc  aa-cc-ti  9China00aR603.T5bT475 202100aWB 55.T50 aTing-ʼdzin,cSman-pa,eauthor.10aBod lugs gso rig dpal ldan rgyud bzhiʼi dpyad gtam dang mtshon rmaʼi dmar khrid dpe ris lung rigs gʹyu yi thang ma zhes bya ba bzhugs so /cMgo-log dbang-chen-ʼbum gyi sman-pa, Ting-ʼdzin gyis brtsams.1 iChinese colophon title:aZang yi wai shang shi jian ji neng quan shi  aPar gzhi dang po bsgrigs. 1aPe-cin :bMi-rigs Dpe-skrun-khang,c2021 loʼi zla 9.  a6, 470 pages :bcolor illustrations, portrait ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aZhong guo zang xue yan jiu zhong xin bei jing zang yi yuan"xin nao xue guan lin chuang he min zu yi ji yi fa chuan cheng ji xin yao yan jiu"xiang mu cheng guo.  aWound healing and plaster bandage in Tibetan medicine.  aIn Tibetan.12aMedicine, Tibetan Traditionalxmethods22aWound Healing22aCasts, Surgical2 aMin zu chu ban she,epublisher.  aAUTH02900cgm a2200517 i 9918299484606676DNLM20260227161652.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u211110s2021    mdu454|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31992  a(DNLM)CIT:31994  a(OCoLC)1333918009  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 212 aNIH MOSAIC Scholars Annual Meetingn(1st :d2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNIH MOSAIC Scholars Annual Meeting /cNIGMS, NIH.1 aNIH MOSAIC Scholars Annual Meeting 2021 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (7 hr., 34 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 14 & 15, 2021.3 a(CIT): This is the first conference of scholars funded through the Maximizing Opportunities for Scientific and Academic Independent Careers (MOSAIC) program to promote faculty diversity. The meeting will feature: Remarks from NIGMS director Dr. Jon Lorsch and NIH leadership, including NIH Director Dr. Francis S. Collins and NIH Chief Officer for Scientific Workforce Diversity Dr. Marie Bernard Scientific sessions by MOSAIC scholars presenting an overview of their research programs, chaired by NIH institute directors: Dr. Eliseo Perez-Stable, National Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities Dr. Nora Volkow, National Institute on Drug Abuse Dr. Richard Hodes, National Institute on Aging Dr. Debara Tucci, National Institute on Deafness and Other Communication Disorders NIH staff panels highlighting programs for early career investigators at academic institutions and within the NIH Intramural Research Program.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed June 16, 2022).22aMaximizing Opportunities for Scientific and Academic Independent Careers (Program)12aVocational Guidance12aBiomedical Research22aHealth Workforcexstandards22aCultural Diversity22aCareer Choice22aGovernment Programs 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aNational Institute of General Medical Sciences (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aMaximizing Opportunities for Scientific and Academic Independent Careers (Program),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4386040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=43871  aAUTH02413cgm a22004454a 101528429DNLM20260227153311.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100406s2010    mdu069|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15708  a(OCoLC)648077697  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3591 aHolzbaur, Erika.10aMicrotubule motors and axonal transport :bfunction and dysfunction in neurodegenerative disease /cErika Holzbaur.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 9 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 15, 2010.3 a(CIT): Dr. Holzbaur is interested in the mechanisms of microtubule-based motility. This motility is involved in basic cellular processes such as organelle trafficking, and in more specialized functions such as the transport of vesicles along the extended axonal processes of the neuron. Her laboratory focuses on the motor cytoplasmic dynein and its activator complex dynactin. Dynein function is essential in higher eukaryotes, yet its basic mechanisms of action including coupling to cargo and cellular regulation remain to be determined.  Currently Dr. Holzbaur"s lab is focusing on (1) biophysical assays for motor function using single molecule approaches; (2) elucidating the mechanisms driving bidirectional transport along the axon; (3) determining the mechanisms that regulate bidirectional transport; and (4) exploring the link between defects in axonal transport and the development of neuropathogenesis in degenerative diseases such as ALS.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeurodegenerative Diseasesxphysiopathology22aAxonal Transportxphysiology22aMicrotubule-Organizing Centerxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15708  aAUTH02123cgm a2200457 i 9918299483306676DNLM20260227161719.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u211110s2021    mdu634|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32015  a(DNLM)CIT:32025  a(OCoLC)1320799154  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 242 aNIH Office of Dietary Supplements 25th Anniversary Scientific Symposiumd(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNIH Office of Dietary Supplements 25th anniversary scientific symposium /cOffice of Dietary Supplements, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c2021.  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (10 hr., 34 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 25 & 26, 2021.3 a(CIT): ODS is holding this 2-day symposium virtually as a WebEx to: Showcase ODS contributions to landmark scientific accomplishments in dietary supplement research; Feature presentations by experts in the field of dietary supplement science; Highlight ODS's part in training the next generation of dietary supplement researchers; Discuss considerations for the future of dietary supplements.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 20, 2022).22aNIH Office of Dietary Supplements.12aDietary Supplements22aBiomedical Researchxhistory22aAnniversaries and Special Events 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aNIH Office of Dietary Supplements,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4250740uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42508  aAUTH01318nam a2200373 i 9918487478006676DNLM20260205113256.0221027r18911891nyua          000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW6bP3 v.74