ed May 10, 2000.3 a(CIT): Symposium featuring leading scientists and philosophers. Natcher Conference Center: Main Auditorium.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aConsciousness22aBrainxphysiology22aMental Processes 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10079  aAUTH01661cgm a22004214a 101267503DNLM20260227145139.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu098 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10127  a(OCoLC)70713336  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4701 aNIH Research Festivald(2000 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNIH Research Festival.nPlenary session 3,p[Utility of whole genome sequences] :b[early glimpses of the sequence-based era] /cchaired by Eric Green, Greg Schuler, Nick Ryba.1 iTitle from PowerPoint screen:aUtility of whole genome sequences :bearly glimpses of the sequence-based era  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 17, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 38 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 12, 2000.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.22aHuman Genome Project.12aGenome, Humanxgenetics 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aGreen, Eric D.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10127  aAUTH02263cgm a22004574a 101267386DNLM20260227144947.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu278|||||||f||||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:9988  a(OCoLC)180195360  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 30000aEconomic perspectives on health disparities /cOBSSR and NCMHD.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 29, 2001).  aStreaming video (4 hr., 38 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 10, 2001.3 a(CIT): This symposium, presented by the Office of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research (OBSSR) and the National Center on Minority Health and Health Disparities (NCMHD) at the National Institutes of Health (NIH), hopes to expand our understanding of the economic implications of racial and ethnic health disparities in the U.S. and ultimately, to encourage research activities in this area.  Papers by economists and policy analysts will be presented, each of which is directed toward estimating an aspect of the economic consequences of racial and ethnic health disparities.  Presenters will include Kevin Murphy, Robert Topel, John Bound, David Meltzer, and Jose Escarce.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aHealth Services Accessibilityxeconomics22aEthnicity22aHealth Services Needs and Demandxeconomics22aHealth Status22aMinority Groups 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?9988  aAUTH01365cgm a22003854a 101267520DNLM20260227145207.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu118 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10148  a(OCoLC)64443993  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 83 AA100aTelemedicine and telecommunications :boptions for the new century /cthe National Library of Medicine (NLM).  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 15, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 58 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 13, 2001.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aTelemedicinextrends 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10148  aAUTH02871cgm a2200433 i 101775932DNLM20260227160659.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2021    mdu100|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31490  a(OCoLC)1244241073  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 50600aDemystifying medicine.pCOVID-19 immunology and vaccines /cLuigi Notarangelo, Barney Graham.1 aCOVID-19 immunology and vaccines 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 40 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded January 19, 2021.3 a(CIT): The Demystifying Medicine course series continues with presentations by NIAID's Luigi Notarangelo and Barney Graham, who will speak about the immunological and genetic dance that determines COVID-19 survival rates and the development of vaccine, respectively. Luigi Notarangelo, M.D., is chief of NIAID's Laboratory of Clinical Immunology and Microbiology, where, ante-pandemus, he studied molecular and cellular bases of novel forms of human primary immune deficiency.  Early in 2020, Notarangelo and others saw that COVID-19 survival was dependent on genetic underpinnings of one's immune system, as well as factors such as age and obesity.  In his presentation, Notarangelo will elucidate how advanced immunology studies may predict who becomes infected with SARS-COV-2 and, if so, who will have a benign or life-threatening course  Barney Graham, M.D., Ph.D., is deputy director of the NIAID Vaccine Research Center.  Building upon their existing work on a coronavirus vaccine, Graham and his colleagues incorporated the viral sequence of SARS-COV-2 and worked with the company Moderna to ready a vaccine candidate for Phase 1 clinical trial in a dizzying two months.  In his presentation, Graham will discuss the remarkable progress in identifying the key structural components of infectious agents and subsequently rapidly developing vaccines to prevent the next pandemic.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 26, 2021).12aCOVID-19ximmunology22aCOVID-19xgenetics22aCOVID-19 Vaccinesximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aNotarangelo, Luigi,espeaker.1 aGraham, Barney S.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41282  aAUTH01654cgm a2200457 i 101684402DNLM20260227155342.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160601s2016    mdu067|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19691  a(OCoLC)953229707  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 5600aHistory of H2AX /cBill Bonner ; DNA Repair Interest Group. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 7 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aHistory of DNA repair lecture  aSubtitle on PowerPoint: History of H2AX and DNA double strand breaks.  aRecorded May 17, 2016.3 a(CIT): DNA Repair Interest group videoconference  Acrobat Slides.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed June 24, 2016).12aHistonesxgenetics22aDNA Breaks, Double-Stranded22aDNA Repair22aHistonesxhisto