cies /cby Henry C. Huntley.  aRockville, Md. :bReprinted with permission by the U.S. Dept. of Health, Education, and Welfare, Health Services and Mental Health Administration, Division of Emergency Health Services,c[1972]  a4 p. ;c27 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aDHEW publication ;vno. (HSM) 72-2031  aCaption title.  a"From Journal of the American College of Emergency Physicians, Vol. 1, No 2, pp. 13-16, March-April 1972.  cTransfer;aOffice of the PHS Historian;d2008.5DNLM12aEmergency Medical Services.1 aUnited States.bDivision of Emergency Health Services.1 aUnited States.bPublic Health Service,edonor.5DNLM 0aDHEW publication ;vno. (HSM) 72-2031.  aAUTH02518cgm a22004694a 101514421DNLM20260227153036.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090902s2009    mdu077|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15250  a(OCoLC)501272656  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 761 aSmedley, Brian D.10aMoving science to practice and policy :baddressing health inequities through a focus on place /cBrian Smedley.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH health disparities seminar series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Oct. 27, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 17 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded July 16, 2009.3 a(CIT): NIH Health Disparities Seminar Series  The National Center on Minority Health and Health Disparities (NCMHD) sponsors the monthly NIH Health Disparities Seminar Series. The forum disseminates information on advances, gaps, and current issues related to health disparities research. It features national and international health disparities research experts including many who are funded by the NCMHD, the other NIH Institutes and Centers, and federal agency partners. The theme for the July seminar series is the social determinants of health.   Place matters: where we live, work, study and play is critically important for health - and more so for children. This presentation will summarize research on the negative effects of racial and socioeconomic segregation on health, and highlight some of the challenges and opportunities in applying research to policy and practice to address health inequities and improve health opportunities.    www.ncmhd.nih.gov.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMedically Underserved Area22aHealth Policy22aHealth Status Disparities22aHealthcare Disparities22aSocioeconomic Factors. 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Center on Minority Health and Health Disparities (U.S.) 0aNIH health disparities seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15250  aAUTH01420cas a22004574a 101278120DNLM20260227090919.0cr|cn#||||||||060601c20069999enk|| p       |    0eng    a  2007243321  a1746-0913l1746-0913  a(OCoLC)173846200  aDNLMcDNLMeCONSER pre-impdDNLM  9England00aW1bFU612910aFuture Microbiol2dnlm00aFuture microbiology.  aLondon :bFuture Medicine3132024- :a[Milton Park, Oxfordshire] :bTaylor & Francis  aEighteen no. a year,b2019-  aFrequency varies,b2006-2018  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1, no. 1 (June 2006).  aDescription based on first issue; title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 2, no. 1 (Feb. 2007).1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, June 2006-1 aPubMedbv1n1, June 2006-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.12aInfections22aBacterial Infections22aMycoses22aVirus Diseases 2aPeriodical0 x1746-09214 uhttps://www.tandfonline.com/journals/ifmb204 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101278120  aAUTH02602cgm a2200457 i 101775938DNLM20260227160711.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2021    mdu110|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31502  a(OCoLC)1245469285  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWH 38000aDemystifying medicine.pBone marrow-based therapies today and tomorrow /cCynthia Dunbar, Harry Malech.1 aBone marrow-based therapies today and tomorrow 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 50 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded January 26, 2021.3 a(CIT): Demystifying Medicine Lecture Series Cynthia Dunbar, M.D., is an NIH Distinguished Investigator and head of the NHLBI Molecular Hematopoiesis Section.  Dunbar"s research focuses on understanding the differentiation of stem cells into multiple types of blood cells in vivo.  Working with non-human primates, Dunbar has been an international leader in improving the safety of gene transfer and the transplantation of primary hematopoietic (blood) cells for therapeutic purposes in human clinical trials.  Harry Malech, M.D., is chief of the NIAID Genetic Immunotherapy Section (GIS), where he applies gene therapy and hematopoietic stem cell transplantation approaches to the treatment of a variety of inherited primary immune deficiencies.  Malech"s gene therapy program -- the gist of GIS --focuses particularly on bench and clinical developments of gene transfer treatments for X-linked chronic granulomatous disease (CGD) and X-linked severe combined immune deficiency (X-SCID).  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 1, 2021).12aBone Marrow Transplantation22aHematopoietic Stem Cell Transplantation22aHematopoietic Stem Cellsximmunology22aGenetic Therapy22aBone Marrowxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aDunbar, Cynthia E.,espeaker.1 aMalech, Harry L.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41283  aAUTH03426cgm a2200469 i 101775940DNLM20260227160716.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2021    mdu065|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31510  a(OCoLC)1248768237  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 20000aBlack spot, black death, black pearl :bthe tales of bacterial effectors /cKim Orth. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 5 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aAccess restricted to HHS only.  aRecorded January 27, 2021.3 a(CIT):  Dr. Orth is a Howard Hughes Medical Institute Investigator and a professor of molecular biology and biochemistry at UT Southwestern Medical Center.  Her lab has uncovered virulence strategies that pathogens -- such as Yersinia, which causes plague, and Vibrio parahaemolyticus, a cause of food poisoning -- use to manipulate host cells.  From these pathogenic strategies, they have gained insight into new chemistry used by molecular signaling systems in cells.  Such chemical mechanisms can be exploited to treat disease.  "My lab is interested in elucidation the activity of virulence factors from pathogenic bacteria so that we can gain novel molecular insight into eukaryotic signaling systems.  The marine bacterium Vibrio parahaemolyticus is the worldwide leading cause of seafood-borne acute gastroenteritis.  We are working on the two V. parahaemolyticus type 3 secretion systems (T3SS1 and T3SS2) and their bacterial effectors to understand how signaling systems in the eukaryotic host can be manipulated by these bacterial pathogens.  Each of the two T3SSs uses a unique repertoire of effectors to manipulate host signaling.  The first T3SS1 is thought to be used maintain V. parahaemolyticus" survival in the environment, while the second T3SS2 is used for pathogenesis in an animal host.  For decades, this pathogen has been studied exclusively as an extracellular bacterium.  However, recent studies from our lab have revealed the pathogen"s ability to invade and replicate within host cells using the second T3SS2.  These studies have elucidated novel evolutionarily conserved mechanism that used by both host and pathogen.  One of these, AMPylation, is an important mechanism used for maintaining homeostasis by all metazoan cells when under stress.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 23, 2021).12aBacterial Infectionsxphysiopathology22aHost-Pathogen Interactions22aDeath Domain Receptor Signaling Adaptor Proteins22aBacteriaxpathogenicity22aVirulence Factors 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aOrth, Kim,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40166  aAUTH02215cgm a2200457 i 101775933DNLM20260227160701.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2021    mdu058|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31491  a(OCoLC)1244241092  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.500aWhat constitutes secondary research and when is IRB review required for this research? /cJonathan M. Green.1 iTitle on PowerPoint:aSecondary research :bfact, fiction, fears and fantasies 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (58 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAt head of title: OHSRP.  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded January 19, 2021.3 a(CIT): Objectives for this session include the following:.Identify when a research activity constitutes secondary research. Recognize when IRB review is needed for secondary research and what information the IRB requires in reviewing an application for this research. Understand the consent requirements for the use of data/specimens in secondary research. Recognize when additional approvals beyond those of the IRB are needed to conduct secondary research .Identify concerns related to sharing data/specimens with collaborators for secondary research.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 26, 2021).12aBiomedical Research22aEthics Committees, Research22aResearch Subjects22aResearch Design 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aGreen, Jonathan M.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Human Subjects Research Protections,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41267  aAUTH01625cgm a22004454a 101267471DNLM20260227145100.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu719 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10085  a(DNLM)CIT:10086  a(OCoLC)68705522  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWS 3402 aSymposium on Pediatric Neurotransmitter Diseasesn(1st :d2002 :cWashington, D.C.)10aFirst Symposium on Pediatric Neurotransmitter Diseases /cNINDS.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 3, 2006).  aStreaming video (11 hr., 58 min., 27 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 18-19, 2002.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aMovement Disorders22aChild22aNervous System Diseases22aNeurotransmitter Agentsxadverse effects 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Neurological Diseases and Stroke.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1008540uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10086  aAUTH02164cgm a2200421 i 101673097DNLM20260227155245.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u151203s2015    mdu304|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19309  a(OCoLC)936194475  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.500aPRAT 50th anniversary scientific symposium 2015 /cNIGMS, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (5 hr., 4 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 6, 2015.3 a(CIT): The National Institute of General Medical Sciences Postdoctoral Research Associate (PRAT) Program will host a Scientific Symposium in honor of the 50th Anniversary of the PRAT Program. This is an all-day event in the Ruth L. Kirschstein Auditorium of the Natcher Conference Center (Building 45) and is open to the public.  Over it's 50-year history, the PRAT Program has trained numerous leaders in academia, government and industry, and across many different scientific disciplines.  This scientific symposium will recognize and highlight the major scientific contributions of PRAT alumni to a variety of diverse research areas in the basic biomedical sciences.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed January 25, 2016).22aNational Institute of General Medical Sciences (U.S.).bPostdoctoral Research Associate Program.12aBiomedical Research22aAcademies and Institutes22aAnniversaries and Special Events 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19309  aAUTH02286cgm a2200433 i 101750714DNLM20260227155958.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190709s2019    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27634  a(OCoLC)1110889101  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQV 35000aWatch your step, there is new chemistry everywhere /cSean F. Brady. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCCIH integrative medicine research  aRecorded June 26, 2019.3 a(CIT): NCCIH Integrative Medicine Research Lecture Series. The National Center for Complementary and Integrative health (NCCIH) presents the Integrative Medicine Research Lecture Series. The series provides overviews of the current state of research and practice involving complementary health approaches and explores perspectives on the emerging discipline of integrative medicine.  Dr. Sean Brady is Tri-Institutional Professor and Evnin Professor Head, Laboratory of Genetically Encoded Small Molecules at the Rockefeller University. Dr. Brady has developed culture-independent methods to circumvent this discovery bottleneck. He will discuss applying these methods to the identification of new antibiotics from the global soil microbiome as well as metabolites encoded by the human microbiome.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed July 19, 2019).12aAnti-Bacterial Agentsxchemistry22aDrug Discovery22aMicrobiota 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aBrady, Sean F.,espeaker.2 aNational Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNCCIH integrative medicine research lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27634  aAUTH01399cas a2200469 a 101279470DNLM20260227090908.0cr|cn ||||||||050412c20069999ne qr p       0   a0eng    a  20052122480 a1555-5542l1555-5542  a(OCoLC)58961758  aNSDcNSDdDLCdDNLM  ansdpalcanlmcopyc  9Netherlands00aQH301b.B39200aW1bBI759P10a574.0121410aBiol Theory2dnlm0 aBiol. theory 0aBiological theory00aBiological theory.  aCambridge, MA :bMIT Press,cc2006-3 32012- :a[Dordrecht