00073044001000094060003300104245009900137260008200236300001500318336002600333337002800359338002700387500005700414650001200471650001700483650003000500650001900530650001400549651001000563710003600573999000900609101549324DNLM20260205114523.0101208s199u    ii a          000 0 eng    aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9India00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1429 no.1500aInsecticide spray strategy for the control of Anopheles culicifacies the rural malaria vector.  a[Delhi :bMalaria Research Centre, Indian Council of Medical Research,c199u]  av. :bill.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a85th Anniversary Indian Council of Medical Research.12aMalaria22aInsecticides22aMosquito Controlxmethods22aInsect Vectors22aAnopheles 2aIndia2 aMalaria Research Centre (India)  aAUTH03155cgm a2200469 a 101595201DNLM20260227154352.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u121102s2012    mdu073|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17626  a(OCoLC)819713397  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWT 1041 aWaite, Linda J.10aSocial connections and health /cLinda J. Waite.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 13 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aMatilda While Riley lecture  aRecorded Oct. 22, 2012.3 a(CIT): NIH BSSR Sixth Matilda White Riley Lecture  Those with more and better higher-quality social connections show better physical and emotional health and better longevity than those who are less well connected.  Some have compared the hazards of poor social connections to the hazards of smoking.  Perhaps the most important social relationship for adults is marriage, which seems to improve health and lengthen life, unless it is unhappy.  But many other social relationships also contribute to health. These include participation in formal organizations such as clubs or religious groups, socializing with friends and family, or membership in a network of others.  Recent research has illuminated the pathways through which social connections are linked to health.  This talk begins by discussing the contributions of Matilda White Riley to theories of aging, especially the links between aging and social structures. Riley suggests that people born into different cohorts experience the life course in unique ways because at key developmental stages they pass through different economic, social, sexual, and health contexts.  Exposure to marriage has changed over time and over cohorts, as have sexual and marital biographies, in ways affecting health. Social isolation, both as observed and as perceived by the individual, is linked to worse health along a number of dimensions, and characteristics of social networks are linked to health in complex ways. The talk describes the National Social Life, Health and Aging Project, a longitudinal study of the link between social relationships and health at older ages and its contributions to our understanding of this link.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aAging12aSocial Participation22aHealth Status22aMarriage22aSocial Support 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research. 0aMatilda White Riley annual lecture in the behavioral and social sciences40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17626  aAUTH01605cgm a2200469 i 101658206DNLM20260227155034.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150505s2015    mdu093|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18973  a(OCoLC)909852209  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.51 aHanover, John A.eauthor.10aDemystifying medicine 2015.pThe future for biomedical scientists /cJohn Hanover, Jonathan Lorsch, John Gallin.1 aFuture for biomedical scientists 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 33 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 28, 2015.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 15, 2015).12aResearch Personnel22aBiomedical Research22aCareer Mobility22aVocational Guidance22aWorkforce 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aLorsch, Jon,eauthor.1 aGallin, John I.,eauthor.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18973  aAUTH00738cam a22002657i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002000073040003500093044001000128060003300138100002800171245006200199250001300261264005100274300001400325336002600339337002800365338002700393650001400420650001200434655001700446999000900463101616038DNLM20260209145229.0130828s1949    cc            000 0 chi    a(OCoLC)29997101  aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1803 no.201 aFang, Wenyuan,eauthor.10aTang yu yin liao /cFang Wenyuan, Zhang Xiurong bian zhu.  aChu ban. 1aShanghai :bJia za zhi she,cMin guo 38 [1949]  a74 pages.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier12aBeverages22aCooking 2aPopular Work  aAUTH00960cam a22002777i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002100073040003500094044001000129060003300139100002700172245005400199264005700253300003100310336002600341337002800367338002700395490005800422650002900480650004100509650002100550830010200571999000900673101616049DNLM20260209145450.0130828s1958    cc a          000 0 chi    a(OCoLC)891353873  aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1803 no.191 aSu, Yingkuan,eauthor.10aChang jian de fu ke bing /cSu Yingkuan bian xie. 1a[Beijing] :bRen min wei sheng chu ban she,c[1958?]  a63 pages :billustrations.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aNong cun tong su wen ku. Wei sheng chang shi. Di 1 ji12aGenital Diseases, Female22aReproductive Physiological Phenomena22aHealth Education 0aNong cun tong su wen ku (Ren min wei sheng chu ban she).pWei sheng chang shi,nDi 1 jivDi 1 ji.  aAUTH02746cgm a2200433 i 101642818DNLM20260227155656.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140926s2014    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18586  a(OCoLC)894125338  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 2071 aSibille, Etienne,eauthor.10aAge-by-disease interactions in the human brain :bevidence & model /cDr. Etienne Sibille. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded September 4, 2014.3 a(CIT): Dr. Sibille's research goals have consistently focused on translational research aimed at identifying the cellular and molecular bases of depression, and specifically of the mood and affect dysregulation components of the illness. Studies in his laboratory encompass parallel investigations in postmortem brains of depressed and control subjects, and in genetic and environmental rodent models, with the aims of characterizing the primary pathology of depression and assessing causal links between identified molecular changes or candidate neurotransmitter systems and mood regulation. Current projects include translating human postmortem findings on the role of the GABA microcircuitry in mood regulation, and specifically of reduced somatostatin-positive dendritic targeting interneuron function. In addition, they have demonstrated that biological pathways affected during aging of the human brain largely overlap with neuropsychiatric and other neurological disease pathways and may in fact promote diseases, together providing a compelling rationale for investigating aging and diseases simultaneously. These latter hypotheses are now being tested in parallel in the human postmortem brain and in large epidemiological studies of subjects at the "vigor-to-frailty transition".  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed October 24, 2014.12aMajor Depressive Disorderxgenetics22aAgingxgenetics22aBrain Mapping22aModels, Genetic 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bGeroscience Interest Group,esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18586  aAUTH01414cas a2200433 a 8110297DNLM20260227090950.0cr|cn ||||||||811015c19819999nyuqr m       0||| 0eng    a0272-8087l0272-8087y0272-0807  aSLDIEE  a(DNLM)S19265000(s)  a(OCoLC)06939715  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bSE489C10aSemin Liver Dis2dnlm 0aSeminars in liver disease00aSeminars in liver disease.  aNew York, N.Y. :bThieme-Stratton,cc1981-3 aNew York, NY :bThieme  av. :bill., ports.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Feb. 1981)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, 1981-1 aPubMedbv1n1, 1981-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3Vol. 14, no. 4 (NOV 1994)c19950520fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paperxFurther review is required to determine exactly when acid-free paper was first used for this title 2aLiver Diseases 2aPeriodical0 x1098-89714 uhttps://www.thieme-connect.de/products/ejournals/journal/10.1055/s-00000069  aAUTH01588cgm a2200433 i 101716389DNLM20260227155806.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u171107s2017    mdu109|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:23497  a(OCoLC)1012797934  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWJ 76200aTRACO.pProstate 