c in sub-Saharan Africa. Women acquire HIV acquisition about 5-7 years earlier than men and young women in the 15-19 year age group have 3-6 fold higher rates of HIV infection compared to males in the same age group. The current HIV prevention options namely abstinence, behaviour change, male and female condoms, HIV testing and medical male circumcision are not feasible options for these young women and underscore the importance of female initiated prevention options. For the past two decades there has been substantial effort in finding a microbicide. In July 2010, the CAPRISA 004 trial demonstrated for the first time that 1% tenofovir gel was efficacious in preventing HIV and HSV-2 infection, 39% and 51% respectively. Sub-group analysis of the CAPRISA 004 data has identified several key lessons for enhancing the efficacy of tenofovir gel and informing the conduct of future PrEP and microbicide trials. These include adherence, drug levels and genital inflammation.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aAnti-HIV Agents22aHIV Infectionsxprevention & control22aWomen's Health 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17211  aAUTH01510cas a2200445 a 101642883DNLM20260227090913.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||141006c20149999enk|| poo     |    0eng    a  2016243047  a2055-1029l2055-1029  a(OCoLC)892883254  aHKPcHKPdOCLCOdOCLCFdEUWdOCLCOdCUSdDNLM  apcc  9England00aW110aHealth Psychol Open2dnlm00aHealth psychology open.13aHPO  aLondon :bSage Publications,c[2014]-  a1 online resource  aTwo issues a year,bc 2015-  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with July/December 2014.0 aPubMedbv1n1, July 2014-0 aPMC  aVolume for 2014 complete in one issue.  aDescription based on: July/December 2014 ; title from journal information scre