Institutes of Health,c2005]  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (76 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3master.  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (76 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (76 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture  aSpeaker, Mary-Claire King.  aPresented January 26, 2005.  aAlso available online.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aBreast Neoplasmsxgenetics22aOvarian Neoplasmsxgenetics22aGenomicsxmethods1 aKing, Mary-Claire,d1946-2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3438  aAUTH01694cgm a22004334a 101268853DNLM20260227151157.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2005    mdu051|||||||f||||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12794  a(OCoLC)173469309  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5681 aZhu, Jeff.10aTranscription factors involved in th2 cell differentiation and expansion /cJeff Zhu.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Sept. 10, 2007).  aStreaming video (51 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 28, 2005.3 a(CIT): The Immunology Interest Group (IIG) organizes activities designed to promote information exchange and interactions among NIH scientists interested in the field of immunology, broadly defined. Interactions are facilitated via weekly meetings on current topics as well as an annual Immunology Retreat.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aTh2 Cellsxcytology12aTh2 Cellsximmunology22aCell Differentiationximmunology22aTranscription Factorsxmetabolism 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12794  aAUTH02987cgm a2200445 i 9918663286606676DNLM20260227164015.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230815s2023    mdu679|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33343  a(DNLM)CIT:33348  a(OCoLC)1459861404  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWE 3042 aUnderstanding and Restoring Whole Joint Health in Pain Management (Workshop)d(2023 :cOnline)jauthor.10aUnderstanding and restoring whole joint health in pain management :ban NIH HEAL initiative workshop /cNCCIH, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],cJuly 25, 2023.  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (11 hr., 19 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded July 25 & 26, 2023.3 a(CIT): The purpose of this Helping to End Addiction Long-term(r) Initiative, or NIH HEAL Initiative(r), workshop is to examine the current literature on joint pain research and identify critical knowledge gaps to gain a comprehensive understanding of the whole joint, including the contributions of various articular and periarticular tissue pathologies that lead to pain in different types of joints. In addition, the workshop will explore manipulations and interventions that may help to restore whole joint health. Of particular interest, workshop participants will examine the relationships and interactions among the different tissue components of joints, including myofascial tissues, and their contributions to and impact on reducing pain by preserving and restoring joint health and function. Five scientific sessions and a final discussion session will cover structural changes in joints, mechanisms of whole joint pathology, differences among joint types and joint pain populations, interventions to address joint pain and disease, and emerging technologies that can be leveraged to advance scientific understanding of joints as complex "organs." The intended audience is interested scientists, clinicians, as well as patients, advocates, and other interested stakeholders.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed August 28, 2024).12aJoint Diseasesxprevention & control12aArthralgiaxtherapy22aPain Management 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNIH HEAL Initiative,ehost institution.2 aNational Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4943740uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49439  aAUTH02178cgm a2200493 i 9918663183006676DNLM20260227163949.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230815s2023    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33307  a(OCoLC)1415740568  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 11000aToward a reasonable middle ground for oversight for enhanced potential pandemic pathogen research /cMarc Lipsitch.1 iTitle on PowerPoint screen:aToward a reasonable middle ground in oversight of enhanced potential pandemic pathogen research 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH-FDA COVID SIG Lecture  aAccess restricted to HHS only.  aRecorded June 30, 2023.3 a(CIT): NIH-FDA COVID SIG Lecture  This is a special Friday, in-person NIH-FDA COVID SIG Lecture with Marc Lipsitch, Ph.D. Lipsitch is a professor of epidemiology and the director of the Center for Communicable Disease Dynamics at Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health. See https://www.hsph.harvard.edu/profile/marc-lipsitch for his bio.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed December 7, 2023).12aDual Use Researchxethics12aPandemic Preparedness22aScientific Misconduct22aBiosecurity22aBiological Warfare Agents22aCriminal Behavior 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aLipsitch, Marc,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bCOVID-19 Scientific Interest Group,esponsoring body. 0aNIH COVID-19 SIG lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49969  aAUTH01405cgm a22004334a 101268735DNLM20260227151036.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2005    mdu056 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12547  a(OCoLC)166278438  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWK 58500aHuman pituitary proteomics /cDominic M. Desiderio ... [et al.].1 iTitle from screen banner:aHuman pituitary proteome  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed July 31, 2007).  aStreaming video (55 min., 53 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 1, 2005.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government.12aPituitary Neoplasms22aAdenoma22aGene Expression Profiling.22aProteomics 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aDesiderio, Dominic M.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12547  aAUTH02690cgm a2200385 i 101690004DNLM20260227155606.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160804s2016    mdu453|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19781  a(OCoLC)958112361  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 26.52 aNIH Workshop on Standards and Modularity of Brain-Computer Interfaces and Neuroprosthesesd(2016 :cRockville, Md.)jauthor.10aNIH workshop on standards and modularity of brain-computer interfaces and neuroprostheses /cNational Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (7 hr., 33 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 30, 2016.3 a(CIT): On 21 November, 2014, Food and Drug Administration's (FDA) Center for Devices and Radiological Health (CDRH) held an open public workshop with the aim of fostering an open discussion on the scientific and clinical considerations associated with the development of Brain-Computer Interfaces (BCI) devices for patients with paralysis and amputation. CDRH plans to use this information to develop regulatory considerations that will promote innovation while maintaining appropriate patient protections. The outcome of this meeting has been published recently in the Journal of Neural Engineering (http://dx.doi.org/10.1088/1741-2560/13/2/023001).  The report concluded that addressing challenges identified by the BCI community (and discussed in the consensus report) will require the involvement of all stakeholders.   The proposed one-day workshop, immediately following the Neural Interfaces Conference in Baltimore, will focus on the issues of standardization, modularity and interoperability of BCI device technologies.  We invite the BCI community to participate at this meeting to assist in the development of standards for BCI devices to ultimately accelerate the translation of these devices for consumers.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed August 30, 2016).12aBrain-Computer Interfaces22aProstheses and Implants 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19781  aAUTH03099cgm a2200445 i 9918503178306676DNLM20260227162805.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u221105s2022    mdu729|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32703  a(DNLM)CIT:32712  a(OCoLC)1390179812  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQV 7782 aAssay Guidance Manual Training Workshopsd(2022 October 18-19 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aAssay guidance workshop for high-throughput screening and lead discovery /cNCATS, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (12 hr., 9 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 18 & 19, 2022.3 a(CIT): The NCATS Assay Guidance Manual (AGM) is hosting a two-day workshop that will cover a broad range of critical concepts underlying assay development and implementation for high-throughput screening and lead discovery projects. This workshop is designed to disseminate critical information about the implementation of robust assay methods and is particularly relevant for researchers developing molecular probes or clinical candidates. Many of the workshop instructors have 20 to 30 years of experience in the field of drug discovery and will share information not readily found in a classroom or published material outside of the AGM. It will also cover emerging technologies and modalities in drug discovery, including the use of stem cells, antibody therapeutics, DNA-encoded libraries and advances in COVID-19 therapeutics.This workshop will provide participants with a broad, practical perspective on assay development and data analysis so they can improve research projects involving drug discovery and know where to find further information; identify reagents, methods and instrumentation that are well suited to robust assays; and develop robust assays and the required counter and secondary assays for targets of interest. In addition, the workshop will provide participants with broad overviews and practical perspectives on new and emerging modalities in drug discovery.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed June 30, 2023).12aHigh-Throughput Screening Assaysxmethods12aDrug Discoveryxmethods22aModels, Theoretical 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (U.S.).bAssay Guidance Manual (Program),ehost institution.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4533940uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45341  aAUTH01178nam a2200301 i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003100073042000800104044001800112060003300130100003800163245009900201264006000300300002200360336002600382337002800408338002700436500007600463504004100539540014200580583003900722583004300761650003000804730003300834999000900867101776506DNLM20260227110924.0210225r18791879nyu         b 000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5538 no.281 aHunt, David,d1845-1907,eauthor.10aSome recent opinions concerning the development of the external ear passages /cby David Hunt. 1aNew York :bTrow's Printing and Bookbinding Co.,c1879.  a11 pages ;c25 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"Reprinted from the American journal of otology, October, 1879"--Cover.  aIncludes bibliographical references.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM12aEar, Externalxembryology0 aAmerican journal of otology.  aAUTH01459cas a2200457 a 101579795DNLM20260209091231.0m        d        cr#|||||||||||110811d20112025pau|| poo     0    0eng    a  2012256059  a2000-8686l2000-8686  a(OCoLC)746359846  aCS1bengcCS1dOCLCQdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW1  aQL75710aInfect Ecol Epidemiol2dnlm00aInfection ecology & epidemiology.1 aInfection ecology and epidemiology  aStockholm :bCo-Action Pub.3132016- :aPhiladelphia, PA :bTaylor & Francis  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1 (2011); ceased with Volume 15 (2025).0 aPubMedbv1, 2011-v15, 20250 aPMC  aIncludes supplements.  aDescription based on: Vol. 1 (2011), viewed Mar. 20, 2012; title from caption on journal home page,  aLatest issue consulted: 2012:2 (viewed Apr. 4, 2012).12aEpidemiology22aBacteriology22aEcology22aVirology 2aPeriodical40uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/toc/ziee20/current40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101579795  aAUTH02851cgm a2200409 i 9918434186406676DNLM20260227162456.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220706s2022    mdu763|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32441  a(DNLM)CIT:32445  a(OCoLC)1382506342  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWI 8202 aCurrent and Future Research Needs in the Era of Highly Effective Modulator Therapies for Cystic Fibrosis (Workshop)d(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aCurrent and future research needs in the era of highly effective modulator therapies for cystic fibrosis /cNIH, NHLBI, NIAAD, NIDDK, and Cystic Fibrosis Foundation. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (12 hr., 43 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 2 & 3, 2022.3 a(CIT): Cystic fibrosis (CF) is a rare inherited disease caused by mutations in a gene called the cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) gene. Mutations in CFTR cause significant morbidity and mortality for patients with CF, primarily affecting the lungs and digestive systems. With no cure for CF, advances in treatment using highly effective modulator therapies (HEMT) are helping patients live longer lives with improvements in clinical outcomes. On Thursday, June 2, and Friday, June 3, 2022, the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (NHLBI), National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (NIAID) and National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases (NIDDK) of the National Institutes of Health (NIH) in planning collaboration with the Cystic Fibrosis Foundation will host a virtual workshop with the objective to bring together experts in Cystic Fibrosis (CF) clinical-translational research to address critical questions and inform the field of the future research needs in the era of highly effective modulator therapies (HEMT) for the treatment of CF. The workshop will highlight challenges and opportunities for research in the context of rapidly shifting treatment options.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 24, 2023).12aCystic Fibrosisxtherapy 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4563140uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45633  aAUTH02426cgm a22004454a 101520524DNLM20260227153237.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u091202s2009    mdu071|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15467  a(OCoLC)520436683  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWB 7201 aKastner, Dan.10aFevers, genes, and histories :badventures in the genomics of inflammation /cDaniel Kastner.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 11 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 18, 2009.3 a(CIT): Fever is a fundamental sign of inflammation, and hereditary disorders that present with recurrent fever provide a unique opportunity to understand the human biology of inflammation.  Stimulated by encounters with patients suffering from hereditary recurrent fevers and utilizing the tools of modern genetics and genomics, it has been possible to identify new genes, gene families, and biochemical pathways that control inflammation, and to discover new meaning for genes and proteins already in the inflammatory lexicon.  In some cases, these discoveries have not only given molecular explanations and suggested targeted therapies for the patients we see today, but have shed light on the histories of human populations and their possible evolving interactions with microbial pathogens.  The NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series includes weekly scientific talks by some of the top researchers in the biomedical sciences worldwide.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aFamilial Mediterranean Feverxgenetics12aFamilial Mediterranean Feverximmunology22aHereditary Autoinflammatory Diseases. 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15467  aAUTH01625cas a22004574a 100964852DNLM20260210112858.0m        d        cr|cnu---|||||000824c19989999txuuu pss     0    0eng    a   00214088 0 a1531-2992l1531-2992  a(OCoLC)44875348  aNSDcNSDdDNLM0 aengaspa  alcdansdpanlmcopyc  9United States10aW10 aInternet j. rescue disaster med. 4aThe Internet journal of rescue and disaster medicine04aThe Internet journal of rescue and disaster medicine.30aJournal of rescue and disaster medicine  a[Sugar Land, Tex.] :bInternet Scientific Publications,c[1998]-  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1-  aTitle from PDF caption (publisher Web site, viewed Jan. 6, 2006).  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 5, no. 1 (viewed Jan. 6, 2006).  aIssued in two issues per vol.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aChiefly in English with some Spanish.  aMerger of: Internet journal of aeromedical transportation, and: Internet journal of disaster medicine.12aDisasters22aEmergency Medical Services. 2aPeriodical14tInternet journal of aeromedical transportation14tInternet journal of disaster medicine40uhttps://ispub.com/IJRDM  aAUTH03230cgm a2200457 i 101772199DNLM20260227160140.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201003s2020    mdu745 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30286  a(DNLM)CIT:30289  a(OCoLC)1204632811  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWE 5502 aNIH HEAL Initiative Workshop on Myofascial Paind(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNIH HEAL Initiative Workshop on Myofascial Pain /cNational Center for Complementary and Integrative Health, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (12 hr., 25 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 16-17, 2020.3 a(CIT): It is estimated that 30 to 85 percent of patients with musculoskeletal pain are affected by myofascial pain syndrome (MPS)--pain originating from muscles and/or associated soft tissues such as fascia. Interactions between fascia and muscles remain mostly unknown and there is a strong need to address the contributions of the myofascial tissues to chronic pain. This represents one of the last "unturned stones" of all the tissue types involved in musculoskeletal pain.  The National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health and the National Institute of Biomedical Imaging and Bioengineering are proud to join forces in organizing NIH HEAL Initiative Workshop on Myofascial Pain. This workshop will take place online from Wednesday, September 16 to Thursday, September 17.  Speakers will present in-depth analyses of the state of science of myofascial pain, current usage of technologies (e.g., methodologies) and their limitations, potential current technologies to be adapted for myofascial pain biomarker imaging or recording, and potential emerging technologies and methodologies.  NIH partners for this event include the National Institute of Arthritis and Musculoskeletal and Skin Diseases, the Eunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development/National Center for Medical Rehabilitation Research, the National Institute of Dental and Craniofacial Research, and the National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 9, 2020).12aMyofascial Pain Syndromes22aMyofascial Pain Syndromesxdrug therapy22aAnalgesics, Opioidxtherapeutic use22aOpioid-Related Disordersxprevention & control 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNIH HEAL Initiative,esponsoring body.2 aNational Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?3028640uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?30289  aAUTH00716cam a22002537i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003500073044001000108060003300118100002600151245004300177250001300220264004000233300003300273336002600306337002800332338002700360650001700387650002800404650002100432999000900453101616102DNLM20260209145706.0961212s1951    cc a          000 0 chi    aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1803 no.171 aZhan, Ziyou,eauthor.10aKou qiang wei sheng /cZhan Ziyou zhu.  aChu ban. 1aShanghai :bJia chu ban she,c1951.  a5, 77 pages :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier12aOral Hygiene22aStomatognathic Diseases22aHealth Education  aAUTH01979cgm a22004934a 101268391DNLM20260227150608.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu060 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11888  a(OCoLC)84671203  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWK 8201 aGorden, Phillip.10aNew insights and approaches to patients with extreme insulin resistance /cPhillip Gorden.  Clinical and genetic insights into multiple endocrine neoplasia type-1 (MEN).  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aClinical Center grand rounds  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 17, 2006).  aStreaming video (59 min., 49 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 17, 2004.3 a(CIT): NIH Clinical Center Grand Rounds, 50th Anniversary Celebration of Clinical Research.  Dr. Phillip Gorden, Chief, Clinical and Cellular Biology Section, CEB, NIDDK.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aInsulin Resistance22aGene Expression Regulation22aInsulin Antibodies22aMultiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 122aReceptor, Insulin 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)02aClinical and genetic insights into multiple endocrine neoplasia type-1 (MEN). 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11888  aAUTH02209cgm a22004454a 101268307DNLM20260227150411.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu051 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11701  a(OCoLC)71138939  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 551 aFenyo, David.10aTesting the significance of protein identification results /cDavid Fenyo.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 30, 2006).  aStreaming video (50 min., 32 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 5, 2003.3 a(CIT): Mass spectrometry and database searching is widely used for protein identification. The proteins in the sample are first separated to obtain a mixture of the proteins of interest. These protein mixtures are then digested with a proteolytic enzyme. The masses of the resulting peptides and their fragments are measured and compared with calculated peptide masses from a protein sequence collection. A score is calculated for the comparison and the protein sequences in the collection are ranked according to the calculated score. Different search engines calculate the score in different ways. The meaning of these scores is in general not easily understood by the non-expert user and they are not amenable to automation.  Proteome Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aProteinsxanalysis22aAlgorithms22aComputer Simulation22aResearch Design 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11701  aAUTH01582cgm a22004094a 101267441DNLM20260227145111.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu506 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10054  a(DNLM)CIT:10055  a(OCoLC)67281649  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 2702 aNational Conference on Drug Abuse Prevention Researchn(2nd :d2001 :cWashington, D.C.)10aSecond National Conference on Drug Abuse Prevention Research :ba progress update /cNational Institute on Drug Abuse (NIDA).  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 12, 2006).  aStreaming video (8 hr., 25 min., 23 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Aug. 9-10, 2001.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aSubstance-Related Disordersxprevention & control 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute on Drug Abuse.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1005440uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10055  aAUTH01610cgm a2200385 a 101516515DNLM20260227153119.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u091002s2009    mdu374|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15314  a(OCoLC)731127362  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 59000aNCCAM wellness workshop /cNational Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine (NCCAM).  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (6 hr., 14 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Sept. 25, 2009.3 a(CIT): This 1-day workshop will review several measures of wellness, identify their strengths and weaknesses, and make recommendations on how best to measure wellness. This information will help NCCAM guide development of questions for the 2012 National Health Interview Survey.    For more information, visit http://nccam.nih.gov/news/events/wellness.htm.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHealth Promotion 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15314  aAUTH01279cam a2200325 i 60420600RDNLM20260206221523.0cr||||||||||||820514s1900    pau           001 0 eng    a(OCoLC)14780145  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aGNbR851m 19001 aRotzell, W. E.q(Willett Enos),d1871-1913,eauthor.10aMan: an introduction to anthropology /cby W.E. Rotzell. 1aPhiladelphia :bEdward Stern & Co., Inc.c1900.  a146 pages ;c20 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes index.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aNLM copy, author's signed presentation copy, inscribed on front flyleaf: "Dr. Robert Fletcher compliments of W.E. Rotzell, May 15, 1900."5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20250618lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aAnthropology22aRacial Groups4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/60420600R  aAUTH03217cgm a2200493 i 101645902DNLM20260227154906.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u141108s2014    mdu053|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18694  a(OCoLC)897023841  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWR 2821 aBautista, Diana,eauthor.10aScratching beneath the surface of itch, touch, and pain /cDiana Bautista. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (53 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Oct. 27, 2014.3 a(CIT): Neuroscience Seminar Series  Dr. Bautista lab's somatosensory system enables numerous behaviors fundamental to human existence, allowing us to eat, communicate and survive.  Acute pain is as a warning signal that alerts us to noxious mechanical, chemical and thermal stimuli, which are potentially tissue damaging. During inflammation or injury, we experience a heightened sensitivity to touch that encourages us to protect the injured site.  Despite this essential protective function, pain can outlast its usefulness and become chronic. Numerous pathophysiological conditions result in the chronic dysregulation of mechanosensory signaling, leading to pain triggered by light touch (allodynia), as well as enhanced sensitivity to noxious mechanical stimuli.   Light-touch receptors, which mediate discriminative touch, enables fine tactile acuity that allows us to manipulate objects with high precision.  As humans, we depend on this skill for everyday tasks that range from eating with utensils to texting.  Discriminative touch is also central to social interactions such as mating, maternal bonding and successful child rearing.  To understand the senses of touch and pain, we must unravel peripheral mechanisms that encode tactile stimuli and discover how the brain interprets these signals to dictate behavior. The transduction of a physical force on the skin into an electrical signal is the first step in the encoding of tactile stimuli.  Dr. Bautista"s group uses molecular biology and cellular physiology to discover the transduction molecules that underlie touch.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed Nov. 17, 2014.12aPruritusxphysiopathology22aPain Perceptionxphysiology22aPainxphysiopathology22aSignal Transduction22aTouch Perceptionxphysiology22aTouchxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18694  aAUTH01207cgm a22003974a 101267774DNLM20260227145758.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s1998    mdu049 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10654  a(OCoLC)86112970  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWG 28000aMIG 7/13/98.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c1998]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 28, 2007).  aStreaming video (48 min., 33 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded July 13, 1998.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.12aMyocardiumxmetabolism22aCalcium22aCalcium Signaling22aMitochondria, Heart 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMitochondria Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10654  aAUTH01790cgm a22004454a 101267922DNLM20260227145903.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu115 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11019  a(OCoLC)84898876  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWS 1032 aNational Advisory Child Health and Human Development Council (U.S.)10aSeptember 2003 National Advisory Child Health and Human Development Council /cthe National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (NICHD).30aNational Advisory Child Health and Human Development Council  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 30, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 54 min., 24 sec. : sd., col.).  aThis is a work of the United States Government. NIH-ONLY events may not be disseminated outside of the NIH network.  aRecorded Sept. 22, 2003.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.)12aGrowth and Development22aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11019  aAUTH02772cgm a2200433 i 9918503180406676DNLM20260227162720.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u221105s2022    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32652  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 55.900aMolecular origami :bthe delicate art of protein folding and misfolding and its relevance to health and disease /cJudith Frydman. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded September 21, 2022.3 a(CIT): Judith Frydman is a biochemist and the Donald Kennedy Chair in the School of Humanities & Sciences and Professor of Genetics at Stanford University. Her research focuses on protein folding. Her laboratory discovered the molecular chaperone TRiC/CCT and determined its mechanism and function for protein folding. She was elected as a Fellow of the American Academy of Arts & Sciences in 2018 and as a Fellow of the Biophysical Society in 2019. In 2017, she was given the American Society for Biochemistry and Molecular Biology-Merck Award. She is an editor of the Journal of Cell Biology. In 2021, she was elected member of the U. S. National Academy of Sciences.  The long term goal of our research is to understand how proteins fold in living cells. The Frydman lab uses a multidisciplinary approach to address fundamental questions about molecular chaperones, protein folding and degradation. In addition to basic mechanistic principles, we aim to define how impairment of cellular folding and quality control are linked to disease, including cancer and neurodegenerative diseases and examine whether reengineering chaperone networks can provide therapeutic strategies.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed December 7, 2022).12aProtein Folding22aProteostasisxphysiology22aIntrinsically Disordered Proteinsxmetabolism22aProteostasis Deficienciesxphysiopathology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aFrydman, Judith,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45984  aAUTH01943cgm a2200505 i 101671883DNLM20260227155115.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u151031s2015    mdu065|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19225  a(OCoLC)930496712  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 5361 aPearson, Steven D.eauthor.14aThe tension between cost and access to hepatitis C treatment :ba test case for tomorrow's cures /cpresenter, Steven Pearson ; review and discussants, Matt Salo and Robert W. Dubois. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 5 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aEthics rounds  aRecorded October 7, 2015.3 a(CIT): Ethics Rounds: The Tension Between Cost and Access to Hepatitis C Treatment: A Test Case for Tomorrow's Cures  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 20, 2015).12aHepatitis Cxdrug therapy12aHepatitis Cxeconomics22aDrug Therapyxeconomics22aDrug Therapyxethics22aHealth Care Costsxethics22aHealth Services Accessibilityxeconomics 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aSalo, Matt,eauthor.1 aDubois, Robert W.,eauthor.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aClinical Center ethics grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19225  aAUTH01262cas a2200409 a 101581949DNLM20260227090908.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||120409c20019999cl || poo     |    0spa    a  2012243479  a0717-6384l0717-6384  a(OCoLC)795563098  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aspaaeng  apcc  9Chile10aW110aMedwave2dnlm00aMedwave.  a[Santiago :bMednet]31aSantiago, Chile :bMedwave Estudios Limitada  aMonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Año 1, no. 1 (enero 2001).1 aMEDLINEbv14n1, 2014-0 aPubMedbv14n1, 2014-  a2015- , available in Spanish and English.  aDescription based on: Año 1, no. 1 (enero 2001); title from PDF caption (publisher's Web site, viewed June 5, 2012).  aLatest issue consulted: Año 12, no. 5 (junio 2012) (viewed June 5, 2012).12aMedicine 2aPeriodical40uhttps://www.medwave.cl/link.cgi/  aAUTH02156cgm a2200433 i 9918503181006676DNLM20260227162704.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u221105s2022    mdu292|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32642  a(OCoLC)1416045519  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.55.H92 aOHRP Exploratory Workshopn(5th :d2022 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aBeyond altruism :bexploring payment for research participation /cOffice for Human Research Protections, HHS.1 aExploring payment for research participation 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (4 hr., 52 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 15, 2022.3 a(CIT): As society moves away from a paternalistic view of protecting research participants, many are revisiting the ethics and utility about payment for research participation. The goal of this workshop is to explore the role of paying people for their participation, the impact of payment, as well as the challenges and considerations for how payments should be made. Session 1 will focus on ethical and theoretical considerations. Session 2 will explore the practical challenges and implementations.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 14, 2023).12aResearch Subjects12aCompensation and Redressxethics22aMotivationxethics22aDiversity, Equity, Inclusion 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aUnited States.bDepartment of Health and Human Services.bOffice for Human Research Protections,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45271  aAUTH01690cgm a2200457 i 101773045DNLM20260227160347.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201105s2020    mdu062|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30354  a(OCoLC)1224228625  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 3004aThe complex interplay of biology and social determinants of health :bstories from the streets of Baltimore /cMichele K. Evans. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCC grand rounds  aRecorded October 21, 2020.3 a(CIT): The Complex Interplay of Biology and Social Determinants of Health: Stories from the Streets of Baltimore  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 19, 2020).12aSocial Determinants of Health22aBlack or African American.22aHispanic or Latino.22aHealth Equity 2aBaltimore 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aEvans, Michele K.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38782  aAUTH03240cgm a2200457 i 101773043DNLM20260227160343.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201105s2020    mdu509 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30348  a(DNLM)CIT:30350  a(OCoLC)1226370106  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 7042 aHEAL Workshop: Target Validation for Non-addictive Therapeutics Development for Paind(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aHEAL Workshop :bTarget Validation for Non-addictive Therapeutics Development for Pain /cNational Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video file (8 hr., 39 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 19 & 20, 2020.3 a(CIT): Despite advances in the understanding of pain physiology there has been a paucity of novel analgesic medications developed to address the still high unmet need of pain patients especially those who rely on addictive opioid medicines for pain relief. The successful development of novel non-opioid, non-addictive therapeutics requires advances in preclinical target validation that demonstrate sufficient human disease relevance to trigger investment in non-clinical development and subsequent clinical trials to obtain proof of biology and early proof of efficacy before registrational studies.  Translating novel target discoveries to the clinic depends on the quality of target validation and the rigorous implementation of research operating plans to provide compelling data supporting a causative link between the modulation of molecular target(s) and a disease phenotype associated with the disease state.  The full target validation process covers target identification to proof-of concept efficacy in human subjects in clinical trials. This workshop will focus on the key preclinical experimental validation package components that de-risk and provide rigorous rationale for the pursuit of novel targets with minimal side effects and a reduced potential of abuse/addiction liability.  The focus will be on the stage appropriate data required to justify further preclinical and clinical therapeutics development activities across the translational spectrum.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 19, 2020).12aPainxdrug therapy22aAnalgesicsxtherapeutic use22aBehavior, Addictivexprevention & control22aMolecular Targeted Therapy22aDrug Discovery 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3886540uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38866  aAUTH01497cgm a22004334a 101268238DNLM20260227150237.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu075 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11563  a(OCoLC)70873961  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4501 aMeltzer, Paul.10aMicroarray technologies /cPaul Meltzer.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 14, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 14 min., 54 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 19, 2002.3 a(CIT): An NHGRI Mini-Course in Genomics and Bioinformatics.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aOligonucleotide Array Sequence Analysis.22aGene Expression Profiling.22aNeoplasmsxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11563  aAUTH01411cgm a22004214a 101268235DNLM20260227150229.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu057 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11560  a(OCoLC)70873958  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4501 aTouchman, Jeff.10aDNA sequencing /cJeff Touchman.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 14, 2006).  aStreaming video (56 min., 20 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 29, 2002.3 a(CIT): An NHGRI Mini-Course in Genomics and Bioinformatics.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aSequence Analysis, DNA22aGenomics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11560  aAUTH03312cgm a22004694a 101496148DNLM20260227153054.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090203s2009    mdu068 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14842  a(OCoLC)319070186  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQX 2031 aBargmann, Cornelia I.10aDissecting a circuit for olfactory behavior /cCori Bargmann.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 24, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 7 min., 35 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 5, 2009.3 a(CIT): Dr. Bargmann's laboratory investigates how the neural circuits in C. elegans develop, identifies the genes and neural pathways for its navigation behaviors, and asks how sensory inputs regulate those circuits.  C. elegans, a worm with just 302 neurons, shows an unexpected sophistication in its ability to detect and respond to olfactory stimuli, making it an ideal subject in which to study these interactions. C. elegans senses hundreds of different odors, discriminates among them, and then generates different behaviors in response. Since the worm's nervous system has so few neurons, it's possible to determine which neurons generate these behaviors. In C. elegans, as in other animals, odors are detected by G protein coupled odorant receptors. Each sensory neuron is primarily dedicated to a single behavioral task, such as attraction or repulsion, and activation of the correct sensory neuron is all it takes to generate a characteristic behavior. Dr. Bargmann's lab investigates how sensory signaling pathways and the neurons downstream translate those sensory cues into behavioral responses. Because so much of how the nervous system functions is dictated by the precise synaptic connections between neurons, Dr. Bargmann is also investigating the development of neural circuits in order to better understand how an animal processes information and routes it along the complex neural web. By combining genetics with C. elegans behavior, she has found a gene that determines whether a worm prefers to eat alone or socially, and another gene that allows them to discriminate among different odors. More recently, work by Dr. Bargmann led to the discovery that the worms she studies are capable of learning and later remembering to reject a novel food that makes them ill.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aCaenorhabditis elegansxphysiology22aDiscrimination, Psychologicalxphysiology22aOlfactory Receptor Neuronsxphysiology22aReceptors, Odorantxphysiology22aSmellxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14842  aAUTH01655cgm a22004454a 101268109DNLM20260227150046.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu051 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11375  a(OCoLC)213489438  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 3251 aWobus, Anna M.10aCompartitive [sic] embryonic and somatic stem cell differentiation /cAnna M. Wobus.3 aComparative embryonic and somatic stem cell differentiation3 aCompartitive embryonic and somatic stem cell differentiation  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 27, 2008).  aStreaming video (50 min., 34 sec. : sd., col.).  aAudio problems at the site make it difficult to understand speaker.  aRecorded Mar. 25, 2002.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aAdult Stem Cellsxcytology22aCell 