ch 18, 1872."  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM22aBoston City Hospital.12aHospitals, Municipalxorganixation & administration.12aHospital Administrators 2aBoston 2aCollected Correspondence1 aBoston (Mass.).bBoard of Aldermen,eissuing body  aAUTH02038cgm a22004574a 101493075DNLM20260227152739.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090106s2008    mdu070|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14809  a(OCoLC)297602466  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 2071 aZarate, Carlos A.,cJr.10aNew insights, new directions for treating major depression and bipolar disorder /cCarlos A. Zarate.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aMedicine for the public lecture series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Jan. 28, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 10 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 2, 2008.3 a(CIT): Mood disorders are serious, debilitating, life-shortening illnesses that affect millions. About one in five Americans could develop a mood disorder during his or her lifetime. The World Health Organization projects that mood disorders will be a leading cause of disability worldwide by 2020. To make treatments work better and find new treatments, we must understand more about what causes mood disorders. Dr. Carlos Zarate will cover the symptoms and treatments for mood disorders and the latest research on what may cause them.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMajor Depressive Disorderxdiagnosis22aBipolar Disorderxdiagnosis22aBipolar Disorderxtherapy22aMajor Depressive Disorderxtherapy 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aMedicine for the public40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14809  aAUTH01512cgm a22004694a 101493091DNLM20260227152830.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090106s2008    mdu053 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14831  a(OCoLC)317249015  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 3801 aKhan, Javedc(Physician)10aTranslational genomics in neuroblastoma /cJaved Khan.1 iTitle on PowerPoint screen:aTranslational genomics of pediatric cancers  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aDirector's seminar series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 4, 2009).  aStreaming video (52 min., 28 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 12, 2008.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeuroblastomaxdiagnosis12aNeuroblastomaxgenetics22aBiomarkers22aGene Expression Profiling.22aPrognosis 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14831  aAUTH02249cgm a2200481 i 101645879DNLM20260227154804.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u141108s2014    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18662  a(OCoLC)897493178  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 41 aBlaser, Martin J.eauthor.10aEarly life microbiome influences on metabolism, immunity, and disease risk /cMartin J. Blaser. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aJoseph J. Kinyoun memorial lecture  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded October 7, 2014.3 a(CIT): Blaser will discuss how the human microbiome influences metabolism, immunity, and disease early in life. The gastrointestinal tract contains a dense and complex microbial environment, where bacterial and human cells coexist and affect the equilibrium and health of our bodies. This environment forms early in life, and the way in which the assembly occurs influences the body's metabolism and immune system development. Altering the microbiome during this important period of formation (with antibiotics, for example) may have substantial consequences on the risk of diseases and conditions such as obesity and diabetes.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed November 28, 2014.12aMicrobiotaxphysiology22aAnti-Bacterial Agentsxpharmacology22aFetal Development22aFetusxmicrobiology22aGastrointestinal Tractxmicrobiology22aHost-Pathogen Interactions 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aKinyoun lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18662  aAUTH01117cam a2200301 i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003500073042000800108044001800116060003200134110003000166245011500196264005200311300002200363336002600385337002800411338002700439540014200466583003900608583004300647610002100690650005400711650002800765651001300793999000900806101778855DNLM20260219155417.0210504s1873    nyu           000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdad20260219dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5574 no.42 aAlbany Hospital,eauthor.10aBy-laws, rules and regulations, governors and officers, with the act of incorporation /cThe Albany Hospital.. 1aAlbany :bVan Benthuysen Printing House,c1873.  a21 pages ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM22aAlbany Hospital.12aHospitals, Privatexorganization & administration12aConstitution and Bylaws 2aNew York  aAUTH01505cas a2200445 i 101774849DNLM20260227090919.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||200616c20209999ne || poo     0    0eng    a  2021229751  a1164453564  a2666-2450l2666-2450  a(OCoLC)1160247798z(OCoLC)1164453564  aSFBbengerdad20260227cSFBdDNLM  apcc  9Netherlands00aW110aCereb Circ Cogn Behav2dnlm00aCerebral circulation - cognition and behavior.1 aCCCB 1a[Amsterdam] :bElsevier B.V.,c[2020]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1 (2020).0 aPubMedbv1, 2020-0 aPMC  aDescription based on: Volume 1 (2020); title from journal homepage (Elsevier ScienceDirect, viewed January 11, 2020).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 2 (2021) (viewed December 10, 2021).12aCerebrovascular Circulation22aBrainxblood supply22aDementia, Vascularxblood22aAlzheimer Diseasexblood 2aPeriodical40uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/cerebral-circulation-cognition-and-behavior40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101774849  aAUTH01886cgm a22004094a 101268816DNLM20260227151145.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2005    mdu415|||||||f||||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12716  a(OCoLC)165043715  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWU 20.52 aNational Institute of Dental and Craniofacial Research (U.S.)10aNIDCR meeting on research training /cNational Institute of Dental and Craniofacial Research.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Aug. 6, 2007).  aStreaming video (6 hr., 55 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jun. 9, 2005.3 a(CIT): The NIDCR Meeting on Research Training is an important component in the ongoing development of an Implementation Plan for the NIDCR Strategic Plan.  Training of both basic and clinical scientists is a cross-cutting area that affects many institutes, academic, public and private organizations and agencies.  The NIDCR Meeting on Research Training meeting will focus on what the NIDCR can learn from the experiences of these groups that can then be applied to the development and refinement of a training portfolio for basic and clinical oral health research.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDental Researchxeducation22aProgram Evaluation22aResearch Design 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12716  aAUTH01430cgm a22004214a 101268689DNLM20260227150937.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2005    mdu067 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12437  a(OCoLC)154875934  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 58.51 aPandey, Akhilesh,cM.D.10aProteomic approaches to study signal transduction pathways /cAkhilesh Pandey.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed June 1, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 6 min., 26 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 4, 2005.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aProteomics22aDatabases, Protein22aIsotope Labeling22aSignal Transduction 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12437  aAUTH01373cgm a22004334a 101289594DNLM20260227145311.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2006    mdu087|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13027  a(OCoLC)1019840678  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWF 3601 aFreire, Maria Cecilia Correa.10aPhilip Chen lecture :btechnology transfer meeting /cMaria Freire.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aPhilip Chen lecture  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 25, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 27 min., 57 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 20, 2006.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aTuberculosis, Pulmonaryxdrug therapy22aAntitubercular Agents22aDrug Design 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aPhilip Chen lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=10481  aAUTH01179cas a2200409 i 101658365DNLM20260224111553.0cr||||||||||||150511c20159999sz || p       |    0eng    a  2016243274  a2296-9381l2296-9357  a(OCoLC)952683429  aDNLMbengerdad20260224dDNLM  apcc  9Switzerland00aW1bKI586T0 aKidney diseases (Basel, Switzerland)10aKidney Dis (Basel)2dnlm10aKidney diseases. 1aBasel :bS. Karger  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan in 2015.0 aPubMedbv1n1, May 2015-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on:  2, 1, 16; title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted:  2, 1, 16.12aKidney Diseases 2aPeriodical0 x2296-93574 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/2618934 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101658365  aAUTH05550nam a2200421 i 9918955064506676DNLM20260204182453.0m     o  d        cr|cn#||||||||241202s2023    dk a    ob    000 0 eng    a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK609255  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Denmark00aW 84.11 aPanteli, Dimitra,eauthor.10aTransforming health service delivery :bwhat can policy-makers do to drive change? /cDimitra Panteli, Nicole Mauer, Juliane Winkelmann, Nick Fahy. Suszy Lessof, Natasha Azzopardi Muscat, Govin Permanand, Josep Figueras [editors]. 1aCopenhagen, Denmark :bEuropean Observatory on Health Systems and Policies,c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (32 pages)) :billustrations.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aPolicy brief,x1997-8073 ;v58  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 a1. The transformation of health service delivery is about achieving large-scale change to meet population health needs and people's expectations, despite resource constraints. Innovative ways of delivering health services, such as new models of care and technology-based solutions, can improve the quality and efficiency of care if they are widely and appropriately implemented.2. Health systems are so complex that change cannot be imposed from the top. Transforming service delivery means engaging with multiple actors, their different interests and interactions. Combining top-down and bottom-up initiatives throughout the process helps.3. The main role of policymakers in the transformation of health service delivery is 1.1. Providing leadership, by setting out a clear vision and strategy for change; aligning governance mechanisms; and cultivating shared commitment; and1.2. Ensuring sufficient resources for transformation, by combining enablers at the system level and support to organizations and people within the system.4. Developing a clear vision and strategy for change includes 1.1. Identifying transformation targets, by leveraging tools such as health system performance assessment, surveys and stakeholder consultation.1.2. Choosing possible solutions that are effective, affordable, equitable and implementable using tools such as ◦ Horizon scanning mechanisms and dedicated funding streams (national and international) to identify and/or test potential options.◦ Health Technology Assessment, to make a compelling case for change and help ensure solutions are good for patients and add value.◦ Analysis of the political economy around change to anticipate and address resistance.5. Governance mechanisms need to be adapted and aligned to facilitate change which means 1.1. Deciding what entities are involved in the change process and clarifying their relationships, the processes for making and implementing decisions and accountability.1.2. Adapting relevant regulatory elements (formal legislation, professional standards etc.).1.3. Adjusting payment and accountability mechanisms so they are geared towards transformation and its context.6. Generating the commitment to implement change is critical, because transformation requires substantial buy-in from stakeholders to succeed. Policy makers need the right skills to achieve this. A system level strategy informed by stakeholder analysis and articulating all the elements of stakeholder engagement, as well as coalition building with civil society, professional associations and others are essential.7. The resources for transformation at the local level must be aligned with its objectives. This means that policymakers must 1.1. Put in place sufficient funding channelled through tailored payment mechanisms.1.2. Use multi-professional and intersectoral workforce planning to put the right staff and skill-mix in the right place.1.3. Nurture organizational and clinical leadership by supporting training opportunities and empower frontline staff by ensuring opportunities for skills development and the space to implement changes on the ground.1.4. Support the necessary technical infrastructures, such as health information systems.1.5. Foster the availability of robust information on good practice and progress on transformation goals, and its communication.8. Transformation is not a ‘one-off' or a quick thing. 1.1. Transformation takes time so expectations need to be managed to sustain momentum.1.2. Effective change requires ongoing monitoring and adaptation, and good communication of successes and challenges.1.3. Success is very context specific so while international lessons and good practices can be shared, initiatives will always need to be tailored to local circumstances.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed Feb. 4, 2026).12aDelivery of Health Care22aHealth Resourcesxeconomics22aPolicy Making1 aMauer, Nicole,eauthor.1 aWinkelmann, Juliane,eauthor.1 aFahy, Nick,d1972-eauthor.2 aEuropean Observatory on Health Systems and Policies,eissuing body.2 aWorld Health Organization.bRegional Office for Europe,esponsoring body. 0aPolicy brief (World Health Organization. Regional Office for Europe) ;vno. 58.x1997-806540uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK609255/  aAUTH01439cgm a22004334a 101289544DNLM20260227151312.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2005    mdu080 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12857  a(OCoLC)84671677  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWB 8801 aHarrington, Anne,d1960-10aIs spirituality good for your health? :bhistorical reflections on an emerging research enterprise /cAnne Harrington.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCCAM distinguished lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 3, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 19 min., 15 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 28, 2005.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aSpiritual Therapies22aHistory, 20th Century22aReligion and Medicine22aSpiritual Therapiesxhistory22aSpirituality 2aWebcast 0aNCCAM distinguished lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12857  aAUTH03596cgm a22004334a 101493096DNLM20260227153022.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090106s2008    mdu073|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14837  a(OCoLC)297598972  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3541 aChan, Leighton.10aNew frontiers in traumatic brain injury :bevaluation and treatment /cLeighton Chan.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Clinical Center's 2008 medicine for the public lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 21, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 13 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 16, 2008.3 a(CIT): NIH Clinical Center's 2008 Medicine for the Public Lecture Series  Because of traumatic brain injury, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention estimate, more than 5 million Americans will need life-long help to perform daily activities. The U.S. Department of Defense's Deployment Health Clinical Center reports that the rate of combat-related brain injuries in service members returning from Iraq and Afghanistan appears to be higher than in military previous conflicts. Dr. Leighton Chan will talk about current research aimed at broader understanding of the effects of traumatic brain injury and more personalized treatments for it.   Dr. Leighton Chan is the Chief of Rehabilitation Medicine at the NIH Clinical Center. He received his B.A. degree from Dartmouth College, Hanover, New Hampshire with a major in political science. He graduated from the UCLA School of Medicine in 1990. Dr. Chan then completed postgraduate training in Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation at the University of Washington. During his training he also obtained a Master of Science degree in rehabilitation science. Subsequently, he completed a Robert Wood Johnson Clinical Scholar Fellowship, earned a master of public health degree at the University of Washington School of Public Health and was a Congressional Fellow for the Honorable Jim McDermott (Washington).  From 1994 to 2006, Dr. Chan was on the faculty of the University of Washington"s Department of Rehabilitation Medicine. From 2002 to 2006, he was associate professor. He is board certified in physical medicine and rehabilitation and in electrodiagnostic medicine.  His research interests include health services research, quality of care given to Medicare beneficiaries, and Medicare payment policy issues. He has published more than 60 peer reviewed articles and numerous book chapters. Dr. Chan"s awards include the Young Academician Award from the Association of Academic Physiatrists and a Presidential Citation Award for excellence in research from the American Academy of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation. He was elected to the Institute of Medicine in 2007.  http://www.cc.nih.gov/about/news/mfp.shtml.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBrain Injuriesxdiagnosis12aBrain Injuriesxtherapy 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center. 0aMedicine for the public40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14837  aAUTH01732cgm a22004454a 101268230DNLM20260227150219.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu055 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11555  a(OCoLC)70873953  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWW 2701 aSieving, Paul A.10aRetinitis pigmentosa :bgenetic retinal photoreceptor degenerations /cPaul A. Sieving.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 12, 2006).  aStreaming video (54 min., 38 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded July 22, 2003.3 a(CIT): 2003 Summer Lecture Series for Students  Research at the Frontier Paul A. Sieving, M.D., Ph.D., Director, National Eye Institute.  For more information, visit the Office of Intramural Training and Education.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aRetinitis Pigmentosa22aIntercellular Signaling Peptides and Proteins22aPhotoreceptor Cells, Vertebrate.22aRhodopsin 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11555  aAUTH01288cam a2200337 i 9917770783406676DNLM20260212091353.0960129r18791879ilu           000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)81929678  aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5511 no.191 aPrince, David,d1816-1889,eauthor.14aThe sanitation of small cities :bsoil, drainage, sewerage, and the disposal of sewage /cby David Prince... 1a[Chicago?] :b[publisher not identified],c[1879]  a8 pages ;c25 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  aDate of publication supplied from IndexCat.  a"From the Transactions of the Illinois State Medical Society for 1879."--Page 1.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM12aSanitary Engineering22aSanitationxmethods 2aIllinois  aAUTH02798cgm a2200469 i 9918573161506676DNLM20260227163224.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2023    mdu061|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32915  a(OCoLC)1398466721  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQV 3600aNeural circuits and therapeutics for pain unpleasantness and its cognitive modulation /cGregory Scherrer. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded January 30, 2023.3 a(CIT): NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series  Pain and Opioids. Pain is a complex and multidimensional experience with sensory and emotional components. The members of the Scherrer Lab aim to elucidate the mechanisms by which our nervous system generates the different dimensions of pain experience, at the genetic, molecular, cellular, neural circuit, and behavioral levels, using the mouse as a model system. We also seek to resolve the mechanisms of action of opioids and understand how these drugs alter activity in neural circuits to produce analgesia, but also deleterious side effects such as tolerance, addiction and respiratory depression. To this aim, we investigate the functional organization of our endogenous opioid system and the localization, trafficking and signaling properties of opioid receptors in neurons in vivo. Collectively, these studies expand our understanding of pain neurobiology and the mechanisms of action of opioids to develop solutions against chronic pain and the opioid epidemic, by identifying novel non-addictive drug targets to treat pain and strategies to disassociate opioid analgesia from side effects.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed September 18, 2023).12aReceptors, Opioidxmetabolism12aReceptors, Opioidxtherapeutic use22aPain Perceptionxdrug effects22aChronic Painxdrug therapy22aAnalgesics, Opioidxpharmacokinetics22aAnalgesics, Non-Narcoticxtherapeutic use 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aScherrer, Gregory,espeaker. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=48964  aAUTH02125cgm a2200517 i 9918367462006676DNLM20260227162106.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220318s2022    mdu179|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32257  a(OCoLC)1350874699  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWS 1302 aBOND-KIDS (Project).bWorking Group 2.bMeetingd(2022 March 11 :cOnline)eauthor.10aBiomarkers of nutrition for development - knowledge indicating dietary sufficiency /cNICHD, NIH.1 aBOND-KIDS :bBiomarkers of Nutrition for Development - Knowledge Indicating Dietary Sufficiency (Working Group 2 Discussion) 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 59 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBOND-KIDS webinar series  aRecorded March 11, 2022.3 a(CIT): Biomarkers of Nutrition for Development - Knowledge Indicating Dietary Sufficiency: BOND-KIDS.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed October 28, 2022).22aBOND-KIDS (Project)12aNutritional Requirements12aChild22aNutritional Status22aChild Nutritional Physiological Phenomena22aEating22aFood Insecurity 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aBOND-KIDS (Project).bWorking Group 2,eorganizer. 0aBOND-KIDS webinar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44916  aAUTH02248cgm a2200505 i 9918384668906676DNLM20260227162147.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220426s2022    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32288  a(OCoLC)1355718385  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 26600aLymphocytes as a "living drug" for the treatment of cancer /cSteven A. Rosenberg. Emergence of the NIH cGMP program to support patient care innovation / Anna Lau. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 0 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCC grand rounds  aRecorded March 30, 2022.3 a(CIT): Lymphocytes as a "Living Drug" for the Treatment of Cancer" and Emergence of the NIH cGMP Program to Support Patient Care Innovation  Anna Lau, MD Chief, Sterility Testing Service Department of Laboratory Medicine Clinical Center, NIH  Steven A. Rosenberg, MD, PhD Chief Surgery Branch Senior Investigator Head, Tumor Immunology Section, NCI, NIH.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 12, 2022).22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aNeoplasmsxtherapy22aLymphocyte Transfusion22aBiological Products22aGovernment Regulation22aSterilizationxlegislation & jurisprudence 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aRosenberg, Steven A.,espeaker.1 aLau, Anna,espeaker.12aRosenberg, Steven A.tLymphocytes as a "living drug" for the treatment of cancer.12aLau, Anna.tEmergence of the NIH cGMP Program to support patient care innovation. 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44876  aAUTH01680cas a2200469 i 9918351172406676DNLM20260227090911.0m     o  d        cr#|||||||||||190521c20179999ii qr poo     0    0eng    a  2022229047  a1165803316  a2588-9842l2588-9834  a(OCoLC)1117862884z(OCoLC)1165803316  aLVTbengerdad20260227cLVTdOCLCQdSRUdOCLCQdBWNdOCLCFdDNLM  apcc  9India00aW110aBiomed Biotechnol Res J2dnlm00aBiomedical and biotechnology research journal.1 aBBRJ 1a[Mumbai, India] :bWolters Kluwer - Medknow,c[2017]-  a1 online resource  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1, issue 1 (July/September 2017).  aVolume 1 complete with two issues.  aSome volumes contain supplements.  aAlso issued in print.  aOfficial publication of Society of Biomedical & Biotechnical Scientists (SMBS).0 aVol. 1, issue 1 (July/September 2017); title from journal home page (publisher's website, viewed March 25, 2022).1 aVol. 6, issue 1 (January/March 2022) (viewed March 25, 2022).12aBiomedical Research22aBiotechnology 2aPeriodical2 aSociety of Biomedical & Biotechnology Scientists,eissuing body.0 x2588-983440uhttps://journals.lww.com/BBRJ/pages/default.aspx  aAUTH01591cgm a22004694a 101289577DNLM20260227151342.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2005    mdu143|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12965  a(OCoLC)84667333  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWP 4801 aSchiller, John T.10aHPV vaccines to prevent cervical cancer /cJohn Schiller and E. Hawk.1 iTitle on screen banner:aTRACO, vaccines and translational research1 aTranslational research in clinical oncology  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from PowerPoint (viewed Oct. 20, 2006).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 23 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 12, 2005.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aUterine Cervical Neoplasmsxprevention & control12aViral Vaccinesxtherapeutic use22aBiomedical Research22aPapillomaviridae22aPapillomavirus Infectionsxprevention & control22aPapillomavirus Vaccines 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12965  aAUTH01921cgm a2200445 a 101595194DNLM20260227154335.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u121102s2012    mdu067|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17615  a(OCoLC)818316864  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2021 aHussain, P.q(Perwez)10aInflammation & epidemiology /cP. Hussain, S. Ambs.1 aInflammation and epidemiology  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 7 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aRedox biology lecture series  aRecorded Oct. 16, 2012.3 a(CIT): The Redox Biology course will be held on Tuesday afternoons (except the   Oct. 4 lectures) from 4:00-6:00 PM starting September 25 through   November 7, 2012 on the Bethesda campus in Bldg. 50, Rooms 1227/1233. In   addition, it will be videocast to the Frederick National Lab at Bldg.   549, Rm. A.   Each lecture will be 50 minutes with ample time for discussion and analysis. A handout for each lecture will be distributed on site.The participation of NIH postdoctoral and clinical fellows interested in redox biology is encouraged.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aNeoplasmsxetiology22aEpidemiologic Methods22aInflammationxcomplications 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aRedox biology seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17615  aAUTH03855cgm a2200433 i 101737002DNLM20260227155827.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u181102s2018    mdu070|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:26128  a(OCoLC)1078196632  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQV 1260 aMolecular mechanisms of neurotransmitter release (Brunger)10aMolecular mechanisms of neurotransmitter release /cAxel Brunger. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2018]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 10 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience series seminar  aRecorded October 22, 2018.3 a(CIT): NIH Neuroscience Series Seminar  Nerve cells communicate by releasing the contents of neurotransmitter-bearing synaptic vesicles into the space between adjoining cells. This process depends on a handful of proteins that promote vesicle and nerve cell membrane fusion. The Brunger lab team uses structural and biophysical tools to capture this machinery at different stages of vesicle fusion. These structures then provide the framework for further investigations, using microscopy and live neurons, into the functional and dynamic aspects of the system.  SNARE proteins, found in both nerve cell and vesicle membranes, set the stage for fusion by zipping together into a parallel, four-helix bundle that juxtaposes the two membranes. Brunger and his collaborators determined the first x-ray crystal structure of the neuronal SNARE complex, as well as the structures of other key components of the synaptic release machinery. Recently, the Brunger"s team visualized the SNARE complex bound to the Ca2+-sensor synaptotagmin-1 and to the regulator complexin, revealing two interfaces that are essential for fast synchronous release of neurotransmitters. The structure of this three-part complex suggests that it is in a primed and locked state. Action-potential-driven Ca2+ ions bind to the synaptotagmin proteins, unlock the complex, and trigger membrane fusion on a sub-millisecond timescale.   After fusion has occurred, SNARE complexes are recycled by the ATPase NSF, which breaks down the SNARE complex into its individual components. The Brunger team visualized this molecular machine at near-atomic level and obtained the first glimpses of how this SNARE-recycling machine works. The SNARE complex resembles a rope with a left-handed twist, and NSF uses adapter proteins called SNAPs to grasp the "rope" in multiple places. The SNAPs wrap around the SNARE complex with a right-handed twist, suggesting that the disassembly occurs via a simple unwinding motion that frees the zipped SNARE proteins.  The Brunger team is also using structural and functional studies to explore other machinery relevant to neurotransmitter release, such as factors involved in priming and pre-synaptic plasticity. Their research may one day provide new possibilities for targeting therapeutics to control neurotransmitter release.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 29, 2018).12aNeurotransmitter Agentsxmetabolism22aSNARE Proteinsxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aBrünger, Axel T.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?26128  aAUTH01463cgm a22004334a 101267906DNLM20260227145803.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu069 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10919  a(OCoLC)68207244  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3071 aMiller, Earl Keith.10aPrefrontal cortex and the neural basis of cognition /cEarl Miller.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 25, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 8 min., 15 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 11, 2000.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aPrefrontal Cortexxcytology22aMacaca22aMental Processesxphysiology22aModels, Animal22aNeuronsxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10919  aAUTH02003cgm a2200445 i 9918282191306676DNLM20260227161600.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u211008s2021    mdu883|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31956  a(DNLM)CIT:31962  a(OCoLC)1305155589  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWB 1462 aBeyond the Symptom: The Biology of Fatigue (Workshop)d(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aBeyond the symptom :bthe biology of fatigue /cNIH Blueprint and Sleep Research Society.1 aBiology of fatigue 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (14 hr., 43 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 27 & 28, 2021.3 a(CIT): On September 27-28, 2021 NIH will host a virtual workshop intitled Beyond the Symptom: Biology of Fatigue to bring together individuals who do research on fatigue, health care providers who see patients with fatigue, and individuals who have fatigue and patient advocates.  This workshop is a joint workshop of the NIH Blueprint Neuroscience Research Program and the Sleep Research Society.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 15, 2022).12aFatigue22aBiomedical Research 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNIH Blueprint for Neuroscience Research,esponsoring body.2 aSleep Research Society,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4256340uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42565  aAUTH02148cgm a2200469 i 101773047DNLM20260227160352.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201105s2020    mdu064|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30356  a(OCoLC)1226370155  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 26700aTherapeutic opportunities in glycoscience /cCarolyn R. Bertozzi. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 4 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded October 21, 2020.3 a(CIT): NIH Wednesday Afternoon Lecture  Cell surface glycans constitute a rich biomolecular dataset that drives both normal and pathological processes. Their "readers" are glycan-binding receptors that can engage in cell-cell interactions and cell signaling. Our research focuses on mechanistic studies of glycan/receptor biology and applications of this knowledge to new therapeutic strategies. Our recent efforts center on pathogenic glycans in the tumor microenvironment and new therapeutic modalities based on the concept of targeted degradation.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 20, 2020).12aNeoplasmsxdrug therapy22aNeoplasmsxgenetics22aLysosomesxmetabolism22aLysosomesxgenetics22aAutophagyxdrug effects22aGlycomics 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aBertozzi, Carolyn R.,d1966-espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38867  aAUTH02623cgm a2200469 i 101774276DNLM20260227160425.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu433 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31384  a(DNLM)CIT:31388  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWS 1072 aDown Syndrome Research: The Intersection of Basic Science and Clinical Cohort Development (Workshop)d(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aDown Syndrome Research :bThe Intersection of Basic Science and Clinical Cohort Development /cOffice of the Director, NIH, INCLUDE Project Working Group. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (7 hr., 13 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 9-10, 2020.3 a(CIT): This virtual workshop will be sponsored by the Office of the Director, National Institutes of Health, in conjunction with the trans-NIH INCLUDE (INvestigation of Co-occurring conditions across the Lifespan to Understand Down syndromE) Project Working Group. The workshop will focus on two components of the INCLUDE project: Component 1: Conduct targeted, high-risk, high-reward basic science studies on chromosome 21.  Component 2: Assemble a large study population of individuals with Down syndrome across the lifespan. Tune in to hear researchers, data scientists, self-advocates, and other members of the Down syndrome research community give presentations on the current state of the science and gaps with regard to basic science and cohort development, and to participate in breakout sessions to discuss these topics.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed January 7, 2021).12aDown Syndrome22aBiomedical Researchxmethods22aResearch Support as Topic22aCohort Studies22aResearch Design 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of the Director,esponsoring body.2 aINCLUDE Project,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4003340uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40034  aAUTH02665cgm a2200469 i 101774280DNLM20260227160434.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu062|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31390  a(OCoLC)1236084531  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWU 140.500aStem cells in jaw growth and disease /cMillie Embree. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIDCR clinical research fellowship grand round  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded November 13, 2020.3 a(CIT): The temporomandibular joint (TMJ) is the synovial joint of craniofacial complex and is critical for dental occlusion, mastication, and speech. TMJ trauma and diseases, such as osteoarthritis, are debilitating and compromise quality of life. Current TMJ treatments are two-fold, involving either palliative care or invasive total joint replacement surgeries. There are no minimally invasive, regenerative TMJ therapies. We have identified TMJ-specific fibrocartilage stem cells that self-organize and regenerate cartilage, fat and vascularized bone. Here we define heterogeneous populations of TMJ fibrocartilage stem cells and the signals regulating their fate in jaw development and disease. Finally we therapeutically exploit endogenous fibrocartilage stem cells as a potential strategy for TMJ regeneration using a pre-clinal, large animal model. Our studies will contribute towards the development of a non-surgical, minimally invasive stem cell-based therapy for TMJ regeneration.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed February 2, 2021).12aTemporomandibular Joint Disordersxtherapy22aTemporomandibular Jointxgrowth & development22aFibrocartilagexcytology22aStem Cells22aBone Regenerationxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aEmbree, Millie,espeaker.2 aNational Institute of Dental and Craniofacial Research (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNIDCR clinical research fellowship grand rounds40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38905  aAUTH02328cgm a22004454a 101289601DNLM20260227151350.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2006    mdu069|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13046  a(OCoLC)84671872  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 7051 aShimojo, Shinsuke,d1955-10aCrossmodal integration :btowards general principles /cShinsuke Shimojo.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 27, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 9 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 6, 2006.3 a(CIT): Dr. Shimojo's research teams recently work on crossmodal integration. Based on their early findings on how much audition can affect vision not just quantitatively but also qualitatively, they have extended their quest for (a) integrations among other modalities, such as tactile-visual, (b) similarities between crossmodal and within-modal integrations, and (c) development of crossmodal integration in infants. Their recent work on these issues indicates common principles of neural computation that govern those seemingly diverse crossmodal phenomena. The NIH neuroscience seminar series features lectures and discussions with leading neuroscientists. Sponsored by NINDS, NIMH, NIDCD, NIDA, NICHD, NEI, NIAAA and NIDCR, this year's series offers advances that span the areas of molecular, cellular, and developmental neuroscience as well as cognitive sciences and neuroscience related topics in pain and other diseases.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aSensationxphysiology22aBiomedical Research22aPerceptionxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13046  aAUTH02072cgm a22004214a 101322951DNLM20260227152341.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071207s2007    mdu347|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14128  a(OCoLC)212399815  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQY 252 aNCI Biospecimen Best Practices Forumd(2007 :cBoston, Mass.)10aNCI Biospecimen Best Practices Forum (Boston, MA) :bNovember 5, 2007 : the Conference Center at Harvard Medical School /csponsored by National Cancer Institute.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 25, 2008).  aStreaming video (5 hr., 47 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 5, 2007.3 a(CIT): The NCI is hosting a series of national forums on the NCI Best Practices for Biospecimen Resources, which outline technical, operational, ethical, legal and policy principles for biospecimen resources. The purpose of these forums is to educate and obtain feedback about the NCI Best Practices from a broad range of perspectives, including those of investigators, physicians, industry representatives, hospital administrators, cancer survivors, patient advocates, and the general public.   Acrobat Slides   Acrobat Slides   Acrobat Slides   Acrobat Slides   For more information, visit  http://www.nci-bestpractices-forum.com.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aSpecimen Handlingxstandards22aNeoplasmsxpathology 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14128  aAUTH03628cgm a2200493 i 101774307DNLM20260227160539.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu473 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31427  a(DNLM)CIT:31430  a(OCoLC)1240392157  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 506.52 aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19d(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19 /cNIAID, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (7 hr., 50 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded December 3-4, 2020.3 a(CIT): NIAID is hosting a virtual Workshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19 on December 3-4, 2020 to summarize existing knowledge on post-acute manifestations of COVID-19 and to identify key knowledge gaps.  The workshop will open with an overview of the current challenges, talks on clinical observations (both US and international), and some insights from the patient's perspective. Focus will switch to pathogenic features of coronaviruses as well as host immunological responses. The first day will wrap up with a series of talks on post-acute sequelae of COVID-19 as reported to date in various focus areas. The second day will start with a talk on the intersection of social determinants of health and race/ethnicity on post-acute COVID-19 sequelae and the charge to the breakout groups, who will dive deeper to identify key knowledge gaps regarding the sequelae in various focus areas. The workshop will be on the Zoom platform; however, due to space restraints on Zoom, it is also available on the videocast.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 23, 2021).12aCOVID-19xcomplications22aPandemics 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (U.S.),eissuing body.2 iAugmented by (work):aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19d(2020 :cOnline).tWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19 breakout session 1 (neurological/psychiatric/neuromuscular)2 iAugmented by (work):aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19d(2020 :cOnline).tWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19 breakout session 2 (cardiovascular)2 iAugmented by (work):aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19d(2020 :cOnline).tWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19 breakout session 3 (pulmonary)2 iAugmented by (work):aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19d(2020 :cOnline).tWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19 breakout session 4 (renal/GI/metabolic)2 iAugmented by (work):aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19d(2020 :cOnline).tWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19 breakout session 5 (immunologic/rheumatologic)2 iAugmented by (work):aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19d(2020 :cOnline).tWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19 breakout session 6 (pediatric)40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3887840uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38879  aAUTH01740cgm a2200481 i 101772197DNLM20260227160138.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201003s2020    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30284  a(OCoLC)1204632607  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aHV 6548.U50 aSuicide prevention (Horowitz)10aSuicide prevention :bfrom research to practice at NIH and beyond /cLisa Horowitz, Barbara Jordan, Deborah Snyder. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[20