uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/dia  aAUTH02055cgm a22004574a 101322941DNLM20260227152228.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071207s2007    mdu072|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14112  a(OCoLC)190751390  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 7041 aIadarola, Michael.10aGood pain, bad pain :bnew advances in pain mechanisms and treatments /cMichael Iadarola.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aMedicine for the public lecture series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Dec. 21, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 12 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 30, 2007.3 a(CIT) There are about 500,000 cancer deaths a year. Of those, about 50,000 people die without ever receiving relief from the excruciating pain caused by the disease. This lecture will cover the development of a novel pain therapy that selectively kills pain-causing cells while leaving other neurons intact. This results in permanent pain relief without the debilitating side effects of traditional pain-killing medications. Experiments in the laboratory and in animals have been successful, and testing in humans will begin soon. The speaker will also discuss the basis of pain and the history of pain treatment.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aPainxphysiopathology22aNerve Fibersxphysiology22aPain Management22aPain Measurement 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aMedicine for the public40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14112  aAUTH02300cgm a2200481 i 9918573277406676DNLM20260227163036.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2022    mdu070|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32810  a(OCoLC)1425218166  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 27400aUsing new definitions and tools to support alcohol recovery /cBrett Hagman, John F. Kelly, Laura Kwako, Helen Jack. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 10 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIAAA webinar  aRecorded December 6, 2022.3 a(CIT): NIAAA Webinar Series: Using New Definitions and Tools to Support Alcohol Recovery    12:00 - 12:05Welcome George F. Koob, Ph.D.  12:05 - 12:10A Brief Introduction to the NIAAA Research Definition of Recovery from DSM-5 Alcohol Use Disorder Brett Hagman, Ph.D.  12:10 - 12:30NIAAA"s New Recovery Definition: Utility and Uptake  John Kelly, Ph.D.   12:30 - 12:40 Brief Description of the Healthcare Professionals Core Resource Including the Recovery Module Laura Kwako, Ph.D.    12:40 - 12:50Using the Health Professionals Core Resource in Clinical Practice  Helen Jack, M.D.  12:50 - 1:15Q & A  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed February 27, 2024).12aAlcoholismxrehabilitation12aAlcoholismxtherapy22aEducation, Medical, Continuingxmethods 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aHagman, Brett,espeaker.1 aKelly, John F. ,cPh. D.,espeaker.1 aKwako, Laura,espeaker.1 aJack, Helenq(Helen E.),espeaker.2 aNational Institute on Alcohol Abuse and Alcoholism (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNIAAA webinar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=46423  aAUTH01528cgm a2200421 i 9918573264806676DNLM20260227162949.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2022    mdu058|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32779  a(OCoLC)1392040330  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 17600aNovel interventions for obsessive-compulsive disorder /cCarolyn Rodriguez. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (58 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aClinical Center grand rounds  aRecorded November 16, 2022.3 a(CIT): Novel Interventions for Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed July 26, 2023).12aObsessive-Compulsive Disorderxdrug therapy22aObsessive-Compulsive Disorderxpsychology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aRodriguez, Carolyn I.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=48593  aAUTH03395cgm a2200469 i 9918367463106676DNLM20260227162042.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220318s2022    mdu428|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32240  a(DNLM)CIT:32243  a(OCoLC)1331665800  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 3002 aBrain Behavior Quantification and Synchronization Workshopd(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aBrain Behavior Quantification and Synchronization Workshop /cBRAIN Initiative, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (7 hr., 8 min.) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded March 1 & 2, 2022.3 a(CIT): The objective of the Brain Behavior Quantification and Synchronization (BBQS) workshop is to stimulate discussion on the advances necessary for a more comprehensive understanding of behavior, by bringing together innovators in sensor and data analysis, translational and basic cognitive neuroscientists conducting human and animal studies, evolutionary, behavioral and social scientists, computational scientists, and ethicists.  This effort will support the development and validation of next-generation tools and analytic approaches to precisely and comprehensively quantify spontaneous and learned behaviors and to integrate this multi-modal, high-density behavioral data with simultaneously recorded brain activity and data from the physical and social environment. The focus of Day 1 sessions is on the big picture of the BBQS effort and its goals, and to identify opportunities for cross-fertilization across the fields participating in the workshop. The focus of Day 2 sessions is on parallel breakout discussions (one on human/clinical and one on organismal/comparative research). Researchers will identify the gaps and limitations in existing technologies and discuss strategies for achieving the goals of BBQS.  Experts in the technology space will present their recent advances to facilitate multi-disciplinary teambuilding. Innovators of sensor technologies will be included so that they can hear from researchers what their unmet needs are and where sensor development/modification could be useful.  This will be foundational in building a network of neuroscientists, behavioral and social systems scientists, and engineers interested in comprehensive measurement and analysis of behavior.https://event.roseliassociates.com/bbqs-workshop/.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed June 15, 2022).12aBehaviorxphysiology12aBrainxphysiology22aBrain Mapping22aInterdisciplinary Communication22aCognitive Neuroscience22aCognitive Psychology 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aBRAIN Initiative,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4454940uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44551  aAUTH02893cgm a2200421 i 101686958DNLM20260227155544.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160707s2016    mdu058|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19747  a(OCoLC)953601710  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 10204aThe genetics of neurodevelopment, a first step toward precision medicine /cElliott Sherr. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (58 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aRecorded June 13, 2016.3 a(CIT): NIH Neuroscience Series Seminar Dr. Elliott Sherr is the founder and Principal Investigator of the Brain Development Research Program. Dr. Sherr is a Child Neurologist, and he is invested in elucidating the genetics of neurodevelopmental disorders such as autism and epilepsy, with a particular focus in structural brain malformations such as agenesis of the corpus callosum (ACC).  In addition to studying the genetics of neurodevelopmental disorders, Dr.  Sherr is also leading the BDRP to better understand how brain structure and function is related to behavior using advanced imaging tools such as fMRI, DTI (diffusion tensor imaging) and MEG technology. In a similar manner, Dr. Sherr is the Principal Investigator of the neuroimaging core of the Simons Variation in Individuals Project (Simons VIP) that studies the most common known cause of autism, chromosomal rearrangements at 16p11.2 The goal of this project is to define the structural and functional relationships within the brain in autistic individuals, with the hope that this knowledge can eventually be used as an essential tool for understanding autism biology and for monitoring the success of potential novel treatments. In addition to his efforts at the BDRP and the Simons VIP, Dr. Sherr co-directs the neurometabolic program and clinic at UCSF, and is an active attending physician in the division of child neurology.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 11, 2016).12aNervous System Physiological Phenomenaxgenetics12aNervous Systemxgrowth & development 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aSherr, Elliott,d1962-espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19747  aAUTH02344cgm a22004934a 101268869DNLM20260227151052.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2005    mdu085 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12492  a(OCoLC)165043766  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 2951 aSmith, Bradleyq(Bradley T.)10aAtlantic storm /cBradley Smith.1 iTitle on screen banner:aAtlantic storm :bexercise simulated multifocal introduction of smallpox by terrorists  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen banner (viewed July 25, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 24 min., 39 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 3, 2005.3 a(CIT): Exercise illuminates transatlantic leaders' reactions to bioterror attack,  Atlantic storm spotlights need for preparedness at international level, Baltimore, MD -- January 17, 2005.  What would world leaders do if they were faced with a bioterrorist attack on cities around the world?  How would they react to a fast-moving and deliberately caused epidemic?  These questions were raised Friday at Atlantic Storm, a table-top exercise that simulated a smallpox attack on the nations of the transatlantic community.  The ministerial bioterrorism exercise was presented by the Center for Biosecurity of the University of Pittsburgh Medical Center, the Center for Transatlantic Relations of Johns Hopkins University, and the Transatlantic Biosecurity Network.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government.  aOpen-captioned.12aBioterrorism22aDisaster Planning22aInternationality22aSecurity Measures22aSmallpox 2aEurope 2aNorth America 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12492  aAUTH02420cgm a2200529 i 101774290DNLM20260227160458.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu061|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31403  a(OCoLC)1246215120  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 2400aNIH virtual town hall, November 2020, 1:00 p.m. ET :bNIH virtual town hall on NIH's response to the COVID-19 pandemic /cFrancis Collins, Lawrence Tabak, Julie Berko.1 aNIH virtual town hall on NIH's response to the COVID-19 pandemic 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded November 20, 2020.3 a(CIT): 6th NIH Virtual Town Hall  NIH Director Dr. Francis Collins hosts the 6th Virtual Town Hall with NIH Principal Deputy Director Dr. Lawrence Tabak and NIH Office of Human Resources Director Julie Berko to provide an update on NIH's scientific contributions to ending the pandemic, return efforts, pandemic fatigue and how to persist in keeping safe and healthy during the holiday season. Dr. Collins also expresses his thanks to staff and performs "Thanksgiving Eve".  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 8, 2021).22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aAcademies and Institutes12aBiomedical Research22aCoronavirus Infectionsxprevention & control22aPandemics22aUnited States Government Agencies22aSafety Management22aWorkplace 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCollins, Francis S.,espeaker,emoderator.1 aTabak, Lawrence A.,d1951-espeaker.1 aBerko, Julie,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40080  aAUTH01703cas a2200505 i 101774447DNLM20260227090908.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||200302c20209999nyuqr poo     0   a0eng    a  20202018560 a2690-7097l2690-7097  a(OCoLC)1142634294  aDLCbengerdad20260227cDLCdDLCdOCLCFdDNLM  ansdpapcc  9United States00aRC70500aW110a610.7117322310aATS Sch2dnlm0 aATS sch. 0aATS scholar00aATS scholar.1 aAmerican Thoracic Society scholar 1aNew York, NY :bAmerican Thoracic Society,c[2020]-  a1 online resource  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1, issue 1 (2020).1 aMEDLINEbv5n1, Mar. 2024-0 aPubMedbv1n1, Mar. 2020-0 aPMC  aArticles are published on an ongoing basis and organized in quarterly issues.  aDescription based on: Volume 1, issue 1 (2020); title from journal home page (publisher's website, viewed January 27, 2021).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 1, issue 4 (viewed January 27, 2021).12aRespiratory Tract Diseases22aRespiratory System 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Thoracic Society,eissuing body.40uhttps://www.atsjournals.org/journal/ats-scholar40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101774447  aAUTH01489cgm a22004334a 101267358DNLM20260227144926.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s1998    mdu000 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:9950  a(DNLM)CIT:9951  a(DNLM)CIT:9952  a(OCoLC)144567990  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 35400aRehabilitation of persons with traumatic injury /cNIH Office of Disease Prevention.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health],cc1998.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 4, 2007).  aStreaming video (20 hr., 46 min., 51 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 26, 1998.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: National Institutes of Health(c)1998.  aOpen-captioned.12aBrain Injuriesxrehabilitation 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Disease Prevention.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?995040uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?995140uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?9952  aAUTH03466cgm a2200481 i 9918434185906676DNLM20260227162503.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220706s2022    mdu582|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32450  a(DNLM)CIT:32457  a(OCoLC)1380822954  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQV 268.52 aAssay Guidance Workshop on 3D Tissue Models for Antiviral Drug Developmentd(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aAssay Guidance Workshop on 3D Tissue Models for Antiviral Drug Development /cNCATS, NIH.1 aAssay Guidance Workshop on Three Dimensional Tissue Models for Antiviral Drug Development 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (9 hr., 8 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 7 & 8, 2022.3 a(CIT): The National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (NCATS) Assay Guidance Manual (AGM) program is hosting a two-day workshop that will cover a broad range of critical concepts, including practical approaches and best practices, for developing standardized 3D cellular assays with the hope of helping the community to successfully develop therapeutics for future pandemic threats. This workshop is jointly organized by NCATS, the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (NIAID) and the Bill &amp; Melinda Gates Foundation. The overall goal of this workshop is to help scientists establish robust, reproducible, scalable, consistent, advanced 3D tissue models to study pandemic threat viruses.Objectives* Introduce participants to the available 3D tissue models and discuss challenges in building these models as well as their utility and limitations.* Provide case studies on how these available 3D tissue models have been utilized in antiviral drug discovery and development as well as tools for understanding and modeling infectious diseases, including studying viral infection and pathogenesis.* Provide guidelines and considerations for developing robust and reproducible 3D tissue models that can be used for testing therapeutics.* Discuss challenges in affordability, accessibility, transferability, and reproducibility of these 3D tissue models and the need to set standards on utilization and readouts.* Discuss the advantages vs disadvantages of these 3D tissue models.* Generate a roadmap to help scientists develop robust and reproducible 3D tissue models to study viruses.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 24, 2023).12aAntiviral Agents22aDrug Development22aTissue Engineering22aCell Culture Techniques, Three Dimensional22aModels, Biological22aGuidelines as Topic 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (U.S.).bAssay Guidance Manual (Program),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4539940uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45401  aAUTH03495cgm a2200409 i 101774303DNLM20260227160532.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu061|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31422  a(OCoLC)1240392123  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 506.300aGenetic studies illuminating pathways important for controlling COVID disease /cHelen Su ; COVID-19 Scientific Interest Group. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded December 3, 2020.3 a(CIT): COVID-19 Scientific Interest Group Dr. Su is chief of the Human Immunological Diseases Section in the NIAIDS Laboratory of Clinical Immunology & Microbiology.  Her lab strives to understand the molecular mechanisms regulating the human immune system and how their derangements cause disease, with the objective of improving diagnosis and treatment.  Her lab studies patients with a spectrum of poorly characterized, inherited immunodeficiencies and autoimmune diseases who lack molecular diagnoses.  Dr. Su is particularly interested in combined immunodeficiencies that lead to susceptibility to viral infections and the new biological insights that studying these patients can provide.  Lecture summary: Only a small proportion of people who become infected with SARS-CoV-2 become critically ill.  Understanding the genetic determinants underlying such outcomes is the goal of our laboratory, in collaboration with the NIAID COVID-19 Consortium and the COVID Human Genetic Effort.  Since monogenic inborn errors of immunity can underlie susceptibility to respiratory viruses, we hypothesized that critical COVID-19 disease can similarly reflect an inborn error of immunity.  We found that rare genetic variants impacting the type I IFN signaling pathway were associated with life-threatening disease.  Functional studies in several showed impaired ability to produce type I IFN and restrict SARS-CoV-2 replication in vitro, which could be restored by the wild-type gene.  Importantly, some patients without such genetic lesions instead had neutralizing autoantibodies against type I IFNs.  These autoantibodies were highly potent and capable of abolishing protection against SARS-CoV-2 replication in vitro.  Together, inherited and acquired defects in the type I IFN pathway accounted for up to 150f critically ill COVID-19 patients in our cohort.  These results underscore the importance of type I IFNs in host immunity against SARS-CoV-2 infection and suggest this pathway as a potential target for therapeutic intervention.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 25, 2021).12aCOVID-19xgenetics12aCOVID-19xetiology22aCOVID-19ximmunology 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aSu, Helen,espeaker.1 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bCOVID-19 Scientific Interest Group,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38875  aAUTH02865cgm a2200505 i 9918503179206676DNLM20260227162743.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u221105s2022    mdu077|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32677  a(OCoLC)1355718686  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 26.55.I400aShutting the digital back door :bcreating tech justice with health data liquidity and the digital humanities /cKim Gallon. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 17 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNLM history talks  a"NLM and NEH"  aRecorded October 4, 2022.3 a(CIT): This talk explores the concept of the digital back door in health care, "technological processes and tools used in health care, such as racially biased algorithms, infrastructural limitations, and dirty data" and its historical roots in structural and systemic racism. The methodological use of the digital humanities offers an opportunity to eliminate digital back doors by creating more humanistic and equitable health information technology (health IT) that address health disparities and inequities. Data liquidity, health information and data that is more computationally agile, usable, and interoperable between patients and clinicians, fosters a reinterpretation of health data into voice, image and text that is both conducted and conveyed through the digital humanities. The talk concludes with the challenges of dismantling health IT praxis and infrastructure that are antithetical to the digital humanities and data liquidity.  Dr. Gallon"s talk is co-sponsored by the National Endowment for the Humanities as part of the NLM/NEH partnership to collaborate on research, education, and career initiatives.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed December 20, 2022).12aHealth Information Exchange12aSystemic Racism22aDigital Divide22aHealthcare Disparities22aHealth Inequities22aSocial Determinants of Health 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aGallon, Kim T.,espeaker.2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aNational Endowment for the Humanities,esponsoring body. 0aNLM history talks40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=46001  aAUTH01165cas a2200421 a 8303162DNLM20260227090959.0cr|cn ||||||||830505c19829999gw  x p       0||| 0ger    a0722-1541l0722-1541  aNERVDI  a(DNLM)N11790000(s)  a(OCoLC)09651279  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 ager  9Germany00aW1bNE207J 0aNervenheilkunde00aNervenheilkunde.  aStuttgart ; New York :bF.K. Schattauer,c[1982-2 aStuttgart :bSchattauer3132018- :aStuttgart :bThieme  av. :bill.  aMonthly,b2006-  aFrequency varies,b1982-2005  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 a1, 1/82 (Feb. 1982)-  aTitle from cover.  aAlso issued online. 2aNeurology 2aPsychiatry 2aPeriodical0 x2567-57884 uhttps://www.thieme-connect.de/products/ejournals/journal/10.1055/s-00034916  aAUTH02453cgm a2200469 i 101686952DNLM20260227155526.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160707s2016    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19737  a(OCoLC)953846636  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 53600aHepatitis C and beyond :bnever a dull moment /cCharles M. Rice. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture. George Khoury lecture  aRecorded June 8, 2016.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series  The Annual George Khoury Lecture  From the emergence of a mystery virus associated with post-transfusion hepatitis to the discovery of the hepatitis C virus (HCV), we are now witnessing a remarkable new era of highly effective, curative treatments. Despite this, challenges remain to effectively implement these medical advances on a national and global scale. For the molecular virologist, many questions remain unanswered. Why are clinical isolates of HCV so difficult to culture? What is the link between chronic HCV and liver cancer? Where did HCV come from and should we rest on our laurels assuming that hepacivirus-associated disease will soon vanish? These and other topics will be the subject of this Khoury lecture.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 13, 2016).12aHepatitis Cximmunology22aAntiviral Agentsxtherapeutic use22aHepacivirusxgenetics22aHepacivirusximmunology22aHepatitis Cxdrug therapy22aHepatitis Cxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aRice, C. M.q(Charles M.),espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pGeorge Khoury lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19737  aAUTH02308cgm a22004574a 101267339DNLM20260227144907.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu847 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:9931  a(DNLM)CIT:9932  a(OCoLC)184962794  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3002 aHuman Brain Project.bConferencen(9th :d2003 :cNational Institutes of Health)14aThe Human Brain Project Conference /cNational Institute of Mental Health.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 11, 2007).  aStreaming video (14 hr., 7 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 12-13, 2003.3 a(CIT): The goal of the Human Brain Project (HBP) is to provide funding to create a distributed system of web based databases, analytical tools and data driven computational models. This will provide a venue for data sharing and will enable the integration of structural, functional and dynamic data. Neuroinformatics combines the concepts and principles developed in neuroscience research with those from the fields of computer science and information technology (informatics) research. The creation of a distributed neuroscience information management system will allow scientists to more easily integrate data both within and across systems and create new knowledge toward understanding nervous system development and function.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aBrainxanatomy & histology12aBrainxphysiology22aBrain Mapping22aInformatics 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?993140uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?9932  aAUTH01335cgm a2200409 a 101595125DNLM20260227154502.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u121025s2012    mdu043|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17539  a(OCoLC)818316858  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQV 771.41 aSimons-Morton, Denise.10aRandomized controlled trials for efficacy and effectiveness research /cDenise Simons-Morton.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (43 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCC grand rounds for clinical fellows  aRecorded Aug. 29, 2012.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aDrug Therapy12aRandomized Controlled Trials as Topic 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aCC grand rounds for clinical fellows40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17539  aAUTH03181cgm a2200505 i 9918592677106676DNLM20260227163538.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230429s2023    mdu889|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33085  a(DNLM)CIT:33113  a(OCoLC)1464579270  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 2662 aNCI Symposium on Cancer Health Disparitiesn(4th :d2023 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10a4th NCI symposium on cancer health disparities (day 1 & 2) /cCenter for Cancer Research, NCI, NIH.1 aFourth National Cancer Institute symposium on cancer health disparities 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (14 hr., 49 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 4 & 5, 2023.3 a(CIT): Cancer health disparities affect millions of people across the United States and globally. Disparities in cancer burden are evident by geography, race/ethnicity, genetic ancestry, immigrant status, culture, gender, sexual orientation (LGBTQ+), and socioeconomic class, among other factors. Cancer disparities are not only due to barriers in access to health care, but also due to cultural barriers, structural racism and environmental disadvantage, ancestry-related risk factors, persistent co-morbidities, and chronic stress exposure because of discrimination and social isolation. The challenge of reducing and eliminating health disparities and achieving equity has been largely attributed to the complex interactions among these various determinants.The symposium will highlight a range of topics to address the systemic barriers and racism that lead to health disparities and showcase ongoing research being done in the social, clinical, epidemiologic, and basic science disciplines. We seek to feature challenges and solutions, and the programs that were developed to address cancer health disparities from researchers in the field and within the intramural NCI program.For more information go to https://ncifrederick.cancer.gov/events/conferences/4thNCICHD.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed October 25, 2024).12aNeoplasmsxtherapy22aHealthcare Disparities22aSocioeconomic Disparities in Health22aMinority Health22aHealth Services Accessibilityxstandards22aMedical Oncology 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aCenter for Cancer Research (National Cancer Institute (U.S.)),ehost institution.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4942140uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49423  aAUTH02853cgm a22004934a 101267571DNLM20260227145248.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu070 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10387  a(OCoLC)68980158  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWF 2001 aCassell, Gail H.10aGlobal health inequities and the critical role of public/private partnerships /cGail Cassell.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 2, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 9 min., 39 sec. : sd., col.).  a"FIC 35th anniversary lecture series"--PowerPoint slide.  aRecorded June 12, 2003.  aIn this lecture, Gail Cassell of the Eli Lilly Company discusses the implications of a rising global population, particularly in disease-prone regions. She notes that population is rapidly growing in eight countries in particular, seven of which are developing nations  also afflicted with high rates of tuberculosis (TB). For example, India's population increases more in one week than does the European Union's in a year. Crowded conditions and lack of treatment result in rising numbers of TB infections and death. Cassell reports that in 1997, she received a call about Paul Farmer and Jim Kim's Partners in Health initiative, and its acute need for tuberculosis drugs known to be effective against multi-drug- resistant TB. Eli Lilly contributed these drugs for Partners in Health's work in Peru, and after four months, the initiative reported an 83.3 percent cure rate for those treated for multi-drug resistant TB.  Eli Lilly also helped to train community workers, many of whom had little previous schooling, and took steps to bring the drugs and treatment method to other countries.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aTuberculosis, Multidrug-Resistantxeconomics12aTuberculosis, Multidrug-Resistantxtherapy22aDelivery of Health Care22aDeveloping Countries22aDirectly Observed Therapy22aPrivate Sector22aTuberculosis, Multidrug-Resistantxepidemiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10387  aAUTH02921cgm a2200481 a 101601866DNLM20260227154535.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130208s2013    mdu071       fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17774  a(OCoLC)828715812  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 530.11 aWalport, Mark.10aGlobal health :bfrom John Snow to genome science /cSir Mark Walport.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 11 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aDavid E. Barmes global health lecture  aRecorded Jan. 30, 2013.3 a(CIT): Fogarty International Center and the National Institute of Dental and Craniofacial Research present the David E. Barmes Global Health Lecture:  Global Health: From John Snow to Genome Science Sir Mark Walport is Director of the Wellcome Trust, a global charitable foundation dedicated to improving health, and a partner on NIH's H3Africa initiative, intended to develop genomics capacity across Africa.   Before joining the Trust, Sir Mark was professor of medicine and head of the division of medicine at Imperial College London. He has been a member of the Prime Minister's Council for Science and Technology since 2004. He is also a member of the India UK CEO Forum, the UK India Round Table and the advisory board of Infrastructure UK and a non-executive member of the Office for Strategic Coordination of Health Research. He is a member of a number of international advisory bodies.  He has undertaken independent reviews for the UK Government on the use and sharing of personal information in the public and private sectors. He received a knighthood in the 2009 New Year Honors List for services to medical research and was elected as Fellow of The Royal Society in 2011. Sir Mark will become the UK's Chief Scientific Adviser on April 1, 2013.  The annual David E. Barmes Global Health Lecture is sponsored by the National Institute of Dental and Craniofacial Research and the Fogarty International Center.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aSnow, John,d1813-1858.12aGlobal Health12aHealth Status Disparities22aGenomics22aHealthcare Disparities22aPublic Healthxhistory22aSocioeconomic Factors. 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aDavid E. Barmes global health lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17774  aAUTH02548cgm a2200457 i 9918226877606676DNLM20260227161216.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210615s2021    mdu693|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31712  a(DNLM)CIT:31716  a(OCoLC)1288657422  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.52 aAnnual Vivian Pinn Symposiumn(5th :d2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aFifth Annual Vivian Pinn Symposium /cORWH, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (11 hr., 33 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded May 11 & 12, 2021.3 a(CIT): The Fifth Vivian Pinn Symposium will serve as a forum to communicate and disseminate broadly across the biomedical research enterprise about how sex and gender influences are being accounted for within research. The Symposium will explore current gaps or barriers within different sectors of the biomedical research enterprise, introduce areas of scientific opportunity that the study of sex and gender presents, and underscore its impact on science and public health, particularly women"s health. The agenda will include panels, presentations, talks, and facilitated discussions on cross-sector integration of sex and gender in biomedical research. Breakout sessions will be used to generate ideas on the most effective strategies and system-wide solutions to integrate sex and gender throughout the research ecosystem and to facilitate discussions among different stakeholders to garner diverse perspectives within the research enterprise.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 10, 2021).12aBiomedical Research22aSex Factors22aGender Identity22aWomen's Health22aSexual and Gender Minorities 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Research on Women's Health,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4182040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41819  aAUTH01512cas a2200421 i 9918249114406676DNLM20260227090913.0m     o  d        cr#|||||||||||200528c20209999enk|| poo     0   a0eng    a  2021229685  a1154518920a1229440748  a2666-5727l2666-5727  a(OCoLC)1232531635z(OCoLC)1154518920z(OCoLC)1229440748  aUHLbengerdad20260227cUHLdSFBdLVTdPFMdOCLCOdPFMdOCLCFdLIPdOH1dOCLCAdDNLM  apcc  9England04aRE65100aW110aThromb Update2dnlm00aThrombosis update. 1a[London] :bElsevier Ltd.,c[2020]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1 (December 2020).01a1i20200 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aCompanion journal to Thrombosis research.  aDescription based on: Volume 1 (December 2020); title from journal homepage (Elsevier ScienceDirect, viewed February 9, 2021).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 5 (December 2021) (viewed September 3, 2021).12aThrombosis 2aPeriodical08iComplemented by (work):tThrombosis researchx0049-3848w(OCoLC)0098599740uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/thrombosis-update  aAUTH01007cam a2200289 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400035000800420008001150440018001230600024001411000062001652450064002272640065002913000021003563360026003773370028004033380027004315000019004585000097004775010060005746500013006346500026006477300035006739990009007089918787484106676DNLM20260213174637.0240327r19561956ilu           000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.431 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aSome musings on reviewing medical books /cWilliam B. Bean. 1a[Chicago, Illinois] :bAmerican Medical Association,c[1956]  a5 pages ;c26 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  a"Reprinted from the A.M.A. Archives of internal medicine, April 1956, vol. 97, pp. 497-501."  aNLM copy, bound 43rd in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM12aMedicine12aBook Reviews as Topic0 aArchives of internal medicine.  aAUTH02778cgm a2200433 a 101598225DNLM20260227154454.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u121215s2012    mdu127       fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17666  a(OCoLC)827775035  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aHV 930400aRe-entry :bwhere public health and mass incarceration collide /cDana Sampson ... [et al.] ; Office of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research.1 aWhere public health and mass incarceration collide  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 7 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded Nov. 15, 2012.3 a(CIT): Incarceration rates in the U.S. are presently higher than any country worldwide. With an approximate 2.3 million Americans incarcerated and an estimated 700,000 offenders released annually, the issue of re-entering communities is critical to address. This event will feature three presentations covering NIH-funded research projects that explore aspects of re-entry and community health"s intersection including: HIV/AIDS, viral hepatitis, substance abuse, mental disorders, clinical research, health services, health disparities, social environment, sexual behavior, and public policy    2:00-2:05 p.m.: Introduction by Dana Sampson, NIH/OBSSR    2:05-2:15 p.m.: Remarks by Redonna Chandler, NIH/NIDA    2:15-2:45 p.m.: "Criminal Justice Involvement, Relationships, and Sexual Risk among a Sample of Non-Violent Drug Offenders" by Kim Blankenship, American University    2:45-3:15 p.m.: "Recovery from our Addiction to Incarceration: Is Critical Time Intervention an Effective Mental and Public Health Response for Men with Mental Illness Leaving Prison?" by Jeffrey Draine, Temple University    3:15-3:45 p.m.: "Impact of a Nurse Case Management Program on Drug and Recidivism Outcomes of Recently Released Homeless Parolees at Six Months" by Adeline Nyamathi, UCLA    3:45-4:00 p.m.: Audience Open Forum.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aPrisoners22aSexual Behavior22aSubstance-Related Disorders. 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aSampson, Dana.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17666  aAUTH00928cam a2200265 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400035000800440018001150600024001332450081001572640081002383000038003193360026003573370028003833380027004115000024004385010060004625040041005226100054005636500018006176510018006359990009006539918787484606676DNLM20260213174633.0240326s1956    ilua     b    000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.4100aBulletin - American Association of Public Health Physicians.nvol. 3, no. 3. 1aOak Brook, Ill. :bAmerican Association of Public Health Physicians,c[1956]  a4 pages :billustrations ;c28 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"March-April 1956."  aNLM copy, bound 41st in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM  aIncludes bibliographical references.22aAmerican Association of Public Health Physicians.12aPublic Health 2aUnited States  aAUTH01819cgm a22004694a 101267756DNLM20260227145544.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu053 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10636  a(OCoLC)84728566  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWW 2701 aFox, Donald A.c(Optometrist)10aLead and calcium overload produce rod photoreceptor cell apoptosis by opening the mitochondrial permeability transition pore /cDonald A. Fox.1 iTitle on PowerPoint screen:aCalcium and lead produce rod photoreceptor-selective apoptosis by opening the mitochondrial permeability transition pore  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 9, 2007).  aStreaming video (53 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 12, 2001.3 a(CIT): Mitochondria Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aRetinal Rod Photoreceptor Cells22aApoptosis22aCalciumxtoxicity22aLeadxtoxicity22aMitochondrial Membrane Transport Proteins22aRetinal Diseasesxetiology 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10636  aAUTH01083cam a2200301 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400035000800420008001150440018001230600024001411000062001652450077002272640057003043000033003613360026003943370028004203380026004485000019004745000039004935000084005325010060006165040041006766500031007177300024007489990009007729918787483906676DNLM20260213174640.0240327r19561956wauc     b    000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.451 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aPhilosophy of medical education in the world of 1956 /cWilliam B. Bean. 1a[Seattle, Washington] :bNorthwest Medicine,c[1956]  a1 sheet :bportrait ;c28 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  asheetbnb2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  aAt head of title: Special article.  a"Reprinted from Northwest medicine, vol. 55, pp. 703-704, June, 1956."--Page 2.  aNLM copy, bound 45th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aEducation, Medicalxtrends0 aNorthwest medicine.  aAUTH05620cgm a2200469 i 9918573276606676DNLM20260227163046.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2022    mdu111|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32819  a(OCoLC)1428106982  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 506.4 AA12 aHighlights of Pandemic Related Successes in Tribal Communities Webinard(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aHighlights of pandemic related successes in tribal communities /cNIH RECOVER, NIH CEAL, NIH Tribal Health Research Office. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 51 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded December 7, 2022.3 a(CIT): The NIH Researching COVID to Enhance Recovery Initiative, also called NIH RECOVER, in partnership with the Community Engagement Alliance (CEAL) and the Tribal Health Research Office (THRO) invites research teams to attend the Highlights of Pandemic Related Successes in Tribal Communities Webinar on Wednesday, December 7, 2022, from 2PM - 4PM EST. This exciting webinar, tailored specifically for research team members across RECOVER, CEAL and NIH, will spotlight successful approaches used during the COVID-19 pandemic to engage members of Tribal Communities. Panelists will share how they met the needs and priorities of these communities and how they overcame barriers to vaccination. The panelists and participants will discuss best practices shown for engagement around COVID related research which might be applicable to other research efforts and public health matters of importance. The webinar will feature a series of presentations, followed by a panel discussion and live Q&A, and will be moderated by Mose A. Herne, MPH, MS, Senior Advisor of the Tribal Health Research Office (THRO). The panelists include: . Laura Hammitt, MD is the Director of Infectious Disease Programs at the Center for Indigenous Health and an Associate Professor at the Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health, where she has been on faculty since 2011. Dr. Hammitt has over 20 years of experience studying viral and bacterial respiratory infections and works in partnership with Native American communities in the southwest United States to understand and address infectious diseases in a culturally informed manner. . Shawnell Damon, MPH, MSc is the Acting Public Health Director of the Navajo Area Indian Health Service. She has worked to implement Health Promotion Disease Prevention programs that include teaching and training through public health activities and events which focuses on self-awareness, health literacy, and cultural competency throughout the Navajo community. . CAPT Holly Van Lew, PharmD, BCPS, AAHIVP is an Advance Practice Pharmacist at the Indian Health Service. She is a national trainer for multiple Pharmacist and Pharmacy Technician Immunization Certificate Programs, having trained over 1,500 pharmacists, students, and technicians to administer vaccines. Holly works at the local, regional, and national level to educate pharmacists, providers, and nurses about immunizations with a focus on addressing patient vaccine needs at every encounter. If you are a part of a research team working in RECOVER, CEAL, or other initiatives striving to engage tribal communities, we invite you to register for the event here: https://nih.zoomgov.com/webinar/register/WN__0nqUNMrTRaDXStXug1kGA NIH RECOVER is a study that aims to understand, prevent, and treat the long-term health effects that make up Long COVID. All of those affected by COVID, whether patients, caregivers, or representatives of all communities, including those hit hardest by COVID, are critical to the RECOVER Initiative"s success. Learn more at https://recovercovid.org/. CEAL works closely with the communities hit hardest by COVID-19 to provide trustworthy, science-based information through active community engagement and outreach to the people hardest-hit by the COVID-19 pandemic, with the goal of building long-lasting partnerships as well as improving diversity and inclusion in our research response to COVID-19. THRO was established in 2015 to ensure meaningful input from and collaboration with Tribal Nations on NIH policies, programs, and priorities. It is the central point of contact at NIH for federally recognized American Indian and Alaska Native (AI/AN) Tribes throughout the U.S. and the coordination hub for Tribal health research activities at NIH.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed February 27, 2024).12aCOVID-19xepidemiology12aAmerican Indian or Alaska Native22aCOVID-19 Vaccines22aCommunity Health Servicesxmethods22aHealth Services Researchxmethods 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNIH RECOVER (Initiative),esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bCommunity Engagement Alliance,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bTribal Health Research Office,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=48565  aAUTH01266cam a2200301 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390350022000800400035001020420008001370440018001450600024001631000062001872450082002492460083003312640062004143000044004763360026005203370028005463380027005745000017006015000248006185010060008666500017009266550012009439990009009559918787486506676DNLM20260212180006.0240325s1955    iauc          000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)1552758235  aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.281 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.14aThe care and feeding of the intellect :bA.D. 1955 /cby Dr. William B. Bean.1 iAt head of title:aCommencement address, August 1955, State University of Iowa 1a[Iowa City, Iowa?] :b[publisher not identified],c[1955]  a1 volume (unpaged) :bportrait ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCover title.  aNLM copy includes tipped in newspaper clipping entitled "Toughen your intellect by continuing to acquire facts, S.U.I. grads told," which is an extract from the commencement address at the State University of Iowa by Dr. William B. Bean.5DNLM  aNLM copy, bound 28th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM12aIntelligence 2aAddress  aAUTH01489cas a2200373 i 101717155DNLM20260226082209.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||170613c20079999tu || poo     |    0tur    a2148-3566l2148-3566y1307-4490  a(OCoLC)1019952811  aDNLMbengerdad20260226cDNLMdDNLM0 aturaeng  9Turkey00aW100aTürkiye çocuk hastalıkları dergisi.11aTurkish journal of pediatric disease 1aAnkara :bAnkara Çocuk Sağlığı ve Hastalıkları Hematoloji Onkoloji Eğitim ve Araştırma Hastanesi,c[2007]-  a1 online resource  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Cilt 1, Sayı 1 (2007).  aArticles chiefly in Turkish with some in English. Search interface available in Turkish and English.  aDescription based on: Cilt 1, Sayı 1 (2007); title from journal home page (publisher's website, viewed December 19, 2017)  aLatest issue consulted: Cilt 11, Sayı 3 (2017) (viewed December 19, 2017)12aPediatrics 2aPeriodical2 aAnkara Çocuk Sağlığı ve Hastalıkları Hematoloji Onkoloji Eğitim Araştırma Hastanesi,eissuing body.4 uhttps://turkjpediatrdis.org/  aAUTH02173cgm a22004574a 101268018DNLM20260227150033.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu062 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11281  a(OCoLC)70119553  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 5051 aFauci, Anthony S.,d1940-10aSARS :ba new challenge to global health /cAnthony Fauci.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aGrand rounds  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 23, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 1 min., 34 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 18, 2003.3 a(CIT): NIH Clinical Center Grand Rounds   Participants will be provided state-of-the-art research on the latest scientific advances in the field as well as practical clinical information for application in patient care through excellent teaching.  This activity is for all interested physicians and clinicians.  The National Institutes of Health/Foundation for Advanced Education in the Sciences (NIH/FAES) is accredited by the Accreditation Council for Continuing Medical Education to sponsor continuing medical education for physicians.   For more information, visit   NIH Clinical Center Grand Rounds.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aSevere Acute Respiratory Syndrome22aCommunicable Disease Control22aSevere acute respiratory syndrome-related coronavirus. 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11281  aAUTH01314cgm a22004214a 101300946DNLM20260227151927.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070302s2007    mdu060 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13646  a(OCoLC)104815765  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWI 7021 aGrompe, Markus.10aLiver stem cells for transplantation therapy /cMarkus Grompe.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 13, 2007).  aStreaming video (59 min., 19 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 21, 2007.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aLiver Regeneration22aHepatocytes22aMetabolism, Inborn Errorsxsurgery22aStem Cell Transplantation 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13646  aAUTH02476cgm a2200457 i 101773030DNLM20260227160312.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201105s2020    mdu064|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30325  a(OCoLC)1224228687  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 168.5.C800aHuman antibody responses to SARS-CoViD-2 /cMichel Nussenzweig. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 4 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded October 7, 2020.3 a(CIT):  Dr. Nussenzweig will speak about the development of antibody responses focusing on neutralizing antibody responses to SARS-CoViD-2.  Over a decade ago, the Nussenzweig laboratory developed methods for rapid antibody cloning from humans in order to understand humoral immune responses to pathogens beginning with HIV-1.  These methods have been widely adapted by others facilitating antibody cloning for multiple human pathogens and their clinical development.  At the start of the COVID-19 pandemic a group of 148 individuals that had recovered from SARS-CoV-2 infection were recruited to Rockefeller University to give blood for analysis.  The lecture will summarize features of their humoral immune response in these individuals and their neutralizing properties in vitro and in animal models.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 19, 2020).12aSevere acute respiratory syndrome-related coronavirusximmunology.12aAntibody Formation22aAntibodies, Neutralizingximmunology22aCoronavirus Infectionsximmunology22aSevere Acute Respiratory Syndromeximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aNussenzweig, Michel,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pWilliam Paul lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38684  aAUTH01784cgm a2200457 i 101682312DNLM20260227155537.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160503s2016    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19605  a(OCoLC)949987105  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWB 6000aDisagreements between clinicians and researchers over proper treatment :bhow should they be resolved? /cdiscussant, Bernie Lo ; case presenter, Courtney Fitzhugh. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aEthics rounds  aRecorded April 6, 2016.3 a(CIT): Ethics Rounds: Disagreements Between Clinicians and Researchers Over Proper Treatment: How Should They Be Resolved?  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 12, 2016).12aEthics, Clinical22aBiomedical Researchxethics22aInterprofessional Relationsxethics22aPatient Care Teamxethics 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aLo, Bernard,eon-screen presenter.1 aFitzhugh, Courtney,eon-screen presenter.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aClinical Center ethics grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19605  aAUTH02393nam a2200361 i 9918987883706676DNLM20260225114050.0230817s2024    nyua     b    001 0 eng    a  2023033305  a9780197662984q(paperback)  a0197662986  a(OCoLC)1395554840  aDLCbengerdacDLCdOCLCOdIG#  apcc  9United States00aRG940b.P68 202400aWQ 10500a362.1982223/eng/2023082904aThe practical playbook.nIII,pWorking together to improve maternal health /cedited by Dorothy Cilenti, Alisahah Jackson, Natalie D. Hernandez, Lindsey Yates, Sarah Verbiest, J. Lloyd Michener, and Brian C. Castrucci ; managing editors, Brittany Hinnant and Tamala Grissett. 1aNew York, NY, United States of American :bOxford University Press,c[2024]  axxv, 632 pages :billustrations (some color) ;c26 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  a"Each day in the US, more than 10,000 women and birthing people give birth.1 While most parents and infants remain healthy through this experience, each day about 136 people will have some severe complication, such as cardiac arrest, acute renal failure, sepsis, the need for a blood transfusion, or respiratory distress syndrome,2 and three people will die.3 More birthing people die because of pregnancy and childbirth in the US than in other comparable countries.4 Black, Indigenous, and Hispanic/Latina women have the highest rates of childbirth and pregnancy complications5 and are more likely to die compared with white women.3,6 These disparities are rooted at the intersection of racism, sexism, poverty, and other systems of oppression that lead to fewer opportunities, less access to resources and protections, increased stress, and poorer quality care. At one of the most vulnerable times in their lives, women and birthing people face inequities that perpetuate illness and disease. This is unacceptable"--cProvided by publisher.12aMaternal Health12aMaternal Health Services22aHealth Equity22aPublic-Private Sector Partnerships1 aCilenti, Dorothy,eeditor.  aAUTH01357cgm a22003854a 101267763DNLM20260227145622.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s1999    mdu036 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10643  a(OCoLC)64588916  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 12500aMItochondria and beta oxidation /cNIH, Mitochondria Interest Group.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c1999]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 1, 2006).  aStreaming video (36 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 22, 1999.