06549918936929106676DNLM20260204160202.0241113s2024    bl ac         000 0 por    a9788584001675  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Brazil00aW 33 DB800a10 anos da lei do ato médico :bo resgate de uma luta /cConselho Federal de Medicina.1 aDiez anos da lei do ato médico 1aBrasília :bCFM,c[2024]  a163 pages :billustrations, portraits ;c21 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier12aBrazil.tLei do ato médico12aLegislation, Medicalxhistory12aPhysiciansxlegislation & jurisprudence22aHistory, 21st Century 2aBrazil2 aConselho Federal de Medicina (Brazil),eissuing body.  aAUTH03690cgm a22004574a 101535332DNLM20260227153508.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100702s2010    mdu072|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15958  a(OCoLC)658535571  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 300 AA11 aEng, Eugenia.10aCultural context and system change :bpartners or odd couple for eliminating health inequalities /cEugenia Eng.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBehavioral and Social Sciences Research lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 12 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 14, 2010.3 a(CIT): Introduction: Seven years after the Institute of Medicine's landmark report, Unequal Treatment, and well after US medical schools and healthcare institutions have mandated nearly universal training programs on culturally competent care, less favorable treatment outcomes, shorter survival time, and excess mortality continue to fuel persistent racial and ethnic health inequities.  Recognition is growing in health disparities research for applied collaborative approaches, such as CBPR, and alternative methodologies, such as systems science.  For community members and researchers to be engaged equitably in systems thinking, what would be the common language and framework?  To achieve racial equity in cancer care, what would systems change look and feel like?  Methods: To address these questions, the Greensboro Health Disparities Collaborative (GHDC) in North Carolina has been engaged for 7 years in grounding its 35 community, academic, and health professional members in CBPR principles and the vocabulary and framework of Undoing Racism (UR).  GHDC codified the UR framework of systems of power and authority for achieving health equity through: the methodologies of Power Analysis, Story Telling on experiencing or contributing to racism in the healthcare system, and Critical Incident Technique interview; a Full Value Contract; Research Ethics Certification for Non-Traditional Investigators; Guidelines for Publications and Dissemination; GHDC By-Laws; and Health Equity Training for medical residents.  Results:  We will define the key concepts and terminology for: (1) the historical context of race-based inequities; (2) the complex mix of behavioral, economic, and social factors of health inequities; and (3) their effects on equitable engagement in systems thinking.  Findings will be presented from our NCI-funded (R21), Cancer Care and Racial Equity Study: A CBPR Approach, on transparency and accountability as essential elements for system change in cancer care.  Implications for systems change intervention research will be discussed, using as an example our pending R01 application, Accountability for Cancer Care through Undoing Racism and Equity.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHealth Status Disparities22aHealth Care Reform22aSocioeconomic Factors. 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research. 0aBSSR lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15958  aAUTH01238cam a2200313 i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002600