|fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33762  a(DNLM)CIT:33764  a(DNLM)CIT:33768  a(OCoLC)1452436833  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 26.55.A72 aToward an Ethical Framework for Artificial Intelligence in Biomedical and Behavioral Research: Transparency for Data and Model Reuse (Workshop)d(2024 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aToward an ethical framework for artificial intelligence in biomedical and behavioral research :btransparency for data and model reuse.1 aTransparency for data and model reuse1 iTitle on title screen:aEthical framework for artificial intelligence in biomedical 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2024]  a1 online resource (3 streaming video files (6 hr., 8 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aStatement of responsibility on title screens: Laura Biven.  aRecorded January 31, February 1 & February 2, 2024.3 a(CIT): The goal of this workshop is to explore and assess the landscape of ethical AI in the biomedical research context by gathering expert input on opportunities and challenges with respect to the ethical use and reuse of data and models in the Artificial Intelligence (AI) development cycle. This interactive workshop will: Begin to develop transparency guidelines for NIH awardees using, developing, or contributing to AI; Identify tools and capability gaps for ethical and transparent AI; Identify trends and future states.For more information go to https://www.scgcorp.com/ethicalframework2024/Default  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed August 7, 2024).12aArtificial Intelligencexethics22aInformation Dissemination22aData Managementxmethods22aData Science22aBiomedical Researchxethics22aBehavioral Researchxethics 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aBiven, Laura,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=5419940uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=5421140uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=54212  aAUTH01497cas a2200505 a 8501014DNLM20260227091027.0cr|cn ||||||||850201c19849999xxubr p       0||| 0eng    a0738-0151l0738-0151y0163-9641  aCASWDD  a(DNLM)C17815700(s)  a(OCoLC)09495904  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bCH642T10aChild Adolesc Social Work J2dnlm 0aChild & adolescent social work journal00aChild & adolescent social work journal :bC & A.30aChild and adolescent social work journal30aC & A30aC and A  a[New York, N.Y. :bHuman Sciences Press,cc1983-3 31999- :aNew York :bKluwer Academic/Plenum Publishers  av.  aBimonthly  aQuarterlyb-1989  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (spring 1984)-  aTitle from cover.0 aMEDLINEbSelected citations only0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.22aAdolescent22aChild22aInfant 2aFamily Therapy 2aMental Disorders 2aSocial Work, Psychiatric 2aPeriodical4 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/10560  aAUTH02144cgm a22004454a 101267652DNLM20260227145924.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu083  |||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10517  a(OCoLC)84671112  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.500aNIH peer resolution panel.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 24, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 22 min., 46 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 21, 2000.3 a(CIT): Peer Resolution Panel is a new option for resolving certain problems between employees and their supervisors.  A Peer Resolution Panel can be requested when you and your supervisor disagree on how to resolve a conflict.  A group of non-supervisory employees (peers) and supervisors hears and decides on the issue.  They determine whether your supervisor followed policy correctly, fairly and consistently in responding to your complaint.  This approach has been used successfully by private industry and other government agencies.  It does not replace other means of resolving workplace disputes--it is simply another option.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aSign interpretation on screen.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration12aEmployee Grievances22aPeer Group22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aUnited States 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10517  aAUTH02277cgm a22004694a 101267634DNLM20260227145825.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu099  |||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10496  a(OCoLC)84658803  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQV 1562 aNIH Research Festivald(2000 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNIH research festival.nPlenary session 1,p[Nitric oxide] :b[molecular physiology, pathology, and therapeutics] /cAlan N. Schechter, chair ; Richard O. Cannon, Mark T. Gladwin, Robert L. Danner.3 aPlenary session 1, nitric oxide1 iTitle from PowerPoint screen:aNitric oxide :bmolecular physiology, pathology, and therapeutics  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 15, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 38 min., 58 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 11, 2000.3 a(CIT): This session will cover recent studies on the molecular, cellular and physiological effects of NO and evidence for pathological states due to decreased NO production. Topics covered include pathophysiological abnormalities of endothelial nitric oxide synthesis, NO transport and delivery by hemoglobin, and NO and reactive oxygen species signaling and gene expression. Therapeutic implications of these mechanisms will be discussed.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aNitric Oxidexphysiology12aVasodilator Agents22aNitric Oxidexadverse effects22aNitric Oxidextherapeutic use 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aSchechter, Alan N.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10496  aAUTH02624cgm a22004454a 101267633DNLM20260227145815.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu102  |||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10495  a(OCoLC)84658798  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWG 5002 aNIH Research Festivald(2000 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNIH research festival.nPlenary session 2,p[Angiogenesis] :b[molecular mechanisms and therapeutic approaches] /cWilliam G. Stetler-Stevenson, chair ; Hynda Kleinman ... [et al.].3 aPlenary session 2, angiogenesis1 iTitle from PowerPoint screen:aAngiogenesis :bmolecular mechanisms and therapeutic approaches  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 24, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 41 min., 6 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 11, 2000.3 a(CIT): Angiogenesis is the development of new blood vessels in response to pathologic stimuli. Vasculogenesis is the process of blood vessel formation during embryonic development. The angiogenic response can function to potentiate the progression of the initiating pathology, e.g. tumor growth and spread during cancer progression, pannus formation in rheumatoid arthritis, diabetic retinopathy. In other disease states the growth of new vessels can compensate for diminished physiologic function, e.g. neoangiogenesis in response to occlusive vascular disease, wound healing, etc. NIH investigators are exploring the molecular mechanisms involved in vasculogenesis, as well as the initiation and regulation of angiogenic response and in various disease processes, with the aim of developing new therapeutic strategies for both promoting and inhibiting angiogenesis.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aNeovascularization, Pathologic22aNeovascularization, Physiologic 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aStetler-Stevenson, William G.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10495  aAUTH01578cgm a22004454a 101289573DNLM20260227151326.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2005    mdu594  |||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12952  a(DNLM)CIT:12955  a(OCoLC)84671781  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2061 aUnited States.bNational Cancer Advisory Board.bMeetingn(136th :d2005 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNational Cancer Advisory Board (NCAB), December 2005 /cthe National Cancer Institute (NCI) Division of Extramural Activities.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 30, 2006).  aStreaming video (9 hr., 53 min., 26 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 6-7, 2005.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.)12aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration12aNeoplasms22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1295240uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12955  aAUTH01567cgmaa22003974a 101262413DNLM20260227144711.0vf|cbahou051128s2005    mdu061 ||         vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:3798  a(OCoLC)63114108  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aWP 32000aManagement of polycystic ovary syndrome /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  aUnited States.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (1 hr., 5 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (1 hr., 5 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aWomen's health special interest group  aTitle from cassette label.  aSpeaker, Katherine Sherif.  aPresented Oct. 28, 2005.  aAlso available online.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aPolycystic Ovary Syndromextherapy1 aSherif, Katherine.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aWomen's Health Special Interest Group (Series)4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3798  aAUTH02249cgm a22004694a 101535265DNLM20260227153500.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100702s2010    mdu063|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15937  a(OCoLC)655615641  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 55.71 aDutzler, Raimund.12aA prokaryotic perspective on pentameric ligand gated ion channel structure and function /cRaimund Dutzler.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 3 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 7, 2010.3 a(CIT): Dr. Dutzler's research group is interested in the mechanisms of transmembrane ion transport. The group takes a multidisciplinary approach combining biochemistry X-ray crystallography and electrophysiology to study the structure and function of ion channels and ion transporters. Dr. Dutzler has solved original x-ray structures of bacterial homologs of several important transporter classes, including the ClC family of chloride channels and transporters and the cys-loop family of neurotransmitter receptors. These structures have yielded important mechanistic insights into both classes of proteins. In addition, Dr. Dutzler's group has used novel combinations of structural and biochemical approaches to obtain functional information regarding these transport proteins.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aIon Channel Gatingxphysiology12aIon Channelsxphysiology22aIon Channelsxchemistry22aLigands22aProkaryotic Cells 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15937  aAUTH01576cas a2200469 a 101285656DNLM20260227090912.0cr|cn ||||||||010608c19999999ii qr p       0    0eng    a  2009243751  a0973-3930l1998-3832  a(OCoLC)47093854  aHMScHMSdOCLdOCLCQdDNLM  apcc  9India00aW1bIN766GBFE10aInt J Diabetes Dev Ctries2dnlm00aInternational journal of diabetes in developing countries.13aIJDDC  aBombay :bPublished ... on behalf of the Research Society for Study of Diabetes in India,c1999-2 3<2009-2010> :aMumbai :bMedknow Publications3 32011- :aNew Delhi :bSpringer India  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 19, no. 3 (Jul./Sept. 1999)-  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 29, no. 3 (July/Sept. 2009).0 aPubMedbv28, 2008-v30n2, Apr.-June 20100 aPMC Inactive  aAlso issued online.12aDiabetes Mellitus22aDeveloping Countries 2aPeriodical2 aResearch Society for the Study of Diabet