day celebration /cFogarty International Center and Office of Research on Women's Health.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 9, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 42 min., 39 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 8, 2005.3 a(CIT): The event features three NIH international female scientists who will participate in a panel entitled, "International Women Scientists at NIH: Their Research and Career Paths."  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomedical Research12aWomen, Working22aAuditory Diseases, Central22aEctodermal Dysplasia22aNeoplasms22aResearch Personnel22aVocational Guidance 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aJohn E. Fogarty International Center for Advanced Study in the Health Sciences.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3978  aAUTH02583cgmaa22004814a 101209925DNLM20260227144429.0vf|czahmuvd|cvaizuvf|cjahouhd|bfa012baap040312s2004    mdu067 ||         vleng    a(OCoLC)56413719  a(DNLM)CIT:2851  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aBF 315.5bVC no.1 200404aThe marvels & illusions of intuitive thinking /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.3 aMarvels and illusions of intuitive thinking  a[Bethesda, Md.] :bNational Institutes of Health,c[2004]  a1 videocassette of 1 (DVCPRO) (67 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/4 in.3master.  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (67 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videodisc of 1 (DVD) (67 min.) :bsd., col. ;c4 3/4 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aThe NIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture  aTitle from cassette spine label.  aSpeaker, Daniel Kahneman.  aPresented Mar. 3, 2004.3 a(Producer) The speaker has been associated with an approach to the study of intuitive thinking, called heuristics and biases, which focuses on the biases and illusions that are associated with the processes of intuitive thought. A seemingly competing approach to the study of intuition has developed, called naturalistic decision making, which focuses on the intuitions of experts doing what they do best. This is an effort to understand the flaws and the marvels of intuitive thinking within a common psychological analysis.  aVideocassette (Betacam SP)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2011.e1 videocassette (67 min.) : sd., col. ; 1/2 in.  aVideodisc (DVD)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2019.e1 videodisc (67 min.) : sd., col. ; 4 3/4 in.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aIntuition22aDecision Making1 aKahneman, Daniel,d1934-2024.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=2851  aAUTH02279cgm a22004334a 101268297DNLM20260227150345.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu075 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11646  a(OCoLC)71138934  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aBF 4411 aGlimcher, Paul W.10aDecisions, uncertainty, and the brain /cPaul W. Glimcher.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 29, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 14 min., 9 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 24, 2003.3 a(CIT): Over the past five years, Dr. Glimcher has focused on the identification and characterization of signals that intervene between the neural processes that engage in sensory encoding and the neural processes that engage movement generations. His laboratory studies these signals in cortex, the basal ganglia, and in the saccadic brainstem. The long-term goal of his research is to describe the neural events that underlie behavioral decision-making. His approach to this problem consolidates mathematical economic approaches to decision-making with traditional neurobiological tools. By using these tools in physiological analyses of the brainstem, cortex, and the basal ganglia, the Glimcher lab hopes to develop a coherent view of how the brain makes decisions.  For more information, visit  Paul W. Glimcher   NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aMental Processesxphysiology22aEconomics22aNeuronsxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11646  aAUTH01722cgm a22004694a 101268314DNLM20260227150416.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu816 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11715  a(DNLM)CIT:11730  a(DNLM)CIT:11731  a(OCoLC)71233030  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWE 8702 aNational Institutes of Health Consensus Development Conferenced(2003 December 8-10 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNIH Consensus Development Conference on Total Knee Replacement /cOMAR ... [et al.].  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Aug. 9, 2006).  aStreaming video (13 hr., 35 min., 51 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 8-10, 2003.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aArthroplasty, Replacement, Knee22aKnee Prosthesis22aReoperation 2