29, 2007).  aStreaming video (48 min., 55 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 13, 2007.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aTransforming Growth Factor betaxphysiology22aNeoplasms22aSignal Transduction 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13755  aAUTH04003cgm a22004454a 101306060DNLM20260227152035.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070430s2007    mdu066 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13746  a(OCoLC)144568133  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 102.81 aMalenka, Robert C.10aSynaptic plasticity :bmultiple mechanisms and functions /cRobert Malenka.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 25, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 5 min., 17 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 9, 2007.3 a(CIT): Long-lasting activity-dependent changes in the efficacy of synaptic transmission play an important role in the development of neural circuits and may mediate many forms of learning and memory. Work from Dr. Malenka's laboratory over the last 10 years has demonstrated that there are a variety of related but mechanistically distinct forms of synaptic plasticity. A major goal of his laboratory is to elucidate both the specific molecular events that are responsible for the triggering of these various forms of synaptic plasticity and the exact modifications in synaptic proteins that are responsible for the observed, long-lasting changes in synaptic efficacy. To accomplish this, they use cellular electrophysiological recording techniques to examine synaptic plasticity in a variety of different in vitro preparations including thin slices of various regions of the rodent brain and primary neurons in culture.  Dr. Malenka's lab also uses cell biological and molecular techniques to examine the activity-dependent modulation of neurotransmitter receptors and to express dominant negative forms of various synaptic proteins so that their exact functions can be determined. An additional complementary approach has involved examining synaptic physiology and synaptic plasticity in various mutant mouse lines lacking specific synaptic proteins.    A related but independent area of research in his laboratory is the elucidation of the synaptic action of drugs of abuse such as the psychostimulants cocaine and amphetamine. Toward this end, they have developed in vitro slice preparations of the nucleus accumbens and ventral tegmental area, brain regions which are thought to mediate several of the behavioral effects of drugs of abuse. They have characterized a novel form of synaptic plasticity in the nucleus accumbens and have done an extensive pharmacological characterization of the synaptic effects of dopamine, cocaine, and amphetamine. Currently they are examining in more detail the underlying mechanisms of dopamine's actions and determining how chronic treatment with drugs of abuse affect the synaptic responses of nucleus accumbens and ventral tegmental area cells. Because chronic exposure to drugs of abuse elicit long-term adaptive changes in critical neural circuits, it is hoped that the knowledge gained from the work on the molecular mechanisms underlying synaptic plasticity will provide important clues to the molecular mechanisms underlying the development of tolerance, dependence and addiction.    NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aSynaptic Transmissionxphysiology22aCannabinoid Receptor Modulators22aNeuronal Plasticityxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13746  aAUTH02120cgm a2200493 i 9918226877406676DNLM20260227161219.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210615s2021    mdu772|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31718  a(DNLM)CIT:31727  a(DNLM)CIT:31736  a(OCoLC)1415741418  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 704.62 aAnnual NIH HEAL Initiative Investigator Meetingn(2nd :d2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNIH HEAL Initiative Investigator Meeting /cNIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (3 streaming video files (12 hr., 52 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded May 17, 18 & 19, 2021.3 a(CIT): Second Annual NIH HEAL Initiative Investigator Meeting - This meeting will bring together more than 500 NIH HEAL Initiative awardees, federal officials, people with lived experience, and other stakeholders to share research breakthroughs, highlight cutting-edge science, explore shared interests, and identify opportunities to advance the goals of the NIH HEAL Initiative.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 17, 2022).12aPain Management22aOpioid-Related Disordersxpsychology22aCOVID-19xpsychology22aHealth Equity22aCommunity Participation 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNIH HEAL Initiative,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4194640uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4209640uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42097  aAUTH02507cas a2200601 a 14830200RDNLM20260213174635.0cr|cn#||||||||hd|bfa012baap820821c18799999dcuar         0    0eng    a0891-1940l0891-1940y0065-9037  a(DNLM)T15640000(s)z(DNLM)SR0064834(s)  a(OCoLC)01480229  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bAM5352 aAmerican Laryngological Association.bAnnual Meeting.10aTrans 