v. 23, no. 3 (2011).0 aPubMedbv21n2, 2009-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.  aArticles in English with abstracts in Arabic and English.  aOfficial publication of the Saudi Dental Society, May 1993?- . 2aDentistry 2aPeriodical2 aJamʻīyah al-Saʻūdīyah li-Ṭibb al-Asnān.0 x1658-35584 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=93136034 uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/10139052  aAUTH01337cas a2200493 a 9511374DNLM20260227091029.0cr||||||||||||951130c19959999enk x p       0||| 0eng    asn 95042723   a1355-4794l1355-4794  aNROCF5  a(DNLM)SR0082120(s)  a(OCoLC)33034019  aPPiUcPPiUdDNLM  9England00aW1bNE323P10aNeurocase2dnlm00aNeurocase.  aOxford, UK :bOxford University Press,cc1995-3132013- :aLondon :bRoutledge  av. :bill.  aSix no. a year,b<1996->  aQuarterly,b1995  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1-  aSubtitle varies.  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv7n1,  2001-0 aPubMedbv7n1,  2001-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online. 2aMental Disorders 2aNervous System Diseases 2aNeuropsychology 2aAbstracts 2aCase Reports 2aPeriodical0 x1465-36564 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/loi/nncs20#.Vd3LvGflvGh  aAUTH03042cgm a22004454a 101310922DNLM20260227152113.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070703s2007    mdu170 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13877  a(OCoLC)165047498  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 530.12 aDisease Control Priorities Project.bMeetingd(2007 :cNational Institutes of Health)10aDisease Control Priorities Project :bimplementing the research agenda (DCPP Meeting) /cFogarty International Center.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed July 11, 2007).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 49 min., 16 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 11, 2007.3 a(CIT): The Disease Control Priorities Project (DCPP) is an ongoing effort to assess disease control priorities and produce evidence-based analysis and resource materials to inform health policymaking in developing countries. Funded primarily through a grant from the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation, DCPP is a joint program of the Fogarty International Center (FIC) and the National Library of Medicine (NLM), NIH; the World Bank; and the World Health Organization.   The purpose of this meeting is to review the key messages of the DCPP and highlight its impact on health policy and programs in developing countries. One year ago, the DCPP released two landmark publications: The second edition of Disease Control Priorities in Developing Countries (DCP2); and Global Burden of Disease and Risk Factors.   The goal of this 4-year effort was to provide an evidence-based analysis and resource materials to inform health policymaking in developing countries. The Population Reference Bureau coordinated the dissemination of project findings and publications. The first edition of Disease Control Priorities in Developing Countries (DCP1) led to major advances in the health care system of many countries, including Mexico.   The second edition has already made an impact in several countries, and these experiences will be highlighted during the meeting. The DCPP website http://www.dcp2.org has become an information resource for the world and received more than half a million visitors from over 100 countries since its launch last year.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aDisease Control Priorities Project.12aHealth Priorities22aCommunicable Disease Control22aDeveloping Countries22aHealth Policy22aProgram Evaluation 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13877  aAUTH02144cgm a22004454a 101310920DNLM20260227152108.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070703s2007    mdu068|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13872  a(OCoLC)156863667  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5681 aTeyton, Luc.10aMolecular mechanisms of T cell activation /cLuc Teyton.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aImmunology Interest Group lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 11, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 8 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jun. 6, 2007.3 a(CIT): Dr. Teyton, trained in both medicine and immunology in France, did post-doctoral work with Per Peterson and Ian Wilson at Scripps, and has remained in La Jolla for the past 20 years.  His interest in immunology has always been colored by his background in rheumatology, and he has worked on the mechanism of antigen processing, the role of invariant chain, the engineering of MHC and TCR for binding and structural studies, the structural basis for autoimmunity (with the determination of the structure of IAg7), and the role of CD1 in NKT cell recognition.  His interest in CD1 has drawn him into studies of the biosynthesis of lipids and the relationship of lipid storage diseases to CD1 biosynthesis and lipid binding.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aT-Lymphocytesximmunology22aLymphocyte Activationxgenetics22aLymphocyte Activationximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aImmunology Interest Group lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13872  aAUTH01914cgm a22004454a 101478269DNLM20260227153012.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080702s2008    mdu064|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14577  a(OCoLC)243818383  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aHQ 7504aThe annual NIH gay, bisexual, lesbian, and transgender (GBLT) pride month /cThe Office of Equal Opportunity and Diversity Management and Salutaris (the NIH Gay, Lesbian, Bisexual and Transgender Employees' Forum).  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNoons-in-June  aTitle from title screen (viewed July 26, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 4 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 24, 2008.3 a(CIT): Same-sex marriage: Public rhetoric, public opinion and public health - a panel discussion  Panel members:  Sharon Scales Rostosky, Ph.D., Ellen Riggle, M. Paz Galupo, Ph.D.   Opening Remarks and Introduction of panel members:  Nic D"Ascoli, Chairman, Salutaris  Closing Remarks:  Andrea M. Wongsam, National Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHomosexuality22aBisexuality22aCivil Rights 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Equal Opportunity and Diversity Management. 0aNIH noon-in-June program40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14577  aAUTH01223cas a2200421 a 101464191DNLM20260227090923.0cr|cn ||||||||080120c20089999enk|| p       |    0eng    a  2008243978  a1753-495Xl1753-495Xy8646-8900  a(OCoLC)269448626  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9England00aW1bOB471M10aObstet Med2dnlm00aObstetric medicine.  aLondon :bRoyal Society of Medicine Press3 3Sept. 2012- :aLondon :bSage  aFour times a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1, no. 1 (Sept. 2008).  aDescription based on first issue; title from cover.0 aPubMedbv7n1, 2014-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.12aObstetrics22aPregnancy Complications 2aPeriodical2 aISOM (Society)0 x1753-49684 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=1014641914 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/obm  aAUTH02035cgm a22004454a 101478247DNLM20260227152746.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080702s2008    mdu062|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14552  a(OCoLC)240988440  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4751 aMarx, Christopher.10aEvolution and biological systems /cChristopher Marx.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aEvolution and medicine  aTitle from title screen (viewed July 22, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 2 min., 28 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 10, 2008.3 a(CIT): Microbes present an opportunity to study evolution on a small scale, both in time and space - and to address particularly vexing biological questions such as how organisms adapt to complex, changing environments.  An emerging leader in the field of experimental evolution, Dr. Marx approaches this question by studying the evolution of physiological systems in microbial populations.  Lecture series presented by National Institute of General Medical Sciences, the Office of Science Education, and the National Human Genome Research Institute. For more information, visit  http://www.nigms.nih.gov/News/Meetings/EvolutionSeries2008.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aEvolution, Molecular22aAdaptation, Biologicalxgenetics22aMethylobacteriumxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aEvolution and medicine lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14552  aAUTH01687cas a2200517 a 101606001DNLM20260227090910.0m     o  d        cr |||||||||||130411c20129999cc qr poo     0    0eng    a  2012250778  a2213-2961l2213-2961  a(OCoLC)809309257  aHNKbengcHNKdBUFdTEFdOCLCQdDNLM  apcc  9China10aW1bJO902U  aGV557b.J6810aJ Sport Health Sci2dnlm00aJournal of sport and health science.13aJSHS  a[Shanghai] :bShanghai University of Sport,cc2012-  aFour issues a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with vol. 1, issue 1 (May 2012).  aProduction and hosting by Elsevier B.V.1 aMEDLINEbv9n1, Jan. 2020-0 aPubMedbv5n1, 2016-0 aPMC  aAlso issued in print.  aDescription based on: Vol. 1, issue 1 (May 2012); title from PDF running title (ScienceDirect, viewed Oct. 15, 2012).  aLatest issue consulted: 2 (2013) (viewed Nov. 26, 2013).12aSports22aHealth Promotion22aMedicine, Chinese Traditional22aPhysical Education and Training22aSports Medicine 2aPeriodical2 aShanghai ti yu xue yuan.0 x2095-254640uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=10160600140uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/20952546  aAUTH02111cgm a22004574a 101319960DNLM20260227152201.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071101s2007    mdu064 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14108  a(OCoLC)190751381  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWV 3011 aVosshall, Leslie B.14aThe scent of man :bgenetics of olfactory perception /cLeslie B. Vosshall.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Nov. 21, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 3 min., 45 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 29, 2007.3 a(CIT): Dr. Vosshall's lab works on understanding how the brain interprets and responds to olfactory signals in the environment that signal food, danger, or potential mating partners.  These experiments are largely carried out in Drosophila melanogaster, which is a simpler yet very powerful model system that still displays a large repertoire of chemosensory behaviors through a nervous system that is less complex than most vertebrates.  Dr. Vosshall's lab is also performing research on host-seeking behavior in the malaria mosquito, Anopheles gambiae as well as engaging in genetic studies of specific anosmias in humans.   NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aSmellxgenetics22aCultural Diversity22aGenetics, Behavioral22aOlfactory Receptor Neuronsxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14108  aAUTH03121cgm a22004694a 101319967DNLM20260227152132.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071101s2007    mdu065 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14089  a(OCoLC)190867185  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 1021 aFeldman, Dan,cPh. D.10aPlasticity and processing in the whisker map in rat somatosensory cortex /cDan Feldman.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Jan. 7, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 4 min., 40 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 22, 2007.3 a(CIT): Dr. Feldman is interested in how circuits in the cerebral cortex process and store information about the sensory environment. His lab focuses on primary somatosensory (S1) cortex of the rat, which contains an orderly map of sensory input from the facial whiskers. The whisker map, like other sensory maps in the brain, is not fixed, but varies strongly with recent sensory experience. This phenomenon, called experience-dependent map plasticity, acts during development to transform immature circuits into appropriately organized connections that mediate adult sensory perception and sensory-guided behavior. In adults, such plasticity persists, and is thought to underlie certain forms of learning, and to contribute to recovery of function after stroke or peripheral injury. Though map plasticity occurs with common features across many brain regions, the cellular and synaptic mechanisms of map plasticity are largely unknown. A major focus of the lab's work is to identify these mechanisms, and to understand how they  drive information storage and optimization of cortical circuits. They are also interested in how S1 encodes and processes somatosensory input, and how plasticity and learning alter this processing.   Dr. Feldman received his Ph.D. in Neurobiology from Stanford University. He conducted postdoctoral research at the University of California, San Francisco, and the National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (NINDS). He started his laboratory at UCSD in 2000, and currently holds the Walter F. Heiligenberg Professorship in Neuroethology.   NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeuronal Plasticityxphysiology22aBrain Mapping22aRatsxphysiology22aSomatosensory Cortexxphysiology22aVibrissaexinnervation 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14089  aAUTH01872cgm a22004574a 101478267DNLM20260227152939.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080702s2008    mdu730 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14575  a(DNLM)CIT:14576  a(OCoLC)310073525  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2062 aNCI Board of Scientific Advisors.bMeetingn(40th :b2008 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNCI Board of Scientific Advisors, June 2008 /cthe National Cancer Institute (NCI) Division of Extramural Activities.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 18, 2009).  aStreaming video (12 hr., 10 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 23 & 24, 2008.3 a(CIT): Provides scientific advice on a wide variety of matters concerning scientific program policy, progress, and future direction of the 