1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, Aug. 2000-0 aPubMedbv1n1, Aug. 2000-0 aPMC1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3v. 1, no. 1 (Aug. 2000)c20010723fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper12aOtolaryngology22aCommunication Disorders22aHearingxphysiology22aLarynxxphysiology22aSpeechxphysiology22aVoicexphysiology 2aPeriodical2 aAssociation for Research in Otolaryngology (U.S.)0 x1438-75734 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/101624 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=100892857  aAUTH01590cgm a22004574a 101319970DNLM20260227152125.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071101s2007    mdu067|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14080  a(OCoLC)181903070  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 1001 aGolenbock, Doug.10aTLR activation :bmechanistic insights into the pathogenesis of human infectious diseases /cDoug Golenbock.3 aToll-like receptor activation  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aImmunology Interest Group lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 15, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 7 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 17, 2007.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aCommunicable Diseasesxgenetics22aGene Expression Regulationximmunology22aSignal Transductionximmunology22aToll-Like Receptorsximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group. 0aImmunology Interest Group lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14080  aAUTH02913cgm a22004454a 101486653DNLM20260227152643.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081007s2008    mdu714 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14673  a(DNLM)CIT:14674  a(OCoLC)288982873  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQV 400aQuantitative and systems pharmacology (QSP) workshop /cNational Institute of General Medical Sciences.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 2, 2008).  aStreaming video (11 hr., 53 min., 12 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 25-26, 2008.3 a(CIT): The Quantitative and Systems Pharmacology (QSP) Workshop will provide state-of-the-art knowledge and perspective about topics at the interface of systems biology and pharmacology. The question of where systems biology, modeling, and more quantitative measurements can be applied to pharmacology and drug discovery/action now and in the near future will be addressed. Accordingly, the talks will emphasize both conceptual information and significant research findings concerning the topic.  The topic will be approached from the standpoint of both a horizontal integration (various networks in various cell systems) and a vertical integration (connections between pathways at different levels of integration, tissues, organs, etc.). Consideration of the current knowledge as well as future research needs in various areas will be made.  The workshop will feature discussion sessions based on the presentations, throughout the two-day period. The meeting goals include highlighting where the state-of-the-art resides in relevant disciplines today, fostering integration of research efforts in these areas with others, identifying barriers (problems merging two disciplines), increasing collaboration between pharmacologists, clinicians, and systems biologists, and establishing pressing and long-term research needs (training, instrumentation, algorithms, etc.) for advancement of the ability of systems biology to inform drug discovery and drug action.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aPharmacology22aDrug Discovery22aInterdisciplinary Communication22aSystems Biology 2aConference Proceedings 