//www.tandfonline.com/loi/istr20  aAUTH01365cas a2200433 a 101542940DNLM20260227090924.0cr||||||||||||101004c20109999ko || p       |    0eng    a  2010243887  a2093-7911l2093-7911  a(OCoLC)688982388  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9Korea (South)00aW1bSH182L10aSaf Health Work2dnlm00aSafety and health at work.11aSH@W  aIncheon :bOccupational Safety and Health Research Institute  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with vol.1, no.1 (Sept. 30, 2010).0 aPubMedbv1, 2010-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: vol.1, no.1 (Sept. 30, 2010); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: vol.1, no. 1 (Sept. 30, 2010).12aOccupational Health22aSafetyxstandards 2aPeriodical2 aSanŏp Anjŏn Pogŏn Yŏn'guwŏn (Korea)0 x2093-79974 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/safety-and-health-at-work4 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101542940  aAUTH02636cgm a2200421 a 101604625DNLM20260227154620.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130322s2013    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17856  a(OCoLC)838447437  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3371 aHaber, Suzanne.10aNeurocircuitry of incentive-based learning :blinking connectivity to function and disease /cSuzanne Haber.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aRecorded Mar. 18, 2013.3 a(CIT): Neuroscience Seminar Series   Dr. Haber's laboratory investigates the neural network that underlies incentive-based learning and decision-making leading to the development of action plans. The cortico-basal ganglia system is at the center of this circuit and comprises a diverse group of structures involved in reward and motivation, cognition, and motor control.  The consequence of basal ganglia dysfunction is emphasized in the range of diseases that involve it, including mental health disorders such as obsessive-compulsive disorder, drug abuse and addiction, and schizophrenia, and motor control disorders including Parkinson"s disease. One set of experiments in Dr. Haber's laboratory address the hypothesis that the cortico-basal ganglia network processes information through both parallel and integrative circuits.  A second set of studies focus on the pathway trajectories from different prefrontal areas to their targets.  A third set of experiments focus on which pathways and terminals fields are likely to be involved in the therapeutic effects of during deep brain stimulation (DBS).  A fourth set of studies address the changes in terminal fields and white matter tracts during postnatal development.   For more information go to <a href='http://neuroseries.info.nih.gov'>http://neuroseries.info.nih.gov.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aLearningxphysiology22aMotivationxphysiology22aNerve Net 2aLecture 2aWebcast 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17856  aAUTH01226cam a22003131  450000100100000000300050001000500170001500700140003200800410004603500200008704000210010704400180012806000230014610000510016924501210022025000170034126000300035830000210038833600260040933700280043533800270046350000570049053301100054754001420065758300390079958300430083869000220088199900090090312430430RDNLM20260206141424.0hd|bfa012baap810725s1926    nyu||||  |||| 00||0|eng    a(OCoLC)14745160  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aQY 450bS881b 19261 aStone, Willard J.q(Willard John),d1877-1943.10aBlood chemistry, colorimetric methods for the general practitioner,bwith clinical comments and dietary suggestions.  a2d ed., rev.  aNew York,bHoeber,c1926.  axi, 129 p.bill.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aNLM microfilm copy shelved as: Film 5199 no. 35DNLM  aMicrofilm.b[Bethesda, Md.] :cNational Library of Medicine,d1993.e1 microfilm reel : negative ; 35 mm.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202602062pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202602065DNLM  aBlood / Chemistry  aAUTH01296cas a2200433   8003418DNLM20260227090950.0cr|cn#||||||||800403c19799999xxuqr p       0||| 0eng    a0191-5886l0191-5886  aJNVBDV  a(DNLM)J31850000(s)  a(OCoLC)12490529  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bJO795J10aJ Nonverbal Behav2dnlm 0aJournal of nonverbal behavior00aJournal of nonverbal behavior.  a[New York,bHuman Sciences Press]3 31999- :aNew York, NY :bKluwer Academic/Plenum Publishers  av.bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 4-    fall 1979-0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.  aContinues Environmental psychology and nonverbal behavior. 2aBehavior 2aNonverbal Communication 2aPsychology 2aPeriodical0 x1573-365310tEnvironmental psychology and nonverbal behavior4 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/10919  aAUTH03679cgm a2200397 i 101686964DNLM20260227155549.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160707s2016    mdu064|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19755  a(OCoLC)954194303  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 56800aDriving CARs to BARs :bthe road to engineered specific human T regulatory cells /cDavid W. Scott ; Immunology Interest Group.1 iSubtitle on PowerPoint screen:aRoad to engineered human specific T regulatory cells 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 4 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 15, 2016.3 a(CIT):  David W. Scott, Ph.D. is Vice Chair for Research in the Department of Medicine at the Uniformed Services School of Health Sciences, Bethesda, MD. An alumnus of Antioch College, he received his M.S. degree from the University of Chicago and Ph.D. from Yale University in 1969. Following a post-doctoral fellowship at Oxford University, he has held tenured faculty positions at Duke University, the University of Rochester, and the University of Maryland Medical School. He assumed his current position in September 2010. Dr. Scott has contributed to over 200 research papers on several subjects on immunologic tolerance, and its application in autoimmune diseases, hemophilia and gene therapy. He is the author of two textbooks, including The Nature of Immunologic Tolerance, and recipient of a number of awards, e.g. Distinguished Service Award from the American Association of Immunologists, a Boarhaave Professorship at Leiden University in Holland, and the 2009 Scientific Achievement Award from AAPS.  Abstract: Clinical application of expanded T regulatory cells (Tregs) offers great promise for the treatment of undesirable immune responses.  However, treatment with polyclonal Tregs, which are not specific for a single epitope, could potentially lead to global immunosuppression. We have engineered human T cells to express chimeric antigen receptors (CARs) using either T-cell receptors from MS or hemophilia patients or specific single chain Fv"s.  Thus, we adapted the CAR approach utilized successfully for leukemia therapy (e.g. CD19 CAR CD8's) to create specific T regulatory cells recognizing myelin basic protein (for MS) or FVIII, in the case of hemophilia. Such cells can actively suppress effector T cell proliferation and cytokine formation, as well as antibody formation in vitro.  Further development with B-cell targeting strategies have also been successful by expressing antigen domains in CD8 T cells that kill B cells specific for FVIII, for example. Application of these engineered T cells to modulate adverse antibody responses in hemophilia and pathogenic responses to CNS proteins will be presented, and the mechanisms further discussed.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 19, 2016).12aT-Lymphocytes, Regulatoryximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aScott, David W.,d1943-espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group,eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19755  aAUTH01436cas a2200409 i 9918266001106676DNLM20260227090921.0m     o  d |      cr#|||||||||||210914c20219999nyu|| poo     |    0eng    a  2022237387  a2667-0747l2667-0747  a(OCoLC)1280064301  aOH1bengerdad20260227cOH1dOCLCFdOCLCOdOCLCQdOCLCOdCUVdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW110aBiophys Rep (N Y)2dnlm00aBiophysical reports. 1a[New York] :bCell Press Elsevier,c[2021]-  a1 online resource  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rda content  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1, issue 1 (September 8, 2021).1 aMEDLINEbv4n1, Mar. 2024-0 aPubMedbv1n1, Sept. 2021-0 aPMC  aDescription based on: Volume 1, issue 1 (September 8, 2021); title from PFD table of contents (Science Direct, viewed October 21, 2021).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 2, issue 3 (September 14, 2022) (viewed November 4, 2022).12aBiophysical Phenomena 2aPeriodical40uhttps://www.cell.com/biophysreports/home40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=9918266001106676  aAUTH01525cgm a22004694a 101483460DNLM20260227152909.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080905s2008    mdu058|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14608  a(OCoLC)262647295  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 26.55.I41 aDuberman, Josh.10aInformation challenges in clinical research :baccess, rights, explosions, and other hot topics /cJosh Duberman, Pamela Sieving.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aClinical Center grand rounds  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 2, 2008).  aStreaming video (58 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Aug. 13, 2008.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aInformation Storage and Retrieval22aAccess to Information22aBiomedical Research22aCopyright22aDatabases, Bibliographic22aInternet 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14608  aAUTH02814cgm a2200493 i 9918265699806676DNLM20260227161504.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210913s2021    mdu544|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31889  a(DNLM)CIT:31890  a(OCoLC)1312792890  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQV 7452 aAssay Guidance Manual Training Workshopsd(2021 September 1-2 :cOnline)jauthor10aAGM Workshop on DNA-Encoded Libraries for Lead Discovery /cNCATS, NIH.1 iPowerPoint title:aAssay Guidance Workshop on DNA-Encoded Libraries for Lead Discovery 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (9 hr., 4 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 1 & 2, 2021.3 a(CIT): While attention around the use of DNA-Encoded Libraries (DEL) has rapidly grown over the past decade key aspects of DEL design and production, as well as their use for lead discovery, are scarcely detailed in the literature. NCATS Assay Guidance Manual (AGM) program has taken keen notice of this emerging technology and is hosting a two-day virtual workshop to seed discussion of best practices for using DELs. Many of the instructors are pioneers and experienced developers of the technology, and sessions are aimed at sharing DEL fundamentals to facilitate uptake and learning for both novice and established users. The workshop will include four sessions and feature seminars that highlight critical concepts about the technology including DEL library design &amp; production, affinity selection strategy and execution, sequencing and data analysis; and will close with a panel discussion about emerging trends and technological advances in the DEL field.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 21, 2022).12aDrug Discoveryxmethods12aGene Library22aSmall Molecule Librariesxchemistry22aDNAxgenetics22aDNAxchemistry22aBiological Assayxmethods 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aNational Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4262640uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42628  aAUTH01406cas a2200457   0152016DNLM20260227090952.0cr|cn ||||||||680903c19689999sz  x p       0    0eng    a0031-7012l0031-7012  aPHMGBN  a(DNLM)P11280000(s)  a(OCoLC)01597962  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Switzerland00aW1bPH28310aPharmacology2dnlm00aPharmacology.  aBasel (Switzerland) :bKarger,  av.bill.  aTwelve no. a year,b2002-  aTwelve no. a year,b1968-1999  a8 no. a year,b2000-2001  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-       1968-1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, 1968-0 aPubMedbv1n1, 1968-1 aIndex medicus  aNo. of issues per vol and the no. of vols. varies, 2002-  aSupplements accompany some numbers.  aAlso issued online.  3Vol. 20, no. 1 (1980)c19920831fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aPharmacology 2aPeriodical0 x1423-031301tMedicina et pharmacologia experimentalisx0543-