of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10579  aAUTH01921cgm a22004094a 101267688DNLM20260227145414.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu078 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10564  a(OCoLC)65616915  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4751 aKunkel, Thomas A.10aRecent studies of DNA mismatch repair /cTom Kunkel ; sponsored by NIH DNA Repair Interest Group.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Mar. 16, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 17 min., 10 sec : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 15, 2002.3 a(CIT): The DNA Repair Interest Group is concerned with all forms of DNA damage and repair. As a major defense against environmental damage to cells DNA repair is present in all organisms examined including bacteria, yeast, drosophila, fish, amphibians, rodents and humans. The members of the DNA Repair Interest Group perform research in areas including DNA repair enzymology and fine structure, mutagenesis, gene and cell cycle regulation, protein structure, and human disease.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aDNA Repairxphysiology22aBase Pair Mismatchxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bDNA Repair Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10564  aAUTH02408cgm a22004694a 101267580DNLM20260227145307.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu225 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10404  a(DNLM)CIT:10406  a(OCoLC)70713342  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 30000a2001 Hispanic heritage month celebration /cHispanic Heritage Month Observance Committee and NIH Hispanic Employee Organization.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 24, 2006).  aStreaming video (3 hr., 44 min., 35 sec. : sd., col.).0 apt. 1. Healthy families = a healthy nation -- pt. 2. Second annual NIH Hispanic scientists day Hispanic heritage month observance.  aRecorded Sept. 19, Oct. 1, 2001.3 a(CIT): Panel Discussion on Health Disparities in Latino/Hispanic Populations.  Introduction: Ms. Ana Anders, NIH-HEO.  Welcome: Dr. Ruth Kirschstein, acting NIH director.  Speakers:  Mr. Ray Suarez, PBS NewsHour (moderator), Washington, D.C.;  Dr. Jane Delgado, National Alliance for Hispanic Health (panelist), Washington, D.C.;  Dr. Joe L. Martinez, University of Texas San Antonio (panelist), San Antonio, TX;  Dr. Fernando Mendoza, Hispanic-Serving Health Professions Schools (panelist), Palo Alto, CA;  Dr. John Ruffin, director, National Center on Minority Health and Health Disparities (panelist), Bethesda, Md.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aHispanic or Latino.22aHealth Services Accessibility22aMedicine 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bHispanic Heritage Month Observance Committee.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1040440uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10406  aAUTH02641nam a2200385 i 9918987892906676DNLM20260225092706.0230217s2024    mauac    b    001 0 eng    aGBC3390242bnb  a9780357766040qpaperback  a0357766040  a(OCoLC)1371814747  aUKMGBbengerdacUKMGBdOCLCFdOCLCOdYDXdY@Y  apcc  9United States 4aRS200b.M65 202400aQV 77904a615.192231 aMoini, Jahangir,d1942-eauthor.10aSterile compounding /cJahangir Moini, Obehi Enabulele, Anthony Scott. 1aBoston, MA :bCengage,c[2024]  axv, 203 pages :bcolor illustrations, portraits ;c28 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.0 aChapter 1. Introduction -- Chapter 2. Safety in the Workplace -- Chapter 3. Equipment, Garments, and Gowning -- Chapter 4. Aseptic Technique -- Chapter 5. Clean Air Environments and Equipment -- Chapter 6. Dosage Calculations -- Chapter 7. Sterile Compounding and Preparations -- Chapter 8. United States Pharmacopeia (USP) -- Chapter 9. Repackaging, Labeling, and Storage -- Chapter 10. Recordkeeping Requirements -- Chapter 11. Quality Control and Assurance.  a"Moini/Enabulele/Scott's STERILE COMPOUNDING represents one of the most critical aspects of the healthcare treatment environment and providing patient care. This brand new offering gives students a comprehensive understanding of all the steps involved with the compounding process and the knowledge base needed to provide the best training to excel within practice. Aligned to American Society of Health-System Pharmacists (ASHP) pharmacy technician program standards and competencies, STERILE COMPOUNDING presents step-by-step procedures and lab activities coupled with case studies and critical thinking questions to help students apply what they are learning to actual practice. Certified Compounded Sterile Preparation Technician (CSPT) style review questions at the end of each chapter help students get certification-ready. A robust instructor support package complete with sample syllabi, test banks and instructor slides enhance STERILE COMPOUNDING for your students" -- Publisher.12aDrug Compoundingxstandards22aSterilizationxstandards22aDrug Dosage Calculations1 aEnabulele, Obehi,eauthor.1 aScott, AnthonycPharmD,eauthor.  aAUTH02233cgm a2200481 i 101707006DNLM20260227155853.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u170603s2017    mdu053|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:23314  a(OCoLC)990961675  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 6800aMyeloid-derived trefoil factor 2 (TFF2) controls epithelial regeneration at the mucosal interface /cDe'Broski Herbert.1 iTitle on PowerPoint screen:aTrefoil factors, helminth infection, and epithelial regeneration 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2017]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (53 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aImmunology Interest Group seminar series  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded May 24, 2017.3 a(CIT): Trefoil factor proteins, an enigmatic family of mucosal cytokines, are known to promote tissue repair.  Despite being discovered decades ago, not much was known until recently about how Trefoil proteins work. Dr. Herbert's laboratory has discovered that these proteins regulate immune responses in allergic asthma and hookworm infection, and that they trigger the regeneration of epithelial cells.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed June 16, 2017).12aTrefoil Factor-2ximmunology22aHelminthiasisximmunology22aImmunity, Mucosal22aIntestinal Diseases, Parasiticximmunology22aRe-Epithelializationximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aHerbert, De'Broski,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group,esponsoring body. 0aImmunology Interest Group lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23314  aAUTH01594cas a2200493 a 100898849DNLM20260227091100.0cr|cnu||||||||000411c20009999gw  x p       0    0eng    a1438-4221l1438-4221  a(OCoLC)44107614  aCUYcCUYdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9Germany 4aQR1b.Z4500aW1bIN769RP10aInt J Med Microbiol2dnlm00aInternational journal of medical microbiology :bIJMM.30aIJMM  aJena, Germany :bUrban & Fischer Verlag,cc2000-