||||680808c19689999sz  x m       0    0eng    a0012-2823l0012-2823  aDIGEBW  a(DNLM)D10800000(s)  a(OCoLC)01566646  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Switzerland00aW1bDI5710aDigestion2dnlm00aDigestion.  aBasel,bKarger.  av.billus.  a8 no. a year,b2000-  aTwelve no. a yearb19??-1992  a6 no. a year,b1993-1999  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-       1968-1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, 1968-1 aPubMedbv1n1, 1968-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso available online.  aSupersedes Gastroenterologia.  3Vol. 20, no. 1 (1980)c19920828fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aGastroenterology 2aPeriodical0 x1421-986712tGastroenterologia4 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/223838  aAUTH03338cgm a2200445 i 101660644DNLM20260227155044.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150602s2015    mdu710 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19024  a(DNLM)CIT:19026  a(OCoLC)918550308  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 4752 aGTEx Symposiumd(2015 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.14aThe GTEx Symposium :ball things considered - biospecimens, omics data and ethical issues /cNational Cancer Institute, National Institute for Mental Health, National Human Genome Research Institute, NIH Common Fund.1 aGenotype-Tissue Expression Symposium 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a2 online resources (1 streaming video file (11 hr., 50 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded May 20 and 21, 2015.3 a(CIT): Co-hosted by the National Cancer Institute (NCI), the National Human Genome Research Institute (NHGRI), the National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH), and the NIH Common Fund, the symposium will highlight the scientific goals of the GTEx program and progress in collecting and analyzing biospecimens from over 25 organ sites from 900 post-mortem donors. This two-day event is designed to inform the research community of the resources and infrastructure that are available for their use.  About GTEx: The Genotype-Tissue Expression (GTEx) program is a National Institutes of Health (NIH) Common Fund study. The overall goal of the GTEx project is to increase our understanding of how changes in our genes contribute to common human disease, in order to improve health care for future generations. To achieve that goal, GTEx has been collecting and analyzing biospecimens from over 900 post mortem donors. With over 800 cases of the total collection completed and analyzed to date, the GTEx program stands poised to make a significant impact on the understanding of human biology and disease. GTEx is a unique program that can provide a deep understanding of normal gene expression, gene mutation, and variation.  This two day symposium will cover a broad range of topics that focus on the collection of normal biospecimens for GTEx. Topics include an overview of the GTEx program; the biospecimen challenges for GTEx; establishing the approach for GTEx prospective collections; new systems that were developed to implement the program; ethical, legal, and societal investigations within the program; and scientific outcomes from the work thus far.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 30, 2015).22aGenotype-Tissue Expression Program.12aGene Expression22aGene Expression Regulation22aSpecimen Handling 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1902440uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19026  aAUTH01264cas a2200421 a 101481266DNLM20260227090922.0cr|cn ||||||||080612c20089999nyubr p       0    0eng    a  2008214562  a1942-7891l1942-7891  a(OCoLC)231763639  aNSDcNSDdNSDdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bDI748310aPopul Health Manag2dnlm00aPopulation health management.13