  vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11291  a(OCoLC)70119560  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2001 aKramer, Barnett S.,d1948-10aCancer screening :bthe clash between intuition and the scientific method /cBarnett Kramer.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 24, 2006).  aStreaming video (56 min., 41 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Aug. 27, 2003.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxprevention & control22aMass Screening22aOutcome Assessment, Health Care22aRisk Assessment 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11291  aAUTH02907cgm a2200457 i 101774299DNLM20260227160519.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu067|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31418  a(OCoLC)1248768686  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 14000aShake, rattle, and roll :bcapturing snapshots of ribonucleotide reductase in action /cCathy Drennan, Ph.D., Professor of Biology and Chemistry, Massachusetts Institute of Technology. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 7 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded December 2, 2020.3 a(CIT): Dr. Drennan is a professor of biology and chemistry at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, as well as a professor and investigator with the Howard Hughes Medical Institute. Her research interests lie at the interface of chemistry and biology, combining X-ray crystallography with cryo-electron microscopy and other biophysical methods in order to "visualize" molecular processes by obtaining snapshots of metalloproteins in action.The use of radical-based chemistry allows for amazing transformations in living organisms. To carry out their functions, radical enzymes often need to be flexible and assume different conformational states.  In this talk, the conformational gymnastics involved in ribonucleotide reduction will be considered. Ribonucleotide reductases (RNRs) are radical enzymes that convert ribonucleotides (the building blocks of RNA) to deoxyribonucleotides (the building blocks of DNA). RNRs are targets for cancer chemotherapies and have been proposed as candidates for antimicrobial therapies. Dr. Drennan will describe how her lab has employed biophysical methods to interrogate how RNRs shake, rattle, and roll to accomplish their critical cellular function.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 21, 2021).12aRibonucleotide Reductasesxphysiology12aRibonucleotide Reductasesxbiosynthesis22aBiosynthetic Pathways22aBiological Transport22aMolecular Targeted Therapy 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aDrennan, Cathy,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38980  aAUTH01748cgm a22004334a 101285727DNLM20260227151538.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060822s2006    mdu070 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13299  a(OCoLC)71328336  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWD 3051 aBolland, Silvia.10aGenes regulating autoimmune disease :binhibitory factors and fine tuning tLRs /cSylvia Bolland.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Sept. 5, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 9 min., 53 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 14, 2006.3 a(CIT): The Immunology Interest Group (IIG) organizes activities designed to promote information exchange and interactions among NIH scientists interested in the field of immunology, broadly defined. Interactions are facilitated via weekly meetings on current topics as well as an annual Immunology Retreat.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aAutoimmune Diseasesxgenetics22aGene Expression Regulationximmunology22aSignal Transductionximmunology22aToll-Like Receptorsximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13299  aAUTH01931cas a2200541   0417337DNLM20260227090927.0cr||||||||||||740618c19569999ru br p       0||| 0rus    a0507-4088l0507-4088  aVVIRAT  a(DNLM)V12120000(s)  a(OCoLC)01769293  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 arusbeng  9Russia (Federation)00aW1bVO64910aVopr Virusol2dnlm 0aVoprosy virusologii00aVoprosy virusologii. 1aMoskva :bMinisterstvo zdravookhranenii︠a︡ SSSR21aMoskva :bMedit︠s︡ina31aMoskva :bFBUN T︠S︡NII Ėpidemiologii Rospotrebnadzora  av.billus.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 ag. 1-       ianv./fevr. 1956-  aPrint issues ceased with v. 63, no. 3 (2018).1 aMEDLINEbv10n1, Jan./Feb. 1965-0 aPubMedbv10n1, Jan./Feb. 1965-0 aOLDMEDLINE1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aIssued by Ministerstvo zdravookhraneniia SSSR.  aTranslated as: Problems of virology, 1957-61; Soviet progress in virology, 1980-1992; Russian progress in virology, 1993- . 2aVirus Diseases 2aViruses 2aPeriodical1 aSoviet Union.bMinisterstvo zdravookhranenii︠a︡.1 tVoprosy virusologii. English. Problems of virologyx0555-2621w(OCoLC)17629101 tVoprosy virusologii. English. Soviet progress in virologyx0734-0311w(OCoLC)792