o00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 146 no.51 aAshford, Bailey K.q(Bailey Kelly),d1873-1934,eauthor.10aClinical investigation of tropical sprue.lSpanish10aInvestigaciones clinicas sobre el espru de los tropicos /cpor Bailey K. Ashford.  aPuerto Rico :bTipografia El Compas,c1923.  a28 pages, 3 unnumbered leaves of plates :billustrations ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"(Traducido de acuerdo especial con el 'American Journal of the Medical Scientist')."  a"Reimpreso del Boletin de la Asociacion Medica de Puerto Rico."  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202102022pda5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20260213lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM12aSprue, Tropicalxdiagnosis22aDiagnostic Techniques and Procedures22aDiagnosis, Differential22aEndocrine System Diseases 2aCase Reports0 aBoletín de la Asociación Médica de Puerto Rico.  aAUTH03266cgm a2200445 i 101634133DNLM20260227155215.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140603s2014    mdu056|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18426  a(OCoLC)881508392  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 2741 aHeberlein, Ulrike,eauthor.10aDrosophila as a model for alcoholism :ban interplay of nature and nurture /cUlrike Heberlein. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (56 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded May 7, 2014.3 a(CIT): Alcohol is one of the most widely used and abused drugs in the world, with devastating medical and social consequences. The estimated prevalence of Alcohol Use Disorders is 8.5 percent in the United States, thus affecting more than 17 million Americans. The difficulty and cost of human studies have led to the development of animal models to investigate the genetic, molecular, and neural mechanisms underlying both the short- and long-term effects of ethanol. Rodent models have been most widely used and have provided important insights into these mechanisms. More recently, the fruit fly Drosophila melanogaster has been developed as a model to study the effects of ethanol. The experimental and genetic accessibility of Drosophila makes it an ideal organism to study molecular, cellular and neurobiological processes, most of which are conserved between flies and mammals.  During acute ethanol exposure, adult flies exhibit locomotor hyperactivity followed by motor incoordination and sedation. Repeated ethanol exposures can induce either tolerance or sensitization in adult flies, depending on the behavioral response being measured; chronic ethanol exposure induces alcohol dependence and relapse-like behavior. In addition, flies exhibit more complex addiction-like behaviors, including a lasting attraction for a cue that predicts ethanol intoxication and a preference for consuming ethanol-containing food even if it"s made unpalatable.    In addition to a discussion about the validity of Drosophila as a model for alcoholism, the lecture will include an analysis of genetic and environmental/experiential factors that contribute to alcohol-induced behaviors.For more information go to <a href='http://wals.od.nih.gov/'>http://wals.od.nih.gov.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed June 18, 2014.12aAlcoholismxphysiopathology22aDrosophilaxdrug effects22aModels, Animal 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18426  aAUTH01692cas a2200469 a 101163270DNLM20260227090912.0cr|cn ||||||||020211c19999999ne fr p       0   