280  a(OCoLC)51510992  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aWB 141bVC no.11 200200aMysterious cases /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  aUnited States  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (77 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (77 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aClinical Center grand rounds great teachers series  aSpeaker, Faith Fitzgerald.  a"June 12, 2002."  aIssued also online.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM12aDiagnosis 2aCase Reports1 aFitzgerald, Faith T.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aClinical Center grand rounds great teachers series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=1002  aAUTH03002nam a2200385 i 9919054233906676DNLM20260223110103.0m     o  d i      cr|cn#||||||||250724t20222023dcua    ob   i000 0 eng    a9789275126196q(PDF)  z9789275126202q(print)  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 27500aNutritional care guide for pediatric cancer /cwritten by Erika Damasco. 1aWashington, D.C. :bPAHO,c2022. 4c©2023  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (48 pages)) :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 aThis guide has been developed as part of the World Health Organization's Global Initiative for Childhood Cancer, known as "Cure All Americas," with the primary purpose of alleviating the challenges faced by pediatric oncology patients. Its mission is to ensure that these young patients enjoy a full and high-quality life while receiving comprehensive nutritional care throughout the course of their illness. In Latin America and the Caribbean, the prevalence of malnutrition in children and adolescents diagnosed with cancer is alarmingly high at the time of diagnosis. Malnutrition not only undermines recovery prospects but also deteriorates the quality of life for these patients. This guide is aimed at the multidisciplinary pediatric oncology care team, including medical professionals, nutritionists, nurses, and other specialists. Drawing from the most robust scientific evidence, it addresses essential aspects such as nutritional screening, risk assessment, nutritional care planning, and the promotion of a healthy lifestyle for cancer survivors. It is worth emphasizing that this guide represents a milestone in the battle against childhood cancer in the region, as it seeks to significantly enhance the quality of life for pediatric patients affected by this disease. By providing healthcare professionals with the necessary tools and knowledge to address malnutrition and its impact on cancer treatment, we are taking a significant step towards a more hopeful future for the children and adolescents facing this challenging battle. The guide is an essential resource that offers comprehensive support in addressing the nutritional and health challenges of these young patients, thereby contributing to more effective treatment and a more promising prognosis.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed on February 23, 2026)12aNeoplasmsxdiet therapy12aChild22aAdolescent22aInfant 2aPractice Guideline2 aPan American Health Organization,eissuing body.40uhttps://iris.paho.org/handle/10665.2/58361  aAUTH02112cam a2200445 i 63150870RDNLM20260217105831.0hd|bfa012baap820626s1902    dcuaf    b   f001 0 eng    a   02023639   a(OCoLC)6244990  aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  9United States00aWAAbK75m 19021 aKober, George M.q(George Martin),d1850-1931,eauthor.10aMilk in relation to public health :bthe necessity for the enactment of Senate bill entitled "A bill to regulate the product