(1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH CRM/SCIG stem cell seminar  aRecorded Sept. 6, 2011.3 a(CIT): Human embryonic stem cells (ESCs) and human induce pluripotent stem cells (iPSCs) can provide theoretically unlimited supplies of human cardiac myocytes(CMs) for research and potentially clinical applications.  Furthermore, generation of iPSCs from genetically diverse samples will enable creation of libraries of iPSCs that will better reflect the genetic diversity of the population as well as generate disease-specific models.  However, the promise of these unique cells is only starting to be realized as technical roadblocks are overcome and proof-of-principle studies are undertaken.  For example, the differentiation of human pluripotent stem cells to cardiomyocytes has been highly inefficient, but here we will described a novel approach using extracellular matrix in combination with growth factors to produce in high yield and purity CMs.  The presentation will also describe the use of human iPSCs to model Pompe Disease and Long QT syndrome.  The final section will describe a unique cardiac progenitor cell population that we have isolated using the SRF-cardiac specific enhancer which may prove useful in cardiac repair.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aEmbryonic Stem Cellsxphysiology12aInduced Pluripotent Stem Cellsxphysiology12aMyocytes, Cardiacxcytology22aCell Differentiationxphysiology22aGlycogen Storage Disease Type II22aLong QT Syndrome 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bStem Cell Interest Group. 0aNIH stem cell seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16831  aAUTH00975cam a22003011  4500001001000000003000500010005001700015007001400032008004100046010001700087035002000104040002100124044001800145060002300163100002100186245002200207250004100229