lted: Vol. 1, no. 3 (Sept. 2009).  aPrint issues ceased with v. 2, no. 4 (2010).1 aMEDLINEbv9, Dec. 2017-0 aPubMedbv1, 2009-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.12aMental Disorders22aCentral Nervous System Diseases22aDevelopmental Disabilities 2aPeriodical0 x1866-19554 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=1014838324 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/11689  aAUTH01805cam a2200349 i 63020300RDNLM20260227130041.0hd|bfa012baap820626s1902    nyu      l    001 0 eng    a(OCoLC)14807443  aDNLMbengerdad20260226dDNLM  9United States00aWAbN576h 19021 aNew York (State),eenacting jurisdiction.14aThe health officers' manual and public health law of the state of New York :bcontaining the Public Health Law (Laws of 1893, Chap. 661), and all statutes relating to the public health, powers and duties of local boards of health and health officers, adulterations of food, medical, dental and veterinary practice, pharmacy, etc. : as amended to the close of the legislative session of 1902 /cwith annotations, forms, and cross-references, by L. L. Boyce. 1aAlbany :bMatthew Bender,c1902.  axii, 289 pages ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aNLM microfilm copy shelved as: Film 3158 no. 15DNLM  aMicrofilm.b[Bethesda, Md.] :cNational Library of Medicine,d1990.e1 microfilm reel : negative ; 35 mm.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202011192pda5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20260227lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM12aPublic Healthxlegislation & jurisprudence22aPublic Health Administration 2aNew York 2aLegislation1 aBoyce, L. L.q(Linn Llewellyn),d1851-ewriter of supplementary textual content.  aAUTH01273cas a2200421   8007862DNLM20260227090954.0cr|cn#||||||||800821c19809999xxubr         0||| 0eng    a0164-0291l0164-0291  aIJPRDA  a(DNLM)I27750000(s)  a(OCoLC)04556695  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bIN775K10aInt J Primatol2dnlm 0aInternational journal of primatology00aInternational journal of primatology.  aNew York,bPlenum Press.3 31999- :aNew York, NY :bKluwer Academic/Plenum Publishers  av.bill.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-    Mar. 1980-0 aMEDLINEbSelected citations only0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.  aOfficial journal of the International Primatological Society. 2aPrimates 2aPeriodical2 aInternational Primatological Society.0 x1573-86044 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/10764  aAUTH02519cgm a2200433 i 101647969DNLM20260227154757.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u141202s2014    mdu054       fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18734  a(OCoLC)900260563  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWD 3001 aMilner, Joshua D.eauthor.10aPathogenic pathways in allergy gleaned from monogenic disease /cJoshua D. Milner. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (54 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Nov. 14, 2014.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Seminar Series. Monogenic diseases can be highly instructive in uncovering basic pathways whose perturbations lead to specific symptoms.  Monogenic diseases associated with allergy present an opportunity to define pathways critical for preventing allergic disease. Previous studies of such diseases have understandably focused on the more severe associated phenotypes such as infection, autoimmunity or neoplasms.  Our lab wishes to mechanistically understand how these mutations lead to the allergic phenotype, in order to learn globally important lessons in allergy pathogenesis. Additionally, we seek to define new genetic diseases of allergy by focusing on allergic symptoms as the inherited trait, and carefully examining more subtle phenotypes which may not have been brought to the attention of a medical professional, but provide invaluable tools in identifying novel disorders. This seminar will focus on several genetic diseases of allergy recently described by our lab along with a known one, and the pathophysiologic insight they have provided.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed January 13, 2015.12aHypersensitivityxgenetics22aHypersensitivityxpathology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18734  aAUTH01195cas a2200409 a 101550313DNLM20260227090921.0cr||||||||||||101014c20119999nyubr p       0    0eng    a  20102015010 a2158-0014l2158-0014  a(OCoLC)670079024  aDLCcDLCdDLCdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW1bBR113BL10aBrain Connect2dnlm00aBrain connectivity.  aNew Rochelle, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1, no. 1 (2011).1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, 2011-0 aPubMedbv1n1, 2011-  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: Vol. 1, no. 1 (2011); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 1, no. 1 (2011).12aBrainxphysiology22aMental Disorders22aNervous System Diseases 2aPeriodical0 x2158-00224 uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/brain  aAUTH01926cgm a22004694a 101296471DNLM20260227152158.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070103s2006    mdu063 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13546  a(OCoLC)85817787  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWG 2101 aVan Eyk, Jennifer E.,d1959-10aProteomic insights into mitochondria in cardiac disease /cJennifer van Eyk.10iTitle on PowerPoint screen:aProteomic Analysis of Cardiac Mitochondria in Health and Disease  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 27, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 2 min., 50 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 8, 2006.3 a(CIT): Speaker: Jennifer van Eyk, PhD  Director, The Hopkins NHLBI Proteomics Center  Director, Bayview Proteomics Group  Associate Professor Medicine, Division of Cardiology  Biological Chemistry and Biomedical Engineering  Johns Hopkins University   For more information, visit   http://proteome.nih.gov.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government.  aOpen-captioned.12aHeart Diseasesxmetabolism22aIschemic Preconditioning, Myocardial22aMitochondriaxmetabolism22aProteomexmetabolism22aProteomics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bProteomics Interest Group40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13546  aAUTH01090cas a2200397 a 9101499DNLM20260227091017.0cr|cn ||||||||910131c19919999njuqr p       0||| 0eng    a1048-1885l1048-1885  a(DNLM)SR0068337(s)  a(OCoLC)20863332  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bPS39710aPsychoanal Dialogues2dnlm 0aPsychoanalytic dialogues00aPsychoanalytic dialogues.  aHillsdale, NJ :bAnalytic Press,cc1991-  av.bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1-  aQuarterly.  aTitle from cover.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online. 2aPsychoanalysis 2aPsychoanalytic Theory 2aPeriodical0 x1940-92224 uhttps://www.tandfonline.com/loi/hpsd20  aAUTH02461cgm a2200421 i 101765812DNLM20260227160059.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u200410s2020    mdu052|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:29046  a(OCoLC)1153551100  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWJ 76200aComplex decision making in prostate cancer /cRandy A. Jones. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (52 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNINR director"s lecture  aRecorded March 5, 2020.3 a(CIT): NINR Director's Lecture. Dr. Jones' research and publications focus on health disparities, prostate cancer, chronic illness, end-of-life care, and decision-making. He has received funding from organizations such as the National Institutes of Health, Robert Wood Johnson Foundation (RWJF), American Cancer Society, and the American Nurses Foundation. He is currently a principal investigator on an NINR-funded study focusing on treatment decision-making among patients with advanced prostate cancer.  Dr. Jones is a fellow in the American Academy of Nursing (AAN), a RWJF Nurse Faculty Scholar Alumnus, and a National Advisory Committee member for RWJF Clinical Scholars and AAN's Institute for Nursing Leadership.  Dr. Jones is committed to promoting research, evidence-based practice, health policy, and enhancing higher education and clinical practice.  The NINR Director"s Lecture Series is designed to bring the nation"s top nurse scientists to the NIH campus to share their work and interests with a transdisciplinary audience.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 30, 2020)12aProstatic Neoplasmsxtherapy22aClinical Decision-Making 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aJones, Randy A.,espeaker.2 aNational Institute of Nursing Research (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNINR director's lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?29046  aAUTH01550cgm a22004574a 101268586DNLM20260227151004.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz