27091018.0cr#un#||||||||941103c19919999ja mr p       0||| 0jpn    asn 95038090   a0915-8669l0915-8669  aIGKEES  a(DNLM)SR0070591(s)  a(OCoLC)31899168  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Japan00aW1bIG385G00aIgaku kensa :bNihon Rinshō Eisei Kensa Gishikai shi = Japanese journal of medical technology.31aJapanese journal of medical technology30aNihon Rinshō Eisei Kensa Gishikai shi  aTōkyō :bDō Gishikai,c1991-3 aTokyo :bNihon Rinsho Eisei Kensa Gishi Kaishi  av. :bill.  aMonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aDai 40-kan dai 1-gō (1991-1)-  aTitle from cover.  aAlso issued online.  aText in Japanese; table of contents also in English.22aNihon Rinshō Eisei Kensa Gishikai. 2aBiomedical Technology 2aPeriodical2 aNihon Rinshō Eisei Kensa Gishikai.00tEisei kensa (Tokyo, Japan : 1967)x0367-052Xw(DLC)sn 84001494w(OCoLC)102636654 uhttps://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/browse/jamt/  aAUTH02865cgm a22004694a 101308578DNLM20260227152056.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070605s2007    mdu073 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13818  a(OCoLC)147471743  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 102.81 aMaricq, Andres Villu.10aGlutamate-activated signaling complexes :bnew insights from genetic analysis /cAndres Villu Maricq.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen (viewed June 19, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 12 min., 5 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 14, 2007.3 a(CIT): Glutamate is the major excitatory neurotransmitter in the brain and exerts its actions by binding to several classes of glutamate-activated receptors. Since dysfunction of glutamate-receptor-mediated signaling is implicated in various mental health and neurological disorders, there is intense interest in gaining a greater understanding of the molecules required for the assembly, localization and function of ionotropic glutamate receptors (iGluRs).    In Dr. Maricq's laboratory, they have demonstrated that AMPA (GLR-1, GLR-2) and NMDA (NMR-1) subtypes of glutamate receptors contribute to specific avoidance and foraging behaviors in C. elegans . How are the function of these receptors regulated and how are they localized to synapses? Using a genetic approach they have identified a new transmembrane protein, SOL-1 that co-localizes with GLR-1 and that is absolutely required for AMPA receptor function. More recently, they have found a second transmembrane protein that is required for glutamate-gated currents. These new discoveries will help provide a mechanistic understanding of how different classes of iGluRs are distributed to and maintained at specific synapses, how they participate in synaptic communication, and how they contribute to the behavior of C. elegans.    NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aReceptors, AMPAxphysiology12aReceptors, N-Methyl-D-Aspartatexphysiology22aBrainxphysiology22aGlutamic Acid22aSynaptic Transmissionxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13818  aAUTH02277cgm a22004334a 101470359DNLM20260227152521.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080401s2008    mdu656 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14366  a(DNLM)CIT:14369  a(OCoLC)261371992  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 58.52 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bRecombinant DNA Advisory Committee.10aRecombinant DNA Advisory Committee (RAC), March 2008 /cOffice of Biotechnology Activities.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 19, 2008).  aStreaming video (10 hr., 56 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 11-12, 2008.3 a(CIT): The RAC is a technical committee whose goal is to consider the current state of knowledge and technology regarding recombinant DNA.  This includes review of human gene transfer trials, and an assessment of the ability of DNA recombinants to survive in nature and the potential for transfer of genetic material to other organisms.  It also considers hypothetical hazards and methods for monitoring and minimizing risks. Approximately one-third of the 15 members do not have scientific expertise but represent public interests and attitudes. This balance is intended to provide a forum for open public debate of social and scientific issues attendant to recombinant DNA research.  The RAC has been overwhelmingly successful in achieving this goal.   For more information, visit the  Recombinant DNA Advisory Committee (RAC) Conference Web Site.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDNA, Recombinant22aClinical Protocolsxstandards22aGenetic Therapy 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1436640uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14369  aAUTH01567cas a2200445 a 9208482DNLM20260227091017.0cr|cn#||||||||920604c19929999nyu x p       0    0eng    a0963-1801l0963-1801  a(DNLM)SR0072221(s)  a(OCoLC)25292033  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bCA4538G10aCamb Q Healthc Ethics2dnlm00aCambridge quarterly of healthcare ethics :bCQ : the international journal of healthcare ethics committees.30aCQ30aCambridge quarterly of health care ethics  aNew York, NY :bCambridge University Press,cc1992-  av. :bill.  aFour no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (winter 1992)-  aQuarterly.  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv1n1,winter 1992-1 aPubMedbv1n1,winter 1992-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso available online.  3Vol. 3, no. 4 (FALL 1994)c19950519fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paperxFurther review is required to determine exactly when acid-free paper was first used for this title 2aEthics, Medical 2aProfessional Staff Committees 2aPeriodical0 x1469-21474 uhttps://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/cambridge-quarterly-of-healthcare-ethics  aAUTH01941ckm a2200421 i 101684695DNLM20260225100048.0cr||||||||||||kp#bo#160607s1915    au nnn            knger    a(OCoLC)971551725  aDNLMbengerdad20260225dDNLM  9Austria  aPP059706.53101 aPietzner, Carl,d1853-1927,ephotographer.10aErzherxogin Maria Josefa /cPietzner. 1aWien :bPostkartenverlag Brüder Kohn,c[1915]  a1 postcard :bblack & white ;c14 x 9 cm  astill imagebsti2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  acardbno2rdacarrier  aAccession no.: 04-22.5DNLM  aNLM provides access to digital images in lieu of originals when electronic copies exist.  Access to originals may require advance notice.  Please contact the NLM Support Center for more information.5DNLM0 aPostcard featuring a black and white image of Princess Maria Josefa of Saxony. She is seated with her hands folded in her lap. She is wearing a nurse's uniform which consists of a long, high-necked dress and white cap. There is a Red Cross armband on her left arm. During WWI she nursed the wounded at the Augarten Palace in Vienna, which served as a hospital.  aAlso available online.  aThis item may be under copyright protection; contact the copyright owner for permission before re-use.5DNLM  cPurchase;aMichael Zwerdling;d2004;e04-22.5DNLM02aMaria Josefa,cPrincess of Saxony,d1867-1944.12aFamous Persons12aNurses22aWorld War I 2aAustria 2aPostcard1 aZwerdling, Michael,eformer owner.5DNLM2 aPostkartenverlag Brüder Kohn,epublisher.4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/101684695  aAUTH01758cgmaa22004694a 101247326DNLM20260227144