resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Volume 1 (November 2023).0 aPubMedbv1, 2023-0 aPMC  aArticles published as they become available and compiled into issues.  aDescription based on: Volume 1 (November 2023); title from resource description page (ScienceDirect, viewed January 25, 2024).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 9 (September 2025) (ScienceDirect, viewed August 15, 2025).12aNeoplasmsxtherapy22aDatasets as Topic22aData Science 2aPeriodical2 aEuropean Society for Medical Oncology,eissuing body.40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=991905363750667640uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/esmo-real-world-data-and-digital-oncology  aAUTH01646cam a2200385 i 9919051931706676DNLM20260206222619.0cr||||||||||||821118r19851982mduac    b   f001 0deng    aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aZ 733.N277bM643h 19821 aMiles, Wyndham D.,d1916-2011,eauthor.12aA history of the National Library of Medicine :bthe nation's treasury of medical knowledge /cby Wyndham D. Miles. 1aBethesda, Maryland. :bU.S. Dept. of Health and Human Services, Public Health Service, National Institutes of Health, National Library of Medicine,c1982.  aviii, 531 pages :billustrations, portraits. ;c26 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aNIH publication ;vno. 82-1904  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20250721lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM22aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.)12aLibraries, Medicalxhistory22aHistory, 19th Century22aHistory, 20th Century22aInformation Storage and Retrievalxhistory 2aUnited States 0aNIH publication ;vno. 82-1904.4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/9919051931706676  aAUTH01774nam a2200349 i 9919052732606676DNLM20260205085151.0m     o  d f      cr|cn#||||||||250709s2025    gaua    ob   f000 0 eng    a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK615719  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQV 6271 aShoeb, Mohammad,eauthor.10aToxicological profile for Acrylonitrile /cU.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Agency for Toxic Substances and Disease Registry. 1aAtlanta, GA :bAgency for Toxic Substances and Disease Registry,cApril 2025.  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (various paging)) :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  a"Contributors & reviewers: Mohammad Shoeb, Ph.D. (Lead)  [and 5 others]"--Page v.  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 aThe ATSDR toxicological profile succinctly characterizes the toxicology and adverse health effects information for the toxic substance described therein. Each peer-reviewed profile identifies and reviews the key literature that describes a substance's toxicological properties. Profiles are updated on a periodic basis and released for a public comment period. For more information visit ATSDR.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed Jan. 28, 2026).12aAcrylonitrilextoxicity22aToxicokinetics1 aUnited States.bAgency for Toxic Substances and Disease Registry,eissuing body.40uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK615719/  aAUTH08120cam a2200373 i 9919052735006676DNLM20260220085042.0m     o  d        cr|cn#||||||||250709s2023    dcua    ob    000 0 eng    a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK615403  aDNLMbengerdad20260220dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWE 5551 aNarayanaswami, Pushpa,eauthor.10aComparing two medicines for adults with myasthenia gravis - the PROMISE-MG study /cPushpa Narayanaswami, Donald B. Sanders, Kathie Bibeau, Andrew Krueger, Jurgen Venitz, Laine Thomas, Dylan Thibault, Rishi Desai, Fan Li, Bo Liu, Jason Blevins, Jeffrey T. Guptill ; the PROMISE-MG Study Group. 1aWashington, DC :bPatient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute,c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (80 pages)) :billustrations.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aFinal research report  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 aBACKGROUND: Myasthenia gravis (MG) is an autoimmune neuromuscular disease caused by autoantibodies directed at the neuromuscular junction. The estimated prevalence is 14 to 40/100 000. There are no comparative effectiveness studies of immunosuppressive treatments for MG. OBJECTIVES: The Prospective Multicenter Observational Cohort Study of Comparative Effectiveness of Disease-Modifying Treatments for Myasthenia Gravis was designed to compare patient-reported outcomes (PROs) and clinician-reported outcomes (CROs) in patients with MG who receive oral azathioprine (AZA) vs oral mycophenolate mofetil (MMF) and to compare PROs and CROs in patients with MG receiving an adequate dosage and duration of AZA or MMF vs patients receiving AZA or MMF at lesser dose/duration. METHODS: A new-user design was used. Patients with MG were recruited when first seen at the study site and followed for up to 36 months. There were no study-imposed interventions; patients were treated with standard of care determined by the treating clinician. The primary outcome was the change from baseline in the Myasthenia Gravis Quality of Life 15 (revised) (MG-QOL15r), a patient-reported (PRO) MG-specific quality-of-life outcome measure. The co-primary outcome measure was a composite clinician-reported outcome (CRO) with two components: clinical improvement defined as achieving the Myasthenia Gravis Foundation of America Post-Intervention Status minimal manifestation or better and adverse effects of treatments being no more than grade 1 Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events side effects. The co-primary outcome was the proportion of patients in each treatment group achieving both components of this composite measure. Secondary outcomes were the changes from baseline in the MG Composite (MGC), MG-Activities of Daily Living Scale (MG-ADL), MG-Manual Muscle Testing (MG-MMT), and hospitalizations for MG. Clinically meaningful reductions in the measures were MG-QOL15r, ≥5 points; MG-ADL, ≥2 points; MGC, ≥3 points; and MG-MMT, ≥3 points. The interventions were oral AZA vs oral MMF and treatment with an adequate dosage and duration of AZA and MMF vs lesser dosage and duration. The adequate dosage and duration for AZA was 2 mg/kg/day for 12 months; for MMF the adequate dosage and duration was 2 g/day for 8 months. Descriptive statistics were used for baseline characteristics after multiple imputation of missing data. Wilcoxon rank-sum test was used for continuous variables, and chi-square test or Fisher exact test was used for categorical variables. Propensity score overlap weighting was used to evaluate the comparative effectiveness of the two agents. A weighted generalized linear regression model with appropriate link functions was used. Robust variance estimators were used to derive 95% CIs. Significance was set at P ≤ .05. Secondary outcomes were not adjusted for multiple comparisons. RESULTS: A total of 167 patients were enrolled; 82 received either MMF or AZA; four were excluded since their outcome measures suggested good disease control at onset of treatment with AZA/MMF, leaving 78 patients in the primary analysis set (47 patients, 60% received MMF and 31, 40% received AZA. A clinically meaningful change in MG-QOL15r was noted in 80.8% of those receiving MMF and 57.1% of those receiving AZA (risk difference [RD], 23.7%; 95% CI, 11% to 35%; P = 0.0003). There was a 3.2-point mean difference in the MG-QOL15r score reduction from start of treatment favoring MMF (95% CI, −7.7 to 1.18; P = 0.15), representing a 35% greater change favoring MMF (95% CI −82.5%-11%; P = 0.13). Approximately 48% of MMF treated patients vs. 28% of AZA treated patients achieved PIS-MM or better with grade 1 or less CTCAE medication side effects (mean difference 19.6% favoring MMF, 95% CI, 5% to 44%, p=0.12). The secondary outcomes were not significantly different between MMF and AZA. Ten of 78 (12.8%) patients required hospitalization for MG, 5 each in the AZA and MMF treated group (AZA vs. MMF 16% vs 10.6%). For MMF, the median time to achieve clinically meaningful improvement ranged from 9.5 months for MG-QOL15r to 3.68 months for MG-MMT, with 5.88 months for MG-ADL and 5.72 months for MGC. For AZA, median times were: MG-QOL15r - 10.25 months, MG-MMT - 3.45 months, MG-ADL - 5.72 months and MG-MMT - 3.45 months. The doses used in patients who did and did not achieve meaningful reduction in MGC were largely similar for both drugs. The dose range of MMF was similar to usually recommended doses, 1-3 gm per day in both groups. However, the doses of AZA were below the usually recommended doses of 1-3 mg/kg in both groups. AEs to AZA were noted in 11/34 (32%) patients. Hepatotoxicity, manifested by elevated serum levels of liver enzymes, was the most common AZA AE, seen in five patients, followed by influenza-like hypersensitivity reaction in three. MMF AEs were reported in 9/48 (19%) patients. The most common MMF AE was gastrointestinal disturbance (7/9). The median duration to maximal effect on MG-QOL15r was 9.5 months for MMF and for AZA, 10.25 months. Thirty-nine of 78 (50%) received adequate dosage and duration, 32 (41%) MMF, and 7 (8%) AZA. No substantial differences were noted between adequate dose and duration vs. lesser dose/duration. CONCLUSIONS: From 57% to 89% of patients treated with either MMF or AZA achieved meaningful reduction in one or more of MG-QOL15r, MGC, MG-ADL or MG-MMT. The proportion of patients on MMF who obtained a clinically meaningful reduction in MG-QOL15r was significantly greater than those on AZA, but the co-primary and secondary outcomes were not significantly different between drugs, despite a larger effect size with MMF in many measures. Statistical imprecision, likely due to the small numbers, precludes the identification of significant differences between the drugs. AEs were potentially more serious in patients who received AZA. LIMITATIONS: The generalizability of these results may be limited because this study was performed in tertiary academic centers. The number of patients on AZA and MMF was lower than planned because the total enrollment was low and the number of patients with ocular MG higher than anticipated; ocular MG is not usually treated with AZA or MMF.. The COVID-19 pandemic affected follow-up, with missed visits and missing data for CROs, which could not be performed during remote visits.  aInstitution Receiving Award: Duke University Medical Center PCORI ID: CER-1609-35953 ClinicalTrials.gov ID: NCT03490539  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed Jan. 28, 2026).12aMyasthenia Gravisxdrug therapy22aTreatment Outcome2 aPatient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aFinal research report (Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute (U.S.))40uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK615403/  aAUTH01813nam a2200349 i 9919052734406676DNLM20260205090547.0m     o  d f      cr|cn#||||||||250709s2025    gaua    ob   f000 0 eng    a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK615457  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQV 633.5.H31 aWilliams, Malcolm Dexter,d1954-eauthor.10aToxicological profile for 1,2-Dichloroethene /cU.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Agency for Toxic Substances and Disease Registry. 1aAtlanta, GA :bAgency for Toxic Substances and Disease Registry,cApril 2025.  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (various paging)) :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  a"Contributors & reviewers: Malcolm Williams, D.V.M., Ph.D. (Lead) [and 8 others]"--Page v.  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 aThe ATSDR toxicological profile succinctly characterizes the toxicology and adverse health effects information for the toxic substance described therein. Each peer-reviewed profile identifies and reviews the key literature that describes a substance's toxicological properties. Profiles are updated on a periodic basis and released for a public comment period. For more information visit ATSDR.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed Jan. 28, 2026).12aDichloroethylenesxtoxicity22aToxicokinetics1 aUnited States.bAgency for Toxic Substances and Disease Registry,eissuing body.40uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK615457/  aAUTH03271cgm a2200445 i 101696079DNLM20260227155512.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u161102s2016    mdu054|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19942  a(OCoLC)971543803  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWK 40000aRole of precision therapy and immune checkpoint inhibitors in the management of thymic epithelial tumors /cArun Rajan. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (54 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCCR grand rounds  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded October 21, 2016.3 a(CIT): NCI's Center for Cancer Research (CCR) Grand Rounds   Dr. Rajan is a staff clinician in the Thoracic and Gastrointestinal Oncology Branch (TGOB), NCI. Dr. Rajan graduated from the University of Delhi, India prior to completing residencies in Medical Oncology and Hematology in 2008 from SUNY Upstate Medical University, Syracuse, New York. He then joined the NCI and has been a Staff Clinician in the TGOB (previously the Medical Oncology Branch) since 2009. Dr. Rajan is board certified in Medical Oncology and Hematology by the American Board of Internal Medicine.   Dr. Rajan"s research focuses on development of novel therapies for patients with thymic epithelial tumors. He has served as the principal investigator and lead associate investigator of a number of clinical trials for patients with thymic cancers that have evaluated the role of drugs such as sunitinib, cixutumumab, belinostat, milciclib, and avelumab.   As part of his research efforts, Dr. Rajan collaborates extensively with the International Thymic Malignancies Interest Group (ITMIG). He is a member of the ITMIG Basic Science and Thymic Carcinoma working groups and the ITMIG virtual tumor board which discusses complex cases of thymic epithelial tumors submitted by clinicians from across the globe. Dr. Rajan has participated in the development of ITMIG guidelines for the radiographic assessment of treatment response of thymic epithelial tumors. He received the Barbara Neibauer Award for the Best Paper on Thymic Carcinoma for the year 2016 at the recently concluded 7th ITMIG Annual Meeting in San Francisco, Ca. Dr. Rajan is also a member of the Thymoma Analysis Working Group of The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) project on Thymic Malignancies.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 30, 2016).12aThymomaxtherapy22aImmunotherapyxmethods22aPrecision Medicinexmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aRajan, Arun,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19942  aAUTH01213cam a2200325 i 60440860RDNLM20260206221613.0cr||||||||||||820514s1903    wiu           000 0 eng    a   03011652   a(OCoLC)14807135  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aHQbB154m 19031 aBailey, J. R.q(James Ross),d1856-1917,eauthor.10aMore light on a dark subject :ba series of lessons in higher physiology for the benefit of parents and posterity /cby Dr. J.R. Bailey. 1aAshland, Wisconsin :bChequamegon press,c1903.  a139 pages ;c21 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc202506132pda5DNLM  aConservedc202506132pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aSex Education12aSexual Health4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/60440860R  aAUTH02147cgm a2200409 a 101581474DNLM20260227154225.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120331s2012    mdu049|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17148  a(OCoLC)789838093  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWT 1041 aKirkland, Jamesq(James L.)10aTargeting aging to delay multiple chronic diseases :ba new frontier /cJames Kirkland ; Geroscience Interest Group.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (49 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded Mar. 8, 2012.3 a(CIT): The Geroscience Interest Group (GSIG) cordially invites you to the seminar listed above. Dr. Kirkland is Professor of Medicine and Physiology and Director of the Robert and Arlene Kogod Center on Aging at the Mayo Clinic in Rochester, Minnesota. His research interests include the effect of aging on preadipocyte differentiation and function, and the role of fat tissue on age-related dysfunction, lifespan and healthspan. His recent paper in Nature, describing amelioration of several age-related phenotypes in mice after removal of senescent cells, gained considerable media attention and was selected by Science among the top 10 discoveries of 2011. In addition to this work, Dr. Kirkland will talk more extensively on the role of aging biology as the major risk factor for most chronic diseases.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aAgingxpathology22aCellular Senescence22aChronic Diseasexprevention & control 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bGeroscience Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17148  aAUTH02903cgm a2200409 i 101684418DNLM20260227155419.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160601s2016    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19679  a(OCoLC)953601624  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 14100aPI3K in immunity, infection and cancer /cKlaus Okkenhaug. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded May 11, 2016.3 a(CIT):  Klaus Okkenhaug is a leader in the rapidly growing field of PI3 Kinases and their many roles in immune cell biology. Klaus obtained his Ph.D. in Immunology from the University of Toronto, where he studied CD28 signalling with Rob Rottapel. In 1999, he joined Bart Vanhaesebroeck's group at the Ludwig Institute for Cancer Research, UK, as a Postdoctoral Fellow, generating  and studying some of the seminal mouse strains. Klaus became a group leader at the Laboratory of Lymphocyte Signalling and Development at the Babraham Institute, Cambridge, UK in 2003. Dr. Okkenhaug's work focuses on how a group of enzymes called phosphoinositide 3-kinases (PI3Ks) are used by cells of the immune system to instruct and coordinate defences against pathogens. Cells of the immune system can express up to eight different forms of PI3K, which act as second messenger signalling molecules within cells that control diverse of cellular functions and genetic programms. The Okkenhaug group uses genetic and pharmacological approaches to address the functions of individual PI3K isoforms in lymphocytes with particular focus on their roles in B cells and T cells and the effects of inhibiting or enhancing the activity of individual forms of PI3K has on immunity to infections and immune responses to tumors. His lecture will focus on the expanding roles for PI3 kinases in primary immunodeficiencies and immune tolerance to cancer. We look forward to hearing his newest and exciting results.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 1, 2016).12aPhosphatidylinositol 3-Kinasesximmunology22aCommunicable Diseasesximmunology22aNeoplasmsximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aOkkenhaug, Klaus,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group,eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19679  aAUTH01773cas a22005291  2984841RDNLM20260224111540.0cr|cn#||||||||850128c19289999ja mr         0    0jpn    a0021-5287l0021-5287  aNGKZA6  a(DNLM)N25080000(s)z(DNLM)J06340000(s)  a(OCoLC)01641116  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 ajpnbeng  9Japan00aW1bNI903R10aNihon Hinyokika Gakkai Zasshi2dnlm00aNihon Hinyōkika Gakkai zasshi.bThe japanese journal of urology.31aJapanese journal of urologyf1930-3 aNihon Hinyōkika Gakkai zasshi  aTokyo :bJapanese Urological Association  av.bill., ports.  aMonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 amaki 17-    ichigatsu 1928-  aIssues for 1928-29 have title in Japanese only.  aPrint issues ceased with v. 107, no. 4 (Oct. 2016).1 aMEDLINEbv56n3,Mar. 1965-1 aPubMedbv56n3,Mar. 1965-0 aOLDMEDLINE1 aIndex medicus  aPublication suspended Nov. 1944-Apr. 1946.  aSupplements accompany some numbers.  aAlso issued online.  aIssues for 1930-44 have table of contents and abstracts in English, German, or French; 1946-    English.  amaki 1-20, 1912-31.  1 v. 2aUrology 2aPeriodical2 aNihon Hinyōkika Gakkai.0 x1884-711000aNippon Hinyōkibyō Gakkai.tZasshi4 uhttps://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/browse/jpnjurol  aAUTH01294cas a2200433 a 101088690DNLM20260227090924.0