sociation for Psychoanalytic Psychotherapy in the National Health Service.0 x1474-97344 uhttps://www.tandfonline.com/loi/rpps20  aAUTH01244cam a2200349 i 60521710RDNLM20260206221838.0cr||||||||||||820514s1903    ilu           000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)14807045  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aHQbS864k 19031 aStockham, Alice B.q(Alice Bunker),d1833-1912,eauthor.10aKarezza : ethics of marriage /cby Alice B. Stockham.1 aEthics of marriage  aNew and revised edition. 1aChicago :bStockham Publishing Company,c[1903]  avii, 141 pages ;c19 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20250613lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aSexual Behavior22aMarriage22aNatural Family Planning Methods 2aPopular Work4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/60521710R  aAUTH01354cam a2200385 i 60630280RDNLM20260206222050.0cr||||||||||||820514s1911    nyu      b    001 0 eng    a(OCoLC)505247  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aHVbS657s 19111 aSmith, Samuel George,d1852-1915,eauthor.10aSocial pathology /cby Samuel George Smith. 1aNew York :bthe Macmillan Company,c1911.  aviii, 380 pages ;c20 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aPublisher's advertisements (10 pages) bound at end.  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 355-360) and index.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc202506102pda5DNLM  aConservedc202506132pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aSociology22aCrime22aPoverty22aCharities22aIntellectual Disability2 aMacmillan Company,epublisher.4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/60630280R  aAUTH02997cgmaa2200481 a 101267528DNLM20260227145212.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vf|cjahzuvd|cvaizuhd|bfa012baap060209s1998    mdu073 ||||||f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10189  a(OCoLC)64588897  aDNLMcDNLMeamimdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWW 105bDVD no.2 19981 aUngerleider, Leslie G.10aNeural mechanisms of human cognition :binsights from brain imaging studies /cLeslie G. Ungerleider.  aUnited States.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c1998]  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (73 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3master.  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (73 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videodisc of 1 (DVD) (73 min.) :bsd., col. ;c4 3/4 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aJoseph Leiter lecture  aRecorded Feb. 25, 1998.  aDr. Harold Varmus, NIH Director, introduces Leslie G. Ungerlieden, NIMH, as the speaker for the annual Joseph Leiter Lecture.  Dr. Ungerleiden worked with Mort Mishkin before becoming Chief of the Laboratory of Brain and Cognition.  Dr. Ungerlieden began measuring the hemodynamic changes in monkeys to determine how visual perception and visual memory occurred.  The monkey has 30 functional visual areas.  The areas of study were narrowed to object discrimination, the what?, in the ventral cortex and landmark discrimination, the where?, spacial recognition in the dorsal cortex.  By using a PET scan, Dr. Ungerleiden and her colleagues applied the monkey studies to humans.  They used matching stimuli and control stimuli to locate changes in the frontal lobe.  They also explored the role of the fusiform gyrus.  Of particular use were patients with prospagnosia, a failure to recongize faces.  In addition, Dr. Ungereiden studied working memory, automatic timing versus experience and explicit memory retrieval.  Dr. Ungerlieden took questions at the end of her lecture.  The lecture was illustrated.  aIssued also online.  aVideocassette (Betacam SP)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2011.e1 videocassette (73 min.) : sd., col. ; 1/2 in.  aVideodisc (DVD)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2011.e1 videodisc (73 min.) : sd., col. ; 4 3/4 in.  aThis item is a government publication and is in the public domain.5DNLM12aVisual Perceptionxphysiology22aBrain22aMemoryxphysiology2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)4 uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10189  aAUTH01575cam a2200373 i 31330410RDNLM20260206221331.0cr||||||||||||hd|bfa012baap810815s1914    nyuab    b    000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)4620294  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aWA 785bN532m 19142 aNew York (N.Y.).bMetropolitan Sewerage Commission,eauthor :10aMain drainage and sewage disposal works proposed for New York City :breports of experts and data relating to the harbor /creport of the Metropolitan Sewerage Commission.1 aReport, Metropolitan Sewerage Commission of New York, 1916 1aNew York :bWynkoop Hallenbeck Crawford Co.,cApril 30, 1914.  a762 pages :billustrations, maps ;c31 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.  aMicrofilm.b[Bethesda, Md.] :cNational Library of Medicine,d1999.e1 microfilm reel : negative ; 35 mm.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc202505062pda5DNLM  aConservedc202506032pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aDrainage, Sanitary12aSewage 2aNew York City4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/31330410R  aAUTH02878cgm a2200457 i 9918418881406676DNLM20260227162401.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220616s2022    mdu162|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32392  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.51 aAnnual Vivian Pinn Symposiumn(6th :d2022 :cOnline)jauthor.14aThe impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on the careers of women scientists /cNational Institutes of Health Office of Research on Women's Health.1 aNIH Vivian W. Pinn Symposium 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 42 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from PowerPoint  aRecorded May 12, 2022.3 a(CIT): The National Institutes of Health (NIH) Office of Research on Women"s Health (ORWH) is thrilled to host the 6th Annual Vivian W. Pinn Symposium. Convened by ORWH each year during National Women"s Health Week, this event honors the first full-time Director of the office, Dr. Vivian W. Pinn.  In her role as the director, one of her objectives was to increase the number of women in leadership roles in research and academic institutions and identify barriers that lead to what we know as the "leadership gap". This year's event highlights the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on the careers of women scientists. Providing the keynote address is Dr. Reshma Jagsi, the Newman Family Professor and Deputy Chair in the Department of Radiation Oncology and Director of the Center for Bioethics and Social Sciences in Medicine at the University of Michigan. After her talk will be a discussion moderated by Dr. Joshua Gordon, the National Institute of Mental Health Director. The panel includes Dr. Marie Bernard, NIH"s Chief Officer for Scientific Workforce Diversity; Dr. Sonia Flores of the University of Colorado Anschutz Medical Campus; and Dr. Michael Lauer, NIH"s Deputy Director for Extramural Research.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 22, 2022).12aBiomedical Research22aWomen22aCareer Choice22aCOVID-1922aOrganizational Policy22aOrganizational Culture 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Research on Women's Health,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45120  aAUTH03075cgm a2200445 i 9918418883706676DNLM20260227162308.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220616s2022    mdu763|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32348  a(DNLM)CIT:32353  a(OCoLC)1368038095  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 145.52 aPrecision Probiotic Therapies--Challenges and Opportunities (Workshop)d(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aPrecision probiotic therapies--challenges and opportunities /cNCCIH, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (12 hr., 43 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 26 & 27, 2022.3 a(CIT): Probiotic interventions may potentially offer beneficial effects, such as alleviation of gastrointestinal symptoms; "strengthening" of the immune system; protection against infectious diseases; prevention of metabolic disorders; improved mental health; promotion of early development; and general well-being. However, one major challenge in assessing the effects of probiotic interventions has been the inconsistent results of clinical trials. We now have a better understanding of some of the biological and behavioral factors that might contribute to inconsistent outcomes from probiotic interventions (e.g., differences in gut microbiota, diet, age, and lifestyle). Innovative research approaches are needed to understand the contributions and interactions of these factors and to advance methods for their assessment. There is growing interest in the development of individualized microbial therapeutics for targeted interventions in the probiotics literature, but wide gaps in knowledge still need to be addressed.  The goals of this workshop are to: 1) identify gaps in our current understanding of the biology of the gut microbiota and of probiotics and 2) identify research questions and methodological challenges posed by those gaps. Knowledge developed through these activities will provide a critical foundation for future research efforts to develop "precision probiotic therapies."  View the full agenda https://go.usa.gov/xtzFT.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed January 12, 2023).12aProbioticsxtherapeutic use22aMicrobiota22aClinical Trials as Topic22aPrecision Medicine 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast22aNational Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4380540uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=43807  aAUTH01313cas a2200445 a 101650615DNLM20260227090921.0cr||||||||||||050712c20069999paufr p       0   a0eng    a  20052149530 a1556-8547l1556-854721  a(OCoLC)60848832  aNSDbengcNSDdNSDdDLCdOCLCQdCUSdOCLCQdHLSdOCLCFdDNLM  ansdpapcc  9United States00aGN474b.M3400aW1bMA32214a291.121410aMagic Ritual Witch2dnlm 0aMagic, ritual, and witchcraft00aMagic, ritual, and witchcraft.  aPhiladelphia, PA :bUniversity of Pennsylvania Press,cc2006-  av. ;c23 cm.  aSemiannual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (summer 2006)-0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.  aTitle from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 11, no. 1 (summer 2016).12aMagic22aCeremonial Behavior22aWitchcraft 2aPeriodical0 x1940-51114 uhttp://muse.jhu.edu/journal/387  aAUTH01713cas a2200481 i 101752190DNLM20260227090916.0m     o  d        cr#|||||||||||190807c20199999ncu|| poo     0   a0eng    a  2020243041  a11385892260 a2631-6129l2631-6110  a(OCoLC)1138748053  z(OCoLC)1138589226  aOG$bengerdad20260227cOG$dUKMGBdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW110aJ Breast Imaging2dnlm 0aJournal of breast imagingb(Online)00aJournal of breast imaging.1 aJBI 1a[Cary, NC] :bOxford University Press,c[2019]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1, issue 1 (March 2019).1 aMEDLINEbv5n1, Jan.-Feb. 2023-0 aPubMedbv5n1, Jan.-Feb. 2023- ; selected citations only before this date  aAlso issued in print.  aOfficial journal of the Society of Breast Imaging.  aDescription based on: Volume 1, issue 1 (March 2019); title from journal home page (publisher's website, viewed February 21, 2020).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 2, issue 1 (January/February 2020) (viewed February 21, 2020).12aBreast Diseasesxdiagnostic imaging22aEarly Detection of Cancer 2aPeriodical2 aSociety of Breast Imaging,esponsoring body.0 x2631-61104 uhttps://academic.oup.com/jbi/pages/About  aAUTH02572cgm a2200481 i 9918916890806676DNLM20260227164832.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u241002s2024    mdu779|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35179  a(DNLM)CIT:35182  a(OCoLC)1492358561  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWI 1402 aImpact of Diet on Mucosal Immunity and Immune-Mediated Digestive Diseases (Workshop)d(2024 :cRockville, Md.)jauthor.10aImpact of diet on mucosal immunity and immune-mediated digestive diseases /cRyan Ranallo. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2024]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (12 hr., 59 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded August 21 & 22, 2024.3 a(CIT): To enhance our understanding of diet-host-microbiome interrelationships and their impact on mucosal immunity and health outcomes, the Office of Nutrition Research (ONR) and NIAID, in collaboration with many other Institutes and Offices at NIH, have organized a  workshop focused on understanding the critical dietary factors that regulate the development and function of the gastrointestinal immune system. Presentations at this workshop will explore the impact of dietary exposures on the functional output of the gut microbiome, and the impact of diet on mechanisms of immune-mediated digestive diseases, such as inflammatory bowel disease, cancer, and food allergies.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed January 16, 2025).12aDigestive System Diseasesximmunology12aDigestive System Diseasesxdiet therapy22aImmunity, Mucosalxphysiology22aHost Microbial Interactionsximmunology22aDiet 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aRanallo, Ryan T.,eorganizer,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Nutrition Research,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=5496740uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=54969  aAUTH01529cas a2200457 i 101664800DNLM20260227090912.0m     o  d        cr |||||||||||150123c20159999enk|| poo     0    0eng    a  2015243313  a905959974a911393791  a2056-306Xl2056-306X  a(OCoLC)900651098  z(OCoLC)905959974z(OCoLC)911393791  aOH1bengerdad20260227cOH1dCUSdOCLCOdDNLM  apcc  9England00aW110aZoological Lett2dnlm0 aZoological lett. 0aZoological letters00aZoological letters. 1aLondon :bBioMed Central,c[2015]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with (2015) 1:1.0 aPubMedbv1, 2015-0 aPMC  aDescription based on: (2015) 1:1 ; title from PDF caption title (BioMed Central, viewed Aug. 5, 2015).  aLatest issue consulted: (2015) 1:19 (viewed Aug. 5, 2015).12aAnimal Population Groups 2aPeriodica