tle screen.  aRecorded Feb. 18, 2014.3 a(CIT): DNA Repair Interest Group videoconferernce.  aDescription based on version viewed March 27, 2014.12aDNA Ligasesxphysiology12aDNA Repair22aSmall Molecule Libraries 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bDNA Repair Interest Group,esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18287  aAUTH02224cgm a2200469 a 101591627DNLM20260227154828.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120901s2012    mdu098|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17448  a(OCoLC)809886752  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.55.H91 aHardy, Ann M.10aNew policies and procedures related to newly proposed human subjects research in active awards /cAnn Hardy.1 iTitle on PowerPoint:aNew NIH policies for prior approval of newly proposed human subjects research in active awards  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 38 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRestricted to HHS and NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded July 31, 2012.3 a(CIT): Overview: This training will discuss the implementation of new policy guidance about the need for prior NIH approval for certain changes in the approved involvement of human subjects in on-going awards (change in scope) and for awards that were submitted with the intent to conduct human subjects research but which were not able to provide definitive plans in the application (delayed onset). The new policies are intended to improve the ability of IC extramural staff to appropriately monitor awards that involve human subjects and to make the approach to these more consistent across NIH.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from caption.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aResearch Subjects22aGovernment Regulation22aOrganizational Policy22aResearch Support as Topicxor