cKeith Horvath. Psoriasis inflammation and its association with cardiometabolic disease / Nehal Mehta.1 aPsoriasis inflammation and its association with cardiometabolic disease 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (52 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCC grand rounds  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Feb. 19, 2014.3 a(CIT): CC Grand Rounds: (1) The Use of Mesenchymal Stem Cells to Improve LV Function as an Adjunct to Surgical Revascularization (2) Psoriasis Inflammation and its Association with Cardiometabolic Disease.  aDescription based on version viewed March 27, 2014.12aVentricular Dysfunction, Leftxtherapy22aMesenchymal Stem Cell Transplantation22aMetabolic Syndromexcomplications22aPsoriasisxetiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast12aHorvath, Keith A.tUse of mesenchymal stem cells to improve LV function as an adjunct to surgical revascularization.12aMehta, Nehal N.tPsoriasis inflammation and its association with cardiometabolic disease.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18291  aAUTH01986cgm a22004214a 101289749DNLM20260227151438.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2006    mdu058|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13171  a(OCoLC)84646400  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 1031 aAkil, H.q(Huda)10aMolecules and circuits of moods and emotions /cHuda Akil.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 18, 2006).  aStreaming video (58 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 17, 2006.3 a(CIT): Dr. Akil's laboratory is focused on understanding the neurobiology of emotions, including pain, anxiety, depression and substance abuse. Early on, her research focused on the role of the endorphins and their receptors in pain and stress responsiveness. Her group provided the first physiological evidence for a role of endogenous opioids in the brain; and showed that endorphins are activated by stress and cause pain inhibition, a phenomenon termed stress-induced analgesia. They defined how the postranslational processing of opioid precursors is modulated by stress, and demonstrated the coordinate actions of the neuropeptide products on behavior.   NIH neuroscience seminar series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aEmotionsxphysiology22aFibroblast Growth Factorsxphysiology22aHippocampusxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13171  aAUTH01244cas a2200397 i 101606715DNLM20260227090917.0cr||||||||||||130423c20139999enk|| p       |    0eng    a  2013243471  a2049-9361l2049-9361  a(OCoLC)856644065  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9England00aW1bTH1293S10aTher Adv Infect Dis2dnlm00aTherapeutic advances in infectious disease. 1aLondon :bSAGE Publications,c[2013]-  aBimonthly  atext2rdacontent  aunmediated2rdamedia  avolume2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1, number 1 (February 2013).0 aPubMedbv1, 2013-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: Vol. 1, number 1 (February 2013); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 1, number 1 (February 2013).12aCommunicable Diseasesxdrug therapy 2aPeriodical0 x2049-937X4 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=1016067154 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/tai  aAUTH01525cgm a2200445 i 101631791DNLM20260227154901.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140501s2014    mdu056       fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18392  a(OCoLC)883365845  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00