227144539.0vf|cbahoucr|cna||||||||vf|cjahou041116s2004    mdu060 ||    f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:3429  a(OCoLC)57422831  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aQH 603.I5400aIon channels :blife's electronic hardware /c[Office of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch].  aUnited States.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (60 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (60 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (60 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aThe NIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture. The DeWitt Stetten, Jr. lecture  aSpeaker, Roderick MacKinnon.  aOriginally broadcast Oct. 27, 2004.3 a(Producer) Ion channels are responsible for generating electrical impulses and mediating numerous cellular processes. To accomplish their tasks in biology, ion channels must exhibit two basic properties, selectivity and gating. Recent developments on the molecular principles of selectivity and gating in potassium channels will be presented.  aAlso available online.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aPotassium Channelsxphysiology22aIon Channel Gating1 aMacKinnon, Roderick,d1956-2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pDeWitt Stetten, Jr. lecture4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3429  aAUTH03151cgm a2200433 i 101716403DNLM20260227155751.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u171107s2017    mdu224|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:23531  a(OCoLC)1029856980  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 5412 aHIV DRP Conference on Innate immunity : Sensing, Signaling, and Selectiond(2017 :cFrederick, Md.)jauthor.10aInnate immunity :bsensing, signaling, and selection : NCI Frederick Building 549 Conference Center Auditorium /cHIV Dynamics and Replication Program, NCI, NIH ; speakers, Paul Bieniasz [and five others]. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2017]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (3 hr., 44 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 18, 2017.3 a(CIT): The HIV Dynamics and Replication Program is hosting this half-day conference to showcase the latest findings in the broad field of innate immunity.  The presentations will highlight recent advances in understanding cell-intrinsic immune strategies by which hosts counteract viral and bacterial infections and the mechanisms by which pathogens circumvent or overcome these immune barriers.  Specific areas of focus will include immune sensing, mutational escape, paleovirology, interferon-induced effector specificity, inflammasomes, and evolutionary conflicts between pathogens and host restriction factors.  An in-depth discussion of recent progress in our understanding host-pathogen interactions within infected cells should lead to new insights into the roles that cellular factors play in preventing infections in uninfected cells, which will aid in the international public health efforts. Invited speakers in the outstanding program include: Paul Bieniasz, Howard Hughes Medical Institute and Laboratory of Retrovirology, The Rockefeller University; Alex Compton, HIV Dynamics and Replication Program, National Cancer Institute; Carolyn Coyne, University of Pittsburgh School of Medicine; David Knipe, Harvard Medical School; Harmit Malik, Howard Hughes Medical Institute and Division of Basic Sciences, Fred Hutchinson Cancer Research Center; and Nan Yan, University of Texas Southwestern Medical Center.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 20, 2017)12aImmunity, Innate22aBacterial Infectionsximmunology22aHost-Pathogen Interactionsximmunology22aVirus Diseasesximmunology 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aBieniasz, Paul D.,espeaker.2 aHIV Dynamics and Replication Program (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23531  aAUTH02096cgm a22004214a 101319951DNLM20260227152148.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071101s2007    mdu079|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14100  a(OCoLC)182613530  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 5841 aRimoin, Anne.10aStudying human monkeypox in the Democratic Republic of the Congo /cAnne Rimoin ; Emergency Preparedness and Biodefense Interest Group.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Nov. 19, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 19 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 23, 2007.3 a(CIT): Since the eradication of smallpox in 1977, monkeypox has remained the only severe poxviral infection of humans.  The causative agent is maintained in animals in central Africa, and is transferred to local residents through contact.  Human monkeypox closely resembles smallpox, but is less contagious and has a lower case fatality rate.  Research on the pathogenesis, prevention and treatment of the disease can both benefit residents of endemic areas and improve US defenses against a deliberate re-introduction of smallpox through bioterrorism.  Dr. Rimoin will provide an overview of human monkeypox and present her research on the epidemiology of the disease in the DRC.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMpox, Monkeypoxxepidemiology.22aDisease Outbreaks 2aCongoxepidemiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bEmergency Preparedness and Biodefense Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14100  aAUTH01394cgm a22004334a 101319941DNLM20260227152123.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071101s2007    mdu071|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14078  a(OCoLC)182613528  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 26.51 aKirschner, Marc.10aSystems questions in cell biology /cMarc W. Kirschner.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Nov. 23, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 11 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 17, 2007.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aSystems Biology22aCell Physiological Phenomena22aModels, Biological 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14078  aAUTH01876cas a2200529 a 8219208DNLM20260227091000.0cr|cn ||||||||821202c19829999nyuqr p       0    0eng    a   82644596 zsn 81001085  a0276-3869l0276-3869  aMRSQDK  a(DNLM)M17070000(s)z(DNLM)SR0054421(s)  a(OCoLC)07374943  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aeng  9United States00aZ 699.5bM39 M48910aMed Ref Serv Q2dnlm 0aMedical reference services quarterly00aMedical reference services quarterly.  a[New York, N.Y.] :bHaworth Press,c[c1982-2 aLondon :bInforma Healthcare3 aNew York :bRoutledge  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (spring 1982)-  aTitle from cover.  aImprint varies.1 aMEDLINEbv3n1, spring 1984-0 aPubMedbv3n1, spring 1984-2 aHospital literature indexx0018-5736b1984-19942 aHospital and health administration indexx1077-1719b1995-19998 aQuarterly.  