g video (1 hr., 5 min., 45 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 13, 2008.3 a(CIT): David's lab is interested in better understanding the immuno-regulatory mechanisms that govern the initiation, regulation, and development of immune effector responses following infection with pathogens of the gastrointestinal tract. Trichuris muris is one of the model systems his lab uses routinely to investigate the cellular and molecular mechanisms that govern immunity and inflammation in the gut. T. muris is a natural gastrointestinal parasite of mice and provides an immunologically well-defined model of human and veterinary infections.  Recent work from his lab has focused on four key research questions: 1) How do intestinal epithelial cells (iEC) and intestinal dendritic cells (iDC) regulate innate responses to Trichuris and other enteric pathogens?; 2) What unique regulatory pathways control CD4+ T helper cell differentiation and the development of T cell memory following infection in the gastrointestinal tract?; 3) What are the immune effector mechanisms elicited by CD4+ Th2 cells that mediate host protective immunity in the gut?; and 4) How have helminth parasites evolved to subvert the mammalian immune defenses?  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aIntestinal Mucosaximmunology22aEpithelial Cellsximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14296  aAUTH01219cas a2200397 a 101249724DNLM20260227090909.0cr|cn ||||||||050607c20059999enkfr p       0    0eng    a  2006243439  a1745-4832l1745-4832  a(OCoLC)62771161  aMRWcMRWdMRWdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9England00aW1bIN456U00aInternational journal of art therapy :bInscape.30aInscape  aAbingdon, Oxforshire, UK :bRoutledge Taylor & Francis Group,cc2005-  av. :bill. ;c30 cm.  aSemiannual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 