trait of the author, owner .  aNLM copy, contains two "Free Carriage Tickets" to the Radium Cancer Sanitarium from anywhere in Indianapolis, Indiana.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202102182pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202102185DNLM22aRadium Cancer Sanitarium.12aRadiumxtherapeutic use22aNeoplasmsxdrug therapy22aRadiumxadministration & dosage22aHospitals, Private22aTreatment Outcome 2aIndiana 2aCase Reports2 aRadium Cancer Sanitarium,epublisher.  aAUTH02364cas a2200613 a 9501297DNLM20260227091027.0cr||||||||||||950105c19889999enkbr p       0    0eng    asn 95038084   a1359-2998l1359-2998y0003-9888  a(DNLM)SR0083103(s)  a(OCoLC)31121552  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcdansdp  9England00aW1bAR453M10aArch Dis Child Fetal Neonatal Ed2dnlm 0aArchives of disease in childhood. Fetal and neonatal edition00aArchives of disease in childhood.pFetal and neonatal edition.30aFetal and neonatal edition  aLondon :bBritish Medical Association,  av. :bill.  aBimonthly,b1993-  aQuarterlyb1988-1992  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan in 1988?  aPublished by: BMJ Pub. Group, 1993?- .  aOnline version called ADC. F&N or ADC online. F&N.  aDescription based on: Vol. 63, no. 7 (July 1988); title from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv63n7,Jul. 1988-0 aPubMedbv63n7,Jul. 1988-0 aPMC Inactive1 aAbridged index medicus1 aIndex medicus  aAlso available online.  aJournal of the British Paediatric Association.  aIssues for July 1988?-1993 were issued as supplements to some issues of Archives of disease in childhood, repeating its numbering and paged continuously with that publication.  Beginning with v. 70 in 1994, issue number and pagination are independent. 2aFetal Diseases 2aInfant, Newborn, Diseases 2aPeriodical2 aBritish Paediatric Association.02aADC. F&N.02aADC online. F&N.02aADC online. Fetal & neonatal.0 x1468-205211tArchives of disease in childhoodx0003-9888w(DLC)sg 38000035w(OCoLC)15138681 tArchives of disease in childhood. Education and practice editionx1743-0585w(DLC)   2004243482w(OCoLC)556117184 uhttp://fn.bmj.com/4 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=9501297  aAUTH03285cgm a22004574a 101468739DNLM20260227152415.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080307s2008    mdu065|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14317  a(OCoLC)219261848  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 58.51 aElnitski, Laura.10aRegulatory and epigenetic landscapes of mammalian genomes /cLaura Elnitski.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCurrent topics in genome analysis  aTitle from title screen (viewed Mar. 29, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 5 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 26, 2008.3 a(CIT): The scientific community celebrated the achievement of the Human Genome Project's major goal in April of 2003: completion of a high-accuracy sequence of the human genome. The significance of this milestone cannot be underestimated, as it firmly marks the entrance of modern biology into the genomic era (and not the post-genomic era, as many have stated). The intelligent use of sequence data from human and other organisms, along with technological innovations fostered by the Human Genome Project, will lead to significant advances in our understanding of diseases that have a genetic basis and, more importantly, in how health care is delivered in the future.  Given the rapid advances in genomics and bioinformatics that have taken place in the past few years, an intensive review of the major areas of ongoing genome research would be of great value to our fellow National Institutes of Health investigators. To that end, the National Human Genome Research Institute is pleased to once again sponsor the Current Topics in Genome Analysis lecture series.  The series consists of 15 lectures on successive Tuesdays, with a mixture of local and outside speakers covering the major areas of genomics. In this eighth edition of the series, rather than splitting the lectures into "laboratory-based" and "computationally-based" blocks, we have intermingled the lectures by general subject area. We hope that this approach conveys the idea that both laboratory- and computationally-based approaches are necessary in order to do cutting-edge biological research in the future.  The lectures are geared at the level of first year graduate students, are practical in nature, and are intended for a diverse audience. Handouts will be provided for each lecture, and time will be available at the end of each lecture for questions and discussion.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aGenomicsxmethods22aEpigenesis, Genetic22aGene Components22aRegulatory Sequences, Nucleic Acid 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aCurrent topics in genome analysis40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14317  aAUTH01442cas a2200493 a 9707113DNLM20260227091036.0cr|cn#||||||||970724c19969999gw qr p       0    0eng    asn 97039302   a1124-4909l1124-4909  a(DNLM)SR0090222(s)  a(OCoLC)37413909  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9Germany00aW1bEA869H10aEat Weight Disord2dnlm00aEating and weight disorders :bEWD.30aEWD  aMilano, Italy :bEditrice Kurtis,cc1996-3132013- :aHeidelberg :bSpringer  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Dec. 1996)-  aTitle from cover.  aPrint issues ceased with v. 14, no. 2/3 (2009)1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, Dec. 1996-0 aPubMedbv1n1, Dec. 1996-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3Vol. 1, no. 1 (Dec. 1996)c19991221fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper12aBody Weight12aFeeding and Eating Disorders22aEmaciation22aObesity 2aPeriodical0 x1590-12624 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/40519  aAUTH02192cgm a2200445 i 9918299483406676DNLM20260227161717.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u211110s2021    mdu218|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32013  a(OCoLC)1291869484  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 2842 aResearch Needed to Advance the Impact of Medications for Opioid Use Disorder (MOUD): Focus on Benefit Design and Payor Reimbursement Practices Virtual Meetingd(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aAddressing barriers to broader prescribing of MOUD /cNIDA, NIH.1 aAddressing barriers to broader prescribing of medications used to treat opioid use disorder 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (3 hr., 38 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 25, 2021.3 a(CIT): The purpose of this meeting is to identify research and evidence gaps related to coverage, benefit design, reimbursement practices, and other payer practices related to reimbursement for medications used to treat opioid use disorder (MOUDs). The goal is to identify whether and how additional research and research resources might further inform the efficient, effective provision of MOUDs to patients with OUD.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 21, 2021).12aOpioid-Related Disordersxdrug therapy22aDrug Therapyxeconomics22aInsurance, Health, Reimbursement22aHealth Services Accessibilityxeconomics 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute on Drug Abuse,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44014  aAUTH02591cgm a22004454a 101468727DNLM20260227152351.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080307s2008    mdu113|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14294  a(OCoLC)314590811  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 7501 aWellems, Thomas E.10aDemystifying medicine.pMalaria :bbig killer and big advances /c[Thomas E. Wellems, John B. Robbins].30aMalaria  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 20, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 53 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 12, 2008.  aPart of the Demystifying Medicine series at the National Institutes of Health (NIH), this lecture by Thomas E. Wellems of the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (NIAID)  and John B. Robbins of the National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (NICHHD) focuses on: 1) public  health measures to combat malaria, and 2) efforts to develop an effective vaccine. Wellems's presentation, "Malaria: History, Burden, and State of the Science" begins with a patient case study and includes a look at the use of bed-netting to control infection, the appearance of chloroquine resistance and the mutations that lead to it, and the importance of alternatives to chloroquine in regions where malaria is still prevalent. Robbins notes that there is good potential for cheap, easy-to-manufacture vaccines that could apply at different stages of transmission and illness. He emphasizes that malaria in Africa is a major cause of stunted growth in children, and renders infected children susceptible to other maladies including pneumococcal meningitis. Thus, vaccine development is critical to prevent long-term health impacts in the population.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government.  aOpen-captioned.12aMalariaxdrug therapy22aMalaria Vaccines22aMalariaxprevention & control 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14294  aAUTH01473cas a2200445   8002834DNLM20260227090950.0cr|cn ||||||||800313c19799999ag  x         0||| 0spa    a0325-7541l0325-7541  aRAMID4  a(DNLM)R29730000(s)  a(OCoLC)06213126  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aspabeng  9Argentina00aW1bRE271P10aRev Argent Microbiol2dnlm 0aRevista Argentina de microbiología00aRevista Argentina de microbiología.  aBuenos Aires,bAsociación Argentina de Microbiología.  av.bill.  aFour no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 11-    enero/abr. 1979-1 aMEDLINEbv11n1, Jan./Apr. 1989-0 aPubMedbv11n1, Jan./Apr. 1989-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aArticles in Spanish; summaries in English and Spanish. 2aMicrobiology 2aPeriodical2 aAsociación Argentina de Microbiología.00tRevista de la Asociación Argentina de Microbiologíax0325-1713w(OCoLC)020160674 uhttp://www.scielo.org.ar/scielo.php/script%5Fsci%5Fserial/pid%5F0325-75414 uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/03257541  aAUTH04754cgm a2200457 i 101775907DNLM20260227160559.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2020    mdu064|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31441  a(OCoLC)1240391704  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 76 AA100aImplications of poetical science for advancing health equity through information visualization /cSuzanne Bakken. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 4 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNLM Ada Lovelace lecture  aRecorded December 7, 2020.3 a(CIT): NLM Ada Lovelace Lecture Series  Suzanne Bakken, PhD, RN, FAAN, FACMI, FIAHSI, Alumni Professor of Nursing and Professor of Biomedical Informatics at Columbia University will present the National Library of Medicine's Ada Lovelace Computational Health Lecture. The title of her talk will be "Implications of Poetical Science for Advancing Health Equity through Information Visualization."  Ada Bryon Lovelace developed the idea of Poetical Science, which emphasizes the importance of intuition and image in mathematics and science. Poetical Science comprises three key features: observation, interpretation, and integration. A brilliant mathematician, Lovelace is broadly recognized as the first computer programmer. A key example of Poetical Science is her observation of a punch card-driven loom in a weaving factory that subsequently led to the mathematics that operationalized Charles Babbage's computing machine. In this talk, Dr. Bakken will use the lens of Poetical Science to describe a program of research focused on advancing health equity of urban Latinos through use of information visualization. This will include the integration of perspectives from the fields of health equity, nursing science, health literacy, health communication, and information visualization.   Suzanne Bakken, PhD, RN, FAAN, FACMI, FIAHSI, is the Alumni Professor of Nursing and Professor of Biomedical Informatics at Columbia University. Following her doctorate in Nursing at the University of California, San Francisco, she completed a post-doctoral fellowship in Medical Informatics at Stanford University. Her program of research has focused on the intersection of informatics and health equity for more than 30 years and has been funded by AHRQ, NCI, NIMH, NINR, and NLM. Dr. Bakken"s program of research has resulted in more than 300 peer-reviewed papers. At Columbia Nursing, she leads the NINR-funded Precision in Symptom Self-Management (PriSSM) Center and Reducing Health Disparities Through Informatics (RHeaDI) Pre- and Post-doctoral Training Program.  She is a Fellow of the American Academy of Nursing, American College of Medical Informatics, International Academy of Health Sciences Informatics, and a member of the National Academy of Medicine. Dr. Bakken has received multiple awards for her research including the Pathfinder Award from the Friends of the National Institute of Nursing Research, the Nursing Informatics Award from the Friends of the National Library of Medicine, the Sigma Theta Tau International Nurse Researchers Hall of Fame, and the Virginia K. Saba Award from the American Medical Informatics Association. Most recently, she was the first nurse recipient of the Francois Gremy Award from the International Medical Informatics Association. Dr. Bakken currently serves as Editor-in-Chief of the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association and a member of the National Library of Medicine"s Board of Regents.   This lecture is sponsored by Dr. Milton Corn, MD, Acting Scientific Director, National Library of Medicine and David Landsman, PhD, Chief, Computational Biology Branch at the NLM"s National Center for Biotechnology Information.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 1, 2021).12aHealth Equity22aHealth Communication22aData Visualization22aSelf-Management 2aUnited States 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aBakken, Suzanne,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aAda Lovelace computational health lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40070  aAUTH00760cam a22002535i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003500073044001000108060003200118100004000150245006400190250001100254264006700265300001700332336002600349337002800375338002700403490002500430650001700455830002500472999000900497101613832DNLM20260209125139.0961217s1951    cc            000 0 chi    aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1788 no.91 aLiu, Yixiang,dactive 1926eauthor.10aYing yang liao fa /cLiu Yixiang zhu ; Cheng