2210930RDNLM20260225165650.0hd|bfa012baap820626s1902    pauaf         001 0 eng    a   02025033   a(OCoLC)9673230  aDNLMbengerdad20260225dDNLM  9United States00aQZbS347g 19021 aSchmaus, Hans,d1862-1905,eauthor.10aGrundriss der pathologischen Anatomie.lEnglish12aA text-book of pathology and pathological anatomy /cby Dr. Hans Schmaus ; translated from the sixth German edition by A.E. Thayer ; edited with additions by James Ewing 1aPhiladelphia :bLea Brothers & Co.,c[1902]  axii, 602 pages, 35 leaves of plats :billustrations ;c25 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes index.  aTranslation of Grundriss der pathologischen Anatomie.  aPublisher's advertisements bound in 16 pages at end.  aNLM microfilm copy shelved as: Film 5421 no. 35DNLM  aMicrofilm.b[Bethesda, Md.] :cNational Library of Medicine,d1993.e1 microfilm reel : negative ; 35 mm.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202011172pda5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20260129lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM12aPathologic Processes12aAnatomy, Regional1 aThayer, A. E.,etranslator.1 aEwing, James,d1866-1943,eeditor,ewriter of supplementary textual content.  aAUTH02985cgm a2200481 i 101775942DNLM20260227160719.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2021    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31512  a(OCoLC)1248767939  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 550.5.S400aSequence-structure-function modeling for the 3D genome /cKatherine S. Pollard.1 iTitle on PowerPoint screen:aSequence-structure-function modeling for DNA 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aAda Lovelace computational health lecture  aRecorded January 28, 2021.3 a(CIT): In this talk, Dr. Pollard will discuss how the human genome sequence folds in three dimensions (3D) into a rich variety of locus-specific contact patterns.  Despite growing appreciation for the importance of 3D genome folding in various aspects of cell biology, there is a lack of models for relating mutations in genome sequences to changes in genome structure and function. Towards that goal, Dr. Pollard and her colleagues discovered that the organization of gene regulatory domains within this structure and the specific sequences that sit at boundaries between domains are under strong negative selection in the human population and over primate evolution. Motivated by this signature of functional importance, Dr. Pollard's team developed a deep convolutional neural network, called Akita, that accurately predicts genome folding from DNA sequence alone. Representations learned by Akita underscore the importance of the structural protein CTCF but also reveal a complex grammar beyond CTCF binding sites that underlies genome folding. Akita has enabled rapid in silico predictions for effects of sequence mutagenesis on the 3D genome, including differences in genome folding across species and in disease cohorts, which are being validated with CRISPR-edited genomes.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 23, 2021).12aWhole Genome Sequencing22aDNA-Binding Proteins22aProtein Binding22aAmino Acid Sequence22aStructure-Activity Relationship22aModels, Molecular 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aPollard, Katherine S.,espeaker.2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aAda Lovelace computational health lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41362  aAUTH02496cas a2200649 a 9418515DNLM20260227091014.0cr|cn ||||||||940113c19939999gw  x p       0