Ebv8n1, Feb. 2005-1 aPubMedbv8n1, Feb. 2005-  aAlso issued online.12aTwinsxgenetics22aGenetics22aMultiple Birth Offspring 2aPeriodical 2aTwin Study2 aInternational Society for Twin Studies.0 x1839-262800tTwin researchx1369-0523w(DLC)sn 99040999w(OCoLC)393182424 uhttps://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/twin-research-and-human-genetics  aAUTH02671cgm a22004334a 101552198DNLM20260227153837.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110119s2010    mdu055|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16338  a(OCoLC)702604698  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3001 aGleeson, Joseph,cPh. D.10aPutting together the human brain :blessons from disease /cJoseph Gleeson.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (55 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 13, 2010.3 a(CIT): Neuroscience Seminar Series  Dr. Gleeson's laboratory studies the genetic and cell biological basis of human brain development, focusing on two major conditions that are important causes of disease in humans. The first is disordered neuronal migration. Neurons that populate the cerebral cortex are not born within the gray matter, but instead they are born along the lining of the lateral ventricle. These young neurons then migrate great distances to achieve proper positioning within the correct lamina of the developing cortex. When this is disrupted, cortical gyri and sulci are malformed and the cortex is improperly laminated. The other condition is congenital ataxia, where humans are born with severe balance disturbances and malformations of the cerebellum. They work on the most common of these disorders known as Joubert syndrome where the cerebellare completely absent. They identified the first genes for this condition, which encode novel proteins. There is evidence to suggest that in this condition the major axonal tracts fail to form properly, suggesting that the genes also play important roles in axonal guidance. The lab is currently studying the molecular basis of Joubert syndrome, identifying additional Jouberin genes, and working to understand the role of these genes in brain development.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBrainxphysiology22aAtaxiaxgenetics22aMalformations of Cortical Development, Group IIxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16338  aAUTH01405cas a2200469 a 101519498DNLM20260227090907.0cr||||||||||||010601c20019999cautr p       0    0eng    a  2001252704  a47190051  a1468-0181l1468-0181  z(OCoLC)47190051  a(OCoLC)47048948z(OCoLC)47190051  aCVUcCVUdWAUdOCLdDLCdOCLCQdMUQdUV0dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aHN1b.G5700aW1bGL362XM04a36122110aGlob Soc Policy2dnlm00aGlobal social policy.13aGSP  aLondon ;aThousand Oaks, CA :bSage Publications,c2001-3 3<2010>- :aLos Angeles :bSage  av. ;c24 cm.  aThree issues a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Apr. 2001)-  aTitle from cover.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online; subsription required for full-text online access.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 10, no. 1 (Apr. 2010).12aPublic Policy22aInternationality 2aPeriodical0 x1741-28034 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/gsp  aAUTH01408cas a2200433 a 101242662DNLM20260227090915.0m        d        cr|cn#||||||||050121c20059999cauuu pss     0    0eng    a  2005243311  a1744-8069l1744-8069  a(OCoLC)57465303  aOUNcOUNdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States10aW1  aRB12710aMol Pain2dnlm00aMolecular pain  a[London] :bBioMed Central,c2005-3132016- :aThousand Oaks, CA :bSage Publications Inc.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 a1:1 (14 Jan. 2005)-  aTitle from PDF caption (publisher Web site, viewed Mar. 22, 2005).  aLatest issue consulted: 1:10 (22 Mar. 2005) (viewed Mar. 22, 2005).1 aMEDLINEbv1, 2005-0 aPubMedbv1, 2005-0 aPMC  aArticles published individually, then compiled into an annual vol.  aMode of access: World Wide Web; may require Adobe Acrobat Reader.12aPainxphysiopathology 2aPeriodical40uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/mpx40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101242662  aAUTH02333cgm a2200445 a 101552205DNLM20260227153929.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110119s2010    mdu064 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16351  a(OCoLC)712765488  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 2701 aBecker, Jill B.10aSex differences in drug abuse :bthe role of estradiol /cJill B. Becker.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video (1 hr., 4 min.) :bsd. col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBSSR lecture series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Feb. 7, 2011).  aRecorded Dec. 15, 2010.3 a(CIT):  There are sex differences in many motivated behaviors, including the motivation to take drugs of abuse.  In females, rapid effects of estradiol on the ascending dopamine (DA) system enhance the female's motivation to take cocaine and other drugs of abuse.  These effects of estradiol can be studied behaviorally and neurochemically when looking at the response to psychomotor stimulant drugs such as cocaine and amphetamine.  For example, we have found that female rats exhibit greater behavioral sensitization to cocaine, acquire cocaine self-administration more rapidly, and work harder for access to cocaine than males.  Estradiol enhances these sex differences. In the striatum and nucleus accumbens, estradiol rapidly potentiates stimulated DA release of female rats.  Results from experiments investigating the mechanisms mediating this effect of estradiol will also be discussed.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aSubstance-Related Disordersxepidemiology22aEstradiol22aSex Factors 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research. 0aBSSR lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16351  aAUTH02924cgm a22004454a 101518943DNLM20260227153059.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u091105s2009    mdu070|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15321  a(OCoLC)503610074  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 504.51 aZiegler, Steven Frank.10aFoxp3, TSLP, and the regulation of immunity and inflammation /cSteven F. Ziegler, Director, Immunology Program, Benaroya Research Institute.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 10 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sep. 30, 2009.3 a(CIT): Dr. Steven Ziegler is Director of the Immunology Program at Benaroya Research Institute. He graduated with honors from the University of Michigan in 1979, and in 1984 received his Ph.D. in Molecular Biology from UCLA. Following post-doctoral training at the University of Washington, Dr. Ziegler spent five years as a staff scientist at Immunex, followed by three years as the Director of Immunology/Molecular Biology at Darwin Molecular. He joined the Benaroya Research Institute at Virginia Mason in 1997, where he is now a Full Member and the Director of the Immunology Program. He is a leading authority on the role of FoxP3 in the control of CD4+CD25+ regulatory T cell and Th17 cell development and function. His group has also been investigating the role of thymic stromal lymphopoietin in the development of Th2-type immunity. His group showed that TSLP responses are absolutely required for the development of Th2-type allergic inflammation in mice. His current studies are focused on deciphering the regulation of TSLP gene expression, signal transduction from the TSLP receptor, and the identification and characterization of downstream mediators of TSLP-mediated inflammation. His group is also developing novel transgenic and knockout mice to understand the role of TSLP in respiratory virus infection.  Steve is an excellent and highly entertaining speaker; so don't miss his exciting talk. The Immunology Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aReceptors, Cell Surface22aCytokinesximmunology22aForkhead Transcription Factorsximmunology22aInflammationximmunology22aMutationximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15321  aAUTH01738cas a2200541 a 8100625DNLM20260227091000.0cr|cn ||||||||810129c19799999it br         0||| 0ita    a0391-5387l0391-5387  aPMECD8  a(DNLM)P06050000(s)z(DNLM)M18485000(s)  a(OCoLC)07259701  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aitabeng  9Italy00aW1bPE156410aPediatr Med Chir2dnlm 0aLa Pediatria medica e chirurgica03aLa Pediatria medica e chirurgica :bMedical and surgical pediatrics.30aMedical and surgical pediatrics30aLa Pediatria medica e chirurgica  aVicenza, Italia :b[s.n.]3 aVicenza :bPediatria Medica E Chirurgica  av. :bill.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: v. 1, n. 1, genn./febr. 1979.  aDescription based on: v. 1, n. 3 (mar./giugno 1979); title from cover.  aPrint issues ceased with v. 36 (2014).1 aMEDLINEbv3n1, Jan./Feb. 1981-1 aPubMedbv3n1, Jan./Feb. 1981-1 aIndex medicus  aVol. 22 (2000) complete in one issue.  aIssues for <2000-> called also <n. 127->  aAls