sance Society of America.2 aChicago, IL :bUniversity of Chicago Press313<2019-> :aCambridge :bCambridge University Press  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 20-       spring 1967-  aIncludes the report of the annual meeting and directory of members of the Renaissance Society of America.0 aMEDLINEbSelected citations only0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online. 2aLiterature, Modern 2aSocieties 2aAbstracts 2aDirectory 2aPeriodical2 aRenaissance Society of America.0 x1935-023600tRenaissance newsx0277-903Xw(OCoLC)5037211405tStudies in the Renaissancex0081-8658w(OCoLC)16239854 uhttps://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/renaissance-quarterly4 uhttps://journals.uchicago.edu/journal/rq  aAUTH04239cgm a22004694a 101470347DNLM20260227152451.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080401s2008    mdu065 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14343  a(OCoLC)301877482  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 102.91 aLichtman, Jeff W.,d1951-10aConnectomics /cJeff W. Lichtman.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 19, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 4 min., 25 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 5, 2008.3 a(CIT): Most neuroscientists agree that connectional maps of the brain would have enormous value in developing models of how the brain works and how it fails when subsets of neurons or synapses are missing or misconnected. Such maps would also provide the first detailed information about how the brain's circuits develop and how they change with age.  I am especially eager to study such maps from the developing nervous system because of a longstanding interest in the circuit changes that occur during mammalian early postnatal life.  My colleagues and I have studied the neuromuscular system where a large proportion of the axonal branches that are established in fetal life are pruned away after birth.  This loss, which is sometimes termed "synapse elimination," may be one of the principal ways a mammalian nervous system comes to match the specific demands of a particular epigenetic landscape. In order to study this phenomenon we initially focused on the events occurring at individual synaptic sites where multiple axons compete and all but one are eliminated. Our results have suggested that the competitive events are in part based on the resources available to each axon at a particular synapse.  Because each axon has many branches all competing roughly at the same time, the resources available at one site are probably affected by the outcome of synaptic competitions at other neuromuscular junctions that are innervated by the same axons.  Hence we have begun to develop techniques to observe all these synaptic interactions at different sites simultaneously. This effort has required computer-assisted axonal tracing and the development of transgenic mice in which different axons are labeled different colors.  These Brainbow mice (Livet et al., 2007) give us an opportunity to see the entire connectional maps (or 'connectomes') for muscles and other neuronal circuits. However, at least for the time being, seeking the details of circuits at the finest level of resolution requires electron microscopy.  But getting electron microscopic images of large three dimensional circuits is cumbersome.  My colleagues Ken Hayworth and N. Bobby Kasthuri have been developing a new kind of microtome and imaging strategy that allows high resolution electron microscopy of hundreds or even thousands of ultra thin (<40 nm) sections that are very large (each section ~4 mm^2 or larger). This automatic tape-collecting lathe ultra microtome (ATLUM) promises to make large scale electron microscopic analysis of volumes routine.  It is our hope that connectomic approaches to studying the nervous system will also have utility in answering fundamental questions about disorders in which there is mis-wiring of the brain's circuits.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeuromuscular Junctionxgrowth & development22aAxonsxphysiology22aLuminescent Proteins22aMicroscopy, Electron22aSynapsesxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14343  aAUTH01431cam a22003374a 101230123DNLM20260226110359.0040917s2005    paua     bf   001 0 eng    a  2004022073  a9780781740883  a0781740886  a(OCoLC)56567044  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWR 39bG335 20050 aGenodermatoses (Spitz)10aGenodermatoses :ba clinical guide to genetic skin disorders /c[edited by] Joel L. Spitz ; illustrations by Vaune J. Hatch.  a2nd ed.  aPhiladelphia :bLippincott Williams & Wilkins,cc2005.  axix, 400 p. :bill.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.0 aDisorders of cornification -- Disorders of pigmentation -- Disorders of vascularization -- Disorders of connective tissue -- Disorders with malignant potential -- Epidermolysis bullosa -- Disorders of porphyrin metabolism -- Disorders with photosensitivity -- Disorders with immunodeficiency -- Disorders of hair and nails -- Disorders of metabolism -- Disorders with chromosome abnormalities -- Disorders with short stature.12aSkin Diseasesxgenetics 2aHandbook1 aSpitz, Joel L.  aAUTH01548cgm a22004454a 101294838DNLM20260227151456.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061206s2006    mdu075|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13151  a(OCoLC)84712887  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 55.21 aBeachy, Philip Arden.10aHedgehog signaling in development and disease /cPhilip Beachy.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 28, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 15 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS and NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Mar. 28, 2006.3 a(CIT): Center for Cancer Research - National Cancer Institute Grand Rounds.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHedgehog Proteinsxphysiology22aCell Differentiationxphysiology22aGene Expression Regulation, Developmentalxphysiology22aGene Expression Regulation, Neoplasticxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13151  aAUTH01356cas a2200433 a 101577885DNLM20260224111546.0m        d        cr||||||||||||111226c20119999sz || poo     0    0eng    a  2012243208  a721198097  a1664-5456l1664-5456  z(OCoLC)721198097  a(OCoLC)769387558z(OCoLC)721198097  aSINUScSINUSdHUAdDNLM  apcc  9Switzerland10aW1  aRC388.5bCDE10aCerebrovasc Dis Extra2dnlm00aCerebrovascular diseases extra.  aBasel :bKarger,c2011-  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1 no. 1 (2011).1 aMEDLINEbv5n1, Jan.-Apr. 2015-0 aPubMedbv1n1, 2011-0 aPMC  aDescription based on: 2011:1; title from PDF caption (publisher's Web site, viewed Mar. 2, 2012).  aLatest issue consulted: 2012:2 (viewed Mar. 2, 2012).12aNeurosciences22aCerebrovascular Disorders 2aPeriodical40uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/25473540uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101577885  aAUTH01321cgm a22004094a 101294822DNLM20260227151631.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061206s2006    mdu122|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13064  a(OCoLC)84717651  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 5031 aMasur, Henry.10aDemystifying medicine.pHIV :badvances and retreats /cHenry Masur, John Coffin.30aHIV :badvances and retreats  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 4, 2007).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 2 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 21, 2006.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government.  aOpen-captioned.12aHIV Infections 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13064  aAUTH03006cgm a2200457 i 101684406DNLM20260227155424.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160601s2016    mdu058|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19695  a(OCoLC)953229566  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 704.600aCell transplants to treat the "disease" of chronic pain /cAllan Basbaum. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (58 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded May 18, 2016.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series  Many chronic pain conditions result from alterations in the how the central nervous system processes injury messages. In this respect chronic pain is a "disease" of the nervous system, rather than a symptom of some other condition. Allan Basbaum's laboratory studies mechanisms by which tissue and nerve injury induce chronic pain. To determine whether different features of the pain experience are evoked by the activity of selected subsets of nerve ("pain") fibers or by patterns of activity generated across populations of pain fibers, his lab studies pain behavior in experimental animals with deletions of different genes that code for molecules that transmit injury messages. More recently, his lab initiated a parallel series of studies that examines the circuits that underlie the production of itch, which like pain, is a stimulus-evoked, highly motivating perception. And with a view to long-term management of chronic pain and itch, Dr. Basbaum's lab is transplanting inhibitory cell precursors into the spinal cord to determine whether re-establishing inhibitory control circuitry lost after injury can treat the "disease" of chronic pain and itch. To date the lab has shown that the cells indeed integrate into the host and can ameliorate the persistent pain and itch associated with nerve damage. The hope is that this approach can be translated to the clinic.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 5, 2016).12aChronic Painxtherapy22aCell Transplantation22aPruritusxtherapy22aSpinal Cordxtransplantation 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aBasbaum, A. I.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19695  aAUTH01302cas a2200457 a 101213381DNLM20260227090923.0cr|cn ||||||||040310c20049999nyu|| p       0    0eng    a  20042147170 a1549-1684l1549-1684  a(OCoLC)54674277  aNSDcNSDdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States10aISSN RECORD00aW1bJO536510aRejuvenation Res2dnlm00aRejuvenation research.  aLarchmont, NY :bMary Ann Liebert Inc.,cc2004-  av. :bill.  aSix no. a year  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 7, no. 1 (spring 2004)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv7n1, spring 2004-0 aPubMedbv7n1, spring 2004-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.12aAgingxphysiology22aLongevity22aRejuve