101738455DNLM20260227155825.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u181205s2018    mdu066|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:26186  a(OCoLC)1080640243  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 56800aStanding on the shoulders of mice :badventures in human immunology /cMark M. Davis. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2018]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 6 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lectures. William Paul lecture  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded November 14, 2018.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series.  William Paul lecture.   This annual lecture, begun in 2016 and part of the Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series, honors the legacy of Dr. William E. Paul. Dr. Paul was the leader of the NIH immunology community and his career is without parallel in the field of immunology.  Dr. Davis's work focuses on how T and B lymphocytes recognize specific antigens and in the unique challenges of (and solutions to) studying the human immune system particularly in the context of infectious diseases and vaccination strategies. While inbred mice have been a very powerful model for analyzing the immune system, recent technological and conceptual advances have made direct studies of the human immune system possible. Such studies are vitally important from a translational perspective as mouse models of disease have not been as productive as hoped for in producing "actionable intelligence" with which to diagnose and treat patients. Another benefit is that human immunology work is almost unexplored territory, in which asking basic questions often results in unexpected answers. The compromises that were necessary to make in laboratory mice-virtually eliminating genetic and environmental heterogeneity-are unavoidable in human beings and have profound effects on their immune responses. Dr. Davis's research is allowing a wealth of new insights into how the immune system works and will allow a new depth of understanding in autoimmunity, cancer, and infectious diseases.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 28, 2018).12aT-Lymphocytesximmunology22aB-Lymphocytesximmunology22aDisease Models, Animal22aMice 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aDavis, Mark M.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pWilliam Paul lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?26186  aAUTH02976cgm a2200469 a 101565232DNLM20260227154301.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110715s2011    mdu060 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16729  a(OCoLC)752782880  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWK 8101 aStainier, Didier.10aCuring diabetes one fish at a time :bthe long road of translational research /cDidier Stainier.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 0 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded June 22, 2011.3 a(CIT): Our work is concerned with the formation, function and homeostasis of organs during vertebrate development.  We are interested in understanding the cellular and molecular events that underlie cellular differentiation, tissue morphogenesis and organ function during the formation of the cardiovascular system (the heart and the blood vessels) as well as the liver and pancreas.  One approach consists of screening for mutations that affect these processes in zebrafish, a vertebrate model system that allows forward genetics as well as embryological studies.  We also utilize the tools of chemical genetics to identify pathways that regulate these processes, taking advantage of the high-throughput methods available in zebrafish.  This talk will focus on pancreas development and glucose homeostasis.  Specifically, it will address our attempts to increase beta-cell mass via mobilization of stem cells and cell reprogramming.  It will also cover the results of our ongoing screens for small molecules designed to 1) enhance beta-cell regeneration, 2) lower gluconeogenesis and 3) enhance beta-cell proliferation.  Altogether, these studies aim to provide new therapeutic avenues to treat diabetes.   The NIH Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series includes weekly scientific talks by some of the top researchers in the biomedical sciences worldwide.  For more information, visit The  NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDiabetes Mellitusxenzymology12aDiabetes Mellitusxgenetics22aModels, Animal22aZebrafishxgenetics22aZebrafishxgrowth & development 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16729  aAUTH01513cas a2200481   8006508DNLM20260227090956.0cr|cn ||||||||800710c19809999enkqr p       0    0eng    a0197-5897l0197-5897  aJPPODK  a(DNLM)J36670000(s)  a(OCoLC)06077515  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9England00aW1bJO859UH10aJ Public Health Policy2dnlm 0aJournal of public health policy00aJournal of public health policy.  a[Burlington, Vt.,bJournal of Public Health Policy, inc.]3 32005- :aBasingstoke, England :bPalgrave Macmillan  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-    1980-1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, 1980-0 aPubMedbv1n1, 1980-1 aHospital literature indexbv1n1, 1980-19941 aHospital and health administration indexb1995-19991 aIndex medicus  aHas occasion