l Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 8 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 21, 2010.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aProtein Transportxphysiology22aSNARE Proteins22aTransport Vesicles 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bStructural Biology Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15573  aAUTH03071cgm a22004454a 101524403DNLM20260227153531.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100213s2010    mdu062|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15571  a(OCoLC)624404655  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWP 8701 aSharan, Shyam.10aFunctional analysis of breast cancer susceptibility genes /cShayam [i.e. Shyam] K. Sharan.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCI CCR grand rounds  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 2 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Jan. 26, 2010.3 a(CIT): Dr. Shyam Sharan obtained his M.Sc. degree in genetics from Delhi University, India, and his Ph.D. degree in genetics from Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, Ohio, under the supervision of Dr. Terry Magnuson. He then joined Dr. Allan Bradley"s laboratory as a Howard Hughes Medical Institute Associate at Baylor College of Medicine in Houston, Texas, where he worked on the functional analysis of BRCA1 and BRCA2 in the mouse. He was the first to show that BRCA2 functions as a tumor suppressor because of its role in maintaining the genomic integrity. His work demonstrated that BRCA2 interacts with a key DNA repair protein RAD51 and participates in the repair of damaged DNA by homologous recombination. In 1998, Dr. Sharan established the Genetics of Cancer Susceptibility Section as a group leader in the ABL-Basic Research Program and later joined the Center for Cancer Research, National Cancer Institute, in 1999. The main focus of his research is to understand the mechanism of genomic instability and how it results in tissue-specific cancer.   His laboratory is generating an array of specific mutations along the length of proteins encoded by BRCA1 and BRCA2 in humanized mouse models. This approach combines the use of existing BRCA1 and BRCA2 knockout mice and bacterial artificial chromosomes (BAC) containing the human BRCA1 or BRCA2 gene. More recently he has utilized mouse embryonic stem cells to develop a functional assay to evaluate the functional significance of variants identified in BRCA1 and BRCA2 genes. In 2008, Dr. Sharan received the NCI Director"s Intramural Innovation Award, and in 2009 he received the NIH merit award for his work on functional analysis of BRCA1 and BRCA2.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBreast Neoplasmsxgenetics22aGenes, BRCA122aGenes, BRCA2 2aLecture 2aWebcast 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15571  aAUTH01371cas a22004454a 101088684DNLM20260227090907.0m        d        cr cn---------010221c20019999enkuu pss     0    0eng    a  2002243113  a1472-6831l1472-6831  a(OCoLC)48983838  aCOUcCOUdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9England10aW110aBMC Oral Health2dnlm00aBMC oral health.3 aBioMed Central oral health30aOral health  aLondon :bBioMed Central,c[2001-  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1 (2001)-  aTitle from BioMed Central archive volume screen (viewed on Feb. 20, 2002).1 aMEDLINEbv9, 2001-0 aPubMedbv1, 2001-0 aPMC  aArticles are added consecutively to the current annual v.  aSystem requirements: Internet connectivity; World Wide Web browser.  aMode of access: Internet.12aMouth Diseases22aTooth Diseases 2aPeriodical40uhttps://bmcoralhealth.biomedcentral.com/40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101088684  aAUTH00904cam a22002657i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002100073040003500094044001000129060003200139245011300171250004300284264007000327300003300397336002600430337002800456338002700484650001700511650001700528655001700545710006700562999000900629101613401DNLM20260209122558.0130724s1953    cc a         f000 0 chi    a(OCoLC)881362523  aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1788 no.700aCheng shi wei sheng chang shi /cZhong yang ren min zheng fu wei sheng bu wei sheng xuan chuan chu bian zhu.  aXiu ding ben; Shanghai chong yi 1 ban. 1aBeijing :bRen min chu ban she,c1953 nian 9 yue [September 1953]  a2, 54 pages :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier12aUrban Health22aPest Control 2aPopular Work1 aChina.bWei sheng bu.bXuan chuan chu,eeditor,eissuing body.  aAUTH02877cgm a2200409 i 101629950DNLM20260227154617.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140408s2014    mdu381|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18305  a(OCoLC)879769184  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 1402 aRare Disease Day (Conference)n(7th :d2014 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aRare Disease Day 2014 /cOffice of Rare Diseases Research, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (6 hr., 21 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded February 28, 2014.3 a(CIT): Rare Disease Day was established to raise awareness with the public about rare diseases, the challenges encountered by those affected, the importance of research to develop diagnostics and treatments, and the impact of these diseases on patients' lives. There are about 7000 rare diseases identified in the United States. About 80 percent of rare diseases are genetic in origin and it is estimated that about half of all rare diseases affect children. Rare diseases can be chronic, progressive, debilitating, disabling, severe and life-threatening. Information is often scarce and research is usually insufficient. People affected face challenges such as delays in obtaining a diagnosis, misdiagnosis, psychological burden and lack of support services for the patient and family. The goals remain for rare disease patients to obtain the highest attainable standard of health and to be provided the resources required to overcome common obstacles in their lives. By highlighting these issues, the NIH Office of Rare Diseases Research hopes to: Raise awareness of rare diseases; Strengthen the voice of patients and patient advocacy groups; Give hope and information to patients; Bring stakeholders closer together; Coordinate policy actions within the United States and with other countries; Inspire continued growth of the awareness of rare diseases; Emphasize rare disease research and the search for new therapeutics; Get equality in access to care and treatment.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed May 6, 2014.12aRare Diseases 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Rare Diseases Research,esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18305  aAUTH01504cas a2200469   0140672DNLM20260227090954.0cr|cn ||||||||680515c19679999nyubr         0||| 0eng    a0006-3398l0006-3398  aBIOEAF  a(DNLM)B18240000(s)z(DNLM)M24480000(s)  a(OCoLC)01536451  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bBI854I10aBiomed Eng (NY)2dnlm00aBiomedical engineering.  aNew York,bConsultants Bureau.3 31999- :aNew York :bKluwer Academic/Plenum Publishing  av.billus.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-       Jan./Feb. 1967-  aPlace of publication varies.1 aMEDLINEbv2n1,Jan./Feb. 1968-v8n3,Mar. 19751 aPubMedbv2n1,Jan./Feb. 1968-v8n3,Mar. 19751 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aOfficial publication of the Ministry of Public Health of the USSR.  aTranslation of Meditsinskaia tekhnika. 2aBiomedical Engineering 2aEquipment and Supplies 2aPeriodical1 aSoviet Union.bMinisterstvo zdravookhranenii︠a︡.1 tMeditsinskaia tekhnika.0 x1573-82564 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/10527  aAUTH01210cgm a22003854a 101289747DNLM20260227151509.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2006    mdu051|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13104  a(OCoLC)84672055  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWS 4211 aHuestis, Marilyn.10aDrug abuse's smallest victims :bin utero exposed neonates /cMarilyn Huestis.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 6, 2006).  aStreaming video (51 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 10, 2006.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeonatal Abstinence Syndrome 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13104  aAUTH02287cgm a22004094a 101267418DNLM20260227145029.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu306 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10030  a(OCoLC)64067491  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.500aNew frontiers of biomedical research, 1945-1980 /ca historical symposium sponsored by the National Library of Medicine, National Institutes of Health.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 13, 2006).  aStreaming video (5 hr., 6 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 29, 2001.3 a(CIT): Recent biomedical research has offered human society some of the most innovative and productive scientific discoveries. The many advances have broadened our knowledge of life and the human organism. While this is a vast story, it can be effectively explored through the insights of the scientists who have been instrumental in the development of biomedical research. This symposium will enlist the personal experiences of three scientists who shaped the field of biomedicine: Dr. Donald S. Fredrickson, Dr. Julius Axelrod, and Dr. Joshua Lederberg. The scientists will discuss their respective contributions to the administration of the U.S. biomedical research establishment, the investigation of the nervous system, and the rise of genetics as a discipline.  For more information, visit the  Symposium Web Site.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aBiomedical Researchxhistory22aHistory, 20th Century 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10030  aAUTH01882cas a22004931  18720400RDNLM20260227090911.0cr|cn#||||||||hd|bfa012baap821218c18769999enkqr p       0    0engd   a0026-4423l0026-4423  aMNDDAB  a(DNLM)M36540000(s)  a(OCoLC)01594357  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9England00aW1bMI619H10aMind2dnlm00aMind; a quarterly review of psychology and philosophy.  aEdinburgh [etc.]3 aOxford :bOxford University Press on behalf of Mind Association  av.bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-16 (no. 1-64), Jan. 1876-Oct. 1891; new ser., v. 1-    no. 1-    ; Jan. 1892-0 aMEDLINEbSelected citations only0 aPubMedbSelected citations o