ered by current list only; 1953 journals, information on coverage by current list only; 1953 journals, no information on coverage by indexing or abstracting services -- Exhibit no. 14. Serials covered by Index Catalogue only; Serials covered by index Catalogue and others but not by Quarterly cumulative index medicus, Current list of medical literature, Chemical abstracts or Excerpta medica, August 1953.  aMicrofilm.b[Bethesda, Md.] :cNational Library of Medicine,d1991.e1 microfilm reel : negative ; 35 mm.  cGift;aNew York Academy of Medicine;d2007.5DNLM 2aMedicine 2aBibliography 2aPeriodical  aMedicine / Abstracting & indexing1 aHimwich, Williamina A.q(Williamina Armstrong),d1912-1993.1 aLarkey, Sanford V.q(Sanford Vincent),d1898-1969.2 aArmed Forces Medical Library (U.S.)2 aNew York Academy of Medicine,edonor.5DNLM  aAUTH01765cgmaa22004694a 101267301DNLM20260227144818.0vf|cbahouvf|cjahoucr|cna||||||||060209s2006    mdu063 ||         vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:4257  a(OCoLC)64760686  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aWE 55900aMuscular dystrophy in humans, mice, and zebrafish /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  aUnited States.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (1 hr., 2 min., 4 sec.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (1 hr., 2 min., 4 sec.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aLouis M. Kunkel.  aPresented Jan. 11, 2006.  aAlso available online.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed captioned.12aMuscular Dystrophiesxgenetics22aHumans22aMicexgenetics22aMuscular Dystrophy, Animalxgenetics22aZebrafishxgenetics 2aLecture1 aKunkel, Louis M.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4257  aAUTH02879cgm a2200469 i 101736993DNLM20260227155845.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u181102s2018    mdu063 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:26100  a(OCoLC)1076548147  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQP 90.200aUnderstanding the source of regenerative ability in animals /cAlejandro Sánchez Alvarado. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2018]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture, DeWitt Stetten Jr. Lecture  aRecorded October 10, 2018.3 a(CIT): NIH Director"s Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series DeWitt Stetten Jr. Lecture  Established by NIGMS in 1982 and presented annually in honor of Dr. Stetten, the third NIGMS director, this annual lecture is part of the Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series. Salamanders and starfish might be "simpler" than humans, but they far surpass us in one major way--the ability to regenerate tissues and regrow lost limbs. Dr. Sánchez Alvarado studies regeneration using the flatworm planaria Schmidtea mediterranea. Remarkably, when halved or quartered (even by high school students) this organism can clone itself from the pieces. More than 100 years ago, that feat captured the attention of geneticist Thomas Hunt Morgan, who studied planarians years before his famed work on fruit flies. As astonishing as the planarian regenerative capacities are, Dr. Sánchez Alvarado thinks we all have a bit of "planaria" hidden in our genomes. "You and I are turning over a number of cells equivalent to our body weight every year," he says. "I lost billions of cells yesterday, but this morning I still recognized myself in the mirror. That"s because basic mechanisms are in place to retain our original form and function.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 21, 2018).12aModels, Animal12aRegeneration22aCell Plasticity22aPlanariansxcytology22aPlanariansxgenetics22aStem Cells 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aSánchez Alvarado, Alejandro,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pDeWitt Stetten, Jr. lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?26100  aAUTH02343cgm a22004454a 101267450DNLM20260227145150.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu918  |||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10063  a(DNLM)CIT:10064  a(OCoLC)70119545  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWK 30000aAsymptomatic primary hyperparathyroidism--a perspective for the 21st century /cNational Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases (NIDDK).  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed on June 12, 2006).  aStreaming video (15 hr., 17 min., 14 sec. : sd., col.).0 aDay 1 -- Day 2.  aRecorded Apr. 8-9, 2002.3 a(CIT): A NIH Consensus Development Conference on Asymptomatic Primary Hyperparathyroidism held in 1990 and sponsored by NIDDK explored the causes and effects of this disease, developing a consensus on how to treat it. Together with a research agenda, the consensus statement provided landmarks for basic and clinical approaches to the disease. In the subsequent years many new advances have occurred, including the identification, cloning, and characterization of the PTH/PTHrP receptor and the Calcium Activated Receptor, as well as a series of studies of surgical treatment of Primary Hyperparathyroidism.   Viewers can submit questions or comments to the Live Event Feedback Form.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aHyperparathyroidism, Primary22aHyperparathyroidism, Primaryxtherapy 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1006340uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10064  aAUTH01514cgm a22003974a 101267550DNLM20260227145241.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s1999    mdu378|||||||f||||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10354  a(OCoLC)181358907  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 1602 aNIH Symposium on the Biology of Stressd(1999 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNIH Symposium on the Biology of Stress /csponsored by the National Institute of General Medical and the NIH Office of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c1999]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 2, 2007).  aStreaming video (6 hr., 18 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 4, 1999.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material.  It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aStress, Physiological 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of General Medical Sciences (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10354  aAUTH01089cas a22003971  15620020RDNLM20260224111601.0cr|cn ||||||||821016c19309999xxuqr         0||| 0eng    a0005-3155l0005-3155y005-3155  aBIOSAN  a(DNLM)B19580000(s)  a(OCoLC)01536471  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bBI902R10aBios2dnlm00aBios.  aMount Vernon, Iowa [etc.]3 aMadison Nj :bBet