 States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aHuman Development22aChild Welfare 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10045  aAUTH02105cgm a2200469 i 101775949DNLM20260227160735.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2021    mdu091|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31525  a(OCoLC)1248768204  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWT 3100aWorkforce to support the needs of older adults /cNIA, ACL, CDC, HRSA. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 31 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aFocus on aging: federal partners' webinar series  aRecorded February 2, 2021.3 a(CIT): The Focus on Aging webinar series addresses important topics for public health and health care professionals, aging services organizations, the research community, and other stakeholders in aging.  In addition to general topics of interest for older adults and those who work with them, each webinar includes information specific to individuals with Alzheimer's disease and other types of dementia, as well as their caregivers.  The February 2021 edition of the webinar series focuses on workforce to support the needs of older adults.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 23, 2021).12aAged12aHealth Services for the Agedxorganization & administration22aHealth Workforce22aSocial Support22aDementiaxrehabilitation22aGeriatrics 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute on Aging,esponsoring body. 0aFocus on aging (Series)40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41344  aAUTH01553cas a2200469 i 101692723DNLM20260227090923.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||131115c20159999nyuqr poo     0   a0eng    a  2013274075  a2332-1539l2332-1512  a(OCoLC)863056169  aDLCcDLCbengerdad20260227dDLCdDNLM  ansdpapcc  9United States10aISSN RECORD00aW110a6152230 aAppl. in vitro toxicol.b(Online)10aAppl In Vitro Toxicol2dnlm 0aApplied in vitro toxicologyb(Online)00aApplied in vitro toxicology. 1aNew Rochelle, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.,c[2015]-  a1 online resource  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Vol. 1, no. 1 (March 2015).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued in print.  aDescription based on: Vol. 1, no. 1 (March 2015); title from caption (Mary Ann Liebert website, viewed Aug. 24, 2015).  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 3, no. 4 (December 2017) (viewed January 17,  2018).12aToxicology22aIn Vitro Techniques 2aPeriodical0 x2332-15124 uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/aivt4 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/aiv  aAUTH01728cgm a2200469 i 101775913DNLM20260227160615.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2020    mdu056|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31461  a(OCoLC)1243259476  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 17200aAdvancing psychiatric care through clinical neuroscience :blessons from research on anxiety /cDaniel S. Pine. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (56 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aClinical center grand rounds  aRecorded December 16, 2020.3 a(CIT): Clinical Center Grand Rounds: Advancing Psychiatric Care through Clinical Neuroscience: Lessons from Research on Anxiety  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 2, 2021).12aAnxiety Disordersxphysiopathology22aAnxiety Disordersxtherapy22aBrainxphysiology22aConditioning, Psychological22aChild22aAdolescent 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aPine, Daniel,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40135  aAUTH03878nam a2200469 i 9918986582306676DNLM20260211141018.0230830s2024    aluab    b    001 0beng    a  2023040104  a9780817321932qhardcover  a0817321934qhardcover  a9780817361426qpaperback  a0817361421qpaperback  z9780817394967qelectronic book  a(OCoLC)1393300841  aDLCbengerdacDLCdOCLCOdOCLCFdALMdYDXdOCLCOdIG#  apcc  an-us---an------  9United States00aRA424.5.G6bB74 202400aWZ 10000a610.92223/eng/20231229  aBIO017000aHIS0380002bisacsh1 aByerly, Carol R.,eauthor.10aMosquito warrior :byellow fever, public health, and the forgotten career of General William C. Gorgas /cCarol R. Byerly.30aYellow fever, public health, and the forgotten career of General William C. Gorgas 1aTuscaloosa :bThe University of Alabama Press,c[2024]  axiii, 412 pages :billustrations, maps ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  a"General William Crawford Gorgas (1854-1920) was a renowned pioneer in tropical disease and public health control. A scion of Confederate elites, the contours of Gorgas's his life were directly shaped by yellow fever from birth, but it wasn't until Gorgas, Walter Reed, and other members of the US Army Medical Corps stationed in Cuba explored Carlos Finlay's revolutionary mosquito theory that Gorgas discovered his ultimate foe-the Aedes aegypti and Anopholes mosquitos responsible for transmitting some of the world's deadliest infectious diseases. After successfully clearing Havana of mosquitos and eradicating the spread of yellow fever on the ground (while Reed's team proved it in the lab), Gorgas became chief sanitary officer for the US in the Panama Canal Zone. There he spent the next eight years fighting mosquitos and developing the medical and public health infrastructure necessary to combat mosquito-borne diseases on the isthmus. His efforts created the conditions necessary for the successful construction of the Panama Canal where others had failed, improving global health and empowering US interests abroad through hard and soft power, alike. Gorgas's subsequent missions to Latin America and Africa for the Rockefeller Foundation sought to reduce human suffering and improve global health but were also ideologically charged, as evidenced in his speech "The Conquest of the Tropics for the White Race." In 1914, Gorgas became Surgeon General of the US Army and presided over the expansion of the Army's medical service and modernization of the public health system in the years leading up to and during World War I. Celebrated during his lifetime, Gorgas has largely faded from historical memory but his story remains relevant to the threat of infectious disease we face today and the challenges to public health in a politically complex, globalized world. Carol R. Byerly's definitive biography explains his effacement and restores Gorgas to the historical narrative, expertly situating his life and work in context with his times and tracing his legacy into the present-a cautionary tale of what happens when public health becomes politicized and a reminder that science alone cannot conquer disease"--cProvided by publisher.12aGorgas, William Crawford,d1854-1920.12aUnited States.bArmy12aPhysicians22aMilitary Personnel22aMosquito-Borne Diseasesxprevention & control 2aUnited States 2aBiography  aAUTH02110cgm a2200397 i 101609778DNLM20260227154711.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130604s2013    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17937  a(OCoLC)849715505  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWT 1161 aSpeakman, J. R.q(John R.)eauthor.10aCaloric restriction studies in humans :bfuture research directions /cJohn Speakman. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr.) :bcolor, sound)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdm2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded May 8, 2013.3 a(CIT): The Trans-NIH Geroscience Interest Group (GSIG) cordially invites you to its spring seminar, featuring John Speakman. Professor Speakman is a biologist at the University of Aberdeen Institute of Biological and Environmental Sciences in the U.K., where he directs the University's Energetics Research Group. The internationally renowned Dr. Speakman will discuss nutritional influences on aging, with a focus on caloric restriction, and improving knowledge in the area of the biology of aging.  The Geroscience Interest Group (GSIG) was formed to enhance opportunities for discussion of the intersection between the biology of aging and the biology of disease and conditions that are of interest across ICs.  It is focused on basic biology, but with a longer view towards translation.  aDescription based on version viewed June 12, 2013.12aCaloric Restrictionxtrends22aBiomedical Researchxtrends 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17937  aAUTH01424cgm a2200409 i 101609773DNLM20260227154701.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130604s2013    mdu110|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17929  a(OCoLC)854907455  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWD 200.5.M61 aLippincott-Schwartz, Jennifer,eauthor.10aDemystifying medicine 2013.pThe mitochondrion and its diseases /cJennifer Lippincott-Schwartz, Lynne Wolfe.1 aMitochondrion and its diseases 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 50 min.) :bcolor, sound)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdm2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Apr. 30, 2013.  aDescription based on version viewed July 2, 2013.12aMitochondrial Diseases22aMitochondriaxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aWolfe, Lynne,eauthor.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17929  aAUTH01830cgm a22004334a 101300955DNLM20260227151841.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070302s2007    mdu064|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13633  a(OCoLC)104816939  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 102.51 aStrittmatter, Stephen M.10aSpectrum of adult axonal growth limited by Nogo and Nogo receptor :bplasticity, sprouting, and regeneration /cStephen Strittmatter.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 26, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 4 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 12, 2007.3 a(CIT): Axonal growth to appropriate synaptic targets is essential for development and for injury repair.  Dr. Strittmatter's laboratory focuses on the molecular basis of axonal extension in three specific areas:  1. Axon Guidance during development  2. Axon growth in the adult CNS  3. Genomics to identify genes induced during successful adult PNS regeneration   NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aAxonsxphysiology22aNerve Regenerationxphysiology22aNeuronal Plasticityxphysiology22aReceptors, Cell Surfacexphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13633  aAUTH01400cas a2200445 a 101176511DNLM20260227090909.0cr unu||||||||020408c20029999enkqr p      