bn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.00gSection I.tAging --g1.tBiological and Physiological Change Theories --g2.tNormal Aging --g3.tPrinciples of Care --g4.tEthical Issues in Care --g5.tSexuality in Older Adults --gSection II.tHealth Promotion and Disease Prevention --g6.tPreventive Screenings and Immunizations --g7.tPromotion of Healthy Aging --g8.tMedication Use in Older Adults --g9.tComplementary and Integrative Therapies for Older Adults --gSection III.tAssessment and Management of Health Problems Common to Older Adults --g10.tClinical Assessment: Cognitive, Psychological, Social, Functional, and Medications --g11.tCommon Geriatric Syndromes --g12.tSensory Changes --g13.tChronic Conditions --g14.tInfectious Diseases --g15.tAcute, Emergent, and Urgent Conditions --g16.tMusculoskeletal and Rheumatologic Conditions in the Older Adult --g17.tCancer --g18.tMental Health/Behavioral Health --gSection IV.tCare Systems for Problems of Older Adults --g19.tHome Care --g20.tAcute Care --g21.tLong-Term Care --g22.tPalliative Care and Hospice --gAppendix 1.tTools --gAppendix 2.tIPEC Competencies  a"Written by NPs and PAs who have a wealth of experience in the care of older adults across all practice settings, Gerontology and Geriatrics for NPs and PAs takes an evidence-based approach to both gerontology and geriatrics, incorporating the latest national and international guidelines and standards of care. This first-of-its-kind text takes an interprofessional, teamwork-based approach that reflects Interprofessional Education Collaborative (IPEC) core competencies, as well as the unique perspectives that NPs and PAs each bring to the collaborative care of older adults."--cPublisher website12aGeriatrics22aHealth Services for the Aged22aNurse Practitionersxeducation22aPhysician Assistantsxeducation1 aBeavers-Kirby, Jill,eeditor.1 aSegal-Gidan, Freddi,eeditor.  aAUTH01572cas a2200493 a 9812512DNLM20260227091050.0cr|cnu||||||||980924c19989999nyuer p       0    0eng    a   98657820 z sn 970014510 a1096-620Xl1096-620X  aJMFOFJ  a(DNLM)SR0092301(s)  a(OCoLC)37830714  aNSDPcNSDPdOUCAdNSDPdDNLM  ansdpalcd  9United States00aW1bJO75910aJ Med Food2dnlm 0aJournal of medicinal food00aJournal of medicinal food.  aLarchmont, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.,cc1998-  av. :bill.  aTwelve no. a year,b2010-  aQuarterly,b1998-2008  aSix no. a year,b2009  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (spring 1998)-  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 5, no. 2 (summer 2002); title from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv5n1, spring 2002-1 aPubMedbv5n1, spring 2002-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso available online.  3v.1, no. 1 (Spr. 1998)c20030121fNotice is not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper that meets the full ANSI standard for Permanent Paper02aFood02aHerbal Medicine02aPlants, Medicinal 2aPeriodical0 x1557-76004 uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/jmf  aAUTH03675cgm a2200457 i 101655801DNLM20260227154933.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150409s2015    mdu523 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18868  a(DNLM)CIT:18873  a(OCoLC)934018945  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 84.32 aNIH BD2K Workshop on Community-Based Data and Metadata Standardsd(2015 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aNIH BD2K Workshop on Community-Based Data and Metadata Standards :bPorter Neuroscience Research Center, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (8 hr., 42 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded February 25 & 26, 2015.3 a(CIT): The ability to harvest the wealth of information contained in biomedical Big Data will advance our understanding of human health and disease; however, lack of appropriate tools, poor data accessibility, and insufficient training, are major impediments to rapid translational impact. To meet this challenge, NIH launched the BD2K trans-NIH initiative to enable biomedical research as a digital research enterprise, to facilitate discovery and support new knowledge, and to maximize community engagement. An important aspect of meeting this challenge is to make biomedical research data and resources maximally shareable and reusable. For this reason, BD2K is formulating approaches to encourage development and facilitate the use of data-related (including metadata) standards more broadly across the biomedical research community and is, therefore, interested in the issues involved in developing Community-Based Standards (CBS).   Goals  1.Effective approaches, processes, and activities that could advance the community-based standards landscape (e.g., creating a collaborative workspace or an advising structure toward standards development, extension, or adoption).  2.Gaps in community-based data standards of relevance to biomedical research, including real use-cases (e.g., emerging fields and technologies, or research domains with multiple existing data standards that could benefit from additional work, integration and/or reconciliation).  3.Lessons learned from existing CBS efforts, particularly examples with field-tested processes and infrastructure or known examples of failures by CBS efforts.  4.Common challenges in CBS development (e.g., methods for community engagement or building interoperability with other related standards).   5.Considerations for evaluating progress and milestones to assess data standards development and utility.  6.Effective approaches for addressing the need to sustain useful standards, and to update existing standards as a field develops.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed January 5, 2016).12aCommunity-Based Participatory Researchxstandards12aData Curationxmethods22aData Accuracy22aData Miningxmethods22aResearch Design 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1886840uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18873  aAUTH01698cgm a2200469 i 101671886DNLM20260227155223.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u151031s2015    mdu128|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19235  a(OCoLC)935632164  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 1371 aPommier, Yves,eauthor.10aTRACO 2015.pTopoisomerase. HIV /cY. Pommier and F. Maldarelli.1 aTopoisomerase1 aHIV1 aTranslational research in clinical oncology 2015 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 8 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aDNA topoisomerases and their poisoning by anticancer and antibacterial drugs / Yves Pommier -- Retroviruses / Frank Maldarelli.  aRecorded October 13, 2015.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 8, 2015).12aDNA Topoisomerasesxdrug effects22aAntineoplastic Agents22aHIVxphysiology22aVirus Replication 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aMaldarelli, F.q(Frank),eauthor.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19235  aAUTH01875cgm a22004334a 101294892DNLM20260227151851.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061206s2006    mdu063|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13520  a(OCoLC)84710258  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 165.5.H31 aShenk, Thomas.10aHCMV persistence and latency /cThomas E. Shenk.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 19, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 3 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 29, 2006.3 a(CIT): Human cytomegalovirus (HCMV) is a ubiquitous member of the ?-herpesvirus family. Although infection is usually asymptomatic in healthy children and adults, HCMV is the leading viral cause of birth defects and a major cause of morbidity and mortality in immunocompromised individuals. HCMV remains a major complication following bone marrow transplantation, despite improved anti-viral therapies.   The NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series includes weekly scientific talks by some of the top researchers in the biomedical sciences worldwide.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aCytomegalovirusxpathogenicity22aCytomegalovirus Infections22aVirus Latency22aVirus Replication 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13520  aAUTH03033cgm a2200469 i 101775908DNLM20260227160602.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2020    mdu058|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31442  a(OCoLC)1240392135  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 76 AA100aAll health is not created equal :bwhere you live matters /cShannon N. Zenk. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (58 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aStephen E. Straus distinguished lecture  aRecorded December 9, 2020.3 a(CIT): Stephen E. Straus Distinguished Lecture  Maybe the saying shouldn't be "you are what you eat," but rather "you are where you eat." Social and economic factors, such as where someone lives, have powerful influences on people's health. This association holds true not only for access to healthy food but also for other things such as housing conditions and green space availability. Such factors impact risk of exposure and susceptibility to many diseases and conditions, including COVID-19.  In her presentation, Dr. Shannon Zenk will explore the science behind social determinants of health and demonstrate how vital effective integrative or multilevel approaches are when addressing health and health inequities. Social determinants of health are conditions in the environments in which people are born, live, learn, work, play, and otherwise spend their time. They affect a wide range of health, functioning, and quality-of-life outcomes and risks. Dr. Zenk will share how to better understand what affects people's health and drives health disparities, and ultimately how to develop effective interventions to improve the public's health and eliminate inequities.  Dr. Zenk brings a unique perspective to this lecture as a nurse, a population health scientist, and, since September 2020, the director of the National Institute of Nursing Research at the National Institutes of Health.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 1, 2021).12aHealth Equity22aResidence Characteristics22aSocial Determinants of Health22aCommunity Networks22aHealthy Lifestyle 2aUnited States 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aZenk, Shannon N.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aStephen E. Straus distinguished lecture in the science of complementary health therapies40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40083  aAUTH01930cgm a22004694a 101294879DNLM20260227151638.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061206s2006    mdu116|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13414  a(OCoLC)123689184  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 3751 aMoody, Terry W.10aRedox biology.pAngiogenesis, carcinogenesis /cRoberts and Yeh [i.e. Terry Moody and David Roberts].30aAngiogenesis, carcinogenesis1 iTitle from title screen:aSignal transduction  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 23, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 56 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 10, 2006.3 a(CIT): Redox Biology (RB). The course is designed for NIH fellows to enhance their knowledge of redox biology. Reactive species such as superoxide, hydrogen peroxide and nitric oxide are associated with cellular toxicity, however, nitric oxide is useful in the treatment cardiovascular disease.  The course will examine the role of reactive nitrogen and oxygen species in carcinogenesis, cancer proliferation and angiogenesis.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aOxidation-Reduction12aSignal Transduction22aFree Radicals22aNeoplasmsxetiology22aNeovascularization, Pathologic 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13414  aAUTH01930cgm a2200481 i 9918418885906676DNLM20260227162423.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220616s2022    mdu055|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32421  a(OCoLC)1365556514  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 24500aTwo sides of a deadly curve :bneurologic post-infectious inflammatory disease /cPeter Richard Williamson, MD, PhD; David E. Kleiner, MD, PhD; Dima A. Hammoud, MD; Seher Anjum, MD. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (55 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aClinical Center grand rounds  a"Clinicopathologic Grand Rounds: Clinical Cases from the NIH Clinical Center."  aRecorded May 25, 2022.3 a(CIT): Two Sides Of A 