ed by Mourade Azrour, Jamal Mabrouki, Azidine Guezzaz, Sultan Ahmad, Shakir Khan, and Said Benkirane.  aFirst edition. 1aBoca Raton, FL :bCRC Press, Taylor & Francis Group,c2024.  axiv, 94 pages :bIllustrations ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.0 aApplications of blockchain in healthcare: review study -- Deep learning for healthcare management -- Exploring lung cancer pathologies using metaphoric interventions of deep learning techniques -- Artificial Intelligence in smart healthcare -- Biometrics and data smart healthcare system -- Analysing the ascendant trend of veganism: a comprehensive study on the shift towards sustainable dietary choices -- Monitoring of water toxicity throught the Internet of Things to protect the health of the population.  a"Machine Learning, Internet of Things (IoT) and data analytics are new and fresh technologies that are being increasingly adopted in the field of medicine. This book positions itself at the forefront of this movement, exploring the beneficial applications of these new technologies and how they are gradually creating a smart healthcare system. This book details the various ways in which machine learning, data analytics and IoT solutions are instrumental in disease prediction in smart healthcare. For example, wearable sensors further help doctors and healthcare managers to monitor patients remotely and collect their health parameters in real-time, which can then be used to create datasets to develop machine learning models that can aid in the prediction and detection of any susceptible disease. In this way, smart healthcare can provide novel solutions to traditional medical issues. This book is a useful overview for scientists, researchers, practitioners and academics specialising in the field of intelligent healthcare, as well as containing additional appeal as a reference book for undergraduate and graduate students"--cProvided by publisher.12aDeep Learning22aInternet of Things22aData Analyticsxmethods22aDelivery of Health Care1 aAzrour, Mourade,eeditor.  aAUTH01338nam a2200337 i 101776514DNLM20260227153811.0210225r18801880pauaf    b    000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5539 no.31 aKerlin, Isaac Newton,d1834-1893,eauthor.10aThree cases of progressive muscular degeneration /cby I.N. Kerlin and Charles K. Mills. 1aPhiladelphia :bCollins, Printer,c1880.  a15 pages, 1 leaf of plates :billustrations ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"Extracted from the Transactions of the American Medical Association."  aIncludes bibliographical references.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM12aMuscular Atrophy22aHypertrophy 2aCase Reports1 aMills, Charles K.q(Charles Karsner),d1845-1931,eauthor.2 aAmerican Medical Association.tTransactions.  aAUTH03383cgm a2200421 a 101546241DNLM20260227153553.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101027s2010    mdu079|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16130  a(OCoLC)688679717  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 55.91 aRose, George D.10aProtein folding :bseeing is deceiving /cGeorge Rose.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 19 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 15, 2010.3 a(CIT): The protein folding problem was first recognized by Hsien Wu (1931) and Mirsky & Pauling (1936), approximately three-quarters of a century ago. The problem (arguably the most significant unsolved problem in chemical biology) is inherently grounded in protein thermodynamics, and thermodynamics is surely our most powerful discipline for understanding biological systems. So why does fundamental understanding of protein folding remain an unresolved question? In work at the NIH, Anfinsen showed that a protein's three-dimensional structure is a spontaneous consequence of its amino acid sequence in water at physiological temperature and pressure. Remarkably, under dilute solution conditions, a purified protein adopts its native fold without either the addition of energy or assistance from auxiliary cellular components (chaperones notwithstanding). The fold of the protein links the one-dimensional, linear world of DNA to the three-dimensional world of biological function; accordingly, protein folding is a cornerstone of life on earth. Yet, in essence, this self-assembly process lies within the province of biophysics, not cell biology. The classic folding paradigm, established by Anfinsen and others, has been interpreted to mean that under folding conditions, the native fold is selected from an astronomical number of conceivable alternatives by the constellation of favorable interactions between and among its amino acid sidechains. This plausible idea is entirely consistent with the characteristic close-packing seen in protein crystal structures, where it is apparent that residues distant in sequence are juxtaposed in space, presumably providing both structural stability and topological specificity. Contrary to this view, I will discuss evidence from both experiment and simulations that the overall fold is established prior to eventual sidechain close-packing. Consequently, formation of the folded, hydrogen-bonded framework and its further stabilization via sidechain locking are separable folding events, an enormously simplifying realization.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aProtein Folding 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16130  aAUTH02291cgm a2200433 i 9918503077806676DNLM20260227162825.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u221105s2022    mdu289|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32721  a(OCoLC)1388203513  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 302 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S).bHealth Disparities Interest Group.bWorkshopn(1st :d2022 :cOnline)eauthor.10aNIH Health Disparities Interest Group Inaugural Workshop :bIntegrating social determinants and structural influence measures in biomedical research /cNational Institutes of Health.1 aInaugural NIH HDIG Workshop1 aIntegrating social determinants and structural influence measures in biomedical research 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (4 hr., 49 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 26, 2022.3 a(CIT): The NIH Health Disparities Interest Group is hosting its inaugural workshop featuring renowned speakers addressing foundational and cutting-edge research relevant to advancing the role of social determinants of health in biological, clinical, behavioral, social, and population sciences research.  The goal of the event is to highlight the importance of integrating social determinants and structural influence measures in biomedical research to further the understanding of health disparities and identify interventions to improve health equity.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 22, 2023).12aSocial Determinants of Health22aBiomedical Research22aHealth Status Disparitiesxeconomics.22aHealth Equityxeconomics 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=46506  aAUTH02096cgm a2200505 i 101645875DNLM20260227154740.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u141108s2014    mdu075|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18658  a(OCoLC)897023839  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWP 8701 aColditz, Graham A.eauthor.10aChildhood and adolescent nutrition and growth drive breast cancer risk :buntapped opportunities for prevention /cGraham Colditz. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 15 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aStars in nutrition & cancer  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded October 6, 2014.3 a(CIT): The lecture will: identify childhood growth as predictors of lifelong risk; identify components of a pre-menarcheal diet that modifies breast cancer risk; identify gaps in understanding nutrition before first pregnancy and breast cancer risk; and understand breast cancer risk accumulation in context of breast development and maturation.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed November 26, 2014.12aBreast Neoplasmsxetiology22aAdolescent22aAdolescent Nutritional Physiological Phenomena22aBreast Neoplasmsxprevention & control22aBreastxgrowth & development22aChild22aChild Nutritional Physiological Phenomena22aRisk Factors 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aStars in nutrition & cancer40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18658  aAUTH03365cgm a2200493 i 101645883DNLM20260227154737.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u141108s2014    mdu166       fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18666  a(OCoLC)898326066  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.55.E700aEthical and regulatory aspects of clinical research.nSession 2,pIRB review, randomized clinical trials, and research with children /cRichard Cannon, Robert Truog.1 aIRB review, randomized clinical trials, and research with children 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 46 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.0 aPurpose and function of IRBs : successes and recent challenges / Richard Cannon -- Ethical conflicts in randominzed controlled trials / Robert D. Truog -- Ethics of research with children / Sara F. Goldkind.  aRecorded Oct. 8, 2014.3 a(CIT): Ethical and Regulatory Aspects of Clinical Research - Session 2    The Department of Bioethics offers this seven to eight week course annually each fall. The course is designed to provide an overview of the important issues in the ethics of human subject research for clinical investigators and others who participate in the conduct of research and is open to the entire NIH community as well as to those from outside NIH. Topics include the history of human subject research ethics, principles and guidelines, study design, subject recruitment, informed consent, and international research. The course is open to the entire NIH community as well as to those from outside NIH, and is also available through Videocast. The recommended textbook is Ethical and Regulatory Aspects of Clinical Research, edited by Emanuel et al (Johns Hopkins University Press). The course is taught by guest faculty and faculty members from the National Institutes of Health. This is a required academic program for Bioethics fellows.    Purpose and Function of IRBs: Successes and Current Challenges,  Richard Cannon MD Chair, NHLBI IRB; Ethics of Randomized Clinical Trials: Clinical Equipoise, Robert Truog MD Professor of Medical Ethics & Anaesthesia (Pediatrics) Harvard Medical School;    Research with Children, Sara Goldkind MD.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed December 5, 2014.12aEthics Committees, Research22aAdolescent22aChild22aHuman Experimentationxethics22aRandomized Controlled Trials as Topicxethics 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCannon, Richard O.,cIII.tPurpose and fuction of IRBs.1 aTruog, Robert.tEthical conflicts in randomized controlled trials.1 aGoldkind, Sara F.tEthics of research with children.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18666  aAUTH02819cgm a2200493 i 101773041DNLM20260227160341.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201105s2020    mdu061|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30342  a(OCoLC)1224228218  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 11 AA100aNew drugs, old problems :bthe sulfonamide revolution and children's health care delivery in the United States, 1933-1949 /cNLM History of Medicine, Cynthia Connolly. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 15, 2020.3 a(CIT): Using pediatric patient records housed at the National Library of Medicine, Dr. Cynthia Connolly explore the transformation wrought by the sulfonamides in medical and nursing practice at Baltimore's Sydenham Hospital.  Published articles, oral histories, and physician memoirs reveal only part of the story of one of the twentieth century's most pivotal scientific breakthroughs.  Through patient records, which rarely survive intact, it is possible to appreciate the ways in which the new therapeutics demanded more intense bedside care, enhanced laboratory facilities, and new levels of cooperation.  It also reveals how and why the care of infants and children with infectious diseases made demands on physicians and nurses that differed from those facing clinicians treating adult patients.  Finally, the clinical practices, policy debates, and legal infrastructure that arose in in the context of the sulfonamides provided a template for pediatric drug development going forward that exists into the current day.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 19, 2020).22aSydenham Hospital (Baltimore, Md.)12aCommunicable Diseasesxdrug therapy22aChild22aInfant22aSulfonamidesxhistory22aSulfonamidesxtherapeutic use22aDrug Evaluationxhistory22aHospitals, Publicxhistory22aHistory, 20th Century 2aUnited States 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aConnolly, Cynthia A.q(Cynthia Anne),espeaker.2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.).bHistory of Medicine Division,eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=35410  aAUTH02029cas a2200505 i 9919013695706676DNLM20260205164026.0m     o  d        cr#|||||||||||240609c20249999nyu|| poo     0    0eng    a  2025227995  a1439686319a1450842867  a2950-4562l2950-4562  a(OCoLC)1443090808z(OCoLC)1439686319z(OCoLC)1450842867  aSFBbengerdad20260205cSFBdOCLKBdOH1dOCLCOdUSUdDNLM  apcc  9United States04aRC86000aW10 aIntestinal Failure (New York, N.Y.)10aIntest Fail2dnlm10aIntestinal failure.1 iAlso known as:aINTF 1a[New York, N.Y.] :bElsevier Inc. on behalf of International Intestinal Rehabilitation & Transplant Association,c[2024]-  a1 online resource  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Volume 1 (January/March 2024).  aOfficial journal of the International Intestinal Rehabilitation & Transplant Association (IIRTA).0 aPubMedbv1, 2024-0 aPMC  aArticles published as they become available and compiled into quarterly volumes.  aContains special issues.  aDescription based on: Volume 1 (January–March 2024); title from resource description page (ScienceDirect, viewed August 23, 2024).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 6 (April-June 2025) (ScienceDirect, viewed July 1, 2025).12aIntestinal Failure22aIntestinesxtransplantation 2aPeriodical2 aInternational Intestinal Rehabilitation & Transplant Association,eissuing body.40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=991901369570667640uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/intestinal-failure  aAUTH01226nam a2200337 i 9919011895906676DNLM20260220080939.0060330s1998    txuc          100 0deng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States 4aRD27.35.D42bM53 199800aWB 100bM621 199800aMichael E. DeBakey, M.D. :bsurgeon, educator, and medical statesman : fifty years of excellence at Baylor College of Medicine. 1a[Houston, Tex.] :b[Baylor College of Medicine],c[1998?]  aiv, 81 pages :bportraits ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  cGiftaMichael E. DeBakey, M.D.;d2009.5DNLM12aDeBakey, Michael E.q(Michael Ellis),d1908-2008.22aBaylor College of Medicine.12aMedicine22aEducation, Medical22aBiomedical Research 2aConference Proceedings1 aDeBakey, Michael E.q(Michael Ellis),d1908-2008,ehonouree.1 aDeBakey, Michael E.q(Michael Ellis),d1908-2008,edonor.5DNLM2 aBaylor College of Medicine  aAUTH02196cgm a22004574a 101473097DNLM20260227152513.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080502s2008    mdu043 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14445  a(OCoLC)228569262  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQV 7601 aNewman, David J.,d1939-10aConserving mother nature's chemistry set for tomorrow's cures :bthe medical value of biodiversity /cDavid Newman.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aGreen hour  aTitle from title screen (viewed May 13, 2008).  aStreaming video (42 min., 11 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 16, 2008.3 a(CIT): The April installment of the NIH Environmental Management System Green Hour Seminar Series   Did you know that the Earth's biological diversity includes almost 350,000 species of plants and at least one million species of fungi and that many of these could be sources of cures for today's devastating diseases? Dr. David Newman, Chief of National Cancer Institute's Natural Products Branch, will discuss how the 60,000 plant samples and 15,000 marine samples that his team has collected worldwide have led to drug discoveries, including medications to treat HIV and tumors. Come learn about the importance of protecting the world's biodiversity.  For more information on the Green Hour or to view upcoming events, please visit  http://www.nems.nih.gov/greenhour.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMateria Medica22aBiodiversity22aPharmaceutical Preparations22aPlants, Medicinal 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aGreen hour40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14445  aAUTH01313cas a2200469 a 9814576DNLM20260224111546.0cr|cn ||||||||980924c19989999enk x p       0    0eng    asn 98039408   a1367-4676l1367-4676  a(DNLM)SR0092172(s)  a(OCoLC)39984608  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9England00aW1bME92337M10aMent Health Relig Cult2dnlm00aMental health, religion & culture.3 aMental health, religion and culture  aAbingdon, Oxfordshire :bCarfax Pub.,cc1998-3 aAbingdon :bCarfax Publishing  av.  aThree no. a year  aTwo no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (May 1998)-  aTitle from cover.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.02aCulture02aMental Health02aReligion02aReligion and Medicine02aReligion and Psychology 2aPeriodical0 x1469-97374 uhttps://www.tandfonline.com/loi/cmhr20  aAUTH01950cgm 