h (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aClinical Center grand rounds great teachers series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=2450  aAUTH01492cam a22004217a 101680205DNLM20260210162323.0930727s1993    mdua     b   f000 0 eng    a28656527  z(OCoLC)28656527  aGPObengcGPOdVYLdUIUdTJMdTOMdOCLCQdOCLCFdOCLCOdOCLCQdOCLCOdDNLM  anlmcopyc  an-us---  9United States 4aQP86b.I48 199300aW6bP3 v.7481 box 2142 no.1  a0447-A-1304a612.680973bIn92200 aHE 20.3852:AG 4/1300aIn search of the secrets of aging /cNational Institutes of Health, National Institute on Aging.  a[Bethesda, Md.? :bDepartment of Health and Human Services, Public Health Service], National Institutes of Health, National Institute on Aging,c[1993]  a35 pages :billustrations ;c28 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aNIH publication ;vno. 93-2756  aShipping list no: 93-0417-P.  a"May, 1993"--Page 4 of cover.  aIncludes bibliographical references.  cTransfer;aOffice of NIH History & Stetten Museum;d2012.5DNLM12aAging22aLife Expectancy22aLongevity 2aUnited States2 aNational Institute on Aging. 0aNIH publication ;vno. 93-2756.  aAUTH02600cgm a2200457 i 101680597DNLM20260227155439.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160404s2016    mdu062|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19571  a(OCoLC)948243700  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWF 60004aThe life of breath :bthe role of stem cells in lung maintenance and repair /cBrigid L. M. Hogan.1 aRole of stem cells in lung maintenance and repair 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aRecorded March 23, 2016.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series.  There have been recent exciting advances in our understanding of the stem and progenitor cells of the mammalian lung. Classic studies, including genetic lineage tracing experiments in the mouse using cell-specific Cre drivers, have shown relatively slow cell turnover and replacement of the respiratory epithelium at steady state. However, when cells are damaged by infection or toxic agents, quiescent progenitor populations are activated to promote repair. Injury models have also revealed evidence for an unexpected level of epithelial cell "plasticity" in which differentiated cells change their phenotype and give rise to either new stem cells or different cell types. Dr. Hogan's lab uses clonal 3D in vitro organoid cultures to identify different populations of stem cells and the niche in which they reside, and to screen for factors promoting proliferation and differentiation at steady state and during injury-induced repair.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 25, 2016).12aLungxcytology12aLungxphysiology22aCell Plasticity22aStem Cells 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aHogan, Brigid,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19571  aAUTH01239cas a2200421 a 9433810DNLM20260227091015.0cr|cn ||||||||940728c19949999mduqr p       0    0eng    asn 93002082   a1071-6076l1071-6076  a(DNLM)SR0078516(s)  a(OCoLC)28692470  aNSDPcNSDPdDNLM  9United States00aW1bPH619T10aPhilos Psychiatr Psychol2dnlm 0aPhilosophy, psychiatry, and psychology00aPhilosophy, psychiatry, & psychology :bPPP.30aPPP3 aPhilosophy, psychiatry, and psychology  aBaltimore, MD :bJohns Hopkins University Press,cc1994-  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Mar. 1994)-0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.02aPhilosophy02aPsychiatry02aPsychology 2aPeriodical0 x1086-33034 uhttp://muse.jhu.edu/journals/philosophy%5Fpsychiatry%5Fand%5Fpsychology/  aAUTH02157cgm a22004574a 101486641DNLM20260227152602.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081007s2008    mdu062 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14658  a(OCoLC)319127387  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3071 aAndersen, Richard A.10aFree-choice activates decision circuits /cRichard Andersen.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Apr. 7, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 1 min., 12 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 22, 2008.3 a(CIT): The posterior parietal cortex of the brain lies between the sensory and the movement regions of the cerebral cortex and serves as a bridge from sensation to action.  Dr. Andersen's lab has found that an anatomical map of plans exists within this area, and that one part is devoted to planning eye movements and another part to planning arm movements.  Action plans in the arm movement area exist in a cognitive form, specifying the goal of the intended movement rather than particular signals to various muscle groups. Andersen's lab has used the information gained about parietal cortex to begin development of a cognitive-based neural prosthesis for paralyzed patients.  NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aParietal Lobexphysiology22aArmxphysiology22aProstheses and Implants22aPsychomotor Performancexphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14658  aAUTH02072cgm a22004694a 101319930DNLM20260227152304.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071101s2007    mdu793 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14062  a(DNLM)CIT:14067  a(OCoLC)181903042  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 20200aCancer and inflammation /cNational Cancer Institute.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 19, 2007).  aStreaming video (13 hr., 13 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS and NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Oct. 9-10, 2007.3 a(CIT): This NCI sponsored conference will host international leaders in the field of cancer and inflammation and should provide an exciting forum for discussion and debate on the state of current understanding of this field. Sessions will include Innate Resistance and Cancer, Colon and Prostate Cancers, The Inflammatory Tumor Micro-Environment, Skin Cancers and Cancers with an Infectious Pathogenesis. The target audience for this conference are researchers interested in the latest developments in the role of inflammation in development and progression of cancer.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxetiology12aNeoplasmsximmunology22aInflammationxcomplications22aInflammationximmunology22aNeoplasmsxprevention & control 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1406240uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14067  aAUTH01465cas a2200493 a 101120122DNLM20260227090908.0cr||||||||||||010516c20019999enktr p       0    0eng    a  2001250020  a1471-8731l1471-8731  a(OCoLC)46952914  aEUWcEUWdSLUdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  ad------  9England14aQH332b.B5200aW1bDE997NAC10aDev World Bioeth2dnlm00aDeveloping world bioethics.  aOxford ;aMalden, MA :bBlackwell,cc2001-3132014- :aAbingdon :bJohn 