 now also with Italian translation.02aGalen.tWorks.kSelections.lLatin.02aGalen.tWorks.kSelections.lItalian.00aNicolaus,cRheginus,d1280-1350.12aHistory of Medicine12aLiterature, Medieval22aHistory, Medieval 2aItaly1 aD'Angelo, Edoardo,eeditor.1 aFusco, Silvia,eeditor.1 aManfredonia, Rosa,d1978-eeditor.0 aNicolaus,cRheginus,d1280-1350,etranslator.02aGalen.tDe uinis.lLatin.02aGalen.tDe uinis.lItalian.02aGalen.tDe ea, quae ex sompniis, dignotione.lLatin.02aGalen.tDe ea, quae ex sompniis, dignotione.lItalian.02aGalen.tDe uirtutibus cprpus nostrum dispensantibus corpus nostrum.lLatin.02aGalen.tDe uirtutibus cprpus nostrum dispensantibus corpus nostrum.lItalian.02aGalen.tDe aquis.lLatin.02aGalen.tDe aquis.lItalian. 0aFonti e studi (Centro europeo di studi normanni) ;vnuova serie, 8.  aAUTH01044cam a2200289 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390350022000800400035001020440018001370600024001551000062001792450077002412640060003183000027003783360026004053370028004313380027004595000019004865010060005056500047005656500034006126500056006466500043007029990009007459918787486206676DNLM20260212180003.0240325s1955    xxu           000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)1542998626  aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.271 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aVitamin K deficiency (hypoprothrombinemia) /cmethod of William B. Bean. 1a[United States] :b[publisher not identified],c[1955?]  apages 304-305 ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  aNLM copy, bound 27th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM12aHypoprothrombinemiasxprevention & control12aHypoprothrombinemiasxtherapy12aVitamin K Deficiency Bleedingxprevention & control12aVitamin K Deficiency Bleedingxtherapy  aAUTH01542cam a2200373 i 9918787488206676DNLM20260212175931.0240319r19551955ilua     b    000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)1540315217  aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.161 aSchedi, Harold P.,eauthor.10aCorrection for color differences between standards and urine extracts and their ketonic fractions in the Callow-Zimmermann reaction /cHarold P. Schedi, Ph.D., William B. Bean, M.D., B.M Stevenson, B.A., and E.R. Schumacher, B.A. 1a[St. Louis, Illinois] :b[publisher not identified],c[1955]  apages 191-214 :billustrations ;c26 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  a"Reprinted from the Journal of Laboratory and clinical medicine, St. Louis, vol. 45, no. 2, pages 191-214, February, 1955."  aNLM copy, bound 16th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM  aIncludes bibliographical references.12a17-Ketosteroidsxurine12aColorxstandards22aUrinalysisxmethods22aReference Standards1 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.1 aStevenson, B. M,eauthor.1 aSchumacher, E. R.,eauthor.0 aJournal of Laboratory and clinical medicine  aAUTH02709cgm a22004094a 101532573DNLM20260227153448.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100604s2010    mdu051|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15865  a(OCoLC)646388415  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5681 aLenardo, Michael.10aMolecular regulation of lymphocyte function and homeostasis /cMichael Lenardo ; Immunology Interest Group.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (51 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 28, 2010.3 a(CIT): Mike Lenardo is one of NIH"s star immunologists. As a member of the Laboratory of Immunology for more than 20 years, Mike has made critical discoveries in the areas of apoptosis, human autoimmune disease and immunodeficiency, and gene transcriptional control. He pioneered the concept of activation induce cell death of lymphocytes, worked out many of the underlying mechanisms, and played a seminal role in determining the involvement of Fas and associated molecules in a spectrum of human immune diseases collectively termed autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome (APLS). This work also led to the unexpected finding that the state of many TNF family receptors is oligomeric rather than monomeric even in the unliganded state and revealed an unanticipated role for caspases in productive T lymphocyte activation. Beyond his outstanding biological studies in these several areas and in the field of NF-kB transcriptional control, autophagy, and other important facets of immune regulation, he has also been a leader in enriching the immunological community at NIH, creating the extraordinarily successful annual IIG retreat and developing the Graduate Partnership Program. He will speak to us this week about "Molecular Regulation of Lymphocyte Function and Homeostasis." This is a "Not to Miss" opportunity to hear about the exciting research and insights of one of NIH"s best.    The Immunology Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aT-Lymphocytesximmunology22aHomeostasis 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15865  aAUTH03465cgm a2200469 i 9918663187806676DNLM20260227163813.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230815s2023    mdu075|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33216  a(OCoLC)1397048653  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 40000aUnleashing the power of prevention to enhance well-being across the lifespan /cMargaret Kuklinski. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 15 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aIntegrative medicine research lecture  aRecorded May 25, 2023.3 a(CIT): How can we promote well-being in young people and prevent problems before they begin? It"s a crucial and timely question, and it has spurred study and discovery of risks to and protective factors for young people"s development. This area of research has also led to tested, effective preventive interventions showing better outcomes that in some cases have lasted for decades and across generations.  In her talk, Dr. Margaret Kuklinski will trace the evolution of prevention science and share the latest findings from longitudinal intervention studies based on the Social Development Strategy. Examples include Guiding Good Choices, a program for parents and caregivers to help them support positive development and the prevention of behavioral problems in their children, and Communities That Care, a prevention system. Dr. Kuklinski will also describe what is needed to increase the equitable reach and impact of effective health promotion and prevention approaches.  Dr. Kuklinski is the director of the University of Washington"s Social Development Research Group (SDRG), which has been developing, testing, and disseminating effective preventive interventions for over 40 years. Dr. Kuklinski"s and SDRG"s work has shown the enduring effects of preventive interventions based on the Social Development Strategy on a variety of health outcomes.  About the Series: This lecture is the first in the spring 2023 season of the Integrative Medicine Research Lecture Series. The series provides overviews of the current state of research and practice involving complementary health approaches and explores perspectives on integrative health. To find out more, visit nccih.nih.gov/news/events/imlectures. For more information go to <a href="https://www.nccih.nih.gov/">https://www.nccih.nih.gov/.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed September 7, 2023).12aPreventive Health Services12aMental Disordersxprevention & control22aAdolescent22aPsychological Well-Being.22aRisk Factors22aAdolescent Behavior 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aKuklinski, Margaret,espeaker.2 aNational Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNCCIH integrative medicine research lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49700  aAUTH02393cgm a2200421 i 101622743DNLM20260227154551.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u131227s2013    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18147  a(OCoLC)868259543  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 102.91 aLomvardas, Stavros,d1975-eauthor.10aMolecular mechanisms of olfactory receptor choice /cStavros Lomvardas. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.) :bcolor, sound).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Nov. 4, 2013.3 a(CIT): Neuroscience Seminar Series  Dr. Lomvardas Lab's research program aims to understand the molecular mechanisms of olfactory receptor gene choice. Olfactory receptor (OR) genes compose the largest mammalian gene family consisted of more than 1000 members. These genes are expressed in a stochastic and mutually exclusive fashion in such a way that only one OR allele is expressed in each olfactory sensory neuron. Their data suggest that this unusual expression pattern is orchestrated by a combination of positive and negative regulatory mechanism. According to their findings, OR genes undergo chromatin mediated silencing at a developmental stage that precedes olfactory receptor activation. One of the 3000 alleles is chosen for activation, by the enzymatic removal of the silencing chromatin marks. They currently combine genetic and biochemical approaches that together with cutting edge microscopy should reveal critical mechanistic details of this process.  aDescription based on version viewed January 2, 2014.12aOlfactory Receptor Neuronsxmetabolism22aReceptors, Odorantxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18147  aAUTH01445nam a2200337 i 9918787481006676DNLM20260224092054.0240402r19571957pau           100 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.702 aAmerican Association for the Study of Liver Diseases.bAnnual Meetingd(1956 :cChicago, Ill)eauthor.10aAbstracts of presentations at the Annual Meeting, Chicago, Illinois, November 8, 1956 :bSymposium, relationship of the liver to hormone function /cWilliam B. Bean, moderator.1 aSymposium, relationship of the liver to hormone function1 aAbstracts of meeting 1a[Philadelphia, Pennsylvania?] :b[publisher not identified],c[1957]  apages 258-264 ;c26 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  a"Reprinted from Gastroenterology, vol. 33, no. 2, August, 1957."  aNLM copy, bound 70th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM12aLiverxmetabolism12aLiver Diseasesxepidemiology12aLiver Diseasesxcomplications 2aConference Proceedings1 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,emoderator.0 aButterworths international medical reviews.  Gastroenterology.  aAUTH00971cam a22002657i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003500073044001000108060003200118245012200150250001400272264012300286300001600409336002600425337002800451338002700479490005100506650002400557650001000581710005700591830004800648999000900696101617846DNLM20260209150856.0130925s1952    cc           f000 0 chi    aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1805 no.700aYu fang er tong za