, col.).  aRecorded Jan. 2, 2002.3 a(CIT): The Immunology Interest Group (IIG) organizes activities designed to promote information exchange and interactions among NIH scientists interested in the field of immunology. Interactions are facilitated via weekly meetings on current topics as well as an annual Immunology Retreat.   For more information, visit the  Immunology Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aInterferon-gamma22aGene Expression Regulation 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11312  aAUTH02266cgm a2200469 i 9918663183606676DNLM20260227163940.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230815s2023    mdu062|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33300  a(OCoLC)1432352565  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aHV 238000aTechnologies and marketplace innovations to improve health care access and outcomes /cRobert M. Califf. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIDCD director's seminar series:  advancing the science of communication to improve lives  aRecorded June 28, 2023.3 a(CIT): FDA Commissioner Robert M. Califf, M.D., will discuss innovative strategies and technologies that move us toward more equitable and effective health care and public health. This will include the recent over-the-counter hearing aid final rule and the potential impact on hearing health innovation in the marketplace and access to hearing devices. Following Dr. Califf"s presentation, NIDCD Director Debara L. Tucci, M.D., M.S., M.B.A., will moderate a follow-up discussion with several NIH leaders.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 17, 2024).12aHealth Services for Persons with Disabilities12aPersons with Hearing Disabilities.22aHealth Services Accessibility22aOutcome Assessment, Health Care22aBiomedical Technology22aQuality Improvement 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCaliff, Robert M.,espeaker.2 aNational Institute on Deafness and Other Communication Disorders (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNIDCD director's seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49581  aAUTH01836cgm a2200445 i 101718223DNLM20260227155756.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u171212s2017    mdu070|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:23599  a(OCoLC)1019836963  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 10200aMicroglia in the retina :broles in health, aging, and disease /cWai T. Wong. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2017]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 10 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aSayer vision research lecture ;v10th  aRecorded November 17, 2017.3 a(CIT): 10th Sayer Vision Research Lecture  Dr. Wai T. Wong, Chief of NEI's Unit on Neuron-Glia Interactions in Retinal Disease, will describe efforts to understand how chronic neuroinflammation contributes to diseases of the retina, the light-sensitive tissue at the back of the eye.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 20, 2017).12aMicrogliaxphysiology12aRetinaxcytology22aRetinal Diseasesxphysiopathology22aSynaptic Transmissionxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aWong, Wai T.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aSayer vision research lecture ;v10th.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23599  aAUTH01814cgm a22004214a 101528396DNLM20260227153314.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100406s2010    mdu120|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15664  a(OCoLC)624405053  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 2701 aVolkow, Nora D.,d1956-10aDemystifying medicine.pDrug addiction :bmarihuana and stronger stuff /cNora Volkow, George Kunos.30aDrug addiction  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner.  aStreaming video (2 hr., 0 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 2, 2010.3 a(CIT): The course includes presentation of patients, pathology, diagnosis and therapy in the context of major disease problems and current research.  Primarily directed toward Ph.D. students, fellows, and staff, it is also of interest to medical students and clinicians.  The course is designed to help bridge the gap between advances in biology and their application to major human diseases.  Each session includes clinical and basic science components which are presented by NIH staff and outside invitees.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aSubstance-Related Disorders.22aBehavior, Addictive 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15664  aAUTH01812cgm a22004094a 101268804DNLM20260227151217.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2005    mdu070|||||||f||||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12694  a(OCoLC)166453355  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQS 6771 aKohn, Kurt W.12aA personal history of DNA damage and repair research, 1960-2005 /cKurt W. Kohn.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Aug. 27, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 10 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 14, 2005.3 a(CIT): The DNA Repair Interest Group is concerned with all forms of DNA damage and repair. As a major defense against environmental damage to cells DNA repair is present in all organisms examined including bacteria, yeast, drosophila, fish, amphibians, rodents and humans. The members of the DNA Repair Interest Group perform research in areas including DNA repair enzymology and fine structure, mutagenesis, gene and cell cycle regulation, protein structure, and human disease.   For more information, visit the  DNA Repair Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDNA Damage12aDNA Repair 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bDNA Repair Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12694  aAUTH01537cgm a22004334a 101268682DNLM20260227151211.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2005    mdu058 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12425  a(OCoLC)144567999  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWP 4801 aSchiffman, Mark,d1954-10aNew molecular epidemiologic data on HPV16 and cervical cancer :bstudies of a uniquely powerful carcinogen /cMark Schiffman.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 30, 2007).  aStreaming video (57 min., 24 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 28, 2005.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aUterine Cervical Neoplasms.22aHuman papillomavirus 1622aMolecular Epidemiology22aPapillomavirus Infections 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12425  aAUTH01453cas a2200421 i 9918227357006676DNLM20260227090910.0m     o  d    