?term=101704276  aAUTH02749cgm a2200445 i 101678409DNLM20260227155429.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160303s2016    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19478  a(OCoLC)946104013  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQT 2600aMove over, mice :bhow the fusion of system biology with "organs on chips" may humanize drug development /cLinda G. Griffith. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 0 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded February 10, 2016.3 a(CIT): Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series   "Mice are not little people," is a refrain that is becoming louder as the strengths and weaknesses of animal models of human disease become more apparent. At the same time, three emerging approaches are headed toward integration: powerful systems-biology analysis of cell-cell and intracellular signaling networks in patient-derived samples; 3D tissue-engineered models of human organ systems, often made from stem cells; and micro-fluidic and meso-fluidic devices that enable living systems to be sustained, perturbed, and analyzed for weeks in culture. Dr. Griffith's lecture will highlight how the integration of these rapidly moving fields is leading to a better understanding of difficult clinical problems, with an emphasis on translating academic discoveries into practical, widespread use. These technologies may have broad implications for regenerative medicine as they may foster the development of therapies that slow or halt the progression of diseases that lead to organ or tissue failure.    For more information go to <a href='https://oir.nih.gov/wals'>https://oir.nih.gov/wals.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 4, 2016).12aLab-On-A-Chip Devices22aSystems Biologyxmethods22aTissue Engineeringxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aGriffith, Linda G.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19478  aAUTH01698cas a22004934a 100896471DNLM20260227091101.0m        d        cr|cnu||||||||990917c19969999vaumr p       0   a0eng    asn 96003531 0 a1092-0684l1092-068421  a(OCoLC)36102377  aNSDcNSDdOCLdDNLM  ansdpalcdanlmcopyc  9United States00aW110a6172120 aNeurosurg. focus10aNeurosurg Focus2dnlm 0aNeurosurgical focus00aNeurosurgical focus.1 iIssues for <Apr. 2004-> called also:aNSF  aCharlottesville, VA :bAmerican Association of Neurological Surgeons,cc1996-  aMonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (July 1996)-  aTitle from title screen (viewed Jan. 25, 2000).  aA preview forum for select articles for the Journal of Neurosurgery.  aLatest issue consulted: Vo. 16, issue 4 (Apr. 2004).1 aMEDLINEbv8n1, Jan. 2000-1 aPubMedbv8n1, Jan. 2000-1 aIndex medicus  aVol. 1, no. 1 preceded by a preview issue called: Apr. 1996.  aHypertext with links to text and non-text files  aMode of access: World Wide Web.12aNeurosurgery 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Association of Neurological Surgeons.40uhttps://thejns.org/focus/view/journals/neurosurg-focus/neurosurg-focus-overview.xml  aAUTH01391cas a2200445 a 101468876DNLM20260227090916.0m        |        cr|cn ||||||||080317c20089999enk|| pss     |    0eng    a  2009243142  a1756-6606l1756-6606  a(OCoLC)233191053  aYAMcYAMdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9England10aW110aMol Brain2dnlm00aMolecular brain.  aLondon :bBioMed Central  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with 1:1