41224s2014    mdu455|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18756  a(OCoLC)902835059  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWJ 3022 aToward Building Better Biomarkers Statistical Methodology (Meeting)d(2014 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aToward Building Better Biomarkers Statistical Methodology /cNIDDK, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (7 hr., 35 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Dec. 2, 2014.3 a(CIT): There is concern regarding the clinical utility of biomarkers in kidney disease (Acute Kidney Injury [AKI], Chronic Kidney Disease [CKD], various forms of glomerulonephritis [GN], and polycystic kidney disease [PKD]) for prediction of diverse clinical outcomes (such as loss of renal function, development of cardiovascular disease, diminution of quality of life, death), as well as in drug development (for refinement of risk stratification or drug response).   There is dissatisfaction with the results of published biomarker studies and the lack of uptake of biomarkers in clinical practice in patients with kidney disease.  Finally, we don"t know the answers to several key questions. Some of these are listed below.   "Of what use is this biomarker to me or my patient in the clinic?"  Has "context of use" or "fit purpose" been identified, or do the present studies evaluate biomarkers on convenience samples? How can we deal with the paradox of well-established but crude measures which biomarkers must enhance?  Can we develop markers for kidney disease that are superior to S[Cr] and proteinuria?   Does the Framingham study provide a better framework for the evaluation of biomarkers - or do biomarker studies have to be designed de novo for each context?   How does biomarker science allow us to move from a population focus to individual considerations:  the patient and the physician in the consultation room (or at the bedside) -- considering a distinct outcome?   Can we as biomarker scientists develop physician / patient perspectives - assessing risk for the individual, or outcome for the individual with drug therapy?  Statistical Considerations   Have we developed a true set of biomarker statistical analyses? Or are we fitting old techniques to new contexts/issues?  What does a biomarker showing independent association with a distant outcome in a Cox regression mean? What are the key elements of design for a meaningful biomarker study?   What metrics are appropriate for assessing incremental value of biomarkers?  What does "prediction" mean?   Is "prediction" different for present measures (e.g., eGFR) compared to future outcomes (risk of future decrement in renal function, need for ESRD treatment, or death)? How can we make predictive models in kidney disease patients more precise?   Can causal links between biomarker levels or changes identified by present statistical methods be strengthened?   This workshop will grapple with these thorny questions.  Breakout groups, composed of biostatisticians and clinical nephrologists, will consider the issues and put forth approaches for moving forward.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed January 28, 2015.12aBiomarkers12aKidney Diseasesxdiagnosis22aKidney Diseasesxmetabolism22aModels, Statistical 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18756  aAUTH02041cgm a2200445 i 9918434187406676DNLM20260227162432.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220706s2022    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32427  a(OCoLC)1344417351  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 168.5.C800aSARS-CoV-2 :bwhat we have learned so far /cYoshihiro Kawaoka. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 0 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCOVID-19 lecture  aRecorded May 26, 2022.3 a(CIT): COVID-19 Scientific Interest Group   Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2), which is responsible for COVID-19, continues to spread around the world and has caused millions of deaths to date. In an effort to develop therapeutics and preventive measures, we are performing numerous research projects with this virus and its variants. In this presentation, I will discuss our findings regarding animal models and their value as tools for evaluating countermeasures against SARS-CoV-2.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed September 8, 2022).12aSARS-CoV-2xpathogenicity22aDisease Models, Animal22aDrug Discovery22aCOVID-19xvirology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aKawaoka, Yoshihiro,d1955-espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bCOVID-19 Scientific Interest Group,esponsoring body. 0aNIH COVID-19 SIG lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45286  aAUTH01307cas a2200421 a 101304861DNLM20260227090922.0cr|cn ||||||||070112c20079999nyu x p       0    0eng    a  20072158400 a1935-472Xl1935-472X  a(OCoLC)77706455  aNSDcNSDdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States10aISSN RECORD00aW1bGE281M00aGenetic engineering & biotechnology news.3 aGenetic engineering and biotechnology news13aGEN  aNew Rochelle, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.  av.  aTwenty-one issues a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 27, no. 4 (Feb. 15, 2007).  aDescription based on first issue; title from caption.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 27, no. 6 (Mar. 15, 2007).  aAlso issued online.12aBiotechnology12aGenetic Engineering 2aPeriodical0 x1937-866100tGenetic engineering newsx0270-6377w(DLC)   91642086w(OCoLC)64633374 uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/gen  aAUTH01402cgm a2200421 i 101622752DNLM20260227155004.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u131227s2013    mdu105|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18163  a(OCoLC)871189069  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWP 4801 aSchiller, John T.eauthor.10aTRACO.pCervical cancer - epigenetics /cJ. Schiller, M. Verma.1 aCervical cancer - epigenetics 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 45 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Nov. 4, 2013.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed Feb. 5, 2014.12aUterine Cervical Neoplasmsxgenetics22aEpigenesis, Genetic 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aVerma, Mukesh,eauthor.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18163  aAUTH01331cas a2200421 a 101622604DNLM20260227090924.0m     o  d        cr |||||||||||130710c20129999gw || poo     |    0eng    a  2014243241  a2213-5979l2213-5979  a(OCoLC)852377763  aHNKcHNKdDNLM  apcc  9Germany10aW1  aTA1770b.P5610aPhotoacoustics2dnlm00aPhotoacoustics.13aPACS  aMunich :bElsevier GmbH  aFour issues a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with vol. 1, issue 1 (Mar. 2013)0 aPubMedbv1n1, 2013-0 aPMC  aSome articles in Vol. 1 (2013) published electronically in 2012.  aDescription based on: Vol. 1, issue (Mar. 2013); title from journal home page (ScienceDirect, viewed July 10, 2013)  aLatest issue consulted: 2 (2014) (viewed Mar. 4, 2014).12aAcoustics12aOptical Imaging40uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/2213597940uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101622604  aAUTH02474cgm a22004574a 101465458DNLM20260227152336.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080201s2008    mdu223 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14246  a(OCoLC)277018140  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQV 75200aDrug discovery :bis nature the answer? /c[Alice M. Clark ... [et al.]].  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aScience in the public health  aTitle from title screen (viewed Oct. 28, 2008).  aStreaming video (3 hr., 42 min., 40 sec. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded Jan. 17, 2008.3 a(CIT): The promise of modern chemistry to supply a pipeline of new drugs to combat life-threatening illnesses and improve public health has not met with its anticipated success. Drug-resistant infectious agents are on the rise. Effective therapies for cancer, diabetes, obesity, heart disease and many other ailments have not kept pace with the needs of the public.   Re-enter nature as an inspiration and source for new drugs. Natural products have made a comeback in recent years as researchers turn to the rich diversity of marine and plant life. But what are the scientific, technical, ethical, legal and economic issues in advancing a natural product from discovery to the marketplace?  Please join us for a forum highlighting recent advances and challenges in exploring planet Earth for compounds that comprise nature's unique and unparalleled molecular library.  Committee Members:  Chair: Christian Ketchum  Members: Martha Hare, Eduardo Montalvo, Nancy Pearson, Linda Porter, Larry Yager.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDrug Design12aPharmacognosy22aBiological Products 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aClark, Alice M.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bStaff Training-Extramural Programs Committee. 0aScience in the public health40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14246  aAUTH02861cgm a22004334a 101465448DNLM20260227152311.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080201s2008    mdu061|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14264  a(OCoLC)421349986  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 571 aZagotta, William N.10aMolecular mechanism of gating in cyclic nucleotide-gated ion channels /cWilliam N. Zagotta.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Apr. 22, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 1 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 28, 2008.3 a(CIT): Cyclic nucleotide-regulated channels, such as CNG channels and HCN channels, underlie the primary electrical signals in phototransduction and olfactory transduction and control the repetitive firing activity of cardiac and neuronal cells. Activation of these channels involves the direct binding of cyclic nucleotide to an intracellular domain of the channel. Dr. Zagotta's lab has been interested in understanding the molecular mechanism for this allostery. To do this, they have employed a number of fluorescence approaches combined with patch-clamp fluorometry, a technique for simultaneously recording electrical current and fluorescence from excised inside-out patches. They explored the structure and conformational rearrangements of the C-terminal gating ring of the cyclic nucleotide-gated channel CNGA1 during activation by cyclic nucleotides. By monitoring fluorescent resonance energy transfer (FRET) between membrane-resident quenchers and fluorophores attached to the channel, Dr. Zagotta's lab detected no movement orthogonal to the membrane during channel activation. By monitoring FRET between fluorophores within the C-terminal region, they determined that the C-terminal end of the C-linker and the end of the C-helix move apart when channels open. They conclude that during channel activation, a portion of the gating ring moves parallel to the plasma membrane, hinging toward the central axis of the channel.   NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aIon Channel Gatingxgenetics12aNucleotides, Cyclicxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14264  aAUTH01519cas a2200457 i 101676532DNLM20260227090909.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||160209c20159999gw || poo     |    0eng    a  2016247765  a2198-4034l2198-4034  a(OCoLC)939979108  aPFMbengerdad20260227cPFMdPFMdOCLCOdDNLM  apcc  9Germany00aW110aInt J Implant Dent2dnlm00aInternational journal of implant dentistry. 1aHeidelberg :bSpringer,c[2015]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with (2015) 1:1.1 aMEDLINEbv7, 2021-0 aPubMedbv1, 2015-0 aPMC  aArticles added as received to annual volume in progress.  aDescription based on: (2015) 1:1; title from journal homepage (Springer Link website, viewed Februray 19, 2016).  aLatest issue consulted: (2016) 2:5 (viewed Feb. 23, 2016).12aDental Implantation12aDentistry22aDental Implants 2aPeriodical2 aDeutsche Gesellschaft für Implantologie.2 aNihon Kōkū Inpuranto Gakkai.40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=10167653240uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/40729  aAUTH01561cam a2200373 i 101677056DNLM20260226124236.0851015s1906    paua          001 0 eng    a   06035474   a(OCoLC)12677915  aDLCbengcKUKdKUKdUBAdOCLCGdOCLCFdOCLCOdOCLCQdOCLCOerdadDNLM1 aenghger  anlmcopyc  9United States00aQD83b.M5200aQDbM489k 19061 aMedicus, Ludwig,d1847-19