iannual journal of research devoted to helminthology and all branches of parasitology."  aAlso issued online.12aParasitic Diseases22aHelminths22aParasites22aParasitology 2aPeriodical2 aHelminthological Society of Washington.0 x1938-295200tJournal of the Helminthological Society of Washingtonx1049-233Xw(DLC)   90641458w(OCoLC)211712144 uhttp://www.bioone.org/loi/copa  aAUTH02722cgm a2200517 i 101774302DNLM20260227160530.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu056|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31421  a(OCoLC)1240391731  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWZ 80.5.W500aRise, Serve, Lead! America's Women Physicians /cAshley Bowen ; NLM History of Medicine.1 aRise, service, lead and publish :bincluding women physicians' writings in Rise, Serve, Lead : America's Women Physicians 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (56 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNLM History of Medicine  aRecorded December 3, 2020.3 a(CIT): NLM History of Medicine  In this lecture, the curator of Rise, Serve, Lead, Dr. Ashley Bowen, will discuss the lives and accomplishments of three very different women featured in the exhibition-- Dr. Rebecca Crumpler, Dr. Helen Rodriguez-Triás, and Dr. Frances Conley-- and then discuss how the National Library of Medicine's collections shaped the exhibition. Each of these remarkable physicians contributed to the medical literature during her lifetime and many are represented in both the general and historical collections at the National Library of Medicine as well as in PubMed Central. She will reflect on how connecting each woman's biography to her published work shaped her thinking about the featured doctors as well as how she understood their impact on the field.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 23, 2021).12aCrumpler, Rebecca Lee,d1831-1895.12aRodríguez-Triás, Helen.12aConley, Frances K.22aRise, Serve, Lead! America's Women Physicians (Exhibition)d(2019 :cOnline)12aPhysicians, Womenxhistory22aMedical Writingxhistory22aExhibitions as Topic 2aUnited States 2aBiography 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aBowen, Ashley Elizabeth,espeaker.2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.).bHistory of Medicine Division,eissuing body. 0aHistory of Medicine seminar40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=35404  aAUTH02600cgm a2200421 i 101684407DNLM20260227155434.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160601s2016    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19699  a(OCoLC)956966069  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQV 74500aHow far can we trust our data? :bthe interpretation, value and use of drug discovery data and the need for a bigger data approach /cTerry Stouch, Ph.D., President of Science For Solutions, LLC. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aFrontiers in data science  aRecorded May 20, 2016.3 a(CIT): NIH Frontiers in Data Science Lecture Series. How reliable is our drug discovery data? Is it qualitative or quantitative? Is it a hard and fast signpost to direct our efforts, or just a hint of what might be? In fact, the percentage of drug discovery data in which we can have high confidence is surprisingly low. And the common range of "true" error in any measurement is many time larger than the conventional error of measurement.  This presentation will address the author's 3-to-10 and 20% rules that capsulize the true error of drug discovery data, the percentage that can be used with high confidence, and how these rules were determined. Examples of sometimes surprising confounding factors from the experience of many laboratories and companies around the world will be highlighted and lead to a discussion of the need for a "bigger" data of drug discovery that can help improve our understanding and increase the value of our results. The importance of data presentation leading to proper interpretation by users will also be discussed.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 5, 2016).12aDrug Discovery12aReproducibility of Results 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aStouch, Terry,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH frontiers in data science series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19699  aAUTH00934cam a22003017i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002100073040003500094041001300129044001000142060003200152100003700184240003600221245007800257250001300335264005000348300003500398336002600433337002800459338002700487650004100514650002900555650002200584655001700606999000900623101616036DNLM20260209145017.0130828s1948    cc a          000 0 chi    a(OCoLC)872630480  aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM1 achiheng  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1804 no.21 aNovak, Emil,d1883-1957eauthor.10aWoman asks the doctor.lChinese10aNü xing sheng li yu bing tai /cNuowake yuan zhu ; Fan Cunheng bian yi.  aChu ban. 1aShanghai :bJia za zhi she,cMinguo 37 [1948]  aii, 100 pages :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier12aReproductive Physiological Phenomena22aGenital Diseases, Female22aGenitalia, Female 2aPopular Work  aAUTH02248cgm a2200457 i 9918282193306676DNLM20260227161523.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u211008s2021    mdu185|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31923  a(DNLM)CIT:31931  a(OCoLC)1322362997  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 26.52 aNIH Virtual Workshop on Broadening Cloud Computing Usage in Biomedical Researchd(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNIH virtual workshop on broadening cloud computing usage in biomedical research /cOffice of Data Science Strategy, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c2021.  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (3 hr., 5 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 13 & 14, 2021.3 a(CIT): To gain insight into barriers toward equitable access to cloud resources, ODSS, NIGMS, NIMHD, CIT will hold a virtual workshop to bring students, faculty, and researchers from universities and colleges within the IDeA-eligible states, Research Centers in Minority Institutions (RCMIs), and Minority Serving Institutions (MSIs), and representatives from funding agencies together to explore the needs, challenges and opportunities of cloud computing in these institutions.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 26, 2022).12aCloud Computing22aBiomedical Research22aBiomedical Technologyxmethods22aMedical Informatics Applications 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Data Science Strategy,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4260740uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42609  aAUTH01840cgm a2200469 i 101774271DNLM20260227160412.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu068|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31377  a(OCoLC)1229081318  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWE 25000aMelorheostosis :btwo forms, two genes with distinct histology and mechanism along one signaling pathway /cJoan Marini. Uncovering the natural history and the molecular bases of Ollier disease and Maffucci syndrome / Nara Sobreira. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 8 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCC grand rounds  aRecorded November 4, 2020.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed January 5, 2021).12aMelorheostosisxgenetics22aSignal Transduction22aEnchondromatosisxhistory22aEnchondromatosisxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aMarini, Joan C.,espeaker.1 aSobreira, Nara,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center,esponsoring body.0 aMelorheostosis.0 aUncovering the natural history and the molecular bases of Ollier disease and Maffucci syndrome. 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38950  aAUTH01215cas a22003974a 101208441DNLM20260227090914.0cr|cnu||||||||040301c20049999ts || p       0    0eng    a  2004243588  a1567-2050l1567-2050  a(OCoLC)56189333  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9United Arab Emirates00aW1bCU684S10aCurr Alzheimer Res2dnlm00aCurrent Alzheimer research.  aSaif Zone, Sharjah, U.A.E. ;aSan Francisco, CA :bBentham Science Publishers,cc2004-  av. :bill.  aFourteen no. a year,b2018-  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Feb. 2004)-  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 15, issue 7 (2018).1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, Feb. 2004-0 aPubMedbv1n1, Feb. 2004-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.12aAlzheimer Disease 2aPeriodical0 x1875-58284 uhttp://www.eurekaselect.com/643/journal/current-alzheimer-research  aAUTH03662cgm a2200445 i 101673101DNLM20260227155203.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u151203s2015    mdu621 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19315  a(DNLM)CIT:19316  a(OCoLC)936194477  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 1002 aClinical Trial Designs for Emerging Infectious Diseases (Workshop)d(2015 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aClinical trial designs for emerging infectious diseases /cFDA. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video file (10 hr., 21 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 9 & 10, 2015.3 a(CIT): The Ebola Virus Disease (EVD) epidemic has highlighted the importance of being able to rapidly evaluate investigational products during a public health emergency, including in resource limited settings. It is generally agreed that access to early investigational interventions that have not yet been well characterized should be provided in a setting that allows for the reliable assessment of benefits and harms.  Ascertaining the safety and efficacy of an investigational product is an important step that is necessary to protect and promote the public health now and in the future. A critical aspect of the response effort is the ability to rapidly establish and conduct clinical trials that are both scientifically valid and ethically appropriate. During the course of the EVD epidemic, there has been significant debate over the design of trials to evaluate investigational therapies and vaccines. Among the trial designs employed during the EVD epidemic to evaluate experimental therapeutics were (1) open-label product administration compared to historical controls, and (2) randomized open-label product administration compared to concurrent "standard of care" controls. Among the proposed trial designs for EVD vaccines are (1) randomized controlled trials, (2) clustered randomized trials, (3) stepped wedge trials, and (4) ring vaccination trials. Trials to support alternative licensure pathways under FDA regulations, such as accelerated approval or the animal rule, have also been proposed. Advocates cited a range of scientific, ethical and practical concerns for and/or against each of these (and other) trial designs for investigational products. Based on our experience, positive and negative,  in designing and conducting clinical trials for EVD, it is important to bring the various stakeholders together to explore the lessons learned, and how we may be better prepared to deploy investigational products in the setting of the next epidemic of an emerging infectious disease (EID).  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed January 25, 2016).12aClinical Trials as Topic12aCommunicable Diseases, Emergingxdrug therapy22aHemorrhagic Fever, Ebolaxdrug therapy22aTherapies, Investigational 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aUnited States.bFood and Drug Administration,esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1931540uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19316  aAUTH01818cgm a2200445 i 101774311DNLM20260227160546.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu099|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31433  a(OCoLC)1239646419  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 506.52 aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19d(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19 breakout session 3 (pulmonary) /cNIAID, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 39 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded December 4, 2020.3 a(CIT): Breakout Session 3. This session will focus on the pulmonary perspective of post-acute sequelae of COVID-19.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 24, 2021).12aCOVID-19xcomplications22aCOVID-19xtherapy22aLung Injuryxvirology22aLung Injuryxprevention & control22aPandemics 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (U.S.),eissuing body.2 iAugmentation of (work):aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19d(2020 :cOnline).tWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38884  aAUTH01670cgm a22004574a 101525626DNLM20260227153543.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100302s2010    mdu074|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15585  a(OCoLC)605752298  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWN 1851 aGore, John C.10aOpportunities and challenges of high field MRI & MRS /cJohn C. Gore.13aOpportunities and challenges of high field and MRS  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 a7T seminar series  aTitle from PowerPoint screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 14 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 29, 2010.3 a(CIT): John Gore is Director of the Vanderbilt University Institute of Imaging Science and an authority on what 7T can do with regard to human studies. His group has generated some of the more impressive data seen to date.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMagnetic Resonance Imagingxmethods22aBiomedical Researchxmethods22aMagnetic Resonance Spectroscopyxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0a7T seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15585  aAUTH03464cgm a2200445 a 101601862DNLM20260227154513.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130208s2013    mdu067|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17762  a(OCoLC)828715808  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3001 aShatz, Carla J.10aCircuit tuning during developmental critical periods :bimmune genes moonlighting in the brain /cCarla J. Shatz.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 7 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aAccess restricted to HHS and NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Jan. 23, 2013.3 a(CIT): Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series  Connections in the adult brain are highly precise, but they do not start out that way. Precision emerges during development as synaptic connections remodel in a process requiring neural activity (action potentials and synaptic transmission). This activity also regulates neuronal gene expression. In an unbiased screen, major histocompatibility class I (MHCI) genes were unexpectedly discovered to be in neurons and regulated by activity and visual experience (Neuron, 21:505-520, 1998; PNAS 104:6828-6833, 2007). To assess requirements for MHCI in the central nervous system (CNS), we examined mutant mice lacking stable surface expression of all MHCI, or specific MHCI genes. Synapse regression in the developing visual system failed, and ocular dominance (OD) plasticity in the visual cortex is greater than in wild type mice (Science 290:2155-2159, 2000; Neuron 64:463-70, 2009). In a search for receptors that could interact with neuronal MHCI, we found that the innate immune receptor PirB was highly expressed in neurons throughout the mouse CNS. In mutant mice lacking PirB, OD plasticity is increased as is the long-term potentiation in the hippocampus (Science 313:1795- 1800,2006). Thus, PirB, like MHCI, appears to brake synaptic plasticity. Moreover, the commonality of phenotypes present in these mice suggests a model in which PirB may bind and transduce signals from the MHCI ligands in neurons (PNAS 106:6784-6789, 2009). Together, the results imply that this family of molecules, thought previously to function only in immunity, may also act at neuronal synapses to limit how much or how quickly synapse strength changes in response to a new experience. These molecules may be crucial for controlling circuit excitability and stability in the developing as well as in the adult brain. Changes in their function could contribute to developmental disorders such as autism and schizophrenia.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aBrainxgrowth & development22aBrain Chemistryxgenetics22aGene Regulatory Networksxgenetics22aSignal Transductionxgenetics 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17762  aAUTH01324cas a2200421 i 101657494DNLM20260227090909.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||141217c20149999enkuu poo     0    0eng    a  2015243216  a2214-1391l2214-1391  a(OCoLC)898215323  aOH1bengerdad20260227cOH1dDNLM  apcc  9England00aW110aInt J Afr Nurs Sci2dnlm00aInternational journal of Africa nursing sciences.13aIJANS30aAfrica nursing sciences 1a[London] :bElsevier Ltd.,c[2014]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with volume 1 (2014).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aDescription based on: Volume 1 (2014); title from journal information screen (ScienceDirect, viewed December 17, 2014).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 2 (2015) (viewed May 7, 2015).12aNursing22aNurses22aStudents, Nursing22aMidwifery 2aAfrica40uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/22141391  aAUTH01281cas a2200385 a 101616706DNLM20260227090911.0m     o  d        cr#|||||||||||130722c20129999gw || poo     0    0eng    a  2013243558  a2277-9167l2277-9167  a(OCoLC)853461271  aHUAcHUAdOCLCOdDNLM  apcc  9Germany10aW110aIndian J Neurosurg2dnlm00aIndian journal of neurosurgery.1 aIJNS  aMumbai :bMedknow Publications and Media Pvt. Ltd.3132015- :aStuttgart :bThieme  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1, no. 1 (Jan./Mar. 2012).  aDescription based on: Vol. 1, no. 1 (Jan./Mar. 2012); title from journal home page (viewed Jan. 25, 2013)  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 2, issue 2 (May/Aug. 2013) (viewed Sept. 30, 2013).12aNeurosurgery 2aPeriodical2 aNeurological Surgeons Society of India.0 x2277-954X40uhttps://www.thieme-connect.com/products/ejournals/journal/10.1055/s-00028728  aAUTH01149cam a2200313 i 450000100100000000300050001000500170001500800410003203500200007304000350009304400180012806000260014624500300017224600210020226400710022330000220029433600260031633700280034233800270037050000430039754001420044058300390058258300720062165000290069365000220072265000240074471000580076899900090082650610020RDNLM20260212101708.0811121s19uu    mau           000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)14805918  aDNLMbengerdad20260211dDNLM  9United States00aWU 28 AM4bF735f 191500aForsyth interne's manual.1 aInterne's manual 1a[Boston, Mass.?] :b[publisher not identified],c[not before 1915]  a58 pages ;c21 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aBlank pages at rear for lecture notes.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20260212lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM12aInternship and Residency22aDental Facilities22aPediatric Dentistry2 aForsyth Dental Infirmary for Children,eissuing body.  aAUTH01361cam a2200373 i 9918787485906676DNLM20260212175912.0240325r19551955xxua     b    000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)1552758137  aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM0 aengaspa  apcc  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.311 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.14aThe syndrome of carcinoid and acquired valve lesions of the right side of the heart /cby William B. Bean, David Olch, and Harry B. Weinberg.1 aCarcinoid and acquired valve lesions 1a[United States] :b[publisher not identified],c[1955]  a6 pages :billustrations ;c28 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  a"Reprinted from Circulation, vol. 12, no. 1, July 1955."  aNLM copy, bound 31st in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM  aIncludes bibliographical references.  aIn English, with conclusion also in Spanish.12aCarcinoid Heart Disease 2aCase Reports1 aOlch, David,eauthor.1 aWeinberg, Harry B.,eauthor.0 aCirculation.  aAUTH03944cgm a2200493 a 101585227DNLM20260227154138.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120525s2012    mdu070|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17208  a(OCoLC)799010471  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 168.5.R181 aGoff, Stephen.10aRetroviral restriction factors :bZAP, ZFP809, and new aspects of innate immunity /cStephen Goff.1 iTitle on PowerPoint screen:aSilencing of retrovirus expression in embryonic stem cells, and A new retroelement in the mollusc Mya arenaria  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 10 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded Apr. 11, 2012.3 a(CIT): Two disparate topics will be presented. 1. Silencing of Retrovirus Expression in Embryonic Stem (ES) Cells It has long been known that ES cells potently block provirus expression via a trans-acting factor that binds to the primer binding site (PBS) for proline tRNA on the murine leukemia virus genome. We purified the silencing complex and identified TRIM28 (Kap-1), a known transcriptional silencer, as an integral component of the complex. Further, we have identified the DNA sequence-specific binding component of the complex as being ZFP809, one of the dozens of zinc finger proteins encoded in the mammalian genome. We show that expression of ZFP809 is sufficient to render even differentiated cells highly resistant to MLV infection. We found that similar silencing occurs at the distinct PBS for at least one other tRNA (namely, Lys1,2) utilized by such viruses as visna and spuma. The protein complex that mediates this silencing is large: at least two more components have been implicated, HP1gamma and EBP-1, and more are likely to be found. We have also begun characterizing a non-PBS directed silencing complex acting more broadly on incoming DNAs in ES cells.  2. A New Retroelement in the Mollusc Mya arenaria Over the past thirty years the soft-shell clam has been subject to a neoplastic disease of rapidly increasing prevalence, variously known as "disseminated neoplasia" (DN) or "haemic neoplasia" (HN). The beds in many locations in the Northeast Atlantic have been decimated by the disease, and the incidence in affected areas can range from 10% to as high as 900f the animals. The disease is similar in many ways to mammalian leukemia, with a huge expansion of blast-like cells in the hemolymph with high mitotic index. The etiology of the disease is mysterious; suggestions have included both environmental pollution and infectious agents. We have discovered that the hemolymph of the diseased animals often contain high levels of extracellular reverse transcriptase. Deep sequencing of RNA preparations from cell-free hemolymph has revealed a novel retroelement with sequence similarity to those of the mammalian retroviruses. We will report our progress in characterizing the element.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aRetroviridaexgenetics22aBivalvia22aCell Transformation, Viralximmunology22aEmbryonic Stem Cellsxvirology22aGene Silencing22aLeukemia Virus, Murinexgenetics22aRetroelementsxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17208  aAUTH01035nam a2200289 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400025000800440018001050600024001232450040001472640065001873000027002523360026002793370028003053380027003335000098003605010060004585040041005186500021005596550016005807000071005967100034006677300035007019990009007369918787481406676DNLM20260224080305.0240401r19571957ilu      b    000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.6900aBooks /cedited by William B. Bean. 1a[Chicago, Illinois] :bAmerican Medical Association,c[1957]  apages 513-516 ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"Reprinted from A.M.A. Archives of internal medicine, September 1957, vol. 100, pp. 513-516."  aNLM copy, bound 69th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aMedical Writing. 2aBook Review1 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eeditor,eauthor.2 aAmerican Medical Association.0 aArchives of internal medicine.  aAUTH00878cam a2200277 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390350022000800400035001020440018001370600024001551000062001792450041002412640060002823000021003423360026003633370028003893380026004175000019004435000016004625010060004786500033005386500020005719990009005919918787486306676DNLM20260223082824.0240325s1955    xxu           000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)1542998457  aDNLMbengerdad20260223dDNLM  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.241 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aScurvy /cmethod of William B. Bean. 1a[United States] :b[publisher not identified],c[1955?]  a1 sheet ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  asheetbnb2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  a"Page 302."  aNLM copy, bound 24th in a vo