 Society.bShanghai Brancheissuing body.  aAUTH02276cgm a2200457 i 101609766DNLM20260227154656.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130604s2013    mdu062|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17921  a(OCoLC)851144990  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQV 77.71 aHarkany, Tibor,eauthor.10aMolecular dissection of cannabis sensitivity in the developing brain /cTibor Harkany. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 2 min.) :bcolor, sound).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdm2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Apr. 29, 2013.3 a(CIT): The Harkany lab focuses on understanding the developmental organization and functions of the endocannabinoid system by using a combination of molecular genetics, neuroimaging, neuroanatomy and electrophysiology approaches. Besides focusing on physiological developmental programs controlled by endocannabinoid signaling during corticogenesis, substantial resources are dedicated to elucidating the neuronal basis of cannabis-induced developmental deficits in the central nervous system. Through their studies, they hope to identify organizing principles of endocannabinoid signaling in the developing brain with particular emphasis on the roles of endocannabinoids in establishing neuronal diversity and connectivity patterns.  aDescription based on version viewed June 17, 2013.12aCannabinoidsxpharmacology22aBrainxdrug effects22aBrainxgrowth & development22aNeurogenesisxdrug effects22aReceptors, Cannabinoidxmetabolism 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17921  aAUTH01030nam a2200277 i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003100073044001800104060003300122