sium 2021 /cNIH Library.1 iTitle on Power Point screen:aBioinformatics & Computational Biology Symposium 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (3 streaming video files (6 hr., 11 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aMorning session : multi-omics -- Afternoon session track 1 : single cell sequencing -- Afternoon session track 2 : genomics.  aAccess restricted to HHS only.  aRecorded September 9, 2021.3 a(CIT): Presented by the NIH Library, the Bioinformatics and Computational Biology Symposium 2021 will feature keynote lectures, presentations on bioinformatics techniques, and research talks from authors of bioinformatics proceedings. The morning session will focus on epigenomics, protein structures, and proteomics. The two concurrent afternoon sessions will feature genomics and single-cell sequencing topics.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed October 1, 2021).12aComputational Biology22aMedical Informatics22aEpigenomics22aProteomics 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bLibrary,epresenter,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4264040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4269140uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42692  aAUTH02370cgm a2200457 i 9918573277506676DNLM20260227162847.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2022    mdu088|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32738  a(OCoLC)1428107697  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 300 AA12 aGender and Health Workshop Concurrent Session: Modifiable Factors & Social Determinants of Healthd(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aGender and health workshop concurrent session :bmodifiable factors & social determinants of health /cNIH, ORWH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 28 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 26, 2022.3 a(CIT): In recognition of the significant health impacts of gender as a social and cultural variable, the NIH ORWH and the Eunice Kennedy Shriver NICHD convened a series of roundtable discussions for NIH staff members in the fall of 2021. Participants discussed the landscape of NIH-supported research on gender roles, gender norms, and gender inequity and identified opportunities in this space.  This scientific workshop is an exciting opportunity to convene the extramural community, NIH scientific and program staff members, and other stakeholders to discuss methods and best practices for biomedical and sociobehavioral research on gender roles, gender norms, and gender inequity.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 1, 2024).12aGender Equity12aHealth Inequities22aXenophobia22aEmigration and Immigration22aSexism22aSocial Determinants of Health 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Research on Women's Health,eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=46412  aAUTH01950cgm a22004574a 101289596DNLM20260227151414.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2006    mdu057|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13032  a(OCoLC)84671850  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWB 601 aMishkin, Barbara.10aResearch subjects engaged in illegal behavior :bhow should clinicians respond? /cBarbara Mishkin and Julie Kohn.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aClinical Center grand rounds  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 13, 2006).  aStreaming video (57 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 1, 2006.3 a(CIT): NIH Clinical Center Grand Rounds Ethics Rounds: How should clinicians respond when research subjects engage in illegal behavior? This program involves presentation of an actual case from the NIH followed by discussion of the ethical issues it raises. The program should be of interest to all those involved in clinical research, as well as individuals interested in important issues in bioethics.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aEthics, Clinical22aClinical Trials as Topicxethics22aClinical Trials as Topicxlegislation & jurisprudence22aResearch Subjectsxlegislation & jurisprudence 2aUnited States 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center. 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13032  aAUTH01411cas a2200409 i 101645472DNLM20260227090924.0cr||||||||||||131118c20149999dcuqr p       0    0eng    a  20132740930 a2332-213621  a(OCoLC)863156655  aDLCerdad20260227bengdDLCdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW1bTR75810aTransl Issues Psychol Sci2dnlm00aTranslational issues in psychological science.1 aTPS 1aWashington, DC :bEducational Publishing Foundation of the American Psychological Assocation  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1, issue S (Aug. 2014).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aEach issue concentrates on a single theme.  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: Volume 2, number 1 (March 2016); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 2, number 1 (March 2016).12aPsychology 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Psychological Association of Graduate Students,esponsoring body.0 x2332-21794 uhttp://search.ebscohost.com/direct.asp?db=pdh&jid=TPS&scope=site  aAUTH02490cgm a2200457 i 101662993DNLM20260227155105.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150625s2015    mdu864 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19053  a(DNLM)CIT:19043  a(OCoLC)915153075  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 5182 aRSV Vaccine Workshopd(2015 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aRSV Vaccine Workshop /cNIH/FDA. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (14 hr., 24 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 1 & 2, 2015.3 a(CIT): The purpose of the workshop is to identify obstacles to RSV vaccine development, discuss approaches to alleviating them, and identify gaps in research that could be addressed to enable vaccine development. The meeting will also review the basic science and clinical data that will inform the regulation of products under development including, for example, immune responses to RSV primary infection and reinfection, and the physiology and kinetics of placental antibody transfer as well as discuss progress towards standardization of immunologic agents and assays.  Lessons learned from previous clinical trials of RSV vaccines, and polyclonal and monoclonal antibodies used for immune-prophylaxis will be discussed to identify potential serological and clinical endpoints for upcoming pivotal trials.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 23, 2015).12aRespiratory Syncytial Virus Infectionsxprevention & control22aDrug Design22aRespiratory Syncytial Virus Infectionsximmunology22aRespiratory Syncytial Virus Vaccinesxtherapeutic use 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aUnited States.bFood and Drug Administration,esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1905340uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19043  aAUTH04592cgm a2200421 i 101674301DNLM20260227155320.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u151224s2015    mdu068|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19375  a(OCoLC)939525271  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 54000aImaging immunity /cRonald N. Germain. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 8 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded December 9, 2015.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture, G. Burroughs Mider Lecture  Immune responses involve cell-cell interactions within lymphoid tissues, trafficking of activated cells to sites of effector function, and the migration of effector cells within peripheral tissues. To gain a more detailed appreciation of the relationships among cell movement, tissue architecture, stromal cell environment, and immune function, we have used intravital multiphoton microscopy and novel multiplex immunohistochemical methods we have developed called Histo-cytometry and Ce3D to analyze immune cell dynamics and tissue micro-anatomy.   The immune system has three major components involving barrier function, innate immunity, and adaptive immunity and the lecture will integrate information on all three levels to develop a comprehensive picture of how the immune system operates to provide host protection against infection. Using dynamic imaging in living animals, we have found that at sites of tissue damage such as the skin, neutrophils that are primary effectors of innate immunity are guided by stimuli from damaged barrier tissue and secondary lipid mediators to develop dense swarms that isolate the damaged area and localize infectious agents. In secondary lymphoid tissues where the adaptive immune response develops, a stromal cell network plays a key role in guiding T lymphocyte trafficking for efficient interaction of rare specific cells with FRC-attached dendritic cells presenting antigens from vaccines or infectious agents. These interactions lead to effector T cell development. After exiting the lymph nodes and then the blood to enter tissue sites, the effector T cells stop on antigen-presenting cells and make mediators that are key to host defense. An important feedback regulatory pathway titrates the effector activity of the T cells so that they do not make more mediators capable of tissue damage than is needed to control the infection.   The role of cell localization in both innate and adaptive immunity has also been addressed using Histo-cytometry that reveals at high resolution the spatial positioning and activation state of cells with complex phenotypes in tissues. With an ability to use as many as 14 different colors and antibodies not only to surface markers but to phospho-proteins and cytokines, and also to conduct imaging in large 3D volumes in an quantitative manner (Ce3D), our multiplex imaging technology is ideally suited to studies of tissue samples from animal models and human patients with respect to the phenotype, number, location, signaling state, and function of immune cells and stromal elements in tissue sites including cancer. Using these static imaging tools, we have discovered the role of dendritic cell positioning in controlling responses to infections and vaccines as well as the localized environment in which regulatory T cells control potential autoimmune responses in normal individuals.    Using these specific examples, this lecture will illustrate the power of in situ imaging for the acquisition of a more accurate picture of the molecular, cellular, spatial, and temporal aspects of cell function and signaling events involved in immune responses.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 2, 2016).12aImmunity22aImage Cytometry 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aGermain, Ron,espeaker. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series 0aG. Burroughs Mider lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19375  aAUTH01770cgm a2200433 a 101552192DNLM20260227153714.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110119s2010    mdu056 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16328  a(OCoLC)723032467  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4701 aChurch, George M.q(George McDonald)10aPersonal genomes, environments & traits (GET) /cGeorge Church.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (56 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture  aTitle from title screen (viewed May 10, 2011).  aRecorded Dec. 8, 2010.3 a(CIT): "Personal Genomes, Environments & Traits (GET) : Technology for Aging, Alcohol, Allergy, Arthritis, Cancer, Cardiovascular, Developmental, Dental, Metabolic,  Mental, Neurological and Sensory Health and Diversity"  Lecture Objectives: 1. Changes in technologies for diagnostic genetics  2. Discuss genome sequencing progress 3. Consenting for broad Trait Data.  aOpen-captioned.12aGenome, Humanxphysiology22aGenetic Testing22aSequence Analysis, DNA 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16328  aAUTH01493cas a2200457   0046052DNLM20260227090921.0cr|cnu||||||||660504c19669999paub| p       0    0eng    a0027-3171l0027-3171  aMVBRAV  a(DNLM)M50760000(s)  a(OCoLC)01292018  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bMU398N10aMultivariate Behav Res2dnlm00aMultivariate behavioral research.30aMBR  aFort Worth, Tex. :bSociety of Multivariate Experimental Psychology313<2009- > :aPhiladelphia, PA  :bTaylor & Francis Group  av.  aSix no. a year,b2009-  aQuart