-  aQuarterly,b-2004  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (spring 1990)-  aQuarterly.  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv6n1,spring 1996-1 aPubMedbv6n1,spring 1996-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.22aAdolescent22aChild22aInfant 2aAdolescent Behaviorxdrug effects 2aChild Behaviorxdrug effects 2aMental Disorders 2aMental Disordersxdrug therapy 2aPsychotropic Drugs 2aPeriodical0 x1557-89924 uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/cap  aAUTH01341cgm a22003974a 101267578DNLM20260227145303.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu056 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10402  a(OCoLC)65195206  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 168.5.H61 aHo, David D.,d1952-14aThe dynamics of HIV-1 replication in vivo /cDavid D. Ho.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 8, 2006).  aStreaming video (56 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 20, 2003.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aHIV-122aSimian Immunodeficiency Virus22aVirus Replication 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10402  aAUTH01930cgm a2200433 i 9918716284506676DNLM20260227164208.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u231128s2023    mdu831|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33496  a(DNLM)CIT:33503  a(OCoLC)1429554846  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 5042 aFrontiers in Basic Immunology (Conference)d(2023 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10a2023 frontiers in basic immunology /cCenter for Cancer Research, NCI, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (13 hr., 51 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 27 & 28, 2023.3 a(CIT): The Center for Cancer Research at the National Cancer Institute is hosting a two-day international symposium on September 27-28, 2023 entitled 2023 Frontiers in Basic Immunology. The program includes recent advances in the field and should be an exciting forum for discussion and debate on the current understanding of basic immunological mechanisms.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 18, 2024).12aImmune System Phenomena22aImmunityxphysiology22aNeoplasmsximmunology 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aCenter for Cancer Research (National Cancer Institute (U.S.)),ehost institution.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=5237740uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=52379  aAUTH03289cgm a2200481 a 101585232DNLM20260227154212.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120525s2012    mdu062|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17217  a(OCoLC)803799305  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 3591 aGuo, Ming,cMD10aMolecular pathways to Alzheimer's and Parkinson's disease /cMing Guo.1 iTitle on PowerPoint screen:aMolecular pathways to Parkinson's disease  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 2 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aRecorded Apr. 16, 2012.3 a(CIT): Dr. Guo is both a physician and a scientist devoted to understanding the fundamental mechanisms that lead to neurological diseases. The Guo lab utilizes Drosophila as models for their disease-related research, specifically on the two most common neurodegenerative diseases, Alzheimer's disease and Parkinson's disease. In their first line of study, the Guo lab seeks to understand the regulation of amyloid precursor protein (APP) cleavage by gamma-secretase, APP steady state levels, and the fate of the APP intracellular domain (AICD). Towards this goal, they have developed an in vivo reporter system to identify these regulators through a function-based genetic screen of the Drosophila eye. They have shown that ubiquilin is antagonistic to presenilin function and promotes neurodegeneration. The second line of study in the Guo lab focuses on mutant flies lacking pink1, which result in defects in mitochondrial morphology and function, increasing the fly"s sensitivity to stress and reducing its life span. Remarkably, they show that overexpression of human PINK1 rescues cellular defects due to loss of pink1 function in flies. In addition, pink1 acts in the same genetic pathway as parkin, another gene linked to familial PD, to regulate mitochondrial function. Furthermore, pink1 and parkin promote mitochondrial fission and/or inhibit fusion. Overall, their studies underscore the importance of mitochondrial dysfunction as a central mechanism for PD pathogenesis. The identification of the pink1/parkin pathway with a mitochondria localized kinase (PINK1) and an E3 ubiquitin ligase (Parkin) opens the doors to the study of intra-mitochondrial signaling, nuclear-mitochondria signaling, and mitochondrial function.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aParkinson Diseasexgenetics22aDrosophila22aMitochondriaxmetabolism22aModels, Animal22aProtein Kinasesxmetabolism22aUbiquitin-Protein Ligasesxmetabolism 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17217  aAUTH02032cas a2200517 a 9103800DNLM20260227091018.0cr||||||||||||910321c19909999mduqr p       0    0eng    a   95659023 zsn 90000076  a1049-2089l1049-2089  a(DNLM)SR0069293(s)  a(OCoLC)21175275  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bJO67BGL10aJ Health Care Poor Underserved2dnlm 0aJournal of health care for the poor and underserved00aJournal of health care for the poor and underserved.18aJournal of health care for the poor & underserved  aNashville, TN :bInstitute on Health Care for the Poor and Underserved, Meharry Medical College,cc1990-3 32004- :aBaltimore, MD :bJohns Hopkins University Press  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (summer 1990)-  aPlace of publication varies.1 aMEDLINEbv1n1,summer 1990-1 aPubMedbv1n1,summer 1990-1 aHospital literature indexx0018-5736bv1n1, summer 1990-19941 aHospital and health administration indexx1077-1719b1995-19991 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3Vol. 2, no. 3 (WINTER 1991)c19911119fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper that meets the full ANSI standard for Permanent Paper 2aHealth Services Accessibilityxeconomics 2aMedical Indigency 2aMedically Underserved Area 2aUnited States 2aPeriodical2 aMeharry Medical College.bInstitute on Health Care for the Poor and Underserved.0 x1548-68694 uhttp://muse.jhu.edu/journals/journal%5Fof%5Fhealth%5Fcare%5Ffor%5Fthe%5Fpoor%5Fand%5Funderserved  aAUTH02032cgm a2200457 i 101660650DNLM20260227155135.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150602s2015    mdu066|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19032  a(OCoLC)916258932  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 7041 aJulius, David,eauthor.10aTRP channels and pain :bfrom physiology to atomic structure /cDavid Julius.1 aTransient receptor potential channels and pain 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 6 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aJohn Daly memorial lecture  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded May 27, 2015.3 a(CIT): Pungent irritants from pepper, mint and mustard plants have served as powerful for identifying molecules, cells and circuits that contribute to sensation and pain. Dr. Julius has exploited the power of natural products to reveal the molecular mechanisms behind how we feel, sense and touch.    It is quite appropriate for him to deliver the lecture in memory of Dr. John Daly, a noted natural products chemist and pharmacologist in NIDDK.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 30, 2015).12aPain22aSensationxphysiology22aTransient Receptor Potential Channelsxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aJohn Daly memorial lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19032  aAUTH01185nam a2200301 i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003100073042000800104044001800112060003200130100005300162245007800215264006000293300003800353336002600391337002800417338002700445500008100472540014200553583003900695583004300734650002600777651002500803730004600828999000900874101778635DNLM20260227123026.0210429r18831883paua          000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5573 no.91 aTryon, J. R.q(John Rufus),d1837-1912,eauthor.10aLeprosy in the Hawaiian Islands /cextract from the report of J.B. Tryon. 1a[Philadelphia?] :b[publisher not identified],c[1883?]  a8 pages :billustrations ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aExtracted from the American journal of the medical sciences for April, 1883.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitiz