IH Director Ruth L. Kirschstein, M.D., will present the first-ever NIH Plain Language Awards honoring NIH communicators who have demonstrated outstanding use of plain language.  Following the awards presentation, Michael Dirda, Ph.D., Pulitzer Prize winning Senior Editor of The Washington Post "Book World," will be the guest speaker.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aWritingxstandards22aAwards and Prizes22aBiomedical Research22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aDirda, Michael.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10513  aAUTH01517cas a2200469 a 101517307DNLM20260227090914.0cr||||||||||||091014c20109999sz || p       |    0eng    a  2010243580  a1878-5077l1878-5077  a(OCoLC)655740747  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9Switzerland00aW1bEP8510aEPMA J2dnlm04aThe EPMA journal.  a[Dordrecht] :bSpringer2 32012-aLondon :bBiomed Central313<2017-> :aCham :bSpringer International Publishing  aFour no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1, no. 1 (2010).  aPrint issues ceased with v. 2, no. 4 (2011).0 aPubMedbv3, 2012-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: Vol. 1, no. 1 (2010); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 1, no. 1 (2010).12aPreventive Health Services22aDiagnostic Techniques and Procedures22aPrecision Medicinexmethods 2aPeriodical2 aEuropean Association for Predictive, Preventive & Personalized Medicine.0 x1878-50854 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=1015173074 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/13167  aAUTH01373cgm a22004334a 101488624DNLM20260227152755.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081104s2008    mdu048 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14687  a(OCoLC)297494447  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 521 aBuchanan, Susan K.10aImport and export across the bacterial outer membrane /cSusan K. Buchanan.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's seminar series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 12, 2008).  aStreaming video (47 min., 44 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 3, 2008.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBacterial Outer Membrane Proteins22aColicins22aProtein Transport 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14687  aAUTH01281cas a2200409 a 101576205DNLM20260224111600.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||120111c20129999dk || poo     |    0eng    a  2012243635  a2245-1919l2245-1919  a(OCoLC)809918228  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9Denmark10aW110aDan Med J2dnlm00aDanish medical journal.1 aDMJ  aCopenhagen :bDanish Medical Association  aMonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with 2012 : 59 (1).1 aMEDLINEbv59n1, Jan. 2012-0 aPubMedbv59n1, Jan. 2012-  aDescription based on: 2012 : 59 (1); title from PDF caption (publisher's Web site, viewed Sept. 3, 2012).  aLatest issue consulted: 59/9 (Sept. 2012) (viewed Sept. 3, 2012).12aMedicine 2aPeriodical2 aAlmindelige danske lægeforening.00tDanish medical bulletinx0907-8916w(DLC)  sn 81004587w(OCoLC)0156587240uhttp://ugeskriftet.dk/dmj  aAUTH02823cgm a22005054a 101488631DNLM20260227152832.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081104s2008    mdu080 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14699  a(OCoLC)298508999  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.51 aDeLisi, Charles,d1941-10aResearch portfolio analysis /cCharles P. DeLisi.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aOPASI rounds lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 1, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 19 min., 37 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 10, 2008.3 a(CIT): OPASI ROUNDS Lecture Series  Dr. DeLisi will be discussing the information revolution and how it has transformed biomedical science, and it is beginning to transform the way organizations manage their investment portfolios.  The NIH research portfolio is extraordinarily complex; developing and implementing information based decision making methods has the potential to broaden and deepen the NIH investments in both basic and clinical research, and to enable more proactive investment strategies. I'll discuss some of the goals and methods of portfolio analysis, and the expected direction and shape of activities in OPASI in the near, intermediate,  and longer term future.  The Office of Portfolio Analysis and Strategic Initiatives (OPASI) provides the National Institutes of Health (NIH) and its constituent Institutes and Centers (ICs) with the methods and information necessary to manage their large and complex scientific portfolios, identifies - in concert with multiple other inputs - important areas of emerging scientific opportunities or rising public health challenges, and assists in the acceleration of investments in these areas, focusing on those involving multiple ICs.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration22aDecision Making, Organizational22aFinancial Management22aOrganizational Innovation22aResource Allocation22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aOPASI rounds lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14699  aAUTH01873cgmaa22004334a 101211862DNLM20260227144232.0vf|cjahouvd|cvaizucr|cnu||||||||040330s2002    mdu799  |||||f|   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:2004  a(DNLM)CIT:2005  a(OCoLC)56066515  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aWP 522bDVD no.1 200200aMenopausal hormone therapy /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  aUnited States.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  a10 videocassettes of 10 (BetacamSP) (13 hr., 18 min., 23 sec.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in. #3 duplication copy.  a1 videodisc (DVD) (7 hr., 8 min., 57 sec.) :bsd., col. ;c4 3/4 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier  aConference sponsored by the NIH Office of Research on Women's Health.0 atapes 1-5. 10/23/02 (6 hr., 9 min. 26 sec.) -- tapes 6-10. 10/24/02 (7 hr., 8 min, 57 sec.).  aPresented Oct. 23-24, 2002.  aAlso available online.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM12aEstrogen Replacement Therapy 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Research on Women's Health.4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=20044 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=2005  aAUTH02507cgm a2200457 a 101576017DNLM20260227154041.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120107s2011    mdu576 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17040  a(DNLM)CIT:17043  a(OCoLC)773342210  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 3202 aNational Children's Study (Project).bSymposiumd(2011 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNational Children's Study Symposium, health disparities among children of immigrants /cNational Children's Study.1 aHealth disparities among children of immigrants  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a2 online resources (2 streaming video files (9 hr., 36 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded Dec. 15 & 16, 2011.3 a(CIT): The National Children"s Study (NCS) will examine the effects of the environment, as broadly defined to include factors such as air, water, diet, sound, family dynamics, community and cultural influences, and genetics on the growth, development, and health of children across the United States, following them from before birth until age 21 years. The goal of the study is to improve the health and well-being of children.  Given the importance of specific influences (for example, family dynamics, community, culture) on immigrant children"s health, the National Children"s Study Program Office proposes a meeting of subject matter experts to have a dialogue on topics related to immigration as it pertains to the NCS. Subject matter experts will provide comments on potential research questions, process, and measurement strategies that could inform NCS Main Study development and how the health and developmental trajectories of children of immigrants might be addressed.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aChild Welfare22aChild22aEmigrants and Immigrants22aHealth Status Disparities 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1704040uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17043  aAUTH03496nam a2200541 i 9918977553806676DNLM20260224150017.0m     o  d        cr#un|---aucuu231121s2024    nyua    ob    001 0 eng    a1409733294  a9781071635452q(electronic bk.)  a107163545Xq(electronic bk.)  z97810716354457 a10.1007/978-1-0716-3545-22doi  a(OCoLC)1410121101z(OCoLC)1409733294  aGW5XEbengerdaepncGW5XEdOCLKBdOCLCFdOCLCO  apcc  9United States 4aQP36000aW1bNE337G v.206 202410aWL 103 7aPSAN2bicssc 7aMED0570002bisacsh 7aPSAN2thema04a612.8223/eng/2023112100aPsychophysiology methods /cedited by Massimiliano Valeriani, Marina de Tommaso. 1aNew York, NY :bHumana Press,c2024.  a1 online resource (xv, 288 pages) :billustrations (some color)  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuromethods,x1940-6045 ;v206  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.0 aRecent Advances in Clinical Applications of P300 and MMN -- Contingent Negative Variation (CNV) -- The Study of Anticipatory Brain Activity in Cognitive Tasks by Means Event-Related Potential, Frequency, and Time-Frequency Methods -- qEEG Methods to Probe Abnormal Brain Rhythms Related to Quiet Vigilance in Patients with Dementia due to Alzheimer's, Parkinson's, and Lewy Body Diseases -- Pain Related Evoked Potentials -- Chemosensory Neuro-Olfactometry, Pheromones Perceptions, and EEG Signal Processing Methods -- Psychophysiology and Electrophysiology of the Visual System -- MEG -- EEG/FNIRS -- Brain-Computer Interfaces -- Methods for the Assessment of Multisensory Processing: Behavioral and Neuropsychological Approaches -- Psychophysiology of Sleep.  aThis volume looks at the latest advancements used by researchers to study psychophysiology and cognitive neurosciences. The chapters in this book cover topics such as classical event-related brain responses (P300, MMN, and CNV); anatomical structures and physiological mechanisms underlying the capability of feeling pain and smelling; magnetoencephalography (MEG); and brain-computer interface techniques using electrical activity generated by the brain. In the Neuromethods series style, chapters include the kind of detail and key advice from the specialists needed to get successful results in your laboratory. Cutting-edge and practical, Psychophysiology Methods is a valuable resource for researchers who want to learn more about the use of psychophysiological techniques in the investigation of human cognition, and increase interest in the clinical puzzle of neurological and psychiatric disorders.  aAlso issued in print.0 aOnline resource; title from PDF title page (SpringerLink, viewed November 21, 2023).12aPsychophysiology22aNeurophysiology.22aCognitive Neuroscience.22aMental Processesxphysiology1 aValeriani, Massimiliano,eeditor.1 aDe Tommaso, Marina,eeditor. 0aNeuromethods ;vv. 206.x0893-233640uhttps://link.springer.com/openurl?genre=book&isbn=978-1-0716-3545-2  aAUTH03118cgm a22004454a 101310917DNLM20260227152015.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070703s2007    mdu117 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13866  a(OCoLC)165047492  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWS 46004aThe health of girls and women across the lifespan.pAdolescents /cOffice of Research on Women's Health.30aAdolescents  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aORWH women's health seminar series  aTitle from title screen (viewed July 10, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 56 min., 3 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 5, 2007.3 a(CIT): Each day we make hundreds of decisions that ultimately affect our health. How can we help adolescents make choices now that will lead to a healthier lifestyle? The Office of Research on Women's Health Seminar Series will present an afternoon of discussion with national experts in the field of adolescent health. Topics range from sleep behaviors in adolescence to physical activity in youth and addictive behaviors. This is your chance to hear from NIH researchers who are working to increase health awareness and ensure healthy habits at a time of life when decision making is often not seen in terms of life long consequences.   Speakers include:  Vivian W. Pinn, M.D. Director, ORWH Introduction and Overview  Christine Bachrach, Ph.D. Chief, Demographic and Behvavioral Sciences Branch NICHD Update on ADD Health Research Project  Ronald Dahl, M.D. Staunton Professor, Pediatrics and Psychology University of Pittsburgh Sleep Behaviors and Adolescents  Russell Pate, Ph.D. Professor, Department of Exercise Science Associate Vice President for Health Sciences Arnold School of Public Health University of South Carolina Physical Activity in Children and Youth  Dianne Neumark-Sztainer, Ph.D., M.P.H., R.D. Professor Department of Epidemiology and Community Health School of Public Health University of Minnesota Obesity and Adolescent Girls: A Slightly Different Perspective  Donald Vereen, M.D. Special Assistant to the Director NIDA Addiction and Adolescents  ORWH Women's Health Seminar Series sponsored by the NIH Office of Intramural Research and the NIH Office of Research on Women's Health.  For more information, visit  http://orwh.od.nih.gov.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aAdolescent12aHealth22aWomen's Health 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Research on Women's Health. 0aORWH women's health seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13866  aAUTH02160cas a2200589 i 9918452086806676DNLM20260227090909.0m     o  d |      cr#|||||||||||211116c20229999enkqr joo     0   a0eng    a  2021202573  aGBC3295952bnb  a1401755318a1422832677a14402485010 a2770-730Xl2770-729621  a(OCoLC)1285382933  aDLCbengerdad20260227dOCLCOdDLCdUKMGBdDLCdOCLCLdDNLM  apccansdp  9England10aISSN RECORD00aW100a573.8223/eng/202312200 aNeuroprotection (Chichester, England)0 aNeuroprotectionb(Chichester Engl. Online)10aNeuroprotection2dnlm 0aNeuroprotectionb(Chichester, England. Online)10aNeuroprotection.1 aNEP3 1a[Chichester] :bJohn Wiley & Sons, Ltd. on behalf of Chinese Medical Association,c2023-  a1 online resource  aQuarterly