ical Center grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19494  aAUTH01162nam a2200301 i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003100073042000800104044001800112060003300130110005800163245007300221264008500294300005000379336002600429337002800455338002700483540014200510583003900652583004300691610004900734650002500783650001800808651002500826999000900851101778761DNLM20260225092455.0210503s1907    dcua          000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5573 no.262 aWashington Seventh-day Adventist Sanitarium,eauthor.10aWashington Seventh-day Adventist Sanitarium :bthe portal to health. 1aTakoma Park, Washington, D.C. :bReview and Herald Pub. Assn. Printers,c[1907?]  a18 unnumbered pages :billustrations ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM22aWashington Seventh-day Adventist Sanitarium.12aHospitals, Religious22aProtestantism 2aDistrict of Columbia  aAUTH02151cgm a2200445 i 9918573157306676DNLM20260227163315.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2023    mdu119|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32970  a(OCoLC)1392039796  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWR 46000aDemystifying medicine.pThe use of JAK inhibitors in autoimmune disease /cAngela Christiano, John O'Shea.1 aUse of JAK inhibitors in autoimmune disease 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 59 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded February 21, 2023.3 a(CIT): The Demystifying Medicine Series, jointly sponsored by FAES and NIH, includes presentations on pathology, diagnosis, and therapy in the context of major disease problems and current research. Primarily directed toward PhD students, clinicians and program managers, this series is designed to help bridge the gap between advances in biology and their application to major human diseases. Each session includes clinical and basic science components presented by NIH staff and invitees. All students, trainees, fellows, and staff are welcome to participate.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed July 26, 2023).12aAlopecia Areataxetiology12aAlopecia Areataxdrug therapy22aJanus Kinase Inhibitorsxtherapeutic use22aCytokinesxmetabolism 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aChristiano, Angela M.,espeaker.1 aO'Shea, John J.,d1952-espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=48821  aAUTH01219cam a2200313 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400035000800420008001150440018001230600024001412450146001652640062003113000032003733360026004053370028004313380027004595000019004865000107005055010060006125040041006726500014007136500023007277100067007507100032008177300047008499990009008969918787485106676DNLM20260213174645.0240326r19561956iau      b    000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.3700aState University of Iowa College of Medicine clinical pathologic conference, October 5, 1955 /cState University of Iowa College of Medicine. 1a[Des Moines, Iowa] :bIowa State Medical Society,c[1956]  a8 unnumbered pages ;c26 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  a"Reprinted from the Journal of the Iowa State Medical Society, January, 1956, pages 28 to 35"--Page 8.  aNLM copy, bound 37th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aDiagnosis22aEducation, Medical2 aState University of Iowa.bCollege of Medicine,eissuing body.2 aIowa State Medical Society.0 aJournal of the Iowa State Medical Society.  aAUTH02532cgm a2200421 i 101779272DNLM20260227161132.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210511s2021    mdu061|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31668  a(OCoLC)1261047700  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 36500aLasker scholar lecture.pCell therapy for epithelial cancers /cDr. Christian Hinrichs.1 aCell therapy for epithelial cancers 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 22, 2021.3 a(CIT): Cell therapy is an emerging treatment modality that is highly effective in hematological cancers. Research by Dr. Hinrichs's team has demonstrated the potential to extend cell therapy to the treatment of common epithelial cancers. Treatment of patients with human papillomavirus (HPV)-associated cancers with tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes showed durable, complete tumor responses, apparently curing some patients with metastatic cancer. A more "off the shelf" approach with peripheral blood T cells genetically engineered to target the HPV E7 antigen also demonstrated robust clinical activity with extensive tumor regression in patients with checkpoint inhibitor-resistant cancer. Genomic and transcriptomic studies revealed the impact of antigen processing and interferon response gene defects on treatment resistance. Current and future research is focused on strategies to overcome resistance mechanisms and to extend the approach to a broader range of cancers. Dr. Hinrichs was selected in 2015 as a Lasker Clinical Research Scholar at the NCI.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed July 16, 2021).12aNeoplasms, Glandular and Epithelialxtherapy22aCell- and Tissue-Based Therapy22aT-Lymphocytesxtransplantation 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aHinrichs, Christian S.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41646  aAUTH01503cas a22004337a 9503946DNLM20260224111547.0cr|cn#||||||||960808c19929999ja qr p       0    0jpn    a1344-6991l1344-6991  a(DNLM)SR0083379(s)  a(OCoLC)35225738  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 ajpnbeng  9Japan00aW1bDO156V00aDōbutsu rinshō igaku =bJournal of Animal Clinical Research Foundation.31aJournal of Animal Clinical Research Foundation1 iVols. for 199 -    have English title:aJournal of animal clinical medicine  aTottori-ken Kurayoshi-shi :bDōbutsu Rinshō Igaku Kenkyūjo,c1992-  av.  aFour no. a year,b1997-  aSemiannual,b-1996  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (1992.9)-  aLatest issue consulted: surrogate of Vol. 10, no. 4 (Mar 2002).  aCalled also: Dai 1-kan dai 1-gō (1992-nen 9-gatsu)- .  aAlso issued online.  aText in Japanese; summaries and table of contents in English. 2aAnimal Diseases 2aVeterinary Medicine 2aPeriodical2 aDōbutsu Rinshō Igaku Kenkyūjo.0 x1881-15744 uhttps://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/browse/dobutsurinshoigaku/  aAUTH02691cgm a2200445 i 101775948DNLM20260227160733.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2021    mdu105|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31523  a(OCoLC)1248767980  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 74000aDemystifying medicine.pViruses and immunodeficiency and skin microbiome /cPhilip Murphy, Heidi Kong.1 aViruses and immunodeficiency and skin microbiome 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 45 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded February 2, 2021.3 a(CIT): How do virus and other pathogens penetrate the fortress that is human skin?  What tricks do they use to slip past the guards?  How do they compromise the immune system, and how do they take advantage of and reveal inherited immunodeficiency?  These are the topics of the next Demystifying Medicine.  Philip Murphy, M.D., is chief of the NIAID Laboratory of Molecular Immunology and its Molecular Signaling Section.  His laboratory studies G protein-coupled receptors of the immune system with the goal of elucidating mechanisms by which the receptors relay chemotactic signals to cells and identifying their exact physiologic roles both in immunoregulation and in immunologically mediated disease.  Heidi Kong, M.D., is a senior investigator in the NIAMS Dermatology Branch and head of its Cutaneous Microbiome and Inflammation Section.  She has dedicated her research to investigating the skin microbes in patients with inflammatory skin diseases and in healthy individuals.  Her translational studies ask clinical questions related to the skin microbiome in atopic dermatitis and immunodeficiency.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 26, 2021).12aImmunologic Deficiency Syndromesxvirology12aImmunologic Deficiency Syndromesximmunology22aSkinxmicrobiology22aHost Microbial Interactions 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aMurphy, Phil,cDr.espeaker.1 aKong, Heidi,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41284  aAUTH01626cas a2200469 a 9200435DNLM20260227091024.0cr|cn ||||||||911219c19909999vaufr p       0    0eng    a   91642062   a1055-3835l1055-3835y0275-8598  a(DNLM)SR0071373(s)  a(OCoLC)22696721  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bJO533PT10aJ Addict Offender Couns2dnlm 0aJournal of addictions & offender c