983K10aIntervirology2dnlm 0aIntervirology00aIntervirology.  aBasel,bKarger.  av.billus.  aSix no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-       1973-  aJournal of the Virology Section of the International Association of Microbiological Societies.  aPrint issues ceased with 2020.1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, 1973-1 aPubMedbv1n1, 1973-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso available online.  3Vol. 14, no. 1 (1980)c19920828fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aVirology 2aPeriodical2 aInternational Association of Microbiological Societies.bSection on Virology.0 x1423-01004 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/224031  aAUTH03630cgm a2200493 i 101749147DNLM20260227155934.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190604s2019    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27581  a(OCoLC)1109437511  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWQ 20800aGetting to "right" :ba blueprint for the adjudication of emerging reproductive technologies /cDr. Eli Y. Adashi. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aDr. Mortimer B. Lipsett lecture  aRecorded May 31, 2019.3 a(CIT): Dr. Mortimer B. Lipsett Lecture  The former Dean of Medicine and Biological Sciences at Brown University (during its 2007 renaming in honor of Mr. Warren Alpert), Dr. Adashi is an academic physician-executive, a graduate of the Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health program in Health Care Management (MS, 2005), and the former president of the Society for Reproductive Endocrinologists (SRE), the Society for Gynecologic Investigation (SGI), and the American Gynecological and Obstetrical Society (AGOS).  As a tenured Professor of Medical Science at Brown University, Dr. Adashi is a member of the Brown Center for Prisoner Health and Human Rights and directs the Healthcare in America course (a highly subscribed medical school elective) and the Healthcare Policy Concentration (a medical school elective for students with special interest in Healthcare Policy).   Dr. Adashi's research focused on the biology of the ovary and the role of growth factors and cytokines in this context. Since 2008, Dr. Adashi has undertaken to focus on matters of policy at the nexus of medicine, law, ethics, and social justice. Dr. Adashi is the former Editor-In- Chief of Seminars in Reproductive Medicine and a former Associate Editor of Endocrinology, Journal of the Society for Gynecologic Investigation, Reproductive Medicine Review, Seminars in Reproductive Endocrinology, Reproductive Medicine Review, and Reviews in Endocrine and Metabolic Disorders  Dr. Adashi, has been the recipient of continuous National Institute of Health (NIH) funding from 1985 to 2005 inclusive of a Research Career Development Award. Mentor to over 50 postdoctoral trainees, and the author or co-author of close to 400 peer-reviewed publications, Dr. Adashi contributed over 120 book chapters/reviews, and co-edited or edited 16 books in the general area of reproductive medicine with special emphasis on ovarian biology.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 12, 2019).12aReproductive Techniques, Assistedxtrends22aBiomedical Researchxethics22aBiomedical Researchxtrends22aPublic Opinion22aPublic Policy22aReproductive Techniques, Assistedxethics 2aUnited Kingdom 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aAdashi, E. Y.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aMortimer B. Lipsett memorial lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27581  aAUTH01167cas a22003854a 101188548DNLM20260227090913.0cr|cn ||||||||030818c20039999gw qr p       0    0ger    a  2003243634  