846-1909.10aZur Kenntniss der Phycomyceten.nI.pZur Morphologie und Biologie der Ancylisteen und Chytridiaceen, zugleich ein Beitrag zur Phytopathologie /cvon W. Zopf.1 aZur Morphologie und Biologie der Ancylisteen und Chytridiaceen, zugleich ein Beitrag zur Phytopathologie  aHalle :bE. Blochmann & Sohn in Dresden für die Akademie in Commission bei W. Engelmann in Leipzig,c1884.  apages 144-236, [10] leaves of plates :bcolor illustrations ;c33 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aNova acta der K. Leop.-Carol. Deutschen Akademie der Naturforscher ;vBd. 47, Nr. 4  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aChytridiomycota22aEntomophthorales 0aNova acta Leopoldina ;vBd. 47, Nr. 4.  aAUTH02967cgm a2200445 i 101680578DNLM20260227155350.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160404s2016    mdu065|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19540  a(OCoLC)946699586  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 16000aInferring host-pathogen interactions from diverse data sources /cMark Craven. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 5 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNational Library of Medicine informatics lecture  aRecorded March 9, 2016.3 a(CIT): National Library of Medicine Informatics Lecture Series  Insight into the mechanisms and context of host-pathogen interactions can be gained by applying computational methods to a broad range of experimental, observational, and secondary data sources. Dr. Craven will discuss his work in three studies that involve developing and applying predictive methods in order to characterize host-pathogen interactions. In the first study, he is focused on inferring host subnetworks that are involved in viral replication from genome-wide loss-of-function experiments. Although these experiments can identify the host factors that directly or indirectly facilitate or inhibit the replication of a virus in a host cell, they do not elucidate how these genes are organized into the biological pathways that mediate host-virus interactions. His team is developing novel computational methods that use a wide array of secondary data sources, including the scientific literature, to transform the measurements from these assays into hypotheses that predict the pathways in the cell that relate implicated genes to viral replication. In the second study, he is applying machine-learning methods to understand how variation in the genome of HSV-1 influences multiple ocular disease phenotypes in a host. In the third study, he is investigating the extent to which risk for various infectious disease phenotypes can be predicted from electronic health records.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 8, 2016).12aHost-Pathogen Interactions12aVirus Replication22aData Miningxmethods22aForecasting 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCraven, Mark,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNLM informatics lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19540  aAUTH01530cgm a22004214a 101268529DNLM20260227150708.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu072 ||||||f|   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12157  a(OCoLC)122498883  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWR 2651 aKraemer, Kenneth H.10aClinical, molecular & epidemiologic studies of xeroderma pigmentosum & related disorders of DNA repair /cKenneth H. Kraemer.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 27, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 11 min., 31 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorde