ences, Harbin Institute of Technology, and the Chinese Research Academy of Environmental Sciences, supervised by China Association for Science and Technology.  aDescription based on: Volume 1 (January 2020); title from journal homepage (ScienceDirect, viewed May 4, 2023).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 16 (October 2023) (viewed May 4, 2023).12aEnvironmental Science22aEcological and Environmental Phenomena22aEngineering22aSustainable Development 2aPeriodical2 aHaerbin gong ye da xue,eissuing body.2 aZhongguo huan jing ke xue yan jiu yuan,eissuing body.0 x2096-964340uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/environmental-science-and-ecotechnology40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=9918453988906676  aAUTH02334cgm a2200493 i 9918367464006676DNLM20260227162022.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220318s2022    mdu410|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32225  a(DNLM)CIT:32229  a(OCoLC)1350873223  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.52 aNIH Fostering Cohort Recruitment Virtual Forumd(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNIH fostering cohort recruitment virtual forum /cChief Officer for Scientific Workforce Diversity, NIH.1 aNational Institutes of Health fostering cohort recruitment virtual forum 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (6 hr., 50 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH scientific workforce diversity seminar series  aRecorded February 23 & 24, 2022.3 a(CIT): The goal of the FCR Virtual Forum is to galvanize the scientific community around the success of cohort recruitment models geared toward enhancing DEIA for faculty. Held over two half days, the FCR Virtual Forum will explore faculty cohort recruitment adoption, implementation, and dissemination, its impact on DEIA, practices that make it successful, barriers that might exist, and finally, the ways to effectively evaluate both implementation and outcomes.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed October 27, 2022 ).22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aResearch Personnel12aCultural Diversity22aPersonnel Selection22aBiomedical Research 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aScientific workforce diversity seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4477040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44772  aAUTH03259cgm a22004574a 101465444DNLM20260227152259.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080201s2008    mdu084|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14254  a(OCoLC)195759666  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4701 aGreen, Eric D.10aTechniques for analyzing genomes I /cEric Green.3 aTechniques for analyzing genomes 1  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCurrent topics in genome analysis  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 13, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 24 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 15, 2008.3 a(CIT): The scientific community celebrated the achievement of the Human Genome Project's major goal in April of 2003: completion of a high-accuracy sequence of the human genome. The significance of this milestone cannot be underestimated, as it firmly marks the entrance of modern biology into the genomic era (and not the post-genomic era, as many have stated). The intelligent use of sequence data from human and other organisms, along with technological innovations fostered by the Human Genome Project, will lead to significant advances in our understanding of diseases that have a genetic basis and, more importantly, in how health care is delivered in the future.  Given the rapid advances in genomics and bioinformatics that have taken place in the past few years, an intensive review of the major areas of ongoing genome research would be of great value to our fellow National Institutes of Health investigators. To that end, the National Human Genome Research Institute is pleased to once again sponsor the Current Topics in Genome Analysis lecture series.  The series consists of 15 lectures on successive Tuesdays, with a mixture of local and outside speakers covering the major areas of genomics. In this eighth edition of the series, rather than splitting the lectures into "laboratory-based" and "computationally-based" blocks, we have intermingled the lectures by general subject area. We hope that this approach conveys the idea that both laboratory- and computationally-based approaches are necessary in order to do cutting-edge biological research in the future.  The lectures are geared at the level of first year graduate students, are practical in nature, and are intended for a diverse audience. Handouts will be provided for each lecture, and time will be available at the end of each lecture for questions and discussion.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aGenome, Human22aChromosome Mapping22aSequence Analysis, DNA 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aCurrent topics in genome analysis40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14254  aAUTH03449cgm a2200445 i 101611641DNLM20260227155133.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130627s2013    mdu057|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17992  a(OCoLC)857718959  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 26.55.I41 aSorensen, Henrik Toft,eauthor.10aWhen the entire country is a cohort :bcollecting and analyzing big data /cHenrik Toft Sorensen. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (57 min.) :bcolor, sound)  atwo-dimensional moving image2rdacontent  avideo2rdamedia  aonline resource2rdacarrier1 aNLM informatics lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Jun. 11, 2013.3 a(CIT): NLM Informatics Lecture Series   Henrik Toft Sorensen, DrMedSci, PhD, is the Head of the Department of Clinical Epidemiology at Denmark's Aarhus University Hospital.  He has been working for more than ten years with "Big Data" that encompasses clinical records, administrative bases, and registries from a whole country.   His main research interests are: Determinants of long-term prognosis for chronic disease, in particular the prognostic impact of the interaction between multiple chronic diseases, complications and outcome; observational studies of safety of medical interventions and treatments; methods in clinical epidemiology.  Dr. Henrik Toft Sorensen earned his MD in 1983, PhD in 1994, and DMSc in 1996, and held clinical employments from 1983 to 1994 and from 1996 to 2004. He is a specialist in internal medicine (1993) and hepatology (1997). He earned a Gold medal award from Aarhus University 1985. From 1998 to 2004, he was a consultant at Aarhus University Hospital's Department of Gastroenterology and Hepatology. Since 2000, Dr. Sorensen has been a professor in clinical epidemiology at Aarhus University, and head of the Department of Clinical Epidemiology at Aarhus University Hospital. He is a Visiting Professor at Stanford University, CA. Dr. Sorensen has more than 700 publications within the fields of clinical epidemiology, pharmacoepidemiology, and determinants of the prognosis of cardiovascular, infection, gastrointestinal and cancer diseases. He is Principal Investigator on several international studies, and supervised more than 45 students who have received a PhD.  He is Editor-in-Chief of the journal Clinical Epidemiology. Dr. Sorensen is a member of the Board of Directors of the Council for Independent Research, Medical Sciences and has assessed grant applications and several professorships in numerous European countries and in the US. He earned the 2012 Hagedorn Prize, and, in 2011, received The Order of Dannebrog by Her Majesty The Queen of Denmark.  aDescription based on version viewed July 19, 2013.12aDatabases, Factual22aCohort Studies22aRegistries 2aDenmark 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNLM informatics lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17992  aAUTH01502cas a22004694a 100888899DNLM20260227091045.0cr|cn#||||||||990104c19999999nyu|| p       0    0eng    asn 98001409 0 a1523-0864l1523-0864  a(OCoLC)40565151  aNSDcNSDdDLCdDNLM  alcanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bAN86610aAntioxid Redox Signal2dnlm00aAntioxidants & redox signaling.3 aAntioxidants and redox signaling  aLarchmont, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.,c1999-  av. :bill. ;c29 cm.  aThirty six no. a year,b2013-  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (spring 1999)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, spring 1999-1 aPubMedbv1n1, spring 1999-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3Vol.1, no. 3  (Dec 1999)c20001117fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper that meets the full ANSI standard for Permanent Paper12aAntioxidants22aOxidation-Reduction22aSignal Transduction 2aPeriodical2 aMary Ann Liebert, Inc.0 x1557-77164 uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/ars4 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/are  aAUTH02856cgm a2200493 i 9918282192306676DNLM20260227161542.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u211008s2021    mdu653|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31943  a(DNLM)CIT:31944  a(DNLM)CIT:31949  a(OCoLC)1322362976  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 4702 aGenetic Modifiers: Lessons from Nervous System Disorders (Workshop)d(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aGenetic modifiers :blessons from nervous system disorders /cNIH Blueprint. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (3 streaming video files (10 hr., 53 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 21, 22 & 23, 2021.3 a(CIT): This workshop will bring together a diverse group of researchers with expertise in genetics, genomics, neurology, pathophysiology, and preclinical translation. Understanding genetic factors that modify the onset, progression or severity of diseases can lead to novel therapeutics, facilitate clinical trial design, and enable precision medicine. The purpose of this workshop is to promote collaborations, confront challenges and highlight successful strategies for genetic modifier discovery, validation, characterization, and clinical implementation as part of the NIH Blueprint mission to foster transformative discoveries in neuroscience.  Day 1: Modifier discovery in human cohorts and model systems, resources available and successful analytical approaches Day 2: Validation and characterization of modifiers, determining mechanisms of disease modification and mapping modifier pathways Day 3: Clinical applications of modifiers as therapeutic targets, in design of clinical trials and in precision medicine.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 18, 2022).12aGenes, Modifierxgenetics12aNervous System Diseasesxgenetics22aGenetic Variationxgenetics22aGenes, Modifierxphysiology22aGenetic Association Studies 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aNIH Blueprint for Neuroscience Research,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4241740uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4241940uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42421  aAUTH01518cas a22004454a 101156268DNLM20260227090911.0cr||||||||||||021003c20019999pau|| p       0    0eng    a  2004243532  a1446-1242l1446-1242  a(OCoLC)56000114  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9United States00aW1bHE578P10aHealth Sociol Rev2dnlm00aHealth sociology review :bthe journal of the Health Section of the Australian Sociological Association.30aJournal of the Health Section of the Australian Sociological Association  aAdelaide :bFlinders University3132015- :aPhiladelphia, PA :bTaylor & Francis  av.  aFour no. a year  aIssued twice a year.  aFive no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 10, no. 2 (2001).  aDescription based on: Vol. 11, 1/2 (2002).1 aMEDLINEbv29n1, 2020-0 aPubMedbv29n1, 2020- ; selected citations only before this date  aAlso issued online. 2aPeriodical2 aAustralian Sociological Association.bHealth Section.0 x1839-355100tAnnual review of health social sciencesx1036-6733w(OCoLC)371677554 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/RHSR  aAUTH02493cgm a2200481 i 101765810DNLM20260227160056.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u200410s2020    mdu054|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:29042  a(OCoLC)1151773472  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 3000aUsing Google street view images to examine links between the built environment and health /cQuynh Nguyen. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (54 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNLM informatics and data science lecture  aRecorded March 4, 2020.3 a(CIT): Advances in neighborhood research have been constrained by the lack of neighborhood data for many geographical areas. Dr. Nguyen will discuss the use of Google Street View (GSV) images as a source of national data on built environment features and the use of computer vision to label images for indicators of walkability, urban development, and physical disorder. She will discuss how they collect images and how they identify relevant built environment features from street images. Dr. Nguyen will present preliminary analyses examining associations between built environment features and health outcomes at the census tract and county levels. GSV images represent an underutilized resource for building national data on neighborhoods and examining the influence of built environments on community health outcomes across the United States.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 22, 2020).12aEnvironmental Health12aBuilt Environment22aGeographic Information Systems22aInternet22aMaps as Topic22aSocial Determinants of Health 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aNguyen, Quynhq(Quynh Camthi),espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNLM informatics lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?29042  aAUTH01431cgm a22004214a 101549216DNLM20260227153832.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101208s2010    mdu059|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16221  a(OCoLC)702604556  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4751 aUhlen, Mathias.10aMapping the building blocks of life, and a global view on protein expression in normal and cancer tissues /cMathias Uhlen.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (59 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 22, 2010.3 a(CIT): Prof. Mathias Uhlen Addresses the Role of Protein Expression on the Human Protein Atlas.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aGene Expression Regulation22aChromosome Mapping22aGene Expression Regulation, Neoplastic 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)