rs  av. :bill.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 23, no. 1 (Jan.-Feb. 1992)-  aAlso issued online. 2aDevelopmental Biology 2aPeriodical2 aConsultants Bureau.1 tOntogenezx0475-1450w(OCoLC)52986050 x1608-332600tSoviet journal of developmental biologyx0049-173Xw(OCoLC)22519714 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/11174  aAUTH01542cgm a22004334a 101268129DNLM20260227150125.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu121 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11439  a(DNLM)CIT:11440  a(OCoLC)70713355  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4501 aBaxevanis, Andreas D.10aNucleotide and protein sequence analysis /cAndy Baxevanis.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 20, 2006).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 57 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 23, Sept. 30, 2003.3 a(CIT): Current Topics in Genome Analysis An NHGRI Mini-Course in Genomics and Bioinformatics.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aSequence Analysis, DNA22aSequence Analysis, Protein 2aLecture 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1143940uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11440  aAUTH04380cgm a2200445 i 101760358DNLM20260227160048.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u200108s2019    mdu068|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:28890  a(OCoLC)1138544103  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWV 27200aSounding out neuronal diversity in the auditory system /cLisa Goodrich. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 8 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience series seminar. Robert Whitney Newcomb memorial lecture  aRecorded December 2, 2019.3 a(CIT): Robert Newcomb Memorial Lecture, NIH Neuroscience Series Seminar   Dr. Goodrich's lab studies the cellular and molecular mechanisms that govern the assembly of neural circuits, from the differentiation of neurons to the formation and maintenance of axonal connections and ultimately the generation of behavior.  Their overarching goal is to understand how essentially generic developmental events are coordinated so that each type of neuron acquires the uniquely specialized properties that underlie mature circuit function. Many of their studies focus on the auditory system, which is poorly understood relative to the other sensory systems, despite the obvious impact of age-related and noise-induced hearing loss on our society. Additional insights come from studies of the retina, where amacrine cells exhibit diverse shapes and patterns of connections that influence how visual information is processed.  Auditory circuit assembly and function: We perceive sound using precisely wired circuits that originate in the cochlea of the inner ear. The primary auditory neurons - the spiral ganglion neurons (SGNs) - exhibit several distinctive features that ensure that sound information is faithfully communicated from the ear to the brain. For instance, each SGN elaborates enormous and unusually rapid synapses that are critical for sound localization. To learn how spiral ganglion neurons acquire these unique properties, they are dissecting the functions of SGN-specific molecules and transcriptional networks and the contribution of peripheral glia and central input to auditory function. By combining next-gen sequencing technology, live imaging, and behavioral analyses with mouse genetics to visualize and access spiral ganglion neurons, they can link cellular and molecular changes in auditory circuits to functional changes in auditory perception.  Neuronal morphogenesis and wiring in the retina: Unlike the cochlea, the eye houses a complex array of neurons that mediate our sense of vision. The varied morphologies and connectivity of amacrine cells in particular modulate the flow of visual information from photoreceptors to ganglion cells via dendrites restricted to the inner plexiform layer. Since this unipolar morphology is critical to amacrine cell function, they are interested in how this morphology is acquired.  They have shown that reliable formation of this unipolar morphology depends on the atypical cadherin Fat3. Ongoing studies will unravel the cellular and molecular events that are mediated by Fat3: what are the activators and effectors of Fat3? What are the cellular events that ensure development of a single apical dendrite?  They address these questions using a combination of biochemistry, live imaging, and mouse genetics.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed January 17, 2020).12aHearingxphysiology22aNeuronsxphysiology22aSpiral Ganglionxphysiology22aHair Cells, Auditoryxphysiology 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aGoodrich, Lisa,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series.pRobert Whitney Newcomb memorial lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?28890  aAUTH01373cas a2200409 i 101764202DNLM20260227090910.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||190715c20199999ne || poo     0    0eng    a  2020243093  a2590-2075l2590-2075  a(OCoLC)1108654806  aWICbengerdad20260227cWICdOH1dOCLCOdOCLdDNLM  apcc  9Netherlands04aQR100.8.B5500aW110aBiofilm2dnlm00aBiofilm. 1a[Amsterdam] :bElsevier B.V.,c[2019]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with V