e  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1 (March 2020).  aOfficial journal the American Society for Preventive Cardiology.0 aPubMedbv1, Mar. 2020-0 aPMC0 aVolume 1 (March 2020); title from journal information screen (Science Direct, viewed January 8, 2021).1 aVolume 5 (March 2021) (viewed January, 8, 2021).12aCardiovascular Diseasesxprevention & control22aRisk Factors22aPrimary Prevention22aSecondary Prevention 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Society for Preventive Cardiology.40uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/american-journal-of-preventive-cardiology40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101769122  aAUTH02615cgm a2200481 i 9918663285906676DNLM20260227164027.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230815s2023    mdu122|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33354  a(OCoLC)1449667352  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 10000aInteractions between diet, the gut microbiome, and epigenetics that influence health and disease /cmoderator, Howard A. Young ; speakers, Theresa Alenghat, Eva L. Feldman, Brad Ferguson. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aRegulation of intestinal health and disease by diet-microbiota-epigenetic interactions / Theresa Alenghat -- Microbiome and neurodegeneration : deciphering gut-brain crosstalk / Eva L. Feldman -- Dietary epigenetic modifiers in cardiovascular disease : where do we go from here / Brad Ferguson.  aRecorded July 27, 2023.3 a(CIT): This virtual seminar will explore how dietary intake and metabolism by the gut microbiome can alter host epigenetics, potentially shaping phenotype and influencing health and disease. It is a collaboration between the Microbiome, Diet, and Health Interrelationships and Foundational Nutrition Science Implementation Working Groups. These workings groups, along with six other topic-based groups, are charged with assisting the NIH Office of Nutrition Research to implement the Strategic Plan for NIH Nutrition Research.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 16, 2023).12aGastrointestinal Microbiome12aDiet22aNutritional Physiological Phenomena22aBrain-Gut Axis22aEpigenesis, Genetic22aGastrointestinal Tractxmicrobiology 2aWebcast1 aYoung, Howard A.q(Howard Alan),emoderator.1 aAlenghat, Theresa,espeaker.1 aFeldman, Eva L.,espeaker.1 aFerguson, Bradley S.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Nutrition Research,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=50023  aAUTH01785cgm a2200433 i 9918249811406676DNLM20260227161450.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210826s2021    mdu011|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31852  a(OCoLC)1280592603  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 11.100aDr. Samuel Wilson :bhistory of DNA repair /cDr. Samuel Wilson. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (11 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded July 27, 2021.3 a(CIT): DNA Repair Interest Group  Sadly, Dr Samuel Wilson passed away on April 23, 2021.  Tributes by several of his colleagues and links to other information can be found in the DNA Repair Interest Group listserve article from June 25, 2021 https://list.nih.gov/cgi-bin/wa.exe?A2=ind2106d&L=DNAREPAIR-L&P=68.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed October 22, 2021).12aWilson, Samuel H.,d1939-2021.12aDNA Repair12aBiotechnologyxhisto