apcc  9Poland00aQH57300aW100aMedical journal of cell biology.1 aMJCB 1aWarsaw, Poland :bDe Gruyter Poland,c[2018]-  a1 online resource  aFour times a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 6, issue 1 (January 2018).  aSearch Interface in English and German.  aDescription based on: Volume 6, issue 1 (January 2018); title from journal home page (Sciendo, viewed February 28, 2020).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 9, Issue 2 (June 2021) (Sciendo, viewed September 28, 2021).12aCell Biology22aMolecular Biology 2aPeriodical2 aFoundation for Cell Biology and Molecular Biology,esponsoring body.01tAdvances in cell biology (Warsaw, Poland)x0324-833Xw(OCoLC)179651440uhttps://sciendo.com/journal/ACB  aAUTH01380cgm a22004094a 101267894DNLM20260227145808.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu061 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10907  a(OCoLC)68207233  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQV 1261 aMechoulam, Raphael.10aEndocannabinoids :ban overview /cRaphael Mechoulam.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 19, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 2 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 29, 2001.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aCannabinoid Receptor Modulators22aReceptors, Cannabinoid 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10907  aAUTH03137cgm a2200481 a 101535289DNLM20260227153526.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100702s2010    mdu088|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15984  a(OCoLC)682070809  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWT 1161 aCarstensen, Laura L.10aOBSSR award lecture, June 2010 /cLaura Carstensen.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBSSR lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 28 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 18, 2010.3 a(CIT): We are approaching a watershed moment in human history, when the number of people over 65 in the USA will surpass the number of children under 15.  By the time our children reach old age, living to 100 will be commonplace. Life expectancy increased so quickly that culture has not had time to catch up.  But rest assured, these demographic changes will change virtually all aspects of life - education, families, financial markets and politics. To the extent that people arrive at old age mentally sharp, physically fit, and financially secure, long-lived societies will thrive. But as Matilda White Riley so eloquently argued there is a structural lag between extended years and cultural supports for those added years. I argue that among the most pressing needs of the modern world is the development of "longevity science," namely, advances and discoveries that can form the basis of a culture that uses added years to improve quality of life at all ages.  To that aim, we must increase our knowledge of human development from early to very late life. In particular, we must better understand the aging mind, its strengths and weaknesses so that we can build cultural supports to improve decisions, preferences and planning. Socioemotional selectivity theory - a life-span theory of motivation- maintains that shifts in time horizons have profound and systematic implications for the types of goals people pursue, the decisions they make, and even what they see, hear and remember. In this talk, I will discuss findings from my research program to illustrate one small slice of a longevity science that can ultimately help people anticipate and plan for longer futures.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aLongevity22aCultural Characteristics22aQuality of Life22aSocial Change22aSocial Support 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research. 0aBSSR lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15984  aAUTH01622cas a2200469 i 101767753DNLM20260227090925.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||190410c20199999enkqr poo     0    0eng    a  2020243152  aGBB1979482bnb  a1117887503a1125720121  a2589-9368l2589-9368  a(OCoLC)1096281175  z(OCoLC)1117887503z(OCoLC)1125720121  aVVLbengerdad20260227cVVLdOCLCFdLVTdOH1dOCLCOdVT2dOCLdPFMdOCLCAdDNLM  apcc  9England04aQH52100aW110aMetabol Open2dnlm00aMetabolism open. 1a[Oxford] :bElsevier B.V.,c[2019]-  a1 online resource  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1 (March 2019).0 aPubMedbv1, Mar. 2019-0 aPMC  aPublished in association with: Metabolism.  aDescription based on: Volume 1 (March 2019); title from journal information screen (ScienceDirect, viewed June 17, 2020).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 7 (September 2020) (viewed June 17, 2020).12aMetabolism 2aPeriodical08iComplemented by (work):tMetabolismx0026-0495w(DLC)   55000220w(OCoLC)175717140uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/metabolism-open40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101767753  aAUTH01458cgm a22004334a 101267816DNLM20260227145540.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu102 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10712  a(OCoLC)123686748  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 2701 aMcCaul, Mary E.10aWomen and addictive behaviors /cMary E. McCaul, Dorothy K. Hatsukami.  