3005249990009005579918787482306676DNLM20260212170724.0240401s1957    xxu           000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.571 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aPantothenic acid deficiency /cWilliam B. Bean 1a[United States] :b[publisher not identified],c[1957?]  a1 sheet ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  asheetbnb2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  aPage 293.  aNLM copy, bound 57th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM12aPantothenic Acidxdeficiency  aAUTH03926cgm a2200409 i 101749126DNLM20260227160022.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190604s2019    mdu062|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27484  a(OCoLC)1107446630  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 13504aThe biosynthesis of lipoic acid :ba saga of death, destruction, and rebirth /cSquire J. Booker. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded May 1, 2019.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series  Dr. Booker's laboratory is endeavoring to understand at the molecular level the reaction mechanisms employed by various enzymes, and then to exploit what is learned to impact favorably on human health. A particular focus is to understand the manner in which enzymes bind and use cofactors--whether simple metal ions, complex metal clusters, or small molecules--to increase their catalytic capabilities beyond that which is supported by the functional groups of the 20 naturally occurring amino acids. His lab is particularly interested in understanding the mechanisms of enzymes that use iron-sulfur clusters and/or S-adenosylmethionine in catalysis.  In his lecture, Dr. Booker will describe the workings of Lipoyl synthase (LipA), a member of the radical S-adenosylmethionine (SAM) superfamily of enzymes. LipA catalyzes the sequential insertion of sulfur atoms at C6 and C8 of an amide-linked octanoyl chain on a lipoyl carrier protein (LCP) to afford the lipoyl cofactor. This cofactor plays key roles in several multienzyme complexes that are involved in energy or amino acid metabolism. LipA contains two essential [4Fe-4S] clusters. One of the clusters interacts with SAM to generate two 5"-deoxyadenosyl 5"-radical intermediates that abstract hydrogen atoms from C6 and C8 of the octanoyl chain, while the second cluster is believed to provide the inserted sulfur atom. Therefore, LipA becomes inactivated upon one full turnover due to destruction of its iron-sulfur (Fe/S) cluster cofactor.  Dr. Booker and his colleagues provide strong evidence for this provocative role for an Fe/S cluster in enzymatic catalysis. When his laboratory conducts reactions under conditions wherein only one sulfur atom is inserted into the organic substrate, the researchers find that the LCP substrate becomes cross-linked to LipA through the Fe/S cluster. Extensive characterization of the cross-linked species by Mossbauer spectroscopy and X-ray crystallography shows that it contains three iron ions and three sulfide ions in a cubane-like structure that is connected to the LCP by a bridging monothiolated octanoyl linker. Upon isolation of the species and its reintroduction into a new reaction mixture, formation of the lipoyl product is observed with kinetics that are consistent with the overall rate of catalysis.   For more information go to <a href="https://oir.nih.gov/wals/2018-2019/">https://oir.nih.gov/wals/2018-2019.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed June 28, 2019).12aThioctic Acidxbiosynthesis 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aBooker, Squire J.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27484  aAUTH01734cas a2200493 a 101157645DNLM20260227090922.0cr|cn ||||||||021023c20019999gw br p       0    0ger    a  2003243457  a1617-0288l1617-0288  a(OCoLC)52969837  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 agerbengfeng  alcd  9Germany00aW1bKI622K00aKinder- und Jugendmedizin :bOrgan der Deutschen Akademie für Entwicklungs-Rehabilitation e.V.  aStuttgart ;aNew York :bSchattauer,cc2001-c2018.3132018- :aStuttgart :bThieme  av. :bill.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 a2001/1-  aTitle from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: 2002/2.  aAlso issued online.  aIn German, with summaries and title page in English.  aVols. for 2002/2- issued also by: Sächsisch-Thüringische Gesellschaft für Kinderheilkunde, and: Deutsche Gesellschaft für das Neugeborenenscreening.12aPediatrics22aAdolescent22aChild22aRehabilitation 2aPeriodical2 aDeutsche Akademie für Entwicklungs-Rehabilitation.2 aSächsisch-Thüringische Gesellschaft für Kinderheilkunde.2 aDeutsche Gesellschaft für das Neugeborenenscreening.0 x2567-577X00tSozialpädiatrie--Kinder- und Jugendheilkunde4 uhttps://www.thieme-connect.de/products/ejournals/journal/10.1055/s-00034917  aAUTH00878nam a2200265 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400025000800440018001050600024001231000062001472450060002092640060002693000027003293360026003563370028003823380027004105000019004375000033004565010060004895040041005496500013005909990009006039918787481706676DNLM20260223114052.0240401s1957    xxu      b    000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.661 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aVitamins in health and disease /cWilliam Bennett Bean. 1a[United States] :b[publisher not identified],c[1957?]  apages 160-177 ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  aAt head of title: Chapter 5.  aNLM copy, bound 66th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aVitamins  aAUTH02472cgm a2200469 a 101512275DNLM20260227153114.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090801s2009    mdu058|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15228  a(OCoLC)732773890  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWP 8701 aLove, Susan M.14aThe Love/Avon Army of Women :ba revolutionary on-line resource to accelerate research /cSusan Love ; Office of Science Planning and Assessment.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (58 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded July 21, 2009.3 a(CIT): The Dr. Susan Love Research Foundation and the Avon Foundation for Women have formed a partnership to discover ways to prevent breast cancer. The two foundations formed the Army of Women, a program with a  goal of getting one million to sign up to help advance breast cancer research. Dr. Susan Love will speak about this program and how it can benefit research.  Dr. Susan Love's goal for the Dr. Susan Love Research Foundation is to eradicate breast cancer within our lifetime. She is known worldwide as one of the founding mothers of the breast cancer advocacy movement and sits on the Boards of the National Breast Cancer Coalition, and the Medical Advisory Board of Young Survival Coalition. She is currently the President and Medical Director of the Dr. Susan Love Research Foundation, and founder and board member of Windy Hill Medical, a breast cancer prevention company.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aLove/Avon Army of Women (Project)12aBreast Neoplasmsxprevention & control22aBreast Neoplasmsxetiology22aDatabases as Topic22aPatient Selection22aResearch Subjects 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.).bOffice of Science Planning and Assessment.2 aAvon Foundation for Women.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15228  aAUTH01983cas a22005414a 101122979DNLM20260227090926.0cr|cn#||||||||010620c20019999nyu x p       0    0eng    a  20012150890 a1535-6760l1535-6760  a(OCoLC)47165975  aNSDcNSDdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bJO908FH10aAppl Biosaf2dnlm00aApplied biosafety :bjournal of the American Biological Safety Association.1 iIssues from 2016- have subtitle:aJournal of ABSA International  aMundelein, IL :bAmerican Biological Safety Association,cc2001-2132016-2020 :aThousand Oaks, California :bSage3132021- :aNew Rochelle, NY :bMary Ann Liebert  av.  aTwo no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 6, no. 1 (2001)-  aTitle from cover.0 aPubMedbv24n1, Mar. 2019- ; Selected citations only before this date0 aPMC  aPublication frequency stated as quarterly, but vols. are complete in 2 issues.  aAlso issued online.12aLaboratoriesxstandards22aContainment of Biohazardsxstandards22aLaboratory Infectionxprevention & control22aOccupational Diseasesxprevention & control22aOccupational Health22aSafetyxstandards 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Biological Safety Association.0 x2470-124600tJournal of the American Biological Safety Associationx1091-3505w(DLC)sn   96004895w(OCoLC)357576254 uhttps://www.liebertpub.com/loi/apb4 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/apb4 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101122979  aAUTH02360cgm a2200457 i 9918681342406676DNLM20260227164042.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230929s2023    mdu796|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33416  a(DNLM)CIT:33419  a(OCoLC)1531997154  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWG 3432 aSleep Health and Dysfunction Across the Spectrum of Pulmonary Vascular Disease (Workshop)d(2023 :cOnline)jauthor.10aSleep health and dysfunction across the spectrum of pulmonary vascular disease /cNHLBI, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (13 hr., 16 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded August 30 & 31, 2023.3 a(CIT): Discuss the state of knowledge of sleep health and dysfunction in all five established pulmonary hypertension (PH) groups. Identify gaps in knowledge and critical unmet needs in the role of sleep disordered breathing (SDB), nocturnal hypoxemia and other sleep disturbances (e.g., insomnia, abnormal sleep duration, sleep fragmentation, etc.) in the spectrum of pulmonary vascular disease, including mild pulmonary hypertension. Discuss novel interventions, trial designs and endpoints to test the potential role of sleep interventions in therapy of pulmonary vascular disease (PVD).  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed September 5, 2024).12aHypertension, Pulmonaryxetiology12aSleep Apnea Syndromesxcomplications22aHypertension, Pulmonaryxcomplications22aHypertension, Pulmonaryxtherapy 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=5107040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=51072  aAUTH02324cgm a2200505 i 101692242DNLM20260227155400.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160907s2016    mdu662 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19816  a(DNLM)CIT:19818  a(OCoLC)959602719  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWG 2102 aNHLBI Forum on Heart, Lung, and Sleep Disorders in American Indian/Alaska Native/Native Hawaiian (AI/AN/NH) Youthd(2016 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aNHLBI Forum on Heart, Lung, and Sleep Disorders in American Indian/Alaska Native/Native Hawaiian (AI/AN/NH) Youth /cNHLBI, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (11 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded August 2 & 3, 2016.3 a(CIT): The goal of this forum is to bring together experts to identify knowledge gaps and research opportunities that can facilitate the prevention and future treatment interventions of HLS disease outcomes among American Indian/Alaska Native/Native Hawaiian (AI/AN/NH) youth. The working group will provide a forum for discussion of research gaps and promising opportunities/recommendations for future research in AI/AN/NH youth.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed Sept. 14, 2016)12aHeart Diseasesxethnology22aAdolescent22aIndians, North American22aInuit22aLung Diseasesxethnology22aNative Hawaiian or Pacific Islander22aSleep Wake Disordersxethnology 2aAlaskaxethnology 2aHawaiixethnology 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute,eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1981640uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19818  aAUTH01441cas a2200421 a 101579537DNLM20260227090909.0cr|cn#||||||||120305c20119999bl || p       |    0eng    a  2012243230  a2237-9363l2237-9363  a(OCoLC)780279379  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9Brazil00aW1bRE311EG00aJournal of Coloproctology  aRio de Janeiro :bSociedade Brasileira de Coloproctologia  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 31, no. 3 (July/Sept. 2011).  aPrint issues ceased with 201