ropology00aAnnual review of anthropology.  aPalo Alto Ca :bAnnual Reviews, Inc.  av.billus.  aAnnual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-       1972-  aEditor: 1972-       B. J. Siegel.0 aMEDLINEbSelected citations only0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online. 2aAnthropology1 aSiegel, Bernard J.q(Bernard Joseph),d1913-0 x1545-429000tBiennial review of anthropologyx0067-8503w(DLC)   59-12726w(OCoLC)15329124 uhttp://www.annualreviews.org/loi/anthro  aAUTH03096cgm a2200469 i 101744887DNLM20260227160014.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190402s2019    mdu058|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27398  a(OCoLC)1103822304  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 35800aTranslating cancer genomics to clinical care /cElaine R. Mardis. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (58 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCI Center for Cancer Research eminent lecture series  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded March 25, 2019.3 a(CIT): NCI Center for Cancer Research Eminent Lecture Series  Elaine Mardis, Ph.D., is co-Executive Director of the Institute for Genomic Medicine at Nationwide Children's Hospital and the Nationwide Foundation Endowed Chair of Genomic Medicine. She also is Professor of Pediatrics at The Ohio State University College of Medicine.  Dr. Mardis joined Nationwide Children's Hospital in 2016. Educated at the University of Oklahoma with a B.S. in Zoology and a Ph.D. in Chemistry and Biochemistry, Dr. Mardis did postgraduate work in industry at BioRad Laboratories. She was a member of the faculty of Washington University School of Medicine from 1993-2016.   Dr. Mardis has authored over 350 articles in prestigious peer-reviewed journals and has written book chapters for several medical textbooks.  She serves as an associate editor for three peer-reviewed journals (Disease Models and Mechanisms, Molecular Cancer Research and Annals of Oncology) and is Editor-in-Chief of Molecular Case Studies, published by Cold Spring Harbor Press.  Dr. Mardis has given lectures at scientific meetings worldwide and was awarded the Morton K. Schwartz award from the American Association for Clinical Chemistry in 2016.  She has been listed since 2013 as one of the most highly cited researchers in the world by Thompson Reuters.  Dr. Mardis has been a member of the American Association for Cancer Research (AACR) since 2007, was the program committee chair for the 2018 AACR Annual Meeting and is the AACR President-elect.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed June 5, 2019).12aBrain Neoplasmsxtherapy22aBrain Neoplasmsxgenetics22aChild22aGenetic Therapy22aGenomics 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aMardis, Elaine,d1962-espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aCCR eminent lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27398  aAUTH02012cgm a22004214a 101486640DNLM20260227152558.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081007s2008    mdu288 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14657  a(DNLM)CIT:14661  a(OCoLC)319129762  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.52 aNIH Director's Pioneer Awards Symposiumn(4th :d2008 :cNational Institutes of Health)10aFourth annual NIH director's pioneer award symposium.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Apr. 7, 2009).  aStreaming video (4 hr., 47 min., 36 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 22 & 23, 2008.3 a(CIT): The event features a keynote address by NIH Director Dr. Elias Zerhouni, talks by scientists who received Pioneer Awards in 2007, poster sessions by Pioneer and New Innovator Award recipients, roundtable discussions, and ample opportunities for informal interaction.  At the start of the symposium, Zerhouni will announce the 2008 recipients of NIH Director"s Pioneer and New Innovator awards. Both programs support exceptionally creative scientists who take highly innovative, and often unconventional, approaches to major challenges in biomedical or behavioral research.   http://nihroadmap.nih.gov/pioneer/Symposium2008/index.aspx.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomedical Research22aAwards and Prizes 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1465740uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14661  aAUTH02930cgm a2200469 i 101769630DNLM20260227160103.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u200715s2020    mdu054|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:29165  a(OCoLC)1176511938  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWQ 50004aThe ROSE sustainment study :bimplementing postpartum depression prevention in prenatal agencies serving low-Income women /cJennifer E. Johnson and Caron Zlotnick.1 aImplementing postpartum depression prevention in prenatal agencies serving low-Income women 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (54 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 10, 2020.3 a(CIT): Postpartum depression is a significant and common public health problem for families that affects one in seven women and one in three low-income women. Reach Out, Stay Strong, Essentials for mothers of newborns (ROSE) is an evidence-based intervention that has been found to reduce cases of postpartum depression in low-income women by half. It has recently been recommended by the U.S. Preventive Services Task Force for the prevention of post-partum depression. One of the attractive features of this intervention is that it is designed for and can be successfully implemented in low-resource settings. The ROSE Sustainment (ROSES) Study, funded by NIMH (project number R01 MH114883) is an implementation trial that uses a sequential multiple assignment randomized (SMART) design to evaluate the effectiveness and cost-effectiveness of a stepwise approach to sustainment of ROSE in 90 outpatient agencies providing prenatal care to pregnant women on public assistance. Operational and methodological strategies for conducting a clinical trial in a low resource setting, as well as lessons learned from implementation and scale-up of ROSE in low resource settings will be presented.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 21, 2020).12aDepression, Postpartumxprevention & control22aMaternal Welfarexeconomics22aInfant Welfarexeconomics22aPrenatal Care22aPoverty 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aJohnson, Jennifer E.,espeaker1 aZlotnick, Caron,espeaker2 aNational Institute of Mental Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?29165  aAUTH01669cgm a2200445 i 101770491DNLM20260227160133.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u200811s2020    mdu239|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:29201  a(OCoLC)1191062505  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 5052 aNHLBI COVID-19 Community-engaged Research Roundtabled(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNHLBI COVID-19 Community-engaged Research Roundtable /cNHLBI, NIH ; Gary H. Gibbons [and six others]. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (3 hr., 59 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacar