  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Volume 3, Issue 1 (Nov. 2012).  aAlso issued in print.  aDescription based on: Volume 3, Issue 1 (Nov. 2012); title from journal home page (publisher's Web site, Aug. 25, 2017).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 8, Issue 1 (Feb. 2017) (viewed Aug. 25, 2017).12aPediatrics 2aPeriodical2 aAnjuman-i Pizishkān-i Kūdakān-i Īrān,eissuing body.0 x2251-815000tIranian journal of pediatric societyx2008-520640uhttp://comprped.com/40uhttps://brieflands.com/journals/journal-of-comprehensive-pediatrics/  aAUTH02265cgmaa22004334a 101180558DNLM20260227144300.0vf|cbahouvf|czahmu030530s2003    mdu060       f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:1894  a(OCoLC)53801794  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 142.5.A8bVC no.1 200300aFascinating strategies used by the bacterial pathogen Listeria monocytogenes to establish an infection /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,cc2003]  a1 videocassette of 1 (DVCPro) (60 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3master.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (60 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aWALS--The Margaret Pittman lecture  aTitle from cassette spine label.0 aFaculty, Pascale Cossart.  aPresented May 7, 2003.3 a(Producer) Listeria monocytogenes is responsible for severe food borne infections characterized by gastroenteritis, brain infections, and abortions.  These clinical features reflect the amazing capacity of Listeria to cross the intestinal barrier, the blood-brain barrier, and the feto-placental barrier.  A number of bacterial and cellular components responsible for the infectious process have been identified.  The study of how Listeria controls its capacity to infect cells has led to the discovery of a novel type of RNA mediated regulation.  aVHS.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aListeria monocytogenesxpathogenicity22aListeriosisxetiology1 aCossart, Pascale.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pNIH director's Margaret Pittman lecture4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=1894  aAUTH02889cgm a2200433 i 9918573262006676DNLM20260227163003.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2022    mdu056|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32786  a(OCoLC)1408714447  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 26.55.A700aKnowledge based data science /cLawrence E Hunter. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (56 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 17, 2022.3 a(CIT): Dr. Lawrence Hunter is a Professor of Biomedical Informatics (School of Medicine), Pharmacology (School of Medicine) and Computer Science (Boulder).  He received a Ph.D. in computer science from Yale University in 1989, and then joined the National Institutes of Health as a staff scientist, first at the National Library of Medicine and then at the National Cancer Institute, before coming to Colorado in 2000.  Dr. Hunter is widely recognized as one of the founders of bioinformatics; he published some of the first papers in biomedical NLP and in machine learning predictions of molecular function; he served as the first President of the International Society for Computational Biology (ISCB); and he created several of the most important conferences in the field, including ISMB, PSB and the Rocky Mountain Conference on Bioinformatics. Dr. Hunter's research interests span a wide range of areas, from cognitive science to rational drug design. He has published more than 200 scientific papers, holds two patents and has been elected a fellow of both the ISCB and the American College of Medical Informatics. His primary focus recently has been the integration of natural language processing, knowledge representation, machine learning and advanced visualization techniques to address challenges in interpreting data generated by high throughput molecular biology.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 6, 2023 ).12aKnowledge Bases12aNatural Language Processing22aData Visualization22aData Science22aComputational Biology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aHunter, Lawrence,d1961-espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bText Mining and Natural Language Processing (NLP) Scientific Interest Group,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=48560  aAUTH02295cgm a22004334a 101268462DNLM20260227150745.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu069 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12035  a(OCoLC)84671456  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 102.51 aShadlen, Michael.10aBanburismus and brain :ba neural mechanism for making decisions /cMichael Shalden [sic].  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 17, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 8 min., 6 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 17, 2004.3 a(CIT): Dr. Shadlen's lab records neural activity from the cerebral cortex during performance of a visual discrimination. They have identified sites in the brain where the visual data are represented and sites critical to the expression of a perceptually guided behavior, such as the generation of eye movements. They sneak between these areas by targeting neurons in cortical association areas that reveal a plan to enact a behavior-well in advance of its execution. Since this plan reflects an interpretation of the visual data, their neurophysiological recordings divulge the subject's visual perception or impression. By combining electrophysiological-psychophysical, and computational techniques they can begin to elucidate the underlying neural circuitry.  For more information, visit Michael Shadlen  NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeuronsxphysiology22aDecision Making22aVisual Perception 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12035  aAUTH00813cam a22002657i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003500073044001000108060003300118100004200151245005000193250001400243264005400257300004100311336002600352337002800378338002700406500001700433650004300450650002100493655002400514999000900538101610278DNLM20260209112921.0970328s1957    cc ac  j      000 0 chi    aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1778 no.141 aZhang, Jiaquan,dactive 1957eauthor.10aWo men zu guo de yi xue /cZhang Jiaquan zhu.  aDi 1 ban. 1aShanghai :bShao nian er tong chu ban she,c1957.  a30 pages :billustrations, portraits  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCover title.12aMedicine, Chinese Traditionalxhistory22aHealth Education 2aJuvenile Literature  aAUTH01634cas a2200505   0377775DNLM20260227090907.0cr|cn#||||||||731218c19589999ja mr         0    0eng    a   62051273 zsn 83005609  a0009-2363l0009-2363  aCPBTAL  a(DNLM)C15520000(s)  a(OCoLC)06067231  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Japan00aW1bCH245N10aChem Pharm Bull (Tokyo)2dnlm 0aChemical & pharmaceutical bulletin00aChemical & pharmaceutical bulletin.  aTokyo,bPharmaceutical Society of Japan.  av.billus.  aMonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 6-       Feb. 1958-  aPrint issues ceased with 2022.1 aMEDLINEbv11n8,Aug. 1963-1 aPubMedbv11n8,Aug. 1963-0 aOLDMEDLINE1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aArticles in English, French, or German.  aCompanion publication 1993- to: Biological & pharmaceutical bulletin. 2aPharmacology 2aPeriodical2 aNihon Yakugakkai.0 x1347-522300tPharmaceutical bulletinx0369-9471w(OCoLC)545175108iCompanion publication totBiological & pharmaceutical bulleting1993-x0918-6158w(DLC)   93642605w(OCoLC)277848304 uhttp://cpb.pharm.or.jp/4 uhttps://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/browse/cpb/  aAUTH03141cgm a2200529 i 9918226877306676DNLM20260227161224.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210615s2021    mdu000|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31719  a(DNLM)CIT:31721  a(DNLM)CIT:31730  a(DNLM)CIT:31740  a(OCoLC)1305156093  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 530.12 aLessons from the Field: Confronting the Challenges of Health Research in Humanitarian Crises (Conference)d(2021 May :cOnline)jauthor.10aLessons from the field :bconfronting the challenges of health research in humanitarian crises /cFogarty International Center, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (4 streaming video files (6 hr., 12 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded May 17, 18, 19 & 20 2021.3 a(CIT): In association with the "Lessons from the field: Confronting the challenges of health research in humanitarian crises" collection in BMC Public Health and Conflict and Health, Fogarty"s Center for Global Health Studies (CGHS) will host an online launch event May 17 - 20, 2021. This virtual event will take place in four sessions over four days. Each session will be 90 minutes, and participants will have the option of joining an additional 30-minute breakout discussion and networking session at the end. The events will feature case study authors, Steering Committee members, and experts in the field. The events will help audiences understand best how to utilize the case studies for educational, training and other purposes, as well as highlight the importance of conducting research in the context of humanitarian crises and share some common themes, strategies and lessons learned. Day 1: An introduction to the collection including presentations by four different authors and discussion on the unique challenges encountered and strategies employed to conduct health research in varying crises contexts globally.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 16, 2022).12aGlobal Health12aInterdisciplinary Research22aAltruism22aRelief Work22aRefugeesxpsychology 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast 2aCase Reports2 aFogarty International Center.bCenter for Global Health Studies,ehost institution.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4207140uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4207240uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4207540uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42076  aAUTH01504cgm a22004454a 101267668DNLM20260227145422.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu059 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10536  a(OCoLC)69391295  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWG 1201 aMatthews, Karen A.10aGender differences in heart disease :blet me count the ways /cKaren A. Matthews.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 3, 2006).  aStreaming video (58 min., 32 sec. : sd