ewed Apr. 22, 2010).  aLatest issue consulted: 3 (2012) (viewed Aug. 2, 2012).12aCognitive Science22aMental Processes22aPhilosophy 2aPeriodical0 x1878-515800tEuropean review of philosophyx1098-7274w(DLC)   98658055w(OCoLC)3238372240uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/13164  aAUTH02632cgm a22004454a 101478242DNLM20260227152640.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080702s2008    mdu086|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14547  a(OCoLC)310073507  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 26.51 aFox, Susannah.10aParticipatory medicine :bhow user-generated media are changing Americans' attitudes and actions, both online and offline /cSusannah Fox, Mary Madden.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 18, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 26 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 10, 2008.3 a(CIT): The "participatory Web," also known as the realm of user-generated media and as Web 2.0, is changing the way people gather and share information online. The future of health communication and health care delivery may depend as much on these new technology developments as it does on traditional communication avenues and office visits. Today's Internet users, especially those under age 35, turn to a diverse range of information sources online, many of which are created and maintained by their peers, not by credentialed experts.   Mary Madden and Susannah Fox, researchers at the Pew Internet Project, will discuss the rise of participatory medicine within the context of emerging online trends. They will review the Project's methods, which rely primarily on national telephone surveys. They will then present an analysis of the many ways various demographic groups--such as offline seniors, omni-social teens, mobile-centric young men, and highly wired e-patients--use the Internet. Tracing the growth of blogs, social networking sites, wikis, and other online resources, they will then connect these findings to broader observations about privacy and health care.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aInternetxtrends22aHealth Educationxtrends22aHealth Knowledge, Attitudes, Practice22aInformation Services 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14547  aAUTH02131cas a2200565 a 9421642DNLM20260227091019.0cr|cn#||||||||940304c19949999nyumr p       0    0eng    asn 93003167   a1073-449Xl1073-449X  aAJCMED  a(DNLM)SR0080213(s)  a(OCoLC)29407978  aNSDPcNSDPdDNLM  9United States00aW1bAM521NF10aAm J Respir Crit Care Med2dnlm 0aAmerican journal of respiratory and critical care medicine00aAmerican journal of respiratory and critical care medicine.  aNew York, NY :bAmerican Lung Association,cc1994-3 32000- :aNew York, NY :bAmerican Thoracic Society  av. :bill.  aMonthly,b2025-  aMonthly,b-2001  aSemimonthly,b2002-2024  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 149, no. 1 (Jan. 1994)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv149n1, Jan. 1994-1 aPubMedbv149n1, Jan.1994-1 aAbridged index medicus1 aIndex medicus  aHas frequent supplements.  aAvailable also online.  a"An official journal of the American Thoracic Society, medical section of the American Lung Association", 1994-1999; Jan. 2000-   issued by: American Thoracic Society.  3Vol. 151, no. 3, PT1 (MAR 1995)c19950517fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paperxFurther review is required to determine exactly when acid-free paper was first used for this title 2aCritical Care 2aRespiratory Tract Diseases 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Thoracic Society.2 aAmerican Lung Association.0 x1535-497000tAmerican review of respiratory diseasex0003-0805w(DLC)   18017