       000  0eng    asn 99039926   a1294-9361l1294-9361  aEPDIFP  a(OCoLC)43206751  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9United States00aW1bEP45610aEpileptic Disord2dnlm00aEpileptic disorders :binternational epilepsy journal with videotape.  aMontrouge, France :bJohn Libbey Eurotext3132023- :a[Hoboken] :bWiley  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Mar. 1999)-  aTitle from cover.  aPrint issues ceased with v.19, no. 4 (2017).1 aMEDLINEbv1n1n,Mar. 1999-0 aPubMedbv1n1n,Mar. 1999-1 aIndex medicus  aEach issue accompanied by a videocassette (sd., col. ; 1/2 in.), which supplements selected articles,   -2002.  aVols. for <2003-> accompanied by a CD-ROM.  aAlso issued online.12aEpilepsy 2aPeriodical0 x1950-69454 uhttps://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/journal/195069454 uhttp://www.jle.com/en/revues/epd/revue.phtml  aAUTH02292cgm a22004454a 101322984DNLM20260227152316.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071207s2007    mdu089|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14124  a(OCoLC)190751401  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.51 aRansohoff, David F.10aChaos in the brickyard /cDavid F. Ransohoff.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aMedicine: mind the gap lecture series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Jan. 4, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 29 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 5, 2007.3 a(CIT): "Chaos in the brickyard" refers to a letter that appeared in Science in 1963, in which Bernard K. Forscher described scientific research as the building of brick edifices:  "Once upon a time, among the activities and occupations of man there was an activity called scientific research and the performers of this activity were called scientists. In reality however, these men were builders who constructed edifices, called explanations or laws, by assembling bricks, called facts If the bricks were faulty or if they were assembled badly, the edifice would crumble?"  When investigators from basic and clinical fields collaborate to do translational research, special problems arise in determining whether research results should be considered strong. Examples of current problems will be discussed, along with some rules of evidence and broader principles that may help improve translational research.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomedical Research22aEvidence-Based Medicine22aReproducibility of Results 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aMedicine: mind the gap lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14124  aAUTH01637cas a2200493 i 101627814DNLM20260227090909.0cr||||||||||||131220c20129999enkqr p       0    0eng    a  2013228081  a2050-2877l2050-287722  a(OCoLC)865786143  aCUXbengerdad20260227dCUVdOCLCQdHLSdDNLM  apcc  9England14aQH438.7b.N4900aW1bEU72DEP10aNew Bioeth2dnlm 4aThe new bioethics (Print)04aThe New bioethics :ba multidisciplinary journal of biotechnology and the body. 1aLeeds, UK :bManey Publishing,c2013-3132016- :aAbingdon :bTaylor & Francis  avolumes ;c25 cm  aQuarterly,b2019-  aTwo issues a year,b2012-2015  aThree issues a year,b2016-2018  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 18, Number 1 (May 2012).1 aMEDLINEbv18n1, May 2012-0 aPubMedbv18n1, May 2012-  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on first issue; title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 19, Number 1 (2013).12aReproduction22aBioethical Issues22aGenetic Phenomena 2aPeriodical0 x2050-288500tHuman reproduction and genetic ethicsx1028-7825w(DLC)sn 98053321w(OCoLC)401783824 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/loi/ynbi20  aAUTH02160cgm a2200481 a 101599124DNLM20260227154413.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130103s2012    mdu088|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17720  a(OCoLC)826287943  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3001 aRock, David,d1967-14aThe neuroscience of leadership /cDavid Rock.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 28 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aDeputy director for management (DDM)  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Dec. 13, 2012.3 a(CIT): On behalf of Colleen Barros, Deputy Director for Management (DDM), the NIH Training Center is pleased to announce the first seminar in the 2013 DDM Seminar Series. The seminar will take place Thursday, December 13th from 11:00 a.m. - 12:30 p.m. in Masur Auditorium, Building 10 and will feature speaker Dr. David Rock. Dr. Rock coined the term "NeuroLeadership" and co-founded the NeuroLeadership Institute, a global initiative bringing neuroscientists and leadership experts together to build a new science for leadership development. He will speak about neuroscience as it relates to leadership during times of complexity and uncertainty.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aBrainxphysiology12aLeadership22aDecision Makingxphysiology22aEmotionsxphysiology22aOrganizational Innovation22aProblem Solvingxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aDeputy director for management seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17720  aAUTH02739cgm a2200481 i 9918573266506676DNLM20260227163135.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2023    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32864  a(OCoLC)1425218207  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aHT 152100aConversations on racial and ethnic equity /cOffice of Co