dlPrinted on acid-free paper that meets the full ANSI standard for Permanent Paper 2aMosquito Control 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Mosquito Control Association.0 tJournal of the American Mosquito Control Association. Supplementx1046-3607w(DLC)   90655010w(OCoLC)198565920 x1943-627000tMosquito newsx0027-142Xw(DLC)sn 78004514w(OCoLC)164407515tMosquito systematicsx0091-3669w(OCoLC)169678315tJournal of the American Mosquito Control Association. Supplementx1046-3607w(DLC)   90655010w(OCoLC)198565924 uhttp://www.bioone.org/loi/moco4 uhttps://mosquito-jamca.org/loi/moco  aAUTH01410cam a2200313 i 450000100100000000300050001000500170001500800410003203500200007304000350009304400140012806000190014210000340016124501030019526400950029830000660039333600260045933700280048533800270051350000570054050001380059754001420073558300390087758300720091665000220098865000140101070000630102499900090108762010610RDNLM20260225075605.0820821s1908    ag af         000 0 spa    a(OCoLC)14808558  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9Argentina00aQXbG816a 19081 aGreenway, Daniel J.,eauthor.10aArtrópodos parásitos /cpor el Dr. Daniel Greenway ; con prologo del Prof. Dr. Pedro Lacavera. 1aBuenos Aires :bLa Ciencia Médica, Libreria y Casa Editora de A. Guidi Buffarini,c1908.  aviii, 230 pages ,13 leaves of plates:billustrations ;c28 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aNLM copy, illegible inscription on title page.5DNLM  aNLM copy, title page contains donation information of the Asociación Ogontologica Argentina, Biblioteca, dated July 12, 1957.5DNLM  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202011232pda5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20260225lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM12aArthropod Vectors22aParasites1 aLacavera, Pedro,ewriter of supplementary textual content.  aAUTH03222cgm a2200469 i 9918592678906676DNLM20260227163520.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230429s2023    mdu482|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33071  a(DNLM)CIT:33112  a(OCoLC)1451505539  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWG 3402 aTransforming Hypertension Diagnosis and Management in the Era of Artificial Intelligence (Workshop)d(2023 :cOnline)jauthor.10aTransforming hypertension diagnosis and management in the era of artificial intelligence /cRashmee Shah, Daichi Shimbo, Eric Spatz. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (8 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded March 29 & 30, 2023.3 a(CIT): High blood pressure, known as hypertension, is still a significant health problem worldwide. To better diagnose and manage the disease, healthcare providers need more reliable and exact measurements. Machine learning or artificial intelligence tools could offer better ways to improve current approaches for the detection, monitoring, and management of hypertension. Join the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute for a virtual workshop that brings together health care providers and researchers with expertise in hypertension along with data scientists, engineers, and implementation scientists. Participants will explore data integration strategies, novel technologies, and innovative analytical methods, with the goal of finding ways to improve hypertension detection, monitoring, and management. The workshop features panel discussions on: Bridging the communication gap between population health, clinical medicine, and engineering Improving blood pressure measurement and control and reducing hypertension risk using artificial intelligence to predict, monitor, and treat hypertension. Addressing real world implementation challenges and issues. Other topics include health disparities, novel technologies, data harmonization, electronic health records, clinical decision tools, medication adherence, and clinical and regulatory issues. The workshop also features a keynote presentation on real-world challenges for communities most impacted by hypertension.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 22, 2024).12aHypertensionxdiagnosis12aHypertensionxtherapy22aArtificial Intelligence 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aShah, Rashmee.1 aShimbo, Daichi.1 aSpatz, Eric.2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4930340uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49305  aAUTH02643cgm a2200433 i 9918716282406676DNLM20260227164240.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u231128s2023    mdu698|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33530  a(DNLM)CIT:33533  a(OCoLC)1467157385  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 503.62 aStrategies for an HIV Cure (Meeting)d(2023 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aStrategies for an HIV cure /cNIAID, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (11 hr., 38 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 12 & 13, 2023.3 a(CIT): The National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (NIAID) will host the sixth biennial Strategies for an HIV Cure meeting on October 12-13, 2023. This is a programmatic meeting to review research progress by awardees funded through the NIH Martin Delaney Collaboratories (MDCs) for HIV Cure Research program, foster communication and collaboration between the ten MDCs, and to collect feedback from representatives from each individual MDC Scientific Advisory Board (SAB) and Community Advisory Board (CAB). The two-day in-person meeting will feature presentations from the ten NIH Martin Delaney Collaboratories for HIV Cure Research. In addition, the meeting will include presentations from the two Centers for Innovative HIV/AIDS Vaccine and Cure Research (CIAVCR). It will also feature abstract-driven poster sessions. This meeting is open to all and anyone with interest in HIV cure can participate. There is no cost to attend, but advance registration is required for in-person attendance.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed October 28, 2024).12aHIV Infectionsxprevention & control22aAcquired Immunodeficiency Syndromexprevention & control 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (U.S.).bDivision of AIDS,ehost institution.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=5247940uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=52481  aAUTH01617cas a22004331  58230920RDNLM20260227090926.0cr|cn ||||||||811121c19049999xxuqr         0||| 0eng    a0006-128Xl0006-128X  a(DNLM)P03080000(s)  a(OCoLC)01532703  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aZ 1009bB582p2 aBibliographical Society of America.10aPap Bibliogr Soc Am2dnlm10aPapers.  aNew York.3 aNew York Ny :bBibliographical Society Of America  av.bill., ports.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-    1904/07-  aVols. 1-3 have title: Proceedings and papers.  aIn 1913, the Papers and the Bulletin of the society were combined in one quarterly publication entitled Papers.0 aMEDLINEbSelected citations only0 aPubMedbSelected citations only0 aOLDMEDLINE  aAlso issued online.  aIndex to v. 26-45, 1932-51, issued as the society's Monograph series, no. 2.  Includes List of members, autumn 1953.  Called also a supplement to Index, v. 1-25, 1899-1931.  aBibliography / Collections2 aBibliographical Society of America.tList of members, 1953. 0aMonograph series (Bibliographical Society of America) ;vno. 2.4 uhttps://www.journals.uchicago.edu/toc/pbsa/current  aAUTH02350cgm a2200421 i 9918592679206676DNLM20260227163344.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230429s2023    mdu389|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32997  a(OCoLC)1450522227  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 1402 aRare Disease Day (Conference)d(2023 :cBethesda, Md.)10aRare disease day at NIH 2023 /cNCATS, NIH, Clinical Center, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (6 hr., 29 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded February 28, 2023.3 a(CIT): Rare diseases affect an estimated 30 million people in the United States. On February 28, 2023, NIH will host its annual Rare Disease Day event to raise awareness about these disorders, the people they affect, and NIH collaborations that advance research for new treatments. Sponsored by the National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (NCATS) and the NIH Clinical Center, the event will feature panel discussions, rare diseases stories, and more. Participants can share their thoughts, photos, and experiences via social media using the hashtag #RDDNIH.  Explore exhibits, view scientific posters, and connect with others in this year's event app:   https://app.hopin.com/events/nih-rare-disease-day-2023. For more information go to <a href="https://ncats.nih.gov/rdd">https://ncats.nih.gov/rdd.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed July 25, 2024).12aRare Diseases 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=46435  aAUTH03126cgm a2200457 i 101743237DNLM20260227155953.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190302s2019    mdu101|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27335  a(OCoLC)1105240253  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWF 14300aDemystifying medicine 2019.pMount Everest and K2 :btoo little and too much oxygen /cLouis Reichardt, Toren Finkel.1 aMount Everest and K2 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 41 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded February 19, 2019.3 a(CIT): Approximately 2 billion years ago, a poisonous gas emerged on Earth that wiped out most organisms and sent others deep underground.  That gas was oxygen, O2, and within a geological wink of an eye, through processes poorly understood, oxygen gas levels rose from near zero to 20 percent of the atmosphere.  The freely available oxygen gave way to the evolution of most visible life seen today.    Yet while oxygen is crucial for human life, too much will kill you.  Breathing 100-percent oxygen for long periods damages the lungs and brain; and aging itself may be the result of slow oxygen poisoning, better known as oxidation and free-radical formation.  For this lecture in the Demystifying Medicine series, we bring you speakers who have seen the dangers of too little and too much oxygen.  Mountaineer-scientist Louis Reichardt is the first American to have reached the summits of both Mt. Everest and K2 (only 200 meters shorter than Everest).  Reichardt will present first-hand accounts of climbing without supplemental oxygen above the "death zone" of 8,000 meters and the subsequent risk of hallucinations, blurred vision, and loss of bodily functions.  Then we hear from our old friend Toren Finkel, who left the NIH in 2017 to become the director of the University of Pittsburgh Aging Institute.  Finkel studies how oxygen metabolism, mitochondrial function, and autophagy regulate mammalian aging.  His lab also is developing novel small molecules that may slow or even reverse aspects of aging and may therefore be of benefit for treating a wide array of age-related diseases.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title bar (viewed April 26, 2019).12aHypoxia22aAgingxphysiology22aHyperoxia22aMountaineering22aOxygenxmetabolism 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aReichardt, Louis F.,espeaker.1 aFinkel, Toren,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27335  aAUTH00913nam a2200289 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400025000800420008001050440011001130600015001241100065001392450054002042640032002583000045002903360026003353370028003613380027003895040041004166100055004576100040005126500018005526500033005706510011006039990009006149918938026806676DNLM20260220080938.0241118s2007    bl a     b    000 0 por    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Brazil00aWA 540 DB81 aSergipe (Brazil).bSecretaria de Estado de Saúde,eauthor.10aPlano estadual de saúde :bSergipe : 2004-2007. 1a[Brazil] :bUnescoc[2007?]  a116 pages :billustrations ;c22 x 28 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aSergipe (Brazil).bSecretaria de Estado de Saúde22aSistema Único de Saúde (Brazil)12aHealth Policy22aPublic Health Administration 2aBrazil  aAUTH01415cgm a22004454a 101486648DNLM20260227152750.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081007s2008    mdu114 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14667  a(OCoLC)317248902  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 5031 aMaldarelli, F.q(Frank)10aTRACO.pHIV and metastasis /cF. Maldarelli, K. Hunter.30aHIV and metastasis3 aTranslational Research in Clinical Oncology  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 4, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 53 min., 7 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 22, 2008.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHIV Infections22aBreast Neoplasmsxgenetics22aHuman T-lymphotropic virus 122aNeoplasm Metastasis 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14667  aAUTH02097cas a2200541 i 101620352DNLM20260227090919.0m     o  d        cr |||||||||||130430c20139999mauqr poo     0    0eng    a  2013247789  a2051-3909l2051-3895  a(OCoLC)860781509  aW2Ubengerdad20260227cW2UdOCLCOdPFMdDNLM  apcc  9United States 4aRC78.A1bJ6810aW1bJO754610aJ Med Radiat Sci2dnlm 0aJournal of medical radiation sciences.00aJournal of medical radiation sciences.1 aJMRS 1a[Malden, MA] :bWiley Publishing Asia Pty Ltd,c[2013]-  a1 online resource  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Volume 60, Issue 1 (March 2013).  a"Official publication of the Australian Institute of Radiography and New Zealand Institute of Medical Radiation Technology."1 aMEDLINEbv62n1, Mar. 2015-0 aPubMedbv60, Mar. 2013-0 aPMC  aFormed by the union of: The Radiographer and Shadows (Christchurch).  aDescription based on: Volume 60, Issue 1 (March 2013); title from journal home page (Wiley Web site, viewed December 9, 2013).  aLatest issue consulted: 60 (2013) (viewed Feb. 12, 2014).12aDiagnostic Imaging12aRadiography 2aPeriodical2 aNew Zealand Institute of Medical Radiation Technology,eissuing body.2 aAustralian Institute of Radiography,eissuing body.0 x2051-389504tShadows (Christchurch, N.Z.)x1170-9758w(OCoLC)17335848604tRadiographerx0033-8273w(OCoLC)10441946w(DLC)sn 840094504 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=1016203524 uhttp://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/journal/10.1002/(ISSN)2051-3909  aAUTH03435cgm a2200445 i 9918226878706676DNLM20260227161152.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210615s2021    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31691  a(OCoLC)1309103554  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 2100aConversations with Asian Americans and Pacific Islanders (AAPI) leaders at NIH :btips on leadership and empowering the next generation /cNIH Chapter of Federal Asian Pacific American Council, NIH Office of Equity, Diversity, and Inclusion. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded May 4, 2021.3 a(CIT): The National Institutes of Health (NIH) Chapter of the Federal Asian Pacific American Council (FAPAC) and the NIH Office of Equity, Diversity, and Inclusion (EDI) are pleased to announce a virtual leadership discussion, "Conversations with Asian Americans and Pacific Islanders (AAPI) leaders at NIH: Tips on leadership and empowering the next generation" in celebration of the 2021 AAPI Heritage Month. The purpose of this Director's Leadership Panel is to recognize AAPI leaders who have made a significant impact in the field of public health, and provide a platform where our leaders can share their insights, leadership tips, and vision for the future. These leaders are visible in the AAPI community and beyond, and their work and leadership are focused on empowering the next generation of leaders. As we continue to celebrate AAPI Heritage Month, we encourage you to also check out the campaign to celebrate AAPI Heritage Month via the EDI website (this page will go live on May 1, 2021) The event will feature an introduction from Dr. Lawrence Tabak, D.D.S., Ph.D., Principal Deputy Director, NIH, followed by a conversation between the three panelists. Panel Members: Noni Byrnes, Ph.D., Director of the Center for Scientific Review (CSR), NIH Michael F. Chiang, M.D., Director of the National Eye Institute (NEI), NIH Rena D'Souza, D.D.S., M.S., Ph.D., Director of the National Institute of Dental and Craniofacial Research (NIDCR), NIH Panel Moderator: Christina Liu, Ph.D., P.E., Chief, Biomedical Technology Branch Div. Biophysics, Biomedical Technology &amp; Computational Biosciences National Institute of General Medical Sciences (NIGMS), NIH.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 23, 2022).12aLeadership12aHealth Personnel22aCultural Diversity22aAsian.22aNative Hawaiian or Pacific Islander. 2aUnited States 2aWebcast2 aFederal Asian Pacific American Council (U.S.).bNIH Chapter,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Equity, Diversity, and Inclusion,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41843  aAUTH01824cgm a22004574a 101310934DNLM20260227152127.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070703s2007    mdu050|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13913  a(OCoLC)156863671  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aZ 733.N2771 aSmith, Kent A.q(Kent Ashton),d1938-10aLaws, leaders, and legends of the modern NLM /cKent A. Smith.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 17, 2007).  aStreaming video (50 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jun. 22, 2007.3 a(CIT): On May 23, 2007, Kent A. Smith, former Deputy Director, NLM, gave the 2007 Joseph Leiter MLA/NLM Lecture - "Laws, Leaders and Legends of the Modern NLM" - at the Annual Meeting of the Medical Library Association in Philadelphia. The lecture is named in honor of NLM's first Associate Director for Library Operations.  In response to popular demand, Kent has agreed to reprise the Lecture.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.)12aLibraries, Medicalxhistory22aHistory, 20th Century22aHistory, 21st Century 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aPersonal Narrative 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13913  aAUTH02583cgm a22003974a 101310960DNLM20260227152144.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070703s2007    mdu058|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13915  a(OCoLC)162143265  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 571 aKlinman, Dennis.10aImmunomodulatory oligonucleotides :bimpact on immunity and disease /cDennis Klinman.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Aug. 9, 2007).  aStreaming video (58 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jun. 20, 2007.3 a(CIT): Dr. Dennis Klinman was among the first to identify and characterize immunostimulatory DNA oligonucleotides. Dr. Klinman headed the Section of Viral Immunology at the Center for Biologics Evaluation and Research of the FDA before joining the Cancer and Inflammation Program, Laboratory of Experimental Immunology of the National Cancer Institute in Frederick as chief of the Immunoregulation Group.   His scientific interest remains the understanding the mechanism by which immunostimulatory CpG and immunosuppressive TTAGGG oligodeoxynucleotides (ODN) alters host susceptibility to cancer, inflammation and infectious diseases. His ongoing studies focus on furthering our understanding of the immunomodulatory activity of CpG and suppressive ODN, especially when combined with other immune response modifiers. This includes analyzing the ability of CpG ODN (alone and in combination with other TLR ligands and small molecule immune potentiators) to accelerate and magnify the protective immune response elicited by vaccines targeting infectious pathogens and tumors. Dr. Klinman's research bridges original fundamental studies of receptor-mediated inflammatory responses with translational applications of his findings to the design of vaccine and modulation of the immune response in cancer and infectious diseases.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aOligonucleotidesximmunology12aOligonucleotidesxtherapeutic use 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13915  aAUTH01458cas a2200421 i 9918821689006676DNLM20260227090921.0m     o  d        cr#|||||||||||220831c20229999ne || poo     |    0eng    a  2024228296  a1391825578a1395048569a1417779264  a2949-7043l2949-7043  a(OCoLC)1344163127z(OCoLC)1391825578z(OCoLC)1395048569z(OCoLC)1417779264  aSFBbengerdad20260227cSFBdAUDdOH1dOCLCOdDNLM  apcc  9Netherlands04aRA441.300aW110aSci One Health2dnlm00aScience in One Health.30aOne Health 1a[Amsterdam] :bElsevier B.V.,c[2022]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Volume 1 (November 2022).0 aPubMedbv1, Nov. 2022-0 aPMC  aDescription based on: Volume 1 (November 2022); title from resource description page (ScienceDirect, viewed October 17, 2023).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 3 (2024) (viewed June 6, 2024).12aOne Health 2aPeriodical40uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/science-in-one-health40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=9918821689006676  aAUTH02418ngm a2200481 i 9918975960106676DNLM20260227164959.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u250104s2024    mdu771|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35376  a(DNLM)CIT:35383  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 1002 aBRAIN neuroAI Workshopd(2024 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aBRAIN neuroAI workshop /cOffice of the Brain Director, NINDS. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2024]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (12 hr., 51 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 12, 2024.3 a(CIT):NIH BRAIN NeuroAI Workshop to inform and coordinate future BRAIN research strategies and priority areas to build on recent scientific advances at the intersection of neuroscience and artificial intelligence, an emerging field known as "NeuroAI". This exciting research area is re-examining the fundamental principles of cognition, neural control, and intelligent behavior that may be shared by living organisms and AI systems. Convergent approaches in NeuroAI include data-driven modeling and representational analysis of neural systems; neuromorphic engineering of computing devices and neural interfaces; and new frameworks for understanding how modern AI systems learn and construct knowledge.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 12, 2026).22aBRAIN Initiative.12aNeurosciences12aArtificial Intelligence22aBrainxphysiology22aNeural Networks, Computer22aModels, Neurological 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aBRAIN Initiative,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=5516040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=55262  aAUTH02236cgm a22004814a 101525640DNLM20260227153226.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100302s2010    mdu232 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15605  a(DNLM)CIT:15606  a(OCoLC)572697969  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQV 77104aThe science of small clinical trials lecture series.pIntroduction, fundamentals /cOffice of Rare Diseases Research (NIH) & Office of Orphan Products Development (FDA).30aIntroduction, fundamentals30aFundamentals  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (3 hr., 52 min. : sd., col.).  aA jointly created course that deals with issues arising in the design and analysis of clinical trials based on small study populations.  aRecorded Feb. 16 & 17, 2010.3 a(CIT): Over two lectures, Dr. Day will define/describe basic terms and concepts involved in clinical trials, including randomization, different forms of bias, blinding, the role of placebos, varieties of controls, exploratory data analysis and forms of statistical testing, multiplicity, precision and accuracy, endpoint selection, P-values and confidence limits, repeated measures, stratification, and analysis of covariance; case studies will be presented for Fabry's Disease & Pediatric Inflammatory Bowel Disease.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aClinical Trials as Topicxmethods22aRare Diseases22aResearch Design22aSample Size 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Rare Diseases Research.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1560540uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15606  aAUTH01382cas a2200409 i 101656077DNLM20260227090916.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||150413c20159999ne || poo     |    0eng    a  2015243397  a2352-8532l2352-8532  a(OCoLC)911198133  aHNKbengerdad20260227cHNKdOCLCFdDNLM  apcc  9Netherlands00aW110aAddict Behav Rep2dnlm00aAddictive behaviors reports. 1a[Amsterdam] :bElsevier B.V.,c[2015]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with volume 1 (June 2015).0 aPubMedbv1, 2015-0 aPMC  aNumbering from content page.  aDescription based on: Volume 1 (June 2015); title from journal information screen (ScienceDirect, viewed June 16, 2015).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 2 (December 2015) (viewed September 2015).12aBehavior, Addictive12aSubstance-Related Disorders. 2aPeriodical40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=10165607740uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/addictive-behaviors-reports  aAUTH02905cgm a2200481 i 9918768585006676DNLM20260227164415.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u240209s2024    mdu073|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33752  a(OCoLC)1443712984  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 18500aOptimizing outcomes for patients with chronic illnesses and disabilities /cMatthew Weed. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2024]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 13 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aDEIA lecture series  aAccess restricted to HHS only.  aRecorded January 30, 2024.3 a(CIT): This presentation has three themes. Its overall theme focuses on improving health professionals" scientists" and policy makers" awareness of patients" lived experiences of chronic health needs whether in the clinic or in the home and work environment. By doing so all parties can make the best possible contribution to optimizing outcomes for tens of millions of Americans with chronic health needs. The second theme focus is on improving the understanding of various NIH constituencies on what can and must be done to accommodate patients and their caregivers in the clinical and research environments. The third area of attention is on supporting all aspects of the NIH community in being aware of, and reducing, barriers to optimal care and support through careful thought and preparation whether at the NIH or through the thousands of scientists, physicians and institutions it works with on a daily basis. Doing so can sharply increase the return on investment in research and strengthen scientists" and health professionals" ability to communicate with millions whether they manage diverse health concerns or act as caregivers for those who do.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed June 25, 2024).12aChronic Diseasextherapy22aHealth Services for Persons with Disabilities22aPersons with Disabilitiesxrehabilitation.22aTreatment Outcome22aQuality Improvement 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aWeed, Matthew,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Intramural Research,esponsoring body. 0aDEIA lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=54025  aAUTH01401cas a2200409 i 101642489DNLM20260227090908.0cr||||||||||||130620c20149999enkfr p       |    0eng    a  2013200341  a2329-7018l2329-7018  a(OCoLC)849653767  aDLCcDLCbengerdad20260227dCUDdOCLCOdDNLM  apcc  9England00aW1bRE2145G10aRes Pract Intellect Dev Disabil2dnlm00aResearch and practice in intellectual and developmental disabilities.1 aRAPIDD 1aAbingdon, Oxfordshire :bRoutledge, Taylor & Francis Group,c[2014]-  aSemiannual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1, issue 1 (July 2014).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: Volume 1, issue 1 (July 2014); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 1, issue 1 (July 2014).12aIntellectual Disability2