tive Disorders, Psychotic12aAnxiety Disorders22aMood Disorders2 aAnxiety & Depression Association of America,eissuing body.403ClinicalKeyuhttps://www.clinicalkey.com/dura/browse/journalIssue/29500044/40uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/journal-of-mood-and-anxiety-disorders40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=9918646056406676  aAUTH01091cam a22002777i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003500073044001200108060003300120100004500153245011400198264003800312300007300350336002600423337002800449338002700477500012400504500007500628504004100703650001400744650002700758651001900785999000900804101689898DNLM20260211084232.0160803r18921892au af    b    000 0 ger    aDNLMbengerdad20260211dDNLM  9Austria00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 2120 no.101 aMakowsky, Alexander,d1833-1908eauthor.14aDer Diluviale Mensch im Löss von Brünn :bmit Funden aus der Mammuthzeit /cvon Prof. Alexander Makowsky. 1aBrünn :bCarl Winiker,c[1892?]  apages 74-84, 3 unnumbered leaves of plates :billustrations ;c29 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"Separatabdruck aus Band XXII [der neuen Folge Band XII] der Mittheilungen der Anthropologischen Gesellschaft in Wien."  a"Mittheilungen d. Anthrop. Gesellsch. in Wien. Bd. XXII. 1892"--P. 73.  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aHominidae22aAnthropology, Physical 2aCzech Republic  aAUTH01446cas a2200481 a 101142708DNLM20260227090925.0cr|cn ||||||||020411c20029999sz  x p       0    0eng    a  2002260266  a50561198  a1424-8581l1424-8581  a(OCoLC)50698625  z(OCoLC)50561198  aAKRcAKRdORUdZCHdVAMdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9Switzerland14aQH431b.C9500aW1bCY75510aCytogenet Genome Res2dnlm00aCytogenetic and genome research.18aCytogenetics and genome research  aBasel ;aNew York :bS. Karger,cc2002-  av. :bcol. ill. ;c28 cm.  aSixteen no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 96, no. 1-4 (2002)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv96n1-4, 2002-1 aPubMedbv96n1-4, 2002-1 aIndex medicus  aEach vol. consists of 4 numbers; 4 vols. per year.  aAlso available online.12aCytogenetics22aGenomics 2aPeriodical0 x1424-859X00tCytogenetics and cell geneticsx0301-0171w(DLC)   73644001w(OCoLC)17870304 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/224037  aAUTH04984cgm a22004694a 101306101DNLM20260227151924.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070430s2007    mdu062 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13751  a(OCoLC)137294961  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3001 aRaichle, Marcus E.12aA default mode of brain function :bhistory of an evolving idea /cMarcus E. Raichle.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 17, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 1 min., 44 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 11, 2007.3 a(CIT): The concept of a default mode of brain function arose out of a focused need to explain the appearance of activity decreases in functional neuroimaging data when the control state was passive visual fixation or eyes closed resting.  The problem was particularly compelling because these activity decreases were remarkably consistent across a wide variety of task conditions. Using PET, we determined that these activity decreases did not arise from activations in the resting state. Hence, their presence implied the existence of a default mode.  While the unique constellation of brain areas provoking this analysis has come to be known as the default system, all areas of the brain have a high level of organized default functional activity. Most critically, this work has called attention to the importance of intrinsic functional activity in assessing brain behavior relationships.   Marcus E. Raichle is known for his pioneering research in the development and use of an imaging technique called positron emission tomography (PET) to map specific brain areas used in tasks such as seeing, hearing, reading and remembering as well as emotion. Raichle was a member of the team that developed PET at Washington University during the 1970's. The technique, equivalent to tissue autoradiography but done safely in vivo, allows researchers to safely and non-invasively study the living human brain and track and record its function in health and disease. By using PET to monitor blood flow and metabolism in the human brain, Raichle and his collaborators were able to show how the brain responds when a subject is asked to perform tasks as diverse as memorizing words or anticipating an unpleasant experience.3 aIn addition, they have mapped areas involved in attention, analyzed chemical receptors in the brain, investigated the physiology of major depression and anxiety and evaluated patients at risk for stroke. Parallel work by Raichle and his colleagues evaluated the relationship between blood flow, metabolism and neuronal activity in the human brain. This work uncovered the fact that blood flow exceeds the brain's oxygen requirements normally during increased neuronal activity. This fact provides the physiological basis for functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI). fMRI is now the primary technique used in mapping the functional architecture of the human brain. Raichle's most recent work has focused on the brain's intrinsic activity, that which it is doing when not responding to the momentary demands of environment. Raichle, a neurologist, joined the faculty at Washington University as a research instructor in Neurology and Radiology in 1971. He became a professor of Neurology in 1978, professor of Radiology in 1979, professor of Biomedical Engineering in 1979 and professor of Neurobiology in 1993. He received a bachelor and medical degree from University of Washington in Seattle. He was honored by the University of Washington as their Distinguished Alumnus in 2006.   His honors include election to the Institute of Medicine in 1991, to the National Academy of Sciences in 1996 and to the American Academy of Arts and Sciences in 1998. He has also received the Bristol-Myers Squibb Award for Distinguished Achievement in Neuroscience Research and the Grawemeyer Award for Psychology.     The NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series includes weekly scientific talks by some of the top researchers in the biomedical sciences worldwide.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBrainxphysiology22aBrain Mapping22aMagnetic Resonance Imaging22aPositron-Emission Tomography 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13751  aAUTH01376cgm a2200457 a 101556005DNLM20260227153759.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110209s2010    mdu048|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16315  a(OCoLC)711828664  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWD 2101 aYanovski, Jack.10aLeptin signaling pathway genes and pediatric obesity /cJack Yanovski.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (48 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 3, 2010.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aObesityxgenetics22aAdolescent22aChild22aLeptinxmetabolism22aObesityxetiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16315  aAUTH01382cgm a22004334a 101296461DNLM20260227151721.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070103s2006    mdu118 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13468  a(OCoLC)104778978  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2001 aYeh, Grace Chao.10aRedox biology.pChemoprevention-cancer therapy  /cYeh and Mitchell.30aChemoprevention-cancer therapy  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 13, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 57 min., 35 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 31, 2006.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxprevention & control22aCarcinogens, Environmental22aChemoprevention22aNeoplasmsxradiotherapy 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13468  aAUTH01518cas a2200469   0315073DNLM20260227091101.0cr|cn ||||||||720228c19719999pau x         0    0eng    a0367-0244l0367-0244  aECFNBN  a(DNLM)E01160000(s)  a(OCoLC)01285522  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bEC91710aEcol Food Nutr2dnlm00aEcology of food and nutrition.  aNew York,bGordon and Breach Science Publishers ltd.3 32002- :aPhiladelphia, PA :bTaylor & Francis  av.billus.  aSix no. a year,b2000-  aFour no. a year,b1971-1999  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-       Nov. 1971-  aPlace of publication varies.  aPrint issues ceased with v. 53 (2014).1 aMEDLINEbv48n1, Jan.-Feb. 2009-0 aPubMedbv48n1, Jan.-Feb. 2009-  aAlso issued online.  aParent journal 1995-   to the monographic series: Culture and ecology of food and nutrition. 2aEcology 2aFood 2aNutritional Physiological Phenomena 2aPeriodical0 x1543-52371 tCulture and ecology of food and nutritiong1995-w(DLC)sn 970339974 uhttps://www.tandfonline.com/loi/gefn20  aAUTH03434cgm a2200433 i 101649927DNLM20260227154939.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u141224s2014    mdu072|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18778  a(OCoLC)904787083  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWW 1051 aMovshon, J. Anthony,eauthor.10aCortical and perceptual processing of visual form /cJoseph Anthony Movshon. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 12 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Dec. 15, 2014.3 a(CIT): Dr. Joseph Anthony Movshon received his doctorate from Cambridge University, where he studied visual neurophysiology and psychophysics. He joined the faculty of the Department of Psychology at New York University in 1975, and have remained here apart from a sabbatical year spent at Oxford University. In 1986, his laboratory moved to the newly-formed Center for Neural Science.  He is interested in how the brain encodes and decodes visual information, and in the mechanisms that put that information to use in the control of behavior. His research concerns the function and development of the primate visual system, especially the visual areas of the cerebral cortex. His laboratory supports work on neurophysiology, neuroanatomy, psychophysics, and imaging; the main experimental tool is electrophysiological recording from single neurons in monkeys. They stress analytical and quantitative approaches to the study of visual receptive fields. Conceptually, much of this research draws on related work in visual psychophysics, and on computational approaches to understanding brain organization and visual processing.  His laboratory works mostly in two broad areas. The first is concerned with analysis of the functions of the visual areas of the primate cerebral cortex, with special emphasis on the roles of those areas in processing information about visual motion, form, and color. He is particularly interested in the relationships between visual signals in these areas, and the perceptual decisions and motor activity they support. The second group of projects focuses on the functional development of the cortical visual system, and on the way that development is affected by forms of abnormal early visual experience that produce a visual system disorder known as amblyopia. Much of their work seeks to uncover the links between brain activity and behavior by studying the relationship between neural activity in the visual system and its perceptual and motor consequences.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed Feb. 13, 2015.12aVisual Perceptionxphysiology22aVisual Cortexxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18778  aAUTH01475cgm a22004574a 101296475DNLM20260227151829.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070103s2006    mdu129 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13551  a(OCoLC)85817788  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2061 aHawk, Ernest T.10aTRACO.pVaccines and translational research /cE. Hawk and J. Schiller.30aVaccines and translational research3 aTranslational Research in Clinical Oncology  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 5, 2007).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 8 min., 32 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 11, 2006.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomedical Research12aNeoplasms22aCancer Vaccines22aPapillomaviridae22aUterine Cervical Neoplasmsxprevention & control 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13551  aAUTH01415cgm a2200445 a 101501589DNLM20260227153046.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090407s2009    mdu072|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14955  a(OCoLC)720559132  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWK 4001 aSinger, Alfred.10aT cells that see antigens independently of MHC /cAlfred Singer.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCI's Center for Cancer Research grand rounds  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 12 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Mar. 10, 2009.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aThymus Glandximmunology22aMajor Histocompatibility Complex22aReceptors, Antigen, T-Cell 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14955  aAUTH01641cgm a22004094a 101556022DNLM20260227153817.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110209s2011    mdu069|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16409  a(OCoLC)728457058  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5681 aMellman, Ira10aCell biology of antigen presentation by dendritic cells /cIra Mellman ; Immunology Interest Group.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video (1 hr., 9 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Jan. 19, 2011.3 a(CIT): The Immunology Interest Group (IIG) organizes activities designed to promote information exchange and interactions among NIH scientists interested in the field of immunology, broadly defined.  Interactions are facilitated via weekly meetings on current topics as well as an Annual Immunology Retreat.  The Immunology Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDendritic Cells22aAntigen Presentation 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16409  aAUTH02388cgm a2200457 i 9918716284106676DNLM20260227164215.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u231128s2023    mdu639|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33502  a(DNLM)CIT:33510  a(OCoLC)1513109165  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWT 1572 aAnti-Beta-Amyloid Passive Immunotherapy for Alzheimer's Dementia and Amyloid Related Imaging Abnormalities (ARIAS): What's Next (Workshop)d(2023 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aAnti-beta-amyloid passive immunotherapy for Alzheimer's dementia and amyloid related imaging abnormalities (ARIA) :bwhat's next? /cNINDS, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (10 hr., 39 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 28 & 29, 2023.3 a(CIT): This workshop brings together leading experts and researchers from around the world to discuss the latest advancements and challenges in the field of anti-beta amyloid passive immunotherapy. Goals of this workshop are to identify scientific gaps and opportunities, discuss potential animal models, learn how to identify patients at risk for serious adverse events, understand the role for comorbidities and their treatments, and identify potential biomarkers or targets to protect patients at risk.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 28, 2025).12aAlzheimer Diseasexdrug therapy22aPlaque, Amyloidxdrug therapy22aAntibodies, Monoclonal, Humanizedxadverse effects22aAmyloid beta-Peptidesxdrug effects22aImmunotherapyxadverse effects 2aCo