ntent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Jan. 1992)-  aTitle from cover.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online. 2aIll-Housed Persons. 2aSocial Problemsxpsychology 2aStress, Psychological 2aPeriodical0 x1573-658X4 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/loi/ysdh20  aAUTH03078cgm a2200469 i 101693826DNLM20260227155651.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u161005s2016    mdu072|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19866  a(OCoLC)969433970  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 10200aDissecting the neural circuits that mediate motivated behavior /cGarret Stuber. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 12 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aRecorded September 19, 2016.3 a(CIT): NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series  Dr. Stuber lab's primary research goal is to further delineate the synaptic mechanisms and functional neural circuitry that underlie motivated behavioral processes that are perturbed in neuropsychiatric disorders such as addiction, depression, and eating disorders.  Throughout his graduate and postdoctoral training, he utilized both in vivo and in vitro methodologies to study rapid dopamine signaling as well as synaptic plasticity during or immediately following behavior.  His lab's current research focuses on the synaptic and neural circuit mechanisms that underlie adaptive and maladaptive behavioral states.  To accomplish this, they employ multiple cutting-edge techniques both in vivo and in vitro.  Specifically, they use in vivo fast-scan cyclic voltammetry to monitor the release of dopamine, in vivo electrophysiology to monitor neural activity during behavior, and ex vivo patch-clamp electrophysiology to measure additional synaptic alterations following learning.  These experimental approaches also incorporate optogenetic stimulation or inhibition in vitro and in vivo to allow for pathway-specific perturbation of neural function.  Collectively, his lab aims to generate a holistic understanding of neural circuit function that underlies complex motivated behavioral processing, with the long term of goal of identifying novel neural circuit elements to therapeutically target for the treatment of neuropsychiatric illnesses.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed Oct. 14, 2016).12aNeural Pathwaysxphysiology22aBehavior, Animal22aBrainxphysiology22aHormonesxphysiology22aMotivationxphysiology22aNeuronsxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aStuber, Garret,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19866  aAUTH02200cgm a22004574a 101267644DNLM20260227145706.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu030  |||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10509  a(OCoLC)84671099  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 29500a9-11 :bthe Public Health Service response /cProgram Support Center, Division of Commissioned Personnel.1 iScreen title:aSeptember 11  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 30, 2006).  aStreaming video (29 min., 39 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sep. 10, 2002.3 a(CIT): The Program Support Center Division of Commissioned Personnel is pleased to announce the presentation of  "9-11: The Public Health Service Response."  From eyewitness accounts, to care given at the World Trade Center, to emotional remembrances of those searching for their loved ones, this 30-minute documentary tells the story of the PHS Commissioned Corps response in the officers' own words. The documentary also highlights the anthrax investigation and medical response.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aUnited States.bPublic Health Service.bCommissioned Corps12aCivil Defensexhistory22aPublic Health Practicexhistory22aSeptember 11 Terrorist Attacksxhistory22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aUnited States 2aPersonal Narrative 2aWebcast2 aDepartment of Health & Human Services Program Support Center (U.S.).bDivision of Commissioned Personnel.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10509  aAUTH01418cgm a22004094a 101267708DNLM20260227145648.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s1999    mdu028 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10585  a(OCoLC)70051255  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 180.5.Y31 aDrotschmann, Karin.10aMutator phenotype of yeast strains heterozygous for mutations in the MSH2 gene /cKarin Drotschmann.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c1999]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 10, 2006).  aStreaming video (27 min., 48 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 23, 1999.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This videocast is the work of the United States Government and no copyright exists.  This may be disseminated freely.12aYeastsxgenetics22aHeterozygote22aMutation22aMutS Homolog 2 Protein 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10585  aAUTH01629cgm a22004694a 101268311DNLM20260227150529.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu061 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11710  a(OCoLC)71138947  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWB 1021 