oscience seminar  aRecorded September 18, 2017.3 a(CIT): NIH Neuroscience Series Seminar  His lab studies the neural ensemble dynamics of sensori-motor processes in awake rodents, combining behavior, multi-neuronal electrophysiology, complex analysis and modeling, pharmacology and optogenetics to probe ongoing spiking activity in real-time. The goal is to eventually move our understanding of this activity forward to the point at which it can be understood online, in single trials, and without reference to external benchmarks (stimulus onset time, for instance) that the animal doesn"t actually know.  The cornerstone of his lab work involves examination of the neural responses to gustatory (taste) stimuli, which are unique in their reliable non-arbitrariness: a gustatory stimulus hits the tongue laden with meaning--each causes an emotional response (yum or yuck), and each causes a behavior (consumption or rejection); much of our research plumbs these processes.  Furthermore, the potency of taste stimuli is such that rats quickly learn about their properties--whether they poison or nourish--and readily learn ABOUT visual and auditory stimuli that are PAIRED with them. They study these processes as well a pursuit which has led them into some experiments that don"t involve taste at all.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed October 10, 2017).12aSensorimotor Cortexxphysiology12aTaste Perceptionxphysiology22aMotor Neuronsxphysiology22aNeural Conductionxphysiology22aNeural Pathwaysxphysiology22aOlfactory Perceptionxphysiology22aPopulation Dynamics 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aKatz, Donaldc(Professor of psychology)espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23467  aAUTH02032cgm a2200469 i 101775903DNLM20260227160550.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2020    mdu043|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31436  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 53600a2020 Nobel lecture in physiology or medicine by Dr. Harvey J. Alter.1 aTwo thousand twenty Nobel lecture in physiology or medicine by Dr. Harvey J. Alter 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (43 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 19, 2020.3 a(CIT): Nobel Laureate Dr. Harvey J. Alter delivers his official Nobel Lecture which describes the history and background of his contributions to the discovery of the hepatitis C virus.   Dr. Alter is a Senior Scholar at the NIH Clinical Center's Department of Transfusion Medicine and shares the award with Michael Houghton, Ph.D., University of Alberta, Canada, and Charles M. Rice, Ph.D., Rockefeller University, New York City.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 25, 2021).12aAlter, Harvey J.12aHepatitis Cxhistory22aPhysiciansxhistory22aTransfusion Reactionxhistory22aHepacivirus22aNobel Prize22aHistory, 20th Century22aHistory, 21st Century 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aAlter, Harvey J.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41230  aAUTH02109cgm a2200457 i 101776734DNLM20260227160803.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210302s2021    mdu019|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31547  a(OCoLC)1249794494  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 50600aPresident Joe Biden visits the NIH campus :bFebruary 11, 2021. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (19 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded February 11, 2021.3 a(CIT): President Joe Biden will visit the NIH campus to deliver remarks recognizing the hard work and outstanding contributions NIH staff have made to combatting the COVID-19 pandemic. This event comes on the heels of Vice President Kamala Harris" visit for her second vaccine dose two weeks ago, and First Lady Dr. Jill Biden's virtual visit with NCI cancer researchers last Wednesday. As we move forward with our tireless efforts to advance therapeutics, vaccines, and diagnostics for COVID-19, we are heartened by the administration's acknowledgement of NIH and the amazing, ground-breaking science that we conduct and support.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 29, 2021).12aBiden, Joseph R.,cJr.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aCOVID-1912aPandemics22aCOVID-19 Vaccines22aMass Vaccinationxorganization & administration22aBiomedical Research22aAnniversaries and Special Events 2aUnited States 2aAddress 2aWebcast40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41581  aAUTH01972cgm a2200421 i 101775915DNLM20260227160620.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2020    mdu424|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31464  a(OCoLC)1241124979  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 309 AA12 aORWH 30th Anniversary Scientific Symposiumd(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aORWH 30th Anniversary Scientific Symposium /cORWH, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (7 hr., 4 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded December 15, 2020.3 a(CIT): This symposium, titled "Advancing the Health of Women Through Science," will feature opening remarks by NIH Director Dr. Francis Collins, a keynote address on women's mental health across the life course, and a panel of directors from several NIH Institutes and Centers discussing their perspectives on women's health. Additional presentations will discuss high-priority women's health topics, such as applying the principle of studying sex as a biological variable across scientific disciplines.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 3, 2021).12aWomen's Health22aBiomedical Research22aSex Factors 2aUnited States 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Research on Women's Health,eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40060  aAUTH02278cgm a2200469 i 101738446DNLM20260227155830.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u181205s2018    mdu064|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:26160  a(OCoLC)1084802551  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 46000aTravelling through tissues one cell at a time :bthe Human Cell Atlas and barrier tissues /cSarah Teichmann.1 iTitle on PowerPoint:aCell atlas technologies & the maternal-fetal interface 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2018]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 4 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded October 31, 2018.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series  Genomics has undergone a resolution revolution, such that the nucleic acid content of individual cells are now sequenced routinely in high-throughput mode. Thus we can define the cellular composition of tissues in an unbiased and comprehensive way using single-cell transcriptomics, revealing the molecular fingerprint of cell states and their predicted signaling circuits in tissues across development and disease. Dr. Teichmann will illustrate this with the maternal-fetal interface in the first-trimester placenta and decidua.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed January 25, 2019).12aGenomicsxmethods22aAtlases as Topic22aGene Expression Profiling.22aPlacentaxcytology22aSingle-Cell Analysis 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aTeichmann, Sarah,d1975-espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?26160  aAUTH01626cas a2200505   7513292DNLM20260227090921.0cr|cn ||||||||751212c19699999fluqr p       0    0eng    a0091-3022l0091-3022  aFNEDA7  a(DNLM)F14280000(s)  a(OCoLC)01388525  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bFR946B10aFront Neuroendocrinol2dnlm 0aFrontiers in neuroendrocrinology00aFrontiers in neuroendocrinology.  aNew York,bRaven Press [etc.]3 aOrlando, FL :bAcademic Press  av.bill., ports.  aQuarterly  aBiennialb1982-1988  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 a1969-  aEditors: v. 1-    W. F. Ganong and L. Martini.  aPlace of publication varies.1 aMEDLINEb1973; v13n1, 1992-1 aPubMedb1973; v13n1, 1992-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3Vol. 16, no. 1 (1995)c19950516fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paperxFurther review is required to determine exactly when acid-free paper was first used for this title 2aEndocrinology 2aNeurology 2aPsychophysiology 2aPeriodical1 aGanong, William F.1 aMartini, Luciano,d1927-2017.0 x1095-68084 uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/00913022  aAUTH01404cgm a22004334a 101294864DNLM20260227151633.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061206s2006    mdu070|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13311  a(OCoLC)84716043  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWI 2501 aRothenberg, Marc E.,d1961-10aEosinophilic esophagitis :bpathogenesis 