 Phys Chem2dnlm00aAnnual review of physical chemistry.  aPalo Alto Ca :bAnnual Reviews  av.bill., ports.  aAnnual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-    1950-2 aMEDLINEbv39, 1988-0 aPubMedbv39, 1988-2 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3(1989)c19890821fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper that meets the full ANSI standard for Permanent Paper 2aChemistry0 x1545-15934 uhttp://www.annualreviews.org/loi/physchem  aAUTH02209cgm a22004334a 101496149DNLM20260227152911.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090203s2009    mdu070 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14844  a(OCoLC)319854703  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 58.71 aAmbros, Victor.10aMicroRNA pathways in animal development /cVictor Ambros.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 10, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 9 min., 38 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 7, 2009.3 a(CIT): MicroRNAs are small regulatory RNA molecules, encoded in the genome, that base-pair with specific messenger RNAs and control their translation. MicroRNAs function in diverse physiological and developmental processes in animals, and in the nematode C. elegans, certain microRNAs figure prominently in the control of developmental timing by the heterochronic gene pathway. The genetic control of C. elegans developmental timing has provided a powerful model system for the genetic analysis of the function of certain microRNAs that are highly conserved between worms and mammals. C. elegans developmental timing mutants have moreover identified proteins that are part of the regulatory machinery associated with microRNAs and that could serve to mediate the regulation of microRNA activity by signaling pathways.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMicroRNAs22aGene Expression Regulation, Developmental 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14844  aAUTH01761cas a2200505 a 101595366DNLM20260227090912.0m     o  d        cr#|||||||||||121106c20139999mau|| poo     0    0eng    a  2013220250  a2049-632Xl2049-632X  a(OCoLC)823140442  aAGLbengcAGLdBUFdUMCdBUFdDNLM  apcc  9United States14aQR46b.P38410aW1bFE548S0 aQR180b.F4610aPathog Dis2dnlm00aPathogens and disease.13aPAD  a[Malden, MA] :bWiley-Blackwell,c[2013]-3 32015- :aOxfordbOxford University Press  aAnnual,b2022-  aNine issues a year,b-2021  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 67, Issue 1 (Feb. 2013).1 aMEDLINEbv67n1, Feb. 2013-0 aPubMedbv67n1, Feb. 2013-0 aPMC  aPublished on behalf of the Federation of European Microbiological Societies.  aDescription based: Volume 67, Issue 1 (Feb. 2013); title from cover image (Wiley Online Library, viewed Feb. 25, 2013).  aLatest issue consulted: 67 (2013) (viewed Apr. 24, 2013).12aDisease22aMicrobiological Phenomena 2aPeriodical2 aFederation of European Microbiological Societies.00tFEMS immunology and medical microbiologyx0928-8244w(DLC)sn 93003666w(OCoLC)2744779740uhttps://academic.oup.com/femspd40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101595366  aAUTH01419cas a2200409 a 101472729DNLM20260224111559.0cr|cn#||||||||080430c20079999ii || p       |    0eng    a  2008243479  a0973-614Xl0973-614X  a(OCoLC)227312312  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9India00aW1bUL751R00aDonald School journal of ultrasound in obstetrics and gynecology.13aJournal of ultrasound in obstetrics and gynecology  aNew Delhi :bJaypee Brothers Medical Publishers