      0||| 0eng    a0964-1866l0964-1866  a(DNLM)SR0075540(s)  a(OCoLC)26983998  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9England00aW1bTH13410aTher Communities2dnlm00aTherapeutic communities.  a[London] :bAssociation of Therapeutic Communities,c[1992-3 a[East Sussex, UK] :bEmerald Pub.  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 13, no. 1 (spring 1992)-  aQuarterly.  aTitle from cover.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.  aContinues: International journal of therapeutic communities. 2aCommunity Mental Health Centers 2aTherapeutic Community 2aPeriodical2 aAssociation of Therapeutic Communities.0 x2052-473010tInternational journal of therapeutic communities4 uhttps://www.emerald.com/insight/publication/issn/0964-1866  aAUTH02511cgm a2200445 i 9918573261206676DNLM20260227163013.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2022    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32793  a(OCoLC)1395882985  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 695.12 aLessons Learned from Global Food and Nutrition Insecurity Webinar Seriesn(3rd :d2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aGlobal food/nutrition insecurity :busing implementation science to address food and nutrition insecurity /cFIC, NIH.1 aUsing implementation science to address food and nutrition insecurity 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 21, 2022.3 a(CIT): The goal is to highlight innovative research projects on nutrition and food insecurity and health outcomes in global settings and identify lessons learned and opportunities for adaption in the US. The objectives are to understand the multi-sector factors and the relationship of food insecurity, diet quality and safety and eating patterns, and diet-related diseases in the international contexts; Explore definition and measurement approaches to food and nutrition insecurity; Learn about innovative research interventions, methodology, and program initiatives across different countries to help address food insecurity and promote health equity in different settings; and Explore gaps, challenges, and opportunities for promoting implementation science research and potential dissemination of impactful food security research interventions.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 22, 2023).12aFood Insecurity22aMalnutrition22aGlobal Health22aImplementation Science22aHealth Plan Implementation 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aFogarty International Center,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=46305  aAUTH01331cgm a22003854a 101267830DNLM20260227145636.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu066 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10743  a(OCoLC)65360776  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 26.51 aLuke, Brian.10aExtracting information from large datasets /cBrian Luke.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 15, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 5 min., 32 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 13, 2003.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aComputational Biology22aInformation Storage and Retrieval 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10743  aAUTH02739cgm a2200469 i 101631786DNLM20260227154735.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140501s2014    mdu056 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18380  a(OCoLC)879769337  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 102.31 aTemple, Sally,eauthor.10aAge-related changes in the adult neural stem cell niche /cSally Temple.1 iPowerPoint title:aAging in the neural stem cell niche 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (55 min., 34 sec.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Apr. 7, 2014.3 a(CIT): Neuroscience Seminar Series   In 1989, Dr. Temple discovered that the embryonic mammalian brain contained a rare stem-like cell. Since then she has continued to make pioneering contributions to the field of stem cell research, focusing on the question of how neural stem cells alter their developmental potential over time to generate diverse progeny.  Her research indicates that internal counting mechanisms, rather than external signals in the progenitors, determine the number of cell divisions they undergo.  She has identified markers on progenitor cell lines and external signaling molecules that participate in maintaining and differentiating them.   Dr. Temple's studies suggest that the limited success to date of embryonic stem cell transplants to repair neural damage could be due to introduction of stem cells at the wrong stage of development. It may be that progenitor cells further along in their differentiation will prove more effective. Through her research on the differentiation of neural progenitors, Dr. Temple brings us closer to developing effective clinical treatments for central nervous system damage due to trauma, neurodegenerative diseases, malignancy, or stroke.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed May 7, 2014.12aNeural Stem Cells22aAdult Stem Cells22aAging22aStem Cell Niche 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18380  aAUTH01161cam a2200337   0075032DNLM20211203182610.0670322m19631968oku||||  |||| 00| 0|eng    aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aZWD 700bC642a2 aCivil Aeromedical Research Institute (U.S.)10aAnnotated bibliography of recently translated material.3 aAviation medicine translations  aOklahoma City,c1963-1968.  a5 v.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aIts Technical publication, no. 1, etc.1 aU. S.  Office of Aviation Medicine.    AM 64-16, etc  aNo. 1- prepared by William E. Collins [et al.] 2aAerospace Medicine 2aTranslations 2aAbstracts1 aCollins, William E.q(William Edward),d1932-2025. 0aTechnical publication (Civil Aeromedical Research Institute (U.S.)) ;vno. 1. 0aU. S.  Office of Aviation Medicine.    AM 64-16, etc.  aNASRELb19991201  aAUTH02074cgm a2200481 i 9918573161406676DNLM20260227163226.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2023    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32916  a(OCoLC)1417059651  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 81000aHow to make vaccines :bshould the information be publicly available? /cMaria Elena Bozzatti, Ezekiel Emanuel. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aEthics grand rounds1 aCC grand rounds  aRecorded February 1, 2023.3 a(CIT): How to Make Vaccines: Should the Information be Publicly Available?   Presenter: Maria Elena Bottazzi PhD Professor of Pediatrics and Co-Director, Texas Children"s Hospital Center for Vaccine Development at Baylor College of Medicine  D