8901 aNahin, Richard L.10aTrends in the use of complementary and alternative medicine :bdata from the National Health Interview Survey /cRichard Nahin.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (58 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 1, 2009.3 a(CIT): The National Health Interview Survey is the primary U.S. Government survey to monitor the Nation"s health on an annual basis. In both 2002 and 2007, a large set of supplemental questions were added to the NHIS in order to capture data on the U.S. public's use of complementary and alternative medicine (CAM). Using data from the 2002 and 2007 NHIS, we will explore what types of CAM therapies are used, the reasons for this use, the cost of this use, and the relation of this use to the use of conventional treatments. The impact of these data on the research community, on policy makers, and on health care insurance coverage will be discussed.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Health Interview Survey (U.S.)12aComplementary Therapiesxtrends 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15140  aAUTH01741cas a2200469 i 101643593DNLM20260227090920.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||940211c19889999nyutr poo     0   a0eng    a  2013201370  a2329-972Xl2329-9711  a(OCoLC)761993765  aAU@bengcAU@erdad20260227dMMUdDLCdPFMdOCLCFdDNLM  apccansdp  9United States10aISSN RECORD00aW100a616.4422310aClin Thyroidol2dnlm 0aClinical thyroidologyb(Online)00aClinical thyroidology. 1aSt. Petersburg, FL :bDaniels Pharmaceuticals,c1988-20003132014- :aNew Rochelle, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.  avolumes ;c28 cm  aMonthly,b2009-  aThree issues per year,b1988-2008  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aPrint began with Volume 1, Issue 1 (Jan./Apr. 1988).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aDescription based on: Volume 13, Issue 1 (March 2001); title from resource home page (American Thyroid Association website, viewed on June 3, 2013).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 27, Issue 9 (September 2015) (Liebert website, viewed October 6, 2015).12aThyroid Diseases 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Thyroid Association.0 x2329-97114 uhttp://www.thyroid.org/thyroid-physicians-professionals/thyroid-disease-information/clinical-thyroidology/4 uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/ct  aAUTH01325cgm a2200397 a 101559808DNLM20260227153934.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110503s2011    mdu075 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16546  a(OCoLC)731127445  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWD 3801 aWakeland, Ward.12aA genomic analysis of predisposition to systemic autoimmunity /cWard Wakeland.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 15 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed June 6, 2011).  aRecorded Mar. 23, 2011.  aOpen-captioned.12aLupus Erythematosus, Systemicxgenetics22aAutoimmunityxgenetics22aGenetic Predisposition to Disease 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16546  aAUTH02209cgm a22004094a 101322964DNLM20260227152203.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071207s2007    mdu063|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14146  a(OCoLC)192041983  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWH 2001 aCyster, Jason G.10aLymphocyte migration and the adaptive immune response /cJason Cyster.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 12, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 3 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 14, 2007.3 a(CIT): Dr. Cyster is a pioneer and international authority on the subject of lymphoid organ development and function, a central problem in immunology.  His numerous seminal discoveries in this area include defining the molecular code for lymphocyte trafficking in lymph node, Peyer's patch and spleen, including the roles of chemokines, sphingosine-1-phosphate, integrins and RGS proteins, among others.  Dr. Cyster's discoveries go quickly from the cutting edge to textbook paradigm in this important area, so this is an IIG seminar you will definitely not want to miss.   The Immunology Interest Group (IIG) organizes activities designed to promote information exchange and interactions among NIH scientists interested in the field of immunology, broadly defined. Interactions are facilitated via weekly meetings on current topics as well as an annual Immunology Retreat.  For more information, visit  The Immunology Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aLymphocytesximmunology22aImmunity 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14146  aAUTH01651cas a2200469 i 9918505286106676DNLM20260227090916.0m     o  d |      cr#|||||||||||221117c20229999enk|| poo     |    0eng    a  2023228305  a2772-6967l2772-6967  a(OCoLC)1352981547  aSFBbengerdad20260227cSFBdOH1dOCLCFdDNLM  apcc  9England00aW110aJSAMS Plus2dnlm00aJSAMS plus.1 aJournal of science and medicine in sport plus 1a[Oxford] :bElsevier Ltd.,c[2022]-  a1 online resource  aAnnual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1 (October 2022).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only0 aPMC Forthcoming  aArticles published as they become available and compiled into annual volumes.  aContains special issues.  aOfficial journal of Sports Medicine Australia (SMA).0 aVolume 1 (October 2022); title from journal homepage (publisher's website, viewed July 26, 2023).1 aVolume 2 (2023) (viewed July 26, 2023).12aSportsxphysiology12aSports Medicine 2aPeriodical2 aSports Medicine Australia,esponsoring body.08iComplemented by (work):tJournal of science and medicine in sportx1440-2440w(OCoLC)3952823040uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/jsams-plus  aAUTH02298cgm a22004814a 101267332DNLM20260227144959.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu399 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:9924  a(DNLM)CIT:9925  a(OCoLC)183827094  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.500aBECON 2003 symposium on catalyzing team science /cNational Institute of Biomedical Imaging and Bioengineering.30aCatalyzing team science  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 27, 2007).  aStreaming video (6 hr., 39 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 23-24, 2003.3 a(CIT): The sixth annual BECON symposium is titled "Catalyzing Team Science."  This symposium is aimed at examining the forces that encourage and discourage team approaches to biomedical research, and to explore ways in which the NIH, academia, and others can stimulate and reward team efforts. The program will consist of plenary presentations, breakout sessions, and case studies.   Dr. Janie Fouke of Michigan State University and Dr. Keith Brodie of Duke University are the Chairs of the BECON 2003 symposium. Dr. Daniel Sullivan of the National Cancer Institute chairs the BECON planning group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomedical Research22aAuthorship22aInterdisciplinary Communication22aResearch Support as Topic 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aFouke, Janie McLawhorn.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bBioengineering Consortium.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?992440uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?9925  aAUTH03010cgm a2200457 i 101635887DNLM20260227155158.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140627s2014    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18504  a(OCoLC)888345072  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQS 5251 aIngber, Donald E.eauthor.10aHuman organs on chips and programmable nanotherapeutics /cDonald E. Ingber. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aTitle from title screen.  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded June 18, 2014.3 a(CIT): Dr. Ingber will describe the work that his group has been carrying out in the Biomimetic Microsystems and Programmable Nanomaterials platforms at the Wyss Institute, as well as their new model for innovation, collaboration, and technology translation. The goal of the first platform is to engineer human "Organs-on-Chips": microchips lined by living human cells created with microfabrication techniques that recapitulate organ-level functions as a way to replace animal testing for drug development. Dr. Ingber will review recent advances his group has made in developing multiple organ chips, including human lung, gut, and bone-marrow chips. He will also describe ongoing efforts to develop more than 10 different organ chips, integrate them into a "human body on chips," and engineer an automated instrument for real-time analysis of cellular responses to pharmaceuticals, toxins, and other chemicals. The goal of the second platform is to create multi-functional nanotechnologies for regenerative medicine and drug-delivery applications with the long-term goal of developing injectable programmable devices for biomedicine. He will describe new biologically inspired nanotechnologies, including a fluid Shear-Activated Nanotherapeutic delivery system that targets drugs specifically to vascular occlusion sites, and a dialysis-like therapeutic device that combines microfluidics and nanoparticles to cleanse pathogens from the blood of septic patients.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed July 16, 2015.12aTissue Culture Techniques22aBiomimetic Materials22aMicrochip Analytical Procedures 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18504  aAUTH01179cam a2200301 i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002000073040003500093044001800128060003200146100005600178245012100234264005900355300002200414336002600436337002800462338002700490540014200517583003900659583007200698650002800770650002400798650002800822651001800850999000900868101188059DNLM20260224075439.0030812s1920    dcu          f000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)41462775  aDNLMbengerdad20260213dDNLM  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 393 no.101 aFay, Albert H.q(Albert Hill),d1871-1937,eauthor.10aAccidents at metallurgical works in the United States :bduring the calendar year 1918 /ccompiled by Albert H. Fay. 1aWashington, D.C. :bGovernment Printing Office,c1920.  a23 pages ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202102242pda5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20260224lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM12aAccidents, Occupational22aAccidental Injuries22aMetalsxadverse effects 2aUnited States  aAUTH02468cgm a22004694a 101303637DNLM20260227151856.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070403s2007    mdu065 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13673  a(OCoLC)124505608  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWH 2001 aFlavell, R. A.q(Richard A.)10aChromosome dynamics in cytokine gene expression /cRichard Flavell.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 9, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 4 min., 31 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 7, 2007.3 a(CIT): In the last few years, we have found that regulatory elements on one chromosome associate with genes on another chromosome. The system that we study is the differentiation of naïve, precursor CD4 T cells into different kinds of effector helper T cells. We first observed interchromosomal association of the TH1 ifny locus and TH2 loci, which are mutually exclusively expressed by two subtypes of differentiated T helper cells, precedes differentiation and may act as a checkpoint for T cell fate determination. Since this primary observation, we have found several other cases of into chromosomal associations between different cytokine loci in the naïve T cell precursors.  Interestingly, upon TH cell differentiation, these associations are terminated and replaced by interactions between expressed loci. Regulatory elements in one locus appear to play an active role in orchestrating processes across these chromosomes.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aT-Lymphocytes, Helper-Inducerxcytology22aCell Differentiation22aChromatin22aCytokinesxgenetics22aGene Expression Regulationxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13673  aAUTH01581cas a2200481 a 101543738DNLM20260224111558.0cr||||||||||||100524c20109999enkqr p       0    0eng    a  2010208799  a1759-2879l1759-2879  a(OCoLC)635483614  aCUDcCUDdDLCdDNLM  apcc  9England00aQ180.55.M4bR48300aW1bRE234DP10aRes Synth Methods2dnlm00aResearch synthesis methods.  aMalden, MA :bJohn Wiley & Sons,c2010-213<Mar. 2014-2024> :aChichester :bWiley Blackwell3132025- :aCambridge :bCambridge University Press  av. :bill. ;c28 cm.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Jan./Mar. 2010)-1 aMEDLINEbv6n1, Mar. 2015-0 aPubMedbv6n1, Mar. 2015- ; selected citations only before this date0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 5, no. 1 (Mar. 2014).12aResearch Design22aMeta-Analysis as Topic 2aPeriodical2 aSociety for Research Synthesis Methodology.0 x1759-28874 uhttps://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/loi/175928874 uhttps://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/research-synthesis-methods4 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101543738  aAUTH01925cgm a2200409 i 101622765DNLM20260227154518.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u131227s2013    mdu054|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18181  a(OCoLC)871617233  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 251 aWarshel, Arieh,eauthor.10aSpecial NIH lecture :b2013 Nobel laureate in chemistry : computer simulations of biological functions /cDr. Arieh Warshel.1 aComputer simulations of biological functions 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (54 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Nov. 20, 2013.3 a(CIT): One of the challenges in molecular biology has been the ability to understand the action of biological systems on a well-defined molecular level, where the structural information is translated to clear functional description. Arieh Warshel, who has been advancing the frontiers of modeling biological functions for more that four decades, will describe the progress in the field since its early days. The lecture will include a discussion of the development of multiscale modeling of complex biological systems as well as a perspective of future directions.  aDescription based on version viewed March 3, 2014.12aModels, Chemical22aComputational Biology22aEnzymesxchemistry 2aLecture 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18181  aAUTH01431cam a2200313 i 450000100100000000300050001000500170001500800410003201000170007303500190009004000350010904400180014406000240016210000410018624502870022726400530051430000460056733600260061333700280063933800270066750400670069454001420076158300390090358300720094261000320101465000140104671000480106099900090110829730580RDNLM20260220080940.0810815s1908    paua     b    001 0 eng    a   08028976   a(OCoLC)5270206  aDNLMbengerdad20260218dDNLM  9United States00aWA 292bL987an 19081 aLynch, Charles,d1868-1937,eauthor.10aAmerican National Red Cross text-book on first aid and relief columns :ba manual of instruction : how to prevent accidents and what to do for injuries and emergencies /cby Major Charles Lynch ;  prepared for and endorsed by the American National Red Cross ; with 74 illustrations. 1aPhiladelphia :bP. Blakiston's Son & Co.,c1908.  aviii, 247 pages :billustrations ;c15 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 239-240) and index.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202011062pda5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20260219lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM22aAmerican National Red Cross12aFirst Aid2 aAmerican National Red Cross,eissuing body.  aAUTH03597cgm a22004694a 101507216DNLM20260227152924.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090605s2009    mdu056|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15120  a(OCoLC)426113872  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 165.5.H31 aPloegh, Hidde L.12aA fresh look at host-pathogen interactions :bnew tools /cHidde Ploegh.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen (viewed June 24, 2009).  aStreaming video (56 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 27, 2009.3 a(CIT): Interactions between pathogens and their hosts are an intricate web of measures and countermeasures, the study of which has uncovered basic mechanisms of how cells operate. My laboratory has been interested in how herpesviruses disable pathways of antigen presentation, an effort that has shed light on mechanisms of glycoprotein quality control and turnover.  Our studies rely on protein modification to visualize cells and proteins within them, and to control their functional properties. This can be done genetically through fusions with fluorescent reporter proteins. Alternatively, installation of tags that can be detected with antibodies or upon chemoenzymatic conversion with colored or fluorescent reporters allows visualization of the proteins of interest. I shall describe sortagging, a new method that exploits a sortase-catalyzed transacylation reaction as a straightforward method to produce proteins labeled in solution or on the surface of cells in site-specific fashion at the C- or the N-terminus. This method of protein labeling succeeds where GFP fails, as in the case of many type II membrane proteins, and further allows the installation of functional groups that cannot be encoded genetically. A related strategy is used to produce circular proteins in high yields, with improvement of stability of the proteins in question as a possible application.  Finally, I shall report results from recent experiments, conducted in collaboration with the laboratory of Rudolf Jaenisch, that exploit somatic cell nuclear transfer to generate new mouse models for infectious disease. By reprogramming the nucleus of an antigen specific T cell, isolated from a mouse actively resolving a virus or parasite infection, embryonic stem cells can be obtained that are capable of transmitting through the germline the rearranged T cell receptor genes harbored by the donor T cell. These mice carry in all of their somatic cells, including T cells, the rearrangements in question, generated.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHerpesviridaexphysiology12aHost-Pathogen Interactionsximmunology22aImmunity, Cellularxphysiology22aToll-Like Receptorsximmunology22aTumor Necrosis Factor Receptor-Associated Peptides and Proteinsximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15120  aAUTH01343cas a2200421 a 101552362DNLM20260227090907.0cr||||||||||||110125c20109999pautr p       |    0eng    a  2012243299  a1838-7357l1837-4905  a(OCoLC)692402683  aAU@bengcAU@dCUSdUV0dCUIdSERSOdDNLM  aanucapcc  9United States00aW1bAD679RM10aAdv Ment Health2dnlm00aAdvances in mental health.  aMaleny, Qld. :beContent Management3132015- :aPhiladelphia, PA :bTaylor & Francis  aThree times a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aBegan with Vol. 9, no. 1 (Aug. 2010).  aTitle from cover.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 10, issue 1 (Oct. 2011).12aMental Health22aMental Health Services 2aAustralia 2aPeriodical0 x1837-490500tAustralian e-journal for the advancement of mental healthx1446-7984w(DLC)   2003243193w(OCoLC)521304164 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/RAMH  aAUTH02324cgm a22004454a 101463208DNLM20260227152353.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080102s2007    mdu213 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14202  a(OCoLC)477041274  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQY 60.R600aMouse research models-Mouse 101 course.pAdvantages and disadvantages of using mice as an animal model for human disease /cC. Khana ... [et al.].30aAdvantages and disadvantages of using mice as an animal model for human disease  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Nov. 17, 2009).  aStreaming video (3 hr., 32 min., 52 sec. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to NIH.5DNLM  aRecorded Nov. 27, 2007.3 a(CIT): Mouse research models play an important role in cancer research. Technologies available today allow researchers to create mouse models of cancer by transferring new genes into animals or inactivating existing genes. Mouse research models can be used to study the changes associated with tumor development, test new approaches to detection and diagnosis and to evaluate prevention and treatment strategies. MRM/Mouse 101 will provide an overview of the procedures used at NIH to conduct research using mice and explore how mouse research models can be used in cancer research. The course is designed for fellows, technicians, and investigators who plan to conduct experiments using mouse research models at NIH.   For more information, visit  https://ccrod.cancer.gov/confluence/display/Mouse101/Mouse+Research+Models-Mouse+101+Course.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMice22aBiomedical Research22aDisease Models, Animal22aNeoplasms 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aKhana, C.q(Chand)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14202  aAUTH01829cgm a2200445 i 101705357DNLM20260227155658.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u170503s2017    mdu318|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:23230  a(OCoLC)999403894  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 2000aCelebrating the legacy of the NIMH Child Psychiatry Branch Chief, Judith L. Rapoport, M.D. /cNIMH, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2017]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (5 hr., 18 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 17, 2017.3 a(CIT): This one-day symposium will celebrate decades of neurodevelopmental, neurobiological, and clinical research conducted by the Child Psychiatry Branch, led by Judith Rapoport, M.D. at the NIMH Intramural Research Program.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 14, 2017).12aRapoport, Judith L.,d1933-22aNational Institute of Mental Health (U.S.).bChild Psychiatry Branch12aBiomedical Research12aNeurobiology22aChild Psychiatry22aNervous Systemxgrowth & development22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Mental Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23230  aAUTH01295cgm a22003734a 101268125DNLM20260227150108.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s1999    mdu208 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11428  a(OCoLC)70713353  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 5200aAdvances in clinical gene therapy /csponsored by NHGRI.