(online)0 aHE 20.7309/2:2 aGonococcal Isolate Surveillance Project (U.S.)10aSexually transmitted disease surveillance.pSupplement.pGonococcal Isolate Surveillance Project (GISP) annual report /cDivision of STD Prevention.30aGonococcal Isolate Surveillance Project (GISP) annual report14aGonococcal Isolate Surveillance Project annual reportf<2004->17aGISP ... surveillance supplementf<2004->  aAtlanta, Ga. :bDept. of Health & Human Services, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, National Center for HIV, STD, and TB Prevention, Division of STD Prevention  av. :bmaps ;c28 cm.  aAnnual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aDescription based on: 1999.  aLatest issue consulted: 2004.  aPrint issues ceased with 2004.  aAlso issued online.12aGonorrhea 2aUnited States 2aStatistics2 aNational Center for HIV, STD, and TB Prevention (U.S.).bDivision of STD Prevention.0 tSexually transmitted disease surveillancew(DLC)   93645657w(OCoLC)238740414 uhttps://www.cdc.gov/sti/?CDC%5FAAref%5FVal=https://www.cdc.gov/std/gisp/default.htm  aAUTH02726ngm a2200421 i 9918916889806676DNLM20260227164854.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u241002s2024    mdu091|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35208  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWW 27000aDevelopment and repair of circuits of the vertebrate retina /cRachel Wong. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2024]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 31 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  a"2024 Joram Piatigorsky Basic Science Lecture and Award."  aRecorded September 11, 2024.3 a(CIT): Rachel O Wong, Ph.D. from the University of Washington, will deliver this year"s 2024 Joram Piatigorsky Basic Science Lecture and Award. The lecture will take place on Wednesday, September 11, 2024, from 12:30 PM - 2:30 PM at the John Edward Porter Neuroscience Research Center, Blg. 35A, 35 Convent Drive on the NIH campus. It will be followed by a light reception. Trainees and scientists from all NIH ICs are encouraged to attend. Made possible by the generous philanthropic support of Lona and Joram Piatigorsky, this series brings attention to notable basic sciences contributions by eye and vision scientists to a diverse general scientific audience, such as experts in molecular biology, genetics, developmental biology, neuroscience, and computer science. With special consideration for basic eye and vision scientists who take risks exploring little-studied species and imaginative ideas, the Lecture and Award promotes and communicates basic discoveries in eye and vision research that result in far-reaching observations that may inform widespread areas of science--from the eye to the world as it were--rather than the other way around.For more information go to https://web.cvent.com/event/070e8ba4-79c6-49da-9f4a-dd7c652d37ef/summary.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 10, 2026).12aRetinaxphysiology22aRetinaxanatomy & histology22aRetinal Neuronsxcytology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aWong, Rachel O.,espeaker. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=55200  aAUTH02091cgm a2200481 i 101775929DNLM20260227160651.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2021    mdu057|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31484  a(OCoLC)1244240988  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 50604aThe heart, artificial intelligence and COVID-19 /cEric Topol. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (57 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCOVID-19 insights webinar  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded January 13, 2021.3 a(CIT): We invite you to join a new biweekly NHLBI webinar series entitled "COVID-19 Insights" to learn about groundbreaking research focusing on the host response to SARS-CoV-2 infection. This exciting cross-disciplinary series, featuring both NHLBI staff and researchers throughout the scientific community, will enhance NHLBI listeners' understanding of the latest advances in this fast-moving research, especially as they relate to the NHLBI mission.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 26, 2021).12aCOVID-19xcomplications12aHeart Diseasesxetiology22aSARS-CoV-2xpathogenicity22aArtificial Intelligence22aHeart Diseasesxtherapy22aTherapy, Computer-Assisted 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aTopol, Eric J.,d1954-espeaker.2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute,esponsoring body. 0aNHLBI COVID-19 insights webinar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38982  aAUTH02343cgm a2200433 i 101775925DNLM20260227160642.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2021    mdu061|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31478  a(OCoLC)1241125056  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 506.200aAntiviral and anti-Inflammatory approaches to COVID-19 /cJohn Beigel. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded January 7, 2021.3 a(CIT):  Dr. Beigel is associate director for clinical research in the NIAID Division of Microbiology and Infectious Diseases.  In this position, he helped the implementation and execution of the Adaptive COVID-19 Treatment Trial (ACTT) platform.  ACTT-1 led to the licensure of remdesivir; ACTT-2 supported the Emergency Use Authorization of baricitinib in COVID-19.   Remdesivir improves outcomes for hospitalized adult patients with COVID-19.  Despite its use, there continues to be significant morbidity and mortality.  Disease severity might be due to a dysregulated inflammatory response.  Modifying the inflammatory state may improve clinical outcomes.  This talk will review current antiviral and anti-inflammatory therapeutics for the treatment of COVID-19, with a focus on the ACTT-1 and ACTT-2 trials.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 10, 2021).12aCOVID-19 Drug Treatment.22aAntiviral Agentsxtherapeutic use22aAnti-Inflammatory Agentsxtherapeutic use22aClinical Trials, Phase I as Topic22aClinical Trials, Phase II as Topic 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aBeigel, John,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bCOVID-19 Scientific Interest Group,eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41233  aAUTH02461cgm a2200469 i 101775931DNLM20260227160656.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2021    mdu166|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31488  a(OCoLC)1244241519  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWS 12500aBreastmilk Ecology: Genesis of Infant Nutrition (BEGIN) meeting series.nWorking group 1,pMaternal inputs /cNICHD, NIH.1 aMaternal inputs1 iTitle on PowerPoint screen:aBEGIN Collaborative Group 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 46 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded January 15, 2021.3 a(CIT): A deeper understanding of the biology of human milk is essential to address ongoing and emerging questions about infant feeding practices. Human milk is a complex biological system, a matrix of many interacting parts, that is more than the sum of those parts. Its production needs to be studied as an ecology that consists of inputs from the mother, her breastfeeding baby, and their respective environments. The Breastmilk Ecology: Genesis of Infant Nutrition (BEGIN) meeting series is designed to examine this ecology, its functional implications for both mother and infant. The goal is to support the research community's efforts to ensure safe, efficacious, and context-specific infant feeding practices in the United States and globally.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 26, 2021).22aBEGIN (Collaborative Group)12aMilk, Humanxphysiology12aBreast Feeding22aMother-Child Relations22aMaternal Health22aEcological and Environmental Phenomena22aInfant Nutritional Physiological Phenomena 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40150  aAUTH01978cgm a2200433 i 101776747DNLM20260227160834.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210302s2021    mdu686 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31563  a(DNLM)CIT:31564  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 141.5.N472 aDissemination of Non-Invasive Imaging Technologies Workshopd(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aDissemination of BRAIN non-Invasive imaging technologies /cNIH BRAIN Initiative. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (11 hr., 26 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded February 18 & 19, 2021.3 a(CIT): The BRAIN Initiative has invested in various cutting-edge technologies for non-invasive brain functional imaging. The goal of this workshop is to bring together BRAIN non-invasive imaging technology development awardees, neuroscience researchers, and industry partners to discuss how future neuroscience research could benefit from these BRAIN-funded projects, and to identify potential pathways for broader dissemination.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed August 24, 2021).22aBRAIN Initiative.12aFunctional Neuroimaging22aInformation Dissemination22aDiffusion of Innovation 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aBRAIN Initiative,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4017340uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40174  aAUTH01857cgm a22004334a 101294875DNLM20260227151653.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061206s2006    mdu578 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13390  a(DNLM)CIT:13391  a(OCoLC)84717672  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 54000aFrontiers in basic immunology /cNCI.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 16, 2006).  aStreaming video (9 hr., 38 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded Sept. 28-29, 2006.3 a(CIT): This NCI sponsored conference will host international leaders in the field of immunology and should provide an exciting forum for discussion and debate on the state of current understanding of basic immunological mechanisms.  Topics will include Signaling and Effector Cell Function; Lymphoid Development and Differentiation; and Cellular and Innate Immunity. The target audience for this conference is immunologists interested in the latest developments in mechanisms mediating cellular and innate immune responses.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aImmunityxphysiology22aImmunotherapy 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1339040u