101629948DNLM20260227154556.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140408s2014    mdu067       fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18299  a(OCoLC)877024227  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQY 60.A61 aGall, Joseph G.eauthor.10aGiant chromosomes and deep sequences :bwhat the frog egg tells us about RNA transcription /cDr. Joseph G. Gall. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 7 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded February 26, 2014.3 a(CIT): The eggs of frogs and salamanders contain exceptionally large chromosomes, known since the 19th century as "lampbrush" chromosomes because of their unusual fuzzy appearance under the microscope. The many "bristles" on these chromosomes are actually loops composed of one or a few actively transcribing genes. These genes synthesize RNA at rates much higher than in typical somatic cells, making it possible to study RNA synthesis in exquisite detail by immunofluorescent staining and in situ hybridization. The giant lampbrush chromosomes are contained in an equally giant nucleus, known as the germinal vesicle (GV). Because the GV has a diameter of up to a millimeter in some species, it can be removed manually from the egg under a dissecting microscope. In this way one can prepare absolutely pure nuclear and cytoplasmic fractions of the egg for molecular analysis. Deep sequencing of nuclear and cytoplasmic RNA confirms that the cytoplasm contains many species of messenger RNA, as expected. However, much of the nuclear RNA consists of sequences derived from the non-coding introns of genes. Some of these introns persist into the developing embryo and could be involved in gene regulatory processes.  aDescription based on version viewed April 14, 2014.12aXenopus laevisxgenetics22aCell Nucleusxgenetics22aOocytesxmetabolism22aRNAxmetabolism22aSalamandridaexgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18299  aAUTH01988pam a2200457 i 9918987895006676DNLM20260217115451.0230815s2024    flua     b    001 0 eng    a  2023027013  a1382625886  a9781032230993q(hbk)  a1032230991  a9781032230979q(pbk)  a1032230975  z9781003275671q(ebk)  a(OCoLC)1382625389z(OCoLC)1382625886  aDLCbengerdacDLCdOCLCOdYDXdOCLCFdOCLCOdIG#dCLE  apcc  9United States00aRC341b.L624 202400aWM 10100a616.8223/eng/202312110 aLifestyle psychiatry (CRC Press)10aLifestyle psychiatry :bthrough the lens of behavioral medicine /cedited by Gia Merlo, MD, MBA, MEd, Christopher P. Fagundes, PhD.  aFirst edition. 1aBoca Raton, FL :bCRC Press,c2024.  axxv, 466 pages :billustrations ;c25 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aLifestyle medicine  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  a"Lifestyle Psychiatry: Through the Lens of Behavioral Medicine is divided into two broad conceptual frameworks. The first half of the book elucidates the processes of neuroscience to provide the theoretical basis for how the brain guides behavior. This foundation then leads to the second area where these fundamental concepts are applied to practical issues related to lifestyle habits and practices. The book concludes with an important section applying these insights to specific populations"--cProvided by publisher.12aMental Health22aHealth Behavior22aLife Style1 aMerlo, Gia,eeditor.1 aFagundes, Christopher P.,eeditor. 0aLifestyle medicine series (CRC Press)  aAUTH02114cas a2200541 a 101125610DNLM20260227090925.0cr|cnu||||||||010719c20029999nyubr p       0    0eng    a  20012153780 a1536-1632l1536-1632  a017035bUSPS  a(OCoLC)47441501  aNSDcNSDdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bCL767IK10aMol Imaging Biol2dnlm00aMolecular imaging and biology.30iEarly issues have subtitle:aMIB  aNew York, NY :bElsevier Science,cc2001-3 32005- :aNew York, NY :bSpringer  av. :bill.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 4, no. 1 (Jan./Feb. 2002)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv5n1, Jan./Feb. 2003-1 aPubMedbv5n1, Jan./Feb. 2003-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  a2002-2010, the official publication of the Academy of Molecular Imaging; in 2010, it becomes the official journal of the World Molecular Imaging Congress and the official journal of the Academy of Molecular Imaging, the Society for Molecular Imaging, and the European Society for Molecular Imaging. In 2012, it became the official publication of the World Molecular Imaging Society.12aDiagnostic Imagingxstatistics & numerical data22aCytological Techniquesxstatistics & numerical data22aMolecular Biologyxmethods 2aPeriodical2 aAcademy of Molecular Imaging.2 aSociety for Molecular Imaging.2 aEuropean Society for Molecular Imaging.2 aWorld Molecular Imaging Society.2 aWorld Molecular Imaging Congress.0 x1860-200200tClinical positron imagingx1095-0397w(DLC)sn 97000770w(OCoLC)374391014 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/11307  aAUTH00943cas a22003251  1285276RDNLM20260212091549.0820626d193819uudcuuu        f0    0eng    aL  41000095   a(DNLM)SR0008475(s)  a(OCoLC)40371579  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bUN6181 no.29300aW6bP3 v.7481 box 915 no. 11 aUnited States.bChildren's Bureau.10aFacts about crippled children.  a[Washington,c1938-]  av.bill.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 a1938-1 aCeased publication.1 a1943- : Publication ;vno. 293.12aChildren with Disabilities. 2aUnited States 0aBureau publication (United States. Children's Bureau) ;vno. 293.  aAUTH02775cgm a2200493 i 9918573276006676DNLM20260227162859.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2022    mdu156|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32748  a(OCoLC)1436450645  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 45000aEthical & regulatory aspects of clinical research.nSession 6,pIncidental findings/return of results/inclusion of native populations /cBen Berkman, Leila Jamal, Katrina Claw.1 aIncidental findings/return of results/inclusion of native populations 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 36 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 2, 2022.3 a(CIT): The Department of Bioethics offers this seven week course annually each fall. The course is designed to provide an overview of the important issues in the ethics of human subject research for clinical investigators and others who participate in the conduct of research and is open to the entire NIH community as well as to those from outside NIH. Topics include the history of human subject research ethics, principles and guidelines, study design, subject recruitment, informed consent, and international research. The course is open to the entire NIH community as well as to those from outside NIH. The recommended textbook is Ethical and Regulatory Aspects of Clinical Research, edited by Emanuel et al (Johns Hopkins University Press). The course is taught by guest faculty and faculty members from the National Institutes of Health. This is a required academic program for Bioethics fellows.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed May 28, 2024).12aGenetic Researchxethics22aIncidental Findings22aDisclosurexethics22aMedical Oncologyxethics22aHealthcare Disparitiesxethics22aAmerican Indian or Alaska Native 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aBerkman, Benjaminq(Benjamin E.),espeaker.1 aJamal, Leila,espeaker.1 aClaw, Katrina G.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center.bDepartment of Bioethics,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45952  aAUTH01561cgm a2200457 i 101655814DNLM20260227154916.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150409s2015    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18888  a(OCoLC)907941403  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWH 5251 aLiberman, Laura,eauthor.10aFrom physician to patient to poet /cLaura Liberman. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aContemporary clinical medicine, great teachers  aRecorded March 11, 2015.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 16, 2015).12aLymphomaxpsychology22aNeoplasms22aPhysiciansxpsychology22aPoetry as Topic22aScience 2aLecture 2aPersonal Narrative 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center,esponsoring body. 0aClinical Center grand rounds great teachers series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18888  aAUTH01269cas a22004334a 101088663DNLM20260227090908.0m        d        cr|cn ||||||||010221c20019999enkuu pss     0    0eng    a  2001243451  a1472-6750l1472-6750  a(OCoLC)47653524  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9England10aW110aBMC Biotechnol2dnlm00aBMC biotechnology.3 aBioMed Central biotechnology30aBiotechnology  a[London] :bBioMed Central,c2001-  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1 (2001)-  aTitle from BioMed Central archive screen (viewed Dec. 17, 2001).1 aMEDLINEbv1, 2001-0 aPubMedbv1, 2001-0 aPMC1 aIndex medicus  aArticles added consecutively to the current annual v.  aMode of access: Internet.12aBiotechnology 2aPeriodical40uhttps://bmcbiotechnol.biomedcentral.com/40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101088663  aAUTH04727nam a2200433 i 9918974879006676DNLM20260205102733.0m     o  d        cr|cn#||||||||250102s2024    dk a    ob    000 0 eng    a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK610054  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Denmark00aQW 451 aAnderson, Michaelc(Research officer),eauthor.10aThe socioeconomic drivers and impacts of antimicrobial resistance :bimplications for policy and research /cMichael Anderson, Gunnar Ljungqvist, Robin van Kessel, Victoria Saint, Elias Mossialos. 1aCopenhagen, Denmark :bEuropean Observatory on Health Systems and Policies,c[2024]  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (41 pages)) :billustrations.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aPolicy brief,x1997-8073 ;v64  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 aThe policy community, international and national, recognizes the significant health and economic impacts of antimicrobial resistance (AMR) on individuals, households, health systems and society. It is seeking sustainable solutions but often neglects the socioeconomic and sociocultural drivers of AMR.1. Socioeconomic and sociocultural factors play a critical role in driving AMR, shaping its health and economic impacts, and influencing the effectiveness of innovations that seek to tackle it.2. AMR policy needs to take socioeconomic drivers and impacts into account.3. Socioeconomic drivers of AMR in humans are complex but understanding them can inform better interventions. The emergence and spread of AMR relate to a mix of factors including gender, living situations, educational level, healthcare access, (poor) governance, human mobility, conflict, climate change, agriculture and pollution. Policy that understands these and the way they interact with one another will be more likely to achieve its aims.4. Building key elements into design of AMR policy can help ensure socioeconomic considerations are embedded during policy implementation, monitoring, and evaluation: 1.1. People-centredness fosters an equity-orientation and encourages policies that are responsive to individuals' needs and challenges.1.2. Multisectorality recognizes AMR policy as a cross-cutting issue and brings on board various government departments and stakeholders reflecting the socioeconomic context.1.3. Effective governance and leadership that are mindful of the drivers of AMR are central to coordinating multisectoral action to address AMR's wider determinants.1.4. Evidence-based policy helps ensure that policy formulation looks beyond the biomedical model and involves interdisciplinary research and surveillance to understand and tackle the socioeconomic drivers of AMR.5. There are four policy areas that policy-makers could usefully regard as priorities: 1.1. Prevention, including infection prevention and control (IPC) in healthcare; biosecurity measures in agricultural settings; and equitable, global access to clean water, sanitation and hygiene (WASH) infrastructure.1.2. Access involves promoting equitable access to diagnostics and treatments for infection. This requires reducing barriers to accessing essential healthcare services, and strengthening procurement and the supply-chain.1.3. Innovation, which requires investment in new technologies and incentives for research and development but which must also respond to global needs and contexts, including in low and middle-income countries (LMICs), and address access and affordability.1.4. Stewardship policies are also needed to conserve pre-existing and emerging antimicrobials and to promote responsible use in ways that acknowledge the constraints and realities of different global contexts and avoid inadvertent discrimination against particular populations.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed Feb. 4, 2026).12aDrug Resistance, Microbial22aSocioeconomic Factors22aHealth Policy1 aLjungqvist, Gunnar,eauthor.1 avan Kessel, Robin,eauthor.1 aSaint, Victoria,eauthor.1 aMossialos, Elias,eauthor.2 aEuropean Observatory on Health Systems and Policies,eissuing body.2 aWorld Health Organization.bRegional Office for Europe,esponsoring body. 0aPolicy brief (World Health Organization. Regional Office for Europe) ;vno. 64.x1997-806540uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK610054/  aAUTH01459cas a2200457 a 101088677DNLM20260227090925.0m        u        cr cn---------010221c20019999enkuu pss     0    0eng    a  2002243089  a1472-6963l1472-6963  aBHSRAX  a(OCoLC)47657385  aCOUcCOUdOCLCQdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9England10aW110aBMC Health Serv Res2dnlm00aBMC health services research.3 aBioMed Central health services research30aHealth services research  aLondon :bBioMed Central,c[2001-  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1 (2001)-  aTitle from BioMed Central archive volume screen (viewed on Feb. 15, 2002).1 aMEDLINEbv1, 2001-0 aPubMedbv1, 2001-0 aPMC1 aIndex medicus  aArticles are added consecutively to the current annual v.  aSystem requirements: Internet connectivity and World Wide Web browser.  aMode of access: Internet.12aHealth Services Research 2aPeriodical40uhttps://bmchealthservres.biomedcentral.com/40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101088677  aAUTH01334cas a2200409 i 101672177DNLM20260227090909.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||150623c20159999enk||  oo     0    0eng    a  2015243480  a2055-0294l2055-0294  a(OCoLC)911380788  aNLGGCbengerdad20260227cNLGGCdCUSdDNLM  apcc  9England00aW