Taylor & Francis Group,c[2014]-  aTwo times per year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1, number 1 (Spring 2014).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: Volume 1, number 1 (Spring 2014); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 1, number 1 (Spring 2014).12aReproductive Health22aWomen's Health 2aPeriodical2 aSociety for Menstrual Cycle Research,eissung body.0 x2329-37134 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/uwrh  aAUTH03076cgm a2200409 a 101581486DNLM20260227154249.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120331s2012    mdu054|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17170  a(OCoLC)786245299  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 58.71 aFerre-D'Amare, Adrian.10aCatalytic and gene regulatory RNAs :bstructural biology, physiology, and evolution /cAdrian Ferre-D'Amare.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (54 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's seminar series  aRecorded Mar. 16, 2012.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Seminar Series  Education: Adrian Ferre-D'Amare received his B.S. in chemistry from the Instituto Tecnologico de Monterrey, in Mexico, and his Ph.D. in molecular biophysics from the Rockefeller University. He has been named a Rita Allen Foundation Scholar and a W.M. Keck Foundation Distinguished Young Scholar in Medical Research.  Research Interests: Despite its chemical simplicity (compared to proteins) RNA can fold into intricate three-dimensional structures that can recognize other RNAs, proteins and small molecules with high affinity and specificity and carry out biological catalysis. We employ structural, biophysical, biochemical, and in vitro genetic techniques, as well as chemical synthesis, to study ribozymes, riboswitches, and ribonucleoprotein complexes.    We study ribozymes (catalytic RNAs), both cellular and in vitro selected, because they starkly demonstrate the chemical versatility of this nucleic acid. We study riboswitches. These are RNA elements of non-coding regions of messenger RNAs that can switch between two functional states, depending on whether they are bound to a metabolite. Riboswitches are used by bacteria and eukaryotes to regulate gene expression at the transcriptional, post-transcriptional, and translational levels. We study protein enzymes responsible for post-transcriptional modification of RNAs. These modifications expand the chemical repertoire of the nucleic acid; the enzymes responsible may also serve as chaperones, facilitating RNA folding in vivo. We study a family of protein enzymes called pseudouridine synthases. These are responsible for the most abundant and phylogenetically conserved modification of cellular RNAs.    For more information, visit:  http://www.nih.gov/about/director/dirsem.htm.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aRNA 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17170  aAUTH01851cgm a22004814a 101268330DNLM20260227150451.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu060 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11756  a(OCoLC)71273660  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 5361 aHoofnagle, Jay H.10aHepatitis C and its treatment /cJay Hoofnagle.  AIDS : opportunistic infections in the era of highly active antiretroviral therapy / Henry Masur.1 iTitle on PowerPoint screen:aInfectious complications of HIV disease in the era of potent antiretroviral therapy  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aGrand rounds  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Aug. 8, 2006).  aStreaming video (59 min., 38 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 7, 2004.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aHepatitis C22aAIDS-Related Opportunistic Infections22aAntiretroviral Therapy, Highly Active22aHepacivirus 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)02aAIDS, opportunistic infections in the era of highly active antiretroviral therapy. 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11756  aAUTH02211cgm a2200445 a 101578493DNLM20260227153844.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120215s2011    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16465  a(OCoLC)779577239  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWB 4001 aMarx, Kitty.10aInvest in nutrition :bmedical nutrition therapy and reimbursement /cKitty Marx ; CMS, Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services [and] Tribal Affairs ; production of the U.S. Department of Health & Human Services and the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  a"Medicare Studio, Baltimore, Maryland."  aRecorded Feb. 16, 2011.3 a(CIT): Experts from the IHS Medical Nutrition Therapy Action Team, Phoenix Indian Medical Center, and Kayenta Service Unit will explain how:  Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) works, saves money, generates revenue from third party reimbursements. You can increase MNT services and reimbursements at your facility.  Presenters: Dr. Yvette Roubideaux, CAPT Kelly Acton, CAPT Tammy Brown, CAPT Edith Clark, CDR Susan Jones, LT Samantha Interpreter, Roslyn Bolzer, LT Kelli Wilson, LCDR Diane Phillips, CDR Leslye Rauth, Brenda Broussard, LT Shanna Moeder, Stefanie McLain, LT Revondolyn Scott, LT Dolores Addison, Kitty Marx, David Nolley.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aNutrition Therapyxeconomics22aIndians, North American22aInsurance, Health, Reimbursement22aMedicare 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aCenters for Medicare & Medicaid Services (U.S.)0 aMedicine dish.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16465  aAUTH03030cgm a2200457 a 101559820DNLM20260227153949.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110503s2011    mdu062 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16559  a(OCoLC)729826539  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5411 aCarr, S. A.q(Steven A.)10aQuantitative biology and biomarker discovery without immunoassays /cSteven Carr.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file : sd., col., 1 hr., 2 min.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen (viewed May 27, 2011).  aRecorded Mar. 30, 2011.3 a(CIT): Lack of antibody reagents and robust quantitative methods with sufficient sensitivity, specificity, reproducibility and throughput has significantly hampered our ability to understand dynamic, protein-based biological processes. This deficiency been particularly acute in the area of disease biomarkers where the lack of methods to assay for the presence and level of large (100"s) numbers of minimally credentialed candidate biomarker proteins in patient samples has stymied the introduction of new protein biomarkers into clinical use. My laboratory is addressing this serious barrier by developing targeted mass spectrometry-based technologies to screen and quantify low abundance proteins in a variety of biological contexts including human plasma. The methods we have developed enable construction of highly multiplexed (>100plex), sensitive (10"s of copies/cell and <1 ng/mL in blood) assays with near clinical-grade performance for nearly any protein of interest.  MS-based assays can also be generically constructed to measure many modifications, mutations and splice variants. We are applying these quantitative approaches in the context of a generalizable proteomics-based discovery-through-verification pipeline to identify early biomarkers of cardiovascular injury, breast cancer and infectious disease.  These studies are beginning to demonstrate that modern proteomic technologies when coherently integrated can yield novel, credentialed protein biomarker candidates of sufficient merit to warrant real clinical evaluation.  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomarkersxanalysis22aMass Spectrometryxmethods22aProtein Array Analysis22aProteinsxanalysis22aProteomicsxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16559  aAUTH01716cas a2200505 a 9107334DNLM20260227091012.0cr|cn#||||||||910523c19919999gw  x p       0    0eng    a0939-5555l0939-5555  aANHEE8  a(DNLM)SR0070677(s)  a(OCoLC)23289683  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Germany00aW1bAN59210aAnn Hematol2dnlm00aAnnals of hematology.  aBerlin ; New York :bSpringer International,cc1991-3 aBerlin :bSpringer Verlag  av. :bill., ports.  aTwelve no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 62, no. 1 (Feb. 1991)-  a12 issues per year.  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv62n1,Feb. 1991-1 aPubMedbv62n1,Feb. 1991-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso available online.  aOrgan of Deutsche Gesellschaft für Hämatologie und Onkologie and other similar societies.  aContinues: Blut.  3Vol. 62, no. 1 (FEB 1991)c19910322fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paperxFurther review is required to determine exactly when acid-free paper was first used for this title 