ng    a(DNLM)CIT:18660  a(OCoLC)897493177  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 30.61 aAguilar-Gaxiola, Sergio,eauthor.10aIf you build it, will they come? :baddressing mental health treatment gaps in Latinos /cSergio Aguilar-Gaxiola. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 27 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH health disparities seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded September 18, 2014.3 a(CIT): In celebration of National Hispanic Heritage Month, the National Institutes of Health welcomes Dr. Sergio Aguilar-Gaxiola as the featured speaker for the NIH Health Disparities Seminar Series on September 18, 2014. Dr. Aguilar-Gaxiola will address mental health treatment gaps in Hispanics/Latinos. Mental disorders are major drivers of suffering, disability, and health care cost, and are associated with poverty. To compound this problem, most of the individuals who suffer from mental disorders and are in need of treatment remain untreated although effective treatments are available. Between 50 percent and 90 percent of people with serious mental disorders have not received mental health care in the previous year. In his presentation, Dr. Aguilar-Gaxiola will examine the extent of this treatment gap in ethnically and racially diverse populations in the United States. He will present examples of how treatment gap data is being translated into policy and services in a way that addresses the unmet mental health needs of underserved populations at state and local levels. He will conclude with a demonstration from a local example of how communities and diverse constituencies are coming together to build partnerships and organize to make decisions and achieve transformative social action that hopefully will lead to the reduction of mental health disparities.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed November 28, 2014.12aCommunity Mental Health Services12aMental Disordersxethnology22aHealthcare Disparitiesxethnology22aHispanic or Latino.22aMinority Health 2aUnited Statesxethnology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH health disparities seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18660  aAUTH03001cgm a2200445 i 9918332580506676DNLM20260227161817.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220104s2021    mdu238|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32093  a(OCoLC)1294916157  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.55.T700aNCATS at 10 :bimproving health for all through translational science /cNational Center for Advancing Translational Sciences, NIH.1 aNCATS at ten1 aNational Center for Advancing Translational Sciences at 10 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (3 hr., 58 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded December 7, 2021.3 a(CIT): This December, NCATS marks 10 years of harnessing the power of translational science to speed solutions for many diseases at a time. Our mission is to remove or bypass scientific and operational obstacles that stand in the way of getting more treatments to more people more quickly. We do this by advancing translational science.  Translational science looks at the big picture to identify common pitfalls and develop innovative solutions, thus enabling promising discoveries to become real-world applications that improve people's health. Our activities exemplify translational science principles, such as boundary-crossing collaborations, bold and rigorous approaches, and efficiency and speed.  To commemorate our 10-year anniversary and usher in the next decade of bold solutions, we're hosting a virtual event on Dec. 7, 2021, from 12:30 p.m. to 5 p.m. EST, to highlight how our innovative and team-science approach addresses unmet needs. Featured topics will spotlight the transformational power of data, novel approaches for de-risking a drug's journey along the preclinical pathway, crosscutting solutions for many diseases, and high-impact innovations in clinical research. Speakers will provide perspective on remaining roadblocks and new opportunities through conversations and lightning round talks.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed January 27, 2022).22aNational Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (U.S.)12aTranslational Science, Biomedical.12aTranslational Research, Biomedical22aAcademies and Institutes22aAnniversaries and Special Events 2aUnited States 2aWebcast2 aNational Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44278  aAUTH02747cgm a22004694a 101549275DNLM20260227153801.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101208s2010    mdu563 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16291  a(DNLM)CIT:16282  a(OCoLC)696713371  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 2400aMoving forward in the efficient management and use of core facilities /cNCRR.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (9 hr., 23 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 15&16, 2010.3 a(CIT): During the past year, NIH and the extramural research community have been focused on bringing improvements to accessing and managing research core facilities. At this year"s conference, presenters will provide updates on the actions NIH has taken to address some of the concerns raised at the conference "The Efficient Management and Use of Core Facilities," held in 2009, and to more deeply explore the key challenges to managing and accessing core facilities. Specifically, this conference will focus on:  <ul><li>Training for core directors <li>Registries of core facilities <li>Software for core management <li>Guidance on compliance with OMB Circular A-21</ul>  Background   A core facility is a centralized shared resource that provides access to instruments, technologies, services, and expert consultation to biomedical or behavioral investigators. NIH funds a significant number of core facilities at institutions throughout the United States. In an era of limited resources and focus on efficiencies, NCRR is committed to bringing the community together to discuss common challenges and to facilitate solutions.  http://www.palladianpartners.com/NCRRCoreFacilities/.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration12aGovernment Agenciesxorganization & administration22aFederal Government22aHealth Resourcesxorganization & administration 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Center for Research Resources (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1629140uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16282  aAUTH01409cas a2200433 a 9110831DNLM20260227091023.0cr|cn ||||||||910808c19919999dcuqr p       0    0eng    a1053-0479l1053-0479  aJPINEH  a(DNLM)SR0071388(s)  a(OCoLC)22439881  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bJO859F10aJ Psychother Integr2dnlm 0aJournal of psychotherapy integration00aJournal of psychotherapy integration.  aNew York :bPlenum Pub. Corp,cc1991-3 32002- :aWashington, DC :bEducational Publishing Foundation  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Mar. 1991)-  aTitle from cover.  aPlace of publication varies.0 aPubMedbSele