805 no.1000aPingshan Xian wei sheng shi yan gong zuo zong jie /cHua bei ren min zheng fu wei sheng bu bian yin. 1aBeiping :bHua bei ren min zheng fu wei sheng bu,cMinguo 38 [1949]  a33 pages :billustrations.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier12aPublic Health Practice22aRural Health Services22aInfant Mortality 2aChina1 aHua bei ren min zheng fu (China).bWei sheng bu,eeditor.  aAUTH01916cas a2200553   0010505DNLM20260205165619.0cr|cn#||||||||680615c19659999moumn p       0    0eng    a   81642269 zsc 78001363  a0016-5107l0016-5107  aGAENBQ  a(DNLM)G01960000(s)  a(OCoLC)01570459  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bGA459E10aGastrointest Endosc2dnlm00aGastrointestinal endoscopy.  aDenver.3 aSt Louis, Mo :bMosby Yearbook  av.billus., ports.  aThirteen no. a year,b2001-  aBimonthlyb19??-1994  aMonthly,b199?-2000  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 12-       Aug. 1965-  aIssues for 1992-   include separately numbered section called Perspectives in endoscopy.1 aMEDLINEbv12n2,Nov. 1965-1 aPubMedbv12n2,Nov. 1965-0 aOLDMEDLINE1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aOfficial publication of the American Society for Gastrointestinal Endoscopy.  aContinues the Bulletin of gastrointestinal endoscopy.  c19950524lNot printed on acid-free paper 2aEndoscopy 2aGastroenterology 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Society for Gastrointestinal Endoscopy.0 aPerspectives in endoscopy.0 x1097-677910tBulletin of gastrointestinal endoscopyx1051-7472w(DLC)   90649604w(OCoLC)177793008iComplemented by (work):tiGIEx2949-7086w(OCoLC)1366192734w(DLC)   20242282904 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/gastrointestinal-endoscopy  aAUTH04810cgm a2200409 i 101676596DNLM20260227155315.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160201s2016    mdu056|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19448  a(OCoLC)942840538  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 9500aNovel roles for oxysterols in resolution of inflammation and critical illness /cMichael B. Fessler ; Immunology Interest Group. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (56 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded January 27, 2016.3 a(CIT): Michael Fessler is a physician scientist who is a Senior Investigator and Deputy Chief of the Immunity, Inflammation, and Disease Laboratory of the intramural research program at NIEHS/NIH.  Mike received his M.D. at Harvard Medical School in 1996, after which he completed an internal medicine residency at Massachusetts General Hospital from 1996-1999, and then a pulmonary-critical care fellowship at University of Colorado from 1999-2002. In 2006, after four years on faculty at National Jewish Hospital, Mike started as a Tenure Track Investigator at NIEHS, and, in 2013, he received tenure.  His laboratory focuses on the innate immune response, with a particular interest in mechanisms by which cholesterol trafficking through macrophages and the lung regulates host defense.  His laboratory has helped to define the role of cholesterol transporters in regulation of lung inflammation and immunity, the role of lipid rafts in TLR signaling, and the therapeutic potential of statins and Liver X Receptor agonists in preclinical models of lung disease.  In 2012, Mike received the Carol Basbaum Award from the American Thoracic Society, and in 2015 was inducted into the American Society for Clinical Investigation. Talk Abstract: Recent reports have revealed there to be substantial steady state flux of cholesterol through the lung, and have interestingly indicated that cholesterol trafficking may be uniquely coupled to immune and inflammatory responses in this organ.  The oxysterol-activated nuclear receptor, Liver X Receptor (LXR), is in particular positioned at the crossroads of lipid homeostasis and immunity as it coordinately promotes cellular cholesterol mobilization and suppresses NF-[kappa]B-dependent inflammation. Here, we identify cholesterol-25-hydroxylase (Ch25h)-derived 25-hydroxycholesterol (25HC) as a lipopolysaccharide (LPS)-induced oxysterol that is uniquely required for promoting LXR-dependent resolution of inflammation in the lung.  LPS inhalation upregulates Ch25h in pulmonary macrophages, inducing 25HC in the murine and human airspace in vivo.  Ch25h-null mice do not induce 25HC after LPS and display abrogated activation of LXR in the lungs and accumulation of lipid-laden macrophage "foam cells." This is associated with deficient resolution of alveolar neutrophilia similar to that seen in Lxr-null mice, and is associated with defects in macrophage-mediated efferocytosis, TGFb induction, and recruitment of Tregs to the airway.  Ch25h is induced in macrophages during efferocytosis, and Ch25h-null efferocytic macrophages display defective LXR activation, failing to induce the LXR target gene and efferocytosis receptor Mertk.  We show that resolution in Ch25h-null mice is rescued by treatment with either 25HC or synthetic LXR agonists.  Suggesting a potential class effect of LXR-active oxysterols in resolution of inflammation, we also show that human alveolar macrophages synthesize the oxysterol cholestenoic acid and that, in acute lung injury patients, plasma cholestenoic acid is inversely related to pro-inflammatory biomarkers and duration of organ failure.  Taken together, oxysterols are bioactive lipids that play a key protective role through coordinate promotion of lipid and inflammatory homeostasis. We propose that these insights have potential therapeutic relevance to human lung disease.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed Mar. 1, 2016).12aCholesterolximmunology22aInflammation22aLungximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aFessler, Michael B.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group,esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19448  aAUTH01468cgm a2200397 i 101682336DNLM20260227155334.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160503s2016    mdu084|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19651  a(OCoLC)950696752  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWI 73500aDemystifying medicine 2016.pHepatocellular cancer : problems and progress in an epidemic disease /cIrwin M. Arias, Tim Greten.1 aHepatocellular cancer :bproblems and progress in an epidemic disease 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 24 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 26, 2016.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 17, 2016).12aCarcinoma, Hepatocellular 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aArias, Irwin M.,espeaker.1 aGreten, Tim F.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19651  aAUTH01745cgm a22004214a 101473095DNLM20260227152604.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080502s2008    mdu327|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14443  a(OCoLC)319853736  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.52 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bDirector's Council of Public Representatives.10aNIH Director's Council of Public Representatives, April 2008.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Apr. 21, 2009).  aStreaming video (5 hr., 27 