OCoLC)123962742  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 2701 aVolkow, Nora D.,d1956-10aAddiction to medications :bwhat are the risks and who is vulnerable? /cNora D. Volkow.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 13, 2007).  aStreaming video (56 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 9, 2004.3 a(CIT): NIH Clinical Center's 2004 Medicine for the Public lecture series.  Most people who take prescription medications take them responsibly, but the non-medical use of these drugs can be dangerous. Non-medical use of certain prescription drugs can lead to abuse and dependence, characterized by compulsive drug-seeking and use. According to the National Survey on Drug Use and Health, an estimated 6.2 million people used prescription medications non-medically in 2002. The National Institute on Drug Abuse's annual Monitoring the Future survey found a staggeringly high increase in reported rates of non-medical use of two pain medications among 12th graders. One-in-ten high school seniors took Vicodin(r) last year, making it the second-most commonly reported illicit drug used by high school seniors, after marijuana.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aSubstance-Related Disorders.22aBehavior, Addictive22aDopaminexphysiology22aPharmaceutical Preparations 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12283  aAUTH01792cas a2200553   9882935DNLM20260227091036.0cr||||||||||||741112c19659999maubr p       0   a0eng    a   70007256   a15856210 a0022-3646l0022-364621  aJPYLAJ  a282040bUSPS  a(DNLM)SR0060235(s)  a(OCoLC)01783379  z(OCoLC)1585621  aDLCcDLCdNSDdDLCdNSDdDLCdSERdOCLdAIPdAGLdOCLdNSTdOCLdIULdCUSdOCLdDNLM  apccansdp  9United States00aQK564b.P4700aW1bJO832E0 aJ. phycol.10aJ Phycol2dnlm 0aJournal of phycology00aJournal of phycology.  a[New York]bPhycological Society of America.313<2015-> :aMalden, MA :bWiley Periodicals Inc.  av.bill.c28 cm.  aBimonthly,b<1992->  aQuarterly,b1965-  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-   1965-1 aMEDLINEbv52n1, Feb. 2016- ; selected citations only before this date0 aPubMedbv52n1, Feb. 2016- ; selected citations only before this date  aHas occasional supplements.  aAlso issued online.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 51, issue 1 (February 2015).12aEukaryota22aSeaweed 2aPeriodical2 aPhycological Society of America.0 x1529-881702aPhycological Society of America.tNews bulletinw(OCoLC)44439494 uhttp://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/journal/10.1111/(ISSN)1529-8817  aAUTH01928cgm a2200409 i 9918367463706676DNLM20260227162028.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220318s2022    mdu390| |||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32234  a(OCoLC)1322362905  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 1402 aRare Disease Day (Conference)d(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aRare disease day at NIH /cNCATS, NIH, Clinical Center, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (6 hr., 30 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded February 28, 2022.3 a(CIT): Rare diseases affect an estimated 30 million people in the United States. On February 28, 2022, NIH will host its annual Rare Disease Day event to raise awareness about these disorders, the people they affect, and NIH collaborations that advance research for new treatments. Sponsored by the National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (NCATS) and the NIH Clinical Center, the event will feature panel discussions, rare diseases stories, and more.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed May 27, 2022).12aRare Diseases 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44155  aAUTH01718cgm a2200457 i 101773032DNLM20260227160318.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201105s2020    mdu080|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30329  a(OCoLC)1224228554  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 2702 aSubstance Abuse Prevention for Youth in Indigenous Communities (Webinar)d(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aSubstance Abuse Prevention for Youth in Indigenous Communities  /cNIAAA, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 20 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 8, 2020.3 a(CIT): Substance Abuse Prevention for Youth in Indigenous Communities Webinar.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 20, 2020).12aAdolescent12aSubstance-Related Disordersxprevention & control22aAdolescent Health Services22aAlaska Natives22aCommunity Health Services22aDrug Users 2aAlaskaxethnology 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute on Alcohol Abuse and Alcoholism (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38651  aAUTH01849cgm a22004454a 101285703DNLM20260227151516.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060822s2006    mdu068|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13246  a(OCoLC)71328326  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 1001 aMechanic, David,d1936-10aPopulation health :bchallenges for science and society /cDavid Mechanic.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aMatilda White Riley annual lecture in the behavioral and social sciences  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Sept. 11, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 8 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 22, 2006.3 a(CIT): The NIH Office of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research is pleased to select David Mechanic as the first recipient of the Annual Matilda White Riley NIH Lecture in the Behavioral and Social Sciences. Dr. Mechanic is the Rene Dubos University Professor of Behavioral Sciences and director of the Institute for Health, Health Care Policy, and Aging Research at Rutgers University.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aPublic Healthxtrends22aDelivery of Health Carextrends22aHealth Status 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast 0aMatilda White Riley annual lecture in the behavioral and social sciences40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13246  aAUTH03917cgm a2200481 i 9918820066306676DNLM20260227164613.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u240501s2024    mdu061|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33876  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 506.4 AA100aBuilding a technical community of practice in the midst of a pandemic :bthe role of SPHERES in genomic surveillance for SARS-CoV-2, mpox and beyond. /cDuncan R. MacCannell.1 aBuilding sustainable pathogen genomics during a public health emergency 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2024]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH-FDA COVID-19 SIG lecture  aRecorded March 14, 2024.3 a(CIT): <p>Duncan MacCannell is the director for the Office of Advanced Molecular Detection (OAMD) at the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC). In this role, he helps coordinate and support the implementation and expansion of pathogen genomics, molecular epidemiology, scientific computing, and the use of innovative laboratory technologies across the public health system.&nbsp; This includes CDC"s four infectious disease centers, state and local health departments throughout the United States, and a growing global network of public health, academic and private sector institutions.&nbsp; Dr. MacCannell is an advocate for rapid, open and equitable exchange of pathogen sequence data; for improving reproducibility, interoperability and access to bioinformatic tools, training and expertise; and the urgent need to build resilient, local capacity for pathogen sequencing, bioinformatic analysis and public health action, throughout the country and throughout the world.</p><p><ul><li>The value of networks and communities is underappreciated by many federal agencies and institutions.</li><li>In times of crisis, strong connections between public health, academia and the private sector can be an important resource or point of leverage.</li><li>While there are many concerns to be addressed, open source, reproducible science and rapid pathogen sequence data sharing have considerable public health benefit, and incredible value as a common good.</li><li>Many funding mechanisms do not consider the "last mile" applications of new technologies to public health, engage frontline users and personnel who will use the tools, or consider how critical resources can be maintained in the long term.</li><li>Development of an applied research ecosystem around pathogen genomics, bioinformatics and molecular epidemiology has been a priority for the AMD program, and several key activities will be discussed, including the Pathogen Genomics Centers of Excellence.</li></ul></p><p></p>For more information go to https://www.niaid.nih.gov/research/covid-19-sig<p></p>.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed October 1, 2024).12aCOVID-19xepidemiology12aMpox, Monkeypoxxepidemiology.22aSARS-CoV-2xgenetics.22aMpox, Monkeypoxxgenetics.22aGenome, Viral22aEpidemiological Monitoring. 2aUnited Statesxepidemiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aMacCannell, Duncan R.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bCOVID-19 Scientific Interest Group,esponsoring body. 0aNIH COVID-19 SIG lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=54374  aAUTH02430cgmaa22005174a 101207893DNLM20260227144419.0vf|czahmuvd|cvaizuvf|cjahouhd|bfa012baap040225s2004    mdu059 ||    f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:2596  a(OCoLC)1117451599  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 50bVC no.66 200400aStory of my roots :bdisease mutations of a population /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  aBethesda, MD :bNational Institutes of Health,c[2004]  a1 videocassette of 1 (DVCPRO) (59 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/4 in.3master.  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (59 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videodisc of 1 (DVD) (59 min.) :bsd., col. ;c4 3/4 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aWALS--the Margaret Pittman lecture  aTitle from cassette spine label.  aSpeaker, Leena Peltonen.  aPresented Jan. 28, 2004.3 a(Producer) Describes the features of the Finnish population and the efforts to search for disease genes for rare and common phenotypes. Well-defined critical chromosomal regions in common diseases like multiple sclerosis and schizophrenia have been exposed in regional subisolates of Finland.  aVideocassette (Betacam SP)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2011.e1 videocassette (59 min.) : sd., col. ; 1/2 in.  aVideodisc (DVD)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2019.e1 videodisc (59 min.) : sd., col. ; 4 3/4 in.  aVHS.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aGenetics, Medical22aGenetics, Population22aLinkage Disequilibrium 2aFinland1 aPeltonen, Leena.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pNIH director's Margaret Pittman lecture4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=2596  aAUTH01685cas a2200481 a 9112559DNLM20260227091028.0cr|cn#||||||||910926c19919999cc qr p       0||| 0eng    a1001-9294l1001-9294  aCMSJEP  a(DNLM)SR0072087(s)  a(OCoLC)24546445  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9China00aW1bCH775H10aChin Med Sci J2dnlm00aChinese medical sciences journal =bChung-kuo i hsüeh k'o hsüeh tsa chih /cChinese Academy of Medical Sciences.31aChung-kuo i hsüeh k'o hsüeh tsa chih  aLondon ; New York :bTaylor & Francis,c1991-3 aBeijing :bChinese Academy of Medical Sciences  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 6, no. 1 (Mar. 1991)-  aQuarterly.  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv6n1, 1991-0 aPubMedbv6n1, 1991-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3Vol. 6, no. 1 (MAR 1991)c19920824fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 26, no. 4 (Dec. 2011). 2aMedicine 2aPeriodical2 aZhongguo yi xue ke xue yuan.00tProceedings of the Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences and the Peking Union Medical Collegex0258-87574 uhttp://cmsj.cams.cn/EN/1001-9294/current.shtml4 uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/10019294  aAUTH01981cgm a22004334a 101319964DNLM20260227152141.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071101s2007    mdu045 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14094  a(OCoLC)185042569  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 30000aNINDS awards ceremony 2007 /cNational Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Dec. 19, 2007).  aStreaming video (44 min., 30 sec. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Oct. 23, 2007.3 a(CIT): NINDS AWARDS CEREMONY  INTRODUCTION Joellen M. Harper Austin Executive Officer, NINDS   OPENING REMARKS Story C. Landis, Ph.D. Director, NINDS   AWARDS PRESENTATIONS   NINDS Individual Merit Awards  NINDS Group Merit Awards  NINDS Merit Award for Mentoring  Length of Service Recognition  2007 NIH Director's Awards  2007 Blueprint Merit Awards  NINDS Competitive Fellowship Awards  Fellows Awards for Research Excellence (FARE)  NINDS Career Transition Awards (K99)  Special Recognition   CLOSING REMARKS Story C. Landis, Ph.D.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Institute of Neurological Diseases and Stroke.12aBiomedical Research12aNervous System Diseases22aAwards and Prizes22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Neurological Diseases and Stroke.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14094  aAUTH02135cgm a22004334a 101488659DNLM20260227152632.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081104s2008    mdu061 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14732  a(OCoLC)300395810  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 6901 aIwasaki, Akiko.10aAutophagy and antiviral immunity /cAkiko Iwasaki ; Immunology Interest Group.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Jan. 9, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 51 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 29, 2008.3 a(CIT): Dr. Iwasaki's research addresses the role of dendritic cells in the induction and maintenance of mucosal immunity, primarily to viral infections, such as HSV-2 and influenza virus.  She has also made fundamental discoveries on the basic biology of DC recognition and control of virus infections, including the first studies showing TLR9 recognition of viral DNA, and TLR7 activation by single-stranded viral RNA, as well as the importance of local plasmacytoid DCs in providing innate immune protection. Most recently, Dr. Iwasaki has demonstrated that the transport of cytosolic viral replication intermediates into the lysosome by the process of autophagy is important pathway for the TLR7-dependent recognition of single-stranded RNA viruses by pDCs.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aAutophagy22aDendritic Cellsximmunology22aRNA Virusesximmunology22aToll-Like Receptor 7ximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14732  aAUTH02662cgm a22004574a 101518976DNLM20260227153208.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u091105s2009    mdu053|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15360  a(OCoLC)529281659  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2021 aWahl, Sharon M.10aHost defense gone awry :bfrom inflammation to cancer /cSharon Wahl.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aThe Anita B. Roberts lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (53 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 15, 2009.3 a(CIT): The Anita B. Roberts Lecture Series  This series highlights outstanding research achievements of women scientists at the NIH. The seminar is dedicated to Dr. Anita Roberts and honors her role as an exceptional mentor and scientist.   Anita joined the NIH in 1976 and spent 30 years at NCI, rising to Chief of the Laboratory of Cell Regulation and Carcinogenesis. She died of gastric cancer in May 2006, leaving a legacy that touched both the professional and personal lives of all who knew her. Her work focused primarily on TGF-beta and its role in the growth of epithelial and lymphoid cells. In 2003, Thomas Scientific's Science Watch listed her among the 50 most-cited scientists during 1982 to 2002, a feature called "Twenty Years of Citation Superstars."  Anita was a superstar to many for her mentoring talent and her ability to balance family and work life. Her successful lab was well known for meeting family needs and for providing an environment both intellectually and emotionally enriching. The lecture series in her name serves to highlight the fact that the NIH recognizes the value and necessity of a supportive workplace.  For more information, visit  http://www1.od.nih.gov/oir/sourcebook/comm-adv/wsa.htm.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxetiology22aCell Transformation, Neoplastic22aInflammation22aSecretory Leukocyte Peptidase Inhibitorxmetabolism 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aAnita B. Roberts lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15360  aAUTH02999cgmaa22004574a 101207889DNLM20260227144816.0vf|czahmuvf|cjahouvd|cvaizuhd|bfa012baap040225s2004    mdu086 ||    f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:2594  a(OCoLC)56482350  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aQC 454.M3bVC no.2 200400aElectrospray wings for molecular elephants /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  a[Bethesda, Md.] :bNational Institutes of Health,c[2004]  a1 videocassette of 1 (DVCPRO) (86 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/4 in.3master.  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (86 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videodisc of 1 (DVD) (86 min.) :bsd., col. ;c4 3/4 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aThe NIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture  aTitle from cassette spine label.  aSpeaker, John B. Fenn.  aOriginally presented Jan. 7, 2004.  aIn a very technical lecture, Dr. Fenn discusses the history and applications of electrospray method for mass spectrometry to physics, chemistry, and biology.  Dr. Fenn won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry (2002) for his work.  He traces the history of the develop of mass spectrometry from gravimetric analysis to the work of Otto Stern's work on molecular beams.  Dr. Stern won a Nobel Prize for Physics (1943).  Dr. Fenn also describes the work of Arthur Krantrowitz and Jerry Grey on high intensity molecular beams.  The original application was for supersonic jets.  In addition, Dr. Fenn illustrates the work of John Zeleny of Yale identifying the electrospray.   He describes the work of Malcolm Dole on determining the molecular weight of polymers.  The goal of the experiments was to develop a method to create a single ion from a large molecule.  Using information from these sources, Fenn and his post-doctoral student Masamachi Yamashita developed the first electrospray mass spectrometer.  aVideocassette (Betacam SP)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2011.e1 videocassette (86 min.) : sd., col. ; 1/2 in.  aVideodisc (DVD)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2011.e1 videodisc (86 min.) : sd., col. ; 4 3/4 in.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aSpectrometry, Mass, Electrospray Ionization1 aFenn, John B.,d1917-2010.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=2594  aAUTH03282cgm a22004694a 101319958DNLM20260227152208.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071101s2007    mdu044|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14110  a(OCoLC)182912981  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 5001 aCarrington, Mary.14aThe influence of variation at immunogenetic loci on human disease /cMary Carrington.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCenter for Cancer Research, National Cancer Institute grand rounds  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 28, 2007).  aStreaming video (44 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS and NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Oct. 30, 2007.3 a(CIT): Center for Cancer Research - National Cancer Institute Grand Rounds Dr. Mary Carrington obtained her Ph.D. at Iowa State University in the Immunobiology Department. She performed her postdoctoral studies in the departments of immunology and microbiology at Duke University and the University of North Carolina, respectively. Prior to her current appointment, she was a faculty member in the Immunology Department at Duke University. Dr. Carrington is a Senior Principal Investigator and Head of the HLA Immunogenetics Laboratory in the Laboratory of Genomic Diversity. She is a member of the International Council on Immunogenetics and Histocompatibility, the International Histocompatibility Working Group and a committee member of the Working Group on Cellular Immunogenetics of Nonhuman Primates sponsored by the Office of AIDS Research (NIH). She has received several awards including the Cecil and Ida Green Visiting Professorship, Green College, University of British Columbia, The Ceppellini Award, European Federation for Immunogenetics and Senior Associate, King's College, Cambridge University. Her research interests involve studies of host genetic effects on complex human diseases including infectious diseases, autoimmune diseases and cancer. Her focus is on genes known to participate in innate and acquired immunity, primarily HLA and KIR. The primary educational objective of these seminars is to provide new information, ideas, and discussion about timely areas of research with impact on the field of oncology.  A secondary educational objective is to elicit participation by individuals from all divisions of the intramural NCI, and thus facilitate more interactions among investigators and groups in the NCI.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aGenetic Variation22aGene Expressionximmunology22aImmunityxgenetics22aImmunogeneticsxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aCenter for Cancer Research (National Cancer Institute (U.S.)) 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14110  aAUTH01537cgm a22004694a 101289545DNLM20260227151236.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2005    mdu064 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12861  a(OCoLC)84671682  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWW 2701 aStevens, Charles F.,d1934-10aWhat rules are architectural principles followed in making a brain larger? /cCharles Stevens.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 23, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 3 min., 5 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 31, 2005.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aRetinaxphysiology22aAxonsxphysiology22aDendritesxphysiology22aFishes22aNeurobiology22aSynaptic Transmissionxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12861  aAUTH01506cgm a22004334a 101267636DNLM20260227145852.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu069  |||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10498