eri dergisi  aKonya :bSelçuk Üniversitesi Veteriner Fakültesi Yayınıdır  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 26, issue 1 (2010).  aAlso issued online.  aText in English and Turkish.  aDescription based on: Vol 26, issue 2 (July/Dec. 2010).  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 27, issue 3 (July/Sept. 2011).12aAnimal Diseases 2aPeriodical2 aSelçuk Üniversitesi.bVeteriner Fakültesi.0 x2146-195300tSelçuk Üniversitesi Veteriner Fakültesi dergisix1011-20574 uhttps://eurasianjvetsci.org/  aAUTH02699cgm a2200433 i 9918299485906676DNLM20260227161621.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u211110s2021    mdu168|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31971  a(OCoLC)1290348237  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.55.H900aEthical & regulatory aspects of clinical research.nSession 3,pSubject selection/Inclusion of pregnant women/recruitment and retention /cHolly Taylor, Anne Drapkin Lyerly, David Wendler.1 aSubject selection/Inclusion of pregnant women/recruitment and retention 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 48 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 6, 2021.3 a(CIT): The Department of Bioethics offers this seven week course annually each fall. The course is designed to provide an overview of the important issues in the ethics of human subject research for clinical investigators and others who participate in the conduct of research and is open to the entire NIH community as well as to those from outside NIH. Topics include the history of human subject research ethics, principles and guidelines, study design, subject recruitment, informed consent, and international research. The course is open to the entire NIH community as well as to those from outside NIH. The recommended textbook is Ethical and Regulatory Aspects of Clinical Research, edited by Emanuel et al (Johns Hopkins University Press). The course is taught by guest faculty and faculty members from the National Institutes of Health. This is a required academic program for Bioethics fellows.For more information go to <a href="https://www.bioethics.nih.gov/courses/ethical-regulatory-aspects.shtml">https://www.bioethics.nih.gov/courses/ethical-regulatory-aspects.shtml.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 20, 2021).12aPatient Selectionxethics22aPregnant People.22aEthics, Research 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aTaylor, Hollyq(Holly Ann),espeaker.1 aLyerly, Anne Drapkin,espeaker.1 aWendler, David,espeaker.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42673  aAUTH02124cgm a2200457 i 9918299484706676DNLM20260227161649.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u211110s2021    mdu625|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31989  a(DNLM)CIT:32011  a(OCoLC)1320799182  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWP 3202 aCardiovascular Risk Across the Lifespan for Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS) Workshopd(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aCardiovascular risk across the lifespan for polycystic ovary syndrome /cNHLBI, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c2021.  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (10 hr., 25 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 13 & 22, 2021.3 a(CIT): The goals of this workshop are to review the state of science on cardiovascular disease across lifespan of women with PCOS and identify knowledge gaps and challenges in PCOS-related cardiovascular disease research. The workshop will take place from 11:00 a.m.‒5:30 p.m. each day October 13, 2021 and October 22, 2021.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 20, 2022).12aPolycystic Ovary Syndromexcomplications22aHeart Disease Risk Factors22aLife Course Perspective 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Research on Women's Health,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4391140uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=43913  aAUTH02126cgm a2200433 i 101772209DNLM20260227160116.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201003s2020    mdu072|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30300  a(OCoLC)1225183102  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 50000aType I interferon induction and exhaustion upon viral infection /cElina Zuniga. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 12 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aIIG seminar  aRecorded September 23, 2020.3 a(CIT): Dr. Zuniga's laboratory discovered that, in response to a viral infection, systemic type I interferons (IFN-I) are initially elevated but are subsequently and rapidly silenced despite continuous pathogen replication. They showed that there is an IFN-I refractory (or exhaustion) phase, during which the infected host fails to elevate IFN-I upon secondary unrelated infections, explaining delayed viral control and enhanced susceptibility to opportunistic infections. Dr. Zuniga will present their recent work on the mechanisms underlying the initial IFN-I elevation and its subsequent, rapid, suppression after viral infection.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 16, 2020).12aVirus Diseasesxvirology22aVirus Diseasesximmunology22aInterferon Type Iximmunology 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aZuniga, Elina,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aImmunology Interest Group lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?30300  aAUTH02823cgm a2200493 i 9918573266106676DNLM20260227163139.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2023    mdu071|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32867  a(OCoLC)1393201726  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 2600aForging a new future :binclusion of people with disabilities in technology research and development /cRory Cooper. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 11 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded January 11, 2023.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series   When technology is properly created and applied it can be of tremendous benefit to people with disabilities. People with disabilities along with their allies know better than any other how being involved in the planning from day one is critical to a successfully accessible product, regardless of how many years in the future it lies. Dr. Cooper will present on technologies and research that he and members of the team at the Human Engineering Research Laboratories are working on ranging from Apps, cushions, homes, robots, and wheelchairs. Dr. Cooper will also speak about innovative education programs to build research and development capacity among people with disabilities and veterans.  ​Dr. Rory Cooper is the founding director at the Human Engineering Research Laboratories, a VA Rehabilitation Research and Development Center of Excellence in partnership with Pitt. Also, he serves as co-director of the NSF Quality of Life Technology 