 a(CIT): Many biochemical processes proceed through the formation of functionally important intermediates. For example, ligand binding, enzyme catalysis and protein folding may all involve the formation of one or more intermediates along the reaction coordinate connecting the initial and final protein states. A complete understanding of each process requires, therefore, characterization of these intermediates in detail. While methods exist for studying the endpoints of these processes at atomic resolution in many cases, similar studies of the intermediates remain elusive. NMR methods for seeing such 'invisible' states will be described, along with a number of applications to protein folding illustrating the power of the methodology.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNuclear Magnetic Resonance, Biomolecular 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13263  aAUTH01924cas a22005294a 100961631DNLM20260227091100.0cr|cn#||||||||000803c19999999hu qr p       000  0hun    a  2001243242  a1419-8711l1419-8711  a(OCoLC)47921832  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 ahunbeng  alcd  9Hungary00aW1bNE342J10aNeuropsychopharmacol Hung2dnlm00aNeuropsychopharmacologia Hungarica :ba Magyar Pszichofarmakológiai Egyesület lapja = official journal of the Hungarian Association of Psychopharmacology.  aBudapest :bNeuroline  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan in 1999.  aDescription based on: 1. évf., supplementum 1 (szept. 1999); title from cover.  aPrint issues ceased with 2018?1 aMEDLINEbv6n1, Mar. 2004-1 aPubMedbv6n1, Mar. 2004-1 aIndex medicus  aSome v. have supplements.  aAlso issued online  aIn Hungarian and English; abstracts and table of contents in both languages.  aJournal of the Magyar Pszichofarmakológiai Társaság, <2001-