ine resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 24, 2008).  aStreaming video (48 min., 5 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 23, 2008.3 a(CIT): This talk will describe approaches to study and manipulate multiple nodes of a signaling pathway in living cells, revealing unexpected relationships between Rho family GTPases in motility. New methods for spatially and temporally controlled manipulation of protein activity will be described, including genetically encoded caged Rac, and Fak activated by drug treatment. These approaches may provide generally applicable tools to study signaling pathways in living cells.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aSignal Transduction22aCell Movement22arho GTP-Binding Proteins 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14522  aAUTH01207cas a2200397 a 101477270DNLM20260227090908.0cr|cn ||||||||070706c20089999enkfr p       0    0eng    a  2007213132  a1750-984Xl1750-984X  a(OCoLC)153267250  aNSDcNSDdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9England00aW1bIN610aInt Rev Sport Exerc Psychol2dnlm00aInternational review of sport and exercise psychology.  aAbingdon :bRoutledge, Taylor & Francis Group  aTwice a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1, no. 1 (Mar. 2008).  aDescription based on first issue; title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 1, no. 2 (Sept. 2008).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.12aPhysical Exertion12aSports22aPsychology, Applied 2aPeriodical0 x1750-98584 uhttps://www.tandfonline.com/loi/rirs20  aAUTH01744cgm a22004934a 101289635DNLM20260227144828.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2006    mdu067 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13154  a(OCoLC)1019840597  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWT 1041 aAmes, Bruce N.10aMitochondrial decay contributes to aging and degenerative diseases :bthe role of micronutrients /cBruce Ames.1 iTitle from title screen:aDelaying (or accelerating) the degenerative diseases of aging  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aStars in nutrition & cancer  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 15, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 6 min., 31 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 27, 2006.3 a(CIT): For more information, visit  the National Cancer Institute, Division of Cancer Prevention Web site.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aAgingxmetabolism12aMitochondria22aDiet22aDNA Damage22aMicronutrientsxtherapeutic use22aOxidative Stress 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.).bDivision of Cancer Prevention. 0aStars in nutrition & cancer40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4914  aAUTH01339cas a2200445 a 8910645DNLM20260227091018.0cr|cn#||||||||890615c19899999nyuqr p       0    0eng    a   93642358 zsn 90031206  a0954-5794l0954-5794  a(DNLM)SR0064596(s)  a(OCoLC)19716359  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bDE997NAF10aDev Psychopathol2dnlm00aDevelopment and psychopathology.  aNew York, NY :bCambridge University Press,cc1989-  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv9n1,winter 1997-0 aPubMedbv9n1,winter 1997-1 aIndex medicus  aSome issues lack chronological designation.  aSome issues published in combined form.  aAlso issued online. 2aDevelopmental Disabilities 2aHuman Development 2aPsychopathology 2aPeriodical0 x1469-21984 uhttps://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/development-and-psychopathology  aAUTH02225cgm a22004694a 101507172DNLM20260227153345.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090605s2009    mdu062 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15068  a(OCoLC)429586317  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aBF 724.55.C631 aLachman, Margie E.10aAging well :bpromoting cognitive and functional health /cMargie Lachman.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBSSR lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 20, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 1 min., 36 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 28, 2009.3 a(CIT): Widespread evidence exists for average declines in cognitive and physical functioning with aging, yet there are large individual differences in the nature and patterns of change. Using a lifespan developmental approach, Dr. Lachman considers modifiable psychosocial and behavioral factors that could potentially account for such individual differences in cognitive performance and changes in physical health. The presentation will include findings from the Midlife in the United States (MIDUS) Study, a large national longitudinal survey of adults between ages 25 and 85. Results support a prescription for a combination of social and behavioral factors to slow or compensate for changes with aging, and suggest possibilities for promoting healthy aging by targeting lifestyle factors.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aCognition22aAge Factors22aHealth Surveys22aHuman Development22aSocioeconomic Factors. 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aBSSR lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15068  aAUTH01966cgm a2200445 i 101682308DNLM20260227155816.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160503s2016    mdu109|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19599  a(OCoLC)949986941  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 36000aDemystifying medicine 2016.pMultiple sclerosis :bmechanisms and imaging the process /cDaniel Reich, Irene Cortese.1 aMultiple sclerosis :bmechanisms and imaging the process 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 49 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 5, 2016.3 a(CIT): Demystifying Medicine is an annual course from January to May designed to help bridge the gap between advances in biology and their application to major human diseases. The course includes presentation of patients, pathology, diagnosis, and therapy in the context of major disease problems and current research, primarily directed toward Ph.D. students, fellows, and staff.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 11, 2016).12aMultiple Sclerosisxdiagnosis22aMagnetic Resonance Imaging22aMultiple Sclerosisxdrug therapy 2aCase Reports 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aReich, Daniel S.,espeaker.1 aCortese, Irene,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19599  aAUTH01239cas a2200409 a 9112393DNLM20260227091022.0cr|cn#||||||||910919c19919999pauqr p       0    0eng    a1048-6666l1048-6666  a(DNLM)SR0068936(s)  a(OCoLC)21015588  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bOP15H10aOper Tech Orthop2dnlm 0aOperative techniques in orthopaedics00aOperative techniques in orthopaedics.  aPhiladelphia, PA :bW.B. Saunders,cc1991-3 aPhiladelphia Pa :bW.B. Saunders  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Jan. 1991)-  aQuarterly.  aTitle from cover.  aEach issue has also a distinctive title.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso available online. 2aOrthopedicsxmethods 2aPeriodical0 x1558-38484 uhttps: