g- és Szervezetfejlesztési Intéznet (Hungary),epublisher.00tGyógyszereink, OGYI közleményekx1787-120440uhttps://www.ogyei.gov.hu/gyogyszereink%5Fogyei/  aAUTH01588cgm a22004694a 101463215DNLM20260227152231.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080102s2007    mdu151|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14212  a(OCoLC)304065553  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 9501 aFox, Judy S.10aClinical trials in foreign countries--emerging nations and minorities, women, and persons at risk /cJudy Fox.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Feb. 4, 2009).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 31 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS and NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Dec. 13, 2007.3 a(CIT): GMAC Subcommittee on Training  http://odoerdb2.od.nih.gov/gmac/gmac/trs_main.html.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMeta-Analysis as Topic22aClinical Trials as Topic22aDeveloping Countries22aHealth Status Disparities22aMinority Groups22aWomen's Health 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14212  aAUTH02521cgm a22004094a 101507162DNLM20260227153731.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090605s2009    mdu052 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15056  a(OCoLC)403358933  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 1711 aZarate, Carlos A.,cJr.10aRaising the bar in drug discovery and development for mood disorders :ba translational perspective /cCarlos Zarate.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed June 9, 2009).  aStreaming video (51 min., 37 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 17, 2009.3 a(CIT): Mood disorders (Bipolar Disorder and Major Depressive Disorder) are common, chronic, recurrent mental illnesses that affect the lives of millions of individuals worldwide. Even with adequate modern pharmacological therapies, a disproportionate number of afflicted individuals continue to have persistent mood episode relapses, residual symptoms, functional impairment, psychosocial disability, and suicidal behavior, with tragic concomitant increases in morbidity and mortality. Although novel therapeutics that are mechanistically distinct and work more quickly and effectively are desperately needed for individuals with mood disorders, the discovery and development of such therapeutics has unfortunately reached a plateau. This lecture will summarize some of the promising Intramural efforts currently being developed, as well as explore some exciting future directions in the discovery and development of novel therapeutics to address this urgent, unmet need. Notably, such proof-of-concept studies also incorporate new, cutting-edge research technologies to analyze and measure affective, cognitive, and behavioral components of mood disorders in relationship to potential biomarkers of response.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMood Disordersxdrug therapy22aDrug Discovery 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15056  aAUTH01530cgm a22004694a 101535258DNLM20260227153538.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100702s2010    mdu067|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15929  a(OCoLC)653090144  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWD 2101 aSpiegelman, Bruce M.10aTranscriptional control of adipogenesis and systemic energy homeostasis /cBruce Spiegelman.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afte