s and Misses Mary & Margaret W. Leitch 1aChicago :bFleming H. Revell Companyc[1900]  a288 pages :billustrations, maps, portraits ;c19 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20250618lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aAlcohol-Related Disordersxprevention & control22aOpioid-Related Disordersxprevention & control22aIndigenous Peoples22aMissionaries22aChristianity1 aCrafts, Wilbur F.,cMrs.,d1845-1930,eauthor.1 aLeitch, Mary,eauthor.1 aLeitch, Margaret W.,eauthor.4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/03040500R  aAUTH02118cgm a22004214a 101532581DNLM20260227153423.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100604s2010    mdu087|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15875  a(OCoLC)649795767  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 1411 aCoppola, Richard.10aMagnetoencephalography (MEG) and neuropsychiatry /cRichard Coppola.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIMH schizophrenia lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 27 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 3, 2010.3 a(CIT): MEG is a neuroimaging method that allows us to study the activity of the brain changes while people are performing cognitive tasks or in other behavioral situations. This helps us understand the parts of the brain used to process information and guide behavior. In patients with different problems, we can study how this activity is modified or even abnormal. An "Ask the Doctor" session will follow the lecture. Participants will learn:   . How MEG "sees" the neural activity of the brain . The methods we use to create images of this activity . How MEG compares to other imaging modalities such as fMRI . The networks of the brain involved in memory and attention that are mapped by MEG . How these networks are modified in patients with schizophrenia, their siblings and possible genetic relations  The NIMH Schizophrenia Lecture Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMagnetoencephalography22aSchizophrenia 2aLecture 2aWebcast 0aNIMH schizophrenia lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15875  aAUTH01496cas a2200457 a 8413200DNLM20260227091014.0cr||||||||||||841129c19849999gw  x p       0    0eng    a   90656005 zsn 84012147  a0174-1578l0174-1578  aJPBPDL  a(DNLM)J17602000(s)  a(OCoLC)10697806  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Germany00aW1bJO595JAB10aJ Comp Physiol B2dnlm00aJournal of comparative physiology.nB,pBiochemical, systemic, and environmental physiology.30aBiochemical, systemic, and environmental physiology  aBerlin ;aNew York :bSpringer-Verlag,c[c1984-  av. :bill.  aEight no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 154, no. 1 (Jan. 1984)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv155n1, 1984-0 aPubMedbv155n1, 1984-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3(1988)c19890818fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aAnimals, Laboratoryxphysiology 2aPhysiology, Comparative 2aPeriodical0 x1432-136X01tJournal of comparative physiologyx0302-9824w(DLC)   74644886w(OCoLC)17941164 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/360  aAUTH01018cas a2200361   7505862DNLM20260224111556.0cr|cn ||||||||750626c18849999dcuqr         0    0eng    a0013-8797l0013-8797  aPESWAB  a(DNLM)P34080000(s)  a(OCoLC)01568029  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bPR585HN10aProc Entomol Soc Wash2dnlm00aProceedings of the Entomological Society of Washington.  aWashington Dc :bEntomological Society Of Washington  av.billus.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-    Feb. 1884-  aAlso issued online. 2aEntomology 2aPeriodical2 aEntomological Society of Washington.0 x2378-64774 uhttp://www.bioone.org/loi/went  aAUTH03136cgm a2200493 i 101671891DNLM20260227155137.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u151031s2015    mdu046|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19247  a(OCoLC)935631990  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWP 8700 aJust say NO (Ridnour)10aJust say NO :bnOS2-derived NO and breast cancer progression /cLisa A. Ridnour.1 anOS2-derived NO and breast cancer progression 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (46 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIC Center for Cancer Research grand rounds  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded October 16, 2015.3 a(CIT): NCI Center for Cancer Research Grand Rounds. Dr. Ridnour joined the research group of Dr. David Wink (NCI) in 2003. Her research interests include nitric oxide (NO) regulation of extracellular matrix during cancer progression. In collaboration with Dr. David Roberts (NCI), a novel biphasic crosstalk relationship between NO and the angiogenesis inhibitor thrombospondin-1 (TSP-1) was discovered. Collaborations with Drs. Stefan Ambs (NCI) identified NO modulation of MMP-9/TIMP-1 ratios inbreast tumors. TIMP-1 nitration of tyrosine residues was identified at NO concentrations that increase both MMP-9 secretion and TIMP-1 binding to cell surface CD63, which promotes survival through CD63/PI3k/Akt/BAD signaling. These observations offer a novel mechanism deciphering how both MMP-9 and TIMP-1 can predict poor cancer patient survival, which may involve at least in part NO-mediated TIMP-1 nitration that redirects its function away from MMP-9inhibition and toward TIMP-1/CD63 pro-survival signaling. More recently, we have explored the role of NOS in tumor response to radiation and found that NOS inhibition improves radiation-induced tumor growth delay by Th1 immunepolarization. NCI's Center for Cancer Research (CCR) Grand Rounds is a weekly lecture series addressing current research in clinical and molecular oncology. Speakers are leading national and international researchers and clinicians.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 8, 2015).12aBreast Neoplasmsxenzymology22aMatrix Metalloproteinases22aNitric Oxide Synthase Type II22aOxidation-Reduction22aTumor Microenvironment 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aRidnour, L.q(Lisa),espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19247  aAUTH02222cgm a2200457 i 9918469380306676DNLM20260227162619.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220910s2022    mdu000|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32532  a(DNLM)CIT:32537  a(DNLM)CIT:32541  a(OCoLC)1388203120  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWH 1732 aAnnual Sickle Cell Disease Meetingd(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10a2022 annual sickle cell disease meeting /cNHLBI, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (22 hr., 44 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded August 8, 9 & 10 2022.3 a(CIT): The division of Blood Diseases & Resources will be hosting its annual Sickle Cell Disease-SCD, meeting this year on August 8-10, 2022. The NHLBI Annual Sickle Cell Disease meeting provides an opportunity for Sickle Cell Disease investigators participating in NHLBI-funded studies to present their recent work to SCD community, interact with the other investigators, and NHLBI program staff.For more information go to <a href="https://intranet.nhlbi.nih.gov/news-events/calendar/nhlbis-annual-sickle-cell-disease-meeting-2022">https://intranet.nhlbi.nih.gov/news-events/calendar/nhlbis-annual-sickle-cell-disease-meeting-2022.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (June 26, 2023).12aAnemia, Sickle Cell22aBiomedical Research22aClinical Trials as Topic 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4582440uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4582240uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45820  aAUTH01419cam a2200349 a 9608276DNLM20211203202250.0he|amb|||bbca960704s1995    xxu|||| b||||o00||0|eng    aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aTD 4.210:95-10 aTD 4.210:95-11 aCollins, William E.q(William Edward),d1932-2025.12aA review of civil aviation accidents in which "lost/disoriented" was a cause/factor, 1981-1990 /cWilliam E. Collins.  aWashington, D.C. (Washington 20591) :bOffice of Aviation Medicine, U.S. Dept. of Transportation, Federal Aviation Administration ;aSpringfield, Va. :bAvailable to the public through the National Technical Information Service,c[1995]  atextbtxt2rdacontent  amicroformbh2rdamedia  amicrofichebhe2rdacarrier  aPerforming organization: FAA Civil Aeromedical Institute.  aCover title.  a"January 1995."  a"DOT/FAA/AM-95/1."  aIncludes bibliographical references (p. 7).  aMicrofiche.bWebster, N.Y. :cPhotographic Sciences Corp.,d1995.e1 microfiche.12aAccidents, Aviation22aOrientation1 aUnited States.bOffice of Aviation Medicine.2 aCivil Aeromedical Institute.  aNASRELb19991201  aAUTH01507cas a2200481 a 9602617DNLM20260227091019.0cr|cn#||||||||960404c19969999cauqr p       0    0eng    asn 94002214 0 a1074-2484l1074-2484  aJCPTFE  a(DNLM)SR0081346(s)  a(OCoLC)29631130  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bJO576210aJ Cardiovasc Pharmacol Ther2dnlm 0aJournal of cardiovascular pharmacology and therapeutics00aJournal of cardiovascular pharmacology and therapeutics.  aNaperville, IL :bChurchill Livingstone Inc.,cc1996-3 32007- :aThousand Oaks, Calif. :bSage Publications  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Jan. 1996)-  aTitle from cover.  aImprint varies.1 aMEDLINEbv5n1, 2000-1 aPubMedbv5n1, 2000-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online. 2aCardiovascular Agentsxtherapeutic use 2aCardiovascular Diseasesxdrug therapy 2aCardiovascular Diseasesxtherapy 2aCardiovascular Systemxdrug effects 2aPeriodical0 x1940-40344 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/cpt  aAUTH03624cgm a2200505 i 101706994DNLM20260227155719.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u170603s2017    mdu071|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:23285  a(OCoLC)990151678  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 31000aOn the role of rat superior colliculus in cognitive control /cCarlos D. Brody.1 iPowerPoint:aNeural circuit mechanisms underlying cognition 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2017]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 11 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience series seminar  aRecorded May 15, 2017.3 a(CIT): NIH Neuroscience Series Seminar  Dr. Brody"s lab uses a combination of computational, behavioral, and electrophysiological techniques. They train rats to perform tasks that require cognitive components that they are interested in studying. For example, they train them to remember a stimulus for a few seconds, and to then make a behavior based on their memory of the stimulus. They can then study neural responses during this behavior, and observe the neural correlates of short-term memory. To help them understand the mechanisms behind their findings, they build computational models of networks of spiking neurons, with which they explore the circuit architectures and mechanistic hypotheses that could explain the experimental results. The models both give them greater insight into potential mechanisms, and help them decide what are the best next experiments to test and distinguish between hypotheses.  Currently they are focused on three behavioral tasks performed by rats, all studied from the combined computational/experimental approach.  The first is a task in which rats are presented with a first stimulus; then there is a pause; then they are presented with a second stimulus; and the rats must compare the two stimuli and make a binary decision based on the comparison. They use this task to study short-term memory and decision-making. In the second task, they study how it is that cognitive state can flexibly determine appropriate rules of behavior. The third task that they are working on is one where rats hear a sound, and must decide whether the sound was long or short. They are using this task to study the neural basis of time perception. But where in the brain is information about time processed and perceived? And how is time represented? These are some of the questions they are addressing with their time discrimination task.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed June 13, 2017).12aRatsxpsychology12aSuperior Colliculixphysiology22aCognitionxphysiology22aMemory, Short-Termxphysiology22aModels, Animal22aModels, Neurological22aNeural Conductionxphysiology22aNeural Pathwaysxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aBrody, Carlos,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23285  aAUTH01018cam a22002777i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003500073044001000108060003300118100003900151245007000190250001100260264008100271300003400352336002600386337002800412338002700440490007200467650001900539655002400558830007400582830007500656999000900731101610305DNLM20260209113021.0130612s1935    cc a   j      000 0 chi    aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1777 no.191 aZhao, Xiong,dactive 1935eauthor.10aQu chu wen ying yun dong /cZhao Xiong bian ; Zhao Jingyuan jiao.  a4 ban. 1aShanghai :bShang wu yin shu guan fa xing,cMinguo 24 nian 6 yue [June 1935]  a1, 35 pages :billustrations.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aXiao xue sheng wen ku ;vdi 1 ji, di 192 ce, sheng li wei sheng lei12aInsect Control 2aJuvenile Literature 0aXiao xue sheng wen ku (Shang wu yin shu guan).nDi 1 ji ;vdi 192 ce. 0aXiao xue sheng wen ku (Shang wu yin shu guan).pSheng li wei sheng lei  aAUTH01999cam a2200361 i 26320010RDNLM20260220080936.0810815s1913    ohua          000 0deng    a(OCoLC)14795661  aDNLMbengerdad20260218dDNLM  9United States00aW9bF258h 19131 aFaudel, Henry J.q(Henry John),d1859-eauthor.10aHorse sense :ba school of practical science upon the perplexing problems of to-day, pertaining to life and health, namely : consumption : bacteria in water, soil, air milk, bread, vinegar and beer : sunshine, the most potent germicide : the up-to-date medical fraternity a menace to life and health : the merits and demerits of the Chicago drainage canal : fresh air and flies : the medical monopoly of ideas : not all so-called quacks are quacks, etc. 1a[Independence, Ohio?] :b[publisher not identified],c[1913?] 4c©1913  aV, 266 pages :billustrations, portrait ;c20 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"Copyrighted 1913 by Henry J. Faudel."  aNLM copy, author presentation copy, inscribed on front flyleaf: "With compliments and best wishes from Henry J. Faudel, the author, to Hon. Woodrow Wilson President of the United States. This is one of the first ten books published of its kind and sent to ten of the most honorable men in the world. Respectfully yours, H.J. Faudel."5DNLM  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202011122pda5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20260219lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM12aTuberculosis22aBacterial Infections22aPhysicians22aQuackery22aSanitation 2aPopular Work  aAUTH02554cgm a2200433 i 101605274DNLM20260227154807.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130402s2013    mdu097|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17872  a(OCoLC)847111536  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 851 aCollins, Linda M.eauthor.10aRaising the bar :bengineering optimized behavioral interventions for increased public health impact /cLinda M. Collins. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 37 min.) :bcolor, sound)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdm2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aMedicine: mind the gap  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Mar. 26, 2013.3 a(CIT): Dr. Collins will discuss why behavioral interventions are important in many areas of public health, for example, smoking cessation, drug abuse prevention, treatment of obesity, management of heart failure symptoms, and promotion of physical activity. Behavioral interventions are typically developed and evaluated using a treatment package approach, in which the intervention is assembled a priori and evaluated by means of a randomized controlled trial (RCT). In this talk, she will review an alternative approach called the Multiphase Optimization Strategy (MOST), an engineering-inspired framework for developing, optimizing, and evaluating behavioral interventions. MOST includes the RCT, as well as other empirical steps aimed at intervention optimization. Dr. Collins proposes that MOST offers several benefits, including the possibility of engineering behavioral interventions to meet explicit criteria of effectiveness and/or efficiency. Once the criteria have been met for a particular intervention, the bar can be raised to aim for ever-increasing public health impact.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed May 29, 2013.12aHealth Behavior22aHealth Promotionxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aMedicine: mind the gap lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17872  aAUTH01240pam a2200397 i 9918986607506676DNLM20260205101403.0t|240122s2024    enk      b    001 0 eng    aGBC4102022bnb  a9780443192524qpaperback  a0443192529qpaperback  z9780443192531qePub ebook8 aCIPO000032673  a(OCoLC)1458051184  aUKMGBbengerdacUKMGBdOCLCO  apcc  9England00aQW 9004a616.922300aMicrobial biofilms :brole in human infectious diseases /cedited by Mukesh Kumar Yadav, Jae Jun Song, Jorge E. Vidal. 1aLondon, United Kingdom :bElsevier/Academic Press,c[2024]  a275 pages ;c24 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aDevelopments in microbiology12aBiofilms22aCommunicable Diseasesxmicrobiology22aAnti-Bacterial Agents1 aYadav, Mukesh Kumar,eeditor.1 aSong, Jae Jun,eeditor.1 aVidal, Jorge E.,eeditor. 0aDevelopments in microbiology  aAUTH01542cam a2200373 i 101745192DNLM20260220150315.0cr||||||||||||940607r18731873paua          000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)1099668652  aHLSbengcHLSdPHAdOCLCOdOCLCQdOCLCAerdadDNLM  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5257 no.221 aBiddle, A. Sydneyq(Algernon Sydney),d1847-189