a  20092081600 a2152-2715l2152-2715  a(OCoLC)477405630  aDLCcDLCdUABdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aISSN RECORD00aW1bCY4410aCyberpsychol Behav Soc Netw2dnlm00aCyberpsychology, behavior and social networking.  aNew Rochelle, NY  :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.  av.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 13, no. 1 (Feb. 2010).  aDescription based on: Vol. 13, no. 1 (Feb. 2010); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 13, no. 1 (Feb. 2010).1 aMEDLINEbv13n1, Feb. 2010-1 aPubMedbv13n1, Feb. 2010-  aAlso issued online.12aBehavior22aComputer Communication Networksxstatistics & numerical data22aMultimediaxstatistics & numerical data22aPsychology, Social22aUser-Computer Interface 2aPeriodical0 x2152-272300tCyberpsychology & behaviorx1094-9313w(DLC)   9800640794w(OCoLC)374406224 uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/cyber  aAUTH01903cas a2200469 a 101586617DNLM20260227090917.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||120619c20129999cau|| poo     |    0eng    a  2013243243  a2280-8000l2280-8000  a(OCoLC)843379012  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW110aJ Appl Biomater Funct Mater2dnlm00aJournal of applied biomaterials & functional materials.1 aJournal of applied biomaterials and functional materials1 aJABFM  aMilano :bWichtig Editore3132018- :aThousand Oaks, CA :bSAGE Publications  aThree no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 10, no. 1, 2012.1 aMEDLINEbv10n1, 2012-0 aPubMedbv10n1, 2012-  aOfficial journal of: Società italiana biomateriali, Consorzio interuniversitario nazionale per la scienza e tecnologia dei materiali, Societa italiana di biomeccanica in ortopedia e traumatologia, Sociedad ibérica de biomecánica y biomateriales, and: Grouppo nazionale di bioingegneria.  aDescription based on: Vol. 10, no. 1(2012); title from PDF cover (journal home page, viewed Feb. 20, 2013).  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 10, no. 3 (2012) (viewed Feb. 20, 2013).12aBiocompatible Materials22aBioengineering22aBiomechanical Phenomena 2aPeriodical2 aSocietà italiana biomateriali.00tJournal of applied biomaterials & biomechanicsx1722-6899w(DLC)   2003243679w(OCoLC)5383977440uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/jbfa  aAUTH02659cgm a2200421 i 101655816DNLM20260227154929.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150409s2015    mdu065|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18890  a(OCoLC)907941409  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 4751 aDoudna, Jennifer A.eauthor.10aCRISPR-Cas genome surveillance :bfrom basic biology to transformative technology /cJennifer Doudna. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 5 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aWednesday afternoon lecture series  aRecorded March 11, 2015.3 a(CIT): Dr. Doudna, who specializes in the study of RNA, will present a brief history of the bacterial RNA-guided CRISPR biology from its initial discovery through the elucidation of the CRISPR-Cas9 enzyme mechanism. Using CRISPR-Cas (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats) technology provides the foundation for remarkable developments in modifying, regulating, or marking genomic loci in a wide variety of cells and organisms. These results highlight a new era in which genomic manipulation is no longer a bottleneck to experiments, paving the way to fundamental discoveries in biology with applications in all branches of biotechnology, and strategies for human therapeutics. Dr. Doudna will discuss recent findings regarding the molecular mechanism of Cas9 and its use for targeted cell-based therapies.   About the annual Margaret Pittman Lecture:  This annual lecture honors Dr. Margaret Pittman, NIH's first female lab chief, who made significant contributions to microbiology and vaccine development, particularly in the areas of pertussis and tetanus, during her long career at the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 16, 2015).12aCRISPR-Cas Systemsxgenetics22aRNA, Bacterialxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18890  aAUTH02604nam a2200349 i 9918976162206676DNLM20260204123648.0m     o  d i      cr|cn#||||||||250107s2024    cf a    ob   i000 0 eng    z9789290314172  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Congo (Brazzaville)00aW 67 HA100aTowards universal health coverage in the WHO African region :btracking financial protection /cWorld Health Organization African Region. 1aBrazzaville, Congo :bWorld Health Organization. Regional Office for Africa,c[2024]  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (xi, 38 pages)) :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 aThe sustainable development agenda commits countries, among other things, to ensure healthy lives and promote well-being for all ages (Sustainable Development Goal 3). Implicit in this agenda is ensuring that the whole population has affordable access to good-quality promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative health services. Concerns about financial barriers to accessing health services have led to the design and implementation of reforms in several countries. While some countries have abolished user fees at the point of care, others have introduced health insurance schemes, and some others have progressively increased reliance on governments to fund health services. Despite these efforts, most countries in the WHO African Region continue to rely heavily on out-of-pocket payments to fund health services, a practice which impedes progress in attaining universal health coverage and contributes to persistent gaps in service delivery and poor health indicators. Out-of-pocket payments at the point of obtaining care are a major barrier to access for many people, especially the poorest and most vulnerable persons. This report helps better understand the implications of such contributions for people’s living standards and their ability to access the health care they need.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed Feb. 4, 2026)12aUniversal Health Care22aHealth Expenditures22aHealth Services Accessibilityxeconomics 2aAfrica2 aWorld Health Organization.bRegional Office for Africa,eissuing body.40uhttps://iris.who.int/handle/10665/379719  aAUTH02303cgm a2200457 i 101645900DNLM20260227154837.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u141108s2014    mdu018 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18690  a(OCoLC)905297252  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 5341 aCollins, Francis S.eauthor.10aNIH media briefing on discharge of Ebola patient from its Clinical Center Special Clinical Studies Unit /cFrancis S. Collins. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (18 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Oct. 24, 2014.3 a(CIT): NIH Clinical Center NIH officials brief reporters about the condition of Nina Pham, the Dallas nurse who was admitted to the NIH Clinical Center on October 16 with Ebola Virus.  Participants will include: Francis S. Collins, M.D., Ph.D., Director of the National Institutes of Health, Anthony S. Fauci, M.D., Ph.D., Director of the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, H. Clifford (Cliff) Lane, M.D., NIAID Clinical Director, John I. Gallin, M.D., Director, NIH Clinical Center, Tara Palmore, M.D., Director, Hospital Epidemiologist, NIH Clinical Center and Director, Infectious Diseases Training Program, NIAID, Rick Davey, M.D., Deputy Clinical Director, NIAID Division of Clinical Research.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed Nov. 26, 2014.12aPham, Nina.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center.bSpecial Clinical Studies Unit12aHemorrhagic Fever, Ebola22aPatient Discharge22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aMaryland 2aAddress 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18690  aAUTH01668cas a2200493 i 101738191DNLM20260227090908.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||170519c20189999nyubr poo     0   a0eng    a  2017200419  a2573-1602l2573-1599  a(OCoLC)987555262  aDLCbengerdad20260227cDLCdDLCdAGLdOCLCFdDLCdDNLM  apccansdp  9United States00aQH440b.C7500aW10 aQH426b.C7510a576.52230 aCRISPR j.b(Print)10aCRISPR J2dnlm 4aThe CRISPR journal04aThe CRISPR journal. 1a[New Rochelle, NY] :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.,c[2018]-  a1 online resource  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1, issue 1 (February 2018)1 aMEDLINEbv2n1, Feb. 2019-0 aPubMedbv2n1, Feb. 2019- ; Selected citations only before this date  aAlso issued in print.  aDescription based on: Volume 1, issue 1 (February 2018); title from journal home page (publisher's website, viewed February 11, 2019).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 1, issue 6 (December 2018) (viewed February 11, 2019).12aClustered Regularly Interspaced Short Palindromic Repeats22aGene Editing22aGenetic Research 2aPeriodical0 x2573-15994 uhttps://www.liebertpub.com/loi