charts ;c26 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aNLM copy, some ms. markings in pencil.5DNLM  aNLM microfilm copy shelved as: Film 10195 no.55DNLM  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  aMicrofilm.b[Bethesda, Md.] :cNational Library of Medicine,d1999.e1 microfilm reel : negative ; 35 mm.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202011062pda5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20260210lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM12aVentilation22aSchools 2aTables1 aWinslow, C.-E. A.q(Charles-Edward Amory),d1877-1957.2 aE.P. Dutton (Firm),epublisher.2 aMilbank Memorial Fund.2 aNew York Association for Improving the Condition of the Poor.  aAUTH01813cgmaa22004694a 101248834DNLM20260227144554.0vf|cbahouvf|cjahoucr|cna||||||||050526s2005    mdu056       f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:3667  a(OCoLC)60792524  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aQU 58.700aJourney matters :bribonucleoprotein partnerships regulating mRNA transport, turnover, and translation /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  aUnited States.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (56 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (56 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's seminar series  aSpeaker, Myriam Gorospe.  aPresented January 14, 2005.  aAlso available online.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aRNA, Messengerxmetabolism12aRNA Transport22aProtein Biosynthesis22aRibonucleoproteins22aGene Expression Regulation22aOligonucleotide Array Sequence Analysis.1 aGorospe, Myriam.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3667  aAUTH03057cgm a2200457 i 101655820DNLM20260227155024.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150409s2015    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18894  a(OCoLC)908629359  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 102.81 aBuffalo, Elizabeth,eauthor.10aNeural signals for exploration and navigation in the medial temporal lobe /cElizabeth Buffalo.1 iTitle on PowerPoint screen:aNeural signals for memory and space in the medial temporal lobe 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aRecorded March 16, 2015.3 a(CIT): The focus of Dr. Buffalo lab's research is to contribute to a better understanding of the neural mechanisms involved in learning and memory. Using neurophysiological techniques, they record simultaneously from multiple electrodes in the hippocampus and surrounding cortex in awake, behaving monkeys. They investigate how changes in neuronal activity correlate with the monkey's ability to learn and remember. They are particularly interested in the activity of neuronal networks that underlie learning and memory processes. They use spectral analysis techniques to investigate the role of oscillatory activity and neuronal synchronization in cognition.    Previous studies have identified a system of structures in the medial temporal lobe that is critical for declarative memory, the ability for the conscious recollection of facts and events. This system of structures includes the hippocampus and the surrounding cortical regions. These are the structures that are affected first in Alzheimer's disease, and lesions of these structures produce profound memory deficits. Their research focuses on potential mechanisms by which these structures set up new memories and how these structures might work together during memory formation and retrieval. Along with modulations in spiking activity of individual neurons, they investigate the role of synchronous oscillatory activity across networks of neurons.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 28, 2015).12aSynaptic Transmissionxphysiology22aMemoryxphysiology22aNeuronsxphysiology22aTemporal Lobexphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18894  aAUTH02573cgm a2200529 i 9918351572406676DNLM20260227161907.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220226s2022    mdu093|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32145  a(OCoLC)1348919632  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 506.62 aNIH RECOVER Listening Sessiond(2022 January 21 :cOnline)jauthor.10aUnderstanding long COVID across communities of color and those hardest hit by COVID /cDr. Andrea Lerner, Dr. Lenora Johnson, Dr. Natasha Williams, Dr. James Hildreth. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 33 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded January 21, 2022.3 a(CIT): In February 2021, the National Institutes of Health (NIH) launched the RECOVER initiative to bring together researchers, scientists, patients, and caregivers to identify the causes and means to prevent and treat post-acute sequelae of SARS-CoV-2 (PASC), including what is commonly called Long COVID. This Listening Session will engage patients and organizations representing communities of color and those hardest hit by COVID to better understand levels of awareness of Long COVID. The discussion will also inform opportunities to promote diverse and inclusive participation in the RECOVER Initiative studies.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed October 13, 2022).22aNIH RECOVER (Initiative)12aCOVID-19xcomplications12aMinority Groups22aPersistent Infection22aResearch Design22aHealth Equity22aHealth Education 2aUnited States 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings1 aLerner, Andreaq(Andrea Marguerite),ehost.1 aJohnson, Lenora.1 aWilliams, Natashaq(Natasha J.),ehost.1 aHildreth, James,cMD,emoderator.2 aNIH RECOVER (Initiative),esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44524  aAUTH01425cas a22004451  20810180RDNLM20260227090923.0cr||||||||||||821218c19349999mdu x         0    0eng    a   35009665   a0037-783Xl0037-783X  aSORSAT  a(DNLM)S28640000(s)  a(OCoLC)01664336  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bSO12510aSoc Res (New York)2dnlm00aSocial research.  aNew York, Ny :bNew School For Social Research31aBaltimore, MD :bJohns Hopkins University Press  av.  aFour no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-    Feb. 1934-0 aMEDLINEbSelected citations only0 aPubMedbSelected citations only0 aOLDMEDLINE  aAlso issued online.  aIssued by the Graduate Faculty of Political and Social Science of the New School for Social Research. 2aPolitics 2aSocial Sciences 2aPeriodical2 aNew School for Social Research (New York, N.Y. : 1919-1997).bGraduate Faculty of Political and Social Science.0 x1944-768X4 uhttp://muse.jhu.edu/journals/social%5Fresearch/  aAUTH02368cgm a22004334a 101546215DNLM20260227153641.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101027s2010    mdu090|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16118  a(OCoLC)692014623  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWQ 2001 aDeCherney, Alan H.10aUpdate on reproductive health :bcontroversies and approaches to fertility /cAlan DeCherney, Teresa K. Woodruff, and Lawrence M. Nelson.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aORWH Women's Health Special Interest Group  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 30 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 17, 2010.3 a(CIT): Women's Health Special Interest Group Lecture Intramural Program on Research on Women's Health In Collaboration with The Intramural Research Program on Reproductive and Adult Endocrinology and the NIH Inter-Institute Endocrinology Fellowship Program   Alan DeCherney, M.D.  Senior Moderator Chief, Reproductive Biology & Medicine Branch (RBMB)     National Institute of Child Health and Human Development Bethesda, Maryland Teresa K. Woodruff, Ph.D. Thomas J. Watkins Professor of Obstetrics & Gynecology Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology Division of Fertility Preservation  Feinberg School of Medicine   Northwestern University   Chicago, Illinois Lawrence M. Nelson, M.D.  Investigator Section on Implantation & Oocyte Physiology National Institute of Child Health and Human Development And Board Member, NIH Intramural Program on Research on Women"s Health Bethesda, Maryland.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aReproductive Medicinexmethods22aFertilityxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bWomen's Health Special Interest Group. 0aWomen's Health Special Interest Group (Series)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16118  aAUTH01548cas a2200493 a 9007504DNLM20260227091016.0cr|cn ||||||||900525c19909999sz  x p       0    0eng    a1015-2008l1015-2008  aPATHEF  a(DNLM)SR0066201(s)  a(OCoLC)21357241  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Switzerland00aW1bPA895510aPathobiology2dnlm00aPathobiology :bjournal of immunopathology, molecular and cellular biology.  aBasel ; New York :bS. Karger,cc1990-  av. :bill.  aSix no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 a58/1 (Jan.-Feb. 1990)-  aBimonthly.1 aMEDLINEbv58n1, 1990-1 aPubMedbv58n1, 1990-1 aIndex medicus  aCalled also v. 58, no. 1- .  aAlso available online.  aFormed by the union of: Experimental cell biology, and: Pathology and immunopathology research.  3Vol. 58, no. 1 (JAN-FEB 1990)c19911012fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aCells 2aImmunity 2aMolecular Biology 2aPathology 2aPeriodical0 x1423-029114tExperimental cell biology14tPathology and immunopathology research4 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/224272  aAUTH02368cgm a22004694a 101463227DNLM20260227152421.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080102s2007    mdu072 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14215  a(OCoLC)288982120  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 102.81 aDarnell, Robert.10aRNA brain maps /cRobert Darnell.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 3, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 11 min., 15 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 17, 2007.3 a(CIT): Dr. Darnell will speak about how brain-specific splicing factors interacting with splicing silencer and enhancer elements lead to RNA isoforms in the brain that are different from the corresponding isoforms in other organs. One of these splicing factors, NOVA, has a predilection for regulating the splicing of RNAs that encode proteins acting at the neuronal synapse.  Indeed, in the absence of NOVA in knock-out mice, synaptic NOVA targets are changed in sequence though not in level, with the consequence that LTP of slow inhibitory postsynaptic currents is completely and specifically absent. So, don't forget splicing when you think of gene expression! And by the way, because NOVA was discovered as a target of an auto-immune reaction in patients with a particular form of a paraneoplastic neurological syndrome, Dr. Darnell's talk should also be interesting to immunologists and clinicians.    NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aSynapsesxphysiology22aBrainxphysiology22aProtein Interaction Mapping22aProtein Isoforms22aRNA Splicing 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14215  aAUTH01408cas a2200469 a 9607822DNLM20260227091013.0cr|cnu||||||||960620c19969999ja br p       0||| 0eng    a1341-7568l1341-7568  aGGSYF5  a(DNLM)SR0086761(s)  a(OCoLC)34770172  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Japan00aW1bID39510aGenes Genet Syst2dnlm00aGenes & genetic systems.3 aGenes and genetic systems  aMishima, Shizuoka, Japan :bGenetics Society of Japan,c1996-  av. :bill.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 71, no. 1 (Feb. 1996)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv71n1,Feb. 1996-0 aPubMedbv71n1,Feb. 1996-1 aIndex medicus  aSupplements, primarily in Japanese, accompany some issues.  aAlso issued online.  aOfficial journal of the Genetics Society of Japan. 2aGenes 2aGenetics 2aPeriodical2 aNihon Iden Gakkai.0 x1880-577900tIdengaku zasshix0021-504Xw(DLC)   94659529w(OCoLC)17135804 uhttps://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/browse/ggs  aAUTH01676cas a2200505   0364535DNLM20260227090915.0cr|cn ||||||||730725c19739999enkbr         0    0eng    a   75640658   a0091-0562l0091-0562  aAJCPCK  a(DNLM)A24045000(s)