apcc  9United States00aWA 300 AA12 aNIH Minority Health Promotion Day Speakers Forumd(2012 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aNIH Minority Health Promotion Day Speakers Forum, Masur Auditorium /cthe National Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities. 1aBethesda :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012.  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hrs. 32 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 19, 2012.3 a(CIT): The session will explore the role of factors such as housing circumstances, environmental influences, transportation, poverty, and social justice in health disparities. It will also highlight work taking place nationally and globally by Federal agencies to address the social determinants of health in an effort to improve the health of minority and other underserved populations.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed October 21, 2020).12aMinority Health22aHealth Status Disparities22aHealth Equity22aHealth Promotion 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=11206  aAUTH02527cgm a2200433 i 101634137DNLM20260227154625.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140603s2014    mdu130|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18438  a(OCoLC)882609945  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 1402 aORWH National Women's Health Week Scientific Forumd(2014 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aORWH National Women's Health Week Scientific Forum /cNIH Office of Research on Women's Health. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 10 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded May 14, 2014.3 a(CIT): Does sex matter for research and health? Get answers at "Sex Differences in Neuroscience," a scientific symposium to highlight 2014 National Women"s Health Week. NIH's Office of Research on Women's Health (ORWH) invites you to hear keynote speaker and National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke Director Dr. Story C. Landis and other leading scientists explore perspectives on the role of sex and gender in the brain and beyond. Other featured speakers include: Dr. Cheryl Bushnell, Associate Professor of Neurology and Director, Wake Forest Baptist Stroke Center; and Dr. Margaret McCarthy, Professor and Chair, Department of Pharmacology, Program in Neuroscience, University of Maryland School of Medicine. In addition, Dr. Janine Austin Clayton, ORWH Director, will provide introductory remarks. Attendance is free. The event will be held on Wednesday, May 14, 2014, at 2 p.m. in the Lipsett Amphitheater on the NIH campus in Bethesda, MD. The event will not be live videocast.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed June 24, 2014.12aNervous System Diseases22aBiomedical Research22aSex Factors22aWomen's Health 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Research on Women's Health,esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18438  aAUTH02596cgm a2200457 i 101607218DNLM20260227154840.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130427s2013    mdu070|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17874  a(OCoLC)857718928  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4771 aAlt, Frederick W.eauthor.10aAntibodies, genome stability, and cancer /cFred Alt. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 10 min.) :bcolor, sound)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdm2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aWednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Mar. 27, 2013.3 a(CIT): Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series  Dr. Alt and his group developed high-throughput, genome-wide translocation sequencing (HTGTS) to elucidate mechanisms that generate and join DNA double strand breaks (DSBs) and mediate translocations. He will discuss several new studies that employ the HTGTS approach and other approaches to address questions about the immunoglobulin heavy locus (IgH) class switch recombination (CSR) including how CSR DSBs are joined in a productive (deletional versus inversional) orientation in the chromosome; how IgH DSBs are synapsed for joining over several 100-kilobyte chromosomal distances to effect physiological levels of CSR; and the potential roles of the nuclear protein kinase ataxia telangiectasia mutated (ATM)-dependent DNA DSB response factors, including 53BP1, in the CSR process. He will also discuss the use of HTGTS to elucidate novel mechanisms that lead to V(D)J recombination-associated oncogenic chromosomal translocations and oncogene activation in ATM-deficient B- and T-cell lymphomas.  aDescription based on version viewed Une 5, 2013.12aDNA Breaks, Double-Stranded22aHigh-Throughput Nucleotide Sequencing22aImmunoglobulin Class Switching22aSequence Analysis, DNA22aTranslocation, Geneticximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17874  aAUTH01532cgm a22004214a 101268591DNLM20260227150823.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu442 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12264  a(OCoLC)130523567  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWK 550bO32 200400aOffice of Research on Women's Health Symposium on Family Hormonal Health /cOffice of Research on Women's Health.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 2, 2007).  aStreaming video (7 hr., 21 min.. 9 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 29, 2004.  aAlso available in print (14 p.)  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aPituitary Diseases22aFamily Health22aSex Factors 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Research on Women's Health.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12264  aAUTH01985cgmaa22004694a 101173029DNLM20260227144244.0vf|cbahoucr|cnu||||||||vf|cjahou030328s2003    mdu060       f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:1887  a(OCoLC)52854160  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 573bVC no.4 200300aRegulating T cell antigen receptor signaling /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (60 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3master.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (ca. 60 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aThe NIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture  aTitle from cassette spine label.0 aSpeaker, Arthur Weiss.  aPresented Mar. 19, 2003.3 a(Producer)  Signal transduction by the T cell receptor (TRC) is initiated through the sequential interaction with Src and Syk kinases.  CD45, an abundant receptor-like tyrosine phosphatase, is required for antigen receptor-mediated signaling.  aAlso available online.  aVHS.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aReceptors, Antig