3803  a(OCoLC)173670595  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 2031 aApud, Jose A.10aDemystifying medicine.pSchizophrenia :bclinical and basic aspects.30aSchizophrenia  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner.  aSpeakers: Jose A. Apud, Stefano Marenco, Joseph H. Callicott.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 55 min. : sd., col.).  aComment: The course includes presentation of patients, pathology, diagnosis and therapy in the context of major disease problems and current research.  Primarily directed toward Ph.D. students, fellows, and staff, it is also of interest to medical students and clinicians.  aRecorded May 1, 2007.3 a(CIT): The course includes presentation of patients, pathology, diagnosis and therapy in the context of major disease problems and current research.  Primarily directed toward Ph.D. students, fellows, and staff, it is also of interest to medical students and clinicians.  The course is designed to help bridge the gap between advances in biology and their application to major human diseases.  Each session includes clinical and basic science components which are presented by NIH staff and outside invitees.  For more information, visit  http://www1.od.nih.gov/oir/DemystifyingMed.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government.  aOpen-captioned.12aSchizophreniaxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13803  aAUTH01219cam a2200337 i 60331120RDNLM20260206221442.0cr||||||||||||820626s1910    iluc          000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)9402331  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aBFbW277su 19101 aWarman, Edward Barrett,d1847-1931,eauthor.10aSuggestion /cby Edward B. Warman. 1aChicago :bA.C. McClurg & Co.,c1910.  a64 pages :bportrait ;c20 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aPsychic science series ;vno. V  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc202506092pda5DNLM  aConservedc202506302pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aSuggestion22aFaith Healingxmethods1 aWarman, Edward Barrett,d1847-1931.tPsychic science series ;vno. 5.4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/60331120R  aAUTH01477cas a2200457 a 101558150DNLM20260227090908.0cr||||||||||||091021c20109999caubr p       |    0eng    a  20092030200 a2151-4585l2151-4585  a(OCoLC)457771293  aDLCcDLCdDLCdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW1bGE457CHF10aGeriatr Orthop Surg Rehabil2dnlm00aGeriatric orthopaedic surgery & rehabilitation.1 aGeriatric orthopaedic surgery and rehabilitation  aThousand Oaks, CA :bSage Publications  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1, no. 1 (Sept. 2010).  aPrint issues ceased with v. 4 (2013)0 aPubMedbv1, 2010-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: Vol. 2, no. 1 (Jan. 2011); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 2, no. 2 (Mar. 2011).12aMusculoskeletal Diseasesxsurgery22aAged22aMusculoskeletal Diseasesxrehabilitation22aOrthopedic Procedures 2aPeriodical0 x2151-45934 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=1015581504 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/gos  aAUTH03777cgm a2200469 a 101557261DNLM20260227153839.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110325s2011    mdu061 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16458  a(OCoLC)756894946  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 530.11 aShah, Rajiv Janardan,d1973-10aAddressing grand challenges :bthe role of science in global health development /cRajiv Shah.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aDavid E. Barmes lecture on global health  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Feb. 15, 2011.3 a(CIT):  Dr. Rajiv Shah is administrator of the United States Agency for International Development (USAID). This agency has provided economic and humanitarian assistance worldwide for nearly 50 years. USAID recently unveiled a new approach that will support President Obama"s vision for high-impact global development, announced by the White House in September. This new approach will be focusing on:Recommitting to the Millennium Development Goals by building sustainable governance and delivery systems to support healthy and productive lives Investing in country-owned models of inclusive growth and development in a focused set of countries that are well-governed, economically stable, globally connected and market-oriented. Developing and delivering scientific and technological breakthroughs. Prior to being appointed USAID Administrator, Dr. Shah served as Under Secretary for Research, Education and Economics and chief scientist at the U.S. Department of Agriculture, where he was responsible for safe, sustainable, competitive U.S. food and fiber system, as well as strong communities, families, and youth through integrated research, analysis, and education. Previously, Shah served as director of agricultural development at the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation. In his seven years with the Gates Foundation, he served as its director of strategic opportunities and as deputy director of policy and finance for the global health program. In these roles, he helped develop and launch the foundation's global development program and helped create both the Alliance for a Green Revolution in Africa and the International Finance Facility for Immunization - an effort that raised more than $5 billion for child immunization.Shah also is co-founder of Health Systems Analytics and Project IMPACT for South Asian Americans. In addition, he has served as a policy aide in the British Parliament and worked at the World Health Organization.Originally from Detroit, Michigan, Shah earned his M.D. from the University of Pennsylvania and his master"s in health economics at the Wharton School of Business. He has attended the London School of Economics, is a graduate of the University of Michigan, and has published articles on health policy and global development.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aGlobal Health22aBiomedical Technology22aDiffusion of Innovation22aInternational Cooperation22aScience 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aDavid E. Barmes global health lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16458  aAUTH02943cgm a2200457 a 101557267DNLM20260227153957.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110325s2011    mdu054 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16469  a(OCoLC)756154828  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3201 aBarbour, Boris.10aParasynaptic glutamatergic transmission in the cerebellum /cBoris Barbour.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (54 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Feb. 14, 2011.3 a(CIT): Neuroscience Seminar Series  Dr. Boris Barbour began his scientific career in the lab of David Attwell at University College London. His PhD studies aimed at characterizing the biophysical properties of glutamate transporters in salamander retinal glial cells and led to the novel finding that glutamate transporters can enhance - rather than reduce - the extracellular concentration of glutamate under ionic conditions similar to those observed during stroke and brain anoxia. He moved to France for a postdoctoral term with Philippe Ascher, and was soon granted an independent position at Ecole Normale Superieure in Paris. With his unique blend of mathematical and electrophysiological approaches, Dr. Barbour provided some of the most insightful quantitative descriptions of glutamate spillover in the brain. These studies remain, to date, a hallmark reference for understanding the extent to which synaptic specificity is preserved or disrupted in different brain areas. Later on, Dr. Barbour became the head of the cerebellum group and the chairman of the neuroscience section at Ecole Normale, while his interests veered towards defining the network mechanisms that underlie the temporal organization of synaptic activity in the cerebellum. In his talk on Feb 14th, Dr. Barbour will share his thoughts on non-synaptic transmission and on its role on network activity, glial tropism and synaptic plasticity.    For more information, visit  http://neuroseries.info.nih.gov.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aCerebellumxphysiology22aBrain Diseasesxphysiopathology22aGlutamic Acidxphysiology22aSynaptic Transmissionxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16469  aAUTH01970cas a2200529 i 101685427DNLM20260227090908.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||160620c20169999enktr poo     0   a0eng    a  2020205065  a2397-1991l2397-1983  a(OCoLC)1018193506z(OCoLC)1156746583  aNLEbengerdad20260227cNLEdCNCGMdUABdVT2dOCLCFdOCLCOdOCLCQdUKMGBdDKUdOCLCQdTXAdSRUdDLCdDNLM  apcc  9England00aRL45100aW104a616.5442230 aJ. scleroderma relat. disord.b(Online)10aJ Scleroderma Relat Disord2dnlm 0aJournal of scleroderma and related disordersb(Online)00aJournal of scleroderma and relat