psychoanalytic reviewx0885-7830w(DLC)sf 85007053w(OCoLC)17782214 uhttp://guilfordjournals.com/loi/prev  aAUTH02560cgm a2200469 i 101774289DNLM20260227160455.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu064|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31402  a(OCoLC)1236084300  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 50500aEthical quandaries in the COVID-19 pandemic /cChristine Grady. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 4 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCOVID-19 Scientific Interest Group lecture series  aRecorded November 19, 2020.3 a(CIT): Dr. Grady is chief of the Clinical Center's Department of Bioethics.  Her research contributions are both conceptual and empirical and are primarily in the ethics of clinical research including informed consent, vulnerability, study design, recruitment, and international research ethics, as well as ethical issues faced by nurses and other health care providers.   Since the beginning of the COVID-19 pandemic, Dr Grady and her colleagues have provided consultation and have written and spoken about a number of ethical issues related to COVID-19 research and clinical care.  In this talk, she will discuss a number of vexing ethical issues that have arisen during the coronavirus pandemic that span clinical ethics, public health ethics, and research ethics.  Included among them will be ethical issues related to health care provider responsibilities, fair allocation of resources, and selection of appropriate research study designs.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed February 3, 2021).12aCOVID-1912aPandemicsxethics22aDelivery of Health Carexethics22aPublic Health Practicexethics22aEthics, Research 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aGrady, Christine,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bCOVID-19 Scientific Interest Group,esponsoring body. 0aNIH COVID-19 SIG lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38874  aAUTH02825cas a2200697   0354343DNLM20260227090917.0cr|cnu||||||||hd|bfa012baap730409c19199999caumr p       0    0eng    a   21005813 zsn 78004704  a0022-0345l0022-0345  aJDREAF  a(DNLM)J18660000(s)  a(OCoLC)01754534  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bJO618K10aJ Dent Res2dnlm 0aJournal of dental research00aJournal of dental research.30aDental research10aJDR  aChicago,bAmerican Dental Assn. [etc.]3 aThousand Oaks, CA :bSage  av.billus., ports.  aMonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-       Mar. 1919-  aPlace of publication varies.  aIssues for <Feb. 1993-> also called: JDR.1 aMEDLINEbv44n5,Sept.-Oct. 1965-1 aPubMedbv44n5,Sept.-Oct. 1965-1 aIndex to dental literaturex0019-3992bv44n5,Sept.-Oct. 1965-v79n3,Mar. 20000 aOLDMEDLINE1 aIndex medicus  aSupplements accompany some numbers.  aAlso issued online.  aMicrofilm.mv. 1 (1919)-34 (1955).b[Bethesda, Md.] :cNational Library of Medicine,d1994.e18 microfilm reels : negative ; 35 mm.  aOfficial publication of the International Association for Dental Research, Apr. 1934-        and some numbers contain the Proceedings or Programs and abstracts of papers of the association's general sessions as well as biographical directories of members.  aIssued also by the American Association for Dental Research, <2003->  3Vol. 74, no. 1 (JAN 1995)c19950523fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aDentistry 2aResearch 2aConference Proceedings 2aDirectory 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Dental Association.2 aInternational Association for Dental Research.2 aInternational Association for Dental, Oral, and Craniofacial Research.2 aAmerican Association for Dental Research.2 aAmerican Association for Dental, Oral, and Craniofacial Research.0 tAdvances in dental researchx0895-9374w(OCoLC)168576670 x1544-059100tJournal of the Allied Dental Societiesx0095-957Xw(DLC)sn 90017574w(OCoLC)175482705tCritical reviews in oral biology and medicinex1045-4411w(OCoLC)201149614 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/jdr  aAUTH01912cgm a2200445 i 101660642DNLM20260227155040.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150602s2015    mdu055|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19016  a(OCoLC)918550293  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQ 158.500aArts and science :btwo old friends talk about their lives and works /cAlan Alda, Roger Rosenblatt. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (55 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIMH director's innovation speaker series  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded May 21, 2015.3 a(CIT): NIMH Director's Innovation Speaker Series  Title: "Arts and Science: Two Old Friends Talk About Their Lives and Works"   The National Institute of Mental Health is pleased to invite you to attend the sixth of a series of lectures dedicated to innovation, invention and scientific discovery  Speakers:  Alan Alda & Roger Rosenblatt.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 30, 2015).12aAlda, Alan,d1936-12aRosenblatt, Roger.12aScience 2aWebcast1 aAlda, Alan,d1936-einterviewee1 aRosenblatt, Roger,einterviewee2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIMH director's innovation speaker series.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19016  aAUTH01503cas a2200469 a 101088679DNLM20260227090914.0m        u        cr cnu||||||||010221c20019999enkuu pss     0    0eng    a  2002243092  a1472-6920l1472-6920  a(OCoLC)47657387  aCOUcCOUdOCLCQdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9England10aW110aBMC Med Educ2dnlm00aBMC medical education.3 aBioMed Central medical education30aMedical education  aLondon :bBioMed Central,c[2001-  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1 (2001)-  aTitle from BioMed Central archive volume screen (viewed on Feb. 15, 2002).1 aMEDLINEbv1, 2001-0 aPubMedbv1, 2001-0 aPMC1 aIndex medicus  aArticles are added consecutively to the current annual v.  aSystem requirements: Internet connectivity and World Wide Web browser.  aMode of access: Internet.12aEducation, Medical, Continuing12aEducation, Medical, Graduate12aEducation, Medical, Undergraduate 2aPeriodical4 uhttps://bmcmededuc.biomedcentral.com/40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101088679  aAUTH03825cgm a2200409 i 101660640DNLM20260227155058.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150602s2015    mdu062|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19013  a(OCoLC)914328274  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 531 aPage, David C.eauthor.10aLost in translation :bdo males and females read their genomes differently? /cDavid C. Page. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture. Annual Marshall Nirenberg lecture  aRecorded May 20, 2015.3 a(CIT): The Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series presents the annual Marshall W. Nirenberg lecture  "Nothing in biology makes sense except in the light of evolution." (Theodosius Dobzhansky, 1973). Dobzhansky's oft-quoted dictum is highly relevant to our understanding of the manifold differences between males and females in health and disease, both within and beyond the reproductive tract. From the origins of sexual reproduction, in single-celled organisms, to the morphing of our own sex chromosomes from ordinary autosomes during the past 200-300 million years, comparative and evolutionary analyses offer deep, biologically grounded insights into the epigenetic, genomic, and phenotypic differences between the sexes. The emerging evolutionary view serves as an alternative, or complement, to the model (chromosomal sex > gonadal sex > phenotypic sex) that has guided teaching and research for the last half century. The opportunity now presents itself to capitalize upon this emerging evolutionary view of sex differences and their origins.  Most recently, Dr. Page and colleagues have conducted comparative genomic and evolutionary studies of the sex chromosomes of humans, other mammals, and birds. These studies have revealed that the Y chromosome's gene content became specialized, through natural selection, to maintain the ancestral dosage of homologous X-Y gene pairs that function as broadly expressed regulators of the genome and gene activity; these X-Y gene pairs regulate transcription, translation, and protein stability (Bellott et al., Nature 508: 494-499, 2014). As a result, there exists a fundamental sexual dimorphism--at a biochemical level and throughout the body--that derives directly from genetic differences between the X and Y chromosomes. This fundamental sexual dimorphism may play an unappreciated role in health and disease in both males and females.  About the Annual Marshall W. Nirenberg Lecture: The lecture, established in 2011, honors Marshall W. Nirenberg for deciphering the genetic code, which resulted in his sharing the 1968 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Dr. Nirenberg's career at NIH spanned more than 50 years and his later research focused on neuroscience, with particular emphasis on neural development. The Nirenberg lecture recognizes scientists who have made outstanding contributions to genetics and molecular biology.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 15, 2015).12aSex Factors22aGenomics 2aLecture 2aWebcast 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pAnnual Marshall Nirenberg lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19013  aAUTH03277cgm a2200457 i 9918367462706676DNLM20260227162050.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220318s2022    mdu341|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32245  a(DNLM)CIT:32246  a(OCoLC)1416045638  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWX 2152 aNational Nursing Research Roundtabled(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNational nursing research roundtable /cNINR, NIH.1 iTitle from narration:aFrom context to care :bunderstanding the convergence of social factors and emergency department care 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (5 hr., 41 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded March 3 & 4, 2022.3 a(CIT): The National Nursing Research Roundtable (NNRR) is an informal association of nursing organizations with a significant research interest that provides clinicians, scientists, educators, scholars, and policy leaders an opportunity to come together to discuss priorities important for both the science and practice of nursing. Annually, NNRR is co-hosted by the National Institute of Nursing Research (NINR) and a research-focused nursing organization. The mission of the National Nursing Research Roundtable (NNRR) is to serve the public's health through stimulating the development of a strong, research-based nursing practice. As a collaborative of nursing organizations, the NNRR provides clinicians, scientists, educators, scholars, and policy leaders an opportunity to come together to discuss priorities in science, practice, and policy, and to envision the future. Purpose of the NNRR: Since 1987, leaders of nursing organizations with a research focus have participated in the annual meeting of the NNRR. Together, they share advances in science and practice as they work to identify, enhance, and leverage research resources. They assess research challenges, gaps, redundancies, and opportunities. These leaders also pinpoint and support strategies, innovations, and policy initiatives that fuel new discoveries in science that drive the translation of these discoveries into improvements in our nation's health. The annual meeting of the NNRR fosters communication, promotes collaboration, disseminates knowledge, and informs research efforts on a national scale.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 12, 2023).12aEmergency Room Visits22aSocial Determinants of Health22aSocial Welfare22aReferral and Consultation 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Nursing Research (U.S.),ehost institution.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4398040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=43982  aAUTH01490cas a22004574a 100892849DNLM20260224111543.0cr|cnu||||||||990708c20009999dcubr p       0    0eng    asn 99008843 0 a1525-7797l1525-7797  aBOMAF6  a(OCoLC)41669992  aNSDcNSDdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bBI852RB10aBiomacromolecules2dnlm00aBiomacromolecules.  aWashington, DC :bAmerican Chemical Society,cc2000-  av. :bill. ;c28 cm.  aBimonthly,b2001-  aQuarterly,b2000  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (spring 2000)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, Spring 2000-1 aPubMedbv1n1, Spring 2000-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso available via the World Wide Web; online version available to subscribers with a site license; files in HTML and PDF format.  3v. 1, no. 1 (Spring 2000)c20011113fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper12aBiological Science Disciplines12aPolymers22aMacromolecular Substances2 aAmerican Chemical Society.0 x1526-46024 uhttp://pubs.acs.org/journals/bomaf6/index.html  aAUTH01720cas a2200481 a 8511159DNLM20260227091025.0cr|cn ||||||||851121c19849999nyu x p       0    0eng    a   85641422 zsn 84000105  a0743-4863l0743-4863  aCRTSEO  a(DNLM)SR0053950(s)  a(OCoLC)10588544  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bCR216ZF10aCrit Rev Ther Drug Carrier Syst2dnlm 0aCritical reviews in therapeutic drug carrier systems00aCritical reviews in therapeutic drug carrier systems.14aCRC critical reviews in therapeutic drug carrier systems  aBoca Raton, Fla. :bCRC Press,cc1984-3 aNew York, NY :bBegell House  av. :bill.  aSix no. a year,b1998-  aQuarterly,b-1997  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, issue 1-  aOn t.p. the registered trademark symbol "TM" is superscript following "reviews" in the title, 1985?- .1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, 1984-0 aPubMedbv1n1, 1984-1 aIndex medicus  aVol. 4, no. 3 (1988) incorrectly dated as 1987.  aAlso issued online.  3Vol. 1, no. 1 (1984)c19911012fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aPharmaceutical Preparationsxadministration & dosage 2aPeriodical0 x2162-660X4 uhttp://www.begellhouse.com/journals/critical-reviews-in-therapeutic-drug-carrier-systems.html  aAUTH04737cgm a2200433 i 101660628DNLM20260227155049.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150602s2015    mdu067|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18989  a(OCoLC)911194483  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWB 1021 aGroopman, Jerome E.eauthor.10aWhen experts disagree :bthe art of medical decision making /cJerome Groopman, Pamela Hartzband. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 7 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aStephen E. Straus distinguished lecture in the science of complementary health therapies  aRecorded May 11, 2015.3 a(CIT): On May 11, the National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH) will hold the annual Stephen E. Straus Distinguished Lecture in the Science of Complementary Health Therapies. Speakers, Jerome Groopman, M.D., the Dina and Raphael Recanati chair of medicine at Harvard Medical School and chief of experimental medicine at Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center and Pamela Hartzband, M.D., assistant professor of medicine at Harvard Medical School and attending physician in the division of endocrinology at Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center will present "When Experts Disagree: The Art of Medical Decision Making." The husband-and-wife team will present a new way to make the best medical decisions. They reveal that each of us has a "medical mind," a highly individual approach to weighing the risks and benefits of treatment. To ascertain our unique medical minds, they will present probing questions. Are you a minimalist or a maximalist, a believer or a doubter, do you look for natural healing or the latest technology? Drs. Groopman and Hartzband explain how pitfalls in thinking and the way statistics are presented in pharmaceutical advertisements, the news media and even scientific reports can mislead all of us. The talk will demonstrate the contrast between the role of population guidelines with the care of the individual, and explain the complexities of end of life care - all factors that contribute to a person"s "medical mind." In their talk, Drs. Groopman and Hartzband will weave vivid narratives from real patient experiences with insights from recent cognitive research to demonstrate how to arrive at choices that serve the individual best. Dr. Groopman"s research interests include studying how viruses cause immune deficiency and cancer, the role of endocannabinoids in hematopoiesis, mechanisms of liver injury due to hepatitis c, and the effects of novel cell cycle inhibitors against mantle cell lymphoma. He has been integral in developing many AIDS-related therapies. Dr. Hartzband"s clinical interests include thyroid disorders, adrenal and pituitary disorders, osteoporosis, and calcium disorders. Together, Drs. Groopman and Hartzband co-authored the book "How Doctors Think" and write a monthly column for the American College of Physicians" publication "ACP Internist." The lecture series was established in honor of Stephen E. Straus, M.D., founding director of NCCAM and an internationally recognized clinician-scientist. Under his leadership, research of complementary health therapies grew threefold. In addition to serving as NCCAM director, Dr. Straus was senior investigator in the Laboratory of Clinical Investigation at the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases. This event is supported by the Foundation for the National Institutes of Health with generous funding from Bernard and Barbro Osher. The lecture begins at 10 a.m. in Masur Auditorium, Building 10. All are invited to attend. It will also be videocast on http://videocast.nih.gov For more information go to <a href='http://nccam.nih.gov'>http://nccam.nih.gov.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed June 12, 2015).12aClinical Medicine12aDecision Making 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aHartzband, Pamela,eauthor.2 aNational Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aStephen E. Straus distinguished lecture in the science of complementary health therapies40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18989  aAUTH01407cas a2200433 i 101630272DNLM20260227090910.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||140411c20149999ie || poo     0    0eng    a  2015243147  a2214-7500l2