ed for the European Society for Developmental Psychology by Psychology Press,cc2004-  av. :bill.  aBimonthly,b2008-  aQuarterly,b2004-2007  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, issue 1 (Mar. 2004)-  aTitle from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 1, issue 2 (June 2004).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.  aOfficial publication of the European Society for Developmental Psychology.12aHuman Development22aPsychology, Adolescent22aPsychology, Child 2aPeriodical2 aEuropean Society for Developmental Psychology.0 x1740-56104 uhttps://www.tandfonline.com/loi/pedp20  aAUTH02921cgm a2200457 i 101607239DNLM20260227154642.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130427s2013    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17915  a(OCoLC)851144856  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3001 aManiatis, Tom,eauthor.14aThe generation and function of single cell diversity in the mammalian brain /cTom Maniatis. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.) :bcolor, sound).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdm2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aWednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Apr. 24, 2013.3 a(CIT): The human brain contains over 85 billion neurons, each of which can engage in thousands of synaptic interactions. Correct neural circuit assembly during development requires interactions between cell surface proteins at the synapse as well as a mechanism that allows individual neurons to distinguish self from non-self. One mechanism to accomplish this ability to distinguish self from non-self is to generate sufficient cell surface diversity to provide individual cells with an identity "barcode." In mammals this barcode appears to be provided by the clustered protocadherin (Pcdh) proteins. These cadherin-like proteins are encoded in three large contiguous gene clusters (a, b, and g) whose organization leads to the generation of enormous cell surface diversity through a mechanism that involves stochastic alternative promoter choice and alternative RNA splicing.  Individual neurons can distinguish self from non-self when this diversity generator is coupled to a strict requirement for homophilic interactions. In this lecture, Dr. Maniatis will discuss the molecular mechanisms by which Pcdh diversity is generated; recent advances in understanding the nature of homophilic Pcdh protein interactions; and, the results of studies directed towards understanding the functions of Pcdh proteins through the analysis of Pcdh gene cluster deletions in the mouse.  aDescription based on version viewed June 12, 2013.12aBrainxmetabolism22aAlternative Splicingxgenetics22aCadherinsxgenetics22aCadherinsxmetabolism22aPromoter Regions, Genetic. 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17915  aAUTH01445cgm a22004454a 101518952DNLM20260227153107.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u091105s2009    mdu056|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15331  a(OCoLC)501272938  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2661 aFoley, Kathleen M.,d1944-10aAdvancing palliative care for cancer patients globally /cKathleen M. Foley.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCenter for Cancer Research-National Cancer Institute grand rounds  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (56 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS and NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Sept. 29, 2009.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxtherapy22aIntractable Painxtherapy22aPalliative Carexmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15331  aAUTH02501cgm a2200457 i 101546198DNLM20260227154002.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101027s2010    mdu060|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16094  a(OCoLC)682072026  aDNLMcDNLMerdadDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWE 5501 aAgar, Jeffrey,espeaker.10aALS-associated structural perturbation of Cu/Zn superoxide dismutase and strategies for protein stabilization /cJeffrey Agar, Ph.D., Department of Chemistry, Brandeis University.  a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2010]  a1 online resource (1 hr.) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdm2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Proteomics Interest Group seminar series  aRecorded September 2, 2010.3 a(CIT): Mutations in the gene encoding Cu/Zn-Superoxide dismutase (SOD1) cause a subset of familial ALS (fALS), through an unknown toxic mechanism. We performed H/D exchange on 13 ALS-associated SOD1 variants and have identified structural defects shared by every fALS SOD1 variant tested. A longstanding question is whether post-translational modifications of wild-type SOD1 could cause sporadic ALS. Using SOD1 purified from human nervous tissue we identified post-translational modifications that affect protein structure to a greater extent than some mutations. We designed secondary "rescue" mutations to prevent some of these modifications, and these mutants completely reverse the toxicity of ALS-associated mutants. Finally, we present first generation compounds that target various disease-associated SOD1 structural modifications, including compounds that result in unprecedented protein stabilization.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aAmyotrophic Lateral Sclerosisxetiology22aCopperxmetabolism22aProtein Conformation22aSuperoxide Dismutasexgenetics22aZincxmetabolism 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH Proteomics Interest Group seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16094  aAUTH01367cas a2200421 a 8806593DNLM20260227091017.0cr|cn ||||||||880616c19879999wauqr p       0    0eng    a0269-8803l0269-8803  zJPSYE  a(DNLM)SR0058209(s)z(DNLM)SR0074113(s)  a(OCoLC)16513165  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bJO858T10aJ Psychophysiol2dnlm00aJournal of psychophysiology.  aOxford :bOxford University Press,cc1987-3 3<1999->:aSeattle, WA :bHogrefe and Huber Publishers  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Mar. 1987)-  aTitle from cover.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.  aPublished jointly by: the British and German Psychophysiology Societies. 2aPsychophysiology 2aPeriodical2 aPsychophysiology Society (Great Britain)2 aDeutsche Gesellschaft für Psychophysiologie und Ihre Anwendung.0 x2151-21244 uhttp://search.ebscohost.com/direct.asp?db=pdh&jid=JOP&scope=site  aAUTH01780cgmaa22004454a 101261147DNLM20260227144708.0vf|cbahouvf|cjahou051107s2005    mdu075 ||    f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:4249  a(OCoLC)63176548  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aQU 7500aChemistry in living systems :bnew tools for probing the glycome /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  aUnited States.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (1 hr., 15 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocasstte of 1 (VHS) (1 hr., 15 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aWALS-the DeWitt Stetten, Jr. lecture  aTitle from cassette lab