mputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with January/December 2015.0 aPubMedbv1, Jan.-Dec. 2015-0 aPMC  aNumbering from content page.  aDescription based on: January/December 2015; title from journal homepage (publisher's Web site, viewed Oct 14, 2015).  aLatest issue consulted: January/December 2015 (viewed Oct. 14, 2015).12aMultiple Sclerosis 2aPeriodical0 tMultiple sclerosis (Houndmills, Basingstoke, England). Multiple sclerosisx1352-4585w(DLC)sn 96039038w(OCoLC)3311998340uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/mso40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101668877  aAUTH03119cgm a2200493 a 101581458DNLM20260227154054.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120331s2012    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17125  a(OCoLC)785706329  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 4751 aYang, Wei,d1963 February 16-10aGenome integrity and cancer prevention :bmolecular mechanisms of DNA repair /cWei Yang.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aWednesday afternoon lecture series1 aG. Burroughs Mider lecture  aRecorded Feb. 22, 2012.3 a(CIT): G. Burroughs Mider Lecture  Established in 1968 in honor of the first NIH director of laboratories and clinics. The lecture is presented by an NIH intramural scientist to recognize and appreciate outstanding contributions to biomedical research.    DNA mismatch repair and translesion synthesis are two essential processes accompanying (or associated with) DNA replication and cell division to ensure genome stability and integrity. Failure in either of these processes leads to an increased mutation rate in all organisms and increased cancer propensity in humans. Although the genes and proteins involved in these pathways were identified more than ten years ago, we are still far away from understanding the molecular mechanisms and from the ultimate goal of using our knowledge to conquer cancer. In this seminar, I will show what we have learned about these processes using structural and molecular approaches. The focus will be on (1) how the mismatch repair proteins MutS and MutL remove mutations and protect us from colon cancers and Lynch Syndrome, and (2) how the special human DNA polymerase functions as a double agent that reduces both UV-damage induced skin cancers and the efficacy of platinum-based chemotherapy.   The NIH Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series includes weekly scientific talks by some of the top researchers in the biomedical sciences worldwide. For more information, visit:  The NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aDNA Damagexgenetics12aDNA Mismatch Repairxgenetics22aColorectal Neoplasmsxgenetics22aColorectal Neoplasmsxprevention & control22aSkin Neoplasmsxgenetics22aSkin Neoplasmsxprevention & control 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series 0aG. Burroughs Mider lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17125  aAUTH03279cgm a2200445 i 101674308DNLM20260227155240.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u151224s2015    mdu067|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19390  a(OCoLC)936194699  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 503.600aUsing data to save more lives and drive progress towards the control of the HIV epidemic /cDeborah L. Birx. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 7 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aJoseph J. Kinyoun memorial lecture  aRecorded December 15, 2015.3 a(CIT): Ambassador-at-Large Dr. Deborah L. Birx, a renowned HIV/AIDS expert, will deliver the 2015 Joseph J. Kinyoun Memorial Lecture. She will discuss how data are being leveraged to save lives and drive progress toward sustained control of the global HIV/AIDS pandemic.   Birx is the U.S. Global AIDS Coordinator and U.S. Special Representative for Global Health Diplomacy. She oversees implementation of the U.S. President"s Emergency Plan for AIDS Relief (PEPFAR), the largest commitment to combat a single disease by any nation in history.   PEPFAR collects and analyzes data to understand health care service cost, quality and achievements at community care sites around the globe. Birx will describe how use of these detailed data has been critical for understanding local HIV prevalence, determining the quality and cost of services and defining key gaps. She will discuss how PEPFAR is working with host countries to use these data to refocus programs for maximal impact.  Over the past 30 years, Birx has focused on HIV/AIDS immunology, vaccine research and global health. As Director of the U.S. Military HIV Research Program, she helped lead the landmark RV144 HIV vaccine trial in Thailand, which provided the first evidence that a vaccine could provide protection against HIV infection. From 2005 to 2014, as Director of the Division of Global HIV/AIDS at CDC, Birx led the agency"s implementation of PEPFAR programs around the world.  Since 1979, NIAID has hosted an annual public lecture in honor of Dr. Joseph J. Kinyoun, a pioneer of modern scientific research. Kinyoun founded the Laboratory of Hygiene, the forerunner of NIH, in 1887, launching a new era of scientific study of infectious diseases.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed Jan. 19, 2016).12aHIV Infectionsxprevention & control22aData Interpretation, Statistical22aGlobal Health22aHIV Infectionsxepidemiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aBirx, Deborah L.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aKinyoun lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19390  aAUTH01382cas a2200421 a 9801724DNLM20260227091042.0cr|cn#||||||||980326c19979999ja tr p       0||| 0eng    a1344-1051l1344-1051  a(DNLM)SR0091613(s)  a(OCoLC)38034248  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9Japan00aW1bCH944G00aChromosome science.  aHigashi-Hiroshima, Japan :bSociety of Chromosome Research,c[1997-3 aHigashi-Hiroshima :bSociety Of Chromosome Research  av. :bill.  aThree no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (1997)-  aTitle form cover.  aAlso issued online.  aOfficial journal of the Society of Chromosome Research.  aMerger of: Senshokutai, and: CIS; chromosome information service.02aChromosomesxgenetics 2aPeriodical2 aSenshokutai Gakkai.14tCIS; chromosome information servicew(DLC)sn 8400135w(OCoLC)626734914tSenshokutaix0385-4655w(OCoLC)22514624 uhttps://dl.ndl.go.jp/pid/10387531  uhttps://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/browse/scr/-char/en  aAUTH01283cas a2200433   7511221DNLM20260227090913.0cr|cn#||||||||751024c19739999xxuqr         0||| 0eng    a0091-6471l0091-6471  aJPSTDG  a(DNLM)J36460000(s)  a(OCoLC)01787711  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aeng  9United States00aW1bJO858E10aJ Psychol Theol2dnlm 0aJournal of psychology and theology00aJournal of psychology and theology.  aRosemead, Calif.,bRosemead Graduate School of Psychology.3 aRosemead Ca :bRosemead Graduate School Of Psychology  av.billus.,ports.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-    Jan. 1973-0 aMEDLINEbSelected citations only0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online. 2aPastoral Care 2aPsychology 2aPeriodical2 aRosemead Graduate School of Psychology.0 x2328-11624 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/ptja  aAUTH01980cgmaa