itle screen (viewed Dec. 6, 2009).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 32 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 30, 2009.3 a(CIT): Clinical Center Ethics Grand Rounds Ethical and Regulatory Aspects of Clinical Research  Department of Clinical Bioethics  This course is designed to provide a historical context for research regulations and to help researchers and others working in human subject research gain insights and skills into the development of research protocols and their ethical implementation. Objectives of the Course: To learn the codes, declarations, and other documents that govern the ethical conduct of human subject research; review the critical elements of informed consent and their implementation in actual informed consent documents for clinical research; explore controversial issues relating to human subject research, including Phase I research, randomization, children in research, international research, etc; review the purpose if IRBs and provide IRB-like experience in reviewing research protocols; understand the experience of human subjects who have participated in research protocols.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aEthics Committees, Researchxorganization & administration22aConflict of Interest22aPatient Selectionxethics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15327  aAUTH01240cam a2200337 i 61340790RDNLM20260206222608.0cr||||||||||||820626s1906    paua     b    001 0 eng    a(OCoLC)33075838  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aQTbP848i 19061 aPorter, William Townsend,d1862-1949,eauthor.13aAn introduction to physiology /cby William Townsend Porter.  aSecond edition. 1aPhiladelphia :bJ.B.Lippincott,Company,c1906.  axxiv, 587 pages :billustrations ;c19 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and 