13-9111  a(DNLM)SR0060731(s)  a(OCoLC)19079114  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aspabeng  9Spain00aW1bGA356G10aGac Sanit2dnlm 0aGaceta sanitaria00aGaceta sanitaria /cS.E.S.P.A.S.  a[Barcelona] :bAjuntament de Barcelona, Àrea de Sanitat, Salut Pública i Medi Ambient,c1987-3 aMadrid :bElsevier España  av. :bill.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, n. 1 (jul.-agosto 1987)-  aTitle from cover.  aImprint varies.1 aMEDLINEbv1n1,Jul.-Aug. 1987-1 aPubMedbv1n1,Jul.-Aug. 1987-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aArticles in Spanish; summaries in English and Spanish.  aOrgan of: the Sociedad Española de Salud Pública y Administración Sanitaria.  aContinues: Gaseta sanitària de Barcelona. 2aPublic Health 2aPublic Health Administration 2aSpain 2aPeriodical 2aStatistics2 aSociedad Española de Salud Pública y Administración Sanitaria.1 aBarcelona (Spain).bSalut Pública i Medi Ambient.0 x1578-128300tGaseta sanitària de Barcelonax0212-0542w(DLC)sn 89036056w(OCoLC)190790814 uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/02139111  aAUTH02643cgm a2200433 a 101585254DNLM20260227154205.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120525s2012    mdu061|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17250  a(OCoLC)797084264  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 1021 aEhlers, Michael D.10aSecretory trafficking and synapse remodeling for neuronal plasticity /cMichael D. Ehlers.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aRecorded May 7, 2012.3 a(CIT): Neuroscience Seminar Series  Michael Ehlers is Vice President, Chief Scientific Officer of the Neuroscience Research Unit.  Prior to joining Pfizer, Dr. Ehlers was the George Barth Geller Professor of Neurobiology and an Investigator of the Howard Hughes Medical Institute at Duke University Medical Center. Dr. Ehlers grew up rural Nebraska and earned his bachelor"s degree in chemistry from Caltech. He holds M.D. and Ph.D. degrees from the Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine, where he studied the regulation and trafficking of glutamate receptors. Dr. Ehlers" research explores the interface between neuronal cell biology and the plasticity of neural circuits. His current efforts are focused on defining mechanisms of neuronal membrane trafficking and glutamatergic synapse plasticity implicated in Alzheimer's disease, schizophrenia, and autism.  He is the recipient of numerous awards including the 2003 Eppendorf & Science Prize in Neurobiology, the 2007 John J. Abel Award in Pharmacology, the 2007 Society for Neuroscience Young Investigator Award, and the 2009 National Alliance for Schizophrenia and Depression Distinguished Investigator Award.    For more information go to <a href='http://neuroseries.info.nih.gov'>http://neuroseries.info.nih.gov.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aNeuronal Plasticity22aModels, Neurological22aNeurosecretionxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17250  aAUTH01317cgm a22004094a 101556017DNLM20260227153927.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110209s2011    mdu059|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16400  a(OCoLC)708544256  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 551 aLatz, Eicke.10aRecognition of danger signals and cytoplasmic DNA by inflammasomes /cEicke Latz ; Immunology Interest Group.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (59 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 12, 2011.3 a(CIT): The Immunology Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aInflammasomes22aCytoplasmxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16400  aAUTH02693cgm a2200421 a 101571673DNLM20260227154116.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u111018s2011    mdu066|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16880  a(OCoLC)762697393  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQT 371 aMikos, Antonios G.10aBiomaterials for tissue engineering /cAntonios Mikos.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 6 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded Sept. 28, 2011.3 a(CIT): Recent developments in biomaterials technology have enabled the production of biodegradable, cytocompatible tissue engineering scaffolds that can be tailored with appropriate mechanical and biological signals to restore damaged tissue in specific sites in the body. For example, our laboratory has developed a variety of biodegradable polymers for the controlled delivery of biologically active agents and/or progenitor stem cell populations to promote regeneration of tissues such as bone and cartilage.   Further, we have explored the application of bioreactor culture of progenitor cell populations to produce extracellular matrix constructs containing biologically active signals to direct cellular differentiation and tissue formation. Ongoing research in collaboration with clinical colleagues also involves the development of materials-based approaches for application in strategies for bone regeneration, with a vision for rapid clinical translation. This talk will present examples of biomaterials from our laboratory to illustrate recent advances in tailored biomaterials-based approaches for bone and cartilage tissue engineering.  The NIH Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series includes weekly scientific talks by some of the top researchers in the biomedical sciences worldwide. For more information, visit:  The NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aTissue Engineering22aBiocompatible Materials 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16880  aAUTH01464cas a2200457 a 101555921DNLM20260227090922.0cr||||||||||||110308c20109999cau|| p       |    0eng    a  2011243198  a2210-4917l2210-4917  a(OCoLC)708602571  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW1bHO558L00aJournal of orthopaedics, trauma and rehabilitation.1 aJOTR  aSingapore :bElsevier3132019- :a[Thousand Oaks, CA] :bSAGE Publications  aBiannually  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 14, no. 2 (Dec. 2010).  aAlso issued online 2016.  aAlso issued online.  aAbstracts in English and Chinese.  aDescription based on: Vol. 14, no. 2 (Dec. 2010); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 14, no. 2 (Dec. 2010).12aOrthopedic Procedures22aRehabilitation22aWounds and Injuries 2aPeriodical2 aHong Kong Orthopaedic Association.2 aHong Kong College of Orthopaedic Surgeons.0 x2210-492500tHong Kong journal of orthopaedic surgeryx1028-26374 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/loi/otr  aAUTH01762cas a2200541 a 9886009DNLM20260227091042.0cr||||||||||||750702c19669999enk||         0   a0eng    a   66017931   a1642126  a0079-6565l0079-65652z  aPNMRAT  a(DNLM)SR0073205(s)  a(OCoLC)01427374  z(OCoLC)1642126  aNEDcNEDdNSDdIULdSERdMULdAIPdOCLdNSDdNSTdDLCdOCLdNSTdNSDdOCLdCUSdDNLM  ansdpalcan