(1 hr. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 15, 2006.3 a(CIT): The Immunology Interest Group (IIG) is organizer.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aLymphocytesximmunology22aClonal Anergyxgenetics22aClonal Anergyximmunology22aLymphocyte Activationxgenetics22aLymphocyte Activationximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13068  aAUTH01962cas a2200517 i 101671667DNLM20260227090921.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||151106c20149999enkar  oo     0    0eng    a  2018243287  aGBB5B19942bnb  a947095000a964837847a967844881a980500311  a2055-6683l2055-6683  a(OCoLC)926183443  z(OCoLC)947095000z(OCoLC)964837847z(OCoLC)967844881z(OCoLC)980500311  aU3Gbengerdad20260227cU3GdUSUdOCLCOdAU@dOCLCFdOCLCQdCASUMdOCLCAdVT2dCUSdUABdOH1dNLEdU3WdREBdTOHdOCLCQdKSUdOCLCOdDNLM  apcc  9England 4aRM95000aW104a617.0322310aJ Rehabil Assist Technol Eng2dnlm 0aJournal of rehabilitation and assistive technologies engineering00aJournal of rehabilitation and assistive technologies engineering.13aRATE 1a[London] :bSAGE Publications Ltd.,c[2014]-  a1 online resource  aAnnual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1 (January/December 2014)0 aPubMedbv1, 2014-0 aPMC  aArticles published as they become available and compiled into volumes.  aDescription based on: Volume 1 (January/December 2014); title from journal home page (SAGE journals, viewed Jan. 16, 2017).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 5 (January/December 2018) (viewed Sept. 7, 2018).12aRehabilitation Research22aSelf-Help Devices22aProstheses and Implants22aBiomedical Engineering40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=10167166740uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/jrta  aAUTH02513cgm a22005174a 101294839DNLM20260227151444.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061206s2006    mdu421 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13174  a(DNLM)CIT:13179  a(OCoLC)84722478  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.500aDefining race and ethnicity in biomedical and behavioral research /cStaff Training in Extramural Programs.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aScience for all  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 29, 2007).  aStreaming video (7 hr., 1 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS and NLM only.5DNLM  aRecorded Apr. 11 and 18, 2006.3 a(CIT): One of NIH's major goals is to reduce disparities in health status experienced by racial and ethnic minority populations. What is "race"? What is "ethnicity"? The definitions for these classifications become crucial elements in pursuing this goal. But, are we doing the right thing?  If we are more than 99% genetically identical, to each other, as human geneticists say, why are we so different? Indeed, genomic data raise disturbing questions about the scientific value of our current mode of classifying and categorizing individuals and populations. What data are obtained and lost in using the current system of classification and does it really allow us to address health disparities? What is the key impact of current policies of inclusion on clinical research and health disparities?  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomedical Researchxmethods22aBehavioral Researchxmethods22aClassificationxmethods22aCultural Diversity22aEthnicity.22aResearch Subjects 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bStaff Training-Extramural Programs Committee. 0aScience for all forum40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1317440uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13179  aAUTH01152cas a2200361 i 101681941DNLM20260227090910.0cr||||||||||||16