 aJRLREO  a(DNLM)SR0082013(s)  a(OCoLC)28601544  aNSDPcNSDPdDNLM1 aenghrus  9United States00aW1bJO8715 0aJournal of Russian laser research00aJournal of Russian laser research.  aNew York :bConsultants Bureau,cc1994-3 31999- :aNew York, NY :bKluwer Academic/Plenum Publishers  av. :bill.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 15, no. 1 (Jan.-Feb. 1994)-  aTitle from cover.  aAlso issued online.  aTranslated from the Russian, principally from the publications of the Lebedev Physics Institute. 2aLasers 2aPeriodical2 aFizicheskiĭ institut imeni P.N. Lebedeva.2 aConsultants Bureau.00tJournal of Soviet laser researchx0270-2010w(DLC)   81640429w(OCoLC)63533104 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/10946  aAUTH03447cgm a22004814a 101308599DNLM20260227151939.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070605s2007    mdu071 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13809  a(OCoLC)162143248  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2001 aDe Pinho, Ronald A.10aModeling and mining cancer genomes /cRonald DePinho.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCenter for Cancer Research--National Cancer Institute grand rounds  aTitle from title screen (viewed July 25, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 10 min., 39 sec. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded May 8, 2007.3 a(CIT): Center for Cancer Research - National Cancer Institute Grand Rounds.    Dr. Ronald A. DePinho is currently Professor of Medicine and Genetics at Harvard Medical School, Director for the Center for Applied Cancer Science, and a member of the Department of Medical Oncology at the Dana Farber Cancer Institute.  He holds the coveted American Cancer Society Research Professorship.   Dr. DePinho has been a leader in the use of the mouse to dissect the molecular mechanisms and biological processes governing the genesis and maintenance of tumorigenesis.  His studies on the Myc oncoprotein have led to insights into how physical and functional interactions among members of the Myc superfamily (Myc, Max and Mad) regulate the growth and development of normal and neoplastic cells.  The demonstration that Mad family members act to repress transcription and suppress cancer in vivo, along with his co-discovery of the mSin3 co-repressor complex and its link to regulators of chromatin architecture, have defined a mechanistically distinct tumor suppressor pathway.   His laboratory has explored and defined the functional relationship between Rb and p53 pathways, particularly p16INK4a and p19ARF, have delineated an intimate link between pathways that control cellular senescence and those that regulate cell cycle entry or cell survival. Dr. DePinho and his colleagues have also led efforts to understand the role of telomerase in cancer, development and aging, and how telomere dynamics inter-relate to DNA damage and recombination pathways.  More recently, he and his colleagues have constructed inducible cancer models that permit an in vivo analysis of the complex symbiotic host-tumor cell communications required for tumor maintenance.  Finally, Dr. DePinho has launched a new academic initiative at Harvard, the Center for Applied Cancer Science, whose mission is to translate basic science discoveries into drug development endpoints.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxgenetics22aAging22aDisease Models, Animal22aGenome22aNeoplasmsxetiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13809  aAUTH02662cgm a2200433 i 101680591DNLM20260227155653.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160404s2016    mdu062|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19563  a(OCoLC)948243392  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 30700aGenerating and shaping novel action repertoires /cRui Costa. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience series seminar  aRecorded March 21, 2016.3 a(CIT): To study actions is to study the way we do things, which is different than studying how we remember stimuli, or facts and events. Some actions are innate or pre-wired (like swallowing or breathing). Others are learned anew throughout life, likely through a process of trial and feedback. Dr. Costa's lab currently focuses on understanding the processes mediating the latter.  A growing body of evidence suggests that cortico-basal ganglia circuits are involved in action generation and selection, in skill learning, and in learning goal-directed actions and habits. They center their efforts on investigating the cortico-basal ganglia mechanisms underlying these processes using an across-level approach, from molecules to circuits.  They chose to implement this integrative approach in mice because they combine the power of genetics, a mammalian brain with canonical cortico-basal ganglia loops that can generate and propagate oscillatory activity, and the possibility of accurately quantifying simple behaviors like action initiation (with EMG recordings or using inertial sensors) and stereotypic skill learning, and more elaborate behaviors like goal-directed actions.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 19, 2016).12aBasal Gangliaxphysiology22aLearningxphysiology22aPsychomotor Performancexphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCosta, Ruiq(Rui M.),espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19563  aAUTH01261cas a2200421 a 101229024DNLM20260227090913.0cr||||||||||||040903c20059999enkqr p       0    0eng    a  2005214150  a1744-6295l1744-6295  a(OCoLC)58802464  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9England00aW1bJO74310aJ Intellect Disabil2dnlm00aJournal of intellectual disabilities :bJOID.30aJOID  aLondon :aThousand Oaks, CA :bSAGE Publications,cc2005-  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 9, no. 1 (Mar. 2005)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv9n1, Mar. 2005-0 aPubMedbv9n1, Mar. 2005-  aAlso issued online.12aIntellectual Disability22aLearning Disabilities 2aPeriodical0 x1744-630900tJournal of learning disabilities (London, England)x1469-0047w(DLC)   2000244534w(OCoLC)436846064 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/jld  aAUTH01293cas a2200445 a 9706294DNLM20260227091035.0cr|cn#||||||||970612c19979999okubr p       0||| 0eng    a   97648235 zsn 970033790 a1092-4221l1092-4221  a(DNLM)SR0090800(s)  a(OCoLC)36235228  aNSDPcNSDPdDNLM  alcd  9United States00aW1bIN771RYM10aInt J Pharm Compd2dnlm00aInternational journal of pharmaceutical compounding.30aPharmaceutical compounding13aIJPC  aEdmond, OK :bIJPC,cc1997-  av. :bill., ports.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Jan./Feb. 1997)-  aTitle from cover.  aPrint issues ceased with 2023.1 aMEDLINEbv16n1, Jan-Feb 2012-0 aPubMedbv16n1, Jan-Feb 2012-  aIssue for Jan.-Feb. 1997 called also Premiere issue.  aAlso issued online02aDrug Compounding 2aPeriodical0 x1943-52234 uhttp://www.ijpc.com/  aAUTH01554cas a2200481   7900832DNLM20260227091001.0cr|cnu||||||||790118c19789999enk|| m       0    0eng    a   79641904 zsn 78006925  a0163-7827l0163-7827  aPLIRDW  a(DNLM)P48010000(s)  a(O