.  aArticles in German; summaries in English and German.  aContinues: Rheumamedizin. 2aRheumatic Diseases 2aPeriodical0 x2567-575310tRheumamedizin4 uhttps://www.thieme-connect.de/products/ejournals/journal/10.1055/s-00034922  aAUTH01552cas a2200445 i 101608315DNLM20260227090919.0cr||||||||||||130515c20129999ii qr p       |    0eng    a  2013343600  a2251-9572l2251-9572  a(OCoLC)857534520  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9India00aW1bRE227FC10aRes Cardiovasc Med2dnlm00aResearch in cardiovascular medicine. 1aTehran IR, Iran :bKOWSAR,c[2012]-31a[Mumbai] :bWolters Kluwer - Medknow  avolumes  :bcolor illustrations ;c28 cm  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Number 1, volume 1, issue 1 (summer 2012).0 aPubMedbv1n1, 2012-v5n4, Nov. 20160 aPMC Inactive  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: Number 1, volume 1, issue1 (summer 2012); title from title page.  aLatest issue consulted: Number 3, volume 1, issue 3 (winter 2013).12aCardiovascular Diseases 2aPeriodical2 aRajaie Cardiovascular Medical and Research Center,eissuing body.2 aDānishgāh-i ʻUlūm-i Pizishkī-i Tihrān,eissuing body.0 x2251-95804 uhttps://www.rcvmonline.com/4 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101608315  aAUTH03383cgm a2200433 i 101716391DNLM20260227155918.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u171107s2017    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:23507  a(OCoLC)1013590320  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWT 15500aSystemic immunity protects the mind :bcan immune checkpoint blockade combat Alzheimer's disease? /cMichal Schwartz. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2017]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 0 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded October 11, 2017.3 a(CIT):  It is now widely accepted that immune surveillance is required for supporting brain functional plasticity and repair. Participating cells include the microglia, the resident myeloid immune cells of the brain, circulating monocytes, and CD4+ T cells. Over the years, we demonstrated that leukocytes supporting the brain can gain access to the brain through a unique compartment within the brain territory, the chroid plexus epithelium (CP) at the blood-cerebrospinal fluid-barrier (B-CSF-B), remote from the brain parenchyma. The CP serves as a selective gateway allowing leukocyte entry to the CNS; its activity is controlled by its cytokine milieu, and specifically by IFN-g. We showed that in mouse models of aging and in Alzheimer"s disease (AD), this interface is suppressed in its ability to allow communication between the brain and the circulating leukocytes. We further found that transiently relieving systemic immune suppression could enhance recruitment of disease-modifying leukocytes to sites of brain pathology. Unleashing the immune system could be achieved by blocking inhibitory immune checkpoints, regulatory pathways that normally maintain systemic immune homeostasis and tolerance. Specifically, we found in three mouse models of AD that targeting the inhibitory checkpoints, PD-1/PD-L1 pathway, was effective in reversing cognitive loss, reducing brain inflammation, and mitigating disease pathology. In addition, our emerging understanding of the fate of microglia in AD, based on single cell immunogenomics, has revealed a role of microglia in combating the disease, and suggests that boosting systemic immunity modifies microglial activity. Overall, our results indicate that targeting the immune system rather than brain-specific disease-escalating factors may provide a multi-dimensional therapy for AD.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 20, 2017).12aAlzheimer Diseaseximmunology12aAlzheimer Diseasextherapy22aImmunotherapyxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aSchwartz, Michal,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23507  aAUTH01110cas a2200409 a 9612305DNLM20260227091017.0cr|cn#||||||||960815c19969999enkqr p       0||| 0eng    a1362-3699l1362-3699  a(DNLM)SR0088258(s)  a(OCoLC)34574143  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9England00aW1bME649W10aMed Confl Surviv2dnlm00aMedicine, conflict, and survival.  aLondon :bF. Cass,cc1996-  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 12, no. 1 (Jan.-Mar. 1996)-1 aMEDLINEbv12n1,Jan.-Mar. 1996-1 aPubMedbv12n1,Jan.-Mar. 1996-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online. 2aGlobal Health 2aSocial Medicine 2aWarfare 2aPeriodical0 x1743-939600tMedicine and war4 uhttps://www.tandfonline.com/loi/fmcs20  aAUTH01858cgm a2200529 i 101770449DNLM20260227160241.0m     o  z f      vz|czau|ucr|cna||||||||200811s2020    mdu048 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:37446  a(OCoLC)1194873382  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 2400aNIH town hall, April 2020 /cFrancis Collins, Alfred Johnson, Julie Berko, Colleen McGowan. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (48 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded April 24, 2020  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed September 1, 2020).22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aAcademies and Institutes12aBiomedical Research22aUnited States Government Agencies22aCoronaviridae I