a(OCoLC)269432282  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWD 205.5.L51 aSidransky, Ellen.10aDemystifying medicine.pGauchers disease :btreating a genetic disease /cEllen Sidransky and Chris Austin.30aGauchers disease  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Sept. 29, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 29 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 8, 2008.3 a(CIT): The course includes presentation of patients, pathology, diagnosis and therapy in the context of major disease problems and current research.  Primarily directed toward Ph.D. students, fellows, and staff, it is also of interest to medical students and clinicians.  The course is designed to help bridge the gap between advances in biology and their application to major human diseases.  Each session includes clinical and basic science components which are presented by NIH staff and outside invitees.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government.  aOpen-captioned.12aGaucher Diseasextherapy 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14417  aAUTH01906cgm a22004574a 101549194DNLM20260227153726.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101208s2010    mdu065|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16196  a(OCoLC)708543863  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWX 1671 aEdwards, John E.,cMD10aMolecular approaches for preventing opportunistic healthcare associated infections  /cJohn E. (Jack) Edwards Jr.1 aMolecular approaches for prevention of opportunistic/healthcare-associated infections  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Clinical Center grand rounds. Great teachers  aTitle from title screen (viewed Mar. 8, 2011).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 5 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 13, 2010.3 a(CIT): Contemporary Clinical Medicine: Great Teachers   Molecular Approaches for Preventing Opportunistic/Healthcare Associated Infections     John E. (Jack) Edwards, Jr, MD, Chief, Division of Infectious Diseases, Harbor/UCLA Medical Center, Professor of Medicine, David Geffen School of Medicine at UCLA.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aCross Infectionxprevention & control22aDrug Resistance22aInfection Control 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center. 0aClinical Center grand rounds great teachers series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16196  aAUTH00919cam a22002657i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003500073044001000108060003200118245011700150264009000267300001900357336002600376337002800402338002700430500002200457650002500479650003300504650002100537651001000558710007600568999000900644101608850DNLM20260209103914.0130522s1971    cc            000 0 chi    aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1773 no.500aYin shi wei sheng xuan chuan cai liao /cBeijing Shi Xicheng Qu ai guo wei sheng yun dong ban gong shi hui bian. 1a[Beijing] :b[Beijing Shi Xicheng Qu ai guo wei sheng yun dong ban gong shi],c[1971]  a4, 3, 74 pages  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aTitle from cover.12aFood Safetyxmethods22aCommunicable Disease Control22aHealth Education 2aChina2 aBeijing Shi Xicheng qu ai guo wei sheng yun dong ban gong shi,eeditor.  aAUTH02277cgm a22004574a 101549172DNLM20260227153606.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101208s2010    mdu159|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16161  a(OCoLC)714029507  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.55.H91 aKarp, Barbara.10aEthical and regulatory aspects of clinical research course.pIRB review, conflicts of interest, and investigator perspectives /cBarbara Karp and Steve Joffe.1 aIRB review, conflicts of interest, and investigator perspectives  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (2 hr., 39 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 29, 2010.3 a(CIT): ECourse Objectives By the end of this course, participants will be able to: Utilize a systematic framework for evaluating the ethics of a clinical research protocol. Apply appropriate codes, regulations, and other documents governing the ethical conduct of human subject research to their own research. Discuss controversial issues relating to human subject research, including Phase 1 research, randomization, children in research, international research, etc. Identify the critical elements of informed consent and strategies for implementing informed consent for clinical research. Describe the purpose, function, and challenges of IRBs. Appreciate the experience of human subjects who have participated in research protocols.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aEthics Committees, Researchxethics12aHuman Experimentationxethics22aConflict of Interest22aEthics, Research22aResearch Personnelxethics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16161  aAUTH01281cas a22004214a 101263166DNLM20260227090916.0cr|cn ||||||||051208c19969999ko br p       0    0eng    a  2006243746  a1226-8372l1226-8372  a(OCoLC)71233646  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9Korea (South)00aW1bBI918NG10aBiotechnol Bioprocess Eng2dnlm00aBiotechnology and bioprocess engineering :bBBE.30aBBE  aSeoul, Korea :bKorean Society for Biotechnology and Bioengineering  av. :bill.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rd