exsurgery22aVaccination4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/101747762  aAUTH01308cam a2200325 i 63020580RDNLM20260227152436.0820626t19011901nyu           000 0 eng    a   01010352   a(OCoLC)14798865  aDNLMbengerdad20260226dDNLM  9United States00aWAbN977h 19011 aNutt, John Joseph,d1870-eauthor.10aHealth and hygiene for the household /cby John Joseph Nutt. 1aNew York :bThe Abbey Press,c[1901?] 4c©1901  a69 pages ;c20 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aIntroduction -- Bacteriology -- Ventilation, heat and light -- Water, milk and sewage -- Foods and drugs -- Care of the person -- Selecting a family physician -- The most dangerous classes of diseases, and how to prevent them.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202011192pda5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20260227lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM12aHygiene12aPublic Health 2aPopular Work  aAUTH01326cgm a22004094a 101294003DNLM20260227152048.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061129s2006    mdu789 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13474  a(DNLM)CIT:13471  a(OCoLC)130536400  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.500aTransdisciplinary team science /cNational Cancer Institute.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar 5, 2007).  aStreaming video (13 hr., 8 min., 39 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 30-31, 2006.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomedical Researchxmethods22aInterdisciplinary Communication 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1347440uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13471  aAUTH03134cgm a22004694a 101496163DNLM20260227152812.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090203s2009    mdu066 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14863  a(OCoLC)319070444  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWE 3481 aGray, Martha.10aTowards imaging biomarkers for osteoarthritis :bsurprises, challenges, and opportunities /cMartha Gray.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 24, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 5 min., 36 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 21, 2009.3 a(CIT): Many new therapeutic strategies have been and are being developed to correct, prevent, or slow the progression of osteoarthritis (OA). Our ability to evaluate the efficacy of these techniques, or to determine the situations for which they might provide the most benefit, critically depends on diagnostic measures that can serve as proxies or "biomarkers" for the present or predicted state of the cartilage. Accordingly, much research over the past decade has been devoted to the development of biomarkers for OA, including considerable efforts towards developing imaging biomarkers for OA.   Biomarker development requires two phases of validation. The first is technical validity: is the marker measuring what it aims to measure? The second is pathophysiologic validity: is the information provided by the marker of pathophysiological or clinical use? Many of the magnetic resonance imaging techniques that have been emerging over the past decades appear promising in that they have shown technical validity in measuring the morphologic and molecular state of cartilage, and with emerging clinical studies, efforts are beginning to offer evidence regarding pathophysiological validity. As a case study, this talk will focus on biomarkers for glycosaminoglycan (GAG). In addition to illustrating technical and pathophysiological validity, and some of the surprising insights that have come along the way, we will also explore the way in which such biomarkers might change the scientific and clinical paradigm for advancing our understanding, diagnosis and treatment of osteoarthritis.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aOsteoarthritis22aBiomarkers22aCartilagexphysiopathology22aGlycosaminoglycans22aMagnetic Resonance Imagingxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14863  aAUTH01679cas a22004934a 100886103DNLM20260227091037.0cr|||#||||||||990311c19989999pl fr p       0    0eng    asn 99039844   a1506-9680l1506-9680  a(OCoLC)42643989  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9Poland00aW1bNU124510aNucl Med Rev Cent East Eur2dnlm00aNuclear medicine review.pCentral & Eastern Europe.3 aNuclear medicine review.pCentral and Eastern Europe30iAt head of title 1998- 2010:aCentral & Eastern Europe30aCentral and Eastern Europe  aGdańsk, Poland :bVia Medica ,c1998-  aTwo no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, nr. 1 (Sept. 1998)-  aTitle from cover.  aPrint issues ceased with 2015.1 aMEDLINEbv6n1, 2003-0 aPubMedbv6n1, 2003-1 aIndex medicus  aVol. 1, nr. 1-   called also:  09/98- .  aAlso issued online.  aOfficial journal of: the Polish Society of Nuclear Medicine, 2011- ,  3v. 4, no. 1 (2001)c20040308fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper12aNuclear Medicine 2aPeriodical2 aPolskie Towarzystwo Medycyny Nuklearnej.0 x1644-434505tProblemy medycyny nuklearnejg2011x0860-34054 uhttps://journals.viamedica.pl/nuclear%5Fmedicine%5Freview  aAUTH01505cas a22004694a 100965525DNLM20260227091057.0cr cnu--------000526c20019999nyubr p       0    0eng    a   00214851 0 a1530-3667l1530-3667  a(OCoLC)44125474  aNSDcNSDdCUSdOCLdAGLdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bVE103E0 aRA639.5b.V43 0aL830  aRA639.5b.V430 aVector borne and zoonotic diseases (Larchmont, N.Y.)10aVector Borne Zoonotic Dis2dnlm10aVector borne and zoonotic diseases.  aLarchmont, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.,c[2001-  av. :bill. ;c28 cm.  aSix no. a year,b2008-  aQuarterly,b-2007  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (spring 2001)-1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, spring 2001-1 aPubMedbv1n1, spring 2001-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online; available by subscription.  3v.1, no. 1 (Mar. 2001)c20030311fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper that meets the full ANSI standard for Permanent Paper12aDisease Vectors22aZoonoses 2aPeriodical0 x1557-77594 uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/vbz  aAUTH02705cgm a22004934a 101522164DNLM20260227153210.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100105s2009    mdu071|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15461  a(OCoLC)529281834  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 102.51 aSchroeder, Charlie.10aDelta awakens :buse of low frequency oscillations in attentive processing /cCharlie Schroeder.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 11 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 30, 2009.3 a(CIT): Charles Schroeder, Director of the Laboratory for Cognitive Neuroscience and Neuroimaging at The Nathan S. Kline Institute for Psychiatric Research and Professor of Psychiatry at Columbia University, is a neuroscientist interested in investigating the neural mechanisms of sensory and cognitive processes using methods that allow one to relate the functioning of single neurons and groups of neurons to that of the brain as a whole. Dr. Schroeder's primary research method is to use brain electrical field potentials and their derivatives, in combination with action potentials, to study brain processing in awake non-human primates. Actual signals are collected from within the brain using multi-electrode arrays, and processed with 96 channel amplifier systems. Dr. Schroeder's research staff is continually developing and refining this approach to increase our resolution of the way the brain interprets basic properties of sensory stimuli, and higher order properties such as an object's biological significance. Of particular interest at this point is the interaction of the stimulus-evoked response with ongoing oscillatory activity at each point in the system.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeuronsxphysiology12aSensationxphysiology22aAttentionxphysiology22aDelta Rhythm22aPeriodicity22aPrimatesxphysiology22aWakefulnessxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15461  aAUTH01846cam a2200469 i 101671608DNLM20260204163608.0150918d20142015gw a     b    101 0 eng  7 a10705499162GyFmDB  a910092687  a9783832539481 (hd.bd.)  a3832539484 (hd.bd.)  a(OCoLC)921884808  z(OCoLC)910092687  aOHXbengerdad20260204dYDXCPdBTCTAdAZSdOCLCOdDNLM  anlmcopyc  9Germany00aQU 460 7aQP2lcco  aQH324.2b.I55 20132 aInternational Beilstein Symposium on Glyco-Bioinformaticsn(3rd :d2013 :cPotsdam, Germany)jauthor.10aDiscov Subtleties Sugars (2013)2dnlm10aDiscovering the subtleties of sugars :bproceedings of the 3rd Beilstein Glyco-Bioinformatics Symposium : June 10th - 14th, 2013, Potsdam, Germany /cedited by Martin G. Hicks and Carsten Kettner. 1aBerlin :bLogos Verlag,c[2014] 4c©2015  avi, 185 pages :billustrations (chiefly col