c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (45 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 17, 2010.3 a(CIT): Grants.gov uses the quarterly webcasts to communicate the status of important program initiatives and operational status to stakeholders that include Applicants, Grantor Agencies, OMB, Congress and Third Party Vendors. The webcasts also offer stakeholders the opportunity to interact with the PMO and guests through live question and answer segments.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aFederal Government12aFinancing, Organized22aDatabase Management Systems22aDatabases, Factual22aGovernment Programs 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aUnited States.bDepartment of Health and Human Services.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16280  aAUTH02501cgm a2200469 a 101559790DNLM20260227153904.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110503s2011    mdu083 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16514  a(OCoLC)756154997  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWP 11 AA11 aSkloot, Rebecca,d1972-14aThe immortal life of Henrietta Lacks /cRebecca Skloot.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 23 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aSpecial NIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture  aTitle from title screen.  aAccess restricted to HHS and NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Mar. 14, 2011.3 a(CIT): Rebecca Skloot is a science writer whose articles have appeared in The New York Times Magazine; O, The Oprah Magazine; and others. She will present the annual J. Edward Rall Cultural Lecture on Monday, March 14, 2011 at 10:00 a.m. in Masur.    Skloot has worked as a correspondent for NPR"s Radiolab and PBS"s NOVA scienceNOW, and is guest editor of The Best American Science Writing 2011. She has taught at the University of Memphis, the University of Pittsburgh, and NYU.  Her debut book, The Immortal Life of Henrietta Lacks, became an instant New York Times bestseller. It won the Chicago Tribune Heartland Prize and the Wellcome Trust Book Prize, and was named The Best Book of 2010 by Amazon.com, and a Best Book of the Year by Entertainment Weekly; O, The Oprah Magazine; The New York Times; and numerous others. It is being translated into more than twenty languages, and adapted into a young adult book, and an HBO film produced by Oprah Winfrey and Alan Ball.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aLacks, Henrietta,d1920-1951.12aTissue Donorsxhistory12aUterine Cervical Neoplasmsxhistory22aHeLa Cells22aHistory, 20th Century 2aLecture 2aWebcast 0aSpecial NIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16514  aAUTH01140cas a22003851  00110640RDNLM20260227090957.0cr|cn#||||||||810622c18499999enkqr p       0||| 0eng    a0029-3970l0029-3970  a(DNLM)N30840000(s)  a(OCoLC)02623018  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9England10aAG 105bN91110aNotes Queries2dnlm00aNotes and queries.  aLondon :bGeorge Bell3 aOxford :bOxford University Press  av.bill., ports.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-    Nov. 3, 1849-2 aMEDLINEbv12, 1965-v53n2, June 20060 aPubMedbv12, 1965-v53n2, June 2006  aVols. 1-[145] Nov. 1849-1923, called ser. [1]-13; v. 199-    Jan. 1954-    called also new ser. v. 1-  aAlso issued online. 2aLiterature 2aPeriodical0 x1471-69414 uhttps://academic.oup.com/nq  aAUTH01661cas a2200469 a 101528681DNLM20260227090918.0cr||||||||||||100312c20109999enkqn p       0   a0eng    a  20102000870 a1878-1241l1878-12412j  a(OCoLC)551672526  aNzcNZHMMdDLCdDNLM  apccansdp  9England00aW1bJO804SE0 aInt. j. orthop. trauma nurs.b(Print)10aInt J Orthop Trauma Nurs2dnlm 0aInternational journal of orthopaedic and trauma nursingb(Print)00aInternational journal of orthopaedic and trauma nursing.  aOxford  :bElsevier  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 14, no. 1 (Feb. 2010).  aDescription based on: Vol. 14, no. 1 (Feb. 2010); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 14, no. 1 (Feb. 2010).1 aMEDLINEbv19n1, Feb. 2015-0 aPubMedbv19n1, Feb. 2015-  aAlso issued online.12aOrthopedic Nursing22aWounds and Injuriesxnursing 2aPeriodical2 aAustralia New Zealand Orthopaedic Nurses Association.2 aCanadian Orthopaedic Nurses' Association.2 aRCN Society of Orthopaedic and Trauma Nursing.0 x1878-129200tJournal of orthopaedic nursingx1361-3111w(DLC)sn 99039859w(OCoLC)427272804 uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/18781241  aAUTH01466cas a22004334a 101145354DNLM20260227090918.0cr|cn ||||||||020515c20029999vraqr p       0    0eng    a  2002243307  a1445-937Xl2055-2335  a(OCoLC)50154983  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9Australia00aW1bAU61210aJ Pharm Pract Res2dnlm00aJournal of pharmacy practice and research :bofficial journal of the Society of Hospital Pharmacists of Australia.  aSouth Melbourne, Vic. :bSociety of Hospital Pharmacists of Australia,c[2002-3 32005 - :aCollingwood, Vic., Australia :bSociety of Hospital Pharmacists of Australia  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 32, no. 1 (Mar. 2002)-  aTitle from cover.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.12aPharmacy22aPharmacology22aPharmacy Service, Hospital. 2aPeriodical2 aSociety of Hospital Pharmacists of Australia.0 x2055-233500tAustralian journal of hospital pharmacyx0310-6810w(OCoLC)10029244 uhttp://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/journal/10.1002/(ISSN)2055-2335  aAUTH01521cgm a22004454a 101268519DNLM20260227150920.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu127 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12139  a(OCoLC)853243738  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2021 aMoody, Terry W.10aTRACO.pCancer pathophysiology /cT. Moody and L. Varticovski.30aCancer pathophysiology3 aTranslational research in clinical oncology  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 13, 2007).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 6 min., 7 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 13, 2004.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxphysiopathology22aBiomarkers, Tumor.22aNeoplasmsxetiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12139  aAUTH01675cgm a22004454a 101528432DNLM20260227153536.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100406s2010    mdu055|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15715  a(OCoLC)624405071  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.55.E700aPanel discussion :bthe institutional review board : safety first /cNIDDK/NIAMS Institutional Review Board.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (55 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 17, 2010.3 a(CIT): Members from the NIDDK/NIAMS Institutional Review Board (IRB) will discuss the purpose of the IRB and how the IRB works with our Investigators to ensure clinical studies have minimized risks to study subjects.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aEthics Committees, Research22aClinical Trials as Topicxstandards22aEthics, Research22aHuman Experimentationxstandards22aRisk Assessmentxmethods 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15715  aAUTH03371cgm a22004814a 101522170DNLM20260227153224.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100105s2009    mdu063|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15469  a(OCoLC)520437703  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 3751 aMeyer, Tobias.10aShotgun siRNA perturbation to dissect growth factor triggered proliferation and migration signaling systems /cTobias Meyer.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 3 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 2, 2009.3 a(CIT): Cells make use of an elaborate control system that integrates inputs from multiple receptors, computes this information and makes decisions about key cellular outputs such as cell migration and proliferation. Our laboratory is focusing on discovering the rules that govern these decision processes by perturbing signaling steps, by monitoring signaling events and cell functions, and by employing mathematical modeling.  We have developed a number of novel biosensors and microscopy techniques to monitor cell signaling and functional processes over time, developed novel chemically induced enzyme activities for rapid signaling pathway perturbations and created a set of 3000 RNAi's to perturb signaling pathways by selectively reducing the expression of most human signaling proteins (shotgun perturbation strategy).    We are particularly intrigued by the roles of calcium, lipid second messenger and small GTPases in the spatial and temporal coordination of cellular responses and a current focus of the laboratory is on the questions how cells polarize and collectively migrate and how they proliferate in response to growth factor stimuli. This talk will give a general introduction into how to investigate complex signaling systems and in a second part focus on growth factor-triggered collective migration of endothelial cells. Using live cell imaging and systematic siRNA perturbations, we identified a modular control structure for this collective migration process. A third part of the talk will focus on an interlinked positive feedback system that we discovered using this same systematic perturbation strategy.  This control system allows quiescent cells stimulated by growth factors to decide in an all-or-none fashion whether they will remove a barrier protein and enter the cell cycle.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aRNA, Small Interfering.12aSignal Transduction22aCell Communication22aCell Movement22aCell Proliferation22aEndothelial Cellsxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15469  aAUTH01422nam a2200385 i 9918935129906676DNLM20260210112412.0230223s2022    iq       b    000 0 ara    a  2023344842  a9789922988672  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  ae-sp---ama-----  9Iraq00aR143b.M826 202200aWZ 80.5.A81 aMuḥammad, Maḥmūd al-Ḥājj Qāsim,eauthor.10aMaktabāt al-tarjamah wa-madāris al-ṭibb al-rāʼidah fī al-turāth al-ʻArabī /cal-Duktūr Maḥmūd al-Ḥājj Qāsim Muḥammad.  aal-Ṭabʻah al-ūlá. 1aal-Mawṣil, al-ʻIrāq :bDār Māshkī lil-Ṭibāʻah wa-al-Nashr wa-al-Tawzīʻ,c2022.  a157 pages ;c20 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aIṣ̣dārāt Rābiṭat al-Tadrīsīyīn al-Jāmiʻīyīn fī Naynawá ;v22  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aMedicine, Arabicxhistory12aTranslations22aTranslating22aLibrariesxhistory22aSchools, Medicalxhistory22aArab Worldxhistory 0aIṣ̣dārāt Rābiṭat al-Tadrīsīyīn al-Jāmiʻīyīn fī Naynawá ;v22.  aAUTH02259cgm a22004454a 101303659DNLM20260227151901.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070403s2007    mdu071|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13714  a(OCoLC)123053264  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 4001 aLandmesser, Lynn T.10aSpontaneous rhythmic activity in spinal motor circuit formation /cLynn Landmesser.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 4, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 11 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 26, 2007.3 a(CIT): Dr. Landmesser will present her research aimed at understanding the role that spontaneous electrical activity plays in the development of spinal cord motor circuits and neuromuscular maturation, and to elucidate the cellular mechanisms that produce such activity. Her laboratory found that such activity occurs during axon outgrowth and that it is required for accurate pathfinding. Unlike mature circuits which are driven by glutamate, early spontaneous activity in both chick and mouse require both cholinergic and GABAergic/glycinergic transmission as well as electrical coupling. By working out in more detail the circuit that produces spontaneous activity in wild type mice, her laboratory can now make use of various transgenic mice in which subpopulations of neurons are genetically deleted or altered.    NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aSpinal Cordxembryology12aSpinal Cordxphysiology22aMotor Neuronsxphysiology22aNeural Pathwaysxembryology22aNeural Pathwaysxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNation