nline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Vol. 1, issue 1  (2010).  aDescripton based on: Vol. 1, issue 1  (Jan. 2010); title from PDF article running title (ScienceDirect, viewed Mar. 24, 2010).  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 1, issue 2 (Apr. 2010) (viewed Mar. 24, 2010).1 aMEDLINEbv10, 2016-0 aPubMedbv10, 2016- ; Selected citations only before this date  aVol. 1, issue 1 preceded by Vol. 1, Supplement 1, published May 2009.  aHas supplements.  aAlso issued in print with abstracts of article only.12aPain22aPain Management22aPalliative Care 2aPeriodical2 aScandinavian Association for the Study of Pain.0 x1877-88604 uhttps://www.degruyter.com/view/j/sjpain  aAUTH03844cgm a22004334a 101501575DNLM20260227152932.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090407s2009    mdu066 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14938  a(OCoLC)359240079  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQS 6041 aRossant, Janet.10aMaking the blastocyst :blinking cell behaviour with cell fate /cJanet Rossant.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen (viewed May 11, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 5 min., 15 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 4, 2009.3 a(CIT): Dr. Rossant has been recognized for her contributions to science with many awards, including the Killam Prize for Health Sciences, the March of Dimes Prize in Developmental Biology, the Conklin Medal from the Society for Developmental Biology, and the CIHR Michael Smith Prize in Health Research, Canada"s most prestigious health research award. She is a fellow of both the Royal Societies of London and Canada, and holds the Lombard Insurance chair in pediatric research at SickKids.   Dr. Rossant is a founding member and serves as Deputy Scientific Director of The Stem Cell Network. She also directs the Centre for Modelling Human Disease in Toronto, which is undertaking genome-wide mutagenesis in mice to develop new mouse models of human disease. She serves on the Governing Council of the Canadian Institutes of Health Research, and chaired the Institutes" Working Group on stem cell research.   Dr. Rossant is a respected voice in the stem cell debate; she was involved with the National Academies of Science report on stem cell guidelines in the United States, and served on the International Society for Stem Cell Research (SSCR) task force charged with developing guidelines addressing the international diversity of cultural, political, legal and religious perspectives. Dr. Rossant has twice been named a Howard Hughes Medical Institute International Scholar.  From Dr. Rossant:  "The mouse blastocyst contains about 100 cells and only three distinct cell types. One cell type, the epiblast, gives rise to all cell types of the body and to pluripotent embryonic stem cells, while the other two cell types give rise to placental and other support cells and cell lines.   By studying both the embryo and its derived stem cells, we have been able to identify some of the key transcription factors specifying cell fate in both situations and used this information to derive new endoderm progenitor cell lines from human embryonic stem cells. But only by studying the embryo itself can we discover how cell behavior in the early developing embryo leads to restriction of cell fate by the blastocyst stage. I will describe our current model for blastocyst formation which implicates cell polarity, cell position and cell sorting in the final assembly of the organized blastocyst."  The NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series includes weekly scientific talks by some of the top researchers in the biomedical sciences worldwide.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBlastocyst22aEmbryonic Stem Cells 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14938  aAUTH00924cam a22002771  450000100100000000300050001000500170001500700140003200800410004603500200008704000210010704400110012806000200013906000310015910000210019024500980021126000280030930000110033733600260034833700280037433800270040250000410042950000570047053301100052799900090063767820600RDNLM20260212092240.0hd|bfa012baap821218s1869    fr            000|0 fre    a(OCoLC)14857702  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9France00aWVBbH821p 186900aW6bP3 v.7481 box 506 no.81 aHorteloup, Paul.10aPlaies du larynx, de la trachée, et de l'oesophage,bleurs conséquences, leur traitement.  aParis,bAsselin,c1869.  a136 p.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aThèse pour l'agrégation - Paris.  aNLM microfilm copy shelved as: Film 4813 no. 25DNLM  aMicrofilm.b[Bethesda, Md.] :cNational Library of Medicine,d1993.e1 microfilm reel : negative ; 35 mm.  aAUTH02326cgm a22004574a 101520531DNLM20260227153205.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u091202s2009    mdu074|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15449  a(OCoLC)529281706  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWB 30000aPersonalized medicine for the mind :bthe role of tailored communication /csponsored by the NCI Communications Strategy Roundtable, Office of Communications and Education, NCI.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCI communication roundtable seminar  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 14 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 17, 2009.3 a(CIT): Personalized medicine has traditionally focused on tailoring interventions to biological characteristics, but psychological, social and cognitive characteristics play an important role in health behavior and health status. At this seminar, researchers from the NCI-funded University of Michigan Center for Communications Research will present findings, ready-to-use tools, and practical, evidence-based guidelines to address numeracy skills in designing risk communications, elicit patient preferences for cancer screening and treatment, measure and tailor to patient communication styles, and use social media to deliver tailored behavior change interventions.  Speakers: Ken Resnicow, Larry An, Ed Saunders, Sarah Hawley, Angie Fagerlin, University of Michigan Center for Communications Research.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aCommunication12aPrecision Medicine22aHealth Promotionxmethods22aNeoplasms22aProfessional-Patient Relations 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNCI Communications Strategy Roundtable. 0aNCI communication roundtable seminar40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15449  aAUTH01813cgm a2200493 i 101770444DNLM20260227160222.0m     o  z f      vz|czau|ucr|cna||||||||200811s2020    mdu001 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:36104  a(OCoLC)1196168622  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 5052 aPresidential Briefing: Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID-19)d(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aPresidential Briefing: Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID-19) :bthe NIH research response /cPresident Donald Trump.1 aCoronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID-19) 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (23 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded March 3, 2020  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed September 16, 2020).22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aCoronavirus Infections12aSevere Acute Respiratory Syndrome22aPandemics22aBiomedical Research22aHealth Priorities 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aCollins, Francis S.,ehost,epanelist.1 aTrump, Donald,d1946-epanelist.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=36104  aAUTH02691cgm a2200409 i 101776732DNLM20260227160758.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210302s2021    mdu111|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31543  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 36000aDemystifying medicine.pMultiple sclerosis :badvances in pathogenesis and treatment /cAlison Wichman, Bibiana Bielekova.1 aMultiple sclerosis 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 51 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded February 9, 2021.3 a(CIT): Multiple sclerosis was first well described in 1868 by Jean-Martin Charcot, a neurologist at the famed Hôpital universitaire la Pitié-Salpêtrière in Paris, as "la sclerose en plaques," numerous scars on the white matter of the brain and spinal cord.  The three most reliable indicators of multiple sclerosis--intention tremor, nystagmus, and scanning speech--became known as Charcot"s triad.    More than 150 years later, MS remains challenging to treat but is less mysterious.  MS is recognized as an autoimmune disease.  And recent studies reveal the interaction of T and B cells in MS pathogenesis, suggesting novel treatment pathways.    Bibiana Bielekova, M.D., is chief of the NIAID Neuroimmunological Diseases Section.  Alison Wichman, M.D., is a neurologist and clinician in Dr. Bielekova"s lab.  Together, the seek to understand the mechanisms that participate in central nervous system injury, as well as those that have neuroprotective functions, to develop effective therapies for neuroimmunological diseases such as multiple sclerosis.  They use proof-of-principle interventional trials supported by biomarker-mechanistic studies and mathematical modeling to investigate major hypotheses about the pathophysiology of MS.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 26, 2021).12aMultiple Sclerosisxphysiopathology12aMultiple Sclerosisxtherapy 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aWichman, Alison,espeaker.1 aBielekova, Bibiana,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41285  aAUTH01372cgm a22004334a 101289729DNLM20260227151616.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2006    mdu415 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13350  a(OCoLC)84672025  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 10202aA symposium to honor Hal Gainer /cVan Leeuwen ... [et al.].  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 27, 2006).  aStreaming video (6 hr., 54 min., 41 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 8, 2006.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aGainer, Harold.12aNervous System Physiological Phenomena22aNeuronsxphysiology22aNeurosciences 2aConference Proceedings 2aFestschrift 2aWebcast1 aLeeuwen, F. W. vanq(Fred W.)2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13350  aAUTH01363cas a2200481 a 9814573DNLM20260227091040.0cr|cn#||||||||981016c19989999gw || p       0    0eng    asn 99040993 0 a1435-9448l1435-9448  a(DNLM)SR0092730(s)  a(OCoLC)40720977  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9Germany00aW1bAN228FG10aAnim Cogn2dnlm00aAnimal cognition.  aBerlin :bSpringer,cc1998-  aSix no. a year,b2009-  aTwo no. a year,b1998  aFour no. a year,b1999-2008  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediated