1aPost-traumatic stress disorder/mild traumatic brain injury :bmilitary perspective  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title frame (viewed Oct. 27, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 21 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 26, 2009.3 a(CIT): Women's Health Special Interest Group (WHSIG) Intramural Program on Research on Women's Health Sponsored by the NIH Office of Intramural Research and the NIH Office of Research on Women's Health.  Come join your fellow NIH colleagues in a scientific exchange on issues related to the biology and pathology of sex and gender differences and effects on women's health.    For more information, visit  http://orwh.od.nih.gov.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aStress Disorders, Post-Traumatic.22aBrain Injuriesxcomplications22aMilitary Medicine 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bWomen's Health Special Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15208  aAUTH02165cgm a2200469 i 101775917DNLM20260227160625.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2020    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31467  a(OCoLC)1241124888  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 50600aHarnessing synthetic biology and deep learning to address the COVID-19 pandemic /cJim Collins, Ph.D., MIT. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 0 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCOVID-19 lecture  aAccess restricted to HHS only.  aRecorded December 17, 2020.3 a(CIT): Dr. Collins is developing innovative ways to reprogram organisms, particularly bacteria, to perform desired tasks, such as detecting and treating infections. These programmed bacteria could lead to cheaper drugs, rapid diagnostic tests, and more effective treatments for antibiotic-resistant infections and a range of complex diseases. This work is part of the new field of synthetic biology, which Jim founded by combining science and engineering to construct biological circuits that can program organisms, much like we program computers now.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 8, 2021).12aCOVID-1912aPandemics22aSynthetic Biology22aDeep Learning22aModels, Biological 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCollins, Jamesq(James J.),espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bCOVID-19 Scientific Interest Group,esponsoring body. 0aNIH COVID-19 SIG lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38876  aAUTH01203cgm a2200385 a 101585235DNLM20260227154246.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120525s2012    mdu099|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17221  a(OCoLC)803799315  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWS 350.8.P41 aThurm, Audrey.10aDemystifying medicine.pAutism :bprogress continues /cAudrey Thurm.1 aAutism :bprogress continues  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 39 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded Apr. 17, 2012.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aAutistic Disorder 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17221  aAUTH01738cas a2200529   0077427DNLM20260227090919.0cr|cnu||||||||670420c19679999enkmr         0    0eng    asn 80002417   a0008-6363l0008-6363y0576-7261  aCVREAU  a(DNLM)C07640000(s)z(DNLM)C07660000(s)  a(OCoLC)01553351  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9England00aW1bCA77210aCardiovasc Res2dnlm00aCardiovascular research.  aLondon,bBritish Medical Assn.3 32008- :aOxford :bOxford Journals  av.billus.  aFourteen issues a year,b1999-  aMonthly,b1972-1998  aQuarterlyb1967-1971  aBimonthlyb1972-1977  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-       Jan. 1967-  aPublished in association with the British Cardiac Society.  aVols. for 1981-    indexed in: Combined cumulative index to cardiology.1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, Jan. 1967-0 aPubMedbv1n1, Jan. 1967-1 aIndex medicus  aSupplements accompany some volumes.  aAlso issued online.  3Vol. 15, no. 1 (1981)c19920824fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aCardiovascular System 2aPeriodical2 aBritish Medical Association.2 aEuropean Society of Cardiology.0 x1755-324505tCardiosciencex1015-5007w(OCoLC)221069084 uhttps://academic.oup.com/cardiovascres  aAUTH01002cam a22003017i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002100073040003500094044001000129060003200139111009000171245009800261246001900359264007400378300001400452336002600466337002800492338002700520650001700547650004300564650002500607650002200632651001000654655002700664999000900691101618055DNLM20260213174041.0130930s1970    cc            100 0 chi    a(OCoLC)874092742  aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1805 no.32 aQuan guo Zhong cao yao xin yi liao fa zhan lan huid(1970 :cBeijing, China)jauthor.10aQuan guo Zhong cao yao xin yi liao fa zhan lan hui ji shu zi liao xuan bian.pWai ke ji bing.1 aWai ke ji bing 1aBeijing :bQuan guo Zhong cao yao xin yi liao fa zhan lan hui,c1970.  a100 pages  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier12aDrug Therapy12aDrugs, Chinese Herbalxtherapeutic use22aExhibitions as Topic22aPlants, Medicinal 2aChina 2aConference Proceedings  aAUTH02199cgm a2200421 i 101671870DNLM20260227155108.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u151031s2015    mdu062|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19186  a(OCoLC)930496689  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWB 5371 aBest, Thomas M.eauthor,espeaker.10aMassage therapies :bfrom lab bench top to the patient /cThomas Best. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCCIH integrative medicine research lecture  aRecorded September 21, 2015.3 a(CIT): During a therapeutic massage session, clinicians rely primarily on patient feedback to determine the amount of force and the length of time a force should be applied to a patient's muscles. Research has shown that biologic tissues are mechanotransductive. As a result, through their work, Dr. Best's lab is highlighting the importance of measuring the force applied during massage in order to best optimize clinical treatment. In this lecture, Dr. Best will provide an overview of the concept of mechanotransduction and how it applies to massage-based therapies, the basic science findings that support the use of massage-based therapies, and the clinical problems for which massage is a common treatment.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 24, 2015).12aMassage22aTreatment Outcome 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (U.S.),eissuing body,esponsoring body. 0aNCCIH integrative medicine research lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19186  aAUTH02467cgmaa22004694a 101208393DNLM20260227144424.0vf|czahmuvd|cvaizuvf|cjahouhd|bfa012baap040301s2004    mdu065 ||    f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:2615  a(OCoLC)56482431  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aWM 280bVC no.28 200400aNeural and psychological basis of compulsive drug-seeking :bimplications for treatment /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  a[Bethesda, Md.] :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004.  a1 videocassette of 1 (DVCPRO) (65 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/4 in.3master.  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (65 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videodisc of 1 (DVD) (65 min.) :bsd., col. ;c4 3/4 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aNIH-WALS  aSpeaker, Barry J. Everitt.  aOriginally presented Feb. 18, 2004.3 a(Producer) Discusses the way that associative learning impacts upon the establishment of addictive behavior. The initial, goal-directed actions by which animals and humans self-administer cocaine can become compulsive and habitual, driven and maintained by the presence of drug-associated cues in the environment--cues that can also precipitate relapse after abstinence. Suggests ways in which understanding neural mechanisms may point toward novel treatments.  aVideocassette (Betacam SP)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2011.e1 videocassette (65 min.) : sd., col. ; 1/2 in.  aVideodisc (DVD)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2020.e1 videodisc (65 min.) : sd., col. ; 4 3/4 in.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aCocaine-Related Disorders22aAssociation Learning22aBrainxphysiology1 aEveritt, Barry J.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=2615  aAUTH01785cgm a22004214a 101528389DNLM20260227153307.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100406s2010    mdu189|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15656  a(OCoLC)624405049  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2022 aNCI Cancer Human Biobank Public Information Meetingd(2010 :cLos Angeles, Calif.)14aThe NCI Cancer Human Biobank (caHUB) Public Information Meeting /csponsored by the National Cancer Institute.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (3 hr., 9 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 1, 2010.3 a(CIT): The NCI invites you to attend one of two public meetings to learn more about the caHUB planning process and mission, and review the implementation, structure, timeline and funding process for this national resource. These meetings will bring together academic and government researchers, non-profit foundations, private industries and interested public.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxgenetics22aBiological Specimen Banksxorganization & administration22aSpecimen Handlingxmethods 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15656  aAUTH01788cgm a2200445 a 101574463DNLM20260227154405.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u111208s2011    mdu555|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16937  a(OCoLC)770841434  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWS 350.8.P41 aInternational Neurotoxicology Conferencen(27th :d2011 :cNational Institutes of Health)10aEnvironmentally triggered neurodevelopmental disorders :bfocus on endocrine disruption and sex differences in autism, ADHD, and schizophrenia : 27th International Neurotoxicology Conference /cNIH.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (9 hr., 15 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded Oct. 31, 2011.3 a(CIT): Environmentally Triggered Neurodevelopmental Disorders Focus on Endocrine Disruption and Sex Differences in Autism, ADHD, and Schizophrenia.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aAutistic Disorderxchemically induced22aAttention Deficit Disorder with Hyperactivityxchemically induced22aChild22aEndocrine Disruptorsxtoxicity22aEnvironmental Pollutantsxtoxicity22aSchizophreniaxchemically induced22aSex Factors 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16937  aAUTH02080cgm a2200433 a 101557281DNLM20260227153939.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110325s2011    mdu900 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16498  a(DNLM)CIT:16499  a(OCoLC)720560198  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWN 2062 aSummit on Management of Radiation Dose in Computerized Tomographyd(2011 :cBethesda, Md.)14aThe summit on management of radiation dose in computerized tomography :btoward the sub-mSv exam, Feburary 24-25, 2011, Bethesda, Md.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (15 hr., 0 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 24 & 25, 2011.3 a(CIT): This is an NIH, CIBR sponsored and FDA, ACR, and ACC supported conference focused on transforming CT technology and its use to achieve minimal public health risks from radiation exposure. A specific goal is to identify the technological steps and associated research required to reduce the routine CT exam dose to less than one mSv. Additional goals in the near term are improving our understanding and management of radiation exposure, and defining steps to achieve best practices. For more information, visit:  http://www.nibibmeetings.org/rds2011/.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aTomography, X-Ray Computedxstandards22aRadiation Dosage 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Biomedical Imaging and Bioengineering (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1649840uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16499  aAUTH02621cgm a2200445 i 9918282192806676DNLM20260227161533.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u211008s2021    mdu490|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31936  a(DNLM)CIT:31937  a(OCoLC)1314048473  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 2032 aGlycobiology of Cancer (Symposium)d(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.14aThe glycobiology of cancer /cNIH Common Fund Glycoscience Program. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c2021.  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (8 hr., 10 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 16 & 17, 2021.3 a(CIT): Glycans (glycoproteins, glycolipids, glycosaminoglycans) are expressed on the cell surface and play key roles in mediating cell-cell and cell-matrix interactions, protein-receptor signaling, and appropriate protein folding and maturation during translation.  Malignant transformation and tumor progression correlate with aberrant changes in cellular glycosylation.  Cells presenting with an altered glycocalyx are recognized by immune cells, leading to induction of inhibitory immune processes which subsequently drive tumor growth and metastasis.   A focus on the glycobiology of cancer thus offers the possibility of revealing both new diagnostic biomarkers, and therapeutic targets (glycosyltransferases, cancer-associated glycans). This symposium will cover recent work on the roles glycans play in cancer (signaling, tumor progression, metastasis), their potential as diagnostics as well as therapeutic targets, and it will highlight several new tools developed in the Common Fund Glycoscience Program for the study of cancer biology.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 28, 2022).12aNeoplastic Processes22aGlycosylation22aGlycomicsxmethods 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aCommon Fund Glycoscience Program,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4264740uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42649  aAUTH01746cas a2200505 a 101308349DNLM20260227090908.0cr|cn ||||||||070215c20089999nyuqr p       0    0eng    a  20072141970 a1935-9705l1935-9705  a(OCoLC)83831862  aNSDcNSDdNYPdCUIdDLCdDNLM  alcdansdpanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bJO663M0 aJ. gay lesbian ment. health10aJ Gay Lesbian Ment Health2dnlm 0aJournal of gay & lesbian mental health00aJournal of gay & lesbian mental health.3 aJournal of gay and lesbian mental health  aNew York, N.Y. :bHaworth Medical Press3 aPhiladelphia :bTaylor & Francis  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with v. 12, no. 1/2 (2008).  a"Official journal of the Association of Gay & Lesbian Psychiatrists"--cover.  aDescription based on first issue; title from title page.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 12, no 4 (2008).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.12aHomosexualityxpsychology22aBisexualityxpsychology22aMental Disorders22aMental Health22aPsychotherapy 2aPeriodical2 aAssociation of Gay & Lesbian Psychiatrists.0 x1935-971300tJournal of gay & lesbian psychotherapyx0891-7140w(DLC)   89659011w(OCoLC)149781214 uhttps://www.tandfonline.com/loi/wglm20  aAUTH01298cas a2200481   0012445DNLM20260227091018.0cr|cn ||||||||680616c19659999sz br p       0    0eng    a0018-716Xl0018-716X  aHUDEA8  a(DNLM)H22000000(s)  a(OCoLC)01641127  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aengafreager  9Switzerland00aW1bHU446C10aHum Dev2dnlm00aHuman development.  aBasel.3 aBasel :bKarger  av.billus.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 8-       Jan. 1965-2 aMEDLINEbv9n3, 1966-v27n3-4, 19842 aPubMedbv9n3, 1966-v27n3-4, 19840 aOLDMEDLINE2 aIndex medicus  aAlso available online.  aArticles mostly in English, with summaries in French and German.  aContinues Vita humana. 2aGrowth 2aPhysiology 2aPsychology 2aPeriodical0 x1423-005410tVita humana4 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/224249  aAUTH01472cas a22004694a 100968761DNLM20260227091058.0cr|cnu||||||||001024c20029999pautr p       0    0eng    a  20012159900 a1538-1501l1538-1501  a(OCoLC)48653906  aNSDcNSDdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bJO671DG10aJ HIV AIDS Soc Serv2dnlm00aJournal of HIV/AIDS & social services.10aJournal of HIV/AIDS and social services3 aBulletin of HIV/AIDS and social services  aBinghamton, NY :bHaworth Press, Inc.,cc2002-3 32009- :aPhiladelphia, PA :bTaylor & Francis  av.  aThree no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (2002)-  aTitle from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: vol. 8, no. 1 (2009).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso available online.  aAdopted by the National Social Work AIDS Network.12aHIV Infections22aAcquired Immunodeficiency Syndrome22aSocial Work 2aPeriodical2 aNational Social Work AIDS Network.0 x1538-151X4 uhttps://www.tandfonline.com/loi/whiv20  aAUTH01670cgm a2200445 i 101770431DNLM20260227160245.0m     o  z f      vz|czau|ucr|cna||||||||200811s2020    mdu272 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:37543  a(OCoLC)1194873613  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 168.5.C82 aCoronavirus, SARS-CoV-2 & Glycans (Workshop)d(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aCoronavirus, SARS-CoV-2 & Glycans /cNIGMS, NIH.1 iTItle on PowerPoint screen:aCommon Fund Glycoscience Program workshop :bCoronavirus, SARS-CoV-2 & glycans1 aCoronavirus, SARS-CoV-2, and Glycans 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (4 hrs, 32 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded May 1, 2020  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed September 3, 2020).12aCoronavirus Infectionsxvirology12aCoronaviridaexphysiology22aPolysaccharides22aGlycoproteins 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aCommon Fund Glycoscience Program,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institute of General Medical Sciences (U.S.)40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=37543  aAUTH03743cgm a2200481 i 101674296DNLM20260227155237.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u151224s2015    mdu669 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19366  a(DNLM)CIT:19368  a(OCoLC)945749739  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 300 AA12 aTribal Ecological Knowledge Workshopd(2015 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aTribal Ecological Knowledge Workshop /cNational Institute of Environmental Health Sciences, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (11 hr., 9 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded December 3 & 4, 2015.3 a(CIT): The Tribal Ecological Knowledge (TEK) workshop will explore the contributions that Native American (NA) and Alaskan Native (AN) tribal communities bring to the research enterprise. This workshop will focus on the value of TEK for environmental health sciences (EHS) and biomedical research. The term TEK denotes "traditional knowledge [that], like Western science, is based on accumulation of observation. It is knowledge that is transmitted through generations, practice in how tribes carry out resource use practices, and beliefs about how people fit into ecosystems" (Berkes, 2000). The term is widely used by tribal communities to denote a range of factors affecting Native health from an indigenous perspective. We propose that TEK is a culturally appropriate form of community-engaged research that could benefit biomedical research focused on environmental factors affecting health, and may also be a way to increase trust and mutual respect in tribal-academic partnership. In addition, we believe that TEK is an example of citizen science, which we would like to highlight due to the increased attention to citizen science as a viable element of research among researchers and federal agencies. The workshop goals are to explore ways to improve trust in academic-tribal research; to identify methods for incorporating community-acquired data and local TEK into environmental health and biomedical research studies; to consider ethical approaches for tribal specific data collection; and to build capacity to respond to long term and immediate disaster events. This workshop has been organized by representatives of seven tribal communities working with NIH (NIEHS and NIMHD), Indian Health Service, Smithsonian Museums, and CDC/ATSDR staff. We believe that hosting a workshop around the theme of TEK will raise awareness of the importance of this type of contribution to research and garner input from those with expertise in TEK to identify the optimal ways to incorporate it into research.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 21, 2016).12aHealth Services, Indigenous12aIndians, North Americanxethnology22aBiomedical Research22aEcosystem22aEnvironmental Health22aInterdisciplinary Communication 2aUnited Statesxethnology 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Environmental Health Sciences,esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1936640uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19368  aAUTH01214cam a2200325 i 9918787288806676DNLM20260212142915.0240318r19541954ohu      b    000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.41 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aPrecordial noises heard at a distance from the chest /cWilliam Bennett Bean. 1a[Cincinnati, Ohio] :b[publisher not identified],c[1954]  apages 121-164, 1 unnumbered page ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"Reprinted from the Cincinnati journal of medicine, 35:121-126, 159-166, 1954."  a"Presented at the Roger Morris Lecture for 1953 at a meeting of the Academy of Medicine on March 3, 1953."  aNLM copy, bound 4th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aHeart Sounds12aNoise22aHearing 2aAddress0 aCincinnati journal of medicine.  aAUTH02506cgm a2200469 i 9918418884106676DNLM20260227162257.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220616s2022    mdu612|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32343  a(DNLM)CIT:32350  a(OCoLC)1378182778  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 832 aGlyco-Informatics at the Interface of Disease and Data (Symposium)d(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aGlyco-informatics at the interface of disease and data /cNIH Common Fund Glycoscience Program. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (10 hr., 12 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 26 & 27, 2022.3 a(CIT): Ongoing technical advances are accelerating the pace and sophistication of data acquisition, and its integration with biological knowledge (genetics, proteomics, pathology, glycobiology). This symposium seeks to highlight the importance of publicly sharing scientific results and their integration with glycan and glycoconjugate data.  Evaluating glycobiological data in the context of genetic mutations, gene expression, and protein function provides new opportunities for systems-level understanding of the role(s) glycosylation plays in normal and disease processes. Scientists who use NCBI, EMBL-EBI,  UniProt, PDB, CAZy, Gene Ontology and other such sources but do not specialize in glycobiology are especially encouraged to take part in this symposium.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 11, 2023).12aPolysaccharidesxphysiology22aGlycoconjugatesxphysiology22aComputational Biologyxmethods22aData Collection22aInformation Dissemination 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aCommon Fund Glycoscience Program,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4518540uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45187  aAUTH03031cgm a2200385 a 101565224DNLM20260227154046.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110715s2011    mdu403 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16712  a(OCoLC)747167639  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 14100aAdvanced medical imaging developments and applications for neuroscience research /cNCRR.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (6 hr., 43 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Jun. 9, 2011.3 a(CIT): Neurobiologists have utilized advanced molecular biological assays and immunohistochemistry to better identify the cause and progression of neurological diseases and disorders. However, these techniques are limited because they are required to be performed on biopsied or postmortem brain samples and therefore lack broad clinical applicability. Medical imaging modalities such as MRI/MRS, PET, CT and optical imaging, as well as their co-modalities, have enabled real-time visualization of the structure and function of the central nervous system in live subjects. Because they do not require biopsied or postmortem samples, most of these technologies are routinely used in clinics and offer minimally-invasive means to detect structural, metabolic and physiologic processes of the brain in action for research and clinical purposes. The development of novel imaging technologies requires strong knowledge and background in chemistry, physics and engineering. For example, chemists synthesize specialized radiopharmaceuticals, contrast agents and probes to improve image contrast and detect cellular targets. Engineers design coils/sensors with different geometries to maximize signal-to-noise for specific areas of interest. Physicists develop specialized pulse sequences and data processing algorithms to acquire and interpret data with good image resolution in order to gain insights into neural structure, function and activity.    Numerous synergistic research efforts across disciplinary borders have produced stunning advances in understanding the brain in health and illness. This symposium brings together leaders in this interdisciplinary science to demonstrate the fruits of such efforts and the promise that imaging continues to hold for transforming human neuroscience.  aOpen-captioned.12aCentral Nervous System Diseasesxdiagnosis12aDiagnostic Imaging 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Center for Research Resources (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16712  aAUTH01001cam a2200289 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390350022000800400035001020420008001370440018001450600023001631000062001862450044002482640065002923000021003573360026003783370028004043380027004325000091004595010059005506500024006097100034006337300035006679990009007029918787288506676DNLM20260212175359.0240318r19541954ilu           000n0        a(OCoLC)1528817227  aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.61 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aTimidity and caveats /cWilliam B. Bean 1a[Chicago, Illinois] :bAmerican Medical Association,c[1954]  a4 pages ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"Reprinted from the A.M.A. Archives of internal medicine, July 1954, vol. 94, pp.1-4."  aNLM copy, bound 6th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM12aJournalism, Medical2 aAmerican Medical Association.0 aArchives of internal medicine.  aAUTH02341cgm a22004334a 101267398DNLM20260227145024.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu225|||||||f||||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10003  a(OCoLC)181358906  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 6802 aInternational Centers for Tropical Disease Research.bMeetingn(10th :d2001 :cNational Library of Medicine)10aTenth anniversary meeting of International Centers for Tropical Disease Research (ICTDR) /cNational Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (NIAID).  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 2, 2007).  aStreaming video (3 hr., 45 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 7, 2001.3 aThis videocast consists of the opening session of the Tenth Anniversary Meeting of International Centers for Tropical Disease Research (ICTDR), sponsored by the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Disease (NIAID).  Researchers from around the world attended the three-day event, which highlighted accomplishments in diagnosing, treating, and preventing tropical diseases. At the meeting, Director Anthony S. Fauci of NIAID unveiled the NIAID Global Health Research Plan for HIV/AIDS, Malaria, and Tuberculosis. This report outlined the Institute's plans for fighting three critical infectious diseases by building sustained research capability and enhancing international partnerships.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aTropical Medicine22aAnniversaries and Special Events22aResearch 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10003  aAUTH01966cgm a22004094a 101267483DNLM20260227145115.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu339 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10100  a(OCoLC)69672709  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWD 21000aPublic meeting on obesity /cFDA Obesity Working Group.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 26, 2006).  aStreaming video (5 hr., 39 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 23, 2003.3 a(CIT): The purpose of this public meeting, which is being sponsored by FDA's Obesity Working Group, is to discuss FDA's role and responsibilities in addressing the major public health problem of obesity, to focus on issues related to promoting better consumer dietary and lifestyle choices that have the potential to significantly improve the health and well-being of Americans, and to obtain stakeholder views on how best to build a framework for messages to consumers about reducing obesity and achieving better nutrition.  For more information, visit  http://www.fda.gov/oc/opacom/hottopics/obesity.html.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aObesityxprevention & control22aHealth Behavior 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aFDA Obesity Working Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10100  aAUTH01663cgm a22004454a 101267385DNLM20260227145041.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu659 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:9985  a(OCoLC)136290026  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 522 aGene Transfer Clinical Research Safety Symposiumn(3rd :d2000 :cNational Institutes of Health)10a3rd Gene Transfer Clinical Research Safety Symposium /cNational Institutes of Health.3 aThird Gene Transfer Clinical Research Safety Symposium  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 26, 2007).  aStreaming video (10 hr., 59 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 14, 2000.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aGene Transfer Techniquesxadverse effects22aCardiovascular Diseasesxtherapy22aClinical Trials as Topicxstandards22aGenetic Vectors22aPatient Selection22aSafety Management 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?9985  aAUTH04581cgm a2200481 i 101757465DNLM20260227160038.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u191102s2019    mdu605 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:28777  a(DNLM)CIT:28779  a(OCoLC)1139640888  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 2742 aBrief Prevention Interventions for Young Adults: Strengthening Effects (Workshop)d(2019 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aBrief Prevention Interventions for Young Adults :bstrengthening effects /cNational Institute on Alcohol Abuse and Alcoholism, NIH.1 iTitle on PowerPoint:aBrief alcohol interventions for young adults 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (10 hr., 5 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 10 &11, 2019.3 a(CIT): College age remains a significant risk period for hazardous/extreme drinking among males and females. Recent data suggests that while drinking patterns are converging across sexes, college-age females are engaging in greater levels of binge drinking. Although brief interventions (BI) designed to reduce alcohol consumption and related harms in college and young adult populations have shown positive effects, the rigor of research on this topic has been impaired by limited outcome measurement, measurement inconsistencies, variability in methodologies, and modest effect sizes. There is a paucity of studies that use comparative designs of efficacious interventions that examine mediators and moderators (e.g., gender, sub-population differences, developmental considerations, behavioral and neurocognitive phenotypes, and environmental factors that contribute to change) and how these relate to behavior change and maintenance of effects. Adapting existing evidence-based intervention designs and tailoring approaches to meet specific individual and contextual characteristics may contribute to greater intervention efficacy, longer term reductions in high risk/extreme drinking and related consequences, and cost savings where resources are limited. The overall goal of this conference is to build upon the existing base of evidence-based alcohol BI for college and young adult populations (e.g., CollegeAIM, recent meta-analyses) and address key areas that will advance the field: 1) methods and challenges to optimizing intervention design, and 2) implementation and scale up of BI approaches. While BIs show modest effects, understanding the active ingredients that contribute to behavior change and refinement of intervention methods is needed to achieve greater magnitudes of intervention effects that will inform implementation practices and personalized approaches. Just as there are different pathways to the transition to alcohol dependence, pathways leading to intervention efficacy and successful outcomes are heterogeneous. Positive effects may be obtained dependent upon intervention components that are unique to the individual; for example, gender, associated comorbidities, reward/incentive-salience, self-regulation. Disentangling mechanisms of action is critical to yielding evidence-based interventions that will translate into efficacious personalized prevention interventions for dissemination and implementation across settings. This conference brings together key researchers from varied perspectives who conduct seminal research in intervention design and methodology, translation of neuroscience to prevention and intervention research, and implementation science in young adult populations.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed January 31, 2020).12aAlcoholismxprevention & control22aAlcohol Drinking in College22aCrisis Intervention22aPreventive Health Services22aYoung Adult 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute on Alcohol Abuse and Alcoholism (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?2877740uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?28779  aAUTH01818cgm a2200433 i 101775904DNLM20260227160553.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2020    mdu093|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31438  a(OCoLC)1239645893  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 506.52 aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19d(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19 breakout session 5 (immunologic/rheumatologic) /cNIAID, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Healt