67766DNLM20260227145641.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu068  |||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10646  a(OCoLC)84671120  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWD 200.5.M600aHAART and mitochondria :beffects of nucleoside analogues and protease inhibitors.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 14, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 7 min., 18 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 9, 2001.3 a(CIT): The Mitochondria Interest Group (MIG) is an Inter-Institute Interest Group at the National Institutes of Health (NIH), with members from NIH and from around the world! MIG is concerned with all aspects of the mitochondrion and diseases in which the mitochondrion is involved.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aMitochondriaxdrug effects12aMitochondrial Diseases22aAntiretroviral Therapy, Highly Activexadverse effects22aProtease Inhibitorsxadverse effects22aReverse Transcriptase Inhibitorsxadverse effects 2aLecture 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10646  aAUTH01461cgm a22004214a 101267979DNLM20260227145939.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu176 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11136  a(OCoLC)213378977  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.51 aWichman, Alison.10aInformed consent :bsession 5 /cAlison Wichman, Steve Joffe, and Christine Grady.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 20, 2008).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 55 min., 15 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 5, 2001.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomedical Researchxethics22aEthics, Research22aHuman Experimentationxethics22aInformed Consent 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11136  aAUTH02944cgm a2200457 i 101771150DNLM20260227160224.0m     o  z f      vz|czau|ucr|cna||||||||200828s2020    mdu059 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35611  a(OCoLC)1197634686  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 30700aSocial cognitive functions of the prefrontal cortex in human infants /cRebecca Saxe. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience series seminar  aRecorded March 2, 20203 a(CIT): Externally observable components of human actions carry only a tiny fraction of the information that matters. Human observers are vastly more interested in perceiving or inferring the mental states - the beliefs, desires and intentions - that lie behind the observable shell. If a person checks her watch, is she uncertain about the time, late for an appointment, or bored with the conversation? If a person shoots his friend on a hunting trip, did he intend revenge or just mistake his friend for a partridge? The mechanism people use to infer and reason about another person’s states of mind is called a ‘Theory of Mind’ (ToM). One of the most striking discoveries of recent human cognitive neuroscience - made in Dr. Saxe lab, and others - is that there is a group of brain regions in human cortex that selectively and specifically underlie ToM. Excitingly, most questions about these brain regions remain open. Dr. Saxe lab studies Theory of Mind as a case study in the deeper and broader question: how does the brain - an electrical and biological machine - construct abstract thoughts? They use functional neuroimaging, behavioural studies with kids and adults, patient studies, and transcranial magnetic stimulation to study abstract representations in the human brain. In addition to Theory of Mind, their recent research investigates brain development, moral reasoning, causal reasoning, and language.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed September 22, 2020).12aPrefrontal Cortexxphysiology22aCognition22aSocial Perception22aTheory of Mind22aInfant 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aSaxe, Rebecca,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=35611  aAUTH01542cgm a22004454a 101267810DNLM20260227145616.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu068 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10704  a(OCoLC)70663579  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5681 aKincade, Paul W.10aEstrogen-dependent and independent regulation of immune system development /cPaul W. Kincade.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 17, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 8 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 25, 2003.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aLymphocytesximmunology22aBone Marrowximmunology22aEstrogensximmunology22aImmune Systemxgrowth & development22aThymus Glandximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10704  aAUTH00872nam a2200253 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390200029000800400025001090420008001340440014001420600010001562450133001662640068002993000056003673360026004233370028004493380027004775040041005046500026005457000038005719990009006099918845384106676DNLM20260226171542.0240628s2022    ag ac    b    000 0 spa    a9789878452166qpaperback  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Argentina00aWL 2100aDesafíos bioéticos en neurociencias del siglo XXI :bel presente y el futuro /cAlejandra T. Rabadán, editora científica. 1aCiudad Autónoma de Buenos Aires :bEdiciones Journal,c[2022]  axxii, 415 pages :billustrations, portrait ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aNeurosciencesxethics1 aRabadán, Alejandra T.,eeditor.  aAUTH02578cgm a22004694a 101306056DNLM20260227151942.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070430s2007    mdu068|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13738  a(OCoLC)137294958  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 551 aLippincott-Schwartz, Jennifer.10aEmerging fluorescence technologies for analysis of protein localization and organelle dynamics /cJennifer Lippincott-Schwartz.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from PowerPoint screen (viewed May 9, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 8 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 4, 2007.3 a(CIT): The development of fluorescent proteins as molecular tags over the past decade has splurred a revolution by allowing complex biochemical processes to be correlated with the functioning of proteins in livings cells. Fluorescent proteins such as green fluorescent protein (GFP) from the jelly fish Aequorea Victoria and its variants can be fused to virtually any protein of interest to analyze protein geography, movement and chemistry in living cells. As such, they have provided an important new tool for understanding protein function, filling an urgent need now that the genome sequence of many organisms is complete. Among the new fluorescent proteins are those that can be photoactivated. These molecules are invisible until activated by a specific wavelength of light, at which point they become brightly fluorescent. I will discuss results from two new techniques employing photoactivatable fluorescent proteins: in cellula pulse-chase analysis and photoactivated localization microscopy (PALM).  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aProteinsxanalysis22aLuminescent Proteins22aMicroscopy, Fluorescencexmethods22aOrganelles22aProteinsxmetabolism 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13738  aAUTH01428cgm a2200409 i 101649930DNLM20260227154931.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u141224s2014    mdu068|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18782  a(OCoLC)903315195  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 4771 aRoukos, Vassilis,eauthor.14aThe biogenesis of chromosome translocations /cVassilis Roukos. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 8 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Dec. 16, 2014.3 a(CIT): DNA Repair Interest Group videoconference  Acrobat Slides.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed Feb. 2, 2015.12aTranslocation, Geneticxphysiology22aOrganelle Biogenesis 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bDNA Repair Interest Grou