(IEEE Transactions on medical robotics and bionics) 1aPiscataway, NJ :bIEEE,c[2019]-  a1 online resource  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1, issue 1 (2019)0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aA publication of the IEEE Robotics and Automation Society, the IEEE Engineering in Medicine and Biology Society.  aDescription based on: Vol. 1, issue 1 (Feb. 2019) ; title from journal home page (publisher's website, viewed August 23, 2019).  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 1, issue 3 (Aug. 2019) (viewed August 23, 2019)12aRobotics.22aBionics22aBiomedical Technology 2aPeriodical2 aInstitute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers,eissuing body.2 aIEEE Robotics and Automation Society,eissuing body.2 aIEEE Engineering in Medicine and Biology Society,eissuing body.40uhttps://ieeexplore.ieee.org/xpl/RecentIssue.jsp?punumber=8253409  aAUTH01502cam a2200385 i 00150620RDNLM20260206220225.0cr||||||||||||810622s1918    nyu           000 0 eng    a   18010282   a(OCoLC)2927729  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aBD 444bF889z 19181 aFreud, Sigmund,d1856-1939,eauthor.10aZeitgemässes über Krieg und Tod.lEnglish10aReflections on war and death /cby Professor Dr. Sigmund Freud, LL. D. ;  authorized English translation by Dr. A. A. Brill and Alfred B. Kuttner. 1aNew York :bMoffat, Yard and Company,c1918.  a72 pages ;c20 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aTranslation of Zeitgemässes über Krieg und Tod.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc202506252pda5DNLM  aConservedc202506272pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aDeath12aWarfare22aArmed Conflicts.1 aBrill, A. A.q(Abraham Arden),d1874-1948,etranslator.1 aKuttner, Alfred B.q(Alfred Booth),d1886-etranslator.4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/00150620R  aAUTH03742cgm a2200445 i 9918332581406676DNLM20260227161804.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220104s2021    mdu350|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32072  a(OCoLC)1294916507  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 3902 aNIH Rural Health Seminard(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aStructural-level determinants of rural health disparities /cNIMHD, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (5 hr., 50 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 18, 2021.3 a(CIT): The NIH Rural Health Special Interest Group works to raise awareness of rural health issues and improve biomedical, behavioral/social science, intervention and implementation research aimed at improving disease prevention, self-management, and care delivery across the care continuum. Our goal is to advance our understanding of rural health disparities and stimulate research to identify multi-level, evidence-based solutions to improve rural health outcomes. We connect translational, clinical, and community-based researchers from diverse disciplines throughout the NIH, to research communities, and decision makers. Among researchers, we also provide opportunities for professional development, networking, and community engagement. The National Organization of State Offices of Rural Health set aside the third Thursday of November to observe National Rural Health Day annually. One of the main activities of the NIH Rural Health Special Interest Group is to join other agencies to observe this annual National Rural Health Day by hosting a NIH Rural Health Seminar to stimulate multidisciplinary discussions to advance rural health research. The 2021 Rural Health Seminar: Structural-Level Determinants of Rural Health Disparities will enhance our understanding of the structural-level determinants of health that contribute to rural health disparities. The 2021 seminar includes engaging panel discussions, presentations, and early-stage investigator talks where participants will hear from the future generation of rural health researchers. Three tracks organize the seminar and include structural-level sociocultural (e.g., racism and discrimination, sociocultural norms), economic (e.g., poverty) and physical environmental determinants (e.g., chemical, built, neighborhood, food environments) that contribute to rural health disparities. The seminar will close with a track focused on evaluating and measuring structural level determinants, and policy implications. Attendees will come away from this virtual seminar with an understanding of the impact of structural determinates on rural populations and the need for multi-level interventions to reduce rural health disparities.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed January 25, 2022).12aRural Health22aHealthcare Disparities22aSocial Determinants of Health22aSocioeconomic Factors22aRace Factors 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=43942  aAUTH01034cam a22003017i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003500073044001000108060003300118245012000151246006300271246008500334250001300419264005800432300003300490336002600523337002800549338002700577500002900604650002100633650001200654650001200666650001500678700003000693999000900723101617671DNLM20260209150353.0961012s1930    cc a          000 0 chi    aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1804 no.2000aXin xue zhi wei sheng jiao ke shu,ndi 2 ce /cbian cuan zhe Cheng Hanzhang ; jiao ding zhe Wang Yunwu, Gu Shoubai.1 aXin xue zhi gao ji xiao xue wei sheng jiao ke shu, di 2 ce1 iEnglish title:aNew system series textbook on hygiene for higher primary schools  a160 ban. 1aShanghai :bShang wu yin shu guan,cMin guo 19 [1930]  a2, 44 pages :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"Da xue yuan shen ding."12aHealth Education22aAnatomy22aBiology22aCurriculum1 aCheng, Hanzhang,eeditor.  aAUTH02184cgm a2200469 i 9918418887006676DNLM20260227162411.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220616s2022    mdu092|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32401  a(OCoLC)1368035629  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQ 172.5.C7400aHow does diversity impact science? /cChief Officer for Scientific Workforce Diversity, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 32 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH scientific workforce diversity seminar series  aRecorded May 17, 2022.3 a(CIT): Scientific Workforce Diversity Seminar Series Join the NIH Chief Officer for Scientific Workforce Diversity (COSWD) Office for the final Scientific Workforce Diversity Seminar Series (SWDSS) event of the 2021-2022 season, "How Does Diversity Impact Science?" Dr. Marie A. Bernard, the NIH COSWD, will moderate a discussion on the impact of workforce diversity on creativity and innovation in science. Panelists will share their perspectives on the benefits of scientific workforce diversity and highlight effective methods for measuring its impact, as well as areas for future research.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed January 27, 2023).12aScience12aWorkforce22aCreativity22aDiversity, Equity, Inclusion22aMentoring 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aBernard, Marie A.,emoderator.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aScientific workforce diversity seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45288  aAUTH01716cas a22004695i 101646666DNLM20260227090908.0cr||||||||||||141119c20149999sa || p       |    0eng    a  2016243285  a2312-0053l2312-0053y1015-8782  a(OCoLC)953244415  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9South Africa00aW1bSO925H10aS Afr J Infect Dis2dnlm00aSouthern African journal of infectious diseases.1 aSAJID 1a[Centurion] :bMedpharm Publications (Pty) Ltd.,c2014-3132019- :aDurbanville, Cape Town, South Africa :bAOSIS Publishing  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Vol. 29, no. 1 (2014).0 aPubMedbv34n1, 2019- Selected citations only before this date0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: Volume 31, number 1 (2016); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 31, number 1 (2016).12aCommunicable Diseases 2aPeriodical2 aFederation of Infectious Diseases Societies of South Africa,eissuing body.2 aPublic Health Association of South Africa,eissuing body.0 x2313-181000tSouthern African journal of epidemiology & infectionx1015-8782w(OCoLC)201010074 uhttps://sajid.co.za/index.php/sajid4 uhttp://tandfonline.com/loi/ojid204 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101646666  aAUTH01366nam a2200373 i 101773129DNLM20260205111642.0980701r18911891nyu           000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)39377316  aNYPbengerdacNYPdKRTASdOCLCQdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5505 no.211 aRutherford, R. C.q(Reuben Clifford),d1823-1895,eauthor.10aWoolen or linen? :ba review of certain articles (editorial and contributory,) in the Irish textile journal /cby R.C. Rutherford. 1aNew York :b[publisher not identified],c[1891?]  a29 pages ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aTitle from cover.  a"Reprinted from The Sanitarian, May, 1891."  aNLM copy, author's presentation copy.5DNLM  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202105042pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202105045DNLM02aIrish textile journal.12aWool Fiber12aFlax22aClothing22aHygiene0 aSanitarian.  aAUTH04335cgm a2200421 i 9918434185106676DNLM20260227162522.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220706s2022    mdu438|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32472  a(DNLM)CIT:32477  a(OCoLC)1388204016  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.55.C52 aNIH Pragmatic Trials Collaboratory.bAnnual Workshopd(2022 :cOnline)eauthor.10aCritical questions for pragmatic clinical trialists :binsights from the NIH Pragmatic Trials Collaboratory's first decade /cNational Institutes of Health. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (7 hr., 18 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 15 & 16, 2022.3 a(CIT): This workshop, which will take place on 2 half-days, will explore questions that are critical to designing and implementing pragmatic clinical trials (PCTs) conducted within health care systems. Pragmatic trials differ from traditional more-explanatory clinical trials, as they test interventions or practices delivered in real-world settings. The NIH Pragmatic Trials Collaboratory has now been supporting PCTs for a decade, including a set of trials in the PRISM (Pragmatic and Implementation Studies for the Management of Pain to Reduce Opioid Prescribing) program of the Helping to End Addiction Long-term(r) Initiative, or NIH HEAL Initiative(r). This workshop is an opportunity to glean the most salient lessons learned from an experienced group of PCT investigators who will openly share the challenges they have encountered, solutions they have developed, and thoughts for the future.  The NIH Pragmatic Trials Collaboratory has launched 22 PCTs that are conducted within a variety of U.S. health care systems--with over 1,000 clinical sites. These trials have served as case studies for addressing challenges of pragmatic research, and the program has developed an extensive set of methods and best practices for PCTs. As the NIH portfolio of PCTs expands, it is increasingly prudent to share knowledge about the keys to success for designing and implementing such trials within health care systems. Many research processes for PCTs have improved over the past decade due to strategies developed by trailblazing PCT investigators and their collaborators in the NIH Pragmatic Trials Collaboratory. Sharing insights from a broad spectrum of PCTs represented by this group can help continue the research transformation.  The workshop will include a series of moderated discussions that focus on critical questions for the design and conduct of successful PCTs. Case examples from the NIH Pragmatic Trials Collaboratory and the PRISM program will be shared to illustrate lessons learned. Topics will include changes and current best practices regarding the use of electronic health records for pragmatic research, considerations for PCT study design, how to optimize a 1-year planning phase for a PCT, and tips for implementing interventions in complex health systems. Perspectives and experiences shared by the NIH Pragmatic Trials Collaboratory investigators may be used to bolster the success of future PCTs, which can address improving care delivered to underserved populations, addressing health disparities in clinical care, and achieving health equity by implementing evidence-based care.For more information go to <a href="https://rethinkingclinicaltrials.org/">https://rethinkingclinicaltrials.org/.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 23, 2023).22aNIH Pragmatic Trials Collaboratory.12aPragmatic Clinical Trials as Topic 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4550440uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45506  aAUTH01648cgm a22004454a 101267504DNLM20260227145153.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu053|||||||f||||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10128  a(OCoLC)70663574  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4702 aNIH Research Festivald(2001 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNIH Research Festival 2001.pKeynote address "Why is sequence comparison useful?" /cDavid Lipmann ; Office of the Director, NIH.30aKeynote address "why is sequence comparison useful?"30aWhy is sequence comparison useful?  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 17, 2006).  aStreaming video (53 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 2, 2001.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.22aHuman Genome Project.12aGenome, Human22aChromosome Mapping 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aLipman, Davidq(David J.)2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of the Director.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10128  aAUTH01503cgm a22004334a 101267361DNLM20260227144942.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu018|||||||f||||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:9953  a(OCoLC)176924670  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 901 aLands, Bill.10aEssential fat in foods.n1,pOverview of essential fatty acids in health and disease /cBill Lands.30aOverview of esse