166278446  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 1021 aArmstrong, Clay M.14aThe body electric :btwo centuries of progress /cClay Armstrong.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series. Robert Whitney Newcomb memorial lecture  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Aug. 7, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 7 min., 3 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 25, 2005.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNervous System Physiological Phenomena22aElectrophysiologyxhistory 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series.pRobert Whitney Newcomb memorial lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12590  aAUTH01941cgm a22004454a 101267608DNLM20260227145639.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu063 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10461  a(OCoLC)64443999  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 1721 aSpiegel, David,d1945-10aHypnosis and group support in medical care :baltering perception and reality /cDavid Spiegel.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 15, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 3 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 6, 2003.3 a(CIT): Distinguished Lectures in the Science of CAM  Dr. Spiegel is a world-renowned authority on mind/body interactions in medicine. His groundbreaking research, supported by rigorous clinical trials, provides a link between psychotherapeutic interventions and changes in brain function, emotional adjustment, and disease outcome that could alter the standard of care for those with medical illness such as chronic pain and cancer.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aStress, Psychologicalxtherapy22aHypnosis22aNeoplasmsxpsychology22aPainxpsychology22aPsychotherapy, Group 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10461  aAUTH02815cgm a2200445 i 9918503180706676DNLM20260227162711.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u221105s2022    mdu649|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32649  a(DNLM)CIT:32656  a(OCoLC)1407098344  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWW 1052 aNeural Foundations of Vision and Cognition: a Symposium in Honor of Dr. Leslie G. Ungerleiderd(2022 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aNeural foundations of vision and cognition :ba symposium in honor of Dr. Leslie G. Ungerleider /cLab of Brain and Cognition, NIMH, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (10 hr., 49 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 19 & 20, 2022.3 a(CIT): The Laboratory of Brain and Cognition of the NIMH Intramural Research Program is organizing a scientific symposium in honor of Leslie G. Ungerleider entitled "Neural Foundations of Vision and Cognition" to be held in-person at the NIH campus in Bethesda, MD, on Sept 19th and 20th, 2022. Dr. Ungerleider was a pioneering neuroscientist whose work spanning five decades, ranged from single cell recordings and anatomical tracings in monkeys to PET and fMRI studies in humans, from sensory processing in V1 to attentional modulation in prefrontal and parietal cortex, and from low-level visual perceptual learning to high-level social cognition. This symposium will celebrate Dr. Ungerleider's sizeable contributions to neuroscience by featuring talks that will highlight her immense scientific impact as well as her outstanding mentoring of hundreds of accomplished scientists. This event will be a gathering of Leslie's colleagues, collaborators, and mentees from around the world to celebrate her contributions to neuroscience over the past 50 years.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed October 10, 2023).12aUngerleider, Leslie G.12aVisual Perceptionxphysiology22aCognitionxphysiology 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aUngerleider, Leslie G.,ehonouree.2 aNational Institute of Mental Health (U.S.).bLaboratory of Brain and Cognition,eorganizer.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4604640uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=46048  aAUTH01565cgm a22004694a 101267628DNLM20260227145701.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s1999    mdu068 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10487  a(OCoLC)68980172  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWV 4401 aMurray, Jeffrey C.10aInternational studies of craniofacial anomalies /cJeffrey Murray.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c1999]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aSeymour J. Kreshover lecture  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 3, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 7 min., 43 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 24, 1999.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This videocast is the work of the United States Government and no copyright exists.  This may be disseminated freely.12aCleft Lipxgenetics22aChild22aCleft Lipxetiology22aCleft Palatexetiology22aCleft Palatexgenetics22aInfant 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aSeymour J. Kreshover lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10487  aAUTH02307cgm a22004334a 101493100DNLM20260227152748.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090106s2008    mdu075|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14811  a(OCoLC)319068802  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 3501 aMarto, Jarrod A.10aQuantitative proteomics analysis of aberrant molecular events in acute myeloid leukemia /cJarrod A. Marto.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Apr. 6, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 15 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 4, 2008.3 a(CIT): Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) remains a highly lethal malignancy with limited therapeutic options. Full transformation in AML requires coupling of aberrant molecular events associated with multiple cellular processes. For example, mutation of the FLT-3 receptor tyrosine kinase is frequently observed in AML and drives abnormal proliferation. Signaling from oncogenic FLT-3 phosphorylates the transcription factor C/EBP [alpha] and leads to disrupted myeloid differentiation. We are using iTRAQ-based quantitative proteomics approaches to explore divergent signaling downstream of two common FLT-3 mutants, and to monitor remodeling of C/EBP [alpha] protein complexes between transcriptionally competent and repressed states. Representative proteomics data and biochemical results will be described, in addition to and methodological improvements in proteomics-related technologies.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aLeukemia, Myeloid, Acutexgenetics22afms-Like Tyrosine Kinase 3xgenetics22aModels, Molecular22aProteomicsxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bProteomics Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14811  aAUTH01061cas a2200385 a 101285072DNLM20260209074452.0cr|cn#||||||||050513c20059999enk|| p       0    0eng    a  2016243129  a1746-6814l1746-6814  a(OCoLC)60376316  aEQOcEQOdDNLM  anlmcopycapcc  9England00aW1bWO93800aWounds UK.  aAberdeen :bWounds UK,c2005-31aLondon :bOmniaMed Communications Ltd.  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (May 2005)-  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 1, issue 3 (Nov. 2005).  aVol. 1, no. 4 never published.  aAlso issued online.12aWounds and Injuries22aWound Healing 2aPeriodical2 aWounds UK (Firm)4 uhttps://wounds-uk.com/journal/  aAUTH01817cgm a22004214a 101298611DNLM20260227151817.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070201s2007    mdu085 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13568  a(OCoLC)85817823  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 2951 aFerguson, Charles D.10aUnderstanding nuclear and radiological terrorism /cCharles Ferguson.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 12, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 24 min., 10 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 4, 2007.3 a(CIT): Dr. Ferguson is an internationally recognized authority on nuclear nonproliferation and security. At the Council on Foreign Relations, he focuses on issues involving Iran and North Korea, nuclear energy, and the prevention of nuclear and radiological terrorism. He recently co-authored the book The Four Faces of Nuclear Terrorism (Routledge, 2005) and was lead author of the report Commercial Radioactive Sources: Surveying the Security Risks, which assessed the threat of radiological dispersal devices such as dirty bombs.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aTerrorism22aNuclear Warfare22aRadiation Injuries 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13568  aAUTH00881nam a2200253 i 4500001001700000003000500017005001700022008004100039020002900080040002