b. 24, 2004.3 a(CIT): "Demystifying Medicine" is a weekly course, primarily for Ph.D. students, post-doctoral fellows, and staff at the National Institutes of Health. The course is led by Dr. Win Arias, Professor of Physiology and Medicine at Tufts School of Medicine in Boston and NIH Adjunct Investigator and Special Assistant in the Office of Intramural Research.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aMultiple Sclerosis 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11838  aAUTH01478cas a2200481 a 9705200DNLM20260227091042.0cr|cnu||||||||970313c19979999sz  x p       0    0eng    asn 97047243   a1420-8008l1420-8008  aDGCDFX  a(DNLM)SR0090847(s)  a(OCoLC)36249364  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Switzerland00aW1bDE13310aDement Geriatr Cogn Disord2dnlm00aDementia and geriatric cognitive disorders.  aBasel ;aNew York :bKarger,cc1997-  av. :bill.  a8 no. a year,b2002-  aBimonthly,b1997-2001  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 a8/1/97 (Jan.-Feb. 1997)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv8n1,Jan.-Feb. 1997-0 aPubMedbv8n1,Jan.-Feb. 1997-1 aIndex medicus  aCalled also v. 8, no. 1- .  aAlso issued online.  3Vol. 8, no. 1 (JAN-FEB 1997)c19970304fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper12aCognition Disorders22aAged22aDementia 2aPeriodical0 x1421-982400tDementia (Basel, Switzerland)x1013-7424w(DLC)sn 90031267w(OCoLC)221549434 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/224226  aAUTH01567cgm a22004334a 101267574DNLM20260227145253.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu050 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10391  a(OCoLC)70217851  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 84 AM51 aGaston, Marilyn H.12aA conversation with Marilyn Gaston, M.D., assistant surgeon general, associate administrator, director, Bureau of Primary Health Care.1 iTitle on screen banner:aConversation with Dr. Marilyn Gaston, M.D  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed June 25, 2006).  aA Michigan PCA/PCO Teleconference.  aStreaming video (50 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Aug. 1, 2000.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aDelivery of Health Care 2aMichigan 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10391  aAUTH02451cgm a22004694a 101268414DNLM20260227150556.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu061 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11944  a(OCoLC)84671286  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 1041 aChao, Moses V.10aUnique features of neurotrophin receptor signaling /cMoses V. Chao.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 27, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 59 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 29, 2004.3 a(CIT): The generation and differentiation of neurons and glial cells are dependent upon cell-cell interactions mediated by a wide variety of growth factors and cytokines. Dr. Chao's laboratory is interested in receptor-mediated mechanisms that direct cell differentiation versus cell proliferation in the nervous system. A major goal is to identify the biochemical steps that provide specificity in NGF signaling. Control of cell survival and death by neurotrophins is dictated by an unusual transduction system consisting of two transmembrane receptors, the TrkA tyrosine kinase and the p75 neurotrophin receptor, a member of the TNF receptor superfamily. The structural and biochemical features of these two receptors are being defined together with their intracellular signaling mechanisms.  For more information, visit  Moses Chao  NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aNerve Growth Factors22aReceptor Protein-Tyrosine Kinases22aReceptors, Nerve Growth Factor22aSignal Transduction 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11944  aAUTH01533cgm a22004454a 101267667DNLM20260227145358.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu054 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10535  a(OCoLC)67281683  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aHV 9100aCeremony to unveil the official portrait of Secretary Donna E. Shalala.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 30, 2006).  aStreaming video (53 min., 17 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 12, 2001.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aShalala, Donna E.12aUnited States.bDepartment of Health and Human Services12aFamous Persons12aGovernment22aAnniversaries and Special Events22aPortraits as Topic 2aUnited States 2aWebcast1 aShalala, Donna E.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10535  aAUTH01775cgm a2200