n of patients, pathology, diagnosis and therapy in the context of major disease problems and current research.  Primarily directed toward Ph.D. students, fellows, and staff, it is also of interest to medical students and clinicians.  The course is designed to help bridge the gap between advances in biology and their application to major human diseases.  Each session includes clinical and basic science components which are presented by NIH staff and outside invitees.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aFragile X Syndrome22aAutistic Disorder22aFragile X Messenger Ribonucleoprotein 122aTrinucleotide Repeat Expansion 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)02aUnderstanding the molecular basis of the FMR1 gene disorders.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15003  aAUTH01846cgm a22004334a 101268327DNLM20260227150443.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu067 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11752  a(OCoLC)71273657  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5681 aSakaguchi, Shimon.10aNaturally arising CD4+ regulatory T cells in immunologic self-tolerance and immune regulation /cShimon Sakaguchi.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Aug. 8, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 6 min., 14 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 17, 2003.3 a(CIT): The Immunology Interest Group (IIG) organizes activities designed to promote information exchange and interactions among NIH scientists interested in the field of immunology, broadly defined. Interactions are facilitated via weekly meetings on current topics as well as an annual Immunology Retreat.  For more information, visit The Immunology Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No c