sional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 7, 2007).  aStreaming video (54 min., 38 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 9, 2004.3 a(CIT): Go Local - A Project of MedlinePlus  Go Local is a pilot project to link MedlinePlus health topics to information about local health services.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aOnline Systems22aCommunity Health Services22aDatabases, Factual22aHealth Resources 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12067  aAUTH02837cgm a2200421 i 9918418883106676DNLM20260227162319.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220616s2022    mdu497|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32354  a(OCoLC)1368047130  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 1002 aAt the Crossroads of the Microbiome, Metabolism & Glycoscience (Symposium)d(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aAt the crossroads of the microbiome, metabolism & glycoscience /cNIH Common Fund Glycoscience Program.1 aAt the crossroads of the microbiome, metabolism and glycoscience 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (8 hr., 17 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 29, 2022.3 a(CIT): For the past decade, the topics of the microbiome and metabolomics have become vital to human biology and disease.  While the microbiota and metabolism can shape many biological processes, recent studies demonstrate that the ability of microbes to not only influence carbohydrate metabolism but also decorate themselves in diverse carbohydrate structures can significantly influence the consequences of host-microbial interactions.  However, unlike assays designed to interrogate microbial communities or examine metabolic changes in a high throughput manner, the tools needed to similarly dissect how microbial glycans influence host biology have been more difficult to develop. Recent investment from the NIH Common Fund (and related funding agencies) to address this issue has resulted in the development of next-generation tools designed to specifically evaluate complex carbohydrate structures and their functions, including the study of carbohydrates and related metabolites in host-microbiota interactions.  This symposium will provide examples of how the microbiome and metabolism influence a variety of critical biological processes, while highlighting how tools designed to study complex carbohydrates may be used to provide new insight into key aspects of host-micr 3c4.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed January 11, 2023).12aMycobiome22aGlycomics22aMetabolomics 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aCommon Fund Glycoscience Program,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45111  aAUTH01988cgmaa22004574a 101180541DNLM20260227144257.0vf|cbahouvf|czahmu030530s2003    mdu072       f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:1893  a(OCoLC)53954326  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQH 324.8bVC no.1 200304aThe Alpha project and the dream of a predictive biology /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  a1 videocassette of 1 (DVCpro) (72 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/4 in.3master.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (72 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aThe NIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture  aTitle from cassette spine label.0 aSpeaker, Roger Brent.  aOriginally presented Apr. 30, 2003.3 a(Producer) Under the Alpha project, we are striving to bring into being computational methods and experimental tools to gain the ability to predict the quantitative behavior of a well-understood and experimentally tractable intracellular signal transduction/information-processing network.  aVHS.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aModels, Biological22aComputer Simulation22aSignal Transduction22aSaccharomyces cerevisiae1 aBrent, Roger.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=1893  aAUTH01529nam a2200385 a 101776512DNLM20260227152414.0920106r18811881ohu      b    000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)25054160  aVXGbengcVXGdOCLCQdRBNdOCLCOdOCLCQdOCLCFdOCLCQdDNLM  ae------an-use--  9United States14aQK101b.J36 188100aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5539 no.21 aJames, Joseph F.q(Joseph Francis),d1857-1897.10aOn the geographical distribution of the indigenous plants of Europe and the northeast United States /cby Joseph F. James.  aCincinnati :bPrinted by James Barclay,c[1881?]  a17 pages ;c22 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  a"From the Journal of the Cincinnati Society of Natural History, April, 1881," p. 51-67.  aNLM copy, author's presentation copy.5DNLM  aIncludes bibliographical references.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM12aPlantsxclassification22aPhylogeography 2aEurope 2aNew England2 aCincinnati Society of Natural History.tJournal.  aAUTH01908cgmaa22005294a 101268040DNLM20260227150011.0m       