bB367c v.3 no.91 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aDelirium cordis :ba heart-to-heart talk on certain problems in present day cardiology in the light of ancient and recent history /cWilliam B. Bean.1 aHeart-to-heart talk on certain problems in present day cardiology in the light of ancient and recent history 1a[Chicago, Illinois] :b[publisher not identified],c[1954]  a13 pages :billustrations ;c25 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  a"This paper is derived from remarks made at the Annual Dinner of the Los Angeles County Heart Association, 20 October 1953 on the occasion of the 23rd Annual Symposium on Heart Disease, and at the Annual Banquet of the Iowa Heart Association and the Iowa Tuberculosis and Health Association in Des Moines, 14 April 1954."  a"Reprinted from Diseases of the chest, vol. XXVI, no. 4, October 1954."  aNLM copy, bound 9th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM12aHeart Diseasesxhistory0 aDiseases of the chest.  aAUTH01849cgm a22004454a 101493067DNLM20260227152741.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090106s2008    mdu067|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14797  a(OCoLC)319068798  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2001 aKramer, Barnett S.,d1948-10aCancer screening :bthe clash of medical science and intuition /cBarnett S. Kramer.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aMedicine for the public lecture series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Apr. 6, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 7 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 25, 2008.3 a(CIT): The notion that early detection saves lives seems a simple and obvious truth, but many generally-accepted cancer-screening procedures have yet to be proven effective, and all have associated harms that are often overlooked. Dr. Barnett Kramer will address what it means for a screening test to be considered effective and differentiate what is known from what makes intuitive sense in the area of cancer screening.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxprevention & control22aMass Screening22aPrecancerous Conditionsxdiagnosis 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aMedicine for the public40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14797  aAUTH02319cgm a2200433 i 101773035DNLM20260227160326.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201105s2020    mdu056|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30333  a(OCoLC)1224228470  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 21700aDynamic genome rearrangements in the ciliate Oxytricha /cLaura Landweber. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (56 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNLM Ada Lovelace computational health lecture  aRecorded October 13, 2020.3 a(CIT): The ciliate Oxytricha trifallax possesses a complex pair of genomes, and massive DNA rearrangements produce a highly fragmented but functional somatic macronucleus from a complex germline micronucleus. This process eliminates nearly all noncoding DNA, including transposons, and rearranges over 225,000 short DNA segments to produce a second genome containing thousands of gene-sized "nanochromosomes". The mature, somatic genome contains over 17,000 nanochromosomes. Noncoding RNAs regulate the entire process of genome rearrangement. Millions of parental 27nt small RNAs provide the critical information to mark and protect the retained DNA segments of the genome. These and other processes illustrate the multifaceted ability of noncoding RNAs to transmit heritable information to the next generation.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 23, 2020).12aOxytrichaxgenetics22aGenome, Protozoanxgenetics22aGenomics 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aLandweber, Laura F.q(Laura Faye),d1967-espeaker.2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aAda Lovelace computational health lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38902  aAUTH00857cam a22002777i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002100073040003500094044001000129060003200139245006200171250001400233264007500247300003000322336002600352337002800378338002700406490003000433650001900463655001700482700004000499830003100539999000900570101611048DNLM20260209115119.0961212s1951    cc a          000 0 chi    a(OCoLC)893183326  aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1777 no.500aEr bi yan hou wei sheng /cLuo Zhaoping, Shao Houze bian.  aDi 1 ban. 1aBeijing :bRen min wei sheng chu ban she,c1951 nian 6 yue [June 1951]  a34 pages :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aWei sheng chang shi du wu12aOtolaryngology 2aPopular Work1 aLuo, Zhaoping,d1920-1999,eeditor. 0aWei sheng chang shi du wu.  aAUTH02115cgm a2200409 i 101759039DNLM20260227160041.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u191210s2019    mdu307|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:28872  a(OCoLC)1137744800  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWG 141.5.A32 aNHLBI Coronary CT Angiography (CTA) Summitd(2019 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aNHLBI Coronary CT Angiography (CTA) Summit, 2019 /cNHLBI, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (5 hr., 7 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 14, 2019.3 a(CIT): The NHLBI DIR is hosting the Coronary CT Angiography (CTA) Summit on Thursday, November 14th. The CTA Summit will focus on discussions around novel vascular disease outcomes derived from coronary CT angiography and effect of interventions on these vascular disease outcomes. By improving earlier detection of atherosclerosis, millions of lives can be improved from the burdens of heart disease and stroke, which directly aligns with the NIH mission "to seek knowledge about the nature and behavior of living systems and the application of that knowledge to enhance health, lengthen life, and reduce illness and disability."  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed January 15, 2020).12aCoronary Angiographyxmethods22aCoronary Artery Diseasexdiagnostic imaging22aTomography, X-Ray Computed 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?28872  aAUTH01639cgm a2200445 i 101647957DNLM20260227154846.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u141202s2014    mdu150|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18716  a(OCoLC)900260791  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.55.E71 aWertheimer, Alan,eauthor.10aEthical and regulatory aspects of clinical research.nSession 6,pEthics of international research /cAlan Wertheimer, Joe Millum, Seema Shah.1 aEthics of international research 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 30 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.1 aExploitation in clinical research / Alan Wertheimer -- Post-trial obligations & responsible availability / Seema Shah  aRecorded Nov. 5, 2014.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed Dec. 29, 2014.12aEthics, Research22aInternationality 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aMillum, Joseph,eauthor.1 aShah, Seema,eauthor.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18716  aAUTH01233cam a22003131  450000100100000000300050001000500170001500700140003200800410004601000170008703500200010404000210012404400180014506000210016310000370018424501140022126000480033530000280038333600260041133700280043733800270046550000570049253301100054954001420065958300390080158300430084069000270088399900090091012210230RDNLM20260206140956.0hd|bfa012baap810725s1926    mou||||  |||| 00||0|eng    a   26012477   a(OCoLC)14745441  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aQY 25bK48m 19261 aKilduffe, Robert Anthony,d1884-12aA manual of clinical laboratory procedure for the use of the general practitioner,cby Robert A. Kilduffe ...  aSt. Louis,bThe C. V. Mosby company,c1926.  a3 p. l., 9-287 p.bill.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aNLM microfilm copy shelved as: Film 10248 no.55DNLM  aMicrofilm.b[Bethesda, Md.] :cNational Library of Medicine,d2000.e1 microfilm reel : negative ; 35 mm.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202602062pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202602065DNLM  aDiagnosis / Laboratory  aAUTH03402cgm a2200493 i 101753638DNLM20260227160035.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190904s2019    mdu308|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:28691  a(OCoLC)1124959726  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 26.52 aNIH OBSSR Methodology Seminar "Text Mining for Behavioral and Social Sciences Research"d(2019 :cRockville, Md.)jauthor.10aText mining for behavioral and social sciences research /cBrendan O'Connor, David Broniatowski, and Quynh Nguyen. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (5 hr., 8 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aTutorial on natural language processing for social science investigation / Brendan O'Connor --  Leveraging social media for neighborhood research to understand the potential influence of social environments of health / Quynh Nguyen -- Infectious disease and behavioral epidemiology on Twitter : from disease to disinfomation / David Broniatowski.  aRecorded August 9, 2019.3 a(CIT): NIH OBSSR Methodology Seminar: The age of Big Data has ushered in a wave of high-volume digital information and much of it is text based. Text mining is a form of data mining that involves collecting and analyzing large volumes of textual data to reveal patterns and relationships. Techniques for mining can be used to extract key concepts, spot trends, summarize content from documents and gain semantic understanding, and index and search text for use in predictive analytics. Text mining has become an important research process with many different commercial and academic applications, and it is becoming more widely applied in social science studies. This one-day methodology seminar sponsored by NIH OBSSR will present a basic introductory overview of principles and techniques of text mining for behavioral and social research and showcase some innovative health research examples.  Attendees of this seminar will gain a broad understanding and appreciation for some of the different tools available to collect and analyze large volumes of text data, and they will learn about some notable example applications in behavioral and social sciences health research.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed October 15, 2019).12aBehavioral Research12aData Miningxmethods12aSocial Sciences22aBig Data22aNatural Language Processing 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aO'Connor, Brendan,d1978-espeaker.1 aBroniatowski, David,d1982-espeaker.1 aNguyen, Quynhq(Quynh Camthi),espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?28691  aAUTH01515cgm a22004454a 101268068DNLM20260227150041.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu067 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11333  a(OCoLC)70256492  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5681 aAliberti, Julio.10aMolecular mimicry of a CCR5 binding domain in the microbial activation of dendritic cells /cJulio Aliberti.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed June 20, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 6 min., 33 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 23, 2003.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aDendritic Cells22aMolecular Mimicry22aReceptors, CCR522aSignal Transduction22aToxoplasma 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11333  aAUTH01751cas a2200481 i 101650225DNLM20260227090920.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||141106c20149999enk|| poo     0    0eng    a  2016243036  a2352-0477l2352-0477  a(OCoLC)895357656  aCNCGMbengerdad20260227cCNCGMdDNLM  apcc  9England00aW110aEur J Radiol Open2dnlm00aEuropean journal of radiology open.1 aEJR open3 aRadiology Open 1a[Oxford] :bElsevier Ltd.,c[2014]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with volume 1 (2014)