M)C27680000(s)  a(OCoLC)01554954  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Netherlands00aW1bCL9330 aClio medica (Amsterdam, Netherlands)10aClio Med2dnlm 0aClio medica10aClio medica :bacta Academiae Internationalis Historiae Medicinae.30aActa Academiae Internationalis Historiae Medicinae  aAmsterdam :bB.M. Israël,3 aAmsterdam :bEditions Rodopi B.V  av. :bill.  aIrregular  aFour no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan in Nov. 1965.1 a1993-   The Wellcome Institute series in the history of medicine  aPublished: Oxford ; New York : Pergamon, 1965-   ; Amsterdam : B.M. Isräel, 1971?-82?; Amsterdam ; Atlanta : Rodopi, 1983?- .  aDescription based on: Vol. 17, no. 4 (Mar. 1983); title from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv6n1, Apr. 1971-v96, 20160 aPubMedbv6n1, Apr. 1971-v96, 20164 aGarrison-Morton (5th ed.)c66561 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aText in English, French, German, or Sanskrit. 2aHistory of Medicine 2aPeriodical2 aInternational Academy of the History of Medicine.0 x1875-6689 0aWellcome Institute series in the history of medicine4 uhttps://brill.com/abstract/serial/CLIO?rskey=3Oo4ZD&result=1  aHISRELb19991201  aAUTH00940cam a22002777i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002000073040003500093044001200128060003200140100003700172245006600209264005100275300003200326336002600358337002800384338002700412500001700439500012200456504004100578650001400619651002000633999000900653101689800DNLM20260211081717.0160803r18911891au b     b    000 0 ger    a(OCoLC)77581869  aDNLMbengerdad20260211dDNLM  9Austria00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 2137 no.41 aToldt, Carl,d1840-1920eauthor.14aDie Körpergrösse der Tiroler und Vorarlberger/cC. Toldt. 1aWien :bAnthropologischen Gesellschaft,c1891.  apages 69-78 :bmap ;c29 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCover title.  a"Separatabdruck aus Band XXI [der neuen Folge Band XI] der Mittheilungen der Anthropologischen Gesellschaft in Wien."  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aBody Size 2aAustria-Hungary  aAUTH03082cgm a2200457 i 101653995DNLM20260227154812.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150314s2015    mdu072|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18829  a(OCoLC)906119354  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 102.51 aFishell, Gord,eauthor.14aThe generation of cortical interneuron diversity :bboth nurture and nature /cGordon Fishell. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 12 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded February 2, 2015.3 a(CIT): The Fishell laboratory is focused on how the diversity of local circuit GABAergic inhibitory interneurons is created. Understanding how this is accomplished during development remains one of the most daunting problems in biology.  In particular, they wish to understand not only how the vast variety of inhibitory interneuron subtypes are generated but how they subsequently integrate into the bewildering array of neural circuits that are embedded in different brain structures.  Their working hypothesis is that this is achieved through a two-step process.  The first involves genetic programs that in accordance with their birthdate create a finite number of cardinal interneuron subtypes (Research Topic 1 - The Embryonic Specification of Interneurons).  Following the tiling of these newly born cardinal subtypes across different brain structures, local cues act to create the definitive subtypes characteristic of the distinct cortical and subcortical areas (Research Topic 2 - Interneuron Integration and Synapse Formation).  Importantly, as they have explored the molecular control of these events, it has become clear that perturbation of this process can result in a variety of brain dysfunctions including autism spectrum disorder (ASD), intellectual disability (ID) and schizophrenia.  A new and growing interest in the laboratory is therefore aimed at seeing if better understanding of these developmental events can lead to the development of new treatments for these disorders (Research Topic 3 - Interneuron Function and Dysfunction).  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed March 31, 2015.12aInterneuronsxphysiology22aCerebral Cortex22aGenetic Variation22aInterneuronsxclassification 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18829  aAUTH01518cas a2200481 a 101254059DNLM20260227090923.0cr|cn ||||||||050729c20059999enkqr p       0    0eng    a  2005243784  a1745-3054l1745-3054  a(OCoLC)61253178  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9England00aW1bJO546T10aJ Vis Commun Med2dnlm00aJournal of visual communication in medicine.16aVisual communication in medicine  aAbingdon, Oxfordshire, UK :bTaylor & Francis,cc2005-3 aLondon :bInforma Healthcare  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 28, no. 1 (Mar. 2005)-  aTitle from cover.  aImprint varies.  aPrint issues ceased with v. 35 (2012).1 aMEDLINEbv28n1, Mar. 2005-1 aPubMedbv28n1, Mar. 2005-  aAlso issued online.  aOfficial journal of the Institute of Medical Illustrators.12aMedical Illustration22aPhotography 2aPeriodical2 aInstitute of Medical Illustrators.0 x1745-306200tJournal of audiovisual media in medicinex0140-511Xw(DLC)sc 79004749w(OCoLC)38799964 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/loi/ijau20  aAUTH00863cam a22002417i 450000100100000000300050001000500170001500800410003204000350007304400100010806000320011824500