arch X. 1a[London] :bElsevier Ltd.,c[2018]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1 (1 December 2018)0 aPubMedbv1, 2018-0 aPMC  aArticles published as they become available and compiled into volumes.  aOpen access mirror journal to: Water research.  aDescription based on: Volume 1 (1 December 2018); title from journal information screen (Science Direct, viewed January 26, 2019).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 4 (1 August 2019) (viewed September 9, 2019).12aWater Pollution22aWater Quality22aWater Purification 2aPeriodical08iComplemented by (work):tWater researchx0043-1354w(OCoLC)176949940uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=10174210940uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/water-research-x  aAUTH01448cas a2200457 a 9422792DNLM20260227091012.0cr|cn ||||||||940317c19949999sz br p       0    0eng    a1021-7762l1021-7762  aFPLOEP  a(DNLM)SR0077878(s)  a(OCoLC)29750095  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aengagerafre  9Switzerland00aW1bFO281M10aFolia Phoniatr Logop2dnlm00aFolia phoniatrica et logopaedica :bofficial organ of the International Association of Logopedics and Phoniatrics (IALP).  aBasel ;aNew York :bKarger,cc1994-  av. :bill.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 a46.1 (Jan.-Feb. 1994)-1 aMEDLINEb46.1, 1994-1 aPubMedb46.1, 1994-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso available online.  aIn English, French, and German.  3Vol. 46, no. 1 (1994)c19930922fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aSpeech Disorders 2aVoice Disorders 2aPeriodical2 aInternational Association of Logopedics and Phoniatrics.0 x1421-997200tFolia phoniatrica4 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/224177  aAUTH02780ngm a2200445 i 9918975957006676DNLM20260227165108.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u250104s2024    mdu068|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35438  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 16100aMechanisms of phage-derived protein antibiotics /cBil Clemons. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2024]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 8 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aWALS-NIH director's lecture  aRecorded December 4, 2024.3 a(CIT): Professor Bil Clemons received his PhD from the University of Utah working under Prof. Venki Ramakrishnan. During this time he spent two years as a visiting scientist at the Laboratory of Molecular Biology in Cambridge, England. The most notable achievement during his graduate work was that he was part of the team that solved the first atomic resolution structure of a small ribosomal subunit. This work led to a fundamental understanding of the translation of the genetic code and provided molecular details of the mechanism of a number of antibiotics.  LECTURE SUMMARY: As antimicrobial resistance continues to expand, new ideas are required to address the growing threat. Phages, viruses of bacteria, offer many lessons that can be leveraged as opportunities to combat bacteria. In this lecture, Prof. Clemons will describe how some phages have developed simple, protein-based mechanisms for breaking through the bacterial cell wall and killing their host. These protein antibiotics can work independent of the phage, yet for years their mechanisms have remained mysterious. The mechanisms for several of these will be revealed. From these discoveries, new biological insight and paths towards potential therapeutics will be shared.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 18, 2026).12aBacteriophages12aBacteriolysis22aBacterial Proteinsxmetabolism22aPhage Therapy22aAnti-Bacterial Agentsxmetabolism 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 