Wernicke Encephalopathy12aSteatorrhea12aLipoma.22aVascular Diseases2 aAmerican Medical Association.0 aArchives of internal medicine.  aAUTH00834nam a2200265 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400025000800440018001050600024001231000062001472450041002092640060002503000021003103360026003313370028003573380026003855000019004115000016004305010060004466500033005066500020005399990009005599918787482006676DNLM20260223085037.0240401s1957    xxu           000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.611 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aScurvy /cmethod of William B. Bean. 1a[United States] :b[publisher not identified],c[1957?]  a1 sheet ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  asheetbnb2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  a"Page 296."  aNLM copy, bound 61st in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM12aScurvyxprevention & control12aScurvyxtherapy  aAUTH03658cgm a22004454a 101531563DNLM20260227153616.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100519s2010    mdu579 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15825  a(DNLM)CIT:15826  a(OCoLC)648079410  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWJ 30000aMYH9 and kidney disease /cNIDDK.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (9 hr., 39 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 19 & 20, 2010.3 a(CIT): In 2008, two teams of researchers identified variations in a single gene, MYH9, that are strongly associated with kidney diseases disproportionately affecting African Americans.  MYH9 risk variants account for nearly all of the increased risk for idiopathic focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS) and HIV-associated FSGS among African Americans compared to European Americans and a portion of the increased risk for hypertensive kidney disease. Surprisingly, however, these variants were not associated with kidney failure arising from diabetes.    The National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases (NIDDK) will convene a conference entitled MYH9 and Kidney Disease:  Clinical and Public Health Implications of Recent Genetic Findings in Populations on April 19-20, 2010, in Bethesda, MD, in partnership with the National Center on Minority Health and Health Disparities (NCMHD), National Human Genome Research Institute (NHGRI), National Heart, Lung and Blood Institute (NHLBI), National Cancer Institute (NCI), Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), and Office of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research (OBSSR).  The conference will review genetic, public health, and ethical and social implications of these new findings, and conference participants will provide feedback to the National Institutes of Health (NIH) regarding future research directions.   The conference will focus on:  (1) possible further basic science and genetic studies; (2) public health approaches; and (3) important questions regarding screening and social and ethical ramifications of the findings and public health recommendations in the U.S. population.  Goals of the conference are to: .Review the current state of knowledge related to the clinical utility, public health implications, and future research needs after identification of MYH9 loci as a factor associated with genetic susceptibility to non-diabetic kidney disease, including hypertensive nephrosclerosis, focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, and HIV-associated nephropathy;  .Stimulate research and clinical application in this area;  .Foster the development of multi-specialty collaborations; and Provide recommendations to NIDDK for future research in advancing this field.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aKidney Diseasesxgenetics22aBlack or African American22aMolecular Motor Proteinsxgenetics 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1582540uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15826  aAUTH03017cgm a2200385 i 9918418882006676DNLM20260227162347.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220616s2022    mdu217|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32372  a(OCoLC)1350873147  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States2 aMoving Beyond BMI: Exploring the Heterogeneity of Obesity (Symposium)d(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aMoving beyond BMI :bexploring the heterogeneity of obesity /cObesity Research Task Force, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (3 hr., 37 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded May 5, 2022.3 a(CIT): Obesity is a chronic disease with deleterious effects on health and well-being, but BMI is an imperfect proxy for health risk.  This symposium, hosted by the NIH Obesity Research Task Force and presented by renowned extramural investigators, will explore the heterogeneity of obesity, including risk for its development and complications, pathophysiology, and response to treatment, as well as implications for implementing obesity prevention and treatments.    "The Role of Adipocytes and Adipose Tissue Metabolism in the Heterogeneity of Obesity" Philipp E. Scherer, Ph.D. Touchstone Diabetes Center The University of Texas Southwestern Medical Center  "Metabolically Healthy and Unhealthy Obesity" Samuel Klein, M.D. Center for Human Nutrition Washington University School of Medicine   "Metabolically Healthy Obesity and Cardiovascular Disease Risk:  Fact or Fiction?" Yvonne Commodore-Mensah, Ph.D., MHN, RN Johns Hopkins School of Nursing Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health  "Genetic Subclassification of Obesity and its Role in Precision Health" Ruth Loos, Ph.D. Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Basic Metabolic Research, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences University of Copenhagen  "Heterogeneity of Obesity in Children from Diverse and Immigrant/Refugee Households:  Using Machine Learning to Inform Intervention Targets" Jerica M. Berge, Ph.D., MPH, LMFT, CFLE Department of Family Medicine and Community Health University of Minnesota Medical School and Allan Tate, Ph.D. Department of Epidemiology and Biostatistics University of Georgia.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed October 27, 2022 ).12aObesity22aBody Mass Index 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bObesity Research Task Force,ehost institution.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44967  aAUTH01828cgm a2200481 i 101609765DNLM20260227154647.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130604s2013    mdu053|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17917  a(OCoLC)851144989  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 10200aMaladaptive cortical plasticity :bimpact of amputation and vision loss /cChristopher I. Baker. Neural circuits mediating unconscious vision in blind sight / David Leopold.1 aNeural circuits mediating unconscious vision in blind sight 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (53 min.) :bcolor, sound).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdm2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCC grand rounds  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Apr. 24, 2013.  aDescription based on version viewed June 17, 2013.12aNeuronal Plasticityxphysiology12aVisual Perception22aAmputation, Surgical.22aBlindnessxphysiopathology22aBrain Mapping22aSensory Deprivationxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast12aBaker, Christopher I.tMaladaptive cortical plasticity.12aLeopold, Davidq(David A.).tNeural circuits mediating unconscious vision in blind sight.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17917  aAUTH02528cgm a2200445 i 101645874DNLM20260227154745.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u141108s2014    mdu182|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18656  a(OCoLC)898053408  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.55.E71 aGrady, Christine,eauthor.10aEthical and regulatory aspects of clinical research.nSession 1,pHistory, guidance, and framework for ethical clinical research /cChristine Grady, Robert Turell, Jeffrey R. Botkin.1 aHistory, guidance, and framework for ethical clinical research 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (3 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded October 1, 2014.3 a(CIT): The Department of Bioethics offers this seven to eight week course annually each fall. The course is designed to provide an overview of the important issues in the ethics of human subject research for clinical investigators and others who participate in the conduct of research and is open to the entire NIH community as well as to those from outside NIH. Topics include the history of human subject research ethics, principles and guidelines, study design, subject recruitment, informed consent, and international research. The course is open to the entire NIH community as well as to those from outside NIH. The recommended textbook is Ethical and Regulatory Aspects of Clinical Research, edited by Emanuel et al (Johns Hopkins University Press). The course is taught by guest faculty and faculty members from the National Institutes of Health. This is a required academic program for Bioethics fellows.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed November 26, 2014.12aBiomedical Researchxethics12aEthics, Research 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aTurell, Robert,eauthor.1 aBotkin, Jeffrey R.,eauthor.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18656  aAUTH02428cgm a2200457 i 101776739DNLM20260227160815.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210302s2021    mdu177|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31554  a(OCoLC)1249794598  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWS 12500aBreastmilk Ecology: Genesis of Infant Nutrition (BEGIN) meeting series.nWorking group 5,pTranslation and integration /cNICHD, NIH.1 aTranslation and integration 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 57 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded February 12, 2021.3 a(CIT): A deeper understanding of the biology of human milk is essential to address ongoing and emerging questions about infant feeding practices. Human milk is a complex biological system, a matrix of many interacting parts, that is more than the sum of those parts. Its production needs to be studied as an ecology that consists of inputs from the mother, her breastfeeding baby, and their respective environments. The Breastmilk Ecology: Genesis of Infant Nutrition (BEGIN) meeting series is designed to examine this ecology, its functional implications for both mother and infant. The goal is to support the research community's efforts to ensure safe, efficacious, and context-specific infant feeding practices in the United States and globally.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 30, 2021).22aBEGIN (Collaborative Group)12aMilk, Humanxphysiology22aBreast Feeding22aEcological and Environmental Phenomena22aInfant Nutritional Physiological Phenomena22aResearch Design22aTranslational Research, Biomedical. 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40154  aAUTH01114cas a2200397 a 101294546DNLM20260227090914.0cr |||||||||||061204c20069999enkqr p       0    0eng    a  2008243556  a1477-7509l1477-7509  a(OCoLC)225054415  aAUcAUdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9England00aW1bCL696EJ10aClin Ethics2dnlm00aClinical ethics.  aLondon :bRoyal Society of Medicine Press,c2006-3 3Dec. 2012- :aLondon :bSage  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Mar. 2006)-  aTitle from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 3, no. 1 (Mar. 2008).0 aMEDLINEbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.12aEthics, Clinical 2aPeriodical0 x1758-101X4 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/cet  aAUTH01575cas a2200445 i 101774590DNLM20260224111543.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||200329c20199999gr || poo     |    0eng    a  20212295647 a3009895-62DE-600  a1155135171a1157179408a1162392779  a2654-1459l2654-1459  z(OCoLC)1155135171z(OCoLC)1157179408z(OCoLC)1162392779  a(OCoLC)1144267539z(OCoLC)1155135171z(OCoLC)1157179408z(OCoLC)1162392779  aSFBbengerdad20260224cSFBdOCLCQdAUDdBRXdVLYdDNLM  apcc  9Greece00aW110aPopul Med2dnlm00aPopulation medicine. 1aCrete, Greece :bEuropean Publishing,c[2019]-  a1 online resource  aMonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1 (November 2019).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aArticles published as they become available and compiled into monthly issues.0 aVolume 1 (November 2019); title from journal homepage (publisher's website, viewed February 2, 2021).1 aVolume 3 (January 2021) (viewed February 2, 2021).12aPopulation Health22aDelivery of Health Care22aPublic Health Systems Research 2aPeriodical40uhttps://www.populationmedicine.eu/Archive  aAUTH01145cas a22003974a 101150318DNLM20260227090921.0cr|cn ||||||||020612c20039999nyub| p       0    0eng    a  20022136170 a1540-4196l1540-4196  a(OCoLC)49981419  aNSDcNSDdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bME961O10aMetab Syndr Relat Disord2dnlm00aMetabolic syndrome and related disorders.  aLarchmont, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.,cc2003-  av. :bill.  aSix no. a year,b2009-  aQuarterly,b2003-2008  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (spring 2003)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv6n1, spring 2008-0 aPubMedbv6n1, spring 2008-  aAlso issued online.12aMetabolic Syndrome 2aPeriodical0 x1557-85184 uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/met  aAUTH01922cgm a22004214a 101308559DNLM20260227151826.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070605s2007    mdu225|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13669  a(OCoLC)144568165  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWT 10400aAging well :bit's better than the alternatives /cStaff Training in Extramural Programs.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner.  aStreaming video (3 hr., 45 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS and NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Mar. 6, 2003 a(CIT): Science for All  We are all getting older.  The question is...How do we best play the cards that we are dealt? What is more influential in determining how we age - our bodies' physiological and structural changes or how we view ourselves and our quality of life?  Need we assume that chronic physical or mental impairments will negatively affect our future or can we actively improve our prospects for healthy living even as we age.  Come join us for a discussion about current, research-based thinking on aging and strategies for health and happiness as we get older.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aAging22aAdaptation, Psychological22aHealth Behavior 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bStaff Training-Extramural Programs Committee.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13669  aAUTH02865cgm a2200457 i 101776726DNLM20260227160745.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210302s2021    mdu075|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31536  a(OCoLC)1246807992  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 26.55.I400aWhere clouds meet the ground :bdemocratizing health data to address community health equity /cL. Ebony Boulware. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 15 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIMHD director's seminar  aRecorded February 4, 2021.3 a(CIT): NIMHD will host Dr. L. Ebony Boulware as the next NIMHD Director's Seminar Series titled "Where Clouds Meet the Ground: Democratizing Health Data to Address Community Health Equity."  Dr. Boulware is the Eleanor Easley Chair at the School of Medicine and the chief of the Division of General Internal Medicine at the Department of Medicine at Duke University. As a general internist and clinical epidemiologist, Dr. Boulware's research empowers families and doctors with knowledge and tools to make healthy decisions and better manage chronic diseases.Dr. Boulware will - Review challenges in translating research and health learning into community health improvement.- Describe efforts to inform community health equity through data democratization efforts.- Consider next steps in research. Dr. Boulware is the Chief of the Division of General Internal Medicine in the Department of Medicine and the Director of the Duke Clinical and Translational Science Institute. She has devoted her career to studying the mechanisms to improve the quality and equity of health care, and health outcomes for patients and populations with chronic diseases, such as hypertension and kidney disease. Her work investigates the influence of attitudinal, social, and environmental contexts on health and health outcomes.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 20, 2021).12aCommunity Networks22aHealth Equity22aCommunity Health Services22aDemocracy 2aUnited States 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aBoulware, L. Ebony,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIMHD director's seminar40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41449  aAUTH01701cas a2200505 a 9809909DNLM20260227091038.0cr|cn ||||||||980625c19989999nyubr p       0    0eng    a   98657867 zsn 98000559  a1099-3460l1099-3460  aJUHEFL  a(DNLM)SR0093306(s)  a(OCoLC)39047564  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcdansdp  9United States00aW1bBU87410aJ Urban Health2dnlm00aJournal of urban health :bbulletin of the New York Academy of Medicine.  aCary, NC :bOxford University Press for the New York Academy of Medicine,cc1998-3 32006- :aNew York, NY :bSpringer  av. :bill.  aBimonthly,b2006-  aQuarterly,b1998-2005  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 75, issue 1 (Mar. 1998)-1 aMEDLINEbv75n1,Mar. 1998-0 aPubMedbv75n1,Mar. 1998-0 aPMC1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3Vol. 75, no. 1 (MAR 1998)c19980622fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper that meets the full ANSI standard for Permanent Paper02aMedicine02aUrban Health 2aPeriodical2 aNew York Academy of Medicine.0 x1468-286900tBulletin of the New York Academy of Medicinex0028-7091w(DLC)   080141154 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/115244 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=9809909  aAUTH01587cas a2200469 i 101674442DNLM20260227090919.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||151124c20159999enk|| poo     0    0eng    a  2015247835  a2055-0936l2055-0936  a(OCoLC)930268999  aOH1bengerdad20260227cOH1dOCLCOdPFMdOCLCOdDNLM  apcc  9England 4aRC96000aW110aTrop Dis Travel Med Vaccines2dnlm1 aTrop. dis. travel med. vaccines 0aTropical diseases, travel medicine and vaccines00aTropical diseases, travel medicine and vaccines. 1a[London] :bBioMed Central,c[2015]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with (2015)1.0 aPubMedbv1, 2015-0 aPMC  aArticles published continuously and compiled into annual volumes.  aDescription based on: (2015) 1; title from journal homepage (BioMed Central, viewed December 11, 2015).  aLatest issue consulted: (2016) 2 (viewed Sept. 6, 2016).12aCommunicable Diseases12aTravel12aTropical Medicine 2aPeriodical40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=10167444240uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/4079440uhttp://tdtmvjournal.biomedcentral.com/  aAUTH02152cgm a2200457 a 101565230DNLM20260227154235.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110715s2011    mdu065 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16725  a(OCoLC)752782874  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 1021 aManzoni, Olivier.14aThe perisynaptic endocannabinoid signalosome :bmechanisms and disease /cOlivier Manzoni.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 5 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded June 20, 2011.3 a(CIT): Neuroscience Seminar Series  The goal of Dr. Manzoni"s lab is to understand the molecular and cellular basis of synaptic plasticity, a fundamental property of neuronal networks. Specifically, his research has investigated the role of the endocannobinoid and opioid system in synaptic plasticity and disease states in the meso-cortical-limbic circuit. Dr. Manzoni is Director of the "Physiology of Synaptic Transmission" team that was first established at University of Bordeaux in 2002 and it has recently moved to INMED, Marseille. In 2010, Dr. Manzoni received a NARSAD Independent Investigator Award.     For more information, visit: http://neuroseries.info.nih.gov.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeuronal Plasticity22aCannabinoid Receptor Modulatorsxphysiology22aEndocannabinoids22aReceptors, Neurotransmitter. 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16725  aAUTH02125cgm a2200493 i 101775930DNLM20260227160654.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2021    mdu061|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31486  a(OCoLC)1244241177  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 506.600aNIH virtual town hall on COVID-19 vaccination plan for NIH staff /cDr. Francis Collins.1 aCOVID-19 vaccination plan for NIH staff 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded January 15, 2021.3 a(CIT): 7th NIH Virtual Town Hall  Moderated by NIH Director Francis Collins and joined by other NIH leadership: Lawrence Tabak, NIH Principal Deputy Director. Colleen McGowan, NIH Office of Research Services Director. James Gilman, NIH Clinical Center CEO. The 7th Virtual Town Hall will provide an overview of the NIH COVID-19 vaccination plan for staff.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 26, 2021).22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aCOVID-19xprevention & control12aCOVID-19 Vaccinesxadministration & dosage22aMass Vaccination22aWorkplace22aSafety Managementxorganization & administration22aOccupational Health Servicesxorganization & administration 2aMaryland 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCollins, Francis S.,espeaker,emoderator.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41396  aAUTH02032cgm a2200457 i 101775920DNLM20260227160632.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2021    mdu682 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31473  a(DNLM)CIT:31474  a(DNLM)CIT:31477  a(OCoLC)1241124972  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 3852 aCuring the Epilepsies: Setting Research Priorities (Meeting)d(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aCuring the Epilepsies :bSetting Research Priorities /cNINDS, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (3 streaming video files (11 hr., 22 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded January 4-6, 2021.3 a(CIT): The NINDS invites investigators and individuals interested in finding cures for the epilepsies to convene and participate in a forum and discussion of the progress made in understanding the mechanisms of the epilepsies, as well as the inroads made towards potential cures. A main outcome and priority of the meeting will be the evaluation and updating of the Epilepsy Research Benchmarks.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 8, 2021).12aEpilepsyxtherapy22aTherapies, Investigational22aBiomedical Research 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3606540uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3606640uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=36073  aAUTH01242cas a2200433 a 101554863DNLM20260224111548.0cr||||||||||||110225c20119999sz || p       |    0eng    a  2011243317  a1664-3828l1664-5502  a(OCoLC)720607908  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9Switzerland00aW1bCA77MDCR10aCardiorenal Med2dnlm00aCardiorenal medicine.  aBasel :bKarger  aFour no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with 1/1/11.  aPrint issues ceased with 2020.1 aMEDLINEbv8n1, 2018-0 aPubMedbv1n1, Jan. 2011-0 aPMC Inactive  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: 1/1/11; title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: 1/1/11.12aHeart Diseases22aKidney Diseases 2aPeriodical0 x1664-55024 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/2546954 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101554863  aAUTH02104cgm a22004694a 101289632DNLM20260227151411.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2006    mdu072 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13146  a(OCoLC)84671960  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 2701 aVolkow, Nora D.,d1956-10aImaging the addicted brain :bfrom molecules to behavior /cNora Volkow.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 29, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 11 min., 25 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 5, 2006.3 a(CIT): Imaging technology has played a pivotal role in advancing our understanding of the neurochemical and functional changes that characterize the addicted brain. Although the involvement of dopamine in drug reinforcement is well recognized, its role in drug addiction is much less clear.  Imaging studies have shown that the reinforcing effects of drugs of abuse in humans are contingent upon large and fast increases in dopamine that mimic but exceed in the intensity and duration those induced by dopamine cell firing to environmental events.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aSubstance-Related Disordersxphysiopathology22aBrainxdiagnostic imaging22aBrainxdrug effects22aRadionuclide Imaging22aReceptors, Dopamine D2xmetabolism 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13146  aAUTH01064cas a22003854a 101236111DNLM20260227090913.0cr|cn ||||||||031204c20059999njuqr p       0    0eng    a  2003202585  a1547-769Xl1547-769X  a(OCoLC)53836498  aNSDcNSDdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bFO615L10aForensic Sci Med Pathol2dnlm00aForensic science, medicine, and pathology.  aTotowa, N.J. :bHumana Press,cc2005-  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (2005)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv4n1, 2008-1 aPubMedbv4n1, 2008-  aAlso issued online.12aForensic Medicine 2aPeriodical0 x1556-28914 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/12024  aAUTH01900cas a2200529 a 9106549DNLM20260227091013.0cr|cn ||||||||910509c19919999gw br p       0    0eng    asn 91033263   a0959-9851l0959-9851  aCAURE9  a(DNLM)SR0069760(s)  a(OCoLC)23835817  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Germany00aW1bCL668KH10aClin Auton Res2dnlm00aClinical autonomic research :bofficial journal of the Clinical Autonomic Research Society.  aOxford, UK :bRapid Communications of Oxford,cc1991-3 32002- :aDarnstadt :bSteinkopff  av. :bill., ports.  aBimonthly,b1992-  aQuarterly,b1991  atextbtx