viewed Nov. 8, 2006).  aStreaming video (11 hr., 47 min. : sd., col.).  aSpeakers: Peter J. Brown, et al.  aRecorded Nov. 3-4, 2005.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aGlobal Health12aHistory of Medicine22aInternational Cooperation22aSocioeconomic Factors. 2aWebcast1 aBrown, Peter J.2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.)40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch/98ef6c79-d5db-11f0-9cf9-12c45c580ad940uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch/99887ce8-d5db-11f0-9cf9-12c45c580ad9  aAUTH01986cgm a2200421 i 101682321DNLM20260227155614.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160503s2016    mdu053|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19621  a(OCoLC)951829983  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 5000aBacteria, their viruses, and how they taught us to perform genome surgery /cBlake Wiedenheft. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (53 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIGMS director's early career investigator lecture  aRecorded April 13, 2016.3 a(CIT): NIGMS Directors Early Career Investigator Lecture. Blake Wiedenheft, Ph.D., will give the first annual NIGMS Director's Early Career Investigator Lecture. The event is geared toward undergraduate students but open to all. Wiedenheft is an assistant professor in the Department of Microbiology and Immunology at Montana State University who does research on the CRISPR gene-editing tool. Following a 30-minute talk about his research, Wiedenheft will answer questions related to his career path.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed June 6, 2016).12aBacteriaxvirology22aClustered Regularly Interspaced Short Palindromic Repeats22aCRISPR-Cas Systems 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aWiedenheft, Blake,espeaker. 0aNIGMS director's early career investigator lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19621  aAUTH00948cam a22003137a 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002100073040001900094042001300113044001000126060003200136100003300168245010700201250001300308260007400321300002000395336002600415337002800441338002700469490002400496650001900520650003100539650001400570655001700584830002400601999000900625101617680DNLM20260209150456.0050711s1950    cc            000 0 chi    a(OCoLC)122884544  aCStcSTFdDNLM  anlmcopyc  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1805 no.81 aZhao, Wenyuan,dactive 1950.10aFu nü wei sheng chang shi /czhu zhe Zhao Wenyuan zhu ; bian ji zheTong su xiao wen ku bian wei hui.  aChu ban.  a[Shandong] :bShandong in hua shu dian,c1950 nian 3 yue [March 1950]  a25 p. ;c16 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aTong su xiao wen ku12aWomen's Health22aFemale Urogenital Diseases22aPregnancy 2aPopular Work 0aTong su xiao wen ku  aAUTH02307cgm a22004694a 101501603DNLM20260227153433.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090407s2009    mdu054|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14973  a(OCoLC)338189881  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 501 aJacobs-Wagner, Christine.10aExploring the bacterial internal organization :bcell polarization and cytoskeleton-dependent cell morphogenesis /cChristine Jacobs-Wagner.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen (viewed May 18, 2009).  aStreaming video (54 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 18, 2009.3 a(CIT): Bacteria, formerly viewed as miniature bags of randomly distributed chemicals, are in fact highly polarized, possess a cytoskeleton, and depend critically on this surprisingly exquisite cellular organization for many cellular functions.   Our laboratory studies the molecular mechanisms involved in this internal organization at multiple levels, from its origin, maintenance and replication in time and space to its function in cell morphogenesis and cell cycle regulation.   We use the morphologically polarized and crescent-shaped bacterium Caulobacter crescentus as a model.   The NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series includes weekly scientific talks by some of the top researchers in the biomedical sciences worldwide.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBacteriaxcytology22aBacterial Physiological Phenomena22aCell Differentiation22aCell Movement22aMorphogenesis 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14973  aAUTH01811cas a2200529 i 101649453DNLM20260227090922.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||130627c20149999nyubr poo     0   a0eng    a  2013201349  a871881960a10585755360 a2329-7670l2329-766221  a(OCoLC)851080826  z(OCoLC)871881960z(OCoLC)1058575536  aDLCbengerdad20260227cDLCdDLCdUV0dOCLCOdOCLCFdMYGdAU@dOCLCOdOCLCAdDNLM  apccansdp  9United States10aISSN RECORD00aW110a621.9882230 a3D print. addit. manuf.b(Online)10a3D Print Addit Manuf2dnlm 0a3D printing and additive manufacturingb(Online)00a3D printing and additive manufacturing. 1aNew Rochelle, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.,c2014-  a1 online resource  aBimonthly  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Vol. 1, no. 1 (March 2014).0 aPubMedbv7n1, Feb. 2020- ; Selected citations only before this date0 aPMC0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (March 2014); title from cover image (publisher's Web site, viewed July 9, 2014).1 aVolume 7, issue 5 (October 2020) (viewed November 10, 2020).12aPrinting, Three-Dimensional22aBioprinting22aBiomimetics 2aPeriodical0 x2329-766240uhttp://www.liebertpub.com/3dp40uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/tdp40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101649453  aAUTH02183cgm a22004454a 101520521DNLM20260227153213.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u091202s2009    mdu060|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15437  a(OCoLC)540015535  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWJ 7621 aGautam, Subhash.10aCurcumin/TRAIL combination therapy for prostate cancer /cSubhash Gautam.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCI OCCAM monthly lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 0 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 10, 2009.3 a(CIT): The NCI's Office of Cancer Complementary and Alternative Medicine (OCCAM) invites you to view its monthly lecture series.  With the goal of informing the National Cancer Institute (NCI) community about the variety of ongoing research in cancer and complementary and alternative medicine (CAM), NCI's Office of Cancer Complementary and Alternative Medicine (OCCAM) has created a monthly lecture series on cancer CAM. These hour long lectures, occurring from 10 a.m. - 11 a.m. on the second Wednesday of each month, will feature a fifty minute presentation on a cancer CAM topic and allow ten minutes for questions.   For more information, visit  http://www.cancer.gov/cam/news/monthly-lecture-series.html.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aProstatic Neoplasmsxdrug therapy22aCurcuminxtherapeutic use22aTNF-Related Apoptosis-Inducing Ligandxtherapeutic use 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.).bOffice of Cancer Complementary and Alternative Medicine. 0aOCCAM monthly lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15437  aAUTH01126cam a22003017i 4500