major disease problems and current research.  Primarily directed toward Ph.D. students, fellows, and staff, it is also of interest to medical students and clinicians.  The course is designed to help bridge the gap between advances in biology and their application to major human diseases.  Each session includes clinical and basic science components which are presented by NIH staff and outside invitees.  For more information, visit  http://www1.od.nih.gov/oir/DemystifyingMed.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDiabetes Mellitusximmunology12aDiabetes Mellitusxtherapy22aAutoimmunity 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15832  aAUTH02186cgm a22004094a 101267832DNLM20260227145547.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu033 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10745  a(OCoLC)65360779  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQC 454.M31 aMoyer, Susanne C.10aFragmentation of phosphotyrosine-containing peptides by atmospheric pressure MALDI ion trap mass spectrometry /cSusanne C. Moyer.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 15, 2006).  aStreaming video (32 min., 55 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Aug. 13, 2002.3 a(CIT): Recently, we combined atmospheric pressure matrix assisted laser desorption (AP-MALDI) with a commercial ion trap mass spectrometer (ITMS) [1]. Coupling the AP-MALDI source with an ion trap mass analyzer combines the benefits of MALDI sample preparation and simplicity of spectral analysis resulting from the production of predominantly singly charged ions, with the MSn capabilities of the quadrupole ion trap mass spectrometer. This configuration has proven to be useful in obtaining structural information from peptides and protein digests as well as for the identification and characterization of posttranslational modifications.  Sponsored by the  Mass Spectrometry Interest Group of the NCI-Frederick.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely. 2aPeptides 2aPhosphotyrosine 2aSpectrometry, Mass, Matrix-Assisted Laser Desorption-Ionization 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10745  aAUTH01990cgm a22004694a 101267958DNLM20260227145929.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu053 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11112  a(OCoLC)68980194  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 501 aMuenke, Max.10aGenetics research in the international setting /cMax Muenke and Kaare Berg.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 18, 2006).  aStreaming video (52 min., 41 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 6, 2002.3 a(CIT): NIH Clinical Center Ethics Grand Rounds A researcher from the US working in an international setting discovered genetic information that could have a significant impact on reproductive decision-making. When exploring how to handle this information he was told by local researchers, "We don't tell that here." The dilemma he faced will be the central question discussed at Ethics Grand Rounds.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aGenetic Counselingxethics22aCross-Cultural Comparison22aDisclosurexethics22aGenetic Predisposition to Disease 2aSouth America 2aUnited States 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih