LM12aHeartxanatomy & histology22aDissectionxmethods1 aFord, Corydon L.q(Corydon La),d1813-1894,eformer owner.5DNLM2 aUniversity of Michigan.bLibrary,eformer owner.5DNLM0 aPhysican and surgeon.  aAUTH01861cas a2200517 i 101609393DNLM20260210101323.0cr||||||||||||120613c20139999nyu x p       0   a0eng    a  20122005570 a2168-2267l2168-226721  a013836bUSPS  a(OCoLC)795360073  aDLCbengerdad20260210dDLCdOCLCQdDNLM  apccansdp  9United States10aISSN RECORD00aW1bIE447500a003/.522310aIEEE Trans Cybern2dnlm0 aIEEE trans. cybern.b(Print) 0aIEEE transactions on cyberneticsb(Print)00aIEEE transactions on cybernetics.3 aITCEB8 1aNew York, NY :bInstitute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers,c2013-  aSix issues yearly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with v. 43, no. 1 (Feb. 2013).2 aMEDLINEbv43n1, Feb. 2013-0 aPubMedbv43n1, Feb. 2013-  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: V. 43, no. 1 (February 2013) (surrogate); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 43, number 2 (April 2013).12aCybernetics22aBiomedical Engineeringxmethods22aMan-Machine Systems 2aPeriodical2 aInstitute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers,eissuing body.2 aIEEE Systems, Man, and Cybernetics Society,eissuing body.0 x2168-227500tIEEE transactions on systems, man, and cybernetics. Part B, Cyberneticsx1083-4419w(DLC)   98655031w(OCoLC)328112724 uhttp://ieeexplore.ieee.org/xpl/RecentIssue.jsp?punumber=6221036  aAUTH03908cgm a22004694a 101549232DNLM20260227153701.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101208s2010    mdu061|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16241  a(OCoLC)695526326  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWP 8701 aNarod, Steven A.10aPredictors of breast cancer risk in BRCA1 carriers /cSteven A. Narod.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCI Center for Cancer Research grand rounds  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 1 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS and NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Nov. 2, 2010.3 a(CIT): Dr. Narod completed medical school at the University of British Columbia, followed by an internship at McGill University-Jewish General Hospital. He completed his residency in community medicine at the University of Ottawa. Dr. Narod then did two fellowships; one in medical genetics at the Hospital for Sick Children in Toronto and one in cancer epidemiology as an International Agency for Research on Cancer fellow in Lyon, France. He worked at McGill University as an assistant professor before working as an associate professor at the University of Toronto. Dr. Narod is currently the director of the Familial Breast Cancer Research Unit at the Women"s College Research Institute in Toronto and is a Tier I Canada Research Chair in Breast Cancer. He is also a professor in the department of public health sciences at the University of Toronto, with cross appointments in the medicine, surgery, pathology and obstetrics and gynecology departments. He co-discovered the BRCA1 and BRCA2 genes and was the first to describe recurrent Jewish mutations. His research goal is to translate emerging knowledge on hereditary cancers into more effective strategies for the prevention and management of breast and ovarian cancers. In addition, he is working on delineating the gene/environment interactions that may underlie hereditary breast cancer in the identification of potential modifiers of high-risk cancers. Dr. Narod has studied hereditary aspects of cancer susceptibility for 20 years and has authored more than 400 papers on this topic. Overall, Dr. Narod is the most cited scientist in the world in the field of breast cancer, and his research has been instrumental in furthering understanding of the role of hereditary factors in breast and ovarian cancers.  NCI"s Center for Cancer Research (CCR) Grand Rounds is a weekly lecture series addressing current research in clinical and molecular oncology. Speakers are leading national and international researchers and clinicians proposed by members of the CCR Grand Rounds Planning Committee and others within the CCR community and approved by the CCR Office of the Director. Lectures occur every Tuesday from 8:00 to 9:00 a.m. in Lipsett Amphitheater in the Clinical Center building on the NIH campus September through July with exceptions around holidays and major cancer meetings. CME credit available via sign-up sheets in the lecture hall and at designated video-bridge sites only.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBreast Neoplasmsxgenetics22aGenes, BRCA122aGenetic Predisposition to Disease22aRisk Factors 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16241  aAUTH02038cas a2200505   0404271DNLM20260227090909.0cr|cn#||||||||hd|bfa012baap740216c19359999mx qr         0    0spa    asn 86012713   a0375-0906l0375-0906  aRGMXAK  a(DNLM)R34760000(s)  a(OCoLC)01778857  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aspabeng  ansdp  9Mexico00aW1bRE398F10aRev Gastroenterol Mex2dnlm00aRevista de gastroenterología de México.  aMéxico :bAsociación Mexicana de Gastroenterología published by Elsevier  av.billus.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 at. 1-        (no. 1-       ; nov. 1935-1 aMEDLINEbv40n1, Jan.-Feb. 1975-v82n4, Oct.-Dec. 20170 aPubMedbv40n1, Jan.-Feb. 1975-v82n4, Oct.-Dec. 20170 aOLDMEDLINE1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aMicrofilm.mv. 1, no. 3 (1935), v. 1, no. 5 (1936), v. 2, no. 8-12 (1937), v. 3, no. 16 (1938), v. 4 (1939)-14 (1949).b[Bethesda, Md.] :cNational Library of Medicine,d2000.e4 microfilm reels : negative ; 35 mm.  aOnline version available in English, 2013- and Spanish,1995- . Search interface available in English and Spanish.  aOfficial organ of the Asociación Mexicana de Gastroenterologá.  aPublished also in an English ed., <2005-> 2aGastroenterology 2aPeriodical2 aAsociación Mexicana de Gastro-enterología.08iIssued also in English:tRevista de gastroenterología de México. English.bEnglish ed.eengx2255-534Xw(DLC)   2015247817w(OCoLC)9112693964 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/revista-de-gastroenterologia-de-mexico  aAUTH02495cgm a22004574a 101549263DNLM20260227153807.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101208s2010    mdu055|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16278  a(OCoLC)697768561  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWI 7001 aHobbs, Helen H.10aGenes versus fast foods :beat, drink and be wary /cHelen Hobbs.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (55 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 17, 2010.3 a(CIT): Lecture Summary:  Insufficient time has elapsed for our genomes to adapt to the caloric abundance and reduced physical activity accompanying industrialization. Diseases of dietary excess, rather than nutritional deficiency are the major causes of death and disability in the Western world. Using human genetics, we have identified new genes and sequence variations conferring susceptibility (and resistance) to metabolic disorders associated with diabetes and heart disease.    Lecture Objectives:   1. Review strategies used to identify genetic variants contributing to common diseases associated with dietary excess.    2. Demonstrate role of genes involved in lipid metabolism in susceptibility (and resistance) to metabolic and cardiovascular disease.    3. Appreciate how human genetics can provide mechanistic insights into the relationship between phenotypes and diseases.  The NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series includes weekly scientific talks by some of the top researchers in the biomedical sciences worldwide.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aFatty Liverxetiology22aCoronary Diseasexetiology22aFast Foodsxadverse effects22aGenetic Predisposition to Disease 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16278  aAUTH02800cgm a2200421 a 101565219DNLM20260227154022.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110715s2011    mdu067 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16699  a(OCoLC)747167635  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 26.55.D21 aMendonca, Eneida.10aBringing scientific evidence into clinical practice :bchallenges, successes, and failures /cEneida Mendonca.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 7 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNLM extramural programs informatics lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded June 8, 2011.3 a(CIT): NLM Extramural Programs Informatics Lecture Series  Delayed, ineffective, and incomplete access to information may lead to uninformed decision making and loss of coordination in patient care that, in turn, may lead to medical errors. Despite the increase access to information resources, gaps in knowledge still exist and can have significant detrimental effects in patient care.  In the Context-initiated Question and Response ("CIQR") project we explored technologies to help clinicians articulate information needs that arise during clinical practice, and address them in a timely manner that fits with clinical workflow. We apply natural language processing technology to identify context, goals and preferences in clinicians" questions. In addition, we explore the performance of human experts as a unique prototyping methodology for developing an information system. Using cognitive methods, the approach leverages human abilities and progressively gives way to automation as the sophistication of the system evolve. This approach also plays an important role in the analysis of the information and clinical workflow. The major innovation was the generation of complex search strategies that exploit this contextual information, based on studies of human searching experts (reference librarians). Challenges, successes, and failures will be discussed.  aOpen-captioned.12aDecision Support Systems, Clinical22aEvidence-Based Medicine 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNLM Extramural Programs informatics lecture series for the NLM 175th anniversary40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16699  aAUTH01565cas a2200481   0404263DNLM20260227090908.0cr|cn#||||||||740216c19509999ck qr p       0||| 0spa    a0034-7434l0034-7434  aRCOGAJ  a(DNLM)R32260000(s)  a(OCoLC)06034303  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Colombia00aW1bRE35710aRev Colomb Obstet Ginecol2dnlm00aRevista colombiana de obstetricia y ginecología.  aBogotác[1950?]-3 aBogota :bSociedad Colombiana De Obstetricia Y Ginecologia  av.billus.  aQuarterly  aSix no. a yearb195?-1986  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aPrint issues ceased with 2015.2 aMEDLINEbv16n3,May-Jun. 1965-v22n6, Nov.-Dec. 1971; v70n1, Jan-Mar 2019-2 aPubMedbv16n3,May-Jun. 1965-v22n6, Nov.-Dec. 1971; v70n1, Jan-Mar 2019-0 aOLDMEDLINE2 aIndex medicus0 aPMC  aAlso available online.  aOrgan of the Sociedad Columbiana de Obstetricia y Ginecología. 2aGynecology 2aObstetrics 2aPeriodical2 aSociedad Colombiana de Obstet