ept. 2005 (viewed Sept. 29, 2005).1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, July 2005-0 aPubMedbv1n1, July 2005-0 aPMC  aArticles added continuously to each vol.  aMode of access: World Wide Web; may require Adobe Acrobat Reader.12aAnthropology12aMedicine, Traditional22aPhytotherapy22aPlants, Medicinal 2aPeriodical2 aBioMed Central Ltd.40uhttps://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101245794  aAUTH01417cas a2200457 a 9426326DNLM20260227091012.0cr||||||||||||960613c19909999txuqr p       0    0eng    a   90641054 zsn 890076170 a1043-4070l1043-4070  a(DNLM)SR0066506(s)  a(OCoLC)19451600  aNSDPcNSDPdDNLM  9United States00aW1bJO928W10aJ Hist Sex2dnlm 0aJournal of the history of sexuality00aJournal of the history of sexuality.13aJHS  aChicago, Ill. :bThe University of Chicago Press,cc1990-31aAustin :bUniversity of Texas Press  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (July 1990)-  aTitle from cover.0 aMEDLINEbSelected citations only0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso available on microfilm from University Microfilms International.  aAlso issued online. 2aSex 2aSexual Behaviorxhistory 2aPeriodical0 x1535-36054 uhttp://muse.jhu.edu/journals/journal%5Fof%5Fthe%5Fhistory%5Fof%5Fsexuality  aHISRELb19991201  aAUTH04213cgm a2200433 i 101690002DNLM20260227155601.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160804s2016    mdu073|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19777  a(OCoLC)958112301  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 55100aEvolution of adaptive immunity :b"double-duty" dendritic cells in cold-blooded vertebrates /cMartin F. Flajnik ; Immunology Interest Group. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 13 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 29, 2016.3 a(CIT): Immunology Interest Group.  Martin Flajnik earned his Ph.D. at the University of Rochester (NY) under Nicholas Cohen (of comparative- and psychoneuro-immunology fame) and then did his postdoc at the Basel Institute for Immunology under the noted amphibian immunologist Louis Du Pasquier. Flajnik's first faculty position was at the University of Miami (1988-"98) and he has toiled at The University of Maryland Baltimore ever since. His research has always centered on the evolution of adaptive immunity in the vertebrates and have included studies of: thymic education; MHC biochemistry and genetics; emergence and function of immunoglobulin (Ig) heavy and light chain isotypes; hypermutation of Ig and TCR genes; gamma/delta TCR structure and function; emergence of costimulatory molecules; the advent of lymphoid tissues and their function in selection; mucosal immunity; and single-variable domain antibodies.  The adaptive immune system arose 500 million years ago in ectothermic vertebrates, which include the extant jawless fish (lamprey and hagfish), cartilaginous fish, bony fish, and amphibians (and most reptiles). The definition of canonical adaptive immunity classically has been the RAG-dependent rearranging antigen receptors immunoglobulins (Ig) and T cell receptors (TCR), and the major histocompatibility complex MHC. These molecules/mechanisms are all present in gnathostomes. However, with the discovery of an adaptive immune system in jawless fish based on an entirely different set of antigen receptors (the variable lymphocyte receptors or VLR), the divergence of T cells and B cells goes back even further, to the origins of the jawed and jawless vertebrates; thus, the transcriptional networks governing lymphocyte differentiation are primordial. Certain immune subfeatures, such as the presence of IgM, conventional TCR, thymus, spleen, and MHC, are present in almost all gnathostomes, while other characteristics like IgD and gamma/delta TCR are quite plastic. In this seminar, Dr. Flajnik will focus on the evolution of gamma/delta T cells, where non-mammalian vertebrates have provided evidence for adaptive functions as well as interesting protein structures. The last major advance in vertebrate evolution is the advent of lymph nodes and formation of germinal centers in mammals; the ectothermic vertebrates allow a study of immunity that preceded the emergence of follicular dendritic cells, the major cellular player in mammalian affinity maturation. He will also discuss work in amphibians suggesting that antigen presenting cells have a dual nature, presenting antigen to both B cells and T cells.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed August 30, 2016).12aAdaptive Immunity22aB-Lymphocytesximmunology22aBiological Evolution22aDendritic Cellsximmunology22aT-Lymphocytesximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aFlajnik, M. F.q(Martin F.),espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19777  aAUTH01580cam a2200385 i 101671172DNLM20260210162153.0cr||||||||||||151029r18811881pau      b    000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)927949562  aDNLMbengerdad20260210dDNLM  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1971 no.291 aAtkinson, I. Edmondsonq(Isaac Edmondson),d1846-1906eauthor.10aMay iodide of potassium excite Bright's disease? /cby I. Edmondson Atkinson. 1a[Philadelphia?] :b[Henry C. Lea's Son & Co.?],c[1881?]  a14 pages ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCover title.  a"Extracted from the American journal of the medical sciences for July, 1881."  a"Read at the meeting of the American Medical Association, at Richmond, May 4, 1881"--Page 1.  aIncludes bibliographical references.  