a1 volume (unpaged) :billustrations  atext2rdacontent  astill imagebsti2rdacontent  aunmediated2rdamedia  avolume2rdacarrier12aChild Welfare22aChild Carexstandards22aHygienexstandards 2aPictorial Work2 aShanghai Shi wei sheng jiao yu guan,eissuing body.  aAUTH01656cas a2200505 a 8406611DNLM20260227091019.0cr|cn ||||||||840531c19829999mdufr p       0    0eng    a   83641415 zsc 83007026  a0736-9387l0736-9387  aANDYDD  a(DNLM)A34865000(s)  a(OCoLC)08872116  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bAN57410aAnn Dyslexia2dnlm 0aAnnals of dyslexia00aAnnals of dyslexia.  aBaltimore, Md. :bOrton Dyslexia Society,cc1982-3 3<2001->:aBaltimore, MD :bInternational Dyslexia Association  av. :bill., ports.  aTwo no. a year,b2004-  aAnnual,b1982-2003  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 32 (1982)-  aJournal of the Orton Dyslexia Society.1 aMEDLINEbv54n1, 2004-0 aPubMedbv54n1, 2004-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aVols. for <2001-> issued by the International Dyslexia Association.  av. 54, no. 1 (May 2004)c20050119fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aDyslexia 2aPeriodical2 aOrton Dyslexia Society.2 aInternational Dyslexia Association.0 x1934-724300tBulletin of the Orton Societyx0474-7534w(DLC)   72626458w(OCoLC)16388764 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/11881  aAUTH03005cgm a22004694a 101522187DNLM20260227153240.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100105s2009    mdu096|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15492  a(OCoLC)688679627  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2661 aWatkins-Bruner, Deborah.10aNCI Translational Working Group pathways :bapplication in symptom management /cDeborah Watkins Bruner.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCI CCR grand rounds  aTitle from title screen.  aOne hour leader before program begins.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 36 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS and NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Dec. 8, 2009.3 a(CIT): Dr. Bruner is the Independence Professor of Nursing Education and Founding Director of the Human Subjects Recruitment, Retention and Outreach Core (RROC) facility at the Abramson Cancer Center of the University of Pennsylvania. Dr. Bruner received her bachelor"s degree in nursing from West Chester University, as well as a master's degree in oncology nursing and a master's degree in nursing administration from Widener University. She completed her doctoral degree in nursing research, with a focus on outcomes research, at the University of Pennsylvania. Prior to moving to the University of Pennsylvania, Dr. Bruner had been at Fox Chase Cancer Center, Philadelphia, Penn., for 16 years where she was the director of the Symptoms and Outcomes Research Program, was the founding director of the Prostate Cancer Risk Assessment Program, and also directed a Clinical Trials Recruitment Core Facility. Dr. Bruner's research has focused on quality of life, patient-reported outcomes (PROs), and symptom management across cancer sites with a focus on pelvic tumors, as well as decision-making, preferences, and utilities for cancer therapies. Dr. Bruner is also internationally recognized for her research in sexuality after cancer therapy. In the area of recruitment and health disparities, Dr. Bruner has applied a novel scientific methodology in health care, has applied social marketing to cancer research, and is leading a unique program to study the behavioral aspects of human subjects recruitment to research trials.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxtherapy22aClinical Trials as Topic22aGuidelines as Topic22aQuality of Life 2aLecture 2aWebcast 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15492  aAUTH02456cas a2200601 i 101752333DNLM20260227090914.0m     o  d        cr#|||||||||||160614c20169999cauqr poo     0   a0eng    a  2016202912  aGBB8244552bnb  a964838208a1032293632a1058144538a1076445615a10864723760 a2473-0114l2473-011422  a(OCoLC)951684753  z(OCoLC)964838208z(OCoLC)1032293632z(OCoLC)1058144538z(OCoLC)1076445615z(OCoLC)1086472376  aDLCbengerdad20260227cDLCdOCLCQdVT2dOCLCFdHUAdPFMdDLCdOCLdU3WdAU@dOCLCOdUABdOCLCOdUKMGBdOCLCOdWYUdOCLCOdOCLCAdDKUdOCLCOdOCLCAdCUSdDNLM  apccansdp  9United States10aISSN RECORD14aRD78100aW110aFoot Ankle Orthop2dnlm 0aFoot & ankle orthopaedics00aFoot & ankle orthopaedics.1 aFoot and ankle orthopaedics1 aFAO 1aThousand Oaks, CA  :bSage Publications,c[2016]-  a1 online resource  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Volume 1, issue 1 (September/December 2016).0 aPubMedbv4n1, Jan. 2019- ; Selected citations only before this date0 aPMC  aArticles are published as they become available before inclusion in an issue.  aVolume 1 complete with one issue.  aContains special issues.  aScholarly journal of the American Orthopaedic Foot & Ankle Society.  aRelated journal: Foot & ankle international  aDescription based on: Volume 1, issue 1 (September/December 2016) (publisher's website, viewed August 17, 2021).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 6, issue 3 (July/September 2021) (viewed August 17, 2021).12aFootxsurgery12aAnklexsurgery22aOrthopedics 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Orthopaedic Foot and Ankle Society,esponsoring body.08iComplemented by (work):tFoot & ankle internationalx1071-1007w(DLC)sn 93002850w(OCoLC)2855203240uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/fao40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101752333  aAUTH02703cgm a2200421 i 9918716281706676DNLM20260227164254.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u231128s2023    mdu386|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33548  a(OCoLC)1504553282  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 2102 a40 Years of Cancer Epigenetics Symposiumd(2023 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10a40 years of cancer epigenetics symposium /c[National Cancer Institute, Division of Cancer Biology].1 aForty years of cancer epigenetics symposium 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (6 hr., 26 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 20, 2023.3 a(CIT): Forty years ago, Drs. Andrew Feinberg and Bert Vogelstein observed that levels of DNA methylation, an epigenetic modification now known as a critical modulator of gene expression, were significantly reduced in cancer cells when compared to normal cells. This landmark discovery is considered by many to be the birth of the field of cancer epigenetics. Since that time, this transformational research area has exploded and there no doubt regarding the importance of these phenomena.  There have been foundational studies leading to groundbreaking discoveries that span benchtop to bedside.  Cancer therapeutics that target epigenetic modifiers have become commonplace. The goal of this symposium is to celebrate the achievements in the field of cancer epigenetics for the past 40 years, highlighting impactful research and discoveries by outstanding scientists intimately involved in this research area. This symposium is extremely relevant to the mission of the NCI - Division of Cancer Biology (DCB), which is to ensure continuity and stability in basic cancer research while encouraging and facilitating the emergence of new ideas, concepts, and technologies.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 3, 2025).12aNeoplasmsxgenetics22aEpigenomics2