Blockade for Organ Transplantation  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 4, 2016).12aOrgan Transplantation 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aKirk, Allan,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center,esponsoring body. 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19482  aAUTH03251pam a2200493 i 9918986652506676DNLM20260225092934.0231106t20242024enka     b    001 0 eng    a  2023041421  a9781032182940q(hardback)  a1032182946  a9781032208749q(paperback)  a1032208740  z9781003265610q(ebook)  a(OCoLC)1413441148  aDLCbengerdacDLCdOCLCOdBDXdOCLCLdOCLCFdLMLdOCLCOdIG#  apcc  9United Kingdom00aRJ505.A7bA793 202400aWS 350.2500a618.92/85882065156223/eng/2023121100aArts therapies and the mental health of children and young people :bcontemporary research, theory, and practice.nVolume 2 /cedited by Uwe Herrmann, Margaret Hills de Zárate, Heather M. Hunter, and Salvo Pitruzzella. 1aLondon ;aNew York :bRoutledge,c2024. 4c©2024  axvii, 203 pages :billustrations ;c25 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aInternational research in the arts therapies  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  a"This second volume expands and develops the discussion on arts therapies begun in volume one on the field's relationship with children and young people's mental health, demonstrating further contemporary research within international contexts. The book responds to a resounding call to address children and young people's mental health. It explores a unique mix of diverse arts modalities including art, music, dance, expressive arts and drama, creating opportunities for discourse and discussion of how the different arts therapies cohere and relate to each other. Chapters are truly global in approach, ranging from schools in India to children's hospices in the UK, refugee transit camps in Greece and residential care programmes for LGBTQ+ youth in the United States. Discussions from Greece, Taiwan, and innovative research from Israel, Norway and Scotland are also featured with reference to diverse social, political and cultural contexts. Ultimately, chapters prioritise the links between research, theory and practice, providing accessible and implication-led dialogue on contemporary issues. This book provides new insights into the expanding field of the arts therapies and will be of great interest to arts therapists as well as academics and students in the fields of arts therapies, social work, psychotherapy, health psychology, and education"--cProvided by publisher.12aSensory Art Therapies22aChild Health22aAdolescent Health22aChild22aAdolescent1 aHerrmann, Uwec(Art therapist)eeditor.1 aHills De Zárate, Margaret,d1958-eeditor.1 aHunter, Heather M.,eeditor.1 aPitruzzella, Salvo,eeditor.  ihas work:aVolume 1 Arts therapies and the mental health of children and young people (Text)4https://id.oclc.org/worldcat/ontology/hasWork 0aInternational research in the arts therapies  aAUTH01456cas a2200469   0135232DNLM20260227090959.0cr|cn ||||||||680405c19679999xna x p       0    0eng    asn 86012712   a0004-8380l0004-8380  aAJDEBP  a(DNLM)A75105000(s)  a(OCoLC)01518662  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Australia00aW1bAU24810aAustralas J Dermatol2dnlm04aThe Australasian journal of dermatology.  a[Sydney]bAustralasian College of Dermatologists.3 aNew South Wales :bWiley-Blackwell  av.billus.  aFour no. a year  aThree no. a yearb19??-1988  aTwo no. a yearb1989-1990  aThree no. a yearb1991-1994  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 9-       June 1967-1 aMEDLINEbv9n1, Jun. 1967-0 aPubMedbv9n1, Jun. 1967-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online. 2aDermatology 2aPeriodical2 aAustralasian College of Derma