0493 a 8601391ADNLM20260204142738.0mr|bf##dnnaoannac198604mr|bf##dnnbdannac198604vf|bj##onvd|bv##znhd|bfa012baap861204s1935    mnu016  |||||     mleng    a(OCoLC)31666535  aDNLMcDNLMeamimdDNLM  9United States00aWF 900bMP16 no.7 193500aRight intrathoracic neurofibroma presenting in posterior mediastinum :b(clinical manifestations and surgical removal) /c[production company unknown] ; S.W. Harrington.  aUnited States.  a[Rochester, Minn. :bMayo Clinic,c1935]  a1 film reel of 1 (570 ft.) :bsi., b&w ;c16 mm.3answer print.  a1 film reel of 1 (570 ft.) :bsi., b&w ;c16 mm.3duplicate negative.  a1 videocassette of 1 (16 min.) :bsi., b&w ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (16 min.) :bsi., b&w ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videodisc of 1 (DVD) (16 min.) :bsi., b&w ;c4 3/4 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  aprojectedbg2rdamedia  afilm reelbmr2rdacarrier  aLive.  aFilmed on location in the USA.  aProduced 1933-1935.  aLast inspected: Apr. 1986. Condition complete; good.5DNLM  aPhotographer, Leonard A. Julin.8 aThis film shows the clinical manifestations and transpleural removal of a right intrathoracic neurofibroma.  In addition, the film includes the patient's history and photographs and roentgenograms before and after surgery.  Surgery includes: posterolateral incision in right thoracic wall; partial resection of 4th rib and entering pleural cavity through posterior periosteum of the rib; resection of 3rd rib; cut through mediastinal pleura; dissection which separates tumor from lung, aorta, and surrounding mediastinal structures; ligature in posterior mediastinum to control hemorrhage; inflation of lung; washing of cavity with salt solution; closure; etc.  Donated film is A-wind.  aVideocassette (Betacam SP)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2004.e1 videocassette (16 min.) : si., b&w ; 1/2 in.  aVideodisc (DVD)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2018.e1 videodisc (16 min.) : si., b&w ; 4 3/4 in.  aThis item may be under copyright protection. Please ask copyright owner for permission before publishing.5DNLM  aReceived: Feb. 1965 as a donation from Mayo Clinic.5DNLM 2aMediastinal Neoplasmsxsurgery 2aNeurofibromaxsurgery 2aInstructional Film and Video1 aHarrington, S. W.q(Stuart William),d1889-  aAUTH01411cas a22004334a 9612039DNLM20260227091013.0cr|cn#||||||||981016c19829999ja qr p       0    0jpn    a0912-3636l0912-3636  a(DNLM)SR0088142(s)  a(OCoLC)173864585  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 ajpnaeng  alcd  9Japan00aW1bSO8898BL00aSoshiki baiyō kenkyū =bTissue culture research communications : the journal of experimental & applied cell culture research.31aTissue culture research communications  aKanazawa :bNihon Soshiki Baiyō Gakkai  av.  aAnnual,b2012-  aIrregular,b-1995  aQuarterly,b1996-2011  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan in 1982.zCf. ISSN file.  aDescription based on: Dai 21-kan dai 4-gō (2002-nen 12-gatsu).  aAlso issued online.  aIn Japanese and English.  aOfficial journal of the Japanese Tissue Culture Association, <Dec. 2002->12aCulture Techniques 2aPeriodical2 aNihon Soshiki Baiyō Gakkai.0 x1881-37044 uhttps://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/browse/jtca/  aAUTH01865cam a2200373 i 30510300RDNLM20260224130854.0cr||||||||||||810815s1926    dcu      bl   001 0 eng    a(OCoLC)14742345  aDNLMbengerdad20260224dDNLM  9United States00aWA 540 AbP89r 19261 aUnited States.bPublic Health Service,eauthor.10aRegulations for the government of the United States Public Health Service :bapproved February 9, 1926, effective April 1, 1926. 1aWashington. :bGovernment Printing Office,c1926.  avii, 261 pages ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aNLM HMD copy, affixed to front flyleaf is a newspaper clipping stamped Feb. 17, 1931, of a letter to the editor of the The Post, from Frank Hughes, New York City, entitled "American ships built in Americas yards of American materials -- why not American crews?"5DNLM  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  3NLM HMD copy,cTransfer;aOffice of the PHS Historian;d2008.5DNLM  aNLM HMD copy, ownership inscription of O.F. Bever, Hospital Division, on front flyleaf.5DNLM  3GenColl copy,aCondition reviewedc20250627lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM1 3GenColl Copy 2adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aUnited States.bPublic Health Service12aPublic Health Administrationxlegislation & jurisprudence 2aUnited States 2aLegislation4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/30510300R  aAUTH02087cgm a2200457 i 101680570DNLM20260227155554.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160404s2016    mdu042|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19523  a(OCoLC)948243678  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 47000aBeyond DNA :bsequence variation in the RNA /cVivian G. Cheung. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (42 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded March 2, 2016.3 a(CIT): DNA is our genetic blueprint. Generally it is expected that RNA sequences are identical to the underlying DNA. However, there are exceptions to this one-to-one relationship, such as the A-to-G RNA editing mediated by ADAR proteins. The Cheung lab uncovered many more sites where RNA sequences differ from the underlying DNA, beyond those mediated by the known RNA-editing mechanisms. Dr. Cheung will describe their findings of RNA-DNA sequence differences, RDDs, and how their study of juvenile amyotrophic lateral sclerosis is leading to an understanding of RDD formation.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 19, 2016).12aBase Sequencexgenetics22aDNA22aRNA22aRNA Editing22aSequence Homology, Nucleic Acid 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCheung, Vivian G.,espeaker2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19523  aAUTH05000cgm a22004574a 101310954DNLM20260227152033.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070703s2007    mdu064|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13870  a(OCoLC)173670605  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQT 36.51 aRoukes, Michael Lee,d1953-10aNanocraft v. nanotechnology :brealizing transformational tools for the life sciences and medicine /cMichael Roukes.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from screen banner.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 4 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jun. 6, 2007.3 a(CIT): Michael Roukes  Professor of Physics, Applied Physics, and Bioengineering  California Institute of Technology, Pasadena, Calif.   Advances in nanoscale devices now allow us to envisage unprecedented measurements at the level of single cells and molecules.  Among such opportunities are mapping the forces generated by living cells in real time, ultimately with piconewton-scale, single-molecule resolution; resolving, calorimetrically, the metabolism of individual cells in real time; and tracking stochastic biochemical processes at the level of single molecules in real time.   I'll describe the state-of-the-art, future challenges, and some intriguing possibilities in these areas  especially in hope of stimulating additional imaginative uses of the new classes of tools emerging. Transitioning nanoscale devices from the realm of one-of-a-kind feats into robust and reproducible nanosystems  that is, tools useful for medical and biological research  is a monumental challenge that transcends the capabilities of any one lab.  Only the very first steps have been taken towards this end, yet such efforts are crucial for realizing the promise of active nanotechnology.  At least two essential elements must be in place to realize the vast applications potential that awaits.  First, an unfamiliar fusion of technologies is required; one that melds techniques from traditionally separate disciplines with a specific scale of approach. Second, robust methods for nanobiotechnological large-scale-integration are required, and these must engender routes to production en masse.     The kind of disciplined assemblage of disparate technologies required is, perhaps, more familiar within the commercial sector than academia.  But it is now essential, whether for pursuing fundamental research in medicine and the life sciences or, subsequently, for the development of future biomedical technology.     Michael Roukes is Professor of Physics, Applied Physics, and Bioengineering at the California Institute of Technology.  He was founding Director of Caltech's Kavli Nanoscience Institute, and recently stepped down to return to full-time, collaborative nanoscience research. Professor Roukes completed undergraduate majors in both physics and chemistry at the University of California Santa Cruz, and thereafter earned a Ph.D. in physics at Cornell University, focusing upon electron transport in microstructures at ultralow temperatures. Subsequently, he joined Bell Communications Research as a Member of Technical Staff / Principal Investigator in the (then-new) Quantum Structures Research Group, where he carried out some of the earliest explorations of the physics of nanoelectronic devices.  In 1992 he joined the faculty at the California Institute of Technology, where he built nanofabrication facilities and has established a large nanoscience research group, now heavily involved in cross-disciplinary collaborations.    Roukes' scientific interests range from fundamental science to applied biotechnology - with a unifying theme centered upon development, application, and large-scale-integration of complex nanostructures.  He has published and written extensively on nanoscience and nanotechnology, has lectured at most major research centers world-wide, and is active on many national and international committees that promote this field.   The NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series includes weekly scientific talks by some of the top researchers in the biomedical sciences worldwide.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNanomedicine12aNanotechnology22aBiomedical Engineering22aBiomedical Research 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13870  aAUTH01087cas a2200373 a 8109900DNLM20260227090958.0cr|cn ||||||||811008c19819999xxuqr         0||| 0eng    a0275-665Xl0275-665X  aOADIDS  a(DNLM)O00765000(s)  a(OCoLC)07192944  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bOB495 0aObstetric anesthesia digest00aObstetric anesthesia digest.  aNew York :bElsevier North Holland,cc1981-3 aNew York Ny :bRaven Press  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Mar. 1981)-  aTitle from caption.  aAlso issued online. 2aAnesthesiology 2aObstetrics 2aPeriodical4 uhttps://ovidsp.ovid.com/ovidweb.cgi?T=JS&NEWS=n&CSC=Y&PAGE=toc&D=yrovft&AN=00132582-000000000-00000  aAUTH03031cgm a2200433 i 101744886DNLM20260227160005.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190402s2019    mdu111|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27390  a(OCoLC)1105226508  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 21000aDemystifying medicine 2019.pInheritable cancer /cSharon Savage, Marston Linehan.1 aInheritable cancer 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 51 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded March 19, 2019.3 a(CIT): Cancer development often is a result of environmental factors, such as exposure to tobacco products and industrial toxins.  Other times, however, cancer is written in the cards, nearly unavoidable, arising almost solely from genetic factors.  These are so-called "inheritable cancers," and common types include cancers of the kidney, colon, and breast.  Of course, what is inherited is a gene defect from a parent, not the cancer itself.  Upwards of 10 percent of cancers may be the result of such inheritable mutations.  For this next lecture in the Demystifying Medicine series, now fortified with CME, we bring you two speakers who are investigating inheritable cancers from a clinical and epidemiological perspective.  Marston Linehan, chief of the NCI CCR Urologic Oncology Branch, pioneered the study of the genetic basis of kidney cancer.  His team identified the genes for the common forms of kidney cancer and described the pathways of these kidney cancer genes.  His recent studies targeting the metabolic basis of kidney cancer have resulted in the regression of metastatic cancer in patients with type 1 and type 2 papillary kidney cancer.  Co-presenter Sharon Savage, DCEG Clinical Director and chief of the Clinical Genetics Branch, has studied the genetic and molecular epidemiology of telomere biology, pediatric cancer etiology, and autosomal-dominant cancer predisposition syndromes, such as Li-Fraumeni syndrome.  She and her team are characterizing the clinical consequences of these disorders and are developing cancer screening programs.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title bar (viewed May 31, 2019).12aNeoplasmsxgenetics12aOncogenes22aGenetic Predisposition to Disease 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aSavage, Sharon A.q(Sharon Ann),espeaker.1 aLinehan, Marston,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27390  aAUTH01314cas a22004214a 101131275DNLM20260227090913.0cr|cn#||||||||010522c20029999nyuqr p       0    0eng    a  20012126350 a1535-2188l1535-2188  a(OCoLC)46983525  aNSDcNSDdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bPE871DK10aSemin Plast Surg2dnlm00aSeminars in plastic surgery.30aPlastic surgery  aNew York, NY :bThieme Medical Publishers,  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Vol. 16, no. 1 (Feb. 2002).  aDescription based on: Vol. 16, no. 2 (May 2002); title from cover.0 aPubMedbv18, 2004-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.12aPlastic Surgery Procedures. 2aPeriodical0 x1536-006700tPerspectives in plastic surgeryx0892-3957w(OCoLC)151821664 uhttps://www.thieme-connect.de/products/ejournals/journal/10.1055/s-000000514 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101131275  aAUTH01295nam a2200361 a 101778278DNLM20260226102402.0010312s1864    mau          l000 0 eng    a  2001560600  a(OCoLC)46561121  aDLCbengcDLCdOCLCQdOCLCFdOCLCQdOCLCOdOCLCAdDNLM  an-us-ma  9United States00aAC901b.D7 box 90, no. 800aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5571 no.142 aBoston City Hospital.10aRules and regulations for the city hospital, 1864.1 iAt head of title:aCity of Boston  a[Boston] :b[publisher not identified],c[1864]  a13 pages ;c24 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aCity document ;vno. 45  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM22aBoston City Hospital.12aHospitals, Municipalxorganization & administration 2aBoston1 aBoston (Mass.).tDocument ;v1864, 45.  aAUTH02210cgm a2200445 i 101776731DNLM20260227160756.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210302s2021    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31542  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWT 10400aThree (formerly) blind mice :breprogramming tissues to be young again /cDavid A. Sinclair. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 0 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded February 9, 2021.3 a(CIT):   Dr. Sinclair is a professor of genetics, Blavatnik Institute, and co-director of the Paul F. Glenn Center for Biology of Aging Research, at Harvard Medical School.  His lab seeks to understand why we age and whether we can extend human health and longevity.  The Sinclair laboratory has demonstrated that it is possible to epigenetically reprogram cells to safely reverse aging in vivo and regain eyesight.  This research, conducted with support from the National Institute on Aging and National Eye Institute, was recently published in Nature (PMID: 33268865; doi: 10.1038/s41586-020-2975-4) and will be the subject of this special Tuesday WALS lecture.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 27, 2021).12aAgingxgenetics22aEpigenesis, Genetic22aDNA Methylation22aCellular Reprogramming Techniques22aTissue Engineering 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aSinclair, Davidq(David A.),espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41438  aAUTH02932cgm a2200481 i 101776727DNLM20260227160747.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210302s2021    mdu238 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31538  a(DNLM)CIT:31539  a(OCoLC)1415740802  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWY 1562 aONS-NCI-NINR Scientific Colloquium "Symptom Science Advances in Oncology Nursing"d(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aSymptom Science Advances in Oncology Nursing /cNINR, NCI, ONS. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (3 hr., 58 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  a"ONS-NCI-NINR Scientific Colloquium "Symptom Science Advances in Oncology Nursing""--PowerPoint.  aRecorded February 4-5, 2021.3 a(CIT): The National Institute of Nursing Research, Center for Cancer Research of the National Cancer Institute, and Oncology Nursing Society Present a Colloquium on Symptom Science Advances in Oncology Nursing. The colloquium brings together researchers, healthcare providers, educators, students, and fellows interested in oncology nursing and symptom science. The first day plenary session begins with Dr. Christine Miaskowski from the University of California San Francisco giving the keynote presentation on "Symptom Science in Oncology During the Pandemic." Following this, Dr. Mary Cooley of the Dana Farber Cancer Institute moderates a panel discussion on "Nursing Advances in Symptom Science." The panelists include Dr. Leorey Saligan of the National Institute of Nursing Research addressing "Precision health and symptom science"; Dr. Peg Rosenzweig of the University of Pittsburgh speaking on "Disparities and symptom science"; and Dr. Terri Armstrong of the National Cancer Institute discussing "Clinical Outcomes Assessment and Risk Prediction for symptoms and toxicities in therapeutic trials".  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 21, 2021).12aOncology Nursing22aSymptom Assessment22aNursing Diagnosis22aNursing Research 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institute of Nursing Research (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aOncology Nursing Society,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3870940uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38711  aAUTH01873cgm a2200445 i 101775906DNLM20260227160557.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2020    mdu095|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31440  a(OCoLC)1239645599  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 506.52 aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19d(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19 breakout session 4 (renal/GI/metabolic) /cNIAID, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 35 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded December 4, 2020.3 a(CIT): Breakout Session 4. This session will focus on the renal, gastrointestinal, and metabolic perspectives of post-acute sequelae of COVID-19.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 24, 2021).12aCOVID-19xcomplications22aKidney Diseasesxvirology22aGastrointestinal Diseasesxvirology22aDiabetes Mellitusxvirology22aPandemics 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (U.S.),eissuing body.2 iAugmentation of (work):aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19d(2020 :cOnline).tWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38883  aAUTH02183cgm a2200433 i 101776733DNLM20260227160801.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210302s2021    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31544  a(OCoLC)1249794808  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aBL 65.M400aHarmonizing the spiritual and scientific worldviews /cDr. Francis S. Collins.1 iTitle on PowerPoint screen:aHarmonizing the spiritual and scientific worldviews in the era of COVID 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 0 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded February 9, 2021.3 a(CIT): Religion, Spirituality and Health Scientific Interest Group The Religion, Spirituality, and Health Scientific Interest Group (RSH-SIG) is pleased to announce that Dr. Francis S. Collins will present the Inaugural Presentation. The title of his talk is "Harmonizing the Spiritual and Scientific Worldviews." Questions for the presenter may be submitted in advance to RSH-SIG@nih.gov. For more information on the Religion, Spirituality and Health Scientific Interest group, or to join its listserv, please visit: https://oir.nih.gov/sigs/religion-spirituality-health-scientific-interest-group.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 29, 2021).12aReligion and Medicine22aReligion and Science22aSpirituality22aCOVID-19xpsychology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCollins, Francis S.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bReligion, Spirituality, and Health Scientific Interest Group,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41402  aAUTH02479cgm a2200445 a 101552207DNLM20260227153829.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110119s2010    mdu056 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16388  a(OCoLC)712765490  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWW 1661 aBennett, Jean.10aSeeing is believing :ba gene therapy success /cJean Bennett.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video (56 min.) :bsd. col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture  aTitle from title screen (viewed Feb. 7, 2011).  aRecorded Dec. 15, 2010.3 a(CIT): Gene therapy has the potential to reverse disease or prevent further deterioration of vision in patients with incurable inherited retinal degeneration. The demonstration of safe and stable recovery of retinal/visual function in 12 children and adults with Leber congenital amaurosis (LCA) due to RPE65 mutations in a trial being carried out at The Children"s Hospital of Philadelphia (CHOP), and encouraging results from four other clinical trials for LCA-RPE65 provide great hope for people with other more common blinding diseases. This presentation will describe the animal studies that led to the clinical trials and the latest safety and efficacy results in the LCA-RPE65 clinical trial being held at CHOP. This Phase 1-2 study is now >3 years past initiation. In addition, it will describe some of the challenges presented by the nature of the targeted disease itself, hurdles that have been navigated in order to conduct the study, and issues of importance for eventual approval of gene augmentation as a therapy for LCA and other blinding diseases.  aOpen-captioned.12aLeber Congenital Amaurosisxtherapy22aClinical Trials as Topic22aDogs22aGenetic Therapyxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16388  aAUTH02157cgm a22004334a 101524374DNLM20260227153518.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100213s2010    mdu069 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15531  a(OCoLC)605750018  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 145.51 aBetz, Joseph M.10aTechnical and regulatory challenges to biomedical research on dietary supplements /cJoseph M. Betz ; National Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 9 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 11, 2010.3 a(CIT): Joseph M. Betz, Ph.D. joined the ODS as Director of the newly formed Dietary Supplements Methods and Reference Materials Program in December of 2001. In this role, he oversees efforts to enhance and stimulate research and training in fields relevant to dietary supplements at the NIH and to promote development of validated analytical methods and reference materials for use by industry, regulatory agencies, and clinical researchers. Prior to this, he was Vice President for Scientific and Technical Affairs at the American Herbal Products Association (AHPA). Before joining the AHPA, he spent 12 years as a research chemist at Food and Drug Administration"s Center for Food Safety and Applied Nutrition.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDietary Supplementsxstandards22aClinical Trials as Topicxstandards22aGovernment Regulation 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15531  aAUTH02832cgm a2200469 i 101749130DNLM20260227155944.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190604s2019    mdu485 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27493  a(DNLM)CIT:27500  a(OCoLC)1105948460  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWQ 2702 aMaternal Mortality in the United States (Workshop)d(2019 :cRockville, Md.)jauthor.10aMaternal Mortality in the United States :bfuture research directions /cNICHD, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (8 hr., 05 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded May 2 & 3, 2019.3 a(CIT): The workshop's purpose is to identify research questions, gaps, and opportunities that could expand knowledge regarding maternal mortality data, trends, and causes.  The workshop presentations and discussions will inform the development of a research agenda for NICHD that could lead to improved measurement and reduction of maternal mortality in the United States.  The panel discussions will focus on three areas:  1. An in-depth examination of definitions, data measurement and quality, trends of maternal mortality, and research needs, gaps and opportunities to enable better measurement and reporting 2. A discussion of clinical obstetrical contributors to maternal mortality in the context of health system factors such as access, insurance/financing, quality of health care, and variations in medical practice; and identification of research gaps, needs, and strategies 3. An overview of root causes of maternal mortality disparities such as medical mistrust, neighborhood environments, and discussion of cultural frameworks that provide an inclusive approach for improving maternal health outcomes for all women.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed June 26, 2019).12aMaternal Deathxprevention & control22aHealthcare Disparities22aMaternal Health22aMaternal Mortality22aMaternal Welfare 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?2749340uhttps://videocast.nih.