atedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Vol. 10, no. 4 (Oct.-Dec. 1979).  aPlace of publication varies.  a"A publication of the American Academy of Insurance Medicine.", <winter 1993->  aDescription based on: Vol. 14, no. 1 (Jan./Mar. 1983); title from cover.  aPrint issues ceased with v. 44, no. 4 (2014).1 aMEDLINEbv23n1, 1991-1 aPubMedbv23n1, 1991-  aVol. 34, no. 3-4 (2002) never published.  aAlso issued online.  aContinues: Insurance medicine. 2aActuarial Analysis 2aInsurance, Health 2aMedicine 2aPeriodical2 aAssociation of Life Insurance Medical Directors of America.2 aAmerican Academy of Insurance Medicine.10tInsurance medicine4 uhttps://meridian.allenpress.com/jim  aAUTH02338cgm a22004934a 101308574DNLM20260227152040.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070605s2007    mdu067|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13807  a(OCoLC)173670596  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2001 aPaulovich, Amanda.10aTowards an optimized biomarker discovery and verification pipeline.1 iTitle from PowerPoint title screen:aDiscovery to verification :boptimizing a biomarker pipeline using a mouse model of human cancer  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner.  aSpeaker: Amanda Paulovich.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 7 min. : sd., col.).  aComment: For cancer, early detection offers the best hope for cure. Unfortunately, for most cancers there are no cost effective ways to screen large populations for the disease. Recent technological advances offer new hope that biomarkers can be discovered to faci.  aRecorded May 4, 2007.3 a(CIT): For cancer, early detection offers the best hope for cure. Unfortunately, for most cancers there are no cost effective ways to screen large populations for the disease. Recent technological advances offer new hope that biomarkers can be discovered to facilitate the early detection and effective treatment of cancers. The application of some of these advances to biomarker discovery will be discussed.   For more information, visit   http://proteome.nih.gov.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government.  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomarkers, Tumorxanalysis22aMice22aModels, Animal22aNeoplasmsxdiagnosis22aNeoplasmsxtherapy 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13807  aAUTH02601cam a2200517 a 101276286DNLM20260223092811.0m        d f      cr|cn#||||||||060510s2005    dcu     sbi  f000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)70874069  aCBTbengcCBTdMTGdOCLdOCLCQdDNLM  anlmcopyc  an-us---  9United States10aZWD 7000 aTD 4.210:05/1  aRC1054.U5bA4 no. 2005-11 aCollins, William E.q(William Edward),d1932-2025.10aIndex to FAA Office of Aerospace Medicine reports, 1961 through 2004 :bfinal report /cWilliam E. Collins, Michael E. Wayda, Katherine Wade ; Civil Aerospace Medical Institute.3 aIndex to Federal Aviation Administration Office of Aerospace Medicine reports, 1961 through 2004  aWashington, D.C. :bU.S. Dept. of Transportation, Federal Aviation Administration, Office of Aerospace Medicine ;aFort Belvior [Belvoir], VA :bAvailable through Defense Technical Information Center ;aSpringfield, VA :bAvailable through the National Technical Information Service,c[2005]  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from PDF cover (viewed on July 21, 2006).  a"January 2005."  a"DOT/FAA/AM-05/1."  aAlso available online via the CAMI website (www.cami.jccbi.gov).  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 aAn index to FAA Office of Aerospace Medicine reports (1961-2004) and Civil Aeromedical Institute reports (1961-1963) is presented, chronologically, alphabetically by author, and alphabetically by subject. A foreword explains how to obtain copies of published Office of Aerospace Medicine reports. A historical vignette describes the earliest efforts to establish new medical leadership at Washington headquarters and the Civil Aeromedical Research Institute.  aIssued also in print.  aMode of access: Internet.12aUnited States.bOffice of Aerospace Medicine22aCivil Aerospace Medical Institute.12aAerospace Medicine22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aBibliography 2aIndex1 aWayda, Michael E.1 aWade, Katherine.2 aCivil Aerospace Medical Institute.1 aUnited States.bOffice of Aerospace Medicine.40uhttps://apps.dtic.mil/sti/citations/ADA460101  aAUTH01430cas a2200469 a 8701186DNLM20260227091015.0cr|cnu||||||||870122c19869999sz  x p       0    0eng    a0257-2753l0257-2753  aDIDIEW  a(DNLM)SR0056714(s)  a(OCoLC)13877772  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Switzerland00aW1bDI57D0 aDigestive diseases (Basel, Switzerland)10aDig Dis2dnlm 0aDigestive diseasesb(Basel)10aDigestive diseases.  aBasel ; New York :bS. Karger,c[c1986-  av. :bill.  aFour no. a year,b2000-  aFour no. a year,b1986-1988  aSix no. a year,b1989-1999  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 a4/1/86 (Apr. 1986)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv4n1, 1986-1 aPubMedbv4n1, 1986-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso available online.  3Vol. 6, no. 1 (1988)c19921001fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aDigestive System Diseases 2aPeriodical0 x1421-987500tSurvey of digestive diseasesx0253-4398w(OCoLC)99280654 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/224231  aAUTH01443cgm a2200445 a 101571657DNLM20260227154044.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u111018s2011    mdu081|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16829  a(OCoLC)762697360  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 300 AA11 aLaVeist, Thomas Alexis.14aThe skin you're in :bmaking