the brain undergoes dramatic reorganization in both its structure and function. Furthermore, these neuroplastic changes are intimately related to compensatory sensory and perceptual behaviors observed in individuals who are blind. We will present how modern neuroimaging has helped reveal the relationship between the brain and behavior as it relates to blindness. We will also highlight differences between individuals who are visually impaired due to ocular causes as compared to those who have developmental damage to the visual cortex.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 8, 2015).12aNeuronal Plasticity22aAdaptation, Physiological22aBlindness22aVisual Cortexxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aMerabet, Lotfi B.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aBSSR lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19243  aAUTH01900cgm a2200445 i 101654000DNLM20260227154852.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150314s2015    mdu065|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18839  a(OCoLC)907021808  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQH 212.F551 aMoerner, W. E.q(William Esco),d1953-eauthor.14aThe story of single molecules, from early spectroscopy in solids to super-resolution nanoscopy in cells and beyond /cW.E. Moerner. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 5 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aSpecial NIH Nobel laureate lecture  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Feb. 5, 2015.3 a(CIT): Special NIH Nobel Laureate Lecture  Dr. W.E. Moerner, who shared the 2014 Nobel Prize in chemistry for the development of super-resolution fluorescence microscopy, will discuss his pioneering work in a special NIH lecture titled "The Story of Single Molecules, from Early Spectroscopy in Solids to Super-Resolution Nanoscopy in Cells and Beyond."  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed March 31, 2015.12aMicroscopy, Fluorescence22aMolecular Imaging22aNanotechnology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aSpecial NIH Nobel laureate lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18839  aAUTH01338cas a2200445 a 8413380DNLM20260227091020.0cr|cn#||||||||841129c19839999nyuqr p       0    0eng    a   97641649 zsc 83007487zsn 82004869  a0734-0451l0734-0451  aSEMHE7  a(DNLM)S19235000(s)  a(OCoLC)08674511  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  ansdp  9United States00aW1bSE488T10aSemin Hear2dnlm 0aSeminars in hearing00aSeminars in hearing.  a[New York, N.Y.] :bThieme-Stratton,c[c1983-  av. :bill., ports.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 4, no. 1 (Feb. 1983)-  aTitle from cover.0 aPubMedbv36n1, 2015-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online. 2aAudiology 2aPeriodical0 x1098-895501tSeminars, speech, language, hearingx0196-108Xw(DLC)   82640777w(OCoLC)57527524 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=84133804 uhttps://www.thieme-connect.de/products/ejournals/journal/10.1055/s-00000067  aAUTH02973cgm a2200493 i 101655827DNLM20260227154918.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150409s2015    mdu053|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18902  a(OCoLC)908629400  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 3851 aPestian, John,eauthor.10aPhenotypical cohort retrieval using the Multi-Institutional Pediatric Epilepsy Decision Support (miPeds) system /cJohn Pestian.1 iTitle on PowerPoint:aMulti-institutional pediatric epilepsy decision support 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (53 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNational Library of Medicine informatics lecture series  aRecorded March 18, 2015.3 a(CIT): National Library of Medicine Informatics Lecture Series:  The Multi-Institutional Pediatric Epilepsy Decision Support (MiPeds) system provides point-of-care surveillance of phenotypically similar pediatric epilepsy patients using the electronic health records (EHR) from Cincinnati Children's Hospital Medical Center, Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, and Children's Hospital of Colorado.  Using this near real-time cohort retrieval system, the three organizations can review similarities and differences in clinical measures like: medication side-effects, types of seizures, seizure frequency, quality of life, neurological abnormalities, and so forth. This talk will describe the successes and challenges of developing MiPeds.  Examples will focus on: aligning the research and clinical needs of each organization with data standards, factors that influence centralization and decentralization, automated method of de-identification, the usefulness of I2B2, developing collaborative measures of data quality and quality of care, searching and visualization.  Efforts to generalize this novel approach to other neuropsychiatric disease will be described as well.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 4, 2015).22aMulti-Institutional Pediatric Epilepsy Decision Support (System)12aDecision Support Systems, Management12aEpilepsy22aChild22aCohort Studies22aInterinstitutional Relations22aPhenotype 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNLM informatics lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18902  aAUTH01366cas a22004335i 101626590DNLM20260227090921.0cr||||||||||||140224c20149999cau|| p       |    0eng    a  2014243393  a2053-3691l2053-3691 