United States00aW 26.51 aBerman, Francine Denise,d1951-eauthor.10aGot data?bbuilding a sustainable ecosystem for data driven research /cFrancine Berman. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 17 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH frontiers in data science series  aRecorded November 5, 2015.3 a(CIT): NIH Frontiers in Data Science Series. Innovation in a digital world presupposes that the data will be there when you need it, but will it? Without sufficient data infrastructure and attention to the stewardship and preservation of digital data, data may become inaccessible or lost. This is particularly problematic for data generated by sponsored research projects where the focus is on innovation rather than infrastructure, and support for stewardship and preservation may be short-term. In this presentation, Dr. Fran Berman discusses sustainability, infrastructure, and data, and explores the opportunities and challenges of creating a viable ecosystem for the data on which current and future research and innovation increasingly depend.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 16, 2015).12aBiomedical Research12aDatasets as Topic22aCooperative Behavior22aData Mining22aEcosystem 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH frontiers in data science series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19303  aAUTH01813cas a2200469 a 9918680785106676DNLM20260213174609.0791219d19711975hiu x         1   a0eng    asn 85012350   a994432185  a0078-76472z  aRPPCD4  a(OCoLC)5812154z(OCoLC)994432185  aCOObengcCOOdNYGdOCLdIULdRCSdAIPdNSTdAIPdNSTdNSDdNSTdNYGdOCLCQdOCLCFdOCLCOdOCLdOCLCOdOCLCAdOCLCQdNZ1dGZMdDNLM  apccansdp 