Collins, Jennie Lucca, Ethan Dmitrovsky.1 aThirty years, a celebration of hope and discovery1 iAt head of title screen:aChildren's Inn at NIH - 30 years :ba celebration of hope and discovery 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 29, 2020.3 a(CIT): Hear NIH Director Dr. Francis S. Collins highlight The Children's Inn's contributions to 30 years of medical discovery and its importance for the future of NIH clinical research. Sponsored by Leidos.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed December 10, 2020).22aChildren's Inn (U.S.)12aResidential Facilities22aChild, Institutionalized22aChild Care22aAnniversaries and Special Events 2aMaryland 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCollins, Francis S.,espeaker.1 aLucca, Jennie,ehost,espeaker.1 aDmitrovsky, Ethan,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aLeidos, Inc.,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38970  aAUTH02641cgm a2200433 i 101663004DNLM20260227155623.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150625s2015    mdu105|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19079  a(OCoLC)918550467  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 851 aVolpp, Kevin,eauthor.10aMatilda White Riley award and lecture in behavioral and social sciences 2015 /cKevin Volpp, Jeanne Brooks-Gunn. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 45 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aMatilda White Riley award and lecture in behavioral and social sciences ;v8th  aRecorded June 23, 2015.3 a(CIT): In 2015, the 8th Annual Matilda White Riley Award and Lecture will be a central feature of the NIH Office of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research 20th Anniversary celebrations. It is fitting that this year's award honors Dr. Kevin Volpp and Dr. Jeanne Brooks-Gunn, two eminent scholars who have made significant contributions to the field of behavioral and social sciences that have furthered the mission of the National Institutes of Health. AWARDEES: Kevin Volpp, Professor of Medicine, Perelman School of Medicine, Professor of Health Care Management at Wharton School  University of Pennsylvania, Perelman School of Medicine, Division of General Internal Medicine, Jeanne Brooks-Gunn, Virginia and Leonard Marx Professor of Child Development and Education, Teachers College and College of Physicians and Surgeons, Columbia University  Co-director, National Center for Children and Families  Co-director, Columbia University Institute for Child and Family Policy.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed August 4, 2015).12aHealth Behavior22aChild Welfare 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aBrooks-Gunn, Jeanne,eauthor.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research,esponsoring body. 0aMatilda White Riley annual lecture in the behavioral and social sciences ;v8th.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19079  aAUTH01646cgm a2200457 i 101773033DNLM20260227160320.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201105s2020    mdu079|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30330  a(OCoLC)1224228386  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 14000aOn the potential of massive open online interventions and digital apothecaries to reduce health disparities worldwide /cRicardo F. Muñoz. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 19 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIMHD director's seminar  aRecorded October 8, 2020.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 20, 2020).12aMental Disordersxprevention & control22aInternet-Based Intervention22aTelemedicine22aPharmacy22aHealthcare Disparities22aHealth Equity 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aMuñoz, Ricardo F.,espeaker.2 aNational Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNIMHD director's seminar40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38847  aAUTH02265cgm a2200457 i 101773036DNLM20260227160328.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201105s2020    mdu062|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30336  a(OCoLC)1224228463  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 5200aMicrobes, metals, music, and lessons in disproving your hypothesis /cMichael D.L. Johnson. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIGMS director's early-career investigator lecture  aRecorded October 14, 2020.3 a(CIT): At the host/pathogen interface, bacteria must acquire those metals from within. While host and microbe battle over metals like iron and manganese, not all host utilized metals are desirable for bacteria. In fact, copper is largely toxic to them; a fact that is exploited by the host. As such, bacteria devote significant resources to maintain metal homeostasis through systems that address the stress of excess or deficiency. Here, Dr. Johnson will look at copper stress affected and overcoming systems, how bacteria use metals to perceive their environment, and how we can take advantage of it all to develop new therapeutics.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 23, 2020).12aBacterial Physiological Phenomena22aBacteriaxpathogenicity22aCopperxmetabolism22aHost-Pathogen Interactions22aDrug Discovery 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aJohnson, Michael D. L.,espeaker.2 aNational Institute of General Medical Sciences (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNIGMS director's early career investigator lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38707  aAUTH01426cas a22004694a 100901183DNLM20260227091059.0cr|cnu||||||||991004c20009999nyuqr p       0    0eng    asn 99009386 0 a1527-0297l1527-0297  aHAMBB7  a(OCoLC)42518268  aNSDcNSDdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bHI21310aHigh Alt Med Biol2dnlm00aHigh altitude medicine & biology.3 aHigh altitude medicine and biology  aLarchmont, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.,cc2000-  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (spring 2000)-  aPrint issues ceased with v. 9 (2008)1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, Spring 2000-0 aPubMedbv1n1, Spring 2000-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3v.1, no. 1 ( Spring 2000)c20010306fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper that meets the full ANSI standard for Permanent Paper12aAltitude Sickness22aAltitude22aBiology22aMedicine 2aPeriodical0 x1557-86824 uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/ham  aAUTH01467cas a2200457 a 101474849DNLM20260227090921.0m        d        cr |||||||||||080523c20089999enkuu pso     0    0eng    a  20082477997 a2455679-82DE-600  a1757-2215l1757-2215  a(OCoLC)262524145  aSMCcSMCdPFMdHEBISdCUSdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9England10aW110aJ Ovarian Res2dnlm00aJournal of ovarian research.3 aJOR  a[London] :bBioMed Central,cc2008-  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with 1:1 (1 Oct. 2008).  aDescripti