 o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2022    mdu937|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32746  a(DNLM)CIT:32747  a(OCoLC)1425218557  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 300 DA152 aLatin America: Synergizing Health Research Across the Hemisphere (Workshop)d(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aLatin America :bsynergizing health research across the hemisphere /cNIMHD, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (15 hr., 37 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 1 & 2, 2022.3 a(CIT): In order to accomplish its mission to lead scientific research to improve minority health and reduce health disparities, NIMHD translates and disseminates relevant research information. This workshop is a platform to discuss research opportunities to reduce health and health care disparities related to non-communicable diseases (NCDs) with the highest disease burden and mortality in Latin America and among U.S. Hispanic/Latino populations.  Experts in diverse disciplines will share advances, challenges, best practices, and lessons learned in clinical, health services/health systems, and community-engaged research related to NCDs conducted throughout Latin America.  A central interest of the workshop is to discuss current and potential research strategies and other perspectives in countries from which most U.S. Hispanics/Latinos identify their heritage, resulting in mutual benefit to both U.S. Hispanic/Latino communities and Latin America.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 28, 2024).12aHealth Inequities22aNoncommunicable Diseases22aMinority Health22aBiomedical Research22aSouth American People22aCentral American People22aHispanic or Latino 2aLatin America 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4645140uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=46453  aAUTH01452cam a2200349 i 0011353DNLM20260206220106.0cr||||||||||||680616s1909    ohu      b    000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)6453544  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aQHbG989d 19091 aGuyer, Michael F.q(Michael Frederic),d1874-1959,eauthor.10aDeficiencies of the chromosome theory of heredity /cby Michael Frederic Guyer. 1aCincinnati :bUniversity Press,c1909.  a19 page ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aUniversity studies ;vser. 2, v. 5, no. 3  a"Read before Section IV (Cytology and Heredity) of the Seventh International Zoological Congress, Boston, August 27, 1907."  aIncludes bibliographical references.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20250715lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aHeredityxgenetics12aChromosomesxgenetics 0aUniversity studies (University of Cincinnati) ;vser. 2, v. 5, no. 3.4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/0011353  aAUTH02324cgm a2200445 i 9918573277306676DNLM20260227162849.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2022    mdu088|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32739  a(OCoLC)1428107093  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 6172 aGender and Health Workshop Concurrent Session: Modifiable Factors & Clinical Interventionsd(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aGender and health workshop concurrent session :bmodifiable factors & clinical interventions /cORWH, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 28 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 26, 2022.3 a(CIT): In recognition of the significant health impacts of gender as a social and cultural variable, the NIH ORWH and the Eunice Kennedy Shriver NICHD convened a series of roundtable discussions for NIH staff members in the fall of 2021. Participants discussed the landscape of NIH-supported research on gender roles, gender norms, and gender inequity and identified opportunities in this space.  This scientific workshop is an exciting opportunity to convene the extramural community, NIH scientific and program staff members, and other stakeholders to discuss methods and best practices for biomedical and sociobehavioral research on gender roles, gender norms, and gender inequity.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 1, 2024).12aGender Identity22aSexism22aGender-Based Violence22aIntersectional Framework22aTransgender Persons22aIndigenous Peoples 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Research on Women's Health,eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=46414  aAUTH01669cas a2200505 a 9426239DNLM20260227091014.0cr|cn#||||||||981016c19729999tu mr p       0    0tur    a  2002243570  a1016-5169l1016-5169  zTKDAE  a(OCoLC)51215886  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aturaeng  alcd  9Turkey00aW1bTU85710aTurk Kardiyol Dern Ars2dnlm00aTürk Kardiyoloji Derneği arşivi :bTürk Kardiyoloji Derneğinin yayın organıdır.1 iIssues for <Sept. 2002-   > have parallel title:aArchives of the Turkish Society of Cardiology  aİstanbul :bTürk Kardiyoloji Derneği3132023- :aİstanbul, Turkey :bKare  av. :bill.  aMonthly,b1999-  a9 nos. a year,b1993-1998  aQuarterly,b<1988>-1992  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan in 1972.zCf. ISSN Online.  aDescription based on: Cilt 8 (Sayi 8, 1979); latest issue consulted: Cilt 30, Sayi 9 (Eylül 2002).  aPrint issues ceased with 2020.1 aMEDLINEbv36n1, Jan. 2008-1 aPubMedbv36n1, Jan. 2008-  aAlso issued online.  aTurkish, with English summaries.12aCardiovascular Diseases22aCardiology 2aPeriodical2 aTürk Kardiyoloji Derneği.0 x1308-44884 uhttps://www.archivestsc.com/  aAUTH01847cgm a2200433 a 101587393DNLM20260227154303.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120630s2012    mdu864 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17262  a(DNLM)CIT:17271  a(OCoLC)803800243  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWT 15500aAlzheimer's disease research summit 2012 :bpath to treatment and prevention /cNational Institute on Aging.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (14 hr., 24 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded May 14-15 2012.3 a(CIT): The Summit convenes national and international experts in Alzheimer's research. The key goal of the AD Research Summit 2012 is to formulate an integrated multidisciplinary research agenda that will accelerate the development of successful therapies for AD across the disease continuum. The Summit will also identify what types of resources/infrastructure and new public private partnerships are necessary to successfully implement this research agenda.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aAlzheimer Diseasextherapy22aBiomedical Research22aPublic-Private Sector Partnerships 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute on Aging.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1726240uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17271  aAUTH03791cgmaa2200517 a 7601687ADNLM20260202093247.0mr|caaadunaeauuac200009vf|cjahouvd|cvaizumr|caaadunarauuac200009hd|bfa012baap760811s1967    cau022  |||||     mleng    a(OCoLC)31656519  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aWF 658bVC no.6 196704aThe embattled cell /cUniversity of Southern California School of Medicine ; [made by] Wexler Films.  aLos Angeles :bThe School ; [New York : for loan by American Cancer Society],cc1967.  a1 film reel of 1 (792 ft.) :bsd., col. ;c16 mm.3master positive.  a1 film reel of 1 (792 ft.) :bsd., col. ;c16 mm.3answer print.  a1 cassette, 22 min. :bsd., col. ;c3/4 in.3viewing copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (22 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videodisc of 1 (DVD) (22 min.) :bsd., col. ;c4 3/4 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  aprojectedbg2rdamedia  afilm reelbmr2rdacarrier  aRating: Highly recommended.  aReview date: Nov. 1973.  aReviewer: American Association of Dental Schools (AADS).  aLearning method: Self instructional.  aSupported in part by the American Cancer Society, California Division.  aRussell P. Sherwin, Arnis Richters.8 aThe purpose of this program is to describe and demonstrate recent advances in research involving cells. Cancer cells which have been taken from a patient with lung cancer are kept alive for periods of weeks and studied by means of a contrast microscope and a time lapse motion picture camera. These studies have become the basis for understanding many diseases which damage or completely destroy lung tissue. While there are many such diseases, the research discussed in this film is concentrated on the complex problems caused by one of the most serious lung diseases - cancer. Over 100 lung cancers have been studied in this manner. Motion picture film of the slides is presented to demonstrate the structure and function of both cancer and normal cells.    The amount of foreign material picked up through the respiratory system is described and the effect of various pollutents such as gas irritants and cigarette smoke on the reserve breathing power and the lungs is discussed. The program emphasizes that cigarette smoke is the most important single factor which causes cancer and emphysema.    The film notes that each cancer displays significantly different characteristics. The preparation of the cells on the slides with the culture medium is demonstrated.  aDental: undergraduate; general and specialty graduate; continuing education.aMedical: undergraduate; continuing education.aSpecialty: pathology; pathology, oral.  aVideocassette (Betacam SP)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2003.e1 videocassette (22 min.) : cd., col. ; 1/2 in.  aVideodisc (DVD)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2016.e1 videodisc (22 min.) : sd., col. ; 4 3/4 in.  aThis item may be under copyright protection. Please ask copyright owner for permission before publishing.5DNLM1 dUniversity of Southern California School of MedicineoResearch date: 09/16/2011sSource: Item. 2aCells, Cultured 2aLung Neoplasmsxpathology1 aSherwin, Russell P.,d1924-1 aRichters, Arnis.2 aUniversity of Southern California.bSchool of Medicine.  aAUTH01314cam a22003137a 450000100100000000300050001000500170001500700140003200800410004603500190008704000210010604400180012706000190014510000420016424501070020626000420031330000110035533600260036633700270039233800350041950000580045453302020051254001420071458300390085658300430089565000210093865000320095999900090099161010160RDNLM20260209154143.0hd#afa012baca820514s1910    nyu     a     000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)7454681  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aQPbC152L 19101 aCalhoun, Laura Annice (Davis),d1847-14aThe law of sex determination and its practical application /cby Laura A. Calhoun (Mrs. E.E. Calhoun).  aNew York :bEugenics Pub. Co.,c1910.  a254 p.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  amicroformbh2rdamedia  amicrofilm reelbhd2rdacarrier  aNLM microfilm copy shelved as: Film 12014 no. 45DNLM  aMicrofilm.bPalo Alto, Calif. :cBay Area Microfilm,d1986.e1 microfilm reel ; 4 in., 35 mm. Low reduction. With: Man and the glacial period / by G. Frederick Wright. New York : D. Appleton, 1896.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202103172pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202103175DNLM12aSex Preselection22aSex Determination Processes  aAUTH02302cgm a22004454a 101267478DNLM20260227145147.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu244 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10092  a(OCoLC)65195196  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQS 11.100aDream anatomy symposium /cthe National Library of Medicine.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 8, 2006).  aStreaming video (4 hr., 4 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 12, 2003.3 a(CIT): Visionary Anatomies A Symposium at the National Library of Medicine.  Anatomical showmanship goes back to anatomy's origins as a scientific discipline: for centuries, anatomy contained a theatrical element that was amazing, but troubling. Today, we are seeing a revival of anatomical spectacle. The work of anatomist Gunther von Hagens, Damien Hirst and other artists, and a host of popular films and television shows featuring graphic images of the body and its interior, have sparked new debates about the meaning, uses, ethics and history of anatomical display. Visionary Anatomies brings together scholars from the fields of anatomy, medicine, cultural history, museum studies, anthropology and the history of medicine and science to discuss the vital issues that attend the art and science of anatomy and anatomical presentation, past and present.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aAnatomy22aAnatomy, Artistic22aDissection22aHistory of Medicine22aHuman Body 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10092  aAUTH03577cgm a2200469 i 9918332578806676DNLM20260227161832.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220104s2021    mdu547|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32118  a(DNLM)CIT:32119  a(OCoLC)1330399850  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 309 AA12 aBuilding Unifying Systems of Care Addressing Comorbidities in Women and Girls (Workshop)d(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aBuilding unifying systems of care addressing comorbidities in women and girls /cNHLBI, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (9 hr., 7 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded December 16 & 17, 2021.3 a(CIT): This virtual workshop aims to address the gaps and opportunities for advancing community adoption and implementation of gender-relevant evidence-based mental health, substance use, cardiopulmonary, and metabolic disease prevention, and treatment interventions for women from high-risk and marginalized communities across the lifespan. Addressing the social determinants of health and building infrastructure to ensure sustainability of innovations will be important cross-cutting themes. The workshop will bring together experts, including researchers, clinicians, and community and federal partners, to: 1) present the current state of research and critical gaps which need to be addressed in order to accelerate adoption of care models that integrate clinical and population health within well-coordinated, data-driven systems of care; 2) examine how an integrated set of evidence-based practices delivered across the entire continuum of health care services (i.e., prevention services, primary care, specialty healthcare, behavioral health care and health maintenance) in community-based settings can improve health outcomes in women and girls.  The workshop will feature talks and discussion on systems of care designed to prevent and treat cardiopulmonary, metabolic and psychiatric disorders, and multi-morbidities across these disease domains by integrating behavioral and biomedical approaches. Building infrastructures that deliver these integrated interventions to women in vulnerable or underserved populations, and/or at high-risk (e.g. peripartum) stages of the lifespan will be an important cross-cutting theme. As well as the expert speakers, workshop registration is open to all with an intention to reach a broad audience including trainees, junior investigators, NIH and other Federal Partners as well as community stakeholders.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed June 7, 2021).12aWomen's Health Services22aMultimorbidity22aVulnerable Populations22aDelivery of Health Care, Integrated22aEvidence-Based Medicine 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute,xsponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4431940uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44321  aAUTH01476cgm a22003734a 101267434DNLM20260227145034.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu012 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10047  a(OCoLC)66524872  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWK 75500aNIH antenatal corticosteroids press conference /cthe primary sponsors of this meeting are the National Institute of Child Health and Human Development and the NIH Office of Medical Applications of Research.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional 