ivision of Extramural Activities.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 30, 2007).  aStreaming video (9 hr., 49 min., 53 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 4-5, 2002.3 a(CIT): This is a regularly scheduled meeting of the National Cancer Advisory Board (NCAB).  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.)12aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration12aNeoplasms22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1146140uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11464  aAUTH04191cgm a2200469 i 101693818DNLM20260227155648.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u161005s2016    mdu071|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19838  a(OCoLC)1074226104  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 20300aInvadopodia, the scaffold protein Tks5, and cancer invasion and metastasis /cSara A. Courtneidge. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 11 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCI's Center for Cancer Research (CCR) grand rounds  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded September 9, 2016.3 a(CIT): NCI's Center for Cancer Research (CCR) Grand Rounds Dr. Sara A. Courtneidge was born in the UK, and received her B.Sc. from the University of Leeds and her Ph.D. from the National Institute for Medical Research, London. Following postdoctoral study at the University of California, San Francisco, and an independent position at the National Institute for Medical Research, she joined the European Molecular Biology Laboratory in Heidelberg, Germany in 1985, where she rose to the position of Senior Scientist. Changing course in 1994, Dr. Courtneidge joined SUGEN Inc., where as Chief Scientist she guided novel kinase discovery and validation efforts in oncology. From 2001-2005 she was Distinguished Scientific Investigator at the Van Andel Research Institute in Grand Rapids, Michigan, and from 2005-2014 she served as Director of Academic Affairs, and Professor and Director of the Tumor Microenvironment and Metastasis Program at the Sanford Burnham Prebys Medical Discovery Institute in La Jolla, California. She joined OHSU in 2014, and is Associate Director for Translational Sciences in the Knight Cancer Institute, as well as a Professor in the Departments of Cell, Developmental and Cancer Biology and Biomedical Engineering, and a member of the OHSU Center for Spatial Systems Biomedicine. Dr. Courtneidge's laboratory has studied the Src family of protein tyrosine kinases for a number of years, and has contributed to the understanding of Src transformation, regulation, substrate selection and function. Currently, her research on Src and it substrates focuses on cancer invasion and metastasis, with particular emphasis on the role of membrane structures called podosomes or invadopodia. She also retains an interest in translational research, with the goal of defining novel therapeutic points of intervention for cancer treatment. Dr. Courtneidge's contributions to cancer research have been recognized with numerous honors, including election to the European Molecular Biology Organization, the Jubilee Lecture and Harden Medal of the British Biochemical Society, the Feodor Lynen Lecture and Lynen Medal, an honorary doctorate from the University of Leeds, and, in 2015, the AACR-Women in Cancer Research Charlotte Friend Memorial Lectureship.  NCI's Center for Cancer Research (CCR) Grand Rounds is a weekly lecture series addressing current research in clinical and molecular oncology. Speakers are leading national and international researchers and clinicians.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (Oct. 24, 2016).12aNeoplasm Invasivenessxphysiopathology12aNeoplasm Metastasisxphysiopathology22aAdaptor Proteins, Vesicular Transportxphysiology22aCell Transformation, Neoplastic22aPodosomesxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCourtneidge, Sara,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19838  aAUTH02312cgm a22004094a 101267827DNLM20260227145629.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu056 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10740  a(OCoLC)65360769  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 58.51 aReynolds, Kristy J.10aDevelopment of the proteolytic 18O labeling method for comparative proteomics /cKristy J. Reynolds.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 14, 2006).  aStreaming video (55 min., 8 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 11, 2003.3 a(CIT): As the sequencing is completed of many species' genomes, the study of the protein compliment to the genome, or the proteome, has emerged as a dynamic field of research. A common approach to characterizing changed states is comparative proteomics, in which the relative amounts of proteins present in two or more samples are compared. In order to determine the  relative amount of the proteins present, a proteolytic method for 18O labeling can be used. Briefly, pools of proteins are enzymatically digested in parallel in H216O and H218O. In the latter pool two atoms of 18O are incorporated into the carboxyl-terminus of each new peptide. Comparative proteomic studies can be performed by mixing the unlabeled peptide pool (generated in H216O) and the isotope labeled peptide pool (from H218O) and analyzing the peptide pairs by mass spectrometry.   Mass Spectrometry Interest Group of the NCI at Frederick.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aProteomics22aDrug Resistance22aMass Spectrometry 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10740  aAUTH01341cgm a22003974a 101267792DNLM20260227145916.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s1999    mdu045 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10672  a(OCoLC)64263501  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWD 200.5.M61 aRennert, Owen M.,d1938-10aClinical considerations of mitochondrial dysfunction /cOwen Rennert.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c1999]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 15, 2006).  aStreaming video (45 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 30, 1999.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aMitochondrial Diseases22aChild22aInfant 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10672  aAUTH01744cgm a22004694a 101267917DNLM20260227145910.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu542 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11010  a(DNLM)CIT:11011  a(OCoLC)123052278  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2061 aUnited States.bNational Cancer Advisory Board.bMeetingn(123rd :d2002 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNCAB, National Cancer Advisory Board /cthe National Cancer Institute (NCI), Di