aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 6, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 12 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 27, 2001.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aEstrogens, Conjugated (USP)xtherapeutic use22aEstradiolxmetabolism22aEstrogensxmetabolism22aHeartxdrug effects22aNervous Systemxdrug effects 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=1235  aAUTH03897nam a2200445 i 9918986616306676DNLM20260225105927.0230919s2024    nyua     bf   001 0 eng    aGBC3I24972bnb  a9781032593180qhardback  a1032593180  a9781032594729qpaperback  a1032594721  z9781000987904qePub ebook  z9781000987881qPDF ebook  a(OCoLC)1400730299  aUKMGBbengerdacUKMGBdOCLCOdBDXdOCLCQdYDXdOCLCO  apcc  9United States 4aR856.6b.J88 202400aW 4904a610.2842231 aJuuso, Ilkka,eauthor.10aMedical-grade software development :bhow to build medical-device products that meet the requirements of IEC 62304 and ISO 13485 /cIlkka Juuso, Ilpo Pöyhönen. 1aNew York, NY :bRoutledge,c2024.  axxvii, 338 pages :billustrations (black and white) ;c26 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"A Productivity Press book".  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  a"This book is a practical guide to meeting IEC 62304 software development requirements within the context of an ISO 13485 quality management system (QMS). It proves it can be done with a minimum amount of friction, overlap, and back-and-forth between development stages. It essentially shows you how you should shape your medical software development processes to fit in with the QMS processes in the smartest and leanest way possible. By following the advice in this book, you can reuse processes from your QMS, ensure your product realization processes meet the requirements for medical software development, and marry all the requirements together using tried and tested solutions into one efficient system. The expertise of the authors here goes beyond just the experiences of one real-world project as they tap into over 30 years of experience and countless software and software assessment projects to distill their advice. The book takes a hands-on approach by first teaching you the top 25 lessons to know before starting to develop a process for medical software development -- It then walks you through the expectations placed on the key aspects of such a process by the key standards. The book progresses from an overview of both standards and the general requirements involved to a detailed discussion of the expected stages from software development and maintenance to risk management, configuration management, and problem resolution. The book provides insightful advice on how the requirements of the IEC 62304 software development lifecycle can be married with ISO 13485 QMS, how the development of the technical file should be organized, and how to address conformity assessment, the daily after approval, and the recent trends that will affect the industry in the coming years. The book is modeled after the IEC 62304 standard and adopts its clause structure in the numbering of sections for easy reference. The book does not attempt to replicate either standard. For the ISO 13485 standard, it recites the necessary requirements succinctly. For IEC 62304, the discussion is in-depth and also addresses the impact of ISO 13485 on the requirements discussed. In this way, the book drills into both standards to expose the core of each requirement and shape these into a practical, cohesive workflow for developing, maintaining, and improving a Lean software development pipeline"--cProvided by publisher.12aEquipment Designxstandards12aSoftware Design22aSoftware Validation22aEquipment Safetyxstandards 2aHandbook1 aPöyhönen, Ilpo,eauthor.  aAUTH01616cgm a22004814a 101268105DNLM20260227150038.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu050 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11370  a(OCoLC)70829534  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5451 aBolland, Silvia.10aInhibitory signaling pathways and their role in preventing autoimmune disease /cSilvia Bolland.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 4, 2006).  aStreaming video (49 min., 15 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 18, 2002.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aAutoimmunity22aAntibodies, Antinuclear22aAntigens, CD22aAutoimmune Diseases22aB-Lymphocytes22aGenetic Predisposition to Disease22aReceptors, IgG22aSignal Transduction 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11370  aAUTH01524cgm a22004214a 101268157DNLM20260227145347.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu325 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11467  a(OCoLC)1019841188  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2062 aNCI Board of Scientific Advisors.bMeetingn(17th :d2001 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNCI, Board of Scientific Advisors /cNCI, Committee Management Office.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 27, 2008).  aStreaming video (5 hr., 24 min., 53 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 5, 2001.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work