ional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 9, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 9 min., 9 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 16, 2008.3 a(CIT): Dr. Nepom has contributed much to our general understanding of the structure and function of MHC class II molecules, and he has specifically focused on their link to autoimmune diseases such as type 1 diabetes. His group has also designed MHC class II multimers, and used these as tools to study human autoreactive T cells. Such measurements of autoantigen-specific T cells are particularly important in the context of measuring specificity and efficacy of new immunomodulatory treatments for autoimmune diseases.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDiabetes Mellitus, Type 1ximmunology22aCD4-Positive T-Lymphocytes22aImmunotherapy 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14437  aAUTH01688cgm a22004334a 101268218DNLM20260227150157.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu124 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11534  a(OCoLC)84714020  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 3501 aBonifacino, Juan S.10aDemystifying medicine.pLysosomes :bbiology and diseases /cJuan Bonaficino [sic] and Bill Gahl.30aLysosomes1 iTitle on title frame:aLysosomes :ba story of discovery at the interface of biology and medicine  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 3, 2007).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 3 min., 4 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 8, 2003.3 a(CIT): "Demystifying Medicine" is a weekly course, primarily for Ph.D. students, post-doctoral fellows, and staff at the National Institutes of Health. 