actices, policies, politics /cOffice of NIH History.  aUnited States.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health, Health & Human Services, 2005]  a7 videocassettes of 7 (DVCPRO) (5 hrs., 51 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/4 in.3duplication copy.  a9 videocassettes of 9  (Betacam SP) (644 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 1, 2006).  aStreaming video (5 hr., 51 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 5-6, 2005.  aVideocassette (Betacam SP)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2012.e9 videocassettes (644 min.) : sd., col. ; 1/2 in.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomedical Researchxhistory22aPublic Policy22aHistory, 20th Century 2aUnited States2 aDeWitt Stetten, Jr. Museum of Medical Research.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=463840uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4639  aAUTH02739cgm a2200445 i 101737000DNLM20260227155837.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u181102s2018    mdu291 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:26122  a(OCoLC)1076548224  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 1602 aHIV DRP Conference on the Establishment and Control of Viral Persistenced(2018 :cFrederick, Md.)jauthor.14aThe establishment and control of viral persistence /cHIV Dynamics and Replication Program, Center for Cancer Research, NCI, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2018]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (4 hr., 51 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aHow does HIV persist and what can we do about it? / Johen W. Mellors -- New strategies for HIV eradication : lessons from CD8 depletion studies / Guido Silvestri -- How Epstein-Barr virus and Kaposi's sarcoma herpesvirus persist in lymphoid infections / Arthur Sugden -- KSHV : immune evasion and persistence / Blossom Damania -- Small RNA pathways and virus persistence in mosquitoes / Maria Carla Saleh -- HPV vaccines : inducing persistent antibodies to prevent a persistent oncogenic infection / John Schiller.  aRecorded October 17, 2018.3 a(CIT): The HIV Dynamics and Replication Program, Center for Cancer Research, NCI is organizing this half-day conference to showcase the latest findings in the broad field of viral persistence.  A major barrier to the successful eradication of many viral infections, including HIV, herpes viruses, Ebola virus, and others, persistence is governed by a complex web of viral and cellular factors acting at the level of individuals and populations.  An in-depth discussion of how viruses persist, while hosts resist, should lead to new strategies aimed at eliminating viruses entrenched in humans.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 26, 2018).12aVirus Latency22aHerpesvirus 4, Humanxphysiology22aHerpesvirus 8, Humanxphysiology22aHIVxphysiology22aPapillomavirus Vaccines 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aHIV Dynamics and Replication Program (U.S.),eorganizer.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?26122  aAUTH01894cas a22005414a 101155398DNLM20260224111542.0m        a        cr|cnu||||||||020823c20029999caufr p       0   a0eng    acn2002301309  a200230130950 a1499-9013l1499-901324  a(OCoLC)50473163  aNLCbengcNLCdDNLM  anlcaisds/canlmcopyc  9United States06aPER.REG.2002.76200aW1bIN766KM1 a61421310aInt J Forensic Ment Health2dnlm0 aInt. j. forensic ment. health 0aInternational journal of forensic mental health00aInternational journal of forensic mental health.30aForensic mental health  aBurnaby, B.C. :bSimon Fraser University, Mental Health, Law and Policy Institute,c2002-3132025- :a[Thousand Oaks, CA] :bSage Publications  av. ;c28 cm.  aSemiannual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (spring 2002)-  aTitle from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 2, no. 1 (spring 2003).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAvailable also online.  a"The official journal of of the International Association of Forensic Mental Health Services".12aForensic Psychiatry22aCriminal Psychology 2aPeriodical2 aSimon Fraser University.bMental Health, Law, and Policy Institute.2 aInternational Association of Forensic Mental Health Services.0 x1932-99034 uhttps://www.tandfonline.com/loi/ufmh204 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/FMH  aAUTH01086nam a2200289 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390350021000800400056001010430012001570440012001690600033001812450196002142640055004103000022004653360026004873370028005133380027005416500043005686500056006116500027006676500027006946510019007217100047007409990009007879918227356906676DNLM20260223134126.0091112r18661865enk           000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)466469880  aCUDbengcCUDdOCLCQdOCLCFdOCLCOdEUWdOCLCQerda  ae-uk---  9England00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5552 no.1900aIdiot asylums :breprinted by permission from the "Edinburgh review", no. CCXLIX, July, 1865 : and published for the benefit of the Earlswood Asylum, Redhill, Surrey /crevised by the author. 1aLondon :bLongman, Green, Reader, and Dyer,c1866.  a44 pages ;c19 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier12aPersons with Intellectual Disabilities22aEducation of Persons with Intellectual Disabilities22aHospitals, Psychiatric22aResidential Facilities 2aUnited Kingdom2 aRoyal Earlswood (Redhill, Surrey, England)  aAUTH02986cgm a2200457 i 101743228DNLM20260227160017.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190302s2019    mdu102|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27302  a(OCoLC)1105307778  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 10000aDemystifying medicine 2019.pExploring deep sea eco systems and human disease /cStefan Sievert, John Dekker.1 aExploring deep sea eco systems and human disease 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 42 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded February 5, 2019.3 a(CIT): Take a voyage with us into the deep.  Stefan Sievert, a microbial oceanographer at the Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution, will provide a glimpse into the harsh and bizarre world at the bottom of the ocean -- where life survives without sunlight and under pressure high enough to crush the metal submersibles sent there to explore.  In what could constitute as an extreme biopsy, Sievert has been among the first to collect microbial samples from the very bottom of the sea floor and below.  His treasure-trove haul is providing an understanding of the diversity and function of microbial life in the oceans and its implication for both health and disease in all species above, including us landlubbers.   Coming up for air, we then will hear from John Dekker, chief of NIAID"s Bacterial Pathogenesis and Antimicrobial Resistance Unit.  Dekker studies extremophiles of our own making in the form of microbes grown resistant to antibiotics.  He uses genomic techniques to understand the evolutionary mechanisms by which resistance emerges in this natural context; and his talk will focus on next-generation sequencing for infectious disease diagnostics, including metagenomic and nanopore-based approaches.     Don your Jacques Cousteau cap and, as always, be prepared to be dazzled and demystified.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title bar (viewed March 24, 2019).12aMicrobiota12aOceans and Seas22aDrug Resistance, Microbialxphysiology22aGastrointestinal Microbiomexdrug effects22aGastrointestinal Microbiomexgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aSievert, Stefan,espeaker.1 aDekker, Johnc(Of National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases)espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27302  aAUTH02834cgm a22004454a 101490638DNLM20260227152802.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081126s2008    mdu067 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14762  a(OCoLC)319068639  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 1601 aCristea, Ileana M.10aUncovering protein complexes & assemblies  /cIleana M. Cristea.3 aUncovering protein complexes and assemblies  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Mar.17, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 6 min., 37 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 7, 2008.3 a(CIT): Isolation of protein complexes, if performed appropriately, can provide an invaluable shortcut to uncovering protein interactions and to gaining clues towards their biological functions. The literature is, justifiably so, replete with approaches designed for the study of protein interactions. Advances are, nevertheless, highly desirable. An ideal isolation would maintain the protein complex or a larger assembly of interest as close as possible to its original state in the cell. To date, achieving this ideal isolation remains a challenge. The identification of transient or weak interacting partners and the stoichiometry within a complex present difficulties. We have recently reported an approach for the rapid and efficient isolation of protein complexes. We demonstrated that a combination of cryogenic-based cell lysis and fast immunoaffinity purifications helps to maintain interactions, minimizing nonspecific associations and maximizing the recovery of transiently interacting partners. This presentation will underline some of the technical aspects that were found to be important in studying protein complexes, as well as macromolecular assemblies. Highlights will be shown from our studies of the dynamic viral-host protein interactions during the course of infections with Sindbis and human cytomegalovirus HCMV. Our results revealed cellular pathways utilized by these viruses to manipulate host systems.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aViral Proteinsxisolation & purification22aCytomegalovirus22aHost-Pathogen Interactions22aSindbis Virus 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14762  aAUTH01438cgm a22004454a 101490644DNLM20260227152734.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081126s2008    mdu142 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14770  a(OCoLC)320492500  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 201 aWilfond, Benjamin.10aEthical and Regulatory Aspects of Clinical Research.pGenetics and stored tissue /cBen Wilfond and Sara Chandros Hull.3 aGenetics and stored tissue  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 7, 2009).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 21 min., 21 sec. : sd., col.).  a"Session 8."  aRecorded Nov. 12, 2008.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aEthics, Research12aGeneticsxethics22aGenetic Privacyxethics22aTissue Banksxethics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14770  aAUTH01367cas a22004455a 101175716DNLM20260227090920.0cr|cn ||||||||030421cuuuu9999cauar         0    0eng    a  2003243396  a1569-1861l1569-1861  a(OCoLC)52852765  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9United States00aW1bHO558JG10aHong Kong J Occup Ther2dnlm00aHong Kong journal of occupational therapy :bHKJOT.30aHKJOT  aHong Kong :bElsevier313<2018-> :aThousand Oaks, CA :bSAGE  av.  aAnnual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aDescription based on: Vol. 12 (Dec. 2002); title from cover.  aPrint issues ceased with 2017.0 aPubMedbv27n1, 2016-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.  aOfficial publication of the Hong Kong Occupational Therapy Association.12aOccupational Therapy 2aHong Kong2 aHong Kong Occupational Therapy Association.0 x1876-43984 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=1011757164 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/hjo  aAUTH01471nam a2200349 i 101779135DNLM20260204134751.0210512s1885    nyu           000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5574 no.281 aHewitt, Abram S.q(Abram Stevens),d1822-1903eauthor.10aProfits and possibilities of the proper organization of charity :ban address by the Hon. Abram S. Hewitt, delivered at the 4th annual meeting of the Charity Organization Society of the City of Buffalo, April 14, 1885. 1a[Buffalo] :b[publisher not identified],c[1887?]  a8 pages ;c21 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  aAt head of title: Charity Organization Society of the City of Buffalo.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM12aCharities22aIndustrial Development22aPoverty22aInterpersonal Relations 2aAddress2 aCharity Organization Society of Buffalo (N.Y.).bAnnual Meetingn(4th :d1885 :cBuffalo, N.Y.)  aAUTH01257cam a2200313 i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002000073040003500093044001800128060003100146100004800177245011600225264006800341300002200409336002600431337002800457338002700485500002200512540014200534583003900676583007200715610004300787650003200830650005400862651001800916999000900934101187266DNLM20260227155020.0030805s1918    pau           000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)40614754  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 404 no.31 aFisher, Charles Perry,d1857-1940,eauthor.13aAn account of the Library of the College of Physicians of Philadelphia, 1788-1918 /c[by Charles Perry Fisher]. 1aPhiladelphia :bCollege of Physicians of Philadelphia,c[1918?]  a17 pages ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aTitle from cover.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202102242pda5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20260227lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM22aCollege of Physicians of Philadelphia.12aLibraries, Medicalxhistory22aLibraries, Medicalxorganization & administration 2aUnited States  aAUTH02319cgm a2200457 i 9918592672206676DNLM20260227163449.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230429s2023    mdu569|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33045  a(OCoLC)1437827972  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWQ 202 aECHO/NIH Return of Individual Research Results to Participants Workshopd(2023 :cOnline)jauthor.10aECHO/NIH return of individual research results to participants workshop (day 1 & 2) /cNIH Environmental influences on Child Health Outcomes (ECHO). 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (9 hr., 29 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded March 16 & 17, 2023.3 a(CIT): Purpose: Identify best practices for large-sample studies of pregnancy and childhood to ethically and feasibly return individual research results to participants.<p>Crosscutting Themes:<ul><li>How should recommendations differ for returning individual results to pregnant people and children versus those of other adults?</li><li>Most research on returning individual results focuses on genetics results; how can we broaden to include environmental data from bioassays, physical measures, and self-reports?</li><li>How can the workshop guide facilitation of equity in return of individual results?</li></ul>  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed May 28, 2024).12aTruth Disclosurexethics12aResearch Subjects12aPregnant People22aChild22aSampling Studies22aBenchmarkingxethics 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aEnvironmental influences on Child Health Outcomes (ECHO) Program,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4933040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49334  aAUTH02402cgm a2200505 i 9918592678406676DNLM20260227163353.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230429s2023    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33002  a(OCoLC)1388202745  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWQ 200 AA100aPreventing unplanned pregnancies in clinical research :bbalancing science, safety, and ethics /cEvan Myers.1 iTitle on PowerPoint screen:aAvoiding unplanned pregnancy in clinical research 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aOHSRP lecture  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded March 2, 2023.3 a(CIT): We are pleased to welcome our guest speaker, Evan Myers, MD, MPH, who is the Walter L. Thomas Distinguished Professor of Obstetrics and Gynecology in the Duke University School of Medicine as well as Vice-Chair for Reproductive Risks at the Duke Health System Institutional Review Board.  Dr. Myers will address the following topics: Brief historical overview of approaches to excluding pregnant people from research; Definitions and implications of "childbearing potential"; The "value" of pregnancy testing; Common issues with protocols and consent forms; Research and policy needs.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed June 22, 2023).12aPregnancy, Unplanned12aResearch Subjects22aPregnant People.22aPatient Selectionxethics22aRisk Reduction Behavior22aHuman Experimentationxethics 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aMyers, Evan R.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Human Subjects Research Protections,esponsoring body. 0aOHSRP education series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49210  aAUTH01575cgm a22004454a 101268364DNLM20260227150610.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu053 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11824  a(OCoLC)71273664  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4771 aGordenin, Dmitry.10aInhibition of mismatch repair by cadmium /cDmitry Gordenin.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Aug. 22, 2006).  aStreaming video (52 min., 10 sec. : sd., col.).  a"Sponsored by NIH DNA Repair Interest Group"--Title screen.  aRecorded Feb. 17, 2004.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aMutagenesisxdrug effects22aBase Pair Mismatchxdrug effects22aCadmiumxtoxicity22aDNA Repairxdrug effects22aMutagensxtox