  vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11068  a(OCoLC)70713346  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWP 8701 aThorp, Richard.10aClinical breast examination simulator /cRichard Thorp.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 10, 2006).  aStreaming video (21 min., 25 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 2, 2000.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aBreast Neoplasmsxdiagnosis12aPhysical Examinationxmethods22aComputer-Assisted Instruction22aEducational Technology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11068  aAUTH02028cgm a2200457 i 101778049DNLM20260227160913.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210408s2021    mdu640 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31590  a(DNLM)CIT:31591  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 3372 aNIA Workshop on Bilingualism and Cognitive Reserve and Resilienced(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNIA Workshop on Bilingualism and Cognitive Reserve and Resilience /cNational Institute on Aging, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (10 hr., 40 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded March 2 & 3, 2021.3 a(CIT): The workshop will feature a series of brief presentations and lively discussion around the following topics: 1) Bilingualism across the lifespan and its impact on reserve and resilience, 2) factors complicating the study of bilingualism and its impact on cognition and the brain, and 3) mechanisms by which bilingualism may drive neuroplasticity in the brain.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed August 25, 2021).12aCognitive Reservexphysiology12aMultilingualism22aBrainxphysiology22aAgingxphysiology22aCognitive Dysfunctionxprevention & control22aNeuronal Plasticity 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute on Aging,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4154040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41551  aAUTH01384pam a2200421 i 9918986604706676DNLM20260219160206.0t|240314s2024    njua        b 001 0 eng    aGBC4275862bnb  a9781119715535qhardback  a1119715539  z9781119715696qePub ebook8 aCIPO000060849  a(OCoLC)1458049571  aUKMGBbengerdacUKMGBdOCLCO  apcc  9United States00aWL 14004a616.82230 aNeurology (Clarke)10aNeurology :ba Queen Square textbook /cedited by Robin Howard, Dimitri Kullmann, David Werring, Michael Zandi.  aThird edition . 1aHoboken, NJ :bWiley Blackwell,c2024.  axxiii, 1253 pages :billustrations ;c28 cm  atext2rdacontent  aunmediated2rdamedia  avolume2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.12aNervous System Diseases1 aHoward, Robinc(Neurologist)eeditor.1 aKullmann, Dimitri,eeditor.1 aWerring, David J.,d1967-eeditor.1 aZandi, Michael,eeditor.2 aInstitute of Neurology, Queen Square.2 aNational Hospital for Neurology and Neurosurgery (London, England)  aAUTH03043cgm a2200493 i 9918681339906676DNLM20260227164125.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230929s2023    mdu128|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33468  a(OCoLC)1524053441  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 300 AA12 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bHealth Disparities Interest Group.bWorkshopd(2023 :cOnline)eauthor.10aNIH Health Disparities Interest Group Workshop 2023 :bwho we are: the multiple dimensions of race and ethnicity /cNational Institutes of Health.1 aHDIG workshop NIH research festival1 aWho we are: the multiple dimensions of race and ethnicity 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 8 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 19, 2023.3 a(CIT): Race and ethnicity are social constructs - meaning they were created for social and political reasons and do not accurately reflect inherent differences in human biology. The existence of racial and ethnic categories is closely linked to United States history. They are also conflated with cultures that people identify with and share, but often group together people with diverse cultural heritages. Meanwhile, self-identified race and ethnicity can be incongruent with socially assigned race and ethnicity, and how individuals are being treated in society appears to depend on socially assigned race and ethnicity. To add further complexity, "genetic ancestry" is often used in the scientific literature in a manner that might appear to justify a biological conception of race. How do these different dimensions of race and ethnicity fit together? How do we measure each of these dimensions? This workshop will present a panel of speakers to discuss these issues, with reserved time for open dialogue with the audience on these topics.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed June 11, 2025).12aRacial Groups22aEthnicity22aHealth Inequities22aSocial Determinants of Health22aKidney Diseasesxgenetics 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bHealth Disparities Interest Group,ehost institution.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bUNITE Initiativeehost institution.2 aNational Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=52264  aAUTH02598cgm a2200421 i 9918681341206676DNLM20260227164106.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230929s2023    mdu423|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33451  a(OCoLC)1467156895  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 202 aEvolution of Mental Health Research (Symposium)d(2023cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.14aThe evolution of mental health research /cNational Institute of Mental Health, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (7 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 13, 2023.3 a(CIT): Starting in September 2023, NIMH will celebrate 75 years of transforming the understanding and treatment of mental illnesses. The celebration kicks off with the "Evolution of Mental Health Research" symposium, which highlights key advances in mental health research over the past 75 years.  This symposium offers a unique opportunity to gain insights from distinguished thought leaders and learn how developments in neuroscience, genetics, and behavioral research are leading to exciting new discoveries shaping the future of mental health research.  Event highlights include:   Opening remarks from leaders at the National Institutes of Health, including NIMH Director Dr. Joshua A. Gordon , M.D., Ph.D..; presentations from scientific experts highlighting exciting breakthroughs and new discoveries in genetics, imaging, systems, neuroscience, services, and interventions; a panel discussion with thought leaders in mental health research; and a poster session featuring work from NIMH"s Division of Intramural Research Programs .  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed October 8, 2024).22aNational Institute of Mental Health (U.S.)12aMental 