4a 101267928DNLM20260227145919.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu035 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11069  a(OCoLC)70713347  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 2901 aKraus, Lewis E.10aDig deep /cLewis Kraus.10iTitle on PowerPoint screen:aDig deeper :ba CD-ROM and website to prevent tobacco initiation  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 11, 2006).  aStreaming video (35 min., 28 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 2, 2000.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aTobacco Use Disorderxprevention & control22aAdolescent22aComputer-Assisted Instruction22aEducational Technology22aSmoking Prevention 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11069  aAUTH04056nam a2200397 i 9918986626506676DNLM20260227084014.0230422s2023    enka     b    001 0 eng    a  2023940185  aGBC3H60282bnb  a0192847287q(hardcover)  a9780192847287q(hardcover)  z9780191939662qebook  z9780192663030q(electronic bk.)  a(OCoLC)1376725418  aYDXbengerdacYDXdOCLCOdUKMGBdOCLCFdOCLCOdOCLCQdYDXdBDXdMUU  apcc  9England 4aQH581.2b.L96 202400aQU 30004a571.62231 aLynch, Michael,d1951-eauthor.10aEvolutionary cell biology :bthe origins of cellular architecture /cMichael Lynch. 1aOxford ;aNew York, NY :bOxford University Press,c2024  axxv, 675 pages :billustrations (chiefly color) ;c25 cm  atext2rdacontent  aunmediated2rdamedia  avolume2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.0 aIntroduction. Evolutionary cell biology -- The origin of cells -- The major lines of descent -- The genetic mechanisms of evolution. The population-genetic environment -- Evolution as a population-genetic process -- Evolution of cellular complexity -- Basic cellular features. The cellular environment -- Evolutionary scaling relationships in cell biology -- Cell growth and division --  The cell life cycle -- Cellular senescence -- Structural evolution. The protein world -- Multimerization -- Protein management -- Lipids and membranes -- Cytoskeleton, cell shape, and motility -- Energetics and metabolism. The costs of cellular features -- Resource acquisition and homeostasis -- Enzymes and metabolic pathways -- Information processing. Intracellular errors -- Transcription -- Environmental sensing -- Organismal complexity. Endosymbiosis -- Origins of organismal complexity.  a"The fields of molecular evolution, genome evolution, and evolutionary genetics are now well-established. Remarkably, however, although all evolutionary modifications begin at the cellular level, and despite the advances made in cell biology and microbiology over the past few decades, there is as yet no recognised discipline of evolutionary cell biology. The goal of this book is to help establish the foundations for this emerging field. Its principal aims are twofold: firstly, to promote an understanding among evolutionary biologists as to why the cellular details matter if we are to understand the mechanisms of evolution; secondly, to make clear to non-evolutionary biologists - cell biologists in particular - that evolution is not just a matter of natural selection and optimization, but a process whose reach depends on other population genetic features such as mutation, recombination, and random genetic drift. Although there are many excellent books on cell biology, microbiology, and biophysics, almost no attention is given to evolution. Likewise, although there are numerous evolutionary biology books on the market, none of them gives more than passing attention to details at the cellular level. Thus Evolutionary Cell Biology is genuinely novel, offering a broader understanding of evolutionary processes and an appreciation for the many interesting problems that remain to be solved at the cellular and subcellular levels. This advanced textbook is aimed at both cell biologists and evolutionary biologists. It will be accessible to upper-level undergraduates in biology, and certainly to graduate students in all areas of the life sciences. Professionals from a wide range of fields - cell biology, microbiology, evolution, biophysics, biochemistry, and mathematics - will be exposed to entirely new ideas not traditionally covered in their primary fields of expertise."--Provided by publisher.12aCell Biology22aBiological Evolution22aCell Physiological Phenomena  aAUTH01118nam a2200301 i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003100073044001800104060003200122100006300154245005700217264005400274300004400328336002600372337002800398338002700426500005700453540014200510583003900652583004300691650001000734650002100744650001200765730003000777999000900807101778216DNLM20260226072915.0210416s1885    nyua          000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5571 no.71 aRanney, Ambrose L.q(Ambrose Loomis),d1848-1905,eauthor.14aThe brain of man, its architecture and requirements. 1a[New York] :b[publisher not identified],c[1885]  apages 632-641 :billustrations ;c25 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aDetached from Harpers monthly magazine, March, 1885.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM12aBrain22aMental Processes22aNeurons0 aHarpers monthly magazine.  aAUTH01419cgm a22004574a 101285706DNLM20260227144806.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u