 Dan,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center,esponsoring body. 0aClinical Center grand rounds great teachers series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49095  aAUTH02338cgm a2200421 i 9918230799506676DNLM20260227161417.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210707s2021    mdu202|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31816  a(OCoLC)1288657135  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWX 1752 aAccelerating FHIR: Virtual Workshop on Advancing the Use of FHIR in Researchd(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aAccelerating fast health care interoperability resources (FHIR) :bvirtual workshop on advancing the use of FHIR in research /cNIH Office of Data Science Strategy and NLM. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (3 hr., 22 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 29, 2021.3 a(CIT): This workshop is sponsored by the National Institutes of Health (NIH) Office of Data Science Strategy (ODSS) and the National Library of Medicine (NLM) to advance the use of FHIR in biomedical research. The goal of this workshop is to convene experts with the knowledge and experience in using FHIR in research to inform approaches that advance data exchange, accessibility, interoperability, and harmonization. This one-day workshop will bring together researchers, patient advocates, technology developers, and health IT organizations to explore the suitability and role of FHIR in research and how NIH can advance the use of FHIR in research.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 10, 2021).12aHealth Information Interoperability22aElectronic Health Records22aResearch 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Data Science Strategy,esponsoring body.2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42177  aAUTH02593cgm a2200445 i 9918230799706676DNLM20260227161412.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210707s2021    mdu528|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31813  a(DNLM)CIT:31817  a(OCoLC)1299139033  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 3012 aFrom BRAIN to Bedside: Translation of Next-Generation Circuit Therapies (Workshop)d(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aFrom BRAIN to bedside :btranslation of next-Generation circuit therapies) /cNIH BRAIN Initiative. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (8 hr., 48 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 29 & 30, 2021.3 a(CIT): The goal of this workshop is to bring together cross-disciplinary investigators to discuss the potential for human translation of next-generation circuit therapies for disorders of the central nervous system (CNS). The focus will be on the use of innovative and novel tools which includes optogenetics, chemogenetics, and gene editing, as well as cutting-edge approaches to neuromodulation. Sessions will discuss existing approaches to circuit manipulation, advances in gene therapy approaches, and how they may inform future CNS circuit therapies. Of particular importance will be the highlighting of CNS translational hurdles and the translational environment. Individuals from a diversity of perspectives and backgrounds are invited to attend. These include stakeholders in academic research and industry; clinicians; representatives from scientific societies, funding bodies, and advocacy organizations; and interested members of the public.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 18, 2022).12aCentral Nervous System Diseasesxtherapy22aGenetic Therapy22aGenetic Techniques22aTranslational Science, Biomedical 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aBRAIN Initiative,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4209040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42091  aAUTH02604cgm a22004454a 101322981DNLM20260227152156.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071207s2007    mdu168 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14166  a(OCoLC)190867225  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 47000aGWAS implementation for program staff /c[NIH].1 iTitle on PowerPoint screen:aGenome-wide association studies  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Jan. 2, 2008).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 47 min., 2 sec. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded Nov. 16, 2007.3 a(CIT): Staff Training for GWAS Implementation (Genome-Wide Association Studies)  As part of its mission to discover new knowledge that will lead to better health for the public, the National Institutes of Health (NIH) is interested in advancing the use of data obtained through genome-wide association studies (GWAS) to identify common genetic factors that influence health and disease. After public consultation with representatives from the scientific and lay communities http://grants.nih.gov/grants/gwas/index.htm, the NIH announced the final policy for the sharing of data obtained through NIH-supported or conducted GWAS http://grants.nih.gov/grants/guide/notice-files/NOT-OD-07-088.html.  This guidance document is for NIH extramural program officials who advise prospective and current applicants on following the policy, including application preparation, adequacy of proposed sharing plans, data submission requirements, data access procedures, and provisions for human subject protections.   Emphasis will be on Program Staff role in implementing the policy. This training will be of value for any NIH extramural or intramural staff who will administer or perform Genome-Wide Association Studies.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aGenetic Research12aGenome, Human22aData Collection22aGenetic Variation 2aUnited States 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14166  aAUTH03501cgm a2200469 i 9918573157506676DNLM20260227163309.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2023    mdu055|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32967  a(OCoLC)1401899086  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 20004aThe power and potential of the microenvironment in cancer metastasis /cRosandra Kaplan. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (55 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's seminar  aAccess restricted to HHS only.  aRecorded February 17, 2023.3 a(CIT): NIH Director"s Seminar  The tumor microenvironment - cellular and molecular changes in non-cancer cells and tissues -drive cancer progression. The pre-metastatic niche is the microenvironment important for metastatic initiation that is established at distant sites in response to primary tumor factors during cancer progression. We first characterized this microenvironment, which involves changes in both stromal and immune populations, in the pre-metastatic lungs of tumor-bearing mice. We have identified unique changes in these pre-metastatic sites by flow cytometry and RNA sequencing approaches, defining a gene signature of pre-metastatic niche formation that includes the upregulation of immune suppression genes consistent across different metastatic tissues, including lung and liver, as well as across species with commonalities in murine and human early metastatic microenvironments. Performing single cell RNA sequencing of the pre-metastatic niche revealed key immune suppression genes were found in the myeloid cell clusters. In addition to the increase of myeloid cells and immunosuppressive pathways, we discovered that T cell populations are reduced in pre-metastatic lungs. We hypothesized that reversing this immunosuppressive environment would restore T cell function and antitumor immunity. We designed a novel approach in which we generated Genetically Engineered Myeloid cells (GEMys) to deliver IL-12, a potent anti-tumor molecule, into the pre-metastatic microenvironment. Our studies demonstrate that IL12-GEMys can functionally modulate the core program of immune suppression in the pre-metastatic niche to successfully rebalance the dysregulated metastatic microenvironment in cancer. Therapies harnessing the power of the tumor microenvironment to modulate cancer progression can hold promise to effectively limit metastatic progression.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed September 18, 2023).12aTumor Microenvironmentxphysiology22aNeoplasm Metastasisxprevention & control22aRNA-Seqxmethods22aSingle-Cell Gene Expression Analysisxmethods22aMyeloid Cellsxphysiology22aGenetic Engineeringxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aKaplan, Rosandra,espeaker. 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49152  aAUTH01721cas a2200457 i 101772860DNLM20260227090913.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||201030c20219999ne || poo     |    0eng    a  2022229061  a2666-7711l2666-7703  a(OCoLC)1269070969  aAUDbengerdad20260227cAUDdDNLM  apcc  9Netherlands00aW1bGE82310aEur J Hist Med Health2dnlm00aEuropean journal for the history of medicine and health.1 aEHMH 1a[Leiden] :bBrill with the European Association for the History of Medicine and Health and the Swiss Society for the History of Medicine and Sciences,c[2021]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 78 (2021).  aPrint issues ceased with 2024.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued in print.0 aVolume 78 (2021); title from journal home page (publisher's website, viewed April 25, 2022).1 aVolume 78 (2021) (viewed April 25, 2022).12aHistory of Medicine22aHealthxhistory 2aPeriodical2 aEuropean Association for the History of Medicine and Health,eissuing body.2 aSchweizerische Gesellschaft für Geschichte der Medizin und der Naturwissenschaften,eissuing body.0 x2666-770300tGesnerusx0016-9161w(OCoLC)175118840uhttps://brill.com/view/journals/ehmh/ehmh-overview.xml  aAUTH01524cgm a22004574a 101480526DNLM20260227153112.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080801s2008    mdu056 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14585  a(OCoLC)262647271  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWI 7351 aThorgeirsson, Snorri S.10aGenomic decoding of liver cancer :bmechanistic and therapeutic implications /cSnorri S. Thorgeirsson.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCenter for Cancer Research-National Cancer Institute Grand Rounds  aTitle from title screen (viewed Oct. 6, 2008).  aStreaming video (55 min., 17 sec. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to NIH.5DNLM  aRecorded July 1, 2008.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aLiver Neoplasmsxgenetics22aGenomics22aLiver Neoplasmsxphysiopathology22aLiver Neoplasmsxtherapy 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14585  aAUTH02272cgm a2200481 i 9918592674506676DNLM20260227163610.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230429s2023    mdu126|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33110  a(OCoLC)1416616888  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 248 AA100aDemystifying medicine.pFat: biology and staying thin /cAaron Cypess, MD, PhD, NIDDK, NIH and Kevin Hall, PhD, NIDDK, NIH.1 aFat: biology and staying thin 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 6 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 18, 2023.3 a(CIT): The Demystifying Medicine Series, jointly sponsored by FAES and NIH, includes presentations on pathology, diagnosis, and therapy in the context of major disease problems and current research. Primarily directed toward PhD students, clinicians and program managers, this series is designed to help bridge the gap between advances in biology and their application to major human diseases. Each session includes clinical and basic science components presented by NIH staff and invitees. All students, trainees, fellows, and staff are welcome to participate.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed December 27, 2023).12aObesityxcomplications22aAdipose Tissuexphysiology22aEnergy Metabolismxphysiology22aAdrenergic beta-3 Receptor Agonistsxtherapeutic use22aDiet, Reducingxmethods22aBody-Weight Trajectory 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCypess, Aaron M.,espeaker.1 aHall, Kevin,d1970-espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=48837  aAUTH01669cgm a22004574a 101268426DNLM20260227150700.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu060 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11970  a(OCoLC)84671336  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWB 3101 aPayne, Richard,d1951-2019.14aThe