1700015008004100032035002600073035002000099040003500119044001400154060003100168100003200199245003800231264003900269300002200308336002600330337002800356338002700384500004400411540013800455583003900593583004400632650002500676650001200701999000900713101177761DNLM20260220140716.0030428s1910    ag            000 0 spa    a(DNLM)IDX:30506791070  a(OCoLC)52302166  aDNLMbengerdad20260220dDNLM  9Argentina00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 395 no.21 aFeinmann, Enrique,eauthor.10aVida enferma /cEnrique Feinmann. 1aBuenos Aires :bM.A. Rosas,c1910.  a10 pages ;c26 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"Publicación de la revista el Libro."  aNational Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202102242pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc2021020245DNLM12aBiological Phenomena22aDisease  aAUTH02286cgm a2200445 i 9918663287106676DNLM20260227164006.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230815s2023    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33326  a(OCoLC)1417460828  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aHV 156800aAbleism :backnowledging it & how to become anti-ableist /cDavid Rice. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 0 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded July 17, 2023.3 a(CIT): This dialogue provides an insightful presentation on anti-ableism, where we will shed light on the common beliefs of ableism in our society and how it affects individuals with Disabilities. You will gain a deeper understanding of the harmful attitudes, beliefs, and practices contributing to ableism and learn how to become a better ally to the Disability community. This presentation is an opportunity to challenge your own assumptions, broaden your perspective, and take actionable steps toward creating a more inclusive and equitable place for all. This program closely coincides with the celebration of the 33rd anniversary of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA).For more information go to https://intranet.cc.nih.gov/deia.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 28, 2023).12aSocial Discriminationxprevention & control12aPersons with Disabilities.22aHuman Rights22aDiversity, Equity, Inclusion 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aRice, Davidc(Director of special emphasis programs)espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=51062  aAUTH02408cgm a2200457 i 9918573277706676DNLM20260227162844.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2022    mdu191|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32737  a(OCoLC)1437827713  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 309 AA12 aGender and Health Workshop Concurrent Session: Impacts of Structural Sexism, Gender Norms, Relational Power Dynamics, and Gender Inequitiesd(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aGender and health :bimpacts of structural sexism, gender norms, relational power dynamics, and gender inequities /cORWH, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (3 hr., 11 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 26, 2022.3 a(CIT): In recognition of the significant health impacts of gender as a social and cultural variable, the NIH ORWH and the Eunice Kennedy Shriver NICHD convened a series of roundtable discussions for NIH staff members in the fall of 2021. Participants discussed the landscape of NIH-supported research on gender roles, gender norms, and gender inequity and identified opportunities in this space.  This scientific workshop is an exciting opportunity to convene the extramural community, NIH scientific and program staff members, and other stakeholders to discuss methods and best practices for biomedical and sociobehavioral research on gender roles, gender norms, and gender inequity.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed May 29, 2024).12aGender Equity12aHealth Inequities12aWomen22aSexism22aSocial Determinants of Health22aHIV Infections 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Research on Women's Health,eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=46317  aAUTH01606cgm a22004814a 101322950DNLM20260227152334.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071207s2007    mdu121 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14126  a(OCoLC)190867219  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWI 5291 aViner, Jaye L.10aTRACO :bcolon cancer and chemotherapy /cJ. Viner and M. Gutierrez ; sponsored the National Cancer Institute.30aColon cancer and chemotherapy3 aTranslational Research in Clinical Oncology  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aTRACO seminar series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Jan. 7, 2008).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 6 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 5, 2007.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aColorectal Neoplasmsxprevention & control22aAntineoplastic Agents22aChemoprevention22aColorectal Neoplasmsxdrug therapy22aColorectal Neoplasmsxepidemiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.) 0aTRACO seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14126  aAUTH04185cgm a2200493 i 9918503178506676DNLM20260227162800.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u221105s2022    mdu000|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32699  a(DNLM)CIT:32702  a(DNLM)CIT:32708  a(OCoLC)1397048786  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 141.5.M22 aISMRM Workshop on MRI of Neuromodulation: Target Engagement, Neural Mechanism & Biomarker Developmentd(2022 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aMRI of neuromodulation /cNIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (17 hr., 16 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 17, 18 & 19, 2022.3 a(CIT): During recent years, neuromodulation techniques such as transcranial direct current stimulation (tDCS), transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS), and deep brain stimulation (DBS), as well as alternative methods using optical and ultrasonic modulations, have become an important means to study how complex neural circuits interact in the brain, to manipulate human cognition, and to treat brain disorders. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) can now be performed either concurrently with or pre- and post-these neuromodulation techniques to visualize their effects on the human brain, to understand the neurophysiological mechanism, and to improve their efficacy. The proposed workshop will bring together a diverse group of scientists and clinicians as well as industry partners who are interested in developing and applying advanced MRI techniques to visualize, understand, and quantify neuromodulation effects on the human brain. This workshop will be the first of its kind on the topic of MRI in neuromodulation in ISMRM history and will integrate presentations with ample discussion periods covering advances in various MRI techniques for neuromodulation (electromagnetic field mapping, functional connectivity, arterial spin labeled perfusion and permeability, temperature and acoustic radiation force imaging, etc.), preclinical animal models and cellular-level mechanisms of neuromodulation, and safety issues related to MRI with neuromodulation devices. Existing and emerging clinical applications for MRI in neuromodulation and biomarker development will be discussed between academic and industry partners.  Upon completion of this activity, participants should be able to:  Explain the state-of-the-art and latest developments of concurrent MRI and neuromodulation techniques including tDCS, TMS, DBS, and emerging optical and ultrasonic modulations; Define the biophysical and neurophysiological parameters that can be measured by MRI of neuromodulation; Recognize the potentials and challenges of in-vivo MRI mapping of neuromodulation; and Identify the suitable clinical applications and associated barriers need to be overcome for the translation ofMRI in neuromodulation.For more information go to <a href="https://www.ismrm.org/workshops/2022/Neuromodulation/">https://www.ismrm.org/workshops/2022/Neuromodulation/.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed August 23, 2023).12aMagnetic Resonance Imagingxmethods22aTranscranial Direct Current Stimulation22aTranscranial Magnetic Stimulation22aDeep Brain Stimulation22aBrain Mapping 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aInternational Society for Magnetic Resonance in Medicine,eorganizer.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4640040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4640240uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=46398  aAUTH02001cgm a2200457 i 9918663184106676DNLM20260227163931.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230815s2023    mdu000|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33294  a(DNLM)CIT:33298  a(DNLM)CIT:33306  a(OCoLC)1455526989  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 7052 aNeural Mechanisms of Conscious Awareness in Humans (Workshop)d(2023 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aNeural mechanisms of conscious awareness in humans /cNIH and NSF. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (22 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 26, 27 & 28, 2023.3 a(CIT): This workshop will summarize the current state of knowledge of consciousness, discuss and design experiments to critically test existing theories of consciousness, define the most exciting new approaches to advance the field,and develop a roadmap for future discoveries in the science of consciousness.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 26, 2024).12aConsciousnessxphysiology22aNervous System Physiological Phenomena 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aNational Science Foundation (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4916040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4916240uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49164  aAUTH01970nam a2200421 i 9918986626006676DNLM20260227074716.0230721s2024    nyua     b    001 0 eng    a  2023027345  a9781621824480qhardcover  a1621824489qhardcover  a9781621824497qpaperback  a1621824497qpaperback  z9781621824503qelectronic publication  a(OCoLC)1391120837  aDLCbengerdacDLCdOCLCOdOCLCFdBDXdYDXdOCLCO  apcc  9United States00aQH434b.E96 202400aQW 25.5.B200a579.3/135223/eng/2023072400aExperiments in bacterial genetics :ba laboratory manual /cedited by Lionello Bossi (Universite Paris-Saclay), Andrew Camilli (Tufts University School of Medicine), and Angelika Gründling (Imperial College London). 1aCold Spring Harbor, New York :bCold Spring Harbor Laboratory Press,c[2024]  ax, 352 pages :billustrations (chiefly color) ;c28 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  a"Bacteria exhibit remarkable natural genetic variation, and their DNA can readily be manipulated for experimental and practical applications. Using both established tools and cutting-edge technologies, the protocols in this laboratory manual illustrate how to characterize the genomes of unknown bacterial species and strains and to perform advanced manipulations of DNA in model bacterial systems"--cProvided by publisher.12aBacteriaxgenetics22aDNA, Bacterial22aBacterial Physiological Phenomena 2aLaboratory Manual1 aBossi, Lionello,eeditor.1 aCamilli, Andrew,eeditor.1 aGründling, Angelika,eeditor.  aAUTH01215cas a2200385 a 8511784DNLM20260227091020.0cr|cn#||||||||851205c19859999gw qr p       0||| 0ger    a0178-7667l0178-7667  zFOPSE  a(DNLM)SR0055283(s)  a(OCoLC)12960736  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 agerbeng  9Germany00aW1bFO946H 0aForum der Psychoanalyse00aForum der Psychoanalyse.  aBerlin ; New York :bSpringer-Verlag,cc1985-  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aBd. 1, Heft 1 (Juli 1985)-  aAlso issued online.  aArticles in German with some summaries in English.  3Vol. 11, no. 4 (DEC 1995)c19960201fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paperxFurther review is required to determine exactly when acid-free paper was first used for this title 2aPsychoanalysis 2aPeriodical0 x1437-07514 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/451  aAUTH01568pam a2200505 i 9918987881106676DNLM20260212094856.0t|240423s2024    paua          001 0 eng    a  2024930463  aGBC4104312bnb  a9780443249648qhardback  a0443249644qhardback  z9780443250064qePub ebook8 aCIPO000076477  a(OCoLC)1457980085  aUKMGBbengerdacUKMGBdOCLCO  apcc  9United States00aWE 1704a617.5107542231 aChapman, Philip R.,eauthor.10aImaging anatomy.pHead and neck /cSurjith Vattoth, Philip R. Chapman, H. Ric Harnsberger.1 aHead and neck  aSecond edition. 1aPhiladelphia, PA :bElsevier,c[2024]  a664 pages :billustrations (colour) ;c28 cm  atext2rdacontent  astill image2rdacontent  aunmediated2rdamedia  avolume2rdacarrier  aIncludes index.  aPhilip R. Chapman's name appears first in the previous edition.12aHeadxanatomy & histology12aNeckxanatomy & histology22aHeadxdiagnostic imaging22aNeckxdiagnostic imaging22aMagnetic Resonance Spectroscopy22aTomography, X-Ray Computed22aUltrasonography 2aAtlas1 aHarnsberger, H. Ric,eauthor.1 aVattoth, Surjith,eauthor.  aAUTH01357cgm a22003974a 101512261DNLM20260227153004.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090801s2009    mdu057|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15204  a(OCoLC)472718329  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 1701 aKoenen, Karestan C.10aWho gets posttraumatic stress disorder? /cKarestan C. Koenen.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 1, 2009).  aStreaming video (57 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 25, 2009.3 a(CIT): NIMH Candidate for PTSD Tenure-Track Investigator  For more information, visit:  http://intramural.nimh.nih.gov/careers/index.html.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aStress Disorders, Post-Traumatic. 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15204  aAUTH02816cgm a2200469 i 9918573155006676DNLM20260227163319.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2023    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32977  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 20300aTissue-resident memory T cells in cancer /cFathia Mami-Chouaib. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 0 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aIIG seminar series  aAccess restricted to HHS only.  aRecorded February 15, 2023.3 a(CIT): The Mami-Chouaib laboratory has identified several constitutive elements of the tumor environment (TME) involved in the regulation of antitumor T-cell response. In particular: the influence of TGF-[beta] on the expression and signaling of CD103 in CD8+ resident memory T cells (TRM), and the role of this integrin to sustain specific cytotoxicity and to promote T-cell recruitment within epithelial tumor regions; the role of hypoxia, epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT) and p53 in controlling tumor cell susceptibility to T-cell-mediated killing; and the effect of Von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) gene mutations in promoting PD-L1 expression on cancer cells and escape from anti-PD-1 therapies. The laboratory's objective is to make new advances in the field of tumor resistance to T-cell-mediated cytotoxicity and immune checkpoint blockade (ICB) immunotherapy. Through its various aspects, the aim of their research is to better understand how the TME influences the behavior and functions of effector T cells, in particular TRM, and impacts response to immunotherapies. Their goal is to improve current cancer treatments and contribute to the development of more effective combination therapies.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 29, 2023).12aNeoplasmsximmunology22aMemory T Cells22aTumor Microenvironmentximmunology22aImmunologic Memory22aNeoplasmsxtherapy22aImmunotherapy 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aMami-Chouaib, Fathia,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group,esponsoring body. 0aImmunology Interest Group lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=48940  aAUTH01167cam a2200289 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400035000800440018001150600024001331000062001572450190002192640064004093000027004733360026005003370028005263380027005545000019005815000099006005010060006996500024007596550016007837100034007997300035008339990009008689918787483006676DNLM20260213174625.0240329r19561956ilu           000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.511 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aMedical research :ba midcentury survey. Vol. I. American medical research: its principle and practice. Vol. II. Unsolved clinical problem: in biological perspective /cWilliam B. Bean. 1a[Chicago, Illinois] :bAmerican Medical Associationc[1956]  apages 534-537 ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  a"Reprinted from the A.M.A. Archives of Internal medicine, October 1956, vol. 98, pp. 534-538."  aNLM copy, bound 51st in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM12aBiomedical Research 2aBook Review2 aAmerican Medical Association.0 aArchives of internal medicine.  aAUTH00819cam a2200253 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400035000800440018001150600024001331000062001572450043002192640060002623000028003223360026003503370028003763380027004045000019004315010060004506500024005106500022005349990009005569918787482206676DNLM20260212170818.0240401s1957    xxu           000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.581 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aPellagra /cmethod of William B. Bean. 1a[United States] :b[publisher not identified],c[1957?]  apages 294-295  ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  aNLM copy, bound 58th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM12aPellagraxdiagnosis12aPellagraxtherapy  aAUTH01337cas a2200397 i 101709029DNLM20260227090908.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||170707c20179999sz || poo     |    0eng    a  2017243331  a2366-7540  a(OCoLC)1008940568  aDNLMbengerdad20260227cDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9Switzerland00aW110aAdv Neurodev Disord2dnlm00aAdvances in neurodevelopmental disorders. 1a[Cham] :bSpringer International Publishing AG,c[2017]-  a1 online resource  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1, issue 1 (March 2017).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued in print.  aDescription based on: Volume 1, issue 1 (March 2017); title from PDF article caption (publisher's Web site, viewed Oct. 24, 2017).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 1, issue 3 (September 2017) (viewed Oct. 24, 2017).12aNeurodevelopmental Disorders 2aPeriodical0 x2366-753240uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/41252  aAUTH02114cgm a2200529 i 101774286DNLM20260227160447.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31399  a(OCoLC)1236084727  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWH 52500aClinical cases from the NIH Clinical Center.pInhibition of the PI3K pathway for untreated follicular lymphoma /cRahul Lakhotia, Stefania Pittaluga, Mark Ahlman, Mark Roschewski.1 aInhibition of the PI3K pathway for untreated follicular lymphoma 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aClinicopathologic grand rounds  aRecorded November 18, 2020.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed February 2, 2021).22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center12aLymphoma, Follicularxdrug therapy22aPhosphoinositide-3 Kinase Inhibitorsxtherapeutic use22aPhosphoinositide-3 Kinase Inhibitorsxpharmacology22aAngiogenesis Inhibitorsxtherapeutic use22aPyrazinesxtherapeutic use22aQuinolinesxtherapeutic use 2aCase Reports 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aLakhotia, Rahul,espeaker.1 aPittaluga, Stefania,espeaker1 aAhlman, Markespeaker.1 aRoschewski, Mark,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center,esponsoring body. 0aNIH Clinical Center clinicopathologic grand rounds40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38951  aAUTH03234cgm a2200433 i 101676576DNLM20260227155250.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160201s2016    mdu059 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19412  a(OCoLC)944306758  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 47500aWhy parents matter (epigenetically) :bgenomic imprinting in health and disease /cMarisa Bartolomei. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded January 6, 2016.3 a(CIT): Imprinted genes are expressed from a single parental allele and most reside in clusters that are located throughout the mammalian genome. The clusters typically contain an imprinting control region (ICR), which harbors allele-specific methylation and governs the imprinting of the entire domain. Although most imprinted clusters use long non-coding RNAs to regulate imprinted gene expression, a few are regulated by the transcriptional regulator CTCF and allele-specific insulator function. One such cluster harbors the H19 and Igf2 imprinted genes, and is controlled by an ICR that contains multiple CTCF binding sites. Gain of maternal methylation and loss of paternal hypermethylation of the H19/IGF2 ICR are associated with the human growth disorders Beckwith-Wiedemann Syndrome and Silver-Russell Syndrome, respectively. Using gene targeting and genome editing, Dr. Bartolomei's team has generated embryonic stem cells, induced pluripotent stem-cell lines and mice to study the mechanisms of imprinting for these imprinted loci and to model the epigenetic mutations in human syndromes. Her lab has also developed SNP-FISH to study the dynamics of allele-specific gene expression at the single cell level in cell lines and tissues with loss of imprinting. In addition, her lab has studied imprinting in animal models of assisted reproductive technologies (ART) and endocrine disrupting chemical exposures (EDCs). Both ART and EDCs are associated with the increased loss of imprinting of various genes and with DNA methylation aberrations. The effects are especially pronounced in the placenta where the regulation of multiple genes and DNA methylation of repetitive elements are perturbed and where morphological changes are evident.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed February 28, 2016).12aGenomic Imprinting22aDNA Methylation22aEpigenomics 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aBartolomei, Marisa,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19412  aAUTH01432cas a2200397   8008366DNLM20260227090953.0cr|cn ||||||||800904c19809999fr qr         0||| 0fre    a0245-5919l0245-5919  aMCRDD7  a(DNLM)M49615000(s)  a(OCoLC)06828327  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9France00aW1bMO949N 0aMortricité cérébrale00aMotricité cérébrale, réadaptation, neurologie du développement.  aParis, New York,bMasson.3 aParis :bMasson  av.bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 at. 1-    1980-  aAlso issued online.  aOffica