dy. 0aNHLBI COVID-19 insights webinar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38871  aAUTH01603cas a2200481 i 101604797DNLM20260224111550.0cr||||||||||||130325c20129999nyu|| p       0    0eng    a  2014243365  a2260-1341l2260-1341  a(OCoLC)878941413  aNLMbengerdad20260224dOCLCOdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW1bJO66110aJ Frailty Aging2dnlm04aThe Journal of frailty & aging.1 aJournal of frailty and aging1 aJFA 1aToulouse :bSerdi Edition3132025- :a[New York] :bElsevier  aFour nos. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1, No 1 (2012).1 aMEDLINEbv5n1, Mar. 2016-0 aPubMedbv5n1, Mar. 2016- ; selected citations only before this date0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: Volume 1, Number 4, 2012; title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 1, Number 4, 2012.12aFrail Elderly22aAging2 aInternational Association of Gerontology and Geriatrics.2 aGlobal Aging Research Network.0 x2273-43094 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/424154 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/the-journal-of-frailty-and-aging4 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101604797  aAUTH01729cgmaa22004574a 101266347DNLM20260227144743.0vf|cbahoucr|cna||||||||vf|cjahou060130s2005    mdu050 ||         vleng    a(OCoLC)65195187  a(DNLM)CIT:3799  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aWF 55300aGender differences in lung function and response to environmental agents /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  aUnited States.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (49 min., 29 sec.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in. +3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (49 min., 29 sec.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in. +3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier0 aWomen's health special interest group  aDarryl C. Zeldin.  aPresented Dec. 16, 2005.  aAlso available online.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aAsthmaxepidemiology12aAsthmaxphysiopathology22aLipopolysaccharides22aLungxphysiology22aSex Factors1 aZeldin, Darryl C.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aWomen's Health Special Interest Group (Series)40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3799  aAUTH03007cgm a22004694a 101522201DNLM20260227153321.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100105s2009    mdu059|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15508  a(OCoLC)592432349  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 821 aRichards-Kortum, Rebecca,d1964-10aFrom cell phones to cell biology :bhigh tech, low cost solutions for global health /cRebecca Richards-Kortum.13iSubtitle on PowerPoint:aHigh-performance, low cost technologies for global health  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (59 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 16, 2009.3 a(CIT): Advances in the biosciences and public health are responsible for dramatic gains in life expectancy achieved over the last century. Yet, the majority of the world has not benefited from this progress. Sustainable and scalable innovations to prevent disease are needed. This talk will describe efforts of bioengineering faculty and students to develop new diagnostic and therapeutic tools which can be used at the point-of-care (POC) to improve health in low resource settings. Advances in MEMS technologies, molecular recognition, and low power sensors now offer the ability to design low-cost, reusable platforms for POC diagnostics.  Efforts to integrate molecular imaging together with miniature microscopes are now yielding new POC diagnostics for infectious and chronic diseases. Driven by advances in consumer electronics, high resolution imaging can be obtained with low cost devices; advances in digital signal processing provide the ability to automate analysis. In parallel, multidisciplinary educational programs are engaging undergraduate students to address POC design problems in developing countries. In creating solutions to real world challenges, students are challenged to think beyond traditional disciplinary and geographic boundaries. The NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series includes weekly scientific talks by some of the top researchers in the biomedical sciences worldwide.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomedical Technology22aBiomedical Engineering22aDeveloping Countries22aPoint-of-Care Systems 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15508  aAUTH02875cgm a2200481 i 9918592674906676DNLM20260227163605.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230429s2023    mdu177|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33103  a(OCoLC)1457225126  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 20.52 aGlobal Health Fellows & Scholars 20th Anniversary Eventd(2023 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aGlobal health fellows & scholars (FICRS) 20th anniversary /cFogarty International Center, NIH.1 aGlobal health fellows and scholars (FICRS) 20th anniversary 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 57 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 13, 2023.3 a(CIT): Fogarty"s flagship Global Health Fellows and Scholars program, now known as LAUNCH, will celebrate its 20th Anniversary in 2023. To mark the occasion, Fogarty invites program alums to join us for a commemorative event at NIH on April 13, 2023.  The event will be an opportunity to celebrate alumni accomplishments and share our hopes for future cohorts. Attendees will also have a chance to network and hear from directors from several NIH Institutes and Centers about the impact of Fogarty Fellows and Scholars in the global health workforce.  The Fellows and Scholars program, established in 2003, aims to foster the next generation of global health scientists by providing trainees, early in their careers, a one-year mentored research training experience at established biomedical and behavioral research institutions and project sites in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs) as well as opportunities for post-professional trainees from the U.S. and LMICs..  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed August 5, 2024).22aFogarty International Center.bGlobal Health Fellows and Scholars Program22aFogarty International Center.bLaunching Future Leaders in Global Health (LAUNCH) Research Training Program12aGlobal Healthxeducation12aFellowships and Scholarships22aPreceptorship22aBiomedical Research22aAnniversaries and Special Events22aAcademies and Institutesxhistory 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aFogarty International Center,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=48887  aAUTH01552cas a2200433 i 9918283075806676DNLM20260227090923.0m     o  d        cr#|||||||||||210527c20229999nyumr poo     0   a0eng    a  20212020430 a2768-3613l2768-360521  a(OCoLC)1252917976  aDLCbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aISSN RECORD00aW110aJ Integr Complement Med2dnlm00aJournal of integrative and complementary medicine. 1a[New Rochelle, New York] :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.,c[2022]-  a1 online resource  aMonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aMEDLINEbv28n1, 2022-0 aPubMedbv28n1, 2022-  aSome volumes contain special issues.0 aVolume 28, issue 1 (January 2022); title from journal homepage (publisher's website, viewed February 3, 2022).1 aVolume 28, issue 1 (January 2022) (viewed February 3, 2022).12aIntegrative Medicine22aComplementary Therapies 2aPeriodical0 x2768-360500tJournal of alternative and complementary medicine (New York, N.Y.)x1075-5535w(DLC)sn 94004883w(OCoLC)3008493740uhttps://home.liebertpub.com/publications/journal-of-integrative-and-complementary-medicine/26  aAUTH02106cgm a22004694a 101268527DNLM20260227150813.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu069 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12155  a(OCoLC)122498880  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 102.81 aGarner, Craig.10aDynamic assembly of CNS synapses /cCraig Garner.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 27, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 8 min., 54 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 20, 2004.3 a(CIT): The main focus of research in Dr. Garner's laboratory is to understand the fundamental cellular and molecular mechanisms that guide the formation, maintenance and elimination of excitatory synapses in the vertebrate CNS. To accomplish these goals, they use molecular and genetic approaches to identify key structural and regulatory proteins of glutamatergic synapses. In recent years, they have used these tools to address basic questions concerning nascent synapse formation.    NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.