iewer.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pNIH director's cultural lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27524  aAUTH02347cgm a22004694a 101310971DNLM20260227152105.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070703s2007    mdu050|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13909  a(OCoLC)173670607  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5401 aKirschner, Denise.10aMulti-scale approaches towards understanding the immune response /cDenise Kirschner.1 iPowerPoint title screen:aA multi-scale approach towards understanding antigen presentation in the immune response to M. tuberculosis  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner.  aStreaming video (50 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jun. 19, 2007.3 a(CIT): Systems Biology Special Interest Group    Dr. Kirschner obtained her Ph.D. in Mathematics from Tulane University in 1991 and has served on the faculty of Texas A & M and Vanderbilt Universities.  In 1997 she joined the University of Michigan School of Medicine where she is Associate Professor of Microbiology and Immunology.  Dr. Kirschner's research interests include microbial pathogenesis, nonlinear dynamical and complex systems.  She is currently Co-Chief Editor of the Journal of Theoretical Biology.   Dr. Denise Kirschner  University of Michigan  University of Michigan Medical Scool  Department of Microbiology and Immunology   http://malthus.micro.med.umich.edu   http://www.nih.gov/sigs/sysbio.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aImmunityximmunology22aAntigen Presentationximmunology22aAntigen-Presenting Cellsximmunology22aModels, Immunological22aMycobacterium tuberculosisximmunology22aSystems Biologyxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Heal