dacarrier02aLeonardo,cda Vinci,d1452-1519.12aFamous Persons22aSexuality22aDreams22aPaintings1 aBrill, A. A.q(Abraham Arden),d1874-1948,etranslator.  ihas work:aLeonardo da Vinci (Text)4https://id.oclc.org/worldcat/ontology/hasWork  aAUTH04175cgm a22004574a 101310957DNLM20260227152050.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070703s2007    mdu205 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13907  a(OCoLC)157002638  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQY 252 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.).bOffice of Biorepositories.10aBiospecimen best practices forums /cNCI Office of Biorepositories.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 16, 2007).  aStreaming video (3 hr., 25 min. : sd., col.).0 aModerator, Dr. Carolyn Compton.  aRecorded Jun. 18, 2007.3 a(CIT): The availability of high-quality biospecimens, collected according to the highest ethical standards, is critical to supporting development of new genomic- and proteomic-based cancer interventions. Recognizing the need to unify and standardize biospecimen resource practices, the NCI will soon publish NCI Best Practices for Biospecimen Resources, which outlines the technical, operational, ethical, legal and policy best practices supporting the collection and use of biospecimens. NCI has initiated an education and outreach program targeted at the scientific and advocacy communities. These forums will provide information about the importance of best practices in guiding biospecimen use, the operational and ethical standards in the NCI Best Practices, and resources for achieving high-quality and accessible biospecimens.3 aProgram format: 8:30 - 8:45 Welcome and Opening Remarks / Carolyn C. Compton, M.D., Ph.D., Director, Office of Biorepositories and Biospecimen Research, NCI. Part 1: NCI Best Practices for Biospecimen Resources /  Carolyn C. Compton. 8:45 - 9:05 NCI Best Practices for Biospecimen Resources Rationale and Development / Carolyn C. Compton. 9:05 - 9:25 Overview of Technical and Operational Best Practices / Mark Sobel, M.D., Ph.D., Executive Officer American Society for Investigative Pathology. 9:25 - 9:35 Audience Questions and Comments. 9:35 - 9:55 Overview of Ethical, Legal, and Policy Best Practices / Karen Thiel, Ph.D., J.D., Associate Patton Boggs, LLP. 9:55  - 10:05 Audience Questions and Comments. 10:05 - 10:25 The Importance of Best Practices to Patients and Advocates / Paula Kim, President and Chief Executive Officer, Translating Research Across Communities (TRAC) . 10:25  - 10:35 Audience Questions and Comments. Part 2: Bioinformatics and Evidence-Based Best Practices for Biospecimen Resources. 11:00 - 11:20 Tools for Tracking and Accessing High-Quality Biospecimens caBIG, caTissue, and Achieving Silver-Level Compatibility / Kenneth H. Buetow, Ph.D., Director NCI Center for Bioinformatics National Cancer Institute. 11:20 - 11:30 Audience Questions and Comments. 11:30 - 11:40 Demonstration of caTissue / Ian Fore, D. Phil. Senior Biomedical Informatics Program Manger Center for Bioinformatics, NCI . 11:40 - 11:50 Audience Questions and Comments. 11:50  - 12:10 Updating the NCI Best Practices for Biospecimen Resources: Toward Evidence-Based Best Practices Importance of Stakeholder Participation in Updating the NCI Best Practices for Biospecimen Resources / Carolyn C. Compton, M.D., Ph.D., Director Office of Biorepositories and Biospecimen Research National Cancer Institute. 12:10  - 12:20 Questions and Comments. 12:20  - 12:30 Closing Remarks / Carolyn Compton.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aSpecimen Handlingxstandards22aAntineoplastic Agents22aBiological Specimen Banksxstandards 2aConference Proceedings 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCompton, Carolyn C.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13907  aAUTH02571ngm a2200469 i 9918976660306676DNLM20260227165130.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u250115s2024    mdu055|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35454  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWU 11300aMoving beyond oral health for some /cJennifer Webster-Cyriaque. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2024]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (55 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aHDIG seminar  aRecorded December 10, 2024.3 a(CIT):Dr. Jennifer Webster-Cyriaque is the Acting Director of the National Institute of Dental and Craniofacial Research, National Institutes of Health. An accomplished clinician, researcher, and leader, Dr. Webster-Cyriaque had previously served as a faculty member at the University of North Carolina (UNC) schools of dentistry and medicine for more than two decades. As a tenured full professor at UNC, Dr. Webster-Cyriaque also served as the attending on clinical service at the UNC Hospital's dental clinic. While there, she led research into a potential etiologic agent for salivary gland disease in patients living with HIV, assessed the oral microbiome and its implications for cancer-causing viruses, and studied the impact of the oral microbiome and oral health on HIV outcomes.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 24, 2026).12aOral Health22aPreventive Dentistry22aTooth Diseasesxprevention & control22aHealth Services Accessibilityxeconomics22aSocial Determinants of Health 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aWebster-Cyriaque, Jennifer,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bHealth Disparities Interest Group,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities (U.S.).bDivision of Intramural Research,esponsoring body. 0aHealth Disparities Interest Group & NIMHD Division of Intramural Research joint lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=55368  aAUTH01250cas a2200421   7708323DNLM20260227090952.0cr|cn#||||||||771112c19779999gw qr         0||| 0ger    a0342-0477l0342-0477  aSSTGDY  a(DNLM)S36320000(s)  a(OCoLC)03793871  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 agerbeng  9Germany00aW1bSP675 0aSprache, Stimme, Gehör00aSprache-Stimme-Gehör.  aStuttgart,bThieme.3 aStuttgart :bGeorg Thieme Verlag  av.bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 a1.-    Jahrg.; März 1977-  aAlso issued online.  aArticles in German; summaries in English and German.  aOfficial organ of the Zentralverband für Logopädie. 2aHearing Disorders 2aSpeech Disorders 2aVoice 2aPeriodical2 aZentralverband für Logopädie (Germany)4 uhttps://www.thieme-connect.de/products/ejournals/journal/10.1055/s-00000082  aAUTH02776cgm a2200469 i 101749142DNLM20260227155937.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190604s2019    mdu062|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27563  a(OCoLC)1112279965  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWG 31000aGenes, lifestyle, and risk for heart attack /cSekar Kathiresan. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded May 22, 2019.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series. Coronary artery disease is the leading global cause of mortality. Long recognized to be heritable, recent advances have started to unravel the genetic architecture of the disease. Common variant association studies have linked approximately 95 genetic loci to coronary risk. Large-scale gene-sequencing efforts and functional studies have facilitated a better understanding of causal risk factors, elucidated underlying biology, and informed the development of new therapeutics. Moving forward, genetic testing could enable precision-medicine approaches by identifying subgroups of patients who are at increased risk of coronary artery disease or have a specific driving pathophysiology that would make a therapeutic or preventive approach most useful. Dr. Kathiresan leverages human genetics to understand the root causes of heart attack and to improve preventive cardiac care. Among his scientific contributions, Dr. Kathiresan has helped highlight new biological mechanisms underlying heart attack, discovered mutations that protect against heart attack risk, and developed a genetic test for personalized heart attack prevention.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed August 13, 2019).12aMyocardial Infarctionxgenetics12aRisk Factors22aGenetic Locixgenetics22aGenetic Testing22aModels, Genetic22aMutationxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aKathiresan, Sekar,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27563  aAUTH01353cas a2200445 a 101582803DNLM20260227090917.0cr|cn ||||||||120420c20119999gw || p       |    0ger    a  2012243385  a2192-9319l2192-9319  a(OCoLC)793085930  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9Germany00aW1bAK996200aAkupunktur & Aurikulomedizin.1 aAkupunktur und Aurikulomedizin1 aZAA1 aZeitschrift für Akupunktur & Aurikulomedizin  aMünchen :bSpringer Medizin  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan in 2011.  aAlso issued online.  aIn German; table of contents also in English.  aDescription based on: 2011/04; title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: 2011/04.12aAcupuncture Therapy22aAcupuncture, Ear22aPain Management 2aPeriodical2 aDeutsche Akademie für Akupunktur.0 x2196-640000tSchmerz & Akupunkturx1611-521Xw(DLC)   2003243655w(OCoLC)538012914 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/15009  aAUTH02867cgm a2200481 i 9918573260606676DNLM20260227163021.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2022    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32799  a(OCoLC)1393202776  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 506.4 AW400aBiomedical research in rural America :bmuch accomplished, much to do /cSally Hodder. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture. DeWitt Stetten, Jr. lecture  aRecorded November 30, 2022.3 a(CIT): NIH Director"s Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series  The DeWitt Stetten Jr. Lecture    West Virginia, the only state located entirely in Appalachia, has among the lowest life expectancy in the nation and ranks at or near the bottom in many chronic disease categories. Moreover, across America, rural mortality rates exceed urban rates, and the gap has accelerated since 1999, even when adjusted for age. The Sars-CoV-2 pandemic has further highlighted rural health disparities with demonstrated higher COVID-19 related mortality and hospitalization rates. To add to the problem is distrust in science that has become more prevalent. This presentation will describe the establishment of a statewide research program and engagement of rural communities in research. Specific research agendas and results will focus on innovative treatment of refractory substance use disorder using deep brain stimulation. Also discussed will be results relating to epidemiology and treatment of SARS-CoV-2.   For Dr Hodder"s research, see https://directory.hsc.wvu.edu/Profile/41751#biography  and https://www.wvctsi.org/about-us/directors-welcome  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed July 27, 2023).12aCOVID-19xepidemiology22aCOVID-19xmortality22aSubstance-Related Disordersxtherapy22aHealth Status Disparities22aRural Population22aEpidemiologic Studies 2aWest Virginia 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aHodder, Sally,espeaker.2 aNational Institute of General Medical Sciences (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pDeWitt Stetten, Jr. lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=46035  aAUTH01996cgm a22004694a 101289621DNLM20260227151423.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2006    mdu066  |||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13126  a(OCoLC)84671947  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 3801 aParada, Luis.10aGenetic modeling of neural tumors in mice /cLuis Parada.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aWALS  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 14, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 5 min., 8 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 22, 2006.3 a(CIT): Neurofibromatosis Type 1 (NF1) is a dominantly inherited disease in which mutation of the NF1tumor suppressor either appears de novo or is transmitted through the germ line.  Biochemical and mouse knockout studies have demonstrated that NF1 is a negative regulator of the ras pathway by acting as rasGap.  Dr. Parada has used conventional knockouts and cre/loxP based strategies to model tumor formation in these mice.  He will discuss the current understanding of neurofibroma formation, a complex peripheral nerve tumor that is prevalent in NF1.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeurofibromaxgenetics22aAstrocytomaxgenetics22aDisease Models, Animal22aMice, Knockout22aNeurofibromatosis 1 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13126  aAUTH01542cgm a22004574a 101463196DNLM20260227152250.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080102s2007    mdu291|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14180  a(DNLM)CIT:14183  a(DNLM)CIT:14184  a(OCoLC)319067002  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 58.52 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bRecombinant DNA Advisory Committee.10aRecombinant DNA Advisory Committee (RAC), December 2007 /cOffice of Biotechnology Activities.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Apr. 6, 2009).  aStreaming video (13 hr., 45 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 3-5, 2007.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDNA, Recombinant22aClinical Protocolsxstandards22aGene Transfer Techniques22aGenetic Therapy 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1418040uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1418340uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14184  aAUTH02787cgm a2200505 i 9918226874306676DNLM20260227161327.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210615s2021    mdu754|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31768  a(DNLM)CIT:31798  a(DNLM)CIT:31787  a(OCoLC)1291165886  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 5602 aGene-Targeted Therapies: Early Diagnosis and Equitable Delivery (Conference)d(2021 June :cOnline)jauthor.10aGene-targeted therapies :bearly diagnosis and equitable delivery /cNational Center for Translational Sciences, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (3 streaming video files (12 hr., 34 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aNational Center for Translational Sciences in the statement of responsibility should read National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences.  aRecorded June 3, 10 & 17, 2021.3 a(CIT): NIH invites stakeholders throughout the scientific research, advocacy, clinical practice, industry, and lay communities, including the general public, to attend the 3-day meeting and submit comments or questions relating to the effective, efficient and equitable distribution of gene-targeted therapies for individuals with rare diseases. The goal of Day 1 is to discuss: Who are the individuals that could benefit from gene-targeted therapies - now and in the future? What novel approaches are needed to enable development of gene-targeted therapies for all genetic rare diseases - now and in the future? When is the optimal time to identify individuals who could benefit from gene-targeted therapies (e.g., newborn screening)?.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 29, 2021).12aTargeted Gene Repair12aRare Diseasesxgenetics22aEarly Diagnosis22aHealth Services Accessibility22aTranslational Science, Biomedical 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4196840uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4196940uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41970  aAUTH01267nam a2200337 i 101778675DNLM20260220080941.0920226s1886    mau           000 0 eng    a1055979133  z(OCoLC)1055979133  a(OCoLC)34228710z(OCoLC)1055979133  aZCHbengerdacZCHdCASUMdEUMdOCLCQdOCLCOdOCLCQdOCLCAdOCLCFdOCLCOdOCLCA  apcc  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5573 no.162 aBoston City Hospital.bTrustees,eauthor.10aAction of the trustees of the Boston City Hospital u