States 4aRC553.A8800aW110aRev J Autism Dev Disord2dnlm00aReview journal of autism and developmental disorders. 1aNew York, NY :bSpringer, [2014]-  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1, issue 1 (March 2014).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued in print.  aDescription based on Volume 1, issue 1 (March 2014); title from home page (SpringerLink, viewed May 25, 2018).  aLatest issue consulted volume 5, issue 2 (June 2018) (viewed May 25, 2018).12aAutism Spectrum Disorder 2aPeriodical0 x2195-717740uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/40489  aAUTH02204cgm a2200445 i 101774297DNLM20260227160512.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu054|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31416  a(OCoLC)1246807875  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 50604aThe ethics of contact tracing during a pandemic /cMichael Parker. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (54 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aEthics grand rounds  aRecorded December 2, 2020.3 a(CIT): Ethics Grand Rounds: The Ethics of Contact Tracing During a Pandemic  Contact tracing is one of the most important public health measures for addressing infectious disease outbreaks and epidemics. New technology using mobile phone apps introduces the possibility of instantaneous contact tracing of a significant proportion of the population. This approach could significantly increase the effectiveness of contact tracing. It also raises important ethical questions: When is it acceptable to monitor individuals" movements? Can it be ethical to make contact tracing mandatory? May cell phone apps be used to monitor physical distancing? In what ways may the information gathered durin