onsiderations for Adeno-Associated Virus (AAV)-Mediated Gene Therapy /cNational Center for Advancing Translational Sciences, NIH, Center for Biologics Evaluation and Research, FDA. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (12 hr., 15 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 30 & December 1, 2020.3 a(CIT): The National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (NCATS) at the National Institutes of Health (NIH) and the Center for Biologics Evaluation and Research (CBER) at the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) are co-hosting a virtual Workshop on Systemic Immunogenicity Considerations for Adeno-Associated Virus (AAV)-Mediated Gene Therapy. This meeting will bring together scientists, therapeutic developers and patient representatives to focus on immunogenicity issues related to the systemic administration of AAV gene therapy products and identify solutions for those issues.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 25, 2021).12aDependovirusxgenetics22aGenetic Therapy22aDependovirusximmunology 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aCenter for Biologics Evaluation and Research (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3854740uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38548  aAUTH03160cgm a2200445 a 101598214DNLM20260227154452.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u121215s2012    mdu065|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17650  a(OCoLC)826287934  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 102.51 aZoghbi, Huda.14aThe value of clinical clues in solving neurogenetic riddles /cHuda Y. Zoghbi.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 5 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded Nov. 7, 2012.3 a(CIT): Astute Clinician Lecture  Dr. Zoghbi"s research interest is in using the tools of modern genetics to understand the proper development of the brain as well as what goes awry in specific neurodevelopmental and neurodegenerative conditions. She has published seminal work regarding the molecular basis of Rett syndrome and of late-onset neurodegenerative diseases.  Dr. Zoghbi is a member of several professional organizations including the McKnight Foundation Neuroscience Board, the Lasker Jury, and serves as a senior editor for the newly founded journal eLife. Among Dr. Zoghbi's honors are the E. Mead Johnson Award from the Society of Pediatric Research, the IPSEN prize in neuronal plasticity, the Bristol Myers-Squibb Neuroscience Distinguished Achievement Award, the Vilcek Prize, and the Gruber prize in Neuroscience. In 2000 she was elected to the Institute of Medicine, and in 2004 she was elected to the National Academy of Sciences.     The NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series, colloquially known as WALS, is the highest-profile lecture program at the NIH. Lectures occur on most Wednesdays from September through June from 3:00 to 4:00 p.m. in Masur Auditorium, Building 10 on the NIH Bethesda campus.   Each season includes some of the biggest names in biomedical and behavioral research. The goal of the WALS is to keep NIH researchers abreast of the latest and most important research in the Unites States and beyond. An added treat is the annual J. Edward Rall Cultural Lecture, which features top authors and other cultural icons. All speakers are nominated by the NIH community.   For more information, visit:  The NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aNeurogenesisxgenetics22aBrainxgrowth & development22aGrowth and Developmentxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17650  aAUTH01456cgm a22004574a 101268694DNLM20260227150916.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2005    mdu086 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12452  a(OCoLC)162143068  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 26.51 aWishart, David Scott.10aProtein structure analysis and protein-protein interactions /cDavid Wishart.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed June 29, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 25 min., 42 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 15, 2005.3 a(CIT): Current Topics in Genome Analysis.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aComputational Biology12aDatabases, Protein22aProtein Binding22aProtein Conformation22aProtein Folding22aProteinsxmetabolism 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12452  aAUTH02344cgm a22004814a 101268725DNLM20260227150456.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu567 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11684  a(DNLM)CIT:11636  a(OCoLC)71464152  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWX 21804aThe functional genomics of critical illness and injury /cCritical Care Medicine Department, Clinical Center, NIH.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Aug. 8, 2006).  aStreaming video (9 hr., 22 min., 33 sec. : sd., col.).0 aDay 1 -- Day 2.  aRecorded Nov. 17-18, 2003.3 a(CIT): The symposium will further explore the potential of functional genomics in the refinement of critical care medicine.  Experts from numerous clinical specialties, micro- and molecular biologists, and experts in high-throughput technologies and computational science will join to exchange information and ideas in areas including:  - diagnostic proteomics  - pharmacogenomics as applied to critical illness and injury  - gene expression in blood components  - genetics of immunity  - primary data from patients and models of critical illness  - policy issues raised by the demands of functional genomics on resources and experimental design.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aCritical Illness22aBiomedical Technology22aCritical Care22aGenetics, Medical22aGenomics 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center.bCritical Care Medicine Department40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1168440uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11636  aAUTH01152pam a2200337 i 9918715688406676DNLM20260220080955.0210507s2021    ne a     b    001 0 eng    aGBC1A45262bnb  a9780128214510q(pbk.)  a0128214511  z9780128214626 (ePub ebook)  a(OCoLC)1259533675  aUKMGBbengerdacUKMGBdOCLCOdBDXdOCLCFdOCLCO  apcc  9Netherlands00aWG 30004a617.4122300aDebulking in cardiovascular interventions and revascularization strategies :bbetween a rock and the heart /cedited by On Topaz. 1aLondon, United Kingdom:bElsevier/Academic Press,c[2022]  axl, 930 pages :billustrations (colour) ;c24 cm  atext2rdacontent  aunmediated2rdamedia  avolume2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.12aCoronary Artery Diseasexsurgery22aCytoreduction Surgical Proceduresxmethods22aCardiac Sur