ent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 20 (March 2020).  aForensic Imaging is dedicated to all aspects of forensic imaging and its implications for forensic medicine. Forensic Imaging covers various noninvasive and minimally invasive examination methods in a forensic context, mainly around the context of postmortem imaging, but also in conjunction with clinical forensic cases. Research subjects that are associated with these domains also include forensic veterinary investigations, forensic anthropology, as well as the use of 3D data acquired with a variety of imaging sensors.  aAlso issued in print.  aDescription based on: Volume 20 (March 2020); title from journal home page (Elsevier, viewed May 21, 2020).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 23 (December 2020) (viewed October 15, 2020).12aForensic Medicine22aDiagnostic Imaging22aAutopsy 2aPeriodical2 aInternational Society of Forensic Radiology and Imaging,eissuing body.2 aInternational Association of Forensic Radiographers,eissuing body.0 x2666-226400tJournal of forensic radiology and imagingx2212-4780w(DLC)   2015243375w(OCoLC)92138740140uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/forensic-imaging  aAUTH01701cas a2200433 a 101775119DNLM20260227090914.0m     o  d        cr |||||||||||181114c20179999gw qr poo     0    0eng    a  2021229594  a1097053984a1117861779a1125677662a1136306834a1192488770a1229892496  a2509-9396l2509-9396  z(OCoLC)1097053984z(OCoLC)1117861779z(OCoLC)1125677662z(OCoLC)1136306834z(OCoLC)1192488770z(OCoLC)1229892496  a(OCoLC)1113440052z(OCoLC)1097053984z(OCoLC)1117861779z(OCoLC)1125677662z(OCoLC)1136306834z(OCoLC)1192488770z(OCoLC)1229892496  aADUbengepnd20260227dAU@dOCLCOdLVTdOCLCFdOCLCQdSFBdAUDdDNLM  apcc  9Germany00aW10 aSynOpen (2017)10aSynOpen2dnlm10aSynOpen.  a[Stuttgart] :bGeorg Thieme Verlag,c[2017]-  a1 online resource  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 01, issue 01 (March 2017)  aTitle from web page (viewed on Nov. 13, 2018).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only0 aVolume 01, issue 01 (March 2017); title from journal home page (publisher's website viewed March 12, 2021).1 aVolume 05, issue 01 (January 2021) (viewed March 12, 2021).12aChemistry22aChemistry Techniques, Synthetic 2aPeriodical40uhttps://www.thieme-connect.com/products/ejournals/journal/10.1055/s-00032269  aAUTH01923cgm a22004574a 101267659DNLM20260227150003.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu056  |||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10524  a(OCoLC)84671115  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQV 20.51 aZasloff, Michael.10aNIH Job Fair keynote address, 2002 :bfrom NIH bench to the biotech bedside /cMichael A. Zasloff.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 24, 2006).  aStreaming video (55 min., 50 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 15, 2002.3 a(CIT): In 1987 Dr. Zasloff, then Chief of the Human Genetics Branch of the NICHD, discovered "Magainin," a family of antimicrobial peptides in the skin of Xenopus laevis. Dr. Zasloff will discuss what followed, including the creation of a biotech company, the development of several new drugs, and scientific explorations in the area of innate immunity.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aAnti-Infective Agentsxtherapeutic use12aBiomedical Research22aBiotechnology22aDrug Industry22aTechnology Transfer 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNIH Job Fair for Postdoctoral Fellowsd(2002 :cBethesda, Md.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10524  aAUTH01514cgm a22004214a 101267747DNLM20260227145505.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu069 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10626  a(OCoLC)69391326  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 3751 aKinnally, Kathleen.10aOn the role of mitochondrial channels and bcl-2 family proteins in apoptosis /cKathleen Kinnally.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 26, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 8 min., 40 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 8, 2001.3 a(CIT): Sponsored by: The Mitochondria Interest Group   Please see the Official Site.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aAutophagy22aApoptosis Inducing Factor22aCytochromes c. 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10626  aAUTH03612cgm a22004574a 101535283DNLM20260227153513.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100702s2010    mdu058|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15972  a(OCoLC)655615740  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3071 aBrecht, Michael.10aMicrocircuits of the medial entorhinal cortex and a theory of spatial memory formation /cMichael Brecht.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience series.  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (58 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 21, 2010.3 a(CIT): Rodent medial entorhinal cortex represents both spatial information (in grid cells) and head-direction information but the microcircuits integrating the two types of signals are unknown. To explore the relationship between activity and neuronal connectivity in the MEC, Dr. Brecht's lab developed a new method for recording and labeling single neurons by juxtacellular stimulation in freely-moving rats. Their method is based on novel a head-mountable, friction-based devise for mechanical stabilization of the glass pipette during the recording. They find that large fraction of grid-cells were spiny stellate neurons organized in about 500-700 small patches in layer 2. Grid cells and other superficial layer neurons were mainly active during exploration. Deep layers neurons in contrast rarely showed exploration activity and became active at rest. Most head-direction-cells were aspiny neurons residing in a system of about 25 pairs of large patches at the dorso-medial borders of medial entorhinal cortex. Head-direction-patches are large clusters of layer 2 neurons inserted in the cortical sheet with little or no other cortical layers associated. They identify three long-range axon systems in the MEC: A system of centrifugal axons, through which most if not all grid-cells axons target single head-direction-patches. A system of centripetal axons, through which most if not all head-direction-cells target single grid patches. A system of circumcurrent axons through which head-direction-cells connected many other head-direction patches. They suggest a microcircuit model how these axons might function in the alignment and formation of entorhinal maps. Accordingly, circumcurrent axons impose a global "one head-direction" constraint. Centripetal axons rotate grid orientations upon head movements and centrifugal axons send a "stop rotate-signal". Assuming that grid cell firing increases with the fit of landmark inputs to grid vertices such a circuit could align head-direction and map information. In a novel environment the entorhinal map could be fractured and aligned to landmarks by the relative rotation of the orientation of grid-patches.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aEntorhinal Cortexxphysiology22aMemoryxphysiology22aNeural Pathwaysxphysiology22aSpatial Behaviorxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15972  aAUTH02417cgm a22004454a 101488633DNLM20260227152840.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081104s2008    mdu142 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14701  a(OCoLC)297494453  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.51 aMiller, Franklin G.10aEthical and regulatory aspects of clinical reserach.nSession 4,pPlacebo controls and research with children /cFrank Miller and Lainie Ross.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Dec. 15, 2008).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 21 min., 48 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 15, 2008.3 a(CIT): Ethical and Regulatory Aspects of Clinical Research  Department of Clinical Bioethics  This course is designed to provide a historical context for research regulations and to help researchers and others working in human subject research gain insights and skills into the development of research protocols and their ethical implementation.   Objectives of the Course:   To learn the codes, declarations, and other documents that govern the ethical conduct of human subject research; review the critical elements of informed consent and their implementation in actual informed consent documents for clinical research; explore controversial issues relating to human subject research, including Phase I research, randomization, children in research, international research, etc; review the purpose if IRBs and provide IRB-like experience in reviewing research protocols; understand the experience of human subjects who have participated in research protocols.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomedical Researchxethics22aChild22aEthics Committees, Research22aEthics, Research22aHuman Experimentationxethics22aPlacebos 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14701  aAUTH02412cgm a22004454a 101488634DNLM20260227152859.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081104s2008    mdu122 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14703  a(OCoLC)280350213  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWD 2102 aNIH Research Festivald(2008 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNIH Research Festival 2008.pOpening plenary session, obesity :ba growing energy crisis /cClifton Bogardus ... [et al.].30aOpening plenary session, obesity :ba growing energy crisis30aObesity :ba growing energy crisis  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Nov. 10, 2008).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 1 min., 14 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 14, 2008.3 a(CIT): Obesity is rapidly increasing in prevalence in the developed and developing world, is associated with considerable morbidity involving multiple organ systems, and is a cause of health disparities in minority populations. The increasing prevalence is most alarming in children and adolescents. In the last decade there has been an exponential increase in our understanding of physiologic and molecular mechanisms regulating this energy imbalance in humans. This knowledge should lead to increasingly effective preventive and therapeutic approaches to combat this growing energy crisis.   <u>Program:</u>  Why are Some People Obese? Studies of the Pima Indians Clifton Bogardus, NIDDK  Pediatric Obesity: Causes and Consequences Jack Yanovski, NICHD  The Role of Brain Reward and Memorizing Circuits in Obesity Nora Volkow, NIDA  Closing the Energy Gap: Treatment Strategies for Obesity Monica Skarulis, NIDDK.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aObesityxetiology22aObesityxethnology22aObesityxtherapy 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aBogardus, Clifton.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14703  aAUTH02485cgmaa22005174a 101232627DNLM20260227144529.0vf|cbahouvf|czahmucr|cna||||||||vf|cjahouhd|bfa012baap041025s2004    mdu067 ||    f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:3426  a(OCoLC)276641078  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aQH 40100aHuman genetic variation and human disease :ba short-term approach for improving health /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.1 iTitle on cassette spine label:aHuman genetic variation & common disease  aUnited States.  aBethesda, MD :bNational Institutes of Health,c[2004]  a1 vidoecassette of 1 (DVCPRO) (67 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/4 in.3master.  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (67 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette 1 (VHS) (67 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aSpeaker, David A. Cox.  aOriginally presented Oct. 6, 2004.  aCommon human diseases are caused by a combination of multiple genetic and environmental factors. Studies suggest that many different genes distributed throughout the human genome contribute to the total genetic variability of a particular complex trait. It is now possible to identify the set of major genetic risk factors contributing to the variability of a complex disease and/or treatment response.  aVideocassette (Betacam SP)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2011.e1 videocassette (67 min.) : sd., col., 1/2 in.  aVHS.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aGenetic Variation22aGenome, Human22aPolymorphism, Single Nucleotide22aRisk Factors1 aCox, David R.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3426  aAUTH01446cgm a22003974a 101267732DNLM20260227145425.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s1999    mdu086 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10610  a(OCoLC)70051262  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4751 aThompson, Larry,cDr.10aHomologous recombinational repair and its role in cancer /cLarry Thompson ; sponsored by the DNA Repair Interest Group.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c1999]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 11, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 25 min., 2 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 19, 1999.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This videocast is the work of the United States Government and no copyright exists.  This may be disseminated freely.12aDNA Repair22aNeoplasmsxgenetics22aRecombination, Genetic 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bDNA Repair Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10610  aAUTH01526cas a2200433 i 9918470777806676DNLM20260227090913.0m     o  d        cr#|||||||||||220921c20229999ts || poo     |    0eng    a  2023228213  a2772-5758l2772-574X  a(OCoLC)1368049850  aDNLMbengerdad20260227cDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United Arab Emirates00aW110aRecent Adv Food Nutr Agric2dnlm00aRecent advances in food, nutrition & agriculture.1 aRecent advances in food, nutrition and agriculture 1a[Saif Zone, Sharjah, U.A.E.] :bBentham Science Publishers,c[2022]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 13, issue 1 (2022).1 aMEDLINEb2022-0 aPubMedb2022-0 aVolume 13, issue 1 (2022); title from journal home page (publisher's website, viewed January 26, 2023).1 aVolume 13, issue 2 (2022) (viewed January 26, 2023).12aFood Technology22aNutritional Physiological Phenomena22aAgriculture 2aPeriodical0 x2772-574X00tRecent patents on food, nutrition & agriculturex2212-7984w(DLC)   2010243655w(OCoLC)50053593940uhttps://www.eurekaselect.com/journal/210  aAUTH02640cgm a22004454a 101522181DNLM20260227153217.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100105s2009    mdu115|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15486  a(OCoLC)503613658  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQV 26.500aCTSA pharmaceutical assets portal :bmatching academia and industry for drug repositioning /cNCRR, NCI, and NIH Clinical Center.30aClinical and Translational Science Awards pharmaceutical assets portal  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 55 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 4, 2009.3 a(CIT): This meeting will introduce the NIH community to the topic of drug repositioning and learn about the CTSA Pharma Portal. NIH Intramural researchers will be able to learn to match their scientific interests with the repositioning needs of the industry, resulting in an increased number of approved drugs for new indications.  Drug repositioning, or finding new uses for drugs originally designed for another purpose, has become more important recently as many pharmaceutical companies are seeing their drug pipelines dry up. With the high cost of drug research and development, drug repositioning offers a way to explore these previously shelved assets. The Clinical and Translational Science Award (CTSA) Pharmaceutical Assets Portal is a tool that aims to match researchers" scientific knowledge of targets and diseases with the repositioning needs of the pharmaceutical industry to potentially increase the number of approved drugs for alternative uses.      Speakers include representatives from Pharma and NIH investigators involved in drug repositioning efforts.  http://www.palladianpartners.com/pharm-assets.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDrug Information Servicesxtrends22aDrug Discoveryxmethods22aDrug Industryxmethods22aInternet22aPharmaceutical Preparationsxadministration & dosage 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Center for Research Resources (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15486  aAUTH01595cas a2200457 a 8405037DNLM20260227090956.0cr||||||||||||840419c19839999dcuqr p       0    0eng    a   84643999 zsc 83009219zsn 83003172zsn 83001995  a0745-5194l0745-5194  aMAQUD5  a(DNLM)M11790000(s)  a(OCoLC)09250614  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bME206D10aMed Anthropol Q2dnlm 0aMedical anthropology quarterly00aMedical anthropology quarterly.  aWashington, D.C. :bSociety for Medical Anthropology,cc1983-31aMalden, MA :bJohn Wiley & Sons Ltd  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 14, no. 2 (Feb. 1983)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv9n1,Mar. 1995-0 aPubMedbv9n1,Mar. 1995-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3Vol. 8, no. 3 (SEPT 1994)c19950516fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paperxFurther review is required to determine exactly when acid-free paper was first used for this title 2aAnthropology 2aPeriodical2 aSociety for Medical Anthropology.00tMedical anthropology newsletterx0543-2499w(OCoLC)027229854 uhttp://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/journal/10.1111/(ISSN)1548-1387  aAUTH02298cas a2200529 a 101299949DNLM20260224111556.0cr|cn ||||||||060731c20079999dcuwn p       0    0eng    a  20062130600 a1932-7447l1932-7447  