ping B-cells /cJohn Monroe.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed June 13, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 13 min., 19 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 5, 2003.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aB-Lymphocytesxcytology22aCell Differentiation22aReceptors, Antigen, B-Cell22aSignal Transduction 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11318  aAUTH01055nam a2200313 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390200018000800400025000980420008001230440010001310600011001412450191001522640035003433000039003783360026004173370028004433380027004714900031004985040041005296500017005706500026005876500022006136550017006357000049006528300031007019990009007329918990594206676DNLM20260223090004.0250212s2024    cl a     b    000 0 spa    a9789566052043  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Chile00aWB 92500aRecetario popular :bsalud comunitaria en tiempos de crisis /cdirección de contenido: Pabla San Martín ; colaboradorxs: Orlando Ramírez, Domínguez, Verna Peréz y Domingo Jara. 1aChile :bGinecosofía,c2024.  a63 pages :billustrations ;c21 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aCuadernos de salud popular  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aPhytotherapy12aMedicine, Traditional22aPlants, Medicinal 2aPopular Work1 aPérez San Martín, Pabla,d1987-eeditor. 0aCuadernos de salud popular  aAUTH02556cgm a2200469 a 101574465DNLM20260227154143.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u111208s2011    mdu068|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16939  a(OCoLC)770841441  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3201 aDu Lac, Sascha.10aCerebellar learning :bbeyond the Purkinje cell /cSascha du Lac.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 8 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aRecorded Oct. 31, 2011.3 a(CIT): Dr. Sascha du Lac's research is focused on the neural mechanisms of learning. They use motor learning in eye movements as a model system to examine how cellular and molecular mechanisms of plasticity operate in the context of neural circuits to improve behavioral performance. Their laboratory employs a variety of techniques to identify mechanisms by which the cerebellum controls motor learning, including behavioral analyses of eye movements in transgenic mice and cellular physiological studies in brain slices.  They study learning in a well-defined behavior, the vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR), which enables clear vision during head movements. Learning in the VOR occurs whenever vision is compromised persistently during head movements. The neural circuitry for the VOR is well understood, enabling us to link specific cellular and molecular mechanisms with their consequences for learning. Their lab studies how changes in intrinsic neuronal excitability and synaptic transmission operate within the circuit for the VOR to transform experience into adaptive changes in behavior.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aCerebellumxphysiology22aLearningxphysiology22aMemoryxphysiology22aNeuronal Plasticityxphysiology22aNeuronsxphysiology22aPurkinje Cellsxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16939  aAUTH02499cgm a2200469 i 101605272DNLM20260227154730.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130402s2013    mdu864 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17866  a(DNLM)CIT:17869  a(OCoLC)840878132  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWG 21000aCancer treatment related cardiotoxicity /cNational Cancer Institute, National Heart, Lung and Blood Institute.1 iSubtitle on PowerPoint:aUnderstanding the current state of knowledge and developing future research priorities 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (14 hr., 24 min.) :bcolor, sound)  atwo-dimensional moving image2rdacontent  acomputer2rdamedia  aonline resource2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen  aRecorded Mar. 20 & 21, 2013.3 a(CIT): Cancer treatment-related cardiotoxicity is a leading cause of treatment-associated morbidity and mortality in cancer survivors. The gaps in knowledge related to cardiotoxicity are large, including mechanisms of cardiac injury, clinical prediction, screening, prevention, and treatment, as well as the similarities and differences between cancer induced cardiotoxicity and primary cardiovascular disease. There is an increasing interest in studying cancer treatment-related cardiotoxicity by both the cardiology and oncology specialties. The goal of this two days conference is to bring together multiple disciplines within cardiology and oncology to identify research priorities in the area of cancer treatment-related cardiotoxicity that will inform future NIH initiatives in this important research area.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed April 5, 2013.12aHeart Diseasesxchemically induced22aAntineoplastic Agentsxadverse effects22aHeartxdrug effects22aHeartxradiation effects22aRadiotherapyxadverse effects 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1786640uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17869  aAUTH01661cgmaa22004454a 101247328DNLM20260227144622.0vf|cbahouvf|cjahoucr|cna||||||||050504s2005    mdu053 ||         vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:3447  a(OCoLC)60414283  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aQU 5500aQuality control of transmembrane proteins /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  aUnited States.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (53 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (53 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aThe NIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture  aSpeaker, Hugh R.B. Pelham.  aPresented March 23, 2005.  aAlso available online.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aMembrane Proteins22aMutation22aProtein Folding22aProtein Transport1 aPelham, H. R. B.q(Hugh R. B.)2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3447  aAUTH01540cgm a22004334a 101268277DNLM20260227150327.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu629 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11613  a(DNLM)CIT:11614  a(OCoLC)71233028  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 26.500aDigital biology :bthe emerging paradigm /cNational Institute of General Medical Sciences.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Aug. 9, 2006).  aStreaming video (10 hr., 28 min., 55 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 6-7, 2003.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aComputational Biology22aComputing Methodologies22aMedical Informatics22aModels, Theoretical 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of General Medical Sciences (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1161340uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11614  aAUTH02279cgm a2200457 i 9918573160206676DNLM20260227163255.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2023    mdu089|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32950  a(OCoLC)1379265609  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 57100aDemystifying  medicine.pThe role of Epstein-Barr virus and molecular mimicry in autoimmune disease /cJudith James, William Robinson.1 aRole of Epstein-Barr virus and molecular mimicry in autoimmune disease 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 29 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded February 14, 2023.3 a(CIT): The Demystifying Medicine Series, jointly sponsored by FAES and NIH, includes presentations on pathology, diagnosis, and therapy in the context of major disease problems and current research. Primarily directed toward PhD students, clinicians and program managers, this series is designed to help bridge the gap between advances in biology and their application to major human diseases. Each session includes clinical and basic science components presented by NIH staff and invitees. All students, trainees, fellows, and staff are welcome to participate.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed May 2, 2023).12aEpstein-Barr Virus Infectionsximmunology12aEpstein-Barr Virus Infectionsxcomplications22aAutoimmune Diseasesxetiology22aAutoimmunityximmunology22aMolecular Mimicry 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aJames, Judithq(Judith A.),espeaker.1 aRobinson, Williamq(William Hewitt),espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=48819  aAUTH02288cgm a2200445 i 9918573277806676DNLM20260227162842.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2022    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32736  a(OCoLC)1437827804  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.500aSCORE Annual Meeting keynote.pNext generation sex and gender science /cCara Tannenbaum.1 aNext generation sex and gender science 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 0 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 1, 2022.3 a(CIT): The Specialized Centers of Research Excellence on Sex Differences, or SCORE, program is the only NIH cooperative agreement program supporting disease-agnostic research on sex differences. Each center serves as a national resource for translational research to identify the role of biological sex differences in the health of women.  The purpose of the SCORE Annual Meeting is to highlight the yearly research achievements of the 11 centers in the SCORE program to disseminate findings and promote collaboration. At this year"s SCORE Annual Meeting, Cara Tannenbaum, M.D., M.Sc., Scientific Director of the Institute of Gender and Health at the Canadian Institutes of Health Research, will present the keynote address "Next Generation Sex and Gender Science.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed May 30, 2024).12aBiomedical Research12aSex Factors22aGender Role22aGender Identity22aResearch Design 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aTannenbaum, Cara,cM.D.espeaker.2 aSpecialized Centers of Research Excellence on Sex Differences (Program),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=46404  aAUTH01373cam a2200373 i 02420360RDNLM20260206220347.0cr||||||||||||810622t19181918nyua          000 0 eng    a   19012562   a(OCoLC)14812119  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aHQ 46bM143w 19181 aMacfadden, Bernarr,d1868-1955,eauthor.10aWomanhood and marriage :bfifty-three lessons in sex hygiene exclusively for women /cby Bernarr Macfadden. 1aNew York City :bThe Physical Culture Publishing Company,c[1918] 4c©1918  a21 parts :billustrations ;c19 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCover title.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20250613lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aMarriage22aSexual Behaviorxethics22aReproductive Health22aWomen2 aPhysical Culture Publishing Company,epublisher.4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/02420360R  aAUTH02089nam a2200421 i 9918986614606676DNLM20260225080710.0230111t20242024flua     b    001 0 eng    a  2022062315  a9780367252809qhardcover  a0367252805qhardcover  a9781032520575qpaperback  a1032520574qpaperback  z9780429293948qelectronic book8 a40032373920  a(OCoLC)1368048404  aDLCbengerdacDLCdOCLCFdOCLCOdYDXdOCLCOdBDXdYUS  apcc  9United States00aQR82.A35bA286 202400aQW 12500a579.3/21223/eng/2023020100aActinomycetes in marine and extreme environments :bunexhausted sources for microbial biotechnology /ceditor, D. Ipek Kurtböke.  aFirst edition. 1aBoca Raton :bCRC Press, Taylor & Francis Group,c2024. 4c©2024  avi, 326 pages :billustrations (some color) ;c25 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  a"Mankind is in search of new and potent therapeutic agents to combat ever increasing antibiotic resistance problem as well as to feed the market of biotechnology in terms of new enzymes and other microbial secondary metabolites sought after by industry. Microorganisms at extreme environments might be the resource in this search as they are continuously subjected to extreme pressures and their metabolic changes in these environments might provide new chemical scaffolds industry needs desperately. The proposed book will cover microorganisms that inhabit in extreme environments and reveal their potential as novel sources of new drugs and other industrially useful metabolites"--cProvided by publisher.12aActinobacteria22aExtreme Environments22aBiotechnologyxmethods1 aKurtböke, Ipek,eeditor.  aAUTH02416cgm a2200409 i 9918681341106676DNLM20260227164108.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230929s2023    mdu334|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33453  a(OCoLC)1416045953  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQV 77.72 aOHRP Exploratory Workshopn(6th :d2023 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aExploring the ethical and practical considerations of psychedelics research /cHHS OHRP. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (5 hr., 34 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 14, 2023.3 a(CIT): Psychedelics are powerful psychoactive substances that alter perception and mood and affect numerous cognitive processes. Their origins predate written history, and early cultures used them in many sociocultural and ritual contexts. The name "psychedelics" was coined by Humphrey Osmond in 1957, suggesting that they have a mind-manifesting capability that may reveal useful or beneficial properties of the mind. For decades, psychedelics have been classified as illegal drugs. Recent research suggests that these substances may provide a potential breakthrough in the treatment of a myriad of mental health conditions. This exploratory workshop will examine the ethical and practical considerations for psychedelics research with the goal of promoting an open and grounded discourse on how to conduct research that is inclusive and protective of participants.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 14, 2023).12aHallucinogensxtherapeutic use22aMental Disordersxdrug therapy22aClinical Trials as Topicxethics 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aUnited States.bDepartment of Health and Human Services.bOffice for Human Research Protections,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49708  aAUTH01579cgm a22004574a 101475712DNLM20260227152530.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080602s2008    mdu055|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14490  a(OCoLC)236052138  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWH 7001 aSchwartzberg, Pamela.10aT lymphocyte signaling and primary immunodeficiencies :blessons from x-linked lymphoproliferative disease /cPamela Schwartzberg.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNational Cancer Institute grand rounds  aTitle from title screen (viewed July 13, 2008).  aStreaming video (55 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded May 13, 2008.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aLymphoproliferative Disordersxgenetics12aLymphoproliferative Disordersximmunology22aReceptors, Antigen, T-Cellximmunology22aSignal Transductionxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNCI grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14490  aAUTH02647cgm a2200433 i 101774272DNLM20260227160415.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu061|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31378  a(OCoLC)1229081311  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 265.5.L50 aGaucher disease (Sidransky)10aGaucher disease :bhow a rare disease provides a window into common neurodegenerative disorders /cEllen Sidransky. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture. Astute clinician lecture ;v23rd  aRecorded November 4, 2020.3 a(CIT): Dr. Sidransky is chief of the Medical Genetics Branch and head of its Molecular Neurogenetics Section in the NIH National Human Genome Research Institute.  Her research includes clinical and basic research aspects of both Gaucher disease and Parkinson disease.  Gaucher disease is a rare disorder caused by a hereditary deficiency of the enzyme glucocerebrosidase and is characterized by easy bruising, fatigue, anemia, low platelet count, and enlargement of the liver and spleen.  Dr. Sidransky played a lead role in establishing the association between glucocerebrosidase and parkinsonism.Dr. Sidransky also has spearheaded two large international collaborative studies regarding the genetics of Parkinson disease and dementia with Lewy bodies.  Her current work also focuses on understanding the complexity encountered in "simple" Mendelian disorders, the association between Gaucher disease and parkinsonism, and the development of small-molecule chaperones as therapy for Gaucher disease and potentially Parkinsonism.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed January 5, 2021).12aGaucher Disease22aNeurodegenerative Diseases 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aSidransky, Ellen,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH di