al/2770596x40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=9918350383706676  aAUTH02698ngm a2200481 i 9918991490706676DNLM20260227165213.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u250207s2025    mdu117|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35528  a(DNLM)CIT:35529  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 26.55.C5800aNIH security best practices for users of genomic controlled access data /cCheryl Jacobs, Maureen Falvella.1 aSecurity update for approved users :bsecuring NIH controlled-access genomic data 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2025]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 57 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded January 8 & 9, 2025.3 a(CIT): Who Should Attend: All researchers and their institutional officials who have access to or are planning to request access to use controlled-access genomics data hosted by NIH data repositories (List of NIH-supported Controlled-Access Repositories).During these information sessions, NIH will provide users of NIH Controlled Access Data repositories and their institutions an overview of Guide Notice NOT-OD-24-157 "Implementing Update for Data Management and Access Practices Under the Genomic Data Sharing Policy" and related Frequently Asked Questions. NIH will provide an overview of the requirements, assist organizations with understanding how they can be compliant with the requirements, and answer any questions users and their institutions have. Users and their institutions are encouraged to submit their questions by December 27th to ensure answers are provided during the informational sessions.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 12, 2026).22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aComputer Security22aDatabases, Factual22aGenetic Privacy22aData Managementxorganization & administration22aInformation Disseminationxlegislation & jurisprudence 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aJacobs, Cheryl,espeaker.1 aFalvella, Maureen,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=5652040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=56521  aAUTH02686cgm a2200433 i 9918282190606676DNLM20260227161614.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u211008s2021    mdu701|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31963  a(DNLM)CIT:31967  a(OCoLC)1310762549  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 612 aMethodological Approaches for Whole Person Research Workshopd(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aMethodological approaches for whole person research  /cNCCIH, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (11 hr., 41 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 29 & 30, 2021.3 a(CIT): The National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health's new strategic plan defines whole person research as including three components: exploring the fundamental science of interconnected systems; Investigating multicomponent interventions or therapeutic systems; examining the impact of these interventions on multisystem or multiorgan outcomes. The Whole Person Research Workshop will discuss examples of research studies in these three areas from diverse fields and explore methodologies potentially appropriate for whole person research. The workshop will be led by the National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH). Workshop collaborators include the National Institute on Aging, National Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities, National Institute of Nursing Research, National Institute of Dental and Craniofacial Research, Fogarty International Center, Office of Research on Women's Health, Office of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research, Office of Disease Prevention, and the Office of Nutrition Research within the National Institutes of Health Office of the Director.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 30, 2022).12aHolistic Health22aBiomedical Research22aResearch Design 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4252840uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42578  aAUTH03021cgm a2200445 i 101769641DNLM20260227160119.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u200715s2020    mdu069|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:29195  a(OCoLC)1229081507  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQT 162.L500aLighting up our lives :bhow light influences our mental and physical health /cHelen Burgess. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 9 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCCIH integrative medicine research lecture, Novel approaches at the intersection of mental health and pain  aRecorded June 30, 2020.3 a(CIT): The National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health presents the inaugural lecture in a series, "Novel Approaches at the Intersection of Mental Health and Pain," part of the Center's Integrative Medicine Research Lecture Series:   The National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health presents the inaugural lecture in a series, "Novel Approaches at the Intersection of Mental Health and Pain," part of the Center"s Integrative Medicine Research Lecture Series: Lighting Up Our Lives: How Light Influences Our Mental and Physical Health Helen Burgess, Ph.D., University of Michigan Natural outdoor light and artificial indoor light can affect our mental and physical health. This is especially relevant in the current times when we are asked to stay home and spend less time outside. Dr. Burgess will examine how light reaches the brain and can alter brain centers that regulate circadian (body clock) timing, sleep, and mood. Specific properties of light, such as intensity, wavelength, and timing, will be discussed, as will individual differences in light sensitivity. The speaker will also examine current approaches to morning light treatment and how it can be used to improve health and reduce symptom burden in a variety of clinical disorders such as chronic pain conditions, depression, and posttraumatic stress disorder.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 30, 2020).12aLight22aCircadian Clocks22aChronic Painxtherapy22aStress Disorders, Traumaticxtherapy22aPhototherapy 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aBurgess, Helenc(Professor of psychiatry)espeaker. 0aNCCIH integrative medicine research lecture.pNovel approaches at the intersection of mental health and pain40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?29195  aAUTH00931cam a22002897a 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040001900073041001300092042001300105044001000118060003200128100005200160240003100212245008600243260004300329300002800372336002600400337002800426338002700454500004800481650004700529650002800576650002800604999000900632101609757DNLM20260209110623.0050804s1951    cc a          000 0 chi    aCStcSTFdDNLM1 achihrus  anlmcopyc  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1778 no.81 aArkhangelʹskiĭ, Boris Aleksandrovich,d1890-10aMatʹ i diti︢a.lChinese10aYing er de yin shi /cA'erhangailiesiji, Sipailangsiji he zhu ; Xue Shensheng yi.  a[Shanghai] :bJia chu ban she,c[1951]  a49 p. :bill. ;c19 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aSelected translation from Matʹ i di