ic pain :bmoving from concept to clinical trials /cLinda R. Watkins. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 13 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aSeymour J. Kreshover lecture  aRecorded May 4, 2015.3 a(CIT): When non-neuronal cells known as glia are activated, they can create persistent, amplified neuroinflammation, thereby promoting the transition from acute to chronic pain. Dr. Watkins' work has shown that glial activation can also compromise the ability of opioids to suppress chronic pain, contributing to the development of drug tolerance and dependence.  Her findings have advanced our understanding of how therapeutics that target a specific glial activation receptor are effective stand-alone treatments for chronic pain and can also increase the efficacy of opioids while decreasing analgesic tolerance and dependence. Two such drugs, developed in the Watkins lab, are moving toward clinical trials.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed June 15, 2015).12aChronic Painxdrug therapy12aChronic Painxmetabolism22aAnalgesics, Opioidxtherapeutic use22aDrug Delivery Systems22aNeurogliaxmetabolism 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Dental and Craniofacial Research (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aSeymour J. Kreshover lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18981  aAUTH01157nam a2200337 i 9918935627506676DNLM20260209152543.0241105s2022    ti a     b    000 0 ara    a  2023469447  a9789938999952  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Tunisia00aWB 4001 aQunayn, Luṭfī,d1956-eauthor.13aal-Taghdhīyah wa-ʻilāj amrāḍihā /ctaʼlīf Luṭfī al-Qunayn. 1aTūnis :bDār al-Mālikīyah lil-Ṭibāʻah wa-al-Nashr wa-al-Tawzīʻ,c2022.  a464 pages :billustrations ;c25 cm  atextbtxt2r