60227090914.0cr||||||||||||110706c20129999nyuqr p       0    0eng    a  20112045670 a2162-2590l2162-259021  a(OCoLC)739102995  aDLCcDLCdDLCdZYUdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW1bJO907Y10aPsychodyn Psychiatry2dnlm00aPsychodynamic psychiatry.  aNew York, NY :bGuilford Press  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 40, no. 1 (spring 2012).1 aMEDLINEbv40n1, Spring 2012-0 aPubMedbv40n1, Spring 2012-  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: Vol. 40, no. 1 (spring 2012); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 40, no. 1 (spring 2012).12aPsychoanalytic Therapy22aPsychoanalysis 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Academy of Psychoanalysis and Dynamic Psychiatry.0 x2162-260400tJournal of the American Academy of Psychoanalysis and Dynamic Psychiatryx1546-0371w(DLC)   2003243242w(OCoLC)521359434 uhttp://guilfordjournals.com/loi/jaap  aAUTH03091cgm a2200433 i 101663000DNLM20260227155621.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150625s2015    mdu058|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19071  a(OCoLC)919859979  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 3751 aYamashita, Yukiko,eauthor.10aAsymmetric stem cell division in drosophila male germline /cYukiko Yamashita. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (58 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded June 17, 2015.3 a(CIT): Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series (WALS)  Adult stem cells continuously supply highly differentiated but short-lived cells, such as blood, skin, intestinal epithelium, and sperm cells, throughout life. To maintain the balance between stem cells and differentiating cells -- a failure of which may lead to tumorigenesis through excess self-renewal or tissue degeneration through excess differentiation -- many stem cells have the potential to divide asymmetrically so that each division produces one stem cell and one differentiating cell.  Drosophila male germline stem cells (GSCs) serve as an ideal model system to study stem-cell behavior. They reside in the stem-cell niche, which specifies stem-cell identity by sending essential signal(s). Stem cells have elaborate cellular mechanisms to ensure the asymmetric outcome of the division, producing one stem cell and one differentiating cell, which is the key to tissue homeostasis. Whereas the stem-cell niche provides essential signals for stem-cell identity and proliferation, it is not well known how the niche signal is limited to stem cells, while other cells in proximity are not misspecified by the niche signaling. Here we show that in Drosophila testis, GSCs extend microtubule-based nanotubes into the hub cells, the major component of the stem-cell niche. We further demonstrate that the nanotube mediates decapentaplegic (Dpp) signaling between the hub and GSCs. We propose that confinement of the Dpp signaling to a spatially limited surface area of the nanotube allows privileged reception of niche-derived signals by stem cells.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed August 31, 2015).12aAsymmetric Cell Division22aDrosophilaxcytology22aStem Cell Niche 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19071  aAUTH01627cgm a2200445 i 101660643DNLM20260227155118.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150602s2015    mdu058|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19020  a(OCoLC)918550300  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWD 21000aObesity and brown fat :bof mice and men /cMarc Reitman.  Obesity management in adults /