chaftlichen Classe der Kaiserlichen Akademie der Wissenschaften.  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 53-54).12aMesenteryxembryology22aMesenteryxanatomy & histology  aAUTH02507cgm a22004334a 101525632DNLM20260227153255.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100302s2010    mdu240|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15593  a(OCoLC)610144329  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWS 1032 aNational Advisory Child Health and Human Development Council (U.S.)10aNational Advisory Child Health and Human Development Council, January 2010 /cEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (NICHD).  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (4 hr., 0 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 28, 2010.3 a(CIT): The NICHD Advisory Council has two specific functions:  (1) to advise the NICHD on policy and procedures affecting the extramural research programs and (2) to provide the second level of review for grant applications requesting Institute funding support. The Council meets for one day three times a year in January, June, and September.    The morning session is Open to the Public and is devoted to bringing Council members up-to-date with a Report of the Director, NICHD, a report by the Council Subcommittee on Planning and Policy, a review of one of the extramural program areas, discussion and review of proposed concepts for NICHD initiatives, the annual review of the Board of Scientific Counselors reports on intramural research programs, and new business items identified by the Council membership and Institute staff. The afternoon session is closed to the Public for the confidential Review of Applications.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.)12aGrowth and Development22aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15593  aAUTH01441cgm a22003974a 101267599DNLM20260227145408.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu088 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10427  a(OCoLC)71797955  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWR 2452 aLupus National Town Hall Meetingd(2001 :cWashington, D.C.)10aLupus National Town Hall Meeting :bunlocking the mysteries of lupus /cLupus Foundation of America, Inc., Office on Women's Health, Department of Health & Human Services.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title frame (viewed Sept. 21, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 27 min., 47 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 16, 2001.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: Copyright 2001 Lupus Foundation of America, Inc.12aLupus Vulgarisxtherapy22aConsumer Advocacy 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aLupus Foundation of America.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10427  aAUTH04263nam a2200457 i 9918986567806676DNLM20260225101913.0230921t20242024enka     b    000 0 eng    a  2023044206  a9781009373241qhardcover  a1009373242qhardcover  a9781009373265qpaperback  a1009373269qpaperback  z9781009373234qelectronic book  a(OCoLC)1401976702  aDLCbengerdacDLCdOCLCFdOCLCOdYDXdOCLCOdGZLdOCLCOdOCLCAdPLLdIG#  apcc  9United Kingdom00aK3611.R43bD54 202400aW 32.600a610.285223/eng/2023100500aDigital health care outside of traditional clinical settings :bethical, legal, and regulatory challenges and opportunities /cedited by I. Glenn Cohen, Harvard Law School, Daniel B. Kramer, Harvard Medical School, Julia Adler-Milstein, University of California, San Francisco, Carmel Shachar, Harvard Law School, Massachusetts.3 aDigital healthcare outside of traditional clinical settings 1aCambridge, United Kingdom ;aNew York, NY :bCambridge University Press,c2024. 4c©2024  axvii, 209 pages :billustrations ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.00gList of figures --gList of tables --gList of contributors --gAcknowledgments --tIntroduction /rCarmel Shachar, Julia Adler-Milstein, Daniel B. Kramer, and I. Glenn Cohen --tIntroduction /rCarmel Shachar --tIn the medical privacy of one's own home : four faces of privacy in digital home health care /rBarbara J. Evans --tPatient access to health device data : toward a legal framework /rCharles Duan and Christopher J. Morten --tChallenges of remote patient care technologies under the General Data Protection Regulation : preliminary results of the Tender project /rDanaja Fabcic Povse --tRenegotiating the social contract for use of health information : lessons learned from newborn screening and implications for at-home digital care /rJodyn Platt and Sharon Kardia --tPatient self-administered screening for cardiovascular disease using artificial intelligence in the home /rPatrick Bächtiger, Mihir A. Kelshiker, Marie E. G. Moe, Daniel B. Kramer, and Nicholas S. Peters --tThe promise of telehealth for abortion /rGreer Donley and Rachel Rebouché --tMonitoring (on) your mind-digital biomarkers for alzheimer's disease /rClaire Erickson and Emily A. Largent --tPhysician and device manufacturer tort liability for remote patient monitoring devices /rDavid A. Simon and Aaron S. Kesselheim --tPost-market surveillance of software medical devices : evidence from regulatory data /rAlexander O. Everhart and Ariel D. Stern --tLabeling of direct-to-consumer medical artificial intelligence applications for "self-diagnosis" /rSara Gerke --t"Internet plus health care" as an impetus for China's health system reform /rWang Chenguang and Zhang Yi --tA pathway for high-value home hospital care in the U.S. : statutory, reimbursement, and cybersecurity strategies in the age of hybrid care /rStephanie Zawada, Nels Paulson, Margaret Paulson, Michael Maniaci, and Bart Demaerschalk --tDigitally enabled Medicaid home and community-based services / Kathryn Huber and Tara Sklar --tEU in-home digital diagnostics : cross-border patient reimbursement under threat? /rKaat Van Delm.  a"This volume reflects on the recent explosion of at-home digital health care. It explores the ethical, legal, and regulatory impacts of this shift away from the 20th-century focus on clinics and hospitals towards a more modern health care model. This title is also available as Open Access on Cambridge Core"--cProvided by publisher.12aDigital Healthxethics12aDigital Healthxlegislation & jurisprudence22aMonitoring, Ambulatoryxethics1 aCohen, I. Glenn,eeditor.1 aKramer, Daniel B.,eeditor.1 aAdler-Milstein, Julia,eeditor.1 aShachar, Carmel,d1985-eeditor.  aAUTH02032cas a2200541 a 9808462DNLM20260227091037.0cr|cn ||||||||980611c19989999nyubr p       0    0eng    a   98657890 zsn 97001456   a1096-6218l1557-7740  aJPAMF9  a(DNLM)SR0092380(s)  a(OCoLC)37830779  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bJO826D10aJ Palliat Med2dnlm00aJournal of palliative medicine.30aPalliative medicine  aLarchmont, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.,cc1998-  av. :bill.  aMonthly,b2009-  aQuarterly,b1998-2001  aBimonthly,b2002-2007  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (spring 1998)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv4n1, Spring 2001-0 aPubMedbv4n1, Spring 2001-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aOfficial journal of American Academy of Hospice and Palliative Medicine, 2001-2005; Center to Advance Palliative Care, 2010- , Australian & New Zealand Society of Palliative Medicine, 2012- ; an office journal of Hospice and Palliative Nurses Association, 2010-  , Japanese Society for Palliative Medicine, 2016- .  3v.1, no. 1 (Spring 1998)c20010306fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper that meets the full ANSI standard for Permanent Paper12aPalliative Care12aTerminal Care 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Academy of Hospice and Palliative Medicine.2 aCenter to Advance Palliative Care.2 aHospice and Palliative Nurses Association.2 aANZSPM (Organization)2 aNihon Kanwa Iryō Gakkai.0 x1557-77404 uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/jpm  aAUTH01239cas a2200397 a 101171970DNLM20260227090919.0cr#cn---------030214c20039999cauar         0    0eng    a  20032132380 a1543-5938l1543-5938  a(OCoLC)51651412  aNSDcNSDdHNKdTJCdNYGdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States14aTJ163.2b.A5500aW1bAN77L  a333.7bA61510aAnnu Rev Environ Resour2dnlm00aAnnual review of environment and resources.  aPalo Alto, Calif. :bAnnual Reviews, Inc.,cc2003-  av. :bill. ;c23 cm.  aAnnual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 28 (2003)-0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso available online via the World Wide Web.12aEnvironment22aEnergy-Generating Resources0 x1545-205000tAnnual review of energy and the environmentx1056-3466w(DLC)   92658535w(OCoLC)236693254 uhttp://www.annualreviews.org/journal/energy  aAUTH02627cgm a22004694a 101319929DNLM20260227144830.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071101s2007    mdu061|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14059  a(OCoLC)182613527  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWI 5291 aAugenlicht, Leonard H.10aGenetic and nutritional modulation of intestinal tumorigenesis /cLeonard Augenlicht.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCI stars in nutrition lecture series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Nov. 19, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 1 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 4, 2007.3 a(CIT): The profound influence that nutrients have on the development of colon cancer is reflected in diet induced acceleration and amplification of tumor formation, and modulation of tumor phenotype, in even the most penetrant of mouse genetic models of intestinal cancer. Moreover, a Western-style mouse diet that mimics the levels of major risk factors for colon cancer (higher fat, lower calcium and vitamin D) can initiate mouse colon tumor formation when fed over two-thirds of the lifespan. Tumor formation in this model of dietary induced sporadic cancer, as well as in mouse genetic models is highly sensitive to calcium and vitamin D levels in the diet. We used the interaction of dietary and genetic factors in the mouse to probe molecular changes in the mucosa that alter probability of tumor formation. Alterations in cell maturation as cells migrate from the progenitor cell compartment are fundamental to tumorigenesis, and shifts in metabolic pathways may also play a significant role in perturbing complex transcriptional and signaling networks that normally maintain homeostasis and proper functioning of the intestinal mucosa.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aColorectal Neoplasmsxetiology22aDiet22aDisease Models, Animal22aMice22aNutritional Physiological Phenomena 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNCI stars in nutrition lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=6156  aAUTH01895cas a2200541   7801255DNLM20260227090950.0cr|cn ||||||||780302c19779999nyu x p       0    0eng    a   77641812   a0147-7307l0147-7307  aLHBEDM  a(DNLM)L03220000(s)z(DNLM)J27480000(s)  a(OCoLC)03173559  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bLA94210aLaw Hum Behav2dnlm00aLaw and human behavior.3 aJournal of law and human behavior  aNew York,bPlenum Press.2 31999- :aNew York, NY :bKluwer Academic/Plenum Publishers2 3<2007-2011> :aNew York :bSpringer3 32012- :aWashington, DC :bAmerican Psychological Association  av.bill.  aBimonthly,b1990-  aQuarterly,b1977-89  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-    1977-  aPublished with the assistance of students at the University of Virginia Law School and in cooperation with the American Psychology-Law Society.1 aMEDLINEbv21n1,Feb. 1997-0 aPubMedbv21n1,Feb. 1997-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aThe official journal of the American Psychology-Law Society/Division 41 of the American Psychological Association. 2aBehavior 2aBehavioral Sciences 2aJurisprudence 2aPeriodical2 aUniversity of Virginia.bSchool of Law.2 aAmerican Psychology-Law Society.2 aAmerican Psychological Association.0 x1573-661X4 uhttp://search.ebscohost.com/direct.asp?db=pdh&jid=LHB&scope=site  aAUTH01434cas a2200433 i 101632875DNLM20260227090907.0cr||||||||||||130605c20149999wauqr p       0    0eng    a  20132013250 a2329-423Xl2329-423X21  a(OCoLC)846852311  aDLCbengerdad20260227dDLCdOCLCQdDLCdDNLM  apccansdp  9United States00aW1bNE494510aNeurophotonics2dnlm00aNeurophotonics. 1aBellingham, WA :bSociety of Photo-Optical Instrumentation Engineers,c[2014]-  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Vol. 1, no. 1 (July/Spetember 2014).  aPrint issues ceased with v. 6 (2019).0 aPubMedbv1n1, 2014-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: Vol. 1, no. 1 (July/September 2014); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 2, no. 1 (January/March 2015).12aNeuroimaging22aOptical Phenomena 2aPeriodical2 aSPIE (Society),epublisher.0 x2329-42484 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=1016328754 uhttp://neurophotonics.spiedigitallibrary.org/journal.aspx  aAUTH01882cgm a22004454a 101298630DNLM20260227151929.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070201s2007    mdu061 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13604  a(OCoLC)85498346  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWP 4801 aLowy, Doug.14aThe science, technology, and promise of preventive HPV vaccines /cDouglas R. Lowy.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aPhilip Chen lecture ;v2nd  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 7, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 35 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 26, 2007.3 a(CIT): Second annual Philip S. Chen, Jr., Ph.D. Distinguished Lecture on Innovation and Technology Transfer. Dr. Douglas R. Lowy will present "The Science, Technology and Promise of Preventive HPV Vaccines." Dr. Lowy is Chief of NCI's Laboratory of Cellular Oncology and a deputy director of NCI's Center for Cancer Research. With colleagues at NIH, he developed the original technology on which the Merck HPV vaccine, Gardasil, is based.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aUterine Cervical Neoplasmsxprevention & control22aPapillomavirus Infectionsxprevention & control22aPapillomavirus Vaccines 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aPhilip Chen lecture ;v2nd.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13604  aAUTH01451cgm a2200433 a 101574460DNLM20260227154032.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u111208s2011    mdu058|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16930  a(OCoLC)770841428  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5681 aSwain, Susan,cDr.10aExpanding roles for memory CD4 T cells in anti-viral responses /cSusan Swain.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (58 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aImmunology Interest Group seminar  aRecorded Oct. 26, 2011.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aCD4-Positive T-Lymphocytesximmunology12aCD4-Positive T-Lymphocytesxvirology22aDrug Design22aVaccines, Syntheticxtherapeutic use 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group. 0aImmunology Interest Group lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16930  aAUTH03486cgm a22004574a 101488629DNLM20260227152614.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081104s2008    mdu063 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14696  a(OCoLC)298508997  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 1361 aGreene, Warner.14aThe APOBECs :ba biodefense against "retro-threats" foreign and domestic /cWarner Greene.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 19, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 2 min., 34 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 8, 2008.3 a(CIT): APOBEC3G (A3G) corresponds to a human cytidine deaminase that can impair the replication of HIV and other retroviruses. When encapsidated into HIV virions, A3G induces extensive dC-dU mutations in the nascent minus strand DNA of the virus formed during reverse transcription. These antiviral effects of A3G are countered by HIV Vif, which binds to A3G, recruits a Cul5/ElonginB/C ubiquitin ligase complex, and triggers A3G degradation by the 26S proteasome thereby precluding effective virion encapsidation of this antiviral enzyme.  The identification of small molecules capable of blocking Vif binding to A3G or Vif recruitment of the E3 ligage complex could ultimately lead to an exciting new class of HIV therapeutics that might re-enable a powerful intrinsic defense against HIV. Apart from its antiviral effects in virions, A3G also exerts antiviral activity within select types of cells. Specifically, a low-molecular-mass form of cellular A3G found in resting CD4 T cells and monocytes potently impairs the replication of incoming viruses by interfering with reverse transcription.   However, when T cells are activated, this defense is lost because LMM A3G is swept into high-molecular-mass ribonucleoprotein complexes. Interestingly, these HMM A3G complexes mediate a second important defensive function. They impair the retrotransposition of endogoneous nonautonomous retroelements most notably Alu. Indeed, the expression of Alu retroelement RNA during T cell activation triggers HMM A3G complex assembly- an event that unfortunately "opens the door" for HIV infection. In contrast to humans where seven APOBEC3 genes have been identified, mice express a single APOBEC3 gene. It appears likely that the expansion of the APOBEC3 family of genes in primates was driven by the need to limit retroelement retransposition. Additional studies describing unexpected functions of the murine APOBEC3 gene product gleaned from studies of Apobec3-deficient mice will also be presented.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aCytosine Deaminase22aHIV22avif Gene Products, Human Immunodeficiency Virus22aVirus Replication 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14696  aAUTH01567cas a2200469 a 101315005DNLM20260227090913.0cr|cn ||||||||070824c20079999it qr p       0    0eng    a  2008243995  a1971-3495l1876-7931  a(OCoLC)271810532  aAUcAUdDNLM0 aengaita  9Italy00aW1bGI615Q10aJ Ultrasound2dnlm00aJournal of ultrasound.  aMilano :bElsevier Masson3132013:a[Milan] :