h translation of Magiae naturalis libri XX. 0aCollector's series in science  aAUTH02227cgm a22004454a 101528443DNLM20260227153353.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100406s2010    mdu057|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15732  a(OCoLC)648077705  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3001 aLerch, Jason.14aThe rapidly changing brain :bof taxi driving mice and maze running men /cJason Lerch.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 a7T seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (57 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 19, 2010.3 a(CIT): How does the shape of the brain change with learning? Initial studies indicated that spatial expertise - in the form of experienced Taxi drivers - enlarged the hippocampus, one of the key structures involved in spatial navigation. Whether regional growth or shrinkage in the brain can be induced with specific training administered over short time periods is, however, unknown. The talk will present results of training studies in both mice and men, including some initial investigations using histology to ascertain the cellular bases of training induced volume changes.   Dr. Jason Lerch is a scientist at the Hospital for Sick Children in Toronto. His research program is focused on how the brain changes it's shape, with activities including the MRI of learning and memory, the effect of specialized training on the human brain, and methods of analyzing neuroanatomy.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBrainxphysiology22aLearningxphysiology22aSpatial Behaviorxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0a7T seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15732  aAUTH01427cgm a22004334a 101470350DNLM20260227153009.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080401s2008 