 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 18, 2016.3 a(CIT): Dr. Francis Collins checks in with Astronaut Kate Rubins coming to us live from the International Space Station to answer questions and discuss the opportunities and challenges of performing biomedical research in space. This streaming video file resides on and is broadcast by YouTube.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed January 23, 2017).22aInternational Space Station.12aBiomedical Research22aExtraterrestrial Environment22aSpace Flight22aWeightlessness 2aInterview 2aWebcast1 aRubins, Kate,d1978-einterviewee.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),efilm producer.2 aYouTube (Firm),ebroadcaster.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19970  aAUTH01225cas a2200397 a 101273663DNLM20260227090924.0cr|cn ||||||||060407c20069999pauqr p       0    0eng    a  2006243406  a1932-2275l1932-2275y0889-8537  a(OCoLC)70052848  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcdansdp  9United States00aW1bAN22J10aAnesthesiol Clin2dnlm00aAnesthesiology clinics.13aAnesthesiology clinics of North America  aPhiladelphia :bSaunders,cc2006-  av. : ill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 24, no. 1 (Mar. 2006)-  aEach issue has also a distinctive title.1 aMEDLINEbv24n1, Mar. 2006-1 aPubMedbv24n1, Mar. 2006-  aAlso issued online.12aAnesthesiology 2aPeriodical00tAnesthesiology clinics of North Americax0889-8537w(DLC)sn 86011457w(OCoLC)140918144 uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/19322275  aAUTH02289cgm a2200421 a 101585219DNLM20260227154121.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120525s2012    mdu073|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17195  a(OCoLC)795553717  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 309 AA100aWomen's history month observance program 2012 /cOEODM.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 13 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Mar. 29, 2012.3 a(CIT): NIH 2012 Women"s History Month Observance Program.  The program will be held on Thursday, March 29, 2012, from 11:00 am until 12:00 noon in Building 1, Wilson Hall.  The national theme for Women"s History Month is "Women's Education - Women's Empowerment."    The National Institutes of Health WHM Observance Program will honor four of NIH"s women in science who exemplify the advantages of education for women. The NIH Women in Science Leadership Panel will be comprised of Dr. Yvonne Maddox, Deputy Director, National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (NICHD), Dr. Belinda Seto, Deputy Director, National Institute of Biomedical Imaging and Bioengineering (NIBIB), Janine Austin Clayton, M.D., Acting Director, Office of Research on Women"s Health and Dr. Susan B. Shurin, Acting Director, National Heart, Lung and Blood Institute (NHLBI).   The program will be moderated by Dr. Cheryl Kitt, Deputy Director, Center for Scientific Review.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aWomenxeducation12aWomen's Health22aWomen's Rights 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Equal Opportunity and Diversity Management40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17195  aAUTH01413cas a22004574a 101120121DNLM20260227090915.0cr|cnu||||||||010525c20049999nyumn p       0    0eng    a  20012126430 a1535-3141l1535-3141  a(OCoLC)47013627  aNSDcNSDdDNLM  alcdanlmcopycansdp  9United States00aW1bFO511H10aFoodborne Pathog Dis2dnlm00aFoodborne pathogens and disease.  aLarchmont, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.,cc2004-  av. :bill.  aMonthly,b2010-  aQuarterly,b-2007  a6 no. a year,b2008  aTen no. a year,b2009  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (spring 2004)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, spring 2004-1 aPubMedbv1n1, spring 2004-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3v.1, no. 1 (Mar. 2004)c20050727fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper that meets the full ANSI standard for Permanent Paper12aFood Microbiology22aFood Parasitology 2aPeriodical0 x1556-71254 uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/fpd  aAUTH02259cgm a22004334a 101317970DNLM20260227152413.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071003s2007    mdu050|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14012  a(OCoLC)176925095  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 1021 aCorfas, Gabriel.10aNeuron-glia interactions :btheir mechanisms and their roles in development and disease /cGabriel Corfas.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 12, 2007).  aStreaming video (50 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 10, 2007.3 a(CIT): Dr. Corfas is interested in understanding the molecular signals that regulate neuron-glia interactions, which play critical roles in several aspects of nervous-system development, including neuronal migration, neuronal and glial differentiation and survival, and the formation and function of synapses. To this end, his research employs molecular and cellular biological techniques, as well as genetically modified mice.     One aspect of Dr. Corfas' work focuses on the growth factor neuregulin (NRG) and its erbB receptors, key mediators of neuron-glia interactions. He and his colleagues have found that these molecules mediate interactions between migrating neurons and the radial glial fibers along which they migrate during the development of the central nervous system, and between several types of glia and neurons in the adult nervous system.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeurogliaxphysiology22aNervous System Physiological Phenomena 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14012  aAUTH02358cgm a2200433 i 101718801DNLM20260227155821.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u180103s2017    mdu700 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:23617  a(DNLM)CIT:23625  a(OCoLC)1019847596  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWD 2102 aPathways to Prevention Workshop: Methods for Evaluating Natural Experiments in Obesityd(2017 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aPathways to Prevention Workshop :bMethods for Evaluating Natural Experiments in Obesity /cNIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2017]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (11 hr., 40 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded December 5-6, 2017.3 a(CIT): The NIH is hosting the Pathways to Prevention Workshop: Methods for Evaluating Natural Experiments in Obesity on December 5-6, 2017, to assess the available scientific evidence and to better understand relevant, high-quality natural experiment research designs in the field of obesity prevention and control. This workshop is designed for obesity researchers, practitioners, and others in related fields who are interested in obesity prevention and control and in natural experiment research. This workshop is sponsored by the Office of Disease Prevention (ODP), the National Cancer Institute (NCI), the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (NHLBI), and the National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases (NIDDK).  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed January 9, 2018).12aObesityxprevention & control22aModels, Theoretical 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),ehost institution,eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?2361740uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23625  aAUTH01507cas a2200517   7507032DNLM20260227090918.0cr||||||||||||750717c19369999nyuqr p       0    0eng    aa  40002517   a0025-9284l0025-9284  aBMCLA4  a(DNLM)B48600000(s)  a(OCoLC)01624125  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bBU85810aBull Menninger Clin2dnlm 0aBulletin of the Menninger Clinic00aBulletin of the Menninger Clinic.  aNew York, NY :bGuilford  av.billus.  aQuarterly,bwinter 1990-  aBimonthlyb-Nov. 1989  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-    Sept. 1936-1 aMEDLINEbv29n5,Sept. 1965-0 aPubMedbv29n5,Sept. 1965-0 aOLDMEDLINE1 aIndex medicus  aIncludes supplements.  aAlso issued online.  aIssued by the Menninger Foundation.  3Vol. 51, no. 1 (1987)c19890816fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aMental Health 2aPsychiatry 2aPsychotherapy 2aPeriodical2 aMenninger Clinic.2 aMenninger Foundation.0 x1943-28284 uhttp://guilfordjournals.com/loi/bumc  aAUTH01389cas a2200433   7505202DNLM20260224111601.0cr|cn ||||||||750626c18909999paubr m       0    0eng    a0002-7162l0002-7162  aAAYPAV  a(DNLM)A34665000(s)  a(OCoLC)01479265  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bAN626K10aAnn Am Acad Pol Soc Sci2dnlm04aThe Annals of the American Academy of Political and Social Science.  aPhiladelphia.3 aPhiladelphia, Pa :bAmerican Academy of Political and Social Science  av.billus.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-    J