 Cortexxphysiology22aReward1 aNewsome, William T.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3436  aAUTH01207cas a2200397 a 101278296DNLM20260227090925.0cr|cn ||||||||060605c20079999enk|| p       |    0eng    a  2008243182  a1751-2433l1751-2433  a(OCoLC)192040039  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9England00aW1bEX52NT310aExpert Rev Clin Pharmacol2dnlm00aExpert review of clinical pharmacology.  aLondon, UK :bFuture Drugs Ltd.213June 2013- :aLondon :bInforma Healthcare3132015- :aAbingdon, Oxford :bTaylor & Francis  aSix no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1, no. 1 (Jan. 2008).  aDescription based on first issue; title from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv4n1, Jan. 2011-0 aPubMedbv4n1, Jan. 2011-  aAlso issued online.12aPharmacology, Clinical 2aPeriodical0 x1751-24414 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/loi/ierj20  aAUTH02795cgm a2200445 a 101556033DNLM20260227153924.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110209s2011    mdu057 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16424  a(OCoLC)729826373  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWR 1021 aGoodman, Miriam Beth.10aUsing C. elegans to deconstruct touch and pain sensation /cMiriam Goodman  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (57 min., 33 sec.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neurosecience seminar  aTitle from title screen (viewed Jun 6, 2011).  aRecorded Jan. 24, 2011.3 a(CIT): The ability to detect touch is conserved from echinoderms to humans. It relies on specialized mechanoreceptor neurons that vary in their sensitivity and association with accessory structures. Despite its importance and conservation across taxa, very little is known about how touch works. Dr. Goodman's lab seeks to improve understanding by studying the nematode C. elegans, a simple animal that has only 30 mechanoreceptor neurons. Their work focuses on two classes of mechanoreceptor neurons: the 6 non-ciliated touch receptor neurons (TRNs) that detect touch applied to the body wall and the paired ciliated ASH neurons that detect noxious mechanical stimuli applied to the nose. Genetic analysis has revealed ion channel genes needed for TRN and ASH function. To learn the precise cellular function of such channel proteins and to investigate their gating mechanisms, they combine genetic dissection with in vivo electrophysiology and biomechanical analysis. The picture emerging from their recent work is that touch activates similar ion channels in the nonciliated TRNs and the ciliated ASH neurons, which differ by 100-fold in their sensitivity to external force. Challenges for the future include understanding the basis for differences in sensitivity and the biophysics of mechanotransduction channel gating.  <A HREF= "http://neuroseries.info.nih.gov/">NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aOpen-captioned.12aTouchxphysiology22aCaenorhabditis elegans22aPain22aSensory Receptor Cells 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16424  aAUTH01561cas a2200469 i 101718377DNLM20260227090909.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||160125c20159999cau|| poo     |    0eng    a  2017247759  a2059-5131l2059-5131  a(OCoLC)1004554095  aLNDbengerdad20260227cLNDdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW104a61010aScars Burn Heal2dnlm00aScars, burns & healing.3 aScars, burns and healing1 asb&h 1aThousand Oaks, CA :bSAGE Publications,c[2015]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1 (January/December 2015).0 aPubMedbv1, 2015-0 aPMC  aArticles published as they become available and compiled into annual volumes.  aDescription based on: Volume 1 (January/December 2015) (publisher's website, viewed January 10, 2018).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 3 (January/December 2017) (viewed January 10, 2018).12aCicatrixxtherapy22aBurnsxtherapy22aWound Healing 2aPeriodical2 aKatie Piper Foundation,esponsoring body.40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=10171837740uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/sbh  aAUTH03065cgm a22004334a 101531580DNLM20260227153556.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100519s2010    mdu069|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15857  a(OCoLC)664318283  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 3751 aSchmid, Sandra Louise.10aProtecting your borders :bregulated entry into the cell /cSandra Schmid.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lectures  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 9 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 28, 2010.3 a(CIT): Cells communicate with their environment and with other cells through their plasma membranes (PM). The major selective pathway for internalization from the plasma membrane is clathrin-mediated endocytosis (CME), which involves the assembly of clathrin coated pits (CCPs) that package cargo molecules, invaginate and pinch off into the cytosol forming clathrin coated vesicles (CCVs). CME governs key cellular processes, including uptake of nutrients, regulation of surface signaling receptors, recycling of synaptic vesicles, and remodeling of PM transporters to control serum homeostasis. Given its importance to cell and organismal physiology, CME is tightly regulated.   We have used quantitative total internal reflection fluorescence microscopy to study CCP dynamics in living cells and identified an endocytosis "checkpoint" that is sensitive to cargo content and regulated by the GTPase dynamin. Dynamin also catalyzes the membrane fission event leading to CCV formation. We have reconstituted dynamin-catalyzed vesicle formation from fluid supported bilayers with excess membrane reservoir (SUPER) templates to gain insight into the mechanisms governing membrane fission. Further insights come from structural studies on the dynamin GTPase domain. Together, these in vivo and in vitro approaches provide evidence that dynamin plays a dual role in CME, functioning at early stages to regulate CCV formation and at later stages to directly mediate membrane fission. Supported by NIH GM42455, GM73165, MH61345   The NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series includes weekly scientific talks by some of the top researchers in the biomedical sciences worldwide.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aEndocytosisxphysiology22aCell Membrane Structuresxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15857  aAUTH01464cgm a22004334a 101268066DNLM20260227150016.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu059 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11331  a(OCoLC)70663581  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWH 2001 aRajewsky, K.,d1936-10aMechanisms controlling lymphocyte homeostasis /cKlaus Rajewsky.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aAnnual Rose Lieberman lecture  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 18, 2006).  aStreaming video (58 min., 19 min., 9 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 10, 2002.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aLymphocytes22aHomeostasis 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aAnnual Rose Lieberman lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11331  aAUTH01695cgm a22004214a 101268231DNLM20260227150308.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu043 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11556  a(OCoLC)70873954  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWN 1801 aLi, King C. P.10aBiomedical imaging in the post-genomic era :bopportunities and challenges /cKing Li.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 12, 2006).  aStreaming video (42 min., 4 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded July 29, 2003.3 a(CIT): 2003 Summer Lecture Series for Students  Research at the Frontier   King Li, M.D., M.B.A.  Associate Director, Radiology and Imaging Sciences, Warren Grant Magnuson Clinical Center.  For more information, visit the Office of Intramural Training and Education.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aDiagnostic Imagingxtrends22aBiomedical Technologyxtrends 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11556  aAUTH01568cgmaa22004214a 101257209DNLM20260227144334.0vf|cbahouvf|cjahoucr|cna||||||||050915s2003    mdu111       f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:2419  a(OCoLC)61745500  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aQZ 22000aCancers in women :bnew approaches /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  aUnited States.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  a2 videocassettes of 2 (Betacam SP) (1 hr., 51 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (1 hr., 51 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aORWH women's health seminar series  aPresented May 29, 2003.  aAlso available online.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM12aNeoplasmsxepidemiology22aHealth Behavior22aRisk Factors22aWomen's Health 2aUnited Statesxepidemiology2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aORWH women's health seminar series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=2419  aAUTH02660cgm a2200469 i 9918434184806676DNLM20260227162528.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220706s2022    mdu664|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32478  a(DNLM)CIT:32481  a(OCoLC)1375633210  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 506.52 aSex/Gender-Specific COVID-19 Outcomes and Management Relevant for Heart, Lung, Blood and Sleep Disorders: From Bench to Bedside (Workshop)d(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aSex/gender-specific COVID-19 outcomes and management relevant for heart, lung, blood and sleep disorders :bfrom bench to bedside /cNIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (10 hr., 46 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 16 & 17, 2022.3 a(CIT): This workshop will bring together basic and clinical scientists to review the state of the science and identify key knowledge gaps and research opportunities to further our understanding of sex/gender differences in COVID-19 outcomes that are relevant to heart, lung, blood, and sleep (HLBS) disorders. A better understanding of the factors that might contribute to sex/gender-specific health outcomes will improve our ability to develop sex/gender-specific prevention, intervention, nd implementation strategies for COVID-19-related HLBS disorders. Ultimately, this enhanced understanding will enable novel approaches to facilitate translation of basic biological discoveries into safe and effective clinical applications.For more information go to https://www.eventbrite.com/e/sexgender-specific-covid-19-outcomes-relevant-for-hlbs-disorders-tickets-327501153767.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 7, 2023).12aCOVID-19xcomplications22aSex Factors22aCardiovascular Diseases22aHematologic Diseases22aLung Diseases22aSleep Wake Disorders 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4547640uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45478  aAUTH01211cas a2200349 a 9426002DNLM20260227091017.0cr|cn ||||||||950727c19859999gw tr p       0||| 0ger    a0178-1715l0178-1715  a(DNLM)SR0068841(s)  a(OCoLC)33258513  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Germany00aW1bO52L00aOP-Journal.  aStuttgart ;aNew York :bThieme,3 aStuttgart :bGeorge Thieme Verlag  av. :bill. (some col.), ports.  aThree no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan in 1985.  aSubtitle varies: Wissenschaftlich-medizinische Informationen für das OP-Personal, Apr. 1986?-   ; Wissenschaftliche, klinische und technische Informationen, Apr. 1995?- .  aDescription based on: 2. Jahrg., Nr. 1 (Apr. 1986); title from cover.  aAlso issued online. 2aSurgical Procedures, Operative 2aPeriodical4 uhttps://www.thieme-connect.de/products/ejournals/journal/10.1055/s-00000046  aAUTH03781cgm a2200445 a 101599120DNLM20260227154445.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130103s2012    mdu074|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17714  a(OCoLC)825102459  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 3281 aLaMantia, Anthony-Samuel.10aSpecification of olfactory and forebrain stem cells during early embryonic development /cAnthony-Samuel LaMantia.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 14 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aRecorded Dec. 10, 2012.3 a(CIT): Neuroscience Seminar Series   Dr. LaMantia is currently Director of the George Washington Institute of Neuroscience and Professor of Pharmacology and Physiology at the George Washington University School of Medicine and Health Sciences. This new Institute brings together 24 researchers from across George Washington"s campus and Children"s National Medical Center. Dr. LaMantia started his career as a graduate student in the laboratory of Pasko Rakic at Yale University. His post-doctoral training was with Dale Purves at Washington University School of Medicine, St. Louis and Duke University. Upon finishing his training, Dr. LaMantia became an Assistant Professor in the Department of Neurology at Duke. He then moved to University of North Carolina School of Medicine, Chapel Hill as an Associate Professor in the Department of Cell and Molecular Physiology. While at Chapel Hill he became Associate Director, NC Center for Mental Health Research and Professor, Department of Cell & Molecular Physiology. In 2010 he was appointed to his current position at George Washington University School of Medicine and Health Sciences. Dr. LaMantia's laboratory explores genetic and molecular mechanisms of early forebrain development, studying signaling and transcriptional regulation of forebrain stem cell identity and the role of forebrain developmental regulatory genes in behavioral and psychiatric diseases including schizophrenia and autism. In addition, his laboratory studies molecular mechanisms that specify neural stem cells in the embryonic as well as adult nervous system, with a focus on the specification of the neural stem cell in the olfactory epithelium. The mechanisms that maintain this stem cell provide a model for considering neuronal regeneration as well as degenerative disorders that disrupt the ongoing replacement of olfactory sensory neurons such as Alzheimer"s and Parkinson"s. His scientific services include several editorial boards, chair/member of numerous NIH study sections including external advisor to the intramural research program at NINDS. In his presentation, Dr. LaMantia will discuss specification of olfactory and forebrain stem cells during early embryonic development.   For more information go to <a href='http://neuroseries.info.nih.gov'>http://neuroseries.info.nih.gov.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aEmbryonic Development12aEmbryonic Stem Cells22aOlfactory Receptor Neurons22aProsencephalonxgrowth & development 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17714  aAUTH01498cas a2200433 i 9918418288306676DNLM20260227090909.0m     o  d        cr#|||||||||||220609c20229999ts || poo     0    0eng    a  2023228271  a2949-6829l2949-6810  aDNLMbengerdad20260227cDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United Arab Emirates00aW110aDrug Metab Bioanal Lett2dnlm00aDrug metabolism and bioanalysis letters. 1a[Saif Zone, Sharjah, U.A.E.] :bBentham Science Publishers,c[2022]-  a1 online resource  aThree issues a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 15, issue 1 (2022).1 aMEDLINEbv15n1, 2022-0 aPubMedbv15n1, 2022-  aAlso issued in print.0 aVolume 15, issue 1 (2022); title from journal hompeage (publisher's website, viewed May 26, 2023).1 aVolume 15, issue 3 (2022) (viewed May 26, 2023).12aDru