r (viewed July 25, 2007).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 21 min., 54 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 23, 2005.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomedical Researchxtrends12aNeoplasms22aGenetic Predisposition to Disease22aRisk Assessment 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12488  aAUTH01885cgm a22004334a 101268842DNLM20260227151140.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2005    mdu060|||||||f||||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12774  a(OCoLC)173201344  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4751 aEisen, Jonathan,cDr.10aMore questions than answers :binsights into DNA repair processes from genome sequencing projects /cJonathan Eisen.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Aug. 31, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 0 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 20, 2005.3 a(CIT): The DNA Repair Interest Group is concerned with all forms of DNA damage and repair. As a major defense against environmental damage to cells DNA repair is present in all organisms examined including bacteria, yeast, drosophila, fish, amphibians, rodents and humans. The members of the DNA Repair Interest Group perform research in areas including DNA repair enzymology and fine structure, mutagenesis, gene and cell cycle regulation, protein structure, and human disease.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDNA Repairxgenetics22aChromosome Mapping22aGenomicsxmethods22aSequence Analysis, DNA 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bDNA Repair Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12774  aAUTH01298cgm a22004094a 101268678DNLM20260227151050.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2005    mdu050 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12421  a(OCoLC)154875830  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 540 AA100aConversation on healthcare with President Bush /cGeorge W. Bush.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 31, 2007).  aStreaming video (49 min., 7 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 26, 2005.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHealth Policy22aDelivery of Health Care22aHealth Care Costs 2aUnited States 2aWebcast1 aBush, George W.q(George Walker),d1946-2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12421  aAUTH01478cas a2200409 i 101681234DNLM20260227090916.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||160415c20169999fr qr poo     0    0eng    a  2016243432  a2452-3186l2452-3186  a(OCoLC)952811559  aLNDbengerdad20260227dOH1dOCLCOdOCLCFdOCLCOdDNLM  apcc  9France04aR500aW110aCurr Res Transl Med2dnlm00aCurrent research in translational medicine.30aTranslational medicine 1a[Issy-les-Moulineaux Cedex] :bElsevier-Masson SAS,c2016-  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with volume 64, issue 2 (April-June 2016).1 aMEDLINEbv64n1, March 2016-0 aPubMedbv64n1, March 2016-  aDescription based on: Volume 64, issue 2 (April-June 2016); title from journal home page (ScienceDirect, viewed July 27, 2016).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 64, issue 3 (July-September 2016) (viewed October 26, 2016).12aTranslational Research, Biomedical. 2aPeriodical00tPathologie-biologiex0369-8114w(DLC)sn 86008735w(OCoLC)13346324 uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/24523186  aAUTH04090cgm a2200409 i 101686954DNLM20260227155531.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160707s2016    mdu050|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19739  a(OCoLC)953601609  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 503.300aMechanisms of virus persistence in blood and tissue in HIV controllers /cDaniel Douek ; Immunology Interest Group. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (50 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 8, 2016.3 a(CIT): Immunology Interest Group. Dr. Douek is a Senior Investigator and the Chief of the Human Immunology Section of the Vaccine Research Center, NIAID / NIH. Dr. Douek studied medicine at the Universities of Oxford and London and then practiced internal medicine before pursuing a Ph.D. in Immunology at the University of London. Dr. Douek was appointed to the NIH Vaccine Research Center in November 2000. His laboratory, the Human Immunology Section, studies the processes that determine the course of human diseases in which the immune system, particularly its T cell arm, plays a central role in their pathogenesis and outcome. He aims to use the knowledge gained to initiate clinical studies of new therapeutic and vaccine approaches. Dr. Douek is a widely published author in the field of human immunology having made significant discoveries in thymic function, immune repertoire diversity, T-cell mediated immunity, mucosal immunology, innate immunity, hematopoietic progenitor cell transplantation. Currently, the main focus of his lab is both the pathogenesis and immune control of HIV infection. Dr. Douek sits on numerous scientific advisory boards and journal editorial boards. Dr. Douek was given the World AIDS Day Award in 2007, the NIH Director's Award in 2008, and was recognized as one of the world's top 50 scientists by Scientific American in 2005. He was named as one of the 2012 POZ 100 for his significant contributions to accelerating the end of AIDS. In 2014 he was named in the Thomson Reuters report entitled "The World's Most Influential Scientific Minds".  Abstract: Targeted HIV cure strategies require definition of the mechanisms that maintain the virus. Here, we tracked HIV replication and the persistence of infected CD4 T cells in individuals with natural virologic control by sequencing viruses, T cell receptor genes, HIV integration sites and cellular transcriptomes. Our results revealed three mechanisms of HIV persistence operating within distinct anatomic and functional compartments. In lymph node, we detected viruses with genetic and transcriptional attributes of active replication in both T follicular helper (TFH) cells and non-TFH memory cells. In blood, we detected inducible proviruses of archival origin among highly differentiated, clonally expanded cells. Linking the lymph node and blood was a small population of circulating cells harboring inducible proviruses of recent origin. Thus, HIV replication in lymphoid tissue, clonal expansion of infected cells, and recirculation of recently infected cells act together to maintain the virus in HIV controllers despite effective antiviral immunity.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 13, 2016).12aHIV Infectionsxvirology22aHIVxgenetics22aVirus Replicationxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aDouek, Daniel,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group,eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19739  aAUTH02087cas a2200529 i 101631612DNLM20260227090920.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||140429c20149999ne || poo     0    0eng    a  2016243271  a885821197a889664417a891467387  a2214-7829l2214-78292j  z(OCoLC)885821197z(OCoLC)889664417z(OCoLC)891467387  a(OCoLC)877906514z(OCoLC)885821197z(OCoLC)889664417z(OCoLC)891467387  aHUAbengerdad20260227cHUAdOCLCOdCUSdPFMdCUSdDNLM  apcc  9Netherlands 4aTK510