, CA] :bSage Publications,c[2018]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1, issue 1 (March 2018)0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued in print.  aDescription based on: Volume 1, issue 1 (March 2018); title from journal home page (Sage journals, viewed April 26, 2018).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 1, issue 3 (September 2018) (viewed December 3, 2018).12aPsychology 2aPeriodical2 aAssociation for Psychological Science,eissuing body.0 x2515-245940uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/ampa  aAUTH01406cgm a22004094a 101496147DNLM20260227152835.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090203s2008    mdu064 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14841  a(OCoLC)319854698  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 55.21 aTesmer, John.10aStructural analysis of an ancient pathway linking Gq-coupled receptors to rhoA /cJohn Tesmer ; [Structural Biology Interest Group].  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 10, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 3 min., 36 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 18, 2008.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aGTP-Binding Protein alpha Subunits, Gq-G1122arhoA GTP-Binding Protein22aSignal Transduction 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bStructural Biology Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14841  aAUTH03140cgm a2200397 i 101680584DNLM20260227155645.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160404s2016    mdu118|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19551  a(OCoLC)946699000  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.500aReproducible research :bmany dimensions and shared responsibilities /cLisa Meier McShane. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 58 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded March 14, 2016.3 a(CIT): Biomedical researchers have an ethical responsibility to ensure the reproducibility and integrity of their work, so that precious research resources are not wasted and, most importantly, flawed or misleading results do not make their way to clinical studies where the faulty evidence could adversely affect study participants. Many factors have been suggested as contributors to irreproducible biomedical research, including poor study design, analytic instability of measurement methods, sloppy data handling, inappropriate and misleading statistical analysis methods, improper reporting or interpretation of results, and, on rare occasions, outright scientific misconduct. These problems can occur in any type of biomedical study, whether preclinical or clinical, large or small. Examples of the many potential pitfalls will be discussed along with suggested approaches to avoid them. The first half of the seminar will focus mainly on issues that arise commonly in preclinical and small clinical studies or studies performed retrospectively using stored biospecimens. The second half will elaborate on aspects that are particularly problematic in research involving the use of novel measurement technologies such as "omics assays" which generate large volumes of data and require specialized expertise and computational approaches for proper data analysis and interpretation. The discussions will emphasize the importance of including in a research team all individuals with the needed expertise as early as possible in a project in order to promote a sense of engagement and facilitate good communication across disciplines. Shared credit for scientific accomplishments should be understood as an acceptance of shared accountability for the integrity of the work.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 11, 2016).12aBiomedical Research22aReproducibility of Results 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aMcShane, Lisa Meier,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),ssponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19551  aAUTH01443cgm a22004094a 101268660DNLM20260227151055.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2005    mdu122 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12401  a(OCoLC)154875789  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.500aHoward Hughes Medical Institute and National Institute of Biomedical Imaging and Bioengineering initiative information meeting /cthe Howard Hughes Medical Institute and the National Institute of Biomedical Imaging and Bioengineering.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 31, 2007).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 1 min., 2 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 10, 2005.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aResearch22aEducation, Graduate22aProgram Development22aTraining Support 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aHoward Hughes Medical Institute.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12401  aAUTH01414cgm a2200433 a 101559803DNLM20260227153919.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110503s2011    mdu061|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16536  a(OCoLC)728458199  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 3751 aKorezky, Gary.10aTCR signaling mutants uncover rare innate-like T cell populations /cGary Korezky.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.) :b: sd. col.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acom