rom the New York medical journal for June 18, 1887.  a"Read before the Medical Society of the County of Kings, March 15, 1887"--Page 1.  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 21-22).  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  3Copy 1aCondition reviewedc20180823lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM1 3Copy 1adigitizedc20222pda5DNLM12aBrain Mapping22aCerebral Cortexxphysiology2 aMedical Society of the County of Kings (Kings County, N.Y.)4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/101729797  aAUTH01317cam a2200361   60530500RDNLM20260206221919.0cr||||||||||||820514s1905    pau           000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)14786814  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aHQbW741s 19051 aWillson, Robert N.q(Robert Newton),d1873-1916,eauthor.14aThe social evil in university life :ba talk with the students of the University of Pennsylvania /cby Robert N. Wilson. 1aPhiladelphia :bThe Vir Publishing Company,c[1905]  a58 pages ;c18 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"For men only."  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20250703lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM22aUniversity of Pennsylvania.12aSexual Behavior22aGonorrhea22aSyphilis22aIllegitimacy22aUniversities4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/60530500R  aAUTH01494cgm a22004694a 101268830DNLM20260227151222.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2005    mdu672 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12754  a(DNLM)CIT:12753  a(OCoLC)173469305  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWS 13000aPerspectives on marketing, self-regulation and childhood obesity /cHHS.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Aug. 31, 2007).  aStreaming video (11 hr., 11 min., 23 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded July 14-15, 2005.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aChild Nutrition Disorders22aChild22aFood Preferences22aMarketing22aObesity22aPersuasive Communication22aPsychology, Child 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aUnited States.bDepartment of Health and Human Services.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1275440uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12753  aAUTH02247cgm a2200445 i 101743234DNLM20260227155911.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190302s2019    mdu056|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27323  a(OCoLC)1096364145  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 2020 aCancer and aging (Campisi)10aCancer and aging :brival demons? /cJudith Campisi. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (56 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded February 13, 2019.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series. Cancer and aging are intricately intertwined. Organisms with dividing cells are at a substantial risk for developing cancer. Evolution "solved" the cancer problem by selecting for tumor suppressive mechanisms, which protect these organisms for cancer - at least for the reproductively active portion of the life span. Beyond that portion of the life span, these mechanisms can drive pathologies associated with aging, including, ironically, cancer. For her lecture, Dr. Campisi will discuss how one complex tumor suppressive mechanism termed cellular senescence is thought to drive a surprising number of age-associated diseases, and current strategies for maintaining tumor suppression while mitigating aging.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 9, 2019).12aAging12aNeoplasmsxetiology22aCellular Senescence 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCampisi, Judith,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27323  aAUTH01127cas a2200397 a 101286577DNLM20260227090917.0cr|cn ||||||||060901c20079999sz  x p       0    0eng    a  2007227519  a1661-7800l1661-7800  a(OCoLC)76838237  aGMMcGMMdEMMdEYMdEEMdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9Switzerland00aW1bBI852P10aNeonatology2dnlm00aNeonatology.  aBasel :bKarger  aEight no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with v. 91, no. 1 (2007).  aDescription based on first issue; title from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv91n1, 2007-0 aPubMedbv91n1, 2007-  aAlso issued online.12aFetus22aInfant, Newborn 2aPeriodical0 x1661-781900tBiology of the neonatex0006-3126w(OCoLC)17980334 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/224215  aAUTH02814cgm a22004934a 101468736DNLM20260227152441.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080307s2008    mdu159 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14313  a(OCoLC)222133949  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQ 175.3710aW 20.52 aNIH Extramural Staff Training Symposiumd(2008 :cNational Institutes of Health)10aNIH Extramural Staff Training Symposium on Reporting Allegations of Scientific Misconduct :bFebruary 20, 2008, Natcher Conference Center /cSally Rockey, John E. Dahlberg, and Sally Amero.13aReporting allegations of scientific misconduct1 iTitle on screen banner:aNIH Extramural Staff Training Symposium on Reporting Allegations of Scientific Misconduct  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Mar. 26, 2008).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 38 min., 51 sec. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded Feb. 20, 2008.3 a(CIT): The NIH and the scientific community expect adherence to exemplary standards of intellectual honesty in the formulation, conduct, and reporting of scientific research.  All allegations of research misconduct received by members of the NIH Extramural Program are assessed and resolved using a consistent process and cooperation between the NIH and the Office of Research Integrity (ORI), Department of Health and Human Services (DHHS).  This training opportunity will provide guidance to the staff of the NIH Extramural Program concerning the Public Health Service (PHS) definition of research misconduct, applicable Federal and Department policies, and procedures that staff of the NIH Extramural Program should follow, when they learn of possible research misconduct.  All Research Integrity Officers (RIOs) and NIH extramural staff members are encouraged to attend.  For more information, visit  http://odoerdb2-1.od.nih.gov/oer/training.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aBiomedical Researchxstandards12aScientific Misconduct22aResearch Support as Topic22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14313  aAUTH01060cas a2200361   0201343DNLM20260227091012.0cr|cn ||||||||690521c19679999enk x         0    0eng    a0533-3164l0533-3164  zGRANE  a(DNLM)G11940000(s)  a(OCoLC)01447188  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9England00aW1bGR8100aGroup analysis.  aNew York, Oxford,bPublished on behalf of the Institute of Group Analysis in collaboration with "Human Context" by Pergamon.3 3<1989->:aLondon :bSage Publications  av.  aFour no.