titutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13341  aAUTH01521cas a2200481 a 101583421DNLM20260227090915.0cr||||||||||||120427c20129999enk|| p       |    0eng    a  2012220208  a2047-7724l2047-7724  a(OCoLC)790117948  aAGLcAGLdDNLM  apcc  9England00aW1bAN627P0 aRA643b.P3810aPathog Glob Health2dnlm00aPathogens and global health.1 aPGH  aLondon, UK :bManey Pub.3132016- :aAbingdon :bTaylor & Francis  av. :bill. ;c30 cm.  aEight issues yearly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with vol. 106, no. 1 (Mar. 2012).1 aMEDLINEbv106n1, Mar. 2012-0 aPubMedbv106n1, Mar. 2012-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: vol. 106, no. 1 (March 2012); title from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: vol. 106, no. 1 (March 2012).12aCommunicable Diseases22aPublic Health 2aPeriodical0 x2047-773200tAnnals of tropical medicine and parasitologyx0003-4983w(DLC)   08000160w(OCoLC)14814154 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=1015834214 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/loi/ypgh20  aAUTH02230cgm a2200469 i 9918663286106676DNLM20260227164025.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230815s2023    mdu529|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33352  a(DNLM)CIT:33353  a(OCoLC)1531998048  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 1452 aWorkshop on Multigenerational Nutrition Influences on Health and Diseased(2023 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aMultigenerational nutrition influences on health and disease /cNIH Office of Nutrition Research (ONR). 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (8 hr., 49 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded July 12 & 13, 2023.3 a(CIT): Nutrition- and diet-related exposures and outcomes travel within, across, and between families and generations. These nutritional influences can occur at the individual, family, community, or societal levels to ultimately have an impact on health and disease. This workshop will bring together thought leaders in nutrition and diet with leaders in family and generational health to identify research opportunities and challenges.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed September 5, 2024).12aNutritional Physiological Phenomena12aFamily Health22aNutritional Status22aIntergenerational Relations22aSocial Determinants of Health 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Nutrition Research,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=5104740uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=51049  aAUTH01353cgm a22004334a 101490646DNLM20260227152700.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081126s2008    mdu128 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14774  a(OCoLC)319068167  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWH 5251 aWilson, Wyndham Hopkins.10aTRACO.pLymphoma and siRNA /cW. Wilson, N. Caplen.30aLymphoma and siRNA3 aTranslational Research in Clinical Oncology  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 13, 2009).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 7 min., 24 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 17, 2008.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aLymphoma22aGene Silencing22aRNA, Small Interfering. 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14774  aAUTH03772nam a2200541 i 9918986569206676DNLM20260220084652.0230805t20242024enka     b    001 0 eng    a  2023930674  a1392165989  a1529770610qhardcover  a9781529770612qhardcover  a1529770599qpaperback  a9781529770599qpaperback  z9781529783964qelectronic book  z9781529785944qelectronic book  z9781529787924qelectronic book  a(OCoLC)1392163212z(OCoLC)1392165989  aYDXbengerdacYDXdBDXdUKEHCdOCLCOdLDSdYDXdOCLCOdUKRCNdNLMAAdOCLCLdNZTOIdUKOBUdNLM  apcc  9United Kingdom 4aRA427.8b.G73 202400aWA 59004a613223/eng/2024052400aGreen & Tones' health promotion :bplanning and strategies /c[edited by] Ruth Cross, James Woodall.33aGreen and Tones' health promotion1 aHealth promotion :bplanning and strategies  a5th edition. 1aLondon ;aThousand Oaks, CA :bSAGE,c[2024] 4c©2024  axviii, 602 pages :billustrations ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aPreceded by Health promotion / Jackie Green, Ruth Cross, James Woodall, Keith Tones. 4th edition. 2019.  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.0 aIntroduction -- Health and health promotion -- Assessing health and its determinants -- The determinants of health actions -- Health promotions planning -- a systematic approach -- Information needs -- Healthy public policy -- Education for health -- Mass communication -- Working with communities -- Settings for health -- Evaluations -- Evidence-based health promotion.  aThis is essential reading for students in health promotion and public health around the world. Updated and edited by Ruth Cross and James Woodall, who are experts in Health Promotion at Leeds Beckett University, this edition features: Full discussion on the impacts from Covid-19 on health strategy - Increased emphasis on upstream interventions rather than patient-centred, individualised (downstream) approaches - Increased internationalisation - more comparative examples and international perspectives prioritising northern Europe, Scandinavia and the USHealth promotion is being taught to an increasingly diverse range of students, either because of background, future career plans or the increasing international student numbers. This book provides international examples, applicable to a range of health professions, to meet the needs of diverse cohorts. The international case studies provided online also mean that international students in the UK have access to cases that can be more relevant to their own countries.It can be a challenge understanding the ′softer side′ of health science, particularly understanding how to think outside an individual responsibility for Health promotion/public health. The focus of this book is setting out the process of planning, implementing and evaluating successful health programmes meaning that you are introduced to the ′softer side′ in an applied way - making it easier for you to see the importance of what you are studying.12aHealth Promotionxmethods22aHealth Educationxmethods22aHealth Planning22aModels, Organizational22aPolicy Making1 iPreceded by (work):aGreen, Jackie,eauthor.tHealth promotion1 aCross, Ruth,eeditor1 aWoodall, James,eeditor.  aAUTH04638cgm a2200445 i 101690011DNLM20260227155630.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160804s2016    mdu058|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19806  a(OCoLC)956966208  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 10804aThe opportunities and challenges of using systematic reviews to summarize knowledge about "what works" in disease prevention and health promotions /cKay Dickersin. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (58 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aMedicine: minding the gap  aRecorded July 25, 2016.3 a(CIT): Medicine Mind the Gap Seminar  Whether discussing priorities for comparative effectiveness research (CER) from a funder's or researcher"s perspective, understanding knowledge gaps, or setting guidelines for care, systematic reviews of existing research hold the promise of scientifically summarizing "what works" at any point in time. However, many of us are content to use nonsystematic methods to synthesize knowledge; non-systematic methods certainly use fewer resources, and in a given field experts believe they know the literature sufficiently to avoid the investment. Yet, by using a nonsystematic approach to summarizing what is known, we are effectively applying a double standard: although we demand that the primary research studies minimize the risk of bias in their design and implementation, we accept the possibility of bias in synthesizing the primary research ("meta-bias"). We will review models of how systematic reviews are being used globally to plan, implement, and derive recommendations from CER. We will then review some of the existing challenges to using systematic reviews and methods being used to address these challenges.  Kay Dickersin, M.A., Ph.D., is Professor of Epidemiology at the Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health. At Johns Hopkins, she also serves as the Director for the Center for Clinical Trials and Evidence Synthesis External Website Policy, whose faculty, staff and students are involved in methodologic research related to clinical trials and evidence synthesis. Dr. Dickersin's major research interests are related to epidemiology, randomized clinical trials, systematic reviews and meta-analysis, reporting biases, trials registers, peer review, evidence-based health care, and patient-centered outcomes research. She has conducted studies in a number of important subject areas, including women's health, eyes and vision, and surgery. Dr. Dickersin"s research has involved comparing internal company documents to the published record, where she and her colleagues have found differing results. She is currently engaged in examining practical issues related to demands for "open access to trial data," when the available data may be discordant.  Dr. Dickersin is also Director of the US Cochrane Center (USCC), one of 13 Centers worldwide participating in The Cochrane Collaboration External Website Policy. The Collaboration aims to help people make well-informed decisions about health by preparing, maintaining, and promoting the accessibility of systematic reviews of available evidence on the benefits and risks of health care. The USCC hosts Consumers United for Evidence-based Healthcare (CUE) External Website Policy. She also serves as Director of the Cochrane Eyes and Vision Review Group, US Satellite External Website Policy.    Your feedback is important to us   Evaluation Survey   For more information go to <a href='https://prevention.nih.gov/programs-events/medicine-mind-the-gap'>https://prevention.nih.gov/programs-events/medicine-mind-the-gap.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed August 15, 2016).12aPreventive Medicine22aComparative Effectiveness Research22aHealth Promotion22aSystematic Reviews as Topic 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aDickersin, Kay,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aMedicine: mind the gap lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19806  aAUTH01337cas a2200445 a 101212092DNLM20260227090918.0cr|cn ||||||||040330c20049999ne || p       0    0eng    a  2004242067  a1572-3887l1572-3887  a(OCoLC)54837371  aIAYcIAYdUIUdMYGdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9Netherlands00aW1bJO851H  aQP551b.J6810aProtein J2dnlm04aThe protein journal.  aDordrecht, The Netherlands ;aNew York :bKluwer Academic/Plenum,cc2004-3 32005- :aDordrecht :bSpringer  av. :bill. ;c27 cm.  aTen no. a year,b2009-  aEight issues yearly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 23, issue 1 (Jan. 2004)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv23n1, Jan. 2004-0 aPubMedbv23n1, Jan. 2004-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.12aProteins 2aPeriodical0 x1875-835500tJournal of protein chemistryx0277-8033w(DLC)   82645681w(OCoLC)76463064 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/10930  aAUTH01871cas a2200529 a 9425965DNLM20260224111542.0cr|cn#||||||||910502c19919999ru  x p       0    0eng    a   92650095   a0959-9436l0959-9436  aMENCEX  a(DNLM)SR0069051(s)  a(OCoLC)23678108  aTJCcTJCdCASdJNAdDLCdNSTdDLCdNSTdHULdAGLdIULdHULdOCLdDLCdMYGdDNLM  alcanlmcopyc  ae-ur---  9Russia (Federation)00aQD1b.M37900aW1bME90830 aQD1.M379 0aX50000a540/.522000aMendeleev communications /cRoyal Society of Chemistry, [Akademii︠a︡ nauk SSSR].  aMoscow :bAcademy of Sciences of the USSR ;aCambridge, UK :bRoyal Society of Chemistry,cc1991-21a[Amsterdam] :bElsevier B.V. on behalf of the N. D. Zelinsky Institute of Organic Chemistry of the Russian Academy of Sciences3132025- :a[Moscow] :bMath-Net.Ru  av. :bill. ;c30 cm.  a6 issues yearly,b1993-  aFour times a year,b1991-1992  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aNo. 1 (Feb. 1991)-  aTitle from cover.  aIssues numbered within the year.  aAvailable also in a CD-ROM ed.  aAlso available via the World Wide Web.12aChemistry 2aPeriodical2 aRoyal Society of Chemistry (Great Britain)2 aAkademii︠a︡ nauk SSSR.0 x1364-551X4 uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/095994364 uhttps://www.mathnet.ru/php/currentissue.phtml?jrnid=mendc&option%5Flang=eng  aAUTH02623cgm a2200457 i 101771149DNLM20260227160226.0m     o  z f      vz|czau|ucr|cna||||||||200828s2020    mdu001 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35613  a(OCoLC)1197632621  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 358.500aNeurodegeneration :bmechanisms to medicines /cGiovanna Mallucci. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (57 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience series seminar  aRecorded March 9, 2020.  a(CIT): Dr. Mallucci lab is interested in understanding mechanisms of neurodegeneration. The central theme is the identification of common pathways across the spectrum of these disorders (which include Alzheimer's and related diseases) that are relevant for both mechanistic insights and therapy. They focus both on ‘toxic’ processes that can be targeted to prevent neuronal death, and on regenerative processes that can be harnessed for repair. Using mouse models, they described the pathogenic role of the unfolded protein response (UPR) in neurodegeneration, which led to the discovery of the first small molecule - an inhibitor of this pathway - to prevent neurodegeneration in vivo. They also recently discovered the phenomenon of failure of synaptic repair processes in neurodegeneration and the underlying mechanisms: failure of another stress response involving 'cold shock' proteins, which they have successfully harnessed for neuroprotection. They aim to translate this research for new treatments for dementia.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed September 22, 2020).12aNeurodegenerative Diseasesxphysiopathology22aNeurodegenerative Diseasesxdrug therapy22aNeurodegenerative Diseasesxgenetics22aRNA-Binding Proteinsxgenetics22aNeuroprotection 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aMallucci, Giovanna,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=35613  aAUTH01203cam a2200313   45000010008000000030005000080050017000130080041000300200018000710200015000890350019001040400021001230440012001440600022001561000032001782450119002102600047003293000026003763360026004023370028004283380027004564900134004835000045006176500023006627000033006858300141007189980021008599990009008801302560DNLM20240610085926.0711020s1971    gw ||||  |||| 00| 0|ger    a9783540055471  a3540055479  a(OCoLC)1464836  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Germany00aQZ 11bD652w 19711 aDoerr, Wilhelm,d1914-1996.10aWandlungen der Krankheitsforschung;büber "Standpunkte" in der Pathologie 150 Jahre nach R. Virchows Geburtstag.  aBerlin, New York,bSpringer-Verlag,c1971.  a38 p.billus., ports.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aHeidelberger Akadamie der Wissenschaften Mathematisch-naturwissenschaftliche Klasse, Sitzungsberichte,vJahrg. 1971. Abhandlung 6  a"Wellen der Medizin" (1 leaf) in pocket. 2aPathologyxhistory1 aVirchow, Rudolf,d1821-1902. 0aSitzungsberichte der Heidelberger Akademie der Wissenschaften, Mathematisch-Naturwissenschaftliche Klasse ;vJahrg. 1971, 6. Abhandlung.  aHISRELb19991201  aAUTH03969cgm a2200433 i 101690010DNLM20260227155431.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160804s2016    mdu057|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19802  a(OCoLC)956966207  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 25000aPolyvalent vaccines targeting oncogenic driver pathways /cMary L. (Nora) Disis. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (57 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aAnnual advances in cancer prevention lecture ;v2016  aRecorded July 21, 2016.3 a(CIT): NCI Cancer Prevention Fellowship Program  The Annual Advances in Cancer Prevention Lecture is sponsored by the Cancer Prevention Fellowship Program within the Division of Cancer Prevention at NCI.  It is also part the course offerings of the annual NCI Summer Curriculum in Cancer Prevention but is open to the entire NIH community and the general public.  This annual lecture addresses current challenges and/or approaches used by investigators to address gaps, advance science, and promote the application of successful strategies in the field of cancer prevention and control.  Dr. Disis will be presenting the 2016 annual lecture on how polyvalent vaccines target oncogenic driver pathways.   Mary L. (Nora) Disis, MD, is the Athena Distinguished Professor of Breast Cancer Research and Associate Dean for Translational Health Sciences in the University of Washington (UW) School of Medicine. She is a Professor of Medicine and Adjunct Professor of Pathology and Obstetrics and Gynecology at UW, and a Member of the Fred Hutchinson Cancer Research Center (FHCRC). She is also an American Cancer Society Clinical Professor and a Komen Scholar. In addition to directing work in the Tumor Vaccine Group, Dr. Disis is the Director of the Institute of Translational Health Sciences and the Director for the Center for Translational Medicine in Women"s Health at the UW.  Dr. Disis is an expert in breast and ovarian cancer immunology and translational research. She is one of the pioneering investigators who discovered that HER-2/neu is a tumor antigen. Her work has led to several clinical trials which evaluate boosting immunity to HER-2/neu with cancer vaccines. Her research interest is in the discovery of new molecular immunologic targets in solid tumors for the development of vaccine and cellular therapy for the treatment and prevention of common malignancies. Dr. Disis is a member of Alpha Omega Alpha and the American Society of Clinical Investigation. She is also the Editor-in-Chief for JAMA Oncology, and is a member of several committees and task forces for both the American Society of Clinical Oncology (ASCO) and the American Association for Cancer Research (AACR).  Dr. Disis received her MD from the University of Nebraska Medical School and completed a residency and chief residency in Internal Medicine at the University of Illinois in Chicago and her fellowship in oncology at UW/FHCRC.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed Aug. 10, 2016).12aCancer Vaccinesxtherapeutic use22aMolecular Targeted Therapy22aProteinsximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aDisis, Mary L.,espeaker.2 aCancer Prevention Fellowship Program (National Cancer Institute),esponsor. 0aAnnual advances in cancer prevention lecture ;v2016.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19802  aAUTH02298cgm a2200421 i 9918819969906676DNLM20260227164632.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u240501s2024    mdu721|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33890  a(DNLM)CIT:33896  a(OCoLC)1506482874  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWT 1452 aCognitive Aging Summit: Precision Aging and Brain Healthn(4th:d2024 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aCognitive aging summit IV (day 1 & 2) /cFNIH, NIA, and McKnight Brain Research Foundation. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2024]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (12 hr., 1 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded March 20 & 21, 2024.3 a(CIT): The fourth Cognitive Aging Summit will be held March 20-21, 2024, in Bethesda, Maryland. The Summit will feature presentations and discussion on promising areas of research into age-related brain and cognitive changes, with a special focus on precision aging and brain health; individual differences in cognitive performance, brain function, and adaptation and the factors that drive these differences; the design of prevention and intervention trials in the context of precision health. The two-day meeting will build on priorities and research directions identified at previous Cognitive Aging Summits. The Summit is convened by the NIA and made possible by the McKnight Brain Research Foundation through a generous grant to the Foundation for the National Institutes of Health.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 5, 2025).12aCognitive Aging22aPrecision Medicine 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute on Aging,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=5250040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=52502  aAUTH01624cgm a22004214a 101267808DNLM20260227145823.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu059 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10699  a(OCoLC)191875349  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 2100a2003 NIH women's history month observance /cOffice of Research on Women's Health.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 23, 2008).  aStreaming video (58 min., 51 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 19, 2003.3 a(CIT): The theme of this year's program is "Women Pioneers in Health:  Past, Present and Future."  Dr. Elias Zerhouni, Director, NIH, will provide the opening remarks.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aPhysicians, Women22aBiomedical Research22aResearch Personnel22aWomen's Rights 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Research on Women's Health.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10699  aAUTH03836cgm a2200421 i 101635871DNLM20260227154649.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140627s2014    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18474  a(OCoLC)884540973  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWX 1751 aSavova, Guergana,eauthor.10aTemporal relation discovery from the clinical narrative /cGuergana Savova. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded June 4, 2014.3 a(CIT): There is an abundance of health-related free text that can be used for a variety of immediate biomedical applications - phenotyping for Genome Wide Studies (GWAS), clinical point of care, patient powered applications, biomedical research. The' presentation will cover current research problems in Natural Language Processing (NLP) such as event and temporal expression discovery, and linking of events to create timelines of patient's clinical histories - a research program funded by grants from the National Library of Medicine. It will also outline computable gold annotations with linguistic tags and inferentially derived relations created under several NIH-funded projects (thyme.healthnlp.org; share.healthnlp.org, cancer.healthnlp.org) used in the research as well as in shared community tasks organized around them (2013 and 2014 CLEF/ShARE Shared task; 2014 and 2015 SemEval Task 7 Analysis of Clinical Text; 2015 SemEval ClinicalTempEval). Applications of NLP to biomedical problems will be discussed within the framework of national networks such as electronic Medical Records and Genomics (eMERGE), Pharmacogenomics Research Network (PGRN), Informatics for Integrating the Biology and the Bedside (i2b2), Patient Centered Outcomes Research Institute (PCORI).   Dr. Guergana Savova is Associate Professor at Harvard Medical School and Boston Children"s Hospital. Her research interests are in NLP especially as applied to the text generated by physicians (the clinical narrative).  She has been creating gold standard annotated resources based on computable definitions and developing methods for computable solutions. The focus of Dr. Savova's research is higher level semantic and discourse processing of the clinical narrative. The result of Dr. Savova's research with her collaborators has led to the creation of the clinical Text Analysis and Knowledge Extraction System (cTAKES; ctakes.apache.org). Dr. Savova is on the editorial board of the Journal of the Medical Informatics Association (JAMIA), and is a reviewer for several journals including Journal of the Biomedical Informatics (JBI), Journal of Language Resources and Evaluation (LREC), and many conferences/workshops. She is also a member of the National Library of Medicine's Biomedical Library and Informatics Review Committee. Dr. Savova holds a PhD in Linguistics with a minor in Cognitive Science and a Master"s of Science in Computer Science from University of Minnesota.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed July 3, 2014.12aElectronic Health Records22aNatural Language Processing22aSpatio-Temporal Analysis 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18474  aAUTH01066cam a22002657i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032019001300073035002000086040009000106044001600196060003300212100004400245245006300289264007900352300006000431336002600491337002800517338002700545500015000572504004100722650002800763999000900791101688745DNLM20260211080236.0890728r18821882sz af    b    000 0 ger    a82548514  z(OCoLC)82548514  aUMMbengerdad20260211dOCLCGdOCLCAdOCLCQdOCLCOdOCLCAdOCLCFdOCLCAdOCLCQdDNLM  9Switzerland00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 2131 no.111 aRoth, M.q(Moritz),d1839-1914eauthor.10aÜber einige urnierenreste beim Menschen /cvon. M. Roth. 1aBasel :bSchultze'sche Universitäts-Buchdruckerei (L. Reinhardt),c1882.  apages 65-87, 1 leaf of plates :billustrations ;c31 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"Festschrift zur Feier des 300 jährigen Bestehens der Julius-Maximilians Universität zu Wurzburg gewidet von der Universität Basel"--Cover.  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aMesonephrosxembryology  aAUTH01219cas a2200385   0030715DNLM20260219155543.0cr|mn#||||||||700226c19579999mdutr        f0    0eng    a(DNLM)M38400000(s)  a(OCoLC)50364622  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aZ 675.M4bU579m2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.).bBoard of Regents.10aMinutes of meetings.1 iOnline issues have title:aMinutes of the Board of Regents1 iTitle on Board homepage:aBoard of Regents minutes  a[Washington]3 aBethesda Md :bU.S. National Library Of Medicine  av.  aThree times a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 a1957-  aAlso issued online.1 aDigitizedc2004010131998-2pda5DNLM1 aWill digitizec2004080131957-19972pda5DNLM22aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.)12aLibraries, Medical22aGoverning Board4 uhttp://www.nlm.nih.gov/od/bor/bor.html  aAUTH01713cgm a22004694a 101267977DNLM20260227145926.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu173 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11133  a(OCoLC)212399742  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.51 aGrady, Christine.10aEthical & regulatory aspects of human subjects research.nSession 4,pEthics of recruitment /cC. Grady,  E. Emanuel.30aEthics of recruitment3 aEthical and regulatory aspects of human subjects research  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 6, 2008).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 52 min., 55 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 29, 2000.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aBiomedical Researchxethics22aClinical Trials, Phase I as Topicxethics22aEthics, Research22aHuman Experimentationxethics22aPatient Selectionxethics22aResearch Subjects 2aPersonal Narrative 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11133  aAUTH01753cas a2200505 a 101212761DNLM20260227090916.0cr|cnu||||||||040407c20039999ii qr p       0    0eng    a  2004325297  a0972-9062l0972-9062  a(OCoLC)54948917  aDLCcDLCdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  aa-ii---  9India00aRC164.I3bM32200aW1bIN21210aJ Vector Borne Dis2dnlm00aJournal of vector borne diseases.  aDelhi :bMalaria Research Centre (Indian Council of Medical Research),c[2003]-2 3<2006-2021>:aNew Delhi :bNational Institute of Malaria Research31a<2022-> :a[New Delhi] :bNational Institute of Malaria Research ;a[Mumbai] :bWolters Kluwer - Medknow  av. :bill. ;c29 cm.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 40, no. 1-2 (Mar.-June 2003)-  aTitle from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 40, no. 3/4 (Sept./Dec. 2003).1 aMEDLINEbv40n1/2, Mar./June 2003-0 aPubMedbv40n1/2, Mar./June 2003-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.12aParasitic Diseasesxprevention & control22aInsect Vectors22aMalariaxprevention & control 2aPeriodical2 aMalaria Research Centre (India)2 aNational Institute of Malaria Research (India)00tIndian journal of malariologyx0367-8326w(DLC)   50019186w(OCoLC)16441794 uhttp://www.jvbd.org/  aAUTH03136cgm a2200445 a 101565236DNLM20260227154114.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110715s2011    mdu073 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16739  a(OCoLC)752782895  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 102.31 aÁlvarez-Buylla, Arturo.10aRegional specifications of adult neural stem cells /cArturo Alvarez-Buylla.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 13 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded June 27, 2011.3 a(CIT): Neuroscience Seminar Series  The Alvarez-Buylla Lab is interested in the function and regulation of neural stem cells in the adult mammalian brain. They address questions about the mechanisms of neuronal birth, migration, and differentiation, and how neural stem cells or their immediate progeny may be related to brain tumor initiation. The subventricular zone (SVZ), the most extensive germinal niche in the adult mammalian brain, contains large numbers of neural stem cells that can generate new neurons and glial cells throughout life. By means of chain migration, young neurons born in the SVZ reach the olfactory bulb in the mouse brain, where they become fully integrated into functional circuits. The Alvarez-Buylla Lab has identified that the neural stem cells in the SVZ are a subpopulation of astrocytes and that adult neural stem cells are heterogeneous with particular types of neurons derived from progenitors from specific locations of the mouse SVZ.  Recently, Dr. Alvarez-Buylla been investigating the properties and ontogeny of astrocytes that function as stem cells in the rodent and human brain, as well as the regulation of their proliferation and the mechanisms that control their migration in the adult brain. How do cells migrate and orient during journeys through the very complex terrain of the adult brain? Once young neurons reach their destination, they need to integrate into neural circuits that are already functional. How is this accomplished? How do these new neurons contribute to plasticity without disturbing circuits that are already active? These are just some of the questions that drive the research in the Alvarez-Buylla lab.       For more information, visit: http://neuroseries.info.nih.gov.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeural Stem Cells22aAdult Stem Cells22aBrainxcytology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16739  aAUTH02269cgm a2200505 i 101635882DNLM20260227155029.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140627s2014    mdu059       fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18498  a(OCoLC)885015049  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWS 4621 aBlakemore, Sarah-Jayne,eauthor.14aThe social brain in adolescence /cSarah-Jayne Blakemore. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded June 16, 2014.3 a(CIT): Professor Blakemore is a Royal Society University Research Fellow and Professor of Cognitive Neuroscience at UCL. She is Leader of the Developmental Cognitive Neuroscience Group at the Institute of Cognitive Neuroscience.  Dr. Blakemore's Developmental Group at the UCL Institute of Cognitive Neuroscience focuses on the development of social cognition and decision-making in adolescence.  Their research involves a variety of behavioural (psychophysics, eye-tracking, motion capture) and neuroimaging (MRI, fMRI and MEG) methods. They are based at the UCL Institute of Cognitive Neuroscience in Queen Square, London, UK.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed July 16, 2014.12aSocial Behavior22aAdolescent22aAdolescent Development22aBrainxgrowth & development22aMagnetic Resonance Imaging22aRisk-Taking22aTheory of Mindxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18498  aAUTH02834cgm a2200457 i 9918503179706676DNLM20260227162730.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u221105s2022    mdu367|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32667  a(OCoLC)1372997395  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWE 8702 aNIAMS Roundtable on Cartilage Preservation and Restoration in Knee Osteoarthritis (Conference)d(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aCartilage preservation and restoration for knee osteoarthritis /cNIAMS, NIH.1 aNIAMS Roundtable on cartilage preservation and restoration in knee osteoarthritis (OA) 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (6 hr., 7 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 22, 2022.3 a(CIT): Over the last decade, a variety of regenerative medicine approaches that either preserve articular cartilage in people who started with a focal cartilage injury and thus are at a higher risk of developing knee osteoarthritis (OA) or restore defective articular cartilage in patients with symptomatic knee OA have been investigated. Recognizing the great promise of regenerative medicine for the treatment of knee OA, the importance of promoting scientific rigor and protecting patient safety, and the timing for the extramural community to adapt to new FDA guidance, NIAMS is organizing a roundtable to engage stakeholders to discuss challenges, gaps, and opportunities regarding regenerative medicine approaches for cartilage preservation and restoration in knee OA and where and how NIAMS could play a role and move the field forward. Stakeholders who will participate in the roundtable include investigators with expertise in regenerative medicine, experts in clinical treatment of knee OA, and FDA representatives.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 1, 2023).12aOsteoarthritis, Kneextherapy22aCartilage, Articularxsurgery22aRegenerative Medicine22aTherapies, Investigationalxmethods 2aUnited States 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institute of Arthritis and Musculoskeletal and Skin Diseases (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45004  aAUTH01412cgm a22004214a 101268401DNLM20260227150541.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu116 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11902  a(OCoLC)84658807  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWF 6581 aCaporaso, Neil.10aTRACO.pLung cancer /cNeil Caporaso, Eva Szabo.31aLung cancer3 aTranslational research in clinical oncology  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 20, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 55 min., 6 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 10, 2003.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aLung Neoplasms 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11902  aAUTH02197cas a2200505 i 101771197DNLM20260227090928.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||140321c20109999ii qr poo     0   a0eng    a  2018237016  a883708363a926165764a942679875a964867076a985052777a993904238a995515243a1011558570a1013904226a1058483870  a0976-5050l0976-5042  a(OCoLC)875133104  z(OCoLC)883708363z(OCoLC)926165764z(OCoLC)942679875z(OCoLC)964867076z(OCoLC)985052777z(OCoLC)993904238z(OCoLC)995515243z(OCoLC)1011558570z(OCoLC)1013904226z(OCoLC)1058483870  aCNCGMbengerdad20260227cCNCGMdEUWdOCLCFdOCLCQdUAB