M copy, bound 64th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM12aHypoprothrombinemiasxprevention & control12aHypoprothrombinemiasxtherapy12aVitamin K Deficiency Bleedingxprevention & control12aVitamin K Deficiency Bleedingxtherapy  aAUTH01484cgm a22004334a 101267640DNLM20260227145507.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu031 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10504  a(OCoLC)66524878  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 26700aPublic/private partnership to overcome barriers to early-phase clinical trials.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 23, 2006).  aStreaming video (30 min., 15 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded July 22, 2002.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxdrug therapy22aClinical Trials as Topic22aDrug Industry22aGovernment Programs22aInterinstitutional Relations 2aUnited States 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10504  aAUTH01767cgm a22003854a 101267734DNLM20260227145441.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu050 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10612  a(OCoLC)64263500  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4771 aSobol, Robert W.10aDNA base damage and repair intermediates :bout of the pan and into the fire /cRobert W. Sobol.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 21, 2006).  aStreaming video (50 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 12, 2002.3 a(CIT): The DNA Repair Interest Group is concerned with all forms of DNA damage and repair. As a major defense against environmental damage to cells DNA repair is present in all organisms examined including bacteria, yeast, drosophila, fish, amphibians, rodents and humans. The members of the DNA Repair Interest Group perform research in areas including DNA repair enzymology and fine structure, mutagenesis, gene and cell cycle regulation, protein structure, and human disease.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aDNA Damage22aDNA Repair 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10612  aAUTH01798cgm a22004454a 101490649DNLM20260227152651.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081126s2008    mdu113 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14778  a(OCoLC)312624769  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 1251 aLeto, Tom.10aRedox biology.pNADPH oxidases and imaging /cT. Leto, [James B. Mitchell].30aNADPH oxidases and imaging  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 13, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 52 min., 55 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 18, 2008.3 a(CIT): Redox Biology (RB)  The course is designed for NIH fellows to enhance their knowledge of redox biology. Reactive species such as superoxide, hydrogen peroxide and nitric oxide are associated with cellular toxicity, however, nitric oxide is useful in the treatment cardiovascular disease.  The course will examine the role of reactive nitrogen and oxygen species in carcinogenesis, cancer proliferation and angiogenesis.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aOxidation-Reduction22aCyclic N-Oxides22aNADPH Oxidases22aNeoplasmsxradiotherapy 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 a