rcía Mónaco, Ricardo,eeditor.1 aCriales, José Luis,eeditor.1 aGutierrez, Fernando R.,eeditor.2 aColegio Interamericano de Radiología. 0aAvances en diagnóstico por imágenes ;v17.  aAUTH02372cgm a22004334a 101267662DNLM20260227150054.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu217 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10529  a(OCoLC)67281665  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 50500aSARS :bdeveloping a research response /cNational Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 27, 2006).  aStreaming video (3 hr., 36 min, 14 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 30, 2003.3 a(CIT): The National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases of the National Institutes of Health will host an all-day scientific workshop at the NIH campus in Bethesda, Maryland, to address research needs on severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS). The workshop will feature international experts in the fields of coronavirus biology, vaccine development, antiviral drug development, laboratory diagnosis, SARS epidemiology, etiology and clinical management.   The purpose of this meeting is to identify the scientific, technical and logistical challenges that must be addressed to develop vaccines, antiviral therapeutics and other interventions in response to SARS. The meeting is designed to promote scientific exchange of ideas and develop elements of a robust research response.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aSevere Acute Respiratory Syndromextherapy12aSevere Acute Respiratory Syndromextransmission22aAntiviral Agents22aSevere acute respiratory syndrome-related