n function and alcohol use disorders. It will bring together scientists from both alcohol and other research fields to highlight recent science advances, identify research gaps, and discuss possible challenges in this area.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed October 24, 2014.12aAlcohol-Induced Disorders, Nervous Systemximmunology22aBrainxdrug effects22aEthanolximmunology22aNeurobiologyxmethods22aNeuroimmunomodulationxphysiology 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute on Alcohol Abuse and Alcoholism (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18580  aAUTH01237cam a22003377i 101761516DNLM20260213174517.0191116s2019    gw a     b    100 0 ger    a9783947303076qpaperback  a3947303076qpaperback  a(OCoLC)1127973258  aOHXbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9Germany00aWE 300 7aRD2lcco2 aGOTS-Expertenmeetingn(5th :d2017 :cKrems an der Donau, Austria)jauthor.10aKnorpel und Arthrose im Sport :b5. GOTS-Expertenmeeting, Krems/Österreich vom 10.11.-12.11.2017 /cStefan Nehrer, Victor Valderrabano, Martin Engelhardt (Herausgeber).  a1. Auflage. 1aJena :bVerlags-Comptoir Rolle, ein Imprint des Verlages Vopelius,c2019.  a161 pages :billustrations (chiefly color) ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier12aCartilagexinjuries22aOsteoarthritisxrehabilitation22aSports Medicinexmethods 2aConference Proceedings1 aNehrer, Stefan,eeditor.1 aEngelhardt, Martin,d1960-eeditor.  aAUTH02089cas a2200601 a 8208839DNLM20260227090958.0cr|cnu||||||||820520c19819999enk x         0||| 0eng    a   86655761 zsc 83009019zsn 81000513  a0275-5408l0275-5408  aOPOPD5  a(DNLM)O07825000(s)  a(OCoLC)07159339  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aeng  9England00aW1bOP21610aOphthalmic Physiol Opt2dnlm 0aOphthalmic & physiological optics00aOphthalmic & physiological optics :bthe journal of the British College of Ophthalmic Opticians (Optometrists).1 iIssues for <Mar. 1998> have title:aOPO3 aOphthalmic and physiological optics  aOxford ; New York :bPergamon Press,cc1981-3 32002- :aOxford :bBlackwell Publishers  av. :bill.  aSix no. a year  aFour no. a yearb-1994  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1-  aTitle from cover.  aImprint varies.1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, 1981-1 aPubMedbv1n1, 1981-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aVols. for <1999->: the journal of the College of Optometrists.  aContinues: The British journal of physiological optics.  3Vol. 7, no. 1 (1987)c19900917fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aOptics and Photonics 2aOptometry 2aVision, Ocular 2aPeriodical2 aBritish College of Ophthalmic Opticians (Optometrists)2 aCollege of Optometrists (Great Britain)0 x1475-131310tBritish journal of physiological optics4 uhttp://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/journal/10.1111/(ISSN)1475-13134 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/44402  aAUTH03204cgm a22004214a 101512467DNLM20260227152637.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090805s2008    mdu104 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14691  a(OCoLC)467700653  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWS 46300aPrevention of teen dating violence :byou can make a difference! /cpresented by the US Dept. of Health and Human Services, Office on Women's Health.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Oct. 2, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 43 min., 49 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 6, 2008.3 a(CIT): October is National Domestic Violence Awareness Month. This year, the Office on Women's Health (OWH) will highlight an important aspect of violence against women (VAW)--teen dating violence.   <i>Prevention of Teen Dating Violence: You Can Make A Difference!</i> will highlight the unique issues facing teens and various approaches to preventing teen dating violence. The two-hour seminar will include opening remarks by the Assistant Secretary for Health and two expert panel discussions. The first discussion will be an overview of the issues surrounding teen dating violence. The second panel will discuss practical applications and implementation issues for national and community providers. An exhibit will be held after the program during which national and local organizations will provide resource materials on violence against women.  <u>Panel Descriptions</u> Panel 1 - Overview of Teen Dating Violence: Point-Counter Point Discussion of Myths and Facts - This panel will provide an overview of teen dating violence and highlight what is known. It will also discuss common myths about teen dating violence and contrast that with the facts. The panelists will discuss the issues from their varied perspectives yet common goals for prevention.   Panel 2 - Reflections from the Field: A Call to Action - This panel will present the challenges in prevention and outreach in communities. It will discuss the successful strategies that organizations use to provide support services for adolescents. The presenters will discuss how their initiatives are implemented to address the various subcultures of adolescents within their everyday realities. They will present a "call to action" for all attendees.  For more information, visit http://womenshealth.gov/violence/programs/dvam.cfm.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aViolencexprevention & control22aAdolescent22aCourtship22aWomen 2aUnited States 2aWebcast1 aUnited States.bDepartment of Health and Human Services.bOffice on Women's Health.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14691  aAUTH01462cas a2200469 a 101306669DNLM20260227090915.0cr||||||||||||070309c20069999enkqr p       0    0eng    a  2007229150  a1565-9801l1565-9801y1565-8187  a(OCoLC)85823464  aIBTcIBTdIBTdFUGdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9England 4aQL1b.I800aW1bIS63V10aIsr J Ecol Evol2dnlm00aIsrael journal of ecology & evolution.3 aIsrael journal of ecology and evolution3 aEcology & evolution  aJerusalem :bScience From Israel,c2006-31aLondon :bTaylor & Francis  av. :bill. (some col.), maps (some col.) ;c24 cm.  aFour no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 52, no. 1 (2006)-  aTitle from cover.  aPrint issues ceased with 2024.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.12aBiological Evolution12aEcology22aZoology 2aPeriodical0 x2224-466200tIsrael journal of zoologyx0021-2210w(DLC)   66000646w(OCoLC)34607254 uhttp://booksandjournals.brillonline.com/content/journals/22244662  aAUTH01321cas a2200421 a 101316472DNLM20260227090920.0cr|cn#||||||||070223c20079999sz br p       0    0eng    a  2008243307  a1661-5425l1661-5425  a(OCoLC)84911431  aIAXcIAXdIAXdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9Switzerland00aW1bSE99EK10aSex Dev2dnlm00aSexual development :bgenetics, molecular biology, evolution, endocrinology, embryology, and pathology of sex determination and differentiation.  aBasel ;aNew York :bS. Karger,c2007-  av. :bill. ;c28 cm.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (2007)-  aTitle from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 1, no. 6 (2007).  aPrint issues ceased with 2024.1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, 2007-1 aPubMedbv1n1, 2007-  aAlso issued online.12aSex Determination Processes12aSex Differentiation 2aPeriodical0 x1661-54334 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/231547  aAUTH02629cgm a2200433 i 101775944DNLM20260227160724.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2021    mdu547 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31516  a(DNLM)CIT:31520  a(OCoLC)1248768065  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 832 aFrontiers in Carbohydrate Synthesis Symposiumd(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aFrontiers in Carbohydrate Synthesis Symposium /cNIH Common Fund Glycoscience Program. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (9 hr., 7 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded January 28 & 29, 2021.3 a(CIT): Almost a decade ago the National Academy of Science, National Research Council (US) released its report Transforming Glycoscience: A Roadmap for the Future.  This report noted the urgent need for broad access to chemically well-defined glycans and pointed out that while "tremendous advances" had been made, glycan synthesis remained "relegated to specialized laboratories capable of producing only small quantities of a given glycan."  They concluded that "For glycoscience to advance, significant further progress in glycan synthesis is needed to create widely applicable methodologies that generate both large and small quantities of any glycan on demand." This symposium, organized by the Common Fund Glycoscience Program, will explore progress made on the synthesis of glycans over the past decade including: catalysis & synthetic methodologies for preparation of glycans and glycoconjugates (Milligram to Kilogram quantities); automation of glycan synthesis; and the democratization of glycan synthesis such that "specialized experts" are not required to routinely synthesize these molecules.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 23, 2021).12aPolysaccharidesxbiosynthesis12aGlycolysisxphysiology22aGlycomics 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aCommon Fund Glycoscience Program,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4139240uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41394  aAUTH03020cgm a2200457 i 101775934DNLM20260227160704.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2021    mdu067|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31493  a(OCoLC)1244241149  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 506.4 AA100aCOVID 19 epidemiology and transmission dynamics :bcontrasting South Africa, China and the U.S. /cCécile Viboud. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 7 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAt head of title: COVID-19 Scientific Interest Group.  aRecorded January 21, 2021.3 a(CIT): Dr. Viboud is a staff scientist at NIH Fogarty International Center, where she specializes in the transmission dynamics and mortality of infectious disease.  Since the earliest days of the COVID-19 pandemic, Viboud has helped to lead international efforts to collect and disseminate epidemiological information and predictive models about SARS-CoV-2 to inform the trajectory or the pandemic and the effectiveness of control measures.  "We will discuss several population-level analyses documenting the burden and transmission dynamics of SARS-CoV-2 based on the data from the US, China and South Africa.  Excess mortality and hospitalization studies will show the direct impacts of the pandemic on respiratory diseases and chronic conditions by geography and age group, which are in part undetected by official statistics.  We will also begin to quantify some of the secondary impacts of COVID-19 interventions on unnatural deaths and circulation of other pathogens.  We will contrast the burden of COVID-19 with that of past influenza pandemics.  We will show how changes in contacts and mobility throughout 2020 have impacted SARS-CoV-2 transmission rates in the US.  Finally, we will describe efforts to combine information from multiple transmission models and academic teams to project the long-term trajectory of COVID-19 and the impact of interventions."  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 26, 2021).12aCOVID-19xepidemiology22aCOVID-19xtransmission22aCross-Cultural Comparison 2aUnited Statesxepidemiology 2aSouth Africaxepidemiology 2aChinaxepidemiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aViboud, Cécile,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bCOVID-19 Scientific Interest Group,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41234  aAUTH02909cgm a2200493 i 101776738DNLM20260227160813.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210302s2021    mdu068|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31552  a(OCoLC)1249794402  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 300 AA100aSavages cry easily and are afraid of the dark :bwhat it means to talk about race and African American health /cNaa Oyo A. Kwate. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 8 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNLM history talk/Annual James H. Cassedy lecture in the history of medicine  aRecorded February 11, 2021.3 a(CIT): This talk examines the impact of racism on African American health, looking at pervasive inequities that drive higher rates of morbidity and death in the United States. Where once explicitly racist theories of African American bodies and minds dominated public and scientific discourse, contemporary understandings of racial inequities in health tend to use less incendiary language, but still conceive of poor health as fundamentally a problem of individuals. Such framing centers health behaviors including diet and visits to the doctor, and leaves the role of social structures uninterrogated. This talk explores the deeply entrenched effects of racism on African American health through institutional policies and practices that defeat socioeconomic opportunity and cause overexposure to harms; stereotypes; day-to-day encounters with racism; and other aspects of American social life. This talk is co-sponsored by the National Endowment for the Humanities, Office of Digital Humanities, as part of the recently reaffirmed partnership between NLM and NEH to collaborate on research, education, and career initiatives.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 30, 2021).12aMinority Healthxethnology12aBlack or African American.22aRacism22aStereotyping22aHealthcare Disparitiesxethnology22aSocial Factors 2aUnited Statesxethnology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aKwate, Naa Oyo A.,espeaker.2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.).bHistory of Medicine Division,esponsoring body. 0aJames Cassedy memorial lecture 0aNLM history talks40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41218  aAUTH01695cam a2200373 i 60631630RDNLM20260206222100.0cr||||||||||||820221t19091908nyua|    b    000 0 eng    a   08012179   a(OCoLC)1086707  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aLBbS971m 19081 aSwift, Edgar James,d1860-1932,eauthor.10aMind in the making :ba study in mental development /cby Edgar James Swift. 1aNew York :bCharles Scribner's Sons,c1909. 4c1908  aviii, 329 pages :billustrations ;c21 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aPartly reprinted from various periodicals.  aIncludes bibliographical references.0 aStandards of human power -- Criminal tendencies of boys : their cause and function -- The school and the individual -- Reflex neuroses and their relation to development -- Some nervous disturbances of development -- The psychology of learning -- The racial brain and education -- Experimental pedagogy -- School-mastering educa