37002800335338002700363490004400390650002200434700003100456830004500487999000900532101249555DNLM20260206080149.0050513s2005    gw a     b    001 0 ger    a9783131400413  a3131400412  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Germany00aQT 26100aSportverletzungen, Sportschäden /cherausgegeben von Martin Engelhardt ... [et al.].  aStuttgart ;aNew York :bThieme,cc2005.  axi, 211 p. :bill.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aPraxiswissen Halte- und Bewegungsorgane12aAthletic Injuries1 aEngelhardt, Martin,d1960- 0aPraxiswissen Halte- und Bewegungsorgane.  aAUTH04475cgm a2200409 i 101622742DNLM20260227154545.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u131227s2013    mdu068|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18143  a(OCoLC)868259296  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 3751 aSchekman, Randy W.eauthor.10aBiogenesis and function of the autophagosome /cRandy Schekman ; NIH Cell Biology and Metabolism Program. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 8 min.) :bcolor, sound)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Nov. 4, 2013.3 a(CIT): The NIH Cell Biology and Metabolism Program will host Randy Schekman, co-winner of the 2013 Nobel Prize for Physiology or Medicine.  The pathway of autophagy has assumed an important position in the analysis of mammalian cellular response to stress, hypoxia and pathogen infection.  Autophagosomes mature by growth and envelopment of cytosolic proteins and organelles that are trapped within the inner membrane of a two-membrane organelle.  Trapped proteins are delivered by autophagosome fusion to the lysosome where protein and polysaccharide degradation permit amino acids and sugars to be recycled.  A pre-autophagsosomal membrane matures by the addition of membrane material from various intracellular sources and the attachment of peripheral proteins that remain bound through a covalent lipidation reaction. The origin and the mechanism of generation of the pre-autophagic membrane are poorly understood.  Published evidence suggests an origin of this membrane on the ER, on the mitochondrial surface or possibly the Golgi or plasma membrane.  We have addressed these issues with the development and analysis of a cell-free reaction that reproduces the lipidation of a major peripheral autophagosomal protein, LC3. Mouse embryonic fibroblasts (MEFs) taken from a mouse strain deficient in Atg5 are unable to lipidate LC3 and other targets of lipidation involved in the autophagic process.  A crude membrane fraction isolated from these MEFs was mixed with cytosol harvested from normal cells that were untreated or subjected to a stress regimen known to induce autophagy.  On addition of ATP, incubation of the mixture resulted in the formation of lipidated endogenous LC3 or exogenous recombinant LC3.  The reaction required both membranes and cytosol and was stimulated 2-5 fold when the cytosol was taken from stress-induced cells, but was inactive when cytosol was used from stressed atg5 mutant MEFs.  Autophagosome maturation requires a class III PI-3 kinase (VPS34 homolog); LC3 lipidation in our cell-free reaction was inhibited by wortmannin and 3-methyladenine, known inhibitors of this kinase, and by the addition of a peptide containing a PI3P-bnding sequence, the FYVE domain.  Using cell fractionation techniques including differential centrifugation and buoyant density sedimentation, we have identified an ERGIC fraction enriched in Sec22 and ERGIC-53 that has the highest specific activity for LC3 lipidation in comparison to other cellular membranes.  Membranes isolated from cells depleted of the ERGIC by use of selective inhibitors of traffic did not retain lipidation activity, but the activity reappeared quickly when traffic and the ERGIC was restored by removal of the inhibitor.  Other early markers of the autophagosome membrane, Atg14L and DFCP1, are recruited to the ERGIC in cultured cells and in our cell-free reaction.  This approach may now be exploited to understand the molecular mechanism of growth, closure and cargo capture by the maturing autophagosomal membrane.  aDescription based on version viewed December 31, 2013.12aAutophagyxphysiology12aPhagosomesxphysiology22aOrganelle Biogenesis 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.).bCell Biology and Metabolism Program,eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18143  aAUTH01701cgm a2200421 i 101739603DNLM20260227155923.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190108s2018    mdu204|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27223  a(OCoLC)1084796598  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 171.52 aNIMH Workshop: Treatment Resistant Depression and the Neurobiology of Suicided(2018 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aTreatment resistant depression & the neurobiology of suicide /cNIMH, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2018]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (3 hr., 24 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded December 6, 2018.3 a(CIT): Join experts from the National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH) for a workshop on treatment resistant depression and the neurobiology of suicide.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed January 31, 2019).12aDepressive Disorder, Treatment-Resistant22aMajor Depressive Disorder22aNervous System Physiological Phenomena22aSuicide 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Mental Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27223  aAUTH01437cas a2200445 a 101466659DNLM20260227090919.0cr|cn ||||||||070723c20089999nyumr p       0    0eng    a  2007213654  a1937-3341l1937-3341  a(OCoLC)156908427  aNSDcNSDdNSDdOCLCQdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bTI844A10aTissue Eng Part A2dnlm00aTissue engineering.nPart A.13aTissue engineering part A  aNew Rochelle, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.  aMonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 14, no. 1 (Jan. 2008).  aDescription based on first issue; title from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv14n1, Jan. 2008-1 aPubMedbv14n1, Jan. 2008-  aAlso issued online.  aContinues in part: Tissue engineering.12aTissue Engineering22aBiocompatible Materials22aGuided Tissue Regeneration 2aPeriodical2 aTissue Engineering & Regenerative Medicine International Society.0 x1937-335X11tTissue engineeringx1076-3279w(DLC)sn 94005129w(OCoLC)304537614 uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/tea  aAUTH01561cas a2200469 a 101235219DNLM20260227090907.0cr|cn ||||||||040909c20059999pauqr p       0    0eng    a  20042155000 a1552-4256l1552-4264  a(OCoLC)56472133  aNSDcNSDdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bJO889DCG10aJ Soc Work End Life Palliat Care2dnlm00aJournal of social work in end-of-life & palliative care.3 aJournal of social work in end-of-life and palliative care  aBinghamton, NY :bHaworth Social Work Practice Press,cc2005-3 aPhiladelphia :bRoutledge  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (2005)-  aTitle from cover.  aImprint varies.1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, 2005-0 aPubMedbv1n1, 2005-  aAlso issued online.  3v.1, no. 1 (May 2005)c20070516fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper that meets the full ANSI standard for Permanent Paper12aSocial Work22aPalliative Carexpsychology22aTerminal Carexpsychology 2aPeriodical0 x1552-426400tLoss, grief & carex8756-4610w(DLC)   86649854w(OCoLC)115669794 uhttps://www.tandfonline.com/loi/wswe20  aAUTH02315cgm a2200481 i 101755458DNLM20260227160032.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u191002s2019    mdu055|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:28737  a(OCoLC)1124968695  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 11.10 aMind-body problems (Posner)10aMind-body problems :blobotomy, science, and the digital humanities /cMiriam Posner.1 aLobotomy, science, and the digital humanities1 iTitle on PowerPoint:aScientists' mind-body problems 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (55 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aJames H. Cassedy memorial lecture in the history of medicine  aRecorded September 19, 2019.3 a(CIT): 11th Annual James H. Cassedy Memorial Lecture in the History of Medicine:  This talk examines the career of Walter Freeman, the world's foremost exponent of lobotomy. Freeman was also an avid photographer, who almost invariably captured before-and-after images of his patients. The talk explains how Freeman's apparently eccentric practice fit into the larger picture of science and medicine in the mid-20th century, and how digital humanities methods have helped give context and nuance to Freeman's work. It concludes with a consideration of what DH methods might offer to historians of medicine in the twenty-first century.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed October 18, 2019).12aFreeman, Walter,d1895-1972.12aPsychosurgeryxhistory22aHistory, 20th Century22aPhotographyxhistory22aPhysiognomy 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aPosner, Miriam,espeaker. 0aJames Cassedy memorial lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?28737  aAUTH03656cgm a2200445 i 101769642DNLM20260227160121.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u200715s2020    mdu061|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:29197  a(OCoLC)1229081689  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 50500aHigh seroprevalence, drastic decline of incidence and low infection fatality rate of sARS-CoV-2 infections in children and adults in the Ski Resort Ischgl, Austria /cDorothee von Laer ; NIH COVID-19 Scientific Interest Group. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH COVID-19 SIG lecture series  aRecorded July 1, 2020.3 a(CIT): NIH COVID-19 Scientific Interest Group  In early March 2020, a SARS-CoV-2 outbreak at a ski resort in Ischgl, Austria, initiated the spread of SARS-CoV-2 throughout Austria and Northern Europe. Thousands of infections can be traced back to Ischgl. In a recent study by Medical University of Innsbruck investigators, 42.4 percent of those living in Ischgl were shown to be carrying the new coronavirus antibodies, indicating they had been infected in the COVID-19 pandemic. Between April 21 and 27, a cross-sectional epidemiologic study targeting the full population of Ischgl (n= app. 1,867), of which 79 percent could be included (n=1473), was performed. For each individual, the study involved a SARS-CoV-2 PCR test for the virus, antibody testing, and a questionnaire. In addition, the SARS-CoV-2 PCR+ cases reported to the authorities were included. The seroprevalence was 42 percent and individuals under 18 showed a significantly lower seroprevalence (27 percent) than adults (45 percent). However, only 105 study participants remembered if they had a previous positive PCR result. The clinical course was generally mild and only two individuals in Ischgl had died from infection corresponding to an infection fatality rate (IFR) of 0.26 percent. In the first week of April, a public screening in Ischgl had found 19 percent of the population to be PCR+. However, only 8 (0.5 percent) individuals were newly diagnosed to be infected with SARS-CoV-2 during the study conducted 2-3 weeks later.  Ischgl was hit early and hard by SARS-CoV-2, which led to a high local seroprevalence of 42 percent, that was lower in individuals below the age of 18 than in adults with a low IFR. As nonpharmaceutical interventions (e.g. social distancing, mask wearing) had already reduced virus spread, mathematical models conclude that the high seroprevalence significantly contributed to the drastic decline of new infections during April.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 30, 2020).12aCOVID-19xepidemiology22aSARS-CoV-2ximmunology22aSeroepidemiologic Studies 2aAustriaxepidemiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aLaer, Dorothee von,d1958-espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bCOVID-19 Scientific Interest Group,esponsoring body. 0aNIH COVID-19 SIG lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?29197  aAUTH01794cas a2200541 a 101262417DNLM20260224111553.0cr|cn#||||||||051128c20069999nyubr p       0    0eng    a  2006249051  a1880-6546l1880-6546y0021-521X  aJPSOE2  a(OCoLC)65355874  aCVUcCVUdZYUdDNLM  alcd  9United States14aQP1b.J300aW1bJA97210aJ Physiol Sci2dnlm04aThe journal of physiological sciences :bJPS.30aJPS  aTokyo, Japan :bPhysiological Society of Japan,cc2006-2 aTokyo :bSpringer Japan3132025- :a[New York] :bElsevier  av. :bill. ;c30 cm.  aSix no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 56, no. 1 (Feb. 2006)-  aTitle from cover.  aImprint varies.1 aMEDLINEbv56n1, Feb. 2006-1 aPubMedbv56n1, Feb. 2006-0 aPMC  aHas annual supplement containing the proceedings of the annual meeting of the Physiological Society of Japan.  aAlso issued online.12aPhysiology 2aPeriodical2 aNihon Seiri Gakkai.0 x1880-656200tJapanese journal of physiologyx0021-521Xw(DLC)   67048264w(OCoLC)17542254 uhttps://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/browse/physiolsci/4 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/125764 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=1012624174 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/the-journal-of-physiological-sciences  aAUTH01548cas a2200481   8006431DNLM20260227090952.0cr|cn ||||||||800710c19809999cauar p       0||| 0eng    a0163-7525l0163-7525  aAREHDT  a(DNLM)A54310000(s)  a(OCoLC)04471173  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bAN7796K10aAnnu Rev Public Health2dnlm 0aAnnual review of public health00aAnnual review of public health.  aPalo Alto, Calif.,bAnnual Reviews Inc.  av.bill.  aAnnual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-    1980-  aEditors: v. 1-    L. Breslow, J. E. Fielding and L. B. Lave.1 aMEDLINEbv1, 1980-1 aPubMedbv1, 1980-1 aHospital literature indexx0018-5736b1980-19941 aHospital and health administration indexx1077-1719b1995-19991 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3Vol. 1 (MAY 1980)c19890816fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper that meets the full ANSI standard for Permanent Paper 2aPublic Health 2aPeriodical1 aBreslow, Lester.1 aLave, Lester B.1 aFielding, Jonathan E.0 x1545-20934 uhttp://www.annualreviews.org/loi/publhealth  aAUTH03391cgm a2200421 i 101773039DNLM20260227160336.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201105s2020    mdu072|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30339  a(OCoLC)1226370540  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 56800aMulticellular control of immunological homeostasis /cHarikesh Wong. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 12 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aIIG seminar  aRecorded October 14, 2020.3 a(CIT): IIG Seminar  Hari completed his Ph.D. in Cell Biology at the University of Toronto, Canada, where he employed single-molecule imaging techniques to study multimolecular interactions in individual immune cells. Specifically, he focused on how the dynamics of the actin cytoskeleton regulated receptor diffusion and immune cell signal transduction at fast timescales. In 2016, Hari joined Dr. Ron Germain's lab at the NIH, seeking to understand how the immune response was regulated over much larger spatiotemporal scales. Here, Hari transitioned to studying multicellular systems and their emergent properties within complex tissue environments. His work to date has predominantly focused on the control of self-antigen specific T cells by regulatory T cells within murine secondary lymphoid organs. Using a combination of multiplexed tissue imaging, clear organ volume imaging, and computational modeling techniques, Hari was able to study physiological numbers of self-reactive T cells, dendritic cells, and regulatory T cells within unperturbed tissues -- a level of in situ analysis that has not been reported to date. As a result, he uncovered an essential feedback control system that routinely constrains autoimmune T cell responses in secondary lymphoid organs, thereby maintaining immunological homeostasis. Such feedback control gives rise to a sharp discriminatory boundary (i.e. an emergent property) between self and non-self. Surprisingly, modest reductions (&lt; two-fold) in critical parameters governing the system are sufficient to alter the discriminatory boundary, enabling some self-reactive T cells to escape control and become threats to the host. These findings help to explain why haploinsufficiency in specific genes, such as CTLA-4, can lead to human autoimmunity. Hari's unique combination of interdisciplinary approaches makes his work of broad interest to immunologists as well as biologists from diverse disciplines.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 18, 2020).12aT-Lymphocytes, Regulatoryximmunology22aHomeostasisximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aWong, Harikesh,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aImmunology Interest Group lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38840  aAUTH02033cgm a2200469 i 101773052DNLM20260227160404.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201105s2020    mdu052|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31372  a(OCoLC)1227080837  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 50500aChallenges of SARS-CoV-2 testing in the hospital laboratory /cKaren Frank. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (52 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCOVID-19 insights webinar  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded November 2, 2020.3 a(CIT): We invite you to join a new biweekly NHLBI webinar series entitled "COVID-19 Insights" to learn about groundbreaking research focusing on the host response to SARS-CoV-2 infection. This exciting cross-disciplinary series, featuring both NHLBI staff and researchers throughout the scientific community, will enhance NHLBI listeners" understanding of the latest advances in this fast-moving research, especially as they relate to the NHLBI mission.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed December 10, 2020).12aCOVID-19 Testing12aCOVID-19xdiagnosis22aLaboratories, Hospital22aSurge Capacity 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aFrank, Karenq(Karen M.),espeaker.2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute,esponsoring body. 0aNHLBI COVID-19 insights webinar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38870  aAUTH01761nam a2200373 i 9917015113406676DNLM20260219135937.0150928q18601879ohua          000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLMdDNLM0 aengager  9United States00aQV 57bL274d 18601 aLangell, D.,eauthor.10aD. Langell's New Asthma Remedy :bfor the cure of asthma or pththisic, and its kindren diseases, such as nasal catarrh, bronchitis, croup, &c.  = D. Lengell's Neues Asthma-Mittel : zur Heilung von Asthma und der verwandten Krankheiten, wie z. B. Schnupfen, Bronchitis, Bräune u. s. w.1 aD. Lengell's Neues Asthma-Mittel 1a[Ohio] :bDavenport Democrat Printing House,c[between 1860 and 1879?]  a1 sheet :billustration ;c47 x 29 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  asheetbnb2rdacarrier  a"D. Langell, inventor and sole proprietor."  aPrinted in English on one side and in German on the other.  aNLM copy, includes a printed and stamped envelope from D. Langell addressed to a recipient in New York and a printed sample sachet of D. Langell's New Asthma Remedy.5DNLM  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202102182pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202102185DNLM12aNonprescription Drugs22aAsthmaxdrug therapy22aInhalation22aPlant Preparations22aRespiratory Tract Diseasesxdrug therapy 2aAdvertisement  aAUTH01066cam a2200289 i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002000073040003500093042000800128044001800136060003100154100004000185245009000225264005500315300002100370336002600391337002800417338002700445500001900472540014200491583003900633583007200672650002300744999000900767101183597DNLM20260227151851.0030701s1901    alu           000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)52639389  aDNLMbengerdad20260211dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 366 no.51 aDuBose, F. G.,d1873-1941,eauthor.10aDiffuse nephritis, acute and subacute, with its medical treatment /cby F. G. DuBose. 1a[Alabama?] :b[publisher not identified],c[1901?]  a2 pages ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202102232pda5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20260227lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM12aNephritisxtherapy  aAUTH03268cgm a2200457 i 101773049DNLM20260227160357.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201105s2020    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31364  a(OCoLC)1225183706  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 300 AA100aDeep and wide :bthe voyage to discover local and global health equity /cLisa A. Cooper. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 0 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture. Rolla E. Dyer lecture  aRecorded October 28, 2020.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series  Rolla E. Dyer Lecture  The Dyer Lecture is among the oldest, continuous speaker series at the NIH, established in 1950 in honor of former NIH director Rolla Eugene Dyer, M.D., a noted authority on infectious diseases.  The lecture series features internationally renowned researchers who have contributed substantially to medical as well as biological knowledge of infectious diseases.  Dr. Cooper is the Bloomberg Distinguished Professor, Health and Healthcare Equity, and the James F. Fries Professor of Medicine at the Johns Hopkins University.  Born in Liberia, she witnessed the effects of social deprivation on the health of many of her fellow citizens and developed a passion for a career in medicine and public health.  Her research at Johns Hopkins University has focused on the crisis of inequity in medical care.  Dr. Cooper was one of the first scientists to document disparities in the quality of relationships between physicians and patients from socially at-risk groups.  A recipient of a MacArthur "Genius" Award, she has designed innovative interventions targeting physicians" communication skills, patients" self-management skills, and healthcare organizations" ability to address needs of populations experiencing health disparities.  Dr. Cooper is now applying these techniques in real time during the COVID-19 pandemic, which is disproportionally affecting marginalized communities, including African American and Hispanic communities in her adopted hometown of Baltimore.  Such local outreach will be a focus of her WALS lecture.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 24, 2020).12aHealth Status Disparities22aHealth Equity22aSocial Determinants of Health22aCommunity-Based Participatory Research 2aUnited States 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aCooper, Lisa A.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pNIH director's R.E. Dyer lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=36083  aAUTH01701cas a2200529   7804071DNLM20260227090954.0cr|cnu||||||||780608c19789999oncbr p       0    0eng    asc 78000735   a0147-958Xl0147-958X  aCNVMDL  a(DNLM)C25800000(s)z(DNLM)M09960000(s)  a(OCoLC)03711976  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aengbfre  ansdp  9Canada00aW1bCL664G10aClin Invest Med2dnlm 0aClinical and investigative medicine00aClinical and investigative medicine.bMédecine clinique et experimentale.30aMédecine clinique et experimentale13aCIM  aOxford, New York :bPergamon Press3 320