onal Institute on Mental Health. For more information go to <a href='http://www.dor.kaiser.org/external/DORExternal/rpgeh/news/NIH_grant.aspx'>http://www.dor.kaiser.org/external/DORExternal/rpgeh/news/NIH_grant.aspx.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aAgingxgenetics22aGenetic Research22aGenome, Humanxgenetics22aMolecular Epidemiologyxmethods22aTelomerexgenetics 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17298  aAUTH00978nam a2200265 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400025000800440012001050600033001171000043001502450073001932640054002663000027003203360026003473370028003733380027004015000017004285000063004455610136005086500017006447100042006619990009007039918227356706676DNLM20260223140048.0210629r18941894enk           000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  9England00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5552 no.201 aJackson, Herbert,d1863-1936,eauthor.10aObservations on the nature of phosphorescence /cby Herbert Jackson. 1a[London?] :b[publisher not identified],c[1894?]  apages 734-744 ;c22 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCover title.  aReprinted from Transactions of the Chemical Society, 1894.  aNLM copy, author's presentation inscription on front cover: "G. Stillingfleet Johnson, Esq., with the author's kind regards."5DNLM12aLuminescence2 aTransactions of the Chemical Society.  aAUTH01901cgm a22004934a 101549249DNLM20260227153911.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101208s2010    mdu209 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16259  a(OCoLC)702604560  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 168.5.H62 aNorman Salzman Symposium in Virologyn(12th :d2010 :cBethesda, Md.)10a12th Annual Norman P. Salzman Symposium :bMemorial Award & Symposium in Virology /cB. Hahn, R. Silverman, K. Berns and many young virologists.1 aTwelth Annual Norman P. Salzman Symposium1 aMemorial Award & Symposium in Virology1 iTitle on screen banner:a12th Annual Norman P. Salzman Memorial Award & Symposium in Virology  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Jan. 21, 2011).  aStreaming video (3 hr., 29 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 5, 2010.3 a(CIT): The event is a virology symposium in Natcher to honor the memory of Norman Salzman. It is sponsored by the NIH Virology Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHIV22aAnti-Retroviral Agents22aHIV Infectionsxdrug therapy22aMeningitis, Viralximmunology22aRetroviridae22aSimian Immunodeficiency Virus 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aHahn, B.q(Beatrice)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16259  aAUTH04452cgm a22004454a 101516493DNLM20260227153146.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u091002s2009    mdu060|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15283  a(OCoLC)477047328  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWV 2501 aGlowatzki, Elisabeth.10aProperties of transmitter release at the hair cell afferent synapse /cElisabeth Glowatzki.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Nov. 23, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 0 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 14, 2009.3 a(CIT): Cochlear hair cells convert sound signals into receptor potentials and subsequently, at their afferent synapses with auditory nerve fibers, into firing rates that are relayed to the central nervous system.  Hair cell ribbon synapses, like retinal ribbon synapses, are highly specialized for fast and continuous glutamate release.  The presynaptic ribbon's role is poorly understood, however, it has been proposed to support the continuous release of vast numbers of vesicles. The activity pattern of auditory nerve fibers is quite diverse, firing rates at rest can vary from less than 1 AP per s to 150 APs per s, and in response to sound, fibers activate with different thresholds and show diverse rate level functions. Auditory nerve fibers with different response patterns can contact the same inner hair cell in the cochlea.  Therefore, properties of the auditory nerve fiber activity must be set at the individual hair cell ribbon synapses.    The Glowatzki lab studies the pre- and postsynaptic properties at inner hair cell ribbon synapses that set and modulate auditory nerve fiber properties.  Mostly dendritic whole cell recordings from auditory nerve fibers directly where they contact the hair cell are used in excised cochlear tissue, sometimes parallel with hair cell recordings. Lately we have characterized the calcium dependence of release at the hair cell synapse and found it to be linear (Goutman and Glowatzki, 2007). We are now investigating the mechanisms and role of multivesicular release at the hair cell ribbon synapse. In my talk I will elaborate on different modes of release at individual synapses that may play a role in setting auditory nerve fiber properties.     Selected Publications:    Glowatzki, E., Fuchs, P.A. Transmitter release at the hair cell ribbon synapse. (2002)  Nature Neuroscience (5) 2:147-154.   Pyott, S., Glowatzki, E., Trimmer, J., Aldrich, R., (2004) Extrasynaptic localization of inactivating BK channels in mouse inner hair cells. Journal of Neuroscience 24(43):9469-9474.    Glowatzki, E., Chen, N., Hiel, H., Jin, L., Yi, E., Tanaka, K., Ellis-Davies, J.C.R., Rothstein, J.D., Bergles, D.E. (2006) The glutamate-aspartate transporter (GLAST) mediates glutamate uptake at inner hair cell afferent synapses in the mammalian cochlea. Journal of Neuroscience 26(29):7659-64.     Tritsch, NX, Yi, E, Gale, JE, Glowatzki, E, Bergles, DE. The origin of spontaneous activity in the developing auditory system. Nature 2007, 450:50-55.   Seal, RP, Akil, O,  Yi, E, Weber, CM, Grant, L, Yoo, J, Clause, A, Kandler, K, Noebels, JL, Glowatzki, E, Lustig LR, Edwards, RH, Sensorineural deafness and seizures in mice lacking vesicular glutamate transporter 3. Neuron 2008, 57(2):263-75.     Martinez-Monedero, R, Yi, E,  Oshima, K, Glowatzki, E,  Edge, ASB (2008) Differentiation of inner ear stem cells to functional sensory neurons. Developmental Biology 68(5):669-84.     For more information see our website - http://neuroseries.info.nih.gov.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHair Cells, Auditoryxphysiology22aNeurotransmitter Agentsxphysiology22aSynaptic Transmissionxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15283  aAUTH03703cgm a2200409 i 101658186DNLM20260227155125.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150505s2015    mdu046|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18941  a(OCoLC)909852092  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWE 5521 aTraynor, Bryan,eauthor.10aGenomics of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis /cBryan J. Traynor. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (46 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's seminar series  aRecorded April 10, 2015.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Seminar Series    Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS; Lou Gehrig's disease) is a fatal neurodegenerative disorder that leads to rapidly progressive paralysis and respiratory failure. ALS is the third most common neurodegenerative disease in the western world, and there are currently no effective therapies. Frontotemporal dementia (FTD) is the most common form of dementia in the population under the age of 65. An overlap between these two clinically distinct neurological diseases has long been recognized, but the molecular basis of this intersection was unknown.    Recently, the Laboratory of Neurogenetics at the National Institute on Aging identified the major genetic cause of both ALS and FTD. To do this, Dr. Traynor organized a worldwide consortium, bringing together groups that had previously been competitors to focus their efforts towards identifying this gene. This was made possible by the next generation sequencing technologies available at the NIH. This innovative approach worked, and his group published the cause of chromosome 9-linked ALS/FTD in the journal Neuron in September 2011. In these cases, the disease is caused by a six base pair segment of DNA that is pathologically repeated over and over again, up to several thousand times. This so-called large hexanucleotide repeat disrupts the C9ORF72 gene located on chromosome 9. This is the most common genetic cause of both ALS and FTD identified to date, accounting for approximately 40% of all familial cases of ALS and FTD in European and North American populations. Further, Dr. Traynor"s group has shown that this mutation underlies about 8% of cases of sporadically occurring ALS and FTD that lack a family history. This represents the first time that a common genetic cause has been identified for the sporadic form of these diseases.   The discovery of the C9ORF72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion is a landmark discovery in our understanding of neurodegenerative disease. It has already greatly effected how these diseases are diagnosed, investigated and perceived, and provides a mechanistic link between two clinically distinct disorders, ALS and FTD. It also provides a distinct therapeutic target for gene therapy efforts aimed at ameliorating the disease, and such efforts are already well underway.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 13, 2015).12aAmyotrophic Lateral Sclerosisxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18941  aAUTH01539cas a2200433 a 101266881DNLM20260227090911.0cr|cn#||||||||060203c20069999ne qr p       0    0eng    a  2006243124  a1871-5257l1871-5257  a(OCoLC)64768954  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9Netherlands00aW1bCU794PW10aCardiovasc Hematol Agents Med Chem2dnlm00aCardiovascular & hematological agents in medicinal chemistry.3 aCardiovascular and hematological agents in medicinal chemistry  aSharjah, U.A.E. ;aSan Francisco, CA :bBentham Science Publishers,cc2006-3 aAmsterdam :bBentham Science Publishers  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 4, no. 1 (Jan. 2006)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv4n1, Jan. 2006-0 aPubMedbv4n1, Jan. 2006-  aAlso issued online.12aCardiovascular Agentsxtherapeutic use12aHematologic Agentsxtherapeutic use 2aPeriodical0 x1875-618200tCurrent medicinal chemistry. Cardiovascular and hematological agentsx1568-0169w(DLC)   2003243485w(OCoLC)530129194 uhttp://www.eurekaselect.com/597/journal/cardiovascular-amp-hematological-agents-medicinal-chemistry  aAUTH02216cgmaa22005054a 101232630DNLM20260227144516.0vf|cbahoucr|cna||||||||vf|czahmuvf|cjahouhd|bfa012baap041025s2004    mdu055 ||    f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:3357  a(OCoLC)57422642  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aWM 27000aTranslational neuroimaging in drug research /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  a1 videocassette of 1 (DVCPRO) (55 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/4 in.3master.  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (55 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (55 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aThe NIH director's Wednesday P.M. lecture  aTitle from cassette spine label.  aSpeaker, Joanna S. Fowler.  aOriginally presented Sept. 8, 2004.  aDr. Fowler discusses her research in using PET scans to follow the pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics of various drugs in the brain. Cocaine, Ritalin, and nicotine are some of the addictive substances that she covers.  aAlso available online.  aVideocassette (Betacam SP)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2011.e1 videocassette (55 min.) : sd., col., 1/2 in.  aVHS.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aSubstance-Related Disorders.22aBrainxdrug effects22aPositron-Emission Tomography1 aFowler, Joanna S.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3357  aAUTH01819cas a2200529 a 8511777DNLM20260227091012.0cr|cn#||||||||851205c19859999ne qr p       0    0eng    a0168-6577l0168-6577  aEJPOEQ  a(DNLM)SR0053525(s)  a(OCoLC)11885330  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aengafre  9Netherlands00aW1bEU72EC10aEur J Popul2dnlm00aEuropean journal of population =bRevue européenne de démographie.31aRevue européenne de démographie  aAmsterdam :bNorth-Holland,cc1985-2 aNorwell Ma :bKluwer Academic31aDordrecht :bSpringer Netherlands  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Jan. 1985)-0 aMEDLINEbSelected citations only0 aPubMedbv32n1, Feb. 2016- ; Selected citations only before this date0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.  aArticles chiefly in English with some in French; summaries in both languages.  aPublished under the auspices of: the European Association for Population Studies.  aContinues: European demographic information bulletin. 2aDemography 2aPopulation 2aEurope 2aPeriodical2 aEuropean Association for Population Studies.0 x1572-988510tEuropean demographic information bulletinx0046-2756w(OCoLC)15684384 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/106804 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=8511777  aAUTH02645cgm a2200457 a 101575995DNLM20260227154528.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120107s2011    mdu810 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17003  a(DNLM)CIT:17004  a(OCoLC)774950597  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 1452 aJoint NIH and USDA Workshop on Using Nanotechnology to Improve Nutrition Through Enhanced Bioavailability and Efficacyd(2011 :cBethesda, Md.)10aJoint NIH and USDA Workshop on Using Nanotechnology to Improve Nutrition Through Enhanced Bioavailability and Efficacy.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (13 hr., 30 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded Nov. 29 & 30, 2011.3 a(CIT): The "Joint NIH and USDA Workshop on Using Nanotechnology To Improve Nutrition Through Enhanced Bioavailability and Efficacy" will discuss and evaluate the state of the science and identify knowledge gaps in the use of nutrients (and bioactive food components) for disease prevention and opportunities to improve safe, targeted delivery and controlled release of these components to improve absorption, distribution, metabolism, and elimination through nanotechnologies. The workshop also aims to encourage collaborations and stimulate ideas for diet and disease prevention research. Sessions will highlight several emerging areas of potential utility in nutrition and food science research, including "Knowledge Gaps in Effective Nutrition and Disease Prevention," "Applications of Nanotechnology To Enhance Nutrition for Disease Prevention," "Opportunities and Challenges in the Food Sciences With Nanotechnologies," and "Ethical, Legal, and Societal Implications of Nutritional Nanotechnology."  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from screen banner.12aNanotechnology12aNutritional Physiological Phenomena22aBiological Availability22aFood Technology 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aUnited States.bDepartment of Agriculture.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1700340uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17004  aAUTH01121nam a2200313 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390200018000800400025000980420008001230440012001310600011001431000055001542450170002092640079003793000040004583360026004983370028005243380027005525040041005796500031006206500023006516500032006747000034007067000030007407000028007709990009007989918900804306676DNLM20260210064853.0240809s2020    uy a     b    000 0 spa    a9789974874817  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Uruguay00aWX 2151 aMachado, Fernandoq(Machado Rodríguez),eauthor.10aManejo del paciente en la emergencia :bpatología y cirugía de urgencia para emergencistas /cFernando Machado, Norberto Liñares, José Gorrasi, Daniel Terra. 1aMontevideo, República Oriental del Uruguay :bEditorial Cuadrado,c2020.  a313 pages :billustrations ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aEmergency Medical Services22aAcute Care Surgery22aEmergency Medicinexmethods1 aLiñares, Norberto,eauthor.1 aGorrasi, José,eauthor.1 aTerra, Daniel,eauthor.  aAUTH01280cam a22003257a 101640625DNLM20260210155805.0890621s1970    dcua         f000 0 eng    aPPCbengcPPCdWAUdOCLCQdDNLM  anlmcopyc  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1842 no.100 aHE 20.2402:Se 200aSedatives :