aSocial Aspects of HIV/AIDS Research Alliance.2 aSouth African Medical Association (1998- ).bHealth and Medical Publishing Group.2 aHuman Sciences Research Council.0 x1813-44244 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=1012262124 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/loi/rsah20  aAUTH01747cgm a22004454a 101518955DNLM20260227144833.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u091105s2009    mdu073|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15335  a(OCoLC)496226689  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4751 aJirtle, Randy L.10aEpigenetics, nutrition, and disease susceptibility /cRandy L. Jirtle.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCI stars in nutrition and cancer lecture  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 13 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 29, 2009.3 a(CIT): NCI Stars in Nutrition and Cancer Lecture  Meeting Objectives: .        To learn about epigenetics; .        To learn about genetic imprinting;  .        To learn about the role that epigenetics and imprinting play in the etiology of human health and disease.      For more information go to: http://prevention.cancer.gov/programs-resources/groups/ns.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aEpigenesis, Genetic22aDisease Susceptibility22aNutritional Physiological Phenomena 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.) 0aNCI stars in nutrition lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=7781  aAUTH03333cgm a2200421 i 101769623DNLM20260227160101.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u200715s2020    mdu064|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:29141  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 168.5.C800aStructural studies of coronavirus fusion glycoproteins /cDavid Veesler, Ph.D., University of Washington. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 4 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH COVID-19 SIG lecture series  aRecorded May 27, 2020.3 a(CIT):  SARS-CoV-2 is a newly emerged coronavirus responsible for the current COVID-19 pandemic. Coronavirus spike (S) glycoprotein trimers promote the virus"s entry into cells and are the main targets of the humoral immune response. We demonstrated that Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE2) is a functional entry receptor for this novel coronavirus and that the receptor-binding domains of SARS-CoV-2 S and SARS-CoV S bind with similar affinities to ACE2, which correlates with the efficient spread of SARS-CoV-2 among humans. We used cryo-electron microscopy to determine the structures of the SARS-CoV-2 S ectodomain trimer, demonstrating spontaneous opening of the receptor-binding domain, and providing a blueprint for the design of vaccines and inhibitors of viral entry. SARS-CoV S murine polyclonal sera potently inhibited SARS-CoV-2 S-mediated entry into target cells, indicating that vaccination can elicit cross-neutralizing antibodies targeting conserved S epitopes. We subsequently isolated a monoclonal antibody (termed S309) from the memory B cells of an individual who recovered from SARS-CoV in 2003 and showed that S309 potently neutralizes SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV pseudoviruses as well as authentic SARS-CoV-2. Using cryo-electron microscopy and binding assays, we show that S309 recognizes a receptor-binding domain--glycan-containing epitope, which is conserved within the sarbecovirus subgenus--without competing with receptor attachment. Antibody cocktails including S309 further enhanced SARS-CoV-2 neutralization and may limit the emergence of neutralization-escape mutants. These results pave the way for using S309- and S309-containing antibody cocktails for prophylaxis in individuals at high risk of exposure or as a post-exposure therapy to limit or treat severe disease.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed July 28, 2020).12aCoronaviridae22aSpike Glycoprotein, Coronavirusxphysiology22aPeptidyl-Dipeptidase Axphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aVeesler, David,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bCOVID-19 Scientific Interest Group,esponsoring body. 0aNIH COVID-19 SIG lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?29141  aAUTH01582cgm a22004334a 101268258DNLM20260227144853.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu074 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11584  a(OCoLC)1019833458  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 26.500aBiological databases :binformation retrieval.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCurrent topics in genome analysis  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 9, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 14 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 7, 2000.3 a(CIT): David Landsman, Ph.D., (NCBI, NIH), Molecular Biologist and Computational Molecular Biologist, Senior Research Scientist.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aComputational Biology22aDatabases as Topic22aInformation Storage and Retrieval 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aCurrent topics in genome analysis40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=462  aAUTH02401cgm a22004214a 101519010DNLM20260227153158.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u091105s2009    mdu060|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15401  a(OCoLC)501272950  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWB 8801 aSternberg, Esther M.10aNon-invasive methods for measuring stress and immune biomarkers to monitor CAM interventions :bdevelopment and challenges /cEsther M. Sternberg.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 0 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 2, 2009.3 a(CIT): A major challenge to conducting rigorous studies to evaluate the efficacy and mechanisms of CAM interventions is the need for sensitive methods for measurement and detection of physiological and molecular outcome measures, which can be applied to subjects with minimal stress and distress. In this lecture, Dr. Sternberg will describe the development of a method to measure neural and immune biomarkers in sweat as one potential approach, and will also touch on other methods, such as heart rate variability, which can be applied under selected conditions to achieve these goals. Case studies using these approaches will be described.  The National Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine presents the Complementary and Integrative Medicine Consult Service lecture series. The series provides NIH Clinical Center staff with the opportunity to learn more about how the integration of various complementary and alternative medicine treatments can affect approaches into the research and practice of medicine.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMind-Body Therapies22aBiomarkers22aStress, Physiologicalximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15401  aAUTH02753cgm a22004814a 101518963DNLM20260227153130.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u091105s2009    mdu160|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15343  a(OCoLC)608617419  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.55.H91 aWertheimer, Alan.10aRecruitment, undue influence and coercion & clinical research with pregnant women /cAlan Wertheimer and Maggie Little.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aClinical Center ethics grand rounds ;v2009/session 3  aTitle from screen banner.  aStreaming video (2 hr., 40 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 7, 2009.3 a(CIT): Ethical and Regulatory Aspects of Clinical Research  Department of Clinical Bioethics  This course is designed to provide a historical context for research regulations and to help researchers and others working in human subject research gain insights and skills into the development of research protocols and their ethical implementation.   Objectives of the Course:   To learn the codes, declarations, and other documents that govern the ethical conduct of human subject research; review the critical elements of informed consent and their implementation in actual informed consent documents for clinical research; explore controversial issues relating to human subject research, including Phase I research, randomization, children in research, international research, etc; review the purpose if IRBs and provide IRB-like experience in reviewing research protocols; understand the experience of human subjects who have participated in research protocols.   Recruitment, Undue influence and Coercion Alan Wertheimer, Ph.D. NIH Clinical Center Dept of Bioethics For more information, visit  http://www.bioethics.nih.gov.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHuman Experimentationxethics12aPregnant People.22aClinical Trials as Topicxethics22aCoercion22aPatient Selectionxethics22aResearch Subjects 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center.bDepartment of Bioethics. 0aClinical Center ethics grand rounds ;v2009/session 3.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15343  aAUTH02951cgm a22004214a 101493080DNLM20260227152817.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090106s2008    mdu072 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14819  a(OCoLC)304073322  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 55.71 aMacKinnon, Roderick,d1956-10aMolecular basis of K+ conduction and selectivity in potassium channels /cRod MacKinnon ; hosted by Neurobiology Interest Group.1 iTitle on screen banner:aIon channels and cell membranes  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Feb. 3, 2009)  aStreaming video (1 hr., 12 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 8, 2008.3 a(CIT): Electrical signals play many roles in the body. They control the pace of the heart, regulate the secretion of hormones into the bloodstream and transfer information from one cell to the next in the nervous system. Dr. MacKinnon's research is aimed at understanding the physical and chemical principles underlying electricity in biology, particularly the passage of inorganic ions, such as potassium and chloride, across cell membranes.  They have used mutational analysis to show that potassium channels are tetramers of identical subunits and that specific signature sequence amino acids are responsible for potassium selectivity. His lab's most recent advance was the elucidation of the atomic structure of a K+ channel from mammalian nerve cells. This first example of a eukaryotic membrane protein structure through recombinant expression opens a new era in the study of membrane proteins from complex organisms.   (http://www.rockefeller.edu/research/abstract.php?id=132)  Dr. MacKinnon received his B.A. in biochemistry from Brandeis University and his M.D. from Tufts University School of Medicine. He completed medical residency at Beth Israel Hospital, Harvard Medical School, and postdoctoral work at Brandeis. He joined the faculty at Harvard Medical School before moving to Rockefeller in 1996. Dr. MacKinnon is a member of the National Academy of Sciences. He is the recipient of numerous scientific awards, including the 2003 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.  NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aPotassium Channelsxchemistry12aPotassium Channelsxmetabolism 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bNeurobiology Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14819  aAUTH01522cam a22004214a 101477791DNLM20260204173122.0080630m20089999gw a     b    101 0 eng    a9783899674156 [v. 1]  a3899674154 [v. 1]  a9783899676396 (v. 2)  a9783899679311 (v. 3)  a9783958532922 (v. 4)  a(OCoLC)248342486  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9Germany00aWO 690bO682 200800aOrgan transplantation :bethical, legal and psychosocial aspects /cW. Weimar, M.A. Bos, J.J. Busschbach (eds.).1 iGlobal challenges :aethical, legal and psychosocial aspects of transplantation  aLengerich ;aMiami :bPabst,c2008-  av. :bill.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.1 a[v. 1]. Towards a common European policy -- v. 2. Expanding the European  platform -- v. 3. Global issues, local solutions -- v. 4. Global challenges.12aOrgan Transplantationxethics22aHealth Policy22aOrgan Transplantationxlegislation & jurisprudence22aOrgan Transplantationxpsychology 2aEurope 2aConference Proceedings1 aWeimar, W.q(Willem)1 aBos, M. A.q(Michael A.)1 aBusschbach, J. J.q(Jan J.)  aAUTH01460cgm a22004214a 101268245DNLM20260227150244.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu095 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11571  a(OCoLC)70217872  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 551 aHogue, Christopher.10aBioinformatics and molecular assembly /cChristoph