helle, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.,c[2014]-  a1 online resource  aQuarterly  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with April 25, 2014.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aNumbering from Table of Contents page.  aCompanion journal to: Thyroid.  aDescription based on: April 25, 2014; title from journal home page (Liebert website, viewed May 1, 2014).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 3, issue 1 (March 2016) (viewed May 10, 2016).12aEndocrine System Diseases 2aPeriodical 2aWebcast2 aAmerican Thyroid Association,eissuing body.08iComplemented by (work):tThyroidx1050-7256w(DLC)sn 90003628w(OCoLC)2157933540uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/ve  aAUTH03126cgm a22004574a 101473084DNLM20260227152503.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080502s2008    mdu049|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14429  a(OCoLC)269432283  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2061 aMeneses, Karen Dow.10aChallenges and explorations in cancer survivorship research /cKaren Dow Meneses.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCCR grand rounds  aTitle from title screen (viewed Sept. 30, 2008).  aStreaming video (49 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS and NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Apr. 15, 2008.3 a(CIT): Center for Cancer Research - National Cancer Institute Grand Rounds    Oncology Nursing Lecture   Dr. Meneses is a Professor and Associate Dean for Research in the School of Nursing at the University of Alabama Birmingham (UAB), and Senior Scientist in the UAB Comprehensive Cancer Center.  She received her doctorate and master's degree in nursing from Boston College and her baccalaureate degree in nursing from Georgetown University.  Dr. Meneses has over 30 years of experience in the care of patients with cancer.  She has devoted her program of research into improving the quality of life of cancer survivors.  She is currently funded by the National Cancer Institute for her research "Rural Breast Cancer Survivor Intervention." Dr. Meneses has written over 100 publications on topics related to breast cancer, quality of life, survivorship, fertility issues, cancer disparities, radiation oncology, and the application of technologies in cancer education. She is the editor of three textbooks:  Nursing Care of Women with Cancer, Contemporary Issues in Breast Cancer, and Nursing Care in Radiation Oncology.  Dr. Meneses is a member of the Oncology Nursing Society, Sigma Theta Tau International, and is a fellow in the American Academy of Nursing.  In 2006, Dr. Meneses was appointed to the National Cancer Advisory Board (NCAB).    The primary educational objective of these seminars is to provide new information, ideas, and discussion about timely areas of research with impact on the field of oncology.  A secondary educational objective is to elicit participation by individuals from all divisions of the intramural NCI, and thus facilitate more interactions among investigators and groups in the NCI.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomedical Research12aNeoplasms22aSurvivors 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14429  aAUTH02334cgm a2200445 a 101581477DNLM20260227154215.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120331s2012    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17154  a(OCoLC)794257287  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWW 2701 aRieke, Fred.10aNoise correlations and neural coding /cFred Rieke.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aRecorded Mar. 12, 2012.3 a(CIT): Neuroscience Seminar Series  Research in the Rieke lab focuses on sensory signal processing, particularly in cases where sensory systems perform at or near the limits imposed by physics. Photon counting in the visual system is a beautiful example. At its peak sensitivity, the performance of the visual system is limited more by the division of light into discrete photons than by biological noise or inefficiencies. This observation guides the lab's investigation of the underlying mechanisms. Some of the current questions in the lab:  . What are the limits to visual sensitivity imposed by noise in the rod and cone photoreceptors? How close does the retina come to realizing these limits?  . How does the retinal circuitry read out the photoreceptor signals? Are there specializations for signals from rod and cone photoreceptors?  How does the retina adapt and maintain sensitivity when the mean or contrast of the light inputs change?  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aRetinal Rod Photoreceptor Cellsxphysiology22aModels, Neurological22aNeural Pathwaysxphysiology22aSensory Thresholdsxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17154  aAUTH02048cas a2200577 a 101140017DNLM20260227090915.0cr|cn ||||||||020131c20029999enk x p       0    0eng    a  2002260061  a1499-2027l1499-2027  a(OCoLC)48909035  aLAScLASdORUdVA@dSBHdMXCdDNLM0 aengbspa  alcdanlmcopyc  9England14aRF286b.I5800aW1bIN7655J10aInt J Audiol2dnlm00aInternational journal of audiology.  aHamilton, Ont. :bBC Decker,cc2002-3 aLondon :bInforma Healthcare  av. :bill. ;c28 cm.  aEight no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 41, no. 1 (Jan. 2002)-  aImprint varies.  