online resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner.  aStreaming video (50 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 24, 2008.3 a(CIT): The first and only kid to receive an NIH "grant" will share his story at the 2008 Take Your Child to Work Day.  When Terence Boylan was 9 years old in 1957, he sent a request to NIH for $10 to build a better rocket ship.  Against all odds, Terence got a little NIH "grant."  His story will show how wonderful things happen when we invest in researchers and kids with big ideas and dreams.  For more information, visit  http://cms.csr.nih.gov/AboutCSR/CSR-NIHHistory.htm.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBoylan, Terence.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bDivision of Research Grants12aResearch Support as Topic22aAircraft22aChild22aFinancing, Organized 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aPersonal Narrative 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14453  aAUTH01517cgm a2200421 i 101678420DNLM20260227155355.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160303s2016    mdu098|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19506  a(OCoLC)945749784  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 57500aDemystifying medicine 2016.pShingles (Herpes zoster) revisited /cJeffery Cohen and Lesia Dropulic.1 aShingles (Herpes zoster) revisited 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 38 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aCohen's first name misspelled on title screen.  aRecorded February 23, 2016.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 20, 2016).12aHerpes Zoster22aHerpesvirus 3, Humanxpathogenicity 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCohen, Jeffreyq(Jeffrey I.),espeaker.1 aDropulic, Lesia,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19506  aAUTH01526cgm a22004454a 101267593DNLM20260227145351.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu061 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10421  a(OCoLC)64588903  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 1101 aGerberding, Julie.10a21st century health threats :bthe new normal /cJulie Louise Gerberding.3 aTwenty-first century health threats  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aKinyoun lecture  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 2, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 1 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 24, 2003.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aCommunicable Diseases, Emerging22aCommunicable Disease Control22aGlobal Health 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aKinyoun lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10421  aAUTH03490cgm a2200481 i 9918716283706676DNLM20260227164219.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u231128s2023    mdu589|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33506  a(DNLM)CIT:33511  a(OCoLC)1467156799  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 1052 aAdvancing Health Equity Through Culture-Centered Dietary Interventions to Address Chronic Diseases (Workshop)d(2023 :cOnline)jauthor.10aAdvancing health equity through culture-Centered dietary interventions to address chronic diseases virtual workshop /cNIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (9 hr., 49 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 28 & 29, 2023.3 a(CIT): Goals and Key Audience: Objectives: 1. Identify gaps and opportunities for research on the cultural adaptation of evidence-based dietary approaches (e.g., DASH and Mediterranean Diet) to address diet-related diseases in racial/ethnic populations; 2. Understand dietary acculturation and its influence on the development and effectiveness of dietary interventions among diverse immigrant populations; 3. Interrogate the influence of dietary acculturation on the gut microbiome, and understand gut microbiome response to traditional and evidence-based diets. Need and Justification: Adherence to evidence-based dietary approaches, such as the Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension (DASH) diet and Mediterranean diet, is associated with a multitude of health benefits, including lowered blood pressure, improved lipid profiles, reduced cardiovascular disease (CVD) incidence, and decreased risk of type 2 diabetes.1-5 Also, adherence to healthy dietary patterns is associated with decrease risk for several cancers and cancer mortality. 6, 7 Yet, the strength of these associations varies by race and ethnicity, though results have been inconsistent; and there are racial and ethnic differences in adherence to these dietary patterns.8-14 These data underscore the notion of diet as a cultural element that is influenced by, among other things, food access, environmental factors (e.g., climate change that may adversely affect certain subgroups of the population), belief systems, history, racial/ethnic identity, and migration. Culture-centered approaches are critical for improving adherence to healthy dietary patterns, and therefore diet-related diseases across racial and ethnic groups.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 1, 2024).12aDietxadverse effects22aChronic Disease22aHealth Promotion22aRacial Groups22aHealth Inequities2