AUTH02633cgm a2200421 i 101655803DNLM20260227154936.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150409s2015    mdu072|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18872  a(OCoLC)908629357  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 3071 aFinlay, Barbara L.q(Barbara Laverne),d1950-eauthor.10aEvo-devo insight into the functional organization of the cerebral cortex /cBarbara Finlay. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 12 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aRecorded March 2, 2015.3 a(CIT): Dr. Finlay lab takes an approach to the structure and function of the vertebrate nervous system, particularly the visual system and the cerebral cortex, that is both developmental and evolutionary. What "units" of variation do brain and behavior offer up to natural selection? Is structural change in brains over evolution piecemeal, related to specific adaptive function, or does the brain change as a coordinated unit? They have found that brains change in a highly constrained coordinated fashion as they enlarge, which can be related to a highly conserved sequence of structural development. Late-generated structures become disproportionately large, in an extremely predictable fashion. This strengthens the case for viewing the cerebral cortex as a general-purpose learning device rather than a collection of specially-adapted mechanisms, and they are interested in the window this gives us on the nature of human cognition. But, brains also differ, and present research is beginning the investigation of how the brain variations associated with particular niches, like nocturnal versus diurnal, are produced by changes in neural development.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed May 5, 2015).12aCerebral Cortexxgrowth & development22aBiological Evolution 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18872  aAUTH01806cgm a2200505 a 101546237DNLM20260227153728.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101027s2010    mdu115|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16145  a(OCoLC)688679715  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2021 aHunter, Kent W.10aTRACO.pMetastasis and health disparities /cK. Hunter and S. Ambs.1 aMetastasis and health disparities1 aTranslational Research in Clinical Oncology1 iTitle from PowerPoint screen:aSystematic genetics of metastatic susceptibility1 iTitle from PowerPoint screen:aProfiling of tumors to identify factors that contribute to cancer health disparities  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 55 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 27, 2010.3 a(CIT): For more information, visit  http://ccr.cancer.gov/careers/traco.asp.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasm Metastasis22aBlack or African American.22aWhite People.22aGenetic Predisposition to Disease22aHealth Status Disparities22aNeoplasmsxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16145  aAUTH01636cgm a22004334a 101268482DNLM20260227150733.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu122 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12078  a(OCoLC)84646353  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 3201 aDuggan, Anneq(Anne L. K.)10aNIH Child Abuse and Neglect Working Group annual public meeting and seminar :bchild maltreatment prevention /cAnne Duggan, John Landsverk and David Olds.30aChild maltreatment prevention  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 5, 2006).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 1 min., 57 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 17, 2004.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aChild Abusexprevention & control22aBehavioral Research22aCommunity Health Services22aProgram Evaluation 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bChild Abuse and Neglect Working Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12078  aAUTH02315cgm a2200469 i 9918573266806676DNLM20260227162935.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2022    mdu391|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32770  a(OCoLC)1417460814  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 5032 aHIV DRP Symposium Honoring Stephen Hughes' Careerd(2022 :cFrederick, Md.)jauthor.10aHIV DRP symposium honoring Stephen Hughes' career /cHIV Dynamics and Replication Program, NCI, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (6 hr., 31 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 19, 2022.3 a(CIT): In recognition of his upcoming retirement, we hosted a symposium to celebrate Dr. Stephen Hughes' illustrious career. The Symposium was held in person at the NCI-Frederick Conference Center as well as virtually. The scientific component of the symposium included talks by the following invited speakers: Eddy Arnold, Mike Bishop, Mario Capecchi, John Coffin, Wei-Shau Hu, Mary Kearney, Jeff Lifson, Dmitry Lyumkis, Bob Siliciano, Ron Swanstrom, Harold Varmus, and Peter Vogt, Jeff Strathern, Nita Maihle, and Larry Arthur also said a few words about Steve's scientific and non-scientific accomplishments.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed January 16, 2024).12aHughes, Stephen H.12aHIV Infectionsxvirology12aResearch Personnel22aHIV-1xgenetics22aHIV Reverse Transcriptase22aHIV Integrase Inhibitors 2aConference Proceedings 2aFestschrift 2aWebcast2 aHIV Dynamics and Replication Program (U.S.),ehost institution.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=48559  aAUTH01766cgmaa22004694a 101249747DNLM20260227144643.0vf|cbahouvf|cjahoucr|cna||||||||050608s2005    mdu057       f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:3458  a(OCoLC)60825532  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aQU 47500aMaking ends meet :bdouble-strand break repair in human cells /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  aUnited States.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (57 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (57 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture  aSpeaker, Stephen C. West.  aPresented May 18, 2005.  aAlso available online.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aDNA Repair22aGenomic Instability22aNeoplasmsxgenetics22aBRCA2 Proteinxgenetics22aDNA-Binding Proteins22aHolliday Junction Resolvases1 aWest, S. C.q(Stephen C.)2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3458  aAUTH02940cgm a2200433 a 101574502DNLM20260227154415.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u111208s2011    mdu666 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16999  a(DNLM)CIT:17001  a(OCoLC)770841455  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 39000aHealth outcomes among children and families living in rural communities /cEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (11 hr., 5 min., 30 sec.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded Dec. 1-2, 2011.3 a(CIT): This trans-agency conference brings together a panel of community members and experts from NIH Institutes and Centers, federal agencies, and organizations to identify gaps in research that address emerging differences between health outcomes for children and families living in rural communities compared to those in urban areas.  Access to care and services remains a critical issue for improving the health of individuals who live in rural and underserved areas. A growing body of evidence supports the concept that a rural place of residence is associated with poor health outcomes and risky health behaviors. Further, multiple reports (e.g., Rural Healthy People 2010) have documented several unmet health needs of children living in rural communities. These children are reportedly less physically active and have higher rates of tobacco use, overweight/obesity, and dental caries than their urban counterparts. Further, differences in health behavior and outcomes can be exacerbated by poverty and low educational attainment within families living in rural areas.  There is a need for collaborative research to fully understand the impact of all these health indicators on the long-term health of children living in rural areas. Additional research is also needed to further understand the barriers and challenges associated with the use of technological advances (e.g., telemedicine, telepsychiatry) to improve the quality-of-life of those living in rural areas.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aRural Health22aChild Welfare22aHealth Status Disparities 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1699940uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17001  aAUTH01281cas a2200421   0374606DNLM20260217092450.0731113d19602018fr  x p       0||| 0fre    a0428-2779l0428-2779  aFBIOA  a(DNLM)F04840000(s)  a(OCoLC)09362759  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9France00aW1bFE85210aFeuill Biol2dnlm00aFeuillets de biologie.  aParis.3 aParis :bEditions Orion  av.  aSix no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-        juin 1960-1 aCeased with n° 345 (novembre 2018).1 aRépertoire médical pratique.  Série biologie0 aMEDLINEbSelected citations only0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aIssued by the Comité intersyndical des biologistes français. 2aBiology 2aMedicine 2aPeriodical2 aComité intersyndical des biologistes français.00tRevue de biologie médicalex2826-6242 0aRépertoire médical pratique.  Série biologie.  aAUTH01145nam a2200337 i 101778277DNLM20260227101103.0980616s1852    mau           000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)82642076  aPPAmPbengcAOWdOCLCQdOCLCOdOCLCFdOCLCQerdadDNLM  an-us-ma  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5571 no.131 aBoston (Mass.).bCity Council,eauthor.10aForeign paupers. 1a[Boston] :b[publisher not identified],c1852.  a4 pages ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aCity document ;vno. 30  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM12aEmigrants and Immigrants22aPoverty22aSocial Welfare 2aBoston1 aBoston (Mass.).tDocument ;v1852, 30  aAUTH05199cgm a22004334a 101465446DNLM20260227152328.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080201s2008    mdu062|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14256  a(OCoLC)213007412  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2021 aPrives, Carol.10aRegulation of p53 in mammalian cells /cCarol Prives.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 29, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 2 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 23, 2008.3 a(CIT): The focus of much of the work in Dr. Prives' laboratory is the functions of p53 and its negative regulator and transcriptional target, Mdm2. Two general areas are being investigated, how p53 functions as a transcription factor and the roles and regulation of Mdm2. P53 is a sequence-specific transcriptional activator of genes involved in cellular outcomes such as cell cycle arrest and apoptosis.  We have identified a new co-regulator of select p53 targets, Cas/Cse1L, a protein previously shown to be involved in nuclear transport.  Cas/Cse1L binds to the promoters of a subset of p53 targets and levels of Cas/Cse1L are correlated with the extent to which these genes are induced by p53. Atne p53 target promoter, PIG3, Cas/Cse1L has a long range inhibitory effect on tri-methylation of histone H3 lysine 27, a repressive modification, which could explain how Cas/Cse1L and p53 cooperate to regulate some p53 targets and apoptosis. Another project stems from our earlier finding that in some cells p53 is impaired in its ability to induce transcription from some of its target genes when cells are blocked in S phase. We have extended these findings by showing that while p53 can bind to its sites within these promoters and recruit histone acetylases, TFIID and RNA polymerase, transcription elongation by RNA polymerase is significantly reduced when the DNA replication checkpoint is activated. Our data implicate the checkpoint kinases in this block to elongation of p53 targets.  Regarding our studies on Mdm2, an extensive yeast two-hybrid screen led to the identification of RPS7, a ribosomal protein that can interact with and regulate Mdm2.  Ribosomal stress leads to the appearance of RPS7 in the nucleoplasm and RPS7 inhibits Mdm2 E3 ligase activity in vivo and in vitro. Our data also suggest that the Mdm2 homologue, MdmX, is involved in RPS7 repression of Mdm2.  Further, ablation of RPS7 causes both increased turnover of Mdm2 and decreased p53 after treatment of cells with several genotoxic agents. Our data thus implicate ribosomal and nucleolar stress as being very important for DNA damage signaling to p53. Interestingly as well, RPS7 itself is a substrate of the Mdm2 E3 ligase activity suggesting a new circuit whereby a negative regulator of Mdm2 is itself an Mdm2 target.  Dr. Prives was born in Montreal, Canada and received her BSc degree in 1962 and Ph.D. in 1966 at McGill University. After spending a year at the University of British Columbia, she did post-doctoral research at Albert Einstein College of Medicine, Bronx, N.Y. in the laboratory of Dr. J.T. August from 1968 through 1971 where she held a Damon Runyon Fellowship. In 1971 she worked in the laboratory of Dr. E. Ehrenfeld.  She then went to the Weizmann Institute on a senior visiting fellowship where she worked on a project jointly sponsored by Professors Ernest Winocour and Michel Revel. In 1974 she was appointed as intermediate scientist, in 1975 she was promoted to the rank of senior scientist (equivalent to Assistant Professor in American universities) and in 1977 she was promoted to Associate Professor with tenure at the Weizmann Institute. She left Israel for a sabbatical in 1978 to work in the laboratory of Dr. George Khoury at the NIH. In 1979 she took a position as Associate Professor at the Department of Biological Sciences at Columbia University. She was awarded tenure at Columbia in 1982 and was promoted to Full Professor in 1987. In 1995 she was appointed the Da Costa Professor of Biology at Columbia. In 1996 she was the recipient of a MERIT award from the NIH and in 1998 she was awarded a Research Professorship from the American Cancer Society. In 2000 she became a member of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences and in 2005 she was elected to the Institute of Medicine.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aTumor Suppressor Protein p53xphysiology22aNeoplasmsxetiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14256  aAUTH01533cgm a2200433 a 101578513DNLM20260227154104.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120215s2012    mdu087       fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17066  a(OCoLC)789835717  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.52 aNHLBI DIR Orloff Science Awardsn(1st :d2012 :cBethesda, Md.)10aOrloff Science Awards 2012 /cNHLBI.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 27 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Jan. 20, 2012.3 a(CIT): The Division of Intramural Research will provide service awards for excellence in research and adminstrative services on January 20, 2012.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.22aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute.bDivision of Intramural Research12aBiomedical Research22aAwards and Prizes22aHeart Diseases22aHematologic Diseases22aLung Diseases 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17066  aAUTH02529cgm a2200433 i 9918226875006676DNLM20260227161309.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210615s2021    mdu494|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31758  a(OCoLC)1296115405  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 4602 aNIH & FDA Glycosciences Research Day (Meeting)n(14th :d2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNIH & FDA glycoscience research day /cGlycobiology Scientific Interest Group, NIH and FDA.1 aNIH & FDA glycoscience research day 20211 aNIH and FDA glycoscience research day 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (8 hr., 14 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 1, 2021.3 a(CIT): The NIH Glycobiology Interest Group (GBIG), with support from the NIH Office of Intramural Research, the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) as well as NCI, NIGMS, and NIDDK will hold its fourteenth annual NIH &amp; FDA-wide Glycoscience Research Day on June 1, 2021 (virtually).  This event will explore a broad range of aspects of the glycosciences; promote communication and interaction among intramural laboratories, as well as with researchers from local universities; and facilitate collaboration. The meeting will include morning and afternoon platform sessions, an abstract competition, and virtual discussion. The Glycobiology Interest Group Steering Committee is composed of scientific staff members from NIH Institutes (NIGMS, NCI, NIDDK, NICHD, NHGRI, NHLBI, NIAID, NIDCR), the FDA, NIST and DOE, who have interest in carbohydrates. The Steering Committee plans and organizes SIG activities including this annual meeting  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed January 13, 2021).12aGlycomics22aLectins22aPolysaccharides 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bGlycobiology Scientific Interest Group,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41964  aAUTH01975cas a2200493 i 101775320DNLM20260227090908.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||190410c20199999ne || poo     0    0eng    a  2021229568  aGBB1979482bnb  a1100520110a1117878667a1125766522a1136168851a1156768405a1162221082a1165736760a1229749872  a2590-0285l2590-0285  a(OCoLC)1096281414  z(OCoLC)1100520110z(OCoLC)1117878667z(OCoLC)1125766522z(OCoLC)1136168851z(OCoLC)1156768405z(OCoLC)1162221082z(OCoLC)1165736760z(OCoLC)1229749872  aVVLbengerdad20260227cVVLdOCLCFdLVTdOH1dOCLCOdVT2dOCLCAdSFBdSRUdCNCGMdUABdVLYdBWNdDNLM  apcc  9Netherlands04aQP552.C600aW110aMatrix Biol Plus2dnlm00aMatrix biology plus. 1a[Amsterdam] :bElsevier B.V.,c[2019]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1 (February 2019).0 aPubMedbv1, Feb. 2019-0 aPMC  aSome special theme volumes issued in combined format.  aCompanion journal to Matrix biology.  aDescription based on: Volume 1 (February 2019); title from journal information screen (ScienceDirect, viewed February 3, 2021).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 8 (November 2020) (viewed February 3, 2021).12aCollagen22aExtracellular Matrix22aConnective Tissue 2aPeriodical08iComplemented by (work):tMatrix biologyx1569-1802w(DLC)sn 94043255w(OCoLC)4304752540uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/matrix-biology-plus40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101775320  aAUTH01199cam a22002771  45000010009000000030005000090050017000140070014000310080041000450350020000860400021001060440016001270600023001431000026001662450245001922600040004373000021004773360026004983370028005243380027005525000060005795010093006395330110007325610070008429990009009122524050RDNLM20260219150858.0hd|bfa012baap840915s1772    sz            000|0 fre    a(OCoLC)14334739  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Switzerland00aWZ 260bS927e 17721 aStruve, Otto Wilhelm.10aEssais ou réflexions intéressantes rélatives a la chymie, la médecine, l'économie et le commerce,bavec une dissertation sur la question: si les causes des maladies de l'ame & des nerfs ont toujours leur siege dans le cerveau? ...  aLausanne,bFranç. Grasset,c1772.  a223 p. ;c18 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aNLM microfilm copy shelved as: Film 71-29.1 no. 15DNLM  aNLM copy, bound with copy2 of: Bouvart, M. P.  Examen d'un livre.  Genève, 1758.5DNLM  aMicrofilm.b[Bethesda, Md.] :cNational Library of Medicine,d1971.e1 microfilm reel : negative ; 35 mm.  aNLM copy, bookplate of Pierre Charles Marchant (died 1842).5DNLM  aAUTH01139cam a22002651  450000100100000000300050001000500170001500800410003201000170007304000210009004400180011106000230012910000360015224500850018826000990027330000240037233600260039633700280042233800270045050001630047754001420064058300390078258300430082199900090086411720450RDNLM20260206132942.0810725s1926    tnu||||  |||| 00| 0|eng    a   26014070   aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aQW 800bP373p 19261 aPeebles, Isaac Lockhart,d1855-14aThe present state of tests, serums, and vaccines.cBy Isaac Lockhart Peebles ...  aNashville, Tenn.,bPrinted for the author, Publishing house of the M. E. church, South,c1926.  a34 p.bill., ports.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"This work was written to awaken the people to the fact that tests for certain diseases are dangerous and useless, and also the folly of vaccines and serums."  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202602062pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202602065DNLM  aAUTH02984cgm a2200481 i 101778098DNLM20260227160920.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210408s2021    mdu311 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31593  a(DNLM)CIT:31594  a(OCoLC)1291166012  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWY 20.52 aNational Nursing Research Roundtabled(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNursing research of the future :busing clinical big data to explore health inequities and social determinants of health. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (5 hr., 11 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aKeynote session by Sandro Galea on March 4, 2021.  aRecorded March 4 & 5, 2021.3 a(CIT): The mission of the National Nursing Research Roundtable (NNRR) is to serve the public's health through a strong research-based nursing practice. To this end, the purpose of the meeting is to bring together individuals representing member organizations -- nurse scientists, practitioners, and educators -- to discuss and disseminate nursing research findings to strengthen nursing practice and improve client outcomes. For practice to benefit from nursing research, findings must be disseminated to, and utilized by, practitioners and educators. During this one-day meeting NNRR members will: Share information on the importance of redoubling efforts to better understand how big data can be used to document healthcare inequities Understand how big data including Electronic Health Records (EHR) can be mined to inform interventions to reduce health inequities Develop strategies constituent organizations might implement to promote research that considers both social needs and determinants of health in order to reduce health inequities Provide a unique opportunity to share and exchange information among the leaders of nursing organizations with a strong research focus.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 23, 2021).12aNursing Researchxtrends22aElectronic Health Records22aHealth Inequities22aSocial Determinants of Health 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aGalea, Sandro,espeaker.2 aNational Institute of Nursing Research (U.S.),ehost institution.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4143240uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41442  aAUTH01403cam a2200361 i 60921020RDNLM20260206222200.0cr||||||||||||820514s1909    nyu           001 0 eng    a(OCoLC)593255  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aQLbD419t 19091 aDepéret, Charles,d1854-1929,eauthor.10aTransformations du monde animal.lEnglish.14aThe transformations of the animal world /cby Charles Depéret. 1aNew York :bD. Appleton and Company,c1909.  axvi, 360 pages, 3 unnumbered folded leaves of plates ;c19 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aInternational scientific series (New York, N.Y.)  aIncludes indexes.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20250627lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aPaleontology22aBiological Evolution2 aD. Appleton and Company,epublisher. 0aInternational scientific series (New York, N.Y.)4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/60921020R  aAUTH01374cas a2200445 a 101494210DNLM20260227090911.0cr|cn#||||||||080827c20099999nyumr p       0    0eng    a  20082128530 a1945-0265l1945-0257  a(OCoLC)244486920  aNSDcNSDdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW1bGE285510aGenet Test Mol Biomarkers2dnlm00aGenetic testing and molecular biomarkers.  aNew Rochelle, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.