cDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWO 2751 aBrown, E. N.q(Emery N.)12aA look at the unconscious brain under general anesthesia /cEmery Brown.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture  aTitle from title screen (viewed Apr. 21, 2011).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 10 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 2, 2011.3 a(CIT): General anesthesia is a drug-induced, reversible condition comprised of five behavioral and physiological states: unconsciousness, amnesia (loss of memory), analgesia (loss of pain sensation), akinesia (immobility), and cardiovascular, respiratory and thermoregulatory stability with control of the stress response. The mechanisms by which anesthetic drugs induce the state of general anesthesia is considered one of the biggest mysteries of modern medicine. We have been using three experimental paradigms to study general anesthesia-induced loss of consciousness in humans: combined fMRI/EEG recordings, high-density EEG recordings and intracranial recordings. These studies are allowing us to establish precise neurophysiological, neuroanatomical and behavioral correlates of general anesthesia. We will discuss the relation between our findings and two other important altered states of arousal: sleep and coma. Our findings suggest that the state of general anesthesia is not as mysterious as currently believed.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aAnesthesia, General12aConsciousnessxdrug effects 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16502  aAUTH01964cas a2200577 a 9012854DNLM20260227091022.0cr|cn ||||||||900830c19899999qucqr p  s    0    0eng    a   90641403 zce 90079047  a0840-6529l0840-6529  a(DNLM)SR0067514