tutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aRedox biology seminar series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Jan. 6, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 31 min., 57 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 27, 2007.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxdiagnosis22aInterferon-gamma22aMagnetic Resonance Imaging22aOxidation-Reduction22aPositron-Emission Tomography22aTomography, X-Ray Computed 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aRedox biology seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14173  aAUTH01470cgm a22004334a 101306044DNLM20260227151913.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070430s2007    mdu058 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13657  a(OCoLC)191875381  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 26.500aData warehouse nVision reporting /cNIH.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 6, 2008).  aStreaming video (58 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS and NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Feb. 28, 2007.3 a(CIT): A special Data Warehouse nVision reporting meeting.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aManagement Information Systems22aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration22aCommercexorganization & administration22aData Collectionxmethods 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13657  aAUTH04605cgm a2200457 i 9918450787306676DNLM20260227162548.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220804s2022    mdu067|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32494  a(OCoLC)1344417374  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWI 10200aAdvancing research on emotional well-being and regulation of eating /cElissa Epel. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 7 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aIntegrative medicine research lecture series  aRecorded June 23, 2022.3 a(CIT): Given the rising levels of global stress, worsened by the COVID-19 pandemic, loneliness and mental health problems are on the rise, adding to the burden of chronic diseases. Most health-oriented research takes a harm-reduction approach, i.e., identifying and mitigating problems to reduce disease burden. Understanding and promoting emotional well-being (EWB) may yield another important strategy to accomplish this and significantly improve people"s health. Little has been known about how to best increase EWB in ways that also improve health.Dr. Epel is principal investigator of a new research network to develop resources and a multidisciplinary community of scholars focused on researching EWB--its links to physical health, interventions that could best increase it, and the processes that explain how EWB impacts health. In addition to this work, Dr. Epel will discuss how stress and compulsive eating are interrelated, a longtime research focus for her. She and her team have developed interventions, including mindfulness-based training modules, as adjuvant components of treatment. Dr. Epel will review findings and lessons learned from clinical trials, the lab, and the field.Biosketch:At the University of California, San Francisco (UCSF), Dr. Elissa Epel is professor and vice chair in the Department of Psychiatry; director of the Aging, Metabolism, and Emotions Center and the Consortium for Obesity Assessment, Study, and Treatment (COAST); and associate director of the Center for Health and Community. For the past 25 years, her research has focused on understanding how psychosocial stress influences health behaviors and biological indices of aging (e.g., inflammation, telomeres, and telomerase). Her other research interests include the long-term effects of adverse states on chronic disease; cellular aging; interconnections between stress, addiction, eating, and metabolic health; and the reduction of health disparities.Dr. Epel received her Ph.D. in clinical and health psychology from Yale University and her B.A. from Stanford University. She completed a clinical internship at the Veterans Affairs Palo Alto Health Care System, followed by a postdoctoral fellowship in psychology and medicine at UCSF. She is past president of the Academy of Behavioral Medicine Research, a member of the National Academy of Medicine, and co-chair of the steering council of the Mind &amp; Life Institute. The NIH funders of Dr. Epel"s research include NCCIH; the National Institute on Aging; the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute; and the National Institute of Mental Health. Her publications include a bestselling book, <i>The Telomere Effect</i>, coauthored with Dr. Elizabeth Blackburn.About the Series:This lecture is part of the spring 2022 season of NCCIH"s Integrative Medicine Research Lecture Series, which provides overviews of the current state of research and practice involving complementary health approaches and explores perspectives on integrative health.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed September 9, 2022).12aAppetite Regulation12aFeeding Behaviorxpsychology22aStress, Psychologicalxcomplications22aMental Health22aEmotions 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aEpel, Elissa,espeaker.2 aNational Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNCCIH integrative medicine research lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45257  aAUTH01334cam a2200325 i 9919051237406676DNLM20260206222456.0cr||||||||||||250616s1910    p