2rdacarrier  aAlternate pages blank.  aErrata on final leaf.  aNLM microfilm copy shelved as: Film 11432 no. 45DNLM  aBooks of reference for answers to practical and suggestive questions: pages 13-14.  aMicrofilm.b[Bethesda, Md.] :cNational Library of Medicine,d2001.e1 microfilm reel : negative ; 35 mm.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc202507032pda5DNLM  aWill conservec202507032pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aPhysiology22aAnatomy22aHygiene4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/61320240R  aAUTH04163cam a2200469 i 101162568DNLM20260213172755.0m     q  d f      cr|cnu||||||||021218s2002    txuac    b   f100 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdad20260110dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWB 480  a000285252 aInternational Conference on Near-Field Optical Analysisn(2nd :d2001 :cHouston, Tex.)jauthor.10aSecond International Conference on Near-Field Optical Analysis :bphotodynamic therapy & photobiology effects :  proceedings of the Second International Conference on NOA, Houston, Texas, USA, May 31-June 1, 2001 /csponsored by Johnson Space Center Biological Systems Office and the Cellular Biotechnology Program, Biological Warfare Defense, Advanced Consequence Management Program, DARPA Defense Sciences Office ; edited by Debra L. Bulgher 1aHouston, Texas :bNational Aeronautics and Space Administration, Johnson Space Center,cOctober 2002.  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (x, 91 pages)) :billustrations, portrait.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNASA conference publication ;v210786  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 aThe International NASA/DARPA Photobiology Conference held at the Johnson Space Center in Houston/TX demonstrated where low level laser therapy (LLLT), respectively low intensity light activated biostimulation (LILAB) and nanotechnological applications employing photobiomodulation techniques will presumably go in the next ten years. The conference was a continuation of the 1st International Conference on Nearfield Optical Analysis organized by Andrei Sommer (ENSOMA Lab, University of Ulm, Germany) in November 2000 at Castle Reisensburg, Germany, which started with a group of ten scientists from eight different countries. The 2nd conference was co-sponsored by DARPA, NASA-JSC and the Medical College of Wisconsin. Despite the short time between events, the 2nd conference hosted 40 international experts from universities, research institutes, agencies and the industry. The materials published here are expected to become milestones forming a novel platform in biomedical photobiology. The multidisciplinary group of researchers focused on LLLT/LILAB-applications under extreme conditions expected to have beneficial effects particularly in space, on submarines, and under severe battlefield conditions. The group also focused on novel technologies with possibilities allowing investigating the interaction of light with biological systems, molecular mechanisms of wound healing, bone regeneration, nerve regeneration, pain modulation, as well as biomineralization and biofilm formation processes induced by nanobacteria. The proceedings of the 2nd NOA, together with the proceedings of the previous meeting held in Germany (Journal of Clinical Laser Medicine & Surgery, 2001, 19, 109-112) demonstrated the synergistic interplay and complementary output of a coherent approach to interrelated scientific questions of excessive clinical relevance, offering results beyond any previous expectation. The 3rd NOA conference is scheduled for 2002, in Brazil.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed September 18, 2020).12aCell Physiological Phenomenaxradiation effects12aLow-Level Light Therapy22aPhotic Stimulation22aPhotobiology22aWound Healingxradiation effects 2aUnited States 2aResearch Support, U.S. Gov't, Non-P.H.S. 2aConference Proceedings1 aBulgher, Debra L.,eauthor.1 aUnited States.bDefense Advanced Research Projects Agency,esponsoring body.2 aLyndon B. Johnson Space Center,esponsoring body. 0aNASA conference publication ;v210786.40uhttps://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20030001592  aNASRELb20030116  aAUTH01045camaa22002897a 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002100073040002100094044001800115060003300133245002400166260005300190300003000243336002600273337002800299338002700327540014200354541003400496561008400530583003900614583004300653650003200696655001800728999000900746101282859DNLM20260204141909.0060720s1900    mou           000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)181358920  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1285 no.1400aPurity in bromides.  aSt. Louis, Mo. :bPeacock Chemical Co.,c[1900?]  a[16] p. :bill. ;c17 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  cGift;aJane B. Lossing.5DNLM  aNLM copy provenance: gift of Jane B. Lossing in memory of Ann Smith, R.N.5DNLM  aWill digitizec202012032pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202012035DNLM12aPharmaceutical Preparations 2aAdvertisement  aAUTH01861cgm a22004694a 101478233DNLM20260227152533.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080702s2008    mdu057 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14536  a(OCoLC)240988375  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 26.55.I41 aKrensky, Alan M.10aNIH preview of the RCDC public webinar /cAlan Krensky and Tim Hays.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed July 23, 2008).  aStreaming 