ide microbiome workshop will strive to cover advances that reveal the specific ways in which the microbiota influences the physiology of the host, both in a healthy and in a diseased state and how the microbiota may be manipulated, either at the community, population, organismal or molecular level, to maintain and/or improve the health of the host. The goal will be to seek input from a trans-disciplinary group of scientists to identify 1. knowledge gaps, 2. technical hurdles, 3. new approaches and 4. research opportunities that will inform the development of novel prevention and treatment strategies based on host/microbiome interactions over the next ten years.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed September 13, 2017).12aMicrobiotaxphysiology22aBiomedical Researchxtrends22aGenetic Therapy22aHost-Pathogen Interactions22aMicrobiotaxgenetics 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?2341940uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?2342340uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23424  aAUTH01426cas a2200457   0372370DNLM20260227090922.0cr|cn#||||||||hd|bfa012baap731018c19479999cauar         0    0eng    a   49000432   a0066-4227l0066-4227  aARMIAZ  a(DNLM)A54150000(s)  a(OCoLC)01325421  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  ansdpalc  9United States00aW1bAN77810aAnnu Rev Microbiol2dnlm00aAnnual review of microbiology.  aPalo Alto, CA :bAnnual Reviews  av.billus.  aAnnual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-        1947-1 aMEDLINEbv19, 1965-0 aPubMedbv19, 1965-0 aOLDMEDLINE1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aMicrofilm.mv. 1 (1947)-10 (1956).b[Bethesda, Md.] :cNational Library of Medicine,d1993.e3 microfilm reels : negative ; 35 mm.  3Vol. 31 (OCTOBER 1977)c19890816fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper that meets the full ANSI standard for Permanent Paper 2aBacteriology 2aMicrobiology0 x1545-32514 uhttp://www.annualreviews.org/loi/micro  aAUTH02354cam a22003974a 101248959DNLM20260204164439.0m        d        cr|cn|||||||||050527s2004    ph      s     100 0 eng    a(OCoLC)61121044  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9Philippines10aQU 14500aWorkshop report :bdevelopment and implementation of intersectoral food and nutrition plans and policies, 15-19th November, 2004, CETC, Suca, Fiji.30aDevelopment and implementation of intersectoral food and nutrition plans and policies  a[Manila :bWorld Health Organization, Regional Office of the Western Pacific Region,c2004?]  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from PDF t.p. (viewed July 9, 2005).3 aThis workshop included participants from 5 member countries: Cook Islands, Fiji Islands, Samoa, Tuvalu and Vanuatu, representing a range of sectors including health, education, finance, planning and  agriculture. The workshop was organized by the Lifestyle Health Section of SPC, with funding support from WHO and JICA, and with collaborative assistance from FAO and UNICEF. During the four and a half day workshop, issues related to the successful development and implementation of national plans of action and policies on nutrition were explored. This included: the historical basis and needs for national plans of action on nutrition, essential components of plans and policies, using emerging issues such as NCDs to promote nutrition plans, developing successful partnerships and advocating for support  and action.  aMode of access: Internet.12aNutrition Policy22aCooperative Behavior22aFederal Government22aHealth Planningxmethods22aPlanning Techniques 2aPacific Islands 2aConference Proceedings2 aSecretariat of the Pacific Community.bLifestyle Health Section.2 aWorld Health Organization.bRegional Office for the Weste