arrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Aug. 6, 2014.3 a(CIT): This presentation will provide an overarching national perspective and regional snapshots on climate change, key health findings from the National Climate Assessment, and best practices to build health resilience in communities.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed Sept. 10, 2014.12aClimate Change22aConservation of Natural Resources22aHealth Policy22aHealth Status 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aUnited States.bOffice of the Assistant Secretary for Health,esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18546  aAUTH02677cgm a2200433 i 101655833DNLM20260227154956.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150409s2015    mdu075|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18910  a(OCoLC)908629413  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWB 1001 aGupta, Sanjay,d1969-eauthor,einterviewee.10aMedicine and the media :ba morning with Sanjay Gupta, M.D. /cSanjay Gupta. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 15 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded March 25, 2015.3 a(CIT): Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series  J. Edward Rall Cultural Lecture   Dr. Sanjay Gupta is an Emmy Award winning journalist and chief medical correspondent for CNN. He appears on several television shows on medicine and health news such as American Morning, Anderson Cooper 360, and many CNN documentaries.   He is a successful practicing neurosurgeon and a member of the American College of Surgeons, the American Association of Neurological Surgeons, and the Congress of Neurological Surgeons. He is a diplomat of the American Board of Neurosurgery and is board certified. From 1997 to 1998, Dr. Gupta was a White House Fellow, one of 15 chosen in the country, serving primarily as an advisor to the First Lady Hillary Rodham Clinton and President Bill Clinton.   He has received numerous humanitarian awards and recognitions for outstanding journalism from several professional societies in medicine. Over the last decade, he has covered every major conflict around the world, including multiple assignments in Iraq, Afghanistan, and the Middle East. In Iraq, he was embedded with the U.S. Navy"s elite medical team, the Devil Docs.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 28, 2015).12aMedicine22aMass Media 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pNIH director's cultural lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18910  aAUTH01696cas a2200517 a 9810948DNLM20260227091046.0cr|cnu||||||||980820c19989999ts  x p       0    0eng    asn 98039363   a1386-2073l1386-2073  a(DNLM)SR0093087(s)  a(OCoLC)39728399  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9United Arab Emirates00aW1bCO35410aComb Chem High Throughput Screen2dnlm00aCombinatorial chemistry & high throughput screening.3 aCombinatorial chemistry and high throughput screening 1aHilversum, Netherlands ;aMiami, FL :bBentham Science Publishers,cc1998-31aSaif Zone, Sharjah, U.A.E. :bBentham Science Publishers  av. :bill., ports.  aTen no. a year,b2006-  aQuarterly,b1998  aBimonthly,b1999-2000  aEight no. a year,b2001-2005  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Apr. 1998)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, Apr. 1998-0 aPubMedbv1n1, Apr. 1998-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 21, no. 4 (2018).02aChemistry, Pharmaceuticalxmethods02aDrug Design02aMolecular Sequence Data02aStructure-Activity