Vol. 1, issue 1 (Mar. 1996)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv9n1, Jan. 2004-0 aPubMedbv9n1, Jan. 2004-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  3v.1, no. 1 (Mar. 1996)c20041101fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper that meets the full ANSI standard for Permanent Paper 2aFunctional Laterality 2aPeriodical0 x1464-06784 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/loi/plat20#.VdymTmflvGi  aAUTH00768cam a22002297i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003500073044001000108060003300118100003900151245009900190250001100289264008500300300002600385336002600411337002800437338002700465650003700492999000900529101626198DNLM20260210112455.0140219s1955    cc c          000 0 chi    aDNLMbengerdad20260210dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1834 no.111 aFu, Lianzhang,d1894-1968eauthor.10aJie he bing de liao yang :b(bing ren yong fang lao xuan jiao bin zhi yi) /cFu Lianzhang zhu.  a3 ban. 1aBeijing :bZhongguo fang lao xie hui zong hui,c1955 nian 12 yue [December 1955]  a13 pages :bportrait.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier12aTuberculosis, Pulmonaryxtherapy  aAUTH02851cgm a2200445 i 101774298DNLM20260227160515.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu434 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31417  a(DNLM)CIT:31424  a(OCoLC)1246807852  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 2742 aNIAAA 50th Anniversary Symposiumd(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNIAAA 50th Anniversary Symposium /cNational Institute on Alcohol Abuse and Alcoholism, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (7 hr., 14 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 30 & December 1, 2020.3 a(CIT): Join us for the NIAAA 50th Anniversary Science Symposium: Alcohol Across the Lifespan: 50 Years of Evidence-Based Diagnosis, Prevention, and Treatment Research, hosted by NIAAA Director Dr. George F. Koob. Day 1: Dr. George F. Koob, Welcome and Overview, Alcohol as a Coping Response: Hyperkatifeia, Deaths of Despair, and COVID-19; Dr. Katherine Keyes, Age, Period, and Cohort Effects in Alcohol Use in the 20th and 21st Century: Implications for the Decades to Come; Dr. Marc Schuckit, AUD Risk, Diagnoses and Course in a 35-year Prospective Study Across Two Generations of 453 Families: Implications for Prevention; Dr. Susan Tapert, Alcohol and the Adolescent Brain: What We've Learned and Where the Data Are Taking Us. Day 2: Dr. George F. Koob, Welcome and Overview; Dr. Rajita Sinha, Alcohol's Dark Side: The Role of Stress Neurobiology in Risk of and Recovery from Alcohol Use Disorder; Dr. Barbara Mason, Looking Back, Looking Ahead: Current and Future Medications for the Treatment of Alcohol Use Disorder; Dr. Michael Charness, Fetal Alcohol Spectrum Disorders: Awareness to Insight in Just 50 Years; Dr. Vijay Shah, Hope through Discovery--Alcohol Associated Liver Disease; Dr. George F. Koob, Conclusion.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 21, 2021).22aNational Institute on Alcohol Abuse and Alcoholism (U.S.)12aAlcoholism22aAlcohol Drinking 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings1 aKoob, George F.,ehost.2 aNational Institute on Alcohol Abuse and Alcoholism (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3897840uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38979  aAUTH00824cam a22002657i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002100073040003500094044001000129060003300139100003600172245005100208250001400259264007700273300003100350336002600381337002800407338002700435650002700462650004300489655001700532999000900549101626202DNLM20260210112619.0140219s1959    cc a          000 0 chi    a(OCoLC)908239801  aDNLMbengerdad20260210dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1834 no.101 aLi, Peng,dactive 1959eauthor.10aMei qi zhong du /cLi Peng, Yang Zuo bian zhu.  aDi 1 ban. 1aShanghai :bKe ji wei sheng chu ban she,c1959 nian 1 yue [January 1959]  a16 pages :billustrations.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier12aNatural Gasxpoisoning22aAir Pollution, Indoorxadverse effects 2aPopular Work  aAUTH03309cgm a2200589 i 101774279DNLM20260227160432.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu062 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31389  a(OCoLC)1236084440  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWZ 11200aMedal winners :bhow the Vietnam war launched Nobel careers /cRaymond S. Greenberg. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH history lecture  aRecorded November 12, 2020.3 a(CIT): NIH History Lecture As the ground war in Vietnam escalated in the late 1960s, the US government leveraged the so-called doctor draft to secure adequate numbers of medical personnel in the armed forces. Among newly minted physicians' few alternatives to military service was the Clinical Associate Training Program at the National Institutes of Health. Though only a small percentage of applicants were accepted, the elite program launched an unprecedented number of remarkable scientific careers that would revolutionize medicine at the end of the twentieth century. Medal Winners recounts this overlooked chapter and unforeseen byproduct of the Vietnam War through the lives of four former NIH clinical associates who would go on to become Nobel laureates. Raymond S. Greenberg traces their stories from their pre-NIH years and apprenticeships through their subsequent Nobel Prize-winning work, which transformed treatment of heart disease, cancer, and other diseases. Greenberg shows how the Vietnam draft unintentionally ushered in a golden era of research by bringing talented young physicians under the tutelage of leading scientists and offers a lesson in what it may take to replicate such a towering center of scientific innovation as the NIH in the 1960s and 1970s.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed February 2, 2021).12aGoldstein, Joseph L.q(Joseph Leonard)12aBrown, Michael S.,d1941-12aLefkowitz, Robert J.,d1943-12aVarmus, Harold.12aUnited States.tSelective Service Extension Act of 195022aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aPhysicians22aNobel Prize22aBiomedical Research22aGovernment Agencies22aFederal Government22aHistory, 20th Century22aHistory, 21st Century 2aUnited States 2aBiography 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aGreenberg, Raymond S.,espeaker.1 iSummary of (work):aGreenberg, Raymond S.tMedal winners.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aONHM biomedical history lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38976  aAUTH01417cas a2200457 a 101144384DNLM20260227090916.0cr|cn ||||||||020403c20029999pauqr p       0   a0eng    a  2002243227  a1542-3050l1542-3050y0022-3409  a(OCoLC)49508609  aSBIcSBIdXIIdNLMdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States 4aBV4000b.J600aW1bJO82810aJ Pastoral Care Counsel2dnlm04aThe journal of pastoral care & counseling :bJPCC.3 aJournal of pastoral care and counseling30aJPCC  aKutztown, PA :bJournal of Pastoral Care Publications,cc2002-3 32015- :aThousand Oaks, CAbSAGE Publications  av. ;c23 cm.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 56, no. 1 (spring 2002)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv56n1, spring 2002-0 aPubMedbv56n1, spring 2002-  aAlso issued online.12aPastoral Care22aCounseling 2aP