icine and surgery00aJournal of avian medicine and surgery.  aBoca Raton, FL :bAssociation of Avian Veterinarians,cc1995-  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 9, issue 1 (Mar. 1995)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv21n1, Mar. 2007-1 aPubMedbv21n1, Mar. 2007-  aAlso issued online.  aOfficial publication of the Association of Avian Veterinarians.22aAssociation of Avian Veterinarians. 2aBird Diseases 2aBirds 2aBirdsxinjuries 2aVeterinary Medicine 2aPeriodical2 aAssociation of Avian Veterinarians.00tJournal of the Association of Avian Veterinariansx1044-8314w(DLC)sn 89008291w(OCoLC)199008834 uhttp://www.bioone.org/loi/avms  aAUTH00805cam a22002657i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002100073040003500094044001000129060003300139245008200172264006000254300001300314336002600327337002800353338002700381500002200408650002100430650001800451650002400469710003700493999000900530101616866DNLM20260209150149.0130911s1956    cc            000 0 chi    a(OCoLC)876335909  aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1804 no.1800aBao hu fu nü he er tong de jian kang /cShanghai Shi wei sheng ju bian yin. 1a[Shanghai] :bShanghai Shi we sheng ju bian yin,c1956.  a13 pages  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aTitle from cover.12aMaternal Welfare12aChild Welfare22aReproductive Health1 aShanghai (China).bWei sheng ju.  aAUTH02383cgm a2200457 i 101775936DNLM20260227160708.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2021    mdu176|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31496  a(OCoLC)1245468913  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWS 12500aBreastmilk Ecology: Genesis of Infant Nutrition (BEGIN) meeting series.nWorking group 3,pInfant inputs /cNICHD, NIH.1 aInfant inputs 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 56 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded January 22, 2021.3 a(CIT): A deeper understanding of the biology of human milk is essential to address ongoing and emerging questions about infant feeding practices. Human milk is a complex biological system, a matrix of many interacting parts, that is more than the sum of those parts. Its production needs to be studied as an ecology that consists of inputs from the mother, her breastfeeding baby, and their respective environments. The Breastmilk Ecology: Genesis of Infant Nutrition (BEGIN) meeting series is designed to examine this ecology, its functional implications for both mother and infant. The goal is to support the research community"s efforts to ensure safe, efficacious, and context-specific infant feeding practices in the United States and globally.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 1, 2021).22aBEGIN (Collaborative Group)12aMilk, Humanxphysiology22aBreast Feeding22aMother-Child Relations22aInfant Nutritional Physiological Phenomena22aInfant Health22aEcological and Environmental Phenomena 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40151  aAUTH01423cgm a22003974a 101267529DNLM20260227145223.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu074 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10269  a(OCoLC)64588898  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 5031 aAnderson, Roy M.14aThe evolution of drug resistance in viral and bacterial populations within the treated patient and spread within the community /cRoy Anderson.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 3, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 14 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 19, 2001.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aHIV Infections22aDrug Resistance22aModels, Theoretical 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10269  aAUTH03111cgm a2200433 i 101776652DNLM20260227160248.0m     o  z f      vz|czaz|ucr|cna||||||||210304s2020    mdu001 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:37432  a(OCoLC)1248768066  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 50600aAnimal models for COVID-19 :ba critical component of the response to the pandemic /cEmmie de Wit. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (72 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH COVID-19 SIG lecture series  aRecorded May 6, 20203 a(CIT): We are learning more about different aspects of COVID-19 disease manifestations on an almost daily basis. Although data on disease in humans are emerging at a steady pace, certain aspects of the pathogenesis of SARS-CoV-2 can only be studied in detail in animal models where repeated sampling and tissue collection is possible. Non-human primate models that recapitulate aspects of human disease are essential for our understanding of the pathogenic processes involved in severe respiratory disease and for the development of medical countermeasures such as vaccines and antivirals. We have developed a rhesus macaque model that recapitulates COVID-19 with regard to virus replication and shedding, the presence of pulmonary infiltrates, histological lesions, and seroconversion. These data allow us to bridge between the rhesus macaques model and the disease observed in humans and to use this animal model to assess the efficacy of medical countermeasures. While a large number of investigational, approved, and repurposed drugs have been suggested for the treatment of COVID-19, preclinical data from animal models can guide a more focused search for effective treatments in humans by ruling out treatments that have no proven efficacy in vivo. Remdesivir (GS-5734) is a nucleotide analog prodrug with broad antiviral activity, including against coronaviruses, that is currently being investigated in COVID-19 clinical trials worldwide. The therapeutic efficacy of remdesivir was tested in our rhesus macaque model of COVID-19. Remdesivir treatment initiated early during infection had a clear clinical benefit in SARS-CoV-2-infected rhesus macaques.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 20, 2021).12aCOVID-1922aModels, Animal22aPandemics 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aDe Wit, Emmie,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH COVID-19 SIG lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=37432  aAUTH00978cam a22002897i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002100073040003500094044001000129060003200139111009000171245007800261264007600339300003400415336002600449337002800475338002700503500002200530650004300552650002500595650002200620651001000642655002700652999000900679101617860DNLM20260213174029.0130925s1971    cc a          100 0 chi    a(OCoLC)874092741  aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1805 no.42 aQuan guo Zhong cao yao xin yi liao fa zhan lan huid(1970 :cBeijing, China)jauthor.10aQuan guo zhong cao yao xin yi liao fa zhan lan hui  :bzi liao xuan bian. 1aBeijing :b[Quan guo zhong cao yao xin yi liao fa zhan lan hui],c1971.  a6, 144 pages :billustrations  atext