loi/jzoo  aAUTH01051cam a22003017i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002100073040003500094041001300129044001000142060003200152245018900184250001400373264005500387300003000442336002600472336003300498337002800531338002700559650001800586650001600604650004700620655001900667710005400686999000900740101608857DNLM20260209104158.0130522s1958    cc a          000 0 chi    a(OCoLC)894626205  aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM1 achihrom  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1773 no.400aYu er hua ce /cLuo ma ni ya ren min gong he guo, Yue Ke lang ge huan deng pian zhi pian chang, Di Si te li hou bian zhu ; Zhong hua ren min gong he guo dui wai wen hua lian luo ju yi.  aDi 1 ban. 1aShanghai :bShanghai wei sheng chu ban she,c1958.  a63 pages :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  astill imagebsti2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier12aChild Rearing22aInfant Care22aInfant Nutritional Physiological Phenomena 2aPictorial Work1 aChina.bDui wai wen hua lian luo ju,etranslator.  aAUTH03594cgm a2200433 i 101738453DNLM20260227155858.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u181205s2018    mdu064|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:26182  a(OCoLC)1080640074  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 28400aOpioids :bepidemic of our time and impact on infectious disease /cRobert R. Redfield. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2018]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 4 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aKinyoun lecture ;v2018  aRecorded November 13, 2018.3 a(CIT): Robert R. Redfield, M.D., director of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), will deliver the 2018 Joseph J. Kinyoun Memorial Lecture on the intersection between the national opioid crisis and the management of infectious diseases.   Titled, "Opioids: Epidemic of Our Time and Impact on Infectious Disease," Dr. Redfield"s talk will explore the impacts of the unprecedented use of opioids in the United States on the management of infectious diseases. While overdose remains the leading cause of death among people who use opioids, this population is also disproportionately affected by viral hepatitis, bacterial endocarditis, HIV and other infections associated with sharing and reusing needles to inject drugs and other behaviors linked to illicit drug use.  The opioid epidemic presents substantial challenges to controlling infectious disease transmission. In the United States, new hepatitis C infections increased by 223 percent from 2010 to 2016, and one in 10 new HIV infections occurs in people who inject drugs. Dr. Redfield will discuss the roles that federal public health partners including CDC and NIH can play in addressing this crisis.  Dr. Redfield became the 18th CDC director and the administrator of the Agency for Toxic Substances and Disease Registry in March 2018, capping more than three decades of leadership in public health, research and clinical care with a focus in virology. After receiving his medical degree from Georgetown University in 1977, Dr. Redfield rose to the rank of Colonel at Walter Reed Army Medical Center, where he led some of the earliest clinical studies enrolling people with AIDS. He later became a co-founder of the University of Maryland"s Institute of Human Virology.  The annual Kinyoun Lecture commemorates Dr. Joseph J. Kinyoun who, in 1887, founded the Laboratory of Hygiene, the institution that later would become NIH. Since 1979, the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases has invited distinguished guests to present their work in the fields of infectious diseases and immunology for this lectureship.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 28, 2018).12aOpioid-Related Disordersxepidemiology22aHIV Infectionsxetiology 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aRedfield, Robert R.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aKinyoun lecture ;v201840uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?26182  aAUTH01918cgmaa22004694a 101207886DNLM20260227144422.0vf|czahmuvd|cvaizuvf|cjahouhd|bfa012baap040225s2004    mdu060 ||    f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:2613  a(OCoLC)1019840702  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 503bVC no.44 200400aImmune control of HIV :bvirus variability and vaccine design /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health],c2004.  a1 videocassette of 1 (DVCPRO) (60 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/4 in.3master.  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (53 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videodisc of 1 (DVD) (60 min.) :bsd., col. ;c4 3/4 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aThe NIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture  aTitle from cassette spine label.  aSpeaker, Andrew J. McMichael.  aPresented February 4, 2004.  aVideocassette (Betacam SP)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2011.e1 videocassette (53 min.) : sd., col. ; 1/2 in.  aVHS.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aHIV Infectionsximmunology22aAIDS Vaccines1 aMcMichael, Andrew J.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=2613  aAUTH01614cgm a22004214a 101294778DNLM20260227151448.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061206s2005    mdu077|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12843  a(OCoLC)84646421  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 5151 aHenderson, David,d1947-10aAvian influenza :bpreparing for the pandemic /cDavid Henderson.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 20, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 17 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 19, 2005.3 a(CIT): NIH Clinical Center's 2005 Medicine for the Public lecture series  Avian influenza, or bird flu, is a major concern for public health authorities and is an increasing threat to the public health. This lecture will cover what it is, how it spreads, and where we can look for possible treatment and prevention.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aInfluenza A Virus, H1N1 Subtype12aInfluenza, Human22aDisease Outbreaks 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12843  aAUTH01456cgm a22004214a 101267895DNLM20260227145838.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu068 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10908  a(OCoLC)68207234  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWV 2501 aDallos, Peter.10aMolecular basis of cochlear amplification by outer hair cells /cPeter Dallos.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 19, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 7 min., 24 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 30, 2000.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aHair Cells, Auditory, Outerxphysiology22aCell Movement22aCochleaxcytology22aMechanotransduction, Cellular 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10908  aAUTH01353cgm