0227145631.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu053 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10645  a(OCoLC)64588919  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 3501 aCampo, Maria Luisa,d1957-14aThe ins and outs of mitochondrial protein import /cMaria Luisa Campo.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 2, 2006).  aStreaming video (53 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 8, 2002.3 a(CIT): The Mitochondria Interest Group (MIG) is an Inter-Institute Interest Group at the National Institutes of Health (NIH), with members from NIH and from around the world! MIG is concerned with all aspects of the mitochondrion and diseases in which the mitochondrion is involved.   For more information, visit the  Mitochondria Interest Group (MIG).  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aMitochondrial Proteins22aProtein Transportxphysiology 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10645  aAUTH01915cgm a22004454a 101267893DNLM20260227145756.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu070 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10906  a(OCoLC)68207232  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQT 1671 aReppert, Steven M.10aCoordination of circadian timing in mammals /cSteven Reppert.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 18, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 9 min., 13 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 28, 2002.3 a(CIT): NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series  A master circadian clock resides in the suprachiasmatic nuclei (SCN) of the anterior hypothalamus of mammals.  Recent discoveries in this and other laboratories have identified a molecular framework for the core clock mechanism in the SCN.  The core mechanism involves interacting positive and negative transcriptional/post-translational feedback loops.   For more information, visit  Steven Reppert.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aCircadian Rhythm22aBiological Clocks22aFeedback, Physiological.22aSuprachiasmatic Nucleus 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10906  aAUTH01290cas a22003854a 101184656DNLM20260210145235.0cr|cn#||||||||030711cuuuu9999it uu         0    0ita    a  2003243662  a(OCoLC)53801282  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aitaaengafre  alcd  9Italy00aW1bCO453L0 aComprendre (Padua, Italy)10aComprendre :barchive international pour l'anthropologie et la psychopathologie phénoménologiques.  aPadova :bLa Garangola  av.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aDescription based on: 12, published in 2002.  aPrint issues ceased with no. 18 (2008).  aAlso issued online.  aChiefly in Italian with some articles in English and French.  a"Organo ufficiale della Società italiana per la psicopatologia."12aPsychopathology 2aPeriodical2 aSocietà italiana per la psicopatologia.0 tPsichiatria generale e dell' età evolutivax0555-5299w(OCoLC)503714004 uhttps://rivistacomprendre.org/  aAUTH02094cas a2200553 a 101502440DNLM20260227090918.0cr||||||||||||751101c19429999ii qr p       0   a0eng    a   87654173 zsn 86013180   a0369-8211l0369-8211  aPAIBA6  a(OCoLC)1759020  aDLCcMULdCOOdHULdSERdOCLdAIPdAGLdDLCdNSTdDLCdHULdNSTdAGLdNSTdOCLCQdDNLM  ansdpapcc  9India00aQ73b.P7600aW1bPR586AD0 aQ73b.N31110aProc Natl Acad Sci India Sect B Biol Sci2dnlm 0aProceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, India. Section B: Biological sciences00aProceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, India.pSection B.1 iIssues for <1942-> have section title:aBiological sciences30aSection B  aAllahabad [India] :bCouncil of the National Academy of Sciences, India,c1942-313<Mar. 2012-> :a[New Delhi?] :bSpringer  av. :bill. ;c24 cm.  aQuarterly,b<2012->  aAnnual,b1960-  aQuarterly,b1942-1959  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 12, pt. 4 (Nov. 1942)-  aSection B contains articles relating specifically to the biological sciences.2 aChemical abstractsx0009-2258  aSince 1960, each vol. issued in 4 pts.  aSome issues include supplements.  aAlso issued online.  aTitle from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 86, issue 1 (Jan/Mar. 2014).12aBiological Science Disciplines 2aPeriodical2 aNational Academy of Sciences, India.0 x2250-174601tProceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, Indiax0369-3236w(DLC)   51025296w(OCoLC)75331024 uhttp://www.springer.com/life+sciences/journal/40011  aAUTH02971cgm a2200421 i 101607220DNLM20260227154911.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130427s2013    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17876  a(OCoLC)857718929  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWT 1041 aFinkel, Toren,eauthor.10aMolecular mechanisms regulating mammalian aging /cDr. Toren Finkel ; Geroscience Interest Group (GSIG). 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.) :bcolor, sound)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdm2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Mar. 28, 2013.3 a(CIT): The Geroscience Interest Group (GSIG) cordially invites you to the seminar listed above. Dr. Finkel is Chief of the Center for Molecular Medicine in the Division of Intramural Research at the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute. Dr. Finkel's lab investigates the role of cellular metabolism and oxidative stress in aging and age-related diseases. His research has included studies on oxidative homeostasis in stem cell biology, cellular senescence as a model for aging, the role of autophagy in age-related diseases, and interrogating pathways in model organisms to understand their role in mammalian aging. Dr. Finkel is Editor-in-Chief of Drug Discovery Today-Disease Mechanisms and Associate Editor of Circulation Research.  Publications relevant to his seminar:  Narayan, N., Lee, I.H., Borenstein, R., Sun, J., Wong, R., Tong, G., Fergusson, M.M., Liu, J., Rovira, I.I., Cheng, H-L., Wang, G., Gucek, M., Lombard, D., Alt, F.W., Sack, M.N., Murphy, E., Cao, L., and Finkel, T. (2012). The NAD-dependent deacetylase SIRT2 is required for programmed necrosis. Nature, 492: 199-204  Finkel, T, Deng, C. and Mostoslavasky (2009) Recent progress in the biology and physiology of sirtuins. Nature, 460: 587-591.  The Geroscience Interest Group (GSIG) is a newly formed trans-NIH group aimed at enhancing opportunities for discussion of the intersection between the biology of aging and the biology of diseases and conditions that are of interest across ICs.   It is focused on basic biology, but with a longer view towards translation.  aDescription based on version viewed June 5, 2013.12aAgingxphysiology22aAgingxpathology22aAutophagyxphysiology22aOxidative Stressxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bGeroscience Interest Group,esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17876  aAUTH01430cam a2200397 i 02620460RDNLM20260206220357.0cr||||||||||||810622s1910    nyu      b    001 0 eng    a   10024199   a(OCoLC)2076711  aDNLMbengerda