undue influence and coercion-2010 /cDave Wendler and Alan Wertheimer.1 aFair subject selection & recruitment, undue influence, and coercion  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (2 hr., 52 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 13, 2010.3 a(CIT):  Course Objectives: By the end of this course, participants will be able to:  Utilize a systematic framework for evaluating the ethics of a clinical research protocol.  Apply appropriate codes, regulations, and other documents governing the ethical conduct of human subject research to their own research.  Discuss controversial issues relating to human subject research, including Phase 1 research, randomization, children in research, international research, etc.  Identify the critical elements of informed consent and strategies for implementing informed consent for clinical research.  Describe the purpose, function, and challenges of IRBs.   Appreciate the experience of human subjects who have participated in research protocols.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHuman Experimentationxethics22aCoercion22aEthics, Research22aPatient Selectionxethics22aResearch Subjects 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16194  aAUTH01071nam a2200361 i 9918957851606676DNLM20260223130830.0240503s2024    it       b    000 0 ita    a9788855451055  a8855451057  a(OCoLC)1458973105  aC3LbengerdacC3L  apcc  9Italy 4aRC00aWP 57014a6162141 aCarbone, Giorgio Maria,eauthor.10aSterilità e fecondazione in vitro :btra scienza, tecnica e etica /cGiorgio Maria Carbone. 1aBologna :bESD,c2024.  a207 pages ;c20 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aLe frecce ;v55  aIncludes bibliog