aBrain Injuries 2aSpinal Cord Injuries 2aPeriodical2 aNeurotrauma Society.0 x1557-904200tCentral nervous system traumax0737-5999w(DLC)sn 83007527w(OCoLC)094215604 uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/neu  aAUTH02816cgm a2200433 i 101622747DNLM20260227154603.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u131227s2013    mdu055|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18151  a(OCoLC)868259593  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 1681 aSaphire, Erica Ollmann,eauthor.14aThe structural basis of Ebola viral pathogenesis /cErica Ollmann Saphire. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (55 min.) :bcolor, sound).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Nov. 6, 2013.3 a(CIT): The Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series presents the NIH Director's Lecture   Dr. Saphire's lab studies viruses with compact genomes that encode just four to seven genes each. Viruses with limited genomes offer a defined landscape of possible protein-protein interactions. Each protein is critical-many are obligated to perform multiple functions and some rearrange their structures to achieve those new functions. As a result, these few polypeptides accomplish a surprisingly complex set of biological functions including immune evasion, receptor recognition, cell entry, transcription, translation, assembly and exit. Dr. Saphire systematically analyzes the structures and functions of each protein encodes by the virus to gain fundamental insights into the biology of entry, immune evasion, and assembly, and to decipher the collaborative roles of these proteins in pathogenesis. In this lecture, Dr. Saphire will illustrate the molecular function throughout the viral life cycle: how the Ebola virus glycoprotein remodels itself during viral entry and how this remodeling affects the antibody response; how the Ebola and Lassa viruses suppress host innate immune signaling; and how the Ebola matrix protein assembles into one structure to bud new virions and into a different conformation to bind RNA and control transcription inside infected cells.  aDescription based on version viewed January 2, 2014.12aEbolavirusxpathogenicity22aEbolavirusxgenetics22aGlycoproteinsxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18151  aAUTH00698cam a22002537i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003500073044001000108060003200118100002500150245004100175250001100216264005000227300001300277336002600290337002800316338002700344650001400371650002100385650002900406999000900435101616035DNLM20260209144914.0130828s1949    cc            000 0 chi    aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1804 no.31 aLiu, Benli,eauthor.10aYun fu bao yang fa /cLiu Benli zhu.  a3 ban. 1aShanghai :bJia za zhi she,cMinguo 38 [1949]  a82 pages  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier12aPregnancy12aMaternal Welfare22aMaternal-Fetal Relations  aAUTH01543cgm a22004694a 101289606DNLM20260227151357.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2006    mdu071  |||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13082  a(OCoLC)84671877  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWG 2801 aChien, Kenneth R.10aHeart like blood :bthe islet-1 cardiovascular progenitor story /cKenneth R. Chien.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH stem cell seminar series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 30, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 10 min., 1 sec. : sd., col.).  aSubti