Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12577  aAUTH01352cas a2200445 a 101231645DNLM20260227090925.0m        d        cr cn|||||||||040908c20049999enk x pss     0    0eng    a  2004243766  a1743-422Xl1743-422X  a(OCoLC)56433791  aOUNcOUNdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9England10aW110aVirol J2dnlm00aVirology journal.  a[London] :bBioMed Central,c2004-  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 a1:1 (26 Aug. 2004)-  aTitle from issue caption (publisher Web site, viewed Oct. 14, 2004).  aLatest issue consulted: 1:6 (23 Sept. 2004) (viewed Oct. 14, 2004).1 aMEDLINEbv1, 2004-0 aPubMedbv1, 2004-0 aPMC1 aIndex medicus  aArticles added consecutively to current annual v.  aAlso issued online.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.12aVirology 2aPeriodical2 aBioMed Central Ltd.40uhttps://virologyj.biomedcentral.com/40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101231645  aAUTH02220cgm a22004334a 101520512DNLM20260227153201.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u091202s2009    mdu055|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15425  a(OCoLC)706780666  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 203.51 aBearman, Peter S.,d1956-10aUnderstanding the increased prevalence of autism /cPeter S. Bearman.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBehavioral and Social Sciences Research lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (55 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 22, 2009.3 a(CIT): This lecture is an installment of the Behavioral and Social Sciences Research Lecture Series sponsored by the NIH Office of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research and organized by the NIH Behavioral and Social Sciences Research Coordinating Committee.  The Behavioral and Social Sciences Research Coordinating Committee (BSSR CC), with support from the Office of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research (OBSSR), convenes a series of guest lectures and symposia on selected topics in the behavioral and social sciences. These presentations by prominent behavioral and social scientists provide the NIH community with overviews of current research on topics of scientific and social interest. The lectures and symposia are approximately 50 minutes in length, with additional time for questions and discussion. All seminars are open to NIH staff and to the general public.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aAutistic Disorderxepidemiology 2aCalifornia 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aBSSR lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15425  aAUTH02569cgm a2200397 i 9918299481806676DNLM20260227161754.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u211110s2021    mdu339|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32046  a(OCoLC)1299139026  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWH 7002 aYet to Be Charted : Mapping the Lymphatic System across Body Scales and Expertise Domains (Workshop)d(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aYet to be charted :bmapping the lymphatic system across body scales and expertise domains /cNational Heart, Lung and Blood Institute, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (5 hr., 39 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 4, 2021.3 a(CIT): Virtual Workshop Sponsored by the NIH/NHLBI, Boston Lymphatic Symposium Pre-Conference. This workshop will bring together clinicians and basic science researchers to explore the challenges and opportunities toward developing an open-access knowledge base and map of the human lymphatic system. We will emphasize the relevance of the fundamental understanding of lymphatic system structure and function, and its coordination and interactions with other body tissues and organs, for improving the clinical management of lymphatic dysfunction and diseases.   International experts will review the state of current fundamental and clinical understanding and the remaining challenges, including presenting the latest advances in lymphatic imaging at the clinical level, and the use of novel single cell technologies to map the lymphatic organs, discoveries of lymphatic system biomarkers, and will engage the audience in a collective effort to leverage modern data management and computational approaches.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 7, 2022).12aLymphatic System 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute,esponsoring body.2 aBoston Lymphatic Symposiumn(4th :d2021 :cOnline)40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=43995  aAUTH01970cgm a2200481 i 9918332582006676DNLM20260227161801.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220104s2021    mdu500| |||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32064  a(DNLM)CIT:32065  a(OCoLC)1294916850  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQS 5252 aAdvancing Bioprinting and Regenerative Medicine Solutions for Obstetric, Gynecologic and Pediatric Applications (Workshop)d(2021 November :cOnline)jauthor.10aAdvancing bioprinting and regenerative medicine solutions for obstetric, gynecologic and pediatric applications /cNICHD, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (8 hr., 20 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 16 & 17, 2021.3 a(CIT): Conference Concept Proposal: Towards Realizing Bioprinted Tissue Construct for OB/GYN and Pediatric Applications.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed January 18, 2022).12aTissue Engineering12aBioprinting22aPrinting, Three-Dimensional22aObstetrics22aGynecology22aPediatrics 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4411240uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44114  aAUTH02080cgm a22004454a 101298627DNLM20260227151803.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070201s2007    mdu066 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13596  a(OCoLC)85498335  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 551 aGriffin, Robert G.10aStructure determination of amyloid and membrane proteins with solid state NMR /cRobert G. Griffin.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 7, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 5 min., 31 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 24, 2007.3 a(CIT): Many biological systems form nanostructures that are well ordered on a microscopic but not a macroscopic scale, and are therefore difficult to examine with standard tools of structural biology.  Two examples that exhibit these properties are amyloid and membrane proteins that assemble, respectively, into fibrils and biological membranes. In this presentation we review the properties of fibrils and membrane proteins and outline approaches to determining the structure of these systems with high resolution solid state magic angle spinning (MAS) NMR techniques.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aAmyloid beta-Peptidesxchemistry12aMembrane Proteinsxchemistry22aNuclear Magnetic Resonance, Biomolecularxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13596  aAUTH01644cas a2200505   0370612DNLM20260227090911.0cr|cn#||||||||730925c19569999at qr p       0||| 0eng    a0045-0421l0045-0421  aADEJA2  a(DNLM)A75240000(s)  a(OCoLC)01518791  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Australia00aW1bAU52210aAust Dent J2dnlm00aAustralian dental journal.  aSydney.3 aSydney :bAustralian Dental Association  av.billus., ports.  aFour no. a year,b1999-  aBimonthly,b-1998  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-        Feb. 1956-1 aMEDLINEbv10n4,Aug. 1965-1 aPubMedbv10n4,Aug. 1965-1 aIndex to dental literaturex0019-3992bv10n4,Aug. 1965-v44n4,Dec. 19990 aOLDMEDLINE1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aIssued by the Australian Dental Association.  aFormed by the union of: Australian journal of dentistry; Dental journal of Australia; and: Queensland dental journal. 2aDentistry 2aPeriodical2 aAustralian Dental Association.0 x1834-781914tAustralian journal of dentistryx0365-359514tDental journal of Australiax0366-927014tQueensland dental journalx1038-703X4 uhttps://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/journal/18347819  aAUTH01382cas a2200457 a 9418453DNLM20260227091018.0cr|cn#||||||||940210c19949999xxuqr p       0||| 0eng    a1068-0667l1068-0667  aJADEEU  a(DNLM)SR0076125(s)  a(OCoLC)27422455  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bJO533T10aJ Adult Dev2dnlm 0aJournal of adult development00aJournal of adult development.  aNew York, N.Y. :bPlenum Pub. Corp.,cc1994-3 31999- :aNew York, NY :bKluwer Academic/Plenum Publishers  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Jan. 1994)-  aTitle from cover.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.  aP