er  aTitle from title screen (viewed Oct. 2, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 11 min., 45 sec. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded May 28, 2009.3 a(CIT): Program Support Center  Asian Pacific American Heritage Month.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aAsian.22aAnniversaries and Special Events22aCultural Diversity 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15134  aAUTH02531cgm a2200493 i 101618068DNLM20260227155055.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130927s2013    mdu079|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18081  a(OCoLC)862322449  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 5800aNIH genetic testing registry (GTR) :bcomprehensive information about genetic tests across the globe /cWendy Rubinstein. Frozen biomaterial collections at the Smithsonian Institution : a way to understand and sustain biodiversity / Pierre Comizzoli.1 aFrozen biomaterial collections at the Smithsonian Institution :ba way to understand and sustain biodiversity 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 19 min.) :bcolor, sound)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aAt head of title: Biospecimen Interest Group meeting.  aRecorded Sept. 12, 2013.3 a(CIT): Biospecimen Interest Group Meeting  NIH Genetic Testing Registry (GTR): Comprehensive Information About Genetic Tests Across The Globe  Wendy Rubinstein, MD, PhD, FACMG, FACP Senior Scientist, NIH/NLM/NCBI Director, NIH Genetic Testing Registry   and    Frozen Biomaterial Collections at the Smithsonian Institution - A Way to Understand and Sustain Biodiversity.  Pierre Comizzoli, DVM, PhD, Reproductive Physiologist Center for Species Survival Smithsonian Conservation Biology Institute National Zoological Park.  aDescription based on version viewed: Oct. 23, 2013.22aSmithsonian Institution.12aDatabases, Genetic22aCryopreservation22aEndangered Species22aGenetic Testing22aRegistries22aReproductive Techniques, Assisted 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aRubinstein, Wendy.tNIH genetic testing registry (GTR).1 aComizzoli, Pierre.tFrozen biomaterial collections at the Smithsonian Institution.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bBiospecimen Interest Group,esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18081  aAUTH02384cgm a22004574a 101298612DNLM20260227151819.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070201s2007    mdu072 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13570  a(OCoLC)85817824  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3021 aHamel, E.q(Edith)10aCortical interneurons and pyramidal cells in neurovascular coupling responses to afferent pathway simulation /cEdith Hamel.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 13, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 11 min., 35 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 8, 2007.3 a(CIT): Dr. Hamel and her colleagues study neurons and glial cells in the brain can locally influence the brain's blood supply. The dysfunction/degeneration of specific populations of cells may have dramatic repercussions on the local regulation of blood flow. Tract tracing studies and immunocytochemistry are used to study the anatomical relationships between neurons and blood vessels as well as the cellular localization, molecular composition, and pharmacological properties of receptors mediating vasomotor responses. The goal of this work is to understand how brain neurons control local cerebral perfusion and how this relationship is altered in pathological states like Alzheimer's Disease and migraine headache. Ultimately, new drugs could be developed to preserve a normal blood supply to the brain in the absence of neurogenic regulation.  NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aCerebrovascular Circulation22aAfferent Pathwaysxphysiology22aBrainxblood supply22aInterneuronsxphysiology22aPyramidal Cellsxphysiology22aVasomotor Systemxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13570  aAUTH01315cas a2200409   0413573DNLM20260227090916.0cr|cn ||||||||740508c19279999iluqr         0||| 0eng    a0037-7961l0037-7961  aSSRVAW  a(DNLM)S29000000(s)  a(OCoLC)01765700  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bSO13310aSoc Serv Rev2dnlm04aThe Social service review.  aChicago.3 aChicago Il :bUniversity Of Chicago Press  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-       Mar. 1927-  aEdited by the faculty of the School of Social Service Administration of the University of Chicago.2 aMEDLINEbv51n4,Dec. 1977-v56n4,Dec. 19822 aPubMedbv51n4,Dec. 1977-v56n4,Dec. 19822 aHospital literature indexx0018-5736bv51n4,Dec. 1977-v56n4,Dec. 1982  aAlso issued online. 2aSocial Work 2aPeriodical2 aUniversity of Chicago.bSchool of Social Service Administration.4 uhttps://www.journals.uchicago.edu/loi/ssr  aAUTH01518cas a22004571  15310430RDNLM20260205094647.0cr||n|||||||||820919c19409999bl tr         0||| 0por    a0365-074Xl0365-074X  aABMNA6  a(DNLM)A66345000(s)  a(OCoLC)38892361  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Brazil00aW1bAR87210aArq Bras Med Nav2dnlm00aArquivos brasileiros de medicina naval.  aRio de Janeiro.3 aRio De Janeiro :bBrazil. Diretoria De Saude Da Marinha  av.bill., ports.  aThree no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aano 1-    Jan. 1940-  aPrint is