22aBiology 2aPeriodical2 aTowarzystwo Biologii Rozrodu.0 x2300-732X4 uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/1642431X  aAUTH01326cam a2200337 i 9918787488506676DNLM20260212175422.0240319r19541954iau      b    000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)1540314847  aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.131 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aRecent advances in internal medicine :ba review of 5 years' progress in the Department of Medicine of the S.U.I. College of Medicine /cWilliam B. Bean, M.D. 1a[Des Moines, Iowa] :bIowa State Medical Society,c[1954]  a8 unnumbered pages ;c28 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  a"Reprinted from the Journal of the Iowa State Medical Society, November, 1954, pages 497 to 504"--Page 8.  aNLM copy, bound 13th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM  aIncludes bibliographical references.22aState University of Iowa.bDepartment of Internal Medicine12aInternal Medicine12aSchools, Medical 2aIowa0 aJournal of the Iowa State Medical Society.  aAUTH02072cgm a2200445 i 9918663285806676DNLM20260227164030.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230815s2023    mdu444|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33355  a(OCoLC)1540314804  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.55.T72 aTransforming Discoveries into Products: Maximizing NIH's Levers to Catalyze Technology Transfer (Workshop)d(2023 :cOnline)jauthor.10aTransforming Discoveries into Products :bMaximizing NIH's Levers to Catalyze Technology Transfer /cOSP, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (7 hr., 24 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded July 31, 2023.3 a(CIT): Workshop Goals: 1. Outline NIH"s current policy and approach, as a research institution, for patenting and licensing biomedical inventions to identify best practices and potential new approaches. 2. Discuss NIH"s role in the broader biomedical research ecosystem in promoting the application of knowledge to enhance human health.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed September 5, 2024).22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aTranslational Research, Biomedical22aTechnology Transfer22aPatents as Topic22aDrug Development 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Science Policy,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=51051  aAUTH02017cgm a2200481 i 9918663287306676DNLM20260227164001.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230815s2023    mdu084|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33324  a(OCoLC)1452436510  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 305 AA12 aSuicide Prevention for LGBTQ+ Youth (Symposium)d(2023 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aSuicide prevention for LGBTQ+ youth /cNIMH Office of the Scientific Director. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 24 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded June 30, 2023.3 a(CIT): In celebration of Pride Month, this interactive hybrid symposium will feature a keynote speaker and moderated panel discussion focusing on the latest interventions, what research still needs to be developed, and resources with respect to Suicide Prevention for LGBTQ+ Youth.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed August 9, 2024).12aSexual and Gender Minoritiesxpsychology12aSuicide Preventionxmethods22aAdolescent22aPrecipitating Factors22aRisk Assessment22aSuicidal Ideation22aAdolescent Behaviorxpsychology 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Mental Health (U.S.).bOffice of the Scientific Director,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49869  aAUTH02593cgm a2200445 i 101671863DNLM20260227155147.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u151031s2015    mdu101|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19163  a(OCoLC)932048725  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 309 AA11 aJacquet, Catherine,eauthor,espeaker.10aFrom private matter to public health crisis :bnursing and the intervention into domestic violence /cCatherine Jacquet. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 41 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 17, 2015.3 a(CIT): The late 20th century witnessed a significant shift in how the medical community responds to victims of domestic violence. Once a "private matter" that doctors shied away from, domestic violence became recognized as a public health crisis requiring serious medical attention. This change came as a result of the tireless efforts of reformers within the medical profession, a substantial number of whom were nurses. In this talk, Dr. Catherine Jacquet will focus on the work of pioneer nurses who made it their life's work to properly identify and treat victims of violence and to prevent further harm to them. In so doing these nurses were critical figures in reforming a once negligent medical system and, ultimately, improving the lives of thousands of women nationwide.  Dr. Jacquet's lecture coincides with the opening of Confronting Violence, Improving Women's Lives, a new special display curated by her, which will open in the NLM History of Medicine reading room on Monday, September 14, accompanied by a web site and traveling banner exhibition.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 24, 2015).12aBattered Women12aSpouse Abusexprevention & control22aCrisis Intervention22aNurse's Role22aPatient Advocacy 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19163  aAUTH01642cas a2200529   0411772DNLM20260227090921.0cr|cn ||||||||740415c19519999nyuqr p       0    0eng    asc 77000316   a0018-5868l0018-5868  aHOTOA8  a(DNLM)H20320000(s)  a(OCoLC)01643911  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bHO89310aHosp Top2dnlm 0aHospital topics00aHospital topics.  aChicago :bHospital Buyer2 aWashington DC :bHeldref Publications3 32010- :aNew York :bRoutledge  av.billus., ports.  aQuarterly,b1990-  aBimonthly,b19??-1989  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 29, no. 9-       Sept. 1951-1 aMEDLINEbv43n9, Sept. 1965-0 aPubMedbv43n9, Sept. 1965-1 aHospital literature indexbv52n8, Sept.-Oct. 1974-19941 aHospital and health administration indexb1995-19990 aOLDMEDLINE1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aContinues in part: Hospital digest. 2aEquipment and Supplies, Hospital 2aHospitals 2aPeriodical0 x1939-927800tHospital topics and buyer's guidex0093-173Xw(OCoLC)0177800115tHospital digestgJan. 1961 w (OCoLC)120758154 uhttps://www.tandfonline.com/loi/vhos20  aAUTH01463cgm a2200421 i 101770475DNLM20260227160214.0m     o  z f      vz|czau|ucr|cna||||||||200812s2020    mdu055 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35789  a(OCoLC)1194873277  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWI 42000aHuman models of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) /cStephen B. Hanauer. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (55 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCC grand rounds  aRecorded February 12, 2020  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed September 3, 2020).12aInflammatory Bowel Diseasesxetiology22aInflammatory Bowel Diseasesxtherapy22aBiomedical Research 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aHanauer, Stephen B.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=35789  aAUTH02000cgm a22004334a 101298625DNLM20260227151743.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070201s2007    mdu066|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13591  a(OCoLC)84730037  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 3751 aLevchenko, Andre.10aSystems biology of signal transduction :bmarriage of genetics, modeling, and microflu[i]dic chips /cAndre Levchenko.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 7, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 6 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 23, 2007.3 a(CIT): Living systems have an uncanny ability to continuously process the information about the outside world and merge it with the information encoded internally to reach appropriate decisions. The underlying mechanisms can vary in complexity and sophistication, but they undoubtedly require systems level description, as I hope to demonstrate in this talk. I will focus on a few examples of signal processing present in bacteria, yeast, and higher eukaryotes, illustrating how the use of diverse techniques can reveal frequently unanticipated sides of information integration by cells and their communities.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aSignal Transductionxphysiology22aMicrofluidicsxmethods22aModels, Biological22aSystems Biologyxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13591  aAUTH01162cas a2200385 a 101300901DNLM20260227090909.0cr|cn#||||||||070305c20069999ts || p       |    0eng    a  2007243152  a1574-8855l1574-8855  a(OCoLC)71284568  aCAIcCAIdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United Arab Emirates00aW1bCU97610aCurr Drug ther2dnlm00aCurrent drug therapy.  aSharjah, U.A.E. :bBentham Science Publishers Ltd.,cc2006-  aThree issues a year,b2019-  aFrequency varies,b2006-2018  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Jan. 2006)-  aTitle from cover.  aLatest issue consulted:  Vol. 2, no. 1 (Jan. 2007).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.12aDrug Therapy 2aPeriodical0 x2212-39034 uhttp://www.eurekaselect.com/591/journal/current-drug-therapy  aAUTH03161cgm a2200469 i 9918663183506676DNLM20260227163942.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230815s2023    mdu616|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33301  a(DNLM)CIT:33304  a(OCoLC)1504552672  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.52 aThe Multiscale Modeling Consortium Celebrates 20 Years of IMAG (Meeting)d(2023 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.14aThe Multiscale Modeling Consortium celebrates 20 years of IMAG (day 1 & 2) /cNIBIB, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (10 hr., 16 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 28 & 29, 2023.3 a(CIT): The Multiscale Modeling Consortium (MSM) celebrates 20 years of the Interagency Modeling and Analysis Group (IMAG)! In this 2-day "Past2Future" meeting, the Day 1 focuses on what IMAG/MSM has accomplished in its 20-year history and what lessons can be gleaned from past and current MSM efforts. Sessions will highlight the early MSM awardees, the path to model credibility, remembering those we lost, current projects and end with an Early Career Investigator Forum (mentor match). Day 2 focuses on the future vision of the MSM Consortium and its larger community. Sessions will discern how to: 1) synergize with major initiatives in IMAG, 2) increase justice, equity, diversity, and inclusion in computational models, and 3) developing actionable plans for future directions in: digital twins, mechanistic-ML modeling, sociobehavioral and SDoH models, quantum computing, and translating models. Early Career investigators will be an important aspect of both days as they will serve as bridges between the past, present, and future. This meeting will be co-chaired by Dr. Jason Haugh, Dr. Denise Kirschner, Dr. Bruce Lee, and Dr. Herbert Sauro from the MSM Consortium. Attendees following the meeting remotely are encouraged to log into the IMAG wiki and post questions in the interactive agenda.  If you cannot access the IMAG wiki, please contact imag-msm@reelimpact.com.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 3, 2025).22aMultiscale Modeling (MSM) Consortium.22aInteragency Modeling and Analysis Group.12aBiomedical Research22aModels, Biological22aComputer Simulation 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Biomedical Imaging and Bioengineering (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4992940uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49931  aAUTH01052nam a2200301 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400025000800440018001050600024001231000062001472450052002092640068002613000033003293360026003623370028003883380026004165000019004425000102004615000018005635010060005816000035006416500015006766550011006917300039007029990009007419918787480306676DNLM20260224114229.0240403r19581958ctuc          000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.761 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aEugene B. Ferris, 1905-1957 /cWilliam B. Bean. 1a[New Haven, Connecticut] :b[publisher not identified],c[1958]  a1 sheet :bportrait ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  asheetbnb2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  a"Reprinted from the Journal of clinical investigation, vol. 37, no. 2, pp. i-ii, February, 1958."  a"Pages i-ii."  aNLM copy, bound 76th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM12aFerris, Eugene B.,d1905-1957.12aPhysicians 2aEulogy0 aJournal of clinical investigation.  aAUTH01219caa a2200337 i 9918787485206676DNLM20260212175848.0240326s1956    paua     b    000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)1554252189  aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.361 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aPhysical and toxic agents /cby William Bean 1a[United States] :b[publisher not identified],c[1956]  apages 231- 257 :billustrations ;c25 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  aChapter 12 of the book: Pathologic physiology : mechanisms of disease. 2nd ed. Philadelphia : Saunders, 1956.  aNLM copy, bound 36th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aEnvironmental Exposure12aAcclimatizationxphysiology22aPhysical Phenomena0 iContained in (work)aPathologic physiology0 tPathologic physiology  aAUTH02055cgm a22004574a 101535252DNLM20260227153458.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100702s2010    mdu058|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15919  a(OCoLC)657027490  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2661 aHaber, Daniel A.10aInterrogating circulating tumor cells to direct targeted cancer therapies /cDaniel Haber.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (58 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 26, 2010.3 a(CIT): Dr. Haber will present the development and characterization of a microfluidic device for the efficient capture of circulating tumor cells (CTCs) from blood specimens of patients with cancer. These cells represent potential biomarkers with which to follow disease progression, as well as providing important biological information into the process of metastasis. Examples will be provided, including the monitoring of tumor genotypes in EGFR-mutant lung cancer, as well as the molecular characterization of CTCs from patients with localized and metastastic prostate cancer.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxtherapy22aNeoplasmsxgenetics22aNeoplastic Cells, Circulatingxpathology22aTargeted Gene Repairxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15919  aAUTH05177cgm a22004574a 101501587DNLM20260227144855.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090407s2009    mdu061|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14952  a(OCoLC)1019837691  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5511 aFinn, Olivera J.10aImmune memory for abnormal self and immunosurveillance /cOlivera J. Finn.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCenter for Cancer Research National Cancer Institute grand rounds  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 1 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to NIH and HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded Mar. 3, 2009.3 a(CIT): Dr. Finn is Professor and Chair of the Department of Immunology, School of Medicine, University of Pittsburgh and the University of Pittsburgh Cancer Institute Immunology Program Leader. Dr. Finn received her PhD from Stanford University and has been working in the area of tumor immunology for over 20 years. Her laboratory was one of the first to study human T cell responses against tumors, and one of the first to identify a human tumor antigen, MUC1, capable of stimulating cytotoxic T cells. Her work led to the identification of a unique peptide epitope on that molecule culminating in 1993 in the approval of the synthetic version of this epitope as an investigational new drug by the FDA, which is now in Phase II clinical trials as a vaccine for pancreatic cancer, prostate cancer and colon cancer. Dr. Finn has recently undertaken a similar research strategy in lung cancer, and her laboratory has identified a cyclin B1 antigen in lung tumor cells. In addition to her interest in MUC1 and cyclin B1 as vaccine candidates and targets for immunotherapy, Dr. Finn is now directing her research towards the use of these and other molecules for early detection and prevention of cancer.  Dr. Finn has published over 160 papers and reviews and has served on editorial boards of many journals, including serving as Senior Editor for Cancer Research and Advisory Editor for the Journal of Experimental Medicine. She is a member of scientific advisory boards of several cancer centers and member of the NCI Board of Scientific Counselors. She is an active member of the American Association for Cancer Research where she serves on the Steering Committee of the Tumor Immunology Working Group.  She is also active in the American Association of Immunologists where she served as President in 2007-2008.  She is elected Counselor of the International Union of Immunology Societies.  Dr. Finn has trained 20 PhD and MD/PhD students and received the University of Pittsburgh Mentor of the Year Award.  Her other honors include University of Pittsburgh Chancellor"s Distinguished Research Award and the University of Pittsburgh Cancer Institute Scientific Leadership Award.  In 2009 she was named University of Pittsburgh Distinguished Professor. She holds Honorary Professorships at the University of Florence, Italy and the Fourth Military Medical University in Xhian, PRC and has been named Honorary Member of the Immunology Society of Serbia.  SELECTED REFERENCES: 1. Cramer DW, Titus-Ernstoff L, McKolanis JR, Welch WR, Vitonis AF, Berkowitz RS, Finn OJ. Conditions associated with antibodies against the tumor-associated antigen MUC1 and their relationship to risk for ovarian cancer. Cancer Epidemiol Biomarkers Prev 2005; 14:1125-31.  2. Egloff AM, Vella LA, Finn OJ. Cyclin B1 and other cyclins as tumor antigens in immunosurveillance and immunotherapy of cancer. Cancer Res 2006; 66:6-9.  3. Finn OJ. Cancer immunology. N Engl J Med 2008; 358:2704-15.  NCI"s Center for Cancer Research (CCR) Grand Rounds is a weekly lecture series addressing current research in clinical and molecular oncology. Speakers are leading national and international researchers and clinicians proposed by members of the CCR Grand Rounds Planning Committee and others within the CCR community and approved by the CCR Office of the Director. Lectures occur every Tuesday from 8:00 to 9:00 a.m. in Lipsett Amphitheater in the Clinical Center building on the NIH campus September through July with exceptions around holidays and major cancer meetings. The lecture schedule is posted on various calendars of events, including at the following link:  http://www.bethesdatrials.cancer.gov/health-care-professionals/grand-rounds.aspx.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aImmunologic Memory22aAntigens, Neoplasmximmunology22aCancer Vaccinesximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=7520  aAUTH02807cgm a2200469 i 101773027DNLM20260227160308.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201105s2020    mdu065|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30317  a(OCoLC)1224228785  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 168.5.C800aNeutralizing antibodies to SARS-CoV-2 /cPaul Bieniasz.1 iTitle on PowerPoint screen:aNeutralizing antibody response to SARS CoV-2 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 5 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCOVID-19 SIG lecture  aRecorded October 1, 2020.3 a(CIT): Dr. Bieniasz seeks to define how host genes influence the replication of viruses, with an emphasis on human and primate immunodeficiency viruses. His lab seeks to characterize the host functions that viruses mimic, manipulate, and otherwise exploit, as well as the defenses cells have evolved against viral infection.  His work focuses on virus-host interactions, specifically how viruses manipulate host functions to enable their replication as well as host functions that have evolved to curtail virus infection.  His lab has worked extensively on host intrinsic defenses and identified two host restriction factors, tetherin and MX2, which inhibit HIV-1 and other viruses.  Additionally, the Bieniasz lab recently found that mammalian cells can deplete viral RNA molecules that are recognized as foreign based on their nucleotide composition.  They are currently conducting a variety of investigations into the nature of innate and adaptive immunity to SARS-CoV-2, including the development of techniques to identify protective antibodies and therapeutics.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 19, 2020).12aSevere acute respiratory syndrome-related coronavirusximmunology.22aAntibodies, Neutralizingximmunology22aAntibody Formation22aCoronavirus Infectionsximmunology22aSevere Acute Respiratory Syndromeximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aBieniasz, Paul D.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bCOVID-19 Scientific Interest Group,esponsoring body. 0aNIH COVID-19 SIG lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38834  aAUTH01806cgm a2200481 i 101773029DNLM20260227160310.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201105s2020    mdu057|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30324  a(OCoLC)1224228401  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 50500aVaccine trials for COVID-19 :bis there an obligation to offer the first vaccine shown to be effective to all participants /cEmily Erbelding, Joseph Millum, Steve Goodman. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (57 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCC grand rounds  aRecorded October 7, 2020.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 19, 2020).12aCoronavirus Infectionsxprevention & control12aSevere Acute Respiratory Syndromexprevention & control22aClinical Trials as Topicxethics22aVaccinationxethics22aTreatment Outcome22aViral Vaccines 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aErbelding, Emily,espeaker.1 aMillum, Joseph,espeaker.1 aGoodman, Steven N.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center,esponsoring body. 0aClinical Center ethics grand rounds40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38781  aAUTH01263cam a2200337 i 9918787482806676DNLM20260212162246.0240329r19571957ilua     b    000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.531 aGray, Charles W.,eauthor.12aA note on long curved nails /cCharles W. Gray, and William B. Bean. 1a[Chicago, Illinois] :bAmerican Medical Association,c[1957]  a3 unnumbered pages :billustrations ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  aAt head of title: Clinical notes, new instruments and techniques.  a"Reprinted from A.M.A. Archives of Dermatology, January 1957, vol. 75, pp. 126-128."  aNLM copy, bound 53rd in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aNails, Malformedxetiology 2aCase Reports1 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.2 aAmerican Medical Association.0 aArchives of Dermatology.  aAUTH00910cam a2200277 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390350022000800400035001020440018001370600024001551000062001792450064002412640060003053000021003653360026003863370028004123380026004405000019004665000019004855010060005046500028005646500031005929990009006239918787487006676DNLM20260212175939.0240322s1955    xxu           000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)1542998744  aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.191 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aNutritive deficiency, general /cmethod of William B. Bean. 1a[United States] :b[publisher not identified],c[1955?]  a1 sheet ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  asheetbnb2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  aPages 297-298.  aNLM copy, bound 19th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM12aMalnutritionxdiagnosis12aMalnutritionxdiet therapy  aAUTH01122cam a2200313 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400035000800420008001150440018001230600024001411000062001652450085002272640060003123000026003723360026003983370028004243380027004525000019004795000080004985010060005785040041006386100030006796500047007096510009007567300034007659990009007999918787485006676DNLM20260213174640.0240326r19561956dcu      b    000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.381 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.14aThe rise and fall of the correlated clinical courses at Iowa /cWilliam B. Bean. 1aWashington, D.C. :b[publisher not identified],c[1956]  apages 97-100 ;c26 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  aReprinted from Journal of medical education, February 1956, vol. 31, no. 2.  aNLM copy, bound 38th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM  aIncludes bibliographical references.22aState University of Iowa.12aEducation, Medical, Undergraduatexmethods 2aIowa0 aJournal of medical education.  aAUTH00891nam a2200289 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400025000800440018001050600024001231000062001472450041002092640060002503000021003103360026003313370028003573380026003855000019004115000021004305010060004516500034005116500026005456500010005716500011005819990009005929918787482106676DNLM20260223082221.0240401s1957    xxu           000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.601 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aRickets/cmethod of William B. Bean. 1a[United States] :b[publisher not identified],c[1957?]  a1 sheet ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  asheetbnb2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  a"pages 295-296."  aNLM copy, bound 60th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM12aRicketsxprevention & control12aRicketsxdrug therapy22aChild22aInfant  aAUTH03723cgm a22004694a 101531559DNLM20260227153440.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100519s2010    mdu045|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15821  a(OCoLC)648079402  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 3401 aHassan, Raffit.10aTargeted immunotherapy for treatment of malignant mesothelioma /cRaffit Hassan.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aDirector's seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (45 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 16, 2010.3 a(CIT): Director's Seminar Series   Over the last several years our work has focused on the development and clinical evaluation of immunotoxins targeting mesothelin-expressing tumors. Mesothelin is a 40-kDa glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI)- linked cell surface glycoprotein that is present on normal mesothelial cells and overexpressed in several tumors especially ovarian cancers, mesotheliomas and pancreatic cancer. Our laboratory studies have shown it to be a promising target for cancer therapy. Since the murine anti-mesothelin monoclonal antibody K1 by itself is not cytotoxic towards mesothelin positive cells, we have developed immunotoxins targeting mesothelin using the potent bacterial toxin, Pseudomonas exotoxin (PE). This toxin consists of three functional domains: the cell binding domain that allows binding to a cell surface protein, the translocation domain that brings an active fragment of the toxin to the cytosol and the ADP-ribosylation domain that inhibits protein synthesis leading to cell death. For making immunotoxins we use a mutated 38 kDa fragment of PE termed PE38, lacking the cell binding domain that can be replaced by a targeting moiety such as the Fv portion of an antibody. SS1(dsFv)PE38 (SS1P), is a recombinant immunotoxin consisting of the anti-mesothelin Fv (obtained by phage display) linked to PE38.   Based on the in vitro and in vivo activity of SS1P and toxicological studies in cynomolgus monkeys, SS1P was approved by the FDA for clinical evaluation in patients. Currently, we are conducting a Phase I clinical trial of SS1P in mesothelin positive tumors at the NCI and 23 patients have been treated thus far. Completion of this Phase I clinical trial will provide valuable information regarding the maximum tolerated dose, dose limiting toxicities, immunogenicity, pharmacokinetics as well as anti-tumor activity of SS1P. Following completion of the Phase I study, we plan to conduct Phase II studies, to evaluate the anti-tumor activity of SS1P in specific tumor types expressing mesothelin. Since greater than 900f mesotheliomas and pancreatic tumors express mesothelin, and there are no effective treatments for these diseases, conduct of phase II studies of SS1P in these tumor types will be a priority.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMesotheliomaximmunology12aMesotheliomaxtherapy22aAntigens, Neoplasmxtherapeutic use22aImmunotherapyxmethods22aImmunotoxinsxtherapeutic use 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15821  aAUTH03052cgm a2200469 i 101772202DNLM20260227160109.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201003s2020    mdu046|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:30290  a(OCoLC)1204632846  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWT 15700aFrom mechanisms to medicines :brealizing the DREAM of an Alzheimer's cure /cMadhav Thambisetty. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (46 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's seminar series  aRecorded September 18, 2020.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Seminar SeriesThe repeated failures of disease-modifying treatments for Alzheimer's disease (AD) have increased the urgency to identify novel therapeutic targets and biomarkers for AD and related dementias (ADRD). Dr. Thambisetty's Clinical and Translational Neuroscience Section has applied a systems biology approach leveraging deep molecular phenotyping by multi-OMICs methods in brain and blood in combination with multi-modal neuroimaging and epidemiological analyses to identify abnormal metabolic pathways in ADRD associated with severity of pathology and expression of clinical symptoms. These studies have added to a growing body of evidence that AD is a pervasive metabolic disorder with dysregulation in multiple interacting pathways. Dr. Thambisetty's findings have uncovered plausible therapeutic targets for ADRD from these dysregulated metabolic pathways. The Clinical and Translational Neuroscience Section has used large chemi-informatics databases to identify drugs that target genetic regulators of these pathways and have nominated several commonly used FDA-approved drugs as candidate AD treatments. Dr. Thambisetty's lab has initiated analyses to test whether exposure to these drugs in large real-world clinical datasets containing 20 million older individuals is associated with altered risk of incident AD/dementia in the Drug Repurposing for Effective Alzheimer's Medicines (DREAM) study.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed November 10, 2020).12aAlzheimer Diseasexdrug therapy22aAlzheimer Diseasexgenetics22aAlzheimer Diseasexmetabolism22aProteomics22aDrug Discovery22aDrug Repositioning 2aLecture 2aWebcast12aThambisetty, Madhav,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?30290  aAUTH01650cas a2200493   0415027DNLM20260211083708.0cr|cn#||||||||740520c19689999coubr         0    0eng    a   70649485 zsn 78000659  a0587-2871l0587-2871y0587-2781  aJAAHBL  a(DNLM)J11380000(s)z(DNLM)A00195000(s)z(DNLM)A30945000(s)  a(OCoLC)01479286  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  ansdp  9United States00aW1bJO908A10aJ Am Anim Hosp Assoc2dnlm 0aJournal of the American Animal Hospital Ass