m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu060 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11344  a(OCoLC)70663586  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5681 aBerg, Leslie.10aSignal transduction pathways in T cell development and activation /cLeslie Berg  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed June 23, 2006).  aStreaming video (59 min., 49 sec. : sd., col.).  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aRecorded Sept. 5, 2001.3 a(CIT): The first speaker for the Immunology Interest Group 2001 Fall series is Leslie Berg, Professor in the Department of Pathology, University of Massachusetts Medical School, Worcester, MA. Dr. Berg has done ground-breaking work using in vivo models to study the role of the T cell antigen receptor and tyrosine kinases in thymic selection and T cell function. For more information, visit the Immunology Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.12aT-Lymphocytes22aLymphocyte Activation22aProtein-Tyrosine Kinases22aReceptors, Antigen, T-Cell22aSignal Transduction 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11344  aAUTH01689cas a2200529   7600747DNLM20260227090911.0cr|cn ||||||||760116c19759999ne br         0    0eng    a   75649414   a0094-5145l0094-5145  zJCMHB  a(DNLM)J17440000(s)  a(OCoLC)02244911  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aeng  9Netherlands00aW1bJO593Q10aJ Community Health2dnlm 0aJournal of community health00aJournal of community health.1 iIssues for <1998-> have subtitle:aPublication for health promotion and disease prevention  aNew York,bHuman Sciences Press.3 aAmsterdam :bSpringer  av.bill.  aBimonthly  aQuarterlyb1975-1989  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-    fall 1975-1 aMEDLINEbv1n1, fall 1975-0 aPubMedbv1n1, fall 1975-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aOfficial journal of the Association of Teachers of Preventive Medicine.  3Vol. 13, no. 1 (May 1990)c20010620fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aCommunity Medicine 2aComprehensive Health Care 2aDelivery of Health Care 2aPreventive Health Services 2aPeriodical2 aAssociation of Teachers of Preventive Medicine.0 x1573-36104 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/10900  aAUTH02775cgm a2200445 a 101595207DNLM20260227154400.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u121102s2012    mdu076|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17632  a(OCoLC)819713404  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 168.5.R181 aJeang, Kuan-Teh.10aNuclear damage and miscounted chromosomes :bhuman T-cell leukemia virus transformation of cells /cKuan-Teh Jeang.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 16 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded Oct. 24, 2012.3 a(CIT): Human T-cell Leukemia Virus type 1 (HTLV-1) is a delta-retrovirus that infects approximately 20 million individuals worldwide. HTLV-1 is the etiological agent of adult T-cell leukemia (ATL), a poorly treatable and prevalently fatal disease. The virus encodes a oncoprotein, Tax, which has been shown to transform primary rodent cells. Tax confers immortalization, anchorage-dependent cell growth, and tumorigenicity to rodent cells. Tax can also immortalize human primary T lymphocytes. However, successful transformation of human cells has not been established using Tax. The Tax protein has pleiotropic effects on host-cell gene expression and activates several pathways such as the cyclic AMP responsive binding protein (CREB), the nuclear factor kappa-B, the cyclin-dependant kinases (CDKs), and the Akt pathways. Tax-expressing cells also exhibit nuclear morphological aberrancy, frequent multinucleation, aneuploidy, and loss of function of the tumor suppressor protein p53. Currently, the mechanisms and factors needed for the initiation of ATL by Tax remain incompletely clarified. Dr. Jeang will speak on research insights gained over the past 25 years on how HTLV-1 infection and Tax expression create nuclear damage and aneuploidy in the process of cellular transformation.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aHuman T-lymphotropic virus 1xpathogenicity22aAneuploidy22aGene Products, tax22aLeukemia-Lymphoma, Adult T-Cellxetiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17632  aAUTH01575cas a2200457 i 101708316DNLM20260227090918.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||160622c20179999nyu|| poo     0    0eng    a  20162027580 a2473-1242l2473-124221  a(OCoLC)952024972  aDLCbengerdad20260227cDLCdPFMdOCLCOdSTFdOCLCOdDNLM  apccansdp  9United States10aISSN RECORD00aW110aHealth Equity2dnlm 0aHealth equity00aHealth equity. 1aNew Rochelle, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.,c[2017]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1, Issue 1 (2017).0 aPubMedbv1n1, 2017-0 aPMC  aArticles published as they become available and compiled into issues.  aDescription based on: Volume 1, Issue 1 (2017); title from journal homepage (Liebert website, viewed February 24, 2017)  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 1, Issue 1 (2017) (viewed January 5, 2018).12aHealth Equity22aDelivery of Health Care22aHealth Status Disparities22aVulnerable Populations 2aPeriodical40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.