dicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc202506032pda5DNLM  aConservedc202506172pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aPsychologyxmethods22aPsychophysiologyxmethods 0aHome university library of modern knowledge ;v41.4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/60310780R  aAUTH02295cgm a22004334a 101267864DNLM20260227145748.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu075 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10878  a(OCoLC)66524891  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWV 3011 aFirestein, Stuart.10aMaking scents of the genome :bfunctional genomics of mammalian olfaction /cStuart Firestein.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 29, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 14 min., 33 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 24, 2003.3 a(CIT): NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series  The activity of all cells in the nervous system is regulated by the interaction of various chemicals, such as neurotransmitters, hormones, and peptides with membrane receptors. The ways in which these substances exert their influence is known generally as signal transduction. We use the vertebrate olfactory receptor neuron as a model for investigating general principles and mechanisms of signal transduction - receptor-ligand interactions, modulation by second messengers, ion channel gating, and the long term mechanisms of adaptation and desensitization. The olfactory neuron is uniquely suited for these studies since it is designed specifically for the detection and discrimination of a wide variety of small organic molecules, i.e. odors.   For more information, visit  Stuart Firestein.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aSmell22aOlfactory Receptor Neurons22aReceptors, Odorant 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10878  aAUTH01241cas a2200457 a 9433814DNLM20260227091016.0cr|cn ||||||||940915c19949999mauqr p       0||| 0eng    asn 92000316   a1064-5462l1064-5462  aARLIEY  a(DNLM)SR0075391(s)  a(OCoLC)26326076  aNSDPcNSDPdDNLM  9United States00aW1bAR955TM10aArtif Life2dnlm 0aArtificial life00aArtificial life.  aCambridge, MA :bMIT Press,cc1994-  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1/2 (fall 1993/winter 1994)-  aTitle from cover.  aPrint issues ceased with v. 20 (2014).1 aMEDLINEb1996-1 aPubMedb1996-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online. 2aArtificial Intelligence 2aModels, Biological 2aOrigin of Life 2aPeriodical0 x1530-91854 uhttp://www.mitpressjournals.org/loi/artl  aAUTH01355cgm a22004094a 101267814DNLM20260227145542.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s1995    mdu091 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10709  a(OCoLC)64588937  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQT 25500aPhysical fitness.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c1995]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aORWH women's health seminar series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 6, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 31 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 30, 1995.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aPhysical Fitness22aExercise22aWomen's Health 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aORWH women's health seminar series40uhttp://videoca