2    mdu398|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17704  a(OCoLC)824605157  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWA 320 AA100aNICHD research for a lifetime /cNational Institute of Child Health and Human Development.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (6 hr., 38 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded Dec. 5, 2012.3 a(CIT): This colloquium will feature leading scientists, including Nobel prize laureates, from multiple disciplines who can discuss not only past research accomplishments, but also how current and emerging research can improve the health of women, children, families, and communities over the life course. Discussions will center around evolving research and its potential impact on human health in such areas as developmental biology; the developmental origins of health and disease; prematurity; reproductive health; neural repair and rehabilitation.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.22aNational Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.)12aChild Welfare22aAcademies and Institutes22aBiomedical Research22aFamily Health22aHuman Development 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17704  aAUTH01627cas a2200505 a 101257109DNLM20260227090916.0cr|cn#||||||||050823c20059999si mr p       0    0eng    a  2005264535  a1750-8460l1750-8460y1462-3935  a(OCoLC)61321318  aOI@cOI@dSBHdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9Singapore00aW1bBR537N0 aBritish journal of hospital medicine (London, England : 2005)10aBr J Hosp Med (Lond)2dnlm10aBritish journal of hospital medicine.3 aBJHM  aLondon :bMA Healthcare,cc2005-3132026- :aSingapore :bIMR Press  av. :bill. ;c30 cm.  aMonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 66, no. 8 (Aug. 2005)-  aTitle from cover.  a"The multidisciplinary review journal for hospital doctors."  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 66, no. 9 (Sept. 2005).  aPrint issues ceased with 2024.1 aMEDLINEbv66n8,Aug. 2005-1 aPubMedbv66n8,Aug. 2005-  aAlso issued online.12aClinical Medicine22aHospitals22aMedicine 2aPeriodical0 x1759-739000tHospital medicine (London, England : 1998)w(DLC)sn 98047100w(OCoLC)383166404 uhttp://www.magonlinelibrary.com/loi/hmed4 uhttps://www.imrpress.com/journal/bjhm  aAUTH01843cgm a2200433 a 101585259DNLM20260227154218.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120525s2012    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17258  a(OCoLC)797084288  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 55.51 aGoldman, Rado.12aA few surprises in protein glycosylation /cRado Goldman.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 0 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aProteomics Interest Goup lecture  aRecorded May 10, 2012.3 a(CIT): Proteomics Interest Goup Lecture     Glycosylation of proteins results in substantial micro-heterogeneity. This provides analytical challenges and opportunities for improved understanding of disease processes. Analysis of site specific protein glycoforms and genomic variants affecting protein glycosylation suggests novel ways to study the progression of liver disease to hepatocellular carcinoma.    For more information, visit  http://proteome.nih.gov.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aGlycosylation22aCarcinoma, Hepatocellularxgenetics22aGlycoproteinsxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bProteomics Interest Group. 0aProteomics Interest Group seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17258  aAUTH02145cgm a2200433 i 101713972DNLM20260227155903.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u170930s2017    mdu067|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:23489  a(OCoLC)1013590111  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 54100aMechanisms of self versus non-self discrimination in the innate immune system /cGreg Barton. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2017]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 7 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aImmunology Interest Group seminar series  aRecorded September 27, 2017.3 a(CIT): Immunology Interest Group Seminar Series  Greg Barton received his undergraduate degree from Princeton University. He performed his graduate work in the lab of Alexander Rudensky at the University of Washington and his postdoctoral training with Ruslan Medzhitov at Yale University. He joined the faculty at Berkeley in 2005. Dr Barton currently holds the position of Endowed Chair and Professor of Immunology and Pathogenesis at UC Berkeley. The Barton Lab studies innate immunity with the goal of understanding strategies of pathogen recognition and self/non-self discrimination.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed November 20, 2017).12aImmunity, Innate22aAutoimmunity22aNucleic Acids 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aBarton, Gregc(Professor of immunology and pathogenesis)eauthor.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group,esponsoring body. 0aImmunology Interest Group lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23489  aAUTH01955cgm a22004214a 101267506DNLM20260227145125.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu134 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10130  a(OCoLC)70663576  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.52 aNIH Research Festivald(2001 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNIH Research Festival 2001.pIC director's seminar "The future direction of biomedical research" /cOffice of the Director, NIH.30aIC director's seminar "The future direction of biomedical research"30aFuture direction of biomedical research  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 17, 2006).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 14 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 4, 2001.3 a(CIT): Dazzling new methods, genetic sequences, and a prodigious rate of profound new discoveries make today part of one of the most exciting eras in the history of biomedical research. Against this backdrop, six NIH Institute Directors will present a plenary session showcasing the NIH Intramural Research Program and the "Future Direction of Biomedical Research."  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aBiomedical Researchxtrends 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of the Director.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10130  aAUTH01648cgm a22004454a 101267490DNLM20260227145118.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s1998    mdu037 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10113  a(OCoLC)69391287  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 901 aNeuringer, Martha.10aOverview of breast feeding vs. formula vs. supplemented formula feeding and long term outcome studies :bcognitive, visual and behavioral outcomes /cMartha Neuringer.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c1998]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 25, 2006).  aStreami