ming video (3 hr., 54 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 25, 2011.3 a(CIT): The January 25, 2011, meeting of the National Advisory Research Resources Council will feature discussion of the Scientific Management Review Board's recommendation to develop a Translational Medicine and Therapeutics Center and the likely impact of the recommendation on other NCRR programs. Also planned are presentations on NCRR's role in building connections among its programs through networking and collaboration, inclusion reporting for 2010, and an update on core consolidation activities.  In addition, NCRR staff members will present Concept Clearances for consideration and approval by Council.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomedical Research22aGovernment Agenciesxorganization & administration 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16430  aAUTH01808cgm a2200397 a 101585228DNLM20260227154133.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u120525s2012    mdu069|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17209  a(OCoLC)803799283  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQV 361 aMaudsley, Stuart.14aThe tip of the therapeutic iceberg :bexpanding the G protein-coupled receptor pharmacopeia /cStuart Maudsley ; Proteomics Interest Group.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 9 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded Apr. 12, 2012.3 a(CIT):  Currently nearly half of the therapeutic pharmacopeia is targeted towards the plasma membrane heptahelical G proteincoupled receptors (GPCRs). Once considered to be relatively simplisitc "on-off" systems, these highly flexible receptor proteins appear now to possess a potentially unprecedented level of functional nuance. Our increased appreciation of this specific receptor system is undoubtedly one of the most important fields of future translational research for the NIH.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aReceptors, G-Protein-Coupledxphysiology22aDrug Discovery 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bProteomics Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17209  aAUTH03699cgm a2200541 i 9918418885806676DNLM20260227162225.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220616s2022    mdu496|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32314  a(DNLM)CIT:32315  a(DNLM)CIT:32316  a(DNLM)CIT:32317  a(OCoLC)1348920142  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 704.62 aAnnual NIH HEAL Initiative Investigator Meetingn(3rd :d2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNIH HEAL initiative investigator meeting - April 2022 /cNIH HEAL Initiative. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (4 streaming video files (8 hr., 16 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aApril 11 morning session 1 (Welcome and Introduction -- Keynote Address: What Are We Doing, and What Can We Do Better to End he Overdose Epidemic? -- Helping to End Addiction Long-term Initiative: Progress and Priorities -- Dialogue: NINDS and NICHD Directors in Conversation With the HEAL Community Partner Committee -- Plenary: At the Intersection of Pain Use Disorder--Bringing the Research Communities Together -- Fireside Chat: Addressing Addiction and the Overdose Epidemic from Many Angles) -- Afternoon session 1 (Scientific Symposium: Advancing Research to Practice / NIDA Director Dr. Nora Volkow in Conversation with Maia Szalavitz, Journalist and Author) -- Morning session 2 (Welcome -- The Opioid Crisis; Scientific Challenges and Opportunities -- Scientific Symposium; Advancing the Pain and Addiction Therapeutic Research Pipeline -- Panel: Better Together--Collective HEALing Through Data Sharing) -- Afternoon session 2 (Federal Roundtable: How can Science Inform the Federal Response to the Opioid Crisis -- Observations and Future Directions for HEAL).  aRecorded April 11 & 12, 2022.3 aThe NIH HEAL Initiative convened the third annual meeting of more than 400 NIH HEAL Initiative awardees, federal officials, people with lived experience, and other stakeholders to share research findings, explore trends and shared interests, and identify opportunities to advance the goals of the initiative. This meeting brought together researchers from around the country who are taking on the pain and opioid crises from all angles. These researchers study a broad range of topics, such as innovative technology-based therapies for pain management; community-based integrated solutions for prevention, overdose, and medication-based treatment for opioid use disorder; and a variety of new approaches for managing pain.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed October 25, 2022).12aPain Management22aOpioid-Related Disordersxpsychology22aOpioid-Related Disordersxtherapy22aHealth Equity22aCommunity Participation 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNIH HEAL Initiative,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4513440uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4513640uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4513840uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45140  aAUTH01465cgm a22004454a 101289651DNLM20260227151454.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2006    mdu069 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13194  a(OCoLC)84659547  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWB 3691 aRosen, Bruce R.10aNeurobiological correlates of acupuncture :bmodern science explores ancient practice /cBruce R. Rosen.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving ima