ams.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aScience in the public health  aTitle from title screen (viewed Aug. 24, 2007).  aStreaming video (3 hr., 59 min., 26 sec. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS and NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded May 10, 2007.3 a(CIT): Science in the Public Health Robotic technologies are revolutionizing medical science, education, and treatments. Just as pilots use simulators to learn how to fly, medical students use lifelike manikin simulators to learn how to treat life-threatening conditions. Robots are used to augment surgical methods thereby minimizing invasive effects. Telesurgery provides patients with access to expert surgeons who may be half way around the world. Robotic methods hold promise for treating patients in dangerous environments. Join us as we discuss how robotic technologies are changing the face of medicine. For more information, visit  http://odoerdb2-1.od.nih.gov/oer/training/step.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomedical Engineering12aRobotics.22aPatient Simulation22aSurgery, Computer-Assisted 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bStaff Training-Extramural Programs Committee. 0aScience in the public health40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13816  aAUTH01450cgm a2200445 a 101555200DNLM20260227153842.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110209s2011    mdu058|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16390  a(OCoLC)710812981  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 7701 aBradol, Jean-Hervé.10aResistant malaria treated by a mediation from Asia to Africa /cJean-Hervé Bradol.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture 