c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (10 hr., 12 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded July 6&7, 2016.3 a(CIT): The proposed workshop will foster the discussion of: Needed evidence on efficacy, effectiveness and patient safety of pharmacological and non-pharmacological therapies for HLBS diseases and risk factors accounting for the racial/ethnic, socioeconomic and cultural diversity of U.S. Hispanic/Latino populations, potential settings and approaches to study medical treatment patterns and their associated health outcomes among Hispanics/Latinos with single or multiple co-existing chronic (HLBS) diseases, research opportunities to narrow gaps in knowledge; translate this knowledge into effective interventions; and reduce health disparities among Hispanics in the U.S.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed September 6, 2016).12aHispanic or Latino12aPrecision Medicine22aHeart Diseases22aHematologic Diseases22aLung Diseases22aSleep Wake Disorders 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1978340uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19784  aAUTH02469cgm a2200469 i 9918332579906676DNLM20260227161824.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220104s2021    mdu159|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32105  a(OCoLC)1334652220  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 212 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bBuilding Interdisciplinary Research Careers in Women's Health (Program).bAnnual Meetingd(2021 :cOnline)eauthor.10aBuilding interdisciplinary research careers in women's health (BIRCWH) annual meeting /cORWH, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 39 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aRuth L. Kirschstein memorial lectureship ;v5th  aRecorded December 13, 2021.3 a(CIT): This event will include the 5th Ruth L. Kirschstein Memorial Lectureship, titled "Bias Is a Habit We Must All Work Hard to Break" and presented by Molly L. Carnes, M.D., M.S.; the Legacy of Leadership Lecture, presented by Claire Pomeroy, M.D., M.B.A.; presentations on research findings by leading BIRCWH Scholars; and a live Q&A session. The e-poster session for the BIRCWH Scholar abstracts will occur in the afternoon.  BIRCWH is an institutional mentored career-development program designed to connect junior faculty, known as BIRCWH Scholars, to senior faculty mentors with shared interest in women"s health and sex differences research. More information about BIRCWH can be found here.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed June 28, 2022).12aVocational Guidance12aBiomedical Research22aWomen22aSexism22aWomen's Health22aSex Factors 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Research on Women's Health,esponsoring body. 0aRuth L. Kirschstein lecture ;v5th.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44184  aAUTH01459cgm a22004214a 101268222DNLM20260227150159.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu117 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11538  a(OCoLC)84717204  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWH 5251 aWilson, Wyndham Hopkins.10aDemystifying medicine.pLymphoma :bthe diseases and advances /cWyndham Wilson and Louis Staudt.30aLymphoma  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 9, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 56 min., 24 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 6, 2003.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aLymphoma22aLymphocytesxultrastructure22aMolecular Diagnostic Techniques 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11538  aAUTH03199cgm a2200481 i 9918418883006676DNLM20260227162322.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220616s2022    mdu875|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32356  a(DNLM)CIT:32359  a(DNLM)CIT:32417  a(OCoLC)1378182456  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 832 aAnnual Common Fund Glycoscience Program All Hands Meetingd(2022 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10a2022 NIH Common Fund Glycoscience Program All Hands Meeting /cNIH Common Fund Glycoscience Program. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (14 hr., 35 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded May 2, 3 & 4, 2022.3 a(CIT): The NIH Common Fund Glycoscience Program is focused on creating new methodologies and resources to study glycans, and to develop these resources so they may be more easily understood and accessed by the broader biomedical research community.  Access to these new resources allows investigators to fully explore the roles of carbohydrates in their own research programs (biological models, pathways, and/or diseases of interest), rather than abandon such discovery due to a lack of tools or expertise. Initiatives of this program have been designed to encourage new strategies and high-risk approaches for methods and tool development, with the subsequent rapid transition of successful projects to commercialization.Program Initiatives included:develop methods and technologies for synthesis of biomedically relevant carbohydratesdevelop accessible tools for probing and analyzing carbohydrates and their interaction partnersdevelop data integration and analysis toolsa sister SBIR/STTR program for commercializing tools/technologies for glycoscienceThe Common Fund Glycoscience Program is now completing. This its final "All Hands Meeting" of programs investigators, collaborators, and commercial partners will highlight the new methods, tools, technologies, and resources developed by the program, discuss their utility to members of the broader scientific community, and the commercialization of these tools.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 11, 2023).12aPolysaccharidesxphysiology22aGlycosylation22aGlycoproteinsxphysiology22aChemistry Techniques, Synthetic 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aCommon Fund Glycoscience Program,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4523340uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4523740uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45235  aAUTH01720cgm a22004454a 101528436DNLM20260227153333.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100406s2010    mdu060|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15720  a(OCoLC)726074544  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWH 1701 aTisdale, John F.10aTherapeutic gene delivery using hematopoietic stem cells in sickle cell disease /cJohn F. Tisdale. Multiple myeloma and its precurser (MGUS) : looking into the future / Ola Landgren.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aClinical Center grand rounds  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 0 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 17, 2010.3 a(CIT): Clinical Center Grand Rounds  John F. Tisdale, MD,  Senior Investigator, Molecular and Clinical Hematology Branch, NHLBI   Ola Landgren, MD, PhD,  Investigator, Medical Oncology Branch,  Center for Cancer Research, NCI.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aAnemia, Sickle Cellxtherapy22aHematopoietic Stem Cell Transplantation22aMultiple Myeloma 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15720  aAUTH01575cgm a22004454a 101267366DNLM20260227145156.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu028|||||||f||||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:9958  a(OCoLC)176924681  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 145.51 aSacks, Frank M.10aEssential fats in foods.n6,pDifferences between preventive nutrition and therapeutic intervention /cFrank M. Sacks.30aDifferences between preventive nutrition and therapeutic intervention  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 17, 2007).  aStreaming video (28 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 20, 2000.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aNutritive Value22aFatty Acids, Essential22aNutrition Therapy22aPrimary Prevention 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?9958  aAUTH02069cgm a22004214a 101473086DNLM20260227152505.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080502s2008    mdu699 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14431  a(DNLM)CIT:14438  a(OCoLC)230847888  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aGT 285000aDecision making in eating behavior /cNIH.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 19, 2008).  aStreaming video (11 hr., 9 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 14-15, 2008.3 a(CIT): Integrating Perspectives from the Individual, Family, and Environment  Meeting Objectives:  To discuss the state of research in decision making and examine those areas that can potentially be applied to the field of nutrition, especially in the area of sustaining positive behavior change and to stimulating research interest in this area.  Meeting Framework:  The meeting is being organized to provide significant audience participation.  Each session will include a plenary speaker who will provide an overview of the session topic, followed by presentations by various panelists who will speak on the topic from their unique perspectives.  A moderated question and answer session will follow the panelists' presentation to allow full interaction among the speakers.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aFeeding Behavior22aDecision Making 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1443140uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14438  aAUTH01643cas a2200481 a 101269157DNLM20260227090922.0cr|cn#||||||||060214c20069999ts br p       0    0eng    a  2006243188  a1871-5303l1871-5303  a(OCoLC)65636905  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9United Arab Emirates00aW1bCU788ER10aEndocr Metab Immune Disord Drug Targets2dnlm00aEndocrine, metabolic & immune disorders drug targets.3 aEndocrine, metabolic and immune disorders drug targets  aSaif Zone, Sharjah, U.A.E. ;aSan Francisco, CA  :bBentham Science Publishers,cc2006-  av.  aEight issues a year,b2019-  aQuarterly,b2006-2017  aSix no. a year,b2018  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 6, no. 1 (Mar. 2006)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv6n1, Mar. 2006-0 aPubMedbv6n1, Mar. 2006-  aAlso issued online.12aAcquired Immunodeficiency Syndromexdrug therapy12aEndocrine System Diseasesxdrug therapy12aMetabolic Diseasesxdrug therapy22aDrug Delivery Systems22aDrug Design 2aPeriodical0 x2212-387300tCurrent drug targets. Immune, endocrine and metabolic disordersx1568-0088w(DLC)   2002243130w(OCoLC)492755184 uhttps://www.eurekaselect.net/journal/59  aAUTH01493cgm a2200421 a 101565223DNLM20260227154036.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110715s2011    mdu053 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16707  a(OCoLC)747167637  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWB 3101 aBercovitz, Anita.10aComplementary and alternative therapies in hospice :bresults from the 2007 national home and hospice care survey /cAnita  Bercovitz.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (53 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCCAM's complementary and integrative medicine consult service lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded June 13, 2011.  aOpen-captioned.12aHospice Care22aComplementary Therapies 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine (U.S.) 0aNCCAM complementary and integrative medicine consult service lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16707  aAUTH02987cgm a2200493 i 101775928DNLM20260227160649.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2021    mdu099|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31483  a(OCoLC)1241125315  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 46000aFueling the next genomic revolution :bmaximizing the impact of bacterial, human, and human metagenome genomic knowledge and technology /cDr. Francis Collins, Dr. Eric Lander, Dr. Claire Fraser, Dr. Charles Rotimi. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 39 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded January 13, 2021.3 a(CIT): WALS will host a lecture and panel discussion in commemoration of the 25th year anniversary of the first complete bacterial genome, the 20th year anniversary of the publication of the human genome, and the 15th year anniversary of the first human metagenome.This lecture and panel discussion will be led by NIH Director Francis Collins and will feature Dr. Eric Lander, President and Founding Director of the Broad Institute of MIT and Harvard; Dr. Claire Fraser, Director of the Institute for Genome Sciences at the University of Maryland; and Dr. Charles Rotimi, Chief of the Metabolic, Cardiovascular and Inflammatory Disease Genomics Branch at NHGRI.  The panel is titled "Fueling the next genomic revolution: Maximizing the impact of bacterial, human, and human metagenome genomic knowledge and technology."  Dr. Collins and the invited speakers will discuss highlights of the last quarter century of achievements in pathogen, commensal, and human genomics; current promises and challenges in genomics; and views on what is next in genomics including future opportunities in integrative omics, functional genomics and genomic engineering.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 10, 2021).12aMetagenomics22aMetagenome22aGenome, Bacterial22aGenetic Research 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCollins, Francis S.,emoderator,espeaker.1 aLander, Eric S.,espeaker.1 aFraser, Claire M.,espeaker.1 aRotimi, Charles N.q(Charles Nohuoma),d1957-espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40165  aAUTH03201cgm a2200445 i 101775914DNLM20260227160618.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2020    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31462  a(OCoLC)1240392377  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 506.404aThe contagion next time :bunderlying socioeconomic and racial divides and our risk from COVID and future pandemics /cSandro Galea. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded December 16, 2020.3 a(CIT): WALSHealth is a product of a broad range of social and economic conditions.  The COVID-19 pandemic showed how longstanding underinvestment in these conditions affected our overall health during a time of crisis and widened health gaps between racial/ethnic and socioeconomic groups.  COVID-19 illustrates how our extraordinary achievement in biomedical science--evidenced by remarkable advances to a COVID-19 vaccine in record time--is not matched by commensurate achievement in creating the conditions that can generate health in populations.Sandro Galea is a physician, epidemiologist, and author, is dean and Robert A. Knox Professor at Boston University School of Public Health. He previously held academic and leadership positions at Columbia University, the University of Michigan, and the New York Academy of Medicine. He has published extensively in the peer-reviewed literature, and is a regular contributor to a range of public media, about the social causes of health, mental health, and the consequences of trauma. He has been listed as one of the most widely cited scholars in the social sciences. He is chair of the board of the Association of Schools and Programs of Public Health and past president of the Society for Epidemiologic Research and of the Interdisciplinary Association for Population Health Science. He is an elected member of the National Academy of Medicine. Galea has received several lifetime achievement awards. Galea holds a medical degree from the University of Toronto, graduate degrees from Harvard University and Columbia University, and an honorary doctorate from the University of Glasgow.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 2, 2021).12aCOVID-19xepidemiology22aSocioeconomic Factors.22aMinority Groups22aPandemics 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aGalea, Sandro,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40149  aAUTH01638cgm a22004574a 101503608DNLM20260227153455.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090428s2009    mdu063 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15007  a(OCoLC)423568399  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWG 2101 aHayward, Reid.10aEffects of exercise on the chronic cardiac dysfunction associated with doxorubicin /cReid Hayward.1 iTitle on title screen:aCuring the cure :battentuating cancer treatment toxicity with exercise  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aOCCAM monthly lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed June 17, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 2 min., 11 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 11, 2009.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHeart Diseasesxchemically induced22aDoxorubicinxadverse effects22aExercise Therapy22aHeartxdrug effects22aNeoplasmsxdrug therapy 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.).bOffice of Cancer Complementary and Alternative Medicine. 0aOCCAM monthly lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15007  aAUTH02791cgm a2200433 i 101673109DNLM20260227155227.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u151203s2015    mdu062|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19329  a(OCoLC)932587878  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 900.11 aMurray, Christopher J. L.eauthor.10aNew insights from the global burden of disease 2013 study /cDr. Christopher J.L. Murray ; Fogarty International Center. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 18, 2015.3 a(CIT): Dr. Murray will present the latest findings from the Global Burden of Diseases, Injuries and Risk Factors Study (GBD), the largest and most comprehensive effort to date to measure epidemiological levels and trends and worldwide. He will discuss the conclusions of four capstone papers recently published in The Lancet. Key points are related to: child and adultmortality; causes of death; communicable, maternal, neonatal and nutritional disorders; non-communicable diseases; injuries and risk factors. A physician and economist, Murray is a professor of global health and director of the Institute for Health Metrics and Evaluation (IHME) at the University of Washington. He is a founder of the Global Burden of Disease, which he describes as a systematic, scientific effort to quantify the comparative magnitude of health loss due to diseases, injuries and risk factors by age, sex, geography and over time. GDB is a collaboration that involves morethan 1,000 researchers in over 100 countries assembling the world"s data. The IHME aims to provide the most accurate and comprehensive population health information to create a strong foundation for strategic decision making. In addition to collecting data, IHME develops methods to understand it, and trains the next generation of data scientists.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed Dec. 11, 2015).12aHealth Status Indicators22aBiomedical Research22aGlobal Health22aHealth Status22aInternationality 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aFogarty International Center,esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19329  aAUTH01827cas a2200553 a 9882736DNLM20260224111542.0cr|cnu||||||||840426c19859999dcuer p       0   a0eng    a   86649842 zsn 84005258 0 a0743-7463l0743-746321  aLANGD5  a737710bUSPS  a(DNLM)SR0059584(s)  a(OCoLC)10666233  aNSDcNSDdNSTdNSDdNSTdAIPdNSTdAGLdNSTdDLCdIULdNSTdNSDdNSTdDLCdNSTdIULdOCLdNSTdHULdCUSdNSDdMYGdDNLM  alcansdpanlmcopyc  9United States00aQD506.A1bL3500aW1bLA60710aLangmuir2dnlm0 aLangmuir 0aLangmuir00aLangmuir :bthe ACS journal of surfaces and colloids.  aWashington, DC :bAmerican Chemical Society,cc1985-  av. ;c28 cm. +esuppl. material (microfiche : negative)  aBiweekly,b<Apr. 3, 1996->  aBimonthly  aMonthly,b<Apr. 1991->  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Jan./Feb. 1985)-  aTitle from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 18, no. 13 (June 25, 2002).2 aMEDLINEbv20n1, Jan. 6 2004-2 aPubMedbv20n1, Jan. 6 2004-2 aIndex medicus  aIssued also in microfiche.  aAlso issued online.12aColloids22aSurface Properties 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Chemical Society.0 tLangmuir. Supporting informationw(DLC)   2001200014w(OCoLC)353230770 x1520-58274 uhttp://pubs.acs.org/journals/langd5/index.html  aAUTH02228cgm a2200409 i 9918716282806676DNLM20260227164233.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u231128s2023    mdu882|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33522  a(DNLM)CIT:33525  a(OCoLC)1504553134  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWH 1732 aSickle Cell in Focus Conferencen(16th :d2023 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aSickle Cell in Focus 2023 (day 1 & 2) /cNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (14 hr., 42 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 10 & 11, 2023.3 a(CIT): SCiF is a free, two-day, intensive, educational update on sickle cell disease. The conference attracts a wide audience of clinicians, academics and other healthcare professionals involved in sickle cell disease research from around the world, as well as patients and their families. Over two days, this year's conference will address the multifactorial aspects of sickle cell disease and foster the exchange of the latest scientific and clinical information related to sickle cell disease through innovative seminars and interactive panel debates. At SCiF, you will have the opportunity to learn from renowned experts, participate in engaging discussions, and network with fellow attendees who share your passion for sickle cell research and advocacy.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 3, 2025).12aAnemia, Sickle Cell 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute,esponsoring body40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=5247540uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=52477  aAUTH01264cas a2200445 a 101127833DNLM20260227090925.0cr|cn#||||||||010914c20019999enk|| p       0    0eng    a  2002243135  a1472-8222l1472-8222  a(OCoLC)49275520  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9England00aW1bEM664N10aExpert Opin Ther Targets2dnlm00aExpert opinion on therapeutic targets.  aLondon :bAshley Publications,cc2001-3 aLondon :bInforma Healthcare  av.  aTwelve no. a year  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent 