y reference book to the universities, hospitals, clinics, laboratories and general medical work of the principal cities of Europe /cby James Henry Honan ; with maps of Berlin, Edinburgh, London and Paris. 1aPhiladelphia :bP. Blakiston's Son & Co.,c1912.  aviii, 261 pages, 6 unnumbered folded leaves of plates :billustrations, maps ;c19 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes index.  aTables on lining-papers.  aPublisher's advertisement on 7 unnumbered pages bound at end.  aNLM microfilm copy shelved as: Film 2408 no. 35DNLM  aMicrofilm.b[Bethesda, Md.] :cNational Library of Medicine,d1991.e1 microfilm reel : negative ; 35 mm.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  3Copy 2cTransfer;aUS Navy Bureau of Medicine & Surgery's Office of Medical History;d2012.5DNLM  aNLM Copy 2, ownership stamp of the U.S. Naval Medical School's Edward Rhodes Stitt Library on title page.5DNLM  aNLM Copy 2, front cover includes gilt stamp (Med. Dept. U.S. Navy F.Y. 1919).5DNLM  3Copy 1aWill digitizec202011122pda5DNLM  3Copy 1aCondition reviewedc202602102pda5DNLM  aWill conservec202602102pda5DNLM12aMedicine22aEducation, Medical22aHealth Facilities22aHospitals22aSchools, Medical 2aEurope 2aDirectory  aAUTH02153cgmaa22005174a 101211537DNLM20260227144437.0vf|czahmuvd|cvaizucr|cnu||||||||vf|cjahouhd|bfa012baap040326s2004    mdu071 ||    f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:2619  a(OCoLC)55878253  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aWG 201bVC no.14 200400aTranscriptional control of heart development & disease /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  aUnited States.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  a1 videocassette of 1 (DVCPRO) (71 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/4 in.3master.  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (71 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videodisc of 1 (DVD) (71 min.) :bsd., col. ;c4 3/4 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aThe NIH director's Wednesday PM lecture  aSpeaker, Eric N. Olson.  aPresented Mar. 16, 2004.  aAlso available online.  aVideocassette (Betacam SP)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2011.e1 videocassette (71 min.) : sd., col. ; 1/2 in.  aVideodisc (DVD)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2019.e1 videodisc (71 min.) : sd., col. ; 4 3/4 in.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aHeartxembryology22aGene Expression Regulation, Developmental22aHeart Diseases22aStress, Psychological22aTranscription Factors1 aOlson, Eric N.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=2619  aAUTH03493cgm a2200469 i 101601853DNLM20260227154442.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130208s2013    mdu064|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17739  a(OCoLC)840048216  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 501 aFreedman, Barry,eauthor.10aGenomic factors impacting health in peoples of African descent /cBarry Freedman. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 4 min.) :bcolor, sound).  atwo-dimensional moving image2rdacontent  acomputer2rdamedia  aonline resource2rdacarrier1 aWednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Jan. 9, 2013.3 a(CIT): Self-reported ethnicity reflects many sources of variation, including genetic. Biologic variation is associated with several population-specific risks for disease. There are several inherited factors associated with African American populations compared to European American, and these include differences in the risk for type 2 diabetes mellitus, non-diabetic nephropathy, kidney stones, atherosclerosis, and osteoporosis. Treatment responses to interferon therapy for hepatitis C also depend on interferon-lambda3 (IL28B) genotypes, which differ markedly across population groups and partially explain ethnic specific response rates. Dr. Freedman will discuss these topics as well as review Regional Admixture Mapping (RAM) and Mapping by Admixture Linkage Disequilibrium (MALD) for detecting genetic associations in admixed populations.   Of particular scientific interest, non-diabetic nephropathy susceptibility in African Americans is dependent on variation in the apolipoprotein L1 gene (APOL1). Coding APOL1 risk variants protect from African sleeping sickness. Risk variants are virtually absent in European-derived populations, but 12 percent of African Americans possess two risk variants.  APOL1-associated nephropathy encompasses up to 40 percent of all end-stage renal disease (ESRD) in African Americans, including HIV-associated nephropathy, idiopathic focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, "hypertension-attributed nephropathy," sickle cell nephropathy, and ESRD associated with lupus nephritis. Second hits, such as genes or environmental factors, appear to be required for nephropathy initiation. This finding alters the epidemiology of ESRD in African Americans; that is, hypertension is not a common cause of nephropathy. Thus, understanding the genetic basis of ethnic differences in disease will clarify the roles of environmental exposures on risk and lead to development of novel treatments.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed Mar. 29, 2013.12aGenetic Predisposition to Diseasexethnology22aBlack or African American.22aMinority Health22aRisk Factors 2aUnited Statesxethnology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17739  aAUTH01192cas a2200385 a 8608609DNLM20260227091017.0cr|cn#||||||||860724d19852025mdumr p       0||| 0eng    a0882-5645l0882-5645y0882-5646  a(DNLM)SR0056120(s)  a(OCoLC)11880370  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bTO54FD 0aTopics in pain management00aTopics in pain management.  aLittleton, Mass. :bPSG Pub. Co.,cc1985-2025.3 3<2000-2025>:aHagerstown, MD :bLippincott Williams & Wilkins  av.  aMonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (June 1985)-1 aCeased with Volume 41(5) (December 2025).  aTitle from caption.  aPlace of publication varies.  aAlso issued online. 2aPain 2aPeriodical0 x2163-83574 uhttps://ovidsp.ovid.com/ovidweb.cgi?T=JS&NEWS=n&CSC=Y&PAGE=toc&D=yrovft&AN=00587875-000000000-00000  aAUTH01522cgm a22004334a 101268457DNLM20260227150630.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu064 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12023  a(OCoLC)84671449  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 5361 aAlter, Harvey J.14aThe natural and unnatural history of hepatitis C :bprolific payoffs from the perfect patient /cHarvey Alter.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aGreat teachers grand rounds  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 14, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 3 min., 17 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 12, 2004.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aHepatitis C22aHepacivirus 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aClinical Center grand rounds great teache