 aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM12aComplementary Therapies22aWomen's Health1 aChesney, Margaret A.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aORWH women's health seminar series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3171  aAUTH01313cas a2200421 a 101465592DNLM20260227090922.0cr|cn ||||||||070702c20089999nyu|| p       |    0eng    a  20072144830 a1938-9736l1946-9837  a(OCoLC)150539420  aNSDcNSDdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States10aISSN RECORD00aW1bSE489RH10aTop Companion Anim Med2dnlm00aTopics in companion animal medicine.  aNew York, N.Y. :bElsevier  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Vol. 23, no. 1 (Feb. 2008).  aDescription based on first issue; title from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv23n1, Feb. 2008-0 aPubMedbv23n1, Feb. 2008-  aAlso issued online.12aVeterinary Medicine22aAnimals, Domestic22aAnimals, Domesticxsurgery 2aPeriodical0 x1946-983700tClinical techniques in small animal practicex1096-2867w(DLC)sn 97000995w(OCoLC)377537474 uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/19389736  aAUTH02027cgm a22004094a 101465457DNLM20260227153358.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080201s2008    mdu059|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14248  a(OCoLC)265646786  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWP 4591 aLaughlin, Shannon.10aUterine fibroids--an understudied condition :bepidemiologic research at NIEHS /cShannon Laughlin and Donna Baird.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 28, 2008)  aStreaming video (59 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 18, 2008.3 a(CIT): Women's Health Special Interest Group (WHSIG) Intramural Program on Research on Women's Health Sponsored by the NIH Office of Intramural Research and the NIH Office of Research on Women's Health.  Come join your fellow NIH colleagues in a scientific exchange on issues related to the biology and pathology of sex and gender differences and effects on women's health.Shannon Laughlin, M.D. NIH Clinical/Translational Fellow in Women's Health Epidemiology Branch, NIEHS  and Donna Baird, Ph.D. Mentor, NIH Clinical/Translational Fellow in Women's Health Epidemiologist and Senior Staff Fellow Epidemiology Branch, NIEHS For more information, visit  http://orwh.od.nih.gov.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aUterine Neoplasmsxepidemiology22aLeiomyomaxepidemiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bWomen's Health Special Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14248  aAUTH01741cas a2200469   0370536DNLM20260224111546.0cr|cn ||||||||730925c19479999dcu x p       0    0eng    a0003-2700l0003-2700  aANCHAM  a(DNLM)A29880000(s)  a(OCoLC)01481078  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bAN191710aAnal Chem2dnlm00aAnalytical chemistry.  aWashington,bAmerican Chemical Society.  av.billus., ports.  aTwenty four no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 19-        1947-  aIncludes 1949?-        annual review issues (applications reviews and fundamental reviews in alternate years); 1964-65, Buyers guide (formerly issued as part of the review issues).2 aMEDLINEbv37n11,Oct. 1965-2 aPubMedbv37n11,Oct. 1965-0 aOLDMEDLINE2 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aContinues Industrial and engineering chemistry; analytical edition.  3Vol. 63, no. 1 (JAN 1 1991)c19911012fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aChemistry Techniques, Analytical 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Chemical Society.0 x1520-688200tIndustrial and engineering chemistry; analytical editionx0096-4484w(DLC)sn 79018915w(OCoLC)169570105tLaboratory guide to instruments, equipment and chemicalsw(OCoLC)55875774 uhttp://pubs.acs.org/journals/ancham/index.html  aAUTH04575cgm a2200445 i 101609783DNLM20260227154954.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130604s2013    mdu074 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17954  a(OCoLC)857718946  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 501 aGalea, Sandro,eauthor.10aIs it all about me? :bthe role of public health in an era of personalized medicine /cSandro Galea.1 aRole of public health in an era of personalized medicine 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 14 min.) :bcolor, sound)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdm2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBSSR lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded May 10, 2013.3 a(CIT): BSSR Lecture Series.  Investment in understanding the genetic basis of disease has long promised a potentially revolutionary way to predict which individuals will get disease and to tailor treatments to individuals with particular genetic sequences. In some respects the genetic revolution has indeed brought important triumphs. On many fronts, however, the promise of personalized medicine remains quite far from realized and the science is encountering substantial stumbling blocks in bridging the gap between genetic sequencing and tailoring medical treatment.  We suggest that despite the promise of personalized medicine approaches, we are not going to be in a position any time soon to rely on an individual-based approach for disease prediction or cure. We base this argument on a formal understanding of the mathematical limits of individual prediction. Using simulation models we show how the drivers of individual disease risk are as much (and in many cases more) features of an individual's environment as they are individual genetic or other characteristics. This argues strongly for a greater re-engagement in the behavioral and social sciences that attempt to identify approaches to improving the fundamental drivers of individual and population health.  Dr. Galea is the Anna Cheskis Gelman and Murray Charles Gelman Professor and Chair of the Department of Epidemiology at the Columbia University Mailman School of Public Health. Dr. Galea is a physician and an epidemiologist. Dr. Galea's research program seeks to uncover how determinants at multiple levels--including policies, features of the social environment, molecular, and genetic factors--jointly influence the health of urban populations. His work also explores innovative methodological approaches to population health questions. His primary focus is on the causes of mental disorders, particularly common mood-anxiety disorders and substance abuse. His work has documented the mental health consequences of mass trauma and conflict worldwide, including as a result of the September 11 attacks, Hurricane Katrina, conflicts in sub-Saharan Africa, and the American wars in Iraq and Afghanistan. The National Institutes of Health, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, and several foundations have funded his research. He has published over 400 scientific journal articles, 50 chapters and commentaries, and 7 books and his research has been featured in The New York Times, NPR, the Wall Street Journal, and many other media outlets.  During Dr. Galea's tenure as Chair, the Department of Epidemiology has launched several new educational initiatives and substantially increased its focus on six core areas: chronic, infectious, injury, lifecourse, psychiatric/neurological, and social epidemiology. Dr. Galea chairs the New York City Department of Health and Mental Hygiene's Community Services Board and sits on its Health Board. He is president of the Society for Epidemiologic Research and an elected member of the Institute of Medicine of the National Academies of Science.  aDescription based on version viewed June 26, 2013.12aGenetic Predisposition to Disease22aGenetics, Medical22aPrecision Medicine 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aBSSR lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17954  aAUTH01821nam a2200385 i 9918986651206676DNLM20260211104606.0200609t20212021fluac    b    001 0 eng    a9781498771580q(hbk)  z9780429154317q(ebk)  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aSF 997.5.R400aDiseases and pathology of reptiles,nvol. 2,pNoninfectious diseases and and pathology of reptiles :bcolor atlas and text /cedited by Michael M. Garner, Elliott R. Jacobson.1 aNoninfectious diseases and and pathology of reptiles  aFirst edition. 1aBoca Raton, FL :bCRC Press,c2021. 4c©2021  axvi, 517 pages :billustrations (chiefly color), portraits ;c29 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  astill imagebsti2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"This book accompanies Infectious diseases and pathology of reptiles, second edition, to cover noninfectious diseases of reptiles."--Page 4 of cover.  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.0 aReptile neoplasia -- Nutritional and metabolic diseases -- Depositional diseases -- Normal and abnormal reptile development -- Degenerative diseases -- Trauma and physical diseases -- Environmental and miscellaneous toxicoses in reptiles -- Physiology and diseases of the reproductive system -- Reptile cardiovascular system -- Bone alteration by disease.12aNoncommunicable Diseasesxveterinary12aReptiles22aAnimal Diseasesxpathology 2aAtlas1 aJacobson, Elliott R.,d1945-eeditor.1 aGarner, Michael M.,eeditor.  aAUTH01797nam a2200469 i 9918990488206676DNLM20260225173903.0241102s2024    sz a     b    100 0 eng    a9783031539046qhardbound  a3031539044qhardbound  a9783031453793qeBook3 a9783031539046  a(OCoLC)1465380112  aOHXbengerdacOHX  apcc  9Switzerland00aW1bSP685MD v.25 202410aQT 34.5 7aTP2lcco 7aQH2lcco2 aInternational Symposium on Mechanobiologyn(4th :d2022 :cSydney, Australia)jauthor.10aMechanobiology :bproceedings of the 4th International Symposium on Mechanobiology, 6th - 9th November 2022. Sydney, Australia /cBoris Martinac, Charles D. Cox, Kate Poole, Sara Baratchi, Daryan Kempe, editors. 1aCham, Switzerland :bSpringer ;a[Melbourne] :bAuSMB, Australian Society for Mechanobiology,c[2024] 4c©2024  axv, 124 pages :billustrations (chiefly color) ;c25 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aSpringer series in biophysics,x0932-2353 ;vvolume 25  aChapter 4 is Open Access.  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aBiomechanical Phenomena 2aConference Proceedings1 aMartinac, Boris,eeditor.1 aCox, Charles D.,eeditor.1 aPoole, Kate,eeditor.1 aBaratchi, Sara,eeditor.1 aKempe, Daryan,eeditor.2 aAustralian Society for Mechanobiology,eissuing body. 0aSpringer series in biophysics ;vv. 25.x0932-2353  aAUTH01488cgm a2200433 i 101771605DNLM20260227160229.0m     o  z f      vz|czau|ucr|cna||||||||200917s2020    mdu059 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35959  a(OCoLC)1200848344  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 33700aHow epilepsy can teach us about human memory /cKareem A. Zaghloul, Sara K. Inati. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCC grand rounds  aRecorded March 11, 2020.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed October 8, 2020).12aMemoryxphysiology22aEpilepsyxphysiopathology22aSeizuresxphysiopathology 2aWebcast 2aLecture1 aZaghloul, Kareem A.,espeaker.1 aInati, Sara K.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=35959  aAUTH02326cgm a2200445 i 101752791DNLM20260227160025.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190815s2019    mdu069|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:28656  a(OCoLC)1120086741  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 27400aIs relapse and recycling necessary for recovery from alcohol use disorder? /cCarlo C. DiClemente. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 9 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIAAA Jack Mendelson lecture ;v11th  aRecorded July 11, 2019.3 a(CIT): Carlo C. DiClemente, Ph.D., is an internationally distinguished scientist whose research has revolutionized the behavioral treatment and prevention of addiction and other health-related disorders. He has made groundbreaking contributions to understanding the process of human intentional behavior change. Dr. DiClemente is the co-developer of the Transtheoretical Model of behavior change (TTM), a model which had its beginnings in the field of smoking and addictive behaviors. The TTM is now used by researchers across multiple disciplines around the world, including alcohol treatment research, cancer prevention, HIV risk reduction, dietary change, exercise, occupational safety, and rehabilitation of health and addictive behaviors.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed September 14, 2019).12aAlcoholismxrehabilitation22aBehavior Therapy22aBehavior, Addictivexrehabilitation22aRecurrence 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aDiClemente, Carlo C.,espeaker.2 aNational Institute on Alcohol Abuse and Alcoholism (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aAnnual Jack Mendelson honorary lecture ;v11th.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?28656  aAUTH03692cgm a22004694a 101465430DNLM20260227152257.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080201s2008    mdu052 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14228  a(OCoLC)288982134  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 58.51 aKaguni, Laurie.10aProtein dynamics at the mitochondrial replication fork /cLaurie Kaguni.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen (viewed Nov. 21, 2008).  aStreaming video (51 min., 27 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 9, 2008.3 a(CIT): The mitochondrial replicase (pol gamma) comprises two subunits, a large catalytic core (pol gamma-alpha) and a smaller accessory subunit (pol gamma-betta) that enhances holoenzyme activity and processivity. Mutagenesis of four conserved sequence elements located within the spacer region between the DNA polymerase and 3'-5' exonuclease active sites in pol gamma-alpha demonstrates their functional roles in holoenzyme activity, processivity and/ or DNA binding affinity. Several mutations also affect differentially DNA polymerase and exonuclease activity, and/ or functional interactions with mitochondrial single-stranded DNA-binding protein (mtSSB). Overexpression of the catalytic core in the nervous system of Drosophila induces severe mtDNA depletion and reduces median life span. A parallel mutagenesis of the human accessory subunit, in combination with the determination of its crystal structure and molecular modeling, elucidates its role as a novel type of processivity factor. Loss of function alleles result in mtDNA depletion and developmental lethality in Drosophila. A human pol gamma/DNA complex model was developed using the structures of the pol gamma beta dimer and the T7 DNA polymerase ternary complex, which suggests multiple regions of subunit interaction between pol gamma beta and the human catalytic core that allow it to encircle the newly synthesized double-stranded DNA, and thereby enhance DNA binding affinity and holoenzyme processivity.  Functional complexes of pol gamma, mtSSB and a novel mitochondrial DNA helicase reconstitute the mitochondrial DNA replication fork. The human mtDNA helicase exists as a hexamer/ heptamer and exhibits a modular architecture that is highly similar to that of bacteriophage T7 primase-helicase and E. coli DnaB protein.  Molecular analysis of active site and selected human disease alleles of the Drosophila homolog by overexpression in Schneider cells results in a dominant-negative lethal phenotype resulting from mtDNA depletion.  This work was supported by NIH grant GM45295.   For more information, visit  http://www.bch.msu.edu/faculty/kagunil.htm   NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDNA, Mitochondrialxgenetics22aDNA Replication22aDNA-Directed DNA Polymerase22aMitochondrial Diseases22aMitochondrial Proteins 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14228  aAUTH02345cgm a2200421 i 101658201DNLM20260227155027.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150505s2015    mdu054|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18967  a(OCoLC)909852234  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 359.51 aRaymond, Lynn A.eauthor.10aNMDA receptor trafficking and synaptic dysfunction in Huntington disease /cLynn A. Raymond. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (54 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aRecorded April 27, 2015.3 a(CIT): Neuroscience Seminar Series  Dr. Raymond is a professor and movement disorders neurologist in the Brain Research Centre, Departments of Psychiatry and Medicine.  She has been actively researching molecular mechanisms underlying neurodegeneration in cell culture and animal models for the past 15 years, with a focus on Huntington disease. Her laboratory uses multiple techniques and approaches, with particular emphasis on electrophysiology to assess changes in neuronal transmission and function, especially those that occur in basal ganglia disorders. Most of these studies describe physiological assessments of glutamate receptor function and modulation, as well as glutamatergic synaptic transmission in striatal or hippocampal neurons. In addition, she has contributed to studies involving observational and clinical trials in human Huntington disease.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 17, 2015).12aHuntington Diseasexphysiopathology22aReceptors, N-Methyl-D-Aspartatexphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18967  aAUTH02327cgm a22004574a 101475725DNLM20260227152648.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080602s2008    mdu067 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14507  a(OCoLC)233260890  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 102.51 aAssad, John A.10aNeuronal encoding of behavioral relevance in parietal cortex /cJohn A. Assad.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen (viewed June 26, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 6 min., 15 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 19, 2008.3 a(CIT): You are invited to attend the next NIH Neuroscience Series lecture on May 19, 2008 at noon in the Lipsett Amphitheater of the NIH Clinical Center, 10 Center Drive, Bethesda, MD. John A. Assad, Ph.D., Professor, Department of Neurobiology, Harvard Medical School, will present his seminar on "Neuronal encoding of behavioral relevance in parietal cortex". Dr. Assad's laboratory uses electrophysiological recording techniques in awake, behaving monkeys to explore mechanisms underlying visual perception. They focus on the question "How does what we know influence what we see?" Dr. Assad's research has demonstrated how a global understanding of the visual scene exerts a powerful influence over how we perceive its details. Dr. Assad's seminar will describe how the neuronal activity in the parietal cortex encodes visual perception. NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeuronsxphysiology22aBehaviorxphysiology22aParietal Lobexphysiology22aVisual Perceptionxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14507  aAUTH03018nam a2200481 i 9918986647706676DNLM20260212082940.0230208s2024    nyua     b    001 0 eng    a  2023005149  a9780826169952q(softcover)  a0826169953q(softcover)  z9780826169969q(ebook)  a(OCoLC)1372499289  aDLCbengerdacDLCdAHHdOCLCFdOCLCOdOCLCLdIG#  apcc  9United States00aRJ505.E9bA35 202400aWS 350.300a618.92/8914223/eng/202303101 aAdler-Tapia, Robbie,eauthor.10aEMDR and the art of psychotherapy with children :bguidebook and treatment manual /cRobbie Adler-Tapia, PhD, Carolyn Settle, MSW, LCSW.  aThird edition. 1aNew York, NY :bSpringer Publishing Company,c[2024]  axvii, 370 pages :billustrations ;c28 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aPreceded by EMDR and the art of psychotherapy with children/infants to adolescents / Robbie Adler-Tapia and Carolyn Settle. Second edition. [2017]  aPreceded by EMDR and the art of psychotherapy with children/infants to adolescents treatment manual / Robbie Adler-Tapia and Carolyn Settle. Second edition. [2017]  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  a"A great deal has changed in the world and in the world of psychotherapy since the publication of the second edition in 2017. Therapists, children, and families have faced a global pandemic, struggled through the traumatization of increased school shootings, and coped with escalating divisiveness within communities. Needless to say, children and families need more support and encouragement than ever. To that end, EMDR and the Art of Psychotherapy With Children: Guidebook and Treatment Manual, 3rd edition, assists child therapists with new EMDR therapy approaches and tools designed to meet these challenges while incorporating the foundational procedures of standard EMDR therapy. This new edition incorporates in one volume the adapted second-edition text and the treatment manual for convenience and accessibility.”--cProvided by publisher.12aEye Movement Desensitization Reprocessingxmethods22aPsychotherapyxmethods22aPsychotherapeutic Processes22aInfant22aChild22aAdolescent1 aSettle, Carolyn,eauthor.1 iPreceded by (work):aAdler-Tapia, Robbie.tEMDR and the art of psychotherapy with children/infants to adolescents.1 iPreceded by (work):aAdler-Tapia, Robbie.tEMDR and the art of psychotherapy with children/infants to adolescents treatment manual.  ihas work:aEMDR and the art of psychotherapy with children treatment manual (Text)4https://id.oclc.org/worldcat/ontology/hasWork  aAUTH01790nam a2200397 i 9918986629706676DNLM20260205134921.0231018s2025    njua     bf   001 0 eng    a  2023046048  a9780826188519  a0826188516  z9780826188526q(ebook)  a(OCoLC)1414175525  aDLCbengerdacDLCdIG#dCQC  apcc  9United States10aRC76b.E96 202500aWB 3900a616.07/54223/eng/2023121300aEvidence-based physical examination handbook /cKate Sustersic Gawlik, DNP, APRN-CNP, FAANP, FNAP, FAAN, Bernadette Mazurek Melnyk, PhD, APRN-CNP, EBP-C, FAANP, FNAP, FAAN, Alice M. Teall, DNP, APRN-CNP, NC-BC, FAANP, FNAP, editors.  aSecond edition. 1aPrinceton, NJ :bSpringer Publishing,c[2025]  axvi, 437 pages :bcolor illustrations ;c18 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  a"Evidence-Based Physical Examination Handbook, Second Edition, was developed as a guide to the strategies and best practices needed by clinicians to assess an individual's health and well-being. This handbook is intended to be used by a broad audience of health science students and clinicians across disciplines who strive to exemplify excellence in evidence-based assessment and practice"--cProvided by publisher.12aPhysical Examinationxmethods22aEvidence-Based Medicine 2aHandbook1 aGawlik, Kate,eeditor.1 aMelnyk, Bernadette Mazurek,eeditor.1 aTeall, Alice M.,eeditor.  aAUTH01433cas a2200433 a 9212591DNLM20260220134434.0cr|||#||||||||920827c19919999cau x m       0    0eng    a   92660565 zsn 91026914  a1063-5823l1063-5823  aCTMEET  a(DNLM)SR0073932(s)  a(OCoLC)24814151  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bCU82IW10aCurr Top Membr2dnlm 0aCurrent topics in membranes00aCurrent topics in membranes.  aSan Diego :bAcademic Press,cc1991-  av. :bill., ports.  aIrregular  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 38-1 aMEDLINEbv67, 2011-0 aPubMedbv67, 2011-  aAlso issued online.  aSome vols. issued by Yale University, Dept. of Cellular and Molecular Physiology. 2aCell Membrane 2aPeriodical2 aYale University.bDepartment of Cellular and Molecular Physiology.00tCurrent topics in membranes and transportx0