urn:nbn:de:bvb:12-bsb10367460-02urn  a(OCoLC)1035851439  aCOObengerdad20260210dOCLCGdDEBBGdOCLCQdDNLM  9Germany00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 2140 no.11 aZenker, F. A.q(Friedrich Albert),d1825-1898eauthor.10aÜber die Regeneration des quergestreiften Muskelgewebes :beine historisch-kritische Untersuchung zur theoretischen Pathologie /cvon Dr. Friedrich Albert Zenker. 1aLeipzig :bJ.B. Hirschfeld,c1864.  a26 pages ;c31 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"Separatabdruck aus: Zenker, Die Veränderungen der willkührlichen Muskeln im Typhus abdominalis. Leipzig, F.C.W. Vogel. 1864"--Title page verso.  aProgramm (Eintritt in den Academischen Senat)--Erlangen.  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aMuscle, Striatedxphysiology22aRegeneration  aAUTH03530pam a2200469 i 9918986651006676DNLM20260225093508.0230211s2023    flua     b    000 0 eng    a  2022052692  aGBC3535542bnb  a9780367627355q(hardback)  a0367627353  a9780367647360q(paperback)  a0367647362  z9781003126010q(ebook)  z9781000873580qePub ebook  z9781000873559qPDF ebook  a(OCoLC)1350521617  aLBSORbengerdacDLCdBDXdUKMGBdYDXdOCLCFdOCLCOdIG#  apcc  9United States00aR853.C55bZ43 202300aW 20.55.C500a610.72/7223/eng/202303241 aZhang, Song,d1976-eauthor.10aDesign and analysis of pragmatic trials /cSong Zhang, Chul Ahn and Hong Zhu.  aFirst edition. 1aBoca Raton :bCRC Press,c2023.  avii, 206 pages :billustrations ;c24 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aChapman & Hall/CRC biostatistics series  aIncludes bibliographical references.0 aPragmatic randomized trials -- Cluster randomized trials -- Matched-pair cluster randomized design for pragmatic studies -- Stratified cluster randomized design for pragmatic studies -- The gee approach for stepped-wedge trial design -- The mixed-effect model approach and adaptive strategies for stepped-wedge trial design.  a"This book begins with an introduction of pragmatic cluster randomized trials (PCTs) and reviews various pragmatic issues that need to be addressed by statisticians at the design stage. It discusses the advantages and disadvantages of each type of PCT, and provides sample size formulas, sensitivity analyses, and examples for sample size calculation. The generalized estimating equation (GEE) method will be employed to derive sample size formulas for various types of outcomes from the exponential family, including continuous, binary, and count variables. Experimental designs that have been frequently employed in PCTs will be discussed, including cluster randomized designs, matched-pair cluster randomized design, stratified cluster randomized design, stepped-wedge cluster randomized design, longitudinal cluster randomized design, and crossover cluster randomized design. It demonstrates that the GEE approach is flexible to accommodate pragmatic issues such as hierarchical correlation structures, different missing data patterns, randomly varying cluster sizes, etc. It has been reported that the GEE approach leads to under-estimated variance with limited numbers of clusters. The remedy for this limitation is investigated for the design of PCTs. This book can assist practitioners in the design of PCTs by providing a description of the advantages and disadvantages of various PCTs and sample size formulas that address various pragmatic issues, facilitating the proper implementation of PCTs to improve health care. It can also serve as a textbook for biostatistics students at the graduate level to enhance their knowledge or skill in clinical trial design"--cProvided by publisher.12aPragmatic Clinical Trials as Topic1 aAhn, Chul,d1956-eauthor.1 aZhu, Hongc(Professor of biostatistics)eauthor. 0aChapman & Hall/CRC biostatistics series  aAUTH01443cas a2200469   7704186DNLM20260227090913.0cr|cn ||||||||770527c19769999ja br         0||| 0eng    a0385-0005l0385-0005  aTJEMDR  a(DNLM)T11820000(s)  a(OCoLC)03155885  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aeng  9Japan00aW1bTO1847CJ10aTokai J Exp Clin Med2dnlm 0aTokai journal of experimental and clinical medicine04aThe Tokai journal of experimental and clinical medicine.  aTokyo,bTokai Univ. School of Medicine and the Tokai Medical Assn.3 aTokyo :bTokai University School Of Medicine  av.bill., ports.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-    Jan. 1976-  aPrint ceased with v. 30, no. 4 (2006)1 aMEDLINEbv5n1,Jan. 1980-1 aPubMedbv5n1,Jan. 1980-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aOfficial publication of the Tokai Medical Association. 2aMedicine 2aPeriodical2 aTōkai Daigaku.bIgakubu.2 aTokai Medical Association.0 x2185-22434 uhttp://mj-med-u-tokai.com/  aAUTH03256cgm a2200445 i 101697732DNLM20260227155539.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u161221s2016    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:20026  a(OCoLC)971623130  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 24100aOverdiagnosis in cancer screening :bovercoming challenges, avoiding mistakes /cRuth B. Etzioni. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aMedicine: mind the gap  aRecorded November 18, 2016.3 a(CIT): Medicine: Mind the Gap Seminar  3-Part Series on Disease Prevention Screening  Series Topic #3: Overdiagnosis in Cancer Screening: Overcoming Challenges, Avoiding Mistakes  Presented by Ruth B. Etzioni, Ph.D.  Chair, Scientific Steering Committee Hutch Data Commonwealth Division of Public Health Sciences Fred Hutchinson Cancer Research Center  The problem of overdiagnosis, the detection by screening of latent cancers that would never have surfaced, has been much in the news lately. What is overdiagnosis, and how significant is the problem? In this presentation, Dr. Etzioni will examine how overdiagnosis arises and will discuss what it takes to validly estimate its frequency. Different types of study designs and estimation methods have been used, but many of these probably yield biased results. Consumers of overdiagnosis studies need to carefully navigate the published literature to properly understand the extent of the problem. Dr. Etzioni will provide a guide using examples from breast and prostate cancer screening to identify the types of studies that are most likely to produce reliable results.  Dr. Etzioni is a biostatistician and full member in the Division of Public Health Sciences at the Fred Hutchinson Cancer Research Center. Her research program focuses on the development of innovative statistical and computer models to learn about the latent process of cancer progression from observed data on disease incidence and mortality. This knowledge is applied to fill in the evidence gaps that inevitably arise when developing policies for cancer detection and management.  Dr. Etzioni will accept questions about her presentation via email at prevention@mail.nih.gov and on Twitter with #NIHMtG.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed December 31, 2016).12aNeoplasmsxdiagnosis22aEarly Detection of Cancer22aMedical Overuse22aModels, Statistical 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aEtzioni, Ruth B.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aMedicine: mind the gap lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?20026  aAUTH01482nam a2200361 i 9918787481606676DNLM20260223153100.0921103r19571957ilu           000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)26907751  aVA@bengerdacVA@dOCLCQdOCLCL  apcc  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.671 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d19