g    a1233-1821l1233-1821  aFOSCFI  a(DNLM)SR0089756(s)  a(OCoLC)32995600  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Poland00aW1bFO99110aFound Sci2dnlm00aFoundations of science.  aWarszawa :bOficyna Akademicka,c1995-  av.  a4 no. a year,b1999-  aTwo no. a year,b-1998  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (1995/96)-0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.  aOfficial journal of the Association for Foundations of Science, Language, and Cognition.02aScience 2aPeriodical2 aAssociation for the Foundations of Science, Language, and Cognition.0 x1572-84714 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/10699  aAUTH03124cgm a22004334a 101317974DNLM20260227152313.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071003s2007    mdu076|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14006  a(OCoLC)182912767  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 26.51 aShneiderman, Ben.10aInformation visualization for medical informatics :bfrom patient histories to clinical research /cBen Shneiderman.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNLM informatics lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 28, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 16 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Aug. 30, 2007.3 a(CIT): NLM Informatics Lecture Series   The current challenge for medical informatics interface designers is to enable users to harness the flood of information into a productive stream of meaningful information that supports creative and effective decision making. Novel strategies in information visualization allow users to explore in systematic yet flexible ways, while recording their interaction history and collaborating with relevant colleagues.   We begin with commercial success stories such as Spotfire, SmartMoney's MarketMap, or the HiveGroup and explore their application to medical informatics. Then we look at research tools for patient history search and clinical data analysis. They place human decision making and discovery at the heart of interface design. A final theme is the integration of personal medical devices into the data collection process so as to increase physician and patient resources for decision making.   Ben Shneiderman http://www.cs.umd.edu/~ben is a Professor in the Department of Computer Science, Founding Director (1983-2000) of the Human-Computer Interaction Laboratory http://www.cs.umd.edu/hcil and Member of the Institute for Advanced Computer Studies at the University of Maryland at College Park. He was elected as a Fellow of the Association for Computing Machinery (ACM) in 1997 and a Fellow of the American Association for the Advancement of Science (AAAS) in 2001. He received the ACM SIGCHI Lifetime Achievement Award in 2001. He is a member of the NLM Board of Scientific Counselors. Ben is the author of several books and textbooks. His recent books include Leonardo's Laptop: Human Needs and the New Computing Technologies (MIT Press) which won the IEEE book award in 2004.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMedical Informatics22aVisual Perception 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.) 0aNLM informatics lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14006  aAUTH01772cgm a22004454a 101317972DNLM20260227152346.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071003s2007    mdu425 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14009  a(OCoLC)190751362  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQV 26900aPhase 0 trials in oncologic drug development /cNational Cancer Institute.3 aPhase zero trials in oncologic drug development  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed Nov. 29, 2007).  aStreaming video (7 hr., 4 min., 48 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 5, 2007.3 a(CIT): The workshop goal is to establish what the Phase 0 effort is about and its potential value; to lay out what is needed to perform Phase 0 trials; to propose ways to evaluate the Phase 0 effort; to determine future research directions for the next 2-3 years.   For more information, visit  http://www.blsmeetings.net/phase0workshop.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aAntineoplastic Agents22aClinical Trials as Topic22aDrug Design22aNeoplasmsxdrug therapy22aNontherapeutic Human Experimentation 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14009  aAUTH00822cam a22002657i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003500073044001000108060003300118245009100151250001400242264004800256300003000304336002600334337002800360338002700388650003300415650001700448650002100465651001000486710005100496999000900547101609762DNLM20260209110920.0970725s1956    cc a          000 0 chi    aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1778 no.1000aNong ye sheng chan he zuo she bao jian yuan ke ben /cHubei Sheng wei sheng ting bian.  aDi 1 ban. 1aHankou :bHubei ren min chu ban she,c1956.  a86 pages :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier12aCommunicable Disease Control22aRural Health22aHealth Education 2aChina1 aHubei Sheng (China).bWei sheng ting,eeditor.  aAUTH01692cgm a2200433 i 9918975958006676DNLM20260227165044.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u250104s2024    mdu198|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35412  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 83.12 aImplementation Research and Next Steps for Direct-to-Consumer Telehealth: Understanding Current Utilization, Lessons Learned & Opportunities (Workshop)d(2024 :cOnlin