nstitute on Aging, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (7 hr., 49 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded March 1 & 2, 2023.3 a(CIT): The National Institute on Aging (NIA) is planning a workshop on March 1 & 2, 2023 exploring computational approaches to advance studies of aging and AD/ADRD.  The workshop would feature a series of brief presentations and discussion including, but not limited to, the following topics: Identifying computational techniques uniquely suited to answer aging and neurodegeneration research questions .Developing computational models of biological and behavioral changes across the lifespan .Identification and development of needed resources and techniques to effectively interrogate large- and multi-scale datasets .Strategies to promote collaboration between computational scientists and aging researchers.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 19, 2024).12aDementia12aAgingxphysiology22aNeurodegenerative Diseases22aComputational Biologyxmethods 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute on Aging,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4920640uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49208  aAUTH01370cgm a22003854a 101267757DNLM20260227145557.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s1999    mdu039 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10637  a(OCoLC)69672714  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 35000aMitochondrial protein import /cNIH - Mitochondria Interest Group.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c1999]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aLunch time review.  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 26, 2006).  aStreaming video (39 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 22, 1999.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This videocast is the work of the United States Government and no copyright exits.  This may be disseminated freely.12aMitochondrial Proteinsxphysiology22aProtein Transportxphysiology 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMitochondria Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10637  aAUTH02638cgm a2200433 i 9918625746406676DNLM20260227163650.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230602s2023    mdu529|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33140  a(DNLM)CIT:33145  a(OCoLC)1455526919  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWF 140.32 aTackling Brain and Muscle Dysfunctions in ARDS Survivors (Workshop)d(2023 :cOnline)jauthor.10aTackling brain and muscle dysfunction in ARDS survivors /cNHLBI, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (8 hr., 49 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 27 & 28, 2023.3 a(CIT): Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) is a serious lung condition that is defined by life-threatening low blood oxygen and often causes dysfunction in multiple organs. People who survive ARDS often face significant cognitive impairment and limited body mobility, which reduces their quality of life. The molecular mechanisms underlying these long-term health issues in ARDS survivors remain elusive. Emerging data suggest that neuroinflammation and muscle wasting contribute to brain and muscle dysfunction in ARDS survivors, but researchers need to better understand the pathophysiology of this area.NHLBI will host a virtual workshop that aims to evaluate the current research, critical knowledge gaps, key barriers, and research opportunities in understanding the pathophysiology of brain and muscle dysfunction in ARDS survivors. The workshop features panel sessions and breakout discussions. Topics include clinical presentation, social determinants of health, pathogenesis and recovery, and interventions.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 26, 2024).12aRespiratory Distress Syndromexphysiopathology12aRespiratory Distress Syndromexcomplications22aNeuromuscular Manifestations 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4958340uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49585  aAUTH01697cas a2200481 a 8913523DNLM20260227091028.0cr|cn ||||||||890803c19889999mdubr p       0    0eng    a0899-3459l0899-3459  a(DNLM)SR0064654(s)  a(OCoLC)18068151  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bTO539V10aTop Magn Reson Imaging2dnlm 0aTopics in magnetic resonance imaging00aTopics in magnetic resonance imaging :bTMRI.30aTMRI  a[Frederick, MD :bAspen Publishers,cc1988-3 aHagerstown, MD :bLippincott Williams & Wilkins  av. :bill.  aBimonthly  aQuarterly,b19??-1995  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Dec. 1988)-  aTitle from cover.  a"An Aspen publication".  aEach issue has a distinctive title.  aPrint issues ceased with v. 27, no. 6 (2018).1 aMEDLINEbv1n1,Dec. 1988-1 aPubMedbv1n1,Dec. 1988-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso available online.  3Vol. 7, no. 2 (SPRING 1995)c19950520fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paperxFurther review is required to determine exactly when acid-free paper was first used for this title 2aMagnetic Resonance Imaging 2aPeriodical0 x1536-10044 uhttps://ovidsp.ovid.com/ovidweb.cgi?T=JS&NEWS=n&CSC=Y&PAGE=toc&D=yrovft&AN=00002142-000000000-00000  aAUTH01788cgm a22004334a 101267701DNLM202