mensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 7, 2007).  aStreaming video (51 min., 17 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 12, 2004.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aReading22aCurriculum22aEducationxmethods 2aWebcast1 aBush, George W.q(George Walker),d1946-2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12019  aAUTH02577cgm a2200445 i 9918573260906676DNLM20260227163219.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230302s2023    mdu059|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32898  a(OCoLC)1398466546  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQ 13000aPicture a scientist :bempowerment of under-represented researchers / women leaders in academic research /cCenter for Interventional Oncology, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (59 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aEmpowerment of under-represented researchers  aRecorded January 24, 2023.3 a(CIT): To kickoff the 2023 season of "Empowerment of Under-represented Researchers (formerly Women Leaders in Academic Research", we will replay the < 1 hour short version of the award-winning film: "Picture a Scientist".   Synopsis: The movie chronicles the groundswell of researchers who are writing a new chapter for women scientists. Biologist Nancy Hopkins, chemist Raychelle Burks, and geologist Jane Willenbring lead viewers on a journey deep into their own experiences in the sciences, ranging from brutal harassment to years of subtle slights. Along the way, from cramped laboratories to spectacular field stations, we encounter scientific luminaries - including social scientists, neuroscientists, and psychologists - who provide new perspectives on how to make science itself more diverse, equitable, and open to all. This was called: "Sweeping in scope yet intimately compelling." - (by Science), and "quietly devastating" & "Fascinating and frightening examination of bias".  pictureascientist.com  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed September 18, 2023).12aWomen, Working12aScience22aEmpowerment22aGender Equity22aMotion Pictures 2aWebcast2 aCenter for Interventional Oncology,esponsoring body.0 aPicture a Scientist. 0aEmpowerment of under-represented researchers40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=48958  aAUTH01609cgm a22004454a 101268368DNLM20260227150644.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu051 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11832  a(OCoLC)72772211  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5681 aAnderson, Stephenq(Stephen K.)10aPlaying dice with NK cells :bprobabilistic switches control ly49 expressions /cStephen Anderson.10iSubtitle from title screen:aSelective gene expression in the immune system  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Sept. 30, 2006).  aStreaming video (50 min., 48 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 18, 2004.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aKiller Cells, Naturalxphysiology22aAntigens, Lyximmunology22aGene Expressionximmunology22aProbability 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11832  aAUTH02420cgm a22004454a 101268416DNLM20260227150615.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu066 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11948  a(OCoLC)84671294  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 1021 aMcEwen, Bruce S.10aFrom molecules to mind :bstress, individual differences and the social environment /cBruce S. McEwen.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 27, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 5 min., 10 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 31, 2004.3 a(CIT): National Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine Distinguished Lectures in the Science of Complementary and Alternative Medicine.  Each year, NCCAM invites two leading figures in science and medicine to speak about their unique perspectives on the field of complementary and alternative medicine (CAM). The lectures shed light on the evolution of CAM practice and research and the current use of CAM by the public.  Dr. McEwen is a renowned neuroendocrinologist who has done extensive research on the impact of stress on brain structure and neurochemistry. During this lecture, Dr. McEwen will discuss the important role that the endocrine and nervous systems play in the communication between the brain and the rest of the body. He will address how common, low levels of stress over time, such as stress caused by the events of everyday life, can affect health.   For more information, visit NCCAM.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeuronal Plasticity22aHormones22aSocioeconomic Factors.22aStress, Physiological 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11948  aAUTH01497cgm a22004214a 101267613DNLM20260227145314.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu070 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10467  a(OCoLC)71138923  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2001 aBernstein, Leslie.10aCancer prevention :bopportunities for action /cLeslie Bernstein.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aAnnual advances in cancer prevention lecture ;v3rd  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Aug. 18, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 9 min., 2 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Aug. 1, 2002.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxprevention & control 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aAnnual advances in cancer prevention lecture ;v3rd.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10467  aAUTH02404cgm a2200433 i 101749131DNLM20260227155931.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190604s2019    mdu064|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27495  a(OCoLC)1105951672  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 37504aThe fast and the furious :bmechanisms underlying rapid cell motility /cJulie Theriot. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 4 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded May 7, 2019.3 a(CIT): Directed crawling motility of animal-cell types ranging from neurons to macrophages requires the coordinated force-generating activity of multiple mechanical elements. Much molecular detail is now known about the constituents of some mechanical submachines such as the polymerizing actin network and the adhesion complexes, but it is not yet clear how these elements all work together to generate coherent, directed motion at the level of the whole cell. In order to understand cellular mechanisms of large-scale coordination, the Theriot laboratory focuses on two extremely fast-moving cell types--the fish epidermal basal keratocyte responsible for the rapid closure of wounds in fish skin, and the human neutrophil that hunts down and kills microbial invaders. Despite their very different biological roles and apparent behaviors, these cells share fundamental mechanisms of self-organization and movement coordination.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed June 26, 2019).12aCell Movementxphysiology22aFibroblasts22aNeutrophils 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aTheriot, Julie,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27495  aAUTH01369cgm a22003974a 101267710DNLM20260227145403.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu115 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10587  a(OCoLC)70051256  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 11.11 aHanawalt, Philip C.,d1931-10aHalf a century of DNA repair :ban historical perspective /cPhilip Hanawalt.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 10, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 54 min., 13 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 16, 2002.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aGeneticsxhistory22aDNA Repair22aDNAxhistory 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10587  aAUTH03803cgm a2200481 i 101749143DNLM20260227155939.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190604s2019    mdu063 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27567  a(OCoLC)1110740502  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWZ 10000aDr. Michael E. DeBakey and his influence in the changing business of healthcare and the delivery of American medicine /cAndrew T. Simpson. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aMichael E. DeBakey lecture in the history of medicine ;v3rd  aRecorded May 23, 2019.3 a(CIT): 3rd Annual Michael E. DeBakey Lecture in the History of Medicine  During his lifetime, Dr. Michael E. DeBakey developed a reputation as a leading voice decrying the growing commercialization of American medicine. At the same time, as the leader of a major medical school and as a clinical and technological innovator, Dr. DeBakey helped to transform how academic medicine and the commercial health care marketplace interacted in Houston and across the globe by working to expand the footprint of cardiovascular surgery in the United States and overseas through program building and consulting agreements as well as developing  partnerships between the government, medical schools, and private industry to create and market new medical technologies and devices. Drawing from his papers held by the National Library of Medicine, this lecture will explore how Dr. DeBakey negotiated the tension between the academic mission and commercial imperative of American medicine during the late 20th century, and how his actions helped to build Houston, and Baylor College of Medicine, into global centers for health care innovation and models for navigating the changing currents of the American healthcare system.  Andrew T. Simpson, a Michael E. DeBakey Fellow in the History of Medicine at the NLM in 2017 is an Assistant Professor of History at Duquesne University in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania. His book, Making the Medical Metropolis: Health Care and Urban Change in Pittsburgh and Houston (under contract with the University of Pennsylvania Press) examines the relationship between cities and not-for-profit health care in the late twentieth century United States. Simpson's other research includes the history of emergency medical services, the history of telemedicine and its connection to NASA, and multidisciplinary work examining the relationship between health vocational training, economic opportunity, and health disparities. Prior to returning to graduate school, he worked in community development and local politics.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 10, 2019).12aDeBakey, Michael E.q(Michael Ellis),d1908-2008.12aSurgeons22aAdministrative Personnelxhistory22aEntrepreneurshipxhistory22aHealth Care Reformxhistory 2aUnited States 2aBiography 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aSimpson, Andrew T.,espeaker.2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.).bHistory of Medicine Division,esponsoring body. 0aMichael E. DeBakey lecture ;v3rd.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27567  aAUTH01703cas a2200541 a 8217190DNLM20260227091001.0cr|cnu||||||||821021c19809999enk x p       0    0eng    a0379-864Xl0379-864X  aCHSED8  a(DNLM)C15615000(s)  a(OCoLC)06398320  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aeng  9England00aW1bCH265J10aChem Senses2dnlm 0aChemical senses00aChemical senses.  a[London :bIRL Press,cc1980-3 aOxford :bOxford University Press  av. :bill.  a9 no. a year,b2001-  aQuarterly,b198?-1988  aBimonthly,b1989-2000  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 avol. 5, no. 1 (Mar. 1980)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEb