l medicine, great teachers  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Feb. 13, 2013.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed May 21, 2013.12aEncephalitis, Herpes Simplex22aGenetic Therapyxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aClinical Center grand rounds great teachers series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17805  aAUTH03600cgm a2200481 a 101604612DNLM20260227154605.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130322s2013    mdu083|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17826  a(OCoLC)838447397  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQ 1811 aPruitt, Stephen L.10aNext generation science standards /cStephen L. Pruitt.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 23 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH science education conversations series ;v#6  aRecorded Feb. 28, 2013.3 a(CIT): The Office of Science Education (OSE), ORIP, DPCPSI is hosting a new seminar series for everyone at NIH with an interest in science education, The NIH Science Education Conversations Series. This series will include topics ranging from learning about what the future holds for tomorrow's science classroom to how to implement findings from research about the science of education.     Stephen Pruitt was named Vice President for Content, Research and Development in November of 2010. He is leading the development of the Next Generation Science Standards. Stephen began his career as a high school Chemistry teacher in Georgia, where he taught for 12 years. In 2003, he joined the Georgia Department of Education (GaDOE) as the Program Manager for Science. He served in that role for four years before becoming Director of Academic Standards, where he oversaw the continued implementation of the Georgia Performance Standards in all content areas. In 2008, he became the Associate Superintendent of Assessment and Accountability, responsible for directing all state assessments and overseeing the No Child Left Behind accountability process. In April 2009, Stephen became Chief of Staff to State School Superintendent Kathy Cox, coordinating the work of the agency and a variety of projects such as Georgia's third-ranked Race to the Top application.    In addition to his state-level work, Stephen also served as President of the Council of State Science Supervisors and a member of the writing team for the College Board"s Standards for College Success Science Standards. Most recently, he served on the National Academies of Science"s Committee on Conceptual Framework for New Science Education Standards, which has developed the Framework for K-12 Science Education. This document is the basis for the development of the Next Generation Science Standards. A native Georgian, Stephen earned a bachelor"s degree in chemistry from North Georgia College and State University, a master"s in science education from the University of West Georgia, and a doctorate of philosophy in chemistry education from Auburn University.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from PowerPoint slide.12aSciencexeducation12aSciencexstandards22aAdolescent22aChild22aPublic Policy22aState Government 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH science education conversations series ;v#640uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17826  aAUTH01971cas a2200493 i 101768931DNLM20260227090917.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||190808c20209999nyu|| poo     0   a0eng    a  20192005730 a2688-4844l2688-484421  a(OCoLC)1111735682  aDLCbengerdad20260227cDLCdDLCdDNLM  apccansdp  9United States10aISSN RECORD00aW10 aWomen's health reports (New Rochelle, N.Y.)0 aWomen's health rep.b(New Rochelle, N.Y.)10aWomens Health Rep (New Rochelle)2dnlm 0aWomen's health reportsb(New Rochelle, N.Y.)10aWomen's health reports. 1aNew Rochelle, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.,c[2020]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1, Issue 1 (April 2020).0 aPubMedbv1n1, 2020-0 aPMC  aArticles published as they are received and compiled into volumes.  a"Publishes peer-reviewed open access articles on a broad range of topics related to the optimal care for women around the globe."  aCompanion journal to: Journal of women's health.0 aDescription based on: Vol. 1, issue 1 (April 2020); title from journal home page (publisher's website viewed July 20, 2020).1 aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 1, issue 1 (April 2020) (viewed July 20, 2020).12aWomen's Health22aWomen's Health Services 2aPeriodical08iComplemented by (work):tJournal of women's health (2002)x1540-9996w(DLC)   2002213698w(OCoLC)5022984740uhttps://www.liebertpub.com/loi/whr40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101768931  aAUTH01801cas a2200493 a 9424209DNLM20260227091015.0cr|cn ||||||||951012c19939999au qr p       0    0ger    asn 95050054   a0943-1950l0943-1950  aPSFOEO  a(DNLM)SR0077044(s)  a(OCoLC)33424151  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 agerafrebeng  9Austria00aW1bPS78IM10aPsychother Forum2dnlm00aPsychotherapie Forum.  aWien ;aNew York :bSpringer-Verlag,c1993-2010  av. :bill.  aFour no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aJahrg. 1, Heft 1-  aNo issues published 2010-2013.  aPrint issues ceased with 2010.  aTitle from cover.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  a<1995- > called <Vol. 3, no. 1- > .  aAlso issued online.  aIn German with summaries in English and German, 1993-   ; in German and French with summaries in English, French, and German, <1995- > .  aIssued by: Österreichischer Bundesverband für Psychotherapie, and also by the Schweizer Psychotherapeuten-Verband, <1995- > ; Österreichischer Bundesverband für Psychotherapie, 2014- . 2aPsychotherapy 2aPeriodical2 aÖsterreichischer Bundesverband für Psychotherapie.2 aAssociation suisse des psychothérapeutes.0 x1613-760401tPsychotherapie-Wissenschaftx1664-9591w(DLC)   2017243251w(OCoLC)7869704754 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/729  aAUTH04055cgm a2200469 i 101778040DNLM20260227160853.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210408s2021    mdu062|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31575  a(OCoLC)1258214793  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQ 33500aAtlas of AI :bmapping the social and economic forces behind AI /cDr. Kate Crawford.1 aAtlas of artificial intelligence1 aMapping the social and economic forces behind AI 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNational Library of Medicine lecture on science, technology & society  aAccess restricted to HHS only.  aRecorded March 1, 2021.3 a(CIT): National Library of Medicine Lecture on Science, Technology &amp; Society Dr. Crawford is the inaugural Visiting Chair of AI and Justice at the École Normale Supérieure, as well as a Senior Principal Researcher at Microsoft Research, and the cofounder of the AI Now Institute at New York University.  NLM is pleased to have her launch this annual series, which aims to raise awareness and spark conversations about the intersection of societal and ethical considerations with the conduct of biomedical research and the use of advanced technologies.  Machine learing systems are already playing a significant role in many of our social institutions, including healthcare, education, hiring and criminal justice. Despite the patina of objectivity and neutrality, many scholars have shown how these systems can reproduce and intensify forms of structural bias and discrimination. In this talk, Dr. Crawford shares insights from her new book, Atlas of AI, to show the historical origins, labor practices, infrastructures, and epistemological assumptions that underlie the production of artificial intelligence. The classificatory logics and predictive approaches raise challenges that extend well beyond the current bias debate. Dr. Crawford offers new paths for thinking through the research ethics and policy implications of the turn to machine learning, which are increasingly urgent in a time of a pandemic and growing inequality in the United States. Dr. Crawford is a leading scholar of science, technology, and society. Over a 20-year career, her work has focused on understanding large scale data systems and AI in the wider contexts of history, politics, labor, and the environment. Dr. Crawford has held academic positions around the world, including Massachusetts Institute of Technology, the University of Sydney, New York University, Microsoft Research, and the École Normale Supérieure. Her collaborative projects, Anatomy of an AI System and Excavating AI, have won international awards including the Ayrton Prize, and she has contributed to policy discussions across the US, EU, UK and Australia. Her research has been published in journals such as Nature, New Media &amp; Society, Science, Technology &amp; Human Values, and her writing has appeared in venues such as The New York Times, Harpers" Magazine, and The Wall Street Journal.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed June 17, 2021).12aArtificial Intelligence22aPrejudice22aClassification 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aCrawford, Kate,d1976-espeaker.2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.),ehost institution. 0aNational Library of Medicine lecture on science, technology & society40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41566  aAUTH01411cgm a22004214a 1