Press,c2022.  axiii, 504 pages, 16 unnumbered pages of plates :billustrations (colour) ;c26 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aCambridge medicine  aPreceded by Leong's manual of diagnostic antibodies for immunohistology / edited by Runjan Chetty, Kumarasen Cooper, Allen Gown. Third edition. 2016.  aPrevious editions: 2016, 2002, 1999.  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aBiomarkersxchemistry22aAntibodies, Monoclonalximmunology22aImmune Sera 2aHandbook1 aChetty, Runjan,eeditor.1 aCooper, Kumarasen,eeditor.1 aCheung, Carol,eeditor.1 aMandavilli, Srinivas,eeditor. 0aCambridge medicine (Series)  aAUTH02985cgm a2200457 i 101774283DNLM20260227160445.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu047|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31396  a(OCoLC)1236084560  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.55.H900aInformed consent procedures in the era of COVID-19 :bbeyond the use of a standard written consent document /cJulie Eiserman. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (47 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded November 17, 2020.3 a(CIT): The COVID-19 pandemic has presented many challenges with regard to conducting informed consent with research subjects.  In addition, the applicability of and requirements for requesting a waiver of consent, a waiver of documentation of consent or an alteration of informed consent procedures can be confusing.  On November 17, 2020, Julie M. Eiserman, a Senior Regulatory Analyst in the IRBO of OHSRP will present "Informed Consent Procedures in the Era of COVID-19: Beyond the Use of a Standard Written Consent Document".  This session will address the following learning objectives related to informed consent:.Describe when might it be appropriate to request a "Waiver of Consent" or a "Waiver of Documentation of Consent" as delineated under 45 CFR 46. Identify the required content in the protocol when requesting a waiver of consent or waiver of documentation of consent. Examine the aspects of informed consent that must be considered when sharing specimens and data. Explain the use of a separate Privacy Act notice when consent for NIH protocols is occurring remotely.Discuss the meaning and applicability of "Alteration of Consent" under 45 CFR 46 .Define "E-Consent (Electronic Consent)", clarify the status of its use in NIH IRP research and cover permissible alternatives to creating wet signatures.Review the applicability of the various approved NIH consent templates.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed February 2, 2021).12aInformed Consent12aResearch Subjectsxlegislation & jurisprudence22aHuman Experimentationxlegislation & jurisprudence22aCOVID-1922aPandemics 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aEiserman, Julie,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38977  aAUTH01374nam a2200433 i 9918986566306676DNLM20260211101256.0230302s2024    enka     b    001 0 eng    a9781529222180q(hardcover)  a1529222184q(hardcover)  z9781529222197q(ePub)  z9781529222203q(ePdf)  a(OCoLC)1370650638  aYDXbengerdacYDXdOCLCOdYDXdOCLCOdJHEdOCLCOdLML  apcc  an-us---  9England 4aTR708b.Z56 202400aQS 11 AA104a610.282231 aZimmerman, Brandon,eauthor.10aDissection photography :bcadavers, abjection, and the formation of identity /cBrandon Zimmerman. 1aBristol :bBristol University Press,c2024.  axiv, 263 pages :billustrations ;c24 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aDeath and culture  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.12aCadaver12aPhotographyxhistory22aDissectionxeducation22aStudents, Medicalxpsychology22aSocial Identification22aHistory, 19th Century22aHistory, 20th Century 2aUnited States  aAUTH02991cgm a2200457 i 9918226878306676DNLM20260227161202.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210615s2021    mdu568|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31701  a(DNLM)CIT:31711  a(OCoLC)1309103596  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWQ 2122 aHuman Placenta Project (Eunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.)).bAnnual Meetingn(6th :d2021 :cOnline)eauthor.14aThe Human Placenta Project /cNICHD, NIH.1 aHPP 2021 - the  Human Placenta Project 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (9 hr., 28 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded May 10 & 11, 2021.  aThe placenta is immensely important for supporting the development of the fetus. Any failure of the placenta to function optimally can impact not only fetal development, but also cause pregnancy complications, such as preeclampsia, increase maternal mortality and morbidity, and impact the lifelong health of both mother and child. Despite its importance, the placenta is the least understood, and least studied, of all human organs. NICHD's HPP is a collaborative research effort to develop new tools for studying how the placenta develops and functions across pregnancy, in real time and in a safe and non-invasive manner. The goal of this HPP meeting is to answer two broad questions: What is the current state of placental knowledge and assessment capability? What are the key gaps and opportunities that need to be addressed going forward? This meeting brings together multidisciplinary experts and broad thinkers, including computer scientists, engineers, placental biologists, clinicians, and patients, to discuss lessons learned from HPP research and engage in active discussions about possible future directions for HPP and placental research.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 21, 2022).22aHuman Placenta Project (Eunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.))12aPlacenta22aPlacenta Diseases 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4203340uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42034  aAUTH03564cgm a2200445 i 101737003DNLM20260227155848.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u181102s2018    mdu054|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:26130  a(OCoLC)1078196822  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 58.700aSurveillance of core cellular pathways in C. elegans for pathogen assaults /cGary Ruvkun. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2018]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (54 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCCR eminent lecture  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded October 22, 2018.3 a(CIT): CCR Eminent Lecture  The Ruvkun lab uses C. elegans molecular genetics and genomics to study miRNA and RNAi pathways. Using genetic and RNA interference approaches, we have identified many genes that positively or negatively regulate RNAi and microRNA pathways. These genes reveal the trajectory of siRNAs and miRNAs as they target mRNAs, as well as components that may be developed as drug targets to enhance RNAi in mammals.  Over the past decade, we discovered that like mammals, C. elegans uses an insulin signaling pathway to control its metabolism and longevity. This analysis has revealed striking congruence of molecular mechanisms at many steps in the pathway, suggesting that insulin regulation of longevity and metabolism is ancient and universal. The new genes of the insulin pathway that have emerged from these studies are conserved in animal phylogeny and represent new targets for diabetes drug development.  Functional genomic analyses using RNAi libraries of every C. elegans gene now allows a systematic study of metabolism and aging. Our lab has surveyed 18,000 genes for their action in regulation of longevity, fat deposition, RNAi, miRNA regulation, and molting. This analysis gives a global view of the molecular machines that operate in these pathways. Current research in the Ruvkun lab attempts to weave these lists of aging regulatory genes into pathways that assess and regulate metabolic tempo and mode, repair and regeneration, and protective and degenerative pathways. A neuroendocrinology of energy balance and longevity will emerge from these studies.  We are developing protocols and instruments that use PCR primers corresponding to universal sequence elements of the 16S RNA gene to search for diverse microbes that may cause diseases unsuspected to be due to pathogens and microbes from extreme environments. One long term goal of this project is to send a robotic thermal cycler with these primers to Mars in search of microbial life that is ancestrally related to life on Earth.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed Novemeber 29, 2018).12aRNA Interferencexphysiology22aCaenorhabditis elegansxgenetics22aMicroRNAsxphysiology22aSignal Transductionxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aRuvkun, Gary B.q(Gary Bruce),espeaker. 0aCCR eminent lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?26130  aAUTH01449nam a2200373 i 9918227380606676DNLM20260223111101.0210617r18971897pau      b    000 