ier0 aVol. 25, no. 1 (Jan./Feb. 1981)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv25n1, 1981-0 aPubMedbv25n1, 1981-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aArticles in English, French or German; summaries in English and French or German.  aFormed by the union of: Nutrition and metabolism, and: Annales de la nutrition et de l'alimentation.  3Vol. 36, no. 1 (1992)c19921001fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aDietetics 2aMetabolic Diseases 2aNutritional Physiological Phenomena 2aPeriodical0 x1421-969714tNutrition and metabolismx0029-6678w(OCoLC)176094414tAnnales de la nutrition et de l'alimentationx0003-4037w(OCoLC)14812644 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/223977  aAUTH01525cas a2200481 a 101581621DNLM20260227090911.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||110706c20129999maumr poo     0    0eng    a  20112045620 a2162-2531l2162-253121  a(OCoLC)739098571  aDLCcDLCdSCBdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aISSN RECORD14aQP62010aW110aMol Ther Nucleic Acids2dnlm00aMolecular therapy.pNucleic acids.1 aNucleic acids  aNew York, NY :bNature Pub. Group3132017- :aCambridge, MA :bCell Press  aMonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan in 2012.0 aPubMedbv1, 2012-0 aPMC  aOfficial journal of: American Society of Gene & Cell Therapy.  aDescription based on: 2012; title from PDF caption (publisher's Web site, viewed June 11, 2012).  aLatest issue consulted: 2012 (viewed June 11, 2012).12aNucleic Acids22aGenetic Therapy22aMolecular Biology 2aPeriodical2 aAmerican Society of Gene & Cell Therapy.4 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=10158162140uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/21622531  aAUTH02737cgm a2200457 i 101634128DNLM20260227154821.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140603s2014    mdu061|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18418  a(OCoLC)881508532  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 172.41 aJha, Amishi,d1970-eauthor.10aBuilding resilience in high stress cohorts with mindfulness training /cAmishi Jha. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCCAM integrative medicine research lecture  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded May 5, 2014.3 a(CIT): The National Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine (NCCAM) presents the Integrative Medicine Research Lecture series. The series provides overviews of the current state of research and practice involving complementary health therapies, and explores perspectives on the emerging discipline of integrative medicine. Amishi Jha, Ph.D., is an Associate Professor of Psychology and the Director of Contemplative Neuroscience for the Mindfulness Research & Practice Initiative at the University of Miami. Dr. Jha's research focuses on the brain bases of attention, working memory, and mindfulness-based training. Her current projects focus on investigating how to promote resilience in high stress cohorts using contemplative and mind training techniques that strengthen the brain's attention networks. In this lecture, Dr. Jha will talk about her lab's recent efforts to offer contemplative training (involving mindfulness and compassion) to high stress cohorts including military service members and spouses. She will also discuss her research results, as well as how to address and evaluate best practices for implementation of short-form, accessible forms of mindfulness training.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed June 9, 2014.12aMindfulness12aStress, Psychologicalxprevention & control22aMilitary Personnelxpsychology22aResilience, Psychological 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuin