ource (1 streaming video file (7 hr., 30 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded June 25, 2014.3 a(CIT): 2nd Annual NCI CSSI Science Day   Since its inception in 2004, the NCI's Center for Strategic Scientific Initiatives (CSSI) has focused on its mission to create and implement exploratory programs focused on the development and integration of advanced technologies, trans-disciplinary approaches, infrastructures, and standards to accelerate the creation of publicly available, broadly accessible, multi-dimensional data, knowledge, and tools for cancer research. Given the range of disciplines supported by Center initiatives and the rapid pace of scientific findings, we decided to convene a "Science Day" meeting where advances in various research endeavors supported by the Center can be shared. Like last year, this year's meeting will focus on sharing ongoing, unpublished work of CSSI-supported investigators, thus facilitating additional scientific conversations amongst these researchers and NCI/CSSI program staff.  The meeting itself will open with an overview of CSSI by Dr. Douglas Lowy followed by sessions developed by each office within CSSI. Each session will have presentations followed by time for audience engagement and participation.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed August 18, 2014.12aBiomedical Research12aNeoplasms 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aCenter for Strategic Scientific Initiatives (National Cancer Institute (U.S.)),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18520  aAUTH01355cas a2200445 a 101239023DNLM20260227090914.0cr|cn ||||||||050119c20059999ja qr p       0    0eng    a  2005243494  a1860-1480l1860-1499  a(OCoLC)60364461  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  alcd  9Japan00aW1bME309LE10aMed Mol Morphol2dnlm00aMedical molecular morphology.  aTokyo, Japan :bSpringer,c[2005]-  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 38, no. 1 (Mar. 2005)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv38n1, Mar. 2005-0 aPubMedbv38n1, Mar. 2005-  aAlso issued online.  aOfficial journal of the Japanese Society for Clinical Molecular Morphology.12aMolecular Biology22aCell Physiological Phenomena22aMicroscopy, Electron 2aPeriodical2 aJapanese Society for Clinical Molecular Morphology.0 x1860-149900tMedical electron microscopyx0918-4287w(OCoLC)312486204 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/795  aAUTH02410cgm a2200433 a 101604613DNLM20260227154608.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130322s2013    mdu065 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17828  a(OCoLC)838447401  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4751 aAdelman, Karen.10aPotentiating signal-responsive transcription :ba dynamic dance between paused RNA polymerase and chromatin /cKaren Adelman.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 5 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aDirector's seminar series  aRecorded Mar. 1, 2013.3 a(CIT): Director's Seminar Series:   Work from the Adelman laboratory has contributed significantly to our understanding of gene regulation, especially within environmentally- or developmentally-responsive networks. The ability to rapidly integrate multiple extra- and intra-cellular signals to produce a specific pattern of gene expression is essential for the growth, development and survival of all organisms; however, the mechanisms that generate and coordinate these responses remain poorly understood. The Adelman laboratory investigates the interplay between environmental stimuli, chromatin architecture and transcription by RNA polymerase II, with a focus on the regulation of early transcription elongation. By expanding our understanding of how cells react to stimuli in a dynamic, yet equilibrated manner, work in the Adelman group has provided fundamental new insights into gene-environment interactions and the mechanisms that maintain normal patterns of gene expression.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aTranscription, Geneticxphysiology22aChromatinxphysiology22aDNA-Directed RNA Polymerasesxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17828  aAUTH01959cgm a22004574a 101507195DNLM20260227152901.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090605s2009    mdu063 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15094  a(OCoLC)432239881  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3591 aSvendsen, Clive.10aModeling and treating neurological diseases with stem cells /cClive Svendsen ; [Stem Cell Interest Group].  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Aug. 2, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 2 min., 31 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 12, 2009.3 a(CIT): The Stem Cell Interest Group was established to enhance communication and to foster collaboration among scientists from varying disciplines interested in stem cells. Topics of interest include fundamental stem cell biology, ontogeny, gerontology, and the therapeutic potential of stem cells. The SCIG serves as an open forum for discussion and dissemination of knowledge about all aspects of stem cell biology.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aParkinson Diseasextherapy22aAmyotrophic Lateral Sclerosisxtherapy22aGlial Cell Line-Derived Neurotrophic Factor22aMuscular Atrophy, Spinalxtherapy22aPluripotent St