s)  a(OCoLC)22789090  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Switzerland00aW1bCE5840 aCerebrovascular diseases (Basel, Switzerland)10aCerebrovasc Dis2dnlm10aCerebrovascular diseases.  aBasel ;aNew York :bKarger,c1990-  av. :bill.  aEight no. a year,b2001-  aSix no. a year,b-2000  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 a1/1 (Jan.-Feb. 1991)-  aTitle from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv8n1,Jan.-Feb. 1998-1 aPubMedbv8n1,Jan.-Feb. 1998-1 aIndex medicus  aIssues also designated as: Vol. 1, no. 1- .  aAlso issued online. 2aCerebrovascular Disorders 2aPeriodical0 x1421-97864 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/224153  aAUTH02625cgm a2200433 a 101595128DNLM20260227154523.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u121025s2012    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17545  a(OCoLC)820532317  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2671 aGarraway, Levi A.10aDefining the spectrum of resistance to be targeted anti-cancer therapeutics /cLevi A. Garraway.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded Sept. 5, 2012.3 a(CIT): Dr. Levi Garraway has made seminal research contributions in cancer genomics, drug resistance, and genomics-driven (or "personalized") cancer medicine. He published the first genome sequencing studies of aggressive primary prostate cancer, and has led other major sequencing initiatives in prostate cancer, melanoma and head/neck cancers. This work identified multiple new cancer genes and uncovered mechanisms by which complex rearrangements arise. At the Broad Institute, he leads the Cancer Cell Line Encyclopedia, a collaboration with Novartis that involves a genomic and pharmacological study of ~1000 human cancer cell lines to characterize sensitivity and resistance to anticancer agents.  Dr. Garraway is perhaps best known for his contributions to precision cancer medicine. He described the first high-throughput adaptation of a genomic technology to profile human tumors for hundreds of "actionable" cancer gene mutations. This provided a basis for tumor mutation profiling as a means to stratify cancer patients for clinical trial enrollment and, in the future, optimal therapeutic choices. He also demonstrated the promise of massive parallel sequencing as a clinical tumor genomic profiling approach.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aNeoplasmsxdrug therapy22aDrug Resistance, Neoplasm22aMolecular Targeted Therapy 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17545  aAUTH05388cgm a22004934a 101510718DNLM20260227153031.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090710s2009    mdu056|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15188  a(OCoLC)727302014  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2001 aPalefsky, Joel.10aChallenges in human papillomavirus infection :bHIV co-infection and HPV vaccines /cJoel Palefsky.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCenter for Cancer Research-National Cancer Institute grand rounds  aTitle from screen banner.  aStreaming video (56 min. : sd., col.).  aRestricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded June 23, 2009.3 a(CIT): Dr. Palefsky is Professor of Medicine at UCSF School of Medicine.  Dr. Palefsky completed his undergraduate medical training and training in Internal Medicine at McGill University and completed his fellowship in Infectious Diseases at Stanford University. He is an internationally recognized expert on the molecular biology, treatment, pathogenesis and natural history of anogenital human papillomavirus infections, particularly in the setting of HIV infection. He is the Director of the world's first clinic devoted to prevention of anal cancer, the Anal Neoplasia Clinic at the UCSF Cancer Center.  He has pioneered diagnostic and treatment methods for anal intraepithelial neoplasia and has been an advocate for anal screening in high-risk populations to prevent anal cancer. He is the head of the HPV Working Group of the AIDS Malignancy Consortium.  He heads the Special Populations Committee of the American Society for Colposcopy and Cervical Pathology and is co-director of the Cancer and Immunology Program of the UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center.  He is the author of over 190 publications.   Dr. Palefsky"s ongoing projects focus on DNA tumor virus infection of the epithelium, specifically human papillomavirus (HPV) and Epstein-Barr virus (EBV).  His work on HPV focuses on understanding the molecular pathogenesis of HPV-related neoplasia, the natural history of HPV infection in HIV-positive men and women; characterization of a new clinical entity, anal intraepithelial neoplasia (AIN) and its relationship to anal cancer; and development of novel approaches to prevent and treat anogenital HPV infection.  His group continues to publish much of the world"s literature on the prevalence and incidence of anal HPV infection and AIN in a variety of at-risk groups of men and women.   Over 50% of HIV-positive MSM develop high-grade AIN, the anal cancer precursor. Treatment of AIN could reduce the incidence of anal cancer, but is very challenging for a number of reasons. In the last few years, much of his group"s clinical research activities have focused on development of better approaches to treating AIN to prevent anal cancer.  His laboratory is also continuing to develop a gene therapy approach that uses a plasmid encoding the HSV-1 thymidine kinase (TK) gene under the control of HPV E2-response elements to drive HPV-specific expression of TK.   Dr. Palefsky"s program on EBV, like the HPV program described above, is focused on epithelial infection. His studies focus on the EBV BMRF-2 protein which his group has shown to play an important role in entry into epithelial cells. His group is also continuing its work on dissemination of EBV to and among epithelial cells and has recently begun to focus more on the role of HIV co-infection in this process.  His group has also expanded its work on molecular interactions between HPV and HIV using novel epithelial models and newer methods to study HPV biology, including the use of HPV pseudovirions.   NCI"s Center for Cancer Research (CCR) Grand Rounds is a weekly lecture series addressing current research in clinical and molecular oncology. Speakers are leading national and international researchers and clinicians proposed by members of the CCR Grand Rounds Planning Committee and others within the CCR community and approved by the CCR Office of the Director. Lectures occur every Tuesday from 8:00 to 9:00 a.m. in Lipsett Amphitheater in the Clinical Center building on the NIH campus September through July with exceptions around holidays and major cancer meetings. The lecture schedule is posted on various calendars of events, including at the following link:  http://www.bethesdatrials.cancer.gov/health-care-professionals/grand-rounds.aspx.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aPapillomavirus Infectionsxcomplications12aPapillomavirus Infectionsxprevention & control22aHIV Infectionsxcomplications22aHIV Infectionsxprevention & control22aPapillomaviridae22aPapillomavirus Vaccinesxtherapeutic use 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15188  aAUTH01789cgm a22004334a 101268412DNLM20260227151143.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu063 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11938  a(OCoLC)84671279  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 3751 aVaux, David.10aApoptosis :ba walk through the valley /cDavid Vaux.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 27, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 2 min., 22 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 24, 2004.3 a(CIT): The Immunology Interest Group (IIG) organizes activities designed to promote information exchange and interactions among NIH scientists interested in the field of immunology, broadly defined. Interactions are facilitated via weekly meetings on current topics as well as an annual Immunology Retreat.  For more information, visit The Immunology Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aApoptosis22aApoptosis Regulatory Proteins22aCaenorhabditis elegans 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11938  aAUTH01785cam a2200361 i 08930310RDNLM20260210085521.0810725s1907    pau           001 0 eng    a(OCoLC)4995896  aDNLMbengerdad20260205dDNLM  9United States00aQV 4bH168ma 19071 aWilcox, Reynold Webb,d1856-1931,eauthor.10aMateria medica and pharmacy /cby Reynold Webb Wilcox.  aSeventh edition, revised. 1aPhiladelphia :bP. Blakiston's Son & Co.,c1907.  aix, 490 pages ;c21 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes index.  aCompanion volume: Pharmacology and therapeutics.  Both titles based on the American edition of: Materia medica and therapeutics by W. Hale-White, ed. by Wilcox.  Beginning with the 8th edition, issued together in one volume under title: Materia medica and therapeutics.  aThe National Librar