33 i 101595166DNLM20260227154325.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u121102s2012    mdu064|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17577  a(OCoLC)822991579  aDNLMcDNLMerdadDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWO 7001 aGielen, A. C.q(Andrea Carlson)eauthor.14aThe science of injury prevention research :bpast successes and future challenges /cAndrea C. Gielen. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 4 min.) :bsound, color).  atwo-dimensional moving image2rdacontent  acomputer2rdamedia  aonline resource2rdacarrier1 aBehavioral and social sciences research  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded September 21, 2012.3 a(CIT): Injuries, unintentional and violent, are the leading cause of death from ages 1 to 44 and they are the second most costly health problem in America. Prevention research in the injury field has matured over the last few decades and results offer optimism for reducing this toll in the future. This talk will provide an overview of the field, its history of accomplishments, as well as the challenges and opportunities that face it in the future. An emphasis will be placed on prevention research of particular relevance to the NIH mission and institutes, with examples drawn from injuries that affect the population across the lifespan.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed December 10, 2012.12aWounds and Injuriesxprevention & control22aPreventive Medicine 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aBSSR lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17577  aAUTH01290cas a2200445 a 9517857DNLM20260227091023.0cr|cnu||||||||960201c19959999enkmr p       0    0eng    asn 96039050   a0949-2321l0949-2321  aEJMRFL  a(DNLM)SR0084188(s)  a(OCoLC)34166356  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9England00aW1bEU72DHT10aEur J Med Res2dnlm00aEuropean journal of medical research.  aMunich, Germany :bI. Holzapfel,cc1995-3 3Jan. 2012- :aLondon :bBioMed Central  av. :bill.  aMonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Oct. 16, 1995)-  aPrint ceased with vol. 16, no. 12 (Dec. 2011).1 aMEDLINEbv1n1,Oct. 1995-0 aPubMedbv1n1,Oct. 1995-0 aPMC1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online. 2aClinical Medicine 2aPeriodical0 x2047-783X4 uhttps://eurjmedres.biomedcentral.com/4 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=9517857  aAUTH02046cas a2200469 i 101778326DNLM20260227090918.0m     o  d        cr#|||||||||||190513c20189999oncqr poo     0    0eng    a  2021229754  a1117893362  a2561-4444l2561-4444  a(OCoLC)1101100735z(OCoLC)1117893362  aINAbengerdad20260227cINAdINAdOCLCOdOCLdLVTdOCLCQdDNLM  apcc  9Canada 4aQP185b.C3600aW110aCan Liver J2dnlm00aCanadian liver journal. 1aToronto, ON Canada :bUniversity of Toronto Press,c[2018]-  a1 online resource  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1, issue 1 (February 2018).  aThe goal of the Canadian Liver Journal, as the official journal of the Canadian Association for the Study of Liver, is to promote research, education and clinical care in liver disease within the Canadian and International Communities. The Canadian Liver Journal welcomes original clinical and translational research, review articles, case reports, commentaries and letters to the editor on all aspects of liver disease. The Canadian Liver Journal is a peer review journal with open access.  aEditor-in-Chief: Eric M. Yoshida; Deputy Editor: Natasha Chandok.0 aPubMedbv1n1, Winter 2018-0 aPMC0 aVol. 1, issue 1 (February 2018); title from journal homepage (viewed December 13, 2021).1 aVol. 4, issue 4 (Fall 2021) (viewed December 13, 2021).12aLiver12aLiver Diseases 2aPeriodical2 aCanadian Association for the Study of the Liver,eissuing body.40uhttps://canlivj.utpjournals.press/40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101778326  aAUTH01725cgm a22004214a 101488660DNLM20260227152646.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081104s2008    dcu072 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14733  a(OCoLC)298509008  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 51500aPlanFirst.pFeaturing HHS Secretary Mike Leavitt.30aFeaturing HHS Secretary Mike Leavitt  a[Washington, D.C.] :bDept. of Health and Human Services,c[2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 2, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 11 min. , 56 sec. : sd., col.).  aAn episode of PlanFirst originally broadcast Oct. 29, 2008.3 a(CIT): Join us for a  special edition of "PlanFirst", featuring Secretary Leavitt and a live audience.  Secretary Leavitt will provide formal remarks regarding the nation's pandemic planning effort and will then join a roundtable discussion to address the nation's level of preparedness and related issues.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aInfluenza, Human22aDisaster Planning22aDisease Outbreaksxprevention & control 2aWebcast1 aLeavitt, Michael O.q(Michael Okerlund),d1951-1 aUnited States.bDepartment of Health and Human Services.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14733  aAUTH01609cas a22005174a 101176443DNLM20260227090914.0cr|cnu||||||||030409c20039999cauqr p       0   a0eng    a  20032122150 a1544-3167l1544-316721  a(OCoLC)52005554  aNSDcNSDdDNLM  ansdpalcdanlmcopyc  9United States10aISSN RECORD00aW1bJO97CP10a61621310aJ Vasc Ultrasound2dnlm0 aJ. vasc. ultrasoundb(Print) 0aJournal for vascular ultrasoundb(Print)00aJournal for vascular ultrasound :bJVU.30aJVU  aLanham, MD :bSociety for Vascular Ultrasound,cc2003-3132018- :a[Thousand Oaks, CA] :bSAGE Publications  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 27, no. 1 (Mar. 2003)-  aTitle from cover.  aApproved for CME credit.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.12aVascular Diseasesxdiagnosis22aAngiography22aBiomedical Technologyxinstrumentation22aUltrasonography 2aPeriodical2 aSociety for Vascular Ultrasound.0 x1544-317500tJournal of vascular technologyx1044-4122w(DLC)sn 88026829w(OCoLC)175055424 uhttps://journals.sagepub.com/home/jvu  aAUTH01078cas a2200373   0035720DNLM20260227090921.0cr|cn ||||||||651223c19639999fr  x p       0||| 0fre    a0031-6032l0031-6032  a(DNLM)P09260000(s)  a(OCoLC)50376711  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9France00aW1bPE872M00aPerspectives psychiatriques.  aSèvresb: EDK3 aParis :bPerspectives Psychiatriques  av.billus.  aFive no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 ano. 1-        jan. 1963-  aRevue of the Groupe d'études de psychiatrie et neurologie.  aCaptions in English and German.  aAlso issued online. 2aPsychiatry 2aPeriodical2 aGroupe d'études de psychiatrie et neurologie.0 x2118-40384 uhttp://www.perspectives-psy.org/  aAUTH03133cgm a22004814a 101475730DNLM20260227152731.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080602s2008    mdu069 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14516  a(OCoLC)237194281  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 1501 aDixit, Vishva.10aRegulation of inflammation by ubiquitin modification of signaling components /cVishva Dixit.1 iTitle from PowerPoint screen:aUbiquitin modification & inflammasome function in innate immunity  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen (viewed July 2, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 8 min., 56 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 21, 2008.3 aProper regulation of inflammation is essential for combating  pathogen invasion and maintaining homeostasis.  Whereas hypo-responsive hosts succumb to infections, unchecked inflammatory reactions promote a variety of  debilitating and fatal conditions including septic shock, autoimmune disease,  atherosclerosis, graft rejection, and cancer. Pathogens, host immune cell  ligands, and pro-inflammatory cytokines such as Tumor Necrosis Factor-a (TNF-a), Interleukin-1-b (IL1-b), and Lipopolysaccharide (LPS) induce an array  of inflammatory responses by activating a variety of cell types. Although much  is known about how inflammatory responses are initiated and sustained, less is  known about how inflammation is attenuated to maintain a homeostatic balance. The A20 protein is now recognized as a central, negative feedback inhibitor, of  NF-kB mediated inflammatory responses.  It was originally discovered as an  NF-kB inducible gene that was recruited to the assembled proximal signaling  complex of pro-inflammatory receptors by virtue of being able to bind to other  proximal signaling adaptors including TRAFs and RIP. Analysis revealed seven  repeats of a novel type of Cys2/Cys2 zinc finger motif at the carboxy terminus  that possessed E3 ligase activity and a amino terminal Ovarian Tumor (OTU)  domain that exhibited deubiquitinating activity. Both enzymatic activities were shown to act in concert to attenuate NF-kB signaling from pro-inflammatory  receptors.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aInflammationxmetabolism22aImmunity, Innatexphysiology22aNF-kappa Bxmetabolism22aSignal Transductionxphysiology22aUbiquitination 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14516  aAUTH01655cgm a2200