ion of the Journal of Autoimmunity.  aDescription based on: Volume 1 (April 2019); title from PDF cover page (ScienceDirect site, viewed August 2, 2019).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 3 (2020) (viewed April 1, 2020).12aAutoimmune Diseases22aAutoimmunity22aTranslational Research, Biomedical. 2aPeriodical08iComplemented by (work):tJournal of autoimmunityx0896-8411w(DLC)   88659269w(OCoLC)1730581440uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/journal-of-translational-autoimmunity40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=101759413  aAUTH01734cgm a22004814a 101279010DNLM20260227145103.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060612s2003    mdu598 ||||||f|   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10093  a(DNLM)CIT:10094  a(OCoLC)70119715  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWZ 33000aVisual culture and public health /cthe National Library of Medicine, History of Medicine Division.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 30, 2006).  aStreaming video (9 hr., 58 min. : sd., col.).0 aDay 1 -- Day 2.  aRecorded Oct. 16-17, 2003.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.22aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.).bHistory of Medicine Division12aLibraries, Medical12aMedicine in the Arts22aArtxhistory22aMotion Picturesxhistory22aPhotographyxhistory22aPublic Health 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.).bHistory of Medicine Division.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1009340uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10094  aAUTH03021cgm a2200517 i 9918592672506676DNLM20260227163629.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230429s2023    mdu340|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33125  a(DNLM)CIT:33128  a(DNLM)CIT:33135  a(OCoLC)1454695343  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWT 202 aGeroscience for the Next Generation (Summit)d(2023 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aGeroscience for the next generation :bthe fourth summit (day 1, 2 & 3) /cNIA, National Institutes of Health.1 aFourth geroscience summit: geroscience for the next generation 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (3 streaming video files (19 hr., 24 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from PowerPoint screen.  aRecorded April 24, 25 & 26, 2023.3 a(CIT): <ol><li>Highlight the need to develop and implement geroscience for all people, setting this discipline in the contexts of precision medicine, minority health, and health disparities.</li><li>Emphasize the need for geroscience research to address comorbidities, multimorbidities and geriatric syndromes.</li><li>Explore further development of measures reporting health and function in aging (alternatives to - or continued reliance on - "years since birth" as the independent variable in geroscience research).</li><li>Consider the importance and utility of mathematical modeling of aging.</li><li>Examine the importance of evidence-based inclusive geroscience in medical practice.</li><li>Discuss feasibility, design and implementation of geroscience clinical trials.The major product of the Summit will be a summary document of discourse throughout the summit, generated with help from the organizers, identifying current gaps and opportunities in the field, and actionable next steps to advance geroscience. Additional opinion pieces and perspectives by the participants will be encouraged.</li></ol>  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed August 5, 2024).12aGeroscience22aAging22aHealth Inequities22aAge Factors22aGeriatric Assessment 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bGeroscience Interest Group,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institute on Aging,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4945640uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4945840uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49460  aAUTH01928cgm a2200421 i 101774312DNLM20260227160548.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu107|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31434  a(OCoLC)1239646082  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 506.52 aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19d(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19 breakout session 2 (cardiovascular) /cNIAID, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 47 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded December 4, 2020.3 a(CIT): Breakout group (2 of 5/6) part of the day 2 of the workshop. The topics for each breakout group are not yet established. The goal of this meeting is to characterize the post-acute sequelae of COVID-19, identify knowledge gaps, and establish scientific priorities to address these gaps.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 24, 2021).12aCOVID-19xcomplications22aCardiovascular Diseasesxvirology22aPandemics 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (U.S.),eissuing body.2 iAugmentation of (work):aWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19d(2020 :cOnline).tWorkshop on Post-Acute Sequelae of COVID-19.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38880  aAUTH01440cgm a22004454a 101294478DNLM20260227151650.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061204s2006    mdu125|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13427  a(OCoLC)191751216  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWR 2672 aNIH Research Festivald(2006 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNIH Research Festival 2006.nPlenary session /cWilliam Gahl ... [et al.].30aPlenary session  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 14, 2008).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 5 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 17, 2006.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHermanski-Pudlak Syndrome22aCoronary Diseasexsurgery22aDrug-Eluting Stents22aPaclitaxelxtherapeutic use 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aGahl, William.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13427  aAUTH03183cgm a2200397 i 9918249811306676DNLM20260227161452.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210826s2021    mdu580|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31854  a(OCoLC)1426283625  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 141.5.N472 aNIMH Workshop on Neuroimaging of Individual Differences and Naturalistic Stimulid(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNIMH workshop on naturalistic stimuli and individual differences /cNIMH, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (9 hr., 40 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded August 2, 2021.3 a(CIT): The Center for Multimodal Neuroimaging is thrilled to announce the upcoming workshop on Naturalistic Stimuli and Individual Differences with a range of extramural and intramural speakers presenting on their cutting-edge work.  The workshop seeks to: bring together top scientists using naturalistic stimuli and/or the study of individual differences in neuroimaging; showcase theoretical, methodological, and analytical advances in these areas of research; and serve as a catalyst for collaborations between presenters, biomedical NIH researchers, and experts from around the world.  Speakers: Chris Baldassano, Janice Chen, Elizabeth DuPre, Emily  S. Finn, Javier Gonzalez-Castillo, Uri Hasson, Jeremy Manning, Carolyn Parkinson, Elizabeth Redcay, Monica Rosenberg, Tamara Vanderwal, Gang Chen, Peter Molfese    Chris Baldassano - https://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42538&start=133  Elizabeth DuPre - https://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42538&start=2831   Janice Chen - https://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42538&start=5927    Gang Chen - https://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42538&start=7450    Emily S Finn - https://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42538&start=10164     Javier Gonzalez-Castillo -  https://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42538&start=13214    Jeremey Manning - https://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42538&start=16766    Carolyn Parkinson - https://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42538&start=18368     Elizabeth Redcay - https://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42538&start=20638     Monica Rosenberg - https://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42538&start=23549    Tammy Vanderwal - https://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42538&start=26564    Uri Hasson - https://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42538&start=29491    https://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42538&start=20638  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 9, 2022).12aFunctional Neuroimaging22aMental Processesxphysiology 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Mental Health (U.S.),ehost institution.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42538  aAUTH01702cgm a22004574a 101268598DNLM20260227150910.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu654 ||||||f|   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12279  a(DNLM)CIT:12280  a(OCoLC)85885143  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2062 aNCI Board of Scientific Advisors.bMeetingn(29th :b2004 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNCI Board of Scientific Advisors, November 2004 /cthe National Cancer Institute (NCI) Division of Extramural Activities.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 9, 2007).  aStreaming video (10 hr., 53 min., 27 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 8-9, 2004.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.)12aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration12aNeoplasms22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1227940uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12280  aAUTH02551cgm a2200457 i 9918265700106676DNLM20260227161457.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210913s2021    mdu235|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31877  a(OCoLC)1312793109  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 3482 aNIH Music and Health (Meeting)n(3rd :d2021 August 25 :cOnline)10aRelating target engagement to clinical benefit :bbiomarkers for brain disorders of aging /cNational Institutes of Health, Foundation for the National Institutes of Health, Renee Fleming Foundation. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (3 hr., 55 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded August 25, 2021.3 a(CIT): This is the last of three meetings that are part of Phase I of an NIH/Foundation for the National Institutes of Health (FNIH) project to develop evidence-based music therapies for brain disorders of aging.This meeting will gather input from participants in the music therapy/music medicine, neuroscience, behavioral intervention development, clinical trial methodology, and patient advocacy/art organization communities. The NIH planning committee will identify and catalog a set of reliable, broadly accessible biomarkers to objectively and quantitatively monitor the responses of the brain and other physiological systems to music-based interventions in clinical studies. This core dataset of biomarkers will be used in future NIH-funded music-based intervention protocols.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 14, 2022).12aBrain Diseasesxtherapy12aMusic Therapy22aAged22aBiomarkers22aAlzheimer Disease 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institute of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body,eissuing body.2 aFoundation for the National Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aRenée Fleming Foundation,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41754  aAUTH01210nam a2200373 i 9918976858106676DNLM20260206083132.0240615s2023    gw ac    b    001 0cger    a9783844089318qhardbound  a3844089314qhardbound  a(OCoLC)1439690988  aOHXbengerdacOHX  apcc  9Germany00aWZ 112.5.O7 7aRE2lcco1 aSchmidt, Dieter,d1939-eauthor.10aZwölf bedeutende Freiburger Augenärzte des 19. und der ersten Hälfte des 20. Jahrhunderts /cDieter Schmidt. 1aDüren :bShaker Verlag,c2023.  aviii, 927 pages :billustrations, portraits ;c25 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBerichte aus der Medizin,x0945-0890  aIncludes bibliographical references and indexes.12aOphthalmologistsxhistory22aOphthalmologyxhistory22aHistory, 19th Century22aHistory, 20th Century 2aGermany 2aBiography 0aBerichte aus der Medizin,x0945-0890.  aAUTH01626cas a2200505   0137356DNLM20260227090951.