on.  3Vol. 6, no. 2 (1994)c19950520fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paperxFurther review is required to determine exactly when acid-free paper was first used for this title22aAged 2aGeriatric Psychiatry 2aMental Disorders 2aPeriodical2 aInternational Psychogeriatric Association.0 x1741-203X4 uhttps://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/international-psychogeriatrics4 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/international-psychogeriatrics  aAUTH02434ngm a2200481 i 9918975956506676DNLM20260227165118.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u250104s2024    mdu119|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35443  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 54504aThe cancer autoimmune connection :bdecoding the paradox /cOffice of Autoimmune Disease Research in the Office of Research on Women"s Health. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2024]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 59 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aScience TALKS0 aThe link between scleroderma and cancer / Ami Shah -- What can cancer immunotherapy teach us about autoimmune endocrinopathies? / Maureen A. Su -- Stem-like T cells in cancer and autoimmunity / Ana C. Anderson.  aRecorded December 5, 2024.3 a(CIT):The purpose of the OADR-ORWH ScienceTALKS series is to examine the state of the science in specific areas impacting research on autoimmune disease and women"s health. This session of Science Talks is focused on the connection of the pathways involved in cancer and autoimmune disease development and will bring together experts in the field to generate discussion and identify opportunities to advance research as it pertains to the link between autoimmune diseases and cancer.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 23, 2026).12aAutoimmune Diseasesximmunology22aAutoimmune Diseasesxcomplications22aNeoplasmsxetiology22aNeoplasmsximmunology22aNeoplasmsxcomplications22aImmunotherapy 2aWebcast1 aShah, Ami M.,espeaker.1 aSu, Maureen A.,espeaker.1 aAnderson, Ana Carrizossa,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Autoimmune Disease Research,eissuing body. 0aOADR-ORWH science talks series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=55174  aAUTH01638cgm a2200445 a 101595126DNLM20260227154509.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u121025s2012    mdu069|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17541  a(OCoLC)820532316  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 1401 aStuder, Lorenz.10aApplications of human pluripotent stem cells--from pain to therapeutic gain? /cLorenz Studer.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 9 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH CRM/SCIG stem cell seminar  aRecorded Sept. 4, 2012.3 a(CIT): NIH CRM/SCIG Stem Cell Seminar Series (Extramural): Dr. Lorenz Studer Director, Sloan Kettering Institute for Stem Cell Biology, Department of Developmental Biology.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aNervous System Diseasesxtherapy22aDisease Models, Animal22aDopaminergic Neuronsxtransplantation22aInduced Pluripotent Stem Cellsxtransplantation 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNCRM/SCIG stem cell seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17541  aAUTH01903cgm a22004454a 101268593DNLM20260227150843.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu050 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12270  a(OCoLC)123962737  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5681 aHendricks, Robert L.10aRegulation of memory CD8+ T cell function during viral latency /cRobert L. Hendricks.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 4, 2007).  aStreaming video (50 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 3, 2004.3 a(CIT): The Immunology Interest Group (IIG) organizes activities designed to promote information exchange and interactions among NIH scientists interested in the field of immunology, broadly defined. Interactions are facilitated via weekly meetings on current topics as well as an annual Immunology Retreat.  For more information, visit the Immunology Interest Group's Web site.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aCD8-Positive T-Lymphocytesximmunology22aAntibody Formationximmunology22aVirus Activationximmunology22aVirus Latencyximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12270  aAUTH01489cas a2200505   0421406DNLM20260227090927.0cr|cn ||||||||740701c19749999gw  x p       0    0ger    asn 86008794   a0001-7868l0001-7868  aAKURAJ  a(DNLM)A19605000(s)  a(OCoLC)06455072  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 agerbeng  9Germany00aW1bAK996K10aAktuelle Urol2dnlm 0aActuelle Urologie00aAktuelle Urologie.30aActuelle Urologie  aStuttgart,bThieme  av.billus.  aSix no. a year,b2004-  aSix no. a year,b1974-1996  aSeven no. a year,b1997-2003  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aBd. 5-       Jan. 1974-1 aMEDLINEbv34n1, Jan. 2003-0 aPubMedbv34n1, Jan. 2003-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aSummaries also in English. 2aUrology 2aPeriodical0 tThieme drug reportx1865-28750 x1438-882000tActuelle Urologiew(OCoLC)3256116305tZeitschrift für Urologie und Nephrologiex0044-3611w(OCoLC)17705474 uhttps://www.thieme-connect.de/products/ejournals/journal/10.1055/s-00000007  aAUTH02878cgm a22004214a 101308566DNLM20260227151959.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070605s2007    mdu062|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13792  a(OCoLC)145014731  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 503.31 aMorris, Martina,d1955-10aLocal acts, global consequences :bnetworks and the spread of HIV /cMartina Morris.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from screen banner.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 2 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 25, 2007.3 a(CIT): For HIV and other sexually transmitted infections (STI), the potential for epidemic spread depends on connectivity in the underlying sexual network. Since most people have few partners, traditional STI theory and prevention strategies have focused on the role of "core groups" and "superspreaders" in establishing the connectivity needed to sustain transmission.  This has been a successful approach for curable STI in low prevalence settings, but is not as effective in explaining or intervening in a generalized epidemic, as HIV has become in many sub-Saharan African countries.  In this context, transmission is well established in the broad group of less active persons.  We show how network connectivity can emerge in such networks, with the central role played by concurrent partnerships-partnerships that overlap in time - rather than high rates of partner change.  This has direct implications for HIV and STI prevention.  One is that a small change in behavior may be quite effective in this context, making behavior change a more viable prevention target than many believe. Another is that the right behavior change message is not to "have fewer partners," but, as Ugandan HIV messages put it 10 years ago, "zero grazing."  We use a combination of empirical data (comparing Thailand, Uganda and the US, and racial groups in the US) and simulation to demonstrate these principles, and their relevance to the disparities in HIV prevalence, both worldwide and within the United States.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHIV Infectionsxtransmission22aHIV Infectionsxepidemiology 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13792  aAUTH02144cas a2200577   0054655DNLM20260227090915.0cr|cn ||||||||660822c19389999sz  x         0    0ger    a0030-3755l0030-3755  aOPHTAD  a(DNLM)O07940000(s)  a(OCoLC)01716836  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Switzerland00aW1bOP33710aOphthalmologica2dnlm00aOphthalmologica.bJournal international d'ophtalmologie.  International journal of ophthalmology.  Zeitschrift für Augenheilkunde.30aInternational journal of ophthalmology30aJournal international d'ophtalmologie30aZeitschrift für Augenheilkunde  aBasel.3 aBasel :bKarger  av.billus.  aSix no. a year  aEight no. a yearb19??-1993  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 96-       Oct. 1938-  aBound with v. 96 is Abhandlungen aus der Augenheilkunde und ihren Grenzgebieten, Heft 26-27, 1938.5DNLM1 aMEDLINEbv147n2, 1964-1 aPubMedbv147n2, 1964-0 aOLDMEDLINE1 aIndex medicus  aMonographic supplements accompany some volumes.  aAlso available online.  aArticles in German, French, or English, with summaries in these languages.  aOrgan of the Schweizerische Ophthalmologische Gesellschaft and the Nederlandsch Oogheelkundig Gezelschap, 1946-  aVol. 96-138, 1938-59.  1 v.  aContinues Zeitschrift für Augenheilkunde.  3Vol. 183, no. 1 (1981)c19920831fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aOphthalmology 2aPeriodical2 aNederlands Oogheelkundig Gezelschap.2 aSchweizerische Ophthalmologische Gesellschaft.0 x1423-026710tZeitschrift für Augenheilkunde4 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/224269  aAUTH03302cgm a22004814a 101308589DNLM20260227152004.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070605s2007    mdu057 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13840  a(OCoLC)156863571  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQL 696.S851 aKnudsen, Eric I.10aMechanics of learning and attention in the auditory system of the barn owl /cEric Knudsen.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen (viewed June 19, 2007).  aStreaming video (56 min., 48 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 23, 2007.3 a(CIT): One of the most fascinating properties of the brain is its ability to learn: its ability to alter its functional properties as a consequence of the interactions of an animal with the environment. The influence of experience on the brain is strong especially during early life and is modulated throughout life by the internal state of the animal. Attention, in particular, has a powerful modulating effect on learning.  I will discuss a well-studied example of these principles: The effects of experience and attention on sound localization and auditory spatial processing in the barn owl.    Dr. Knudsen is the Edward C. and Amy H. Sewall Professor at Stanford University School of Medicine. His research focuses on mechanisms of learning and attention and strategies of information processing in the central auditory system. He investigates how the capacity for learning changes with development and the effects of experience on structure and function in the brain.   Dr. Knudsen received his Ph.D. from the University of California, San Diego in 1976, completed a postdoctoral fellowship at the California Institute of Technology in 1979, when he joined the faculty of the Department of Neurobiology at Stanford University. Dr. Knudsen has received awards that include the Newcomb Cleveland Prize, the Society for Neuroscience Young Investigator Award, McKnight Neuroscience Development and Senior Investigator Awards, the Troland Research Award, and the Peter Gruber Prize in Neuroscience. Dr. Knudsen is a member of the National Academy of Sciences.   The NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series includes weekly scientific talks by some of the top researchers in the biomedical sciences worldwide.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aAuditory Pathwaysxphysiology12aStrigiformes22aAttentionxphysiology22aLearningxphysiology22aNeuronal Plasticityxphysiology22aSound Localizationxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13840  aAUTH02480cgm a2200481 i 101774291DNLM20260227160500.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu224|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31404  a(OCoLC)1246215267  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 3902 aNIH Rural Health Seminard(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aRural Health Seminar :bchallenges in the era of COVID-19 /cNIH.1 a2020 NIH Rural Health Seminar1 aNIH Rural Health Seminar1 aChallenges in the era of COVID-19 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (3 hr., 44 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded November 19, 2020.3 a(CIT): Today, approximately 20% of the U.S. population--about 60 million people--live in rural areas, which make up 97% of the land area in the United States. People living in rural America have less access to health care and are more likely than residents of urban areas to die from chronic conditions such as heart disease, cancer, stroke, and chronic lower respiratory disease. Moreover, long-standing systemic health and social inequities have put many rural residents at increased risk of getting COVID-19. The rural/urban inequities in health and health care warrant more rigorous and innovative scientific research to improve testing, contact tracing, and future vaccination for rural Americans. This seminar will explore the impact of the COVID-19 on rural populations, systems and workforce issues and community engagement to respond to the pandemic.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 8, 2021).12aRural Health12aCoronavirus Infections22aPandemics22aHealthcare Disparities22aRelief Work 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=38788  aAUTH02321cgm a2200481 i 9918282192006676DNLM20260227161550.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u211008s2021    mdu556|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31948  a(DNLM)CIT:31953  a(OCoLC)1314048501  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWS 1202 aExploring the Science Surrounding the Safe Use of Bioactive Ingredients in Infant Formula: Considerations for an Assessment Framework (Workshop)d(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aExploring the science surrounding the safe use of bioactive ingredients in infant formula :bconsiderations for an assessment framework /cNICHD, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (9 hr., 16 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 23 & 24, 2021.3 a(CIT): This 2-day, virtual workshop-- Exploring the Science Surrounding the Safe Use of Bioactive Ingredients in Infant Formula: Considerations for an Assessment Framework-- focuses on the functional state-of-the-science of biologically active human milk components and analogs and the implications for safety assessments when used in infant formula.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 29, 2022).12aInfant Formulaxanalysis12aFood Ingredientsxanalysis22aMicrobiotaximmunology22aMilk, Humanximmunology22aInfant Nutritional Physiological Phenomena22aFood Safety 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4254540uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42547  aAUTH01321cas a2200433 a 9308798DNLM20260227091014.0cr||||||||||||930520c19939999nz mr p       0    0eng    asn 94038011   a1172-0360l1172-0360  aDTHPEE  a(DNLM)SR0076299(s)  a(OCoLC)29573587  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9New Zealand00aW1bDR893D10aDrugs Ther Perspect2dnlm00aDrugs & therapy perspectives :bfor rational drug selection and use.3 aDrugs and therapy perspectives  aMairangi Bay, Auckland, N.Z. :bAdis International Ltd.,cc1993-3 aAuckland :bAdis, Springer International  av. :bill.  aMonthly,b2002-  aTwenty four no. a year,b-2001  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Jan. 25, 1993)-  aTitle from caption.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online. 2aDrug Therapy 2aPharmaceutical Preparations 2aPeriodical0 x1179-19774 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/40267  aAUTH02370cgm a2200469 i 101774295DNLM20260227160509.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu663 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31411  a(DNLM)CIT:31409  a(OCoLC)1246807817  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 5062 aNIH COVID-19 Research Workshopd(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNIH COVID-19 Research Workshop /cNCI, NIH and FDA.1 iHTML header:aNIH COVID-19 Research Workshop :bGeneral Session 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (11 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded October 29 & 30, 2020.3 a(CIT): Last year brought unprecedented changes to the world with the COVID-19 pandemic. In response to this challenge, the scientific community has mobilized on many fronts with advances in vaccines, diagnostics, treatments, pathology, immunology, virology, structural biology, cell biology, and many other areas.  To help facilitate interactions and highlight work from NIH/FDA investigators, the NIH COVID-19 scientific interest group is hosting a virtual workshop for NIH/FDA staff on Thursday, October 29, and Friday, October 30, 2020, from 11:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. We look forward to your active participation to make this workshop a success during this challenging time.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 19, 2021).12aCOVID-19 2aConference Proceedings 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aUnited States.bFood and Drug Administration,eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4013740uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40138  aAUTH02241cgm a2200445 i 9918282191706676DNLM20260227161553.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u211008s2021    mdu323|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31952  a(OCoLC)1314048641  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.55.H92 aOHRP Exploratory Workshopn(4th :d2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aReview of third-party research risks :bis there a role for IRBs? /cOHRP. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (5 hr., 23 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 24, 2021.3 a(CIT): This workshop has a morning and an afternoon session. Only invited speakers and a few OHRP are expected to attend in-person. Speakers will give each give a short presentation. There will be a one-hour panel discussion following the morning and the afternoon sessions on September 24, 2021. In the morning session, the agenda is "What do we mean by third parties in research? What rights and protections, if any, might they merit?" In the afternoon session, the agenda is "Do IRBs have a role in the review of third-party research risks and if so, when?".  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 28, 2022).12aHuman Experimentationxethics12aThird-Party Consentxethics22aRisk Assessment22aClinical Trials as Topicxethics22aEthics Committees, Researchxstandards 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings1 aUnited States.bDepartment of Health and Human Services.bOffice for Human Research Protections,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41987  aAUTH03429cgm a22004454a 101319980DNLM20260227152153.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u071101s2007    mdu066|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14106  a(OCoLC)182912983  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5701 aBeutler, Bruce.10aHow we sense microbes /cBruce Beutler.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aKinyoun lecture  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 28, 2007).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 6 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 25, 2007.3 a(CIT): The Kinyoun Lecture  The less you understand, the better forward genetics works, says Bruce Beutler, M.D., describing the technique he has used to unveil fundamental actions of the immune system. Forward genetics starts when scientists observe a novel trait or appearance in a mouse or other model organism, and then turn to the search for the responsible gene or genes. What this approach may tell us about how mammals detect infection is the topic of the 2007 Joseph J. Kinyoun lecture. Beutler, of The Scripps Research Institute (TSRI) in La Jolla, California, will present his lecture "How We Sense Microbes."  At the core of immunology, notes Beutler, is the question of how we distinguish self from non-self. Ever since microbes were recognized as the cause of infectious disease, a corollary seemed obvious: plants, animals, and people must have ways to sense and respond to invading (non-self) microbes. For example, says Beutler, researchers observed decades ago that mammals react swiftly and dramatically to injections of what was originally called endotoxin a component of many bacterial cell walls now known to be a lipopolysaccharide (LPS). It was also observed years ago that some strains of mutant mice failed to react at all to LPS, adds Beutler, implying that the mice lacked a sensor. But the identity of the putative LPS sensor remained elusive, he says.   In 1998, Beutler, who was then at the University of Texas, Southwestern, and his colleagues used forward genetics to explain the mutant animals inability to detect LPS. The researchers determined that the mice had mutations in the Lps gene, which eliminated the action of Toll-like receptor 4 (TLR4) protein and caused the mice to be insensitive to LPS. The finding, published in Science, was the first demonstration of an infection-sensing role for a mammalian TLR protein. The paper is regarded as a seminal contribution to understanding innate immunity. Bruce Beutler, M.D., Professor and Chairman of the Department of Genetics and Professor of Immunology, The Scripps Research Institute, La Jolla, CA.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aToll-Like Receptorsxgenetics12aToll-Like Receptorsximmunology22aImmunity, Innate 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aKinyoun lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14106  aAUTH01427cas a2200469 a 9421097DNLM20260227091030.0cr|cnu||||||||950202c19949999enk|| p       0    0eng    a1070-5503l1070-5503  aIJBMFT  a(DNLM)SR0078749(s)  a(OCoLC)28407276  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9England00aW1bIN7655MB10aInt J Behav Med2dnlm 0aInternational journal of behavioral medicine00aInternational journal of behavioral medicine.  aHillsdale, NJ :bLawrence Erlbaum Associates,cc1994-3 aLondon :bInforma Healthcare  av. :bill.  aFour no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1-  aTitle from cover.  aImprint varies.1 aMEDLINEbv9n1, 2002-1 aPubMedbv9n1, 2002-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso available online.  aOfficial journal of: The International Society of Behavioral Medicine. 2aBehavioral Medicine 2aDiseasexpsychology 2aPeriodical2 aInternational Society of Behavioral Medicine.0 x1532-75584 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/12529  aAUTH01844cas a2200505   0376473DNLM20260227090921.0cr|cn ||||||||hd|bfa012baap731128c19049999dcubr p       0    0eng    a0033-2909l0033-2909  aPSBUAI  a(DNLM)P54580000(s)  a(OCoLC)01681351  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bPS55710aPsychol Bull2dnlm00aPsychological bulletin.  aWashington [etc.] :bAmerican Psychological Association  av.billus.  aBimonthly  aMonthly,b1904-1945  aBimonthly,b1