 genetic instability and aging, <1989-1996->; Reviews in genetic toxicology, <1975-1996->; Genetic toxicology testing (called Genetic toxicology, May 1994- ), <1991-1996>; Mutation research genomics, <1997-2001>; Reviews in mutation research, <1997->; Genetic toxicology and environmental mutagenesis, <1997->; all in a single numbering sequence.1 aMEDLINEbv2n1, Feb. 1965-0 aPubMedbv2n1, Feb. 1965-0 aOLDMEDLINE1 aIndex medicus  aBeginning with 2012 each section has its own numbering.  aPublished 1973-    with vol. numbering not in chronological sequence.  aVol. 462, no. 2-3 (Apr. 2000) was defective.  It was reprinted subsequently with p. 395-428 added.  aAlso issued online.  Each section has its own site.  3Vol. 226, no. 3 (1989)c19900917fNotice publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aChromosome Aberrations 2aMutation 2aPeriodical02aDNA repair.02aMutation research letters.02aEnvironmental mutagenesis and related subjects including methodology.02aDNAging.02aReviews in genetic toxicology.02aGenetic toxicology testing.02aSection on environmental mutagenesis.02aGenetic toxicology and environmental mutagenesis.02aMutation research genomics.02aReviews in mutation research.02aDNA repair reports.0 x1873-135X01tDNA repairx1568-7864w(DLC)   2002233074w(OCoLC)489535874 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/mutation-research-dna-repair4 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/mutation-research-dna-repair-reports4 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/mutation-research-dnaging4 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/mutation-research-environmental-mutagenesis-and-related-subjects4 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/mutation-research-fundamental-and-molecular-mechanisms-of-mutagenesis4 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/mutation-research-genetic-toxicology4 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/mutation-research-genetic-toxicology-and-environmental-mutagenesis4 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/mutation-research-letters4 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/mutation-research-mutation-research-genomics4 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/mutation-research-reviews-in-genetic-toxicology4 uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/mutation-research-reviews-in-mutation-research  aAUTH02258cam a2200397 i 63220270RDNLM20260210092454.0hd|bfa012baap820724s1901    nyuabf   b    001 0 eng    a(OCoLC)14798035  aDNLMbengerdad20260205dDNLM  9United States00aWAAbR514a 19011 aRichards, Ellen H.q(Ellen Henrietta),d1842-1911,eauthor.10aAir, water, and food, from a sanitary standpoint /cby Ellen H. Richards and Alpheus G. Woodman.  aFirst edition. 1aNew York :bJohn Wiley & Sons ;aLondon :bChapman & Hall,c1901.  a3 unnumbered leaves, 226 pages, 2 unnumbered leaves of plates (1 folded) :billustrations, map ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aPublisher's short-title catalogue on 16 pages bound at end.  aNLM microfilm copy shelved as: Film 7507 no. 65DNLM  aIncludes "Bibliography" on pages 213-218 and index.0 aChapter I. Three essentials of human existence -- II. Air: composition, impurities, relation to human life -- III. The problem of ventilation -- IV. Methods of examination -- V. Water: source, properties, solvent power, as a carrier -- VI. The problem of safe water and interpretation of analyses -- VII. Methods of examination -- VIII. Food in relation to human life, definition, sources, classes, dietaries -- IX. Adulteration and sophistication of food materials -- X. Methods of food analysis -- Appendices, tables, reagents -- Bibliography.  aMicrofilm.b[Bethesda, Md.] :cNational Library of Medicine,d1997.e1 microfilm reel : negative ; 35 mm.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202011192pda5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20260210lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM12aSanitation22aEnvironmental Pollution22aFood Quality22aSanitary Engineering1 aWoodman, A. G.q(Alpheus Grant),d1873-eauthor.  aAUTH02533cgm a2200469 i 9918249812906676DNLM20260227161422.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210826s2021    mdu907|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31821  a(DNLM)CIT:31823  a(OCoLC)1291869617  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 832 aAnnual Common Fund Glycoscience Program All Hands Meetingd(2021 July :cOnline)jauthor..10a2021 Annual Common Fund Glycoscience Program All Hands Meeting /cNIH Common Fund Glycoscience Program. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (15 hr., 7 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded July 6 & 7, 20213 a(CIT): The NIH Common Fund Glycoscience Program is focused on creating new methodologies, tools, and technologies (MTTs) to study glycans. The MTTs developed are expected to be easily adapted by the broader biomedical research community. Achieving this goal is expected to allow investigators to fully explore the roles of carbohydrates in relevant biological models, pathways, and/or diseases of interest, rather than abandon such discovery due to a lack of tools or expertise. Initiatives of this program have been designed to encourage new strategies and high-risk approaches for methods and tool development with subsequent rapid transition of successful projects to commercialization. This is the annual meeting of participating investigators in this program.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed January 3, 2022).12aPolysaccharidesxphysiology22aGlycosylation22aGlycoproteinsxphysiology22aChemistry Techniques, Syntheticxmethods22aChemistry Techniques, Syntheticxinstrumentation 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aCommon Fund Glycoscience Program,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4231040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42312  aAUTH03100cgm a2200457 i 101744883DNLM20260227155908.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190402s2019    mdu067|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27384  a(OCoLC)1097610997  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.500aIn scientific method we don't just trust :bor, why replication has more value than discovery /cJohn P.A. Ioannidis. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 7 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture, Robert S. Gordon, Jr. lecture  aRecorded March 13, 2019.3 a(CIT): NIH Director's Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series  Science is the best thing that has happened to Homo sapiens. It is important to apply the scientific method in ways that are the most efficient in leading to--and translating--important discoveries. However, this goal is not easy. There are many situations where research practices are applied in suboptimal ways, resulting in a reproducibility crisis in which trust in scientific findings is diminished. In his lecture, Dr. Ioannidis will discuss how we can improve the robustness, efficiency, and transparency of research practices. In particular, emphasis on replication may improve the credibility track record of current investigations across many different disciplines. Several disciplines--such as genetic epidemiology--that have already incorporated replication as a sine qua non have seen a dramatic improvement of their credibility. In most scientific disciplines, replication has far more scientific and practical value than discovery. In the current environment, in most fields, discovery without replication is mostly a nuisance. This annual lecture is named in honor of Robert S. Gordon Jr., former Assistant Surgeon General of the U.S. Public Health Service and Special Assistant to former NIH Director James Wyngaarden. Topics focus on clinical research and epidemiology. Speakers are selected by the NIH Office of Disease Prevention.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 16, 2019).12aBiomedical Research12aReproducibility of Results22aInterdisciplinary Research22aResearch Design22aScience in Literature 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aIōannidēs, Iōannēs P. A.espeaker.2 aNational Institute of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pRobert S. Gordon, Jr. lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27384  aAUTH01915cgm a2200505 i 101631781DNLM20260227154622.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140501s2014    mdu093|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18366  a(OCoLC)880685407  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWK 8101 aSatterfield, Dawn,eauthor.10aHealing our community through narrative :bthe power of storytelling /cDr. Dawn Satterfield, Dr. Lawrence Agodoa. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 33 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNational Native American heritage 2014 speaker series  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded April 3, 2014.3 a(CIT): The program focuses on the power of personal narratives and storytelling in promoting the health and well-being of Native communities.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed May 16, 2014.12aDiabetes Mellitusxethnology12aIndians, North American22aDiabetes 