NCI OCCAM monthly lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (52 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 9, 2009.3 a(CIT): The NCI's Office of Cancer Complementary and Alternative Medicine (OCCAM) invites you to view its monthly lecture series.  With the goal of informing the National Cancer Institute (NCI) community about the variety of ongoing research in cancer and complementary and alternative medicine (CAM), NCI's Office of Cancer Complementary and Alternative Medicine (OCCAM) has created a monthly lecture series on cancer CAM. These hour long lectures, occurring from 10 a.m. - 11 a.m. on the second Wednesday of each month, will feature a fifty minute presentation on a cancer CAM topic and allow ten minutes for questions.   For more information, visit  http://www.cancer.gov/cam/news_lectures.html.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aProstatic Neoplasmsxdrug therapy22aAntineoplastic Agents, Phytogenicxtherapeutic use22aBoswellia22aLeukemiaxdrug therapy22aPhytotherapy 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.).bOffice of Cancer Complementary and Alternative Medicine. 0aOCCAM monthly lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15522  aAUTH01792cas a2200493   7505881DNLM20260227090916.0cr|cn#||||||||hd|bfa012baap750626c19449999enkqr p       0||| 0eng    a0029-6651l0029-6651  aPNUSA4  a(DNLM)P40880000(s)  a(OCoLC)01760948  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9England00aW1bPR586HN10aProc Nutr Soc2dnlm04aThe Proceedings of the Nutrition Society.  aLondon,bCambridge Univ. Press [etc.]3 3<1999->:a[Wallingford, Oxon, UK] :bPublished on behalf of the Nutrition Society by CABI Publishing  av.billus.,ports.  a4 no. a year,b1998-  aThree no. a year,b-1997  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-5, 1944-1946/47; v. 12-    1953-  aPlace of publication varies.  aProceedings for 1947-52 issued in the British journal of nutrition.1 aMEDLINEbv24n2, 1965-1 aPubMedbv24n2, 1965-0 aOLDMEDLINE1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aMicrofilm.mv. 1 (1944)-5 (1947), v. 12 (1953)-25 (1966).b[Bethesda, Md.] :cNational Library of Medicine,d1991.e5 microfilm reels : negative ; 35 mm.  3Vol. 50, no. 3 (1991)c19920909fNotice not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aNutritional Physiological Phenomena 2aPeriodical2 aNutrition Society (Great Britain)0 x1475-27194 uhttps://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/proceedings-of-the-nutrition-society  aAUTH04740cam a2200757 a 9414106DNLM20260206222415.0cr||||||||||||hd|bfa012baap941125s1499    it            000 0 lat    a(OCoLC)1237242123  aDNLMcDNLMedcrmbdDNLM  9Italy00aWZ 230bM758c 14991 aMontagnana, Bartolomeo,dactive 1422-1460.10aConsilia medica10aConsilia Magistri Bartholomei Montagnane ;bTractatus tres de balneis patauinis ; De compositione et Dosi medicinarum ; Anthidotarium eiusdem. Consilia D[omi]ni Antonii Cermisoni. Tractatus de theriaca a Fra[n]cisco caballo edit[us].  a[Venice :bSimon de Luere, for Andreas Torresanus,c20 Aug. 1499]  a[6], 414 leaves (leaf 406 blank) ;c31 cm. (fol.)  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aEdited by Jacobus de Vitalibus.  aReprinted, with additions, from the 1497 Scotus-Locatellus edition (Goff M815). Contains the text of Consilia medica (leaves 1-335), De balneis Patavinis (leaves 336v-339), De compositione et dosi medicinarum (leaves 339-344v), and Antidotarium (leaves 344v-354v) by Bartolomeo Montagnana; De mirachiali dispositione, also known as the De dispositione hypochondriaci (leaves 335-336) doubtfully attributed to Montagnana in text; Consilia medica (leaves 355-405) by Antonio Cermisone, and De theriaca (leaves 406-414) by Franciscus Caballus.  aImprint from Goff.  aTwo colophons. First colophon on leaf 405 reads: Hoc uolumen Impressum est Venetijs per Simonem de Luere Impe[n]sis D[omi]ni Andree Torresani de Asula. xx. Aug. M.ccccic. Second colophon on leaf 414 reads: Finit liber de a[n]i[m]ali theria pastillos theriaca[m]q[ue] [con]ficiente a Francisco Caballo Brixiensi viro preclaro: Venetijs editus.  aSignatures: a⁶ b-y⁸ ç⁸ [et]⁸ [con]⁸ [rum]⁸ 2a-2s⁸ 2t¹⁰ 2v-2z⁸ 2[et]¹⁰ 2[con]¹⁰ 2[rum]⁸; 2[con]10 blank.  aPrinted on Chancery size paper in double columns; 69 lines, plus headline, to a full page; area of text: 234 (245) x 152 mm.; col. width: 74 mm. Initials spaces, with guide-letters, printed paragraph marks; without catchwords.  Register on leaf 405v.  aNLM copy, leaf size: 308 x 210 mm.5DNLM  aNLM copy, not rubricated.5DNLM  aNLM copy, extensive marginal manuscript annotations in several hands and underlinings in brown ink throughout; annotations in brown on front and back endpapers.5DNLM  aNLM microfilm copy shelved as: Film 48-71 no. 25DNLM4 aBM 15th cent.,cV, p. 574 (IB.24651)4 aGoffcM8164 aGWcM252824 aKlebsc689.44 aSchullian, D.M.  NLM Incun.,c329  aMicrofilm.b[Bethesda, Md.] :cNational Library of Medicine,d1948.e1 microfilm reel : negative ; 35 mm.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  cPurchase;aLeo S. Olschki.5DNLM  aNLM copy, ownership inscription of Adam Krentzer on front endpaper.5DNLM  aNLM copy, bound in blind stamped pigskin over wood boards, with bevelled edges; vellum manuscript fragments (rubricated text in Latin) used as guards inserted at front and rear boards; metal clasps, restored; shelf mark '93' and titles inked on fore-edge.5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc201911262pda5DNLM  aConservedc201911262pda5DNLM  ainspection and duplication of camera master microfilm deferredc200501055DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aMedicine22aHumoralism22aBalneology22aAntidotes22aMateria Medica 2aCollected Work 2aIncunabula1 aVitalibus, Jacobus de,eeditor.1 aLuere, Simone da,eprinter.1 aTorresanus, Andreas,cde Asula,d1451-1529,epublisher.12aMontagnana, Bartolomeo,dactive 1422-1460.tDe balneis Patavinis.12aMontagnana, Bartolomeo,dactive 1422-1460.tDe compositione et dosi medicinarum.12aMontagnana, Bartolomeo,dactive 1422-1460.tAntidotarium.12aCermisone, Antonio,d-1441.tConsilia medica.12aCaballus, Franciscus,d-1540.tDe theriaca.2 aLeo S. Olschki (Firm),ebookseller.5DNLM02aDe dispositione hypochondriaci.4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/9414106  aAUTH04270cgm a22004214a 101518968DNLM20260227153134.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u091105s2009    mdu067|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15350  a(OCoLC)489622804  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 3751 aPiwnica-Worms, David Rolland,d1956-10aReal-time imaging of signal transduction pathways using genetically-encoded reporters in vivo /cDavid Piwnica-Worms.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 7 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Oct. 13, 2009.3 a(CIT): Dr. Piwnica-Worms is Professor of Radiology, Professor of Developmental Biology, and Director of the Molecular Imaging Center at Washington University in St. Louis. He earned his bachelor's degree from Stanford University, and graduated from the Duke University Medical Scientist Training Program. He continued his training at the Brigham & Women"s Hospital in Diagnostic Radiology and began his first faculty appointments at Harvard Medical School. After rising to Associate Professor, Dr. Piwnica-Worms was recruited to Washington University School of Medicine and the Mallinckrodt Institute of Radiology. Dr. Piwnica-Worms has been a pioneer of the field of molecular imaging. His research interests focus on development of reporter systems for imaging signal transduction pathways and protein-protein interactions in live cells and animal models in real time. He has developed fusion reporters for bioluminescent analysis of protein processing in living cells and animals in vivo, IaBk processing, mTOR signaling and the Wnt/B-catenin pathway. He also is interested in biochemical analysis of the function and regulation of the multidrug resistance P-glycoprotein family of transporters. He has spearheaded translational research directed toward imaging cell-penetrating peptides and technetium-99m labeled radiopharmaceuticals in medical imaging applications. He was an Established Investigator of the American Heart Association, a Research Scholar of the Radiological Society of North America and received the Stauffer Award from the Association of University Radiologists. He has delivered several named lectureships, including the President"s Distinguished Lectureship at Memorial Sloan-Kettering Cancer Center. He has been a member or chair of several NIH review panels, serves on committees of the AACR and Institute of Medicine, and is an Associate Editor of Cancer Research and on the Editorial Board of Molecular Imaging. He is a founding member and former president of the Society for Molecular Imaging, and recipient of the Society for Molecular Imaging Achievement Award as well as the Distinguished Alumnus Award, Duke University Medical School.  NCI"s Center for Cancer Research (CCR) Grand Rounds is a weekly lecture series addressing current research in clinical and molecular oncology. Speakers are leading national and international researchers and clinicians proposed by members of the CCR Grand Rounds Planning Committee and others within the CCR community and approved by the CCR Office of the Director. Lectures occur every Tuesday from 8:00 to 9:00 a.m. in Lipsett Amphitheater in the Clinical Center building on the NIH campus September through July with exceptions around holidays and major cancer meetings. The lecture schedule is posted on various calendars of events, including at the following link:  http://www.bethesdatrials.cancer.gov/health-care-professionals/grand-rounds.aspx.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aSignal Transduction22aMolecular Imaging 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15350  aAUTH01450cam a2200313 i 450000100100000000300050001000500170001500700150003200800410004703500200008804000350010804400180014306000190016124502080018026400660038830000450045433600260049933700280052533800270055350001670058054001420074758300720088958300310096165000130099270000300100570000500103585600420108599900090112760311510RDNLM20260206221431.0cr||||||||||||820626s1900    nyua          000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)14781979  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aBFbN338p 190000aHypnotism and hypnotic suggestion :ba scientific treatise on the uses and possibilities of hypnotism, suggestion and allied phenomena, by thirty authors /cedited by E. Virgil Neal and Charles S. Clark. 1aRochester, N.Y. :bNew York State Publishing Company,c[1900]  a1 v. (unpaged) :billustrations ;c22 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCompilation of various articles including title-pages: Hypnotism and hypnotic suggestion by thirty authors and Hypnotism and hypnotic suggestion by seven authors.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20250603lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM22aHypnosis1 aNeal, E. Virgil,eeditor.1 aClark, Charles S.q(Charles Samuel),eeditor.4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/60311510R  aAUTH01166cas a2200421 a 101225070DNLM20260227090920.0cr|cn ||||||||030805c20049999nyuqr p       0    0eng    a  20032149880 a1545