6DNLM20260227091016.0cr|cnu||||||||930729c19939999dk tr p       0||| 0eng    asn 93038519   a0906-6713l0906-6713  a(DNLM)SR0072480(s)  a(OCoLC)28479411  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9Denmark00aW1bPE795510aPeriodontol 20002dnlm00aPeriodontology 2000.30aPeriodontology two thousand  aCopenhagen ; Cambridge, MA :bMunksgarrd,cc1993-3 aCopenhagen :bBlackwell Munksgaard  av. :bill.  aThree no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1-  aEach issue has also a distinctive title.1 aMEDLINEbv1,Feb. 1993-1 aPubMedbv1,Feb. 1993-1 aIndex to dental literaturex0019-3992bv1,Feb. 1993-n21,Oct. 1999  aAlso issued online. 2aPeriodontics 2aPeriodical0 x1600-07574 uhttp://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/journal/10.1111/(ISSN)1600-0757  aAUTH03406cgm a2200421 i 101647966DNLM20260227154742.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u141202s2014    mdu063       fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18730  a(OCoLC)900260793  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 2021 aBarbacid, Mariano,d1949-eauthor.10aTargeting k-Ras driven cancer /cDr. Mariano Barbacid; Frederick National Laboratory for Cancer Research. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Nov. 12, 2014.3 a(CIT): Mariano Barbacid is AXA-CNIO professor of molecular oncology at Centro Nacional de Investigaciones Oncologicas (Spanish National Cancer Research Center) in Madrid, Spain. Dr. Barbacid trained as a postdoctoral fellow at NCI from 1974 to 1978, when he started his own group to work on the molecular biology of human tumors. His work led to the isolation of the first human cancer gene, H-RAS, in 1982 and to identification of the first mutation associated with the development of human cancer. In 1984, he moved to NCI-Frederick to head the Developmental Oncology Section and in 1988, joined the Bristol Myers-Squibb Pharmaceutical Research Institute in Princeton, NJ. In 1998, he returned to his native Madrid to create and direct the Spanish National Cancer Research Center. He stepped down as director in 2011 to concentrate on his own research that currently focuses on the design of new animal models of cancer and on the identification and validation of molecular targets with potential therapeutic value. About NCI's RAS Initiative:  The Frederick National Laboratory for Cancer Research (FNLCR) is an integral part of a coordinated research and development initiative led by the National Cancer Institute to further scientific understanding of cancers driven by mutations in the RAS family of genes. Researchers have known for more than two decades that about one-third of all human cancers - including a high percentage of pancreatic, lung and colorectal cancers - are driven by mutations in RAS genes. Thus far, RAS-driven cancers have been virtually impossible to treat successfully and, in some cases, are actually aggravated by current targeted therapies. The FNLCR is enlisting collaborators from all sectors of the global research community with the goal of developing effective new therapies to treat RAS-driven cancers. It is a high-risk undertaking that, if successful, could result in major dividends for human health.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed January 13, 2015.12aOncogene Protein p21(ras)xdrug effects22aNeoplasmsxdrug therapy 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aFrederick National Laboratory for Cancer Research,esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18730  aAUTH01474cgm a2200457 a 101557257DNLM20260227153859.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110325s2011    mdu062|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16453  a(OCoLC)720560171  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 4001 aJang, Mi-Hyeon.10aAdult hippocampal neurogenesis as a therapeutic target for treatment of mental disorders /cMi-Hyeon Jang.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 2 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 9, 2011.3 a(CIT): NIMH Human Genetics Candidate.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMental Disordersxtherapy22aAdult22aHippocampusxphysiology22aMental Disordersxphysiopathology22aMultipotent Stem Cellsxphysiology22aNeurogenesisxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16453  aAUTH02332cgm a22004214a 101486619DNLM20260227152736.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081007s2008    mdu056 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14626  a(OCoLC)280345105  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWK 7251 aWilliams, John,cPh. D.10aMechanisms of dopamine transmission in the VTA /cJohn Williams.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 19, 2008).  aStreaming video (55 min., 46 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 8, 2008.3 a(CIT): Dendritic release of dopamine in the ventral tegmental area acts on D2-dopamine receptors to mediate an inhibitory postsynaptic current (IPSC). This study examines how the rise and fall in extracellular dopamine regulates the kinetics of the IPSC. Extracellular dopamine was measured electrochemically during simultaneous recording of the IPSC. Both the rise and fall of dopamine were rapid relative to the IPSC, suggesting that G-protein dependent potassium channel activation determined the time course of the IPSC. The kinetics of D2 receptor dependent activation of potassium currents was studied using outside-out patch recordings and rapid application of dopamine. Dopamine applied at a minimum concentration of 10 ℗æM for a maximum of 100 ms mimicked the IPSC. Shorter applications of higher concentrations did not change the kinetics of the outward current. These measurements were used to construct a model predicting that dopamine travels less then 500 nm from the site of release before binding to D2 receptors.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDopaminexphysiology22aReceptors, Dopamine D222aVentral Tegmental Area 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14626  aAUTH01201cam a22003617a 101568020DNLM20260204173404.0110531m20102013pl ac    b    100 0 pol    a9788385399902 (v. 1)  a8385399909 (v. 1)  a9788385399452 (v. 2)  a(OCoLC)727737511  aEUWcEUWdDNLM0 apolarusbeng  anlmcopyc  ae-pl---  9Poland00aWZ 402 aKrajowy Zjazd Polskiego Towarzystwa Historii Medycyny i Farmacjin(22nd :d2010 :cŁódź, Poland)10aSzkice z historii medycyny /credaktor, Jerzy Supady.  aŁódź :bWojskowa Druk. w Łodzi,c2010-2013.  a2 v. :bill., ports. ;c29 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographic references.  aText chiefly in Polish, with three articles in Russian. Summaries in English; article titles also in English.12aHistory of Medicine22aHistory of Pharmacy 2aConference Proceedings1 aSupady, Jerzy.  aAUTH01417cam a2200361 i 101179545DNLM20260220150244.0cr||||||||||||030521s1875    pau           000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)52381966  aDNLMbengerdad20260220dDNLM  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 282 no.211 aChamberlaine, Samuel,eauthor.10aSilk culture and home industry :bread before the Association April 8th, 1875 /cby Dr