unmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aBd. 1, Heft 1 (März 1991)-0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso available online.  aArticles in German; summaries also in English.12aBehavior Therapy 2aPeriodical0 x1423-04024 uhttp://www.karger.com/Journal/Home/224158  aAUTH02308cgm a22004214a 101546207DNLM20260227153534.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101027s2010    mdu068|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16109  a(OCoLC)688679707  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWH 2001 aTedder, Thomas.10aRegulatory B10 cells in inflammation and autoimmunity /cThomas Tedder ; Immunology Interest Group.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 8 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 15, 2010.3 a(CIT): Dr. Thomas Tedder has extensive training in microbiology, biochemistry, cellular immunology and molecular biology. As a graduate student with Dr. Max Cooper at the University of Alabama in Birmingham, a postdoctoral fellow with Dr. Stuart Schlossman at Harvard Medical School, as an independent investigator at Dana-Farber Cancer Institute and Harvard Medical School, and as a Professor of Immunology at Duke University Medical Center, Dr. Tedder has worked to identify human and mouse B lymphocyte-restricted and -associated cell surface molecules, and to determine their roles in lymphocyte function. Dr. Tedder has been among the first to describe and characterize regulatory B cells (B10 cells) in mice and humans and currently lists more than 10 publications on the subject. His lab has been instrumental in determining the cell surface phenotype, mechanism of action, and molecular characterization of B10 cell regulation in autoimmune disease and inflammation.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aB-Lymphocyte Subsets22aAutoimmunityximmunology22aInflammationximmunology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16109  aAUTH01316cam a22003137a 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040005000073043001200123044001800135060003300153074001200186086002200198110009000220245004400310260026400354300003800618336002600656337002800682338002700710490004300737541005700780650003700837651001800874710005700892830004400949999000900993101626699DNLM20260210112954.0770316s1976    mdu          f000 0 eng    aGPObengcGPOdOCLCQdWAUdOCLCQdOCLCFdDNLM  an-us---  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1812 no.24  a493-C-10 aHE 20.5402:H 34/21 aUnited States.bPublic Health Service.bDivision of Health Maintenance Organizations.10aHealth maintenance organizations today.  a[Rockville, Md.] :bDept. of Health, Education, and Welfare, Public Health Service, Health Services Administration, Bureau of Medical Services, Division of Health Maintenance Organizations ;aWashington :bFor sale by the Supt. of Docs., U.S. G.P.O.,c[1976?]  a1 folded sheet ([4] p.) ;c22 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aDHEW publication ;vno. (HSA) 76-13038  cTransfer;aOffice of the PHS Historian;d2008.5DNLM12aHealth Maintenance Organizations 2aUnited States1 aUnited States.bPublic Health Service,edonor.5DNLM 0aDHEW publication ;vno. (HSA) 76-13038.  aAUTH02043cgm a2200469 i 101776742DNLM20260227160822.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210302s2021    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31558  a(OCoLC)1249794507  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 300 AA100aPeering beyond the blindspot seeking authentic risk factors :ba case study /cEve J. Higginbotham. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 0 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded February 17, 2021.3 a(CIT): Dr. Higginbotham will discuss the arguments against "race," why this topic is important, and its impact on science and healthcare.  In her case study, she will highlight the outcomes of a 20-year clinical trial that uncovered a new biologically measurable risk factor.  This is intended to be a lecture that will inspire additional thinking around the use of race in science and medicine.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed May 5, 2021).12aRace Factors12aRacism22aClinical Trials as Topicxhistory22aRisk Factors22aGlaucomaxethnology 2aUnited Statesxethnology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aHigginbotham, Eve J.,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series.pNIH director's Margaret Pittman lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=41238  aAUTH02613cgm a2200445 i 101775923DNLM20260227160640.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2021    mdu066|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31476  a(OCoLC)1241125043  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWB 17600aSMART biosensors :ba new modality to objectively quantify pain /cOmowunmi "Wunmi" Sadik. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 6 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aRecorded January 6, 2021.3 a(CIT): Conventional methods of assessing pain are mostly subjective, and the challenge of objectively quantifying the problem is a daunting task.  As pain levels increase, so do the amount and variety of pain killers required to manage pain successfully.  Dr. Sadik"s current research focuses on creative new kinds of biosensors to objectively detect and quantify pain biomarkers.  Her lab has developed novel multidimensional biosensors that have been tested in more than a thousand patients.  This will be the focus of her talk.Dr. Sadik is a distinguished professor and chair in the department of chemistry and environmental sciences at the New Jersey Institutes of Technology.  She is the director of the NJIT BioSensors Materials for Advanced Research &amp; Technology (the BioSMART Center).  Her group focuses on understanding interfaces, particularly the electrochemical interface, and how to use this knowledge to pursue innovative sensor technologies to improve human health and the environment.  Dr. Sadik holds several U.S. patents in biosensors and sustainable nanotechnology.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 8, 2021).12aPain Measurement12aPainxdiagnosis22aBiosensing Techniques22aPain Management 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aSadik, Omowunmi A.,d1964-espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40164  aAUTH01955cgm a2200445 i 101775909DNLM20260227160604.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210206s2020    mdu060|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31443  a(OCoLC)1241125175  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.55.C500aNIH-funded basic experimental studies with humans (BESH) :bregistration and results reporting /cRebecca Williams and Elisa Golfinopoulos. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 0 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded December 7, 2020.3 a(CIT): Office of Science Policy, NIH Join the NIH to learn about Basic Experimental Studies with Humans (BESH). Rebecca Williams, Acting Director of ClinicalTrials.gov, and Elisa Golfinopoulos will present the results of the National Library of Medicine analysis of challenges and considerations for registration and results information reporting of BESH in ClinicalTrials.gov.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 2, 2021).22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aClinical Trials as Topic22aDatabases as Topic22aRegistries22aPublications 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aWilliams, Rebeccaq(Rebecca J.),espeaker.1 aGolfinopoulos, Elisa,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40141  aAUTH01504cgm a22004454a 101468725DNLM20260227152331.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080307s2008    mdu072 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14290  a(OCoLC)265647951  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWJ 3581 aLinehan, Marston.10aGenetic basis of kidney cancer :bfoundation for disease-based therapy /cW. Marston Linehan.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCenter for Cancer Research--National Cancer Institute grand rounds  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 14, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 11 min., 36 sec. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Feb. 12, 2008.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aCarcinoma, Renal Cellxgenetics12aCarcinoma, Renal Cellxtherapy22avon Hippel-Lindau Disease 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14290  aAUTH01629cas a2200445 i 101629363DNLM20260227090914.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||130204c20149999ncu|| poo     |    0eng    a  20132005070 a2326-4403l2326-440321  a(OCoLC)826630619  aDLCbengerdad20260227cDLCdOCLCQdDNLM  apccansdp  9United States10aISSN RECORD00aW110aHealth Behav Policy Rev2dnlm00aHealth behavior and policy review.1 iIssues from 2014-2016 have title:aHealth behavior & policy review1 aReview 1aOak Ridge, NC :bParis Scholar Publishing, Ltd.,c[2014]-  a1 online resource  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Volume 1, Number 1 (January 2014).  aVolume 1, Number 1 (January 2014) also called: Number 1 (January 2014).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aDescription based on: Volume 1, Number 1 (January 2014); title from banner (Paris Scholar Publishing website, viewed August 08, 2014).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 4, Number 2 (March 2017) (Ingenta Connect site, viewed on Feb. 16, 2017).12aHealth Behavior22aHealth Policy 2aPeriodical40uhttp://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/psp/hbpr40uhttps://www.parisscholarpublishing.org/  aAUTH02019cgm a2200457 i 101776737DNLM20260227160810.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210302s2021    mdu054|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31550  a(OCoLC)1249794648  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 50600aIntegrating inflammation and coagulation to understand COVID-19 /cYogen Kanthi, M.D., NHLBI, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (54 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNHLBI COVID insights webinar  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded February 10, 2021.3 a(CIT): We invite you to join a new biweekly NHLBI webinar series entitled "COVID-19 Insights" to learn about groundbreaking research focusing on the host response to SARS-CoV-2 infection. This exciting cross-disciplinary series, featuring both NHLBI staff and researchers throughout the scientific community, will enhance NHLBI listeners' understanding of the latest advances in this fast-moving research, especially as they relate to the NHLBI mission.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 29, 2021).12aCOVID-19xblood22aBlood Coagulation Disorders22aCOVID-19ximmunology22aInflammation 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aKanthi, Yogen,espeaker.2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute,esponsoring body. 0aNHLBI COVID-19 insights webinar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40175  aAUTH02276cgm a2200445 i 101702176DNLM20260227155711.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u170310s2017    mdu061|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:22151  a(OCoLC)983213200  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 27000aDissection of the neuronal circuits and synapses that mediate the effects of drugs of abuse /cVeronica A. Alvarez. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2017]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 1 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's seminar series  aRecorded February 24, 2017.3 a(CIT): Director's Seminar Series.  Research in the laboratory headed by Dr. Alvarez is aimed at identifying the neuronal circuits that mediate and regulate reward-motivated and compulsive behaviors. The laboratory has focused on the behaviors toward substance of abuse, such as cocaine and ethanol, in addition to consummatory behaviors towards natural rewards like food and water. Dr. Alvarez pursues an integrative approach that includes the use of electrophysiology, 2-photon laser scanning microscopy and fast-scanning cyclic voltammetry for dopamine detection together with behavioral testing and in vivo manipulation of circuit activity using optogenetic and chemogenetic tools.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 14, 2017).12aSubstance-Related Disordersxphysiopathology22aBrainxphysiopathology22aCompulsive Behaviorxphysiopathology22aDopamine 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aAlvarez, Veronica A.q(Veronica Alicia),espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?22151  aAUTH01633cgmaa22004214a 101269100DNLM20260227144751.0vf|cbahouvf|cjahoucr|cna||||||||060214s2006    mdu065 ||    f    vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:4275  a(OCoLC)64760766  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aQW 168.5.F504aThe structure and function of flaviviruses /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  aUnited States.  aBethesda, MD :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006.  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (1 hr., 5 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (1 hr., 5 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aMichael G. Rossmann  aPresented Feb. 1, 2006.  aAlso available online.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aOpen captioned.12aFlavivirusxgenetics12aFlavivirusxphysiology1 aRossmann, M. G.q(Michael G.),d1930-2019.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4275  aAUTH01501cas a2200433 i 101648127DNLM20260227090907.0cr||||||||||||121228c20149999dcuqr p       0   a0eng    a  20122739040 a2325-9965l2325-9965  a(OCoLC)883826965  aAU@bengcAU@erdad20260227d