,c[2012]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (10 hr., 50 min.) :bcolor, sound)  atwo-dimensional moving image2rdacontent  acomputer2rdamedia  aonline resource2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded October 4-5, 2012.3 a(CIT): This NCI-sponsored conference will host international leaders in the field of basic immunology. The program includes recent advances in this field and this meeting should provide an exciting forum for discussion and debate on the current understanding of basic immunological mechanisms.  aClosed-captioned.12aImmunity 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1760540uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17604  aAUTH01461cgm a2200433 i 101622756DNLM20260227155011.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u131227s2013    mdu113|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18167  a(OCoLC)870890348  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 58.71 aCaplen, N.q(Natasha)eauthor.10aTRACO.pRNAinterference - angiogenesis /cN. Caplen, E. Zudaire.1 aRNAinterference - angiogenesis1 aTRACO.pRNA interference - angiogenesis 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 53 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Nov. 12, 2013.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed Feb. 5, 2014.12aRNA Interference22aNeovascularization, Physiologic 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aZudaire, Enrique,eauthor.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18167  aAUTH03960cgm a2200445 i 101642827DNLM20260227155840.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140926s2014    mdu062|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18608  a(OCoLC)892697177  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 3001 aHe, Bin,d1957-eauthor.10aHow to map the dynamics of your brain :bfrom EEG to BCI /cBin He. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 2 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCCAM integrative medicine research lecture  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Sept. 15, 2014.3 a(CIT): NCCAM Integrative Medicine Research Lecture  The National Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine (NCCAM) presents the Integrative Medicine Research Lecture series. The series provides overviews of the current state of research and practice involving complementary health therapies, and explores perspectives on the emerging discipline of integrative medicine. Dr. Bin He is a Distinguished McKnight University Professor of Biomedical Engineering, Medtronic-Bakken Endowed Chair for Engineering in Medicine, Director of the Institute for Engineering in Medicine and the Center for Neuroengineering at the University of Minnesota. His research interests cover a broad spectrum in biomedical engineering, mainly in neuroengineering and biomedical imaging.Dr. He has made significant original contributions to electrophysiological source imaging, multimodal neuroimaging, and brain-computer interface. He has published over 200 peer reviewed journal articles and is the sole editor of the text book entitled Neural Engineering (2nd Ed, 2013).  In this lecture, Dr. Bin He will discuss how brain activity is distributed over a 3-dimensional volume and evolves through time. Mapping spatio-temporal distribution of brain activation with high spatial and temporal resolution is of great importance for understanding the brain, and aiding clinical diagnosis and management of brain disorders. Electrophysiological source imaging from noninvasively recorded electroencephalogram (EEG) has played a significant role in advancing our ability to image brain function and dysfunction. Dr. He will discuss the merits and challenges of EEG source imaging and multimodal functional neuroimaging by integrating electrophysiological and hemodynamic measurements. Dr. He's work indicates EEG and functional MRI can be integrated in a principled way, leading to substantially enhanced spatio-temporal resolution for functional imaging of dynamic brain activation. Dr. He will discuss his recent work in quantifying and imaging pain using EEG and functional MRI. Dr. He will also discuss his work to map "thoughts" from electrophysiological and hemodynamic measurements. Dr. He's brain-computer interface (BCI) work demonstrates that humans can control a quadcopter by "mind" from noninvasive EEG signals, and that mind-body awareness training, including yoga and meditation, can help improve the performance of BCI.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed Oct. 8, 2014.12aBrainxphysiology22aBrain-Computer Interfaces22aElectroencephalography 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNCCAM complementary and integrative medicine consult service lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18608  aAUTH01289cam a2200325 i 27220860RDNLM20260206221126.0cr||||||||||||810815s1918    nyua     r    000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)13698662  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aW 22 AA1bU5c 19181 aUnited States.bCouncil of National Defense.bCommittee of Medical Women,ecompiler.10aCensus of women physicians, November 11, 1918 /ccompiled by the Committee of Medical Women, General Medical Board, Council of National Defense. 1a[New York] :bAmerican Women's Hospitals,c[1918]  a125 pages :billustration ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aTitle from cover.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20250609lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aPhysicians, Women22aCensuses 2aDirectory4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/27220860R  aAUTH03634cgm a22004334a 101537548DNLM20260227153551.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100804s2010    mdu062|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16026  a(OCoLC)671464388  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWP 4581 aMutch, David G.10aChanging management of endometrial cancer /cDavid G. Mutch.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCCR grand rounds lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 2 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded July 13, 2010.3 a(CIT): Dr. David Mutch is the Judith and Ira Gall Professor of obstetrics and gynecology and the director of gynecologic oncology at the Washington University School of Medicine in St. Louis. He is a member of the Division of Gynecologic Oncology of the American Board of Obstetrics and Gynecology and serves as a reviewer for multiple medical journals related to women"s health and cancers of the female reproductive tract. He has authored or co-authored more than 200 peer-reviewed publications, as well as numerous book chapters on the subject as well. Dr. Mutch has received multiple research grants from the American Cancer Society and the National Institutes of Health (NIH), among others. He is the principal investigator (PI) for the Gynecologic Oncology Group at Washington University and served as the program chair of the 2004 meeting of the Society of Gynecologic Oncologists. Dr. Mutch is the immediate past president of the Society of Gynecologic Oncologists. He is currently the co-PI on an NIH grant looking at defective DNA mismatch repair in endometrial cancers, as well as a co-PI on a recently awarded endometrial Special Project of Research Excellence (SPORE) grant. Dr. Mutch attended the Washington University School of Medicine, where he also completed his residency training. He underwent fellowship training at Duke University Medical Center in Durham, NC, before returning to Washington University in 1988. He recently received the Washington University School of Medicines Distinguished Alumni Award and is a member of Alpha Omega Alpha.  NCI"s Center for Cancer Research (CCR) Grand Rounds is a weekly lecture series addressing current research in clinical and molecular oncology. Speakers are leading national and international researchers and clinicians proposed by members of the CCR Grand Rounds Planning Committee and others within the CCR community and approved by the CCR Office of the Director. Lectures occur every Tuesday from 8:00 to 9:00 a.m. in Lipsett Amphitheater in the Clinical Center building on the NIH campus September through July with exceptions around holidays and major cancer meetings. The lecture schedule is posted on various calendars of events, including at the following link: http://bethesdatrials.cancer.gov/health-care-professionals/grand-rounds.aspx.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aEndometrial Neoplasmsxtherapy22aEndometrial Neoplasmsxhistory 2aLecture 2aWebcast 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16026  aAUTH00808cam a22002537i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002100073040003500094044001000129060003200139110006800171245007600239264008300315300001600398336002600414337002800440338002700468650001800495650001500513655001700528999000900545101613451DNLM20260209122703.0130725q19721990cc           l000 0 chi    a(OCoLC)888342258  aDNLMbengerdad20260209dDNLM  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1788 no.81 aHuangpu Qu (Shanghai, China).bWei sheng fang yi zhan,eauthor.10aXiao du sha chong yao wu /cHuangpu Qu wei sheng fang yi zhan gong gao. 1a[Shanghai] :bShanghai Shi wei sheng jiao yu guan yin,c[between 1972 to 1990]  a2, 53 pages  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier12aDisinfectants12aPesticides 2aPopular Work  aAUTH01075cam a22003017a 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032035002100073040002100094044001800115060003300133245003100166260003600197300002000233336002600253337002800279338002700307500001900334540014200353541003400495561008400529583003900613583004300652650002200695650004700717999000900764101277309DNLM20260204124613.0060523s1902    xxu           000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)181358912  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 1285 no.1100aUnsanitary wall coverings.  a[United States :bs.n.,c1902?]  a12 p. ;c16 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  cGift;aJane B. Lossing.5DNLM  aNLM copy provenance: gift of Jane B. Lossing in memory of Ann Smith, R.N.5DNLM  aWill digitizec202012032pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202012035DNLM12aArsenicxtoxicity22aInterior Design and Furnishingsxstandards  aAUTH04709cgm a2200409 i 101671902DNLM20260227155150.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u151031s2015    mdu057|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19275  a(OCoLC)930496512  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.55.C51 aMurray, David M.eauthor.10aDesign and analysis of studies to evaluate multi-level interventions in public health and medicine /cDavid M. Murray. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (57 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aMedicine: mind the gap  aRecorded October 23, 2015.3 a(CIT): Increasingly, interventions are designed to operate at multiple levels, including, for example, the individual, the family, the health care provider, and the larger physical and social environment. To evaluate these multi-level interventions, investigators must plan their studies to accommodate both the extra variation associated with the multiple levels of influence and the often limited degrees of freedom available to estimate those sources of variation. Group- or cluster-randomized trials have been suggested as the gold standard for evaluation of multi-level interventions, as they have the same strengths as randomized clinical trials for interventions that involve only a single level of influence. Even so, a number of other methods have been identified as alternatives. This presentation will review the options available to evaluate multi-level interventions and will discuss their strengths and weaknesses.  Dr. Murray has spent his career evaluating intervention programs designed to improve the public health. He has worked with all age groups, in a variety of settings, and with a variety of health behaviors and disease outcomes. Beginning in the late 1980s, Dr. Murray focused on the design and analysis of group-randomized trials in which groups are randomized to conditions, and members of those groups are observed to assess the effect of an intervention. Dr. Murray wrote the first textbook on that material, published by Oxford University Press in 1998. He has worked on many of these trials, collaborating with colleagues around the country on their design, implementation, and evaluation. He has also conducted research to develop and test new methods for their design and analysis. Dr. Murray served as the first Chair of the Community-Level Heath Promotion study section, which reviews many of the prevention-related group-randomized trials submitted to the NIH. After 35 years at the University of Minnesota, the University of Memphis, and the Ohio State University, Dr. Murray joined the National Institutes of Health in September 2012 as the Associate Director for Prevention and Director of the Office of Disease Prevention. In this role, he is responsible for promoting and coordinating prevention research among and between NIH Institutes and Centers and other public and private entities. He led the development of the first Strategic Plan for the Office, which was released in February 2014. Consistent with his longstanding involvement in methodological research, one of the strategic priorities for the Office is to promote the use of the best available methods in prevention research and to support the development of better methods.  Medicine: Mind the Gap is a lecture series that explores issues at the intersection of research, evidence, and clinical practice--areas in which conventional wisdom may be contradicted by recent evidence. From the role of advocacy organizations in medical research and policy, to off-label drug use, to the effectiveness of continuing medical education, the seminar series will aim to engage the National Institutes of Health community in thought-provoking discussions to challenge what we think we know and to think critically about our role in today's research environment.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed Nov. 20, 2015).12aRandomized Controlled Trials as Topic 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aMedicine: mind the gap lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19275  aAUTH01669cas a2200457 a 101585369DNLM20260227090908.0m     o  d        cr#|||||||||||120530c20129999ne || poo     0    0eng    a  2013243628  a847879539  a2001-0370l2001-0370  a(OCoLC)829963344  z(OCoLC)847879539  aHNKcHNKdNTEdERDdDNLM  apcc  9Netherlands10aW1  aTP248.24b.C651510aComput Struct Biotechnol J2dnlm00aComputational and structural biotechnology journal.13aCSBJ  aGothenburg, Sweden :bResearch Network of Computational and Structural Biotechnology31aAmsterdam :bElsevier B.V. on behalf of Research Network of Computational and Structural Biotechnology  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with vol. 1, issue 1 (Apr. 2012).0 aPubMedbv1, 2012-0 aPMC  aDescription based on: Vol. 1, no. 1 (Apr. 2012); title from journal home page (publisher's Web site, viewed Mar. 14, 2013).  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. no. 8, issue 11 (Aug. 2013) (viewed Nov. 19, 2013).12aBiotechnology22aComputational Biology 2aPeriodical2 aResearch Network of Computational and Structural Biotechnology.40uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=10158536940uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/20010370  aAUTH01619cas a2200493 a 101255062DNLM20260224111541.0cr|cn#||||||||050812c20049999ii  x p       0    0eng    a  2009243488  a0189-6725l0974-5998  a(OCoLC)311811390  aAU@bengcAU@dDNLM  apcc  9India00aW1bAF5310aAfr J Paediatr Surg2dnlm00aAfrican journal of paediatric surgery :bAJPS.30aAJPS  aJos, Plateau State, Nigeria :bDept. of Surgery, Jos University Teaching Hospital3 3<2008-> :aMumbai :bMedknow Publications  aQuarterly,b2013-  aTwo no. a year,b2005-2009  aThree no. a year,b2010-2012  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with v. 1 in 2004.  aDescription based on: Vol. 4, no. 1 (Jan./June 2007); title from title page.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 5, issue 2 (July/Dec. 2008).1 aMEDLINEbv61n1, Jan./June 2009-0 aPubMedbv6n1, Jan./June 2009-0 aPMC  aAlso issued online.12aSurgical Procedures, Operative22aChild22aInfant 2aPeriodical2 aAssociation of Paediatric Surgeons of Nigeria.0 x0974-59984 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=1012550624 uhttps://journals.lww.com/ajps/pages/default.aspx  aAUTH01721cgm a2200457 i 9918418884406676DNLM20260227162250.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220616s2022    mdu349|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32332  a(OCoLC)1372997298  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 5032 aHTLV & AIDS Symposium: Research Achievements of Dr. Marjorie Robert-Guroffd(2022 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aHTLV & AIDS Symposium :bResearch Achievements of Dr. Marjorie Robert-Guroff /cNIH.1 aHTLV and AIDS Symposium 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (5 hr., 49 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 21, 2022.3 a(CIT): HTLV &amp; AIDS Symposium: Research Achievements of Dr. Marjorie Robert-Guroff.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 1, 2023).12aRobert-Guroff, Marjorie.12aHIV Infections22aAcquired Immunodeficiency Syndrome22aAIDS Vaccines22aBiomedical Research 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45183  aAUTH02142cgm a2200421 i 9918503077506676DNLM20260227162832.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u221105s2022    mdu140|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32726  a(OCoLC)1365556444  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 1082 aSleep 101 Symposiumd(2022 :cOnline)10aSleep 101 Symposium /cNHLBI, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 20 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 21, 2022.3 a(CIT): The Sleep 101 Symposium highlights advances and opportunities in sleep and circadian science and research to the sleep and circadian community, and federal research funding representatives. PRESENTATIONS Link between Sleep and Metabolism: An Overview KRISTEN KNUTSON, PHD Northwestern University Sleep Disorders and Cardiovascular Outcomes NEOMI SHAH, MD, MPH, MS Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai Sleep Disturbance and Alzheimer"s Disease ADAM P. SPIRA, PHD Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health Sleep and Pregnancy: What do We Need to Know? JUDETTE LOUIS MD, MPH University of South Florida Live Q&A with the Speakers MODERATED BY MARISHKA BROWN, PHD Director of the National Center on Sleep Disorders Research.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based online resource; title from title screen (viewed January 17, 2023).12aSleep Wake Disorders22aSleepxphysiology22aCircadian Rhythmxphysiology22aSleep Apnea Syndromes 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=46256  aAUTH03141cgm a2200457 i 101697703DNLM20260227155505.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u161221s2016    mdu056|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19964  a(OCoLC)971625539  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWH 20000aControlling innate T cell development in the thymus by cytokine receptor signaling /cHyun Park. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (56 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aDirector's seminar series  aRecorded October 28, 2016.3 a(CIT): T cells are generated in the thymus and then exported into the periphery where they undergo further maturation and differentiation into effector T cells.  Lineage choice into distinct effector T cell subsets is tightly controlled by cytokine signaling.  Specifically, cytokines of the common g-chain (gc) family play critical roles in cell fate decision and acquisition of effector function.  Conventionally, effector T cell generation has been viewed as a process limited to peripheral tissues.  Recent reports, however, revealed that the thymus also produces effector T cells, and that these cells are phenotypically mature and functionally competent without having encountered antigens.  Thymus-derived innate phenotype T cells include, among others, NKT cells, a subset of gd T cells and IFN-g producing innate CD8 T cells.  Analogous to their peripheral counterparts, thymus-generated effector T cells also require cytokine signaling for lineage choice and effector function.  Unlike peripheral effector T cells, however, the molecular basis for cytokine-driven lineage choice and effector molecule expression remains largely unknown.  Here we address the role of gc cytokines in thymic NKT subset differentiation, and reveal a role for the IL-4 receptor/STAT6 axis in controlling cell fate decision of both NKT and innate CD8 T cells.  Interrogating the mechanisms of innate effector T cell differentiation is important, as it provides further clues to understand the cellular basis of inflammation, autoimmunity, and other immunological diseases.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed January 23, 2017).12aNatural Killer T-Cellsxmetabolism22aCell Differentiation22aReceptors, Cytokinexmetabolism22aSignal Transduction22aThymus Glandxcytology 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aPark, Hyunq(Jung-Hyun),espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19964  aAUTH01899cas a2200517 i 101681801DNLM20260227090922.0cr||||||||||||160425c20169999fr || p       0    0eng    a  2017243011