 apcc  9United States10aQU 4501 aWilfond, Benjamin.10aGenetics, stored tissue, and conflicts of interest.nSession 7.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 8, 2006).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 28 min. : sd., col.).  aSpeakers: Benjamin Wilfond, Sara Chandros Hull, and Ezekiel J. Emanuel.  aRecorded Nov. 30, 2005.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aGeneticsxethics22aConflict of Interest22aEthics, Research22aInformed Consentxethics22aTissue Preservationxethics 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12927  aAUTH02461cgm a2200481 i 101616663DNLM20260227154908.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130907s2013    mdu082|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18065  a(OCoLC)863034580  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 1401 aWyatt, Gail Elizabeth,eauthor.10aDisparities in mental health for underserved populations :bbest practices for affordable care /cGail Wyatt. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 22 min.) :bcolor, sound).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH health disparities seminar  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded July 18, 2013.3 a(CIT): About 25 percent of all U.S. adults have a mental illness, and nearly 50 percent of U.S. adults will develop at least one mental illness during their lifetime. In her presentation, Dr. Gail Wyatt will discuss mental health disparities, some of the current concerns and best practices to address these disparities, based on her research at the Center for Culture, Trauma and Mental Health Disparities. She will examine the cumulative effects of lifetime trauma and stress in African Americans and Hispanics that is often overlooked and not addressed in mental health systems as we know them now. She will also describe the screener that her research team has developed to identify those at risk for symptoms of Post- traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) and depression, that can be used in future primary care settings.  aDescription based on version viewed November 8, 2013.12aMental Disordersxethnology22aBlack or African American.22aHealthcare Disparities22aHispanic or Latino.22aMental Healthxethnology22aVulnerable Populations 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH health disparities seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18065  aAUTH01523cgm a22004094a 101267505DNLM20260227145144.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu088 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10129  a(OCoLC)70663575  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 58.52 aNIH Research Festivald(2001 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNIH Research Festival 2001.nPlenary session 1,pProteomics : from protein structure to disease mechanisms /cOffice of the Director, NIH.30aPlenary session 1, proteomics30aProteomics  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 17, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 28 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 3, 2001.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aProteomicsxtrends 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of the Director.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10129  aAUTH01512cas a2200457 i 101625127DNLM20260227090914.0cr|||||||||||