ial 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of the Director.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1001740uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10322  aAUTH02706cgm a2200457 i 101647955DNLM20260227154810.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u141202s2014    mdu064|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18714  a(OCoLC)903315305  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 359.51 aCheung, Melanie,eauthor.10aBrain resilience training for Huntington's disease /cDr. Melanie Cheung.1 iAt head of title:aHealth disparities 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 4 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aFulbright visiting scholar lecture  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Nov. 4, 2014.3 a(CIT): Fulbright visiting scholar lecture: Brain Resilience Training for Huntington's Disease.    Dr. Melanie Cheung (Ng©Øti Rangitihi, Te Arawa), a M©Øori native and Fulbright New Zealand scholar, is developing a brain resilience training program for Huntington"s disease.  Huntington"s disease is a progressive brain disorder affecting an estimated 3 to 7 per 100,000 people of European ancestry.  Dr. Cheung is committed to exploring both Indigenous and Western scientific paradigms to help people with neurodegenerative diseases.  Her work integrates experimental neurobiology, bioethics, tikanga (ceremony/customary) and M©Øtauranga M©Øori (Maori traditional knowledge).  The Outreach Lecturing Fund allows Fulbright Visiting Scholars, like Dr. Melanie Cheung, to travel across the country to share their specific research interests, speak on the history and culture of their home countries, and exchange ideas with U.S. students, faculty and community organizations.  Sponsored by the National Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities, National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke, and Fogarty International Center.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed Dec. 29, 2014.12aHuntington Disease22aBrainxphysiopathology22aNeuronal Plasticity 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aFulbright visiting scholar lecture40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18714  aAUTH02580cgm a22004694a 101267551DNLM20260227144323.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu076 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10355  a(OCoLC)1019836339  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWH 11 AA100aDeWitt Stetten, Jr., memorial fellow in the history of biomedical sciences and technology /cJessie Saul and Harvey Klein.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 21, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 15 min., 39 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 17, 2003.3 a(CIT): Jessie Saul will be presenting this year's Stetten lecture, with Dr. Harvey Klein.  Ms. Saul's talk will be based on research she has conducted on blood safety at the NIH during her year as the DeWitt Stetten Jr., Memorial Fellow in the History of Biomedical Sciences.  The History of Blood Safety Practices, Policies and Property at NIH, 1945-2003  Location: Lipsett Auditorium, NIH Clinical Center (Bldg. 10)  BRHIG is open to everyone interested in the history of biomedical research at NIH or elsewhere. In addition to the presentation and discussion of work-in-progress, the group will serve as a forum for discussion of issues of common interest, such as the identification and development of source materials; the uses and pitfalls of oral histories in research; and collaborations between historians and the biomedical community.   For more information, visit  BRHIG.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aBlood Transfusionxhistory22aBlood Donorsxhistory22aBlood Specimen Collectionxhistory22aDisease Transmission, Infectiousxprevention & control22aSafety Managementxhistory 2aUnited States 2aWebcast1 aSaul, Jessie Elizabeth.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=2449  aAUTH01291cas a22004094a 101239891DNLM20260227090910.0cr|cn#||||||||050131c20059999ts  x p       0    0eng    a  2005243687  a1573-4021l1573-4021  a(OCoLC)58947770  aCAIcCAIdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United Arab Emirates00aW1bCU788JBGk10aCurr Hypertens Rev2dnlm00aCurrent hypertension reviews.30aHypertension reviews  aSaif Zone, Sharjah, U.A.E. ;aSan Francisco, CA :bBentham Science Publishers  av. :bill.  aTwo no. a year,b2017-  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Jan. 2005)-  aTitle from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 13, no. 2 (2017).1 aMEDLINEbv9n1, Feb. 2013-0 aPubMedbv9n1, Feb. 2013- ; selected citations only before this date  aAlso issued online.12aHypertension 2aPeriodical0 x1875-65064 uhttp://www.eurekaselect.com/598/journal/current-hypertension-reviews  aAUTH01525cam a2200385 i 59830920RDNLM20260206221401.0cr||||||||||||820626s1922    nbu      b    000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)2546478  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aZWG 220bP892ce 19221 aPoynter, C. W. M.q(Charles William McCorkle),d1875-1950,eauthor.10aCongenital anomalies of the arteries and veins of the human body, with bibliography /cC.W.M. Poynter. 1aLincoln, Nebraska :bUniversity of Nebraska,c1922.  a106 pages ;c25 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aUniversity studies / University of Nebraska ;vv. 22, no. 1-2  aTitle from cover.  aIncludes bibliographical references.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc202505282pda5DNLM  aConservedc202506202pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aVascular Malformationsxphysiopathology22aCongenital Abnormalities 2aBibliography 0aUniversity studies (University of Nebraska (Lincoln campus)) ;vv. 22, no, 1-2.4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/59830920R  aAUTH03604cgm a2200445 i 9918418882506676DNLM20260227162335.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220616s2022    mdu067|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32364  a(OCoLC)1370213098  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 300 AA12 aAnnual NIH Asian American, Native Hawaiian/Pacific Islander Health Research Conferencen(1st :d2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aAnnual NIH AA and NHPI health research conference :bMay 4-5, 2022, virtual & open to the public, commemorating AANHPI Heritage Month /cNCI, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 7 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  a"Host: NIH Asian American, Native Hawaiian, Pacific Islander Health Scientific Interest Group (AANHPI-HSIG), sponsor: National Cancer Institute, NIH."  aRecorded May 4, 2022.3 a(CIT): Overview A month-long annual observance in May, Asian American, Native Hawaiian/Pacific Islander (AANHPI) Heritage Month is to recognize the history and to celebrate the achievements and contributions of AANHPIs to our nation.  NIH has established the Asian American, Native Hawaiian, and Pacific Islander Health Scientific Interest Group (NIH AAPI-HSIG) in April 2021, which has now renamed as "AANHPI-HSIG" in line with recently reinstated and reinvigorated White House Initiative on Asian Americans, Native Hawaiians, and Pacific Islanders (WHIAANHPI). NIH AANHPI-HSIG's mission is to advance NIH's mission in improving the research and enhancing the health and well-being for AA and NHPI populations. It serves as an NIH high-quality trusted resource for advancing research and education. It also provides a scientific exchange and collaboration platform for assemblies of scientists and staff at the NIH, HHS, extramural academia, and/or other federal and non-federal entities (https://oir.nih.gov/sigs/AAPI-HSIG).  The NIH AANHPI-HSIG will host the Inaugural "Annual NIH AA and NHPI Health Research Conference" on May 4-5, 2022. It will be virtual in 2022. The main theme of the conference is "Mechanisms and Translational Research to Improve Health and Therapeutic Outcomes for AA and NHPI Population". This conference will bring together the world's leading scientific and regulatory experts in the field of AA and NHPI research from government, academia, and the community.  Objectives: celebrate and provide education during the month of May on AANHPI heritage. highlight research achievements in the population, and address research needs in order to provide input for developing recommendations for NIH's AA and NHPI research strategic plan.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 8, 2023).12aAsian American Native Hawaiian and Pacific Islander22aHealth Status Disparities22aTranslational Research, Biomedical22aNeoplasmsxethnology 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45360  aAUTH01879cgm a22004454a 101488636DNLM20260227152916.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081104s2008    mdu056|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14705  a(OCoLC)288982974  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2661 aMoody, Terry W.10aSignal transduction /cTerry Moody.1 iTitle from title screen:aRedox biology.pSignal transduction  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner.  aStreaming video (56 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 14, 2008.3 a(CIT): Redox Biology (RB)  The course is designed for NIH fellows to enhance their knowledge of redox biology. Reactive species such as superoxide, hydrogen peroxide and nitric oxide are associated with cellular toxicity, however, nitric oxide is useful in the treatment cardiovascular disease.  The course will examine the role of reactive nitrogen and oxygen species in carcinogenesis, cancer proliferation and angiogenesis.   For more information, visit  http://ccr.cancer.gov/careers/courses/rb.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxtherapy22aNitric Oxidextherapeutic use22aReactive Oxygen Speciesxtherapeutic use22aSignal Transductionxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14705  aAUTH01799cgm a22004214a 101498016DNLM20260227152906.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090303s2009    mdu308|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14894  a(OCoLC)390452930  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.504aThe NIH biowulf cluster :b10 years of scientific supercomputing /cNIH/CIT.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen (viewed June 5, 2009).  aStreaming video (5 hr., 8 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 3, 2009.3 a(CIT): The NIH Biowulf cluster is a unique parallel processing system that has accelerated NIH intramural research in such fields as statistics, molecular dynamics, structural modeling, bioinformatics, genomics and imaging since its inception in 1999.  In the last decade, the NIH Biowulf cluster has grown from 80 processors to 6300+ processors, making it one of the largest general-purpose biomedical computing clusters in the world.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration22aComputer Systems22aMedical Informatics Computing 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aCIT (National Institutes of Health)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14894  aAUTH01737cgm a22004454a 101268479DNLM20260227150649.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu060 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12072  a(OCoLC)84646351  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 3451 aBrennan, Murray F.10aCaution, socratic surgeon at work /cMurray E. [sic] Brennan.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aGreat teachers grand rounds  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 5, 2006).  aStreaming video (59 min., 25 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 16, 2004.3 a(CIT): NIH Clinical Center Grand Rounds Contemporary Clinical Medicine: Great Teachers.  Murray E. Brennan, M.D., Chairman, Department of Surgery, Benno C. Schmidt Chair in Clinical Oncology, Memorial Sloan-Kettering Cancer Center.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aSarcoma22aMultiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 1 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aClinical Center grand rounds great teachers series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12072  aAUTH02194cgm a2200409 i 9918249812206676DNLM20260227161437.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210826s2021    mdu177|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31833  a(OCoLC)1292727572  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.55.E72 aA Match Made in Science: Integrating Bioethics and Biomedical Research (Symposium)d(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.12aA match made in science :bintegrating bioethics and biomedical research /cOffice of Science Policy, NIH.1 aIntegrating bioethics and biomedical research 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 57 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded July 20, 2021.3 a(CIT):  Moral principles evolve over time in society and our understanding of the world around us changes with each new discovery. To enable the rapid translation of research discoveries into practice, good science and robust ethics must go hand in hand. Consideration of ethical aspects of research in tandem with the research process itself offers many benefits, not only for society but for the research itself. The goals of this symposium are to highlight the integral role that bioethics plays in advancing science and to discuss strategies for fostering collaboration between bioethicists and biomedical researchers.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed January 10, 2022).12aBiomedical Researchxethics22aBioethical Issues 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Science Policy,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42402  aAUTH02518cgm a22004694a 101268566DNLM20260227151024.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu102|||||||f||||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12223  a(OCoLC)214299000  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2671 aMarquez, Victor,cDr.10aHispanic heritage month 2004.nPart 2 /cVictor Marquez and Carlos Caban.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 17, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 42 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 13, 2004.3 a(CIT): Fifth NIH Hispanic Scientist Day (5thNHSD)  This event seeks to promote and showcase the contributions of the Hispanic and Hispanic-American scientists at NIH, FDA-CBER, and USHUS.  One of the main purposes of this activity is to foster communication and interaction among post-doctoral fellows, students and interns with senior scientists at NIH.  It also provides an excellent opportunity to present and share your work with the rest of the NIH scientific community.  The Day's activities will start with Dr. Victor Marquez, Chief Laboratory of Medicinal Chemistry, presenting "Zebularine: A Molecule Destined for Clinical Trials as a Candidate for Cancer Epigenetic Therapy. The Magic of its Chemistry and Biology",  followed by a brief presentation by Dr. Carlos Caban, NIH Extramural Programs Policy Officer from the Office of the Director, titled "Opportunities for NIH extramural funding".  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxdrug therapy22aAnniversaries and Special Events22aCytidinextherapeutic use22aFinancing, Government22aHispanic or Latino.22aResearch Support as Topic 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bHispanic Employee Organization.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12223  aAUTH01334cgm a22004214a 101289555DNLM20260227151305.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s200