deo file (6 hr., 38 min.) :bcolor, sound)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Aug. 2, 2013.3 a(CIT): Over the past decade in vivo imaging approaches utilizing probes with molecular targets have been developed by many research laboratories funded by NIBIB and other ICs.  While some probes and techniques have been successfully translated to clinical applications, most molecular imaging probes and technologies that have been developed to date remain as laboratory tools or in the pre-clinical arena.  Challenges remain in achieving reliable and quantifiable results from in vivo molecular imaging tools for use in human diagnoses, clinical trials, drug development and clinical decision-making. This workshop will bring together researchers, practicing and research clinicians, representatives from  pharmaceutical companies, molecular imaging probe developers, and experts in regulatory science to promote discovery and accelerate validation and translation of molecular imaging probes to human applications.  aDescription based on version viewed Sept. 11, 2013.12aMolecular Imaging22aMolecular Probe Techniques 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18053  aAUTH01788cas a2200565 a 8411543DNLM20260227091023.0cr|cn ||||||||841018c19849999enkmr p       0    0eng    a   96648170 zsn 83008667  a0739-9332l0739-9332  aHCWIDQ  a(DNLM)SR0051242(s)  a(OCoLC)09837689  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  ansdp  9England00aW1bHE299KG10aHealth Care Women Int2dnlm 0aHealth care for women international00aHealth care for women international.  aWashington, D.C. :bHemisphere Pub. Corp.,cc1984-3132005- :aLondon :bRoutledge  av. :bill.  aTwelve no. a year,b2009-  aQuarterly,b-1992  aBimonthly  a10 no. a year,b2003-2008  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 5, no. 1-3-  aTitle from cover.  aImprint varies.1 aMEDLINEbv5n1-3, 1984-1 aPubMedbv5n1-3, 1984-1 aInternational nursing indexx0020-8124bv5n1-3, 1984-v20n6, Nov./Dec. 1999  aAlso issued online.  aThe journal of the International Council on Women's Health Issues, <1996-> 2aGynecology 2aNeonatology 2aObstetrics 2aWomenxpsychology 2aPeriodical2 aInternational Council on Women's Health Issues.0 x1096-466500tIssues in health care of womenx0161-5246w(DLC)sc 80000801w(OCoLC)39677144 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/loi/uhcw  aAUTH02527cgm a22004574a 101267780DNLM20260227145443.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu597 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10660  a(DNLM)CIT:10661  a(OCoLC)64588926  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 58.500aSystems biology approaches to health care :bmitochondrial proteomics /cNIST ... [et al.].30aMitochondrial proteomics  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 3, 2006).  aStreaming video (9 hr., 57 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 17-18, 2002 in Gaithersburg, Md. at NIST.3 a(CIT): The health care community, particularly the clinical diagnostics community, is moving towards a systems biology or proteomic approach to disease detection and diagnosis. A broad set of human health issues relate to mitochondrial function, from the biology of cancer development to issues relating to homeland security that include bioterrorism and chemical toxicities.  Mitochondrial proteins are encoded by both nuclear and mitochondrial genes, making the human mitochondrial proteome a good candidate for an integrated systems biology/proteomics model system. The human mitochondrion is central to basic life functions for the generation of cellular energy, and as such is the site of key components of the biosynthetic pathways, as well as the cellular decision points leading to apoptosis (programmed cell death), and other important functions.  For more information, visit  Mitochondrial Proteomics.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aProteomics22aMitochondriaxphysiology22aMitochondrial Proteins 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Standards and Technology (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1066040uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10661  aAUTH02626cgm a2200517 i 101613589DNLM20260227154747.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130726s2013    mdu089|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18031  a(OCoLC)856623055  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.500aResearch milestones :b60 years of clinical research /cJohn I. Gallin. The human mammary tumor virus / James F. Holland. Location, location, location : the mycobacterial susceptibility story / Steven M. Holland.1 aHuman mammary tumor virus1 aLocation, location, location 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 29 min.) :bcolor, sound).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aClinical Center grand rounds  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded July 10, 2013.3 a(CIT): During his opening remarks, "Research Milestones: 60 Years of Clinical Research," Dr. John I. Gallin, Director, NIH Clinical Center, will highlight some of the most significant milestones in the Clinical Center"s history.  Dr. James Holland, Distinguished Professor of Neoplastic Diseases in the Department of Medicine at the Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai in New York, will present "The Human Mammary Tumor Virus," and Dr. Steven Holland, Chief, Laboratory of Infectious Diseases, NIAID and Deputy Director for Intramural Clinical Research, NIH, will speak on "Location, Location, Location: The Mycobacterial Susceptibility."  aDescription based on version viewed August 13, 2013.12aBiomedical Researchxhistory22aBreast Neoplasmsxvirology22aDisease Susceptibility22aMycobacterium Infections22aMycobacteriumxpathogenicity22aTumor Virus Infections 2aLecture 2aWebcast12aGallin, John I.tResearch milestones.12aHolland, James F.,d1925-tHuman mammary tumor virus.12aHolland, Steven M.q(Steven Matthew),d1957-tLocation, location, location.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center,eissuing body. 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18031  aAUTH01398cas a2200457 a 101198162DNLM20260227090909.0cr||||||||||||030423c20029999ne tr p       0    0eng    a  2003220099  a1568-7767l1568-7767  aPRHEBS  a(OCoLC)52111456  aAGLcAGLdCASdOCLCQdOCLdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9Netherlands00aW1bPH987L0 aQK861b.P49 0aF60010aPhytochem Rev2dnlm 0aPhytochemistry reviewsb(Print)00aPhytochemistry reviews :bproceedings of the Phytochemical Society of Europe.  aDordrecht ;aBoston :bKluwer Academic,cc2002-3132005- :aDordrecht :bSpringer  av. :bill. ;c26 cm.  aThree no. a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (2002)-  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 3, no. 1/2 (2004).0 aChemical abstractsx0009-22580 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.12aPlantsxchemistry 2aPeriodical2 aPhytochemical Society of Europe.0 x1572-980X4 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/11101  aAUTH01482cgm a22004334a 101268081DNLM20260227150113.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu063 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11346  a(OCoLC)70164397  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5681