aBokerii︠a︡, L. A.q(Leo Antonovich)  aAUTH01379cgm a22003974a 101267728DNLM20260227145439.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s1998    mdu077 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10606  a(OCoLC)69391315  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 1371 aMatson, Steve.10aProtein-protein interactions that modulate the activity of DNA helicase II /cSteve Matson.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c1998]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 17, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 16 min., 30 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 22, 1998.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aDNA Helicases22aAdenosine Triphosphatases22aDNA Repair 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10606  aAUTH03387nam a2200433 i 9918986571806676DNLM20260225090533.0230918s2024    ncua     b    001 0 eng    a  2023028150  a1390725616  a9781478026006q(paperback)  a1478026006  a9781478025788q(hardcover)  a1478025786  z9781478059240q(ebook)  a(OCoLC)1390726355z(OCoLC)1390725616  aNcD/DLCbengerdacDLCdOCLCOdOCLCFdOCLCOdNDDdIG#dPSC  apcc  9United States00aR118b.B625 202400aWA 59000a610.1/4223/eng/20231211  aSOC002010aMED0780002bisacsh1 aBriggs, Charles L.,d1953-eauthor.10aIncommunicable :btoward communicative justice in health and medicine /cCharles L. Briggs.30aCommunicative justice in health and medicine 1aDurham :bDuke University Press,c2024.  axii, 323 pages :billustrations ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages [283]-306) and index.0 aPart I: Philosophical Dialogues in Search of Incommunicability. The Incommunicable Menace Lurking within Locke's Charter for Communicability -- W.E.B. Du Bois: Incommunicability and/as The Veil -- Frantz Fanon: Doctors, Tarzan, and the Colonial Inscription of Incommunicability -- Georges Canguilhem and the Clinical Production of Incommunicability -- Part II: How Incommunicability Shapes Entanglements of Language and Medicine -- Biocommunicable Labor and the Production of Incommunicability in "Doctor-Patient Interaction" -- Health Communication: How In/communicabilities Jump Scale -- Interlude: Social Movements and Incommunicability-Free Zones -- Part III: Communicable Contours of the COVID-19 Pandemic. Pandemic Ecologies of Knowledge: In Defense of COVID-19 Conspiracy Theories, Sort of -- Pandemic Ecologies of Care.  a"Incommunicable builds on philosophical dialogues of language and medicine to analyze incommunicability in the context of medical practice and public health discourse. A contrast to the concepts of communicability and biocommunicability that Charles L. Brigg's has developed throughout his career to study circulatory and biomedical power, incommunicability instead highlights the moments in which forms of communication face failure. Incommunicable questions dominant notions of communicability, which construct discourse and pathogens as inherently mobile, by rethinking the works and lives of philosopher-physicians John Locke, Frantz Fanon, and George Canguilhem, as well as W. E. B. Du Bois. Drawing on examples such as doctor-patient interaction within racialized communities and an extensive study of the COVID-19 pandemic, Briggs addresses the erosion of trust and rejection of expertise that has become prominent in science and medicine. As a study rooted in anthropological and linguistic analysis, Incommunicable intends to decolonize understandings of language and communication within medicine and health"--cProvided by publisher.12aHealth Communication22aAnthropology, Medical22aPhysician-Patient Relations  aAUTH01172nam a2200313 i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003100073042000800104044001800112060003300130100006600163245004200229264005600271300002200327336002600349337002800375338002700403500001600430500008400446540014200530583003900672583004300711650003500754655001200789710004800801999000900849101778644DNLM20260225111216.0210429r18861886cau           000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5573 no.131 aSimmons, Gustavus L.q(Gustavus Lincoln),d1832-1910eauthor.10aReport on surgery /cby G.L. Simmons. 1a[Sacremento] :b[publisher not identified],c[1886]  a12 pages ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCover title  aReprinted from Proceedings of the Medical Society of State of California, 1886.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM12aSurgical Procedures, Operative 2aAddress2 aMedical Society of the State of California.  aAUTH01244cgm a22003974a 101535335DNLM20260227153426.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100702s2010    mdu014|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15906  a(OCoLC)723031495  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4751 aKang, Tae-Hong,d1975-10aCircadian control of nucleotide excision repair /cTae-Hong Kang ; DNA Interest Group.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (14 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 18, 2010.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDNA Repair22aCircadian Clocks 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bDNA Repair Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15906  aAUTH03201cgm a2200457 a 101556025DNLM20260227154000.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110209s2011    mdu056 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16414  a(OCoLC)723032938  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 501 aGoldstein, David B.10aHuman genetics in the next generation :brare variants and common cures /cDavid Goldstein.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1streaming video file (56 min.) :bsd., col.).  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture  aTitle from title screen (viewed May 9, 2011).  aRecorded Jan. 19, 2011.3 a(CIT): Genome wide association studies have proven successful in identifying regions of the genome that contain gene variants that influence both common diseases and drug responses. In most instances however it has not been possible to track these associations down to the causal variants that are responsible, and this greatly reduces the utility of these findings in drug development and disease prediction. Sequencing based strategies on the other hand offer the promise of identifying the precise mutations and the genes they influence that are responsible both for predisposition to common disease and drug responses.  I outline how sequencing the entire genomes of patients is likely to change our understanding of human disease genetics over the next several years. I first contrast human traits that are and are not strongly influenced by common variants. I next review the role of rare copy number variants in schizophrenia and epilepsy and outline a framework for discovery of other kinds of rare high penetrant risk factors focusing on whole genome sequencing and evaluation of candidate variants through a combination of a) testing for cosegregation in multiplex families, b) bioinformatic analyses, and c) frequency comparison in large case control cohorts. Finally, I discuss what I see as the near term opportunities for discovery genetics for both disease predisposition and drug response. The over riding message is of cautious optimism.  Although the translational impact of genomic analysis of common diseases and drug responses has been modest to date, there are good reasons to believe that the tools are in place for a more rapid translation of genomic discoveries.  aOpen-captioned.12aGenetics, Medicalxmethods22aGenome-Wide Association Study22aGenomic Structural Variation22aPharmacogeneticsxmethods22aSequence Analysis, DNA 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16414  aAUTH01971cgm a22003974a 101267686DNLM20260227145326.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu075 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10561  a(OCoLC)64588908  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWR 2651 aCleaver, J. E.10aHistory of DNA repair :bmending human genes /cJames Cleaver.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 1, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 15 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jun. 19, 2001.3 a(CIT): The DNA Repair Interest Group is concerned with all forms of DNA damage and repair. As a major defense against environmental damage to cells DNA repair is present in all organisms examined including bacteria, yeast, drosophila, fish, amphibians, rodents and humans. The members of the DNA Repair Interest Group perform research in areas including DNA repair enzymology and fine structure, mutagenesis, gene and cell cycle regulation, protein structure, and human disease.  The DNA Repair Interest Group is open to all interested scientists at NIH or at other institutions. Meetings consist of lectures by NIH researchers or invited speakers.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aXeroderma Pigmentosum22aDNA Repair 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10561  aAUTH01257cam a22003251  11311610RDNLM20260206125347.0810725s1926    nyu||||  |||| 00||0|eng    a(OCoLC)14745817  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aQW 25bM624L 19262 aMichigan State College.bDepartment of Bacteriology and Hygiene.10aLaboratory manual in general microbiology,cprepared by the Laboratory of Bacteriology and Hygiene.  a3d ed.  aNew York,bWiley,c1926.  axxvi, 472 p.bill.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aWiley Technical series  a2d ed. issued by the institution under its earlier name: Michigan. Agriculture College, East Lansing. Dept. of Bacteriology and Hygiene.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202602062pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202602065DNLM 2aBacteriology 2aLaboratory Manual  aMicro-organisms  aAUTH01981nam a2200421 i 9918986583406676DNLM20260211102943.0230804t20242024mauab    b    001 0 eng    a  2023036126  a9780674268609qhardcover  a0674268601qhardcover  a(OCoLC)1395137984  aMH/DLCbengerdacDLCdYDXdOCLCOdBDXdOCLCFdOCLCOdYDX  apcc  an-us---  9United States00aRA448.5.B53bH55 202400aQU 46000a362.1089/96073223/eng/202308241 aHilliard, Constance B.,eauthor.10aAncestral genomics :bAfrican American health in the age of precision medicine /cConstance B. Hilliard. 1aCambridge, Massachusetts ;aLondon, England :bHarvard University Press,c2024. 4c©2024  a198 pages :billustrations, maps ;c22 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.00tOur History: In the Shadow of Timbuktu --tThe System: American Medicine's Incomplete Paradigm --tOur Health: Tracking Down Ancestral Clues --tThe Algorithm: Applying Genetic Ancestry to Case Studies --tAncestral Genomics: Embracing a Multi-Genomic Nation.  a"Race is a social reality, not a biological one. Yet African Americans are poorly served by even advanced genetic medicine because it is built on European DNA. Constance Hilliard explores the benefits and drawbacks of racial heuristics in medicine and argues for nonessentializing methods of harnessing genomic science on behalf of people of color"--cProvided by publisher.12aGenomics12aBlack or African Americanxgenetics22aHealth Status Disparities22aRace Factors22aPrecision Medicine 2aUnited States  aAUTH01534cgm a22004574a 101535274DNLM20260227153511.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100702s2010    mdu048|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15955  a(OCoLC)658535531  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 1951 aPalmore, Tara N.10aHospital infections :brumors and reality /cTara Palmore.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aClinical Center grand rounds1 aGreat teachers1 aJohn Laws Decker memorial lecture ;v7th  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (48 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 9, 2010.3 a(CIT): John Laws Decker Memorial Lecture: Hospital Infections: Rumors and Reality.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aCross Infection 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center. 0aClinical Center grand rounds great teachers series 0aAnnual John Laws Decker memorial lecturev7th.