itute (U.S.)12aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration12aNeoplasms22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1162540uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11626  aAUTH01529cgm a22004214a 101268356DNLM20260227150738.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu063 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11807  a(OCoLC)84667206  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 241 aVon Eschenbach, Andrew C.,d1941-10aCharting our progress :ba dialogue with the director of the National Cancer Institute /cAndrew C. von Eschenbach.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 10, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 2 min., 36 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 9, 2004.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. NIH-ONLY events may not be disseminated outside of the NIH network.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.)12aAcademies and Institutes22aOrganizational Objectives22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11807  aAUTH02363cgm a2200457 i 9918226875906676DNLM20260227161251.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210615s2021    mdu559|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31745  a(DNLM)CIT:31747  a(OCoLC)1258214688  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 704.62 aNIH Pain Consortium.bAnnual Symposiumn(16th :d2021 :cOnline)eauthor.10aPain and pandemics :bchallenges and opportunities in the current social and healthcare climate /cSixteenth Annual NIH Pain Consortium Symposium, May 24-25, 2021. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (9 hr., 19 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded May 24 & 25, 2021.3 a(CIT): On May 24th - 25th, the NIH Pain Consortium will convene the 16th Annual NIH Pain Consortium Symposium on Advances in Pain Research via a virtual platform. The symposium: "Pain and Pandemics: Challenges and Opportunities in the Current Social and Healthcare Climate", will address a timely topic on the impact of the current socioeconomic, healthcare and social justice challenges on pain management. Topics will include the impact of COVID-19 on acute and chronic pain conditions, comorbidities and disparities, with a focus on vulnerable populations. The symposium will also discuss current opportunities for interventions to reduce health disparities and increase effective pain management across populations.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed June 21, 2021).12aPain Management22aCOVID-1922aOpioid Epidemic22aHealth Equity22aHealthcare Disparities22aSocial Determinants of Health 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4214040uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=42141  aAUTH01388cgm a22004214a 101268300DNLM20260227150352.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu061 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11686  a(OCoLC)72772198  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 102.51 aJahn, Reinhard,d1950-10aMolecular mechanisms of neuronal exocytosis /cReinhard Jahn.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Sept. 1, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 36 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 1, 2003.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeurons22aExocytosis22aSNARE Proteins 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11686  aAUTH02095cgm a22004454a 101268423DNLM20260227150548.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu090 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11959  a(OCoLC)84671319  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWI 4201 aMannon, Peter J.10aInflammatory bowel disease /cPeter J. Mannon.  The immunology of inflammatory bowel disease / Warren Strober.1 iTitle on screen banner:aDemystifying medicine, inflammatory bowel disease :bthe diseases, mechanisms  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitles from PowerPoint screens (viewed Nov. 1, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 29 min., 5 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 6, 2004.3 a(CIT): "Demystifying Medicine" is a weekly course, primarily for Ph.D. students, post-doctoral fellows, and staff at the National Institutes of Health. Physicians and other students are also welcome to participate. The course is led by Dr. Win Arias, Professor of Physiology and Medicine at Tufts School of Medicine in Boston and NIH Adjunct Investigator and Special Assistant in the Office of Intramural Research.  For more information, visit Demystifying medicine.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aInflammatory Bowel Diseases22aInterleukin-12 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)02aImmunology of inflammatory bowel disease.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11959  aAUTH05392nam a2200457 i 9918986633406676DNLM20260217065218.0231102t20242024nyu      b    001 0 eng    a  2023044789  a1452284206  a9781032620992qhardcover  a1032620994qhardcover  a9781032620985qpaperback  a1032620986qpaperback  z9781032620978qelectronic book  a(OCoLC)1398211429z(OCoLC)1452284206  aDLCbengerdacDLCdOCLCOdYDXdAU@dIG#  apcc  9United States00aR724b.C886 202400aWB 142.500a174.2223/eng/202401101 aCutter, Mary Ann Gardell,eauthor.13aAn ethics of clinical uncertainty :blessons from the COVID-19 pandemic /cMary Ann G. Cutter. 1aNew York :bRoutledge, Taylor & Francis Group,c2024. 4c©2024  ax, 123 pages ;c24 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aRoutledge annals of bioethics  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.0 aCover -- Half Title -- Series Information -- Title Page -- Copyright Page -- Dedication -- Table of Contents -- Preface -- Acknowledgments -- 1 Introduction -- Focus of Considerations -- The Case of COVID-19 -- Outline of Inquiry -- 2 Decision-Making in Clinical Medicine -- The Context of Clinical Uncertainty: Clinical Decision-Making -- Approaches in Clinical Decision-Making and the Search for Clinical Certainty -- Need for More Discussion On Clinical Uncertainty -- 3 Clinical Uncertainty: Epistemological Roots -- The Challenge of Clinical Uncertainty -- The Notion of Uncertainty -- Clinical Uncertainty: Source, Issue, and Locus -- Source -- Issue -- Locus -- Intersections Among Source, Issue, and Locus -- Probability, Ambiguity, and Complexity -- Need for a Wider Lens of Clinical Uncertainty -- 4 Clinical Uncertainty: Ontological and Axiological Roots -- There's More to the Story -- Clinical Uncertainty: Nature and Values -- The Uncertainty of Clinical Problems: Limit, Change, Complexity -- The Uncertainty of Clinical Values: Ambiguity, Variation, and Choice -- Need for an Expanded Taxonomy of Clinical Uncertainty -- 5 A Taxonomy of Clinical Uncertainty -- An Expanded and Integrative Account of Clinical Uncertainty -- A Taxonomy of Clinical Uncertainty -- Intersectionality of Clinical Uncertainty -- Need for Guidance On How to Manage Clinical Uncertainty -- 6 Managing Clinical Uncertainty -- A Practical Focus -- Suggestions On Managing Clinical Uncertainty -- A Taxonomy of Managing Clinical Uncertainty -- Need to Think Through the Ethical Implications of Clinical Uncertainty -- 7 Our Ethical Duty to Manage Clinical Uncertainty -- Prominent Duties in Medicine -- Welfare, Rights, Justice, and Care -- Welfare and Risk Assessment -- Rights and Informed Consent -- Justice and Health Equity -- Care in Clinical Relationships.8 aManaging Clinical Uncertainty as a Precursor to Satisfying Clinical Duties -- Reflective Equilibrium and Intersectional Thinking -- Need for Further Reflection On Managing Moral Distress -- 8 Managing Moral Distress and Building Moral Resilience -- Pandemic Stress -- Recognizing Moral Distress -- Building Moral Resilience -- A Taxonomy of Moral Distress and Resilience -- Need for Further Reflection and Action -- 9 Toward an Ethics of Clinical Uncertainty -- Some Summary Reflections -- Personal Notes -- Suggestions for Future Work and Action -- References -- Glossary of Terms in Biomedical Ethics -- Index.  a"This book explores the ethical implications of managing uncertainty in clinical decision-making during the COVID-19 pandemic. It develops an ethics of clinical uncertainty that brings together insights from the clinical and biomedical ethical literatures. The book sets out to recognize the central role uncertainty plays in clinical decision-making and to acknowledge the different levels, kinds, and dimensions of clinical uncertainty. It also aims to aid clinicians and patients in managing clinical uncertainty, and to recognize the ethical duty they have to manage clinical uncertainty. The book addresses four ethical duties related to clinical uncertainty: (1) the duty to advance the welfare of those in clinical medicine, (2) to respect the rights of those in clinical medicine, (3) to promote just access to health care, and (4) to care for one another in clinical medicine. These duties took on select urgency during the COVID-19 pandemic because clinical risk assessments about COVID-19 were limited, we were asked to give informed consent in the context of limited and changing knowledge, the pandemic unearthed myriad problems about the distribution of health care, and the pandemic raised questions about how we care for each other in medicine. An Ethics of Clinical Uncertainty will appeal to scholars, advanced students, and medical professionals working in philosophy of medicine, biomedical ethics, clinical medicine, nursing, public health care, and gerontology"--cProvided by publisher.12aClinical Decision-Makingxethics12aUncertainty22aEthics, Medical 0aRoutledge annals of bioethics  aAUTH01773cas a2200481 i 101642117DNLM20260227090924.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||140709c20149999ne || poo     0    0eng    a  2017243202  a2214-0301l2214-0301  a(OCoLC)900312830z(OCoLC)885883464  aLNDbengerdad20260227cLNDdOCLCOdOH1dOCLCOdAU@dCUSdOCLCOdOCLCFdOCLCOdOCLCQdOCLCOdUABdDNLM  apcc  9Netherlands 4aTP248.3b.M48500aW110aMetab Eng Commun2dnlm00aMetabolic engineering communications.1 aMEC 1a[Amsterdam] :bElsevier B.V.,c[2014]-  a1 online resource  atext2rdacontent  acomputer2rdamedia  aonline resource2rdacarrier1 aBegan with volume 1, issue 1 (December, 2014).0 aPubMedbv1, Dec. 2014-0 aPMC  aArticles published and added to an annual volume.  aCompanion journal to: Metabolic engineering.  aDescription based on Volume 1 (December 2014); title from journal home page (ScienceDirect, viewed July 28, 2017).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 5 (December 2017) (viewed July 28, 2017).12aBiomedical Engineering22aBiochemistry22aMetabolism 2aPeriodical2 aInternational Metabolic Engineering Society,eissuing body.08iComplemented by (work):tMetabolic engineeringx1096-7176w(OCoLC)3786157740uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=10164211740uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/22140301  aAUTH01864cgm a22004334a 101566827DNLM20260227154049.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110811s2011    mdu099|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16770  a(OCoLC)752783527  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 26.55.C71 aFox, Susannah.10aPeer-to-peer healthcare :bhow the Internet is transforming health communications by providing us with access to information and each other /cSusannah Fox.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 39 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aMedicine: mind the gap  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Aug. 2, 2011.3 a(CIT): The Medicine: Mind the Gap series explores a wide range of issues at the intersection of research, evidence, and clinical practice--especially areas in which conventional wisdom may lead us astray.   Susannah Fox, Associate Director of the Pew Research Center's Internet and American Life Project, will discuss how changes in the health communications landscape have impacted how people search for healthcare information.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHealth Communicationxmethods12aInternet 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aMedicine: mind the gap lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16770  aAUTH04096pam a2200397 i 9918987892306676DNLM20260220144547.0250203s2024    njua     b    001 0 eng    a  2023026387  a1350423237a1350428287a1350437294a1350438287a1350440642  a9781394188833q(hardback)  a1394188838q(hardback)  z9781394188857q(epdf)  z9781394188840q(epub)  z9781394188864q(ebook)  a(OCoLC)1385323112z(OCoLC)1350423237z(OCoLC)1350428287z(OCoLC)1350437294z(OCoLC)1350438287z(OCoLC)1350440642  aDLCbengerdacDLCdYDXdBDXdOCLCFdOCLCOdIG#  apcc  9United States00aQP671.F52bP658 202400aQV 22300a572/.2223/eng/2023062100aPolyphenols: food, nutraceutical, and nanotherapeutic applications /cedited by Mithun Rudrapal, Department of Pharmaceutical Sciences, School of Biotechnology and Pharmaceutical Sciences, Vignan's Foundation for Science, Technology & Research, Guntur, India. 1aHoboken, New Jersey :bJohn Wiley & Sons,c[2024]  axiii, 380 pages :billustrations ;c26 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  a"The book titled "Polyphenols: Food, Nutraceutical, and Nanotherapeutic Applications" provides us with detailed information on potential health benefits of food polyphenols against human diseases and their therapeutic interventions as nutraceuticals and nanoformulations. Plant-based foods including nutraceuticals, functional foods and dietary supplements have gradually become an area of increasing interest because of the potential health implications of polyphenols-based food products available in the market globally. Long-term consumption of foods rich in polyphenols has been reported to improve conditions in diabetes, osteoporosis, cancer, neurological disorders and cardiovascular disorders. The market demand for polyphenols-based food products and nutraceutical preparations is increasing rapidly with the social and economic development and the improvement of people's health awareness. The beneficial effects of polyphenols-based nutraceuticals and their nanoformulations attract more consumers who use polyphenols-based food products. However, many polyphenols have low oral bioavailability, which limits the application of polyphenols as nutraceuticals and in the form of nanomedicine. This book would be a useful resource for researchers working in the areas of dietary polyphenols, nanodeliveries and nutraceutical applications. The book chapters provide up-to-date information relating to the ongoing research on food polyphenols along with their health and therapeutic implications as antioxidants. The chapters are described in a lucid and consistent manner in order to aid flow, continuity and technical presentation. In this book, the health benefits of plant polyphenols in the prevention of various major diseases such as cancer, neurological disorders, CNS disorders, cardiovascular disorders, bacterial infections, and inflammatory disorders are summarized. This book particularly delineates food polyphenols and their antioxidant potentials with special emphasis on their nutraceutical applications and nanodeliveries. The bioavailability and pharmacokinetic issues and toxicity are also enunciated herein. This book on will be very much useful to drug discovery scientists (R&D), pharmaceutical scientists, biomedical scientists, food scientists, healthcare professionals, phytochemists, biochemists, clinicians, pharmacologists, dietician, nutritionists, research students, professors, and other researchers working in the field of polyphenols, nutraceuticals and nanomedicine research"--cProvided by publisher.12aPolyphenols22aDietary Supplements22aPlants, Edible1 aRudrapal, Mithun,eeditor.  aAUTH01452nam a2200397 i 9918986660206676DNLM20260220080947.0240313t20242024enka     b    001 0 eng    a9780443185168q(hardback)  a0443185166q(hardback)  a(OCoLC)1426035499  aLPUbengerdacLPUdLPUdOCLCOdIAX  apcc  9England00aW04a616.8044222300aNeuropsychiatric disorders and epigenetics /cedited by Jacob Peedicayil, Dennis R. Grayson, Trygve Tollefsbol.  aSecond edition. 1aLondon :bAcademic Press,c[2024] 4c©2024  axvii, 398 pages :billustrations (black and white, and colour) ;c25 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediated