0211300003800263336002600301337002800327338002700355490002800382500001900410500003800429500004100467583007500508650001900583650002100602651001100623810004200634999000900676101778275DNLM20260226102141.0210419s1838    mau           000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5571 no.121 aBoston (Mass.).bCommon Council,eauthor.10aPure water. 1a[Boston] :b[publisher not identified],c[1838]  a1 folded sheet (3 pages) ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aCity document ;vno. 11  aCaption title.  aAt head of title: City of Boston.  a"In Common Council, March 15, 1838."  aWill not digitizec20221028lscanned by another institution2pda5DNLM12aDrinking Water22aLocal Government 2aBoston1 aBoston (Mass.).tDocument ;v1838, 11  aAUTH02478cgm a2200469 i 9918503182006676DNLM20260227162640.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u221105s2022    mdu054|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32613  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aHD 7256.U500aDEIA listening session.pPeople with disabilities /cLarry Tabak, Tara Schwetz, Kevin Williams, Julie Berko, Marie Bernard, James Hopkins.1 aDiversity, equity, inclusion, and accessibility listening session1 aPeople with disabilities 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (54 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded September 9, 2022.3 a(CIT): This is the listening session focusing on the People with Disabilities Community at NIH.   In each listening session, NIH senior leaders heard directly from the community about specific workforce-related challenges as well as recommendations for effecting change. The goal of each listening session is to provide a safe space for members of each population and their allies to share their insights. The seven populations include Asian Americans, Native Hawaiians and Pacific Islanders, Black/African Americans, Hispanic/Latinos, Native Americans, People with Disabilities, Sexual and Gender Minorities, and Women. Any community member or ally had the opportunity to share their perspectives on workforce-related challenges and solutions.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed March 1, 2023).22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)12aPersons with Disabilities.12aGovernment Employees22aWorkplace22aDiversity, Equity, Inclusion22aGovernment Agencies 2aUnited States 2aWebcast1 aTabak, Lawrence A.,d1951-espeaker,emoderator.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45895  aAUTH01197cas a2200421   0253023DNLM20260227091034.0cr|cn#||||||||700806c19489999mauqr         0    0eng    a   50013779   a0031-5826l0031-5826  aPPSYAQ  a(DNLM)P08740000(s)  a(OCoLC)01263860  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bPE870210aPers Psychol2dnlm00aPersonnel psychology.  aBaltimore [etc.]3 3<2004->:aMalden, MA :bBlackwell Pub.  av.billus.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-       spring 1948-  a"A journal of applied research."  aPlace of publication varies.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online. 2aPersonnel Management 2aPsychology, Industrial 2aPeriodical0 x1744-65704 uhttp://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/journal/10.1111/(ISSN)1744-6570  aAUTH03118cgm a22004334a 101480535DNLM20260227152825.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080801s2008    mdu719 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14598  a(DNLM)CIT:14601  a(OCoLC)244264631  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWI 8302 aTowards an Artificial Pancreas Public Meetingd(2008 :cNational Institutes of Health)10aTowards an Artificial Pancreas Public Meeting /cFDA, NIH, and the Juvenile Diabetes Research Foundation.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Aug. 11, 2008).  aStreaming video (11 hr., 59 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded July 21 & 22, 2008.3 a(CIT): The artificial pancreas is one of FDA's Critical Path Initiatives, a program dedicated to accelerating the availability of much needed medical products.   The Interagency Artificial Pancreas Working Group, a group of multi-disciplined scientists and clinicians from the FDA and NIH, was established to support this initiative.  The goals of the Artificial Pancreas Initiative are two fold; to provide infrastructure for narrowing the gap between basic biomedical knowledge and clinical application of novel technologies, and to cross-fertilize and partner with stakeholders in order to identify and overcome the clinical and scientific challenges to the development of an artificial pancreas.  Through collaborative efforts, such as this workshop, the group strives to develop innovative strategies to achieve their goals.  World-renowned experts will present information on key topics in the development of artificial pancreas during sessions that  will be followed by roundtable discussions. Session topics will include:  .State-of-the-art design of closed-loop glycemic control systems  .Results of recently conducted clinical trials  .Clinical Trial design, including how to define success and failures of a closed-loop system  .Algorithms and in silico models  .Engineering challenges   .Patient considerations   .Metabolic monitoring   .Paths for developing a marketable closed-loop system   The agenda for this public workshop is available on the internet at http://www.blsmeetings.net/artificialpancreas08/agenda.pdf. For more information see the meeting webpage at http://www.blsmeetings.net/artificialpancreas08/intro.cfm.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aPancreas, Artificialxtrends22aDiffusion of Innovation22aInsulin Infusion Systemsxtrends 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1459840uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14601  aAUTH01638nam a2200385 i 101776483DNLM20260204150256.0220121s1878    kyu           000 0 eng    a  2021658635  a(OCoLC)31185677  aDLCbengerdacDLCdPAUdOCLCFdUMCdOCLCOdOCLdOCLCQdOCLCOdOCLCLdOCLCOdDNLM1 aenghger  apcc  9United States00aRA793b.S3600aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5508 no.271 aSchreiber, Josef,d1835-1908,eauthor.10aMeteorology in the service of medicine :ban address delivered before the Austrian Meteorological Society /cby Dr. J. Schreiber. 1aLouisville, Ky. :bRichmond and Louisville medical journal Book and Job Steam Print.,c1878.  a12 pages ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"Translated from the "Oesterreichische zeitschrift fur meteorologie" by W.H. Geddings."  a"From February no. Richmond and Louisville medical journal."  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM12aMeteorological Concepts 2aAddress1 aGeddings, Wm. H.q(William H.),d1838-1892,etranslator.2 aÖsterreichische Gesellschaft für Meteorologie.0 aRichmond and Louisville medical journal.  aAUTH02305cgm a2200433 i 9918434184406676DNLM20260227162537.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220706s2022    mdu334|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32483  a(DNLM)CIT:32488  a(OCoLC)1455527430  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 3002 aBRAIN Initiative.bAnnual Investigators Meetingn(8th :d2022 :cOnline)eauthor.10a8th annual BRAIN meeting.pConcurrent sessions /cNIH BRAIN Initiative.1 aEighth annual BRAIN meeting1 aConcurrent sessions 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (5 hr., 34 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 21 & 22, 2022.3 a(CIT): The Brain Research Through Advancing Innovative Neurotechnologies(r) (BRAIN) Initiative is aimed at revolutionizing our understanding of the human brain. By accelerating the development and application of innovative technologies, researchers will be able to produce a revolutionary new dynamic picture of the brain that, for the first time, shows how individual cells and complex neural circuits interact in both time and space. Long desired by researchers seeking new ways to treat, cure, and even prevent brain disorders, this picture will fill major gaps in our current knowledge and provide unprecedented opportunities for exploring exactly how the brain enables the human body to record, process, utilize, store, and retrieve vast quantities of information, all at the speed of thought.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 22, 2023).12aBrain 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aBRAIN Initiative,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4566640uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45672  aAUTH02379ngm a2200433 i 9918592678206676DNLM20260227163355.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230429s2023    mdu666|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33005  a(DNLM)CIT:33006  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 300 AA12 aHealth Disparities Among Sexual and Gender Minorities (SGM) Workshopd(2023 :cOnline)jauthor.10aHealth disparities among sexual and gender minorities (SGM)  /cNIMHD, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (11 hr., 6 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded March 1 & 2, 2023.3 a(CIT): The goal of this virtual two-day meeting is to identify priority research areas to understand and address SGM health disparities. Specifically, the workshop objectives are to identify research gaps in: Data sources and measures that can be used to assess and address SGM health disparities; Mechanisms and pathways that drive/cause SGM health disparities; Interventions with the potential to mitigate/reduce SGM health disparities. The workshop will bring together a diverse set of researchers with expertise on important health outcomes (e.g., cancer, mental/behavioral health, cardiovascular disease, diabetes and obesity, and pandemic-related disparities) among SGM (e.g., lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender, two-spirit, queer, and others) populations.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed February 18, 2026).12aSexual and Gender Minorities12aHealth Inequities 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4913540uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49137  aAUTH01383cgm a22004094a 101267888DNLM20260227145813.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu070 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10901  a(OCoLC)68207227  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 3071 aHeeger, David.10aAttention and sensory signals in human visual cortex /cDavid Heeger.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline 