lor, sound).  atwo-dimensional moving image2rdacontent  avideo2rdamedia  aonline resource2rdacarrier1 aNIH Wednesday afternoon lecture  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded June 5, 2013.3 a(CIT): Wednesday Afternoon Lecture Series  Specificity of metazoan transcription is determined combinatorially: different subsets of broadly expressed regulatory factors assemble as gene-, cell- and physiology-specific complexes at genomic response elements. Dr. Yamamoto"s laboratory studies suggest that key regulatory factors such as glucocorticoid receptor (GR) receive and integrate multiple signals as allosteric effectors, thus altering GR conformation and producing novel patterns of functional surfaces that nucleate distinct assemblies.  aDescription based on version viewed July 10, 2013.12aTranscription, Genetic22aAllosteric Regulationxgenetics22aReceptors, Glucocorticoidxgenetics22aSignal Transductionxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17986  aAUTH01256cas a22003977a 9426825DNLM20260227091023.0cr|cn#||||||||950223c19869999hu qr p       0    0hun    a0237-7896l0237-7896  a(DNLM)SR0059913(s)  a(OCoLC)21833178  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 ahunbeng  9Hungary00aW1bPS239F10aPsychiatr Hung2dnlm00aPsychiatria Hungarica :bA Magyar Pszichiátriai Társaság tudományos folyóirata.  aBudapest :bMagyar Pszichiátriai Társaság,  av.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan in 1986.  aDescription based on: 2 évf., 1. sz., márc. 1987; title from cover.1 aMEDLINEbv20n1, 2005-1 aPubMedbv20n1, 2005-  aAlso issued online.  aIn Hungarian, summaries and table of contents also in English. 2aMental Disorders 2aPeriodical2 aMagyar Pszichiátriai Társaság.4 uhttps://mptpszichiatria.hu/folyoirat.aspx  aAUTH01971cgm a2200481 i 9918418885106676DNLM20260227162234.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220616s2022    mdu612|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32321  a(DNLM)CIT:32322  a(OCoLC)1372997207  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 3002 aBrain Across the Lifespan: Tools and Methodologies for Measuring the Changing Brain Virtual Workshopd(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.14aThe brain across the lifespan :btools and methodologies /cNICHD, NIH.1 iTitle on PowerPoint:aBrain across the lifespan :btools and methodologies for measuring the changing brain virtual workshop 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (10 hr., 12 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 18 & 19, 2022.3 a(CIT): The Brain Across the Lifespan: Tools and Methodologies - Day 1 & Day 2.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 13, 2023).12aBrainxgrowth & development22aBrain Mapping22aHuman Development22aLife Cycle Stages22aLongevity 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4503940uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=45041  aAUTH02491cgm a2200421 i 9918299482606676DNLM20260227161734.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u211110s2021    mdu201|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32031  a(OCoLC)1294916766  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWQ 2002 aReproductive Health Care for Persons with Disabilities Across the Transition from Adolescence to Adulthood (Meeting)d(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aReproductive health care for persons with disabilities across the transition from adolescence to adulthood /cNICHD, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (3 hr., 21 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded October 27, 2021.3 a(CIT): Meeting Objective: To identify scientific gaps related to the preventive reproductive health needs of adolescents and young adults with physical, sensory, and intellectual disabilities. The focus is on 1) barriers to and facilitators of appropriate care (including accessing knowledgeable providers, continuity of care, and provision and receipt of preventive counseling and screening services during visits); 2) effective use of existing reproductive health interventions, medications, and devices; and 3) inclusion in relevant and appropriate research. The meeting scope is inclusive of persons of all gender identities. Participants will also consider in what ways the different types of disability populations have enough commonalities to be considered together and where their needs are so disparate that they require separate efforts.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed January 3, 2022).12aReproductive Health Services22aPersons with Disabilities22aAdolescent22aYoung Adult 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=43864  aAUTH01698cgm a22004574a 101267915DNLM20260227151213.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu471 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11008  a(DNLM)CIT:11009  a(OCoLC)847934566  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2061 aUnited States.bNational Cancer Advisory Board.bMeetingn(122nd :d2002 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNational Cancer Advisory Board (NCAB) /