tific Advisors.bMeetingn(22nd :b2002 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNCI, Board of Scientific Advisors /cthe National Cancer Institute (NCI) Division of Extramural Activities.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 9, 2007).  aStreaming video (11 hr., 6 min., 49 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Nov. 14-15, 2002.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.)12aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration12aNeoplasms22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1147840uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11479  aAUTH01442nam a2200349 i 101776489DNLM20260204111854.0210225r18781878nju      b    000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5508 no.291 aRyerson, Thomas,eauthor.10aRectification of abnormal fœtal positions, or, Some abnormities of childbirth and their treatment /cby Thomas Ryerson.30aRectification of abnormal fœtal positions30aSome abnormities of childbirth and their treatment 1aNewark, NJ :bL.J. Hardham, Printer,c1878.  a32 pages ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCover title.  a"Extracted from the Transactions of the Medical Society of New Jersey."  aIncludes bibliographical references.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM12aObstetric Labor Complicationsxtherapy12aObstetric Labor Complicationsxprevention & control0 aTransactions of the Medical Society of New Jersey.  aAUTH02187cgm a2200445 i 101658203DNLM20260227155032.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150505s2015    mdu633 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18969  a(DNLM)CIT:18971  a(OCoLC)909852207  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWQ 2122 aHuman Placenta Project (Eunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.)).bAnnual Meetingn(2nd :d2015 :cBethesda, Md.)eauthor.14aThe Human Placenta Project :bplacental structure and function in real time /cNICHD, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (10 hr., 33 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded April 27 and 28, 2015.3 a(CIT): The placenta is the least understood human organ and arguably one of the more important organs in the body, not only for the health of a woman and her fetus during pregnancy but also for the lifelong health of both. To address this lack of knowledge, the NICHD believes a concerted effort, the Human Placenta Project, would make substantial inroads.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 15, 2015).22aHuman Placenta Project (Eunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.))12aPlacentaxanatomy & histology22aPlacenta Diseases22aPlacentaxphysiology 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1896940uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18971  aAUTH03884cgm a22004334a 101549274DNLM20260227153733.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101208s2010    mdu055|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16290  a(OCoLC)696713370  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWH 3801 aAppelbaum, Frederick R.14aThe grand challenges of hematopoietic cell transplantation /cFrederick R. Appelbaum.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCI's Center for Cancer Research grand rounds  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (55 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS and NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Nov. 23, 2010.3 a(CIT): Frederick R. Appelbaum received his M.D., from Tufts University School of Medicine. He completed his internship and residency at the University of Michigan Medical Center and his fellowship in medical oncology at the National Cancer Institute (NCI). He joined the Fred Hutchinson Cancer Research Center"s (FHCRC) scientific staff in 1978 and in 1988 became a full member of the FHCRC faculty and a professor of medicine in the Division of Oncology at the University of Washington. He has been senior vice president and director of the Clinical Research Division at FHCRC since 1993 and was named executive director of the Seattle Cancer Care Alliance and head of the Division of Medical Oncology at the University of Washington School of Medicine in 1998. He is a diplomat on the American Boards of Internal Medicine and Medical Oncology and is past chair of the Board of Scientific Advisors of NCI. In addition, he has been a board member of several scientific societies, including the American Society of Hematology, American Society of Clinical Oncology, American Society for Blood and Marrow Transplantation and is chair of the Leukemia Committee of the Southwest Oncology Group. Dr. Appelbaum served two terms as associate editor for Blood and also serves on the editorial boards of numerous scientific journals. Dr. Appelbaum is head of the Clinical Transplant Research Program in the FHCRC/University of Washington Cancer Consortium and principal investigator on several National Institutes of Health grants. He is the author of more than 800 scientific articles and was the lead author on the first paper to describe the successful use of autologous marrow transplantation. Dr. Appelbaum"s current research interests center on the biology and treatment of hematologic malignancies.   NCI's Center for Cancer Research (CCR) Grand Rounds is a weekly lecture series addressing current research in clinical and molecular oncology. Speakers are leading national and international researchers and clinicians proposed by members of the CCR Grand Rounds Planning Committee and others within the CCR community and approved by the CCR Office of the Director. Lectures occur every Tuesday from 8:00 to 9:00 a.m. in Lipsett Amphitheater in the Clinical Center building on the NIH campus September through July with exceptions around holidays and major cancer meetings. CME credit available via sign-up sheets in the lecture hall and at designated video-bridge sites only.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHematopoietic Stem Cell Transplantation 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aCenter for Cancer Research (National Cancer Institute (U.S.)) 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16290  aAUTH01580cam a2200409 i 101205198DNLM20260220150116.0cr||||||||||||040122s1871    pau           000 0 eng    a(DNLM)IDX:11005261150  a(OCoLC)18690972  aDNLMbengerdad20260220dDNLM  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 718 no.2000aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5071 no.221 aParry, John S.q(John Stubbs),d1843-1876eauthor.10aInfant mortality and the necessity of a foundling hospital in Philadelphia :bread May 5, 1871 /cby John S. Parry. 1a[Philadelphia?] :b[publisher not identified],c[1871?]  a28 pages ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  aAt head of title: Social Science Association