06500032005416500029005739990009006029918976860706676DNLM20260220080944.0250115s2023    gw c     b    000 0 ger    a9783662677216qhardbound  z9783662677223qebook  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Germany00aWM 1401 aSchönknecht, Peter,eauthor.10aReflexive psychopathologie :berkenntnislehre und menschenkunde im klinischen kontext /cPeter Schönknecht. 1aBerlin, Germany :bSpringer,c[2023]  axi, 175 pages :bportrait ;c25 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aMental Disordersxpathology22aPsychopathologyxmethods  aAUTH02644cgm a22004334a 101465462DNLM20260227152501.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080201s2008    mdu085|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14234  a(OCoLC)213007422  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQV 381 aRoden, D. M.q(Dan M.)10aPharmacogenomics /cDan Roden.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aOPASI rounds lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 29, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 25 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 11, 2008.3 a(CIT) Administration of virtually any drug to a group of patient's results in a spectrum of responses: some patients may derive great benefit, in others there is no effect, and some patients will have adverse effects. The concept that such variability in drug response might include a heritable component goes back over 100 years.  The identification of responsible variants in individual genes, and the sequencing of the human genome and its variants now opens the door to an era of personalized prescribing in which genomic information becomes a routine component of clinical decision-making.  This talk will highlight current evidence linking genomic variants to variable drug responses, and will describe obstacles and their potential solutions to the implementation of such a personalized prescribing future.    The Office of Portfolio Analysis and Strategic Initiatives (OPASI) provides the National Institutes of Health (NIH) and its constituent Institutes and Centers (ICs) with the methods and information necessary to manage their large and complex scientific portfolios, identifies in concert with multiple other inputs important areas of emerging scientific opportunities or rising public health challenges, and assists in the acceleration of investments in these areas, focusing on those involving multiple ICs.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aPharmacogeneticsxtrends22aPharmaceutical Preparations 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aOPASI rounds lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14234  aAUTH02474cgm a2200481 i 9918282192106676DNLM20260227161548.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u211008s2021    mdu572|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31945  a(DNLM)CIT:31951  a(OCoLC)1314048627  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 1402 aNINDS Health Disparities and Inequities in Neurological Disorders (HEADWAY) Workshopd(2021 :cOnline)jauthor.10aNINDS health disparities and inequities in neurological disorders (HEADWAY) workshop /cNational Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke, NIH.1 aHealth disparities and inequities in neurological disorders (HEADWAY) workshop1 iPowerPoint:aHEADWAY health disparities and inequities in neurological disorders workshop 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (9 hr., 32 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 22 & 23, 2021.3 a(CIT): The goal of the Health Disparities and Inequities in Neurological Disorders (HEADWAY) Workshop is to identify evidence-based interventions that are feasible and widely scalable, to diminish disparities and inequities in neurological disorders. This workshop will assemble subject matter experts in various disciplines from clinical and health services researchers, to implementation and behavioral scientists, along with community stakeholders and patient advocates.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 2, 2022).12aNervous System Diseases22aHealthcare Disparities22aHealth Status Disparities22aSocial Determinants of Health 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aNational Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4383440uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=43837  aAUTH02000cgm a2200397 i 9918592681206676DNLM20260227163326.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u230429s2023    mdu236|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32986  a(OCoLC)1451505615  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQY 952 aMini-Symposium on Spectral Flow Cytometryd(2023 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aIIG mini-symposium "spectral flow cytometry" /cNIH, FDA. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (3 hr., 56 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAccess restricted to HHS only.  aRecorded February 24, 2023.3 a(CIT): Our goal is to spur conversations about the technology, including best practice to design panels, new pipelines to process data and new prospects for immunology!  For that reason, this symposium will be for in-person participation only. Seminars will be recorded for archival and for scientists who cannot attend.  We are planning a combination of presentations (seminars and/or posters) from users of the technique in the NIH/FDA community, as well as updates from commercial suppliers.  Novice/curious scientists as well as expert users are encouraged to participate.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 22, 2024).12aFlow Cytometry 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=49221  aAUTH02148cgm a2200457 i 101635892DNLM20260227154654.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140627s2014    mdu745 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18508  a(DNLM)CIT:18509  a(OCoLC)888345074  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 8052 aNIAID/FDA Workshop on Common Barriers in Vaccine Research and Developmentd(2014 :cRockville, Md.)jauthor.10aNIAID/FDA - common barriers in vaccine research and development /cNIAID/FDA.1 aCommon barriers in vaccine research and development 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (12 hr., 25 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded June 19-20, 2014.3 a(CIT): This workshop seeks to gather vaccine developers, academic researchers, and government agencies to achieve two goals. The first is to identify gaps and barriers that impair vaccine development; the second is to identify and discuss novel solutions that may advance the field.  Areas to be discussed include: Lessons Learned from vaccine developers; Novel Approaches to Antigen Selection; Induction and Maintenance of Vaccine Memory; and Correlates of Protection Based on Natural History and Clinical Studies.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed July 27, 2014.12aBiomedical Research12aVaccines 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1850840uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18509  aAUTH01434cas a2200421 a 101590955DNLM20260227090924.0m     o  d        cr |||||||||||120524c20139999nyu|| poo     0    0eng    a  20122029170 a2167-9436l2167-943621  a(OCoLC)794233644  aDLCcDLCdDLCdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aISSN RECORD10aW110aMonoclon Antib Immunodiagn Immunother2dnlm00aMonoclonal antibodies in immunodiagnosis and immunotherapy.  aNew Rochelle, NY :bMary Ann Liebert, Inc.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Vol. 32, no. 1 (Feb. 2013).1 aMEDLINEbv32n1, Feb. 2013-0 aPubMedbv32n1, Feb. 2013-  aDescription based on: Vol. 32, no. 1 (Feb. 2013); title from PDF cover (publisher's Web site, viewed Feb. 22, 2013).  aLatest issue consulted: Vol 32, no. 1 (Feb. 2013) (viewed Feb. 22, 2013).12aAntibodies, Monoclonal22aImmunologic Tests22aImmunotherapy 2aPeriodical00tHybridoma (2005)x1554-0014w(DLC)   2004216384w(OCoLC)5731469840uhttp://online.liebertpub.com/loi/mab  aAUTH01577nam a2200445 i 9918986605506676DNLM20260225104758.0t|231107s2024    enka     b    001 0 eng    aGBC4077502bnb  a9780323958950qhardback  a0323958958  z9780323958967qePub ebook8 aCIPO000001203  a(OCoLC)1458046195  aUKMGBbengerdacUKMGBdOCLCOdYDXdOCLCO  apcc  9United Kingdom 4aQR185.6b.M653 202400aWH 22004a571.9673622300aMolecular biology of B cells.  aThird edition /bedited by Tasuku Honjo, Michael Reth, Andreas Radbruch, Frederick Alt, Alberto Martin. 1aLondon ;a[Amsterdam] :bAcademic Press,c[2024]  axviii, 529 pages :billustrations (color) ;c28 cm  atext2rdacontent  aunmediated2rdamedia  avolume2rdacarrier  aPrevious edition: edited by by Frederick W. Alt, Tasuku Honjo, Andreas Radbruch, Michael Reth. Amsterdam: Elsevier Academic Press, 2015.  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.12aB-Lymphocytesxcytology12aB-Lymphocytesximmunology22aCell Differentiationximmunology1 aHonjo, T.q(Tasuku),d1942-eeditor.1 aReth, Michael,eeditor.1 aRadbruch, A.q(Andreas),eeditor.1 aAlt, Frederick W.,eeditor.1 aMartin, Alberto,cPhD,eeditor.  aAUTH01026nam a2200313 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390200015000800200018000950350022001130400028001350420008001630440012001710500024001830600010002071300046002172450108002632640063003713000059004343360026004933370028005193380027005475040051005746500014006256500026006397000038006659990009007039918994993206676DNLM20260220080951.0230823s2024    enka     b    001 0 eng    a0443154910  a9780443154911  a(OCoLC)1394894969  aYDXbengerdacYDXdCDN  apcc  9England 4aRS201.L55bL57 202400aQU 930 aLiposomes in drug delivery (Antimisiaris)10aLiposomes in drug delivery :bwhat, where, how and when to deliver /cedited by Sophia G. Antimisiaris. 1aLondon, United Kingdom :bElsevier/Academic Press,c[2024]  axxxv, 660 pages :billustrations (some color) ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.12aLiposomes12aDrug Delivery Systems1 aAntimisiaris, Sophia G.,eeditor.  aAUTH01820cgm a22004214a 101486649DNLM20260227152621.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081007s2008    mdu098 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14669  a(OCoLC)280345182  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.55.A500aNIH extramural staff training on the animal events response toolkit /cNorka Ruiz Bravo ... [et al.].  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 19, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 37 min., 12 sec. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded Sept. 25, 2008.3 a(CIT): The NIH values the safety, security, and well-being of the researchers, animals, and institutions it funds. NIH-funded projects that use animal models are sometimes threatened or attacked by animal rights extremists. This training opportunity reviews a newly created set of resources and processes to help to the staff of the NIH Extramural Program give guidance aimed at preparing for and responding to threats and incidents against NIH-funded projects.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aAnimal Experimentation22aAnimal Rights22aCivil Disorders 2aWebcast1 aRuiz Bravo, Norka.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14669  aAUTH02215cgm a22004454a 101486630DNLM20260227152547.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081007s2008    mdu068 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14640  a(OCoLC)288982861  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5042 aNational Advisory Allergy and Infectious Diseases Council (U.S.).bMeetingd(2008 Sept. :cNational Institutes of Health)10aNational Advisory Allergy and Infectious Diseases Council, September 2008 /cAnthony Fauci and Kathryn Zoon.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Dec. 2, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 7 min., 36 sec. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Sept. 15, 2008.3 a(CIT): The NAAIDC provides second level peer review of grant applications and discusses concept clearances and other matters pertaining to grant applications for NIAID.  At each meeting, Dr. Antony Fauci, NIAID Director, presents information about the Institute, including new staff, status of the budget, status of research and other areas of interest to the council.  A guest speaker also does a presentation.  At the September 2008 meeting it will be Dr. Kathryn Zoon, Director of NIAID's Division of Intramural Research.  Dr. Zoon will present information about the division and its research.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (U.S.)12aHypersensitivity22aCommunicable Diseases22aUnited States Government Agenciesxorganization & administration 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast1 aFauci, Anthony S.,d1940-40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14640  aAUTH01507cgm a2200457 a 101510701DNLM20260227153000.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090710s2009    mdu067|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15164  a(OCoLC)720559167  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 55.61 aLindquist, Susan.10aHSF and the balancing act between neurodegeneration and cancer /cSusan Lindquist.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH Director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 7 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 10, 2009.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aHeat-Shock Proteinsxmetabolism22aNeoplasmsxetiology22aNeurodegenerative Diseasesxetiology22aProtein Folding22aProteostasis Deficienciesxgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15164  aAUTH01435nam a2200361 i 101778756DNLM20260220123213.0210503s1897    nyu           000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5573 no.241 aStuart, Francis H.q(Francis Hart),d1846-1910,eauthor.10aReport of the Obstetric Department, Brooklyn Hospital :bLow Maternity and Gynecological Clinic, for year 1896 /cby Francis H. Stuart. 1aBrooklyn :bEagle Book and Job Printing Department,c1897.  a23 pages ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM22aBrooklyn Hospital.bLow Maternity.22aBrooklyn Hospital.bGynecological Clinic.12aObstetrics and Gynecology Department, Hospital12aLabor, Obstetric22aPregnancy Complications22aGenital Diseases, Female 2aNew York City 2aCase Reports2 aBrooklyn Hospital.bObstetric Department,eissuing body.  aAUTH03193cgm a22004694a 101486624DNLM20260227152634.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u081007s2008    mdu061 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14634  a(OCoLC)301878344  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 1411 aSchlessinger, Joseph.10aCell signaling by receptor tyrosine kinases :bfrom basic principles to cancer therapy /cJoseph Schlessinger.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 19, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 54 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Sept. 10, 2008.3 a(CIT): Receptor tyrosine kinases (RTKs) comprise a large family of cell surface receptors that control many critical cellular processes.  Various human diseases are caused by dysfunction in RTKs or in their intracellular signaling pathways.  Stem Cell Factor (SCF) initiates its multiple cellular responses by binding to the extracellular region of the RTK KIT, resulting in receptor dimerization and tyrosine kinase activation.   We have determined the crystal structure of the entire extracellular region of KIT before and after SCF stimulation.  The structures show that Kit dimerization is driven by SCF binding whose sole role is to bring two KIT molecules together.  Receptor dimerization is followed by conformational changes that enable lateral interactions between membrane proximal Ig-like domains D4 and D5 of two KIT molecules.  Experiments with cultured cells show that KIT activation is compromised by point mutations in amino acids critical for D4-D4 interaction.  Moreover, a variety of oncogenic mutations are mapped to the D5-D5 interface.  Since key hallmarks of KIT structures, ligand-induced receptor dimerization and the critical residues in the D4-D4 interface are conserved in other receptors, the mechanism of KIT stimulation underlies the mechanism of activation of many RTKs.  Sutent/SU11248 is a new drug that blocks the actions of KIT, PDGF receptor, VEGF receptor and RET.  Sutent has been approved by the FDA for the treatment of gastrointestinal stromal tumors (GIST) and for advanced kidney cancers.  The approval marks the first time the FDA has approved a new oncology product for two indications simultaneously.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aReceptor Protein-Tyrosine Kinases22aAntineoplastic Agents22aDimerization22aSignal Transduction22aStem Cell Factor 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14634  aAUTH01447cgm a22003974a 101267705DNLM20260227145612.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu029 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10582  a(OCoLC)70051252  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4751 aLevine, Robertq(Robert Louis)10aMutagenesis induced by endogenous DNA adducts in human cells /cRobert Levine.1 iTitle on PowerPoint screen:aMutagenesis induced by endogenously derived DNA 