cebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 27, issue 1 (2016).1 aMEDLINEbv27n1, 2016-0 aPubMedbv27n1, 2016-  aNumbering from contents page.  aCompanion journal to: Mitochondrial DNA Part B.  aDescription based on: Volume 27, issue 1 (2016); title from journal home page (publisher's Web site, viewed Sept. 7, 2016).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 27, issue 5 (2016) (viewed Sept. 7, 2016).12aDNA, Mitochondrial22aSequence Analysis, DNA 2aPeriodical0 x2470-139401tMitochondrial DNAx1940-1736w(DLC)   2007213338w(OCoLC)17330086808iComplemented by (work):tMitochondrial DNA. Part B, Resourcesx2380-2359w(DLC)   2015202243w(OCoLC)91435572840uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/imdn  aAUTH02616cgm a2200445 i 101678424DNLM20260227155502.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160303s2016    mdu057|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19514  a(OCoLC)945749653  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 5600aRNA-dependent chromatin insulator function /cElissa Lei. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (57 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's seminar series  aRecorded February 26, 2016.3 a(CIT): A central question in cell biology is how a single genome can give rise to many different cell types with specialized functions and properties. It has become increasingly apparent that proper control of gene expression requires complex organization of DNA at the level of chromatin. Chromatin insulators are DNA-protein complexes that influence gene expression by establishing chromatin domains subject to distinct transcriptional controls, likely through alteration of their spatial arrangement within the nucleus. Our work has focused on elucidating mechanisms of chromatin insulator function and regulation. Using a combination of genetics, biochemistry, cell biology, and high throughput sequencing approaches in the highly tractable model organism Drosophila melanogaster, we have uncovered a noncoding function by which certain mRNAs contribute to chromatin insulator activity. Moreover, our findings have revealed roles for RNA-binding proteins in mediating tissue-specific regulation of chromatin insulators. This lecture will focus on the discovery of these novel paradigms and explore the new questions raised by these insights.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed March 20, 2016).12aChromatinxgenetics22aDrosophila melanogasterxgenetics22aInsulator Elementsxgenetics22aRNA 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aLei, Elissa,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19514  aAUTH01432cgm a22004454a 101289599DNLM20260227151347.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u061011s2006    mdu061|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13044  a(OCoLC)84671866  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 1651 aBrent, David A.10aSuicidal risk in adolescents :bassessment and treatment /cDavid Brent.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2006]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aGreat teachers  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Nov. 27, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 1 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 8, 2006.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aSuicidexpsychology22aAdolescent Behavior22aPsychology, Adolescent22aRisk Assessmentxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center. 0aClinical Center grand rounds great teachers series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13044  aAUTH01683cgm a22004694a 101514415DNLM20260227153151.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090902s2009    mdu062|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15240  a(OCoLC)463631306  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQV 7361 aDouglas, Frank L.10aIs the pharmaceutical industry ready for personalized medicine? /cFrank L. Douglas.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aClinical Center grand rounds  aTitle from title screen (viewed Oct. 27, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 2 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Aug. 5, 2009.3 a(CIT): Clinical Center Grand Rounds  Frank L. Douglas, PhD, MD   Senior Fellow   Ewing Marion Kauffman Foundation    For more information, visit  http://www.cc.nih.gov/about/news/grcurrent.html.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aDrug Industryxeconomics22aDrug Therapyxeconomics22aGenetics, Medicalxeconomics22aHealth Policy 2aUnited States 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center. 0aClinical Center grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15240  aAUTH03216cgm a2200421 i 101710194DNLM20260227155744.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u170727s2017    mdu076|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:23400  a(OCoLC)1000303758  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQZ 20200aRe-visiting the causes of cancer /cSir Richard Peto. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2017]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 16 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aAnnual advances in cancer prevention lecture ;v2017  aRecorded July 20, 2017.3 a(CIT): The Annual Advances in Cancer Prevention Lecture is sponsored by the Cancer Prevention Fellowship Program (CPFP) within the Division of Cancer Prevention (DCP) at NCI.  It is also part of the course offerings of the annual NCI Summer Curriculum in Cancer Prevention , but is open to the entire NIH community and general public.  This annual lecture addresses current challenges and/or approaches used by investigators to address gaps, advance science, and promote the application of successful strategies in the field of cancer prevention and control.  Sir Richard Peto, FRS, will be presenting the 2017 annual lecture on re-visiting the causes of cancer.  Sir Richard Peto, FRS, is Professor of Medical Statistics and Epidemiology at the University of Oxford, UK, and co-director of the Clinical Trial Service Unit and Epidemiological Studies Unit (CTSU). He collaborated for decades with Richard Doll on cancer epidemiology, and in 1981 they co-authored an influential report, "The Causes of Cancer - Quantitative estimates of avoidable risks of cancer in the US today" (JNCI 66:1191). Since then, his close attention to the details of how epidemiological evidence and trial evidence is interpreted has shown that the importance of factors such as tobacco, blood pressure and cholesterol have been much underestimated: we actually know more than we thought we did about major avoidable causes of cancer and of vascular disease.  In 1989, Peto was made a Fellow of the Royal Society of London for introducing meta-analyses of randomised trials, was knighted by Queen Elizabeth in 1999 for services to epidemiology, and received in 2010 and 2011 the Cancer Research UK and the BMJ Lifetime Achievement Award.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed July 28, 2017).12aNeoplasmsxetiology22aNeoplasmsxprevention & control 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aPeto, Richard,d1943-espeaker.2 aCancer Prevention Fellowship Program (National Cancer Institute),esponsoring body. 0aAnnual advances in cancer prevention lecture ;v2017.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?23400  aAUTH01870cgm a22004574a 101268713DNLM20260227150947.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2005    mdu057 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12498  a(OCoLC)157002496  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2411 aHeath, James R.10aNano systems biology and cancer /cJames R. Heath.1 iTitle on title screen:aNanosystems biology & cancer  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 9, 2007).  aStreaming video (56 min., 9 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 8, 2005.3 a(CIT): Systems biology and predictive and preventive medicine are both data driven and, accordingly, both require new tools for making large numbers of measurements.  Microfluidics, chemical, and nanotechnologies will revolutionize our ability to generate comprehensive data sets that span from individual cells to patients, and will allow us to build multiparameter analysis tools for cancer diagnosis and molecular imaging probes for spatially localizing specific cancers.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxdiagnosis22aBiomarkers, Tumor.22aMolecular Probes22aNanostructures22aNanotechnology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?12498  aAUTH01444cgm a22004454a 101268750DNLM20260227151043.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2005    mdu061 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:12566  a(OCoLC)171258043  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWW 2701 aFine, Stuart L.,d1942-10aAge-related macular degeneration :bscourge of elderly, hope for future /cStuart L. Fine.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2005]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aGreat teachers  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Aug. 7, 2007