an, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.10aWilliam Harvey and the experimental method /cby William Bennett Bean, M.D. 1a[Medford, Massachusetts?] :b[publisher not identified],c[1959?]  a8 unnumbered pages :billustrations ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCover title.  a"Reprinted from the Pharos, January 1959, pages 35 to 40."  aThe Pharos contained a reprint of the article from the A.M.A. archives vol. 102, July 1958.  aNLM copy, bound 3rd in a volume of 99 pamphlets.5DNLM  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aHarvey, William,d1578-1657.12aBiomedical Researchxmethods0 aPharos.  aAUTH01157cas a2200385 a 101469708DNLM20260227090921.0cr|cn ||||||||070228c20089999cauar         0    0eng    a  20072151020 a1936-122Xl1936-122X  a(OCoLC)85449594  aNSDcNSDdNSDdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  9United States00aW1bAN77EF10aAnnu Rev Biophys2dnlm00aAnnual review of biophysics.  aPalo Alto, CA :bAnnual Reviews  av.  aAnnual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 37 (2008).  aDescription based on first issue; title from title page.1 aMEDLINEbv37, 2007-0 aPubMedbv37, 2007-  aAlso issued online.12aBiophysics0 x1936-123800tAnnual review of biophysics and biomolecular structurex1056-8700w(DLC)   92656233w(OCoLC)238844964 uhttp://www.annualreviews.org/loi/biophys  aAUTH05480cgm a2200433 i 101660626DNLM20260227155155.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150602s2015    mdu053|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18987  a(OCoLC)911194477  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWY 1561 aBarton-Burke, Margaret,eauthor.10aImproving outcomes, quality, and experiences of care :bthe critical role of the oncology nurse in advancing evidence and transforming care /cMargaret Barton-Burke. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (53 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNCI Center for Cancer Research grand rounds  aAccess restricted to HHS only.5DNLM  aRecorded May 8, 2015.3 a(CIT): NCI Center for Cancer Research Grand Rounds  Dr. Barton-Burke received her B.S. in Nursing from William Paterson College, her M.S. in Teaching Specialty & Oncology Nursing from Boston University and her Ph.D. from the University of Rhode Island. She spent 21 years in the Army Nurse Corps Reserve Component, retiring from the Massachusetts Army National Guard as Deputy Commander. She has held various elected positions within the Oncology Nursing Society ONS)  including president at the local chapter level, member of ONS national committees including Annual Congress Chairperson in 1993, formerly as Director-at-Large, and currently as President. She is often at the cusp of issues and challenges as they emerge in oncology nursing, and specifically has identified important areas that need to be addressed like cancer chemotherapy education for nurses, cancer-related fatigue initiative, and socio-cultural aspects of cancer care. She has authored books and chapters on cancer chemotherapy for nurses beginning with her first AJN-award winning book in 1991 when there was a dearth in textbooks that related to the specialty of cancer care, specifically chemotherapy and nursing care of the person receiving cancer chemotherapy. Dr. Barton-Burke is a recognized expert in cancer chemotherapy and was a member of the ONS Task Force that developed the ONS Chemotherapy and Biotherapy Course, the standard for administration, safe handling, and care of persons with cancer, which is now a standard across the United States with thousands of nurses taking the ONS Chemotherapy and Biotherapy course. She was instrumental in visioning, funding, and hosting the first ONS State of the Science meeting on fatigue and cancer, which then became the ONS FIRE (Fatigue Initiative through Research and Education(r)) Initiative. This initial work brought attention to a previously unidentified and untreated side effect of cancer treatment that is identified in the majority of persons with cancer. Her research and published body of work identified that this symptom continues into cancer survivorship, when the disease is essentially non-existent. Dr. Barton Burke"s program of research began before the IOM studies on survivorship and health disparities finding that black women who are diagnosed with, treated for, and survive breast cancer have a different experience and health-related quality of life than their white counterparts. Her current research focuses on culturally-appropriate interventions, outreach, and programs for the community of color. Her funded research is in the area of culture and health disparities focusing specifically on black women breast cancer survivors. She works with the next generation of nurse scientists combining their interests with her research sharing authorship and grantsmanship. The issue of Seminars in Oncology Nursing that she edited was written by a group of multidisciplinary including a reverend, a physician, a lawyer and nurses and covers such topics as racism, vulnerable populations, fatalism revisited, gero-oncology as a health disparity, and recruitment into clinical trials and research studies for invisible populations. This publication was groundbreaking in presenting alternatives to conventional thinking about health disparities. Her research in the United States led her to be invited faculty for a methodology workshop for the African Doctoral Dissertation Research Fellowship Awards sponsored by the African Population and Health Research Centre (APHRC) and the International Development Research Centre (IDRC), Nairobi, Kenya. The fellowship program is intended to build research capacity in sub-Sharan Africa. More than 155 ADDR Fellows have completed this fellowship since its inception in 2008.  For more information go to <a href=https://ccrod.cancer.gov/ccrlectures/>https://ccrod.cancer.gov/ccrlectures.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed June 12, 2015).12aOncology Nursingxmethods22aNeoplasmsxnursing 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aCenter for Cancer Research (National Cancer Institute (U.S.)),esponsoring body. 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18987  aAUTH01252cas a2200445   7610751DNLM20260227090920.0cr|cn#||||||||760924c19769999xxuqr         0||| 0eng    a0363-7751l0363-7751  aCOMODN  a(DNLM)C32565000(s)  a(OCoLC)02313630  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bCO427310aCommun Monogr2dnlm 0aCommunication monographs00aCommunication monographs.  aFalls Church, Va.bSpeech Communication Assn.  av.bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 43-    Mar. 1976-0 aMEDLINEbSelected citations only0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aAlso issued online.  aContinues Speech monographs. 2aCommunication 2aSpeech 2aSpeech Disorders 2aPeriodical2 aSpeech Communication Association.0 x1479-578710tSpeech monographs4 uhttps://www.tandfonline.com/loi/rcmm20  aAUTH02380cgm a2200445 i 101776730DNLM20260227160753.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u210302s2021    mdu171|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31541  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWS 12500aBreastmilk Ecology: Genesis of Infant Nutrition (BEGIN) meeting series.nWorking group 4,pIntegration and application /cNICHD, NIH.1 aIntegration and application 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2021]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (2 hr., 51 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded February 5, 2021.3 a(CIT): A deeper understanding of the biology of human milk is essential to address ongoing and emerging questions about infant feeding practices. Human milk is a complex biological system, a matrix of many interacting parts, that is more than the sum of those parts. Its production needs to be studied as an ecology that consists of inputs from the mother, her breastfeeding baby, and their respective environments. The Breastmilk Ecology: Genesis of Infant Nutrition (BEGIN) meeting series is designed to examine this ecology, its functional implications for both mother and infant. The goal is to support the research community's efforts to ensure safe, efficacious, and context-specific infant feeding practices in the United States and globally.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed April 27, 2021).22aBEGIN (Collaborative Group)12aMilk, Humanxphysiology22aBreast Feeding22aEcological and Environmental Phenomena22aMother-Child Relations22aInfant Nutritional Physiological Phenomena22aResearch Design 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aEunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40153  aAUTH02600cgm a22004934a 101267349DNLM20260227144900.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu843 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:9941  a(DNLM)CIT:9942  a(DNLM)CIT:9943  a(OCoLC)191875343  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWG 12000aCardiovascular health for all /cNHLBI ... [et al.].  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Jan. 23, 2008).  aStreaming video (14 hr., 3 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 11-13, 2002.3 a(CIT): Cardiovascular Health for All: Meeting the Challenge of Healthy People 2010 - A National Conference  This conference will address the cardiovascular challenges set forth in Healthy People 2010 as well as the overarching goals of increasing years of healthy life and of eliminating health disparities.  The conference is sponsored by the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (NHLBI) in collaboration with the DHHS Office of Disease Prevention and Health Promotion, American Heart Association, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services, and Health Resources and Services Administration.  This conference targets a variety of health professionals who want to be at the forefront of meeting the challenge of Healthy People 2010 including practicing physicians, internists, nurses, dietitians, health educators, and community health specialists.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.22aHealthy People 2010 (Group)12aCardiovascular Diseasesxtherapy22aCardiovascular Diseasesxprevention & control22aHealth Education22aHealth Status Disparities 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?994140uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?994240uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?9943  aAUTH01438cas a2200433 a 101601065DNLM20260227090917.0m     o  d        cr||||||||||||120719c20139999txuqr poo     |    0eng    a  2012200380  a2168-8370l2168-8362  a(OCoLC)800704348  aDLCbengcDLCdOCLCQdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW1bTI82810aTissue Barriers2dnlm00aTissue barriers.  a[Austin, TX ] :bLandes Bioscience313Oct. 2014- :aAbingdon, Oxfordshire, OX :bTaylor & Francis  aFour times a year  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Vol. 1, issue 1 (Jan./Feb./Mar. 2013).1 aMEDLINEbv4n1, Jan-Mar 2016-0 aPubMedbv1, 2013-0 aPMC  aDescription based on: Vol. 1, issue 1 (Jan./Feb./Mar. 2013); title from PDF cover (publisher's Web site, viewed Sept. 3, 2013).  aLatest issue consulted: 1:3 (July/Aug./Sept. 2013) (viewed Sept. 3, 2013).12aBiological Transport22aTissues 2aPeriodical0 x2168-83624 uhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/journals/?term=10160106540uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/loi/ktib20  aAUTH02046cgm a2200433 a 101601861DNLM20260227154504.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u130208s2013    mdu110|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:17760  a(OCoLC)828176181  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 5361 aCoffin, John M.10aDemystifying medicine, 2013.pHepatitis C and HIV :bthe Borghia effect /cJohn Coffin, Shyamasundaran Kottilil.1 aHepatitis C and HIV  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2013]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 50 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded Jan. 22, 2013.3 a(CIT): The 2013 Demystifying Medicine Series, which is jointly sponsored by FAES and NIH, will begin January 8th and includes the presentation of patients, pathology, diagnosis and therapy in the context of major disease problems and current research. Primarily directed toward Ph.D. students, clinicians and program managers, the course is designed to help bridge the gap between advances in biology and their application to major human diseases. Each session includes clinical and basic science components presented by NIH staff and invitees. All students, fellows and staff are welcome, as well.For more information go to <a href='http://demystifyingmedicine.od.nih.gov/'>http://demystifyingmedicine.od.nih.gov/.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aHepatitis B12aHepatitis C22aComorbidity22aHIV