sis in 3D epithelial cultures /cOffice of Research Services, Medical Arts and Photography Branch.  aBethesda, MD :bNational Institutes of Health,c[2004]  a1 videocassette of 1 (DVCPRO) (67 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/4 in.3master.  a1 videocassette of 1 (Betacam SP) (68 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (67 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aThe NIH director's Wednesday P.M. lecture  aTitle from cassette spine label.  aSpeaker, Joan S. Brugge.  aOriginally presented Sept. 15, 2004.  aHighlights the use of an in vitro 3-dimensional model in mammary epithelial cells to probe the biological activities of candidate oncogenes that are associated with human breast tumors and the mechanistic insights discerned to date.  aVideocassette (Betacam SP)bBethesda, Md. :cNational Library of Medicine,d2011.e1 videocassette (68 min.) : sd., col., 1/2 in.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM  aClosed-captioned.12aEpithelial Cellsxpathology22aBreast Neoplasmsxpathology22aOncogenes22aSignal Transduction22aCell Culture Techniques1 aBrugge, Joan.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aNIH director's Wednesday afternoon lecture series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=3424  aAUTH01115cas a22003734a 101168447DNLM20260227090923.0cr|cn ||||||||030224c20009999gw qr p       0    0ger    a  2003243645  a1439-5142l1439-51422z  a(OCoLC)53801274  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 agerbeng  alcd  9Germany00aW1bBA4900aBalint-Journal :bZeitschrift der Deutschen Balint-Gesellschaft.  aStuttgart :bThieme  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan in 2000.  aDescription based on: 4. Jahrg. 2 (Juni 2003); title from cover.  aAlso issued online.  aIn German; summaries also in English.12aPsychoanalytic Therapy 2aPeriodical2 aDeutsche Balintgesellschaft.4 uhttps://www.thieme-connect.de/products/ejournals/journal/10.1055/s-00000114  aAUTH02110cgm a22004214a 101507210DNLM20260227152913.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090605s2009    mdu064|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15110  a(OCoLC)416213564  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWC 1951 aKaech, Susan.10aPathways of memory CD8 T cell development during infection /cSusan Kaech.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed June 18, 2009).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 4 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 20, 2009.3 a(CIT): Susan Kaech will be visiting NIH on May 20th as our speaker for the IIG seminar series.  As most of you probably know, Sue has been at the forefront of characterizing the CD8+ T cell response to viral infections  both as a post-doc in Rafi Ahmed's lab and since starting her own group at Yale in 2004. She has recently focused on finding ways of discriminating short-lived effector T cells from the precursors of  long-lasting  memory T cells in response to model pathogens. Recent publications from her lab include these: Expression of IL-7R alpha is necessary but not sufficient for the formation of memory CD8 T cells during viral infection http://www.cell.com/immunity/retrieve/pii/S1074761307003718  For more information, visit  The Immunology Interest Group.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aInfectionsximmunology22aCD8-Positive T-Lymphocytesximmunology22aImmunologic Memory 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bImmunology Interest Group.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15110  aAUTH01900cgm a22004574a 101478248DNLM202602271