9, 2012.3 a(CIT): Neuroscience Seminar Series  Dr. Fawcett's Lab work concerns the role of extracellular matrix proteoglycans in the central nervous system. In the damaged CNS, they are trying to understand how axonal growth is inhibited by the increased expression of inhibitory proteoglycans, and how to overcome this inhibition. They work on how to increase the intrinsic regenerative potential of axons, using integrin engineering and local protein translation. In addition, plasticity is turned off in adults by extracellular matrix structures called perineuronal nets. They work on the actions of these structures, and methods to modulate their function so as to improve functional recovery and memory in the adult CNS.       For more information go to <a href='http://neuroseries.info.nih.gov'>http://neuroseries.info.nih.gov.  aClosed-captioned.  aTitle from title screen.12aNerve Regenerationxphysiology22aExtracellular Matrixxphysiology22aNeuronal Plasticityxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?17175  aAUTH01435cgm a22004094a 101268243DNLM20260227150239.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu085 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11569  a(OCoLC)219253563  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4701 aOuellette, B. F. Francis.10aBiological databases :bdefining and building /cFrancis Ouellette.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 12, 2008).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 24 min., 26 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Apr. 16, 2002.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aDatabases, Nucleic Acid.12aDatabases, Nucleic Acidxclassification22aComputational Biology 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11569  aAUTH01651cgm a22004094a 101469019DNLM20260227152411.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u080317s2008    mdu059|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:14287  a(OCoLC)236051937  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQW 5701 aSpringer, Timothy A.10aBidirectional signal transmission in integrins /cTimothy Springer.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2008]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 2, 2008).  aStreaming video (59 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Feb. 6, 2008.3 a(CIT): Tim has been seeking to understand, at the atomic level in three dimensions, such conformational changes of the adhesion molecules and the resulting signal transmission events across the membrane bilayer. The Springer lab found inherited defects of integrins in leukocyte adhesion deficiency, ICAM-1 as the cellular receptor for rhinovirus, and SDF-1 as the natural ligand for the HIV coreceptor CXCR4.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aIntegrins22aCell Movement22aSignal Transduction 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?14287  aAUTH01401cas a2200469 a 9209844DNLM20260227091020.0cr|cn ||||||||920625c19929999xxuqr p       0||| 0eng    a1058-0476l1058-0476  aJFEIEE  a(DNLM)SR0073933(s)  a(OCoLC)24203047  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bJO6443G10aJ Fam Econ Issues2dnlm 0aJournal of family and economic issues00aJournal of family and economic issues.  aNew York :bHuman Sciences Press,cc1992-3 31999- :aNew York, NY :bKluwer Academic/Plenum Publishers  av. :bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2r