ering.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed October 8, 2014.22aNational Institute on Deafness and Other Communication Disorders (U.S.)12aStuttering 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute on Deafness and Other Communication Disorders (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNIDCD speaker series 0aBeyond the lab, understanding communication disorders speaker series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18604  aAUTH01846cgm a22004214a 101267748DNLM20260227145455.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu048 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10627  a(OCoLC)69391327  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWD 200.5.M61 aScaglia, Fernando.10aMitochondrial porin deficient mice :ba murine model of Mendelian respiratory chain defects and encephalopathy /cFernando Scaglia.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 26, 2006).  aStreaming video (47 min., 39 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 13, 2003.3 a(CIT): The Mitochondria Interest Group (MIG) is an Inter-Institute Interest Group at the National Institutes of Health (NIH), with members from NIH and from around the world! MIG is concerned with all aspects of the mitochondrion and diseases in which the mitochondrion is involved.   For more information, visit the  Mitochondria Interest Group (MIG).  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aMitochondrial Diseases22aMice, Knockout22aVoltage-Dependent Anion Channelsxdeficiency 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10627  aAUTH02395cas a22006011  2985179RDNLM20260227090910.0cr|cn#||||||||hd|bfa012baap850512c19209999enkbr p 