stitutes of Health,c2003]  a2 videocassettes of 2 (Betacam SP) (2 hrs., 11 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3duplication copy.  a1 videocassette of 1 (VHS) (2 hrs., 11 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.3viewing copy.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aORWH women's health seminar series  aSpeakers, Connie M. Weaver, Deborah E. Sellmeyer.  aPresented December 9, 2003.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.5DNLM12aOsteoporosisxprevention & control12aOsteoporosisxtherapy22aCalciumxtherapeutic use22aExercise1 aWeaver, Connie,d1950-2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bMedical Arts and Photography Branch. 0aORWH women's health seminar series4 uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=2478  aAUTH03178cgm a2200433 a 101535271DNLM20260227153452.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u100702s2010    mdu057|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15947  a(OCoLC)682070806  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWV 2721 aRicci, Anthony.10aLocalizing mechanotransducer channels to the tips of auditory hair cell stereocilia /cAnthony Ricci.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (57 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded June 14, 2010.3 a(CIT): The auditory system is a remarkable feat of engineering capable of detecting motion at the atomic level and transmitting this information to the brain with precise timing and fidelity. Dr. Ricci's lab uses advanced electrophysiologic, imaging, molecular and pharmacologic techniques to probe mechanisms of mechanotransduction and synaptic transmission at the auditory periphery. There are several independent lines of research in their laboratory.   Mechanotransduction, the conversion of mechanical stimulation into an electrical signal, is complex and involves a variety of proteins, many of which have not yet been identified. A major goal of their laboratory is to delineate the functional relevance of mechanotransduction and to identify proteins and their function in this process. To date, they have identified and characterized the tuning properties of the sensory hair bundle and mechanotransducer channels, identifying at least two new physiologically relevant contributions of these channels. They have performed the only single channel study of mechanotransducer channels, demonstrating tonotopic variations in the intrinsic channel properties. They have also performed the only kinetic analysis of activation, again demonstrating tonotopic variations in the kinetics of the mechanotransduction channel. In addition, they have pharmacologically characterized and biophysically mapped the transducer channel pore.   Recently Dr. Ricci's lab has developed a high speed confocal imaging system that will allow them to optically monitor calcium changes associated with mechanotransduction, allowing them to localize the site of mechanotransduction and directly investigate mechanisms of calcium, regulation.   <A HREF= "http://neuroscience.info.nih.gov">NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aMechanotransduction, Cellularxphysiology22aHair Cells, Auditoryxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15947  aAUTH02342cgm a2200457 i 101690013DNLM20260227155635.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160804s2016    mdu399 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19808  a(DNLM)CIT:19809  a(OCoLC)956966294  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.52 aBest Practices of Biomedical Research: Improving Reproducibility and Transparency of Preclinical Research (Conference)d(2016 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aBest Practices of Biomedical Research :bImproving Reproducibility and Transparency of Preclinical Research /cThe National Library of Medicine, Research!America, and the Friends of the National Library of Medicine. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (6 hr., 39 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 9&10, 2016.3 a(CIT): Reproducibility of biomedical research, which is the ability to conduct projects that lead to the same results multiple times, was the focus of this conference, featuring the nation's leading experts. The conference highlights not only the need to improve the rigor and reproducibility of preclinical research, but also the best practices that can make the 21st century biomedical research more precise and more productive.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed August 15, 2016).12aBiomedical Research22aEvaluation Studies as Topic22aReproducibility of Results 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aResearch!America (Organization),esponsoring body.2 aNational Library of Medicine (U.S.).bFriends,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1980840uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19809  aAUTH01743cgm a2200421 i 101654005DNLM20260227154849.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u150314s2015    mdu750 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18846  a(DNLM)CIT:18859  a(OCoLC)907941324  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW 20.52 aNIH Workshop on Building a Precision Medicine Research Cohortd(2015 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aBuilding a large U.S. cohort for precision medicine research /cNational Institutes of Health. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2015]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (12 hr., 30 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Feb. 11-12, 2015.3 a(CIT): On February 11-12, NIH will host a workshop to discuss the opportunities and challenges around building a large research cohort focused on precision medicine. We look forward to hearing from leading experts from many disciplines and sectors.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on version viewed April 15, 2015.12aBiomedical Research 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1884640uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18859  aAUTH02306cgm a2200445 i 101774292DNLM20260227160503.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu584 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31408  a(DNLM)CIT:31414  a(OCoLC)1246807670  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 5062 aNIH COVID-19 Research Workshopd(2020 :cOnline).eFlash Talk Sessionsjauthor.10aNIH COVID-19 Research Workshop :bFlash Talk Sessions /cNCI, NIH and FDA. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (9 hr., 44 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAccess restricted to NIH only.  aRecorded October 29, 2020.3 a(CIT): Last year brought unprecedented changes to the world with the COVID-19 pandemic. In response to this challenge, the scientific community has mobilized on many fronts with advances in vaccines, diagnostics, treatments, pathology, immunology, virology, structural biology, cell biology, and many other areas.  To help facilitate interactions and highlight work from NIH/FDA investigators, the NIH COVID-19 scientific interest group is hosting a virtual workshop for NIH/FDA staff on Thursday, October 29, and Friday, October 30, 2020, from 11:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. We look forward to your active participation to make this workshop a success during this challenging time.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 19, 2021).12aCOVID-19 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aUnited States.bFood and Drug Administration,eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4016740uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40168  aAUTH02270cgm a2200469 i 9918384669706676DNLM20260227162128.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220426s2022    mdu332|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32270  a(DNLM)CIT:32273  a(OCoLC)1347358687  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 3562 aBrain Attack Coalition Symposium "Inequities in Access and Delivery of Acute Stroke Care"d(2022 :cOnline)jauthor.10aBrain attack coalition symposium "inequities in access and delivery of acute stroke care" /cNINDS, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (5 hr., 32 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded March 17 & 18, 2022.3 a(CIT): The Inequities in Access and Delivery of Acute Stroke Care Symposium is a day and a half virtual event during which diverse teams of public health and stroke experts will share findings on equity issues, known and unknown causes or obstacles, and best practices or possible solutions concerning stroke care. The Symposium encourages the participation, feedback, and new ideas from interested stakeholders and providers in the broader stroke community.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed Oct. 7, 2022).12aStroke Rehabilitation22aHealth Inequities22aSocial Determinants of Health22aHealth Services Accessibility 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body.2 aBrain Attack Coalition,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=4473340uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44735  aAUTH02602cgm a22005294a 101267455DNLM20260227145219.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu000 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10069  a(DNLM)CIT:10070  a(DNLM)CIT:10071  a(OCoLC)70119546  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 4752 aProtein Production and Crystallization Workshopd(2003 :cBethesda, Md.)10aProtein structure initiative /cNational Institute of General Medical Sciences ; Protein Production and Crystallization Workshop, April 9-11, 2003.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from PowerPoint screen (viewed on June 9, 2006).  aStreaming video (17 hr., 34 min., 7 sec. : sd., col.).  aAt head of title: NIGMS Protein Structure Initiative.0 aDay 1 -- Day 2 -- Day 3.  aRecorded Apr. 9-11, 2003.3 a(CIT): This workshop follows on the successful March 2002 NIGMS Protein Structure Initiative (PSI) Workshop in Protein Production. The aim of the workshop is to foster interactions and to share information regarding protein production and crystallization between scientists involved in the NIH-funded Protein Structure Initiative, and between the PSI group and members of the international structural genomics community.    This workshop follows on the successful March 2002 NIGMS Protein Structure Initiative (PSI) Workshop in Protein Production.   Sponsored by the  NIGMS Protein Structure Initiative.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aProtein Biosynthesis22aCloning, Molecularxmethods22aCrystallization22aProtein Conformation22aProteinsxisolation & purification 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of General Medical Sciences (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1006940uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?1007040uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10071  aAUTH01651cgm a22004574a 101268155DNLM20260227145343.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu658 ||||||f|   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11465  a(DNLM)CIT:11466  a(OCoLC)1019841252  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2062 aNCI Board of Scientific Advisors.bMeetingn(14th :b2000 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNCI Board of Scientific Advisors /csponsored by the National Cancer Institute.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 9, 2007).  aStreaming video (10 hr., 57 min., 5 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 23-24, 2000.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Cancer Institute (U.S.)12aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration12aNeoplasms22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=23440uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=235  aAUTH01195cas a2200409 a 101468590DNLM20260227090917.0cr|cn ||||||||080313c20089999nyu|| p       |    0eng    a  2008243701  a1