LCAdDNLM  an-us-ny  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5573 no.302 aAlbany Hospital.10aBy-laws, rules and regulations of the Albany Hospital :band a list of governors and officers : with the act of incorporation.  aAlbany :bJoel Munsell,c1851.  a33 pages ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM22aAlbany Hospital.12aHospitals, Privatexorganization & administration12aConstitution and Bylaws 2aNew York  aAUTH01195cam a2200337 i 9918787483306676DNLM20260213174635.0240328r19561956ilu      b    000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdad20260212dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aW7bB367c v.3 no.491 aChapman, John S.q(John Stephen),d1927-2012,eauthor.10aIatrogenic heatstroke /cJohn Chapman and William B. Bean. 1a[Chicago, Illinois] :bAmerican Medical Association,c[1956]  apages 1375-1377 ;c28 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title.  a"Reprinted from the Journal of the American Medical Association, Aug. 4, 1956, vol. 161."  aNLM copy, bound 49th in a volume of 92 pamphlets.5DNLM  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aHeat Strokexetiology22aIatrogenic Disease 2aCase Reports1 aBean, William B.q(William Bennett),d1909-1989,eauthor.2 aAmerican Medical Association.0 aJAMA.  aAUTH03674cgm a2200469 i 9918768585706676DNLM20260227164403.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u240209s2024    mdu557|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33728  a(DNLM)CIT:33731  a(OCoLC)1504552603  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 3542 aNINDS Traumatic Brain Injury (TBI) Classification and Nomenclature Workshopd(2024 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aTraumatic brain injury (TBI) classification and nomenclature workshop /cNational Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (NINDS). 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2024]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (9 hr., 17 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded January 22 & 23, 2024.3 a(CIT): The National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (NINDS) is planning a Traumatic Brain Injury (TBI) Classification and Nomenclature Workshop to be held January 22 - 23, 2024, at the National Institutes of Health (NIH) in Bethesda, MD. Under the guidance of the Steering committee, six working groups have been developed; clinical experts and stakeholders in the TBI field have been invited to participate in these working groups. Working group members are currently meeting regularly working towards meeting their working group objectives. The workshop will be two-fold: 1) to highlight the working groups" findings after reviewing data from previous and existing clinical studies and 2) to seek public input and feedback from the TBI community and stakeholders with the goal of informing the development of a more precise, and evidence-based classification system - beyond the terms that are currently used "mild, moderate, severe".3 a(CIT): Day 2: The National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (NINDS) is planning a Traumatic Brain Injury (TBI) Classification and Nomenclature Workshop to be held January 22 - 23, 2024, at the National Institutes of Health (NIH) in Bethesda, MD.   Under the guidance of the Steering committee, six working groups have been developed; clinical experts and stakeholders in the TBI field have been invited to participate in these working groups. Working group members are currently meeting regularly working towards meeting their working group objectives. The workshop will be two-fold: 1) to highlight the working groups" findings after reviewing data from previous and existing clinical studies and 2) to seek public input and feedback from the TBI community and stakeholders with the goal of informing the development of a more precise, and evidence-based classification system - beyond the terms that are currently used "mild, moderate, severe".  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online; title from title screen (viewed March 5, 2025).12aBrain Injuries, Traumaticxclassification22aBrain Injuries, Traumaticxdiagnosis22aClassificationxmethods22aTerminology as Topic 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (U.S.),eissuing body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=5411840uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=54125  aAUTH04461cgm a2200481 i 101750710DNLM20260227155947.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190709s2019    mdu734 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:27624  a(DNLM)CIT:27627  a(OCoLC)1124973922  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWA 1082 aPathways to Prevention Workshop: Achieving Health Equity in Preventive Servicesd(2019 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aPathways to Prevention Workshop :bachieving health equity in preventive services  /cNIH Office of Disease Prevention, NIMHD, NCI, NHLBI, NIDDK. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (12 hr., 14 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded June 19-20, 2019.3 a(CIT): Chronic diseases, such as heart disease, cancer, and diabetes are responsible for seven of every 10 deaths among Americans each year and account for 75% of the nation"s health spending. Many of these chronic conditions can be prevented, delayed, or caught and treated early when patients work closely with their primary care providers. Despite the proven value of preventive services, such as various screenings, provider counseling, and preventive medications, implementation by providers and uptake by patients of these evidence-based practices varies. Demographic and geographic differences in the use of these services are significant and may contribute to disparities in disease burden and life expectancy.  The National Institutes of Health (NIH) is hosting the Pathways to Prevention Workshop on Achieving Health Equity in Preventive Services to assess the available scientific evidence on achieving health equity in the use of clinical preventive services in a health care setting. The workshop will focus on the three leading causes of death in the United States: cancer, heart disease, and diabetes.  The workshop will also address the following questions:   What is the effect of impediments and barriers on the part of providers to the adoption, promotion, and implementation of evidence-based preventive services that contribute to disparities in preventive services? Which of them are most common?  What is the effect of impediments and barriers on the part of minority, rural, and other disadvantaged patient groups to the adoption, promotion, and implementation of evidence-based preventive services that contribute to disparities in preventive services? Which of them are most common?  What is the effectiveness of different approaches and strategies between providers and patients that connect and integrate evidence-based preventive practices for reducing disparities in preventive services? What is the effectiveness of health information technologies and digital enterprises to improve the adoption, implementation, and dissemination of evidence-based preventive services in settings that serve disparity populations?   What is the effectiveness of interventions that health care organizations and systems implement to serve disparity populations to reduce disparities in preventive services use?   The workshop is co-sponsored by:  National Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities National Cancer Institute National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases NIH Office of Disease Prevention  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed October 8, 2019 ).12aPreventive Health Services22aDiabetes Mellitusxprevention & control22aHealth Services Accessibility22aHealthcare Disparities22aHeart Diseasesxprevention & control22aNeoplasmsxprevention & control 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of Disease Prevention,ehost institution.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?2762440uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?27627  aAUTH04306cgm a2200457 i 9918716284906676DNLM20260227164159.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u231128s2023    mdu586|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:33490  a(DNLM)CIT:33499  a(OCoLC)1425218291  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aSF 910.P342 aPain in Animals Workshopn(4th :d2023 :cBethesda, Maryland)jauthor.10aPain in Animals Workshop 2023 :badvancing outcome measure development and analytical approaches /cNINDS, NIH. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2023]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (9 hr., 46 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded September 26 & 27, 2023.3 a(CIT): Annually millions of people and animals suffer from chronic and acute pain. The challenge is to accurately measure this pain in order to develop analgesics for treatment. Along with the welfare concerns of animals and people being in pain, there is the need to develop new and effective analgesic options that may offer alternatives to opioids. The Pain in Animals Workshop (PAW) meetings are held every other year in partnership with the National Institutes of Health (NIH), bringing key stakeholders together to better understand pain in animals and humans. The 2-day workshop will be held on Sept 26-27th and is open to academia, government, and industry experts in human and animal pain assessment, as well as analgesic intervention development. Pain is universal, and biomedical researchers are constantly working towards safer and more effective treatment options. The ability to measure pain is fundamental to this goal and the ability to measure and interpret data from animal models - especially the naturally occurring models across different species - has the potential to enhance translational research and advance pain control in veterinary species. The 2023 workshop will focus on updates on validated approaches to measuring pain, highlighting opportunity areas for additional outcome measure development, as well as study design and analytic approaches to the use of outcome measures in clinical trials (see agenda). The presentations and discussions will cover both companion animals (dogs, cats, horses) and production species, with comparative information from humans and rodents. It will be a truly comparative symposium! Day 1 The morning will cover validated approaches to measuring pain with the focus on new information available since the 2019 PAW meeting. The day will begin with the first annual Michele Sharkey Memorial Lecture, followed by discussions on the measurement properties of pain scoring instruments in farm animals, pain biomarkers, and the application of artificial intelligence/machine learning to large animal pain assessment. The afternoon will focus on opportunities for biopsychosocial outcome measure development including discussions on the domains impacted by acute and chronic pain, as well as differentiation between the sensory and affective components of pain. Day 2 The day will focus on study design and analytic approaches to the utilization of outcome measures in clinical trials including defining clinically meaningful changes and success/failure criteria for outcome measures, as well as the use of composite endpoints and adaptive study designs.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed February 27, 2024).12aPainxveterinary12aPain Measurementxveterinary22aAnimals, Domestic22aPain Managementxveterinary22aClinical Trials, Veterinary as Topic 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (U.S.),esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=5247240uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=52508  aAUTH01259cas a2200421   0042510DNLM20260227090924.0cr|cn ||||||||660313c19659999xxubr         0||| 0eng    a0003-6838l0003-6838z1573-8183  aAPBMAC  a(DNLM)A57570000(s)  a(OCoLC)02413358  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW1bAP52110aAppl Biochem Microbiol2dnlm00aApplied biochemistry and microbiology.  aNew York : Consultants Bureau [etc.]3 31999- :aNew York :bSpringer  av.billus.  aNine no. a year,b2010-  aBimonthly,b1965-2009  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-       Jan./Feb. 1965-  aAlso issued online.  aIssued by the Academy of Sciences USSR. 2aBiochemistry 2aMicrobiology 2aPeriodical2 aAkademii︠a︡ nauk SSSR1 iTranslation of:tPrikladnaia biokhimiia i mikrobiologiia0 x1608-30244 uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/10438  aAUTH02707cgm a2200445 i 101752796DNLM20260227160130.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u190815s2019    mdu398|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:28676  a(OCoLC)1121602775  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 503.32 aFinding and Characterizing HIV Reservoirs (Workshop)d(2019 :cBethesda, Md.)jauthor.10aFinding and Characterizing HIV Reservoirs /cNIH and the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2019]  a1 online resource (2 streaming video files (9 hr., 14 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded July 30-31, 2019.3 a(CIT): A workshop entitled "Finding and Characterizing HIV Reservoirs." Sponsored by the NIH and the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation, the intent of this workshop will be to assemble leading scientists in the field to review our understanding about the replication-competent reservoir (RCR) of HIV in ART-suppressed nonhuman primates and humans, to highlight gaps in our knowledge, to review state-of-the-art approaches towards addressing these gaps, and to encourage collaborations that might not otherwise arise. We recognize that comprehensive definition of the RCR in geographically- and demographically-dispersed cohorts might enable assessment of possible quantitative and qualitative variations in its size and disposition, and the discovery of circulating biomarker(s) that could be used to monitor it over time. In turn, such biomarkers might ultimately facilitate evaluation of the impact of interventions designed to durably suppress the reservoir. We hope that this workshop will motivate discussions and experiments that are focused on these goals.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed September 13, 2019).12aHIV Infectionsxetiology22aBiomarkers22aDisease Reservoirsxvirology22aHIV Infectionsxvirology 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body.2 aBill & Melinda Gates Foundation,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?2867640uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?28683  aAUTH02439cgm a2200457 i 101629960DNLM20260227155001.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140408s2014    mdu055|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18321  a(OCoLC)879416835  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQX 5051 aScott, Kristin,eauthor.10aTaste recognition in Drosophila /cKristin Scott. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (55 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNeuroscience seminar series  aTitle from title screen.  aAccess restricted to NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Mar. 10, 2014.3 a(CIT): Dr. Scott Lab studies the gustatory system in Drosophila melanogaster to understand how sensory information is processed by the brain to give rise to specific behaviors. Drosophila sense many of the same taste stimuli as mammals, including sugars, salts, acids, alcohols and noxious chemicals. These compounds mediate acceptance or avoidance behaviors, and activation of a single sensory neuron is sufficient to produce a behavior. Although innate, taste behaviors may be modified by learning and experience. The simplicity of ligands and behaviors, along with the molecular, genetic, calcium imaging and electrophysiological approaches available in Drosophila, allows them to examine taste processing from sensory input to motor output in a system that can also be modified by learning.  Their aims are to understand how different tastes are distinguished by the brain and how taste percepts and behaviors are modified by experience.  aDescription based on version viewed April 9, 2014.12aDrosophilaxphysiology12aTastexphysiology22aBehavior, Animalxphysiology22aTaste Perceptionxphysiology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18321  aAUTH01435cas a2200421 a 101628882DNLM20260227090916.0m     o  d        cr |||||||||||130502c20139999ne qr poo     0    0eng    a  2014243309  a2212-6198l2212-6198  a(OCoLC)841494641  aUV0cUV0dTXAdOCLCFdCUSdDNLM  apcc  9Netherlands 4aQP144.F85bB5510aW1 0aBioactive carbohydrates and dietary fibre00aBioactive carbohydrates and dietary fibre.  aAmsterdam :bElsevier Ltd.  a1 online resource  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Vol. 1, issue 1 (Jan. 2013).  aOfficial journal of the International Complex Carbohydrates Consortium.  aDescription based on: Vol. 1, issue 1 (Jan. 2013); title from PDF article running title (ScienceDirect Web site, viewed May 3, 2013).  aLatest issue consulted: 3 (2014) (viewed Apr. 4, 2014).12aCarbohydrates12aDietary Fiber12aFunctional Food 2aPeriodical2 aInternational Complex Carbohydrates Consortium.40uhttp://www.sciencedirect.com/science/journal/22126198  aAUTH02237cgm a2200433 i 101629958DNLM20260227154720.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u140408s2014    mdu063|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:18319  a(OCoLC)879416764  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 7001 aGrigg, Michael,eauthor.10aGenetic basis of protozoan pathogen emergence and disease /cMichael Grigg. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2014]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 3 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH director's seminar  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Mar. 7, 2014.3 a(CIT): Dr. Grigg's research program investigates the emergence and pathogenesis of prevalent zoonoses that infect a broad spectrum of animal and human hosts. His group is particularly focused on the protozoan parasites Toxoplasma, Leishmania, Giardia, and Cryptosporidium. He performs population and molecular genetic analyses, using both forward and reverse genetics, to identify virulence genes that control pathogenesis and perturb immune system homeostasis in animal models of natural infection. Recent work is exploring the extent to which protozoan parasites utilize their sexual cycles to rapidly produce new virulent lines that extend their host range or cause outbreaks. Ultimately he is identifying how strain-specific variations in metabolic potential and virulence impact host pathogenesis.  aDescription based on version viewed April 15, 2014.12aProtozoan Infectionsxepidemiology12aProtozoan Infectionsxgenetics22aGlobal Health 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNIH director's seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?18319  aAUTH01787cas a2200553 a 101140654DNLM20260227090913.0cr|bn ||||||||020130c20029999nyubr p       0   a0eng    a  2002213440  a1538-6341l1538-6341y0014-735421  aPSRHAP  a080470bUSPS  a(OCoLC)48897306  aNSDcNSDdORUdGUAdMXCdNSDdDNLM  ansdpalcdanlmcopyc  an-us---  9United States14aHQ763b.F3300aW1bFA453T10a36321310aPerspect Sex Reprod Health2dnlm0 aPerspect. sex. reprod. health 0aPerspectives on sexual and reproductive health00aPerspectives on sexual and reproductive health.  aNew York, NY :bAlan Guttmacher Institute,cc2002-  av. :bill. ;c28 cm.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 34, no. 1 (Jan./Feb. 2002)-  aTitle from cover.  aPrint issues ceased with v. 45, no. 4 (Dec. 2013).1 aMEDLINEbv34n1, Jan./Feb. 2002-0 aPubMedbv34n1, Jan./Feb. 2002-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.12aFamily Planning Services22aDeveloping Countries22aHealth Knowledge, Attitudes, Practice22aSexual Behavior 2aPeriodical2 aAlan Guttmacher Institute.0 x1931-239300tFamily planning perspectivesx0014-7354w(DLC)   72620943w(OCoLC)8297614 uhttp://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/journal/10.1111/(ISSN)1931-2393  aAUTH02664cgm a2200469 i 9918351572806676DNLM20260227161857.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u220226s2022    mdu114|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:32138  a(OCoLC)1342099295  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQW 10000aDemystifying medicine.pMalnutrition :bthe role of the microbiome /cJeffrey Gordon. How gut microbiome controls growth of liver tumors / Tim Gretin.1 aMalnutrition1 aHow gut microbiome controls growth of liver tumors 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 54 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSecond speaker's name is misspelled on the title screen.  aRecorded January 18, 2022.3 a(CIT): Demystifying Medicine   Dr. Gordon is director of the Center for Genome Sciences and Systems Biology at Washington University in St. Louis.  Often cited as the "father of the microbiome," Gordon is known internationally for his lab"s research on how gut microbial communities affect normal intestinal function, shape various aspects of human physiology, and affect predisposition to diseases.  Dr. Greten is an NIH senior investigator, deputy chief of the NCI Thoracic and GI Malignancies Branch, and a lead investigator in numerous clinical trials at the NIH Clinical Center.  Greten is an expert in gastrointestinal oncology and tumor immunology with a specific research focus on hepatocellular carcinoma.  His lab hopes to better understand how tumors in the liver interact with the immune system.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed August 8, 2022).12aGastrointestinal Microbiome22aChild Nutrition Disorders22aInfant Nutrition Disorders22aLiver Neoplasmsximmunology 2aWebcast1 aGordon, Jeffreyq(Jeffrey I.),d1947-espeaker.1 aGreten, Tim F.,espeaker.12aGordon, Jeffreyq(Jeffrey I.),d1947-tDemystifying medicine.pMalnutrition12aGreten, Tim F.tDemystifying medicine.pHow gut microbiome controls growth of liver tumors.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=44345  aAUTH01500cgm a22004454a 101267903DNLM20260227145739.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu069 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10916  a(OCoLC)68207241  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWW 1031 aMovshon, J. Anthony.10aStability and plasticity in primate visual development /cJ. Anthony Movshon.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Apr. 21, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 8 min., 33 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 3, 2001.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aVisual Pathwaysxgrowth & development22aPrimates22aVision, Ocular22aVisual Cortex22aVisual Perception 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10916  aAUTH01953cgm a22004574a 101306045DNLM20260227151945.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070430s2007    mdu055|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13703  a(OCoLC)136310077  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2021 aGardner, Kevin,cM.D.10aGenomic approaches to understanding mechanisms of gene regulation /cKevin Gardner.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCenter for Cancer Research-National Cancer Institute grand rounds  aTitle from screen banner (viewed May 14, 2006).  aStreaming video (55 min. : sd., col.).  aAccess restricted to HHS and NIH only.5DNLM  aRecorded Mar. 20, 2007.3 a(CIT): Center for Cancer Research - National Cancer Institute Grand Rounds    The primary educational objective of these seminars is to provide new information, ideas, and discussion about timely areas of research with impact on the field of oncology.  A secondary educational objective is to elicit participation by individuals from all divisions of the intramural NCI, and thus facilitate more interactions among investigators and groups in the NCI.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aGene Expression Regulation, Neoplasticxgenetics12aNeoplasmsxgenetics22aGenomexgenetics 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.) 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13703  aAUTH01367cgm a22003974a 101267833DNLM20260227145600.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu036 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10746  a(OCoLC)65360781  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 551 aO'Keefe, Barry.10aMass spectrometry of proteins and peptides in the molecular targets discovery program /cBarry O'Keefe.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 15, 2006).  aStreaming video (35 min., 9 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 8, 2002.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aProteins22aMass Spectrometry22aPeptides 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10746  aAUTH02271cgm a22004334a 101268408DNLM20260227150558.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2004    mdu069 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11911  a(OCoLC)84671256  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 102.81 aSilver, R. Angus.10aMechanisms underlying central synaptic transmission /cAngus Silver.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2004]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 17, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 8 min., 6 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 22, 2004.3 a(CIT): The main aim of Dr. Silver's research is to elucidate the basic mechanisms underlying central synaptic transmission and to understand how the properties of synaptic connections influence higher level processing of electrical signals between neurons. His lab uses a variety of experimental approaches, including high temporal resolution confocal microscopy and patch-clamp recording from neurons in brain slices together with quantitative analysis and modeling methods. Specific areas of interest include the dynamics and modulation of transmitter release. To this end, the Silver lab examines how transmitter release probability is governed by the activity of the presynaptic neuron and how it is modulated by spillover of transmitter from other cells.  For more information, visit Angus Silver, NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aSynaptic Transmission22aNeurotransmitter Agents22aSynapses 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11911  aAUTH01571cgm a22004574a 101267978DNLM20260227145931.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2002    mdu160 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11134  a(OCoLC)213378976  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 20.51 aRosenstein, Donald.10aInability to give consent :bsession 6 /cDon Rosenstein and Alan Fleischman.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2002]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Feb. 20, 2008).  aStreaming video (2 hr., 39 min., 8 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Dec. 4, 2002.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aBiomedical Researchxethics22aChild22aEthics Committees, Research22aEthics, Research22aHuman Experimentationxethics22aInformed Consentxethics22aVulnerable Populations 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11134  aAUTH01467nam a2200313 i 4500001001000000003000500010005001700015008004100032040003100073044001800104060002200122100005900144245036300203264006100566300004300627336002600670337002800696338002600724500007300750540014200823583003900965583004301004650002101047650002501068650002501093650001301118650001301131999000901144101758282DNLM20260220142403.0191120s1852    maua          000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aQS 26bW652r 18521 aWieting J. M.q(John Manchester),d1817-1888,eauthor.10aRepetition of Dr. Wieting's lectures :bscientific and useful entertainment for ladies and gentlemen : popular lectures on human and comparative anatomy and physiology : health and disease : the causes which destroy health, and the way to preserve and restore it : how to invigorate the system, prolong life, increase happiness, &c. : and physical education. 1a[Boston, Mass.?] :b[publisher nor identified],c[1852?]  a1 sheet :billustrations ;c60 x 25 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  asheetbnb2rdacarrier  aPublication date surmised from the latest date of a review on verso.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM12aModels, Anatomic22aCollections as Topic22aExhibitions as Topic22aManikins22aMedicine  aAUTH02249cgm a22004454a 101267843DNLM20260227145746.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu073 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10857  a(OCoLC)65616940  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 951 aHerz, Joachim,cM.D.10aMechanisms of signaling by neuronal apoE receptors /cJoachim Herz.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Mar. 21, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 12 min., 29 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Jan. 27, 2003.3 a(CIT): Apolipoprotein E is the only gene that has been unambiguously identified as a risk factor for late-onset Alzheimer's Disease.  The molecular mechanisms by which the e4 isoform of ApoE predisposes to the disease remains shrouded in mystery.  Dr. Herz's laboratory investigates the biology of a family of ApoE receptors, also known as the LDL receptor gene family, and their functions in neurons and in other tissues.  Their findings show that ApoE receptors are intrinsic components of classical signaling pathways, where they control brain development as well as the integrity of the vascular wall.   For more information, visit  Joachim Herz  The NIH Neuroscience Seminar Series features lectures and discussions with leading neuroscientists.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aLow Density Lipoprotein Receptor-Related Protein-122aNerve Tissue Proteins22aNeurons22aSignal Transduction 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?10857  aAUTH01399cgm a22004094a 101267939DNLM20260227145848.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2000    mdu039 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11084  a(OCoLC)70164384  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 2901 aBudman, Simon H.10aREBELS :bthe battle for a tobacco-free future /cSimon Budman.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2000]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed June 14, 2006).  aStreaming video (38 min., 38 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 2, 2000.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.12aTobacco Use Cessationxmethods22aAdolescent22aComputer-Assisted Instructionxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11084  aAUTH03425cgm a22004574a 101306103DNLM20260227151957.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u070430s2007    mdu060 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:13782  a(OCoLC)144568143  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQV 2691 aEgorin, Merrill J.10aRelevance of old-fashioned clinical pharmacology to modern anticancer chemotherapy /cMerrill J. Egorin.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2007]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aCCR grand rounds  aTitle from title screen (viewed May 25, 2007).  aStreaming video (59 min., 52 sec. : sd., col.).  aNIH access only.5DNLM  aRecorded Apr. 24, 2007.3 a(CIT): Center for Cancer Research - National Cancer Institute Grand Rounds    Dr. Egorin received his medical degree and training in internal medicine from the Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine and Hospital. Early in his career, he joined the Baltimore Cancer Research Center, then part of the National Cancer Institute.  In 1981, he became a staff physician at the University of Maryland Hospital, where he rose to the position of Professor of Medicine, Pharmacology and Experimental Therapeutics and Oncology. Dr. Egorin was recruited to the University of Pittsburgh Cancer Institute in 1998 to lead its clinical and preclinical pharmacology activities. His research focuses on rational development and application of antineoplastic agents. Key concepts regularly addressed in Dr. Egorin's research involve the pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamic relationships of investigational and licensed antineoplastic agents and how those relationships can be assessed and modeled. Dr. Egorin's professional affiliations include a fellowship in the American College of Physicians and memberships in the American Association for Cancer Research, the American Society for Clinical Oncology, the American Society for Clinical Pharmacology and Therapeutics and the American Society for Pharmacology and Experimental Therapeutics. He serves on the editorial boards of several medical journals and is editor-in-chief of Cancer Chemotherapy and Pharmacology. Dr. Egorin has authored or co-authored numerous book chapters and more than 250 articles in peer-reviewed journals.   The primary educational objective of these seminars is to provide new information, ideas, and discussion about timely areas of research with impact on the field of oncology.  A secondary educational objective is to elicit participation by individuals from all divisions of the intramural NCI, and thus facilitate more interactions among investigators and groups in the NCI.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aAntineoplastic Agentsxpharmacology22aNeoplasmsxdrug therapy22aPharmacology, Clinical 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aCenter for Cancer Research (National Cancer Institute (U.S.)) 0aCCR grand rounds40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?13782  aAUTH02382cgm a2200421 i 101682332DNLM20260227155422.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u160503s2016    mdu056|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:19643  a(OCoLC)949987084  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 102.700aSynaptic signaling in cerebellar circuits /cIndira Raman. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2016]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (56 min.)) :bcolor, sound.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH neuroscience seminar series  aRecorded April 25, 2016.3 a(CIT): NIH Neuroscience Series Seminar  Information in the nervous system is transmitted by electrical signals (action potentials) within neurons and by chemical signals between neurons. The research interests of Dr Raman's lab are in studying the biophysical properties of voltage- and ligand-gated ion channels intrinsic to neurons, as well as in understanding the physiological consequences of the different patterns of activity characteristic of specific neurons.  They are currently studying neurons of the cerebellum, a brain region that participates in controlling movements. Projects in the lab focus on the ion channel properties responsible for specific patterns of firing, the transmission of synaptic signals from Purkinje to cerebellar nuclear cells, the responses of cerebellar nuclear cells to different patterns of synaptic input, and the activity and changes in cerebellar neuronal spiking during motor behaviors and learning.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed May 15, 2016).12aNeuronsxphysiology12aSynaptic Transmissionxphysiology22aCerebellumxphysiology 2aWebcast1 aRaman, Indira,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),esponsoring body. 0aNIH neuroscience seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?19643  aAUTH01664cgm a22004814a 101268397DNLM20260227150553.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu105|||||||f||||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11898  a(OCoLC)84671219  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2671 aSausville, Ed.10aTRACO.pApoptosis and molecular targets /cSausville and Tsokos.30aApoptosis and molecular targets3 aTranslational research in clinical oncology  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed Oct. 16, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 45 min., 29 sec. : sd., col.).  aTsokos begins speaking about apoptosis at 25 min., 35 sec.  aRecorded Oct. 6, 2003.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasmsxdrug therapy22aApoptosis22aClinical Trials as Topic22aDrug Delivery Systems22aDrug Design 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11898  aAUTH01617cas a2200493 a 101242019DNLM20260227090917.0cr|cn#||||||||040518c20039999sa fr p       0    0eng    a  2005243292  a1728-0583l1728-0583  a(OCoLC)55198950  aOI@cOI@dOCLCQdDNLM  alcdanlmcopyc  af-sa---  9South Africa00aW1bSO925DM10aJ Child Adolesc Ment Health2dnlm00aJournal of child and adolescent mental health.  aGrahamstown, South Africa :bNISC,c2003-  av. ;c28 cm.  aSemiannual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 15 (1) (2003)-  aTitle from cover.  a"Official journal of the South African Association for Child and Adolescent Psychiatry and Allied Professions".1 aMEDLINEbv26n1, 2014-0 aPubMedbv26n1, 2014-  aAlso issued online.12aMental Disorders22aAdolescent22