York :bs.n.,c1896?]  a7 p. ;c15 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCover title.  a"The Board of Trustees of the Roosevelt Hospital, having decided to open, in November, 1896, a school for ... professional nurses, issues the following circular ..."--p. [3].  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202012032pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202012035DNLM22aRoosevelt Hospital (New York, N.Y.).bTraining School for Nurses12aSchools, Nursing 2aNew York City  aAUTH01199cam a2200325 i 60421680RDNLM20260206221543.0cr||||||||||||820514t19161912nyua     b    001 0 eng    aga 18000230   a(OCoLC)9695640  aDNLMbengerdad20260206dDNLM  9United States00aHAbK54e 19161 aKing, Willford Isbell,d1880-1962,eauthor.14aThe elements of statistical method /cby Willford I. King. 1aNew York :bMacmillan Company,c1916. 4c©1912  axvi, 1 leaf, 250 pages :billustrations ;c20 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20250626lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM1 adigitizedc20262pda5DNLM12aStatistics as Topicxmethods4 uhttp://resource.nlm.nih.gov/60421680R  aAUTH01298cas a2200421 a 9502210DNLM20260227091016.0cr|cn#||||||||950302c19949999enk x p       0    0eng    a1103-8128l1103-8128  a(DNLM)SR0079329(s)  a(OCoLC)32144548  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  9England00aW1bSC151S10aScand J Occup Ther2dnlm00aScandinavian journal of occupational therapy.  aOslo, Norway :bScandinavian University Press,cc1994-2 aLondon :bInforma Healthcare31a[London] :bTaylor & Francis Group  av. :bill.  aSeven issues a year,b2019-  aFrequency varies,b-2018  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no 1-  aImprint varies.1 aMEDLINEbv12n1, 2005-1 aPubMedbv12n1, 2005-  aAlso issued online.  3v. 1, no. 1 (Mar. 1994)c20050922fNotice  not publishedlPrinted on acid-free paper 2aOccupational Therapy 2aPeriodical0 x1651-20144 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/loi/iocc20  aAUTH02265cgm a22004334a 101569747DNLM20260227154318.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110913s2011    mdu457 ||||||fo   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16799  a(OCoLC)756157270  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aW 83 AA12 aMHealth Evidence Workshopd(2011 :cBethesda, Md.)10aMHealth Evidence Workshop /cRobert Wood Johnson Foundation, McKesson Foundation, National Science Foundation, and the NIH.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (7 hr., 37 min.) :bsd., col.)  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aRecorded Aug. 16, 2011.3 a(CIT): Mobile health (mHealth) has the potential to simultaneously reduce the cost of health care and improve our health by encouraging healthy behaviors, providing continuous monitoring to prevent or reduce health problems, reducing acute health care visits, and providing personalized, real-time intervention in the mobile environment. However, traditional methods of evaluation needed to address efficacy and safety in mHealth are not well aligned to the pace of technological development. The mHealth Evidence Meeting will bring together individuals with diverse expertise in data analysis and experimental design to identify innovative methods that can accelerate the evaluation of the efficacy and safety of mHealth technologies. Results from the workshop will be used to define the research agenda for evaluation of mobile health technology.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aTelemedicine22aDiffusion of Innovation22aTreatment Outcome 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast2 aRobert Wood Johnson Foundation.40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16799  aAUTH01318cas a2200457   7805798DNLM20260227091001.0cr|cnu||||||||780803c19769999ja qr         0    0eng    a0388-1350l0388-1350  aJTSCDR  a(DNLM)J40500000(s)  a(OCoLC)04558770  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM0 aengajpn  9Japan00aW1bJO966JK10aJ Toxicol Sci2dnlm 0aJournal of toxicological sciences04aThe Journal of toxicological sciences.30aJournal of the toxicological sciences  aSapporo, JapanbDoku Sayō Kenkyūkai  av.bill.  aQuarterly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 1-    Jan. 1976-1 aMEDLINEbv3n1,Jan, 1978-1 aPubMedbv3n1,Jan, 1978-1 aIndex medicus  aAlso issued online.  aArticles in English or Japanese; summaries in English. 2aToxicology 2aPeriodical2 aDoku Sayō Kenkyūkai.0 x1880-39894 uhttp://www.jsot.gr.jp/4 uhttps://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/browse/jts/  aAUTH02640cgm a22004454a 101549223DNLM20260227153721.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u101208s2010    mdu060|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16230  a(OCoLC)698580034  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQU 26.51 aWu, Cathy H.10aLinking data mining, text mining & ontology for functional interpretation of proteomics data in systems biology context /cCathy H. Wu, Proteomics Interest Group.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2010]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNIH ProtlG seminar  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (1 hr., 0 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Oct. 7, 2010.3 a(CIT): NIH Proteomics Interest Group Seminar Series  Many bioinformatics tools have been developed for the management and analysis of proteomics data. However, functional analysis and interpretation of large-scale proteomics data remain challenging and require effective use of advanced bioinformatics methods and databases for data integration, mining, and comparative analyses. Our group has developed a protein-centric framework for systems integration and mining of omics data, and knowledge capture of protein forms and complexes. The system features include: (i) integration of over 100 molecular and omics databases, along with gene/protein ID mapping from disparate data sources; (ii) data mining and text mining capabilities for literature-based knowledge extraction; and (iii) interoperability with ontologies to capture functional properties of proteins, splice isoforms, post-translational modifications and complexes. Scientific use cases will be presented to illustrate how the integrative bioinformatics approach supports biological pathway and network analysis of proteomics data and facilitates hypothesis generation and target identification in systems biology context.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aData Mining12aProteomics22aSystems Biology 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bProteomics Interest Group. 0aProteomics Interest Group seminar series40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16230  aAUTH01750cas a2200517 a 101521073DNLM20260224111545.0m        d        cr |||||||||||091009c20099999dcumr p o     0   a0eng    a  20092002437 a2532386-62DE-6000 a1948-5875l1948-587521  a(OCoLC)455500725  aDLCcDLCdHEBISdMYGdDLCdCUSdAGLdDNLM  apccansdp  9United States10aISSN RECORD14aRS40010aW10 aRS400b.A2710a6152140 aACS med. chem. lett.10aACS Med Chem Lett2dnlm 0aACS medicinal chemistry letters00aACS medicinal chemistry letters.13aAmerican Chemical Society medicinal chemistry letters13aMedicinal chemistry letters  aWashington, D.C. :bAmeric