|fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11299  a(OCoLC)70713352  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2661 aRosenberg, Steven A.10aClinical Center 50th anniversary :bdevelopment of immunotherapy for human cancer & bone marrow to brain tumors /cSteven Rosenberg and Howard Fine.30aDevelopment of immunotherapy for human cancer30aBone marrow to brain tumors3 aClinical Center fiftieth anniversary  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2003]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 10, 2006).  aStreaming video (1 hr., 8 min., 55 sec. : sd., col.).  aSecond lecture, Bone marrow to brain tumors, begins at 35 min., 35 sec.  aRecorded Sept. 24, 2003.3 a(CIT): NIH Clinical Center 50th Anniversary Celebration of Clinical Research   The Development of Immunotherapy for Human Cancer  Steven Rosenberg, M.D., Ph.D.  Chief, Surgery Branch, Center for Cancer Research, NCI   Bone Marrow to Brain Tumors: Changing Paradigms and Their Therapeutic Implications  Howard Fine, M.D.  Branch Chief, Neuro-Oncology Branch, NCI and NINDS  Participants will be provided state-of-the-art research on the latest scientific advances in the field as well as practical clinical information for application in patient care through excellent teaching.  This activity is for all interested physicians and clinicians.  The National Institutes of Health/Foundation for Advanced Education in the Sciences (NIH/FAES) is accredited by the Accreditation Council for Continuing Medical Education to sponsor continuing medical education for physicians.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aCopyright: This is a work of the United States Government. No copyright exists on this material. It may be disseminated freely.  aOpen-captioned.22aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bClinical Center12aNeoplasmsxtherapy22aBrain Neoplasmsxtherapy22aGene Transfer Techniques22aGenetic Therapyxmethods22aImmunotherapyxmethods 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?11299  aAUTH01810cas a2200505   7701852DNLM20260227090919.0cr|cn#||||||||770311c19769999nyubr p       0    0eng    asn 79018920   a0378-6323l0378-6323  aIJDLDY  a(DNLM)I09360000(s)  a(OCoLC)02884822  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  ansdp  9United States00aW1bIN208F10aIndian J Dermatol Venereol Leprol2dnlm 0aIndian journal of dermatology, venereology, and leprology00aIndian journal of dermatology, venereology and leprology.13aIndian journal of dermatology, venereology & leprology  aVellore,bIndian Assn. of Dermatologists, Venereologists and Leprologists.2 aMumbai :bMedknow Publications3132021- :aPittsford, NY :bScientific Scholar on behalf of Indian Association of Dermatologists, Venereologists & Leprologists  av.bill.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 av. 42-    Jan./Feb. 1976-  aPrint issues ceased with 2015.1 aMEDLINEbv71n1, Jan./Feb. 2005-0 aPubMedbv71n1, Jan./Feb. 2005- ; Selected citations only before this date  aIssued also online. 2aDermatology 2aLeprosy 2aSexually Transmitted Diseases 2aPeriodical2 aIndian Association of Dermatologists, Venereologists, and Leprologists.0 x0973-392200tIndian journal of dermatology and venereologyx0019-5162w(OCoLC)016043834 uhttps://ijdvl.com/  aAUTH02521cgm a22004454a 101507214DNLM20260227152918.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u090605s2009    mdu055 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:15116  a(OCoLC)429586326  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aBF 319.5.E91 aQuirk, Gregory J.10aPrefrontal control of fear expression :btranslational studies in rats and humans /cGregory J. Quirk.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2009]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from screen banner (viewed July 22, 2009).  aStreaming video (54 min., 42 sec. : sd., col.).  aRecorded May 19, 2009.3 a(CIT): Classical studies of fear learning have emphasized the central role of the amygdala in acquiring and expressing fear memories.  Recent work, however, is emphasizing the role of the medial prefrontal cortex in regulating the expression of these memories, via descending projections to the amygdala and other structures.  In rats, converging evidence from recording, infusion, and microstimulation studies indicate that the infralimbic and prelimbic subregions of mPFC serve as "on" and "off" switches for fear expression.  Plasticity in the infralimbic cortex is essential for retrieval of extinction memory, and is modulated by monoamines, NMDA-mediated bursting activity, and BDNF.  Deficits in these mechanisms could explain the failure to retrieve extinction memory and may underlie symptoms of PTSD.  Human homologues of rodent prefrontal areas show predicted changes in subjects undergoing extinction, as well as extinction failure in PTSD patients.  Prefrontal activity levels could serve as "biomarkers" that predict extinction success and failure, and even clinical response to extinction-based therapies.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aExtinction, Psychological22aFear22aLimbic System22aPrefrontal Cortex22aStress Disorders, Traumaticxtherapy 2aLecture 2aWebcast2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.)40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?15116  aAUTH01529cgm a22004574a 101267842DNLM20260227145736.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2001    mdu059 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:10856  a(OCoLC)65616935  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWL 30000aIschemic preconditioning :ba model of neural plasticity.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2001]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2