tionxhistory22aPublic Health Practicexhistory22aHistory, 17th Century22aHistory, 18th Century 2aItaly  aAUTH01618nas a2200433 i 9919093175306676DNLM20260220101302.0m     o  d        cr#|||||||||||241124c20259999ne qr poo     0    0eng    a  2026223510  a1473190762a1482279663  a2950-2616l2950-2616  a(OCoLC)1475018088z(OCoLC)1473190762z(OCoLC)1482279663  aSFBbengerdaepncSFBdOCLKBdOCLCQdOH1dOCLCO  apcc  9Netherlands04aRC254.A100aW110aIntell Oncol2dnlm00aIntelligent oncology. 1a[Amsterdam] :bElsevier B.V. on behalf of KeAi Communications Co. Ltd.,c[2025]-  a1 online resource  aQuarterly.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Volume 1, issue 1 (January 2025).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aArticles published as they become available and compiled into issues.  aDescription based on: Volume 1, issue 1 (January 2025); title from resource description page (ScienceDirect, viewed April 22, 2025).  aLatest issue consulted: Volume 2, issue 1 (January 2026) (ScienceDirect, viewed February 20, 2026).12aMedical Oncology12aArtificial Intelligence 2aPeriodical2 aKeAi Communication Co. Ltd.,eissuing body.40uhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/intelligent-oncology  aAUTH02320nam a2200409 i 9919061168706676DNLM20260224085713.0m     o  d i      cr|cn#||||||||250802c20259999sz a    ob   i000|0 eng    a9789240111110q(electronic)  z9789240111127q(print)  a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK616307  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Switzerland00aWC 5242 aWorld Health Organization,eauthor,eissuing body.10aWHO guidelines for clinical management of arboviral diseases :bdengue, chikungunya, zika and yellow fever. 1aGeneva :bWorld Health Organization,c[2025]  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (ix, 110 pages)) :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 aArboviral diseases, primarily transmitted by Aedes mosquitoes, pose an increasing threat to global health, particularly in the context of climate change, urbanization, and growing human mobility. With over 5.6 billion people worldwide at risk of arboviral infection, it is essential that healthcare professionals have access to evidence-based recommendations to effectively manage these infections in patients. The new WHO guidelines provide clinical management recommendations for four of the most widespread arboviruses affecting humans: dengue, chikungunya, Zika, and yellow fever. An integrated approach is vital, as these four diseases often present with similar symptoms, especially in the early stages of infection, and multiple arboviruses may circulate simultaneously in certain regions. This makes clinical differentiation challenging, particularly where diagnostic testing is not readily available.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed February 24, 2026).12aArbovirus Infectionsxdiagnosis12aArbovirus Infectionsxtherapy22aDengue22aChikungunya Fever22aZika Virus Infection22aYellow Fever 2aPractice Guideline40uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK616307/  aAUTH01588nas a2200445 i 9919061072606676DNLM20260211073904.0m     o  d        cr#|n|||||||||170310c20119999ck ar jso     0   b0eng    a  2024238814  a990742904  a2174-0798l2174-0798221  a(OCoLC)981020856z(OCoLC)990742904  aVT2bengerdaepncVT2dS4SdCUSdOCLCFdWYUdOCLCQdFUG  apcc  9Colombia00aW110aRev Odontopediatr Latinoam2dnlm0 aRev. odontopediatr. latinoam. 0aRevista de odontopediatria latinoamericana00aRevista de odontopediatria latinoamericana. 1aBogotá, Colombia :bAsociación Latinoamericana de Odontopediatría ALOP,c[2011]-  a1 online resource  aAnnual,b2022-  aSemiannual,b2011-2021  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with: Volume. 1 no. 1 (2011).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only0 aVol. 1 Núm. 1 (Enero-Junio 2011); title from publisher's website, viewed March 13, 20241 aVol. 15 (2025), viewed February 10, 2026).12aPediatric Dentistry22aDental Care for Children 2aPeriodical2 aAsociación Latinoamericana de Odontopediatría,eissuing body40uhttps://www.revistaodontopediatria.org/index.php/alop/index  aAUTH02551nam a2200385 i 9919061166006676DNLM20260224082815.0m     o  d i      cr|un#||||||||250802s2025    sz      ob   i000|0 eng    a9789240104907q(electronic)  a9789240104914q(print)  a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK616637  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Switzerland00aWC 1402 aWorld Health Organization,eauthor,eissuing body.10aGuidelines for the management of asymptomatic sexually transmitted infections. 1aGeneva :bWorld Health Organization,c[2025]  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (x, 47 pages))  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliopgraphical references.3 aWHO's global health sector strategies for HIV, viral hepatitis, and sexually transmitted infections (STIs) aim to reduce gonorrhoea and syphilis infections by 90% by 2030, emphasizing improved prevention, diagnostic, and treatment services. Addressing asymptomatic STIs is crucial, as many infections remain undetected, particularly in women and in non-urethral sites like the pharynx and rectum. The rising antimicrobial resistance in pathogens such as N. gonorrhoeae and M. genitalium complicates treatment, with ceftriaxone currently being the last effective option for gonorrhoea in many countries. WHO's new guidelines focus on managing asymptomatic STIs with evidence-based recommendations for screening N. gonorrhoeae and C. trachomatis, complementing existing guidelines for syphilis, STI partner services, and symptomatic infections. These guidelines also aim to support national health programs in reaching the 2030 targets and are intended for policy-makers, healthcare workers, and organizations involved in STI care and prevention. The recommendations were developed using the GRADE approach, with systematic reviews and expert evaluations ensuring their robustness.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed February 23, 2026).12aSexually Transmitted Diseasesxdiagnosis22aSexually Transmitted Diseasesxdrug therapy22aSexually Transmitted Diseasesxprevention & control22aAsymptomatic Infections 2aPractice Guideline40uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK616637/  aAUTH02916nam a2200361 i 9919061168906676DNLM20260224121232.0m     o  d i      cr|un#||||||||250802s2025    sz a    ob   i000|0 eng    a9789240110472q(electronic)  z9789240110489q(print)  a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK616288  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Switzerland00aWI 7102 aWorld Health Organization,eauthor,eissuing body.10aWHO guidelines for the treatment of patients with cystic echinococcosis. 1aGeneva :bWorld Health Organization,c[2025]  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (x, 33 pages)) :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 aThe purpose of these guidelines is to provide guidance on the choice of treatment so that patients (adults and children) with CE cysts can be offered and receive appropriate and equitable treatment. The aim is to ensure that patients receive the most appropriate and affordable management in the context of infrastructure and expertise sufficient to ensure its safety, and without unnecessary invasive procedures or treatment, to avoid iatrogenic complications by using invasive interventions. For complicated CE liver cysts, surgery is usually the treatment of choice, based on best medical practice as conveyed by the WHO-IWGE. These guidelines are focused on the different choices for uncomplicated liver cysts. Pulmonary CE is usually managed by surgical intervention, but these guidelines evaluate the option of using ALB alone to treat small pulmonary cysts. For uncomplicated inactive cysts, there are no recommendations in these guidelines since current best medical practice is follow-up with imaging (ultrasonography, CT or MRI), also known as the "watch and wait" approach. Surgery should be avoided as far as possible unless the inactive cyst is causing complications (e.g. cyst causing portal hypertension). These guidelines were developed for clinicians, health facility managers and health practitioners practising at all levels of health services (primary, secondary and tertiary health care) and at all resource levels (low, middle-and high-income countries) that provide care for patients with CE. They were also developed to inform health care policy-makers, health system administrators, insurance companies and NTD programme implementors.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed February 24, 2026).12aEchinococcosis, Hepaticxtherapy12aEchinococcosis, Pulmonaryxtherapy 2aPractice Guideline40uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK616288/  aAUTH01042nam a2200313 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400025000800420008001050440010001130600032001231000025001552450069001802500013002492640073002623000040003353360026003753370028004013380027004294900069004566500017005256500017005426500021005596550024005808300059006048300056006639990009007199919209971306676DNLM20260206085808.0251125s1936    cc a   j      000 0 chi    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5702 no.61 aLi, Zongfa,eauthor.10aYan er de wei sheng /cLi Zongfa bian [zhu] ; Xu Yingchang jiao.  aZai ban. 1aShanghai :bShang wu yin shu guan,cZhong hua min guo 25 nian [1936]  a41 pages :billustrations ;c19 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aXiao xue sheng fen nian bu chong du ben, 3 nian ji, Wei sheng ke12aEye Diseases22aEar Diseases22aHealth Education 2aJuvenile Literature 0aXiao xue sheng fen nian bu chong du ben.pWei sheng ke 0aXiao xue sheng fen nian bu chong du ben.p3 nian ji  aAUTH00868nam a2200313 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400025000800420008001050440010001130600032001231000032001552450041001872500013002282640042002413000039002833360026003223370028003483380027003764900026004036500019004296500017004486500016004656500021004816550017005028300026005199990009005459919208866806676DNLM20260220080946.0251118s1954    cc a   g      000 0 chi    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5702 no.31 aShangguan, Wuchen,eauthor.10aXue ya /cShangguan Wuchen bian xie.  aChu ban. 1aBeijing :bJian kang shu dian,c1954.  a22 pages :billustrations ;c19 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aJian kang xiao wen ku12aBlood Pressure22aHypertension22aHypotension22aHealth Education 2aPopular Work 0aJian kang xiao wen ku  aAUTH00924nam a2200313 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400025000800410013001050420008001180440010001260600032001361000058001682400034002262450054002602500013003142640039003273360026003663370028003923380027004204900030004476500041004776500024005186500012005426550017005548300030005719990009006019919209971006676DNLM20260206071526.0251126s1952    cc a          000 0 chi    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM1 achihrus  apcc  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5702 no.71 aPobedinskiĭ, M. N.q(Mikhail Nikolaevich),eauthor.10aGigiena zhenshchiny.lChinese10aFu nü wei sheng /cPupiqinsiji zhu ; Hui Si yi.  aChu ban. 1aShanghai :bBei xin shu ju,c1952.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aYi xue wei sheng cong shu12aReproductive Physiological Phenomena12aReproductive Health22aHygiene 2aPopular Work 0aYi xue wei sheng cong shu  aAUTH01015nam a2200337 i 9919208866906676DNLM20260206132720.0970925s1970    cc a     f    000 0 chi    a(OCoLC)1461830228  aSLYbengerdacSLYdOCLCOdOCLCQdX2QdOCLCO  apcc  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5702 no.2  aR821. 72clc  aR821.72clc/400aYe ying xun lian wei sheng chang shi /cRen min wei sheng chu ban she bian.  aDi 1 ban. 1aBeijing :bRen min wei sheng chu ban she,c1970. 3c1971  a29 pages :billustrations ;c15 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aTitle from cover.12aMilitary Hygienexmethods22aFirst Aid22aHealth Education 2aHandbook2 aRen min wei sheng chu ban she,eeditor,epublisher.  aAUTH06439nam a2200433 i 9919116874406676DNLM20260204093426.0m     o  d        cr|un#||||||||250902s2020    dcua   |ob    000|0 eng    a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK616938  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWL 3561 aReeves, Mathew J.,eauthor.10aComparing programs to support stroke survivors and their caregivers during the transition home from the hospital or a rehabilitation facility--The MISTT study /cMathew J. Reeves, Michele C. Fritz, Anne K. Hughes, Amanda T. Woodward, Paul P. Freddolino, Constantinos K. Coursaris, Sarah J. Swierenga. 1aWashington, DC :bPatient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute,c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (166 pages)) :billustrations.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aFinal research report  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 aBACKGROUND: For many stroke patients and caregivers, navigating the transition between hospital and home is associated with substantial psychosocial and health-related challenges. OBJECTIVE: To improve patient outcomes during the transition between hospital and home by testing 2 interventions: (1) a 60-day home-based social work case management (SWCM) program, and (2) access to a patient-centered online information and support resource (Michigan Stroke Transitions Trial [MISTT] website). DESIGN: MISTT was a pragmatic, open clinical trial in which acute stroke patients were randomly assigned to 3 groups: (1) usual care (UC), (2) the SWCM program, or (3) SWCM program plus access to the MISTT website (SWCM+MISTT website). SETTING: Communities surrounding 3 mid-Michigan hospitals. PARTICIPANTS: Acute stroke patients discharged directly home or who returned home within 4 weeks of discharge to a rehabilitation facility. Primary caregivers (if available) were also enrolled. INTERVENTION: For the SWCM program, trained social workers provided in-home and phone-based case management services for 60 to 90 days. SWCMs focused on increasing support, preparedness, and stroke prevention and on addressing unmet needs. The MISTT website provided stroke education as well as prevention, recovery, and community resource information. PRIMARY OUTCOMES: Data were collected via telephone 7 days and 90 days after the patient returned home. Patient outcomes included quality-of-life Patient-Reported Outcomes Measurement Information System-10 (PROMIS-10) Global Physical Health (GPH) and Global Mental Health (GMH) subscales (reported as T-scores with standardized mean [SD], 50 [10]) and the Patient Activation Measure (PAM; score range, 0-100). Caregiver outcomes included the Bakas Caregiving Outcomes Scale (BCOS; score range, 15-105) and the Patient Health Questionnaire, 9-item (PHQ-9; score range, 0-27) to measure depression. Sample size estimates determined that 214 patients were needed. We used an intention-to-treat (ITT) available case analysis using a difference-in-differences (D-in-D) linear model to compare the change in outcomes (90 day minus 7 day) between the 3 treatment groups. We performed multiple imputation as a sensitivity analysis to account for missing data. RESULTS: We randomly assigned 265 stroke patients and 169 caregivers to 3 groups. Data from 240 (91%) patients and 148 (88%) caregivers were included in the ITT analyses. There were statistically significant treatment group differences in the change in PROMIS-10 GPH T-scores (D-in-D interaction P = 0.003) and PAM scores (D-in-D interaction P = 0.042) but no significant differences in PROMIS-10 GMH T-scores (D-in-D interaction P = 0.56). The mean change in GPH T-scores for SWCM+MISTT was significantly higher than that for both UC (T-score difference, 3.37; 95% CI, 1.41-5.33; P < 0.001) and SWCM (T-score difference, 2.40; 95% CI, 0.46-4.34; P = 0.016). The mean change in PAM scores for SWCM+MISTT was significantly higher than that for SWCM only (score difference, 6.67; 95% CI, 1.26-12.08; P = 0.016) and marginally higher than that for UC (score difference, 5.02; 95% CI, −0.47 to 10.52; P = 0.07). Results from analyses using imputed data were concordant with the ITT results. No statistically significant treatment group differences were found for caregiver BCOS (D-in-D interaction, P = 0.30) or PHQ-9 (D-in-D interaction, P = 0.67). CONCLUSIONS: The combination of SWCM plus access to a curated online website produced greater gains in patient-reported physical health and activation than those with SWCM only or UC. The observed gains in physical health represent modest clinical improvements, while increases in activation exceeded the minimal important difference. However, these increases occurred despite low overall use of the website. Further research is needed to understand the reasons behind these observed changes, including quantifying the effects of specific components of the intervention, as well as identifying the potential role of response shift. LIMITATIONS: Phone interviewers were not blinded to group assignment; however, measurement bias is unlikely because primary outcomes were based on validated, multi-item instruments with standardized response options that did not require interviewer interpretation. The MISTT population included fewer patients than intended with severe stroke or those discharged to subacute rehabilitation.  aOriginal Project Title: Improving Care Transitions for Acute Stroke Patients Through a Patient-Centered Home-Based Case Management Program--The MISST Study PCORI ID: IHS-1310-07420-01 ClinicalTrials.gov ID: NCT02653170  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (Feb. 4, 2026).12aStrokextherapy12aStroke Rehabilitationxmethods22aTransitional Carexmethods.22aHome Care Servicesxmethods.22aTelerehabilitationxmethods22aCaregivers 2aComparative Study2 aPatient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aFinal research report (Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute (U.S.))40uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK616938/  aAUTH00849nam a2200277 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390350021000800400037001010440010001380600032001481000041001802450058002212500013002792640047002923000039003393360026003783370028004043380027004325000022004596500045004816500021005266500015005479990009005629919209970806676DNLM20260220080939.0080509s1952    cc a          000 0 chi    a(OCoLC)879968070  aSLYbengerdacSLYdOCLCQdOCLCL  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5702 no.81 aGao, Yunpeng,dactive 1952,eauthor.10aZen yang fang yu xi jun zhan /cGao Yunpeng bian zhu.  aChu ban. 1aShanghai :bZhong xing chu ban she,c1952.  a54 pages :billustrations ;c15 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aTitle from cover.12aBiological Warfarexprevention & control22aHealth Education22aPropaganda  aAUTH01950nas a2200469 i 9919251316806676DNLM20260224152717.0m     o  d        cr#cn|||||||||250114c20249999enk|| poo     0    0eng    a  2026223512  a3049-5784l3049-5784  a(OCoLC)1528365599  aSFBbengerdaepncSFBdOCLCOdOCLCQ  apcc  9England00aW110aBMJ Connect Oncol2dnlm00aBMJ connections.pOncology.1 aBritish medical journal connections oncology1 aOncology1 aBMJ Connect Oncol 1aLondon :bBMJ Publishing Group,c[2024]-  a1 online resource  aAnnual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 1, issue 1 (November 2024)  aCompanion title to: BMJ Oncology.  aBMJ Connections Oncology aims to be a leading international journal for peer-reviewed cancer research, bridging clinical practice, scientific research, and translational applications. BMJ Connections Oncology follows an open access model, ensuring free and immediate availability of all articles to enhance global knowledge dissemination and advancements in oncology.0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aArticles published as they become available and compiled into annual volumes.0 aVolume 1, issue 1 (November 2024); title from journal home page (BMJ Journals, viewed February 23, 2026).1 aVolume 3, issue 1 (January 2026) (viewed February 23, 2026).12aMedical Oncology22aNeoplasms 2aPeriodical08iComplemented by (work):tBMJ oncologyx2752-7948w(OCoLC)140477713740uhttps://connectionsoncology.bmj.com/  aAUTH05665nam a2200433 i 9919230193306676DNLM20260225080020.0m     o  d        cr|un#||||||||260102s2023    dcua    ob    000|0 eng    a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK619815  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 1721 aHoge, Elizabeth,eauthor.10aComparing meditation versus medicine for patients with anxiety disorders /cElizabeth Hoge, Eric Bui, Mihriye Mete, Mary Ann Dutton, Amanda W. Baker, Naomi M. Simon. 1aWashington (DC) :bPatient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute (PCORI),c2023.  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (58 pages)) :billustrations.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aPatient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute final research report  aCaption title.  aIncludes bibliographical references3 aBACKGROUND: Anxiety disorders are common, highly distressing, and impairing conditions. Although effective treatments exist, many patients do not have access to them or respond to them. Mindfulness-based interventions such as mindfulness-based stress reduction (MBSR) are popular and can decrease anxiety, but it is unknown how they compare with standard, first-line treatments. In addition, as virtual health care becomes increasingly common and may reduce barriers to care, it is important to determine how virtually delivered treatment compares with traditional, in-person treatments. OBJECTIVES: To determine whether MBSR is noninferior to escitalopram, a commonly used first-line psychopharmacologic treatment (both delivered in person) (aim 1); to determine whether MBSR delivered online is noninferior to MBSR delivered in person (aim 2); and to determine whether MBSR (delivered online) is noninferior to escitalopram (also delivered online) (aim 3). METHODS: This randomized controlled study (NCT03522844) included a noninferiority design with a prespecified noninferiority margin based on published trials and minimal clinically important difference scores. Participants were adults diagnosed with an anxiety disorder recruited by 3 urban medical centers and randomly assigned to 8 weeks of the drug escitalopram or 8 weeks of MBSR. The outcome was assessed by blinded clinical interviewers at baseline, the week 8 end point (end of the active intervention period), and follow-up visits (12 and 24 weeks). The primary outcome measure was the Clinical Global Impression of Severity. RESULTS: 1. Aim 1 (in-person treatments): The primary (completer) noninferiority sample consisted of 208 patients (n = 102 MBSR; n = 106 escitalopram), with a mean (SD) age of 33 (13) years and 156 women (75%). At treatment end point, the Clinical Global Impression of Severity was reduced by mean (SD) 1.35 (1.06) points for MBSR and 1.43 (1.17) points for escitalopram. The difference (SE) between the treatment groups was −0.07 (0.16 [95% CI, −0.38 to 0.23]; P = .65), where the lower bound of the interval fell within the predefined noninferiority margin of −0.495, indicating noninferiority of MBSR compared with escitalopram.2. Aim 2: After online treatment delivery was completed, we compared in-person MBSR with online delivery of MBSR using videoconferencing. We found a difference between the treatment groups of −0.04 (95% CI, −0.33 to 0.25), where the lower bound of the interval fell within the predefined noninferiority margin of −0.40, indicating noninferiority of online MBSR compared with in-person MBSR.3. Aim 3: We also compared online MBSR and online delivery of pharmacotherapy with escitalopram, but the results were inconclusive. CONCLUSIONS: We conducted the first randomized controlled anxiety disorder treatment trial comparing a standardized, evidence-based, mindfulness-based intervention with pharmacotherapy and found that MBSR was noninferior to escitalopram. This rigorous, well-controlled study provides evidence for clinicians, insurers, and health care systems to recommend, include, and provide reimbursement for MBSR as an effective treatment for anxiety disorders. In addition, virtual delivery of MBSR using videoconferencing seems to be noninferior to in-person classes, increasing the delivery options for this intervention. LIMITATIONS: The study treatments were not matched for time and attention (participants in the MBSR group spent more time engaged in treatment-related activities than those in the escitalopram group). In addition, the sample was predominantly made up of women with a relatively high education level, and recruitment took place at 3 urban academic medical centers, which may limit the generalizability of findings.  aOriginal Project Title: Comparative Effectiveness of Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction and Pharmacotherapy for Anxiety PCORI ID: CER-2017C1-6522 ClinicalTrials.gov ID: NCT03522844  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed February 23, 2026).12aAnxiety Disordersxtherapy22aMindfulness22aEscitalopramxtherapeutic use22aMental Health Teletherapy22aComparative Effectiveness Research 2aRandomized Controlled Trial2 aPatient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aFinal research report (Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute (U.S.))40uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK619815/  aAUTH01147nam a2200325 i 9919251816006676DNLM20260205105012.0260129s1951    cc       l    000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM0 aengafre  apcc  9China00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5703 no.122 aWorld Health Assemblyn(4th :d1951 :cGeneva, Switzerland)jauthor10aInternational sanitary regulations :bWHO regulations no. 2.1 aWHO regulations no. 21 aGuo ji wei sheng fa gui 1a[China?] :b[publisher not identified],c[1951]  a35 leaves, 1 unnumbered leaf ;c27 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCaption title, with Chinese title on cover.  aIn English, some appendices contain parallel English and French text.12aCommunicable Disease Controlxlegislation & jurisprudence22aInternational Cooperation22aGlobal Healthxlegislation & jurisprudence 2aLegislation  aAUTH07557nam a2200421 i 9919230192906676DNLM20260223134805.0m     o  d        cr|un#||||||||260102s2020    dcua    ob    000|0 eng    a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK619817  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 2741 aBrooks, Adamc(Behavioral scientist and clinical psychologist),eauthor.10aUsing graphic novels when choosing treatment for alcohol use disorder /cAdam Brooks, Mary Tabit, John S. Cacciola, Kimberly C. Kirby, Lawrence M. Schoen, Alvin Kingcade, David Gastfriend. 1aWashington (DC) :bPatient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute (PCORI),c2020.  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (178 pages)) :billustrations.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aPatient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute final research report  aCaption title.  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 aBACKGROUND: Many patients with alcohol use disorder (AUD) are not provided evidence-based counseling and medication-assisted treatment (MAT) as part of their treatment experience because MAT is a technology that has not been adopted by many typical treatment agencies. Counseling programs are pressed for training resources and time to engage in counselor training. Medicine for patients with AUD is being offered more regularly as programs adopt medication solutions for patients with opioid use disorder; however, many counselors do not have a lot of information about MAT for AUD and in fact are often unaware that medication can enhance outcomes. Similarly, patients are unaware of their treatment options for using medication to assist in their recovery. This randomized controlled trial (RCT) was designed to examine the development and use of a 4-volume graphic novel series designed to assist counselors and patients to engage in a shared decision-making approach regarding the use of medication to address AUD. OBJECTIVES: This study involves the following 4 aims: 1. Aim 1: Adapt and redesign an existing evidence-based toolkit intervention by a patient and provider team into a narrative graphic novel curriculum useful in substance abuse counseling2. Aim 2: Provide training and support to increase patient access to MAT for AUD at a centrally located federally qualified health care center3. Aim 3: Test whether exposure to the toolkit can reduce substance use and HIV risk, while increasing engagement with MAT, by conducting a 6-month RCT of 60 counselors and 350 patients with active AUD enrolled in inpatient and outpatient treatment to determine group differences in (1) substance use and treatment engagement and (2) rates of engagement of AUD medication4. Aim 4: Disseminate the toolkit and study findings METHODS: This study was originally conceived as a fully powered RCT. In phase 1, we formed a patient and counselor team to develop a 4-volume graphic novel curriculum toolkit and expanded access to MAT for patients with AUD at a local health center. However, the phase 1 activities took much longer than expected, and our team struggled to recruit participating treatment sites and patients. In phase 2, our study was stopped early due to project delays and slow recruitment. In phase 2, we conducted a small portion of the randomized trial of the Health Education Toolkit (HET) at 2 community treatment agencies. We randomly assigned 30 counselors to (1) the toolkit condition, in which counselors received brief training to learn to use the toolkit in counseling sessions; or (2) treatment as usual (TAU), a comparable credible training alternative in which counselors received brief training to cover comparable topic areas (without the benefit of the toolkit). At posttraining, toolkit counselors used the toolkit to conduct a 6-week counseling series, and TAU counselors conducted a 6-week series on similar topic areas using their standard counseling approaches. We screened and recruited 49 patients with active AUDs out of the trained counselors' caseloads to participate in the trial. Research staff collected assessments of substance use through self-report and urinalysis at baseline and at 6 weeks and 12 weeks. We used our data to examine some exploratory hypotheses, such as comparing patients from each treatment group on satisfaction and the number of sessions spent discussing MAT for AUD, days of substance use, rates of treatment engagement, and rates of MAT evaluation and initiation. Our team also conducted 2 counselor and 2 patient focus groups consisting of counselors and patients who used the HET. RESULTS: Our project took longer than expected to complete the toolkit revisions, which put our team behind schedule. Additionally, due to concurrent and conflicting quality improvement efforts going on in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, some of our larger sites declined to participate. Our team also struggled to recruit enough patients meeting the criteria for AUD. Due to problems with patient recruitment for all counselors participating in the research, we limited our analysis to counselors (n = 11) from the first treatment site who successfully worked with at least 1 recruited patient, providing our team with 37 patients. There were no observable differences between the HET and TAU conditions regarding the number of patients who reported discussing MAT for AUD with their counselors or the number of sessions these conversations involved. There were also no observable differences between conditions on patient satisfaction or on any substance use or treatment engagement outcome. Patients in the TAU condition were more likely to report actually following up on a MAT evaluation or referral (33% of TAU participants compared with 5.9% of toolkit participants). Qualitative analysis of the toolkit focus groups indicated that users found the toolkit quality to be high and the story lines relatable. CONCLUSIONS: This failed trial of the HET does not allow our team to draw conclusions about the effectiveness of the tool. Participants and counselors reported that it was useful and relatable for stimulating conversations regarding MAT for AUD; however, we were unable to test whether it was an effective strategy to stimulate SDM conversations compared with simply educating counselors and informing them how to make a referral for an evaluation. Our team is planning future research to test situations in which the toolkit would be most useful.  aInstitution Receiving Award: Treatment Research Institute, Inc, at Public Health Management Corporation Original Project Title: Testing the Effectiveness of a Graphic Novel Health Education Curriculum for Patients with Addiction PCORI ID: CDR-1310-07308 ClinicalTrials.gov ID: NCT02378181  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed February 23, 2026).12aAlcohol-Related Disordersxdrug therapy22aGraphic Novels as Topic22aPatient Education as Topicxmethods22aComparative Effectiveness Research 2aRandomized Controlled Trial2 aPatient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aFinal research report (Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute (U.S.))40uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK619817/  aAUTH02323nam a2200349 i 9919230197806676DNLM20260224121202.0m     o  d i      cr|un#||||||||260102s2025    sz      ob   i000|0 eng    a9789240117044q(electronic)  z9789240117051q(print)  a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK619432  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Switzerland00aWQ 2482 aWorld Health Organization,eauthor,eissuing body.10aWHO recommendations on care for women with diabetes during pregnancy. 1aGeneva :bWorld Health Organization,c[2025]  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (xiv, 58 pages))  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 aWHO maternal and perinatal health guidelines are relevant to those providing care and support during pregnancy, labour, childbirth and postnatal periods, in any health-care setting. The primary audience for this guideline includes policy-makers or service providers who are responsible for developing national and local health-care protocols and policies related to care during pregnancy, childbirth and the postnatal period, and those directly providing care to women during pregnancy, including obstetricians, midwives, endocrinologists, nurses, general practitioners, dietitians and diabetes educators, and managers of maternal and child health programmes, in all settings. The guideline will also be useful for developing clinical tools for pre- and in-service training of health workers and health-system strengthening efforts to enhance their delivery of clinical care. It is expected that the guideline will be of interest to professional societies involved in the care of pregnant women; nongovernmental organizations concerned with the promotion of woman-centered maternal care; and implementers of maternal and child health programmes.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed February 24, 2026).12aPregnancy in Diabetics 2aPractice Guideline40uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK619432/  aAUTH01742nam a2200385 i 9919230197606676DNLM20260224121125.0m     o  d i      cr|un#||||||||260102s2025    sz a   |ob   i000|0 eng    a9789240115606q(electronic version)  z9789240115613q(print version)  a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK619447  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Switzerland00aWP 63000aSelected practice recommendations for contraceptive use.  aFourth edition. 1aGeneva :bWorld Health Organization, Human Reproduction Programme,c[2025]  a1 online resource (1 PDF file (xii, 92 pages)) :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographicas references.3 aThis document is part of the process for improving the quality of care in family planning. Selected practice recommendations for contraceptive use (SPR) presents current World Health Organization (WHO) recommendations on how to use contraceptive methods safely and effectively once they are deemed to be medically appropriate.  aIssued also in print.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF title page (viewed February 13, 2026).12aContraceptionxmethods22aContraceptive Agents 2aPractice Guideline2 aSpecial Programme of Research, Development, and Research Training in Human Reproduction (World Health Organization),eissuing body.40uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK619447/  aAUTH01359nam a2200397 i 9919230200106676DNLM20260213104438.0m     o  d        cr|un#||||||||260102s2025    be abc||ob    000|0 eng    a9782930229966  a(DNLM)BKSHLF:NBK616525  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Belgium00aWK 8101 aMagliano, Dianna,eauthor.10aIDF diabetes atlas10aDiabetes atlas /cco-chairs, Dianna J. Magliano, Edward J. Boyko.1 aIDF diabetes atlas1 aI.D.F. diabetes atlas1 aInternational Diabetes Federation diabetes atlas  a11th edition. 1aBrussels :bInternational Diabetes Federation,c2025.  a1 online resource :billustrations, maps, portraits  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references  aDescription based on online resource; title from title screen (viewed February 13, 2026).12aDiabetes Mellitus1 aBoyko, Edward J.,eauthor.2 aInternational Diabetes Federation,eissuing body.40uhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK616525/  aAUTH01924cgm a2200433 i 101774300DNLM20260227160524.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u201211s2020    mdu091|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:31419  a(OCoLC)1248767964  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWC 5032 aWorld AIDS Day (Conference)d(2020 :cOnline)jauthor.10aWorld AIDS Day 2020 :bscience and community working together to prepare for the unexpected /cOffice of AIDS Research, NIH.1 aScience and community working together to prepare for the unexpected 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2020]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 31 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aRecorded December 1, 2020.3 a(CIT): The Office of AIDS Research (OAR) is hosting the NIH World AIDS Day event, "Science and Community Working Together to Prepare for the Unexpected." During the World AIDS Day event, viewers are invited to submit questions to the panelists and speakers.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed April 22, 2021).12aAcquired Immunodeficiency Syndromexepidemiology22aCOVID-19xepidemiology22aIntersectoral Collaboration22aCommunity-Institutional Relations 2aWebcast 2aConference Proceedings2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bOffice of AIDS Research,esponsoring body.40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=40035  aAUTH04931cgm a2200481 i 9918975957806676DNLM20260227165049.0m     o  z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u250104s2024    mdu093|||||||fo|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:35418  aDNLMbengerdad20260227dDNLM  apcc  9United States00aQU 57000aI am the data, the data is me! :bcentering culture and indigenist ethics and principles in building an indigenous reference genome /cKrystal Tsosie, Ph.D., M.P.H., M.A., Alex Brown, Ph.D., M.P.H., FCSANZ, FAHMS Phillip Wilcox, Ph.D. 1a[Bethesda, Md.] :b[National Institutes of Health],c[2024]  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (1 hr., 33 min.)) :bcolor, sound  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNative American Heritage Month  aRecorded November 26, 2024.3 a(CIT): In honor of Native American Heritage Month, the NIH Tribal Health Research Office invites you to a distinguished panel presentation featuring world renown Indigenous genomic researchers and bioethicists as they share their insights in developing cultural and ethical practices for developing an Indigenous reference genome. An Indigenous reference genome is a genomic sequence that represents the genetic material of a specific Indigenous population or community. Unlike standard reference genomes, which may be based on a single or a few individuals from a broader population, an Indigenous reference genome aims to capture the unique genetic diversity and characteristics of a particular Indigenous group. This type of reference is crucial for various research applications, such as medical studies, conservation efforts, and understanding evolutionary history. It helps ensure that genomic analyses are more accurate and relevant for that specific Tribal population given that unique genetic variations and adaptations that are not likely represented in more general reference genomes. Creating an Indigenous reference genome involves direct consent, consultation, and deep collaboration with Indigenous communities to ensure Indigenous research laws are upheld and that ethical considerations and cultural sensitivities are respected throughout the research process. Our Indigenous expert panel will provide invaluable global insights into this complex and vital topic.   Panelists  Krystal Tsosie, Ph.D., M.P.H., M.A. (Dine"/Navajo Nation, USA), Geneticist-bioethicist, Assistant Professor, School of Life Sciences Human Dimensions Associate Director, Biodiversity Knowledge Integration Center Arizona State University  Alex Brown, Ph.D., M.P.H.,(FCSANZ, FAHMS)(Yuin/Djuwin Nation, New South Wales, AUS) Medical doctor and genomics researcher, chronic health among aboriginal populations Director, National Centre for Indigenous Genomics Professor of Indigenous Genomics, Telethon Kids Institute The Australian National University  Phillip Wilcox, Ph.D. (Ngāti Rakaipaaka, Rongomaiwahine, Ngāti Kahungunu ki te Wairoa, NZ)  Quantitative geneticist and bioethicist Associate Professor, Department of Mathematics and Statistics Te Tari Pakarau me Tātauranga University of Otago - Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka  NIH Tribal Health Research Office Theme: "I am the Data, the Data is Me!"  This theme guides our ongoing initiative to develop an Indigenous Data Sovereignty (IDS) Policy--a groundbreaking effort by the NIH, set for release in Spring 2025. The IDS Policy aims to affirm the unique sovereign rights of Tribal Nations in data governance. As part of this commitment, we are conducting Tribal consultations this December, gathering insights from this year"s listening sessions with Tribal leaders.  We welcome you to join this important discussion and hear from our distinguished speakers on November 26. Together, let"s contribute to a deeper understanding of Indigenous perspectives on data sovereignty.  aClosed-captioned.  aDescription based on NIH CIT Videocast website; title from title screen (viewed January 21, 2026).12aGenomicsxethics12aIndigenous Peoplesxgenetics22aGenome, Human22aGenetic Privacyxethics22aGenetic Privacyxlegislation & jurisprudence 2aLecture 2aWebcast1 aTsosie, Krystal,espeaker.1 aBrown, Alex,cPh.D.espeaker.1 aWilcox, Phillip,espeaker.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.).bTribal Health Research Office,esponsoring body.2 aNational Institutes of Health (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNative American Heritage Month scientific lecture40uhttps://videocast.nih.gov/watch=55379  aAUTH01420cgm a2200421 a 101557277DNLM20260227153847.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u110325s2011    mdu370|||||||fs|||vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:16494  a(OCoLC)720560182  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aQZ 2062 aNCI Board of Scientific Advisors.bMeetingn(48th :d2011 :cBethesda, Md.)10aNCI, Board of Scientific Advisors, March 2011 /cNCI.  a[Bethesda, Md. :bNational Institutes of Health,c2011]  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aTitle from title screen.  aStreaming video (6 hr., 10 min. : sd., col.).  aRecorded Mar. 1, 2011.3 a(CIT): The 48th Meeting of the National Cancer Institute Board of Scientific Advisors.  aMode of access: World Wide Web.  aOpen-captioned.12aNeoplasms22aBiomedical Researchxorganization & administration22aUnited States Government Agencies 2aUnited States 2aConference Proceedings 2aWebcast40uhttp://videocast.nih.gov/launch.asp?16494  aAUTH01598cgm a22004454a 101267913DNLM20260227151010.0m        z f      cr|cna||||||||vz|czaz|u060209s2003    mdu261 ||||||fs   vleng    a(DNLM)CIT:11005  a(OCoLC)104748535  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States10aWM 201 aUnited States.bNational Advisory Mental Health Council.10aSeptember 2003 N